  <div class='def'>2. Mars, the planet</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a diminutive of the male given name <a href="bword://Αριστείδης">Αριστείδης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. a diminutive of the male given name <a href="bword://Αριστοτέλης">Αριστοτέλης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>5. a diminutive of the male given name <a href="bword://Βελισσάριος">Βελισσάριος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἄρης.</div><b>Άρκανσας</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) Arkansas (a state in the eastern United States)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) Arkansas (a river in the eastern United States)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English Arkansas.</div><b>Άρνος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Arno (a river in Tuscany)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek Ἄρνος, from Latin Arnus.</div><b>Άρτα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Arta (a town in Epirus, Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Old spelling Ἄρτα, a form of 17th century or older, of unknown etymology, with various theories proposed such as Ancient Greek, or Latin, or Slavic. Probably from the Greek river&#x27;s name Άραχθος, via an intermediary Albanian form that merged -kt- &gt; -tt-.</div><b>Άρτεμη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>standard form of the formal of <a href="bword://Άρτεμις">Άρτεμις</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Older spelling: Ἄρτεμη</div><b>Άρτεμις</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) Artemis, the name of a Greek goddess; the Roman equivalent is Diana.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a female given name, Artemis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἄρτεμις.</div><b>Άσαμ</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Assam (a state of India)</div><b>Άσσηρος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Assiros (a town in Thessaloniki regional unit, Central Macedonia, Greece)</div><b>Άστρος</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Astros (a town in Arcadia regional unit, Peloponnese, Greece)</div><b>Άτλας</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) Atlas</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a satellite of Saturn</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Atlas (a mountain range in northwest Africa)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἄτλας”).</div><b>Άττουδα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Denizli (a city in Denizli Province, Turkey)</div><b>Έβαν</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a transliteration of the English male given name Evan</div><b>Έδεσσα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Edessa (a city in Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἔδεσσα, of unknown etymology..</div><b>Έιμι</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a transliteration of the English female given name Amy</div><b>Έιντζελ</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a transliteration of the English female given name Angel</div><b>Έκτορας</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) Hector</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a male given name, Ektoras, equivalent to English Hector</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derived from Ancient Greek Ἕκτωρ.</div><b>Έλλην</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Έλληνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://Έλληνας">Έλληνας</a>, written in monotonic system</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> As in Ancient Greek Ἕλλην.</div><b>Έλληνας</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (plural Έλληνες, feminine Ελληνίδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Greek or Hellene (a native, citizen or inhabitant of Greece, or someone who has Greek (Hellenic) ancestry)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθαν ένας <b>Έλληνας</b> και μία Ελληνίδα. Είναι οι έλληνες φίλοι μου. - One Greek (man) and one Greek (woman) came. They are my Greek friends.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. for adjectival use, see <a href="bword://έλληνας">έλληνας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἕλλην, from the accusative singular τόν Ἕλληνα. In Hellenistic Christian times: &quot;Gentile, pagan&quot;. In Medieval Greek, the sense was either a &quot;Greek&quot; or a derogatory term for an &quot;idolater&quot;, regaining its &quot;Greek&quot; sense after the 11th century. In Early Modern Greek the ethnonymic terms used instead were Ρωμιός or Γραικός.</div><b>Έμπλα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Ebla</div><b>Έντγκαρ</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Edgar</div><b>Έντι</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a transliteration of the English male given name diminutive Eddie</div><b>Έντουαρντ</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a transliteration of the English male given name Edward</div><b>Ένωση Σοβιετικών Σοσιαλιστικών Δημοκρατιών</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) Union of Soviet Socialist Republics (a former transcontinental country in Europe and Asia (1922–1991), now split into Russia and 14 other countries; short form Σοβιετική Ένωση)</div><b>Έξοδος</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Exodus (a book of the Bible)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek Ἔξοδος, from Ancient Greek ἔξοδος.</div><b>Έξω Μετόχι</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Exometochi (a village in Nicosia district, Cyprus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, έξω + μετόχι.</div><b>Έρικ</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a transliteration of the English male given name Eric</div><b>Έρως</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek) Eros (god of love)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἔρως.</div><b>Έρωτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. erotic love</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://Έρως">Έρως</a></div><b>Ήβη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek) Hebe (goddess of youth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἥβη.</div><b>Ήλιος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (plural Ήλιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sun</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἥλιος.</div><b>Ήπειρος</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Epirus (a historical region and ancient kingdom in Southeast Europe, today split politically between northwestern Greece and southwestern Albania)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Epirus (a geographic region and administrative region in northwest Greece, one of the thirteen peripheries of modern Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἤπειρος.</div><b>Ήρα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine (plural Ήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) Hera, known to the Romans as Juno</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a female given name, Ira, equivalent to English Hera</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Juno, the asteroid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἥρα.</div><b>Ήφαιστος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek) Hephaestus (Greek god of fire and technology)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἥφαιστος, of Pre-Greek origin, and therefore of unknown ultimate meaning.</div><b>Ίαν</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a transliteration of the English male given name Ian</div><b>Ίκαρος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek) Icarus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἴκαρος, from *Ϝίκαρος, from Tyrsenian.</div><b>Ίμπιζα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Ibiza (an island of the Balearic Islands, Spain)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Ibiza (a city and municipality of Ibiza)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Spanish Ibiza, likely via English Ibiza due to the use of the letter ζ (z) instead of θ (th) if it were to be borrowed directly from Spanish. Ultimately from Catalan Eivissa, from Arabic يَابِسَة, from Latin Ebusus, from Phoenician 𐤀𐤉𐤁𐤔𐤌.</div><b>Ίος</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Ios, the Greek island in the Aegean Sea belonging to the Cyclades group</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek Ἴος. Compare to the dialectal Νιος f (Nios).</div><b>Ίσις</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptian) Isis (Egyptian goddess)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek Ἶσις.</div><b>Ό.Έ.Δ.</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>initialism of όπερ έδει δείξαι: QED, quod erat demonstrandum</div><b>Όλγα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a female given name from Russian, equivalent to English Olga</div><b>Όλιβερ</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a transliteration of the English male given name and surname Oliver</div><b>Όλυμπος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Olympus (name of mountains, chiefly, the tallest mountain in Greece and dwelling of the Ancient Greek gods)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Uncertain origin. From Ancient Greek Ὄλυμπος, see there for more.</div><b>Όμαχα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) Omaha (a city in Nebraska, United States)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) Omaha beach (a point for the D-Day landings)</div><b>Όμηρος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Homer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ὅμηρος.</div><b>Όμοδος</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Omodos (a village in Limassol district, Cyprus)</div><b>Όσλο</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Oslo (a county and municipality, the capital city of Norway)</div><b>Όφις</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Serpens (a constellation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄφις.</div><b>Ύδρα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Hydra (a constellation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Hydra (a satellite of Pluto)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Greek) Hydra</div>
  <div class='def'>4. The name of a Greek island.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. and see: ύδρα</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὕδρα. Doublet of βίδρα.</div><b>Ύδρος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Hydrus (a constellation)</div><b>Ύπνος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek) Hypnos, the god of sleep</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ὕπνος.</div><b>Ώλαντ</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Åland</div><b>Α</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek, numeral) The number one (1).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Ancient Greek letter Α (A, “alpha”) derived from the Phoenician letter 𐤀 (ʾ /⁠ʼ⁠/, “aleph”) ()</div>
