  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) a male given name from Hebrew, equivalent to English Adam</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ᾱ̓δᾱ́μ, Biblical Hebrew אָדָם.</div><b>Αδελαΐδα</b>
  <div><i>name</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) Adelaide (a city in South Australia)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) a female given name, equivalent to English Adelaide</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable, usually) Adelaide Island (an island of Marguerite Bay, Antarctic Peninsula, Antarctica)</div><b>Αδελαίδα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://Αδελαΐδα">Αδελαΐδα</a></div><b>Αδελαϊδα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://Αδελαΐδα">Αδελαΐδα</a></div><b>Αδριανούπολη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Hadrianopolis, <a href="bword://Α">Α</a>drianopolis, Edirne (the name for various centres of population)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Edirne (a province of Turkey)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἁδριανούπολις, equivalent to Αδριανού Hadrian”) + πόλη.</div><b>Αδριανούπολις</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Αδριανούπολη">Αδριανούπολη</a></div><b>Αδριανός</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a male given name, Adrianos, from Latin, equivalent to English Adrian</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) a male given name, Adrianos, from Latin, equivalent to English Hadrian</div>
  <div class='ex'>το τείχος του <b>Αδριανού</b> - Hadrian&#x27;s Wall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἁδρῐᾱνός, from Latin Hadriānus.</div><b>Αδριατική</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Adriatic, Adriatic Sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἀδρίας.</div><b>Αδριατική θάλασσα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Adriatic Sea, Adriatic</div><b>Αδριατικής θάλασσας</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://Αδριατική">Αδριατική</a> Θάλασσα</div><b>Αδριατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine Αδριατική, neuter Αδριατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Adriatic</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Αδριατική Θάλασσα (the Adriatic Sea)</div><b>Αδόλφος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male given name, Adolfos, from Latin, equivalent to English Adolphus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Αδόλφος, from Latin Adolphus, from Germanic.</div><b>Αετός</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Aquila (constellation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek Ἀετός (the constallation) as at ἀετός. Compare to the modern αετός.</div><b>Αζέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Ααζέρες, masculine Αζέρος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Azerbaijani (a female person from Azerbaijan or of Azerbaijani ethnicity).</div><b>Αζέρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Ααζέροι, feminine Αζέρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Azerbaijani (a person, usually male, from Azerbaijan or of Azerbaijani ethnicity).</div><b>Αζερικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative letter-case form of <a href="bword://αζερικά">αζερικά</a></div><b>Αζερμπαϊτζάν</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Azerbaijan (a country in the South Caucasus in Asia and Europe)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish آذربایجان</div><b>Αζόρες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the Azores</div><b>Αθήνα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Athens (the capital city of Greece)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>Αθήνα</b> είναι η πρωτεύουσα της Ελλάδας. - Athens is the capital city of Greece.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἀθῆναι.</div><b>Αθίγγανη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αθίγγανες, masculine Αθίγγανος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. A gypsy, a Roma, a member of the Romani people.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) Follower of the Christian sect of Melchizedek.</div><b>Αθίγγανος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (plural Αθίγγανοι, feminine Αθίγγανη or Αθιγγανίδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. A Gypsy, a Rom, a member of the Romani people.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) Follower of the Christian sect of Melchizedek.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek Ἀθίγγανος, Ἀτσίγγανος from privative ἀ- (a-) + the ancient θιγγάνω, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰeyǵʰ-, same source as Sanskrit देग्धि, Latin fingō, Avestan 𐬛𐬀𐬉𐬰𐬀, and Old Armenian դէզ. See τσιγγάνος.</div><b>Αθανάσιος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (feminine Αθανασία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) a male given name, Athanasios, equivalent to English Athanasius</div>
  <div class='ex'>informal, common use: Θανάσης (Thanásis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἀθανάσιος.</div><b>Αθανασάκειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine Αθανασάκεια or Αθανασάκειος, neuter Αθανασάκειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related, belonging or founded by a person with the surname <a href="bword://Αθανασάκης">Αθανασάκης</a>.</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>Αθανασάκειος</b> Όμιλος. - The Athanasakis Society.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Male&#x27;s surname Αθανασάκης formed with Αθανάσιος + -άκης, with suffix for adjectives from Ancient Greek -ειος (-eios, “showing belonging, relation”).</div><b>Αθανασάκης</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (feminine Αθανασάκη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male surname, equivalent to English Athanassakis — held by</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αλέξιος Αθανασάκης, a wealthy merchant of Portaria and benefactor of the Archaeological Museum of Volos</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Αθανάσ(ιος), “Athanasius”) + -άκης</div><b>Αθανασέλης</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (feminine Αθανασέλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male surname, feminine equivalent Αθανασέλη</div>
  <div class='ex'>spelling variant: Αθανασέλλης (Athanaséllis)</div><b>Αθανασέλλης</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (feminine Αθανασέλλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male surname, spelling variant of <a href="bword://Αθανασέλης">Αθανασέλης</a></div><b>Αθανασία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a female given name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine form of Αθανάσιος m, from α- (a-, “absence of”) + θάνατος m</div><b>Αθηνά</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) Athena the Roman goddess Minerva and the daughter of Zeus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a female given name, Athina, equivalent to English Athena</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἀθηνᾶ.</div><b>Αθηναΐς</b>
  <div><i>name</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a female given name from Ancient Greek</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a female given name from Ancient Greek; <a href="bword://Αθηναΐς">Αθηναΐς</a> Νέγκα (born 1974), Greek radio broadcaster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek Ἀθηναΐς</div><b>Αθηναία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αθηναίες, masculine Αθηναίος or Αθηνιώτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Athenian (female citizen or inhabitant of Athens)</div><b>Αθηναίισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αθηναίισσες, masculine Αθηνιώτης or Αθηναίος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Αθηναία">Αθηναία</a> (female citizen or inhabitant of Athens)</div><b>Αθηναίος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Αθηναίοι, feminine Αθηναία or Αθηνιώτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Athenian (male citizen or inhabitant of Athens)</div><b>Αθηνιώτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Αθηνιώτες, feminine Αθηνιώτισσα or Αθηναία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Αθηναίος">Αθηναίος</a> (citizen or inhabitant of Athens)</div><b>Αθηνιώτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αθηνιώτισσες, masculine Αθηνιώτης or Αθηναίος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Αθηναία">Αθηναία</a> (female citizen or inhabitant of Athens)</div><b>Αθιγγανίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αθιγγανίδες, masculine Αθίγγανος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Αθίγγανη">Αθίγγανη</a></div><b>Αθωνίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Αθωνίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Athos (a person from Athos).</div><b>Αιγέας</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek) Aegeus</div><b>Αιγαίο</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short form of <a href="bword://Αιγαίο">Αιγαίο</a> Πέλαγος: Aegean, the Aegean Sea (sea in the eastern Mediterranean)</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα νησιά του Αιγαίου - the Aegean islands</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Αἰγαῖον, by ellipsis of πέλαγος.</div><b>Αιγαίο πέλαγος</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Aegean Sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Αἰγαῖον πέλαγος, from Αἰγαῖος, &quot;of the mythic king of Athens Αἰγεύς&quot; and πέλαγος.</div><b>Αιγαίου πελάγους</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://Αιγαίο">Αιγαίο</a> <a href="bword://πέλαγος">πέλαγος</a></div><b>Αιγαιοπελαγίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Αιγαιοπελαγίτες, feminine Αιγαιοπελαγίτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Aegean (a person, usually male, from the Aegean Sea and the surrounding area)</div><b>Αιγαιοπελαγίτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αιγαιοπελαγίτισσες, masculine Αιγαιοπελαγίτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Aegean (a female person from the Aegean Sea and the surrounding area)</div><b>Αιγυπτία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αιγύπτιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) older form of <a href="bword://Αιγύπτια">Αιγύπτια</a> as in the ancient Αἰγυπτία</div><b>Αιγυπτιακά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αιγυπτιακά">αιγυπτιακά</a></div><b>Αιγυπτιώτης</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (plural Αιγυπτιώτες, feminine Αιγυπτιώτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (demonym) Egyptiot, a Greek of Egypt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. for adjectival use, see <a href="bword://αιγυπτιώτης">αιγυπτιώτης</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Αίγυπτ(ος), “Egypt”) + -ιώτης</div><b>Αιγόκερος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Αιγόκερως">Αιγόκερως</a></div><b>Αιγόκερως</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Capricornus</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Τοξότης είναι δίπλα στον <b>Αιγόκερω</b>. - Sagittarius is next to Capricornus.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Capricorn (both the sign itself and persons born under it)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γεννήθηκα τον Ιανουάριο, άρα είμαι <b>Αιγόκερως</b>. - I was born in January, so I&#x27;m a Capricorn.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek αἰγόκερως, from stem αἰγ- (aig-) of αἴξ + connective -ο- (-o-) + κέρας + Attic declension suffix -ως.</div><b>Αιγύπτια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αιγύπτιες, masculine Αιγύπτιος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Egyptian (a female person from Egypt or of Egyptian ethnicity).</div><b>Αιγύπτιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Αιγύπτιοι, feminine Αιγύπτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Egyptian (a person, usually male, from Egypt or of Egyptian ethnicity).</div><b>Αιθίοπας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Αιθίοπες, feminine Αιθιοπίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Ethiopian (a person, usually male, from Ethiopia or of Ethiopian ethnicity).</div><b>Αιθιοπία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Ethiopia (a country in East Africa)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Αἰθιοπίᾱ.</div><b>Αιθιοπίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αιθιοπίνες, masculine Αιθίοπας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Ethiopian (a female person from Ethiopia or of Ethiopian ethnicity).</div><b>Αικατερίνη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a female given name, equivalent to English Catherine or Katherine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Αἰκατερίνη.</div><b>Αιμίμοντος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine singular</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) Haemimontus (an ancient region and later former Roman province situated in the Balkans, mainly in what is now Bulgaria and Turkey)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Αἱμίμοντος.</div><b>Αινείας</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) Aeneas</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a male given name, Aineias, equivalent to English Aeneas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Αἰνείᾱς.</div><b>Αιολία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Αιολίδα">Αιολίδα</a></div><b>Αιολίδα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Aeolis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Αἰολίς.</div><b>Αισχίνης</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male given name, equivalent to English Aeschines</div><b>Αισχύλος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Aeschylus, a Greek dramatic poet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) a male given name, Aeschylus</div><b>Αιτωλοακαρνανία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a prefecture of Greece, the nome of Aetolia-Acarnania.</div><b>Ακάθιστος</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(the) Akathistos (a Lenten hymn)</div><b>Ακίνδυνος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Monotonic spelling of Ἀκίνδυνος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Monotonic spelling of Koine Greek Ἀκίνδυνος, a name of a historic figure, as in the ancient adjective ἀκῐ́νδῡνος -see modern ακίνδυνος-.</div><b>Ακαδημία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the Platonic Academy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. any Academy</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ακαδημία</b> Αθηνών - the Athens Academy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. name of educational institutions</div>
  <div class='ex'>η Βασιλική <b>Ακαδημία</b> Μουσικής - the Royal Academy of Music</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek Ἀκαδημία from ancient Ἀκαδήμεια. For general sense: semantic loan from French académie &lt; Italian accademia from Latin Academia &lt; Hellenistic Koine Ἀκαδημία.</div><b>Ακρωτήριο Χορν</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Cape Horn</div><b>Ακρωτήριου Χορν</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive of Ακρωτήριο Χορν</div><b>Ακρόπολη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Acropolis (acropolis of Athens)</div><b>Ακτή Ελεφαντοστού</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Ivory Coast, Côte d&#x27;Ivoire (a country in West Africa)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακτή + ελεφαντοστό; calque of French Côte d&#x27;Ivoire.</div><b>Ακτή του Ελεφαντοστού</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Ivory Coast, Côte d&#x27;Ivoire</div><b>Ακτής Ελεφαντοστού</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of Ακτή Ελεφαντοστού</div><b>Ακτής του Ελεφαντοστού</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of Ακτή <a href="bword://του">του</a> Ελεφαντοστού</div><b>Ακταίων</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male given name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derived from Ancient Greek Ἀκταίων</div><b>Αλάριχος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Alaric (Visigoth king)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a male given name</div><b>Αλάσκα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alaska (a state of the United States, formerly a territory)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English Alaska, from Aleut alaxsxaq.</div><b>Αλέκος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a diminutive of the male given name <a href="bword://Αλέξανδρος">Αλέξανδρος</a>, equivalent to English Alex, Alec, or Xan</div><b>Αλέξανδρος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male given name, Alexandros, equivalent to English Alexander</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Ἀλέξανδρος.</div><b>Αλαμινός</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a village in Larnaca district, Cyprus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare Turkish Aleminyo.</div><b>Αλαμπάμα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alabama (a state of the United States)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English Alabama.</div><b>Αλβέρτος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male given name, equivalent to English Albert</div><b>Αλβανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αλβανές, masculine Αλβανός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Albanian (a female person from Albania or of Albanian ethnicity).</div><b>Αλβανία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Albania (a country in Southeast Europe)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek Ἀλβανός, from Latin Albanus.</div><b>Αλβανίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αλβανίδες, masculine Αλβανός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Albanian (a female person from Albania or of Albanian ethnicity)</div><b>Αλβανικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative letter-case form of <a href="bword://αλβανικά">αλβανικά</a></div><b>Αλβανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Αλβανοί, feminine Αλβανίδα or Αλβανή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Albanian (a person, usually male, from Albania or of Albanian ethnicity).</div><b>Αλβιόνα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Albion</div>
  <div class='ex'>η πονηρή <b>Αλβιόνα</b> - perfidious Albion</div><b>Αλγέρι</b>
