  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French Algérie, from Arabic اَلْجَزَائِر.</div><b>Αλγερινή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural Αλγερινές, masculine Αλγερινός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Algerian (a female person from Algeria or of Algerian ethnicity).</div><b>Αλγερινός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural Αλγερινοί, feminine Αλγερινή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Algerian (a person, usually male, from Algeria or of Algerian ethnicity).</div><b>Αλεξάνδρα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a female given name, the Greek form of Alexandra</div><b>Αλεξάνδρεια</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine (plural Αλεξάνδρειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (usually) Alexandria (a city in Egypt)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (usually) Alexandria (a governorate of Egypt)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (usually) any one of the ancient cities named &quot;Alexandrias&quot; founded by Alexander the Great, bearing his name</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (uncommon) Alexandria (any other town so named)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (uncommon) Alexandria (town in Imathia in Greece)</div>
  <div class='ex'>(name before 1953)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek Ἀλεξάνδρεια -see adjective Ἀλεξάνδρειος- from the ancient name Ἀλέξανδρος. The Egyptian city is named for and by its founder Alexander the Great.</div><b>Αλεξανδρούπολη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alexandroupoli (a city in Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Αλέξανδρος + πόλη. Named not for Alexander the Great but for King Alexander of Greece, who visited the town in 1920. Until that point, it had the Turkish name Dedeağaç (in Greek: Δεδεαγάτς).</div><b>Αλεξανιάν</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) a surname from Armenian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Armenian Ալեքսանյան (Alekʻsanyan).</div><b>Αλεξόπουλος</b>
