  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Εὐτῠχῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>Ευτύχιος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (usually uncountable, plural Ευτύχιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) a surname</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) a male given name, Eftychios, equivalent to English Eutychius</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Εὐτύχιος. From εὖ + τύχη + -ιος (-ios)</div><b>Ευφροσύνη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine (plural Ευφροσύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) Euphrosyne, one of the three Charites and the goddess of joy.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a female given name, Efrosyni, equivalent to English Euphrosyne</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Any of the several saints whose feast day in the Greek Orthodox tradition is 25 September.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek Εὐφροσύνη from εὐφροσύνη.</div><b>Εφτάνησα</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Επτάνησα">Επτάνησα</a></div><b>Εωσφόρος</b>
  <div><i>name</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Lucifer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Morning Star, the planet Venus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from Ancient Greek Ἑωσφόρος, from ἕως + φέρω.</div><b>Εύα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Eve</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a female given name, Eva, from Hebrew, equivalent to English Eve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Εὔα, from Biblical Hebrew חַוָּה.</div><b>Εύβοια</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Euboea (second largest island in Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Εὔβοια.</div><b>Εύη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Eve</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a female given name, Evi, variant of <a href="bword://Εύα">Εύα</a>, equivalent to English Eve</div><b>Εύξεινος Πόντος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Black Sea, Euxine Sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Εὔξεινος Πόντος, a euphemistic renaming of the Ἄξενος Πόντος.</div><b>Ζ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Greek numeral representing the number seven (7), typically romanized as the Roman numeral VII.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Αντίοχος Ζʹ Σιδήτης ή Ευεργέτης ήταν αυτοκράτορας της Δυναστειας των Σελευκιδών, βασιλέων της Συρίας κατά την ελληνιστική περίοδο.
Antiochus VII the Sidetan or Do-Gooder was an emperor of the Dynasty of the Seleucids and king of Syria during the Hellenistic period.</div><b>Ζάγκρεμπ</b>
