  <div class='def'>Thira, Santorini (an island in the Cyclades, Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Θήρᾱ.</div><b>Θεία Κοινωνία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Holy Communion, Eucharist</div><b>Θεοδοσία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Theodosia, a female given name</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Feodosia (a port and resort city and raion in Crimea, internationally recognized as part of Ukraine but de facto in Russia)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Θεοδοσία.</div><b>Θεοδωράκης</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a diminutive of the male given name <a href="bword://Θεόδωρος">Θεόδωρος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>more common: Θοδωράκης (Thodorákis)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a male surname, feminine equivalent Θεοδωράκη</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Θεόδωρ(ος), “Theodore”) + -άκης</div><b>Θεοδωρίδης</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine (feminine Θεοδωρίδη or Θεοδωρίδου)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male surname, Theodoridis</div><b>Θεοδώρα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a female given name, Theodora</div>
  <div class='def'>2. any of the several saints whose feast day in the Greek Orthodox tradition is 12 February</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Female form of Ancient Greek Θεόδωρος.</div><b>Θεοδώριχος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Theodoric, the male given name.</div><b>Θεοτόκος</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>God-bearer; Mother of God; Holy Mother of God; Theotokos, a sacred title given to the Virgin Mary as the mother of Jesus Christ</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek Θεοτόκος &lt; θεοτόκος, also Medieval, &lt; θεο- (theo-) Koine Greek Θεός, ancient θεός + Ancient Greek τόκος from τίκτω.</div><b>Θεοφάνια</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Theophany</div><b>Θεοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a male given name, Theoforos, equivalent to English Theophorus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Theophorus, a name for St Ignatius</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Θεοφόρος.</div><b>Θεσσαλία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Thessaly (a historical region and modern administrative region of north-central Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Θεσσαλία.</div><b>Θεσσαλονίκη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Thessaloniki (a port city, the capital of Central Macedonia, in northern Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Θεσσαλονίκη.</div><b>Θευδέριχος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a male given name, Thevderichos, from Gothic, equivalent to Latin Theodericus or English Theoderic, Theodoric, or Derek</div>
  <div class='ex'>Theoderic or Theodoric:</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a male given name, Thevderichos, from Gothic, equivalent to Latin Theodericus or English Theoderic, Theodoric, or Derek; a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Theodoric the Great (c. 454–526 CE), King of the Ostrogoths (from 475), of the Ostrogothic Kingdom of Italy (from 493), and of the Visigoths (from 511)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a male given name, Thevderichos, from Gothic, equivalent to Latin Theodericus or English Theoderic, Theodoric, or Derek; a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Theodoric I (c. 390–451 CE), King of the Visigothic Kingdom from 418 to 451</div>
  <div class='def'>4. a male given name, Thevderichos, from Gothic, equivalent to Latin Theodericus or English Theoderic, Theodoric, or Derek; a male given name of historical usage, notably borne by Theodoric II (c. 426–466 CE), King of the Visigothic Kingdom from 453 to 466</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek Θευδέριχος via Late Latin Theodericus from a Germanic origin, likely via Gothic 𐌸𐌹𐌿𐌳𐌰𐍂𐌴𐌹𐌺𐍃 from Proto-Germanic *Þeudarīks, from *þeudō (“people”) + *rīks (“king”).</div><b>Θεόδουλος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male given name, Theodoulos</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Θεόδουλος.</div><b>Θεόδωρος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a male given name, Theodoros, equivalent to English Theodore</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Any of the several saints whose feast day in the Greek Orthodox tradition is 17 February.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *dʰeh₁-
Proto-Indo-European *-s
Proto-Indo-European *dʰéh₁s
Proto-Hellenic *tʰehós
Ancient Greek θεός
Proto-Indo-European *deh₃-
Proto-Indo-European *-r̥
Proto-Indo-European *dóh₃r̥
Proto-Indo-European *-os
Proto-Indo-European *déh₃rom
Proto-Hellenic *dṓron
Ancient Greek δῶρον
Ancient Greek Θεόδωρος
Greek Θεόδωρος
Inherited from Ancient Greek Θεόδωρος, from θεός + δῶρον.</div><b>Θεός</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Judaism, usually, with definite article) God (single deity of various monotheistic religions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>Θεός</b> έδωσε τις δέκα εντολές στον Μωυσή. - God gave the ten commandments to Moses.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Θεός.</div><b>Θεός σχωρέσ&#x27; την</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>God rest her soul (said when a dead person is mentioned)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μάνα μου, <b>Θεός σχωρέσ’ την</b>, της άρεσαν τα ανέκδοτα. - My mother, God rest her soul, liked jokes.</div><b>Θεός σχωρέσ&#x27; τον</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>God rest his soul (said when a dead person is mentioned)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας μου, <b>Θεός σχωρέσ’ τον</b>, του άρεσε πολύ να πίνει. - My father, God rest his soul, loved to drink.</div><b>Θηρεσία</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a female given name, Thiresia, equivalent to English Teresa or Theresa</div><b>Θιβέτ</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Tibet (a geographic region in Central Asia, the homeland of the Tibetan people)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Tibet (an autonomous region of China)</div><b>Θοδωρής</b>
