  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French Aude.</div><b>Ωρίων</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) formal type of Ωρίωνας; Orion, the hunter killed by Artemis.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. formal type of Ωρίωνας; Orion, the constellation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ὠρίων.
Ancient Greeks claim the name to be derived from ουρον, meaning urine or semen, as an explanation for the means of his conception. However, this is still debated, and might remain as folk etymology</div><b>Ωρίωνος</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the ancient genitive of <a href="bword://Ωρίων">Ωρίων</a>) modern: Ωρίωνα, genitive singular of Ωρίωνας)</div><b>Ωραία Κοιμωμένη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Sleeping Beauty (fairy tale and character)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adjective ωραίος and the feminine κοιμωμένη of the ancient participle κοιμώμενος of contracted passive verb κοιμῶμαι—in Modern Greek κοιμάμαι—of active κοιμάω.</div><b>Ωραίας Κοιμωμένης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of Ωραία Κοιμωμένη</div><b>Ωρολόγιον</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter</i></div>
