  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Horomazes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ὡρομάζης.</div><b>ά.</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://άλλως">άλλως</a>: otherwise</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://άρθρο">άρθρο</a>: article</div><b>άβαθα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shallowly, not deeply</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shallows, shallow waters</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί λέτε συνέχεια «ρηχά είναι, μπείτε» αφού εδώ δεν είναι <b>άβαθα</b>; - Why do you keep saying &quot;Hey it&#x27;s shallow, get in&quot; since this is no shallows?</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άβαθος">άβαθος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>compare to αβαθή (avathí) of the more formal αβαθής (avathís)</div><b>άβαθος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβαθη, neuter άβαθο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a demotic form &amp; inflection of the more formal <a href="bword://αβαθής">αβαθής</a></div><b>άβακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άβακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abacus (for arithmetic operations)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) abacus (sand tray used in mathematics)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. abacus (structure at the top of a classical column)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. type of printing plate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄβαξ from the accusative singular &quot;τόν ἄβακα&quot;.</div><b>άβαλτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβαλτη, neuter άβαλτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not worn, unworn, new (of clothing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. out of position, unplaced, not placed</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unplanted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + βαλτός from βαλ- (val-) of verb βάζω + -τός.</div><b>άβατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άβατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enclosure (An area in a convent, etc with restricted access)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://άβατος">άβατος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://άβατος">άβατος</a></div><b>άβατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβατη, neuter άβατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unpassable, impassable, inaccessible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sacrosanct</div>
  <div class='def'>3. untrodden</div><b>άβαφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβαφη, neuter άβαφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. uncoloured, undyed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unpainted, unpolished</div>
  <div class='def'>3. not made-up, not wearing make-up</div>
  <div class='def'>4. untempered</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + βάφω.</div><b>άβαφτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβαφτη, neuter άβαφτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άβαφος">άβαφος</a></div><b>άβγαλτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβγαλτη, neuter άβγαλτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. innocent, inexperienced, unsophisticated</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not risen, not having appeared</div>
  <div class='def'>3. not taken off, still on (clothing, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. unsprouted, ungerminated</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) naive, green, wet behind the ears</div><b>άβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβιος or άβια, neuter άβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lifeless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “non-”) + βίος.</div><b>άβλαβος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβλαβη, neuter άβλαβο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undamaged, unhurt, unharmed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (“prefix for opposites”) + βλάβη (“damage, harm”) + -ος (“suffix”). See αβλαβ(ής).</div><b>άβλαπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβλαπτη, neuter άβλαπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undamaged, unhurt, unharmed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἄβλαπτος, synonym of ἀβλαβής. Also see άβλαφτος.</div><b>άβλαφτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβλαφτη, neuter άβλαφτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undamaged, unhurt, unharmed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἄβλαφτος from Koine Greek ἄβλαπτος (with [pt &gt; ft]), synonym of ἀβλαβής.</div><b>άβολα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άβολος">άβολος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>awkwardly, uncomfortably, inconveniently</div><b>άβολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβολη, neuter άβολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>awkward, uncomfortable, inconvenient</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός ο καναπές είναι πολύ <b>άβολος</b>. - This sofa is very uncomfortable.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἄβολος, with semantic shift based on reinterpretation of the second component, βολή, with the newer sense of &quot;convenience&quot;.</div><b>άβουλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβουλη, neuter άβουλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irresolute, indecisive, weak-willed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek άβουλος. By surface analysis, α- (a-) + βουλ(ή), “will”) + -ος (-os). Compare Ancient Greek ἄβουλος.</div><b>άβραστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβραστη, neuter άβραστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. uncooked, unboiled, raw</div>
  <div class='def'>2. undercooked</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unfermented (wine)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + βραστος (vrastos, “boiled”).</div><b>άβρεχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβρεχη, neuter άβρεχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άβρεχτος">άβρεχτος</a></div><b>άβρεχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβρεχτη, neuter άβρεχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dry, unwetted, not wet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dry, not raining, rainless</div><b>άβροχα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rainlessly, drily, dryly</div><b>άβροχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άβροχη, neuter άβροχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rainless, not raining, dry</div><b>άβυσσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άβυσσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abyss, deep chasm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abyss, abyssal zone</div>
  <div class='def'>3. chaos</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) deep despair when facing the abyss of extinction or death</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἄβυσσος. For the scientific term, semantic loan from English abyss or French abysse.</div><b>άβυσσος η ψυχή του ανθρώπου</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>there&#x27;s nowt so queer as folk (people can behave very oddly sometimes)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ξαφνικά του ήρθε να παραιτηθεί από την δουλειά του και να ταξιδέψει. Τι να πει κανείς, <b>άβυσσος η ψυχή του ανθρώπου</b>! - He suddenly got the urge to quit his job and go travelling. What can one say, there&#x27;s nowt so queer as folk!</div><b>άγαλμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγάλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄγαλμα.</div><b>άγαμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγαμη, neuter άγαμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. celibate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unmarried, single</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bachelor</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άγαμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. celibate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bachelor</div><b>άγαν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>too much, in excess</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄγαν.</div><b>άγανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άγανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. awn, beard (the whisker(s) on the ear of grasses and cereal crops)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fine fishbone</div><b>άγαρμπα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clumsily, awkwardly, inelegantly</div><b>άγαρμπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγαρμπη, neuter άγαρμπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ungainly, clumsy, awkward, lummox</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inelegant, unshapely</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + γάρμπος.</div><b>άγγειλα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of ήγγειλα, first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://αγγέλλω">αγγέλλω</a>, found in compounds</div><b>άγγελμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγγέλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. news</div>
  <div class='def'>2. announcement</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sign</div><b>άγγελος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άγγελοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. angel</div>
  <div class='ex'>φύλακας <b>άγγελος</b> - guardian angel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. messenger, news bearer, envoy, herald</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) a loved one</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άγγελέ</b> μου - my sweetheart</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) a selfless person</div>
  <div class='def'>5. Chondrichthyes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Sense &quot;angel&quot;, inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἄγγελος, a semantic loan from Hebrew מַלְאָךְ (mal&#x27;ach, “angel” or “messenger of God”).
Sense &quot;messenger&quot;, learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἄγγελος.</div><b>άγγιαγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγγιάγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άγγιγμα">άγγιγμα</a></div><b>άγγιαχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγγιαχτη, neuter άγγιαχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άγγιχτος">άγγιχτος</a></div><b>άγγιγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγγίγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. touch (the effect or sensation produced)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. caress</div><b>άγγισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγγίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άγγιγμα">άγγιγμα</a></div><b>άγγιχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγγιχτη, neuter άγγιχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. untouched; untouchable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unwounded</div><b>άγγλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άγγλοι, feminine αγγλίδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in adjectival use) Englishman</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γνώρισα έναν Άγγλο και γίναμε φίλοι. Ο <b>άγγλος</b> φίλος μου […] - I met an Englishman and we became friends. My English friend […]</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Άγγλος.</div><b>άγδαρτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγδαρτη, neuter άγδαρτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unflayed, unskinned (intact, without the skin removed)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ungrazed</div><b>άγδυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγδυτη, neuter άγδυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clothed, dressed, not stripped</div><b>άγευστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγευστη, neuter άγευστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tasteless, insipid (food, drink, etc)</div><b>άγημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. detachment, group of troops</div>
  <div class='def'>2. landing party</div><b>άγια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://άγιος">άγιος</a>:; feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://άγιος">άγιος</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>well</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of άγιο</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sanctuary especially in plural, in the phrase</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα άγια των αγίων</div><b>άγιασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) sanctification</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) canonisation</div><b>άγιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγία or άγια, neuter άγιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. holy, saintly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Saint</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Saint; used in the names of churches and places → <a href="bword://Άγιος">Άγιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. address for priests</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) a pious person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἅγιος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άγιοι, feminine αγία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. saint</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γιαγιά μου διάβαζε βίους <b>αγίων</b>. - My grandmother used to read Lives of Saints.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. saint; for names of Saints see → <a href="bword://Άγιος">Άγιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) person with a saint&#x27;s characteristics (patience, love, piousness)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δάσκαλός μου είναι ένας <b>άγιος</b>. Πώς με αντέχει; Κάνω συνεχώς λάθη. - My teacher is a saint. How does he put up with me? I keep making mistakes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἅγιος.</div><b>άγιος άρτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>communion wafer, sacramental bread, host</div><b>άγκιστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άγκιστρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hook</div>
  <div class='def'>2. surgical instrument</div>
  <div class='def'>3. &quot;{ }&quot; braces, curly brackets</div>
  <div class='def'>4. fish hook</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄγκιστρον.</div><b>άγκυρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άγκυρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anchor</div>
  <div class='ex'>ρίχνω <b>άγκυρα</b> - to drop anchor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄγκυρα.</div><b>άγλυκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγλυκη, neuter άγλυκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unsweetened, not sweet enough</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) tasteless, insipid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + γλυκός.</div><b>άγλωσσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. aglossal, tongueless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. speechless (unable to speak your mother tongue)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. having a speech defect (aphasic, stammering, etc)</div><b>άγν.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://άγνωστος">άγνωστος</a>: uncertain</div><b>άγναντος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγναντη, neuter άγναντο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prominent, high up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prominent, obvious</div><b>άγνεστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγνεστη, neuter άγνεστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unspun (of wool, etc)</div><b>άγνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) ignorance, unfamiliarity, unawareness, unbeknownst</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εν <b>αγνοία</b> μου πούλησε το αμάξι μου. - Unbeknownst to me, he sold my car.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) absent without leave, AWOL (a military member who is absent without an excuse and has not been declared a deserter yet)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄγνοια.</div><b>άγνωμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγνωμη, neuter άγνωμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prudent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. indecisive</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unopinionated</div><b>άγνωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγνωρη, neuter άγνωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rare) unrecognisable, unfamiliar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) altered beyond recognition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἄγνωρος, from ἀ- (a-, “un-”) + γνωρίζω.</div><b>άγνωστης ταυτότητας ιπτάμενα αντικείμενα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of άγνωστης ταυτότητας ιπτάμενο <a href="bword://αντικείμενο">αντικείμενο</a></div><b>άγνωστης ταυτότητας ιπτάμενο αντικείμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άγνωστης ταυτότητας ιπτάμενα αντικείμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unidentified flying object (literally: of unknown identity flying object)</div><b>άγνωστοι αι βουλαί του Κυρίου</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) God works in mysterious ways (a seemingly unfortunate or unfavourable situation or change may be beneficial later or in the long run)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ἄγνωστοι αἱ βουλαί τοῦ Κυρίου.</div><b>άγνωστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγνωστη, neuter άγνωστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(also) unknown, strange</div>
  <div class='ex'>μνημείο του <b>άγνωστου</b> στρατιώτη - memorial to the unknown soldier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ιnherited from Ancient Greek ἄγνωστος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άγνωστοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stranger (a person one does not know)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unknown</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ιnherited from Ancient Greek ἄγνωστος.</div><b>άγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be led</div><b>άγονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγονη, neuter άγονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>infertile, barren, sterile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄγονος.</div><b>άγουρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγουρη, neuter άγουρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unripe, green</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άγουρο</b> συκαλάκι - unripe fig</div>
  <div class='def'>2. immature</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άγουρο</b> παιδάκι - immature child</div>
  <div class='def'>3. inexperienced</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄωρος, from ἀ- (a-, “un-”) + ὥρα.</div><b>άγουστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγουστη, neuter άγουστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tasteless, gross</div><b>άγρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άγρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) hunting, the hunt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) canvassing, soliciting (at elections)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἄγρα.</div><b>άγραφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγραφη, neuter άγραφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blank</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unwritten</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unspoken (not formally articulated or stated)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄγραφος.</div><b>άγραφτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγραφτη, neuter άγραφτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άγραφος">άγραφος</a></div><b>άγρια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://άγριος">άγριος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άγριος">άγριος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wildly, savagely, fiercely</div><b>άγριο κύμινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγριοκύμινο">αγριοκύμινο</a></div><b>άγριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγρια, neuter άγριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of animals) undomesticated, untamed, feral, wild</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of plants) wild, uncultivated</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of persons) uncouth, unsociable, uncivilised (UK), uncivilized (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄγριος.</div><b>άγρυπνα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>watchfully, vigilantly</div><b>άγρυπνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άγρυπνη, neuter άγρυπνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. watchful, awake</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sleepless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄγρῠπνος.</div><b>άγρωστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άγρωστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bentgrass, couch grass (any of numerous reedy grass species of the genus Agrostis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French agrostis, from Ancient Greek ἄγρωστις.</div><b>άγχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγχώνομαι">αγχώνομαι</a></div><b>άγχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άγχη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stress, strain, anguish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pathological anxiety</div>
  <div class='def'>3. something that causes stress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the ancient verb ἄγχ(ω)) + ending for neuter nouns -ος (-os). Compare the modern verb άγχομαι.</div><b>άγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lead</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to force against the will</div>
  <div class='ex'>expression: άγω και φέρω, (especially in the passive) άγομαι και φέρομαι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄγω, from Proto-Hellenic *ágō, from Proto-Indo-European *h₂éǵeti, from the root *h₂eǵ-.</div><b>άδαρτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδαρτη, neuter άδαρτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unbeaten, unhit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unchurned (milk-butter)</div><b>άδεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άδειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. permission</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leave of absence</div>
  <div class='def'>3. permit</div>
  <div class='def'>4. licence (UK, Australasia, Canada), license (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄδεια, with semantic loan from French permission, permis, licence.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://άδειος">άδειος</a>:; nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://άδειος">άδειος</a>:; nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>άδεια διαμονής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άδειες διαμονής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>residence permit</div><b>άδεια οδήγησης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άδειες οδήγησης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>driver&#x27;s license</div><b>άδεια οδηγήσεως</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άδειες οδηγήσεως)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άδεια">άδεια</a> οδήγησης</div><b>άδειασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αδειάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>depletion, emptying</div><b>άδειες οδήγησης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άδεια">άδεια</a> οδήγησης</div><b>άδειες οδηγήσεως</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άδεια">άδεια</a> οδηγήσεως</div><b>άδειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδεια, neuter άδειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>empty</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το κουτί είναι <b>άδειο</b>. - The box is empty.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄδειος.</div><b>άδειπνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδειπνη, neuter άδειπνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undined, without supper</div><b>άδενδρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδενδρη, neuter άδενδρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άδεντρος">άδεντρος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See άδεντρος. Here, the ancient pronunciation [nd] of &lt; νδ &gt; of the Hellenistic Koine Greek ἄδενδρος is transformed to [nð] due to formal correction.</div><b>άδεντρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδεντρη, neuter άδεντρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>treeless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἄδενδρος with the ancient pronunciation of &lt; νδ &gt; as [nd] retained. Morphologically, from ά- + δέντρο. Also see άδενδρος.</div><b>άδετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδετη, neuter άδετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loose, not tied, undone</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα κορδόνια σου είναι άδετα. - His shoelaces are undone.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unbound (pages, book, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. laceless (shoes, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. untied, loose (animal)</div><b>άδηλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδηλη, neuter άδηλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. latent (existing or present but concealed or inactive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dubious</div>
  <div class='def'>3. uncertain, unknown</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄδηλος.</div><b>άδικα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unfairly, unjustly, wrongly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in vain, for no reason</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άδικο">άδικο</a></div><b>άδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wrong, injustice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. error</div><b>άδικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδικη, neuter άδικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unfair, wrongful, unjust</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄδικος.</div><b>άδιωχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδιωχτη, neuter άδιωχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unexpelled, not dismissed, not deported</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not kicked out, not sacked</div><b>άδολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδολη, neuter άδολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sincere, guileless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pure, unadulterated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄδολος.</div><b>άδοξος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδοξη, neuter άδοξο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inglorious, without fame, without glory, without pride</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “un-”) + δόξα.</div><b>άδοτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδοτη, neuter άδοτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ungiven</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unpaid (dowry)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unsold (goods)</div><b>άδουλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδουλη, neuter άδουλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unemployed, not working, unoccupied</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (obsolete) not owning slaves</div><b>άδραγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αδράγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grasp, grip</div><b>άδροσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδροση, neuter άδροσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unrefreshed</div><b>άδυτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άδυτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sanctuary, sanctum</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα <b>άδυτα</b> των <b>αδύτων</b> - holy of holies, inner sanctum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) innermost depths</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://άδυτος">άδυτος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://άδυτος">άδυτος</a></div><b>άδυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδυτη, neuter άδυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inaccessible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun) sanctuary (in temple, church, etc)</div><b>άδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ᾄδω.</div><b>άδωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άδωρη, neuter άδωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>useless (especially of gifts)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δώρο άδωρο (useless gift, white elephant)</div><b>άε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://άι">άι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://αϊ">αϊ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ἄε from Ancient Greek ἄγε sg (with deletion of ‑γ-) of verb ἄγω (“I lead”). Also see etymologies of άντε, άιντε, άμε.</div><b>άεργος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άεργη, neuter άεργο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unemployed, not working, jobless, idle</div><b>άζευτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άζευτη, neuter άζευτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unyoked, uncoupled, unharnessed</div><b>άζουμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άζουμη, neuter άζουμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>without juice</div><b>άζυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άζυμη, neuter άζυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unleavened, without yeast</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άζυμος</b> άρτος - unleavened bread</div><b>άζωστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άζωστη, neuter άζωστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beltless, ungirt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not roped off</div><b>άζωτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural άζωτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) nitrogen</div><b>άζωτον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://άζωτο">άζωτο</a></div><b>άηχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άηχη, neuter άηχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. silent, noiseless, soundless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. voiceless, unvoiced</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>άηχα</b> σύμφωνα της νεοελληνικής είναι τα [p t k c], [f x ç θ s] και [ts]. - The voiceless consonants of Modern Greek are [p t k c], [f x ç θ s] and [ts].</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἄηχος. By surface analysis, ά- + ήχος.</div><b>άθαφτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθαφτη, neuter άθαφτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unburied</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἄθαφτος, from the Ancient Greek ἄθαπτος with [pt] &gt; [ft]</div><b>άθελα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άθελος">άθελος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with personal enclitic) unintentionally, unwittingly, without meaning to, inadvertently</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Γιασμίν με πλήγωσε <b>άθελά της</b>. - Jasmine hurt me without meaning to.</div><b>άθελος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθελη, neuter άθελο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unintentional, unwilling, involuntary</div><b>άθεος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθεη, neuter άθεο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. godless, atheist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. atheist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄθεος.</div><b>άθερμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθερμη, neuter άθερμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unheated or unwarmed; not having been warmed or heated up.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. feverless or apyretic; not running a fever.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄθερμος. By surface analysis, α- (a-, negationary prefix) + -θερμος (-thermos) or θερμός.</div><b>άθερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθερη, neuter άθερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unreaped, unmown, standing</div><b>άθικτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθικτη, neuter άθικτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. untouched</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intact, undamaged, unscathed</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unaffected</div>
  <div class='def'>4. untouched, uneaten (food on table)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. untouched, not discussed (item on agenda)</div><b>άθιχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθιχτη, neuter άθιχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άθικτος">άθικτος</a></div><b>άθλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αθλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sport</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἄθλημα.</div><b>άθληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αθλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gymnastics, PE, physical education, PT, physical training</div>
  <div class='def'>2. athletics, exercise</div><b>άθλιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθλια, neuter άθλιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. miserable, wretched, abject</div>
  <div class='def'>2. squalid</div>
  <div class='def'>3. forlorn</div>
  <div class='def'>4. destitute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄθλιος.</div><b>άθλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άθλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. feat, accomplishment</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>άθλοι</b> του Ηρακλή - the labours of Heracles</div>
  <div class='def'>2. exploit, contest</div>
  <div class='def'>3. task</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἆθλος.</div><b>άθολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθολη, neuter άθολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not blurred, clear</div><b>άθραυστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθραυστη, neuter άθραυστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unbreakable, shatterproof</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άθραυστο</b> γυαλί - unbreakable glass</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) steadfast</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare) unbroken</div><b>άθρησκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθρησκη, neuter άθρησκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unreligious, not religious</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) irreligious</div><b>άθροιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αθροίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>addition, adding up, summation</div><b>άθροισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αθροίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sum, total (of a list of numbers)</div>
  <div class='ex'>γενικό <b>άθροισμα</b> - sum total</div><b>άθρωποι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rare spelling of άνθρωποι</div><b>άθυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άθυμη, neuter άθυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>despondent, dispirited, depressed</div><b>άθυρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αθύρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. toy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) plaything</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἄθυρμα.</div><b>άι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, informal, offensive) go!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άι</b> στο διάβολο! - Go to the devil! (to hell)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ow, ouch, argh (on feeling pain, surprise, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ἄε from Ancient Greek ἄγε sg (with deletion of ‑γ-) of verb ἄγω (“I lead”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of Αϊ</div><b>άι γαμήσου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άντε">άντε</a> γαμήσου</div><b>άι σιχτίρ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(offensive, vulgar) fuck off, piss off, bugger off, get lost</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άι σιχτίρ</b> μωρέ, που θα μας πεις και τι θα κάνουμε! - Fuck off, would you, you have the nerve to tell us what to do!</div><b>άιβαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ajvar, a starter dish from peppers, found in the cuisines of Southern Europe, Turkey and Cyprus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Serbo-Croatian ajvar.</div><b>άιντε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άντε">άντε</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Either from fusion of άι and άντε, or borrowed from Ottoman Turkish هایده (hayde), هایدی (haydi).
Also see etymologies of άντε, άμε, άι.</div><b>άκαιρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκαιρη, neuter άκαιρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. untimely</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mistimed</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ill-timed</div><b>άκακος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκακη, neuter άκακο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. harmless</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το φάρμακο είναι <b>άκακο</b>, δεν έχει παρενέργειες. - This medicine is harmless, it has no side effects.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) harmless</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>άκακος</b>, σωστό αρνί. - He is harmless, a real lamb.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκᾰκος. Morphologically, ά- + κακός.</div><b>άκαμπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκαμπτη, neuter άκαμπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflexible, rigid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) inflexible, immovable, steadfast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἄκαμπτος. Morphologically, from ά- + κάμπτω καμπτ- + -ος (-os). The figurative sense, semantic loan from French inflexible.</div><b>άκανθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άκανθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal, literary) formal of <a href="bword://αγκάθι">αγκάθι</a>: thorn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) obstacle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sharp boney projection</div>
  <div class='def'>4. acanthus-like adornment (in Corinthian style)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄκανθα f. Sense for architecture, semantic loan from French acanthe &lt; Latin acanthus from Ancient Greek ἄκανθος m for which also see άκανθος m. Also see αγκάθι n.</div><b>άκανθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άκανθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acanthus (plant and flower)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. acanthus-like adornment (in Corinthian style)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἄκανθος, from Ancient Greek ἄκανθα.</div><b>άκαπνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκαπνη, neuter άκαπνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smokeless, non-smoking</div>
  <div class='def'>2. peaceful, tranquil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκαπνος.</div><b>άκαρδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκαρδη, neuter άκαρδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heartless (without feeling)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + καρδος (kardos, “heart”).</div><b>άκαρπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκαρπη, neuter άκαρπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barren, unfruitful, fruitless, infertile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) fruitless, unsuccessful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄκαρπος. By surface analysis, ά- + (καρπός) καρπ- karp-, “fruit”) + -ος.</div><b>άκατος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άκατοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. launch, longboat, large dinghy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. space pod</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκατος.</div><b>άκαυλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκαυλη, neuter άκαυλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stemless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, vulgar) Dickless, lacking confidence or power.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + καυλός + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix).</div><b>άκαυστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκαυστη, neuter άκαυστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. non-flammable, incombustible; fireproof</div>
  <div class='def'>2. non-flammable, incombustible; unburnt, unburned</div><b>άκαυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκαυστη, neuter άκαυστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fireproof, non-flammable, incombustible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unburnt, unburned</div><b>άκεφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκεφη, neuter άκεφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gloomy, dispirited, having low spirits</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- (“non”, privative α-) + κέφ(ι) (“high spirits”) + -ος (suffix for masculines).</div><b>άκλ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://άκλιτος">άκλιτος</a>: indeclinable, undeclinable, invariable, invariant (see: Glossary)</div><b>άκλαυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκλαυτη, neuter άκλαυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unmourned</div><b>άκλειστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκλειστη, neuter άκλειστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not closed</div><b>άκληρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκληρη, neuter άκληρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. childless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. heirless</div><b>άκλητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακάλεστη, neuter ακάλεστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uninvited, not asked to attend</div><b>άκλιτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκλιτη, neuter άκλιτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uninflected, invariable</div><b>άκλωστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκλωστη, neuter άκλωστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unspun (wool, cotton, etc)</div><b>άκμων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άκμονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of άκμονας</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκμων.</div><b>άκομψος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκομψη, neuter άκομψο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inelegant, tasteless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clumsy, without grace</div><b>άκοπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκοπη, neuter άκοπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. easy, not hard (work)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. easy (money)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. uncut</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ά- + κόπος</div><b>άκοσμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκοσμη, neuter άκοσμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>improper, indecorous, indecent (not in keeping with conventional moral values)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκοσμος, equivalent to ά- + κόσμος.</div><b>άκουα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of άκουγα</div><b>άκουρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκουρη, neuter άκουρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) unshorn (sheep, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) needing a haircut, unkempt</div><b>άκουσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ακούσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hearing (the act)</div>
  <div class='ex'>στο <b>άκουσμα</b> της ιστορίας - upon hearing the story</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rumour</div><b>άκουσον</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>listen, hear this</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From imperative form of Ancient Greek ἀκούω.</div><b>άκουσον, άκουσον</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literally) listen to this! (response at hearing something with surprise and displeasure)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Ancient Greek ἄκουσον, aorist imperative of ᾰ̓κούω</div><b>άκρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extremely</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://άκρος">άκρος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άκρος">άκρος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hands and feet, (the) extremities (collectively)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άκρο">άκρο</a></div><b>άκρατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκρατη, neuter άκρατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. extreme, unbridled, pure</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άκρατη</b> απληστία - pure greed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unadulterated, pure</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άκρατο</b> κρασί - unadulterated wine</div><b>άκρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άκρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. end (of rope, road, stick, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. point, tip</div>
  <div class='def'>3. edge, border, rim</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκρα, from the feminine of ἄκρος, from Proto-Indo-European *h₂eḱrós.</div><b>άκρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άκριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) edge, border, rim</div>
  <div class='def'>2. significant, friends, important contacts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See άκρη.</div><b>άκριτα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncritically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άκριτος">άκριτος</a></div><b>άκριτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκριτη, neuter άκριτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inconsiderate, unthinking, thoughtless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. untied, not brought to justice</div><b>άκρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άκρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. end, edge, extremity</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>άκρο</b> της πόλης - the edge of the city</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rim, border</div>
  <div class='def'>3. limb (arm or leg)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural) (the) extremities, (the) hands and feet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκρον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://άκρος">άκρος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://άκρος">άκρος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκρον.</div><b>άκρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκρα, neuter άκρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>utmost, extreme, uttermost</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄκρος.</div><b>άκρως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extremely</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άκρως</b> απόρρητο - top secret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄκρως. By surface analysis, άκρ(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>άκτιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκτιστη, neuter άκτιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unbuilt, not developed, not built up</div><b>άκυρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκυρη, neuter άκυρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>invalid, null and void</div><b>άκων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άκουσα, neuter άκον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, literary) unwilling, unintentional</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Ancient Greek ἄκων.</div><b>άλαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλαλη, neuter άλαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mute, speechless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dumb (without speech)</div><b>άλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) salt (a product of neutralisation between an acid and a base)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, in plural) * see: άλατα back trouble</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, in plural) * see: άλατα back trouble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἅλας. Doublet of the inherited αλάτι.</div><b>άλγεβρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>algebra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Medieval Latin algebra, from Arabic اَلْجَبْر (al-jabr, “reunion, resetting of broken parts”).</div><b>άλγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλγη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄλγος.</div><b>άλειμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αλείματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άλειμμα">άλειμμα</a></div><b>άλειμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αλείμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smearing, spreading, anointment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. greasing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄλειμμα, from ἀλείφω, ἀλειφ- + -μα (-ma, suffix for neuter nouns).</div><b>άλειωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλειωτη, neuter άλειωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άλιωτος">άλιωτος</a></div><b>άλεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αλέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grinding, milling</div><b>άλεσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αλέσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grinding, milling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grist</div><b>άλεστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλεστη, neuter άλεστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unground, unmilled</div><b>άλευρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλευρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://αλεύρι">αλεύρι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. flours</div><b>άλεφ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) aleph (the Hebrew letter א)</div><b>άληκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άληκτη, neuter άληκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unexpired, not terminated</div><b>άληστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άληστος, neuter άληστον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) memorable, unforgettable, unforgotten</div><b>άλικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crimson, scarlet</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://άλικος">άλικος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://άλικος">άλικος</a></div><b>άλικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλικη, neuter άλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scarlet, crimson (a bright, vivid red)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish آل (al) + -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>άλιωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλιωτη, neuter άλιωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. undissolved</div>
  <div class='def'>2. uncrushed</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unmelted</div>
  <div class='def'>4. undecomposed (of the dead)</div><b>άλκαλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλκαλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αλκάλιο">αλκάλιο</a></div><b>άλκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) moose, elk</div><b>άλκιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλκιμη, neuter άλκιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sturdy, vigorous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄλκιμος.</div><b>άλλα λόγια ν&#x27; αγαπιόμαστε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>say no more, let&#x27;s not go there (what has already been said conveys all the meaning and information needed to draw a conclusion concerning a matter which it would be imprudent to discuss further)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον βρήκα στο κρεβάτι με την φίλη μου. Τέλος πάντων, <b>άλλα λόγια ν’ αγαπιόμαστε</b>. - I found him in bed with my friend. Anyway, let&#x27;s say no more about it.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “other words so we can love one another”.</div><b>άλλα τα μάτια του λαγού κι άλλα της κουκουβάγιας</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apples and oranges (said of a comparison of items that are not comparable)</div><b>άλλαγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) change, changing, replacement (the process)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See αλλαγή</div><b>άλλο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>any more, anymore</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://άλλος">άλλος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://άλλος">άλλος</a></div><b>άλλοθεν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>from somewhere else, from another place</div><b>άλλοθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) alibi (defense under which a person on trial for a crime proves or attempts to prove being in another place when the alleged act was committed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ακλόνητο <b>άλλοθι</b>· - solid alibi</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, indeclinable) excuse (especially one used to avoid responsibility or blame)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο καθένας χρησιμοποιεί το <b>άλλοθι</b> ότι είχε κακά παιδικά χρόνια. - Everyone uses the excuse that they had a bad childhood.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄλλοθι, semantic loan from Latin alibi.</div><b>άλλος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine (feminine άλλη, neuter άλλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (contrastive) other</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τρώω τα μήλα, τα <b>άλλα</b> φρούτα δεν τα τρώω. - I eat apples, I don&#x27;t like other fruit.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (contrastive) else</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλετε κάτι <b>άλλο</b>; - Do you want anything else?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (contrastive) someone else</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όχι, αυτός που ήρθε ήταν <b>άλλος</b>. - No, the one who came was someone else.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (contrastive) more</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άλλη</b> μια φορά - one more time, another time</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (contrastive) next</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>άλλος</b> κόσμος - the next world</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (without article) dissimilar, different</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άλλος</b> κόσμος - a different world</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄλλος, from Proto-Hellenic *áľľos, from Proto-Indo-European *h₂élyos.</div><b>άλλοτε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. formerly, sometime, once</div>
  <div class='def'>2. at another time</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sometimes</div><b>άλλως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αλλιώς">αλλιώς</a></div><b>άλλωστε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>besides, after all, furthermore, moreover, and beyond that</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄλλως τε.</div><b>άλμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bound, leap, jump</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) spectacular progress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἅλμα.</div><b>άλμα εις ύψος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high jump</div><b>άλμα επί κοντώ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pole vault</div><b>άλμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) sea water</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) brine, salt water, salty water</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) pickling brine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἅρμη, from Ancient Greek ἅλμη.</div><b>άλμπατρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) albatros (seabird)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) albatros (a hole in 3 under par)</div><b>άλμπουμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) album, CD, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) album (for photographs, stamps, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) sketchbook</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Latin album (“tablet, register”).</div><b>άλμπουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλμπουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan arboro (“tree”).</div><b>άλνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άλνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alder (tree species)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μαύρη <b>άλνος</b> - The black alder</div><b>άλογο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://άλογος">άλογος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://άλογος">άλογος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλογα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. horse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. male horse, stallion</div>
  <div class='def'>3. knight, a colloquial alternative of <a href="bword://ίππος">ίππος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. horsepower, a collloquial alternative of <a href="bword://ίππος">ίππος</a> (for engines of cars)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> For the animal, inherited from Koine Greek ἄλογον, neuter form of Ancient Greek ἄλογος.
For the unit of power, a semantic loan from Greek ίππος, from ancient Ancient Greek ἵππος.</div><b>άλογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλογη, neuter άλογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>illogical, irrational</div><b>άλουστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλουστη, neuter άλουστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unshampooed, unwashed (hair)</div><b>άλπειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλπεια, neuter άλπειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alpine</div><b>άλσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλση)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small wood, grove, copse, boscage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. park with trees</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄλσος which is of unknown origin.</div><b>άλτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άλτες, feminine άλτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jumper, vaulter</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άλτης</b> του ύψους - highjumper</div><b>άλτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άλτριες, masculine άλτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jumper, vaulter</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άλτρια</b> του ύψους - highjumper</div><b>άλυπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλυπη, neuter άλυπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. untroubled (by misfortune and sadness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. serene, painless</div><b>άλυσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άλυσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://αλυσίδα">αλυσίδα</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. alyssum</div><b>άλυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλυτη, neuter άλυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. insoluble, unsolvable, unresolved, unsolved</div>
  <div class='ex'>—Δάσκαλε, το πρόβλημα είναι <b>άλυτο</b>!
—Όχι, δεν είναι ανεπίλυτο. Είναι απλώς δυσεπίλυτο. - —Teacher, the problem is unsolvable!
—No, it is not unsolvable, it is just difficult to solve.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fastened, incapable of being untied</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄλῠτος (“not to be loosed”).</div><b>άλυωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άλυωτη, neuter άλυωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άλιωτος">άλιωτος</a></div><b>άλφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) alpha, the name for the first letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) beginning.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) top, premium.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄλφα, ultimately from Phoenician 𐤀𐤋𐤐.</div><b>άλφα αρσενικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άλφα">άλφα</a> <a href="bword://αρσενικό">αρσενικό</a></div><b>άλφα αρσενικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άλφα αρσενικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alpha male</div><b>άλως</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άλωες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. halo (on religious paintings)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. halo (around celestial objects)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lens flare</div>
  <div class='def'>4. areola</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἅλως. Compare the inherited doublet αλώνι, from a diminutive.</div><b>άλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fall, capture</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with capital) η <a href="bword://Άλωση">Άλωση</a>: the fall of Constantinople in 1453</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἅλωσις, from the verb ἁλίσκομαι.</div><b>άμα</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. if</div>
  <div class='def'>2. when</div>
  <div class='def'>3. since</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἅμα.</div><b>άμαθος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμαθη, neuter άμαθο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://αμάθητος">αμάθητος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄμαθος.</div><b>άμαξα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άμαξες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carriage (wheeled vehicle)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. railway carriage (UK), railroad car (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἅμαξα.</div><b>άμαχοι</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative masculine plural of <a href="bword://άμαχος">άμαχος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>civilians, non-combatants</div><b>άμαχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμαχη, neuter άμαχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. non-combatant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) non-combatant, civilian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄμαχος.</div><b>άμβικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άμβικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άμβυκας">άμβυκας</a></div><b>άμβλυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) blunting, bluntening</div><b>άμβλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αμβλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abortion (induced abortion)</div><b>άμβυκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άμβυκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alembic, early distillation apparatus, still</div><b>άμβωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άμβωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pulpit, ambo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄμβων.</div><b>άμε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, idiomatic) second-person singular present active imperative of <a href="bword://πηγαίνω">πηγαίνω</a>; go!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άμε</b> στο καλό! - Go [singular] to good (with good luck)!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, idiomatic) second-person singular present active imperative of <a href="bword://πηγαίνω">πηγαίνω</a>; go!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άμε</b> στο διάβολο! - Go [singular] to the devil (to hell)!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἄμε, singular imperative of πηγαίνω, from ἄγωμε (with deletion of –γ- and avoidance of hiatus) from Ancient Greek ἄγωμεν (“let&#x27;s go!”), subjunctive of ἄγω (“I lead”). Two interchangeable plurals exist:</div><b>άμεμπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμεμπτη, neuter άμεμπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spotless, unblemished</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blameless, irreproachable, unimpeachable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄμεμπτος.</div><b>άμεσα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άμεσος">άμεσος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>directly, immediately (without anything intervening; without intermediation)</div><b>άμεσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμεση, neuter άμεσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. direct, without intermediary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. immediate, without delay, swift</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἄμεσος, which appears to be from α- (a-, “a-, not”) + μέσος; compare English immediate for a semantic parallel from the same component roots.</div><b>άμεστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αμέστη, neuter αμέστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αμέστωτος">αμέστωτος</a></div><b>άμετε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, idiomatic) second-person plural present active imperative of <a href="bword://πηγαίνω">πηγαίνω</a>; go!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άμετε</b> στο καλό! - Go [plural] to good (with good luck)!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, idiomatic) second-person plural present active imperative of <a href="bword://πηγαίνω">πηγαίνω</a>; go!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άμετε</b> στο διάβολο! - Go [plural] to the devil (to hell)!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἄμε, singular imperative of verb πηγαίνω. Plural imperative formed in harmony with proparoxytone imperatives ‑ετε e.g. λέγετε. The alternative form αμέτε in harmony with paroxytone imperatives ‑άτε e.g. ελάτε.
Also see etymologies of άντε, άιντε, άι.</div><b>άμετρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμετρη, neuter άμετρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unmeasured, immeasurable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incalculable</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) excessive, countless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + μετρο (metro, “measurement”).</div><b>άμιλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) friendly rivalry</div><b>άμισθος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμισθη, neuter άμισθο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unpaid, unsalaried, unwaged</div>
  <div class='def'>2. voluntary, honorary</div><b>άμμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άμμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sand (material found on beaches)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα παιδιά έπαιζαν στην <b>άμμο</b> με τα φτυαράκια τους. - The children played in the sand with their shovels.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. beach (especially a sandy one)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄμμος.</div><b>άμοιαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμοιαστη, neuter άμοιαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unlike, unalike, different, dissimilar</div><b>άμοιρη</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://άμοιρη">άμοιρη</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άμοιρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unfortunate woman</div><b>άμοιρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμοιρη, neuter άμοιρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ill-fated, unlucky, hapless, unfortunate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deprived, devoid</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άμοιροι, feminine άμοιρη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unfortunate man</div><b>άμορφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμορφη, neuter άμορφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shapeless, formless, amorphous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄμορφος.</div><b>άμουσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμουση, neuter άμουσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unmusical</div>
  <div class='def'>2. uncultured</div><b>άμπακας</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) a lot, large quantity mainly used in the phrase</div>
  <div class='ex'>τρώει τον <b>άμπακα</b> - (s)he ate too much, s(he) is voracious</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abacus, as in the Medieval ἄμπακος, <a href="bword://άβακας">άβακας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> An αντιδάνειο: From άμπακος with a metaplasm of the ending to -ας (-as), inherited from Medieval Byzantine Greek ἄμπακος, from Italian abaco (“abacus”), from Latin abacus, from Ancient Greek ἄβαξ.
The sense &quot;voracious, large quantity&quot; from &quot;he knows a lot, from using the abacus&quot;.</div><b>άμπακος</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άμπακας">άμπακας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> An αντιδάνειο.</div><b>άμπελος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άμπελοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αμπέλι">αμπέλι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄμπελος.</div><b>άμποτε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>if only, I wish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αν (an, “if”) + ποτέ.</div><b>άμποτες</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άμποτε">άμποτε</a></div><b>άμπωτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) low tide, ebb tide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄμπωτις with modern ending -η.</div><b>άμυαλα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frivolously</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άμυαλος">άμυαλος</a></div><b>άμυαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμυαλη, neuter άμυαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brainless, mindless, foolish, daft</div>
  <div class='def'>2. frivolous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + μυαλος (myalos, “brain, mind”).</div><b>άμυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άμυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>starch, amylum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄμυλον.</div><b>άμυνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άμυνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. defence (UK), defense (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. defenders, defence</div><b>άμφιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άμφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vestment</div><b>άμωμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άμωμη, neuter άμωμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>immaculate, unblemished</div><b>άναμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ανάμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lighting, kindling, rekindling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) excitement</div><b>άνανδρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cowardly (in that fashion)</div><b>άνανδρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνανδρη, neuter άνανδρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unmanly, cowardly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνανδρος. By surface analysis, α- (a-, “not, without”) + άνδρας + -ος (-os).</div><b>άνανθος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνανθη, neuter άνανθο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flowerless, blossomless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inconspicuous</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) unfulfilled, having not blossomed</div><b>άναντρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άναντρη, neuter άναντρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άνανδρος">άνανδρος</a></div><b>άναξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άνακτες, feminine άνασσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>king</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄναξ.</div><b>άναρθρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inarticulately</div><b>άναρθρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άναρθρη, neuter άναρθρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inarticulate (speech)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lacking an article</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄναρθρος.</div><b>άναρχα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άναρχος">άναρχος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anarchically, unregulatedly</div><b>άναρχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άναρχη, neuter άναρχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. without beginning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. without authority, unregulated</div><b>άνασσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άνασσες, masculine άναξ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>queen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ᾰ̓́νᾰσσᾰ. Feminine counterpart of άναξ.</div><b>άναστρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άναστρη, neuter άναστρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. starless (cloudy)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) unlucky</div><b>άναυδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άναυδη, neuter άναυδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mute, speechless, dumbfounded (with surprise)</div><b>άναυλα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. without being chartered</div>
  <div class='def'>2. without paying the fare (passenger)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. by force</div>
  <div class='def'>4. willy-nilly</div><b>άναυλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άναυλη, neuter άναυλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unchartered</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fare dodging, unable to pay the fare (as a passenger)</div><b>άναφτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άναφτη, neuter άναφτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unlit, unlighted</div><b>άνγκστρομ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) angstrom (unit of length: 10⁻¹⁰ metre, 0.1 nanometre)</div><b>άνδηρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άνδηρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (raised) terrace (especially an ancient one)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. embankment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνδηρον.</div><b>άνδρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άνδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a more formal alternative of <a href="bword://άντρας">άντρας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned influence on άντρας, with ⟨νδ⟩ as in the ἄνδρα, accusative singular of ἀνήρ pronounced in Modern Greek as [nð]. Compare to the pronunciation of the inherited άντρας.</div><b>άνεμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άνεμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνεμος, Proto-Indo-European *h₂enh₁mos, a nominal derivative of *h₂enh₁- (“breathe”).</div><b>άνεργοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the unemployed</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative masculine plural of <a href="bword://άνεργος">άνεργος</a></div><b>άνεργος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνεργη, neuter άνεργο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unemployed, jobless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unused</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνεργος.</div><b>άνεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ανέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. comfort</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leisure, relaxation, rest</div>
  <div class='def'>3. convenience</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural) modern conveniences</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνεσις.</div><b>άνετα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. comfortably, leisurely</div>
  <div class='def'>2. naturally</div><b>άνετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνετη, neuter άνετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. comfortable, spacious</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for people) easy-going, relaxed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνετος.</div><b>άνευ</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) without (+ genitive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνευ. (see also: on).</div><b>άνευ προηγουμένου</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, literary) unprecedented, surprisingly unexpected.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αναίδειά του είναι <b>άνευ προηγουμένου</b>. - His impertinence is unheard of.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned stereotyped expression: preposition from Ancient Greek ἄνευ &amp; the genitive neuter of participle προηγούμενος. Compare French sans précédent.</div><b>άνευρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνευρη, neuter άνευρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stringless</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άνευρα</b> φασόλια - stringless beans</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nerveless</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) flabby, spineless</div><b>άνηβος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνηβη, neuter άνηβο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prepubescent, immature</div><b>άνηθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) dill</div><b>άνθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ανθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flowering, blooming</div><b>άνθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άνθος">άνθος</a></div><b>άνθινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνθινη, neuter άνθινος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>floral</div><b>άνθιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ανθίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flowering, blooming</div><b>άνθισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ανθίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flowering, blooming</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) blooming, flourishing</div><b>άνθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άνθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) flower</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) peak, prime (i.e., flower in bloom)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι στο άνθος της ηλικίας του. - He&#x27;s in the prime of his life.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) blossom</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα <b>άνθη</b> της αμυγδαλιάς - the almond blossom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἄνθος, a learned form of ἄθος after its etymon Ancient Greek ἄνθος, from ἀνθῶ.</div><b>άνθος αραβοσίτου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cornflour (UK), cornstarch (US)</div><b>άνθρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (countable and uncountable, plural άνθρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (countable, uncountable) carbon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (countable, uncountable) coal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (countable, uncountable) charcoal</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (countable, uncountable) anthrax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄνθραξ, from the accusative singular &quot;τόν ἄνθρακα; compare dialectal άθρακας.
For the chemical sense, semantic loan from French carbone. For the disease, semantic loan from English anthrax, from Latin anthrax from Ancient Greek ἄνθραξ in sense &quot;carbuncle&quot;.</div><b>άνθραξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άνθρακας">άνθρακας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἄνθραξ.</div><b>άνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άνθρωποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a human; person; the species man</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) a human; person; the species man; people</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄνθρωπος, as also are variants like άθρωπος, already since ἄθρωπος of the 3rd century.
According to other etymologists, an internal learned borrowing, replacing άθρωπος.</div><b>άνιπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνιπτη, neuter άνιπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άνιφτος">άνιφτος</a></div><b>άνισος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνιση, neuter άνισο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unequal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unfair, inequitable, unjust</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνισος.</div><b>άνιφτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνιφτη, neuter άνιφτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unwashed</div><b>άνοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άνοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. increase, rise, ascent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. anode</div>
  <div class='def'>3. route to the top, route between coast and interior, route from south to the north</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν έρχεστε από την <b>άνοδο</b> της εθνικής οδού, βγαίνετε δεξιά στην έξοδο Αθήνα-Λεωφ. Συγγρού. - If you&#x27;re traveling northbound on the national highway, take the exit for Athens-Syngrou Avenue.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. one-way street that routes from city center outboard</div><b>άνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dementia, senility (progressive decline in cognitive function)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄνοια.</div><b>άνοιγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ανοίγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in clothing) opening</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for door) opening</div>
  <div class='def'>3. opening; gap</div>
  <div class='def'>4. opening; slit, mouth</div>
  <div class='def'>5. opening; clearing</div>
  <div class='def'>6. opening up (flowers, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. deficit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ανοίγω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>άνοιξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ανοίξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spring, springtime, springtide</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αέρας μυρίζει <b>άνοιξη</b>. - Spring is in the air.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνοιξις, from ἀνοιγ (anoig) + -ση; see ᾰ̓νοίγω for more. Displaced earlier Ancient Greek ἔαρ.</div><b>άνομβρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνομβρη, neuter άνομβρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rainless</div><b>άνομος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνομη, neuter άνομο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. illegal, illicit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lawless</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unjust</div><b>άνοπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνοπτη, neuter άνοπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary, uncommon) unfired</div><b>άνορακ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) anorak</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English anorak, from Greenlandic annoraaq.</div><b>άνορχις</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άνορχεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>an anorchic person</div><b>άνοστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνοστη, neuter άνοστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tasteless, unseasoned, flavourless (UK), flavorless (US)</div><b>άνους</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνους, neuter άνουν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stupid, dull, brainless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ά- + νους (nous, “mind”).</div><b>άντ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contraction of <a href="bword://άντε">άντε</a>”)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άντ’</b> από δω - clear off!</div><b>άντε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (idiomatic) go! (imperative indicator 2nd person singular) or αντέτε)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άντε</b> από δω - clear off!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) go on</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άντε</b> ντε - go on!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (idiomatic) c&#x27;mon, come on (requires promptness)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άντε</b> γρήγορα! - hurry up!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (idiomatic) c&#x27;mon, come on (expressing surprise, disbelief or irony)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άντε</b>, τι μου λες! - get away with you!</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (idiomatic) let&#x27;s</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άντε</b> ξεκινήστε! - let&#x27;s start!</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (idiomatic) + a number (shows maximum limit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κοπέλα είναι σαράντα ετών, <b>άντε</b> σαράντα πέντε - The young lady is forty years old, ok, maybe forty five.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Babiniotis sees it as an imperative from, stemming from άιντε &lt; *άιτε &lt; *άετε (with deletion of -γ-) from Ancient Greek ἄγετε, imperative plural of the verb ἄγω.
Others have it from Byzantine Greek άμετε) with syncope of unstressed -ε- and /mt/ &gt; /nt/ &gt; /nd/. The variation άιντε seems to have a different etymology.</div><b>άντε γαμήσου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(offensive, vulgar) fuck you, go fuck yourself</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι γυρεύεις εδώ πέρα; <b>Άντε γαμήσου</b>, μωρέ. - What are you doing here? Go fuck yourself, would ya?</div><b>άντε στο διάλο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άντε">άντε</a> στο διάολο</div><b>άντε στο διάολο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(offensive, vulgar) go to hell, to hell with you, get lost</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άντε στο διάολο</b> και παραπέρα! - Go to hell and beyond!</div><b>άντερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άντερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έντερο">έντερο</a></div><b>άντες</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άντε">άντε</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From άντε with suffixation of ‑ς in harmony with 2nd person singular imperatives, e.g. λες (les), πες (pes) (“say!”) .</div><b>άντικρυς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αντίκρυ">αντίκρυ</a></div><b>άντλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αντλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bucket (for drawing water)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bucketful of water</div><b>άντληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αντλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pumping, bailing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) milking, pumping (money)</div><b>άντρακλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) husky, hefty, lusty man</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> άντρας + -άκλας</div><b>άντρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άντρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. man (adult male)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. husband</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek ἄντρας, from the Ancient Greek ἄνδρα, accusative singular of ἀνήρ, retaining the ancient pronunciation [nd] of ⟨νδ⟩ written as ⟨ντ⟩. Compare to άνδρας. Cognate with Mariupol Greek а́ндра.</div><b>άντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hideout, den, retreat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. den, lair</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>άντρο</b> των λύκων - the wolves&#x27; den</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cave, grotto</div>
  <div class='def'>4. found in naming of some body parts</div>
  <div class='ex'>μαστοειδές <b>άντρο</b> - mastoid process</div><b>άντυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άντυτη, neuter άντυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. undressed, naked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. improperly dressed</div>
  <div class='def'>3. not covered</div><b>άνυδρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άνυδρη, neuter άνυδρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. very dry, arid, waterless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. anhydrous</div><b>άνυσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ανύσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vector</div><b>άνω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>upper, over</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄνω.</div><b>άνω γνάθοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://γνάθος">γνάθος</a></div><b>άνω γνάθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άνω γνάθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maxilla, upper jawbone</div><b>άνω κάτω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. upside down, topsy-turvy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mixed up, disorderly, confused</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek adverbs ἄνω &amp; κάτω in parataxis.</div><b>άνω κάτω τελεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άνω κάτω τελείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://κάτω">κάτω</a> <a href="bword://τελεία">τελεία</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally up down stop
: ἄνω + κάτω + τελεία</div><b>άνω κάτω τελείες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://κάτω">κάτω</a> <a href="bword://τελεία">τελεία</a></div><b>άνω και κάτω τελεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άνω και κάτω τελείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The modern Greek colon ⟨:).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From άνω + και (kai, “and”) + κάτω + τελεία.</div><b>άνω και κάτω τελείες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://κάτω">κάτω</a> <a href="bword://τελεία">τελεία</a></div><b>άνω στιγμές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://στιγμή">στιγμή</a></div><b>άνω στιγμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άνω στιγμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncommon) raised point, upper stop, upper point, upper dot, the Greek semicolon, the “·”</div><b>άνω στιγμής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://στιγμή">στιγμή</a></div><b>άνω στιγμών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://στιγμή">στιγμή</a></div><b>άνω τελεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άνω τελείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>raised point, upper stop, upper point, upper dot, the Greek semicolon, the “·”</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ἄνω + τελεία</div><b>άνω τελείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://τελεία">τελεία</a></div><b>άνω τελείες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://τελεία">τελεία</a></div><b>άνω τελειών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://τελεία">τελεία</a></div><b>άνω-κάτω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>script with hyphen of <a href="bword://άνω">άνω</a> <a href="bword://κάτω">κάτω</a></div><b>άνωθεν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. from above, from on high</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) from Heaven, from higher authority</div><b>άνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) buoyancy, lift, upthrust</div><b>άξαντος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άξαντη, neuter άξαντο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncarded (wool, etc)</div><b>άξαφνα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suddenly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek έξαφνα, from Ancient Greek ἐξαίφνης, from ἐξ- (ex-, “ex-, out-”) + ἄφνω.</div><b>άξαφνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άξαφνη, neuter άξαφνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sudden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> άξαφνα + -ος (-os).</div><b>άξενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άξενη, neuter άξενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inhospitable</div><b>άξεστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άξεστη, neuter άξεστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rude, crude, coarse, vulgar</div><b>άξια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://άξιος">άξιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άξιος">άξιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>worthily</div><b>άξιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άξια, neuter άξιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deserving, worthy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄξιος.</div><b>άξονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άξονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. axis</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>άξονας</b> της γης - the Earth&#x27;s axis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. axle, shaft</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (proper noun) the Axis</div><b>άξυστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άξυστη, neuter άξυστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unscratched, not scratched</div><b>άξων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άξονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://άξονας">άξονας</a></div><b>άοκνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άοκνη, neuter άοκνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diligent</div><b>άοπλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άοπλη, neuter άοπλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unarmed</div><b>άοσμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άοσμη, neuter άοσμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>odourless (UK), odorless (US)</div><b>άουτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) out, out of play (in touch)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English out.</div><b>άουτο ντα φε</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) auto da fe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, indeclinable) destruction by fire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Portuguese auto da fé.</div><b>άπαγε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>absent, away from here</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπαγε.</div><b>άπαικτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπαικτη, neuter άπαικτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άπαιχτος">άπαιχτος</a></div><b>άπαις</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>childless, a childless person</div><b>άπαιχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπαιχτη, neuter άπαιχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unperformed, not performed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unplayed (card, piece of music)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. not out (film)</div><b>άπαντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>collected works, complete works, oeuvre</div><b>άπαξ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>once</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα το πω <b>άπαξ</b> και δε θα το επαναλάβω. - I will say this once and will not repeat it.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἅπαξ.</div><b>άπαξ δια παντός</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>once and for all, for good</div><b>άπαξ λεγόμενα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άπαξ">άπαξ</a> λεγόμενον</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άπαξ">άπαξ</a> λεγόμενο</div><b>άπαξ λεγόμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άπαξ λεγόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άπαξ">άπαξ</a> λεγόμενον hapax legomenon</div><b>άπαξ λεγόμενον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άπαξ λεγόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) hapax legomenon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa ἅπαξ λεγόμενον, as with the ancient adverb ἅπαξ and the neuter of the participle λεγόμενος of λέγω, from the Latin term; see hapax legomenon.</div><b>άπαπα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Used to express strong denial or rejection.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εγώ να πάω στη στα χιόνια; <b>Άπαπα</b>, να σου λείπει το βύσσινο. - Me, to go to snowy areas? No way, it&#x27;s out of the question.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic, similar to Ancient Greek ἀππαπαῖ.</div><b>άπαρση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural απάρσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>weighing anchor, sailing, departure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀπαίρω.</div><b>άπαρτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπαρτη, neuter άπαρτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impregnable, untaken, secure, invincible</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>άπαρτο</b> κάστρο - the impregnable castle</div><b>άπας</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπασα, neuter άπαν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>everything, everyone</div><b>άπατα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) on the bottom</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άπατος">άπατος</a></div><b>άπατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπατη, neuter άπατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bottomless, very deep</div><b>άπατρις</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural απάτριδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stateless person</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unpatriotic person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπατρις.</div><b>άπαυστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπαυστη, neuter άπαυστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endless, ceaseless</div><b>άπαυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπαυτη, neuter άπαυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άπαυστος">άπαυστος</a></div><b>άπαχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπαχη, neuter άπαχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thin, skinny, lean</div><b>άπειρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) infinity</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://άπειρος">άπειρος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://άπειρος">άπειρος</a></div><b>άπειρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπειρη, neuter άπειρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inexperienced</div>
  <div class='def'>2. infinite</div>
  <div class='def'>3. infinity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπειρος, from ἀ- (a-, “opposite”) + πεῖρα.</div><b>άπελπις</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπελπις, neuter άπελπι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>desperate, in despair</div><b>άπεπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπεπτη, neuter άπεπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undigested</div><b>άπερκατ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) uppercut</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English uppercut.</div><b>άπεφθος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπεφθη, neuter άπεφθο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pure, genuine (chiefly of metals)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άπεφθος</b> χρυσός - pure gold</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπεφθος.</div><b>άπηκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπηκτη, neuter άπηκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άπηχτος">άπηχτος</a></div><b>άπηχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπηχτη, neuter άπηχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not set, unset, uncoagulated</div><b>άπιαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπιαστη, neuter άπιαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. I am untouched/unused, I am new</div>
  <div class='def'>2. I am at large/uncaught</div><b>άπικρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπικρη, neuter άπικρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bitterless, not bitter (taste)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτά τα υλικά ενισχύουν την όξινη αλλά <b>απικρή</b> γεύση. - These ingredients enhance the acidic yet bitterless taste.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπικρος.</div><b>άπιοτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπιοτη, neuter άπιοτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not drunk (of a beverage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. undrinkable</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sober</div><b>άπιστοι Θωμάδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άπιστος">άπιστος</a> <a href="bword://Θωμάς">Θωμάς</a></div><b>άπιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπιστη, neuter άπιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unbelieving, doubting, unfaithful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) atheist, atheistic</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, noun) atheist</div><b>άπιστος Θωμάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άπιστοι Θωμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doubting Thomas (one who doubts)</div><b>άπλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άπλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. space, spaciousness (especially around something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. expanse (of flat area)</div><b>άπλαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπλαστη, neuter άπλαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unformed, without form, formless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unmade, not created</div><b>άπλεκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπλεκτη, neuter άπλεκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άπλεχτος">άπλεχτος</a></div><b>άπλερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπλερη, neuter άπλερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. frail, delicate, slender</div>
  <div class='def'>2. immature, young</div><b>άπλετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπλετη, neuter άπλετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) dazzling, brilliant (light)</div><b>άπλεχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπλεχτη, neuter άπλεχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unknitted</div><b>άπληστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπληστη, neuter άπληστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greedy, avaricious, covetous</div><b>άπλοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) confinement in port due to bad weather or detention</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πλέω.</div><b>άπλυτα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άπλυτος">άπλυτος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dirty washing (UK), dirty laundry (US)</div><b>άπλυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπλυτη, neuter άπλυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unwashed, unclean, filthy</div><b>άπλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural απλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spreading out, spreading, stretching</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially) the spreading out of washing to dry</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (especially) the spreading out of washing to dry; clothesline, clotheshorse</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (literary) open area</div>
  <div class='def'>5. diffusion</div><b>άπνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άπνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. calmness, dead calm, stillness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cessation of respiration, apnea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἄπνοια.</div><b>άπνοος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπνοη, neuter άπνοο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. windless, without wind</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lifeless, without breath, dead</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) frail, weak</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄπνοος. By surface analysis, ά- + -πνοος (-pnoos, “of breath”). See πνέω and for stem πνο-, πνοή.</div><b>άπνους</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπνους, neuter άπνουν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated synonym of <a href="bword://άπνοος">άπνοος</a> chiefly in the sense &quot;breathless, lifeless&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄπνους, contracted form of ἄπνοος -modern άπνοος. By surface analysis, ά- + -πνους (-pnous, “of breath”) for which see -πνοος (-pnoos), πνοή and πνέω.</div><b>άποικος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural άποικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colonist</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι Συρακούσες της Σικελίας ήταν αποικία των Κορινθίων. - Siracusa in Sicily was a colony of Corinth.</div><b>άπονα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άπονος">άπονος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπονος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heartlessly, uncaringly, unfeelingly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπονος.</div><b>άπονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπονη, neuter άπονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heartless, uncaring, unfeeling, unsympathetic (not caring about the pain or suffering of others)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν εντελώς <b>άπονη</b> προς τους άλλους. - She was completely uncaring towards others.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπονος, morphologically ά- + πόνος.</div><b>άπορος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπορη, neuter άπορο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. destitute, poor, needy, in penury</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pauper</div><b>άπους</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπους, neuter άπουν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) legless, footless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἄπους, from ἀ- (a-, “alpha privative”) + πούς m, stem ποδ-.</div><b>άποψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural απόψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>point of view, opinion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄποψις.</div><b>άππαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άππαροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cyprus form of <a href="bword://άλογο">άλογο</a></div><b>άπραγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπραγη, neuter άπραγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inexperienced, callow</div><b>άπρακτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπρακτη, neuter άπρακτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>empty-handed, idle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπρακτος</div><b>άπραχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπραχτη, neuter άπραχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άπρακτος">άπρακτος</a></div><b>άπρεπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπρεπη, neuter άπρεπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://απρεπής">απρεπής</a></div><b>άπροικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπροικη, neuter άπροικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dowryless, without a dowry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄπροικος.</div><b>άπταιστα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άπταιστος">άπταιστος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fluently</div><b>άπταιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπταιστη, neuter άπταιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fluent, perfect</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο άνδρας μιλούσε <b>άπταιστα</b> ελληνικά. - The man spoke fluent Greek.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> α- (a-, “a-, un-”) + πταίω + -τος (-tos, “verbal adjective suffix”).</div><b>άπτερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπτερη or άπτερος, neuter άπτερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wingless, flightless</div><b>άπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to touch, be related to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο ερώτημα <b>άπτεται</b> της ουσίας του θέματος. - The question touches the heart of the matter.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἅπτομαι, mediopassive of ἅπτω.</div><b>άπτωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άπτωτη, neuter άπτωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indeclinable</div><b>άπω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>far away, distant</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άπω</b> Ανατολή - Far East</div><b>άπωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural απώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. repulsion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. repulsion</div><b>άρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>therefore, consequently, accordingly, so</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αδερφός μου θα είναι εδώ το καλοκαίρι, <b>άρα</b> θα τον δεις σίγουρα. - My brother will be here in the summer, so you&#x27;ll definitely see him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄρα.</div><b>άρα κατάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άρες κατάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) a hearty curse</div><b>άρα μάρα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rare singular form of άρες μάρες (expressing indifference)</div><b>άραβας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άραβες, feminine αράβισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the proper noun <a href="bword://Άραβας">Άραβας</a> in adjectival use</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γνώρισα έναν Άραβα και γίναμε φίλοι. Ο <b>άραβας</b> φίλος μου … - I met an Arab and we became friends. My Arab friend …</div><b>άραγε</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. used to convey wonder or questioning, roughly equivalent to I wonder</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί <b>άραγε</b> αργεί τόσο πολύ; - I wonder why he is so late.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (nonstandard, proscribed) therefore, consequently)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Κρήτη είναι νησί, <b>άραγε</b> θα πάω με το πλοίο εκεί.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἆρά γε, consisting of ἄρα + γε (ge, “in fact, at least”).</div><b>άραγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αράγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anchoring, mooring</div><b>άρατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άρατη, neuter άρατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in phrases) vanished</div>
  <div class='ex'>γίνομαι <b>άρατος</b> - vanish (into thin air)</div><b>άραφτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άραφτη, neuter άραφτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unsewn, not made</div><b>άραχνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άραχνη, neuter άραχνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. full of spiders (usually through disuse or abandonment)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dismal, gloomy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αράχνη f.</div><b>άρβυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άρβυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αρβύλα">αρβύλα</a></div><b>άργασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αργάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tanning (of leather)</div><b>άργητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) delay, tardiness</div><b>άργιλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άργιλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clay, potters&#x27; clay, argil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ἄργιλλος</div><b>άργυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) silver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἄργῠρος, from Proto-Indo-European *h₂erǵ-.</div><b>άρδευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αρδεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irrigation</div><b>άρδην</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>utterly, completely, radically</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄρδην</div><b>άρειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άρεια, neuter άρειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relating to Mars (planet)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relating to Ares (god)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄρειος</div><b>άρες κατάρες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://άρα">άρα</a> <a href="bword://κατάρα">κατάρα</a></div><b>άρες μάρες</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>balderdash, piffle, baloney, hogwash, poppycock</div>
  <div class='ex'>λέτε <b>άρες μάρες</b> - you talk piffle</div><b>άρες-μάρες</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of άρες μάρες</div><b>άρθρ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://άρθρο">άρθρο</a>: article</div><b>άρθρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άρθρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. article</div>
  <div class='ex'>οριστικό <b>άρθρο</b> - definite article</div>
  <div class='def'>2. article</div>
  <div class='ex'>κύριο <b>άρθρο</b> - lead story</div>
  <div class='def'>3. article of a charter, law, contract etc.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>άρθρο</b> πίστεως - article of faith</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (formal) limb</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἄρθρον.</div><b>άρθρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αρθρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄρθρωσις.</div><b>άριος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural άριoi)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Aryan</div><b>άριστα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άριστος">άριστος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>excellent, full marks</div><b>άριστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άριστη, neuter άριστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. excellent, first-rate, thorough</div>
  <div class='def'>2. noble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄριστος.</div><b>άρκευθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άρκευθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>juniper (plant and flavouring)</div><b>άρκτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άρκτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) bear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἄρκτος. See αρκούδα.</div><b>άρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tank</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) chariot</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a carnival chariot</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural) arms</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (historical, literary, plural normally) weapons, armour</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σύντροφοι! Σηκώστε τ’ <b>άρματα</b>! - Comrades! To arms!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἅρμα.</div><b>άρμα μάχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άρματα μάχης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>battle tank, heavy armoured vehicle</div><b>άρματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>arms, nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άρμα">άρμα</a></div><b>άρμεγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αρμέγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. milking</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) milking, exploitation</div><b>άρμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άρμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sail</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) sailing ship</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) rigging</div><b>άρμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άλμη">άλμη</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἅρμη, from Ancient Greek ἅλμη.</div><b>άρμοση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αρμόσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>matching, fitting</div><b>άρμπουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άρμπουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άλμπουρο">άλμπουρο</a></div><b>άρνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αρνήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. refusal, rejection</div>
  <div class='def'>2. denial, negation</div><b>άρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>arum (Arum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἄρον. Semantic loan from translingual Arum.</div><b>άρον άρον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (idiomatic) willy nilly, forcibly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) in a hurry, hurriedly, hastily</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγανε <b>άρον άρον</b> για να προλάβουν το πλοίο. - They left in a hurry to catch the ship.</div>
