  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άφρων">άφρων</a></div><b>άφροντις</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφροντις, neuter άφροντι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic) carefree, nonchalant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic) unimportant, uninteresting</div><b>άφρυκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφρυκτη, neuter άφρυκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) undercooked</div><b>άφρων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφρων, neuter άφρον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foolish, frivolous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄφρων</div><b>άφταστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφταστη, neuter άφταστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άφθαστος">άφθαστος</a></div><b>άφτερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφτερη, neuter άφτερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άπτερος">άπτερος</a></div><b>άφτιαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφτιαστη, neuter άφτιαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not done, not made</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not made-up, without makeup</div><b>άφτιαχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφτιαχτη, neuter άφτιαχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άφτιαστος">άφτιαστος</a></div><b>άφτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άφτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://άφθα">άφθα</a> (mouth ulcer)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wick</div>
  <div class='def'>3. kindling, tinder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄφθα.</div><b>άφτυστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφτυστη, neuter άφτυστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not spat upon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄπτυστος</div><b>άφυλλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφυλλη, neuter άφυλλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sexless</div><b>άφυλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφυλη, neuter άφυλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leafless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. without sexual organs; asexual</div><b>άφωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφωνη, neuter άφωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voiceless, mute, silent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄφωνος</div><b>άφωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άφωτη, neuter άφωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dark (night)</div><b>άχαρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ungracefully, awkwardly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://άχαρος">άχαρος</a></div><b>άχαρις</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχαρις, neuter άχαρι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic) unappreciated, thankless (task)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic) ungrateful, unthankful (manner, character)</div><b>άχαρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχαρη, neuter άχαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ungraceful, ungainly, awkward (lacking grace)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thankless (of a task: not appreciated or rewarded)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. joyless, bleak (cheerless)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄχαρις. By surface analysis, ά- + χάρ(η), “grace, thanks”) + -ος (-os).</div><b>άχειλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχειλη, neuter άχειλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lipless, lack of lips</div><b>άχερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άχερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άχυρο">άχυρο</a></div><b>άχθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άχθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>burden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἄχθος.</div><b>άχλωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχλωρη, neuter άχλωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ungreen, arid</div><b>άχνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) light mist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) whisper</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) light breath</div><b>άχνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural άχνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) vapour</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) a fine material, dust</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (especially, uncountable, usually) icing sugar</div><b>άχνουδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχνουδη, neuter άχνουδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>without down, downless</div><b>άχολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχολη, neuter άχολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not choleric</div>
  <div class='def'>2. even-tempered, good-tempered, not easily angered</div><b>άχορδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχορδη, neuter άχορδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unstrung, without string(s)</div><b>άχου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Expresses satisfaction from nice gesture etc</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άχου</b>, τι ωραίο δώρο. - Wow, what a nice gift.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Expresses happiness from something</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άχου</b>, τι όμορφο μωρό! - Oh, what a beautiful baby?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Expresses affection or tenderness, aww</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άχου</b>, το μωρούλι μου, κοίτα πώς κοιμάται. - Aww, look at my little baby and how he&#x27;s sleeping.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. Expresses disappointment</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άχου</b>, ξέχασα εντελώς ότι είναι τα γενέθλιά σου. - Oh no, I forgot completely that it was your birthday.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. Expresses sadness</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άχου</b>, τι έπαθε ο καημένος. - Oh the poor guy, did you see what happened to him?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>άχραντος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχραντη, neuter άχραντο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>immaculate, pure, unblemished</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄχραντος</div><b>άχρηστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχρηστη, neuter άχρηστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. useless, unusable (thing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) ineffective (of a person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μα τι <b>άχρηστος</b> που είσαι! Ούτε ένα πρόβλημα δεν μπορείς να λύσεις; - Oh you are so useless! Can&#x27;t you solve even one problem?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (offensive) useless (of a person, usually in the vocative)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άντε ρε <b>άχρηστε</b>! - Shoo, you useless man!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄχρηστος. By surface analysis, ά- (privative a-) + χρηστός.</div><b>άχρι</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) until, till (with genitive)</div><b>άχρις</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άχρι">άχρι</a></div><b>άχρονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχρονη, neuter άχρονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>timeless, unaffected by time</div><b>άχροος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχροη, neuter άχροο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colourless (UK), colorless (US), without colour (UK), color (US)</div><b>άχρωμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχρωμη, neuter άχρωμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. colourless (UK), colorless (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bleak (without colour)</div><b>άχτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) enmity, grudge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) yearning, longing</div><b>άχτιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχτιστη, neuter άχτιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άκτιστος">άκτιστος</a></div><b>άχυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχυμη, neuter άχυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. juiceless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) lifeless, insipid, anodyne</div><b>άχυρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural άχυρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. straw (the stalk of wheat, barley, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄχυρον.</div><b>άχωστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άχωστη, neuter άχωστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not driven in (nails, etc), unburied (the dead)</div><b>άψα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άψες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άψη">άψη</a></div><b>άψαλτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άψαλτη, neuter άψαλτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unsung</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unpraised, without church rites</div><b>άψαχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άψαχτη, neuter άψαχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unsearched, not rummaged, not rifled</div><b>άψε σβήσε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>right away, in a jiffy</div><b>άψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άψες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irritability, heat, piquancy</div><b>άψητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άψητη, neuter άψητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. uncooked, undercooked, under baked, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) naive, inexperienced</div><b>άψιλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άψιλη, neuter άψιλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>broke, penniless</div><b>άψογα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impeccably, immaculately</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://άψογος">άψογος</a></div><b>άψογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άψογη, neuter άψογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impeccable, faultless, flawless, top notch, off the charts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἄψογος.</div><b>άψυκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άψυκτη, neuter άψυκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncooled</div><b>άψυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άψυχη, neuter άψυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inanimate, lifeless (having no life)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spineless, poor-spirited</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) lifeless (dull or lacking vitality)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄψυχος. By surface analysis, ά- + ψυχ(ή)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>άωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άωρος or άωρη, neuter άωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>untimely, ill-timed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄωρος</div><b>άωτον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>found in the phrase</div>
  <div class='ex'>το άκρον άωτον - the height of, acme</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἄωτον</div><b>άϋλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine άϋλη, neuter άϋλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άυλος">άυλος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἄϋλος.</div><b>έαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, uncountable) spring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἔαρ, from Proto-Hellenic *wéhər, from Proto-Indo-European *wósr̥.</div><b>έβγαλα άκρη</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://βγάζω">βγάζω</a> <a href="bword://άκρη">άκρη</a></div><b>έβδομος</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine (feminine έβδομη or εβδόμη, neuter έβδομο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seventh</div><b>έβενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural έβενοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) ebony (wood or tree)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔβενος.</div><b>έγγαμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έγγαμος or έγγαμη, neuter έγγαμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>married</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εν- (en-, “in”) + γάμος</div><b>έγγραφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έγγραφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>document</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the adjective έγγραφος. From the plural τὰ ἔγγραφα of the Koine Greek ἔγγραφος, from ἐγγράφω. Morphologically, from εν- (εγ-) + γράφ(ω) + -o.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://έγγραφος">έγγραφος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://έγγραφος">έγγραφος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the adjective έγγραφος. From the plural τὰ ἔγγραφα of the Koine Greek ἔγγραφος, from ἐγγράφω. Morphologically, from εν- (εγ-) + γράφ(ω) + -o.</div><b>έγγραφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έγγραφη, neuter έγγραφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in writing, written</div><b>έγερση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγέρσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. waking up, rising, awakening, wakening (from sleep and bed)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. raising</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔγερσις, from ἐγείρω.</div><b>έγκαιρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> έγκαιρος + -α (-a, “adverbial suffix”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://έγκαιρος">έγκαιρος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>έγκαιρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έγκαιρη, neuter έγκαιρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>timely, opportune</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔγκαιρος.</div><b>έγκαυμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εγκαύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. burn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sunburn</div><b>έγκλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εγκλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crime, felony</div>
  <div class='ex'>ειδεχθές <b>έγκλημα</b> - heinous crime</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔγκλημα.</div><b>έγκλημα πολέμου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>war crime, literally: &quot;a crime of war&quot;</div><b>έγκληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγκλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. indictment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔγκλησις.</div><b>έγκλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγκλίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grammatical mood, mood</div>
  <div class='def'>2. enclisis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔγκλισις.</div><b>έγκριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγκρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>approval, permission, sanction</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ανησυχία για βιαστική <b>έγκριση</b> του εμβολίου αυξάνεται. - Concerns about a hasty approval of the vaccine are growing.</div><b>έγκυος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έγκυος or έγκυα, neuter έγκυο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pregnant</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είμαι τεσσάρων μηνών <b>έγκυος</b>. - I am four months pregnant.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔγκῠος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έγκυοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pregnant woman, gravida</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔγκῠος.</div><b>έγκυρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έγκυρη, neuter έγκυρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. valid (acceptable, proper or correct; in accordance with the rules)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. authoritative (information, source, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: αξιόπιστος (axiópistos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εγ- (eg-) + κύρ(ος)) + -ος (-os), modelled on άκυρος.</div><b>έγνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έγνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>care, concern, preoccupation (feelings of responsibility)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek and Ancient Greek ἔννοια.</div><b>έδαφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εδάφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ground</div>
  <div class='ex'>κερδίζω <b>έδαφος</b> - Ι gain ground</div>
  <div class='def'>2. soil, land, earth</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>έδαφος</b> εδώ ήταν κάποτε γόνιμο. - The soil was once fertile here.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. territory, soil</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βρεθήκαμε πάλι σε ελληνικό <b>έδαφος</b>. - We were back on Greek soil.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἔδαφος.</div><b>έδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. platform, dais (raised stage on which one can stand or sit in order to see everyone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δάσκαλος μίλησε από την <b>έδρα</b>. - The teacher spoke from the platform.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. seat, chair, bench (usually raised)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι δικαστές πάντα κάθονται στις <b>έδρες</b>. - The judges always sit on the bench.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) seat, headquarters, base (chief location of a governing body or other thing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>έδρα</b> της κυβέρνησης είναι στην Αθήνα. - The seat of government is in Athens.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. ) Holy See (episcopal jurisdiction of the Pope)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) home ground (a team&#x27;s own ground, which is used for their home games)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>έδρα</b> του Ολυμπιακού είναι στον Πειραιά. - Olympiacos&#x27;s home ground is in Piraeus.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively) office (official position or a position of responsibility)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Αρχιεπίσκοπος έχασε την <b>έδρα</b> του μετά το σκάνδαλο. - The Archbishop lost his office after the scandal.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (figuratively) seat (electoral district or number of members, especially for a national legislature)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μετά τις εκλογές του Σεπτεμβρίου 2015, η Σύριζα διαθέτει 145 <b>έδρες</b>. - After the election of September 2015, Syriza has 145 seats.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. anal ring (the ring of the anus)</div>
  <div class='def'>9. (broadly) seat, buttocks, behind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἕδρα. For sense &quot;headquarters&quot;, a semantic loan from French siège.</div><b>έδωκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of έδωσα, first-person singular simple past active indicative of <a href="bword://δίνω">δίνω</a></div><b>έθιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έθιμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>custom, tradition</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν επιθυμείτε να αλλάξετε το <b>έθιμο</b> αυτό, μπορείτε βέβαια να το αλλάξετε, αλλά, σας παρακαλώ, μην λέτε πράγματα τα οποία είναι εντελώς λανθασμένα. - If you want to change this custom, you can certainly change it, but please do not say things here which are completely false.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔθιμον), neuter of ἔθιμος, from ἔθος + -ιμος (-imos), with semantic loan from French coutume.</div><b>έθνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έθνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a culture and the group of people that belong to it</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nation, tribe, kin, band</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔθνος.</div><b>έικρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) acre (unit of area: 4,840 square yards, 0.405 hectares, 4,046.9 square metres)</div><b>έκανε κι η μύγα κώλο κι έχεσε τον κόσμο όλο</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) Said of those who have risen above their station, often in an arrogant manner.</div><b>έκδοση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκδόσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. edition (of a book, newspaper, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγόρασα την πρώτη <b>έκδοση</b> του βιβλίου. - I bought the first edition of the book.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. extradition (formal removal from one country to another)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. release (version of software)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. issue (of a book, newspaper, etc. and banknotes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἔκδοσις.</div><b>έκθεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκθέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exhibit, an item on show</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek ἔκθεμα. Morphologically, έκ- + θέμα n.</div><b>έκθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. exhibition (public showing), exposition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. showroom</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fair</div>
  <div class='def'>4. essay</div>
  <div class='def'>5. report</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το δεύτερο έγγραφο ήταν μια <b>έκθεση</b> του ιταλικού Ελεγκτικού Συνεδρίου. - The second document was a report from the Italian Court of Auditors.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. show</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἔκθεσις, from ἐκ (ek) + θέσις f &amp; semantic loan from French exposition. By surface analysis, έκ- + θέση.</div><b>έκθυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έκθυμη, neuter έκθυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, figuratively) heartful, from the heart, full of soul or compassion.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἔκθυμος.</div><b>έκκληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκκλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>appeal, call (an entreaty or invocation: for help, support, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἔκκλησις, with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>έκκροτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έκκροτη, neuter έκκροτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plosive</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έκκροτα</b> σύμφωνα - plosive consonants</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔκκροτος, from Ancient Greek ἐκκρούω. See κρότος m.</div><b>έκλειψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκλείψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eclipse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔκλειψις. See εκλείπω.</div><b>έκπληκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έκπληκτη, neuter έκπληκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>surprised</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἔκπληκτος.</div><b>έκπληξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκπλήξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>surprise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔκπληξις.</div><b>έκπτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκπτώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. discount</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έκπτωση</b> 15% - 15% discount</div>
  <div class='def'>2. falling from office, fall from rank</div><b>έκρηξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκρήξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>explosion, blast</div><b>έκτακτα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://έκτακτος">έκτακτος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exceptionally well, wonderfully</div><b>έκτακτη ανάγκη</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emergency (literally: extraordinary need)</div><b>έκτακτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έκτακτη, neuter έκτακτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. extraordinary, emergency</div>
  <div class='def'>2. temporary, emergency</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) wonderful, marvellous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔκτακτος.</div><b>έκταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκτάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. extent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. extension</div>
  <div class='def'>3. area, expanse</div>
  <div class='def'>4. stretching out</div>
  <div class='def'>5. ectasis (the lengthening of a short vowel to a long one)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔκτασις.</div><b>έκτασις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://έκταση">έκταση</a></div><b>έκτον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sixthly</div><b>έκτοπες κυήσεις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of έκτοπη <a href="bword://κύηση">κύηση</a></div><b>έκτοπη κύηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έκτοπες κυήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ectopic pregnancy</div><b>έκτοπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έκτοπη or έκτοπος, neuter έκτοπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ectopic, mispositioned</div><b>έκτοπος κύηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of έκτοπη <a href="bword://κύηση">κύηση</a></div><b>έκτος</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine (feminine έκτη, neuter έκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sixth</div><b>έκτοτε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>since, since then</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἔκτοτε.</div><b>έκτρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκτρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abortion (induced abortion)</div><b>έκφραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκφράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expression</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔκφρασις.</div><b>έλα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://έρχομαι">έρχομαι</a>: &quot;Come!&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έλα</b> εδώ! - Come here!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek ἔλα, imperative of the Ancient Greek ἐλαύνω, possibly from a cry at the Hippodrome.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. you don&#x27;t say; come on.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έλα</b>, πλάκα κάνεις! - come on, you must be joking!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an informal greeting used when answering the telephone, &quot;go ahead&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>Α, με παίρνει ο Γιάννης στο κινητό. &quot;Ελα&quot; - Oh, Yiannis is calling me on the cell. &quot;Go ahead.&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek ἔλα, imperative of the Ancient Greek ἐλαύνω, possibly from a cry at the Hippodrome.</div><b>έλα μουνί στον τόπο σου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, vulgar) fucking hell, Jesus H. Christ, bloody hell, fuck, shit, crikey (expression of great surprise)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είδες ότι πέθανε ο Μπάμπης; <b>Έλα μουνί στον τόπο σου</b>! - Did you see that Babis died? Fucking hell!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A shortened version of the full sentence, έλα μουνί στον τόπο σου και πούτσο μη γυρεύεις</div><b>έλα να δεις</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, uncountable) mayhem, pandemonium, commotion, upheaval</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη λαϊκή σήμερα γινόταν <b>το έλα να δεις</b> με τις προσφορές. - There was mayhem at the market today with the special offers.</div><b>έλα παππού μου, να σου δείξω τ&#x27; αμπελοχώραφά σου</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(sarcastic) teach grandma how to suck eggs (telling an expert how to do things)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “come here grandpa so I can show you your vineyards”.</div><b>έλαιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έλαια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oil</div><b>έλατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έλατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fir tree, conifer</div><b>έλεγχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έλεγχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>control, check, audit</div>
  <div class='ex'>έλεγχος διαβατηρίων - passport control</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἔλεγχος.</div><b>έλεος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελέη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mercy, compassion, pity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. charity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔλεος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enough!</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε πρωί ξημερώματα τραγουδάει δυνατά – <b>έλεος</b> πια! - Every morning at dawn he sings loudly – enough already!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔλεος.</div><b>έλιγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελίγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>No definition available</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἕλιγμα. By surface analysis, ελίσσω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>έλκηθρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έλκηθρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sled, sledge, sleigh</div><b>έλκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έλκη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ulcer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) peptic ulcer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἕλκος. Unrelated to έλκω.</div><b>έλκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (no past) to draw, pull</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο μαγνήτης <b>έλκει</b> το σίδηρο. - The magnet attracts iron.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (no past) to draw, pull; to tow, pull</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το όχημα αυτό <b>έλκει</b> ημιρυμουλκούμενα. - This vehicle tows semi-trailers.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (no past) to draw (ancestry, rights) from</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έλκει</b> την καταγωγή του από σπουδαία οικογένεια. - S/he comes from a distinguished family.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Ancient Greek ἕλκω. Unrelated to έλκος n.</div><b>έλλειμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελλείμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deficit</div><b>έλλειψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελλείψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ellipse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ellipsis</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dearth, lack, shortage, want</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔλλειψις.</div><b>έλληνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έλληνες, feminine ελληνίδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adjectival use of <a href="bword://Έλληνας">Έλληνας</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθαν ένας Έλληνας και μία Ελληνίδα. Είναι οι <b>έλληνες</b> φίλοι μου. - One Greek (man) and one Greek (woman) came. They are my Greek friends.</div><b>έλξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έλξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. attraction, pull (an attractive force which causes motion towards the source)</div>
  <div class='ex'>νόμος της παγκόσμιας <b>έλξης</b> - law of universal gravitation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. traction (the act of pulling something along a surface using motive power)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pull-up</div>
  <div class='def'>4. attraction (the feeling of being attracted)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. attractiveness, pull (appeal, charm)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. attraction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἕλξις, with semantic loan from English attraction.</div><b>έλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) marsh, swamp, bog</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἕλος</div><b>έμβασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμβάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>remittance, postal or remote payment</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πολλές οικογένειες συντηρούνται από τα <b>εμβάσματα</b> των ξενιτεμένων μελών τους. - Many families are dependent on remittances from their emigrant members.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εμβάζω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>έμβλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμβλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emblem</div><b>έμβρυο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έμβρυα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>embryo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔμβρυον.</div><b>έμεσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμέσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vomit (regurgitated contents of stomach, usual term)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vomitus, vomit (regurgitated contents of stomach)</div><b>έμετος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έμετοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εμετός">εμετός</a></div><b>έμμεσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έμμεση, neuter έμμεσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indirect</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έμμεσο</b> αντικείμενο - indirect object</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἔμμεσος.</div><b>έμμετρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έμμετρη, neuter έμμετρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in verse, metrical</div><b>έμμονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έμμονη, neuter έμμονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obsessive, persistent, fixated, persevering (repeated and unyielding)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έμμονη</b> κακοκαιρία - persistent bad weather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔμμονος, from Ancient Greek ἐμμένω.</div><b>έμπειρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έμπειρη, neuter έμπειρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>experienced, adept, skilled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔμπειρος.</div><b>έμπνευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμπνεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inspiration (the act of an elevating or stimulating influence upon the intellect, emotions or creativity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inspiration (a new idea, especially one which arises suddenly and is clever or creative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔμπνευσις with semantic loan from French inspiration. By surface analysis, εμπνέω + -ση (-si).</div><b>έμπορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έμπορες, feminine εμπόρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) merchant, trader, dealer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έμπορ(ος)) + -ας (-as) &lt; Ancient Greek ἔμπορος.</div><b>έμπορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural έμποροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. merchant, trader, dealer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trafficker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἔμπορος.</div><b>έμπρακτα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://έμπρακτος">έμπρακτος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>actively, in practice</div><b>έμπρακτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έμπρακτη, neuter έμπρακτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>active, effective, practical, put into practice, real (given effect or manifested in reality by means of action)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έμπρακτη</b> βοήθεια - active/practical help/assistance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔμπρακτος with semantic loan from German tatsächlich.</div><b>έμφαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμφάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emphasis</div><b>έμφραγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμφράγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. heart attack, myocardial infarction</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) infarction</div><b>έμφραγμα του μυοκαρδίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμφράγματα του μυοκαρδίου)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>myocardial infarction</div><b>έμψυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έμψυχη, neuter έμψυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>animate (that lives)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ζωντανός (zontanós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔμψυχος.</div><b>ένα μήλο την ημέρα, τον γιατρό τον κάνει πέρα</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>an apple a day keeps the doctor away (apples are good for your health)</div><b>ένα χελιδόνι δεν φέρνει την άνοιξη</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>one swallow does not a summer make</div><b>έναντι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with genitive) towards (in part payment)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with genitive) against (for a receipt)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. comparatively</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔναντι.</div><b>έναρξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενάρξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beginning, commencement, inception</div>
  <div class='ex'>η τελετή <b>έναρξης</b> των Ολυμπιακών Αγώνων - the opening ceremony of the Olympic Games</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek ἔναρξις + -ση (-si).</div><b>ένας</b>
  <div><i>article, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indefinite) a, an</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἕνας, from Ancient Greek ἕνα the accusative of εἷς, from Proto-Hellenic *hens, *hmia, from Proto-Indo-European *sḗm, *smih₂.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>num, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>one</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἕνας, from Ancient Greek ἕνα the accusative of εἷς, from Proto-Hellenic *hens, *hmia, from Proto-Indo-European *sḗm, *smih₂.</div><b>ένας κούκος δε φέρνει την άνοιξη</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>one swallow does not a summer make</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην περιμένεις τώρα ότι θα αλλάξει εντελώς – <b>ένας κούκος δε φέρνει την άνοιξη</b>. - Don&#x27;t expect that he&#x27;ll completely change – one swallow does not a summer make.</div><b>έναστρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έναστρη, neuter έναστρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>starry, starfilled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἔναστρος.</div><b>ένατος</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine (feminine ένατη, neuter ένατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ninth, 9th</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔνατος.</div><b>έναυσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εναύσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trigger, spark (something that incites action)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔναυσμα.</div><b>ένδεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) lack, paucity (deficiency or insufficiency of something necessary)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) destitution, indigence, need, neediness, penury, poverty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔνδεια.</div><b>ένδειξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενδείξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indication, sign (a fact that shows that something exists or may happen)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clue (information which may lead one to a certain point or conclusion)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. reading (a value indicated by a measuring device)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. indication (any symptom or occurrence in a disease, which serves to direct to suitable remedies)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔνδειξις with -ξη (-xi) ending.</div><b>ένδεκα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://έντεκα">έντεκα</a></div><b>ένδοξος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ένδοξη, neuter ένδοξο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glorious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἔνδοξος.</div><b>ένδυμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενδύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. garment, item of clothing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dress, style of clothing</div>
  <div class='ex'>βραδινό <b>ένδυμα</b> - evening dress</div><b>ένεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. injection (method of drug administration)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) syringe; ampoule</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) injection (of cash)</div><b>ένζυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ένζυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enzyme</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from German Enzym (in its new meaning) from Byzantine Greek ἔνζυμον a substantivized neuter from the adjective ἔνζυμος, formed from the ancient ἐν + ζύμη.</div><b>ένθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>infix, interfix</div><b>έννεν Γιάννης, εν Γιαννάκης</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cyprus form of <a href="bword://όχι">όχι</a> Γιάννης, <a href="bword://Γιαννάκης">Γιαννάκης</a></div><b>έννοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έννοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. concept</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meaning, sense</div>
  <div class='def'>3. alternative form of <a href="bword://έγνοια">έγνοια</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔννοια. Morphologically from εν- (“in”) + νους (nous, “mind”).</div><b>ένοικος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ένοικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>resident of a house, occupant, either owner or tenant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔνοικος. By surface analysis, έν- + οίκος.</div><b>ένοπλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ένοπλη, neuter ένοπλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. under arms, armed</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>ένοπλες</b> δυνάμεις - the armed forces</div>
  <div class='def'>2. armed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ένοπλη</b> ληστεία - armed robbery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔνοπλος. Morphologically εν- (en-, “in”) + όπλο + -ος (-os).</div><b>ένορκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ένορκη, neuter ένορκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>under oath, sworn</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ένορκη</b> διοικητική εξέταση - a sworn administrative enquiry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔνορκος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ένορκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>juror</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔνορκος.</div><b>ένοχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ένοχη, neuter ένοχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guilty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔνοχος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ένοχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>culprit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔνοχος.</div><b>ένρινα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in a nasal manner</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ένρινος">ένρινος</a></div><b>ένρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έρρινη, neuter έρρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έρρινος">έρρινος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See έρρινος.</div><b>ένσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. objection</div>
  <div class='def'>2. plea, objection</div><b>ένστασις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://ένσταση">ένσταση</a></div><b>ένστικτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>instinct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French instinct.</div><b>ένστολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ένστολη, neuter ένστολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in uniform, uniformed</div><b>ένταλμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εντάλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>order, warrant (written authorisation, order, command, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔνταλμα.</div><b>ένταξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εντάξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accession, integration, incorporation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔνταξις.</div><b>ένταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εντάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. volume (music, sound)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intensity (emotional)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. intensity (light)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. gauge (knitting/crochet swatch)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐντείνω.</div><b>έντεκα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eleven</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἕνδεκᾰ.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek э́ндэка, э́ндека.</div><b>έντερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έντερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intestine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔντερον.</div><b>έντεχνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έντεχνη, neuter έντεχνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. art (of music, songs, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. artful, artistic</div>
  <div class='def'>3. skillful, masterful</div>
  <div class='def'>4. subtle, clever, cunning</div><b>έντιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έντιμη, neuter έντιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. honourable (UK), honorable (US) (worthy of respect; conforming to what honour dictates)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. honest, upright (scrupulous with regard to truth and ethics)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (as an honorific address or title, in the superlative Εντιμότατος) Honourable (UK), Honorable (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔντιμος with semantic loan from French honnête and honorable.</div><b>έντομο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έντομα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔντομον.</div><b>έντονα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://έντονος">έντονος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intensely, heavily</div><b>έντονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έντονη, neuter έντονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strong, powerful</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έντονα</b> χρώματα - strong colours/colors</div>
  <div class='def'>2. severe, intense</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έντονος</b> πόνος - severe pain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔντονος, from ἐν- (en-) + τείνω, stem τον- + -ος (-os). Stem also found at ἐντείνω. Compare English intense.</div><b>έντρομος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έντρομη, neuter έντρομο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terrified, frightened, terror-stricken (utterly scared)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι ξεχύθηκαν <b>έντρομοι</b> στους δρόμους μετά τον σεισμό. - Everyone spilled out terror-stricken into the streets after the earthquake.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἔντρομος, equivalent to εν (en, “in”) + τρόμος.</div><b>έντυπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έντυπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. print, printed matter, copy, form</div>
  <div class='def'>2. form (to be filled in)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έντυπο</b> αίτησης - application form</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nominal neuter form of έντυπος, from εν- (en-) + τύπος.</div><b>ένωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. uniting, joining, combining, connecting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. union, fusion, association</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ευρωπαϊκή <b>Ένωση</b> - European Union</div>
  <div class='def'>3. combination, compound</div>
  <div class='def'>4. sexual union</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἕνω(σις)) + modern ending -ση (-si), the former a nominal formation from ενώνω. The modern sense of &quot;association of companies or states&quot; is a semantic loan from French union.</div><b>έξαλλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έξαλλη, neuter έξαλλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frantic, frenzied, very angry</div><b>έξαφνα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άξαφνα">άξαφνα</a></div><b>έξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>habit (action performed repeatedly and automatically, usually without awareness)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἕξις.</div><b>έξι</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) six</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἕξ, from Proto-Hellenic *hwéks, from Proto-Indo-European *swéḱs.
Compare Mariupol Greek экс (eks).</div><b>έξοδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έξοδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in plural) expenses</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://έξοδος">έξοδος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔξοδος with change of gender.</div><b>έξοδοι κινδύνου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://έξοδος">έξοδος</a> κινδύνου</div><b>έξοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έξοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exit, way out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔξοδος.</div><b>έξοδος κινδύνου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έξοδοι κινδύνου)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emergency exit</div><b>έξοχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έξοχη, neuter έξοχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>superb, excellent</div><b>έξτρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French extra.</div><b>έξτρα παρθένο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extra virgin</div>
  <div class='ex'>έξτρα παρθένο ελαιόλαδο - extra virgin olive oil</div><b>έξυπνα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://έξυπνος">έξυπνος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cleverly, intelligently, smartly</div><b>έξυπνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έξυπνη, neuter έξυπνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clever, intelligent, sharp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἔξυπνος, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἔξυπνος, from Ancient Greek ἐξ- (ex-) + ὕπνος. Doublet of ξύπνιος.</div><b>έξω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>out, outside</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἔξω.</div><b>έξω κούκλα, μέσα πανούκλα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>describing a beautiful but troublesome woman</div><b>έξω φρενών</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wrathful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔξω φρενῶν.</div><b>έπαθλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έπαθλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prize, award (an honour or reward striven for in a competitive contest)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔπαθλον.</div><b>έπαινος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έπαινοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. praise, credit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. award, credit</div><b>έπειτα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>after; next</div><b>έπιπλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έπιπλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>furniture (one piece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔπιπλον, ἔπιπλα.</div><b>έπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έπη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. epic (an extended narrative poem in elevated or dignified language, celebrating the feats of a deity, demigod (heroic epic), other legend or traditional hero)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ηρωικό <b>έπος</b> - heroic epic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. epic (a series of events considered appropriate to an epic)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔπος with the sense &#x27;epic&#x27; from its plural, ἔπη.</div><b>έπρεπε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal) third-person singular imperfect active indicative of <a href="bword://πρέπει">πρέπει</a>; it was fitting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (impersonal) third-person singular imperfect active indicative of <a href="bword://πρέπει">πρέπει</a>; had to, ought, should</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έπρεπε</b> να τελειώσω την δουλειά. - I had to finish the job.</div><b>έρανος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έρανοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fund, collection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔρανος.</div><b>έρβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) erbium</div><b>έργα τέχνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://έργο">έργο</a> τέχνης</div><b>έργο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έργα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. work, project; film, stage play, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>2. work, project; scientific research project</div>
  <div class='def'>3. work, project; art work, painting, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>4. work, project; building project, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>5. work (measured in joules)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. thermodynamic work</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔργον, from Proto-Indo-European *wérǵom.</div><b>έργο τέχνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έργα τέχνης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>work of art</div><b>έρευνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έρευνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. research, examination (especially scientific or legal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. investigation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἔρευνα, from ἐρευνάω/ἐρευνῶ.</div><b>έρευσα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Simplified spelling of έρρευσα</div><b>έρημος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έρημη, neuter έρημο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. desolate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lonely, lonesome (U.S.), friendless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔρημος, Attic of ἐρῆμος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έρημοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>desert, wilderness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔρημος, Attic of ἐρῆμος.</div><b>έριδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έριδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. feud (a violent, persistent and mutual hostility)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://έρις">έρις</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἔρις.</div><b>έρις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έριδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. discord, conflict</div>
  <div class='def'>2. feud, vendetta</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔρις.</div><b>έρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ballast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. support, prop</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) principles</div><b>έρπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://έρπης">έρπης</a></div><b>έρπης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έρπητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>herpes</div><b>έρπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to crawl, creep</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το φίδι και όλα τα ερπετά, <b>έρπουν</b>. - The snake and all reptiles crawl.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cringe, grovel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἕρπω.</div><b>έρρινα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in a nasal manner</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://έρρινος">έρρινος</a></div><b>έρρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έρρινη, neuter έρρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sound coming from the nasal cavity</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η προφορά του είναι πολύ <b>έρρινη</b> - His accent is very nasal.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nasal</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα <b>έρρινα</b> σύμφωνα μι και νι - the nasal consonants mu and nu</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From earlier ἔνρινος, with regressive assimilation; from Ancient Greek ἐν (en, “in”) + ῥίς + -ος (-os).
The phonetic sense is a semantic loan from French nasal.
The Hellenistic Koine noun ἔρρινον is semantically unrelated.</div><b>έρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to come</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την είδα να <b>έρχεται</b> στην κατεύθυνσή μου. - I saw her coming in my direction.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cost</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πόσο <b>έρχεται</b> αυτό το αμάξι; - How much is this car?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with <a href="bword://σε">σε</a> (se) ) come to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τελικά, οι δυό τους <b>ήρθαν</b> στα χέρια. - In the end, they came to blows.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to suit, match (go well with)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το φόρεμα δε σου <b>ήρθε</b> καλά. - That dress doesn&#x27;t suit you.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (before and followed by να (na) ) feel like, get the urge (to have a desire for)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς σου <b>ήρθε</b> να φορέσεις τόσο άσχημο παλτό; - What possessed you to wear that ugly coat?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔρχομαι, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁ergʰ- (“to move, go”). The meaning shift and the perfective forms are from the suppletive aorist ἦλθον (with regular shift λθ &gt; ρθ). Compare also the Albanian form erdha (“I came”).</div><b>έρχομαι στα χέρια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to come to blows (to fight physically)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ευτυχώς που ήταν εδώ ο πατέρας σου, διότι κόντεψαν να <b>έρθουν στα χέρια</b> οι δύο τους. - It was lucky that your father was here because the two of them nearly came to blows.</div><b>έρωντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έρωντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dittany</div><b>έρως</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έρωτας">έρωτας</a> (love)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔρως.</div><b>έρωτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έρωτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. love</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔρως, ἔρωτᾰς.</div><b>έσοδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έσοδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. revenue, income</div>
  <div class='def'>2. turnover</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔσοδος, εἴσοδος with change of gender.</div><b>έσοξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pike, Esox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔσοξ.</div><b>έσσω</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) home</div><b>έστω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. even so</div>
  <div class='def'>2. let&#x27;s say, let&#x27;s suppose, let&#x27;s assume</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>“let it be!” (archaic, in set phrases from Ancient Greek)</div><b>έτη φωτός</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://έτος">έτος</a> φωτός</div><b>έτοιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έτοιμη, neuter έτοιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ready, prepared</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek ἕτοιμος.</div><b>έτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. year</div>
  <div class='def'>2. volume (issues of a periodical over a period of one year)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔτος.</div><b>έτος φωτός</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έτη φωτός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>light year</div><b>έτρεχε να μη βραχεί κι έπεσε στο ποτάμι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>In cases where trying to solve one problem leads to an even bigger one.</div><b>έτσ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elided form of <a href="bword://έτσι">έτσι</a></div><b>έτσι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thus; like this; like that</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) for no reason or for no money</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Ancient Greek οὕτως. There is a strengthened form οὑτωσῑ́ in Attic Greek. Middle forms include Byzantine Greek έτις, possibly via the variants *έτω or *έτωσι. Derivation from Latin etsi should be ruled out for semantic reasons.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable) (always with article) indicates a known person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Ancient Greek οὕτως. There is a strengthened form οὑτωσῑ́ in Attic Greek. Middle forms include Byzantine Greek έτις, possibly via the variants *έτω or *έτωσι. Derivation from Latin etsi should be ruled out for semantic reasons.</div><b>έτσι κ&#x27;έτσι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έτσι">έτσι</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://έτσι">έτσι</a></div><b>έτσι κέτσι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έτσι">έτσι</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://έτσι">έτσι</a></div><b>έτσι και έτσι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>so-so, middling</div>
  <div class='ex'>«Πώς πήγε σήμερα η μέρα σου;» «Έτσι κι έτσι.» - &quot;How are you today?&quot; &quot;So-so&quot;</div><b>έτσι κι έτσι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έτσι">έτσι</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://έτσι">έτσι</a></div><b>έτσι κι αλλιώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>one way or another</div><b>έτυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural έτυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔτυμον.</div><b>έφεδρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έφεδρη, neuter έφεδρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reserve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔφεδρος.</div><b>έφεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εφέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. appeal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. appeal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ἔφεσις</div><b>έφηβη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έφηβες, masculine έφηβος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female teenager</div><b>έφηβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έφηβοι, feminine έφηβη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>male teenager</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔφηβος.</div><b>έφιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural έφιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Dolichophis caspius, the Caspian whipsnake.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in compounds) Certain other whip snakes. (usually Dolichophis sp.)</div><b>έφιππος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine έφιππη, neuter έφιππο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mounted, on horseback</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) synonym of <a href="bword://καβαλάρης">καβαλάρης</a> m</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἔφιππος.</div><b>έφοδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://έφοδος">έφοδος</a></div><b>έφοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έφοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. attack, assault</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) round (postman&#x27;s, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔφοδος.</div><b>έφορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural έφοροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. supervisor, superintendent, curator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ephor (leader in ancient Sparta)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔφορος.</div><b>έχθρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έχθρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hostility, enmity, hate, dislike</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔχθρᾱ.</div><b>έχιδνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έχιδνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adder, viper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) an evil, cunning woman</div>
  <div class='def'>3. echidna (any of the four species of small spined monotremes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔχιδνα.</div><b>έχομε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of έχουμε</div>
  <div class='ex'>first-person plural present of έχω (écho)</div><b>έχοντας</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) having, with</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κινείται <b>έχοντας</b> δύο μηχανές. - It moves with two engines</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as auxiliary) + infinitive to form periphrastic perfect participles</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του έδωσα προαγωγή <b>έχοντας</b> πεισθεί για τις ικανότητες του. - Ι promoted him, having been convinced of his abilities.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of έχω. It functions as an adverb. From the accusative τόν ἔχοντα of the participle of Ancient Greek ἔχων.</div><b>έχουνε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of έχουν: &quot;they have&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>third-person plural present indicative of έχω (écho)</div><b>έχτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έχτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έχθρα">έχθρα</a></div><b>έχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to have/hold, in the sense of holding (in one&#x27;s possession) (a [legal/personal/possibly &#x27;undesired&#x27;]) property</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έχω</b> αυτοκίνητο - I have a car</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be (have/hold a property, characteristic)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έχει</b> κρύο σήμερα. - It is cold today.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to feel, have feelings</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>έχεις</b>; Γιατί κλαις; - What&#x27;s [wrong] with you? Why are you crying?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (auxiliary) to have +verbs (or participles) to form perfect tenses.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έχω</b> διαβάσει - I have read</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔχω. Cognate with Mariupol Greek э́ху.</div><b>έχω ακουστά</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (idiomatic, transitive) to have heard of (to be aware of through second-hand knowledge)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτόν τον ηθοποιό τον <b>έχεις ακουστά</b>; - Have you ever heard of that actor?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic, intransitive) to know of (to know of something in general but not precise details)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έχω ακουστά</b> για τον θάνατό της αλλά δεν γνωρίζω λεπτομέρειες. - I know about her death but I don&#x27;t know the details.</div><b>έχω μια ερώτηση</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I have a question</div><b>έχω τα μάτια μου δεκατέσσερα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) keep one&#x27;s eyes peeled, keep an eye out (to be on the alert)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να <b>έχεις τα μάτια σου δεκατέσσερα</b> σ’ αυτόν τον δρόμο· είναι πολύ επικίνδυνος. - Keep your eyes peeled on that road; it&#x27;s very dangerous.</div><b>έψιλον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) epsilon, the fifth letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἒ ψιλόν, further from ἔ + neuter of ψιλός. Thus, “simple e”, in contrast with αι (ai), which had become identical in pronunciation by the Koine period.</div><b>έως</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with accusative) until</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάω <b>έως</b> την άκρη του κόσμου. - I go to the end of the world.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with accusative) until, before</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα είμαι στο σπίτι <b>έως</b> τις έξι. - I&#x27;ll be home before six.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἕως.</div><b>ή στραβός είν&#x27; ο γιαλός ή στραβά αρμενίζουμε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(ironic) Expresses acknowledgement, under compulsion of circumstances, that one is responsible for having made a mistake.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “either the shore is awry or we&#x27;re sailing awry”.</div><b>ήβη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) puberty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) pubis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἥβη.</div><b>ήδη</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. already (prior to some time)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. by now, by this time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἤδη.</div><b>ήθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) straining, filtering</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἤθησις, from Ancient Greek ἠθέω.</div><b>ήθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ήθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ethos</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) ethos: social or cultural behaviour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἦθος.</div><b>ήλεκτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ήλεκτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. amber (fossil pine resin)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. electrum (alloy of silver and gold)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἤλεκτρον.</div><b>ήλιον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of ήλιο</div><b>ήλιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ήλιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sun (star that the Earth orbits)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Γη περιστρέφεται γύρω από τον <b>Ήλιο</b>. - The Earth revolves around the Sun.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) sunlight, daylight</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) sunny weather</div>
  <div class='def'>4. sun (any star orbited)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) sunflower</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἥλιος, from Proto-Hellenic *hāwélios, from a pre-Hellenic or later Proto-Indo-European *sāwélios, from Proto-Indo-European *sóh₂wl̥.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek и́люс.</div><b>ήλιος του μεσονυκτίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) midnight sun</div><b>ήλιος του μεσονυχτίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ήλιος">ήλιος</a> <a href="bword://του">του</a> μεσονυκτίου</div><b>ήλιου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://ήλιος">ήλιος</a> m and ήλιο n</div><b>ήμαρτον</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. forgive me, have mercy, mea culpa</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ήμαρτον</b> Θεέ μου! - God forbid!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) that&#x27;s it, enough, no more (expression of indignation at discomfort or suffering)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ἥμαρτον, the aorist first person singular of Ancient Greek ἁμαρτάνω, a use popularised in Byzantine Greek from the New Testament.</div><b>ήμεθα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of ήμαστε</div><b>ήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ήμερη, neuter ήμερo)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. domesticated, domestic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tame, placid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἥμερος.</div><b>ήμισυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ημίσεα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. half</div>
  <div class='def'>2. minim</div><b>ήμισυς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ημίσεια, neuter ήμισυ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μισός">μισός</a> (half)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο ακίνητο ανήκει κατά το <b>ήμισυ</b> στο δημόσιο και κατά το <b>ήμισυ</b> σε ιδιώτη. - The property is half public and half private.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἥμισυς.</div><b>ήντα</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://είντα">είντα</a></div><b>ήπαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ήπατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) liver</div>
  <div class='ex'>μόλυνση του <b>ήπατος</b> - infection of the liver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἧπαρ, from Proto-Hellenic *eyékʷər, from Proto-Indo-European *Hyékʷr̥.</div><b>ήπειρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://ήπειρος">ήπειρος</a></div><b>ήπειρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ήπειροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. continent, landmass</div>
  <div class='ex'>η Γη έχει 6 <b>ηπείρους</b> - the earth has 6 continents</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mainland</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἤπειρος.</div><b>ήπια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://πίνω">πίνω</a> &quot;I drank&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>older form: ἔπιον</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ήπιος">ήπιος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>older form: ἠπία</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ήπιος">ήπιος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>older spelling: ἤπια</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mildly, calmly</div><b>ήπιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ήπια, neuter ήπιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mild</div><b>ήρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ryegrass, darnel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. weed</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular imperfect of <a href="bword://αίρω">αίρω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://αίρω">αίρω</a></div><b>ήρεμα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calmly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἠρέμα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ήρεμος">ήρεμος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἠρέμα.</div><b>ήρεμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ήρεμη, neuter ήρεμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quiet, peaceful, calm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἤρεμος, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁rem- (“to rest”).</div><b>ήρωας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ήρωες, feminine ηρωίδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hero (courageous person; chief character in drama, book, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἥρως.</div><b>ήσκιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ήσκιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ίσκιος">ίσκιος</a></div><b>ήσυχα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quietly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ήσυχος">ήσυχος</a></div><b>ήσυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ήσυχη, neuter ήσυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quiet, calm, not busy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἥσυχος.</div><b>ήτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) eta, the seventh letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἦτᾰ, from ἧτᾰ.</div><b>ήττα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ήττες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>defeat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἧττα, from ἧσσα.</div><b>ήχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ήχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sound, noise, tone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἦχος.</div><b>ίβηξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ibex, wild goat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Latin ībex</div><b>ίδιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ίδια, neuter ίδιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (emphatic) own</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την είδα με τα <b>ίδια</b> μου τα μάτια. - I saw her with my own eyes.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (emphatic) myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, themselves</div>
  <div class='ex'>εγώ ο <b>ίδιος</b> - I myself</div>
  <div class='def'>3. identical (in size, number etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχουν <b>ίδιο</b> μέγεθος. - They are the same size.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. same, similar</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχουν τα <b>ίδια</b> χρώματα. - They are the same colours.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἴδιος.</div><b>ίδρυμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ιδρύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>institution, foundation, institute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Koine Greek ἵδρῡμα. Also semantic loan from English institute, foundation or French institut, fondation.</div><b>ίδρυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιδρύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>founding, foundation, establishment (the act whereby something is founded or established; the result thereof)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἵδρυσις. By surface analysis, ιδρύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>ίδρωτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ίδρωτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ιδρώτας">ιδρώτας</a></div><b>ίκτερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural ίκτεροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) jaundice</div><b>ίλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ίλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>squadron</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἴλη.</div><b>ίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fibre (UK), fiber (US) (a single elongated piece of a given material)</div>
  <div class='ex'>οπτική <b>ίνα</b> - optical fibre</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fibre (UK), fiber (US) (a long tubular cell found in bodily tissue)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μυϊκή <b>ίνα</b> - muscle fibre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἴνα, accusative of ἴς.</div><b>ίνδαλμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ινδάλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>idol, cultural icon</div><b>ίνδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural ίνδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) indium</div><b>ίντα</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://είντα">είντα</a></div><b>ίντερνετ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Internet, indeclinable) internet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English internet.</div><b>ίντσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ίντσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inch</div><b>ίππος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ίπποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) horse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) knight</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pommel horse</div>
  <div class='def'>4. horsepower</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἵππος. Sense 4 is a semantic loan from French cheval.</div><b>ίπταμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fly, rise into the air</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἵπταμαι, late metathetic form of Ancient Greek πέτομαι from Proto-Indo-European *péth₂eti (“to fly”) from the root *peth₂- (literally “to spread one’s wings”).</div><b>ίριδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ίριδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. iris (part of the eye)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. iris (flowering plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rainbow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See Ancient Greek ἶρις.</div><b>ίρις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ίριδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. iris (flowering plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. iris (part of the eye)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, the Ancient Greek ἶρις. See ίριδα.</div><b>ίσα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ίσια">ίσια</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. come on</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hang on</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ίσος">ίσος</a></div><b>ίσαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ίσαλος or ίσαλη, neuter ίσαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>waterline, sea-level (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ίσ(ος)) + Ancient Greek ἅλ(ς), “sea”) + -ος (-os), analogously to ύφαλος.</div><b>ίσαλος γραμμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ίσαλοι γραμμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>waterline</div><b>ίσαμε</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. until, till, til, &#x27;til</div>
  <div class='def'>2. about</div><b>ίσια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. straight ahead</div>
  <div class='def'>2. straightforwardly</div><b>ίσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ίσια, neuter ίσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. straight, level</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ποδήλατο έχει <b>ίσιο</b> τιμόνι. - The bicycle has straight handlebars</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) frank, straightforward, direct</div><b>ίσκιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ίσκιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shadow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἥσκιος, from Ancient Greek σκιά. Doublet of σκιά.</div><b>ίσον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>equals</div>
  <div class='ex'>πέντε συν ένα <b>ίσον</b> έξι - five plus one equals six</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantive neuter formation from ίσος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) equals sign</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ίσον</b> ισχύει όταν α = β. - The equals sign comes into force when a = b.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantive neuter formation from ίσος.</div><b>ίσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ίση, neuter ίσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. equal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. equal (in size, quality, value, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. having equal rights</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἴσος.</div><b>ίσως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>might, may, perhaps, maybe (expressing possibility)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ίσως</b> να είναι σπίτι. - He might be home.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἴσως, adverbial form of ἴσος. By surface analysis, ίσος + -ως (-os).</div><b>ίχνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ίχνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. track (impression left by foot, paw, wheel, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spoor (animal track)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trace (very low concentrations of one material in another)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βρέθηκαν <b>ίχνη</b> Pb στο νερό. - They found traces of Pb (lead) in the water.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἴχνος.</div><b>ίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>virosis</div><b>α</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Expresses wonder, surprise or joy; ah, ooh, oh, wow</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Α</b>, τι πανέμορφη θέα! - Wow, what a gorgeous view!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Expresses desire or wish; oh</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Α</b> και να ερχόταν να με δει! - Oh, if only he would come to see me!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Expresses anger or threat; oh</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Α</b> που να σε πάρει ο διάολος! - Oh, the devil take you!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. Expresses impatience; oh, ah</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Α</b> δεν μπορώ να περιμένω άλλο! - Ah, I can&#x27;t wait any longer!</div>
  <div class='def'>5. Expresses confirmation of a fact; oh, ah</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Α</b> ναι βεβαίως ήταν αυτός! - Ah, yes, of course it was him!</div>
  <div class='def'>6. Expresses desire against; oh, ah</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Α</b> όχι αυτός πάλι! - Oh, not him again!</div>
  <div class='def'>7. Answer word, can be either positive or negative; eh, meh</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα ’ρθεις απόψε στο πάρτι; <b>Α</b>, μπα! - Will you be coming to the party tonight? Ah, nah!</div>
  <div class='def'>8. Answer word of indifference; meh</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πως πάει το σχόλιο τελευταία; <b>Α</b>, έτσι κι έτσι! - How&#x27;s school going lately? Meh, so-so!</div>
  <div class='def'>9. contraction of <a href="bword://άντε">άντε</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Α</b> να χαθείς! - Oh, get lost!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἆ.</div><b>α λα καρτ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>older spelling of <a href="bword://αλά">αλά</a> καρτ: à la carte</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κυβέρνηση κατηγορείται ότι ακολουθεί τις αρχές της <b>αλά καρτ</b>. - The government are being accused of following their principles in an à la carte manner.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French à la carte. See α λα (a la) &amp; αλά.</div><b>α να χαθείς</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) get lost, sod off, buzz off, do one (go away)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Α να χαθείς</b>, παλιάνθρωπε! - Get lost, you old git!</div><b>α ποστεριόρι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αποστεριόρι">αποστεριόρι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin a posteriori.</div><b>α στο διάλο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. literally: go to the devil! (go to hell!)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://άντε">άντε</a> στο διάλο</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (idiomatic) really? go on!</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κέρδισε 1.000.000 ευρώ στο λαχείο.
<b>Α στο διάλο</b>! Μη μου το λες! - He won 1,000,000 euros on the lottery.
Go on! Are you serious (literally: Do not tell me)!</div><b>α στο διάολο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. literally: go to the devil! (go to hell!)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://άντε">άντε</a> στο διάολο</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (idiomatic) really? go on!</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κέρδισε 1.000.000 ευρώ στο λαχείο.
<b>Α στο διάολο</b>! Μη μου το λες! - He won 1,000,000 euros on the lottery.
Go on! Are you serious (literally: Do not tell me)!</div><b>α-λα-καρτ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://α">α</a> <a href="bword://λα">λα</a> καρτ (a la kart)</div><b>α΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Α΄">Α΄</a></div><b>α΄ συνθ.</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://α΄">α΄</a> <a href="bword://συνθετικό">συνθετικό</a> (“1st combining form”)</div><b>α΄ συνθετικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://α΄">α΄</a> <a href="bword://συνθετικό">συνθετικό</a></div><b>α΄ συνθετικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural α΄ συνθετικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(first person, in compounds) 1st combining form</div><b>αέναα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ceaselessly, continuously, perpetually, in an uninterruptedly continuous manner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αένα(ος), “perpetual”) + -α (-a, suffix for adverbs)</div><b>αέναος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αέναη, neuter αέναο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>continuing uninterruptedly: perpetual, ceaseless</div><b>αέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αέρηδες or αέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. air</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (often) wind</div>
  <div class='def'>3. demeanour, demeanor, air</div>
  <div class='def'>4. goodwill (part of the value of a business)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. choke</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (colloquial) key-money</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀήρ</div><b>αέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncommon) a different utterance of <a href="bword://αγέρι">αγέρι</a></div><b>αέρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αέρινη, neuter αέρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. graceful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diaphanous</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ethereal, airy</div><b>αέριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gas</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wind, flatulence, fart</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://αέριος">αέριος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://αέριος">αέριος</a></div><b>αέριο του θερμοκηπίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αέρια του θερμοκηπίου)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) greenhouse gas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, αέριο + θερμοκήπιο, literally “gas of the greenhouse”.</div><b>αέριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αέρια, neuter αέριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. air (attributive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gas (attributive), gaseous</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αέριος</b> γίγαντας - gas giant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ᾱ̓έριος with semantic loan from French gazeux.</div><b>αέρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αερίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ventilation, airing, aeration</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ventilation (mechanical)</div><b>αέτειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αέτεια, neuter αέτειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. aquiline</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αέτεια</b> μύτη - aquiline nose</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of eagles</div><b>αέτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αετώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pediment, gable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αετός + -ωμα (-oma)</div><b>αήθης</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unethical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From adjective ἀήθης.</div><b>αήρ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://αέρας">αέρας</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. a small cloth used cover religious objects</div><b>αήττητος</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unbeaten, undefeated, invincible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀήττητος.</div><b>αίγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αίγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(common, formal) goat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek αἴγα, from Ancient Greek αἴξ, αἶγα.</div><b>αίγαγρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αίγαγροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ibex, chamois, wild goat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek αἴγαγρος.</div><b>αίγειρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αίγειροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a species of poplar (tree)</div><b>αίγλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) glitter, glamour (UK), glamor (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) glory, prestige</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) halo</div><b>αίθουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αίθουσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>room, chamber, hall, auditorium (generally large and public)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek αἴθουσα.</div><b>αίθριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αίθρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. atrium (square internal courtyard in a Roman house)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. patio</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://αίθριος">αίθριος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://αίθριος">αίθριος</a></div><b>αίθριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αίθρια, neuter αίθριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fair, cloudless, bright and sunny</div><b>αίλουρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αίλουροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cat, a feline or member of Felidae (lion, tiger, puma etc).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αἴλουρος.</div><b>αίμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blood</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek αἷμα / αἷμαν, from Ancient Greek αἷμα, which is of ultimately uncertain and highly contested origin (see Ancient Greek lemma for various proposed derivations from Proto-Indo-European or Northwest Semitic languages).</div><b>αίνιγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αινίγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. riddle, puzzle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. enigma</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek αἴνιγμα.</div><b>αίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>praise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αἶνος.</div><b>αίρεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αιρέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. heresy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sect, a sect&#x27;s followers</div><b>αίρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be lifted</div>
  <div class='ex'>In polytonic script: αἴρομαι</div><b>αίρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) to lift and carry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) to lift and carry; to take up, assume (a burden)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Χριστός <b>αίρει</b> τις αμαρτίες του κόσμου. - Christ takes upon himself the sins of the world.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to remove (an obstacle), lift (a ban or an embargo), raise (a siege)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αίρεται</b> η παρεξήγηση - the misunderstanding is resolved</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to remove (an obstacle), lift (a ban or an embargo), raise (a siege); to revoke, recall (a decision) (especially in legal terminology)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η βουλή <b>αίρει</b> την εμπιστοσύνη της προς την κυβέρνηση. - Parliament lifts its confidence to the government.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to rise to a superior level</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην Αναγέννηση η τέχνη <b>αίρεται</b> στην τελειότητα. - In the Renaissance, art is lifted to perfection.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek αἴρω.</div><b>αίσθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αισθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feeling</div><b>αίσθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αισθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sensation, sense</div>
  <div class='ex'>πέντε <b>αισθήσεις</b> - five senses</div>
  <div class='def'>2. perception</div>
  <div class='def'>3. feeling</div>
  <div class='def'>4. impression</div>
  <div class='def'>5. notion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αἴσθησις.</div><b>αίσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αίσια, neuter αίσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>auspicious, favourable (UK), favorable (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αίσιο</b> τέλος - happy ending</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αἴσιος, from αἶσα.</div><b>αίσχιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αίσχιστη, neuter αίσχιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>most shameful, execrable</div><b>αίσχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αίσχη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (sometimes) disgrace, shame (something to regret)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είδαμε το αισχος της πείνας στις χώρες της Αφρικής.<b></b> - We saw the disgrace of hunger in the African countries.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (attributive, broadly) bad, awful, crap (anything bad or of low quality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ταινία ήταν <b>αίσχος</b>. - The film was awful.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αἶσχος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shame, disgraceful, outrageous</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι οι εργάτες στην απεργία φώναζαν «<b>αισχος</b>!». - All the workers on strike were shouting &#x27;shame!&#x27;.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αἶσχος.</div><b>αίτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αιτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>request, demand</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>αίτημα</b> των μαθητών απορρίφθηκε - the students&#x27; request was rejected</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αἴτημᾰ.</div><b>αίτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αιτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. request</div>
  <div class='def'>2. application</div>
  <div class='ex'>συμπληρώστε την <b>αίτηση</b> - fill in the application form</div>
  <div class='def'>3. petition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αἴτησις.</div><b>αίτια και αιτιατά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://αίτιο">αίτιο</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://αιτιατό">αιτιατό</a></div><b>αίτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αίτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αιτία">αιτία</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://αίτιος">αίτιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://αίτιος">αίτιος</a></div><b>αίτιο και αιτιατό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αίτια και αιτιατά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cause and effect</div><b>αίτιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αίτια, neuter αίτιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>responsible for, causative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek αἴτιος.</div><b>αίφνης</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suddenly</div><b>αα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Α">Α</a>.<a href="bword://Α">Α</a>. (A.A.)</div><b>αβάγιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάγιστη, neuter αβάγιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflexible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unpersuaded, unpersuadable, uncompromising</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek αβἄγιστος.</div><b>αβάδιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάδιστη, neuter αβάδιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impassable, unpassed</div><b>αβάθων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>see άβαθων, genitive plural of <a href="bword://άβαθα">άβαθα</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>more formal αβαθών (avathón) of αβαθή (avathí)</div><b>αβάντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβάντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. help, advantage</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δάσκαλός μου είναι μεγάλη <b>αβάντα</b>. - My teacher is a great help.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. benefit, advantage, profit, gain (especially when it is unfair or objectionable)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. backing, support</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Old Italian avantare.</div><b>αβάντζο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβάντζα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. raise (gambling stakes)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) advantage, benefit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. prepayment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian avanzo.</div><b>αβάντσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβάντσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. advance (of wages, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prepayment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. advantage</div>
  <div class='def'>4. condescending behaviour</div><b>αβάντσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβάντσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβάντζο">αβάντζο</a></div><b>αβάπτιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάπτιστη, neuter αβάπτιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unbaptised (UK), unbaptized (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί δεν έγραψες το όνομά σου, <b>αβάπτιστος</b> είσαι; - Why didn&#x27;t you write your name? Are you not baptized?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) unbeliever, infidel</div><b>αβάρα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. order to fend off</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as noun) in the phrases</div>
  <div class='ex'>βάζω <b>αβάρα</b> / κάνω <b>αβάρα</b> - I fend off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from αβαράρω</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (vernacular) fend off!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://αβαράρω">αβαράρω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://αβαράρω">αβαράρω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from αβαράρω</div><b>αβάρετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάρετη, neuter αβάρετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indefatigable, tireless, untiring, unwearying</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unharmed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “un-”) + βαρετός.</div><b>αβάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://αββάς">αββάς</a></div><b>αβάσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάσιμη, neuter αβάσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unsubstantiated, groundless, unjustified, baseless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “un-”) + βάσιμος.</div><b>αβάσκαντος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάσκαντη, neuter αβάσκαντο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unaffected by the evil eye</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not giving or casting the evil eye</div><b>αβάστακτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάστακτη, neuter αβάστακτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβάσταχτος">αβάσταχτος</a></div><b>αβάσταχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάσταχτη, neuter αβάσταχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. uncontrollable, irresistible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unbearable, intolerable, insufferable</div><b>αβάφτιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβάφτιστη, neuter αβάφτιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβάπτιστος">αβάπτιστος</a></div><b>αβέβ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://αβέβαιος">αβέβαιος</a>: uncertain</div><b>αβέβαια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncertainly, doubtfully</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://αβέβαιος">αβέβαιος</a></div><b>αβέβαιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβέβαιη, neuter αβέβαιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. uncertain, unsure, doubtful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unclear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἀβέβαιος.</div><b>αβέλτερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβέλτερη, neuter αβέλτερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stupid, foolish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mentally slow</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (as a noun) someone who can be described as such</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀβέλτερος.</div><b>αβέρτα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. openly, bluntly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. profusely, freely, ad lib, without limit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. openly (in full view)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://αβέρτος">αβέρτος</a></div><b>αβέρτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβέρτη, neuter αβέρτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. open</div>
  <div class='def'>2. roomy, uncluttered</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unfurled (sails)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) open, clear, honest, direct, open hearted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan averto, from Latin apertus.</div><b>αβίαστα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. effortlessly, easily</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unrestrainedly, without coercion</div><b>αβίαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβίαστη, neuter αβίαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. effortless, leisurely, unhurried</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unrestrained, natural</div>
  <div class='def'>3. intact, unbroken</div><b>αβίδωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβίδωτη, neuter αβίδωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. screwed, not screwed in, not fastened with a screw</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unbolted, not bolted on, not fixed with a bolt</div><b>αβίωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβίωτη, neuter αβίωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unbearable, intolerable, miserable, wretched, impossibly difficult</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του έκανε το βίο <b>αβίωτο</b>. - He has made his life unbearable.</div><b>αβαείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβαεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abbey</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abbacy (position of abbot)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A learned creation ἀββαεῖον, from ἀββ(ᾶς)) + neuter suffix -εῖον &gt; modern -είο, with simplification of double consonant and accents. A calque of French abbaye (“abbey”), from Latin abbatīa.</div><b>αβαθής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβαθής, neuter αβαθές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shallow, not deep (of water, vessel, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) superficial, shallow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἀβαθής. By surface analysis, α- (a-, “not, un-”) + βαθύς.</div><b>αβαθμολόγητα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with no grade given</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://αβαθμολόγητος">αβαθμολόγητος</a></div><b>αβαθμολόγητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβαθμολόγητη, neuter αβαθμολόγητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unmarked (of test, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. uncalibrated, not graduated (of instruments)</div><b>αβαθώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://άβαθα">άβαθα</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>older spelling: ἀβαθῶς (abathôs)</div><b>αβαλσάμωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβαλσάμωτη, neuter αβαλσάμωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unembalmed, not embalmed</div><b>αβανγκάρντ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) avant-garde</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French avant-garde.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pioneer, avant-garde</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French avant-garde.</div><b>αβανγκαρντισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αβανγκαρντισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>avant-gardism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αβανγκάρντ + -ισμός.</div><b>αβανγκαρντιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αβανγκαρντιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>avant-gardist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αβανγκάρντ + -ιστής, calque of French avant-gardiste.</div><b>αβανεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to defame, malign, slander, blacken</div><b>αβανιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβανιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. defamation, slander</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) false accusation</div><b>αβανιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to defame, malign, slander, blacken</div><b>αβανιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβανιάρα, neuter αβανιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>defamatory, insulting</div><b>αβαντάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) advantage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French avantage.</div><b>αβαντάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to support, help (especially with advancement)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian avantare, from Latin adventare.</div><b>αβανταδόρικα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in an unfairly advantageous fashion</div><b>αβανταδόρικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβανταδόρικη, neuter αβανταδόρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>taking advantage (especially unfair advantage)</div><b>αβανταδόρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβανταδόρισσες, masculine αβανταδόρος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barker, someone employed to impersonate a customer in casino or market in order to attract business.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accomplice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αβανταδόρος + -ισσα (-issa, “feminine form”)</div><b>αβανταδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αβανταδόροι, feminine αβανταδόρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barker, hustler (someone employed to attract business)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shill, stooge (someone falsely endorsing a product)</div><b>αβαντζάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) to advance (payment), prepay, deposit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) to exceed, have extra, be in excess</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αβαντσάρω, borrowed from Italian avanzare (pronounced with /t͡s/), with consonant change /t͡s/ &gt; /d͡z/.</div><b>αβαντσάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβαντζάρω">αβαντζάρω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian avanzare. Also see the variant pronunciation αβαντζάρω.</div><b>αβαράρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fend off, push away (to prevent vessels touching)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian varare with development of α- (a-) form να (na, “to”).</div><b>αβαρής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβαρής, neuter αβαρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. weightless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lightweight</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) not bothered; brainless</div><b>αβαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. damage, suffer damage (to ship or cargo)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jettisoning (cargo)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (usually) resulting financial loss</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) flexibility, compromise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian avaria.</div><b>αβαρεσιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) willingness (to work), diligence</div><b>αβαρυγκόμιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβαρυγκόμιστη, neuter αβαρυγκόμιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncomplaining, long-suffering</div><b>αβασάνιστα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unthinkingly, thoughtlessly, rashly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αβασάνιστος.</div><b>αβασάνιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβασάνιστη, neuter αβασάνιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rash, unthinking</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unbothered, untortured</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unplanned, badly thought out</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν είναι ποτέ σοφό να έχουμε <b>αβασάνιστες</b> απόψεις. - It&#x27;s never wise to have opinions not well thought-out .</div><b>αβασίλευτα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in an ungoverned fashion</div><b>αβασίλευτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβασίλευτη, neuter αβασίλευτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not governed by a monarch, kingless, queenless</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αβασίλευτη</b> δημοκρατία - republic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not set (of the sun, moon, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εκεί βγαίνει ο ήλιος, ήλιος λαμπρός και <b>αβασίλευτος</b>. - Here comes the sun, a sun bright and unset.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. boundless</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αβασίλευτη</b> δόξα - boundless glory</div><b>αβασκαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βασκαίνω">βασκαίνω</a></div><b>αβασταγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβασταγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βασταγή">βασταγή</a></div><b>αβασταγό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβασταγά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beast of burden, pack animal</div><b>αββάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αββάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>older or etymological spelling of <a href="bword://αβάς">αβάς</a>, abbot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek ἀββᾶς with no simplification of the double consonant.</div><b>αββαείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αββαεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>older or etymological spelling of <a href="bword://αβαείο">αβαείο</a></div><b>αβγά μάτια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://αβγό">αβγό</a> <a href="bword://μάτι">μάτι</a> (fried eggs)</div><b>αβγατίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be increased</div><b>αβγατίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to increase (in size, weight, quantity, number, …)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀβγατίζω &lt; ἐβγατίζω, ultimately from the Hellenistic ἐκβατός from the ancient verb ἐκβαίνω. There is no relation to αβγό.</div><b>αβγαταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to increase (in size, weight, quantity, number, …)</div><b>αβγατισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αβγατισμένη, neuter αβγατισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>increased, multiplied</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of αβγατίζω.</div><b>αβγοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβγοειδής, neuter αβγοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egg-shaped, ovoid, oviform, oval</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυγουλομάτη λέγoμε αυτόν που έχει <b>αυγοειδή</b> μάτια. - Avghulomati we call the person who has egg-shaped eyes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αβγ(ό), “egg”) + -ο- (-o-) + -ειδής, a calque of Ancient Greek ᾠοειδής. Unrelated to the Hellenistic αὐγοειδής.</div><b>αβγοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβγοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eggcup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αβγ(ό), “egg”) + -ο- (-o-) + -θήκη</div><b>αβγοκομμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine αβγοκομμένη, neuter αβγοκομμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of sauces, of soups) with added lemon and egg juice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αβγοκόβομαι, passive voice of αβγοκόβω (“add lemon and egg”). See αβγό + κομμένος”)</div><b>αβγοκόβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to add a mixture of egg and lemon juice) to a soup or sauce</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την έχω <b>αυγοκόψει</b> τη σουπίτσα, ξυνή, ξυνή όπως σάς αρέσει. - I have added egg and lemon sauce to the soup, really sour, the way you like it.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αβγό + κόβω.</div><b>αβγολέμονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) avgolemono, an egg and lemon sauce added in soups or in other sauces</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αβγό + λεμόνι</div><b>αβγοτάραχο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) fish roe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially, uncountable) bottarga</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀβγό + τάριχον.</div><b>αβγουλάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αβγουλάδες, feminine αβγουλού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egg merchant, egg seller</div><b>αβγουλάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβγουλάτη, neuter αβγουλάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egg-shaped, ovoid, oviform, oval</div><b>αβγουλιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβγουλιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eggcup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αβγούλι + -ιέρα</div><b>αβγουλού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβγουλούδες, masculine αβγουλάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egg merchant, egg seller</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αβγό + -ού</div><b>αβγουλωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβγουλωτή, neuter αβγουλωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egg-shaped, ovoid, oviform, oval</div><b>αβγό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβγά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. egg (spherical or ellipsoidal body produced by birds, reptiles, insects and other animals, housing the embryo during its development)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>αβγό</b> της χελώνας είναι μικρό και ολοστρόγγυλο. - A turtle&#x27;s egg is small and totally round.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. egg (of a hen, used as food)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για να φτιάξεις μπισκότα χρειάζεσαι <b>αβγά</b>, αλεύρι και βούτυρο. - To make biscuits, you need eggs, flour and butter.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) egg (anything shaped like an egg)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το Πάσχα στην Ευρώπη τρώνε σοκολατένια <b>αβγά</b>. - At Easter in Europe, chocolate eggs are eaten.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. egg, ovum (female primary cell)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύο άντρες χρειάζονται το <b>αβγό</b> μιας γυναίκας για να έχουν ένα παιδί. - Two men need a woman&#x27;s egg to have a child.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from the plural αβγά, itself extracted from ταβγά, a regular phonological development of Ancient Greek τά ᾠά:
:τά ᾠά /ta.oˈa/ → *τα ωγά /ta.oˈɣa/ → [tawγá] /tau̯ˈɣa/ → ταβγά /tavˈɣa/.
Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *h₂ōwyóm via Proto-Hellenic *ōyyón.</div><b>αβγό μάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβγά μάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, common, in plural) fried egg</div><b>αβγότσουφλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβγότσουφλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eggshell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αβγό + τσόφλι.</div><b>αβδέλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβδέλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βδέλλα">βδέλλα</a></div><b>αβδελλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated, rare) to infested with liver flukes or leeches (livestock)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated, obsolete, rare) to be bled with leeches</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (dated, rare) to fish (join two pieces of timber with metal plates)</div><b>αβδελλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβδελλιάζω">αβδελλιάζω</a></div><b>αβδηρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αβδηρίτες, feminine αβδηρίτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. idiot, fool, scatterbrained person</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vain person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἀβδηρίτης, after the inhabitants of Ἄβδηρα in Thrace who were reported in ancient times to be foolish and vain.</div><b>αβδηρίτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβδηρίτισσες, masculine αβδηρίτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>idiot, fool</div><b>αβεβήλωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβεβήλωτη, neuter αβεβήλωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undesecrated, unprofaned</div><b>αβεβαίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, dated) uncertainly, in an uncertain manner</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ἀβεβαίως</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Katharevousa from the Hellenistic Koine ἀβεβαίως, from adjective ἀβέβαι(ος) + suffix for adverbs -ως. See the Modern Greek adverb αβέβαια from adjective αβέβαιος.</div><b>αβεβαίωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβεβαίωτη, neuter αβεβαίωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unconfirmed, not ascertained</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not determined</div><b>αβεβαιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβεβαιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. uncertainty, doubtfulness, incertitude</div>
  <div class='def'>2. precariousness, uncertainty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀβεβαιότης, equivalent to αβέβαιος + -ότητα.</div><b>αβελτερία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβελτερίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβελτηρία">αβελτηρία</a></div><b>αβελτηρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβελτηρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stupidity, mental dullness</div><b>αβερνίκωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβερνίκωτη, neuter αβερνίκωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unvarnished, unpolished (nails, shoes, etc)</div><b>αβερτοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβερτοσύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sincerity, openness, candor</div><b>αβησσυνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβησσυνιακή, neuter αβησσυνιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) Abyssinian (currently: Ethiopian)</div><b>αβιογένεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) abiogenesis, abiogeny, biopoiesis</div><b>αβιομηχάνητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβιομηχάνητη, neuter αβιομηχάνητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unprocessed (has not be subject to industrial treatment)</div><b>αβιομηχάνιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβιομηχάνιστη, neuter αβιομηχάνιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβιομηχάνητος">αβιομηχάνητος</a></div><b>αβιομηχανοποίητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβιομηχανοποίητη, neuter αβιομηχανοποίητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unprocessed, nonstandard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. raw</div><b>αβιοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβιοτική, neuter αβιοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abiotic, nonliving, inanimate</div><b>αβιταμίνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβιταμινώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vitamin deficiency, avitaminosis</div><b>αβλέμονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) deep blue sea (especially near a steeply shelving coast)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) suitable anchorage</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, uncountable) vast quantity</div><b>αβλέπτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αβλεπτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oversight, lacuna</div>
  <div class='def'>2. misprint, erratum, typo, lacuna</div><b>αβλαβής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβλαβής, neuter αβλαβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. harmless, innocuous, inoffensive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. undamaged, unhurt, unharmed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ἀβλᾰβής, from ἀ- (“prefix for opposite sense”) and βλᾰ́βη.
Stems: αβλαβησ- and αβλαβεσ- from which forms are produced: e.g. αβλαβεσ-ος &gt; αβλαβούς, αβλαβέσ‑τερος, ...</div><b>αβλεπτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (no past) to overlook, ignore</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (no past) to neglect, forget (to do), overlook</div><b>αβλεψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβλεψίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carelessness, negligence, oversight</div><b>αβλόγητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβλόγητη, neuter αβλόγητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unblessed, unconsecrated</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cohabiting, out of wedlock, (not married in church)</div><b>αβοήθητα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>helplessly</div><b>αβοήθητος</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. helpless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unaided, unhelped, unassisted</div><b>αβοκάντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) avocado (tree or plant)</div><b>αβοκέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβοκέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. avocet (a genus of four species)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pied avocet, Recurvirostra avosetta (the species found in Europe)</div><b>αβολίδωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβολίδωτη, neuter αβολίδωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blank (unbulleted ammunition)</div><b>αβολιδοσκόπητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβολιδοσκόπητη, neuter αβολιδοσκόπητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unplumbed, unfathomed, unsounded (of sea of unmeasured depth)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) not sounded out (of someone whose opinion hasn&#x27;t be sought)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “un-”) + βολιδοσκοπώ.</div><b>αβομβάρδιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβομβάρδιστη, neuter αβομβάρδιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not bombarded</div><b>αβοτάνιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβοτάνιστη, neuter αβοτάνιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unweeded, uncultivated</div><b>αβουλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβουλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indecisiveness, indecision, lack of willpower</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abulia</div><b>αβουλησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβουλησίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aboulia, abulia</div><b>αβουτύρωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβουτύρωτη, neuter αβουτύρωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>butterless, unbuttered, without butter</div><b>αβούλητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβούλητη, neuter αβούλητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. involuntary, unintentional</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unwitting</div><b>αβούλιαχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβούλιαχτη, neuter αβούλιαχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unsinkable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. afloat, unsunk</div>
  <div class='def'>3. undented</div><b>αβούλωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβούλωτη, neuter αβούλωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not plugged, unstopped, unplugged</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unblocked, unclogged</div>
  <div class='def'>3. uncorked (bottle)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. uncaulked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “not”) + βουλώνω.</div><b>αβούρτσιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβούρτσιστη, neuter αβούρτσιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unbrushed</div><b>αβρά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>courteously</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://αβρός">αβρός</a></div><b>αβράβευτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβράβευτη, neuter αβράβευτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unrewarded, unhonoured, without a prize (but worthy of distinction)</div><b>αβράδιαστα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endlessly</div><b>αβράδιαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβράδιαστη, neuter αβράδιαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) endless</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αβράδιαστος</b> πόνος - endless pain</div><b>αβράχνιαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβράχνιαστη, neuter αβράχνιαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not husky, not hoarse</div><b>αβροδίαιτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβροδίαιτη, neuter αβροδίαιτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. well mannered, prim, prissy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. living luxuriously, sybaritic</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of a person) spoilt, pampered, delicately nurtured</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἁβροδίαιτος.</div><b>αβροδιαιτώμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lead the easy life</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to luxuriate</div><b>αβροφροσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβροφροσύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>courtesy, politeness</div><b>αβροχιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) aridity, dryness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) drought</div><b>αβρόμιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβρόμιστη, neuter αβρόμιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clean</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unsoiled, untainted, undirtied</div><b>αβρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβρή, neuter αβρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tender, affectionate, delicate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. courteous, polite, refined</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἁβρός.</div><b>αβρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αβρότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. courtesy, politeness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tact, tactfulness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀβρότης, equivalent to αβρός + -ότητα.</div><b>αβρόφρων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβρόφρων, neuter αβρόφρον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>well-mannered</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αβροφροσύνη f.</div><b>αβυσσαλέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβυσσαλέα, neuter αβυσσαλέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abyssal, deep, bottomless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) deep, immeasurable (large)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αβυσσαλέο</b> μίσος - deep hatred</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from άβυσσ(ος), “abyss”) + -αλέος.</div><b>αβόγκητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβόγκητη, neuter αβόγκητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncomplaining</div><b>αβόλετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβόλετη, neuter αβόλετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unaccommodating</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cantankerous</div><b>αβόλευτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβόλευτη, neuter αβόλευτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unemployed (person)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inconvenient, not arrangeable</div><b>αβόσκητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβόσκητη, neuter αβόσκητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ungrazed (pasture)</div><b>αβύζαχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβύζαχτη, neuter αβύζαχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unsuckled</div><b>αβύθιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αβύθιστη, neuter αβύθιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unsinkable</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο «Τιτανικός», όλοι πίστευαν πως ήταν <b>αβύθιστος</b>. - The Titanic, everyone was thought it was unsinkable.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not sunk, unsunk</div><b>αγάλι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slowly, gradually</div><b>αγάλια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγάλι">αγάλι</a></div><b>αγάλλομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be delighted, be filled with joy, be made happy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be proud, take pride</div><b>αγάμητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγάμητη, neuter αγάμητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) unfucked (never having had, or not recently having had sex)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Αγάμητος</b> θα ’ναι, γι’ αυτό είναι πάντα θυμωμένος. - He&#x27;s probably not getting any; that&#x27;s why he&#x27;s always angry.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἀγάμητος, from ἀ- (a-, “a-, un-, not”) + γαμέω.</div><b>αγάντα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. avast, hold fast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cheer up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Imperative form of αγαντάρω</div><b>αγάνωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγάνωτη, neuter αγάνωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not plated, unplated, untinned (of a metal object)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) brazen, shameless</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) worthless, uneducated</div><b>αγάπανθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγάπανθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>agapanthus</div><b>αγάπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγάπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. love (affection, liking, attraction)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. love (the object of affection)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (endearing) love (term of address)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγάπη. Compare Mariupol Greek ага́п.</div><b>αγάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. agha, aga (Ottoman title)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) tyrant, despot</div>
  <div class='ex'>ζω σαν <b>αγάς</b> - live like a lord; live off the fat of the land</div><b>αγέλαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγέλαστη, neuter αγέλαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unsmiling, not cheerful, sullen, sombre</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deadpan</div>
  <div class='def'>3. undeceived, unduped</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “not”) + γελαστός.</div><b>αγέλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. herd (of cows)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flock (of sheep, goats)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pack (of wolves)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pride (of lions)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. mob (of people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγέλη.</div><b>αγέμιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγέμιστη, neuter αγέμιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unstuffed, unfilled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. empty, not loaded</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “not, un-”) + γεμιστός.</div><b>αγένεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγένειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>discourtesy, rudeness, impoliteness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγένεια.</div><b>αγένειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγένειος, neuter αγένειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beardless, unbearded</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) naive, youthful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀγένειος from privative ἀ- (“not”) + γένειον. By surface analysis, α- + γένι (“beard in Modern Greek”).</div><b>αγέννητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγέννητη, neuter αγέννητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unborn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. without beginning</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγέννητος.</div><b>αγένωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγένωτη, neuter αγένωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncommon spelling of <a href="bword://αγίνωτος">αγίνωτος</a></div><b>αγέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγέρηδες or αγέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αέρας">αέρας</a></div><b>αγέραστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγέραστη, neuter αγέραστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ageless, eternal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. looking younger (than something&#x27;s age)</div><b>αγέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) breeze, light wind</div><b>αγέρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγέρινη, neuter αγέρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αέρινος">αέρινος</a></div><b>αγέρωχα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. haughtily, imperiously, arrogantly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cocky, boastful</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://αγέρωχος">αγέρωχος</a></div><b>αγέρωχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγέρωχη, neuter αγέρωχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) arrogant, haughty, imperious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀγέρωχος</div><b>αγία</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://άγιος">άγιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural άγιες, masculine άγιος, neuter άγιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. saint (female)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ζει ζωή <b>αγίας</b>. - She lives a life of a saint.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) with a saint&#x27;s characteristics (patience, love, piousness)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η πεθερά μου είναι μία <b>αγία</b>. Πώς άντεχε το γιο της; - My mother-in-law is a saint. How did she put up with her son?</div><b>αγίασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγιάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. holy water</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a sacred spring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἁγίασμα.</div><b>αγίνωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγίνωτη, neuter αγίνωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. immature, unripe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incomplete, undone, not done, not made</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unprepared, uncooked</div><b>αγαθά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://αγαθό">αγαθό</a>: (many) possessions</div>
  <div class='def'>2. goods, commodities</div>
  <div class='def'>3. wealth, riches</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. favourably, kindly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. naively</div><b>αγαθεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to look gullible, appear naive</div><b>αγαθιάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαθιάρες, masculine αγαθιάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dupe, gull (a gullible or naive person)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://αγαθιάρης">αγαθιάρης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://αγαθιάρης">αγαθιάρης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://αγαθιάρης">αγαθιάρης</a></div><b>αγαθιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαθιάρα, neuter αγαθιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gullible, naive, credulous, innocent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun) dupe, gull (a gullible or naive person)</div><b>αγαθοεγός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαθοεγή, neuter αγαθοεγό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>benevolent, charitable</div><b>αγαθοεργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαθοεργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>charity, benevolence, beneficence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγαθοεργία.</div><b>αγαθοεργός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαθοεργός, neuter αγαθοεργό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>charitable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγαθοεργός, from ἀγαθός + ἔργον + -ός.</div><b>αγαθοπιστία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαθοπιστίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>credulity, gullibility</div><b>αγαθοποιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαθοποιός, neuter αγαθοποιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beneficent, charitable</div><b>αγαθοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) naïvety</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) goodness</div><b>αγαθοφέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to look gullible, appear naive</div><b>αγαθούλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαθούλα, neuter αγαθούλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. naive, simple</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stupid</div><b>αγαθό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγαθά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. commodity, good</div>
  <div class='def'>2. possession</div>
  <div class='def'>3. possessions, wealth</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://αγαθός">αγαθός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://αγαθός">αγαθός</a></div><b>αγαθός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαθή, neuter αγαθό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. decent, kind, good-hearted</div>
  <div class='def'>2. simple, naive or naïve</div>
  <div class='def'>3. benign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγαθός.</div><b>αγαθότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) goodness, kindness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀγαθότης, equivalent to αγαθός + -ότητα.</div><b>αγαλήνευτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαλήνευτη, neuter αγαλήνευτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unruly, turbulent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stormy, rough (sea condition)</div><b>αγαλακτία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαλακτίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγαλαξία">αγαλαξία</a></div><b>αγαλαξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαλαξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>agalactia, agalactorrhea</div><b>αγαλβάνιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαλβάνιστη, neuter αγαλβάνιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ungalvanised (UK), ungalvanized (US)</div><b>αγαλλίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) exultation, joy, jubilation, rejoicing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγαλλίασις.</div><b>αγαλλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to delight, fill with joy, make happy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be joyful, rejoice, be happy</div><b>αγαλλιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγαλλιάζω">αγαλλιάζω</a> and <a href="bword://αγάλλομαι">αγάλλομαι</a></div><b>αγαλμάτινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαλμάτινη, neuter αγαλμάτινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statuelike, statuesque</div><b>αγαλματένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαλματένια, neuter αγαλματένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statuesque, as beautiful as a statue</div><b>αγαλματοποιία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαλματοποιίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sculpture (the art and process of producing statues)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. statuary, statues</div><b>αγαλματοποιείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγαλματοποιεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sculptor&#x27;s studio or workshop</div><b>αγαλματοποιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural αγαλματοποιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sculptor of statues</div><b>αγαλματώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαλματώδης, neuter αγαλματώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statuesque, as beautiful as a statue</div><b>αγαλούχητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαλούχητη, neuter αγαλούχητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. uninitiated</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unnurtured</div><b>αγαμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. celibacy</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>αγαμία</b> του κλήρου - the celibacy of the clergy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bachelorhood, bachelorship</div><b>αγανάκτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) rage, outrage, anger</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) exasperation, indignation, resentment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγανάκτησις.</div><b>αγανάχτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγανάκτηση">αγανάκτηση</a></div><b>αγανακτισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αγανακτισμένη, neuter αγανακτισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>angry, indignant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of αγανακτώ, a verb without passive forms. The expected form of participle would be *αγανακτημένος, but the form -ισμένος prevailed (as though from the -ίζω ending verbs; like ορίζω)</div><b>αγανακτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be angry, be outraged</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to resent, anger</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to tire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀγανακτῶ, uncontracted form of ἀγανακτέω. Also see αγαναχτώ.</div><b>αγαναχτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγανακτώ">αγανακτώ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek ἀγαναχτῶ, from Ancient Greek ἀγανακτῶ with [k] &gt; [x], contracted form of ἀγανακτέω. Compare to the more formal pronunciation αγανακτώ.</div><b>αγανοϋφαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to weave loosely/thinly</div><b>αγαντάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hold onto, clap hands onto</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to shove, heave against, push hard</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to endure, withstand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian agguantare</div><b>αγανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγανή, neuter αγανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loosely woven, loose, sparse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγᾰνός (“mild, gentle”).</div><b>αγαπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to love</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο άντρας <b>αγαπάει</b> τη γυναίκα του. - The man loves his wife.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to like</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Αγαπάει</b> το καλό κρασί. - He likes good wine.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern αγαπώ, αγαπ- + -άω, from Ancient Greek ἀγαπῶ, contracted form of ἀγαπάω.
Compare Mariupol Greek агапу́.</div><b>αγαπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to reconcile, conciliate</div><b>αγαπημένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amicably, peaceably</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://αγαπημένος">αγαπημένος</a></div><b>αγαπημένη</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://αγαπημένος">αγαπημένος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαπημένες, masculine αγαπημένος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweetheart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. girlfriend</div><b>αγαπημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αγαπημένη, neuter αγαπημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dear, loved, beloved</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγαπητέ Γιάννη! Σου γράφω γιατί είμαστε <b>αγαπημένοι</b> φίλοι. - Dear John! I am writing to you, because we are beloved friends.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. favourite (UK), favorite (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος είναι ο <b>αγαπημένος</b> σου συγγραφέας; - Who is your favourite writer?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγαπιέμαι, passive voice of active αγαπάω/αγαπώ (“I love”). Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀγαπημένος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγαπημένοι, feminine αγαπημένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweetheart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boyfriend</div>
  <div class='def'>3. girlfriend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγαπιέμαι, passive voice of active αγαπάω/αγαπώ (“I love”). Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀγαπημένος.</div><b>αγαπημός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγαπημοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. love</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) reconciliation</div><b>αγαπησιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαπησιάρα, neuter αγαπησιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. amorous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flirtatious</div><b>αγαπητικιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγαπητικιές, masculine αγαπητικός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweetheart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lover</div><b>αγαπητικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγαπητικοί, feminine αγαπητικιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweetheart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lover (sexual partner)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγαπητική, neuter αγαπητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loving</div><b>αγαπητός</b>
