  <div class='def'>a female Anglican, woman of the Anglican church</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine of αγγλικανός.</div><b>αγγλικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλικανική, neuter αγγλικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Anglican, of the Anglican church</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Medieval Latin Anglicanus.</div><b>αγγλικανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Anglicanism</div><b>αγγλικανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγγλικανοί, feminine αγγλικανή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a male Anglican, a man of the Anglican church</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Medieval Latin Anglicānus.</div><b>αγγλικό κόρνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγγλικά κόρνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cor anglais, English horn (type of woodwind instrument)</div><b>αγγλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλική, neuter αγγλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>English (related to the country, people or language)</div><b>αγγλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγγλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anglicism, a word or phrase idiomatic of English or England</div><b>αγγλιστί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in English (language)</div><b>αγγλοαμερικανική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural αγγλοαμερικανικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) American English (variety of English spoken in the United States)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine inflection of αγγλοαμερικανικός.</div><b>αγγλοθρεμμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλοθρεμμένη, neuter αγγλοθρεμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>English in manner, of English upbringing.</div><b>αγγλοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) power exerted by England</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Άγγλος + -κρατία.</div><b>αγγλοκρατούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be occupied by the British</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be under British rule</div><b>αγγλομαθής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλομαθής, neuter αγγλομαθές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anglophone, English-speaking, especially a non-native speaker</div><b>αγγλομανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλομανής, neuter αγγλομανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Anglomaniac, English mad</div><b>αγγλομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγγλομανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Anglomania</div><b>αγγλονορμανδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλονορμανδική, neuter αγγλονορμανδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Anglo-Norman</div><b>αγγλοποιημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αγγλοποιημένη, neuter αγγλοποιημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anglicised (UK), anglicized (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγγλοποιούμαι, passive voice of αγγλοποιώ.</div><b>αγγλοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to anglicise (UK), anglicize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to translate into English</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγγλο- (anglo-) + -ποιώ</div><b>αγγλοσαξονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλοσαξονική, neuter αγγλοσαξονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Anglo-Saxon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. related to the people of the British Isles</div><b>αγγλοσαξωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλοσαξωνική, neuter αγγλοσαξωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγγλοσαξονικός">αγγλοσαξονικός</a></div><b>αγγλοφέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sound English, behave like an English person</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to do something in the English manner, mimic the English</div><b>αγγλοφιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγγλοφιλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Anglophilia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Άγγλ(ος), “English”) + -ο- (-o-) + -φιλία.</div><b>αγγλοφοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγγλοφοβίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Anglophobia</div><b>αγγλόφιλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλόφιλη, neuter αγγλόφιλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Anglophile, English loving, pro-English</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγγλό- + -φιλος (-filos, “loving, liking, -phile”).</div><b>αγγλόφωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγγλόφωνη, neuter αγγλόφωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anglophone, English-speaking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French anglophone. By surface analysis, αγγλό- + -φωνος (-fonos).</div><b>αγγουριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγγουριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cucumber plant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αγγούρι + -ιά.</div><b>αγγουροντομάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγγουροντομάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tomato and cucumber salad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγγούρι + ντομάτα</div><b>αγγούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγγούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cucumber</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αγγούρια</b> και ντομάτες σαλάτα - cucumber and tomato salad</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, idiomatic) stinker (a difficult exam, test, job, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory, figuratively) twerp, nerd (someone lacking social and intellectual skills)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀγγούριν, from Koine Greek ἀγγούριον. Either diminutive of Ancient Greek ἄγγουρον from ἄγγουρος from ἄγουρος or alternatively from Arabic عَجُّور) or from Persian &quot;angarah&quot; with [a &gt; u] under the influence of [ŋɡ] and [r] phonetically similar to ἄγγουρος</div><b>αγγούρι της θάλασσας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγγούρια της θάλασσας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sea cucumber (Holothuroidea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, αγγούρι + της (tis, “of the”) + θάλασσας, literally “sea cucumber”.</div><b>αγγούρκα ττορφαντά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) nonsense!, rubbish!</div><b>αγγριφίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to (grasp with a) hook</div><b>αγγόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγγόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εγγόνι">εγγόνι</a> (folk language, demotic)</div><b>αγδίκητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγδίκητη, neuter αγδίκητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγδίκιωτος">αγδίκιωτος</a></div><b>αγδίκιωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγδίκιωτη, neuter αγδίκιωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unrevenged, unavenged</div><b>αγελάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγελάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) a fat woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγελάς, from ἀγέλη.</div><b>αγελαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγελαία, neuter αγελαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gregarious, herd loving (of animals)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) vulgar, commonplace (of the common herd)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἀγελαῖος.</div><b>αγελαδάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγελαδάρηδες, feminine αγελαδάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cowman, cowboy, cowhand</div><b>αγελαδάρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγελαδάρισσες, masculine αγελαδάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cowgirl, cowhand</div><b>αγελαδινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγελαδινή, neuter αγελαδινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of or related to cow or cow&#x27;s milk</div>
  <div class='ex'>αγελαδινό βούτυρο, γιαούρτι και τυρί - cow&#x27;s milk butter, yogourt and cheese</div><b>αγελαδοκόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural αγελαδοκόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cattle breeder</div><b>αγελαδοτρόφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural αγελαδοτρόφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cattle farmer, cattle raiser</div><b>αγεληδόν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in a flock, in a herd, in a mob</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έδωσαν <b>αγεληδόν</b> την ψήφο τους. - Herdlike, they cast their votes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from αγέλη</div><b>αγελοκαλημάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγελοκαλημάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sociable lapwing, sociable plover, Vanellus gregarius</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See αγέλη + καλημάνα</div><b>αγελοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγελοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. An instance or the process of animals gathering; herding, flocking.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of humans) Engagement in mob mentality or groupthink</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, αγέλη + -ο- (-o-) + -ποίηση.</div><b>αγενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγενής, neuter αγενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rude, impolite, uncivil (bad-mannered)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η υπάλληλος ήταν <b>αγενέστατη</b>. - That employee was most rude.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. base (as in base metal), nonprecious (metals such as lead, copper)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγενής.</div><b>αγενώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rudely, discourteously</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγενῶς.</div><b>αγερασιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) the property of remaining young; ever youthfulness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) agelessness</div><b>αγερικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγερικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αερικό">αερικό</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://αγερικός">αγερικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://αγερικός">αγερικός</a></div><b>αγερικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγερική, neuter αγερικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αερικός">αερικός</a></div><b>αγερωχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγερωχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary, uncommon) haughtiness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary, uncommon) haughtiness; cockiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἀγερωχία, from the ancient adjective ἀγέρωχος.</div><b>αγεφύρωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγεφύρωτη, neuter αγεφύρωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unbridgeable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unbridged</div><b>αγεωγράφητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγεωγράφητη, neuter αγεωγράφητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not geographical, ungeographical, ungeographic; something not of geographical significance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not geographical, ungeographical, ungeographic; someone without a knowledge of geography</div><b>αγεωμέτρητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγεωμέτρητη, neuter αγεωμέτρητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ungeometric, ungeometrical</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unable to do geometry</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ignorant of geometry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγεωμέτρητος.</div><b>αγεώργητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγεώργητη, neuter αγεώργητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncultivated, fallow</div><b>αγιάγκαθο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγιάγκαθα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blessed thistle, Cnicus benedictus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγι- (“holy”) + αγκάθ(ι)-ο (“thistle”)</div><b>αγιάζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) frost, bitter cold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) hoarfrost</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) frosty night</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish آیاز (ayaz).</div><b>αγιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be blessed, be sanctified</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be canonised</div><b>αγιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to consecrate, bless, sprinkle with holy water</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be canonised, made a saint</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (familiar, informal, transitive) to bless, sprinkle with holy water</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (familiar, figuratively, humorous, informal, intransitive) to earn one&#x27;s wings (by showing extreme kindness, or from suffering)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>αγιάσω</b> με τόσα βάσανα που περνώ. - I&#x27;ll certainly earn my wings with the troubles I&#x27;ve seen.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (familiar, figuratively, informal, intransitive) to lose weight, become very thin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἁγιάζω from the Ancient Greek ἁγίζω.</div><b>αγιάτρευτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγιάτρευτη, neuter αγιάτρευτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. incurable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. uncured</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hopeless</div><b>αγιασμένο νερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) holy water</div><b>αγιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αγιασμένη, neuter αγιασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. consecrated</div>
  <div class='def'>2. be canonised</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sprinkled with holy water</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγιάζομαι, passive voice of αγιάζω (“sprinkle with holy water; I am canonized -see two pronunciations-”).</div><b>αγιασματάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγιασματάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>holy water stoup</div><b>αγιασματάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγιασματάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγιασματάρι">αγιασματάρι</a></div><b>αγιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blessing (especially one with holy water)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sanctification</div><b>αγιαστήρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγιαστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγιαστούρα">αγιαστούρα</a></div><b>αγιαστούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγιαστούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aspergillum, holy water sprinkler, aspersorium</div><b>αγιατολάχ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ayatollah (religious leader in Twelver Shi&#x27;ism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English ayatollah with simplification of two &#x27;ls&#x27;, from Persian آیتالله.</div><b>αγιοβασιλιάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγιοβασιλιάτικη, neuter αγιοβασιλιάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Connected with St Basil&#x27;s day (1st January)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tawdry, cheap and of low quality</div><b>αγιογδύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγιογδύτες, feminine αγιογδύτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (broadly) thief of sacred objects, church robber; a thief without conscience</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) thief of sacred objects, church robber; crook, swindler, profiteer, usurer</div><b>αγιογδύτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγιογδύτισσες, masculine αγιογδύτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (broadly) thief of sacred objects, church robber; a thief without conscience</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) thief of sacred objects, church robber; crook, swindler, profiteer, usurer</div><b>αγιογράφηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγιογραφήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decoration of a church (the process of embellishment; especially with icons)</div><b>αγιογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγιογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hagiographer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. painter of icons</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγιο- (agio-, “holy”) + γράφω</div><b>αγιογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγιογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. religious painting, icon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hagiography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγιο- (agio-, “holy”) + γράφω</div><b>αγιογραφημένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine αγιογραφημένη, neuter αγιογραφημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of churches) with painted icons</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγιογραφούμαι, passive voice of αγιογραφώ (“paint icons”). See αγιο- (agio-, “holy”), γράφω.</div><b>αγιογραφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to paint icons, make church paintings</div><b>αγιοδημητριάτικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγιοδημητριάτικοα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chrysanthemum (the plant and its flower)</div><b>αγιοκέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγιοκέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beeswax candle used in churches</div>
  <div class='def'>2. taper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγιο- (agio-, “holy”) + κερί</div><b>αγιοκατατάσσομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be: canonised (UK), canonized (US), sanctified, beatified</div><b>αγιοκατατάσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>canonise (UK), canonize (US), sanctify, beatify</div><b>αγιολόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγιολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hagiology (a book about saints; such literature in general)</div><b>αγιοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγιοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>canonisation (UK), canonization (US)</div><b>αγιοποιημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αγιοποιημένη, neuter αγιοποιημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>canonised (UK), canonized (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγιοποιούμαι, passive voice of αγιοποιώ (“sanctify”).</div><b>αγιοποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be canonised, be made a saint</div><b>αγιοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to canonise (UK), canonize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sanctify</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ἁγιοποιῶ. By surface analysis, αγιο- (agio-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>αγιοπούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγιοπούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rosy starling, rose-coloured starling, Pastor roseus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αγιο- (agio-, “holy”) + πουλί.</div><b>αγιορείτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγιορείτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>monk of Mount Athos</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Koine Greek Άγιον Όρος</div><b>αγιορείτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγιορείτικη, neuter αγιορείτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to Mount Athos</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Koine Greek Άγιον Όρος</div><b>αγιοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) holiness, saintliness, sanctity</div><b>αγιουρβέδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural αγιουρβέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) Ayurveda</div><b>αγιούπας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγιούπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) vulture</div><b>αγιωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγιωτική, neuter αγιωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saintly, saintlike, pious, virtuous</div><b>αγιόκλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγιοκλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>honeysuckle, woodbine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, αγιό- + κλήμα, from Byzantine Greek ἁγιόκλημα, a rebracketing of earlier αἰγόκλημα, from αἴξ + κλῆμα.</div><b>αγιόρταστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγιόρταστη, neuter αγιόρταστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncelebrated, not to be celebrated (of feast day, etc)</div><b>αγιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>holiness, saintliness, sanctity</div><b>αγιότητος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formal variant of αγιότητας: genitive singular of <a href="bword://αγιότητα">αγιότητα</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>older spelling: ἁγιότητος (hagiótētos)</div><b>αγιώνυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγιώνυμη, neuter αγιώνυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>named after a saint</div><b>αγκάθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκάθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. large thorn or prickle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. backbone, spine</div><b>αγκάθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκάθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thorn, prickle</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα τριαντάφυλλα έχουν <b>αγκάθια</b>. - Roses have thorns.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) thorn in the side</div>
  <div class='def'>3. thistle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ἀκάνθιον, diminutive of ἄκανθα.</div><b>αγκάθινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκάθινη, neuter αγκάθινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>made of thorns</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>αγκάθινο</b> στεφάνι - the crown of thorns</div><b>αγκάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. embrace, arms, bosom</div>
  <div class='ex'>H μητέρα έσφιξε το παιδί στη ζεστή <b>αγκάλη</b> της. - The mother held the child in her warm embrace.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. affection</div>
  <div class='ex'>μητρική <b>αγκάλη</b> - parental embrace</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bay (sea area enclosed by headlands)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀγκάλη.</div><b>αγκάλιασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκαλιάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hug, embrace, cuddle, squeeze</div><b>αγκίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. splinter, sliver, prick (of wood)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>αγκίδα</b> στο δάχτυλό μου. - I have a splinter in my finger.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thorn</div>
  <div class='def'>3. barb</div><b>αγκίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barb (on arrow)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fluke (on anchor)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * Α diminutive of αγκίδα</div><b>αγκίθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκίθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγκίδα">αγκίδα</a></div><b>αγκίστρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fishhook, fishing hook, hook</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bait</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀγκίστριν, from Koine Greek ἀγκίστριον, from Ancient Greek ἄγκιστρον.</div><b>αγκίστρωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκιστρώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mounting, fixing (of a hook upon a line)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dressing, placement (of bait upon a hook)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bite, hooking (catching of fish upon the hook)</div><b>αγκίστρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκιστρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hooking</div>
  <div class='def'>2. anchoring</div><b>αγκαζάρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκαζαρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commitment, reservation</div><b>αγκαζάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be reserved, hired</div><b>αγκαζάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to reserve, book set aside (theatre seat, restaurant table, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Αγκαζάρισα</b> δύο εισιτήρια του θεάτρου. - I have booked two tickets for the theatre.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to commit, make a commitment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to hire, engage (taxi, workmen)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Αγκαζάρισα</b> ένα ταξί - I hired a taxi.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγκαζ(έ), “reserved”) + -άρω.</div><b>αγκαζέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) reserved, booked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) occupied, engaged</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) arm in arm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French engagé.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>arm in arm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French engagé.</div><b>αγκαζαρισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αγκαζαρισμένη, neuter αγκαζαρισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reserved, hired</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγκαζάρομαι, passive voice of αγκαζάρω (“reserve”).</div><b>αγκαθένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκαθένια, neuter αγκαθένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγκάθινος">αγκάθινος</a></div><b>αγκαθερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγκαθερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>three-spined stickleback (Gasterosteus aculeatus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αγκαθερός.</div><b>αγκαθερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκαθερή, neuter αγκαθερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prickly, thorny</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) prickly, touchy, quick to take offence</div><b>αγκαθιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to prick or sting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be thorny</div><b>αγκαθοκαλημάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκαθοκαλημάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spur-winged lapwing, spur-winged plover, Vanellus spinosus</div><b>αγκαθωτα σύρματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of αγκαθωτό <a href="bword://σύρμα">σύρμα</a></div><b>αγκαθωτό σύρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκαθωτα σύρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barbed wire, razor wire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> αγκαθωτό + σύρμα</div><b>αγκαθωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκαθωτή, neuter αγκαθωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prickly, thorny, sharp edged</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) penetrating, sharp</div>
  <div class='def'>3. barbed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αγκαθωτό</b> σύρμα - barbed wire</div><b>αγκαθότοπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αγκαθότοποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>place with many thorns</div><b>αγκαθώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to prick (with a thorn, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to hurt with words</div><b>αγκαλά</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, literary) although, even though</div><b>αγκαλίτσα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with an embrace</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. embrace, cuddle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cove, small bay</div><b>αγκαλιά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>embraced, in someone&#x27;s arms, in each other&#x27;s arms</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. embrace, hug</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cuddle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. arms, lap</div>
  <div class='def'>4. armful</div><b>αγκαλιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be embraced, be hugged</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cuddle</div><b>αγκαλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to embrace, clasp</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cuddle, hug</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀγκαλιάζω from ἀγκάλ(η) f, “bosom”) + -ιάζω.</div><b>αγκαλιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αγκαλιασμένη, neuter αγκαλιασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>embraced, cuddled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγκαλιάζομαι, passive voice of αγκαλιάζω (“embrace”).</div><b>αγκαλιαστά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in an embrace</div><b>αγκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of αγκιό</div><b>αγκιδωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκιδωτή, neuter αγκιδωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ακιδωτός">ακιδωτός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from αγκίδα</div><b>αγκινάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκινάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. artichoke, globe artichoke</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cardoon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀγκινάρα, variant of Ancient Greek κινάρα.</div><b>αγκινάρα της Ιερουσαλήμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκινάρες της Ιερουσαλήμ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Jerusalem artichoke</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English Jerusalem artichoke.</div><b>αγκιού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of αγκιό</div><b>αγκιστριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκιστριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the casting of a fishing line</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the laying of a line of hooks in the sea</div><b>αγκιστροειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκιστροειδής, neuter αγκιστροειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hook-shaped, hooked</div><b>αγκιστρωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αγκιστρωμένη, neuter αγκιστρωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hooked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αγκιστρώνομαι, passive voice of αγκιστρώνω (“I hook”).</div><b>αγκιστρώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be hooked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be shackled</div><b>αγκιστρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hook, (catch with a fishhook)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to entice, catch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀγκιστρώνω, from Koine Greek ἀγκιστρόω; equivalent to άγκιστρο + -ώνω.</div><b>αγκιών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of αγκιό</div><b>αγκλέορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) a poisonous hellebore found in the Balkans, Helleborus cyclophyllus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) used figuratively, as in</div>
  <div class='ex'>τρώω τον αγκλέορα - to stuff oneself; to make a pig of oneself</div><b>αγκλέουρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγκλέορας">αγκλέορας</a></div><b>αγκλίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκλίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκλίτσα">γκλίτσα</a></div><b>αγκολέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκολέζικη, neuter αγκολέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Angolan (of or pertaining to Angola or its people)</div><b>αγκομάχημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκομαχητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wheezing, shortness of breath, panting, heavy breathing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. death rattle</div><b>αγκομαχάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to pant, splutter, sigh</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to chug along (car, boat, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to suffer, flounder</div><b>αγκομαχητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκομαχητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. panting, heavy breathing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shortness of breath</div>
  <div class='def'>3. death rattle</div><b>αγκομαχώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγκομαχάω">αγκομαχάω</a></div><b>αγκουρέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκουρέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grapnel, small anchor</div><b>αγκουσεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to distress</div><b>αγκούσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) wheezing, choking (difficulty with breathing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) moaning</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, uncountable) sadness, heartache</div><b>αγκράφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκράφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. buckle (of a belt)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clasp, fastener for clothing</div><b>αγκρέμιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκρέμιστη, neuter αγκρέμιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. undemolished, standing, still standing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intact</div><b>αγκριώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) to cling, catch, hitch</div><b>αγκυλωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκυλωμένη, neuter αγκυλωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. suffering from ankylosis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stiff</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ossified</div>
  <div class='def'>4. sclerotic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from αγκυλώνω</div><b>αγκυλωματιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκυλωματιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sting, prickle</div><b>αγκυλωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αγκυλωτή, neuter αγκυλωτó)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crooked, bent, hooked, pointed, prickly</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αγκυλωτός</b> σταυρός - swastika, hooked cross</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἀγκυλωτός, from stem ἀγκυλω- of verb ἀγκυλόω with ending -τός, from ἀγκύλος. Semantic loan from German Hakenkreuz.</div><b>αγκυλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sting, prick, prickle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to hurt (with words)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀγκυλώνω, from Koine Greek ἀγκυλόω; equivalent to αγκύλος + -ώνω.</div><b>αγκυροβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκυροβίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anchoring, mooring (the process)</div><b>αγκυροβολώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to anchor, moor (a ship or boat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to anchor (make something firm and stable)</div><b>αγκυροβόλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκυροβόληματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. anchoring, mooring (the process)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) settling down</div><b>αγκυροβόληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκυροβολήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anchoring, mooring (the process)</div><b>αγκυροβόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκυροβόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. anchorage, mooring (the location)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) refuge</div><b>αγκυροβόλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκυροβόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. anchorage, mooring (the location)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) refuge</div><b>αγκωνάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκωνάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cornerstone, block</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) cornerstone, mainstay</div>
  <div class='ex'>H νοικοκυρά είναι το <b>αγκωνάρι</b> του σπιτιού. - The housewife is the cornerstone of the home.</div><b>αγκωνή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκωνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. corner of a room or building</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crust, heel or corner of a loaf of bread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀγκωνή, formed from Ancient Greek ἀγκών + γωνία.</div><b>αγκωνιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to angulate, make angular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to push into a corner</div><b>αγκύλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αγκύλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. curve, bend, angle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (often) bracket; square bracket ([ ])</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncommon) bracket; brace or curly bracket ({ })</div>
  <div class='def'>4. joint: knee, elbow</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://αγκύλος">αγκύλος</a></div><b>αγκύλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αγκύλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. splinter, point, sliver</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hook, barb</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) calumny, slander</div>
