  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ακολουθούμαι">ακολουθούμαι</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>passive of ακολουθάω (akoloutháo)</div><b>ακολουθούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be followed</div><b>ακολουθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to follow, pursue</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ακολούθησε</b> τον Οδυσσέα στην αυλή. - He/She/It followed Odysseas into the yard.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to follow, come next</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to go with, escort</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to follow, listen to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀκολουθῶ, contracted form of ἀκολουθέω.</div><b>ακολούθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ακολουθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>result, outcome, consequence</div><b>ακολούθως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>afterwards, next</div>
  <div class='ex'>ως <b>ακολούθως</b> - as follows</div><b>ακομμάτιαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακομμάτιαστη, neuter ακομμάτιαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whole: uncut, unsliced, uncarved</div><b>ακομμάτιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακομμάτιστη, neuter ακομμάτιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impartial, nonpartisan</div><b>ακομπανιάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be accompanied</div><b>ακομπανιάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to accompany</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian accompagnare (“to accompany”).</div><b>ακομπανιαμέντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ακομπανιαμέντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accompaniment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian accompagnamento (“accompaniment”).</div><b>ακονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sharpen, hone, whet</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ακόνισα</b> το μαχαίρι - I sharpened the knife</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to stimulate, whet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ακονίζω, from Ancient Greek ἀ̆κονά̆ω, with the verbal suffix replaced by -ίζω.</div><b>ακονητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακονητές)</i></div>
