  <div class='def'>1. not tuned up, untuned</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unwound, not wound up (watch, clockwork)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. not wound up, not angry, not worked up</div><b>ακούρευτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακούρευτη, neuter ακούρευτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unshorn (sheep, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. needing a haircut, unkempt</div><b>ακούρντιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακούρντιστη, neuter ακούρντιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ακούρδιστος">ακούρδιστος</a></div><b>ακούσια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ακούσιος">ακούσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ακούσιος">ακούσιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inadvertently, unintentionally</div>
  <div class='def'>2. involuntarily</div><b>ακούσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακούσια, neuter ακούσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. involuntary, inadvertent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unintentional, unintended</div>
  <div class='def'>3. spontaneous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀκούσιος.</div><b>ακούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to hear</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άκουσα</b> κάποια δυσάρεστα νέα. - I heard some bad news.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to listen, listen to</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Άκουγαν</b> τη διάλεξη. - They were listening to the lecture.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to hear (to have the ability to hear)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι κωφοί δεν <b>ακούνε</b>. - Deaf people cannot hear.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀκούω, from Proto-Hellenic *akouhō, from Proto-Indo-European *h₂ḱh₂owsyéti.</div><b>ακράδαντα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firmly</div><b>ακράδαντος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακράδαντη, neuter ακράδαντο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firm, unshakeable, unshakable</div><b>ακράτεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) intemperance (the lack of self-restraint)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) incontinence (urinary or faecal)</div><b>ακράτητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακράτητη, neuter ακράτητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unrestrained, impetuous</div><b>ακρέμαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακρέμαστη, neuter ακρέμαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unhung, unhooked, not hung</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unhanged, not hanged (not executed)</div><b>ακρίβεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακρίβειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accuracy, exactness, exactitude</div>
  <div class='def'>2. precision, preciseness</div>
  <div class='def'>3. punctuality</div>
  <div class='def'>4. dearness, expensiveness</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (broadly) dearness, expensiveness; an item of good quality; an exclusive item</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀκρῑ́βεια. By surface analysis, ακριβής + -εια (-eia).</div><b>ακρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>locust, grasshopper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀκρίς.</div><b>ακρίτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a more frequent form of <a href="bword://ακρίτης">ακρίτης</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Pontic Greek ακρίτας, from Byzantine Greek ἀκρίτας, a form of ἀκρίτης as in the Trapezund manuscript of the Digenes Acrites epic of 12th century. Also see ακρίτης.</div><b>ακρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (error lua exec) frontiersman; a person who lives in the border regions</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (error lua exec) in Byzantium, a soldier who guarded and defended the borders of the Byzantine Empire</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (error lua exec) a soldier who guarded the borders of Greece</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ἀκρίτης from ἄκρ(α) f, “edge”) / άκρ(η)) + -ίτης. The alternative ακρίτας with ending -ίτας is based on the Pontic dialect.</div><b>ακραίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακραία, neuter ακραίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. extreme, reactionary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. marginal, borderline, at the edge</div><b>ακραιφνής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακραιφνής, neuter ακραιφνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pure, true</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vegan</div><b>ακρατής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακρατής, neuter ακρατές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intemperate (lacking self-restraint)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incontinent (urinary or faecal)</div><b>ακρατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακρατική, neuter ακρατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stateless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, α- (a-, “not, without”, negationary prefix) + κράτος + -ικός.</div><b>ακρεοφαγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) abstention/abstinence from eating meat, meatless (day, diet, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Παρασκευή είναι ημέρα <b>ακρεοφαγίας</b>. - Friday is a meatless day.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From α- (a-, “not”) + κρεοφαγία. First attested 1893.</div><b>ακρεωφαγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ακρεοφαγία">ακρεοφαγία</a></div><b>ακρησάριστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακρησάριστη, neuter ακρησάριστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unsieved, unsifted, ungraded, unbolted</div><b>ακριανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριανή, neuter ακριανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extreme, at the end</div><b>ακριβά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expensively (at high price, cost)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ακριβός">ακριβός</a> (expensive)</div><b>ακριβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to go up, mark up, increase (price)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to put up, raise, increase (price)</div><b>ακριβογιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακριβογιοί, feminine ακριβοθυγατέρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. much loved son, beloved son</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) only son</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακριβο- (akrivo-, “valued”) + γιος (gios, “son”)</div><b>ακριβοδίκαιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριβοδίκαιη, neuter ακριβοδίκαιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scrupulously fair</div>
  <div class='def'>2. impartial, evenhanded</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακριβο- (akrivo-, “meticulousness”) + δίκαιος</div><b>ακριβοεξετάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to scrutinise (UK), scrutinize (US), to examine</div><b>ακριβοθυγατέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακριβοθυγατέρες, masculine ακριβογιός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. much loved daughter, beloved daughter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) only daughter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακριβο- (akrivo-, “valued”) + θυγατέρα</div><b>ακριβοθώρητη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακριβοθώρητες, masculine ακριβοθώρητος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elusive person, person seldom seen</div><b>ακριβοθώρητος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακριβοθώρητοι, feminine ακριβοθώρητη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elusive person, person seldom seen</div><b>ακριβολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) scrupulousness, fastidiousness, particularity</div><b>ακριβολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to speak with precision</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to enunciate clearly</div><b>ακριβολόγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριβολόγος, neuter ακριβολόγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. speaking with and valuing precision</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun) such a person, precisionist</div><b>ακριβομίλητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριβομίλητη, neuter ακριβομίλητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>speaking rarely, of few words, brief, curt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακριβο- (akrivo-, “in high price -here: precious-”) + (μιλάω) μιλη- mili-, “to talk”) + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ακριβοπληρώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be paid handsomely</div><b>ακριβοπληρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to buy at a high price, buy dearly, make a sacrifice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to pay handsomely</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακριβο- (akrivo-, “valued”) + πληρώνω</div><b>ακριβοπουλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sell at a high price</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to sell dearly, make a sacrifice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακριβο- (akrivo-, “valued”) + πουλώ</div><b>ακριβοχέρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριβοχέρα, neuter ακριβοχέρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mean, tight-fisted, stingy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακριβο- (akrivo-, “care”) + χέρι</div><b>ακριβούτσικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριβούτσικη, neuter ακριβούτσικ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>somewhat expensive, quite pricey</div><b>ακριβόλογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριβόλογη, neuter ακριβόλογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ακριβολόγος">ακριβολόγος</a></div><b>ακριβός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριβή, neuter ακριβό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. expensive, dear, precious (costing a lot of money)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) dear, precious (emotionally endearing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Late Koine Greek ἀκριβός, an alternative form of Ancient Greek ἀκριβής. Doublet of ακριβής.</div><b>ακριβός στα πίτουρα και φτηνός στ&#x27; αλεύρι</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) penny wise and pound foolish (prudent with small amounts of money but wasteful with large amounts)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “stingy with the bran and cheap with the flour”. ακριβός is used in its older sense of “careful; stingy”—compare Ancient Greek ἀκριβής.</div><b>ακριβώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. exactly, precisely</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ώρα είναι δέκα <b>ακριβώς</b>. - It is ten o&#x27;clock precisely.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sharp, exactly, precisely (referring to an event)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το λεωφορείο φεύγει στις 9 π.μ. <b>ακριβώς</b>. - The bus leaves at 9 am sharp.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. right, just</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο άνδρας στεκόταν <b>ακριβώς</b> πίσω της. - The man was standing right behind her.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀκριβῶς, from ἀκριβής.</div><b>ακριλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριλική, neuter ακριλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://ακρυλικός">ακρυλικός</a></div><b>ακριμάτιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριμάτιστη, neuter ακριμάτιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sinless, innocent</div><b>ακρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακρινή, neuter ακρινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extreme, at the edge</div><b>ακρισία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lack of judgement</div><b>ακριτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριτική, neuter ακριτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relating to frontier</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ακριτικά</b> νησιά - frontier islands</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relating to Byzantine frontiersmen, especially of the poetry about them</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ακριτική</b> ποίηση - acritic poetry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ἀκριτικός from ἀκρίτ(ης), “frontier soldier”) -from ἄκρα f- + -ικός. Not to be confused with άκριτος and its root from κρίνω.</div><b>ακριτολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to blabber, prattle</div><b>ακριτομύθια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) prattle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) indiscretion</div><b>ακριτόμυθος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακριτόμυθη, neuter ακριτόμυθο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indiscreet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. babbling, prattling</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (as a noun) babbler</div><b>ακροάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to auscultate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to listen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀκροάζομαι. For auscultate, semantic loan from French ausculter.</div><b>ακροάτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακροάτριες, masculine ακροατής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (usually) listener (someone who is listening)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εγώ σε συζητήσεις προτιμώ να είμαι <b>ακροάτρια</b> παρά ομιλήτρια. - In discussions, I prefer to be the listener rather than the speaker.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. listener (someone who listens to the radio)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια εξοργισμένη <b>ακροάτρια</b> κάλεσε την εκπομπή να διαμαρτυρηθεί. - A furious listener called the show to complain.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακροατής + -τρια (-tria).</div><b>ακροαματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακροαματική, neuter ακροαματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relating to a hearing</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ακροαματική</b> διαδικασία - court hearing</div><b>ακροαματικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακροαματικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>listenership, ratings (number who listened to a certain programme)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχασε <b>ακροαματικότητα</b> όταν άλλαξε την ώρα μετάδοσης της εκπομπής τής. - She lost listeners when she changed the time she broadcast her show.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ακροαματικός + -ότητα.</div><b>ακροαστικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Sounds heard using auscultation that are symptomatic of a disease.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγα στο γιατρό και μου βρήκε <b>ακροαστικά</b>. - I went to the doctor and he found my acroastics.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ακροαστικός, from ακρόαση.</div><b>ακροατήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ακροατήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. audience, (a group of listeners or watchers.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. <a href="bword://Α">Α</a> place for audiences.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. attendance, crowd size (at sporting event, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀκροατήριον.</div><b>ακροατής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακροατές, feminine ακροάτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (usually) listener (someone who is listening)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εγώ σε συζητήσεις προτιμώ να είμαι <b>ακροατής</b> παρά ομιλητής. - In discussions, I prefer to be the listener rather than the speaker.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. listener (someone who listens to the radio)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σήμερα προσφέρουμε δώρα στους <b>ακροατές</b> μας. - Today, we are giving our listeners gifts.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀκροῶμαι + -τής.</div><b>ακροβάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακροβάτες, feminine ακροβάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acrobat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tightrope walker, aerialist</div>
  <div class='def'>3. genus Acrobates</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἀκροβάτης.</div><b>ακροβάτησα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, uncommon) An ungrammatical simple past tense form of <a href="bword://ακροβατώ">ακροβατώ</a></div><b>ακροβάτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακροβάτισσες, masculine ακροβάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acrobat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aerialist</div><b>ακροβασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακροβασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acrobatics (the art and the performance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>ακροβασία</b> και η σχοινοβασία είναι τέχνες του τσίρκου. - Acrobatics and tightrope walking are arts of the circus.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. any action or art which involves extreme skill</div>
  <div class='def'>3. any action or art which involves extreme skill; aerial acrobatics, stunt flying</div>
  <div class='ex'>αεροπορικές <b>ακροβασίες</b> - aerial acrobatics</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) acrobatics, risky behaviour, speech or actions based on unsound or dubious arguments</div>
  <div class='ex'>πολιτικές ακροβασίες<b></b> - political acrobatics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Reborrowing from French acrobatie &lt; acrobate, itself from Koine Greek ἀκροβάτης from ἄκρον -βάτης.
For figurative sense, it is a calque of French faire des acrobaties.</div><b>ακροβατικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>acrobatically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>acrobatics</div><b>ακροβατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ακροβατική, neuter ακροβατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>acrobatic</div><b>ακροβατισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ακροβασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acrobatics (performance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) acrobatics, risky behaviour</div><b>ακροβατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to perform acrobatics, tumble</div><b>ακροβολίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to skirmish</div><b>ακροβολισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακροβολισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in plural) skirmish order (troops deployed in an irregular open formation in order to harass an enemy)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) A stage of combat when irregular distant fire is directed at an enemy before close engagement.</div><b>ακροβολιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ακροβολιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skirmisher</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀκροβολιστής, equivalent to ακροβολισμός + -ιστής.</div><b>ακροβολιστί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) in skirmishing fashion</div><b>ακροβούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ακροβούνια)</i></div>
