  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be averse to</div><b>αποστέρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αποστερήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deprivation</div><b>αποστέωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αποστεώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emaciation, ossification</div><b>αποστήθιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) memorisation (UK), memorization (US)</div><b>αποσταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, literary) to tire, get tired</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀποσταίνω, through its passive aorist ἀποστάθηκα from stem αποστ- (apost-) of the ancient verb ἀφίσταμαι and metaplasm to the verb suffix -αίνω in the pattern of verbs like ζεστάθηκα ~ ζεσταίνω. Also see the Hellenistic form ἀφιστάνω.
A rare spelling αποστένω, according to Andriotis, from the passive past ἀποστάθηκα of the Mediaeval form ἀποστέκω.</div><b>αποσταγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine αποσταγμένη, neuter αποσταγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) distilled (obtained by distillation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>also see δυϊλισμένος (dyïlisménos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of αποστάζομαι, passive voice of αποστάζω (“distil”) &amp; see απεσταγμένος.</div><b>αποσταθεροποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αποσταθεροποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>destabilisation (UK), destabilization (US)</div><b>αποσταθεροποιητικός</b>
