  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αρχινοσοκόμοι, feminine αρχινοσοκόμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>head nurse</div><b>αρχινώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αρχινάω">αρχινάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek ἀρχινῶ. See the ancient ἀρχή f.</div><b>αρχιπέλαγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αρχιπελάγη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>archipelago</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A learned αντιδάνειο. Learned borrowing from Italian arcipelago, from Byzantine Greek ἀρχιπέλαγος which is Ancient Greek ἀρχι- (arkhi-, “main”) + πέλαγος, a designation for the Aegean Sea. Attested since 1728 in Katharevousa as ἀρχιπέλαγος. By surface analysis, αρχι- (archi-) + πέλαγος.</div><b>αρχιπλοίαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αρχιπλοίαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a naval rank with the NATO grade OF-6; commodore in the Royal Navy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a naval rank with the NATO grade OF-6; rear admiral (lower half) (abbreviated RDML) in the US Navy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. commodore, a rank in the Hellenic Coast Guard</div><b>αρχιπλοηγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αρχιπλοηγοί)</i></div>
