  <div class='def'>1. unchilled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) not alienated</div><b>αψύχωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αψύχωτη, neuter αψύχωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spineless, without courage, poor-spirited</div><b>αψώνιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αψώνιστη, neuter αψώνιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unpurchased, not bought</div><b>αϊ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aiee (admonitory, urging)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic interjection.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of Αϊ</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Normally written with capital A. Prefix for masculine names of Saints (and their churches) formed from Αγι- &lt; Άγιο- &lt; Άγιος”).</div><b>αϊ-Βασίλης</b>
  <div><i>name</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Santa Claus, Father Christmas, Kris Kringle, Saint Nicholas, Santa</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Άγιος Βασίλειος, referring to Basil of Caesarea), άγιος and Βασίλης, familiar form of Hellenistic name Βασίλειος, from the ancient βασίλειος.</div><b>αϊβασιλιάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγιοβασιλιάτικος">αγιοβασιλιάτικος</a></div><b>αϊδημητριάτικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αϊδημητριάτικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) chrysanthemum (the plant and its flower)</div><b>αϊνστάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αϊνσταΐνιο">αϊνσταΐνιο</a></div><b>αϊνσταΐνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) einsteinium</div><b>αϊτινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αϊτινή, neuter αϊτινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Haitian</div><b>αϊτοφωλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αϊτοφωλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αετοφωλιά">αετοφωλιά</a></div><b>αϊτός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αϊτοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αετός">αετός</a></div><b>αϋπνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αϋπνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insomnia, sleeplessness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἀϋπνία f.</div><b>αόμματος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αόμματη, neuter αόμματο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who has no eyes; blind</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αόμματοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a blind man</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ελεήστε τον <b>αόμματο</b>. - Alms for the blind.</div><b>αόρ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://αόριστος">αόριστος</a>: past tense, aorist</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://αόριστος">αόριστος</a>: indefinite</div><b>αόρατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αόρατη, neuter αόρατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>invisible, unseen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀόρατος.</div><b>αόριστ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://αόριστος">αόριστος</a>: past tense, aorist</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://αόριστος">αόριστος</a>: indefinite</div><b>αόριστο άρθρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αόριστα άρθρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(article, indefinite) indefinite article</div><b>αόριστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αόριστη, neuter αόριστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vague</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indefinite) indefinite</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>αόριστο</b> άρθρο - indefinite article</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (preterite) preterite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀόριστος. The noun is a learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἀόριστος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αόριστοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(aorist, past, perfective) aorist, past tense, simple past, perfective past</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἀόριστος. The noun is a learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἀόριστος.</div><b>αύθις</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>again, anew</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek αὖθις</div><b>αύλακα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a groove or notch to align with a spline</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rifling</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a slit or groove on an organ</div><b>αύλαξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) furrow, groove</div><b>αύξηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αυξήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. increase (growth of a quantity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. raise (increase in pay)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. augment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αὔξησις.</div><b>αύξοντες αριθμοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://αύξων">αύξων</a> <a href="bword://αριθμός">αριθμός</a></div><b>αύξων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αύξουσα, neuter αύξον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rising, growing, increasing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αὔξω.</div><b>αύξων αριθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural αύξοντες αριθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>serial number</div><b>αύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>breeze</div>
  <div class='ex'>θαλάσσια <b>αύρα</b> - sea breeze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek αὔρα</div><b>αύριο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tomorrow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αὔριον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) tomorrow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) the future</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek αὔριον.</div><b>αύτανδρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine αύτανδρη, neuter αύτανδρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all hands</div>
  <div class='ex'>το καΐκι βυθίστηκε <b>αύτανδρο</b> - the caique sank with all hands</div><b>β΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Β΄">Β΄</a></div><b>β΄ συνθ.</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://β΄">β΄</a> <a href="bword://συνθετικό">συνθετικό</a> (“2nd combining form”)</div><b>β΄ συνθετικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://β΄">β΄</a> <a href="bword://συνθετικό">συνθετικό</a></div><b>β΄ συνθετικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural β΄ συνθετικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in compounds, second person) 2nd combining form</div><b>βάβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαβά">βαβά</a>, (granny)</div><b>βάβουλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαβούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hubbub, row , din</div><b>βάβω</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαβά">βαβά</a></div><b>βάδην</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>walking</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) racewalking</div><b>βάζο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βάζα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vase</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian vaso.</div><b>βάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (passive, transitive) to put (put on, put in, put forth), place</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βάζει</b> τις κάλτσες του. - He puts on his socks.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (passive, transitive) to make (force someone to do something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας του τον <b>έβαλε</b> να προσέχει τον αδερφό του. - His father made him look after his brother.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (passive, transitive) to put on, switch on, plug in (a device)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βάλε</b> την τηλεόραση! - Turn on the TV!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (passive, transitive) to start, begin (crying, shouting, laughing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάλι <b>βάζουν</b> τις φωνές οι διπλανοί. - The next door neighbours are starting to shout again.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (passive, transitive) to grade, give a score (in an exam, test etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δάσκαλος του <b>έβαλε</b> μηδέν επειδή αντέγραψε. - The teacher gave him a zero because he copied.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (passive, transitive) to mess with, mess around with, foolishly involve with</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην <b>τα βάλεις με</b> εμάς! - Don&#x27;t mess with us!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A suppletive verb. The imperfective stem is from Ancient Greek βιβάζω. The perfective stem as seen in έβαλα is from Ancient Greek βάλλω.</div><b>βάζω κέρατα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://βάζω">βάζω</a>, κέρατα.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν ντύνεται κανείς σαν διάβολος, <b>βάζει κέρατα</b>. - When one is dressing up as the devil, one puts on horns.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, idiomatic, transitive) to put horns on, horn, cuckold, cuck (make a cuckold of someone by being unfaithful)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παίρνει διαζύγιο επειδή η γυναίκα του τού <b>έβαλε κέρατα</b>. - He is getting a divorce because his wife cuckolded him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare similar horn-related terms in French (cocufier) and Italian (fare le corna).</div><b>βάζω στο μάτι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://βάζω">βάζω</a>, στο (sto), <a href="bword://μάτι">μάτι</a>.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic, transitive) to have one&#x27;s eye on, have designs on (to seek or to pursue something not rightfully one&#x27;s own)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ξάδελφος μου <b>βάζει</b> την περιουσία του παππού μας <b>στο μάτι</b>. - My cousin has his eye on our grandfather&#x27;s fortune.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (idiomatic, transitive) to take aim at, go after (pursue in attempt to do something negative)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η δημοσιογράφοι την έχουν <b>βάλει στο μάτι</b> και παρακολουθούν ο,τι πει και κάνει. - The journalists are going after her and paying attention to anything she says or does.</div><b>βάζω χέρι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://βάζω">βάζω</a>, <a href="bword://χέρι">χέρι</a>.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) to grope (intentionally and inappropriately touch another person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον απέλυσα διότι <b>έβαλε χέρι</b> σε μια συνεργάτιδα. - I fired him because he groped a coworker.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (idiomatic) to chastise, scold</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο προϊστάμενός μας <b>έβαλε χέρι</b> για τα λάθη μας. - Our manager chastised us for our mistakes.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (idiomatic) to take something (not rightfully one&#x27;s own)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έβαλαν χέρι</b> στην πατρική περιουσία.. - They took the family fortune.</div><b>βάθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βάθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bottom, depth (of sea, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η λίμνη έχει μεγάλο <b>βάθος</b>. - The lake is very deep.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. depth (of a cave, well, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σπήλαιο έχει ανυπολόγιστο <b>βάθος</b>. - The cave has an unknown depth.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) profundity, background (of character)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάθος.</div><b>βάκιλλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βάκιλλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bacillus (Bacillus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Bacillus, from Latin bacillus.</div><b>βάκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Cyprus) the sheep&#x27;s tail</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Cyprus) a stick for collecting fruit from trees</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Cyprus) the back of the traditional Greek island costume, that looks like a tail.</div><b>βάλανος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βάλανοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glans penis (conical forming the head of the penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγα στο γιατρό επειδή έκαιγε η <b>βάλανός</b> μου. - I went to the doctor because my glans was burning.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάλανος.</div><b>βάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to attack</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fire, shoot</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to accuse, reprove, criticise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βάλλω.</div><b>βάλσαμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βάλσαμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. balsam, balm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hypericum, St. John&#x27;s wort</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάλσαμον.</div><b>βάλτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βάλτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swamp, quagmire, bog</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Proto-Indo-European *bʰelH- (“white”). Cognate with Proto-Slavic *bolto (“swamp”), Romanian baltă (“mud, swamp”) and Albanian baltë (“mud”). Often considered an Illyrian substratum word.</div><b>βάμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βάμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tincture, solution in ethyl alcohol</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>βάμμα</b> βενζοΐνης - tincture of benzoin (Friar&#x27;s balsam)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάμμα.</div><b>βάπτισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαπτίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baptism, christening (the sacrament)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάπτισμα. By surface analysis, βαπτίζω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>βάρβαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βάρβαροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barbarian</div>
  <div class='ex'>Constantine Cavafy (1863-1933), Περιμένοντας τους Bαρβάρους [Waiting for the Barbarians] and audio at The official website of the Cavafy Archive
Τι περιμένουμε στην αγορά συναθροισμένοι;
Είναι οι βάρβαροι να φθάσουν σήμερα.
Ti periménoume stin agorá synathroisménoi?
Eínai oi várvaroi na fthásoun símera.
What are we waiting for, assembled in the forum? - The barbarians are due here today.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάρβαρος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βάρβαρη, neuter βάρβαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barbarous, barbaric, uncivilised</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>βάρβαρος</b> άνθρωπος! Χτυπάει τη γυναίκα του. - What a barbaric man he is! He hits his wife.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάρβαρος.</div><b>βάρδια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βάρδιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. watch (duty period or the team working it)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shift (work period or the team working it)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan vardia, from Old High German warta, from Proto-Germanic *warþō, an extension of a stem-verb *warōną (“be on guard”), from Proto-Indo-European *wer-.</div><b>βάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) barium</div><b>βάρκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βάρκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small boat or launch, dinghy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βάρκα attested in the 6ᵗʰ century (John the Lydian), a medieval αντιδάνειο n from Late Latin barca, from Vulgar Latin *bārica, from Latin bāris (“Egyptian shallow wide flat-bottomed river boat”), from Ancient Greek βᾶρις, from Demotic Egyptian br, from Egyptian bꜣjr, further origin uncertain.</div><b>βάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βάρη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. weight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. weight, heaviness</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>βάρος</b> ενός αντικειμένου είναι η δύναμη του αντικειμένου που οφείλεται στη βαρύτητα. - The weight of an object is the force of the object due to gravity.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. burden, load, cargo</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) onus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βάρος. Doublet of μπαρ (bar).</div><b>βάσανος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βάσανοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) probe, investigation (careful and attentive examination)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να περάσει <b>βάσανο</b> πριν δημοσιευτεί. - It has to go through an examination before it&#x27;s published.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάσανος.</div><b>βάση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. base, foundation (the lowest part of something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>βάση για μακιγιάζ (makeup foundation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. base</div>
  <div class='def'>3. basis (underlying reason)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. base (the lowest part of something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάσις, from Proto-Indo-European *gʷémtis.</div><b>βάση δεδομένων</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βάσεις δεδομένων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>database</div><b>βάσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βάσιμη, neuter βάσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>justified, legitimate, reliable, sound, valid, well-founded</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ως εκ τούτου, ο ισχυρισμός δεν θεωρείται <b>βάσιμος</b>. - Hence the claim isn&#x27;t considered legitimate.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάσῐμος. Morphologically βάση + -ιμος (-imos, suffix forming adjectives).</div><b>βάτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural βάτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bramble</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) a kind of skate fish, superorder Batoidea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάτος. The fish, from βατίς f</div><b>βάτραχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βάτραχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frog</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάτραχος.</div><b>βάφλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βάφλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>waffle</div><b>βάφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be painted, dyed</div><b>βάφτιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαφτίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. baptism, baptizing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. christening</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek βάπτισις, from Ancient Greek βάπτισις. By surface analysis, βαφτίζω + -ση (-si).</div><b>βάφτισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαφτίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://βάπτισμα">βάπτισμα</a></div><b>βάφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to paint (house, door, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to put on makeup (nail varnish, lipstick, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to dye</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, figuratively) to be in trouble having made a mistake</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την <b>έβαψες</b>! - You&#x27;re for it!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βάφω, from Ancient Greek βάπτω with metaplasm from aorist stem ‑βαψ- according to a common pattern (έβαψα βάφω, έγραψα γράφω). Related to βαφτίζω. Traditionally related to βυθός, βαθύς, although Beekes disagrees).</div><b>βέβαια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>certainly, of course</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βέβαιος + -α (-a).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>really (used ironically)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βέβαιος + -α (-a).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://βέβαιος">βέβαιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βέβαιος">βέβαιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βέβαιος + -α (-a).</div><b>βέβαιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βέβαιη or βεβαία, neuter βέβαιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sure, certain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek βέβαιος.</div><b>βέβηλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βέβηλη, neuter βέβηλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>profane (one who treats sacred things with contempt, disrespect, irreverence, or scorn)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βέβηλος.</div><b>βέλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βέλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. arrow, dart, arrow-shaft</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cursor, pointer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βέλος, from Proto-Indo-European *gʷélHos, from *gʷelH-.</div><b>βέμπερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) weber (SI unit of magnetic flux)</div><b>βέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wedding ring</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, figuratively) wedding</div>
  <div class='def'>3. engagement ring</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (broadly, figuratively) engagement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βέρα, from Venetan vera, from Latin viria.</div><b>βέσπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βέσπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>motor scooter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian Vespa.</div><b>βήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. step, pace (an advance or movement made from one foot to the other)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bema, tribune, platform (a raised platform from which speakers addressed an assembly, a place or opportunity to express one&#x27;s opinion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βῆμα.</div><b>βήρυλλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) beryl</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βήρυλλος.</div><b>βήτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) beta, the second letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) second-rate</div>
  <div class='ex'>βήτα κατηγορίας</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) second, in contradiction with first.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βῆτᾰ.</div><b>βήχας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cough</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βήχας, from the accusative singular τὸν βῆχα of the Ancient Greek βήξ.</div><b>βήχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cough</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βήσσω.</div><b>βία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) violence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) force</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) bustle, rush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βία.</div><b>βίαια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βίαιος">βίαιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. forcibly (in a forcible manner, by force)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. violently</div><b>βίαιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βίαιη, neuter βίαιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>violent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek βίαιος. By surface analysis, βία + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>βίασα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://βιάζω">βιάζω</a>; &quot;I forced; I raped&quot; (see Etymology 1)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://βιάζω">βιάζω</a>; &quot;I rushed&quot; (see Etymology 2)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative form: έβιασα (éviasa)</div><b>βίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. screw (threaded fastener)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) odd or peculiar person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βίδα, from Venetan vida (“vine”), due to the similarly-helical shape of vines to screws. Doublet of ιτιά.</div><b>βίδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βίδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>otter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a Slavic language, from Proto-Slavic *vydra, from Proto-Indo-European *údreh₂. Doublet of Ύδρα and ύδρα.</div><b>βίζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βίζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>visa (permit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French visa.</div><b>βίζιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βίζιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated) visit, house call (a professional or friendly one)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, derogatory) A house call by a prostitute.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) call girl</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian visita.</div><b>βίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) wiki (collaborative website)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of wiki</div><b>βίντεο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>video (TV recording: method, instrument and process)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English video.</div><b>βίος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βίοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. quality of life</div>
  <div class='def'>2. biography, life story</div>
  <div class='def'>3. life</div>
  <div class='def'>4. lifespan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βίος.</div><b>βίπερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>acronym of <a href="bword://βιβλίο">βιβλίο</a> and περιπτέρου, paperback (UK), softback (US) (chiefly crime and romance novels bought in kiosks)</div><b>βίσονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βίσονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bison</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βίσων, a 2nd century borrowing from Proto-Germanic *wisundaz (“bison, wisent”) (see for full etymology).</div><b>βίσωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βίσωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βίσονας">βίσονας</a></div><b>βίτσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βίτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vice (bad habit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το κάπνισμα είναι το μόνο <b>βίτσιο</b> του. - Smoking is his only vice.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) fetish (sexual attraction to or arousal at something considered unnatural)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>βίτσιο</b> της είναι να την δένουνε. - Her fetish is being tied up.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) obsession, fetish (irrational or abnormal fixation or preoccupation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει <b>βίτσιο</b> με την αμερικάνικη μουσική. - He has an obsession for American music.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βίτσιον, from Italian vizio (“vice, bad habit”).</div><b>βαΐζω</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) black woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian vajzë (“girl”).</div><b>βαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to develop, go on (used in set phrases)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κρίση <b>βαίνει</b> προς εκτόνωση. - The crisis is heading toward a détente.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βαίνω.</div><b>βαβά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) grandma, granny</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) nanny, nurse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (childish, indeclinable) booboo (wound, injury)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Proto-Slavic *baba. Compare Old Church Slavonic баба (baba).</div><b>βαβουΐνος</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπαμπουίνος">μπαμπουίνος</a></div><b>βαβουίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαβουίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπαμπουίνος">μπαμπουίνος</a> (baboon)</div><b>βαβούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hubbub, racket</div><b>βαγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bend, to sag</div><b>βαγαμπόντης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαγαμπόντηδες, feminine βαγαμπόντισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπαγαπόντης">μπαγαπόντης</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian vagabondo (“vagabond”)</div><b>βαγγέλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαγγέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apheretic form of <a href="bword://ευαγγέλιο">ευαγγέλιο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βαγγέλιον, βαγγέλιο, a phonetically reshaped apheretic form of εὐαγγέλιον; doublet of ευαγγέλιο via a direct loan from the earlier ancient form.</div><b>βαγοτομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαγοτομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vagotomy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French vagotomie.</div><b>βαγόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαγόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carriage, wagon, goods wagon, freight car on a railway</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian vagone.</div><b>βαδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to walk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek βαδίζω.</div><b>βαδίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαδίστριες, masculine βαδιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female racewalker</div><b>βαδιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαδιστές, feminine βαδίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>racewalker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βαδιστής, equivalent to βαδ- (vad-, “to racewalk”) + -ιστής.</div><b>βαδώννω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) close, bar</div><b>βαθ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://βαθμός">βαθμός</a>: grade, rank, degree</div><b>βαθιά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://βαθύς">βαθύς</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βαθύς">βαθύς</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deeply, profoundly</div><b>βαθμίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαθμίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) stair, step (a single step in a staircase)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grade, level, rung (a position in a hierarchy)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. step (an interval between two contiguous degrees of the scale)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. rank (a level in a scientific taxonomy system)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βᾰθμῐ́δᾰ, accusative singular of βᾰθμῐ́ς, with semantic loan from French grade.</div><b>βαθμιαία</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://βαθμιαίος">βαθμιαίος</a>:; nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://βαθμιαίος">βαθμιαίος</a>:; nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gradually, by degrees</div><b>βαθμιαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαθμιαία, neuter βαθμιαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gradual</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from βαθμ(ός)) + -ιαίος, a calque of French graduel.</div><b>βαθμολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαθμολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. set of grades/marks</div>
  <div class='def'>2. score</div>
  <div class='def'>3. grading, marking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from βαθμολογώ + -ία.</div><b>βαθμολογημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βαθμολογημένη, neuter βαθμολογημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. marked, rated, graded</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα γραπτά είναι <b>βαθμολογημένα</b> και οι μαθητές μπορούν να μάθουν τους βαθμούς τους. - The exam papers are marked and the students may be notified of their grades.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. calibrated, graduated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βαθμολογούμαι, passive voice of βαθμολογώ.</div><b>βαθμολογούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be given marks, be marked</div><b>βαθμολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to mark, grade</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to recalibrate</div><b>βαθμοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural βαθμοφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>officer, a person of rank</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βαθμός + -φόρος</div><b>βαθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grade (of job, exams, position in employment)</div>
  <div class='ex'>(δ) «συγγενής»: συγγενείς του εργαζομένου εξ αίματος ή εξ αγχιστείας μέχρι δευτέρου βαθμού, συμπεριλαμβανομένων των ανάδοχων τέκνων και των νόμιμων κηδεμόνων, ή ο/η σύντροφος με σύμφωνο συμβίωσης, αν το σύμφωνο αυτό προβλέπεται από το εθνικό δίκαιο·
(d) «syngenís»: syngeneís tou ergazoménou ex aímatos í ex anchisteías méchri deftérou vathmoú, symperilamvanoménon ton anádochon téknon kai ton nómimon kidemónon, í o/i sýntrofos me sýmfono symvíosis, an to sýmfono aftó provlépetai apó to ethnikó díkaio;
(d) &quot;relative&quot; means a worker´s relatives up to the second degree of consanguinity or affinity, including foster children and legal guardians, or partner in civil partnership, where such partnerships are envisaged by national law; - Amendement 51, Report of 2018.08.23. at European Parliament Greek-English.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rank</div>
  <div class='def'>3. degree (unit of temperature)</div>
  <div class='ex'>— Πόσους <b><b>βαθμού</b>ς έχει</b> σήμερα; —Έχει 35 <b>βαθμούς</b>. - How many degrees is it today? It is 35 degrees [°C].</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (plural) marks, grades</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη φετινή βαθμολογία οι <b>βαθμοί</b> των μαθητών ήταν πολύ χαμηλοί. - In this year&#x27;s [exam] grading the students&#x27; marks were very low.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. degree (severity of burns)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εγκαύματα δεύτερου <b>βαθμού</b> - second degree burns</div>
  <div class='def'>6. degree (of an adjective)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο θετικός <b>βαθμός</b>, ο συγκριτικός <b>βαθμός</b>, ο υπερθετικός <b>βαθμός</b> - the positive degree, the comparative degree, the superlative degree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek βαθμός, Ancient Greek βαθμός, with stem βα-) + -θμός.</div><b>βαθμός Κέλβιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαθμοί Κέλβιν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kelvin, degree Kelvin, degree absolute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βαθμός m &amp; Κέλβιν, as in the expression βαθμός της f (tis, “of the”) κλίμακας f (by) Kelvin, a learned borrowing from English kelvin.</div><b>βαθμός Κελσίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαθμοί Κελσίου)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. degree Celsius</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (UK, informal) degree centigrade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βαθμός m &amp; Κελσίου the genitive of Κέλσιος</div><b>βαθουλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dent</div><b>βαθούλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαθουλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hollow, concavity</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pothole (in roadway)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βαθουλώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>βαθυκύανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαθυκύανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teal (a bluish-green colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βαθύ + κυανός.</div><b>βαθυκύανος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαθυκύανη, neuter βαθυκύανο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teal coloured (a bluish-green colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βαθύ + κυανός</div><b>βαθυσκάφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαθυσκάφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bathyscaphe, bathyscaph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βαθύς + σκάφος</div><b>βαθύ μπλε</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) deep blue</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) deep blue</div><b>βαθύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαθιά, neuter βαθύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. deep (downward or sometimes inward)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) deep, dark (of shades and colours)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>βαθύ</b> μπλε - deep blue</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) profound, deep</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βαθύς.</div><b>βακαλάος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βακαλάοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπακαλιάρος">μπακαλιάρος</a></div><b>βακτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βακτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bacterium, bacillus</div><b>βακτηρίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βακτηρίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bacterium</div><b>βακτηριολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) bacteriology</div><b>βακτηριολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βακτηριολογική, neuter βακτηριολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bacteriological</div><b>βακτριανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βακτριανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bactrian camel, Asian camel</div><b>βαλές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαλέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. jack</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) valet (personal male attendant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French valet.</div><b>βαλίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) valine (amino acid)</div><b>βαλίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαλίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suitcase</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian valigia.</div><b>βαλανίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαλανίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βελανίδι">βελανίδι</a></div><b>βαλανιδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαλανιδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βελανιδιά">βελανιδιά</a></div><b>βαλβίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαλβίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. valve</div>
  <div class='def'>2. valve (in engine or tyre)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. valve (in heart)</div><b>βαλκανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαλκανική, neuter βαλκανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Balkan, of the Balkans</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بالقان (balkan) + -ικός.</div><b>βαλλίστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαλλίστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crossbow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ballista</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βαλλίστρᾱ</div><b>βαλς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
