  <div class='def'>a short or light waltz (diminutive of <a href="bword://βαλς">βαλς</a> (vals))</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βαλς (“waltz”) + -άκι (“diminutive of”)</div><b>βαλσάμικα ξίδια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of βαλσάμικο <a href="bword://ξίδι">ξίδι</a></div><b>βαλσάμικο ξίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαλσάμικα ξίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>balsamic vinegar</div><b>βαλσάμωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαλσαμώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stuffing (especially of animals)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. embalming</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βαλσαμώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>βαλσαμωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαλσαμωμένη, neuter βαλσαμωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>embalmed</div><b>βαλσαμώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be embalmed</div><b>βαλσαμώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to embalm, mummify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to stuff</div><b>βαμβάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαμβάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cotton (fabric)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cotton (plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cotton wool</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βαμβάκιον, from Ancient Greek βάμβαξ; compare also Latin bombacium, Italian bombagio, bambagia, Middle Persian pmbk&#x27;.</div><b>βαμβακερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαμβακερή, neuter βαμβακερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cotton</div>
  <div class='ex'>κλωστή βαμβακερή (cotton thread)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from βαμβάκι</div><b>βαμβακιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαμβακιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gossypium (Gossypium)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βαμβάκι + -ιά.</div><b>βαμπίρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) vampire (creature who rises from the grave at night and drinks the blood of the living)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French vampire.</div><b>βαμπιρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαμπιρική, neuter βαμπιρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vampiric, relating to vampires.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French vampirique. By surface analysis, βαμπίρ + -ικός.</div><b>βαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) van</div>
