  <div class='def'>baptismal, baptism</div><b>βαπόρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαπόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steamship, steamboat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian vapore + neuter noun suffix -ι (-i), modeled after καράβι.</div><b>βαράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) to bang, ring</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) to thrash, beat</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) to give a headache to</div><b>βαρέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαρέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barrel for storage (of large size)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from Italian barella (“barrel”)</div><b>βαρέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαρέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barrel for storage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from Italian barella (“barrel”)</div><b>βαρέως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in the expression φέρω <a href="bword://βαρέως">βαρέως</a>) heavily (with a great weight)</div>
  <div class='ex'>φέρω <b>βαρέως</b> (κάτι) - (something) weighs heavily on me</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βαρέως. By surface analysis, βαρε-) + -ως (-os).</div><b>βαρήκοος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαρήκοη, neuter βαρήκοο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hard of hearing, with impaired hearing</div><b>βαραίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to increase in weight, weigh down</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to load</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to be a burden on, weigh down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βαραίνω, from Ancient Greek βαρύνω. By surface analysis, βαρ(ύς)) + -αίνω.</div><b>βαρβάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαρβάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shelduck</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βαρβάρα.</div><b>βαρβάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. not castrated (of animals)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. butch</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hefty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βαρβᾶτος, from Latin barbatus. Cf. also Romanian bărbat, Aromanian bãrbat.</div><b>βαρβαρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαρβαρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grammatical mistake, barbarism</div>
  <div class='ex'>for other senses of barbarism see βαρβαρότητα f (varvarótita)</div><b>βαρβιτουρικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βαρβιτουρικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barbiturate (salt or derivative of barbituric acid)</div><b>βαρεία</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
