  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βαρέα, from Ancient Greek βαρεῖα, feminine form of βαρύς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://βαρύς">βαρύς</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βαρύς">βαρύς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. heavily</div>
  <div class='def'>2. seriously (injured, wounded, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>βαριέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be bored, be tired</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Αντώνης <b>βαρέθηκε</b> να περιμένει στην κίνηση. - Antony was bored of waiting in traffic.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βαριοῦμαι from the Ancient Greek βαρέομαι, βαροῦμαι, of active βαρέω, βαρῶ, from βαρύς. Τhe endings according to the sequence: -οῦμαι &gt; -ιοῦμαι &gt; -ιέμαι..</div><b>βαριακούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be hard of hearing</div><b>βαρκάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαρκάρηδες, feminine βαρκάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boatman (especially the owner or skipper)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βάρκα + -άρης.</div><b>βαρκάρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαρκάρισσες, masculine βαρκάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. boatman&#x27;s wife</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boatwoman (especially the owner or skipper)</div><b>βαρυστομαχιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαρυστομαχιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indigestion, dyspepsia</div><b>βαρυτική αλληλεπίδραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gravitational interaction</div><b>βαρυτική δύναμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gravitational force</div><b>βαρόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαρόνες, masculine βαρόνος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baroness (rank in the nobility; wife of a baron)</div><b>βαρόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαρόνοι, feminine βαρόνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baron (rank in the nobility)</div><b>βαρότραυμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural βαροτραύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) barotrauma</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, βάρος + τραύμα.</div><b>βαρύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαριά or βαρεία, neuter βαρύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. heavy, weighty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. harsh, strong</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βαρύς, from Proto-Hellenic *gʷarus, from Proto-Indo-European *gʷréh₂us.</div><b>βαρύτητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαρύτητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gravity</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βαρύτητα</b> ονομάζεται η ιδιότητα των υλικών σωμάτων να έλκουν και να έλκονται αμοιβαία με άλλα υλικά σώματα. - Gravity is defined as the property of material bodies to attract and to be mutually attracted to other material bodies.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. weight, (importance or influence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτές οι πέντε οµάδες θα έχουν την ίδια <b>βαρύτητα</b> για σκοπούς βαθµολογίας. - All five groups will have the same importance for assessment purposes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βᾰρῠ́της, equivalent to βαρύ- + -ύτητα.</div><b>βαρύτονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαρύτονη, neuter βαρύτονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barytone, (of a word) having an unstressed last syllable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. baritone of a singer or musical instrument</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βαρύτονος.</div><b>βαρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαράω">βαράω</a></div><b>βαρώνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαρώνες, masculine βαρώνος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαρόνη">βαρόνη</a></div><b>βαρώνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαρώνοι, feminine βαρώνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαρόνος">βαρόνος</a></div><b>βασίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to base</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>βασισμένα</b> στην εμπειρία - based on experience</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βάση + -ίζω.</div><b>βασίλειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βασίλεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kingdom (nation with a monarch)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. kingdom (taxonomic division below Domain and above Phylum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βασίλειον.</div><b>βασίλεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βασιλέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literally, metaphoric) the setting (of the sun, stars, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βασίλεμα, synchronically analyzed as βασιλεύω to set”) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>βασίλισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασίλισσες, masculine βασιλιάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. queen (king&#x27;s wife)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Μαρία Αντουανέτα ήταν <b>βασίλισσα</b> της Γαλλίας. - Marie Antoinette was the queen of France.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. queen (ruling female monarch)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ελισάβετ Β’ ήταν η <b>βασίλισσα</b> του Ηνωμένου Βασιλείου. - Elizabeth II was the queen of the United Kingdom.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. queen (reproductive female animal in a hive, such as an ant, bee, termite or wasp)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>βασίλισσα</b> των μελισσών - the queen bee</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) queen (powerful female figure)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κόρη του είναι <b>βασίλισσα</b> της ομορφιάς. - His daughter is a queen of beauty.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. queen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασίλισσα.</div><b>βασανίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be tortured (to undergo infliction of severe pain)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο φυλακισμένος <b>βασανίστηκε</b> από τους φρουρούς. - The prisoner was tortured by the guards.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to be tortured, be tormented (to undergo physical or mental anguish)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βασανίζομαι</b> από τις ανησυχίες μου. - I am tormented by my worries.</div><b>βασανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to torture (intentionally inflict severe pain or suffering on)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βασάνισαν</b> τον αιχμάλωτο για να ομολογήσει. - They tortured the prisoner so that he would confess.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to torture, torment, rack, be killing (cause severe suffering to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το αγόρι <b>βασανίζει</b> τους καημένους τους γονείς του. - This boy is torturing his poor parents.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to torment, rack (cause mental suffering to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μήνες τώρα με <b>βασανίζουν</b> οι τύψεις. - For months now, I&#x27;ve been tormented with guilt.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (formal, rare, transitive) to probe, pore over, investigate (examine something carefully and attentively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να <b>βασανίσω</b> τα στοιχεία προτού καταλήξω σε απόφαση. - I have to pore over the facts before I come to a decision.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασανίζω, from βάσανος + -ίζω, literally “to test with touchstone”. The modern sense of &quot;torture&quot; developed from the association of investigations and inquisitions of prisoners with physical tortures.</div><b>βασανίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασανίστριες, masculine βασανιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>torturer, tormentor (someone who tortures)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ίλσα Κοχ ήταν κακόφημη <b>βασανίστρια</b> στα στρατόπεδα συγκεντρώστης. - Ilse Koch was an infamous torturer in the concentration camps.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασανίστρια, equivalent to βασανίζω + -ίστρια.</div><b>βασανιστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βασανιστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. torture (intentional causing of somebody&#x27;s experiencing agony)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μαστίγωση είναι γνωστό <b>βασανιστήριο</b>. - Whipping is a known (method of) torture.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, proscribed) torture, torment, anguish, suffering (extreme mental or emotional pain)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πέρασα πολλά <b>βασανιστήρια</b> στη ζωή μου. - I went through many torments in my life.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασανιστήριο, equivalent to βασανίζω + -τήριο.</div><b>βασανιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βασανιστές, feminine βασανίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>torturer, tormentor (someone who tortures)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>βασανιστές</b> του έβγαλαν τα νύχια. - The torturers pulled his fingernails off.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασανιστής, equivalent to βασανίζω + -ιστής.</div><b>βασανιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βασανιστική, neuter βασανιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brutal, agonising</div><b>βασιβουζούκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βασιβουζούκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bashi-bazouk, irregular soldier of the Ottoman army</div>
  <div class='def'>2. someone who doesn&#x27;t obey the rules, also causes trouble and wants to be independent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish باشی بوزوق.</div><b>βασικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>basically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βασικός">βασικός</a></div><b>βασικά αμινοξέα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of βασικό <a href="bword://αμινοξύ">αμινοξύ</a></div><b>βασικό αμινοξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural βασικά αμινοξέα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) essential amino acid</div><b>βασικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βασική, neuter βασικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>basic, essential, fundamental, primary</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>βασικό</b> αμινοξύ - essential amino acid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek βάση + -ικός.</div><b>βασιλέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βασιλείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βασιλιάς">βασιλιάς</a></div><b>βασιλεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασιλείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>monarchy, kingdom, kingship</div><b>βασιλεύς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βασιλείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://βασιλιάς">βασιλιάς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασιλεύς.</div><b>βασιλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to reign, rule (also figurative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to set, go down, wester (sun, stars, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to set, go down, wester (sun, stars, etc); to decline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βασιλεύω. The metaphorical extension to &#x27;sunset&#x27; is attested since medieval times.</div><b>βασιλιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βασιλιάδες, feminine βασίλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. king</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>Βασιλιάς</b> των Λιονταριών - The Lion King</div>
  <div class='def'>2. king</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) tycoon (king of) + type of business</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Φορντ ήταν <b>βασιλιάς</b> της αυτοκινητοβιομηχανίας. - Ford was the king of automobile industry.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βασιλιάς &lt; βασιλέας with synizesis [ʎa] to avoid hiatus. From Ancient Greek βασιλεύς, from Proto-Hellenic *gʷatiléus; the word, attested in some form since Mycenaean times, is of uncertain ultimate etymology; possibly of Anatolian origin. Also see βασίλισσα.</div><b>βασιλική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασιλικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. basilica</div>
  <div class='def'>2. royalist, a supporter of monarchy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βασιλική στοά.</div><b>βασιλικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βασιλικοί or βασιλικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. basil, Ocimum basilicum (culinary garden herb)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. royalist, a supporter of monarchy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek βασιλικόν φυτόν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βασιλική, neuter βασιλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. royal, kingly, regal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sumptuous, worthy of a king</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασιλεύς.</div><b>βασιλοκτονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασιλοκτονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>regicide (the killing of a king)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βασιλιάς + -κτονία</div><b>βασιλομήτωρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασιλομήτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, formal) queen mother (mother of a ruling king or queen)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>βασιλομήτωρ</b> της Βρετανίας πέθανε το 2002. - Britain&#x27;s Queen Mother died in 2002.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek βασιλεύς + μήτηρ. First attested 1868.</div><b>βασιλοπούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασιλοπούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>princess (daughter of a monarch)</div><b>βασιλόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασιλόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vasilopita (a sweet bread or cake of Greece, traditionally containing a coin and eaten on New Year&#x27;s Day - St Basil&#x27;s day)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Βασίλειος + πίτα</div><b>βασκαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to give or put the evil eye</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασκαίνω, from βάσκανος.</div><b>βασκανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασκανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evil eye</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βασκανία, from Ancient Greek βάσκᾰνος.</div><b>βαστάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to carry, support (bring something as a load)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to bear, withstand (loss, etc)</div><b>βαστάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. I hold, contain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to hold, carry (on yourself: money, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to hold, carry, look after (a secret)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to hold back</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) to hold back (control yourself)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to support, bear (be leaned against)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (figuratively) to look after, care for</div>
  <div class='def'>8. to descend from genealogically</div>
  <div class='def'>9. to last, endure (a period of time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βαστώ + -άω, inherited from Byzantine Greek βαστῶ, from Ancient Greek βαστάζω.</div><b>βασταγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βασταγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bundle, lump, parcel (of something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sheaf, bale (of straw, etc)</div><b>βασταγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βασταγμένη, neuter βασταγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. held</div>
  <div class='def'>2. held back</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) endured</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βαστιέμαι, passive voice of βαστάω, βαστώ (“hold”).
Also, perfect participle of βαστάζομαι, passive voice of βαστάζω (“carry”)</div><b>βασταγό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βασταγά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβασταγό">αβασταγό</a></div><b>βαστηγμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine βαστηγμένη, neuter βαστηγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βασταγμένος">βασταγμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βαστιέμαι, passive voice of βαστάω, βαστώ (“hold”).</div><b>βαστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαστάω">βαστάω</a></div><b>βατ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) watt (derived SI unit of power)</div><b>βατομουριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βατομουριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blackberry plant</div><b>βατραχάνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βατραχάνθρωποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frogman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βάτραχος + ἄνθρωπος.</div><b>βατραχοπέδιλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βατραχοπέδιλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swim fin, flipper</div><b>βατραχοπόδαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βατραχοπόδαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) frog leg</div>
  <div class='def'>2. each one of frog legs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βατραχο- (vatracho-) + -πόδαρο</div><b>βατραχοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βατραχοφάγος, neuter βατραχοφάγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frog eating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βάτραχος + -φάγος</div><b>βατόμουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βατόμουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blackberry, bramble berry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βάτο + μουρο (mouro, “berry”)</div><b>βατός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. able to be gone through: passable, accessible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) easy (requiring little effort or skill)</div><b>βαυαρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαυαρική, neuter βαυαρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bavarian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Βαυαρία + -ικός.</div><b>βαφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dyeing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dye</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βαφή.</div><b>βαφλιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βαφλιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>waffle iron (cooking appliance used to make waffles)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βάφλα + -ιέρα</div><b>βαφτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαπτίζω">βαπτίζω</a></div><b>βαφτισιμιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαφτισιμιές, masculine βαφτισιμιός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goddaughter</div><b>βαφτισιμιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βαφτισιμιοί, feminine βαφτισιμιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>godson, godchild</div><b>βαφτιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βαφτιστική, neuter βαφτιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαπτιστικός">βαπτιστικός</a></div><b>βγάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (passive) to take off, remove</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βγάζει</b> το σακάκι του. - He takes off his jacket.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (passive) to take out, remove</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βγάζω</b> τα ρούχα από την ντουλάπα. - I take the clothes from the wardrobe.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (passive) to eject, kick out</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (passive) to produce</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή μηχανή <b>βγάζει</b> 1.000 κομμάτια τη μέρα. - This machine produces 1,000 pieces a day.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (passive) to earn</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (passive) to devise, contrive</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (passive) to appear, flow</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (passive) to conclude</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐβγάζω &lt; ἐκβάζω, from Ancient Greek ἐκβιβάζω, from ἐκ- (ek-, “ex-, out”) + βιβάζω.</div><b>βγάζω άκρη</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to connect the dots</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to make sense out of something</div><b>βγάζω γλώσσα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://βγάζω">βγάζω</a>, <a href="bword://γλώσσα">γλώσσα</a>.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) to mouth off, shoot one&#x27;s mouth off (to talk impudently or insolently)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν φτάνει που μας έκλεψε, <b>έβγαλε</b> και <b>γλώσσα</b> κι από πάνω! - Not only did he rob us but he shot his mouth off at us as well!</div><b>βγάζω νόημα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (passive) to figure out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (passive) to make sense</div><b>βγάλθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of βγάζομαι</div><b>βγαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to go out, come out, exit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to come up, rise</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (usually) to germinate, shoot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐβγαίνω, with metathesis from Ancient Greek ἐκβαίνω), from ἐκ- (ek-, “out”) + βαίνω.</div><b>βδέλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βδέλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leech</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) sticker</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) leech, parasite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek βδέλλα.</div><b>βδέλυγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βδελύγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abomination</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρόδωσε τους συντρόφους του στον εχθρό κι έγινε <b>βδέλυγμα</b> για όλους. - He betrayed his companions to the enemy and he became an abomination to everyone.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenisitc Koine Greek βδέλυγμα.</div><b>βδελλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αβδελλιάζω">αβδελλιάζω</a></div><b>βεβαίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>certainly, of course</div><b>βεβαίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βεβαιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confirmation, certificate</div><b>βεβαιωτ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://βεβαιωτικός">βεβαιωτικός</a>: affirmative, positive</div><b>βεβαιωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βεβαιωτική, neuter βεβαιωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(affirmative, positive) affirmative, positive</div><b>βεβαιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βεβαιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>certainty, confidence, guarantee</div>
  <div class='ex'>μετά <b>βεβαιότητος</b> - with certainty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βεβαιότης. By surface analysis, βέβαιος + -ότητα.</div><b>βεβαιώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to make sure</div><b>βεβαιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to confirm, assure</div><b>βεβιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βεβιασμένη, neuter βεβιασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hurried, rushed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βεβῐᾰσμένος. Formal perfect participle of βιάζομαι, passive voice of βιάζω². It retains the ancient reduplication βε-β...-.</div><b>βεγγέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βεγγέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a celebratory evening with food, drink and friends</div><b>βεγγαλικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βεγγαλικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sparkler (hand-held firework that emits sparks)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &#x27;Bengalese&#x27;, from Βεγγάλη. Compare to English term Bengal light.</div><b>βεγκέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βεγκέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βεγγέρα">βεγγέρα</a></div><b>βελάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of sheep and goats) to bleat, to baa</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βελάζω, of onomatopoeic origin.</div><b>βελανίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βελανίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>acorn (fruit of the oak tree)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βαλανίδι, from a diminutive of Ancient Greek βάλανος, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *gʷelh₂-.</div><b>βελανιδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βελανιδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oak tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βελανίδι + -ιά.</div><b>βελγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βελγική, neuter βελγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Belgian (relating to Belgium or its people)</div><b>βελονίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βελονίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. belonid, needlefish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) Belonidae</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek βελονίς, from Ancient Greek βελόνη. Has since developed a semantic loan from translingual Belonidae.</div><b>βελονιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βελονιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stitch</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) pinprick</div><b>βελονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) acupuncture</div><b>βελονόουρη περιστερόκοτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βελονόουρες περιστερόκοτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pin-tailed sandgrouse (Pterocles alchata)</div><b>βελονόουρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βελονόουρη, neuter βελονόουρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pin-tailed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βελόνα + ουρά</div><b>βελτίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βελτιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>improvement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek βελτίω(σις)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>βελτιστοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βελτιστοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>optimization</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from βελτιστοποιώ + -ση (-si). Also analyzable as βέλτιστ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποίηση.</div><b>βελτιστοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to optimize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from βέλτιστ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>βελτιωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βελτιωτική, neuter βελτιωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Embettering; which improves or enhances something.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βελτιώνω + -τικός.</div><b>βελτιώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to improve, get better</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κατάστασή του <b>βελτιώνεται</b>. - His condition improved.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to better oneself</div><b>βελτιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to improve, enhance, better</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βελτιόω, deriving from βελτίων, from Proto-Indo-European *bel-.</div><b>βελόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βελόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. needle (for sewing, knitting etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hypodermic needle (for medical injections)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. needle, pointer, hand (on dial etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. stylus, needle (for record player, gramophone, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. pine needle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βελόνη with metaplasm of -η to -α.</div><b>βελόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βελόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βελόνα">βελόνα</a></div><b>βελόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βελόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small needle</div><b>βενεζουελανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βενεζουελανή, neuter βενεζουελανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Venezuelan (relating to Venezuela or its people)</div><b>βενετ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://βενετικός">βενετικός</a>: Venetian</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://βενετικά">βενετικά</a>: Venetian (language)</div><b>βενετικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Venetian (the historical language of the Venice city state)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βενετικός">βενετικός</a></div><b>βενετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βενετική, neuter βενετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Venetian, of or related to Venice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Venetian, of or related to Venetian, its medieval and modern language</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek Βενετικός, from Latin veneticus (“Venetic, Venetian”), q.v. Equivalent to Βενετία + -ικός.</div><b>βενετσιάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βενετσιάνικη, neuter βενετσιάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://βενετικός">βενετικός</a>, Venetian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Βενετσιάνος + -ικός.</div><b>βενζίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βενζίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βενζίνη">βενζίνη</a></div><b>βενζίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βενζίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>petrol (UK), gasoline (US), gas (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French benzine.</div><b>βενζεναμίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βενζεναμίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alternative name for <a href="bword://ανιλίνη">ανιλίνη</a></div><b>βενζινάδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βενζινάδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gas station, filling station, petrol station, garage (where cars, etc are refuelled)</div><b>βενζοΐνη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) benzoin</div>
  <div class='ex'>βάμμα <b>βενζοΐνης</b> - tincture of benzoin (Friar&#x27;s balsam)</div><b>βενζυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βενζυλική, neuter βενζυλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>benzylic</div><b>βενζόλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βενζόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>benzene</div><b>βεντούζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βεντούζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plunger (used by plumbers)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. suction cup, sucker (of a flexible material)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sucker (found on octopuses, leeches, lamphrey, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (dated) cup used in fire cupping.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French ventouse, from Latin ventosa (cucurbita)</div><b>βεράντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βεράντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>veranda (roofed open gallery)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian veranda.</div><b>βερίκοκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βερίκοκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apricot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βερίκοκκον ~ πραικόκιον.</div><b>βερβένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βερβένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vervain (Verbena)</div><b>βερβερίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βερβερίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) squirrel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Proto-Slavic *věverica (“squirrel”).</div><b>βερικοκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βερικοκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apricot tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βερίκοκο + -ιά.</div><b>βερμούτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) vermouth</div><b>βερμπάσκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βερμπάσκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>verbascum, mullein</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Verbascum.</div><b>βερνίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βερνίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. varnish, polish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) varnish (often hiding the unpleasant truth)</div><b>βερνίκωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βερνικώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>varnishing, polishing, shining (the process of varnishing, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βερνικώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>βερνικώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to varnish, polish</div><b>βερύκοκκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βερύκοκκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://βερίκοκο">βερίκοκο</a></div><b>βερύκοκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βερύκοκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βερίκοκο">βερίκοκο</a></div><b>βεσέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) WC, water closet, (UK) toilet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English WC. The letters, pronounced as in French WC.</div><b>βετεράνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βετεράνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>veteran, old hand</div><b>βετούλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βετούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(nonstandard) A birch, a tree of the genus Betula.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Betula, from Latin betula.</div><b>βηματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to pace, to step (to walk back and forth in a small distance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek βηματίζω”). By surface analysis, βήμα, βήματος + -ίζω.</div><b>βηρύλλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural βηρύλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) beryllium</div><b>βιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be forced</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be raped</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to hurry, be in a hurry</div><b>βιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to force, pressure</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην <b>βιάζεις</b> το παιδί, θα φάει όταν είναι έτοιμο. - Don&#x27;t pressure the child, he&#x27;ll eat when he&#x27;s ready.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to rape, violate, ravish (to force sexual intercourse or other sexual activity without consent)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κατηγορείται ότι <b>βίασε</b> δεκαεξάχρονη κοπέλα. - He is accused of raping a sixteen-year-old girl.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to rape (to plunder, to destroy or despoil)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πόλεμος <b>βίασε</b> την όμορφη χώρα μας και την κατάντησε έρημο τοπίο. - The war has ravaged our beautiful country and turned it into a deserted landscape.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to urge on, rush, hurry</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη <b>βιάζεσαι</b>! Θα κάνεις λάθη. - Do not hurry! You will make mistakes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βιάζω. The forced sexual intercourse sense is from Byzantine Greek and a semantic loan from French violer.</div><b>βιαίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. violently</div>
  <div class='def'>2. forcibly (in a forcible manner, by force)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βιαίως. By surface analysis, βίαι(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>βιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βιασμένηη, neuter βιασμένηο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>raped, forced</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βιάζομαι, passive voice of βιάζω (“I rape”) (see Etymology 1).</div><b>βιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rape</div><b>βιαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βιαστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a rapist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βιαστής. By surface analysis, βιάζω + -στής.</div><b>βιαστικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βιαστικός">βιαστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hurriedly, in a hurry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hastily</div><b>βιαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βιαστική, neuter βιαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in a hurry, hurried, pressed for time</div>
  <div class='def'>2. urgent, pressing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rash, reckless, ill-judged</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βιασ- (vias-) + -τικός. Related to Sanskrit ज्या, जय.</div><b>βιασύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βιασύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>haste, hurry, rushing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βιάση + -σύνη.</div><b>βιβλίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βιβλία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>book</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βιβλίον, from βυβλίον, from βύβλος, from the city of Βύβλος, from Phoenician.
Compare Mariupol Greek вивли́о.</div><b>βιβλιάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βιβλιάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. booklet (usually recorded entries)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carnet, passbook</div><b>βιβλιάριο καταθέσεων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bank passbook</div>
