  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of βλέπε: q.v., see</div><b>βλ.λ.</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of βλέπε <a href="bword://λέξη">λέξη</a>: q.v., see word</div><b>βλάβη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βλάβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>damage, detriment, harm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βλάβη.</div><b>βλάκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βλάκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated) person with moderate learning difficulties</div>
  <div class='def'>2. idiot, moron, imbecile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βλάξ from the accusative singular τὸν βλάκα, from Proto-Indo-European *mel- (“soft, weak, tender”). Cognates include Latin mollis (“soft, mild”), Sanskrit मृदु and English mild.</div><b>βλάμης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βλάμηδες, feminine βλάμισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated, dialectal) blood brother</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated, dialectal) friend, companion</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (dated, dialectal) brave, tough man</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (dated, dialectal) lover</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian vëllam, vllam.</div><b>βλάπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to damage, harm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to cause damage (especially to health)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably borrowed (in this form) from Ancient Greek βλάπτω.</div><b>βλάστηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vegetation (plants, taken collectively)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vegetation (the act or process of vegetating, or growing as a plant does; vegetable growth)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. germination (the process of germinating; the beginning of vegetation or growth from a seed or spore)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βλάστησις. By surface analysis, βλάστη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>βλάττη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(name, uncountable) cockroach</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin blatta (“cockroach”).</div><b>βλάφτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of βλάπτομαι</div><b>βλάφτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βλάπτω">βλάπτω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek, from Ancient Greek βλάπτω with [pt] &gt; [ft].</div><b>βλάχο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://βλάχος">βλάχος</a></div><b>βλάχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βλάχοι, feminine βλάχα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://Βλάχος">Βλάχος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. wandering shepherd</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) yokel, bumpkin</div><b>βλέμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βλέμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gaze, glance, stare, look</div><b>βλέννα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βλέννες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mucus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. snot (colloquial)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βλεννός, probably of Pre-Greek origin hinted by the variations between π ~ β, νν ~ ν.</div><b>βλέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be seen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to meet with, meet up with (see one another.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>βλεπόμαστε</b> συχνά πια. - We don&#x27;t see each other much anymore.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) to be tolerable, be presentable, be acceptable, be bearable (in terms of appearance or content; said of people, films, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ταινία <b>βλέπεται</b>. - The film is bearable.</div><b>βλέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to see, watch, look at</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έβλεπα</b> τα βουνά από το αμάξι. - I watched the mountains from the car.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to consider, think of, see (give an assessment or opinion)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν τα <b>βλέπω</b> καλά τα πράγματα. - I don&#x27;t see things looking that good.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to scrutinise, look into (examine in more detail)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτά θα τα <b>δούμε</b> σε μελλοντική συνεδρίαση. - We&#x27;ll look into those at a later meeting.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to examine (of a doctor)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιατρός θα σας <b>δει</b> τώρα. - The doctor will see you now.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of inanimate things) to face, overlook (look out onto, as in a view)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μπαλκόνι <b>έβλεπε</b> στον δρόμο. - The balcony looked out on the street.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βλέπω, from Proto-Hellenic *gʷlépō. The perfective forms (stem δ-/ειδ-) are from the root of Ancient Greek εἶδον.</div><b>βλέπω το φως</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://βλέπω">βλέπω</a>, <a href="bword://το">το</a> (to), <a href="bword://φως">φως</a> (fos).</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν <b>δεις το φως</b> του ήλιου, τότε θα ξέρεις ότι είναι πρωί. - When you see the light of the sun, then you&#x27;ll know it&#x27;s morning.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to see the light of day, see the light (to appear or to be realised)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλα τα σχέδιά μου ποτέ δεν <b>είδαν το φως</b> στο τέλος. - All my plans never saw the light of day in the end.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French voir le jour.</div><b>βλέπω τον Χριστό φαντάρο</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, idiomatic, past, regional) reel, see stars (to be delirious, especially as a result of injury or shock)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν έπεσα με τη μούρη, <b>είδα τον Χριστό φαντάρο</b>! - When I fell on my face, I saw stars!</div><b>βλέφαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βλέφαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eyelid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βλέφαρον, from βλέπω.</div><b>βλήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. projectile, missile, shell (anything fired at a target)</div>
  <div class='ex'>διηπειρωτικό <b>βλήμα</b> - intercontinental missile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) fool, dimwit</div><b>βλίτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amaranth greens (vegetable); blite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βλίτον.</div><b>βλαβερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βλαβερή, neuter βλαβερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harmful, damaging, deleterious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βλαβερός, from βλάβη.</div><b>βλακεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βλακείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated) learning difficulties, mental retardation (of a moderate degree)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) nonsense, stupidity, idiocy, rubbish (something foolish)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν μεγάλη <b>βλακεία</b> αυτό που έκανες. - What you did was very stupid.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βλακεία, from βλάξ.</div><b>βλαμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βλαμμένη, neuter βλαμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, offensive) mentally deranged, psychologically unbalanced</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>βλαμμένος</b>· στο ραντεβού μας, συνέχεια μιλούσε για τη μαμά του. - He is weird; on our date, he kept talking about his mom.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively, offensive) irritating person (also substantivized)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άντε ρε <b>βλαμμένε</b>, φύγε από δω. - Get lost, you stupid, go away (from here).</div>
  <div class='def'>3. meaning of the participle: one who has been damaged</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, dated, derogatory, offensive) stupid, retarded</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βλάπτομαι, passive voice of βλάπτω (“I damage”). Also substantivized. From Ancient Greek βεβλαμμένος, passive participle of βλάπτω, with omission of reduplication.</div><b>βλαξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βλάκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ancient form of <a href="bword://βλάκας">βλάκας</a>, also used ironically</div><b>βλαπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βλαπτική, neuter βλαπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harmful, deleterious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek βλαπτικός. By surface analysis, βλάπτω + -τικός.</div><b>βλαστάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βλασταίνω">βλασταίνω</a></div><b>βλαστήμια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βλαστήμιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oath, swear word</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blasphemy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βλασφημέω.</div><b>βλασταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sprout, bud</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) give birth, produce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βλασταίνω from the Ancient Greek βλαστ(άνω), “bud, sprout, grow”) + -αίνω, a metaplasm of the suffix based on the past stem βλαστησ-. Doublet of βλαστάνω.</div><b>βλαστοκύτταρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stem cell</div><b>βλαστός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βλαστοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shoot, sprout (of plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. scion (plant shoot detached during grafting)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) scion (child or descendant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βλαστός, formed from βλᾰστᾰ́νω.</div><b>βλασφήμια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βλασφήμιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βλαστήμια">βλαστήμια</a></div><b>βλασφημία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blasphemy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the roots *blas-, *mlas- from Proto-Indo-European *mel- (“to deceive”) + φημί.</div><b>βλασφημώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to blaspheme</div><b>βλεφαρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βλεφαρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. eyelash</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cilium</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βλεφαρίς, from βλέφαρον.</div><b>βλογιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βλογιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) smallpox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Clipping of ευλογιά.</div><b>βλόκκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rustic form of <a href="bword://φλόκκος">φλόκκος</a></div><b>βοήθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>help, assistance, aid, succour</div>
  <div class='ex'>δίνω τη <b>βοήθειά</b> μου - I give my help</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βοήθεια.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>help</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βοήθεια.</div><b>βοήθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. help (usually financial), subsidy, handout</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vade mecum, reference book</div><b>βογγάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://βογκάω">βογκάω</a></div><b>βογγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://βογκώ">βογκώ</a></div><b>βογκάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to moan, whine (loudly, with pain)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to whine</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μηχανή του παλιού φορτηγό <b>βογκάει</b> στον ανήφορο. - The truck&#x27;s engine whined up the hill.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βογκ(ώ)) + -άω. Simplification of spelling of the mediaeval Byzantine Greek γογγῶ falsely associated to βοΐζω. From Hellenistic Koine Greek γογγύζω.</div><b>βογκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βογκάω">βογκάω</a></div><b>βοδινό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://βοδινός">βοδινός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://βοδινός">βοδινός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βοδινά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beef (meat of cattle)</div><b>βοδινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βοδινή, neuter βοδινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bovine, beef</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to or derived from βόδι.</div><b>βοηθάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to help, aid</div>
  <div class='ex'>βοηθάω - I help</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to contribute to/towards</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern βοηθώ + -άω, from Ancient Greek βοηθῶ, contracted form of βοηθέω, from βοηθός.</div><b>βοηθημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βοηθημένη, neuter βοηθημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>assisted, helped</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βοηθιέμαι or of βοηθούμαι, passive voices of βοηθάω and βοηθώ.</div><b>βοηθητικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>helpfully, favourably (UK), favorably (US)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βοηθητικός">βοηθητικός</a></div><b>βοηθητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βοηθητική, neuter βοηθητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ancillary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. helpful, assistant, assistive</div>
  <div class='def'>3. auxiliary</div>
  <div class='def'>4. collateral</div><b>βοηθούμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βοηθούμενη, neuter βοηθούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who is being assisted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of βοηθούμαι, passive voice of βοηθώ (βοηθάω) (“hlp”).</div><b>βοηθός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural βοηθοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>helper, assistant</div><b>βοηθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>variant of <a href="bword://βοηθάω">βοηθάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βοηθῶ, contracted form of βοηθέω, from βοηθός. Also see βοηθάω.</div><b>βοημικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βοημική, neuter βοημικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bohemian (relating to Bohemia and its people)</div><b>βοιωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Boeotian</div><b>βολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shot (the result of launching a projectile or bullet)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fire (the bullets or other projectiles fired from a gun or other ranged weapon)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. throw</div>
  <div class='def'>4. range (maximum distance or reach of capability of a projectile)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) salvo, volley (of invective, accusations, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (familiar) convenience, amenity (a thing or circumstance that is welcome and makes life a little easier or more pleasant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βολή.</div><b>βολίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βολίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bolide</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meteor</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fireball</div>
  <div class='def'>4. lead, plumb (for measuring depth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βολίς.</div><b>βολβός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βολβοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bulb (daffodil, crocus, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bulb (swollen root of these plants)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βολβός.</div><b>βολεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to arrange, fit into</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to arrange (affairs, responsibilities)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to serve well, be convenient (something for someone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an unattested *εὐβολεύω (*evvolev́o), from Hellenistic Koine Greek εὐβολῶ, εὐβολέω from εὖ + -βολος (-bolos), the latter component from βολή. The original sense was &quot;to accurately throw dice&quot;, which shifted to &quot;be convenient, arrange&quot;.</div><b>βολιβιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βολιβιανή, neuter βολιβιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bolivian (relating to Bolivia or its people)</div><b>βολιδοσκοπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sound out, enquire, investigate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sound, plumb, measure the depth of</div><b>βολικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comfortably, conveniently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from βολικός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βολικός">βολικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from βολικός</div><b>βολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βολική, neuter βολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. handy, convenient</div>
  <div class='def'>2. comfortable</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός ο καναπές είναι πολύ <b>βολικός</b>. - This sofa is very comfortable.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βολή + -ικός.</div><b>βολτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) volt</div><b>βολτίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βολτίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://βόλτα">βόλτα</a> f</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βόλτ(α)) + -ίτσα.</div><b>βολφράμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tungsten, wolfram</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from German Wolfram.</div><b>βομβάρδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βομπάρδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπομπάρδα">μπομπάρδα</a></div><b>βομβίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βομβίστριες, masculine βομβιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bomber, bomb maker</div><b>βομβαρδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bomb, bombard</div><b>βομβαρδισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βομβαρδισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bombardment, bombing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from βομβαρδίζω + -μός with calque of French bombardement.</div><b>βομβαρδιστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βομβαρδιστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bomber (military aircraft designed to drop bombs)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://βομβαρδιστικός">βομβαρδιστικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://βομβαρδιστικός">βομβαρδιστικός</a></div><b>βομβαρδιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βομβαρδιστική, neuter βομβαρδιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to bombs, bombers and bombing</div><b>βομβιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βομβιστές, feminine βομβίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bomber (person who uses or makes bombs)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βόμβα + -ιστής. First attested 1839.</div><b>βοοειδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cattle (horned, ruminant animals collectively)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. The Bovidae.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized noun from the adjective βοοειδής as a collective noun in neuter plural, from Koine Greek βοοειδής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of βοοειδής</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of βοοειδής</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of βοοειδής</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized noun from the adjective βοοειδής as a collective noun in neuter plural, from Koine Greek βοοειδής.</div><b>βορβόλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βορβόλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρικόλακας">βρικόλακας</a></div><b>βορείως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>north</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from βόρει(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>βορειοαμερικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βορειοαμερικανική, neuter βορειοαμερικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>North American</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βόρειος + αμερικανικός</div><b>βορειοανατολικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northeast</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βορειοανατολικός">βορειοανατολικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northeast</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βορειοανατολικά</b> της Αθήνας θα βρείτε την Πεντέλη. - Northeast of Athens you will find Penteli.</div><b>βορειοανατολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βορειοανατολική, neuter βορειοανατολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northeast, northeasterly</div><b>βορειοανατολικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northeast</div>
  <div class='ex'>στη θαλάσσια περιοχή <b>βορειοανατολικώς</b> της Λήμνου - in the sea area northeast of Limnos</div><b>βορειοδυτικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northwest</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βορειοδυτικός">βορειοδυτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northwest</div><b>βορειοδυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βορειοδυτική, neuter βορειοδυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northwest, northwesterly, northwestwards, northwestern</div><b>βορειοδυτικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northwest</div><b>βοριάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βοριάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. north wind</div>
  <div class='def'>2. north</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cold weather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek Βορέας.</div><b>βορράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) north</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek Βορρᾶς.</div><b>βοσκάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to graze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern βοσκ(ώ) + -άω. A metaplasm of βόσκω in the pattern of the 2nd Conjugation Class I verbs.</div><b>βοσκή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βοσκές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. natural pasture and grassland</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vegetation growing there</div><b>βοσκοπούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βοσκοπούλες, neuter βοσκόπουλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>young shepherd girl</div><b>βοσκοτόπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βοσκοτόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) pasture, pasturage, pastureland, meadow</div><b>βοσκόπουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βοσκόπουλα, feminine βοσκοπούλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>young shepherd boy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βοσκός + -πουλο (-poulo)</div><b>βοσκός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βοσκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shepherd</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βοσκός.</div><b>βοσκότοπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βοσκότοποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pasture, pastureland, meadow</div><b>βοσκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βοσκάω">βοσκάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βοσκῶ, from Ancient Greek βόσκω. Also see βοσκάω.</div><b>βοσνιακά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bosnian (one of the languages of Bosnia and its people)</div><b>βοσνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βοσνιακή, neuter βοσνιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bosnian (relating to Bosnia or its people)</div><b>βοτ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://βοτανική">βοτανική</a>: botany</div><b>βοτάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βοτάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βότανο">βότανο</a></div><b>βοταν.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://βοτανική">βοτανική</a>: botany</div><b>βοτανική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βοτανικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>botany</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French botanique, from Ancient Greek βοτανικός.</div><b>βοτανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βοτανική, neuter βοτανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>botanical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βοτανικός, from βοτάνη, from βόσκω.</div><b>βοτανολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural βοτανολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>botanist</div><b>βουβάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βουβάλες, masculine βούβαλος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female buffalo, cow buffalo</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) fat clumsy woman</div><b>βουβάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βουβάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) oaf, clumsy person</div>
  <div class='def'>2. buffalo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βούβαλος. Cf. also Latin bubalus.</div><b>βουβουζέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βουβουζέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vuvuzela</div><b>βουβώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βουβώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>groin (depression between thigh and abdomen)</div><b>βουκαμβίλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βουκαμβίλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπουκαμβίλια">μπουκαμβίλια</a></div><b>βουκράνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βουκράνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bucranium</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βουκράνιον.</div><b>βουλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βουλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. parliament, legislative body, house of elected representatives</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Congress</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) will, ruling, decision, verdict, judgment, counsel, decree</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>βουλές</b> του Θεού - the will of God</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βουλή.</div><b>βουλβοδυνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural βουλβοδυνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) vulvodynia</div><b>βουλγαρικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bulgarian (the language of Bulgaria and its people)</div><b>βουλγαρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βουλγαρική, neuter βουλγαρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bulgarian (relating to Bulgaria or its people or language)</div><b>βουλευτάδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(for the masuline only) vernacular, demotic form of βουλευτές, nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://βουλευτής">βουλευτής</a></div><b>βουλευτάδων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(for the masuline only) vernacular, demotic form of βουλευτών, genitive plural of <a href="bword://βουλευτής">βουλευτής</a></div><b>βουλευτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βουλευτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) bouleuterion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βουλευτήριον.</div><b>βουλευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural βουλευτές or βουλευτάδες, feminine βουλευτίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. member of Parliament</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deputy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. congressman, assemblyman</div>
  <div class='def'>4. congresswoman, assemblywoman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βουλευτής.</div><b>βουλευτίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βουλευτίνες, masculine βουλευτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. member of Parliament</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deputy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. congresswoman, assemblywoman</div><b>βουλευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βουλευτική, neuter βουλευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parliamentary</div><b>βουλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sink, bring down</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dent (e.g. of cars)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βουλίζω &amp; βουλιάζω form the Koine Greek βολίζω (“heave the lead”), βολίζομαι from βολίς.</div><b>βουλκανίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βουλκανίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vulcanite, vulcanised rubber</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ebonite</div><b>βουλκανιζατέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tyre/tire repair shop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French vulcanisateur.</div><b>βουλκανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βουλκανισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vulcanisation, vulcanization</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) retread tyres</div><b>βουλοκέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βουλοκέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sealing wax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βουλλοκέρῐον.</div><b>βουλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to caulk</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to seal, plug, cap, block, clog, close, obstruct</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βούλωσε</b> ο νεροχύτης. - The sink is clogged.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. an increased ability to perceive that which is difficult to understand, confusing or indistinct</div>
  <div class='ex'>Διαβάζω <b>βουλωμένο</b> γράμμα. - I can read between the lines.</div><b>βουνίσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βουνίσια, neuter βουνίσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mountainous</div><b>βουνοκορφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βουνοκορφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>summit, mountaintop, peak</div><b>βουνοσειρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βουνοσειρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mountain range, mountain chain, sierra, ridge</div><b>βουνό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βουνά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mountain</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το όρος Έβερεστ είναι το ψηλότερο <b>βουνό</b> της οροσειράς των Ιμαλαΐων. - Mount Everest is the highest mountain in the Himalayan massif.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mountainous countryside</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) heavy workload, mountain of work</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek βουνό, βουνόν, a metaplasm to neuter of the Ancient Greek βουνός of uncertain etymology, probably in accordance to the masculine ὄρος. A connection to βουβών is very unlikely.
Compare Mariupol Greek вуно́.</div><b>βουπρήστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βουπρήστεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Any jewel beetle of the genus Buprestis.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Buprestis, from Latin būprēstis, from Ancient Greek βούπρηστῐς.</div><b>βουπρηστίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Buprestidae, the buprestids.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Buprestidae, as βουπρήστης + -ίδες.</div><b>βουρβούλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βουρβούλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρικόλακας">βρικόλακας</a></div><b>βουρβόλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βουρβόλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρικόλακας">βρικόλακας</a></div><b>βουρκόλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βουρκόλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρικόλακας">βρικόλακας</a></div><b>βουρκώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to start crying</div><b>βουρτσίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to brush (to clean with a brush)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βουρτσίζω</b> τα δόντια μου. - I brush my teeth.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βουρτσίζω. By surface analysis, βούρτσα + -ίζω.</div><b>βουτάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to sink, dive, immerse, plunge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to grab, collar</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to dunk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βουτώ + -άω, from Byzantine Greek βουτῶ, from a metaplasm of -ώ with the past stem βουτισ- (boutis-) of βουτίζω, from Ancient Greek βυθίζω.</div><b>βουτιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βουτιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dip, swim</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dive</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) dive, dip (large, sudden decrease to prices, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βουτάω + -ιά.</div><b>βουτυροκομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) butter production</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βούτυρο + -κομία. First attested 1891.</div><b>βουτυρωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βουτυρωμένη, neuter βουτυρωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>buttered</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>βουτυρωμένο</b> ψωμί - the buttered bread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βουτυρώνομαι, passive voice of βουτυρώνω.</div><b>βουτυρόγαλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural βουτυρογάλατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) buttermilk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βούτυρο + γάλα</div><b>βουτυρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to butter (treated or coated with butter)</div><b>βουτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βουτάω">βουτάω</a></div><b>βοϊδινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βοϊδινή, neuter βοϊδινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βοδινός">βοδινός</a></div><b>βούβαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βούβαλοι, feminine βουβάλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>buffalo, male or bull buffalo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βούβαλος.</div><b>βούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seal (stamp and its impression used to seal envelopes and authenticate documents)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dimple (in chin, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bull</div>
  <div class='def'>4. spot, dot</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγόρασα ένα κόκκινο φόρεμα με μωβ <b>βούλες</b>. - I bought a red dress with purple spots.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βούλλα, from Latin bulla.</div><b>βούληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βουλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>will, volition</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: θέληση f (thélisi)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βούλησις, with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>βούρλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βούρλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rush (aquatic plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) stupid, fool</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βροῦλον.</div><b>βούρτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βούρτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. broom, brush (for cleaning and sweeping)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. paintbrush</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hairbrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βούρτσα, also spelled βρούτσα, βούρτζα, βρούτζα, via Vulgar Latin from Proto-Germanic *burstiz.</div><b>βούτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βουτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bakery product (biscuit, pretzel, etc) usually eaten with coffee, tea, etc often by dipping or dunking</div><b>βούτυρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βούτυρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>butter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βούτῡρον, compound of βοῦς and τῡρός.</div><b>βράδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βράδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://βράδυ">βράδυ</a></div><b>βράδυ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>at night, by night, nightly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι βγήκε <b>βράδυ</b> μέσα στο σκοτάδι να κάνει βόλτα; - What was he doing going out for a walk at night in the dark?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βραδύ, from Ancient Greek βραδύς, due to it being the last part of the day and for it seeming to be the longest wait for it to arrive (compare the similar semantic development of Spanish tarde).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βράδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. evening (period between sunset and full dark)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>βράδυ</b> πάμε πάντα περίπατο στη θάλασσα. - We always go for a stroll by the sea in the evening.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) night, evening (period between sunset and sunrise)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός είναι παράξενος· τον βρίσκεις να τριγυρνάει πάντα το <b>βράδυ</b>. - He&#x27;s odd; you always find him wandering around at night.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βραδύ, from Ancient Greek βραδύς, due to it being the last part of the day and for it seeming to be the longest wait for it to arrive (compare the similar semantic development of Spanish tarde).</div><b>βράζει το αίμα μου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to be very lively, active, energetic</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν ήμουν νέος, <b>το αίμα μου έβραζε</b>. - When I was young, I used to be very active.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Presumably a calque of Ottoman Turkish قانی قاینامق (see there for further etymology), as this is a common idiom in Turkic languages which is not attested in Greek prior to the Ottoman period.</div><b>βράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be boiled</div><b>βράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to boil</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βράζω</b> το νερό για τα μακαρόνια. - I boil the water for the spaghetti.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to boil, to be very hot</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βράζω</b> στον πυρετό. - I am burning with fever.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, idiomatic, intransitive) to boil, to be very hot; to be furious, be worked up, seethe</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βράζω</b> απ’ το κακό μου. - I am absolutely furious.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek βράζω from Ancient Greek βράσσω.</div><b>βράκτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βράκτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bract</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Internationalism from Latin bractea, though reinterpreted as a neuter noun (e.g. *bractium).</div><b>βράχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βράχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rock, cliff</div>
  <div class='def'>2. large stone</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) rock (stable person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βράχος m, from Hellenistic Koine Greek βράχος n with the change from neuter to a masculine as an augmentative element, from the plural in Ancient Greek βράχεα), from βραχέα, neuter, plural of adjective βραχύς as in the phrase &quot;βραχέα sc. ὕδατα &quot;shallow waters&quot;, already in Herodotus.</div><b>βρέφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρέφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baby, infant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βρέφος, from Proto-Indo-European *gʷrebʰ-.</div><b>βρέχει καρεκλοπόδαρα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rain cats and dogs (to rain very heavily)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally: &quot;to rain chairlegs&quot;</div><b>βρέχει με το τουλούμι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>it&#x27;s raining cats and dogs (to rain very heavily)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally: &quot;to rain with the goatskin&quot;</div><b>βρέχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to get wet</div><b>βρέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to wet, dampen</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>βρέξω</b> τα ρούχα πριν τα σιδερώσω. - I&#x27;ll wet the clothes before I iron them.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, transitive) to wet, dampen; to offer treat (usually a drink) for good luck for newly acquired goods</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγόρασα παπούτσια. Πάμε να τα <b>βρέξουμε</b>. - I bought new shoes. Let&#x27;s go wet them [drink for their good luck].</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to send rain</div>
  <div class='ex'>For quotations using this term, see Citations:βρέχω.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to rain (3rd person singulars are impersonal) βρέχει</div>
  <div class='ex'>καὶ πάντα κεῖ ποὺ πολεμᾷ, στράφτει, βροντᾶ καὶ βρέχει. - and always in the place he fights, it flashes, thunders and it rains.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βρέχω. The impersonal 3rd singulars, by ellipsis of noun God, or Zeus, or the cloud, etc. Unrelated to βρέγμα bregma”), assumed as related by Hippocrates and Aristotle.</div><b>βρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to insult, call names, abuse, run down (to use offensive language against)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του έδωσα μπουνιά επειδή με <b>έβριζε</b>. - I punched him because he was calling me names.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to swear, curse, cuss (use offensive language)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας μου <b>βρίζει</b> συνεχώς όταν έχει ματς στην τηλεόραση. - My father is always swearing when there&#x27;s a match on the telly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βρίζω, from Ancient Greek ὑβρίζω, from ὕβρις.</div><b>βρίκιον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brig, brigantine (two-masted sailing ship)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> English brig</div><b>βρίσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρισίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρισιά">βρισιά</a></div><b>βρίσκομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be located, to find oneself somewhere</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πλοίο <b>βρίσκεται</b> στο λιμάνι. - The ferry is located in the harbour.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be found, to be discovered</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το χαμένο παιδί <b>βρέθηκε</b> ζωντανό. - The lost child was found alive.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to meet, get together</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πότε θα <b>βρεθούμε</b>; - When shall we meet?</div><b>βρίσκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to find, discover, locate</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βρήκαμε</b> το ξενοδοχείο μας. - We found our hotel.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. see: <a href="bword://βρίσκομαι">βρίσκομαι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βρίσκω, from Ancient Greek εὑρίσκω.
The simple past comes from the old perfect εὕρηκα.</div><b>βραβείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βραβεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prize, award</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βραβείο</b> Ίψεν - Ibsen Award</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βραβεῖον, related to βραβεύς, from Proto-Hellenic *mŕ̥gʷ-, probably ultimately of Pre-Greek origin, suggesting that the athletic games themselves could have been of Pre-Greek heritage.</div><b>βραβευμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βραβευμένη, neuter βραβευμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commended, who have been awarded (something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βραβεύομαι, passive voice of βραβεύω</div><b>βραβεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to award, bestow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to commend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βραβεύω. See βραβείο.</div><b>βραγχιόποδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Branchiopoda, the branchiopods; certain crustaceans.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοινό ζωοπλαγκτικό γένος της υπόταξης των κλαδοκεραιωτών, ταξινομική ομάδα που περιλαμβάνεται στην κλάση των <b>βραγχιόποδων</b> καρκινοειδών. - A common zooplanktonic genus of the suborder Cladocera, a taxonomic group included in the class Branchiopoda of crustaceans.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Branchiopoda.</div><b>βραδέως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) slowly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βραδέως. By surface analysis, βραδε-) + -ως (-os).</div><b>βραδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βραδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. evening</div>
  <div class='def'>2. night</div><b>βραδιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be excessively late</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to the night is coming onto one (before one can get somewhere etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (impersonal) to be nightfall</div><b>βραδινή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βραδινές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evening performance</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://βραδινός">βραδινός</a></div><b>βραδινό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βραδινά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evening meal, dinner, supper</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://βραδινός">βραδινός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://βραδινός">βραδινός</a></div><b>βραδινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραδινή, neuter βραδινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of the evening or night</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from βράδυ n</div><b>βραδυκαρδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bradycardia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from New Latin bradycardia. By surface analysis, βραδυ- (vrady-) + καρδ(ιά)) + -ία.</div><b>βραδύπους</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βραδύποδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sloth (South American mammal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek βραδύπους + πούς).</div><b>βραδύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραδεία, neuter βραδύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βραδύς.</div><b>βραζιλιάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραζιλιάνικη, neuter βραζιλιάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Brazilian (relating to Brazil or its people)</div><b>βρακί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρακιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. briefs, panties, knickers (female underclothing), underpants (male underclothing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>, also the plural βρακιά (vrakiá) (for both sexes) / for women only: κιλότα f (kilóta)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (ironic) a medieval term similar to vraka, traditional breeches, trousers (chiefly male clothing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα ’κανε στα <b>βρακιά</b> του - S/He shitted in his underpants. [was scared to death]</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βρακί &lt; βρακίν, from Koine Greek βράκιον, a diminutive noun with ending -ιον (-ion) to a plural noun βράκες or βράκαι from which, see the modern βράκα m.</div><b>βρακοζώνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βρακοζώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) belt for the traditional vraka, or for underpants</div>
  <div class='ex'>τὸ βρακοζούνιν αὐλινθῆ μόνον του. - (please add an English translation of this quotation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βρακί and βράκα + -ο- + ζώνη, already as late Byzantine Greek βρακοζώνι. Suffixed with diminutive -ι (-i) in its neuter forms and with augmentative -α (-a) in its modern most common form.</div><b>βρακοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βρακοφόρα, neuter βρακοφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wearing breeches, breeched</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βράκα + -φόρος</div><b>βρασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) boiling, fermentation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) turmoil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βράζω + -μός.</div><b>βραστήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βραστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kettle, teakettle (vessel with a spout for boiling water)</div><b>βραστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραστή, neuter βραστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. boiled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boiling, hot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adjectival formation related to βράζω; see there for more.</div><b>βραχίονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βραχίονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. upper arm, arm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the muscles of the upper arm</div><b>βραχιόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βραχιόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bracelet</div><b>βραχιόνιο οστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βραχιόνια οστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>humerus, funny bone</div><b>βραχνιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to become hoarse, get hoarse</div><b>βραχοκιρκίνεζο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βραχοκιρκίνεζα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kestrel (bird of prey)</div><b>βραχονησίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βραχονησίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skerry, rocky islet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from βράχ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + νησ(ί)) + -ίδα.</div><b>βραχυκυκλώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be short circuited</div><b>βραχυκυκλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to short circuit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βραχυ- (vrachy-, “short, small”) + κυκλώνω, calque of English short circuit.</div><b>βραχυκύκλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βραχυκυκλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short circuit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βραχυ- (vrachy-, “short, small”) + κύκλωμα, calque of English short circuit.</div><b>βραχυπρόθεσμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραχυπρόθεσμη, neuter βραχυπρόθεσμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short-term</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βραχυ- (vrachy-, “short”) + προθεσμία, calque of English short-term.</div><b>βραχύβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραχύβια, neuter βραχύβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short-lived</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βραχύβιος. By surface analysis, βραχύ- + βίος.</div><b>βραχύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραχεία, neuter βραχύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. short (having little duration)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. short (having a small distance from one end or edge to another, either horizontally or vertically)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>βραχέα</b> κύματα - shortwave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βραχύς.</div><b>βραχύχρονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραχύχρονη, neuter βραχύχρονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short (of a vowel, of a sound)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βραχύ- + χρόνος.</div><b>βραχώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βραχώδης, neuter βραχώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rocky, craggy (filled with rocks)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βράχος.</div><b>βρε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, informal) hey, eh, dude (a sort of vocative familiar call to a friend or acquaintance)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βρε</b> παιδί μου, γιατί δε μ’ ακούς; - Why won&#x27;t you listen to me, (my) child?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, informal, offensive) hey, oi, you (a sort of vocative familiar call to strangers and unfamiliar people to deliberately offend)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε ποιον μιλάς έτσι, <b>βρε</b> κάθαρμα; - Who do you think you&#x27;re talking to like that, you bastard?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, informal) jeez, gosh, yikes, my, what (expressing surprise or bewilderment, often repeated)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Βρε, βρε</b> τι πάθαμε. - Oh jeez, look what happened to us.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βρέ, developed phonetically, from Byzantine Greek μρέ, from Ancient Greek μωρέ, the vocative form of μωρός. Doublet of μωρέ, μρε (mre), μπρε (bre), ωρέ, and ρε (re). See μωρέ.
Or, inherited from Byzantine Greek βρέ, from Ottoman Turkish بره (bre) (Turkish bre), itself taken from the same Greek root.</div><b>βρεγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βρεγμένη, neuter βρεγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βρέχομαι, passive voice of βρέχω (“I wet -also: rain-”).</div><b>βρεμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine βρεμένη, neuter βρεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρεγμένος">βρεγμένος</a></div><b>βρετανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βρετανική, neuter βρετανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>British, Britannic</div><b>βρετονικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Breton (the language of Brittany and its people)</div><b>βρετονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βρετονική, neuter βρετονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Breton (relating to Brittany or its people or language)</div><b>βρεφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βρεφική, neuter βρεφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) infant (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek βρεφικός. By surface analysis, βρέφος + -ικός.</div><b>βρεφοκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρεφοκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foundling hospital (hospital and care facility for babies that have been abandoned by their mothers)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Γιάννης πέρασε τα πρώτα του χρόνια σ’ ένα <b>βρεφοκομείο</b>. - Giannis spent his first years in a foundling hospital.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βρέφος + -κομείο. First attested 1862.</div><b>βρεφοκτονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βρεφοκτονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>infanticide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βρέφος + -κτονία</div><b>βρικόλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βρικόλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vampire (the Greek folklore depiction, a dead person who rises from the grave at night and drinks the blood of the living)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγαινε να κοιμηθείς, πριν έρθει ο <b>βρικόλακας</b> να σε φάει! - Go to sleep before the vampire comes to eat you!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) unpleasant reminder, unpleasant remnant (of the past)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το Άουσβιτς και τα άλλα στρατόπεδα εξόντωσης είναι οι <b>βρικόλακες</b> του Ολοκαυτώματος. - Auschwitz and the other extermination camps are the unpleasant reminders of the Holocaust.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βουρκόλακας, from Bulgarian върколак, from Proto-Slavic *vьlkolakъ (“werewolf”).</div><b>βρισίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρισίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slanging match (argument in which names are called)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άκουσα <b>βρισίδι</b> από τους διπλανούς. - I heard the neighbours having a slanging match.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βρίζω + -ίδι.</div><b>βρισιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βρισιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. insult, abuse (form of speech against someone deliberately intended to be rude)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε ντρέπεται να ρίχνει <b>βρισιές</b> σε ηλικιωμένο άνθρωπο; - Isn&#x27;t he ashamed to be showering abuse on an elderly person?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. curse, oath, swear word, expletive (word considered taboo and impolite or offensive)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος έμαθε στο παιδάκι <b>βρισιές</b>; - Who taught that little child swear words?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βρισιά, from Ancient Greek ὑβρισία, from ὕβρις, equivalent to βρίζω + -ιά.</div><b>βρογχίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βρογχίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) demotic form of <a href="bword://βρογχίτιδα">βρογχίτιδα</a></div><b>βρογχίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βρογχίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bronchitis</div><b>βρογχοσκόπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural βρογχοσκοπήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) bronchoscopy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βρόγχος + -σκόπηση.</div><b>βρομάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to smell (of), stink (of), reek (of) (to give off a bad odour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα πόδια σου <b>βρομάνε</b>. - Your feet smell.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, intransitive) to stink, be fishy (give an impression of dishonesty or untruth)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ιστορία του <b>βρομάει</b>. - His story is fishy / Something&#x27;s fishy about his story.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βρομώ + -άω. From Ancient Greek βρομῶ, contracted form of βρομέω, from βρόμος, from βρέμω. Related to βρόμη. Modern sense acquired from association of certain loud noises with a bad smell following. Compare Irish English bang (“strong smell”).</div><b>βρομοθήλυκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρομοθήλυκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) dirty bitch, dirty slut (general abuse term for a female)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε από ’δω, ρε <b>βρομοθήλυκο</b>! - Get out of here, you dirty bitch!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βρομο- (vromo-, “dirty”) + θηλυκό.</div><b>βρομόκαιρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βρομόκαιροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) lousy weather (bad weather in general, rain etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τί <b>βρομόκαιρος</b> αυτός· μια φυσάει, μια βρέχει! - What horrible weather; wind one minute, rain the next!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βρομό- + καιρός.</div><b>βρομώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, intransitive, transitive) (uncommon) alternative form of <a href="bword://βρομάω">βρομάω</a></div><b>βροντάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rumble, thunder</div><b>βροντή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βροντές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thunder, thunderclap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βροντή.</div><b>βροντοχτυπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive, transitive) to bang, clack, hit (make a loud and bothersome noise)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη <b>βροντοχτυπάς</b> τις χάντρες,
η δουλειά κάνει τους άντρες. - Don&#x27;t clack the beads,
Hard work makes the man.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ) βροντo- (vronto-, “to thunder, to bang”) + χτυπάω + [Term?] (“to hit”).</div><b>βροντοχτυπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βροντοχτυπάω">βροντοχτυπάω</a></div><b>βροντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rumble, thunder</div><b>βρουσονέτια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βρουσονέτιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>broussonetia (Broussonetia)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένα από αυτά είναι και η <b>Βρουσονέτια</b> η παπυροφόρος ή όπως αποκαλείται σε πολλά μέρη Τούρκικη Μουριά καθώς έφθασε στη χώρα μας από την Ασία με τελευταία στάση τη γείτονα χώρα.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Broussonetia.</div><b>βροχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βροχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rain, shower</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το χώμα ρουφούσε διψασμένο τις πρώτες σταγόνες της φθινοπωρινής <b>βροχής</b>. - The thirsty earth drank in the first drops of the autumn rain.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek βροχή.</div><b>βροχερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βροχερή, neuter βροχερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rainy, showery, wet</div><b>βροχόνερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rainwater (water sourced from rain)</div><b>βροχόπτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rainfall (the occurrence of liquid precipitation, the fall of rain)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rainfall (the amount of rain that falls on a single occasion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from βροχή + -ό- + πτώση , a calque of German Regenfall.</div><b>βρυκόλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βρυκόλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρικόλακας">βρικόλακας</a></div><b>βρυχηθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βρυχηθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>roar, roaring (of lion, etc)</div><b>βρυχιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to roar (of animals, especially a lion: to make a loud deep noise)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to roar (of people: to make a loud, deep cry, especially from strong emotion)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to roar (generally, of inanimate objects etc.: to make a loud resounding noise)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βρυχῶμαι, contracted form of βρυχάομαι, + -ιέμαι.</div><b>βρυχώμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to roar (of animals, especially a lion: to make a loud deep noise)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to roar (of people: to make a loud, deep cry, especially from strong emotion)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to roar (generally, of inanimate objects etc.: to make a loud resounding noise)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βρυχῶμαι, contracted form of βρυχάομαι.</div><b>βρυόφυτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Bryophyta, the bryophytes or mosses.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Bryophyta.</div><b>βρωμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://βρομάω">βρομάω</a></div><b>βρωμίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρωμίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bromide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βρώμιο + -ίδιο, calque of English bromide.</div><b>βρωμοθήλυκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρωμοθήλυκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρομοθήλυκο">βρομοθήλυκο</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε από ’δω, ρε <b>βρωμοθήλυκο</b>! - Get out of here, you dirty bitch!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> βρωμο- (vromo-, “dirty”) + θηλυκό.</div><b>βρωμοκούναβο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρωμοκούναβα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polecat</div><b>βρωμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://βρομώ">βρομώ</a></div><b>βρόμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) oat, oats (widely cultivated cereal grass, typically Avena sativa)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βρόμος.
The traditional derivation from Byzantine Greek βρόμη, from Ancient Greek βρόμος, from βρέμω (with sense development explained as being from the movement and sound of grain in the wind), appears to be folk etymology.</div><b>βρόμικα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://βρόμικος">βρόμικος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dirtily, filthily</div><b>βρόμικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βρόμικη or βρόμικια, neuter βρόμικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dirty, filthy, unclean (covered with or containing dirt)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επειδή ήταν πολύ <b>βρόμικος</b>, κανείς δεν τον πλησίαζε. - Because he was very dirty, no one approached him.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) dirty, filthy (corrupt, illegal or improper; obscene or indecent)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προσπαθούν να ξεπλύνουν το <b>βρόμικο</b> χρήμα. - They&#x27;re trying to launder the dirty money.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. smelly, stinky, malodorous (giving off unpleasant odour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν τόσο <b>βρόμικα</b> εκεί μέσα που βγήκα έξω. - It was so smelly in there I went out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βρόμα + -ικος (-ikos), the former from an unattested Byzantine Greek *βρόμα, nominal formation from Ancient Greek βρομέω / βρομῶ, from βρόμος, from βρέμω.</div><b>βρόμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρώμιο">βρώμιο</a></div><b>βρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βρόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brome (Bromus-genus plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek βρόμος. Semantic loan from translingual Bromus.</div><b>βρόντησα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of βρόντηξα</div><b>βρόχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βρόχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. noose</div><b>βρύο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βρύα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moss (member of the bryophytes)</div><b>βρύση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βρύσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tap, faucet or public water source</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βρύση, from earlier, or Hellenistic Koine Greek βρύσις, formed from βρῡ́ω invoking its later sense of &quot;burst, gush&quot;.</div><b>βρώμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρόμη">βρόμη</a></div><b>βρώμικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βρώμικια or βρώμικη, neuter βρώμικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dirty, filthy, polluted</div>
  <div class='def'>2. obscene, sleazy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See βρόμικος.</div><b>βρώμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural βρώμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) bromine (halogen)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French brome, neologism from Ancient Greek βρῶμος.</div><b>βρώσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βρώσιμη, neuter βρώσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>edible (that can be eaten without harm; suitable for consumption)</div><b>βυζί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βυζιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) tit, boob (the breast)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με αυτό το σουτιέν που φοράει, της πέφτουν τα <b>βυζιά</b> απ’ έξω. - With that bra that she&#x27;s wearing, her tits are falling out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βυζίον, from βυζάνω, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰuH- (“to swell, bend, curve”). Cognates include English bosom, German Busen (“breast”), Dutch boezem (“breast”) Albanian buzë (“lip”), Romanian buză (“lip”), Irish bus (“lip”), and Latin bucca (“cheek”).</div><b>βυζαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to suckle (to suck milk from a nursing mother)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to suckle, to breastfeed, to give suck to (to feed with breastmilk)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to suck</div><b>βυζαγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βυζαγμένη, neuter βυζαγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suckled, breastfed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βυζαίνομαι, passive voice of βυζαίνω (“suckle”).</div><b>βυζαντινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βυζαντινή, neuter βυζαντινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Byzantine, pertaining to Byzantium</div><b>βυζοκούππιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus, colloquial, vulgar) bra, brassiere</div>
  <div class='ex'>(Cypriot Dialect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, βυζί + κούπα</div><b>βυθίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sink, submerge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to immerse, plunge, submerge</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to scuttle (deliberately sink one&#x27;s own ship)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek βυθίζω, from βυθός + -ίζω.</div><b>βυθισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine βυθισμένη, neuter βυθισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>submerged, sunken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of βυθίζομαι, passive voice of βυθίζω (“I sink -something-”).</div><b>βυθός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βυθοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seabed, riverbed, bottom, ground</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βυθός.</div><b>βυρσοδέψης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βυρσοδέψες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tanner (someone who converts hides into leather)</div><b>βυσσινής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βυσσινιά, neuter βυσσινί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crimson, cherry red</div><b>βυσσινί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βυσσινία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crimson, cherry red</div><b>βυσσινιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βυσσινιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sour cherry tree, morello cherry tree</div><b>βωμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βωμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. altar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. place of religious ceremony or sacrifice</div><b>βωξίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) bauxite</div><b>βόας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βόες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boa, boid (snake of the Boidae)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English boa, suffixed with -ας (-as).</div><b>βόδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ox</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) oaf, lout</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek βόδιν, from Koine Greek βοΐδιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek βοῦς.</div><b>βόθρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βόθροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cesspool, cesspit, septic tank</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantically similar to Proto-Indo-European *bʰedʰh₂- (“to pierce, to dig”), however, the expected outcome of this root in Greek would be **πόθρος (based on Grassmann&#x27;s law). Pre-Greek origin or influence from Ancient Greek βαθύς”) are possible alternatives.</div><b>βόιδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βόιδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βόδι">βόδι</a></div><b>βόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal, literary) bullet</div><b>βόλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βόλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βώλος">βώλος</a></div><b>βόλτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βόλτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. turn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. walk, stroll, drive, ride</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάνω μια <b>βόλτα</b> / πάω <b>βόλτα</b> - I go for a walk (or drive)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. thread (of screw or bolt)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian volta.</div><b>βόμβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βόμβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bomb (device filled with explosives)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bombshell (sudden revelation of bad news)</div><b>βόμβα μολότοφ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βόμβες μολότοφ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Molotov cocktail</div><b>βόμβα μολότωφ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βόμβες μολότωφ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βόμβα">βόμβα</a> μολότοφ</div><b>βόμβα υδρογόνου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βόμβες υδρογόνου)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydrogen bomb</div><b>βόμβες μολότωφ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://βόμβα">βόμβα</a> μολότωφ</div><b>βόμβες υδρογόνου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://βόμβα">βόμβα</a> υδρογόνου</div><b>βόμβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βόμβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bumblebee</div>
  <div class='def'>2. buzzing, humming (sound)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tinnitus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek βόμβος.</div><b>βόρ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://βόρειος">βόρειος</a>: northern, northerly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://βορράς">βορράς</a>: north</div><b>βόρεια-βορειοανατολικά</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>north-northeast</div><b>βόρεια-βορειοδυτικά</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>north-northwest</div><b>βόρειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine βόρεια or βόρειος, neuter βόρειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>north, northerly (toward the north, from the north)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>βόρειος</b> άνεμος είναι ψυχρός. - The northern wind is cold.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βόρειοι, feminine βόρεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northerner</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>βόρειοι</b> έχουν συχνά ξανθά μαλλιά. - Northerners often have blond hair.</div><b>βόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural βόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) boron</div><b>βόσκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(ambitransitive) to graze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek βόσκω.</div><b>βότανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βότανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. herb</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flower, plant</div><b>βότκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βότκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vodka</div><b>βότσαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small stone rounded and eroded by a body of water; pebble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unknown. Possibly from Byzantine Greek βήσσαλον, from Late Latin bessalis (“small brick, tile”). Alternatively, has been interpreted as deriving from Italian bozzolo (“cocoon”), although this interpretation remains generally unsupported by most linguists due to semantic and phonological challenges. Another possibility is that it derives from Ancient Greek βολάχι, itself a diminutive form of βόλος. The affrication of the phoneme /k/ before the front vowel /i/ may have transformed βολάχι to *βολάτσι and eventually βότσαλο. This etymology may be attested in γιαλόβολος and γιαλόβουλος, both meaning &quot;sea-pebble.&quot;</div><b>βύδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βύδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βίδρα">βίδρα</a></div><b>βύθιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural βυθίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sinking</div>
  <div class='def'>2. falling, dipping</div>
  <div class='def'>3. running into, bumping into</div>
  <div class='def'>4. dip (gymnastic or bodybuilding exercise on parallel bars in which the performer, resting on his hands, lets his arms bend and his body sink, and then raises himself back by straightening his arms)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From βυθίζω + -ση (-si).</div><b>βύσσινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural βύσσινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sour cherry, morello cherry</div><b>βώλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural βώλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>marble (glass ball)</div><b>γ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Γ΄">Γ΄</a></div><b>γάγγραινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gangrene</div><b>γάδαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάδαροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γάιδαρος">γάιδαρος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek γάδαρος. See the etymology of γάιδαρος.</div><b>γάδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cod (marine fish of the family Gadidae)</div><b>γάζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γάζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gauze (bleached cotton fabric used as a surgical dressing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French gaze.</div><b>γάιδαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάιδαροι, feminine γαϊδούρα or γαϊδάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. donkey, ass, Equus asinus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) lout, boor, ass, jackass, pig, jerk (an uncultred, ill-bred man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>γάιδαρος</b> ήταν ο αδελφός της, που δεν μας είπε ούτε ένα «γεια»! - What a boor her brother was, he didn&#x27;t even say &quot;hi&quot; to us!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek γάδαρος of 2nd century CE. Medieval γάιδαρος/γάδαρος/γαΐδαρος or γαϊδάριον
Origin theories:</div><b>γάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γάλατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>milk</div>
  <div class='ex'>κατσικίσιο <b>γάλα</b> - goat&#x27;s milk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γάλα, from Proto-Indo-European *glakt-, *galakt-.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek га́ла.</div><b>γάλλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gallium</div><b>γάλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>male turkey (bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian gallo (d&#x27;India) (literally “rooster of India”).</div><b>γάμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) gamma, the third letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) L-shaped as in <a href="bword://σχηματίζω">σχηματίζω</a> Γ.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γάμμα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://γαμάω">γαμάω</a> and <a href="bword://γαμώ">γαμώ</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://γαμάω">γαμάω</a> and <a href="bword://γαμώ">γαμώ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>γάμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://γάμα">γάμα</a></div><b>γάμοι ομοφύλων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://γάμος">γάμος</a> ομοφύλων</div><b>γάμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. marriage, wedding (ceremony)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. marriage (social institution)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γάμος, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵem- (“to marry”).
Compare Mariupol Greek га́мус.</div><b>γάμος ομοφυλοφίλων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάμοι ομοφυλοφίλων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gay marriage, same-sex marriage</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τους πρώτους <b>γάμους ομοφυλοφίλων</b> στη Βρετανία χαιρέτισε τα μεσάνυχτα ο πρωθυπουργός της Βρετανίας Ντέιβιντ Κάμερον - Britain&#x27;s first gay marriages were welcomed at midnight by British Prime Minister David Cameron.</div><b>γάμος ομοφυλόφιλων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάμοι ομοφυλόφιλων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gay marriage, same-sex marriage</div><b>γάμος ομοφύλων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάμοι ομοφύλων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gay marriage, same-sex marriage</div><b>γάμπια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γάμπιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>topsail</div><b>γάντζος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γάντζοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hook</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hook (used for hanging stock)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. butcher&#x27;s hook</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan ganzo. Also from Ottoman Turkish قانجه (kanca, kance) in the forms γάντζα, κάντζα.</div><b>γάντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γάντια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glove</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French gant.</div><b>γάρμπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) elegance, courtesy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian garbo (“politeness”).</div><b>γάστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γάστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Dutch oven (cooking pot with a tight-fitting lid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γάστρα.</div><b>γάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cat (domestic species)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) crafty person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γάτα, an 11th-century parallel form of κάττα, from Medieval Latin gatta, from Late Latin catta.
Or, from Hellenistic κάττα with sound change from liaison with the accusative of the feminine article τήν /tin ˈkata &gt; tiŋˈɡata &gt; ti ˈɣata/.
The mediaeval or Hellenistic form κάττα and the simplified spelling κάτα survives in modern form κάτα, diminutives), dialectal and regional. From Late Latin catta. Also see the masculine γάτος, from Late Latin cattus.</div><b>γάϊδαρε</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of γάιδαρε vocative singular of <a href="bword://γάιδαρος">γάιδαρος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>The umlaut (diaeresis) is not needed</div><b>γάϊδαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of γάιδαρο accusative singular of <a href="bword://γάιδαρος">γάιδαρος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>The umlaut (diaeresis) is not needed</div><b>γάϊδαροι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of γάιδαροι nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://γάιδαρος">γάιδαρος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>The umlaut (diaeresis) is not needed</div><b>γάϊδαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://γάιδαρος">γάιδαρος</a></div><b>γέεννα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hell, gehenna (one of various hells in Abrahamic religions)</div><b>γέλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laughter, mirth, laugh</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γέλιο, from γελῶ.</div><b>γέμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεμίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>filling, stuffing</div><b>γένεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the beginning of a phaenomenon (literally: birth, creation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γένεσις.</div><b>γένεσις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://γένεση">γένεση</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek γένεσις.</div><b>γένι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γένια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beard (facial hair) (rarely used in singular, see plural)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γένι, γένιν, from Koine Greek γένειον, from Ancient Greek γένειον, from or related to γένυς.</div><b>γέννα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γέννες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>birth (process of childbearing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γέννα from verb γεννῶ. The Ancient Greek noun γέννα had a different meaning: “ancestry, descendant”.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://γεννάω">γεννάω</a> (also: γένναγε)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://γεννάω">γεννάω</a> (also: γέννησε)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form of the verb γεννάω / γεννώ.</div><b>γέννηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεννήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. birth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) start, inauguration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γέννηση, from Ancient Greek γέννησις.</div><b>γέννησις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεννήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://γέννηση">γέννηση</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek γέννησις.</div><b>γένος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. family</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. genus</div>
  <div class='def'>4. gender (masculine, feminine, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. maiden name, née</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ελένη Παπαδοπούλου, <b>το γένος</b> Μενεγάκη. - Eleni Papadopoulou née Menegaki.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γένος. Also see γίγνομαι.</div><b>γέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to tilt, lean</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to bow down</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to sag</div><b>γέρο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://γέρος">γέρος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://γέρος">γέρος</a></div><b>γέρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γέροι, feminine γριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. old man (elderly male person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>γέρος</b> καθόταν όλη μέρα στο καφενείο. - The old man sat in the coffee shop all day long.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, impolite, intimate) old man (one&#x27;s father)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με ενοχλεί συνέχεια ο <b>γέρος</b> μου να βρω δουλειά. - My old man is constantly bothering me to get a job.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γέρων.</div><b>γέφυρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γέφυρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bridge (construction that spans a divide)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κρεμαστή <b>γέφυρα</b> - suspension bridge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bridge</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bridge</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pons</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γέφυρα.</div><b>γήινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terrestrial, earthly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γήινος.</div><b>γήπεδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γήπεδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ground, field, pitch, court, course (piece of land on which a specified sport takes place)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γήπεδο</b> ποδοσφαίρου - football field</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) site, ground, plot, strip (open area of land)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γήπεδο</b> προσγειώσεως - landing strip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γήπεδον.</div><b>γήρανση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) aging, growing old</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γήρανσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>γίγαντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γίγαντες, feminine γιγάντισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. giant (mythical anthropomorphic creature of great size) written with initial capital Γίγαντας</div>
  <div class='def'>2. giant (large person or organisation)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. giant star</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γίγαντας, from Ancient Greek γίγαντᾰ).</div><b>γίγνεσθαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, literary) the progress of transformation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek γίγνεσθαι infinitive, used by Katharevousa also in use in Modern Greek.</div><b>γίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γίδι.</div><b>γίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kid, young goat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γίδιν, from Ancient Greek αἰγίδιον, diminutive of αἴξ.</div><b>γίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to become; to become, turn into</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κοπέλα ντράπηκε και <b>έγινε</b> κόκκινη. - The young girl was embarrassed and turned red.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to become; to become, be created, come into being, come into existence</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ελλάδα <b>έγινε</b> ανεξάρτητη το 1832. - Greece became independent in 1832.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to happen; to happen, occur, take place, go on</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είδες τι <b>γινόταν</b> εκεί; - Did you see what was happening there?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to happen; to become of, happen with, get to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>έγινε</b> με τον αδερφό σου, ζει ακόμα στην Αγγλία; - What became of your brother, is he still living in England?</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of plants and fruit) Plant and fruit senses.; to grow</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα λεμόνια δεν <b>γίνονται</b> στη βόρεια Ευρώπη. - Lemons don&#x27;t grow in northern Europe.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (of plants and fruit or food in general) Plant and fruit senses.; to ripen, be fully ripe, be ready</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έγιναν</b> ακόμα αυτά τα λεμόνια; - Are those lemons ripe yet?</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (with να) to be (possible), can, may</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γίνεται</b> να έχει κανείς καλή δουλειά χωρίς απολυτήριο; - Is it possible for one to have a good job without a certificate?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek γίνομαι, from Ancient Greek γίγνομαι.</div><b>γίντις</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Yiddish (language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Yiddish ייִדיש (yidish).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Yiddish (pertaining to the language or people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Yiddish ייִדיש (yidish).</div><b>γαίμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αίμα">αίμα</a></div><b>γαβ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(onomatopoeic) woof, bow wow (sound a dog makes when barking)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>γαβγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to bark, woof, yelp, yap</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο σκύλος <b>γαβγίζει</b> - the dog barks</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to yell, shout, bark</div><b>γαβιάλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαβιάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gharial, gavial (Gavialis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Gavialis.</div><b>γαδολίνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gadolinium</div><b>γαελικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαελική, neuter γαελικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Gaelic, Celtic (relating to the Celtic people of Ireland, Scotland and the Isle of Man and their languages and customs)</div><b>γαζέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαζέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gazelle</div><b>γαζί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαζιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>row of stitching, seam (especially machine sewn)</div><b>γαζία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαζίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the needle bush or sweet acacia (Vachellia farnesiana)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the flower of the needle bush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> An αντιδάνειο. Borrowed from Venetan gazia (compare Italian gaggia), from Late Latin acacia, from Ancient Greek ἀκακία, from ἀκή. Doublet of ακακία.</div><b>γαιοπροωθητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαιοπροωθητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γεωπροωθητής">γεωπροωθητής</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαιο- (gaio-, “earth”) + προωθητής</div><b>γαλάζια φάλαινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαλάζιες φάλαινες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blue whale (Balaenoptera musculus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, γαλάζιος + φάλαινα.</div><b>γαλάζιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλάζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>azure, sky blue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter of the adjective γαλάζιος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://γαλάζιος">γαλάζιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://γαλάζιος">γαλάζιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter of the adjective γαλάζιος.</div><b>γαλάζιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαλάζια, neuter γαλάζιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sky blue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γαλάζιος, from Koine Greek κάλαϊς, probably from Ancient Greek καλάϊνος”).</div><b>γαλέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαλέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) galley (oared vessel)</div><b>γαλήνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) serenity, equanimity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) calm, quiet, peaceful</div><b>γαλήνια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://γαλήνιος">γαλήνιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γαλήνιος">γαλήνιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calmly, peacefully, serenely</div><b>γαλήνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαλήνια, neuter γαλήνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. calm, quiet, peaceful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clear (of the sky)</div><b>γαλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a small turkey</div><b>γαλαζοκότσυφας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαλαζοκότσυφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Monticola solitarius: the blue rock thrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαλάζιος + κότσυφας</div><b>γαλαζοπαπαδίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλαζοπαπαδίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blue tit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γαλάζιος + παπαδίτσα.</div><b>γαλαζοπράσινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλαζοπράσινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turquoise, teal, an intermediate bluish-green colour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γαλάζιο + πράσινο.</div><b>γαλακτικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lactic acid</div><b>γαλακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαλακτική, neuter γαλακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of or pertaining to milk</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lactic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γαλακτικός. By surface analysis, γάλα + -ικός.</div><b>γαλακτοκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλακτοκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dairy, dairy farm, creamery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γάλα + -κομείο.</div><b>γαλακτοκομικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dairy products, milk</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γαλακτοκομικός">γαλακτοκομικός</a></div><b>γαλακτοκομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαλακτοκομική, neuter γαλακτοκομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dairy (referring to products produced from milk)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γαλακτοκομία + -ικός, the former from γαλακτο- (galakto-) + -κομία.</div><b>γαλακτομπούρεκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλακτομπούρεκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a dessert of phyllo dough and custard</div><b>γαλακτοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλακτοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dairy shop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. milk bar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γάλα n γαλακτο- + -πωλείο</div><b>γαλανομάτης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαλανομάτα, neuter γαλανομάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blue-eyed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαλανός + μάτι</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαλανομάτηδες, feminine γαλανομάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a person with blue eyes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαλανός + μάτι</div><b>γαλανό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pale blue, light blue, sea blue</div><b>γαλανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαλανή, neuter γαλανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>light blue, pale blue, sea blue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an unattested Byzantine Greek *γαλεανός, from Koine Greek καλλεανός, a derivative of κάλαϊς, probably from Ancient Greek καλάϊνος”). Doublet of γαλάζιος.</div><b>γαλαξίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαλαξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>galaxy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek γαλαξίας.</div><b>γαλατάδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλατάδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dairy shop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. milk bar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γάλα + -άδικο</div><b>γαλατάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαλατάδες, feminine γαλατού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>milkman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γάλα + -άς.</div><b>γαλβανίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be galvanised</div><b>γαλβανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to galvanise (UK), galvanize (US)</div><b>γαλβανισμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαλβανισμένη, neuter γαλβανισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>galvanised (UK), galvanized (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>σιδηροσωλήνας γαλβανισµένος (galvanised iron pipes)</div><b>γαλβανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) galvanisation, galvanization</div><b>γαλεάσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαλεάσσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) galleass, a galley with three masts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Byzantine Greek γαλεάτσα, from Italian galeazza. A later variant γαλεάσσα is found in dictionaries and encyclopaedias of the 19th century, adapted in the fashion of other European languages, like the French galéasse (and see English galleass, German Galeasse).</div><b>γαλεάτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαλεάτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαλεάσσα">γαλεάσσα</a></div><b>γαλιάτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαλιάτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαλεάσσα">γαλεάσσα</a></div><b>γαλιόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλιόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>galleon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan galion or Italian galeone.</div><b>γαλλ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γαλλικός">γαλλικός</a>: French</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γαλλικά">γαλλικά</a>: French (language)</div><b>γαλλικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γαλλικός">γαλλικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. French (the language)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) French (vulgar language)</div><b>γαλλικά κλειδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of γαλλικό <a href="bword://κλειδί">κλειδί</a></div><b>γαλλικά κόρνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of γαλλικό <a href="bword://κόρνο">κόρνο</a></div><b>γαλλικό κλειδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλλικά κλειδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adjustable spanner (UK), adjustable wrench (US)</div><b>γαλλικό κόρνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλλικά κόρνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>French horn (type of brass instrument)</div><b>γαλλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαλλική, neuter γαλλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>French, Gallic</div><b>γαλλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαλλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gallicism</div><b>γαλοπούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαλοπούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turkey (bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γάλος + -πούλα. Also see πουλί.</div><b>γαλουχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) breastfeeding</div><b>γαλουχώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to nurture, educate</div><b>γαλούχηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) nursing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) nurture</div><b>γαλόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stripe, chevron, braid (indicating rank)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gallon (imperial unit of volume)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian galloni.</div><b>γαλόπουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαλόπουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a small turkey</div><b>γαμάτα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γαμάτος">γαμάτος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γαμάτος">γαμάτος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γαμάτος">γαμάτος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, informal) superbly, awesomely, smashingly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περάσαμε <b>γαμάτα</b> σ’ αυτές τις διακοπές. - We had a smashing time on this holiday.</div><b>γαμάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαμάτη, neuter γαμάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, informal) awesome, ace, smashing, great, super, dope (of the highest quality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το νέο του άλμπουμ είναι <b>γαμάτο</b>, αγόρασέ το! - His new album is smashing, buy it!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαμώ + -άτος.</div><b>γαμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) to fuck, screw, shag</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έπιασε τον αδερφό του να <b>γαμάει</b> την γυναίκα του. - He caught his brother fucking his wife.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to fuck someone over</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δήθεν φίλος μου, που με <b>γάμησε</b> εντελώς. - My so-called friend, who fucked me over completely.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern γαμώ + -άω. Inherited from Ancient Greek γαμῶ, contracted form of γαμέω, from γάμος.</div><b>γαμήλιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαμήλια, neuter γαμήλιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wedding, bridal, nuptial</div>
  <div class='def'>2. matrimonial, marital</div><b>γαμήσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαμήσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(offensive, vulgar) fuck, shag (act of sexual intercourse)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν το καλύτερο <b>γαμήσι</b> που είχα ποτέ. - It was the best fuck I ever had.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γαμέω.</div><b>γαμβριάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαμβριάτικη, neuter γαμβριάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαμπριάτικος">γαμπριάτικος</a></div><b>γαμβρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαμβροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://γαμπρός">γαμπρός</a>, used in few learned compounds</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γαμβρός. Compare to γαμπρός.</div><b>γαμημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine γαμημένη, neuter γαμημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, literally, vulgar) fucked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, emphatic, intensifier, vulgar) fucking, damned, bloody</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το <b>γαμημένο</b> το σκυλί με δάγκωσε. - That fucking dog bit me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of γαμιέμαι, passive voice of γαμάω- / γαμώ (“to fuck”).</div><b>γαμιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαμιάδες, feminine γαμιού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) fuckboy, shagger (man who sleeps with many partners)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, vulgar) great fuck (man who is skilled at sex)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γαμέας, from Ancient Greek γαμέω.</div><b>γαμιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) to be fucked / screwed / shagged, get fucked / screwed / shagged</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γυναίκα <b>γαμιόταν</b> από τον άντρα της. - The woman was being fucked by her husband.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, vulgar) to fuck, screw, shag</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γαμιέται</b> τρεις φορές την εβδομάδα. - He fucks three times a week.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively, vulgar) to be overworked, be suffering</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γαμιέμαι</b> στη δουλειά. - I&#x27;m being badly overworked.</div><b>γαμιόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαμιόλες, masculine γαμιόλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) fucker, whore, bitch (generic term of abuse for a woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πες της να πάει να γαμηθεί, την <b>γαμιόλα</b>! - Tell her to fuck off, the bitch!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γαμώ, following the model of the equally abusive καριόλα.</div><b>γαμιόλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαμιόληδες, feminine γαμιόλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) fucker, asshole, dickhead (generic term of abuse for a man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πες του να πάει να γαμηθεί, τον <b>γαμιόλη</b>! - Tell him to fuck off, the fucker!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γαμώ, following the model of the equally abusive καριόλης.</div><b>γαμοσταυρίδια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) effing and blinding, abuse, curses (forms of speech against someone deliberately intended to be rude)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας μου έριξε μερικά <b>γαμοσταυρίδια</b> όταν έπεσε το σφυρί στο πόδι του. - My father cursed up a storm (literally:threw a few curses out) when the hammer fell on his foot.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to γαμοσταυρίζω.</div><b>γαμοσταυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, intransitive, vulgar) to swear like a trooper, swear like a sailor, eff and blind (to curse freely and indiscriminately)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν τράκαρα, βγήκε ο άλλος οδηγός και άρχισε να <b>γαμοσταυρίζει</b>. - When I crashed my car, the other driver got out and started cursing up a storm.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, transitive, vulgar) to curse, cuss out, curse out, cuss up and down (berate using profane language)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μετά από τέσσερις προσπάθειες χωρίς αποτέλεσμα, έφυγε <b>γαμοσταυρίζοντας</b> την κακή του τύχη. - After four attempts without result, he left cursing his bad luck.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γαμώ + σταυρός, an allusion to the curse &#x27;γαμώ τον σταυρό σου&#x27; (I fuck your cross).</div><b>γαμπριάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαμπριάτικη, neuter γαμπριάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of the bridegroom</div><b>γαμπρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαμπροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. son-in-law, specifically daughter&#x27;s husband</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brother-in-law, specifically sister&#x27;s husband</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bridegroom, groom</div>
  <div class='def'>4. eligible bachelor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γαμπρός, form of γαμβρός, from Ancient Greek γαμβρός, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵem-.</div><b>γαμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαμάω">γαμάω</a></div><b>γαμώ και δέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, vulgar) to be invincible, be unbeatable, be unstoppable</div>
  <div class='ex'>Που να τον νικήσεις αυτόν στο σκάκι, <b>γαμάει και δέρνει</b>! - How could you possibly beat him at chess? He&#x27;ll fucking destroy you!</div><b>γαμώ τη</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαμώ">γαμώ</a> <a href="bword://το">το</a></div><b>γαμώ το</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive, vulgar) fuck it, for fuck&#x27;s sake, damn it</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έσπασα το κινητό μου, ρε <b>γαμώτο</b>. - Oh fuck it, I broke my phone.</div><b>γαμώτη</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαμώ">γαμώ</a> <a href="bword://το">το</a></div><b>γαμώτο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαμώ">γαμώ</a> <a href="bword://το">το</a></div><b>γαντοφορεμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαντοφορεμένη, neuter γαντοφορεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gloved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γάντ(ι)) + -ο- (-o-) + φορεμένος.</div><b>γαρ</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) when introducing a new proposal : as well is</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, ironic) used to emphasize or highlight the faculty or the property of something or someone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γάρ.</div><b>γαρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shrimp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καρίς.</div><b>γαρίφαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαρίφαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαρύφαλλο">γαρύφαλλο</a></div><b>γαργάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαργάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gargling (act of cleaning one&#x27;s mouth by holding water or some other liquid in the back of the mouth and blowing air out from the lungs)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιατρός μου είπε να κάνω <b>γαργάρα</b> για τον πονόλαιμό μου. - The doctor told me to gargle for my sore throat.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) gargle (liquid used to do the above action)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιατρός μού έγραψε <b>γαργάρες</b>. - The doctor prescribed me a gargle.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαργαρίζω + -άρα.</div><b>γαργαλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to tickle (touch repeatedly or stroke delicately in a manner which causes laughter and twitching)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γαργάλησε</b> τον γιο του μέχρι δακρύων. - He tickled his son to the point of tears.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to excite, titillate, tickle (stimulate or excite pleasantly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μυρωδιά του φαγητού μου <b>γαργαλούσε</b> το στομάχι. - The smell of the food excited my stomach.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From modern γαργαλώ + -άω, from Byzantine Greek γαργαλῶ, from Ancient Greek γαργαλίζω.</div><b>γαργαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαργαλάω">γαργαλάω</a></div><b>γαρδούμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαρδούμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chitterling (an oven-baked food piece made from lamb or goat offal wrapped in intestines, a traditional Easter dish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γαρδούμιον, from Latin caldūmen (“offal”).</div><b>γαρκινία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαρκινίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>garcinia, Garcinia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Garcinia.</div><b>γαρμπής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) the libecchio (a southwesterly Mediterranean wind)</div><b>γαρνίρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be decorated, garnished or embellished</div><b>γαρνίρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to decorate, garnish, embellish</div><b>γαρνιτούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαρνιτούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. garnish, decoration, trimmings (food)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. decoration, trimmings (clothing)</div><b>γαρούφαλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαρούφαλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαρύφαλλο">γαρύφαλλο</a></div><b>γαρούφαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαρούφαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαρύφαλλο">γαρύφαλλο</a></div><b>γαρύφαλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαρύφαλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clove (spice)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carnation (flower)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γαρούφαλο, from Venetan garofolo, from Latin caryophyllus (“clove”), ultimately from Ancient Greek καρυόφυλλον.</div><b>γαρύφαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαρύφαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γαρύφαλλο">γαρύφαλλο</a></div><b>γασμούλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γασμούλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Gasmoulos</div><b>γαστεροπλήξ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glutton</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γάστρα.</div><b>γαστρίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαστρίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gastritis</div><b>γαστραία</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turnip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γάστρα.</div><b>γαστρεντερολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαστρεντερολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gastroenterology</div><b>γαστροοισοφαγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γαστροοισοφαγική, neuter γαστροοισοφαγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gastroesophageal</div><b>γαστρορραγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαστρορραγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gastrorrhagia</div><b>γατί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γατιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kitten, small cat</div><b>γατόπαρδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γατόπαρδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheetah</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian gattopardo (“serval, ocelot”).</div><b>γαϊδαράκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of γαϊδαράκος</div><b>γαϊδουράγκαθο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαϊδουράγκαθα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. milk thistle (Silybum marianum)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cotton thistle, Scotch thistle (Onopordum acanthium)</div><b>γαϊδουράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαϊδουράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://γαϊδούρι">γαϊδούρι</a> n</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαϊδούρ(ι) n, “donkey”) + -άκι</div><b>γαϊδουριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαϊδουριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rude act, rude behaviour, discourtesy (literally: a donkey&#x27;s behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν μεγάλη <b>γαϊδουριά</b> να μην ευχαριστήσει τους γονείς του για τα δώρα. - It was very rude of him not to thank his parents for the gifts.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαϊδούρι + -ιά.</div><b>γαϊδουροκαλόκαιρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαϊδουροκαλόκαιρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Indian summer (stretch of sunny and warm days during late autumn)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γαϊδούρι + καλοκαίρι</div><b>γαϊδούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γαϊδούρες, masculine γάιδαρος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. jenny, she-ass (female donkey)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) lout, boor, ass, jackass, pig, jerk (an uncultred, ill-bred woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πως μιλάει έτσι η <b>γαϊδούρα</b>; - What kind of way is that for that jackass to talk?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γάιδαρος + -ούρα.</div><b>γαϊδούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαϊδούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γάιδαρος">γάιδαρος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Είδες πώς μίλησε στον παππού μου αυτό το <b>γαϊδούρι</b>; - Did you see how that lout spoke to my grandfather?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See etymology at γάιδαρος</div><b>γαϊτάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γαϊτάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Decorative cotton, wool or silk cord for trimming and hemming.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γαϊτάνιν. Compare Ottoman Turkish غیطان (gaytan), Arabic قِيطَان, linkable to قُطْن, and Medieval Latin gaitanum</div><b>γαύρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γαύροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. anchovy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) various small fish</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a supporter of the Athens&#x27; Olympiakos football club.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. Carpinus betulus, the hornbeam, a European tree.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an earlier unattested *γραυρος (*gravros) (with assimilatory deletion of the *ρ (*r)), from *γλαύρος (with assimilation *λ (*l) &gt; *ρ (*r)), from Byzantine Greek ἔγγραυλος (with metathesis of the λ (l) and ρ (r)), from Ancient Greek ἔγγραυλῐς.</div><b>γδέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to graze, scuff</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to flay, skin</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to fleece, swindle, rook</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκδέρω, from ἐκ- (ek-, “out”) + δέρω.</div><b>γείσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γείσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cornice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brim (projecting rim of a hat)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γεῖσον.</div><b>γείτονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γείτονες, feminine γειτόνισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neighbour (UK), neighbor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γείτων.
Compare with Mariupol Greek ито́с and и́тун.</div><b>γείτσες</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bless you, gesundheit (to someone sneezing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the plural form of υγεία + -ίτσα, minus the initial vowel.</div><b>γείωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γειώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>earth (UK), earthing (UK), ground (US).</div>
  <div class='ex'>ηλεκτρική <b>γείωση</b> - electrical earth</div><b>γεγονός</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γεγονότα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>event, fact, occurrence</div>
  <div class='ex'>ιστορικό <b>γεγονός</b> - historical event</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γίγνομαι.</div><b>γειά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obsolete spelling of <a href="bword://γεια">γεια</a> (geia)</div><b>γειά στα χέρια σου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>literally, &quot;bless your hands&quot;; a toast or congratulation on good work, especially good cooking</div><b>γεια</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hi, hello</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bye</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cheers, your health</div>
  <div class='def'>4. bravo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑγίεια, from ὑγιής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://υγεία">υγεία</a> (health)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑγίεια, from ὑγιής.</div><b>γεια σας</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) hello, goodbye</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γεια σας</b>, κύριε Γιώργο. - Hello Mr George (polite) ^†</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literally) your health</div><b>γεια σου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal) hello, goodbye</div>
  <div class='def'>2. your health</div><b>γεια χαρά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hi</div>
  <div class='def'>2. see you later</div><b>γεια χας</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) (Paphos): hello, greetings</div><b>γειτονεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be a neighbour (UK) / neighbor (US) (of)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to neighbour (UK), to neighbor (US) (to be adjacent to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: συνορεύω (synorévo)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γειτονεύω. By surface analysis, γείτον(ας)) + -εύω.</div><b>γειτονιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γειτονιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neighbourhood (UK), neighborhood (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γειτονία, from γείτων + -ία.</div><b>γειτονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γειτονική, neuter γειτονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neighbouring (UK), neighboring (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γείτον(ας)) + -ικός.</div><b>γειτόνισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γειτόνισσες, masculine γείτονας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neighbour (UK), neighbor (US)</div><b>γελάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γελάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγελάδα">αγελάδα</a></div><b>γελάει καλύτερα όποιος γελάει τελευταίος</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>he who laughs last laughs best, he who laughs last laughs hardest (success is better after having previously endured ridicule)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French rira bien qui rira le dernier or of English he who laughs last laughs best.</div><b>γελάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to laugh</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν τον είδα να έρχεται, άρχισα να <b>γελώ</b>. - When I saw him coming, I started laughing.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to cheat, trick, deceive</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην τον εμπιστεύεσαι, θα σε <b>γελάσει</b>! - Don&#x27;t trust him, he&#x27;ll trick you!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> With ending -άω to γελώ in the pattern of 3rd person -άει. From Byzantine Greek γελῶ, from Ancient Greek γελῶ, contracted form of γελάω, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵelh₂-.</div><b>γελέκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γελέκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γιλέκο">γιλέκο</a></div><b>γελαδάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γελαδάρηδες, feminine γελαδάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cattle egret (bird - Bubulcus ibis)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://αγελαδάρης">αγελαδάρης</a></div><b>γελαδάρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γελαδάρισσες, masculine γελαδάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγελαδάρισσα">αγελαδάρισσα</a></div><b>γελαδινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γελαδινή, neuter γελαδινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγελαδινός">αγελαδινός</a></div><b>γελασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine γελασμένη, neuter γελασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mistaken, having miscalculated (also ironic)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είσαι <b>γελασμένος</b> άμα νομίζεις ότι θα το ανεχθεί αυτό. - You are mistaken if you think he&#x27;ll tolerate that.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of γελιέμαι, passive voice of γελάω/γελώ (“I laugh”).</div><b>γελαστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γελαστή, neuter γελαστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smiling, cheerful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γελαστός with shifted meaning.</div><b>γελοίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γελοία, neuter γελοίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ridiculous, ludicrous, farcical, laughable (something illogical, worthy of laughter or derision)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γελοῖος.</div><b>γελούν και οι κότες μαζί μου</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to be a laughing stock</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον καημένο. Έτσι που τον άφησε η γυναίκα του, <b>γελούν και οι κότες μαζί του</b>. - The poor guy. Since his wife left him, he&#x27;s been a laughing stock.</div><b>γελωτοποιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γελωτοποιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jester, fool, joker, buffoon, clown (person employed to amuse a court)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>γελωτοποιός</b> του βασιλιά - the king&#x27;s jester</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γελωτοποιός, from Ancient Greek γέλως.</div><b>γελώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γελάω">γελάω</a></div><b>γεμάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεμάτη, neuter γεμάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>full of, loaded with, plump</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γεμάτος, γεμᾶτος, from Ancient Greek γέμω + -τος (-tos).</div><b>γεμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fill, fill up</div>
  <div class='ex'>300 BCE – 200 BCE, Septuagint, Genesis 45.17</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to stuff</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek, Ancient Greek γεμίζω, from γέμω + -ίζω.</div><b>γεμενί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γεμενιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traditional headscarf (worn for warmth, cleanliness or fashion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish یمنی (modern Turkish yemeni), from Arabic يَمَنِي.</div><b>γεμιστά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (usually) gemista</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύο μερίδες <b>γεμιστά</b>, παρακαλώ. - Two portions of gemista, please.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. synonym of κολοκυθάκια <a href="bword://γεμιστά">γεμιστά</a>: a dish with courgettes stuffed with rice and/or minced meat</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (used adjectively) dish with stuffed ingredients such as vegetables (tomatoes, peppers, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter plural of γεμιστός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γεμιστός">γεμιστός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter plural of γεμιστός</div><b>γεμιστήρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεμιστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of γεμιστήρας</div><b>γεμιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεμιστή, neuter γεμιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. filled, full of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stuffed</div>
  <div class='ex'>συνταγές για κοτόπουλο <b>γεμιστό</b>’’ - recipes for stuffed chicken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γεμιστός, from Ancient Greek γεμίζω.</div><b>γεν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) yen (Japan&#x27;s monetary unit or coin) (symbol: ¥)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration in Greek, of English yen, from Japanese (see etymology).</div><b>γεν.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γενική">γενική</a>: genitive</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γενικά">γενικά</a>: general, in general</div><b>γενέθλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>birthday</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γενέθλια, the neuter plural of γενέθλῐος.</div><b>γενέσθαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, literary) to have been done.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek γενέσθαι infinitive used in Katharevousa.</div><b>γενίκευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενικεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>generalization</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γενικεύω + -ση (-si). Calque of French généralisation.</div><b>γενίτσαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γενίτσαροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>janissary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish یڭیچری, from یڭی + چری.</div><b>γενεά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενεές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γενιά">γενιά</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek γενεά.</div><b>γενεαλογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενεαλογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genealogy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek γενεαλογία.</div><b>γενειάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενειάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beard (especially a long one)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γενειάς in the accusative singular case γενειᾰ́δᾰ. Compare Mariupol Greek е́ня.</div><b>γενετική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενετικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genetics (molecular biology)</div><b>γενιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kin, ancestry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. generation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. generation; a generation&#x27;s actions in specific era or decade, mainly of artistic and political movements but not with <a href="bword://γενεά">γενεά</a>)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>γενιά</b> του μεσοπολέμου - the interwar generation</div>
  <div class='def'>4. see also expressions with <a href="bword://γενεά">γενεά</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γενεά.</div><b>γενικ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γενικότερα">γενικότερα</a>: generally, in general</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γενικά">γενικά</a>: general, in general</div><b>γενικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>generally, in general</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γενικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γενικός">γενικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γενικός.</div><b>γενική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(genitive) genitive</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γενική</b> πτώση - genitive case</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://γενικός">γενικός</a></div><b>γενικεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to generalize (to extend from specific cases to more general cases or principles)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γενικ(ός)) + -εύω, a calque of French généraliser.</div><b>γενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γενική, neuter γενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>general, unspecific</div><b>γενικότ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γενικότερα">γενικότερα</a>: generally, in general</div><b>γενικότερα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of γενικότερος, the comparative degree of <a href="bword://γενικός">γενικός</a></div><b>γενικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>generally, in general</div><b>γενν.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γέννηση">γέννηση</a>: birth</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of γεννήθηκε: born</div><b>γεννάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to give birth, beget</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From modern γενν(ώ) + -άω. Inherited from Ancient Greek γεννῶ, contracted form of γεννάω. Also see γένος, γενέτης, γόνος and the Proto-Indo-European root ǵenh₁.</div><b>γεννήθηκα στα πούπουλα</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>born with a silver spoon in one&#x27;s mouth</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε <b>γεννήθηκα στα πούπουλα</b>. - I wasn&#x27;t born with a silver spoon in my mouth.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;was born with feathers&quot;</div><b>γεννήτορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γεννήτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. genitor, parent, procreator, progenitor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. generator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γεννήτωρ, from γεννᾰ́ω + -τωρ, equivalent to Proto-Hellenic *génetōr from Proto-Indo-European *ǵénh₁tōr, from *ǵenh₁- (“to produce, to beget”) + *-tōr (agent noun). Compare Latin genitor.</div><b>γεννήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεννήτριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>generator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French génératrice (“generator”).</div><b>γενναία</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bravely</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://γενναίος">γενναίος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γενναίος">γενναίος</a></div><b>γενναίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γενναία, neuter γενναίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brave, gallant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. generous, lavish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γενναῖος with semantic loan from French généreux.</div><b>γενναιοδωρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενναιοδωρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) generosity (the trait of being willing to donate money, resources, or time)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (countable) generosity, generous act</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γενναιόδωρ(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>γενναιόδωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γενναιόδωρη, neuter γενναιόδωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>generous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γενναί(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + δώρ(ο)) + -ος (-os), a loose calque of French généreux.</div><b>γενναιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bravery, courage, fortitude</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek γενναιότης. By surface analysis, γενναί(ος)) + -ότητα.</div><b>γενναιόφρων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γενναιόφρων, neuter γενναιόφρον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>generous</div><b>γεννημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine γεννημένη, neuter γεννημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>born</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πότε είστε <b>γεννημένος</b>; - When were you born?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of γεννιέμαι, passive voice of γεννάω/γεννώ (“I give birth”).</div><b>γεννιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be born, originate</div><b>γεννώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γεννάω">γεννάω</a>: give birth, beget</div><b>γενοκτονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γενοκτονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genocide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γένος + -κτονία.</div><b>γερά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soundly, thoroughly, strongly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γερός">γερός</a></div><b>γερά ποτήρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of γερό <a href="bword://ποτήρι">ποτήρι</a></div><b>γεράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γεράκια, feminine γερακίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bird of prey, hawk, falcon, buzzard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) belligerent person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γεράκιν, from Koine Greek ἱεράκιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek ἱέραξ.</div><b>γεράματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>old age (latter part of life)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στα <b>γεράματα</b>, αποφάσισε να ταξιδέψει. - In her old age, she decided to travel.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γηράματα, plural of γήραμα, from Ancient Greek γηρῶ.</div><b>γερακάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γερακάρηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>falconer</div><b>γερακίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γερακίνες, masculine γεράκι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bird of prey, hawk, falcon, buzzard</div><b>γερανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γερανοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crane (bird)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crane, derrick</div>
  <div class='ex'>ανυψωτικός γερανός (lifting crane)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ladder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γέρανος.</div><b>γερασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine γερασμένη, neuter γερασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who has aged, who looks aged</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι σχετικά νέα, αλλά μοιάζει πολύ <b>γερασμένη</b>. - She is quite young, but she looks as though she has aged much</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of γερνάω, a verb without passive voice forms.</div><b>γερμ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γερμανικός">γερμανικός</a>: German, Germanic</div><b>γερμάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) germanium</div><b>γερμανικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>German (the language)</div><b>γερμανικά κλειδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of γερμανικό <a href="bword://κλειδί">κλειδί</a></div><b>γερμανικό κλειδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γερμανικά κλειδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>open-ended spanner (UK), open-ended wrench (US)</div><b>γερμανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γερμανική, neuter γερμανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>German, Germanic (related to the country, people or language of Germany)</div><b>γερνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to grow old, age</div><b>γερνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γερνάω">γερνάω</a></div><b>γεροκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γεροκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γηροκομείο">γηροκομείο</a></div><b>γεροντοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gerontocracy (government by elders)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γεροντο- (geronto-, “geronto-, old”) + -κρατία. First attested 1897.</div><b>γεροντοκόρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεροντοκόρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spinster, old maid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) older woman who acts strangely</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γεροντο- (geronto-, “old ...”) + κόρη</div><b>γεροντότερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεροντότερη, neuter γεροντότερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, comparative, irregular) older</div><b>γερουσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γερουσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>senate</div><b>γερουσιαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γερουσιαστές, feminine γερουσιαστίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>senator (member of a senate)</div><b>γερουσιαστίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γερουσιαστίνες, masculine γερουσιαστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>senator (member of a senate)</div><b>γερούνδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γερούνδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(gerund) gerund</div><b>γερό ποτήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γερά ποτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toper, boozer, bibber, drunk, inebriate</div><b>γερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γερή, neuter γερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of people) strong, sturdy, able-bodied, robust, strapping (fit and healthy)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν με νοιάζει αν είναι αγόρι ή κορίτσι, αρκεί να είναι <b>γερό</b> παιδί. - I don&#x27;t care if it&#x27;s a boy or a girl as long as it&#x27;s healthy.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) strong, sturdy, tough, solid, (capable of producing or withstanding great physical force)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σπίτι είναι <b>γερό</b> και θα αντέξει την καταιγίδα. - The house is sturdy and will withstand the storm.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. strong, able (impressive or good)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο επιστήμονας έχει <b>γερό</b> μυαλό. - The scientist has a strong mind.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of items) unbroken, intact (not having been destroyed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχει αφήσει <b>γερό</b> ποτήρι στο σπίτι! - She hasn&#x27;t left a cup intact in the house!</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of fruit and nuts) unspoiled, unspoilt (not rotten)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γερά</b> αμύγδαλα - unspoiled almonds</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γερός, from Ancient Greek ὑγιερός, from ὑγιής.</div><b>γευστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γευστική, neuter γευστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tasty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γευστικός, from γεῦσις + -τῐκός, from γεύομαι + -σις (-sis).</div><b>γεφύρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γεφύρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small bridge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bridge</div><b>γεωγρ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γεωγραφία">γεωγραφία</a>: geography</div><b>γεωγραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεωγραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γεωγραφία, from Ancient Greek γεωγράφος, from γεω- + γράφω.</div><b>γεωγραφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεωγραφική, neuter γεωγραφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geographical, geographic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γεωγραφικός. Morphologically, from γεωγραφ(ία) + -ικός.</div><b>γεωδαίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γεωδαίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geodesist, cartographic surveyor, land surveyor</div><b>γεωδαισία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) geodesy, geodetics</div><b>γεωδαιτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεωδαιτική, neuter γεωδαιτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geodetic, geodesic</div><b>γεωειδές</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γεωειδή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geoid</div><b>γεωθερμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεωθερμική, neuter γεωθερμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geothermal, geothermic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French géothermique. By surface analysis, γεωθερμ(ία)) + -ικός or γεω- (geo-) + θερμικός.</div><b>γεωκεντρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεωκεντρική, neuter γεωκεντρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geocentric</div><b>γεωλ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γεωλογία">γεωλογία</a>: geology</div><b>γεωλογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) geology</div><b>γεωλόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γεωλόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geologist</div><b>γεωμ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γεωμετρία">γεωμετρία</a>: geometry</div><b>γεωμαθηματικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geomathematics</div><b>γεωμετρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεωμετρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geometry</div><b>γεωπλήσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>near-Earth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γεω- (geo-, “geo-, Earth”) + πλησίον + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix), a calque of English near-Earth.</div><b>γεωπολιτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geopolitics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English geopolitics, from Swedish geopolitik. By surface analysis, γεω- (geo-) + πολιτική.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://γεωπολιτικός">γεωπολιτικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>γεωπολιτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεωπολιτική, neuter γεωπολιτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geopolitical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γεωπολιτ(ική)) + -ικός.</div><b>γεωπονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) agronomy</div><b>γεωπροωθητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γεωπροωθητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bulldozer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γεω- (geo-, “earth”) + προωθητής</div><b>γεωργ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γεωργία">γεωργία</a>: agriculture</div><b>γεωργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) agriculture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γεωργία.</div><b>γεωργιανά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Georgian (the Caucasian language of Georgia)</div><b>γεωργιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεωργιανή, neuter γεωργιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Georgian (relating to Georgia, its people or language)</div><b>γεωργικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γεωργική, neuter γεωργικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) agricultural (of or pertaining to agriculture)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γεωργικός. By surface analysis, γεωργ(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>γεωργοκτηνοτρόφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γεωργοκτηνοτρόφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>livestock farmer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γεωργός + κτηνοτρόφος</div><b>γεωργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γεωργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>farmer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γεωργός.</div><b>γεύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meal, especially lunch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γεῦμα, from γεύω.</div><b>γεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to taste (to sample the flavor of something orally)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to taste (to experience)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γεύομαι, mediopassive form of γεύω.</div><b>γεύση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. taste (sense)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. taste (sweet, sour, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) impression (left by someone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου άφησε πολύ άσχημη <b>γεύση</b>. - He left a very bad taste.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) impression (sampling of an experience)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήραμε μια <b>γεύση</b> της ελληνικής ζωής. - We got a taste of Greek life.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek γεῦσις.</div><b>γεώμηλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γεώμηλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(obsolete) potato (vegetable, plant or dish)</div><b>γεώτρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γεωτρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>borehole</div><b>γη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) world, earth (planet)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) world, earth (its people)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) earth, land, soil (in which plants grow)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (uncountable) land (as sighted by sailors)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (uncountable) land, country</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>Γη</b> της Επαγγελίας - the Promised Land</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γῆ / γαῖα, other details uncertain.</div><b>γηγενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γηγενής, neuter γηγενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>native; indigenous</div><b>γηπεδούχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γηπεδούχος or γηπεδούχα, neuter γηπεδούχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in plural, noun from verb) home team</div>
  <div class='def'>2. home-team (attributive), playing at home</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γήπεδ(ο)) + -ούχος.</div><b>γηροκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γηροκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nursing home, old people&#x27;s home (hospital and care facility for the elderly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γιαγιά ζει εδώ και τρία χρόνια στο <b>γηροκομείο</b>. - Granny has been living in a nursing home for the last three years.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γέρος + -κομείο.</div><b>γητεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be bewitched</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be enchanted, vernacular, poetic alternative form of <a href="bword://γοητεύομαι">γοητεύομαι</a></div><b>γητεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (vernacular) use magic to enchant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://γοητεύω">γοητεύω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γητεύω, from Ancient Greek γοητεύω. Also see γοητεύω.</div><b>γι</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://γι">γι</a>’ (gi’), contraction of <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia)</div><b>γι&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contraction of <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia, “for, about”)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γι’</b> αυτό το λόγο - for this reason</div><b>γιάντα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cretan dialect form of <a href="bword://γιατί">γιατί</a>”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>synonym of γιατί (giatí, “why?”) (Standard Modern Greek)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Combination of conjunction για (“for”) or διά + pronoun είντα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cretan dialect form of <a href="bword://γιατί">γιατί</a>”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>synonym of γιατί (giatí) &amp; διότι (dióti, “because”) (Standard Modern Greek)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Combination of conjunction για (“for”) or διά + pronoun είντα.</div><b>γιάρδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιάρδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. yard (3 feet)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shipyard</div><b>γιάτρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιάτρισσες, masculine γιατρός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) (female) doctor</div><b>για</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with accusative) for</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>για</b> στάσου - wait a minute</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (reason, with accusative) for</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φυλακίστηκε <b>για</b> κλοπή. - He was imprisoned for theft.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. for, by</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός ο δρόμος δεν είναι <b>για</b> μηχανάκια. - This road is not for motorcycles.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (genitive, with accusative) for</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>για</b> σένα - for you</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (with accusative) about</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου μίλησε <b>για</b> ό,τι συνέβη. - He told me about what happened.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek διά. Doublet of διά.</div><b>για καλά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for good, once and for all</div><b>για πέταμα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia), <a href="bword://πέταμα">πέταμα</a>.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic, indeclinable) useless, awful, crappy, rubbish (of bad quality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>για πέταμα</b> αυτά τα παπούτσια. - These shoes are rubbish.</div><b>για σπίτι</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia), <a href="bword://σπίτι">σπίτι</a>.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η πολυθρόνα είναι <b>για σπίτι</b> πλούσιο. - This armchair is for a rich home.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of people) marriageable, nubile (suitable to marry and set up a home with)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι πας μαζί μ’ αυτήν; Δεν είναι κοπέλα <b>για σπίτι</b>. - What are you going out with her for? She&#x27;s not a girl you&#x27;d want to marry.</div><b>για τα καλά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for good, once and for all</div><b>για ψύλλου πήδημα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for any reason, over the slightest thing (for some insignificant cause or pretext)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “for a flea&#x27;s leap”.</div><b>για όνομα του Θεού</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. for God&#x27;s sake, for Christ&#x27;s sake, for heaven&#x27;s sake (exclamation of anger, surprise, or impatience)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταματά να παίζεις μουσική στις τρεις το πρωί, <b>για όνομα του Θεού</b>! - Stop playing music at three in the morning, for God&#x27;s sake!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. for God&#x27;s sake, for Christ&#x27;s sake, for heaven&#x27;s sake, in the name of God (exclamation of despair and begging)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Για όνομα του Θεού</b>, λυπήσου με! - In the name of God, pity me!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “in God&#x27;s name”.</div><b>γιαβρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιαβριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A term of endearment, most commonly used in the possessive combination <a href="bword://γιαβρί">γιαβρί</a> <a href="bword://μου">μου</a>.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish یاورو (yavru).</div><b>γιαγιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιαγιάδες, masculine παππούς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grandmother, grandma, granny, nan, nanna (mother of one&#x27;s parent)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>γιαγιά</b> μου ήταν πάντα η καλύτερη μαγείρισσα. - My grandmother was always the best cook.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) granny, grandma (to address or refer to any old woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θες βοήθεια να διασχίσεις το δρόμο, <b>γιαγιά</b>; - Do you need help to cross the street, grandma?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nursery word, compare μαμά, μπαμπάς, νταντά, and νενέ.</div><b>γιαείντα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot dialect form of <a href="bword://γιατί">γιατί</a>”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>synonym of γιατί (giatí, “why?”) (Standard Modern Greek)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Combination of conjunction για (“for”) + pronoun είντα.</div><b>γιακάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γιακάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. collar (part of garment)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lapel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish یاقه (yaka).</div><b>γιαλός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γιαλοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(usually) seashore, beach</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γιαλός, from Ancient Greek αἰγιαλός.</div><b>γιαούρτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιαούρτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yogurt, yoghurt, yogourt (Canada)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Aromanian yaourti, from
Ottoman Turkish یوغورت.</div><b>γιαπί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιαπιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>building site, construction site (unfinished building)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ήλιος χρυσίζει, χέρια σπαρμένα,
Βουνά και <b>γιαπιά</b>, πελάγη απλωμένα. - The sun shines golden, hands spread wide,
Mountains and building sites, wide open seas.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish یاپی.</div><b>γιαπωνέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γιαπωνέζικη, neuter γιαπωνέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ιαπωνικός">ιαπωνικός</a></div><b>γιασεμί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιασεμιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jasmine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Middle Persian yʾsmn&#x27;
Classical Persian یَاسَمِینbor.
Ottoman Turkish یاسمینbor.
Greek γιασεμί
Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish یاسمین (yasemin). Compare Koine Greek ἰάσμη.</div><b>γιατάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιατάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, dated) bunk, bunk bed, berth</div>
  <div class='ex'>Απάνω στο <b>γιατάκι</b> σου φίδι νωθρό κοιμάται,
Και φέρνει βόλτες ψάχνοντας τα ρούχα σου η μαϊμού. - On your bunk bed, a sluggish snake is sleeping,
And the monkey goes about, searching through your clothes.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, dated) cabin (interior of a boat, enclosed to create a small room)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish یتاق (yatak, “bed”).</div><b>γιατί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(interrogative) why</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γιατί</b> είσαι εδώ; - Why are you here?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From για (gia, “for”) + τί. Compare similar constructions in the Romance languages: Italian perché, Spanish por qué, French pourquoi.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>because</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν πήγα, <b>γιατί</b> είμαι άρρωστος. - I did not go, because I was sick.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From για (gia, “for”) + τί. Compare similar constructions in the Romance languages: Italian perché, Spanish por qué, French pourquoi.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) why, the reason why</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω να μάθω το <b>γιατί</b>. - I want to discover why.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From για (gia, “for”) + τί. Compare similar constructions in the Romance languages: Italian perché, Spanish por qué, French pourquoi.</div><b>γιατρέσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιατρέσες, masculine γιατρός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) (female) doctor</div><b>γιατρίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιατρίνες, masculine γιατρός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. doctor (female)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (obsolete) doctor&#x27;s wife</div><b>γιατρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιατρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A less common form of <a href="bword://ιατρείο">ιατρείο</a></div><b>γιατρειά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιατρειές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) therapy, remedy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from mediaeval Byzantine Greek γιατρειά, from Ancient Greek ἰατρεία.</div><b>γιατρεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be cured</div><b>γιατρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cure, heal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) heal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γιατρεύω, from Ancient Greek ἰατρεύω with [ia] &gt; [ʝa].</div><b>γιατρικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιατρικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ιατρικό">ιατρικό</a></div><b>γιατρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γιατροί, feminine γιάτρισσα or γιατρίνα or γιατρέσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doctor, healer.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γιατρός, from Ancient Greek ἰατρός. Doublet of ιατρός.</div><b>γιγάκυκλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γιγάκυκλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gigacycle</div><b>γιγάντισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιγάντισσες, masculine γίγαντας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>giantess</div><b>γιγαβάτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) gigawatt</div><b>γιγαχέρτζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) gigahertz</div><b>γιδοβοσκός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γιδοβοσκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goatherd</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γιδο- (gido-, “goat”) + βοσκός</div><b>γιδοπρόβατα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sheep and goats collectively</div><b>γιδόδερμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιδοδέρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kidskin, kid</div><b>γιεν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://γεν">γεν</a>: &quot;yen&quot; (symbol: ¥)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Phonetic spelling of English yen, from Japanese (see etymology).</div><b>γιλέκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιλέκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>waistcoat, vest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish یلك (yelek).</div><b>γινάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γινάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (often) stubbornness, obstinacy, pigheadedness (state of refusing to move or to change one&#x27;s opinion)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άσε τα <b>γινάτια</b> και καν’ το! - Stop being pigheaded and do it!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spite (ill will or hatred toward another)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το έκανε αυτό μόνο και μόνο για το <b>γινάτι</b> μου. - He did that purely to spite me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish عناد (inad, inat, “stubbornness, spite”) from Arabic عِنَاد.</div><b>γιογιό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (childish, colloquial, indeclinable) potty (for babies and toddlers to toilet train)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μωρό έκανε κακά στο <b>γιογιό</b>. - The baby did a poo in the potty.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) yo-yo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Imitative of babbling sounds produced by small children.</div><b>γιορτάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be celebrated</div><b>γιορτάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to celebrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to Greek εορτάζω; see there for more.</div><b>γιορτή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιορτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. celebration</div>
  <div class='def'>2. feast day, holy day</div>
  <div class='def'>3. holiday</div>
  <div class='def'>4. name day</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (in plural) period between Christmas and Twelfth Night</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from Ancient Greek ἑορτή.</div><b>γιορτασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γιορτασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>celebration</div><b>γιορτινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γιορτινή, neuter γιορτινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>festive, celebratory</div><b>γιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>son, male child</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γιός &lt; ὑγιός with deletion of the unstressed vowel, from Ancient Greek υἱός (see its Koine pronunciation).</div><b>γιοτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) yacht</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) Yot, the uncommon Greek letter ϳ.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English yacht.</div><b>γιοττάμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιοττάμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yottametre</div><b>γιουβέτσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιουβέτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an earthenware cooking pot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. giouvetsi (a stew cooked in such a pot)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كوكج.</div><b>γιουβαρλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιουβαρλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a meatball of ground meat and rice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Turkish yuvarlak (“round”)</div><b>γιουβαρλάκια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a dish of meatballs served in avgolemono sauce.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Plural of γιουβαρλάκι</div><b>γιουχαΐζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive, transitive) to shout down, boo, jeer (to insult someone by whistling and making loud noises and not letting them speak)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν δε ντρέπεσαι να καθίσεις πίσω
Έλα Ηπείρου κι Αχαρνών να σε γιουχαΐσω - If you&#x27;re not ashamed to sit behind,
Come to Iperus and Aharnes (street junction) so I can jeer you.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish یوها, یوخا (yuha, “to boo, to shout with derision or mockery”)(Turkish yuh, yuf, yuha) + verb ending -ίζω.</div><b>γιοφύρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γιοφύρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γεφύρι">γεφύρι</a></div><b>γιούδας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) police officer, snitch</div><b>γιούφτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γιούφτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, slang) gypsy</div><b>γιρλάντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γιρλάντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>garland</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian ghirlanda.</div><b>γιωτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γιοτ">γιοτ</a> (giot)</div><b>γιώτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) iota; the ninth letter of the Greek alphabet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἰῶτα, from the same Semitic source as English yodh, namely Proto-Semitic *yad- (“hand”).</div><b>γκάζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γκάζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gas</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accelerator</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) reprimand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French gaz (“gas”).</div><b>γκάιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκάιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκάιντα">γκάιντα</a></div><b>γκάιντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκάιντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bagpipes, gaida</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish غایده (gayda, “bagpipes”), from Bulgarian гайда (gajda). Akin to Macedonian гајда (gajda, “bagpipes”), Serbo-Croatian gajde (“bagpipes”), Czech gajdy and Slovak gajdy.</div><b>γκάσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) shoes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic جزمة (gazma).</div><b>γκάστρωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γκαστρώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, informal) knocking up (act of impregnation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>γκάστρωμα</b> της σκύλας - the impregnation of a bitch (female dog)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γκαστρώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>γκέι</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) gay (of, or concerning homosexuals)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γκέι</b> κουλτούρα - gay culture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English gay.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) gay man</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English gay.</div><b>γκέισα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκέισες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geisha</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Through English geisha, from Japanese 芸者(げいしゃ) (geisha).</div><b>γκέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bounce</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dice roll that makes a player skip a turn</div>
  <div class='def'>3. failure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish [Term?], whence Turkish gele (“dice roll that makes a player skip a turn in backgammon”), ultimately akin to gelmek (“to come”).</div><b>γκίμπνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot, indeclinable) cheese</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic جبنه (gibna).</div><b>γκαζολίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncommon, uncountable) gasoline (US), petrol (UK)</div><b>γκαζόζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκαζόζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carbonated beverage, especially soda water and lemonade</div><b>γκαζόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lawn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French gazon (“lawn”).</div><b>γκαλερί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) art gallery (not usually a public gallery)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French galerie.</div><b>γκαμπιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γκαμπιανή, neuter γκαμπιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Gambian, relating to Gambia.</div><b>γκαμπονέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γκαμπονέζικη, neuter γκαμπονέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Gabonese (of or pertaining to Gabon or its people)</div><b>γκανέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γκανέζικη, neuter γκανέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Ghanaian, Ghanan</div><b>γκαντόφλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκαντόφλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) clam</div><b>γκαράζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) garage (place for storing cars; automobile repair workshop)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French garage.</div><b>γκαράμ μασάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) garam masala</div><b>γκαρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to bray</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to shout, to speak very loudly, especially out of anger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek (ὀ)γκαρίζω ((o)nkarízō) from Hellenistic Koine Greek ὀγκαρίζω, probably a reborrowing from Latin oncare from Ancient Greek ὀγκάομαι / ὀγκῶμαι.</div><b>γκαρσονιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκαρσονιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. single room apartment, flatlet (often used for sexual encounters)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. servant&#x27;s quarters</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French garçonnière.</div><b>γκαρσόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable, feminine γκαρσόνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκαρσόνι">γκαρσόνι</a></div><b>γκαρσόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκαρσόνες, masculine γκαρσόνι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female waiter, waitress</div><b>γκαρσόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γκαρσόνια, feminine γκαρσόνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>male waiter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French garçon.</div><b>γκαστρωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine γκαστρωμένη, neuter γκαστρωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal) (mainly for animals, also for women): impregnated</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γίδα είναι <b>γκαστρωμένη</b>. - The goat is impregnated.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, informal) preggers, knocked up, up the duff, in a family way (pregnant)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είδες την κόρη τους; Μόνο τρεις μήνες παντρεμένοι και ήδη <b>γκαστρωμένη</b>! - Did you see their daughter? Only three months married and already up the duff!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) extremely annoyed</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) impregnating events, developments.</div>
  <div class='ex'>«Η κάλπη είναι <b>γκαστρωμένη</b>». - “The ballot box is pregnant”.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of γκαστρώνομαι, passive voice of γκαστρώνω (“Ι impregnate”).</div><b>γκαστρώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, informal) to be knocked up (be made pregnant)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γκαστρώθηκε</b> στα 16 και έπρεπε να βάλει το μωρό της για υιοθεσία. - She got pregnant at 16 and had to put her baby up for adoption.</div><b>γκαστρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, informal, transitive) to knock up (impregnate a woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την <b>γκάστρωσε</b> και μετά την παράτησε. - He knocked her up and then left her.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive) to pester, beleaguer, bug (annoy someone excessively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας <b>γκάστρωσες</b> με τις ερωτήσεις σου! - You&#x27;ve bugged the hell out of us with your questions!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐγγαστρώνω, from Koine Greek ἐγγάστριος from ἐν (en, “in, on, at”) + γᾰστήρ.</div><b>γκεσέμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γκεσέμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) ram or billy goat leading the herd</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Alteration of κεσέμι, changing first sound /k/ to /ɡ/, that from Ottoman Turkish كوسم.</div><b>γκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of γκιό</div><b>γκιλοτίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκιλοτίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guillotine (for capital punishment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French guillotine, after Dr Joseph-Ignace Guillotin.</div><b>γκιουρουλτί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, dated, dialectal, indeclinable) tumult, noise</div>
  <div class='ex'>όσα φημίζονται είναι ψεύματα και κινήματα του Αλή Πασσά δια να φέρει <b>γκιουρουλτί</b> (ταραχήν) - all that is rumoured are lies and conspiracies by Ali Pasha as to cause tumult (disorder)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish گورلدی. See Turkish gürültü for further etymology. Cognate with Cappadocian Greek χαριλτού and Pontic Greek κιουρτιτή f.</div><b>γκιού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of γκιό</div><b>γκισάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γκισάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pochard</div><b>γκισέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) counter, window (for information, tickets, transactions, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γκισέ</b> πληροφοριών - information counter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French guichet.</div><b>γκιόνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γκιόνηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Eurasian scops owl (Otus scops)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian gjon.</div><b>γκιών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of γκιό</div><b>γκλάσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γκλάσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γλάσο">γλάσο</a></div><b>γκλίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκλίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shepherd&#x27;s crook</div><b>γκλομπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) truncheon (UK), baton (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English club.</div><b>γκλοπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκλομπ">γκλομπ</a> (gklomp)</div><b>γκοζιόκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γκοζιόκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short coat with pleats in the back</div>
  <div class='ex'>τζουμπέδες καὶ φουστάνια <b>γκοζιόκαις</b> ταὶς χρυσαὶς</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Proto-Slavic *kožuxъ. Compare Aromanian cujuc, cojoc, Ottoman Turkish غوجوق (gocuk).</div><b>γκολ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) goal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English goal.</div><b>γκολφ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) golf</div><b>γκονγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) gong (percussion instrument)</div><b>γκουβέρνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γκουβέρνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, ironic) the government (nowadays chiefly derisive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γκουβέρνο from Old Italian guberno, from Latin gubernum or an αντιδάνειο borrowed from Italian governo m, from Latin gubernum n, from Latin gubernō (verb), from Ancient Greek κυβερνῶ.</div><b>γκουγκλάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, neologism, slang) to google (search for on the Internet)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>γκούγκλαρα</b> για να μάθω περισσότερα. - I googled him to find out more.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English google, + -άρω.</div><b>γκουγκλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, neologism, slang) to google (search for on the Internet)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την <b>γκούγκλισα</b> για να μάθω περισσότερα. - I googled her to find out more.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English google, + -ίζω.</div><b>γκουρού</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) guru</div><b>γκρέιπ φρουτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκρέιπφρουτ">γκρέιπφρουτ</a></div><b>γκρέιπ-φρουτ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκρέιπφρουτ">γκρέιπφρουτ</a></div><b>γκρέιπφρουτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) grapefruit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English grapefruit.</div><b>γκρέμισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demolition</div><b>γκρέυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) gray (the derived SI unit of absorbed dose)</div><b>γκρέϊπφρουτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://γκρέιπφρουτ">γκρέιπφρουτ</a></div><b>γκρίζος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γκρίζα, neuter γκρίζο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grey (UK), gray (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan grixo (“grey”), derived through the medium of Old French or Occitan from Frankish *grīs, from Proto-Germanic *grīsaz (“grey”).</div><b>γκρίνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκρίνιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. moaning, complaining, querulousness (expression of feelings of pain, dissatisfaction, or resentment)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κόψε την <b>γκρίνια</b> κι άρχισε να απολαμβάνεις την ζωή σου! - Quit moaning and start enjoying your life!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of babies and infants) crying, moaning, wailing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>γκρίνια</b> του μωρού μας κράτησε άγρυπνους όλη νύχτα. - Our baby&#x27;s wailing kept us up all night.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γκρίνια, a borrowing from Italian grigna, from French grigner.</div><b>γκρανκάσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bass drum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (offensive) An elderly and usually obese woman.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian grancassa.</div><b>γκρελίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκρελίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ghrelin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English ghrelin.</div><b>γκρεμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to demolish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to throw something from a great height</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κρημνίζω, with simplification [mn &gt; m].</div><b>γκρεμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γκρεμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cliff, precipice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) destruction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γκρεμνός with [mn] &gt; [m] -also as the in medieval form ἐγκρεμός-, from Ancient Greek κρημνός with pronunciation of accusative with liaison: τὸν κρημνόν. Compare to γκρεμνός and κρημνός.</div><b>γκρι</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) grey (UK, CAN), gray (US) (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French gris (“grey”).</div><b>γκριζάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to grey (UK), gray (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα μαλλιά του άρχισαν να <b>γκριζάρουν</b>. - His hair began to turn grey.</div><b>γκριζομάλλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γκριζομάλλα or γκριζομαλλούσα, neuter γκριζομάλλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grey-haired (UK), gray-haired (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γκριζο- (gkrizo-) + -μάλλης</div><b>γκριμπός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γκριμπή, neuter γκριμπό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>humpbacked, hunchbacked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Aromanian gribos (“humpbacked”).</div><b>γκρινιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to grumble, complain, moan, whine (express feelings of pain, dissatisfaction, or resentment)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός ο άντρας όλο <b>γκρινιάζει</b> και δε λέει ποτέ κάτι θετικό. - That man is always complaining and never says anything positive.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of babies and infants) to cry, moan, wail</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μωρό τους πάντα <b>γκρινιάζει</b> αργά το βράδυ όταν θέλουμε να κοιμηθούμε. - Their baby is always wailing late at night when we&#x27;re trying to sleep.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γκρινιάζω, γρινιάζω. Either from γκρίνια + -άζω (but possibly vice versa, a back-formation of γκρίνια from the verb). Or from dialectal Italian grignare (“grind teeth”) (contemporary Italian digrignare), from the Frankish *grīnan (“to grin”). Also, possible influence from the mediaeval synonym γρυνίζω, associated to the ancient γρυλίζω.</div><b>γκροτέσκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γκροτέσκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grotesque</div><b>γκρουμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) hotel porter (UKL)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) bellboy, bellhop (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English groom.</div><b>γκρουπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) group (small collection, especially of people)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) band (of musicians)</div><b>γκόμενα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γκόμενες, masculine γκόμενος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, familiar) girlfriend (female partner in an unmarried romantic relationship)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ασπασία είναι η <b>γκόμενα</b> του Μάκη. - Aspasia is Makis&#x27; girlfriend.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) babe, hottie, doll (attractive young woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα εκεί μια <b>γκόμενα</b>, πω πω πω. - Look at that babe over there, wow.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian gommeno, from French gommeux (“dandy”).</div><b>γκόμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γκόμενοι, feminine γκόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, familiar) boyfriend (male partner in an unmarried romantic relationship)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Περικλής είναι ο <b>γκόμενος</b> της Τούλας. - Pericles is Toula&#x27;s boyfriend.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) hunk, beefcake (attractive young man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχουν πολλοί <b>γκόμενοι</b> στην παραλία σήμερα. - There are a lot of hunks on the beach today.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian gommeno, from French gommeux (“dandy”).</div><b>γλάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γλάροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gull, seagull</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λάρος.</div><b>γλάσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλάσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cake coating:; icing (UK)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cake coating:; frosting (US)</div><b>γλάστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλάστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pot, flowerpot (container in which plants are grown)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη μια <b>γλάστρα</b> έβαλα μαργαρίτες και στην άλλη γεράνι. - I put daisies in one flowerpot and a geranium in the other.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively, humorous) decorative showgirl (woman who appears on television shows or public events who is chosen only for beauty and to stand silently and/or display prizes, results, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στα τηλεπαιχνίδια βάζουν πάντα μερικές <b>γλάστρες</b> για να ανεβάσουν τη θεαματικότητα. - On game shows they always have a few showgirls to raise the views.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γάστρα, with epenthesis of an λ (l).</div><b>γλέντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλέντια, diminutive γλεντάκι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>party, carousal, celebration, revelry, shindig, spree (festive social gathering with drinking)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish اكلنتی, with removal of initial vowel and stress placement on -έν- producing a noun-verb pair γλέντι / γλεντώ that mirrors κυνήγι / κυνηγώ.</div><b>γλήγορα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cretan dialect form of <a href="bword://γρήγορα">γρήγορα</a></div><b>γλήορα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot dialect form of <a href="bword://γρήγορα">γρήγορα</a></div><b>γλακώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to run and catch up</div><b>γλαροπούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλαροπούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seabird</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gull, seagull</div><b>γλαύκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλαύκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>owl</div>
  <div class='ex'>κομίζω <b>γλαύκα</b> εις Αθήνας - take coals to Newcastle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γλαῦξ.</div><b>γλαύκωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλαυκώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glaucoma</div><b>γλείφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to lick one&#x27;s lips</div><b>γλείφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lick (with the tongue)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to suck up to, flatter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Cognate with Mariupol Greek глы́фту.</div><b>γλειφιτζούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλειφιτζούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lollipop (confectionery on a stick)</div><b>γλειφομούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλειφομούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) muff diving, pussy eating, carpet munching, lip service (oral stimulation of the vulva)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο φίλος μου μού έκανε ένα πολύ ωραίο <b>γλειφομούνι</b> και σαν ανταμοιβή τον τσιμπούκωσα. - My boyfriend did a good job eating me out (lit.: did me a good muff diving), and as a reward, I sucked him off.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compound of γλείφω + μουνί.</div><b>γλεντάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to celebrate, revel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to enjoy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to fritter away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derived from Ottoman Turkish اكلنمك.</div><b>γλεντοκοπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to have fun</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to feast</div><b>γλεντοκοπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γλεντάω">γλεντάω</a></div><b>γλεντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γλεντάω">γλεντάω</a></div><b>γλεύκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλεύκη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>must, new wine</div>
  <div class='ex'>προσδιορισμός σακχάρων σε <b>γλεύκη</b> - measurement of sugar content in new wines</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γλεῦκος.</div><b>γλιστράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to make something to slip, glide</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to slip, to glide</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of snakes) to slither</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to be slippery: as impersonal 3rd person γλιστράει</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γλιστρ(ώ)) + -άω, inherited from Byzantine Greek γλιστρῶ from ἐγλιστρῶ, itself from Koine Greek ἐκλιστρῶ, contracted form of ἐκλιστράω with muted initial vowel ἐ).</div><b>γλιστρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλιστρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common purslane</div><b>γλιστρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a less common variant of <a href="bword://γλιστράω">γλιστράω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γλιστρῶ and see: γλιστράω.</div><b>γλιτώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to get away from, avoid (danger or peril)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γυναίκα <b>γλίτωσε</b> από τον άντρα της που την έδερνε. - The woman got away from her husband who beat her.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to survive</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κανείς δε <b>γλίτωσε</b> από το δυστύχημα. - Nobody survived the accident.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to save (money or time)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άμα πας έτσι, θα <b>γλιτώσεις</b> χρόνο. - If you go that way, you&#x27;ll save time.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to save (from danger or peril)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τη <b>γλίτωσε</b> από τη θάλασσα - He saved her from the sea.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γλυτώνω with simplification of ⟨υ⟩ to ⟨ι⟩, from the Ancient Greek ἐκλυτώνω, from ἔκλυτος. The popular simplification of the etymological spelling γλυτώνω may be considered as a mistake.</div><b>γλουτένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gluten (a vegetable protein)</div><b>γλουταμίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) glutamine (amino acid)</div><b>γλουταμικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) glutamic acid</div><b>γλουτός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γλουτοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>buttock, rump, gluteus maximus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek γλουτός</div><b>γλυκά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweetly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γλυκ(ός), “sweet; gentle”) + -ά</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γλυκός">γλυκός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://γλυκό">γλυκό</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>γλυκά καλαμπόκια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://γλυκό">γλυκό</a> <a href="bword://καλαμπόκι">καλαμπόκι</a></div><b>γλυκάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλυκάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated, euphemistic) vinegar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sweetbread</div><b>γλυκάνισος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γλυκάνισοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of γλυκάνισο</div><b>γλυκίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) glycine (amino acid)</div><b>γλυκίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural γλυκιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) glycation</div><b>γλυκαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sweeten, to sweeten up (to make sweet to the taste)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sweeten (to make less painful or laborious; to relieve)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to sweeten (to make mild or kind; to soften)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to entice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γλυκαίνω. By surface analysis, γλυκ(ός)) + -αίνω.</div><b>γλυκερίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) glycerol, glycerine</div><b>γλυκογόνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλυκογόνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glycogen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French glycogène.</div><b>γλυκολέμονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλυκολέμονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sweet lime, sweet lemon</div><b>γλυκοπατάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλυκοπατάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sweet potato</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γλυκο- (glyko-, “sweet”) + πατάτα, calque of English sweet potato.</div><b>γλυκό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλυκά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweet, pudding, dessert</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pastry, cake, confection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See γλυκός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://γλυκός">γλυκός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://γλυκός">γλυκός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See γλυκός.</div><b>γλυκό καλαμπόκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλυκά καλαμπόκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sweet corn</div><b>γλυκό νερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fresh water, sweet water</div><b>γλυκό του κουταλιού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλυκά του κουταλιού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A spoon sweet; a small confection served on a spoon as a gesture of hospitality.</div><b>γλυκόζη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) glucose, dextrose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French glycose (“glucose”).</div><b>γλυκόριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλυκόριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>liquorice (UK), licorice (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γλυκύρριζα, from γλυκύς + ῥίζα.</div><b>γλυκόρριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλυκόρριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γλυκόριζα">γλυκόριζα</a></div><b>γλυκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γλυκιά, neuter γλυκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweet in taste</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not brackish, not salted, fresh water (when qualifying water)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γλυκό</b> νερό - fresh water</div>
  <div class='def'>3. not salted (of foods which may be salted or not like bread, butter, almond)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γλυκό</b> βούτυρο, όχι αλατισμένο - sweet butter, not salted</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) pleasant, mild, calm, soft</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γλυκιά</b> βραδιά - <b>γλυκό</b> αεράκι - <b>γλυκιά</b> φωνή - <b>γλυκό</b> φιλί - sweet night - soft breeze - sweet voice - sweet kiss</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of persons) sweet (beloved, darling)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γλυκιά</b> μου αγάπη, αγάπη μου <b>γλυκιά</b>! - my sweet love!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the medieval γλυκός, from Ancient Greek γλυκύς with metaplasm of ending as in adjectives in -ός. Compare to the formal modern usage of γλυκύς.</div><b>γλυκύριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλυκύριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γλυκόριζα">γλυκόριζα</a></div><b>γλυκύρριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλυκύρριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γλυκόριζα">γλυκόριζα</a></div><b>γλυκύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γλυκιά, neuter γλυκύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) used only in the term <a href="bword://γλυκύς">γλυκύς</a> <a href="bword://βραστός">βραστός</a> (of coffee)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. formal variant of <a href="bword://γλυκός">γλυκός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γλυκύς. Compare to γλυκός.</div><b>γλυοξαλάση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) glyoxalase</div><b>γλυπτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλυπτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sculpture</div><b>γλυπτοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλυπτοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glyptotheque</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French glyptothèque. By surface analysis, γλυπτό + -θήκη, literally “sculptureplace”.</div><b>γλυπτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλυπτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sculpture</div><b>γλυτώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γλιτώνω">γλιτώνω</a> (etymological spelling)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See γλιτώνω.</div><b>γλυφός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γλυφή, neuter γλυφό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>somewhat salty, brackish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek γλυφός, βλυχός, γλυχός</div><b>γλωσσ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abbreviation of <a href="bword://γλωσσολογία">γλωσσολογία</a>: linguistics</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abbreviation of <a href="bword://γλώσσα">γλώσσα</a>: language</div><b>γλωσσικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γλωσσική, neuter γλωσσικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. linguistic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. glossal, relating to the tongue</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lingual</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γλώσσα + -ικός.</div><b>γλωσσοδέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γλωσσοδέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tongue-twister</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γλωσσοδέτης</b>: Μια πάπια, μα ποια πάπια; Μια πάπια με παπιά. - Tongue-twister: A duck, but which duck? A duck with ducklings.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tongue-tie, ankyloglossia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γλωσσο- (glosso-, “tongue”) + δένω.</div><b>γλωσσολαλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γλωσσολαλιά">γλωσσολαλιά</a></div><b>γλωσσολαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) glossolalia, speaking in tongues, speaking with tongues</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γλωσσο- (glosso-, “language”) + λαλιά, reborrowing from English glossolalia</div><b>γλωσσολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) linguistics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γλωσσο- (glosso-, “tongue, language”) + -λογία, a calque of French linguistique. Different meaning for the mediaeval or Hellenistic γλωσσολογία.</div><b>γλωσσολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γλωσσολογική, neuter γλωσσολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistic, linguistical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γλωσσολογ(ία)) + -ικός, a calque of French linguistique.</div><b>γλωσσολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γλωσσολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γλωσσολογία f.</div><b>γλωσσομαθής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γλωσσομαθής, neuter γλωσσομαθές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a person) multilingual (able to communicate in a number of languages)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γλωσσομαθής</b> νομικός - lawyer-linguist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γλωσσο- (glosso-) + -μαθής.</div><b>γλύκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλύκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweetness (the condition of being sweet)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) sweetness (the quality of giving pleasure to the mind or senses; pleasantness, agreeableness)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (endearing) sweetness, sweet (term of address)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γλύκα. By surface analysis, γλυκός + -α (-a).</div><b>γλύκισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γλυκίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pastry, cake, confection</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sweet, pudding, dessert</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek γλύκυσμα with spelling according to the -ισμα (-isma) neuters. Babiniotis proposes an etymological spelling with upsilon.</div><b>γλύπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γλύπτες, feminine γλύπτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sculptor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek γλύπτης</div><b>γλύπτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλύπτριες, masculine γλύπτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sculptor</div><b>γλώσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλώσσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tongue (muscular organ in the mouth used to eat and speak)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρόσεξε μη δαγκώσεις κατά λάθος τη <b>γλώσσα</b> σου. - Be careful not to accidentally bite your tongue.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of a shoe) Figurative senses.; tongue (the flap of material that goes between the laces and the foot)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>γλώσσα</b> του παπουτσιού - the tongue of the shoe</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) Figurative senses.; tongue (an individual point of flame from a fire)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πύρινες <b>γλώσσες</b> φωτιάς - fiery tongues of fire</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) Figurative senses.; tongue (a long, narrow strip of land, projecting from the mainland into a sea or lake)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γλώσσα</b> στεριάς που προχωρεί στη θάλασσα - a tongue of land which projects into the sea</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (metonymically) language, tongue (words and methods of combining them understood by a community and used as a form of communication)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ελληνική είναι πλούσια <b>γλώσσα</b>. - Greek is a rich language.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. sole (Solea solea, a flatfish of the family Soleidae)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φάγαμε χθες <b>γλώσσα</b> για βραδινό. - We ate sole yesterday for supper.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γλῶσσα. Cognate with Mariupol Greek гло́са and Tsakonian γρούσσα.</div><b>γλώσσα προγραμματισμού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γλώσσες προγραμματισμού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>programming language</div><b>γλώσσες προγραμματισμού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://γλώσσα">γλώσσα</a> προγραμματισμού</div><b>γνάθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γνάθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jawbone (mandible or maxilla)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γνάθος.</div><b>γνέθω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to spin (wool, cotton, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γνέθω, from νέω + νήθω.</div><b>γνοιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://νοιάζομαι">νοιάζομαι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See νοιάζομαι.</div><b>γνωμικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γνωμικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saying, adage (short phrase expressing a basic truth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek γνωμικόν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of γνωμικός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>γνωρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to meet, get to know each other</div><b>γνωρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to know (something), be aware, recognize</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σὲ γνωρίζω ἀπὸ τὴν κόψι
τοῦ σπαθιοῦ τὴν τρομερή,
σὲ γνωρίζω ἀπὸ τὴν ὄψι
ποὺ μὲ βία μετράει τὴν γῆ.
Se gnorízo apó tin kópsi
tou spathioú tin tromerí,
se gnorízo apó tin ópsi
pou me vía metráei tin gi.
I know you from the blade
of the fearsome sword,
I know you from the face
which with haste gazes the land
Dionysios Solomos, Hymn to Liberty (1823), 1st stanza</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to know (someone); get to know, meet</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to introduce, make acquaintance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γνωρίζω.</div><b>γνωριμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γνωριμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acquaintance, a familiar (a person one knows)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. familiarity, acquaintance, acquainted with knowledge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γνώριμος + -ία.</div><b>γνωστικισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Gnosticism</div><b>γνωστοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γνωστοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>announcement (the act and the result)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γνωστοποιώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>γνωστοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to make known, to announce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γνωστ(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ, with semantic loan from German bekanntmachen.</div><b>γνωστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γνωστή, neuter γνωστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>known, familiar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γνωστός, from γιγνώσκω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γνωστοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>acquaintance, a person whom one knows; a familiar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γνωστός, from γιγνώσκω.</div><b>γνώμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γνώμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>opinion, perception (subjective thought)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γνώμη.</div><b>γνώμονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γνώμονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. square, set square (for making right angles)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gnomon (component of sun dial)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γνώμων.</div><b>γνώριμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γνώριμη, neuter γνώριμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>familiar, known</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γνώριμος. By surface analysis, γνωρ(ίζω), “I know, recognise”) + -ιμος (-imos)</div><b>γνώση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γνώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. knowledge, scholarship (knowing; understanding)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prudence, circumspection (only in the singular)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek γνῶσις.</div><b>γνώστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γνώστες, feminine γνώστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expert</div><b>γνώστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γνώστριες, masculine γνώστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expert</div><b>γογγυλοκράμβη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γογγυλοκράμβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kohlrabi</div><b>γογγύζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to grumble, to murmur (to complain or express discontent in a low, muttering voice)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek γογγύζω.</div><b>γογγύλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γογγύλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. turnip (vegetable)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. turnip (vegetable family)</div><b>γοερά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γοερός">γοερός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mournfully, plaintively</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έκλαιγαν <b>γοερά</b>. - They were weeping mournfully.</div><b>γοερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γοερή, neuter γοερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mournful, plaintive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γοερός.</div><b>γοητεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) charm, attractiveness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γοητεία, from Ancient Greek γόης, from Ancient Greek γοάω, perhaps from Proto-Indo-European *geh₃- (“to sing, cry”) or Proto-Indo-European *gewH- (“to call, invoke, cry out”).</div><b>γοητευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γοητευτική, neuter γοητευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>charming, enchanting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek γοητευτικός with semantic loan from French charmant and fascinant.</div><b>γοητεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be enchanted</div><b>γοητεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to charm, enchant, enthrall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γοητεύω (“bewitch”).</div><b>γομάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γομάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>donkey, ass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γομάρι(ν)), from Koine Greek γομάριον, derived from Ancient Greek γόμος.</div><b>γομαλάκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural γομαλάκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) shellac</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian gommalacca.</div><b>γομολάστιχα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γομολάστιχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eraser, rubber</div><b>γομφίος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γομφίοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>molar (tooth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γομφίος.</div><b>γονέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γονείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parent, a term for a mother or a father</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γονεύς.</div><b>γονίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γονίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gene (unit of heredity)</div><b>γονιδιοθεραπεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gene therapy</div><b>γονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γονική, neuter γονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. parental</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) seminal (of semen)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek γονικός.</div><b>γονιμοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural γονιμοποίησεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) fertilisation (UK), fertilization (US)</div><b>γονιμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) fertility, fecundity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γονιμότης, equivalent to γόνιμος + -ότητα.</div><b>γονιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γονιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γονέας">γονέας</a></div><b>γονυκλισία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γονυκλισίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genuflection</div><b>γονυπετής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γονυπετής, neuter γονυπετές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) on bended knee (especially when supplicating, beseeching)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γονυπετής</b> σας παρακαλώ να με αθωώσετε. - I beg you, on my knees, to acquit me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γονῠπετής.</div><b>γονυπετώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, literary) with bended knees (especially when supplicating, beseeching)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γονῠπετῶς. Derives from adjective γονυπετής.</div><b>γορίλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γορίλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gorilla</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory) bouncer, bodyguard</div><b>γορίλλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γορίλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://γορίλας">γορίλας</a></div><b>γοργόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γοργόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mermaid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a person who appeared out of the blue, without any recorded history, like a mermaid coming out of the sea</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Kaliarda) escort, chaperone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Γοργώ</div><b>γοργός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swift, rapid, fast</div><b>γουίντ-σέρφινγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) windsurfing</div><b>γουανάκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γουανάκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guanaco</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Spanish guanaco, from Quechua wanaku; with the masculine suffix -ος (-os).</div><b>γουβλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γουβλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pike (fish)</div><b>γουδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γουδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mortar (vessel used to grind ingredients, especially spices or pharmaceutical preparations)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γουδί</b> του φαρμακείου - pharmacist&#x27;s mortar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γδί(ν)), from Koine Greek ἰγδίον, diminutive of Ancient Greek ἴγδις, of Pre-Greek origin. Ultimate origin may be from Hurrian 𒀉𒆠 (/⁠idki, itki⁠/, “mortar”), from the root 𒀉 (/⁠id-⁠/, “to beat”). Doublet of ιγδίο.</div><b>γουδοχέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γουδοχέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pestle (instrument)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γουδί + χέρι.</div><b>γουλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γουλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smooth, tender stalk or shoot of a plant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. edible root of a plant</div>
  <div class='def'>3. garlic clove, husked seed</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) pebble (often used to name beaches)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial) shaven head</div><b>γουνάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in Limassol) small mountain, hill, barrow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek βουνάριον, from βουνός.</div><b>γουνάριν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γουνάρι">γουνάρι</a></div><b>γουργουρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to purr (from a contented cat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to gurgle</div><b>γουργούρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γουργουρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. purr (the sound of a contented cat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coo</div>
  <div class='def'>3. gurgle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γουργουρίζω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>γουρουνόπουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γουρουνόπουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>piglet</div><b>γουρούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γουρούνες, masculine γουρούνι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sow (female pig)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>γουρούνα</b> τρέφει τα γουρουνάκια της. - The sow is feeding her piglets.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, offensive) pig, fat bitch (an overweight and mean-spirited woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ντρέπεται αυτή η <b>γουρούνα</b>, που τρώει και δεν δίνει τίποτα στα παιδιά της; - Isn&#x27;t that fat bitch ashamed to be eating and giving nothing to her children?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, slang) quad bike</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι δίπλωμα χρειάζομαι για να οδηγήσω αυτή τη <b>γουρούνα</b>; - What licence do I need to drive this quad bike?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γουρούνι.</div><b>γουρούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γουρούνια, feminine γουρούνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pig, hog, swine (male pig)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>γουρούνια</b> είναι ευχαριστημένα όταν παίζουν στη λάσπη. - The pigs are happy when they&#x27;re playing in mud.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, offensive) pig, fat pig, beast (an overweight and mean-spirited man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο άντρας της είναι σκέτο <b>γουρούνι</b> και πήγε να με χτυπήσει όταν τόλμησα να της μιλήσω. - Her husband is a real pig of a man and he tried to hit me when I dared to talk to her.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γουρούνιν, earlier γουρούνιον, from Koine Greek [Term?], from a diminutive of Ancient Greek γρώνη, from γρῦ.</div><b>γουστάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to want, desire</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to like (something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan gustare.</div><b>γοφός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γοφοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γόμφος.</div><b>γούβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γούβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small pit, depression</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an area with a smaller altitude than its surroundings</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γουβάς, further origin unclear. Akin to Albanian guvë, Aromanian guvã (“hole, cavity”). Ultimately either from Albanian or a byform of Latin cavea (“hollow, cavity”).</div><b>γούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γούνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pelt, hide, fur (of an animal)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γούνα</b> λύκου - wolf skin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. such fur used for clothing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. any material or garment of similar structure</div>
  <div class='ex'>συνθετική <b>γούνα</b> - synthetic fur</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γούνα, see there for more.</div><b>γούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. good luck</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lucky charm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish اوغور.</div><b>γούστο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γούστα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. taste (person&#x27;s implicit set of preferences)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχεις θαυμάσιο <b>γούστο</b> στη μουσική. - You have a wonderful taste in music.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. good taste</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το διαμέρισμά της δείχνει το <b>γούστο</b> της. - Her apartment shows her great taste.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) fun</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου χάλασε το <b>γούστο</b> ο καβγάς. - The fight spoiled my good fun.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γοῦστο, from Venetan gusto (“taste”).</div><b>γούτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) time, duration</div><b>γρ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γραπτός">γραπτός</a>: written</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γραφή">γραφή</a>: writing</div><b>γράδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γράδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, rare) hydrometer, areometer (device for measuring density of liquids)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, rare) measure of liquid density</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σιρόπι είναι δέκα <b>γράδα</b>. - The syrup is of ten grades [density].</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian grado (“grade, degree”).</div><b>γράμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γράμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. letter (written or printed symbol)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. character</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural, informal) education, literacy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ξέρεις <b>γράμματα</b>; - Can you read?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. letter (correspondence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο ’πα και στο ξαναλέω
μη μου γράφεις <b>γράμματα</b>
γιατί <b>γράμματα</b> δεν ξέρω
και με πιάνουν κλάματα. - I&#x27;ve told you and I&#x27;m telling you again
don&#x27;t write me letters
because I can&#x27;t read (letters)
and tears overcome me</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (in plural) tails)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (in plural) literature, letters</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα <b>γράμματα</b> και οι τέχνες. - Literature and the arts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γράμμα. Morphologically γράφω + -μα (-ma, result noun suffix).</div><b>γράνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γράνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ditch (for irrigation or drainage)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος θα δει την κομμένη <b>γράνα</b>; - Who will notice the dissected irrigation ditch?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γράνα, a Slavic loan related to Proto-Slavic *grana (“branch, twig”) and Proto-Slavic *granь (“side, edge”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *gʰrān- (“edge, end, tip”). Cognates include Old Church Slavonic грань, dialectal Bulgarian гран (gran)/Bulgarian грана (grana, “twig, small branch”) and Serbo-Croatian грана (“field, branch”).</div><b>γράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to write, pen</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γράφετε</b> βιβλίο; - Are you writing a book?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to record</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to issue a ticket (for traffic violation, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γράφω, from Proto-Hellenic *grəpʰō, from Proto-Indo-European *gerbʰ-. For the stems, see Related terms.</div><b>γράχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) write</div><b>γράψιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραψίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. writing (written letters or symbols that express some meaning)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hand (style of penmanship), handwriting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γράφω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>γρήγορα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. quickly, fast</div>
  <div class='ex'>άντε <b>γρήγορα</b>! - hurry up!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. soon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γρήγορος + -α (-a).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γρήγορος">γρήγορος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γρήγορος + -α (-a).</div><b>γρήγορος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γρήγορη, neuter γρήγορο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quick, fast, swift, brisk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γρήγορος, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐγρήγορος (“awake”), γρηγορῶ, from the present perfect of Ancient Greek ἐγείρω.</div><b>γρίπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) influenza, flu, &#x27;flu</div><b>γρίπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γρίποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fishing equipment, fishing net</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trawler, fishing boat</div><b>γρίφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γρίφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. conundrum (difficult question or riddle)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. puzzle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. enigma</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γρῖφος; first used in the sense of &quot;riddle&quot; by Aristophanes.</div><b>γραίγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) northeasterly wind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan grego from Italian greco (“Greek”), from Latin graecus (“Greek”), from Ancient Greek Γραικός.</div><b>γραβάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραβάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tie, necktie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French cravate.</div><b>γραδάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, literary, poetic, rare) to measure density (of a liquid, with a hydrometer)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σ’ έστειλε ο πρώτος τα νερά να πας για να γραδάρεις.
S’ ésteile o prótos ta nerá na pas gia na gradáreis.
The engineer sent you to go and check the water&#x27;s density.
Also see 1st stanza Greek text &amp; translation by Evgenia Russell @books.google retr:2018.10.28.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to get the measure of, figure out (understand someone&#x27;s character)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>γραδάρισα</b> εγώ! Είναι σκάρτος χαρακτήρας. - I got the measure of him! He is no good.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian gradare, equivalent to γράδο + -άρω.</div><b>γραικύλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γραικύλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a Greek which is unworthy of the national traditions</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the deposed or decadent Greek</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a Greek submitted to strangers, foreign interests</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin Graeculus, diminutive of Graecus. The term was used in an ironical manner during the Byzantine Empire, by Greeks, to denote non-Greeks living within the Empire and which were trying to behave as Greeks.</div><b>γραμμ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γραμματικός">γραμματικός</a>: grammatical</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γραμματικός">γραμματικός</a>: grammar</div><b>γραμμάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραμμάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gram (SI unit of weight)</div><b>γραμμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γραμμένη, neuter γραμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. written, covered with writing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. written, registered, recorded</div><b>γραμμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραμμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. line</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γραμμή</b> τερματισμού - finishing line</div>
  <div class='def'>2. row (sequence of items, lined up)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. policy, orders</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek γρᾰμμή.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. straight, directly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. successively, consecutively</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek γρᾰμμή.</div><b>γραμματέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γραμματείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. secretary (a person who keeps records, takes notes and handles general clerical work)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. secretary (a managerial or leading position in certain non-profit organizations, such as political parties, trade unions, international organizations)</div>
  <div class='ex'>γενικός <b>γραμματέας</b> - secretary general; general secretary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γραμματεύς.</div><b>γραμματεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραμματείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. literature (the body of all written works in a given historical period)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: λογοτεχνία f (logotechnía)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. secretariat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek γραμματεία, with semantic loan from French littérature in the &#x27;literature&#x27; sense. By surface analysis, γραμματ(έας)) + -εία.</div><b>γραμματική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) grammar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γρᾰμμᾰτῐκή.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://γραμματικός">γραμματικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γρᾰμμᾰτῐκή.</div><b>γραμματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γραμματική, neuter γραμματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grammatical (relating to grammar)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grammatical (using correct grammar)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γραμματικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γραμματικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grammarian</div>
  <div class='def'>2. scribe, secretary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γραμματικός.</div><b>γραμματοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραμματοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. letter rack, letter case</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mailbox</div><b>γραμματοκιβώτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραμματοκιβώτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>letterbox, mailbox (box or slot into which a courier or postal worker puts letters for a recipient to collect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γραμματο- (grammato-, “postal”) + κιβώτιο</div><b>γραμματοσειρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραμματοσειρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>font, fount</div><b>γραμματοστοιχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραμματοστοιχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>font, fount</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γραμματο- (grammato-, “writing”) + στοιχείο</div><b>γραμματόσημο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραμματόσημα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>postage stamp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γραμματό- + -σημο (-simo, “stamp”).</div><b>γραμμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linear</div><b>γραμμοκωδικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γραμμοκωδικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barcode</div><b>γραμμομόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) gram molecule (unit quantifying matter)</div><b>γραμμόφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραμμόφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gramophone (dated), record player</div><b>γρανάζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γρανάζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gear, cogwheel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cog, tooth of cogwheel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French engrenage (“gear”).</div><b>γραπτά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>written, in written form</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://γραπτό">γραπτό</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γραπτός">γραπτός</a></div><b>γραπτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραπτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. anything filled in written examinations (sheet, paper etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. destiny</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://γραπτός">γραπτός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative neuter singular of <a href="bword://γραπτός">γραπτός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://γραπτός">γραπτός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://γραπτός">γραπτός</a></div><b>γραπτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γραπτή, neuter γραπτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>written</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γράφω.</div><b>γραπτώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>on paper, in writing, in written form</div><b>γρασίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γρασίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grass, lawn, turf</div><b>γρατζουνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to scratch, graze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic for a scratch (γρατς γρατς (grats grats)); for similar formations in other languages, compare English scratch, German kratzen (“to scratch”), Persian خراشیدن.</div><b>γρατζουνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γρατζουνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>graze, abrasion, scratch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γρατζουνάω + -ιά.</div><b>γρατσουνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γρατσουνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γρατζουνιά">γρατζουνιά</a></div><b>γραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. writing, script (written characters that express some meaning)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. handwriting, hand (style of writing)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. the Bible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γραφή.</div><b>γραφίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραφίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) author; writer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pen; fountain pen</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) skill at writing</div><b>γραφίς</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραφίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://γραφίδα">γραφίδα</a></div><b>γραφίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γραφίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>graphite</div><b>γραφεία τελετών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of <a href="bword://γραφείο">γραφείο</a> τελετών</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of <a href="bword://γραφείο">γραφείο</a> τελετών</div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of <a href="bword://γραφείο">γραφείο</a> τελετών</div><b>γραφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραφεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. desk</div>
  <div class='def'>2. office</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γραφεῖον, from γράφω + -εῖον. Possibly cognate to Albanian gërfyell.</div><b>γραφείο τελετών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γραφεία τελετών)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(euphemistic, formal) funeral home, funeral parlor (mortuary where bodies are prepared for burial)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το φέρετρο θα έρθει με τη νεκροφόρα από το <b>γραφείο τελετών</b>. - The coffin will come in the hearse from the funeral home.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;office of ceremonies&quot;.</div><b>γραφείου τελετών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://γραφείο">γραφείο</a> τελετών</div><b>γραφείων τελετών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of <a href="bword://γραφείο">γραφείο</a> τελετών</div><b>γραφειοκράτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γραφειοκράτες, feminine γραφειοκράτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bureaucrat</div><b>γραφειοκράτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραφειοκράτισσες, masculine γραφειοκράτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bureaucrat</div><b>γραφειοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) bureaucracy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γραφείο + -κρατία. Calque of French bureaucratie. First attested 1851.</div><b>γραφειοκρατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γραφειοκρατική, neuter γραφειοκρατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bureaucratic, clerical</div><b>γραφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γραφική, neuter γραφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. written, graphical, graphic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. picturesque, colourful</div>
  <div class='def'>3. biblical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γραφικός.</div><b>γραφομηχανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γραφομηχανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>typewriter (the machine)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γραφο- (grafo-, “writing”) + μηχανή</div><b>γραφτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γραφτή, neuter γραφτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γραπτός">γραπτός</a></div><b>γρεκάνικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the Griko Italiot Greek dialect</div><b>γριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γριές, masculine γέρος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. old woman, old lady (elderly female person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βοηθώ μια <b>γριά</b> να περάσει τον δρόμο. - I help an old lady to cross the road.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) old lady (one&#x27;s mother)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον ενοχλεί συνεχώς η <b>γριά</b> του να παντρευτεί. - His old lady is constantly bothering him to marry.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γραῖα.</div><b>γρικάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dialectal, literary) listen, hear</div>
  <div class='ex'>See also: αφουγκράζομαι (afougkrázomai, “listen attentively”), ακουρμάζομαι (akourmázomai) / ακουρμαίνομαι (akourmaínomai)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hence: understand</div>
  <div class='ex'>&amp; see standard καταλαβαίνω (katalavaíno)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (for the passive form (<a href="bword://α">α</a>) γρικιέμαι ((a)grikiémai): I am heard by someone and we are in agreement.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Form of αγρικάω as γρικ(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω. Simplified spelling of the inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀγροικῶ with multiple forms and uncertain propositions for its etymology.</div><b>γρικώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>variant of <a href="bword://γρικάω">γρικάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Form of αγρικώ with simplified spelling of the inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀγροικῶ and see: γρικάω.</div><b>γρινιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκρινιάζω">γκρινιάζω</a></div><b>γροίκησα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of γροικάω and <a href="bword://γροικώ">γροικώ</a></div><b>γροθιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γροθιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fist (a hand with the fingers clenched or curled inward)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. punch, blow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γρόνθος + -ιά.</div><b>γροικώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) to hear, understand</div><b>γροιλανδικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greenlandic, the language of Greenland</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γροιλανδικός">γροιλανδικός</a></div><b>γροιλανδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γροιλανδική, neuter γροιλανδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greenlandic, Greenland</div><b>γρονθοκοπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to punch (to strike with one&#x27;s fist)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek γρονθοκοπῶ, whence Byzantine Greek γροθοκοπώ.</div><b>γρυλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to grunt (of a pig, other animal or person: to make a short snorting sound)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to growl, to snarl (to give forth an angry, grumbling sound)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γρυλίζω.</div><b>γρόνθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γρόνθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) fist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γρόνθος.</div><b>γρύλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γρύλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cricket</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jack, scissor jack, grasshopper jack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γρύλλος.</div><b>γρύπας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γρύπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>griffin (mythical beast having the body of a lion and the wings and head of an eagle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γρύψ.</div><b>γυάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γυάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fishbowl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crystal ball</div><b>γυάλινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γυάλινη, neuter γυάλινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glass (attributive) (made of glass)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γυαλ(ί)) + -ινος (-inos).</div><b>γυαλάκιας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυαλάκηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) four-eyes, gig-lamps</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γυαλί + -άκιας</div><b>γυαλένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γυαλένια, neuter γυαλένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://γυάλινος">γυάλινος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γυαλ(ί)) + -ένιος.</div><b>γυαλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γυαλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. glass (material)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shard, glass fragment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) television, the box</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural) glasses, spectacles</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, rare) glass for wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὕαλος. Doublet of ύαλος.</div><b>γυαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to polish, sheen, buff up, glaze, sparkle, glisten, shine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (metaphoric) to tempt someone (typically with money)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γυάλισα</b> ένα κατοστάρικο και με βοήθησε. - I flashed a 100 Euro note and he helped me.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (passive) to observe one&#x27;s reflection, be reflected</div><b>γυαλιά ηλίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sunglasses</div><b>γυαλιά του ηλίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of γυαλιά <a href="bword://ηλίου">ηλίου</a></div><b>γυαλισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine γυαλισμένη, neuter γυαλισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polished</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of γυαλίζομαι, passive voice of γυαλίζω.</div><b>γυαλιστερή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γυαλιστερές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smooth clam</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://γυαλιστερός">γυαλιστερός</a></div><b>γυαλιστερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γυαλιστερή, neuter γυαλιστερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. polished, shiny</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει <b>γυαλιστερά</b> παπούτσια. - He has polished shoes.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. elegant</div><b>γυαλοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γυαλοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://υαλοπωλείο">υαλοπωλείο</a></div><b>γυαλόχαρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γυαλόχαρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sandpaper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γυαλόχαρτο &lt; γυαλί + χαρτί</div><b>γυιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γιος">γιος</a> (gios)</div><b>γυλιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυλιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>haversack, shoulder bag</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γυλιός.</div><b>γυμν.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γυμναστική">γυμναστική</a>: gymnastics</div><b>γυμνάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to exercise, to train, to work out (to perform physical activity for health or training)</div><b>γυμνάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to exercise, drill, train, school</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Γυμνάζω</b> πολλές πλούσιες παντρεμένες. - I train a lot of rich married women.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to exercise, to train, to work out (to perform physical activity for health or training)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα έπρεπε να <b>γυμνάζομαι</b>. - I should be working out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γυμνάζω. By surface analysis, γυμνός + -άζω.</div><b>γυμνάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γυμνάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. middle school (individual schools for ages 12–15)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. middle school (that level of education)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (obsolete) gymnasium (building for physical exercise)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. drill, physical exercise (especially military)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γυμνάσιον.</div><b>γυμναστ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://γυμναστική">γυμναστική</a>: gymnastics</div><b>γυμναστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γυμναστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gymnasium (place for indoor sports), gym, sports hall</div><b>γυμναστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυμναστές, feminine γυμνάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>athletic trainer, fitness trainer, personal trainer, trainer of athletes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γῠμνᾰστής.</div><b>γυμναστική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γυμναστικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exercise, workout, working out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γυμναστική (τέχνη)).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of γυμναστικός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>γυμνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nudism, naturism</div><b>γυμνιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυμνιστές, feminine γυμνίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nudist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from γυμν(ός)) + -ιστής with semantic loan from French nudiste.</div><b>γυμνοσάλιαγκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυμνοσάλιαγκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slug (gastropod)</div><b>γυμνοσάλιαγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυμνοσάλιαγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γυμνοσάλιαγκας">γυμνοσάλιαγκας</a></div><b>γυμνός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γυμνή, neuter γυμνό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>naked, unsheathed, bare.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γυμνός, from Proto-Hellenic *gʷomnós, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *nogʷós.</div><b>γυμνότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nudity, nakedness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γυμνότης, equivalent to γυμνός + -ότητα.</div><b>γυμνώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to undress, strip off (clothing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to remove, strip (covering)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αφού <b>γυμνώσεις</b> στην άκρη τα καλώδια, ένωσε το κόκκινο και το μπλε. - After stripping wires, join red and blue</div><b>γυναίκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γυναίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. woman</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>γυναίκα</b> έχει το παλτό μου. - The woman has my coat.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wife</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γυναίκα, from Ancient Greek γυνή, γυναῖκα. Cognate with Tsakonian γουναίκα and Cappadocian Greek ναίκα.</div><b>γυναικάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυναικάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>womanizer (male seducer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From γυναίκα + -άς.</div><b>γυναικεία</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://γυναικείος">γυναικείος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://γυναικείος">γυναικείος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>femininely, as a woman</div><b>γυναικεία περιτομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γυναικείες περιτομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female circumcision</div><b>γυναικείες περιτομές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://γυναικεία">γυναικεία</a> <a href="bword://περιτομή">περιτομή</a></div><b>γυναικείος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γυναικεία, neuter γυναικείο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine, womanly (having the qualities associated with a woman)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek γυναικεῖος.</div><b>γυναικολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gynecology</div><b>γυναικολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural γυναικολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gynecologist</div><b>γυναικωνίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυναικωνίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rooms for women, women&#x27;s quarters</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γυναικών.</div><b>γυπαετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυπαετοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lammergeier or bearded vulture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French gypaète, from Ancient Greek γύψ + ἀετός.</div><b>γυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to turn, return, switch</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to return</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γυρίζω, from Ancient Greek γῦρ(ος), “ring, circle”) + -ίζω. Compare to the variant γυρνάω/γυρνώ.</div><b>γυρίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυρίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tadpole</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γῠρῖνος.</div><b>γυρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) to look for, want</div>
  <div class='ex'>πάω <b>γυρεύοντας</b> - to ask for trouble, to ask for it, to look for trouble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γυρεύω, from Hellenistic Koine Greek γῡρεύω from the Ancient Greek γῦρος.</div><b>γυρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>return (from somewhere)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γυρισμός. By surface analysis, γυρισ-) + -μός.</div><b>γυρνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://γυρίζω">γυρίζω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γυρν(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek γυρνῶ, a metaplasm based on the aorist (past tense) stem γυρισ- (gyris-) (medieval and early modern (ἐ)γύρισα ((e)gúrisa)) in the pattern of metaplasms like κέρασα - κερνώ.</div><b>γυρνιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be turned</div><b>γυρνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γυρνάω">γυρνάω</a></div><b>γυρολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γυρολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hawker, peddler</div><b>γυροσκοπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γυροσκοπική, neuter γυροσκοπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gyroscopic, gyro</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γυροσκοπική</b> πυξίδα - gyrocompass</div><b>γυροσκόπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γυροσκόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gyroscope</div><b>γωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. angle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. corner</div>
  <div class='def'>3. viewpoint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek γωνία.</div><b>γωνιακοί τροχοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://γωνιακός">γωνιακός</a> <a href="bword://τροχός">τροχός</a></div><b>γωνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine γωνιακή, neuter γωνιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corner, on the corner, around the corner</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γωνιακό</b> μαγαζί - corner shop</div><b>γωνιακός τροχός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γωνιακοί τροχοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>angle grinder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> γωνιακός + τροχός</div><b>γόβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γόβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>court shoe, pump</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan goba.</div><b>γόμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γόμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://γομολάστιχα">γομολάστιχα</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. latex adhesive</div><b>γόμφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γόμφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peg (fastener)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γόμφος, from Proto-Hellenic *gómpʰos, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵómbʰos.</div><b>γόνατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γόνατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>knee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from the plural γόνατα, from Ancient Greek γόνυ), from Proto-Hellenic *gónu, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵónu.</div><b>γόνδολα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γόνδολες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gondola (Venetian boat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gondola (airship passenger compartment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> An learned αντιδάνειο, borrowed from Venetan góndoƚa, most likely from Byzantine Greek κοντούρα/κονδοῦρα”).</div><b>γόνιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fertile, capable of growing abundant crops</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fertile, capable of reproducing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) fertile, productive or prolific</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γόνιμος.</div><b>γόνυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γόνατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) knee. Used in the set phrase (in indication of respect)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κλίνω το <b>γόνυ</b> - I show respect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γόνυ.</div><b>γόπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γόπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bogue (Boops boops) (fish)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) fag-end, cigarette end, butt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an unattested Medieval Greek *γῶπα, from an unattested *βῶπα, an accusative form of Byzantine Greek βῶψ, βόωψ”), from Ancient Greek βῶξ, a contracted form of βόαξ.
The &quot;cigarette butt&quot; sense is in reference to the resemblance of cigarette butts to bogues.</div><b>γύμνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nakedness, nudity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek γύμνια. By surface analysis, γυμν(ός)) + -ια (-ia).</div><b>γύπας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γύπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>any vulture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γύψ.</div><b>γύρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pollen</div><b>γύρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural γυρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. revolve, revolving (action or result of revolve)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. twist, twisting (action or result of twist)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. turn, turning (action or result of turning)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. return, returning (action or result of returning)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. paying back (of a road or in life)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. turning point (of a road)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (plural normally) turning point (of a road); turning point (in life)</div>
  <div class='def'>8. shooting, filming</div>
  <div class='def'>9. hem (border of an article of clothing doubled back)</div>
  <div class='def'>10. switching scales</div>
  <div class='def'>11. transferring the ball backwards</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * From Byzantine Greek γύρισμα, from Ancient Greek γυρίζω.</div><b>γύρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γύροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. round, perimeter, rim</div>
  <div class='ex'>Sýmfona me to mýtho o Días apó ton Ólympo áfise dyo aetoús na petáxoun pros antíthetes katefthýnseis kai ta pouliá, afoú ékanan to gýro tis Gis, synantíthikan s’ aftó to simeío. - According to the myth Zeus released from Olympus two eagles flying in opposite directions; and the birds, after making a tour of the Earth, met at this spot [Delphi].</div>
  <div class='def'>2. round, perimeter, rim; brim (of a hat)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ας υποθέσουμε πως του καπέλου ο γύρος
άξαφνα εφάρδυνε, μα εστένεψαν, κολλούν
τα παντελόνια μας [...]
As ypothésoume pos tou kapélou o gýros
áxafna efárdyne, ma esténepsan, kolloún
ta pantelónia mas [...] - Let us assume that the hat&#x27;s brim
suddenly widened; but tightened, stuck
are our trousers …</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bout, round, movement on a circle</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάμε το γύρο της πατρίδας σου, και θα ’χεις κάμει το γύρο του κόσμου (@books.google) - Make a tour of your country, and you will have done the tour of the world.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. a walk or stroll</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάμε ένα <b>γύρο</b> στην πλατεία να δούμε τα παιδιά; - Shall we go for a stroll to the square to see the guys?</div>
  <div class='def'>5. bypass, detour, diversion from main route</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γίνονται έργα στη λεωφόρο και κάναμε ολόκληρο <b>γύρο</b> για να φτάσουμε. - There&#x27;s construction at the avenue, so we made a long detour to get there.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. spread (of news)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η είδηση του θανάτου της έκανε το <b>γύρο</b> του κόσμου. - The news of her death made international headlines.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. lap, round, tour (sport, game, elections)</div>
  <div class='ex'>προκριματικός <b>γύρος</b>, δεύτερος <b>γύρος</b> - preliminary round, second round</div>
  <div class='def'>8. tour, turn (work)</div>
  <div class='def'>9. gyro, döner kebab</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάω στο σουβλατζίδικο να φάω μία πίτα με <b>γύρο</b>, ντομάτα, κρεμμύδι και τζατζίκι. - I&#x27;m going to the souvlaki-shop to eat a gyro-wrap with tomato, onion(s) and tzatziki.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek γῦρος, substantivized from Ancient Greek γῡρός, from Proto-Hellenic *gūrós, possibly from Proto-Indo-European *guH-ró-s, from *gew- (“to bend, curve”) + *-rós. Possible cognate with Sanskrit गोल (gola, “circle”).</div><b>γύρω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. around, close by (in the locality of)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. around, approximately</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>γύρω</b> στα 2 κιλά - about 2 kilos</div>
  <div class='def'>3. around</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γη γυρίζει <b>γύρω</b> από τον ήλιο. - The Earth goes around the Sun.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek γύρω, from γύρος).</div><b>γύφτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γύφτοι, feminine γύφτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gypsy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) a person who is very mean</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory) a very dark-skinned person</div>
  <div class='def'>4. smith, ironsmith, blacksmith</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek Γύφτος, from Ancient Greek Αἰγύπτιος, from the mistaken belief that gypsies came from Egypt.</div><b>γύψος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural γύψοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gypsum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. plaster (wall coating)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. plaster of Paris, plaster cast (treatment for broken bones)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek γύψος.</div><b>γώπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural γώπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γόπα">γόπα</a></div><b>δ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Δ΄">Δ΄</a></div><b>δάγκασα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of δάγκωσα</div>
  <div class='ex'>first-person singular simple past active of δαγκάνω (dagkáno)</div><b>δάγκωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δαγκώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bite, biting (the act of biting)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δάγκωμα.</div><b>δάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. burning torch</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) source of knowledge or wisdom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δαίω.</div><b>δάκρυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δάκρυα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tear (from crying)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δάκρυ, from Proto-Indo-European *dáḱru-.</div><b>δάκτυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δάκτυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δάχτυλο">δάχτυλο</a></div><b>δάνειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δάνεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loan</div>
  <div class='def'>2. loanword</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δάνειον.</div><b>δάπεδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δάπεδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>floor of a room, flooring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δάπεδον.</div><b>δάσκαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δάσκαλοι, feminine δασκάλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. great artist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a person who gives advice</div>
  <div class='def'>3. teacher (especially one in a primary school or elementary school)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. instructor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Aphetic form of Ancient Greek διδάσκαλος, from διδάσκω.</div><b>δάσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δάση)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wood, forest</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ρομπέν των <b>Δασών</b> - Robin Hood</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δάσος, from δασύς.
Compare typologically English thicket, Russian ча́ща, akin to ча́стый.</div><b>δάφνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δάφνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. laurel, bay (tree)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bay, bay leaf</div>
  <div class='ex'>φύλλο <b>δάφνης</b> - bay leaf</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) victory, triumph</div>
  <div class='ex'>δρέπω <b>δάφνες</b> - reap the laurels</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δάφνη.</div><b>δάχτυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δάχτυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>finger, digit</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δάχτυλο</b> χεριού - finger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δάκτυλος with change of gender.</div><b>δέ</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) but, and</div><b>δέκα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) ten</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δέκα, from Proto-Hellenic *dékə, from Proto-Indo-European *déḱm̥.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek дъэ́ка.</div><b>δέκατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δέκατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tenth (part of a whole)</div>
  <div class='ex'>τρία δέκατα (three tenths)</div><b>δέκατος</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine (feminine δέκατη or δεκάτη, neuter δέκατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, numeral) tenth, 10th</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Οκτώβριος είναι ο <b>δέκατος</b> μήνας του έτους. - October is the tenth month of the year.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δέκατος.</div><b>δέκατος τρίτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δέκατη τρίτη, neuter δέκατο τρίτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thirteenth (13th, ordinal number)</div><b>δέκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δέκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. recipient, receiver</div>
  <div class='def'>2. receiver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δέκτης, from δέχομαι.</div><b>δέλτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) delta, the fourth letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) river delta</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δέλτᾰ.</div><b>δέμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>package, bundle, packet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δέμα, from Ancient Greek δέω + -μα (-ma, nominal suffix).</div><b>δένδρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δένδρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δέντρο">δέντρο</a></div><b>δένομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be attached to</div><b>δέντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δέντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tree</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η φλαμουριά είναι ένα ωραίο <b>δέντρο</b>. - The lime is a beautiful tree.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tree (type of diagram)</div>
  <div class='ex'>γενεαλογικό <b>δέντρο</b> - family tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δέντρο(ν)) from Ancient Greek δένδρον, retaining the pronunciation [nd].
Cognate with Mariupol Greek дъендро.</div><b>δένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to coagulate, bind together</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to tie, bind, tie up</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δένει</b> κόμπο. - He ties a knot.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to moor, tie up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το καΐκι <b>δένει</b> στο λιμάνι. - The caique ties up in the harbour.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) to tie someone&#x27;s hands, constrain someone&#x27;s actions</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο νόμος μάς <b>δένει</b> τα χέρια. - The law ties our hands.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δέω.</div><b>δέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pray to God</div><b>δέον</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δέων">δέων</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δέον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δέοντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. that which is right, proper, necessary</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ανησυχείς υπέρ το <b>δέον</b>. - You worry too much, more than necessary.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) a set of actions or measures that must be taken for a given situation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έπραξε τα <b>δέοντα</b>. - He did what had to be done.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) compliments, respects</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>δέοντα</b> στον πατέρα σας! - My compliments to your father!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δέον.</div><b>δέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) awe (a feeling of fear and reverence)</div><b>δέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δέρατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hide, pelt, skin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fleece</div><b>δέρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δέρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. skin (of human or animal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δέρμα, from δέρω.</div><b>δέρνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be beaten</div><b>δέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively, transitive) to batter, hit (to fall on something with might and power)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σπίτι μας το <b>δέρνει</b> συνεχώς ο άνεμος. - Our house is constantly being battered by the wind.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to be suffering from, be being killed by.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με <b>δέρνει</b> η πείνα απόψε. - The hunger is killing me tonight.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory, figuratively, transitive) to be suffering from, be afflicted by, be characterised by (as a quality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι βλακεία τον <b>δέρνει</b> πρόσφατα; - What kind of stupidity is he suffering from recently?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to beat, hit, smack, batter (to strike a person or animal with the hand or other implement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα σε <b>δείρω</b> αν δεν σταματήσεις τις αταξίες. - I&#x27;ll smack you if you don&#x27;t stop misbehaving.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δέρω.</div><b>δέσμευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. binding (the act whereby something or someone is bound (obliged, restrained, or held, by authority, law, duty, etc.))</div>
  <div class='def'>2. commitment (the act or an instance of committing, putting in charge, keeping, or trust)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. commitment (being bound emotionally or intellectually to a course of action or to another person or persons)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ηθική <b>δέσμευση</b> - moral/ethical commitment</div>
  <div class='def'>4. freezing (the act of preventing the movement or liquidation of a person&#x27;s financial assets)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δέσμευσις with semantic loan from English binding. By surface analysis, δεσμεύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>δέσμιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δέσμια, neuter δέσμιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imprisoned, captive, detained</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δέσμιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prisoner, captive</div><b>δέσποινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δέσποινες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated) a most revered lady, Lady of … (also used figuratively)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δέσποινα</b> των λογισμών</div>
  <div class='def'>2. for the feminine name and Holy Mary&#x27;s epithet see <a href="bword://Δέσποινα">Δέσποινα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δέσποινα, female counterpart to δεσπότης. Compare to Δέσποινα f; feminine name”).</div><b>δέχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to accept, receive</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δέχομαι</b> συγχαρητήρια - I receive congratulations (praisings)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δέχομαι.</div><b>δέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (active) to make possible (dated, also used ironically) e.g.</div>
  <div class='ex'>εδέησα (edéisa, past tense, retaining the old syllabic augment ε-)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (active, impersonal, third person) to make possible (dated, also used ironically) e.g.; [with να (na)]</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μετά την ξηρασία, <b>εδέησε</b> να βρέξει. - After the drought, it finally rained.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. See <a href="bword://δέομαι">δέομαι</a>.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δέω.</div><b>δέων</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine δέουσα, neuter δέον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in the ancient fashion) appropriate, proper, necessary</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε συνέχεια του περιστατικού, νομοθέτησαν τους <b>δέοντες</b> νόμους. - Following the incident, they legislated the necessary laws.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Active present participle of δέω. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek δέων.</div><b>δήθεν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) so-called, supposed, alleged (someone or something that is meant to have a certain characteristic but doesn&#x27;t in reality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>δήθεν</b> φίλη μου με εγκατέλειψε στο πλάι του δρόμου. - My so-called friend abandoned me at the side of the road.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δῆθεν, compound of δή + -θεν (-then).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supposedly, ostensibly, allegedly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έκανε πως <b>δήθεν</b> δεν μας είδε. - He pretended not to see us.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δῆθεν, compound of δή + -θεν (-then).</div><b>δήλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δηλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statement, report, announcement, declaration</div>
  <div class='ex'>φορολογική <b>δήλωση</b> - tax statement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δήλωσις f, ancient sense: &quot;explanation&quot; and semantic loan from French déclaration for official written statements.</div><b>δήλωσις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://δήλωση">δήλωση</a></div><b>δήμαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δήμαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mayor (leader of a city, town or a municipality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γυναίκα μου έγινε <b>δήμαρχος</b> πριν δυό χρόνια. - My wife became mayor two years ago.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δήμαρχος, from δῆμος + ἄρχω.</div><b>δήμιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δήμιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. executioner</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hangman</div>
  <div class='def'>3. headsman</div><b>δήμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δήμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. municipality, deme</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>Δήμος</b> Αθηναίων - the Municipality of Athenians (of Athens)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) people, common people (the body of citizens of a city-state in ancient Greece)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. common people</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (historical) deme (a subdivision of ancient Attica)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δῆμος.</div><b>δίαιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δίαιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diet, as in the food eaten or in the description of low-calorie foods</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an assembly of representatives on national issues in some countries</div>
  <div class='ex'>H Δίαιτα της Iαπωνίας είναι το εθνικό νομοθετικό σώμα της Ιαπωνίας. - The National Diet of Japan is the national legislature of Japan.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fast, low calorie diet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek δίαιτα.</div><b>δίγαμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) digamma, Ϝ, ϝ, the sixth letter of the archaic Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίγαμμα.</div><b>δίγαμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίγαμη, neuter δίγαμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bigamous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun) bigamist</div><b>δίγλωσσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίγλωσση, neuter δίγλωσσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bilingual (speaking two languages; written in two languages)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. who expresses different opinions to different people, hypocrite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δί- (δις), “two, twice”) + γλώσσα</div><b>δίδακτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural only) tuition fees</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek δίδακτρα. By surface analysis, διδάσκω + -τρo (-tro, “neuter singular suffix”), here in plural.</div><b>δίδυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίδυμη, neuter δίδυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. twin, twinned, being one of a pair of twins</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δίδυμα</b> αδέλφια - twin brothers</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) matching exactly</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δίδυμα</b> πυροβόλα, <b>δίδυμοι</b> πύργοι - twin cannon, twin towers</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίδυμος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δίδυμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twin (either of two people who shared the same uterus, or of two similar or closely related objects)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίδυμος.</div><b>δίδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δίνω">δίνω</a></div><b>δίεδρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίεδρη, neuter δίεδρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dihedral, two sided</div><b>δίεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of notes) sharp (raised by one semitone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το Πρελούδιο σε ντο <b>δίεση</b> μινόρε του Ραχμάνινοφ. - The Prelude in C-sharp minor by Rachmaninoff.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sharp (♯, the sharp symbol itself)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο κλαρινοπαίκτης έπαιξε λάθος νότα, διότι ο αντιγραφέας ξέχασε να βάλει <b>δίεση</b> μπροστά στη νότα. - The clarinettist played the wrong note, since the copyist forgot to put a sharp in front of the note.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French dièse, from Ancient Greek δίεσις, from διίημι.</div><b>δίκαια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fairly, justly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίκαιος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δίκαιος">δίκαιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίκαιος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://δίκαιο">δίκαιο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίκαιος.</div><b>δίκαιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δίκαια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>law</div>
  <div class='ex'>διεθνές <b>δίκαιο</b> - international law</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. genitive masculine singular of <a href="bword://δίκαιος">δίκαιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δίκαιος">δίκαιος</a></div><b>δίκαιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίκαιη, neuter δίκαιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. just (of a legal process or judgement)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. equitable, fair</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίκαιος.</div><b>δίκαννο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>double-barrelled shotgun</div><b>δίκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίκη.</div><b>δίκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) that which is right, correct, just</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δίκιον, a synezesis of Ancient Greek δῐ́καιον, neuter nominative singular of δῐ́καιος.</div><b>δίκλινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δίκλινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>double room</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://δίκλινος">δίκλινος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δίκλινος">δίκλινος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δίκλινος">δίκλινος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δίκλινος">δίκλινος</a></div><b>δίκλινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίκλινη, neuter δίκλινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>double (especially of a double room)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δι- (di-) and -κλινος (-klinos).</div><b>δίκταμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δίκταμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dittany (Origanum dictamnus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίκταμνον.</div><b>δίκτυο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δίκτυα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. network, internet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reticulum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δίκτυον. Doublet of δίχτυ.</div><b>δίκυκλο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://δίκυκλος">δίκυκλος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δίκυκλος">δίκυκλος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δίκυκλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two-wheeled vehicle, two-wheeler</div><b>δίκυκλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίκυκλη, neuter δίκυκλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two-wheeled, birotate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δι- (di-, from δις) + κύκλ(ος)) + -ος (-os), a calque of French bicycle.</div><b>δίλημμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διλήμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dilemma, quandary (a circumstance in which a choice must be made between two or more alternatives that seem equally undesirable)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dilemma</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δίλημμα with semantic loan from French dilemme.</div><b>δίνε του</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) Push off!, Get lost!</div><b>δίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vortex, whirlpool</div><b>δίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be given (to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν σου <b>δίνεται</b> η ευκαιρία. - When you are given the opportunity.</div><b>δίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to give (pass something; transfer ownership)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δίνει</b> το βιβλίο. - She gives the book.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to give, hold (an event)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ελένη <b>έδωσε</b> ένα πάρτι γενεθλίων. - Eleni threw a birthday party.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The earlier form is δίδω with metaplasm to -νω (-no), from Byzantine Greek δίδω, from Ancient Greek δίδωμι, from Proto-Indo-European *dédeh₃ti, reduplicated present of *deh₃- (“to give”).</div><b>δίνω σημασία</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://δίνω">δίνω</a>, <a href="bword://σημασία">σημασία</a>.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic, transitive, usually, with negation) to pay attention, give heed (to focus one&#x27;s attention)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν δεν του <b>δώσεις σημασία</b>, θα βαρεθεί και θα σταματήσει. - If you don&#x27;t pay attention to him, he&#x27;ll grow tired and stop.</div><b>δίοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δίοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. way, (mountain) pass, passage connecting two points</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diode (electronic component)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίοδος, from δια- (dia-, “through”) + ὁδός.</div><b>δίπλα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. next, next to, beside</div>
  <div class='def'>2. side-by-side, abreast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From adjective διπλός, plural διπλά, with stress shift similar to παρά, πάρα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δίπλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pleat, fold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. puff pastry</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) diples, rolled pastry dessert</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from διπλώνω.</div><b>δίπλα-δίπλα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>side-by-side, abreast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δίπλα + δίπλα</div><b>δίπλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διπλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. folding of map etc (especially folding in half)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diploma, certificate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. degree (certificate)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. driver&#x27;s license)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίπλωμα, equivalent to διπλώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>δίπλωμα οδήγησης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διπλώματα οδήγησης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (India, Ireland, Malta, Pakistan, UK) driving licence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Australia, Canada, New Zealand) driver&#x27;s licence</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (US) driver&#x27;s license</div><b>δίσεκτο έτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δίσεκτα έτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leap year</div><b>δίσκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δίσκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. record, disc or disk.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. disk</div>
  <div class='def'>3. discus</div>
  <div class='def'>4. tray</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίσκος.</div><b>δίφθογγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δίφθογγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diphthong</div><b>δίφυλλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίφυλλη, neuter δίφυλλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bifoliate, two-leaved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δίφυλλος. By surface analysis, δι- (di-, “two”) + φύλλο + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix).</div><b>δίχτυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δίχτυα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. net (mesh of string, cord or rope)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. net (device)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) net (trap, usually in plural)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δίχτυ(ν)), from Ancient Greek δίκτυον. Doublet of δίκτυο.</div><b>δίχως</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with accusative) without</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δίχως</b> άλλο - without doubt, certainly</div><b>δίψα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) thirst</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) lust, craving, thirst, eagerness</div><b>δίωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δίωρη, neuter δίωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) two hours, lasting two hours</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δί- (δις), “twice”) + ώρα + -ος (-os)</div><b>δαίμονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δαίμονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δαίμων.</div><b>δαγκάθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of δαγκώθηκα</div>
  <div class='ex'>first-person singular simple past passive of δαγκώνω (dagkóno)</div><b>δαγκάνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of δαγκώνομαι</div>
  <div class='ex'>passive of δαγκάνω (dagkáno)</div><b>δαγκάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δαγκώνω">δαγκώνω</a> to bite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δαγκάνω. Also see etymology of δαγκώνω.</div><b>δαγκώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to bite</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δαγκώνω</b> το μήλο. - I bite the apple.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) to bite; to be vindictive</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in passive voice) mainly δαγκώθηκα (past), and rarely δαγκώνομαι (present) to bite oneself; contain, hold inside, avoid expressing oneself</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δαγκώθηκα</b> για να μην κλάψω. - I bit [my lips] myself, not to cry.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in passive voice) mainly δαγκώθηκα (past), and rarely δαγκώνομαι (present) to bite oneself; reacting to a gaffe, a blooper, or to bad, unexpected news</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δαγκώθηκα</b> όταν ρώτησα την ηλικία της. Τι γκάφα ήταν αυτή! - I bit [my lips] myself, when I asked what her age was. What a gaffe!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek δαγκάνω with metaplasm after -ώνω verbs. From the root of δάκνω, from Proto-Indo-European *denḱ-.
Or from Byzantine Greek δακώνω, based on the ancient aorist ἔδακον, with the voicing of [k] influenced by Hellenistic Koine δαγκάνω.
Also see Etymology of δαγκάνω.</div><b>δαγκώνω τη γλώσσα μου</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://δαγκώνω">δαγκώνω</a>, <a href="bword://τη">τη</a> (ti), <a href="bword://γλώσσα">γλώσσα</a>, <a href="bword://μου">μου</a> (mou).</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δάγκωσα τη γλώσσα μου</b> κατά λάθος. - I bit my tongue accidentally.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) to bite one&#x27;s tongue, bite one&#x27;s lip (to forcibly prevent oneself from speaking in order to avoid saying something inappropriate or likely to cause a dispute)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προσβλήθηκα απ’ ο,τί είπε, αλλά <b>δάγκωσα τη γλώσσα μου</b> για να μην γίνει καβγάς. - I was insulted by what he said but I bit my tongue so there wouldn&#x27;t be a fight.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French se mordre la langue.</div><b>δαδούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δαδούχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>torchbearer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δᾳδοῦχος. By surface analysis, δάδα + -ούχος.</div><b>δαιδαλώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δαιδαλώδης, neuter δαιδαλώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>labyrinthine (resembling a labyrinth)</div><b>δαιμονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δαιμονισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demonic possession (control over a person by a supposed demonic presence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δαιμονισμός, from δαίμων + -ισμός.</div><b>δαιμονοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demonization, demonisation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δαιμονοποιώ + -ση (-si) or δαίμον(ας)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποίηση.</div><b>δαιμονοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to demonize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δαίμον(ας)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>δακράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δακράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>primrose, oxlip</div><b>δακρυϊκός πόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δακρυϊκοί πόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lacrimal gland, tear duct</div><b>δακρύζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to tear up, to get teary-eyed, to become tearful, to weep</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive) to drip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δακρύζω, from Ancient Greek δακρύω.</div><b>δακτυλίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δακτυλίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δαχτυλίδι">δαχτυλίδι</a></div><b>δακτυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δακτυλική, neuter δακτυλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. digital</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dactylic</div>
  <div class='ex'>δακτυλικό εξάμετρο (dactylic hexameter)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δακτυλικός. By surface analysis, δάχτυλο + -ικός.</div><b>δακτυλογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δακτυλογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>typist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French dactylographe (“secretary”).</div><b>δακτυλογραφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to type, use a typewriter</div><b>δακτύλιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δακτύλιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. annular shape</div>
  <div class='def'>2. annular structure in the mammalian digestive tract</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ring</div>
  <div class='ex'>δακτύλιοι του Κρόνου - rings of Saturn</div>
  <div class='def'>4. algebraic structure</div>
  <div class='def'>5. annular part of worm, insect or arthropod</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δακτύλιος, diminutive of δάκτυλος.</div><b>δαλτονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δαλτονισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Daltonism, colour blindness (UK), color blindness (US), achromatopia</div><b>δαλτωνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δαλτωνισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δαλτονισμός">δαλτονισμός</a></div><b>δαμάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be tamed</div><b>δαμάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to tame, subdue, control</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δαμάζω.</div><b>δαμάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δαμάλες, masculine δαμάλι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) heifer, young cow</div><b>δαμάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δαμάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δαμάλι(ν)), from Koine Greek δαμάλιον, from a diminutive of Ancient Greek δάμαλις, from δαμάζω.</div><b>δαμάσκηνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δαμάσκηνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plum (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δαμάσκηνον, from Koine Greek Δαμασκός.</div><b>δαμέ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot dialect form of <a href="bword://εδώ">εδώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Έλα <b>δαμέ</b> γλήορα. - Come over here quickly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐδά + Ancient Greek χαμαί.</div><b>δαμαλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δαμαλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hummingbird hawkmoth (Macroglossum stellatarum)</div><b>δαμαλίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δαμαλίδες, masculine δαμάλι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. heifer, young cow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. smallpox vaccine</div><b>δαμαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>To administer a cowpox vaccine to; to immunize against cowpox.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δαμαλίδα + -ίζω.</div><b>δαμασκηνής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δαμασκηνής, neuter δαμασκηνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plum, damson (color/colour)</div><b>δαμασκηνί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δαμασκηνιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plum, damson (color/colour)</div><b>δανέζικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Danish (the language of Denmark and its people)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δανέζικος">δανέζικος</a></div><b>δανέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δανέζικη, neuter δανέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Danish (related to the country, people or language)</div><b>δανείζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to borrow</div><b>δανείζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to lend, to loan</div><b>δανείστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δανείστριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lender</div><b>δανειολήπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δανειολήπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>borrower</div><b>δανειολήπτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δανειολήπτριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>borrower</div><b>δανεισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δανεισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loan, lending, borrowing</div><b>δανειστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δανειστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lender</div><b>δανικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Danish (the language of Denmark and its people)</div><b>δανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δανική, neuter δανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Danish (related to the country, people or language)</div><b>δαπάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δαπάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. expenditure, outlay</div>
  <div class='def'>2. expenses, costs</div>
  <div class='def'>3. payment</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) energy, time spent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek δαπάνη.</div><b>δασεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δασείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rough breathing, spiritus asper (Ancient Greek diacritical mark)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek δασεῖα, substantivised feminine form of the ancient δασύς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://δασύς">δασύς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>δασικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δασική, neuter δασικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forest, wood, wooded</div><b>δασκάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δασκάλες, masculine δάσκαλος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female teacher (especially one in a primary or elementary school)</div><b>δασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. excise duty, duty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. import tariff, tariff</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δασμός, from δαίω.</div><b>δασολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forestry (the science of planting and growing trees in forests)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δάσ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -λογία.First attested in 1843.</div><b>δασοπονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δασοπονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>silviculture, forestry (the art and practice of cultivating, exploiting and renewing forests for commercial purposes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δασοπόν(ος)) + -ία, a calque of German Forstwirtschaft. First attested in 1891.</div><b>δασοφύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δασοφύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forester, forest ranger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δάσος + -ο- (-o-) + φύλακας, a calque of French garde forestier.</div><b>δασυουρόμορφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Dasyuromorphia, an order of mammals.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Piece-for-piece calque of translingual Dasyuromorphia, as δασύς + ουρά + μορφή + -α (-a, neuter plural substantive suffix).</div><b>δασυποδόμορφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Cingulata.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, δασύς + πόδι + μορφή.</div><b>δασόκοτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δασόκοτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hazel grouse (Tetrastes bonasia)</div><b>δασύλλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δασύλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://δάσος">δάσος</a> n: copse, coppice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δάσ(ος)) + -ύλλιο.</div><b>δασύπους</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δασύποδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>armadillo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δᾰσῠ́πους.</div><b>δασύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δασεία, neuter δασύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aspirated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δασύς.</div><b>δασώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δασώδης, neuter δασώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forested, wooded</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, δάσος + -ώδης.</div><b>δατούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δατούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ντατούρα">ντατούρα</a></div><b>δαφνόφυλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δαφνόφυλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bay leaf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δαφνόφυλλον. By surface analysis, δάφν(η)) + -ο- (-o-) + φύλλο.</div><b>δαχτυλήθρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δαχτυλήθρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thimble (cup-shaped cap worn on the tip of a finger)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thimble, thimbleful (a small amount of liquid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δακτυλήθρα. Compare English dactylethra.</div><b>δαχτυλίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δαχτυλίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ring for the finger</div>
  <div class='def'>2. something shaped like a ring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δαχτυλίδιον, from Koine Greek δακτυλίδιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek δακτύλιον, itself a diminutive of δάκτυλος.</div><b>δαχτυλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δαχτυλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smudge, mark (made by fingers)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην αφήνεις <b>δαχτυλιές</b> στην τηλεόραση! - Don&#x27;t leave finger marks on the television!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δάχτυλο + -ιά.</div><b>δαχτυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δαχτυλική, neuter δαχτυλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δακτυλικός">δακτυλικός</a></div><b>δαχτυλογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δακτυλογράφος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δακτυλογράφος">δακτυλογράφος</a></div><b>δαύτος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine (feminine δαύτn, neuter δαύτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) this (often used in a derogatory, disparaging manner; often in the accusative)</div><b>δε</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. on the other hand used together with <a href="bword://μεν">μεν</a> (men)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο μεν γαμπρός ήταν ευτυχισμένος, η <b>δε</b> νύφη έκλαιγε. - On the one hand, the groom was happy, on the other hand the bride was crying.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. also, but also (introductin a new discussion subject)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω <b>δε</b> να προσθέσω ότι … - Also I wish to add that …</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in transitional use) especially</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάντοτε ήταν επιθετικός. Τώρα <b>δε</b>, που άρχισε να πίνει, έχει γίνει επικίνδυνος. - He has always been aggressive. And/especially now, that he started drinking, he has become dangerous.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δέ.</div><b>δείγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δείγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sample, specimen, token</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κανένα <b>δείγμα</b> δεν πρέπει να έχει τιμή άνω των 20 ευρώ. - No sample may have a value exceeding 20 euros.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δεῖγμα from δείκνυμι + -μα (-ma).</div><b>δείκτες νοημοσύνης</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://δείκτης">δείκτης</a> νοημοσύνης</div><b>δείκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δείκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indicator, quotient, measure (something indicating a value)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δείκτης</b> νοημοσύνης - intelligence quotient</div>
  <div class='def'>2. index finger, forefinger</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pointer of an instrument</div>
  <div class='def'>4. subscript</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δείκτης and semantic loan from French indice, indicateur and German Anzeiger, Anzeige.
The sense &quot;index finger&quot; from Koine δεικτικός by ellipsis of the noun δάκτυλος.</div><b>δείκτης νοημοσύνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δείκτες νοημοσύνης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intelligence quotient</div><b>δείνα</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) such-and-such, so-and-so</div><b>δείξε μου τους φίλους σου, να σου πω ποιος είσαι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a man is known by the company he keeps</div><b>δείπνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δείπνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evening meal, supper, dinner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δεῖπνον.</div><b>δείχνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to show off</div><b>δείχνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to indicate, point out, point to</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to demonstrate, show how</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to seem</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Medieval Byzantine Greek δείχνω, from Ancient Greek δείκνυμι, δεικνύω.</div><b>δεδικασμένο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεδικασμένα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>precedent (prior judgment)</div><b>δεδομένο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεδομένα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>datum, item of data, piece of information</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://δεδομένος">δεδομένος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δεδομένος">δεδομένος</a></div><b>δεδομένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεδομένη, neuter δεδομένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. given</div>
  <div class='def'>2. granted</div><b>δεδομένου ότι</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>since, given that</div><b>δειγματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sample, take a sample</div><b>δειγματοληπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δειγματοληπτική, neuter δειγματοληπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sample, sampling (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δειγματ-) + -ο- (-o-) + Ancient Greek ληπτικός, a loose calque of French échantillonneur.</div><b>δειγματοληψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sampling (the process or technique of obtaining a representative sample)</div>
  <div class='ex'>τυχαία <b>δειγματοληψία</b> - random sampling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δειγματ-) + -ο- (-o-) + -ληψία, a calque of French prise d&#x27;échantillons.</div><b>δεικνύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://δείχνω">δείχνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δείκνυμι and δεικνύω.</div><b>δεικτικές αντωνυμίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of δεικτική <a href="bword://αντωνυμία">αντωνυμία</a></div><b>δεικτική αντωνυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεικτικές αντωνυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(demonstrative, pronoun) demonstrative pronoun</div><b>δεικτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεικτική, neuter δεικτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indicating</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (demonstrative) demonstrative</div>
  <div class='ex'>δεικτική αντωνυμία (demonstrative pronoun)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek δεικτῐκός.</div><b>δειλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to show cowardice, quail</div><b>δειλινό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δειλινά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dusk, nightfall, sunset (U.K.), sundown (U.S.), evening twilight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. afternoon</div><b>δειλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δειλή, neuter δειλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cowardly, pusillanimous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. timid, timorous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δειλός.</div><b>δεινά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δεινός">δεινός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hardships, plight, suffering (dire situation; the circumstances or events causing this)</div><b>δεινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. terrible, horrible, fearful, astounding</div>
  <div class='def'>2. skillful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δεινός.</div><b>δεινόσαυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δεινόσαυροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dinosaur</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) dinosaur</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowing from English dinosaur, itself coined from Ancient Greek.</div><b>δεισιδαίμων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεισιδαίμων, neuter δεισιδαίμον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>superstitious</div><b>δεισιδαιμονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>superstition (especially belief in the supernatural negative influence of random events or things)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: πρόληψη f (prólipsi)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δεισιδαιμονία, from Ancient Greek δεισιδαίμων.</div><b>δεκάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεκάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ten units of something</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a set of ten</div><b>δεκάλεπτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεκάλεπτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ten-minutes (a period of that length)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. quarter (a period into which a game is divided)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύο <b>δεκάλεπτα</b> του έδωσαν τη νίκη! - Two quarters gave victory!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ten cents (of euro)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. ten lepta (old money)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://δεκάλεπτος">δεκάλεπτος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δεκάλεπτος">δεκάλεπτος</a></div><b>δεκάλεπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεκάλεπτή, neuter δεκάλεπτος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ten minute</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δεκάλεπτο</b> διάλειμμα - a ten-minute break</div><b>δεκάξι</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sixteen, colloquial form of <a href="bword://δεκαέξι">δεκαέξι</a></div><b>δεκάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεκάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ten-cent coin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ten-cent coin; dime (a coin worth one-tenth of a dollar)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dime, penny, brass farthing (a small amount of money, of trivial value)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. ten-day punishment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δέκ(α)) + -άρα.</div><b>δεκαέξι</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sixteen</div><b>δεκαδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεκαδική, neuter δεκαδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decimal</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δεκαδικό</b> αριθμός - decimal number</div><b>δεκαεννέα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nineteen</div><b>δεκαεννιά</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nineteen</div><b>δεκαεξαδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεκαεξαδική, neuter δεκαεξαδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hexadecimal, sexadecimal</div><b>δεκαεπτά</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seventeen</div><b>δεκαετία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεκαετίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decade (a period of ten years)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δεκαετία, from δέκα + ἔτος.</div><b>δεκαεφτά</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seventeen</div><b>δεκανέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δεκανείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corporal (the NATO military rank OR-4)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δεκανός, from Ancient Greek δέκα. First attested 1833.</div><b>δεκαοκτώ</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eighteen</div><b>δεκαοχτούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεκοχτούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>collared dove</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δεκαοχτώ.</div><b>δεκαοχτώ</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eighteen</div><b>δεκαπέντε</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fifteen</div><b>δεκαπενθήμερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεκαπενθήμερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fifteen days, two weeks, a fortnight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δεκαπέντε + ημέρα + -ο (-o, neuter ending).</div><b>δεκαπλασιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δεκαπλασιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decupling, tenfolding</div><b>δεκατέσσερα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable, noun, numeral) fourteen</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of <a href="bword://δεκατέσσερις">δεκατέσσερις</a></div><b>δεκατέσσερις</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, numeral) fourteen</div><b>δεκατρία</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable, noun, numeral) thirteen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δεκατρία. By surface analysis, δέκα + τρία.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek дъэкатри́я, дъекатри́я.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of <a href="bword://δεκατρείς">δεκατρείς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δεκατρία. By surface analysis, δέκα + τρία.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek дъэкатри́я, дъекатри́я.</div><b>δεκατρείς</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, numeral) thirteen</div><b>δεκατόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεκατόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decimetre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, δέκατος + μέτρο.</div><b>δεκοχτούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεκοχτούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δεκαοχτούρα">δεκαοχτούρα</a></div><b>δελεάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to bait, lure, tempt, entice, allure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to seduce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek δελεάζω, derived from δέλεαρ</div><b>δελεαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δελεαστική, neuter δελεαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tempting, enticing, alluring, seductive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δελεαστικός. By surface analysis, δελεασ-) + -τικός.</div><b>δελτία αστυνομικής ταυτότητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://δελτίο">δελτίο</a> αστυνομικής ταυτότητας</div><b>δελτίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δελτία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. card, form, document</div>
  <div class='def'>2. index card</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bulletin, information issued to the public by an agency or an authority</div>
  <div class='ex'>το δελτίο καιρού - the weather bulletin/forecast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δελτίον, hypocoristic diminutive of δέλτος.</div><b>δελτίο αστυνομικής ταυτότητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δελτία αστυνομικής ταυτότητας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ID card, identity card, identity document</div><b>δελτίο ταυτότητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δελτία ταυτότητας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ID card, identity card, identity document</div><b>δελφίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δελφίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dolphin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (capitalized, sometimes) hydrofoil (the name attached to some services)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an unattested Byzantine Greek *δελφίνιον, diminutive of Koine Greek δελφίν.</div><b>δελφίνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δελφίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>larkspur (Delphinium)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δελφίνιον.</div><b>δεμάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεμάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bale, sheaf (of hay, straw, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the mediaeval Byzantine Greek δεμάτιον, δεμάτιν, δεμάτι from Koine Greek δεμάτιον, diminutive of δέμα. From δέω.</div><b>δεμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine δεμένη, neuter δεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tied</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα χέρια μου είναι <b>δεμένα</b>. - My hands are tied.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of δένομαι, passive voice of δένω (“I tie”).</div><b>δεν είμαστε καλά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>no way, you must be joking / kidding, good heavens, holy cow (expression of shock and/or disbelief)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα φύγεις από την δουλειά σου και θα ταξιδέψεις; Ε <b>δεν είμαστε καλά</b>. - You&#x27;re going to quit your job and go travelling? You must be joking.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “we are not well”.</div><b>δεν καταλαβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I don&#x27;t understand.</div><b>δεν ξέρω</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I don&#x27;t know.</div><b>δεν υπάρχει καπνός χωρίς φωτιά</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>no smoke without fire</div><b>δενδρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δενδρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dendrite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δενδρίτης. By surface analysis, δένδρο + -ίτης.</div><b>δενδρολίβανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δενδρολίβανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rosemary (shrub and herb) (Salvia rosmarinus, syn. Rosmarinus officinalis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek δενδρολίβανον.</div><b>δενδροχρονολόγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δενδροχρονολογήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dendrochronology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δένδρο + χρονολόγηση.</div><b>δενδρόκεδρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δενδρόκεδρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Syrian juniper (Juniperus drupacea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Koine Greek δενδρόκεδρον. By surface analysis, δένδρο + κέδρο.</div><b>δενδρύλλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δενδρύλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sapling (young tree)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δένδρ(ο)) + -ύλλιο.</div><b>δεντρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεντριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) tree</div><b>δεντρολίβανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεντρολίβανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δενδρολίβανο">δενδρολίβανο</a></div><b>δεντροτσομπανάκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://δεντροτσομπανάκος">δεντροτσομπανάκος</a></div><b>δεντροτσομπανάκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δεντροτσομπανάκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative pronunciation of <a href="bword://δεντροτσοπανάκος">δεντροτσοπανάκος</a>, from the variant τσομπάνος of τσοπάνος</div><b>δεντροτσοπανάκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://δεντροτσοπανάκος">δεντροτσοπανάκος</a></div><b>δεντροτσοπανάκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δεντροτσοπανάκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nuthatch (Sitta europaea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δεντρο- + τσοπανάκος, diminutive of τσοπάνος m, a variant of τσοπάνης m.</div><b>δεντρόσπιτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεντρόσπιτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tree house</div><b>δεξής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεξιά, neuter δεξί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alternative form of <a href="bword://δεξιός">δεξιός</a></div><b>δεξαμενές σκέψης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://δεξαμενή">δεξαμενή</a> σκέψης</div><b>δεξαμενή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεξαμενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tank (container)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reservoir</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δεξαμενή.</div><b>δεξαμενή σκέψης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεξαμενές σκέψης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>think tank</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English think tank.</div><b>δεξιά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>right</div>
  <div class='ex'>στρίψε <b>δεξιά</b> - turn right</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δεξιός + -ά.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(capitalized, often, uncountable) the Right</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δεξιός + -ά.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://δεξιός">δεξιός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δεξιός">δεξιός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δεξιός + -ά.</div><b>δεξιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεξιά, neuter δεξιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. right; right-handed</div>
  <div class='ex'>alternative variant: δεξής m (dexís), δεξιά f (dexiá), δεξί n (dexí)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of the right / right-wing</div>
  <div class='ex'>see also: συντηρητικός (syntiritikós, “conservative”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δεξιός. Sense for politics (also substantivised), semantic loan from French droit.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δεξιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>right-winger</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός είναι <b>δεξιός</b> και ο αδερφός του είναι αριστερός. - He is a right-winger and his brother is left-winger.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δεξιός. Sense for politics (also substantivised), semantic loan from French droit.</div><b>δεξιόστροφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεξιόστροφη, neuter δεξιόστροφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clockwise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dextrorotatory</div><b>δεξιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεξιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skill, ability, craft, prowess</div><b>δεοντολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεοντολογική, neuter δεοντολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deontological, ethical</div><b>δερβένι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) narrows, usually referring to a narrow mountain pass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish دربند (dervent, derbent, “narrows, pass; derbend”), from Classical Persian دربند (darband, “mountain or road pass, narrows”).</div><b>δερματολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) dermatology</div><b>δερματολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δερματολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dermatologist</div><b>δερματοστιξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tattoo (skin marking)</div><b>δεσμά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chains, shackles</div>
  <div class='def'>2. imprisonment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) ties, bonds</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα δεσμά του γάμου (the shackles of marriage)</div><b>δεσμεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to bind (to oblige, restrain, or hold, by authority, law, duty, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to commit (to pledge or bind)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to freeze (to prevent the movement or liquidation of a person&#x27;s financial assets)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δεσμεύω with semantic loan from French lier and English bind.</div><b>δεσμοφύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δεσμοφύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prison officer, jailer, warder, gaoler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δεσμοφύλαξ</div><b>δεσμωτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεσμωτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prison, cell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. house of bondage</div><b>δεσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δεσμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bond, tie (emotional, social)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bond</div>
  <div class='def'>3. relationship, affair</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural) imprisonment, chains</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δεσμός.</div><b>δεσποινίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεσποινίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. young woman, girl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. miss, Miss address for young, unmarried women</div><b>δεσποινίς</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δεσποινίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. miss, young unmarried woman</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς μεγάλωσε η Σοφία! Είναι πια μια μικρή <b>δεσποινίς</b>! - How she has grown up! She is now a little miss!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Miss (title of respect for young unmarried women)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>δεσποινίς</b> Μαρία. - Miss Mary.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. miss, missy (mode of address for young unmarried women)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δεσποινίς</b>! Ελάτε παρακαλώ! - Miss! Come here, please!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δεσποινίς, from Ancient Greek δέσποινα + -ις (-is).</div><b>δεσποτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεσποτική, neuter δεσποτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>despotic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δεσποτικός. By surface analysis, δεσπότης + -ικός.</div><b>δεσποτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) despotism, autocracy</div><b>δεσπόζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dominate, domineer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δεσπόζω from δεσπότης.</div><b>δεσπότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δεσπότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. despot, tyrant (ruler with absolute power)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν πήρε εξουσία, ονόμασε τον εαυτό του <b>δεσπότη</b>. - When he took power, he named himself as despot.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bishop</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την ευλογία σου, <b>δέσποτα</b>! - Your blessing, please, Bishop!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Lord (term of address for God)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ευλόγησον, <b>δέσποτα</b>! - Bless us, O Lord!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. master, ruler</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (historical) despot (title of a Byzantine regional ruler)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δεσπότης.</div><b>δετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δετική, neuter δετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to tying/binding</div><b>δευτεραγωνιστές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of δευτεραγωνιστής</div><b>δευτεραγωνιστή</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive/accusative/vocative singular of δευτεραγωνιστής</div><b>δευτεραγωνιστών</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of δευτεραγωνιστής</div><b>δευτερεύων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δευτερεύουσα, neuter δευτερεύον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. secondary, subsidiary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subordinate (of sentences)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δευτερεύουσα πρόταση - subordinate clause</div><b>δευτεροστόμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δευτεροστόμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deuterostome (Deuterostomia)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from δευτεροστόμια, from translingual Deuterostomia.</div><b>δευτερόλεπτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δευτερόλεπτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second (unit of time)</div><b>δεύτερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δεύτερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second (unit of time)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://δεύτερος">δεύτερος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δεύτερος">δεύτερος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δεύτερος">δεύτερος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δεύτερος">δεύτερος</a></div><b>δεύτερον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>secondly</div><b>δεύτερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δεύτερη or δευτέρα, neuter δεύτερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθε <b>δεύτερος</b> στο Μαραθώνιο. - He came second in the marathon.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inferior, seconds</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δεύτερης</b> ποιότητας - inferior quality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δεύτερος.</div><b>δηλαδή</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. specifically, namely, that is to say</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η χώρα μας, <b>δηλαδή</b> η Ελλάδα. - Our country, namely Greece.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. i.e., namely, in other words</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα αλκάνια είναι κορεσμένοι αλειφατικοί (<b>δηλαδή</b> μη κυκλικοί) υδρογονάνθρακες. - Alkanes are saturated aliphatic (i.e. acyclic) hydrocarbons.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. so, therefore</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δηλαδή</b> τι θες να κάνω εγώ; - So what do you want me to do, then?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δηλαδή, univerbation of δῆλά δη, from δῆλᾰ”) + δή.</div><b>δηλητήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δηλητήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poison, venom, toxin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δηλητήρῐον, substantivized neuter of δηλητήριος.</div><b>δηλητηριώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δηλητηριώδης, neuter δηλητηριώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poisonous</div><b>δηλωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine δηλωμένη, neuter δηλωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. declared</div>
  <div class='def'>2. registered</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of δηλώνομαι, passive voice of δηλώνω (“declare”).</div><b>δηλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to state, declare</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to profess, declare</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to certify</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to admit</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to reveal, manifest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δηλώνω from Ancient Greek δηλῶ, contracted form of δηλόω.</div><b>δημήτριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cerium</div><b>δημαγωγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δημαγωγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demagogue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek δημαγωγός.</div><b>δημαγωγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to speak (like a demagogue)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to orate</div><b>δημαρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημαρχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mayoralty (office or term of office of a mayor)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό χτίστηκε υπό την προηγούμενη <b>δημαρχία</b>. - That was built under the previous mayoralty.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) town hall, city hall (building that houses the government offices of a municipality, town or city)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αύριο θα υπάρξει μια μικρή διαδήλωση έξω από τη <b>δημαρχία</b>. - Tomorrow, there&#x27;ll be a small protest outside the town hall.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δημαρχία, equivalent to δήμαρχος + -ία.</div><b>δημαρχίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημαρχίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) mayor, mayoress (leader of a city, town or a municipality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>δημαρχίνα</b> διαδέχτηκε τον άντρα της. - The mayor succeeded her husband.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) mayoress (wife of the mayor)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να με πούνε <b>δημαρχίνα</b> κι ας ψοφάω από την πείνα - Let them call me mayoress, even if I&#x27;m dying of hunger.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δήμαρχος + -ίνα. First attested 1893.</div><b>δημαρχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δημαρχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>town hall, city hall (building that houses the government offices of a municipality, town or city)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να πάω αύριο στο <b>δημαρχείο</b> να βρω το πιστοποιητικό γέννησής μου. - I have to go down to the town hall tomorrow to find my birth certificate.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δήμαρχ- + -είο. First attested 1832.</div><b>δημαρχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δημαρχική, neuter δημαρχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) mayoral, mayoric (of a mayor)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, δήμαρχος + -ικός.</div><b>δημεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to confiscate (by the government)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek δημεύω, from δῆμος.</div><b>δημητριακά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cereal (a member of that class of grains)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breakfast cereal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek δημητριακά, neuter of δημητριακός, from Ancient Greek Δημήτηρ”).</div><b>δημιουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημιουργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>creation</div><b>δημιουργημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine δημιουργημένη, neuter δημιουργημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>created</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of δημιουργούμαι, passive voice of δημιουργώ (“create”).</div><b>δημιουργικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δημιουργική, neuter δημιουργικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>creative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δημιουργικός. By surface analysis, δημιουργός + -ικός.</div><b>δημιουργικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημιουργικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>creativity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δημιουργικός + -ότητα.</div><b>δημιουργούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be created</div><b>δημιουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δημιουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. creator of original works, craftsman, artisan</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Demiurge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δημιουργός. Synchronically analysable as being from stem δημι-, from δῆμος + -ουργός, from the root of έργο.</div><b>δημιουργώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to create, make, build, generate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek δημιουργῶ, contracted form of δημιουργέω.</div><b>δημιούργημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δημιουργήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>creation (the act of creation or the thing created)</div><b>δημογέροντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δημογέροντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>village elders, demogeron</div><b>δημογεροντία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημογεροντίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demogerontia</div><b>δημοκράτης</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>democrat</div><b>δημοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημοκρατίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. democracy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. republic</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ελληνική <b>Δημοκρατία</b> - Hellenic Republic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δημοκρᾰτῐ́ᾱ. By surface analysis, δημο- (dimo-, “from δῆμος the people”) + -κρατία.</div><b>δημοκρατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δημοκρατική, neuter δημοκρατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. democratic, liberal, pertaining to democracy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. republican</div><b>δημοπρασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημοπρασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>auction (public sales event)</div><b>δημοσίευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δημοσιεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>publication, article</div>
  <div class='ex'>είχε πολλά <b>δημοσιεύματα</b> - he had many publications</div><b>δημοσίευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημοσιεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. publication, disclosure, communication (the act of publication)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>δημοσίευση</b> των αποτελεσμάτων - the publication of the scores</div>
  <div class='def'>2. publication, literature (published material)</div>
  <div class='ex'>επιστημονικές <b>δημοσιεύσεις</b> σε περιοδικά - the scientific literature in journals</div><b>δημοσιευμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine δημοσιευμένη, neuter δημοσιευμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>published</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of δημοσιεύομαι, passive voice of δημοσιεύω (“publish”).</div><b>δημοσιεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to publish, print</div><b>δημοσιογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δημοσιογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>journalist, reporter</div><b>δημοσιογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) journalism</div><b>δημοσιονομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δημοσιονομική, neuter δημοσιονομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>public finance (attributive), budgetary, fiscal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δημοσιονομ(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>δημοσιοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disclosure, publication (the communication of information to the general public)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δημοσιοποιώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>δημοσιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>publicity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δημόσι(ος)) + -ότητα, a loose calque of French publicité.</div><b>δημοσκόπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημοσκοπήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>opinion poll, survey, (colloquially) Gallup poll</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προεκλογικά, γίνονται πολλές <b>δημοσκοπήσεις</b>. - Before elections, many polls are conducted.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δημο- (dimo-, “demo-”) + -σκόπηση or a learned borrowing from German Demoskopie. See δήμος m &amp; σκοπός m. A word creation with Greek combining forms to replace the loanword γκάλοπ n.
The initial sense of the synonym σφυγμομέτρηση f -attested since 1891- was &quot;measuring pulsation&quot;.</div><b>δημοτικά σχολεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://δημοτικό">δημοτικό</a> <a href="bword://σχολείο">σχολείο</a></div><b>δημοτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) any vernacular form of a language, in contrast to learned varieties</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) shortened form of <a href="bword://δημοτική">δημοτική</a> <a href="bword://γλώσσα">γλώσσα</a> f</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Reborrowing from French démotique (“initially referring to Egyptian language and script of Hellenistic times”). From Ancient Greek δημοτικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://δημοτικός">δημοτικός</a>: of the people</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δημοτική</b> αρχή - municipal authority</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>δημοτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δημοτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>primary school</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://δημοτικός">δημοτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δημοτικός">δημοτικός</a></div><b>δημοτικό σχολείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δημοτικά σχολεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>primary school, elementary school</div><b>δημοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δημοτική, neuter δημοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. municipal</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δημοτική</b> αρχή - municipal authority</div>
  <div class='def'>2. folk, traditional</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δημοτική</b> μουσική - folk music</div>
  <div class='def'>3. demotic, of the people</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δημοτικός, from δημότης, from δῆμος. For music, songs: calque of German Volkslieder.</div><b>δημοτικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δημοτικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>popularity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δημοτικός + -ότητα, a calque of French popularité.</div><b>δημοφιλής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δημοφιλής, neuter δημοφιλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>popular, likeable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δῆμος + φιλέω + -ος (-os) i.e. &quot;of one who is loved by the people&quot;.</div><b>δημοψήφισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δημοψηφίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>referendum, plebiscite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French plébiscite. Morphologically from δημο- (dimo-, “demo- from δῆμος”) + ψήφισμα.</div><b>δημωνύμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δημωνύμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demonym</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Internationalism. Compare English demonym.</div><b>δημόσια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>publicly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://δημόσιος">δημόσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δημόσιος">δημόσιος</a></div><b>δημόσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) the state in general, especially in its relation to private citizens</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://δημόσιος">δημόσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δημόσιος">δημόσιος</a></div><b>δημόσιοι υπάλληλοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://δημόσιος">δημόσιος</a> <a href="bword://υπάλληλος">υπάλληλος</a></div><b>δημόσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δημόσια, neuter δημόσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>public</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δημόσια</b> υγεία - public health</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δημόσῐος. The sense as in the neuter τὸ δημόσιον.</div><b>δημόσιος υπάλληλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δημόσιοι υπάλληλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>civil servant, public servant</div><b>δημώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δημώδης, neuter δημώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. demotic, vernacular</div>
  <div class='ex'>στη <b>δημώδη</b> γλώσσα - in the demotic language</div>
  <div class='def'>2. folk</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα <b>δημώδη</b> άσματα - folk songs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek δημώδης</div><b>διά</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. by, for, through</div>
  <div class='def'>2. divided by</div>
  <div class='ex'>δέκα <b>διά</b> πέντε ίσον δύο - ten divided by five equals two</div>
  <div class='def'>3. this is the name of the division symbol</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek δῐᾰ́. Doublet of για (gia).</div><b>διά ζώσης</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in person, face to face</div><b>διάβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crossing; pedestrian crossing, zebra crossing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crossing; bridge</div>
  <div class='ex'>ανισόπεδη <b>διάβαση</b> - flyover, overpass</div>
  <div class='def'>3. crossing; ford</div>
  <div class='def'>4. crossing; mountain pass</div><b>διάβαση πεζών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαβάσεις πεζών)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pedestrian crossing</div><b>διάβασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαβάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reading (the process of interpreting written language)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. study</div>
  <div class='def'>3. scriptural recitation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From past stem διαβασ- (diavas-) of verb διαβάζω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>διάβολε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(offensive) (expresses anger, surprise) damn!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διάβολε</b>! Ξέχασα το διαβατήριο! - Damn it! I forgot the passport.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vocative singular of <a href="bword://διάβολος">διάβολος</a></div><b>διάβολος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάβολοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively, literally) The Devil, devil.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>διάβολος</b> ζει στην κόλαση. - The Devil lives in Hell.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, figuratively, literally) Hell, hell, underworld, hellhole</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα πας στον <b>διάβολο</b>! - You&#x27;ll go to hell!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διάβολος.</div><b>διάβρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαβρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corrosion</div><b>διάγγελμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαγγέλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>speech, address (a formal session of speaking, especially a long oral message given publicly by one person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διάγγελμα.</div><b>διάγνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαγνώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diagnosis</div><b>διάγραμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαγράμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diagram</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διάγραμμα.</div><b>διάγραμμα Φάινμαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαγράμματα Φάινμαν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Feynman diagram</div><b>διάγραμμα ροής</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flow chart, flow diagram</div><b>διάδοση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαδόσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dissemination, spreading, transmission (of ideas, messages, news, trends, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. propagation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) rumours (UK), rumors (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διάδοσις, with -ση (-si) ending and semantic loan from German Verbreitung and English spreading (of rumours) in the sense &#x27;rumours&#x27;.</div><b>διάδοχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διάδοχος or διάδοχη, neuter διάδοχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>successor (coming after the other in a series)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>διάδοχο</b> πολιτικό σχήμα στην εξουσία της χώρας - the successor political shape in the country&#x27;s power</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διάδοχος, from Ancient Greek διαδέχομαι. Also substantivised.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διάδοχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. successor (person or thing that immediately follows another in holding an office)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Τραμπ ήταν ο <b>διάδοχος</b> του Ομπάμα. - Trump was Obama&#x27;s successor.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. heir, heiress (someone who is designated to inherit the throne in monarchies)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Πρίγκιπας της Ουαλίας είναι ο <b>διάδοχος</b> του αγγλικού θρόνου. - The Prince of Wales is the heir to the English throne.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) firstborn son, eldest boy (in a family unit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να σας ζήσει ο <b>διάδοχος</b>! - Long live your eldest!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διάδοχος, from Ancient Greek διαδέχομαι. Also substantivised.</div><b>διάδρομος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάδρομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. corridor, passage (in building, train, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passage, way (cleared space through crowd)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. aisle (corridor in a shop with shelves on both sides)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. strait (sea way)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. lane (on athletics running track)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. runway</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διάδρομος</b> απογείωσης - takeoff runway</div>
  <div class='def'>7. treadmill (exercise machine that allows one to walk or run in place)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διά- + δρόμος</div><b>διάθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. disposal (making available money or services or making oneself available)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι παίκτες της ομάδας επέστρεψαν από τις διακοπές τους και τέθηκαν στην <b>διάθεση</b> του προπονητή. - The squad has returned from their holidays and are available to the coach.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mood, disposition</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σήμερα έχασα το ρολόι μου και δεν έχω <b>διάθεση</b> να διασκεδάσω. - I lost my watch today and I am in no mood to enjoy myself.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. spirit</div>
  <div class='ex'>αγωνιστική <b>διάθεση</b> - fighting spirit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διάθεσις.</div><b>διάθλαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαθλάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refraction (the bending of an electromagnetic wave)</div><b>διάκονος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάκονοι, feminine διακόνισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deacon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek διάκονος</div><b>διάκριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διακρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. distinction (the act of distinguishing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. distinction (that which distinguishes)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. discrimination (the act of discriminating, noting or perceiving differences between things, with the intent to understand rightly and make correct decisions)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. discrimination (differential treatment of an individual or group to their disadvantage)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. distinction (a mark of honour, rank, eminence or excellence)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. discretion (the authority to exercise one&#x27;s own judgement in making decisions that have effect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διάκρισις with semantic loan from French distinction, discrimination and discrétion. By surface analysis, διακρί(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>διάκριτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διάκριτη, neuter διάκριτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>discrete, separate, distinct, individual</div><b>διάλειμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαλείμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pause, break, recess (a rest or pause, usually from work); elevenses (morning break)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι μαθητές στο σχολείο κάνουν πέντε <b>διαλείμματα</b> κάθε μέρα. - Students at school have five breaks daily.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intermission, interval of a venue, concert</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>διάλειμμα</b> της θεατρικής παράστασης διαρκεί δέκα λεπτά. - The play&#x27;s interval lasts ten minutes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διάλειμμᾰ. By surface analysis, διαλείπω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>διάλεκτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διάλεκτοι or διάλεκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dialect (of a language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δῐᾰ́λεκτος.</div><b>διάλεξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαλέξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lecture</div><b>διάλογος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάλογοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dialogue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek διάλογος.</div><b>διάλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διάβολος">διάβολος</a></div><b>διάλυμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαλύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>solution (homogeneous mixture of different substances)</div><b>διάλυσα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of διέλυσα</div><b>διάλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαλύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. break-up, dispersal, dismantling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dissolving, dissolution, solution</div>
  <div class='def'>3. liquidation</div><b>διάμεσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διάμεση, neuter διάμεσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intermediate, intervening</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διάμεσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. median</div>
  <div class='def'>2. median (constructed line in a triangle)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. intermediary, go-between</div><b>διάμετρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διάμετροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diameter</div><b>διάναξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) caulking, caulk (material used when caulking)</div><b>διάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>male turkey (bird)</div><b>διάνυσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διανύσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vector</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantic loan from French vecteur. From Hellenistic Koine Greek διάνυσμα. Morphologically, from δι- (δια-) (“to go through”) + άνυσμα (“vector, distance covered”).</div><b>διάολος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάολοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διάβολος">διάβολος</a></div><b>διάπλαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formation (the process during which something comes into being and gains its characteristics)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διάπλασις, with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>διάρθρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαρθρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. structure, layout (how parts of a whole are arranged)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μοριακή <b>διάρθρωση</b> - molecular structure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conformation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek διάρθρωις</div><b>διάρκεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) duration, length</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το τεστ έχει <b>διάρκεια</b> δύο ώρες. - The test has a duration of two hours.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek διάρκεια, from διαρκής. For the modern sense of &quot;duration&quot;, semantic loan from French durée.</div><b>διάρρηξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαρρήξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. burglary, break-in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breach, rupture</div><b>διάρροια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διάρροιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diarrhoea (UK), diarrhea (US) (frequent and very fluid or watery bowel movements)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έφαγε χαλασμένο ψάρι και την έπιασε <b>διάρροια</b>. - She ate a spoiled fish and got diarrhea.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διάρροια.</div><b>διάσειση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασείσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concussion</div><b>διάσημος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διάσημη, neuter διάσημο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>famous, celebrated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διάσημος, from δια- (dia-, “dia-, through”) + σῆμα + -ος (-os).</div><b>διάσκεψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασκέψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conference, meeting</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διάσκεψη</b> για την Κλιματική Αλλαγή - Conference on Climate Change</div><b>διάσπαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. breakdown, breakup, decomposition, fission, splitting (separation of a thing into components)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breakdown, decomposition (breaking of chemical bonds within a compound to produce simpler compounds or elements)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. decay</div>
  <div class='def'>4. disruption (an interruption to the regular flow or sequence of something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διάσπασις with -ση (-si) ending and semantic loan from English fission and disruption.</div><b>διάσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dimension (length, width, height)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stretching, growth, increase</div>
  <div class='def'>3. separation</div>
  <div class='def'>4. astride (position when standing with legs apart sideways)</div><b>διάστημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαστήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interval (period of time)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κατά <b>διαστήματα</b> - at intervals</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interval, space (spatial distance; intervening gap)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. interstellar space</div>
  <div class='def'>4. interval</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διάστημα; morphologically δια- (dia-) + στῆναι + -μα (-ma).</div><b>διάστιχο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leading</div><b>διάσωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rescue</div><b>διάταξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διατάξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. provision (a clause in a legal instrument, a law, etc., providing for a particular matter)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. agenda (a list of matters to be taken up (as at a meeting))</div>
  <div class='ex'>ημερήσια <b>διάταξη</b> - daily agenda, order of the day</div>
  <div class='def'>3. arrangement, disposition, layout (a particular way in which items are organized and placed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διάταξις for the senses &quot;provision; agenda&quot; and from Ancient Greek διάταξις for the sense &quot;arrangement, disposition, layout&quot;. By surface analysis, διατάσσω + -ξη (-xi).</div><b>διάττοντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάττοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shooting star, falling star, meteor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ellipsis of διάττοντας [αστέρας]. From Ancient Greek διᾴττων”), present participle of διᾴττω.</div><b>διάττοντας αστέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διάττοντες αστέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shooting star, meteor</div><b>διάττοντες αστέρες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://διάττοντας">διάττοντας</a> <a href="bword://αστέρας">αστέρας</a></div><b>διάττων αστήρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://διάττοντας">διάττοντας</a> <a href="bword://αστέρας">αστέρας</a></div><b>διάφορος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διάφορη, neuter διάφορο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) synonym of <a href="bword://διαφορετικός">διαφορετικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διάφορος.</div><b>διάφραγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαφράγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diaphragm (any of various membranes or sheets of muscle or ligament which separate one cavity from another)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diaphragm (any of various membranes or sheets of muscle or ligament which separate one cavity from another); diaphragm (sheet of muscle separating the thorax from the abdomen)</div>
  <div class='ex'>θωρακικό <b>διάφραγμα</b> - thoracic diaphragm</div>
  <div class='def'>3. septum</div>
  <div class='ex'>ρινικό <b>διάφραγμα</b> - nasal septum</div>
  <div class='def'>4. diaphragm (a flexible membrane separating two chambers)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. diaphragm (in a speaker, the thin, semi-rigid membrane which vibrates to produce sound)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. diaphragm (a thin opaque structure with a central aperture, used to limit the passage of light into a camera or similar device)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. diaphragm (a contraceptive device consisting of a flexible cup, used to cover the cervix during intercourse)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διάφραγμα with semantic loan from French diaphragme for non-anatomical technical senses and English diaphragm for the contraceptive device.</div><b>διάχυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) diffusion</div><b>διάχυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διάχυτη, neuter διάχυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diffuse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pervasive, widespread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek διάχυτος, from the διαχυ- stem of Ancient Greek διαχέω + -τος (-tos).</div><b>διέλευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διελεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passage, crossing, transit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. short stay during travel, layover</div><b>διένεξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διενέξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dispute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek διένεξις, based on the διενεγκ- aorist stem of Ancient Greek διαφέρω, with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>διέξοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural έξοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. exit, way out, outlet, thoroughfare</div>
  <div class='def'>2. solution, alternative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διέξοδος, from δια- (dia-) + ἔξοδος.</div><b>διέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past, regional, third person) to be defined by, governed by</div><b>διέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past, regional, third person) to apply, determine, govern</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διέπω.</div><b>διέρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pass by, pass through, go across, traverse, flit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δῐέρχομαι. Morphologically, from δι- (δια-) (“through”) + έρχομαι (“come”).</div><b>διήθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διηθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>filtration, sift</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek διήθησῐς, with -η modern ending. See διηθώ (“I filter”). Also see ἤθησις f, ἡθμός m.</div><b>διήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διήμερη, neuter διήμερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two-day (lasting two days)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek διήμερος. Morphologically, δι- (di-, “δι- δις twice”) + ημέρ(α), “day”) + -ος (-os)</div><b>δια</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>informal script of <a href="bword://διά">διά</a> without accent, as pronounced; like <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia)</div><b>διαίρεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαιρέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. division, partition, splitting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. division</div><b>διαβάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to read (look at and interpret letters or other information that is written)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην τον ενοχλείς όταν <b>διαβάζει</b>. - Don&#x27;t bother him when he&#x27;s reading.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be able to read, be literate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς δεν πήγε σχολείο και γι’ αυτό δεν <b>διαβάζει</b>. - Grandad didn&#x27;t go to school and that&#x27;s why he can&#x27;t read.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to study (review materials already learned in order to make sure one does not forget them)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω πονοκέφαλο και δε θα <b>διαβάσω</b> απόψε. - I&#x27;ve a headache and won&#x27;t be studying tonight.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to coach, instruct (help to study)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αδερφή μου με <b>διαβάζει</b> στη μουσική. - My sister is coaching me in music.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, transitive) to read (be able to recognise or interpret)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διάβασα</b> την αλήθεια στα μάτια της. - I read the truth in her eyes.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to read (speak aloud words, especially during service)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παπάς <b>διαβάζει</b> τα Ευαγγέλια. - The priest reads the Gospels.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (transitive) to bless, exorcize (to confer blessing upon or rid of demons)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθε ο παπάς να <b>διαβάσει</b> τον άρρωστο. - The priest came to bless the sick person.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek διαβάζω, simplification of Ancient Greek διαβιβάζω. By surface analysis, δια- (“through”) + βιβάζω (“cause to mount”), βιβάζω being the causative of βαίνω. Cognate with Mariupol Greek дъавазу.</div><b>διαβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to slander, to calumniate (to baselessly speak ill of; to spread false accusations about)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: δυσφημώ (dysfimó), δυσφημίζω (dysfimízo), κακολογώ (kakologó)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαβάλλω. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + βάλλω.</div><b>διαβάσεις πεζών</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://διάβαση">διάβαση</a> πεζών f</div><b>διαβήτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαβήτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pair of compasses (usually called a compass; an instrument used to draw circles)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diabetes, diabetes mellitus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> For the instrument, learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαβήτης from διαβαίνω.
For medicine, learned borrowing from Koine Greek sense.</div><b>διαβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cross, traverse, go through</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to pass, roll by (of time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek διαβαίνω &lt; δια- (dia-) + βαίνω.</div><b>διαβατήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαβατήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passport (personal travel document)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) passport (something which enables someone to do or achieve something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the singular of Ancient Greek διαβατήρια, nominal formation from διαβατήριος, adjectival formation from διαβαίνω.</div><b>διαβεβαιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to assure (somebody of something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε <b>διαβεβαιώνω</b> ότι είμαι καλύτερα από ποτέ - I assure you that I feel better than ever.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek διαβεβαιῶ, contracted form of διαβεβαιόω. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-, “through”) + βεβαιώνω.</div><b>διαβλέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to foresee, infer, interpret</div><b>διαβολοκόριτσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαβολοκόριτσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>naughty, clever and lively girl</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διαβολο- (diavolo-, “devilish”) + κορίτσι</div><b>διαβολοσκορπίσματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>things scattered</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διάβολος + σκόρπισμα, from: σκορπίζω (“to scatter”)</div><b>διαβρωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαβρωτική, neuter διαβρωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. corrosive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) insidious</div><b>διαβόητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαβόητη, neuter διαβόητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>notorious, infamous</div><b>διαγνωστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαγνωστική, neuter διαγνωστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diagnostic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαγνωστικός. By surface analysis, διάγνωση + -τικός</div><b>διαγνώσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαγνώσιμη, neuter διαγνώσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diagnosable</div><b>διαγουμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) synonym of λεηλατώ</div><b>διαγράμματα Φάινμαν</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://διάγραμμα">διάγραμμα</a> <a href="bword://Φάινμαν">Φάινμαν</a></div><b>διαγράμματα ροής</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://διάγραμμα">διάγραμμα</a> ροής</div><b>διαγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cross out, to strike through, to strike out (draw a line through to mark deletion)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to delete, to erase</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to trace (:curved line, trajectory, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to delineate, to outline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαγράφω, with semantic loan from French décrire. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + γράφω.</div><b>διαγραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαγραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crossing out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deletion, erasure</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cancellation (of debt)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. removal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διαγραφή.</div><b>διαγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) behaviour (UK), behavior (US), conduct</div><b>διαγωνίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to compete, take part in a competition or examination</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαγωνίζομαι. Morphologically, from δια- (“through”) + αγωνίζομαι (“struggle”).</div><b>διαγωνιζόμενη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαγωνιζόμενες, masculine διαγωνιζόμενος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contestant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. candidate (for examination)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Participle of διαγωνίζομαι</div><b>διαγωνιζόμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαγωνιζόμενοι, feminine διαγωνιζόμενη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contestant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. candidate (for examination)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Participle of διαγωνίζομαι</div><b>διαγωνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαγωνισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. competition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. competitive examination</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) examination, exam, test</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαγωνισμός.</div><b>διαγώνισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαγωνίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>test, examination (at secondary/high school level)</div><b>διαδέχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to succeed, follow (to come next after in order)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Γεώργιος Α’ <b>διαδέχθηκε</b> τον Όθωνα το 1863. - George I succeeded Otto in 1863.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαδέχομαι. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-, “through”) + δέχομαι.</div><b>διαδήλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαδηλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demonstration, protest</div><b>διαδίκτυο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαδίκτυα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>internet, the Internet, computer network</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διά + δίκτυο</div><b>διαδανεισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαδανεισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interlibrary loan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δια- (dia-, “inter-”) + δανεισμός.</div><b>διαδεδομένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διαδεδομένη, neuter διαδεδομένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>widespread, prevalent, common</div><b>διαδηλωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαδηλωτές, feminine διαδηλώτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demonstrator, protester</div><b>διαδηλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to demonstrate, protest; literally: &quot;I demonstrate/protest&quot;</div><b>διαδηλώτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαδηλώτριες, masculine διαδηλωτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demonstrator, protester</div><b>διαδικασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαδικασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. procedure, process, protocol</div>
  <div class='def'>2. function, subroutine, procedure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δῐᾰδῐκᾰσῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>διαδικτυακά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://διαδικτυακός">διαδικτυακός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Internet) online</div><b>διαδικτυακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαδικτυακή, neuter διαδικτυακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Internet, attributive) internet (attributive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Internet) online</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διαδίκτυ(ο)) + -ακός.</div><b>διαδοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαδοχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. series, succession (sequence of things in order)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>διαδοχή</b> των γεγονότων που οδήγησαν στον πόλεμο - the series of events that led to the war</div>
  <div class='def'>2. succession (act of following in sequence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>διαδοχή</b> του θρόνου - the succession of the throne</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαδοχή, from Ancient Greek διαδέχομαι.</div><b>διαδοχικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://διαδοχικός">διαδοχικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consecutively, successively, in turn, one after the other</div><b>διαδοχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαδοχική, neuter διαδοχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>successive, consecutive (coming one after the other in a series.)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διαδοχικές</b> νίκες - successive victories</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαδέχομαι. First attested 1833.</div><b>διαδραματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to play a role, act a part</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to play a role</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι μεγάλες δυνάμεις <b>διαδραματίζουν</b> σπουδαίο ρόλο στη διεθνή πολιτική σκηνή. - The great powers play a significant role on the stage of international politics.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to take place, happen</div><b>διαδραστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαδραστική, neuter διαδραστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interactive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δια- (dia-) + δραστικός.</div><b>διαδρομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαδρομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>route, path, drive</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια πολύ ωραία κυκλική <b>διαδρομή</b> βρίσκεται εντός του Διαμερίσματος των Λιμνών. - A beautiful circular route can be found in the Lake District.</div><b>διαζύγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαζύγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>divorce (dissolution of a marriage, separation from politics, etc.)</div><b>διαθέσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαθέσιμη, neuter διαθέσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>available, spare</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation since 1833 of διάθεσις + -ιμος (-imos), formed as a calque of French disponible.</div><b>διαθέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαθέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>testator</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: κληροδότης m (klirodótis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαθέτης.</div><b>διαθέτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to have at one&#x27;s disposal, to have available, to have, to possess</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to have in stock, to stock</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to make available, to place at someone&#x27;s disposal, to provide</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to spend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διατίθημι with semantic loan from French disposer.</div><b>διαθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>will, testament (document)</div><b>διαθλασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διαθλασμένη, neuter διαθλασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refracted, diffracted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διαθλώμαι, passive voice of διαθλώ (“refract”).</div><b>διαθλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to refract, diffract</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to divide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek διαθλάω, from θλάω, of uncertain ultimate origin, but possibly related to Scottish Gaelic dlùth (“close, near, adjacent”).</div><b>διαιθυλαιθέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) diethyl ether</div><b>διαιθυλαμίδιο του λυσεργικού οξέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lysergic acid diethylamide, LSD, acid</div><b>διαιρεμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διαιρεμένη, neuter διαιρεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>divided</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διαιρούμαι, passive voice of διαιρώ (“divide”).</div><b>διαιρετέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαιρετέα, neuter διαιρετέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>divisible, that which can be divided</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαιρετέον n with semantic loan from French dividende. By surface analysis, διαιρώ + -τέος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαιρετέοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dividend (the number to be divided; numerator)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαιρετέον n with semantic loan from French dividende. By surface analysis, διαιρώ + -τέος.</div><b>διαιρετός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαιρετή, neuter διαιρετό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. divisible, capable of being split or divided</div>
  <div class='def'>2. divisible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> 5th century BC (Sophocles) Ancient Greek διαιρετός, from δι- (di-) + αἱρετός, the latter being αἱρέω + -τος (-tos)</div><b>διαιρούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be divided, be disunited</div>
  <div class='ex'>H Kρήτη <b>διαιρείται</b> σε τέσσερις νομούς. - Crete is divided into four nomes.</div><b>διαιρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to divide, split, disunite</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο εμφύλιος πόλεμος <b>διαίρεσε</b> τους Έλληνες. - The Greek civil war divided the Greeks.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαιρῶ contracted form of διαιρέω. Morphologically from δι- (δια-) + αιρώ, the ancient αἱρῶ.</div><b>διαισθάνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sense, intuit, foresee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαισθάνομαι.</div><b>διαιτησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. arbitration</div>
  <div class='def'>2. refereeing, umpiring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διαιτη(τής)) + -σία, a calque of French arbitrage.</div><b>διαιτητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαιτητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. referee (umpire, judge, the supervisor of a game)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. arbitrator</div><b>διαιτητεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to arbitrate (to make a judgment (on a dispute) as an arbitrator or arbiter)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to referee, to umpire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διαιτητ(ής)) + -εύω.</div><b>διαιτητική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαιτητικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dietetics</div><b>διαιτολόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαιτολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diet (predetermined food programme)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diet book</div><b>διαιτολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διαιτολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dietitian</div><b>διαιτώμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal, no past) to feed oneself with a specific diet, be on a diet</div><b>διακήρυξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διακηρύξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>an official proclamation, declaration or announcement</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οικουμενική <b>διακήρυξη</b> των δικαιωμάτων του ανθρώπου - the Universal Declaration of Human Rights</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek διακήρυ(ξις) &lt; (-σις) &lt; (-sis), “sale by auction”) + -ση (-si) &gt; -ξη (-xi), from διακηρύσσω and semantic loan from French proclamation.</div><b>διακίνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. movement, transportation (of goods, people, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trafficking (the illegal trade or exchange of goods)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διακίνηση</b> ναρκωτικών - drug trafficking</div>
  <div class='def'>3. circulation, dissemination (of ideas)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διακίνησις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>διακαμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διακαμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(obsolete) phosphorescence in the sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διακαίω + -μός, from Ancient Greek διακαίω.</div><b>διακανονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διακανονισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>settlement (of a transaction or dispute)</div><b>διακατέχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be gripped, to be possessed</div><b>διακατέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to grip, to possess (of an emotion: to have a strong effect upon, to dominate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διακατέχω. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + κατέχω.</div><b>διακεκριμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διακεκριμένη, neuter διακεκριμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://διακρίνω">διακρίνω</a>:; distinguished (celebrated, well-known or eminent because of past achievements; prestigious)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://διακρίνω">διακρίνω</a>:; aggravated</div>
  <div class='def'>3. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://διακρίνω">διακρίνω</a>:; discrete, distinct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See διακρίνω. The &#x27;distinguished&#x27; sense is a calque of French distingué.</div><b>διακινήθηκαν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person plural simple past of διακινούμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διακινώ">διακινώ</a></div><b>διακινδυνεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to endanger, to imperil, to jeopardize</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to risk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διακινδῡνεύω, with semantic loan from English endanger. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + κίνδυν(ος)) + -εύω.</div><b>διακινώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to transport (especially goods)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to traffic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διακινῶ, contracted form of διακινέω. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + κινώ.</div><b>διακλάδωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διακλαδώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. branch (any of the parts of something that divides like the branch of a tree)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. branch, bifurcation, knot (the point in which that division starts)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ramification</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First written appearance 1843</div><b>διακομιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διακομιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carrier (person or object that carries someone or something else)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. server (hardware or software)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek διακομιστής, equivalent to διακομ- (diakom-, “to move, to carry”) + -ιστής. Computing term is a semantic loan from English server.</div><b>διακομιστής μεσολάβησης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διακομιστές μεσολάβησης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proxy server</div><b>διακοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διακοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stoppage, break, disruption, interruption, shutdown</div>
  <div class='def'>2. break, adjournment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) holidays</div>
  <div class='def'>4. interrupt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek διακοπή; see διακόπτω for more.</div><b>διακοπτόμενη συνουσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coitus interruptus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διακοπτόμενη + συνουσία</div><b>διακοσμητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διακοσμητική, neuter διακοσμητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decorative, ornamental (that serves to decorate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διακοσμητικός with semantic loan from French ornamental and décoratif. By surface analysis, διακοσμώ + -τικός.</div><b>διακοσμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to adorn, decorate (mainly of structures, place)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διακοσμῶ, contracted form of διακοσμέω and semantic loan from French décorer. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + κοσμώ.</div><b>διακρίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to distinguish, to differentiate (to recognize someone or something as different from others based on its characteristics)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to distinguish, to differentiate (to show or be the difference or distinction between things)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to distinguish (to make noticeably different or better than others through accomplishments)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to distinguish oneself (to make oneself noticeably different or better than others through accomplishments)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to distinguish, to discern, to make out, to perceive, to detect (to see someone or something clearly or distinctly)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διακρίνω (via Byzantine Greek) with semantic loan from French se distinguer (“to distinguish oneself”).</div><b>διακριτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διακριτική, neuter διακριτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. discreet (respectful of privacy or secrecy; quiet; diplomatic)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. discrete, distinctive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διακριτικός.</div><b>διακριτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διακριτή, neuter διακριτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. discernible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. discrete, distinct</div>
  <div class='def'>3. discrete</div><b>διακυβεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to risk, imperil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δῐᾰκῠβεύω.</div><b>διακόνισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διακόνισσες, masculine διάκονος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deaconess</div><b>διακόπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διακόπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>switch (device to isolate an electric current)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διακόπ(τω)) + -της (-tis), a calque of French interrupteur.</div><b>διακόπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be interrupted; literally: &quot;I am interrupted&quot;</div><b>διακόπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to interrupt; disrupt; stop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to terminate; discontinue; break off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διακόπτω. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-, “through”) + κόπτω.</div><b>διακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(noun, numeral) two hundred</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of διακόσιοι</div><b>διακόσμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διακοσμήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. decoration, ornamentation (the act of adorning or embellishing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. decoration, decor (manner of adornment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δῐᾰκόσμησῐς with semantic loan from French décoration. By surface analysis, διακοσμώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>διαλέγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be chosen, be selected</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to discuss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive voice of διαλέγω</div><b>διαλέγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to choose, select, pick out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek διαλέγω, from δια- (dia-) + λέγω).
For the passive voice sense &quot;discuss&quot;, a learned internal borrowing from Ancient Greek διαλέγομαι)</div><b>διαλειτουργικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) interoperability</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διαλειτουργικός + -ότητα.</div><b>διαλεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαλεκτική, neuter διαλεκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dialectical (of or pertaining to dialectic)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dialectal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαλεκτικός.</div><b>διαλεκτικός υλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dialectical materialism</div><b>διαλελυμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine διαλελυμένη, neuter διαλελυμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διαλυμένος">διαλυμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διαλύομαι, passive form of διαλύω (“dissolve”), from the Hellenistic Koine Greek δῐᾰλελῠμένος. Also, semantic loan from French dissous. Morphologically, from δια- (“through”) + λελῠμένος.</div><b>διαλογή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sorting (categorizing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαλογή”).</div><b>διαλογίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to meditate, contemplate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαλογίζομαι (“consider”). Morphologically, from δια- (“trans-”) + λογίζομαι (“think”).</div><b>διαλογισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαλογισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. meditation, contemplation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rumination (deep thought or consideration)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διαλογισμός (directly inherited from Koine Greek in the &#x27;deep thought&#x27; sense). By surface analysis, διαλογίζομαι + -μός.</div><b>διαλυμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διαλυμένη, neuter διαλυμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dissolved</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το χάπι είναι <b>διαλυμένο</b> σε νερό. - The pill is dissolved in water.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dismantled</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το κόμμα είναι <b>διαλυμένο</b> σε δύο παρατάξεις. - The party is split into two camps.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) wrecked, broken up, tired, exhausted</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αισθάνομαι <b>διαλυμένη</b> απ’ την κούραση και την απογοήτευση. - I feel exhausted from fatigue and frustration.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διαλύομαι, passive voice of διαλύω (“dissolve”). Descendant of the Hellenistic διαλελυμένος. Morphologically, from δια- (“through”) + λυμένος (“untie; solve”).</div><b>διαλυτικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diaeresis (UK), dieresis (US), umlaut</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nominalization of the neuter plural of the adjective διαλυτικός.</div><b>διαλυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαλυτική, neuter διαλυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. resolutive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. solvent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek δῐᾰλῠτῐκός.</div><b>διαλυτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαλυτή, neuter διαλυτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soluble, dissolvable (of a solid in a solvent)</div><b>διαλυτότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαλυτότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>solubility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διαλύω + -ότητα.</div><b>διαλύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. literally: &quot;I dismantle/dissolve/disband&quot;</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dismantle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to dissolve (a solid in a solvent)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διέλυσα</b> 5 γρ. ζάχαρη σε λίγο νερό. - I dissolved 5 grams of sugar in a little water.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to disband, dissolve, break up, disperse</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η εταιρεία <b>διαλύθηκε</b>. - The company disbanded.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to wreck, break up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου ζήτησε να χωρίσουμε και <b>διαλύθηκα</b>. - He/She asked me for a divorce/separation and I became a wreck.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαλύω and Semantic loan from French dissoudre. Morphologically δια- (dia-) + λύω.</div><b>διαμάντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαμάντια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diamond</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek διαμάντιν, from Italian diamante, from Late Latin diamas, from Latin adamās, from Ancient Greek ἀδάμας.</div><b>διαμάχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαμάχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conflict, dispute, strife, controversy</div><b>διαμένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, intransitive) to reside (to dwell, to have one&#x27;s abode)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαμένω with semantic loan from French résider.</div><b>διαμέρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαμερίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. apartment, flat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. district, region</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French appartement, from διαμερίζω + -μα (-ma, “nominal suffix”), the former from δια- (dia-) + μερίζω. Attested since 1871.</div><b>διαμαρτυρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαμαρτυρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>protest (formal objection)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πορεία διαμαρτυρίας - protest march</div><b>διαμείβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to be exchanged (something said during discussions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ξέρω τι <b>διαμείφθηκε</b> μεταξύ τους, αλλά φαίνεται ότι ήταν σοβαρό: δεν ξαναμίλησαν ποτέ ο ένας στον άλλον. - I do not know what was exchanged between them, but it must have been serious: they never spoke to each other ever since.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek διαμείβομαι, from Ancient Greek διαμείβω. By surface analysis, δι- (δια-) (“inter-”) + αμείβομαι.</div><b>διαμεσολαβήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαμεσολαβήτριες, masculine διαμεσολαβητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mediator, conciliator</div><b>διαμεσολαβητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαμεσολαβητές, feminine διαμεσολαβήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mediator, conciliator</div><b>διαμετακόμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transit (the conveyance of goods from one country to another)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διαμετακομίζω + -ση (-si), a calque of French transit.</div><b>διαμηνύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be forwarded</div><b>διαμηνύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to forward, convey, pass on a message, report</div>
  <div class='ex'>O εισαγγελέας <b>διεμήνυσε</b> στον ανακριτή να διενεργήσει προκαταρκτική εξέταση. - The prosecutor signalled to the examining magistrate to conduct a preliminary enquiry.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) to forward message through someone else, have something announced</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek διαμηνύω and Hellenistic Koine Greek διαμηνύω. Morphologically, from δια- (“through”) + Ancient Greek μηνύω.</div><b>διαμοιράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be divided, distributed</div><b>διαμοιράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to apportion, divide out, share out (divide and distribute portions of a whole)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διαμοίρασε</b> τα υπάρχοντά του στους φτωχούς. - He apportioned his belongings to the poor.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek διαμοιράζω. Morphologically, from δια- + μοιράζω.</div><b>διαμοιραστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαμοιραστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>server, sharer (of files, data, etc.)</div><b>διαμονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαμονές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stay</div>
  <div class='def'>2. whereabouts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαμονή, from διαμένω, from δια- (dia-) + μένω. In the modern sense of &quot;stay, residence&quot;, semantic loan from French résidence.</div><b>διαμορφώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be formed - literally: I am formed</div><b>διαμορφώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to mould, arrange, form, give form to</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to train, form (spiritually)</div><b>διαμόρφωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαμορφώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. formation, shaping</div>
  <div class='def'>2. configuration</div>
  <div class='def'>3. modulation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διαμόρφωσις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>διανέμω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to distribute, hand out, share out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to deliver mail</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δια- (dia-) + νέμω.</div><b>διανεμήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διανεμήτριες, masculine διανεμητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dispatcher</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dispenser</div><b>διανεμητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διανεμητές, feminine διανεμήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dispatcher</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dispenser</div><b>διανεμητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διανεμητική, neuter διανεμητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relating to distribution, distributive</div><b>διανθίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to intersperse, lard, interlard</div><b>διανοητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διανοητική, neuter διανοητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dianoetic; relating to thought, the mind, intellect, etc.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διανοητικός.</div><b>διανομέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διανομείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dispatcher</div>
  <div class='def'>2. router (a device connecting local area networks to form a larger internet)</div><b>διανομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διανομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>distribution, issuance (act of giving out or delivering items etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>διανομή</b> μιας ταινίας - the distribution of a film</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διανομή, from Ancient Greek διανέμω.</div><b>διανοούμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διανοούμενοι, feminine διανοούμενη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intellectual (an intelligent, learned person, especially one who discourses about learned matters)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διανοούμενος, present middle participle of διανοέομαι. A calque of French intellectuel.</div><b>διανυκτέρευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διανυκτερεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>overnight, overnight stay (in a hotel, etc.)</div><b>διανυκτερεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be up all night, stay up all night</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to hold a vigil</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to be open all night, stay open all night</div><b>διανυσματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διανυσματική, neuter διανυσματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vectorial, vectoral (less common)</div><b>διανόηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) intelligentsia</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) thought</div><b>διανύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be traversed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to have passed through a period of time</div><b>διανύομεν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of διανύομε and διανύουμε</div><b>διανύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to traverse a distance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to go through a period of time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek δῐᾰνῠ́ω. By surface analysis, δι- (di-, “through”) + Ancient Greek ᾰ̓νῠ́ω.</div><b>διαολοκόριτσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαολοκόριτσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διαβολοκόριτσο">διαβολοκόριτσο</a></div><b>διαολοσκορπίσματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διαβολοσκορπίσματα">διαβολοσκορπίσματα</a></div><b>διαπαιδαγώγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) education</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) edification</div><b>διαπερασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διαπερασμένη, neuter διαπερασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>passed through</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διαπερνιέμαι, passive voice of διαπερνάω, διαπερνώ (“pass through”).</div><b>διαπεραστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαπεραστική, neuter διαπεραστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. penetrating, piercing (of temperature, extremely cold so that it penetrates through clothing and shelter)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. penetrating, pungent (odour)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. penetrating, piercing (appearing to look deeply into: gaze, look, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. piercing (of sound, loud and sharp; shrill)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek διαπερασ-) + -τικός, a calque of French pénétrant.</div><b>διαπερνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to pass through, pierce</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to influence</div><b>διαπερνιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be passed through</div><b>διαπερνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διαπερνάω">διαπερνάω</a></div><b>διαπιστευτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαπιστευτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) credential (document of authority)</div><b>διαπιστώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to ascertain, to find out, to realize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δια- (dia-) + πιστώ + -ώνω.</div><b>διαπλάθω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to mould (UK), to mold (US), to shape, to form</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαπλάσσω adapted to Modern Greek consistently with πλάθω from Ancient Greek πλάσσω.</div><b>διαπλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sail across</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Κολόμβος <b>διέπλευσε</b> τον Ατλανικό. - Columbus sailed across the Atlantic.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαπλέω. Morphologically, from δια- (dia-, “through”) + πλέω.</div><b>διαπλανητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαπλανητική, neuter διαπλανητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interplanetary</div><b>διαπληκτίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to have it out, quarrel, bicker, fight (have a verbal dispute)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διαπληκτιζόντουσαν</b> πολλή ώρα πριν από το διαζύγιο. - They were quarrelling quite a lot before the divorce.</div><b>διαπνέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be characterized by an idea or inspiration</div><b>διαπνέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be main characteristic, instill main idea, draw inspiration from (usually in the passive)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το έργο του <b>διαπνέεται</b> από πατριωτισμό. - His work transpires (is characterized by) patriotism.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) to discharge vapour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Sense evaporate from Ancient Greek διαπνέω (“blow through, breathe”).
For sense characterized, semantic loan from French s&#x27;inspirer.</div><b>διαποδιαμορφωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαποδιαμορφωτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>modem</div><b>διαποτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of liquids) to penetrate completely</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to affect to a very large extent, imbue</div><b>διαπρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to distinguish oneself; literally: &quot;I distinguish myself&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαπρέπω. By surface analysis, δια- (“through”) + πρέπω.</div><b>διαπραγμάτευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαπραγματεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. negotiation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) negotiation; negotiations, talks</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare) discussion (detailed presentation of a topic)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διαπραγματεύ(ομαι)) + -ση (-si), a calque of French negotiation.</div><b>διαπραγματευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαπραγματευτές, feminine διαπραγματεύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>negotiator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διαπραγματεύ(ομαι)) + -τής.</div><b>διαπραγματεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive, transitive) to negotiate</div><b>διαπροσωπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαπροσωπική, neuter διαπροσωπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interpersonal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δια- (dia-) + προσωπικός, a calque of French interpersonnel.</div><b>διαρθρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to restructure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to reassemble</div><b>διαρκής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαρκής, neuter διαρκές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constant, endless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαρκής.</div><b>διαρκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to endure, last</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαρκῶ, contracted form of διαρκέω, from δι- (di-) + ἀρκέω.</div><b>διαρκώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constantly</div><b>διαρρέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be leaked / literally: &quot;I am leaked on purpose&quot; (of classified information)</div>
  <div class='ex'>διαρρέεται - it is leaked, let be known</div><b>διαρρέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of rivers) to flow through</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Νείλος <b>διαρρέει</b> πολλές χώρες. - The Nile flows through many countries.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to leak (liquid or gas through a hole)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of time) to leak (liquid or gas through a hole); to pass</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διέρρευσε</b> πολύς χρόνος μετά τη λήξη της προθεσμίας. - A long time passed by after the deadline.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) to leak (secrets, confidential information)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διέρρευσαν</b> μυστικά έγγραφα. - Classified documents are leaked.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial, figuratively, neologism, transitive) to leak (secrets, confidential information); to leak on purpose</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πηγές του κόμματος <b>διαρρέουν</b> ότι ο υπουργός θα παραιτηθεί σύντομα. - Party sources are leaking that the minister will soon resign.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (colloquial, figuratively, impersonal, neologism, transitive) to leak (secrets, confidential information); to leak on purpose; to be leaked, to be let known</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (of people) to disperse, diminish in numbers</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι οπαδοί <b>διαρρέουν</b> προς άλλα κόμματα. - Party followers are moving to other parties.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. to drain (container; area of land)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δῐᾰρρέω. For sense information leaks, semantic loan from English leak. For sense people move out, semantic loan from French écouler.</div><b>διαρρήκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαρρήκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>burglar, cat burglar</div><b>διαρροή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leak (in pipe, tank, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) leak (disclosure of confidential information), defection, loss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαρροή, invoking the sense of &quot;flowing through&quot; &gt; &quot;the thing that flows&quot;.</div><b>διαρρυθμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to arrange, to lay out (:items within a space or the space itself)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διαρρυθμίζω. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + ρυθμίζω.</div><b>διασκέδαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασκεδάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. amusement, entertainment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diversion (an activity that distracts the mind)</div><b>διασκέπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be in conference</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek διασκέπτομαι (“examine all around”). Morphologically, from δια- (“through”) + σκέπτομαι (“think”).</div><b>διασκεδάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be dispersed (of suspicions, rumours)</div><b>διασκεδάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to have fun, have fun with</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to enjoy oneself</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to amuse oneself</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to entertain, divert</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to disperse (usually of suspicions, negative feelings)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The same as Etymology 2, with semantic loan from French dissiper, se divertir.</div><b>διασκεδάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασκεδάστριες, masculine διασκεδαστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reveller, merrymaker</div>
  <div class='def'>2. entertainer</div><b>διασκεδαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διασκεδαστές, feminine διασκεδάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reveller, merrymaker</div>
  <div class='def'>2. entertainer</div><b>διασκεδαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διασκεδαστική, neuter διασκεδαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. entertaining</div>
  <div class='def'>2. funny, amusing</div><b>διασκελίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to jump, hop</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διασκέλισα</b> τον φράχτη για να δραπετεύσω. - I hopped over the fence to escape.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to stride (walk with long steps)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πέρασε τον βάλτο <b>διασκελίζοντας</b>. - He crossed over the swamp by striding.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Active form from the mediopassive Koine Greek διασκελίζομαι (“having the legs parted”).</div><b>διασκευάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to adapt (modify or remodel for a different purpose)</div><b>διασκευή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασκευές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>revision, adaptation, cover version</div><b>διασπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διασπώ">διασπώ</a></div><b>διασπορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) dispersion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) diaspora</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek διασπορά.</div><b>διασπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to penetrate, break through</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to split, rupture</div><b>διασταυρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to cross, to place crosswise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to crosscheck, to cross-check, to check, to verify, to compare (to verify by using an alternative source or method)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to cross, to hybridize (join one species to another)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διασταυρῶ, διασταυρόω + -ώνω; by surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + σταυρώνω. The modern senses are derived via semantic loan from French croiser.</div><b>διασταύρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασταυρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hybridisation, crossbreeding (the act/process)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hybrid, cross, crossbreed (the product)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. crossing (intersection where roads, lines, or tracks cross)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. cross-checking (verify by using alternative sources)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from διασταυρώνω + -σις (-sis) (with shortening of the suffix to modern -ση (-si)), formed as a calque of French croisement.</div><b>διαστημικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαστημική, neuter διαστημικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spatial (relating to space)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from διάστημ(α), “space, interval”) + -ικός.</div><b>διαστημόπλοιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διαστημόπλοια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spaceship, spacecraft</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διάστημα + -ό- + πλοίο.</div><b>διαστολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαστολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. expansion (for metals)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dilation, dilatation (for organs)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. distention</div>
  <div class='def'>4. diastole</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαστολή.</div><b>διαστρεβλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to distort, to twist (to give false account of, to misrepresent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαστρεβλῶ / διαστρεβλόω, with -ώνω suffix. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + στρεβλώνω.</div><b>διαστροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαστροφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of writing) distortion, misrepresentation (false representation so as to injure someone&#x27;s character etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έγινε <b>διαστροφή</b> του κειμένου, προκειμένου να φανεί ανίκανος. - The piece of writing was distorted in order for him to appear incompetent.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. perversion, sexual deviance (sexual practice or act considered abnormal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η παιδοφιλία είναι γνωστή σεξουαλική <b>διαστροφή</b>. - Paedophilia is a known sexual perversion.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαστροφή, from the verb διαστρέφω.</div><b>διασυνοριακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διασυνοριακή, neuter διασυνοριακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cross-border, transfrontier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δια- (dia-) + συνοριακός.</div><b>διασφαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to secure, to safeguard (to keep safe)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διασφαλίζομαι, from which the active voice is back-formed as per ασφαλίζω. By surface analysis, δι- (di-, from δια- (dia-)) + ασφαλίζω.</div><b>διασχίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to traverse, cross</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to flow through</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowing from Ancient Greek διασχίζω. Morphologically δια- (dia-) + σχίζω.</div><b>διασωλήνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασωληνώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intubation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pipelining</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δια- (dia-, “through”) + σωλήνωση, a calque of English intubation</div><b>διασωληνωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διασωληνωμένη, neuter διασωληνωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intubated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διασωληνώνομαι, passive voice of διασωληνώνω</div><b>διασωληνώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be intubated</div><b>διασωληνώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to intubate</div><b>διασύνδεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διασυνδέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. link, link up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interconnection</div>
  <div class='def'>3. interface</div><b>διασώζεται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular present indicative of διασώζομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διασώζω">διασώζω</a></div><b>διασώζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rescue</div><b>διασώθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of διασώζομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διασώζω">διασώζω</a></div><b>διασώθηκαν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person plural simple past of διασώζομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διασώζω">διασώζω</a></div><b>διατάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to command, to order (to do: να + verb) (to issue an instruction)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek διατάζω, from Koine Greek διατάσσω, from Ancient Greek διατάσσω.</div><b>διατήρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. retention</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conservation, maintenance, preservation (the keeping (of a thing) in a safe or entire state)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διατήρηση</b> της ειρήνης - peacekeeping</div>
  <div class='def'>3. conservation (lack of change in a measurable property of an isolated physical system)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διατήρηση</b> της ενέργειας - conservation of energy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διατήρησις. By surface analysis, διατήρη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>διαταγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαταγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>command, order</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + ταγή.</div><b>διαταραχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαταραχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disorder (a physical or mental malfunction)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαταραχή with semantic loan from French trouble.</div><b>διατεθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of διατίθεμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διαθέτω">διαθέτω</a>:; third-person singular dependent passive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of διατίθεμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διαθέτω">διαθέτω</a>:; passive nonfinite form</div><b>διατεθούν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person plural dependent of διατίθεμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διαθέτω">διαθέτω</a></div><b>διατηρημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διατηρημένη, neuter διατηρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preserved, maintained, kept</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διατηρούμαι, passive voice of διατηρώ (“keep”).</div><b>διατηρούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be kept, to be preserved</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Διατηρείται</b> καλά, παρά την ηλικία της. - She is in good shape, despite her age.</div><b>διατηρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to have, harbour, keep, retain</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ωστόσο, <b>διατηρώ</b> ορισμένες επιφυλάξεις. - However, I harbour a few reservations.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to own, hold</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to maintain, conserve, keep up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διατηρέω, διατηρῶ, formed from διά- + τηρέω, τηρῶ, with some senses influenced by French conserver.</div><b>διατρέφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to nourish, feed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to maintain, support, sustain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek διατρέφω, from δια- (dia-) + τρέφω</div><b>διατριβή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διατριβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dissertation, thesis</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο υποψήφιος διδάκτορας έστειλε τη <b>διατριβή</b> του στην κριτική επιτροπή των καθηγητών. - The doctoral candidate sent his dissertation to the dissertation review board.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. treatise, discourse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διατριβή.</div><b>διατροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διατροφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nutrition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alimentation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sustenance</div>
  <div class='def'>4. food</div>
  <div class='def'>5. alimony</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek διατροφή. Morphologically δια- (dia-) + τροφή.
For sense &quot;alimony&quot;, semantic loan from French pension alimentaire.</div><b>διατροφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διατροφική, neuter διατροφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eating, feeding (attributive), dietary</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διατροφικές</b> συνήθειες - eating habits</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διατροφ(ή)) + -ικός.</div><b>διατρυπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to impale, transpierce, transfix, skewer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to penetrate, nail, perforate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to transfix (with terror)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to carve through for fretwork</div><b>διατυπώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to declare, state, give voice to;</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to formulate;</div><b>διαφάνεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαφάνειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transparency (the quality of being transparent)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transparency (openness; accessibility to scrutiny)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. slide, transparency (a transparent plate bearing an image to be projected to a screen)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαφάνεια with semantic loan from French transparence.</div><b>διαφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to differ, be different, vary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαφέρω, from δια- (dia-) + φέρω.</div><b>διαφήμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαφημίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. advertisement (commercial solicitation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. publicity (advertising or other activity designed to rouse public interest in something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French réclame. See the initial sense of the ancient Greek verb διαφημίζω. Attested since 1887 in Katharevousa as διαφήμισις with its modern sense.</div><b>διαφανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαφανής, neuter διαφανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diaphanous, transparent, clear, see-through</div><b>διαφεύγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to escape, elude, get away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαφεύγω.</div><b>διαφημίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to advertise (UK), advertize (US), to publicise (UK), publicize (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek διαφημίζω. By surface analysis, διά- + φήμη + -ίζω.</div><b>διαφημιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διαφημιστές, feminine διαφημίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>advertiser, adman</div><b>διαφημιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαφημιστική, neuter διαφημιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>advertising (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διαφημιστ(ής)) + -ικός.</div><b>διαφθορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαφθορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corruption, vice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαφθορά.</div><b>διαφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. difference (in characteristics, position, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. difference (the result of subtraction)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dispute, difference (of opinion, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. dispute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαφορά.</div><b>διαφορά δυναμικού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>potential difference, voltage</div><b>διαφορετικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>otherwise; else; differently</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://διαφορετικός">διαφορετικός</a></div><b>διαφορετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαφορετική, neuter διαφορετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dissimilar, different</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διαφορά + -ικός.</div><b>διαφοροποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαφοροποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diversification</div>
  <div class='def'>2. differentiation</div><b>διαφοροποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to differentiate (to show or be the difference or distinction between two things)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to differentiate (to recognize a difference or distinction between two things)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διάφορ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ, a calque of French différencier.</div><b>διαφυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαφυγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. escape (from dangerous or unpleasant situation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>για να εμποδιστεί η <b>διαφυγή</b> του καταζητούμενου - preventing the escape of the fugitive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leak (gas, water, radioactivity, etc)</div><b>διαφυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intersex</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transgender</div><b>διαφωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαφωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disagreement, dispute, dissent, clash</div><b>διαφωνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to disagree</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διαφωνώ</b> - I disagree (literal translation)</div><b>διαφωτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to enlighten (to give knowledge or understanding to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μήπως μπορείς να με <b>διαφωτίσεις</b>; - Would you care to enlighten me?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διαφωτίζω. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + φωτ- (fot-) + -ίζω.</div><b>διαφωτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enlightenment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διαφωτίζω + -μός, a calque of German Aufklärung.</div><b>διαχέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be diffused, spread; literally: &quot;I am diffused&quot;</div><b>διαχέομε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of διαχέουμε: first-person plural present of <a href="bword://διαχέω">διαχέω</a></div><b>διαχέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to diffuse, spread, shed about; literally: &quot;I diffuse&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαχέω. Morphologically, from δια- (“in different directions”) + the ancient χέω (“pour, scatter”).</div><b>διαχείριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαχειρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>management</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: διοίκηση f (dioíkisi), μάνατζμεντ n (mánatzment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διαχείρισις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>διαχειρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to manage, handle (direct or be in charge of)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διαχειρίζομαι</b> την πολυκατοικία - to manage an apartment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαχειρίζομαι.</div><b>διαχειρίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαχειρίστριες, masculine διαχειριστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. administrator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. manager</div>
  <div class='def'>3. financial manager</div><b>διαχειριστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαχειριστές, feminine διαχειρίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>administrator, director, operator, manager</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>διαχειριστής</b> του λογαριασμού δύναται να απαιτεί να συνοδεύονται τα υποβαλλόμενα έγγραφα από επικυρωμένη μετάφραση. - The administrator of the account may require that the documents submitted include a certified translation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διαχειρ- (diacheir-, “to manage”) + -ιστής; see δῐᾰ- + χείρ for more on the first component. First attested 1856.</div><b>διαχρονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διαχρονική, neuter διαχρονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diachronic (of, pertaining to or concerned with changes that occur over time)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intertemporal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. longitudinal (sampling data over time rather than merely once)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. timeless (not decreasing over time in quality and appeal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French diachronique. By surface analysis, διαχρον(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>διαχωρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to separate, disconnect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to distance (yourself from something), dissociate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαχωρίζω. By surface analysis, δια- (dia-) + χωρίζω.</div><b>διαχωρίζω την ήρα από το στάρι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) to separate the wheat from the chaff</div><b>διαχωρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διαχωρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. separation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. demarcation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διαχωρισμός. By surface analysis, διαχωρίζω + -μός.</div><b>διαχύνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal variant of of <a href="bword://διαχέομαι">διαχέομαι</a></div><b>διαχύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal variant of <a href="bword://διαχέω">διαχέω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διαχέω with metaplasm according to the pattern χέω &gt; χύνω.</div><b>διαψευσιμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>falsifiability</div><b>διαψεύδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to contradict, refute</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to deny</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to prove wrong</div><b>διαύγεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διαύγειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. translucency</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) clarity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διαύγεια.</div><b>διγαμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διγαμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bigamy</div><b>διγλωσσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διγλωσσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diglossia (the coexistence of two languages within a population, one of which is more prestigious than the other)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bilingualism (the use of two languages within a community)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) hypocrisy (in politics: the mouthing of different policies, dependent upon circumstances), doublespeak</div><b>διδ. φιλ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://διδάκτωρ">διδάκτωρ</a> φιλοσοφίας; Ph.D., D.Phil.</div><b>διδάκτορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διδάκτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doctor (a person who has attained a doctorate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διδάκ(τωρ)) + -τορας (-toras), an adaptation to Demotic Greek.</div><b>διδάκτωρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διδάκτορας">διδάκτορας</a></div><b>διδάκτωρ φιλοσοφίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Doctor of Philosophy</div><b>διδάσκαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διδάσκαλοι, feminine διδασκάλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teacher</div><b>διδάσκομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be taught</div><b>διδάσκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to teach</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διδάσκω.</div><b>διδακτική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://διδακτικός">διδακτικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διδακτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teaching, didactics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised feminine of the adjective διδακτικός, from the Hellenistic διδακτικός. Semantic loan from German Didaktik, itself from διδακτικός.</div><b>διδακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διδακτική, neuter διδακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teaching, educational, education</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διδακτικός</div><b>διδακτορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διδακτορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doctorate (qualification or course of study)</div><b>διδακτορικά διπλώματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of διδακτορικό <a href="bword://δίπλωμα">δίπλωμα</a></div><b>διδακτορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διδακτορική, neuter διδακτορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doctoral</div><b>διδασκάλισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διδασκάλισσες, masculine διδάσκαλος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female teacher</div><b>διδασκαλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διδασκαλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. teaching, tuition, instruction</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) doctrine, teaching</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διδασκαλία.</div><b>διδαχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διδαχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. instruction, teaching</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lecture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek.</div><b>διείσδυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διεισδύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. penetration (the act of penetrating something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. penetration (the act of penetrating something); penetration (the insertion of the penis (or similar object) during sexual intercourse)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διείσδυσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>διεγείρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stimulate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to incite</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to turn on someone sexually</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διεγείρω from δια- (dia-) and ἐγείρω. By surface analysis, δι- (δια-) + εγείρω.
For psychological and sexual senses, semantic loan from French exciter.</div><b>διεγερμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διεγερμένη, neuter διεγερμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stimulated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διεγείρομαι, passive voice of διεγείρω (“stimulate”).</div><b>διεθνής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διεθνής, neuter διεθνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>international</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δι- from δια- (di- from dia-) + έθν(ος), “nation”) + -ής. Calque of French international.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διεθνείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>international, international player (who has been a member of a national team)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δι- from δια- (di- from dia-) + έθν(ος), “nation”) + -ής. Calque of French international.</div><b>διεθνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διεθνισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. internationalism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. internationalism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διεθνής (“international”) + -ισμός</div><b>διεθνολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διεθνολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>international relations expert, global affairs specialist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διεθν(ής)) + -ο- (-o-) + -λόγος.</div><b>διεθνοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διεθνοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>internationalisation (UK), internationalization (US)</div><b>διεθνοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to internationalise (UK), internationalize (US)</div><b>διεθνώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>internationally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διεθν(ής)) + -ώς.</div><b>διεκδίκηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διεκδικήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. claiming (the act of making a claim)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. claim (a demand of ownership made for something)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. claim (demand of ownership for previously unowned land)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the διεκδίκη- stem of διεκδικώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>διεκδικώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to claim, to lay claim to, to stake a claim to</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to demand (to claim a right to something)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to vie for</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διεκδικῶ with semantic loan from Latin vindicō and later from French revendiquer. Morphologically, δι- from δια- (di- from dia-) + ἐκδικῶ, for which see the modern εκδικούμαι.</div><b>διεμφυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transgender, transsexual</div>
  <div class='ex'>2010, Jamrat Mason (translator unattributed) Ούτε άντρας, ούτε γυναίκα. Είμαι τρανσέξουαλ.
Επιτρέψτε μου να σας πω, όταν είσαι διεμφυλικός, δε μπορείς να ξεφύγεις απ’ το σεξισμό, ωθείσαι μέσα σε ένα τεράστιο τέλμα σεξισμού.
Epitrépste mou na sas po, ótan eísai diemfylikós, de boreís na xefýgeis ap’ to sexismó, otheísai mésa se éna terástio télma sexismoú. - Let me tell you, when you&#x27;re a transexual, you do not escape sexism, you are pushed right into an enormous swamp of sexism.</div><b>διενεργώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to carry out, to conduct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διενεργῶ. By surface analysis, δι- (di-) + ενεργώ.</div><b>διεξάγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to carry out, conduct (of talks, diplomatic talks, games)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για να εντοπίζεται η προέλευση της ασθένειας και να αποτρέπεται η εξάπλωσή της, πρέπει να <b>διεξάγεται</b> ενδελεχής επιδηµιολογική έρευνα. - In order to identify the origin of the disease and prevent its spread, a thorough epidemiological investigation must be carried out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek διεξάγω. Morphologically, δι- from δια- (di- from dia-) + εξ- (ex-) + άγω.</div><b>διεξαγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διεξαγωγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conduct, conducting, holding, carrying out</div><b>διεξαχθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular dependent of διεξάγομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διεξάγω">διεξάγω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive nonfinite form of διεξάγομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://διεξάγω">διεξάγω</a></div><b>διεπαφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διεπαφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interface</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δι- (di-) + επαφή, a rendering of English interface.</div><b>διεπαφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διεπαφική, neuter διεπαφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interfacial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διεπαφή + -ικός.</div><b>διεπιφάνεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διεπιφάνειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interface</div><b>διεργασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διεργασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. process (a path of succession of states through which a system passes)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. process</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek διεργάζομαι + -ία.</div><b>διερευνητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine διερευνήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>investigator (male)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διερευνώ + -τής.</div><b>διερευνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to investigate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διερευνῶ.</div><b>διερεύνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διερευνήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>investigation, enquiry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek διερεύνησις.</div><b>διερμηνέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διερμηνείς, feminine διερμηνεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interpreter (person who interprets something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interpreter</div><b>διερμηνεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διερμηνείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interpretation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interpretation (the discipline or study of translating spoken language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διερμηνεία, whence Pontic Greek δα̤ρμενεία.</div><b>διερμηνευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διερμηνευτές, feminine διερμηνεύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interpreter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interpreter (person who interprets something)</div><b>διερμηνεύτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διερμηνεύτριες, masculine διερμηνευτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) interpreter (person who interprets something)</div><b>διετής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διετής, neuter διετές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of or lasting two years</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διετής. By surface analysis, δι- from δις) + -ετής from έτ(ος) n, “year”) + -ής.</div><b>διετία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διετίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>biennium</div><b>διευθυντής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διευθυντές, feminine διευθύντρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. manager, director</div>
  <div class='def'>2. principal, headmaster, governor</div>
  <div class='def'>3. conductor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διευθύνω.</div><b>διευθύντρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διευθύντριες, masculine διευθυντής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. headmistress</div>
  <div class='def'>2. manageress</div>
  <div class='def'>3. director</div>
  <div class='def'>4. governess</div><b>διευθύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to manage, direct, organise (UK), organize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to command, order</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to coordinate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek διευθύνω, from δια- (dia-, “through”) + εὐθύνω. For the modern sense of &quot;to direct, manage&quot;, semantic loan from French diriger.</div><b>διευκολύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to facilitate, to make easier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δι(α)- (di(a)-) + ευκολύνω with semantic loan from French faciliter.</div><b>διευκρίνιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διευκρινίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clarification (the act of freeing from obscurities; disambiguation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clarification (an explanatory comment on, or question about, a point of obscurity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διευκρίνησις. By surface analysis, διευκρίνι-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>διευκρινίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to clarify (to make easily understood)</div><b>διευρύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to broaden, widen, expand</div>
  <div class='ex'>Διευρύνω τον κύκλο των γνωριμιών μου. - I widen my circle of acquaintances.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to increase</div><b>διεφθαρμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διεφθαρμένη, neuter διεφθαρμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corrupt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of διαφθείρομαι, passive voice of διαφθείρω. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek διεφθαρμένος &amp; semantic loan from French corrompu. Morphologically, from δι- (δια-) (di- (dia-)) + the ancient participle ἐφθαρμένος, modern φθαρμένος and formal εφθαρμένος.</div><b>διεύθυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διευθύνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. management, direction, directorate, organisation (of business, organisation, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. home address, abode</div>
  <div class='def'>3. direction of travel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διευθύνω + -ση (-si, “-sis”).</div><b>διηγούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to narrate, relate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διηγέομαι.</div><b>διηθητικό χαρτί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διηθητικά χαρτιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>filter paper</div><b>διηθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to percolate, strain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διηθέω / διηθῶ (contracted), morphologically from δῐᾰ́ + ἠθέω.</div><b>διηρημένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine διηρημένη, neuter διηρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διαιρεμένος">διαιρεμένος</a>: divided</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The ancient Ancient Greek διῃρημένος, as formal variant of the modern διαιρεμένος. Perfect participle of διαιρούμαι, passive voice of διαιρώ (“divide”).</div><b>δικάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to judge, sit in judgement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δικάζω.</div><b>δικάταρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δικάταρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a two-masted ship or boat, ketch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δι- (di-, “δις, twice”) + κατάρτι</div><b>δικάταρτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two-masted, double-masted, twin-masted</div><b>δικαίωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δικαιώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. right, entitlement</div>
  <div class='ex'>ανθρώπινα <b>δικαιώματα</b> - human rights</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) right, entitlement; rights</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το Ευρωπαϊκό Δικαστήριο Ανθρωπίνων <b>Δικαιωμάτων</b> - The European Court of Human Rights</div>
  <div class='def'>3. right, warrant, authority, power</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν είχες το <b>δικαίωμα</b> να πυροβολήσεις. - You didn&#x27;t have the authority to shoot.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δικαίωμα, by surface analysis, δίκαιος + -ωμα (-oma). In parts a semantic loan from French droit.</div><b>δικαίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fairly, justly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δίκαιος.</div><b>δικαιοδοσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δικαιοδοσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jurisdiction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δικαιοδοσία.</div><b>δικαιολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δικαιολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. excuse (explanation designed to avoid or alleviate guilt or negative judgment)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>δικαιολογία</b> του ήταν ότι το λεωφορείο του καθυστέρησε. - His excuse was that the bus was late.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. justification, reason, rationale (explanation which provides support for behavior or for a belief or occurrence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν άκουσε ο δικαστής την <b>δικαιολογία</b> του, τον άφησε ελεύθερο. - When the judge heard his justification, he let him go free.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δικαιολογία, from the verb δικαιολογῶ, from δίκαιος + λόγος.</div><b>δικαιολογημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine δικαιολογημένη, neuter δικαιολογημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://δικαιολογώ">δικαιολογώ</a>:; justifiable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://δικαιολογώ">δικαιολογώ</a>:; justified (having a justification)</div><b>δικαιολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to justify (to provide an acceptable explanation for)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to justify, to warrant (to be a good, acceptable reason for)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δικαιολογῶ, from Ancient Greek δικαιολογέομαι, with semantic loan from French justifier.</div><b>δικαιοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) justice, judicial system</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (capitalized, uncountable) Justice (the law personified)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) justice (impartiality, fairness)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δικαιοσύνη.</div><b>δικαιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be entitled to</div><b>δικαιούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δικαιούχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>holder, rightholder, proprietor</div><b>δικαιωματικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δικαιωματικός">δικαιωματικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rightfully, by right</div><b>δικαιωματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δικαιωματική, neuter δικαιωματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rightful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the δικαιωματ- stem of δικαίωμα + -ικός.</div><b>δικαιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to exonerate, justify, vindicate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δικαιόω.</div><b>δικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δικανική, neuter δικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forensic, legal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δικανικός.</div><b>δικαστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δικαστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>court</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δικαστήριον. By surface analysis, δικάζω + -τήριο.</div><b>δικαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δικαστές, feminine δικαστίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>judge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δικαστής. By surface analysis, δικάζω + -τής.</div><b>δικαστίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δικαστίνες, masculine δικαστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) judge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) judge&#x27;s wife</div><b>δικαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δικαστική, neuter δικαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relating to courts, judges, judgements, etc</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δικαστικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magistrate</div><b>δικηγόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δικηγόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>attorney, solicitor, barrister</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συγχρόνως, ο <b>δικηγόρος</b> θα είχε το πλεονέκτημα να μην φέρει ο ίδιος την αποκλειστική ευθύνη όταν υποπτευθεί μία σοβαρή εγκληματική δραστηριότητα. - At the same time, attorneys would have the advantage of not being left to cope by themselves when they are suspected of criminal activity.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek δικήγορος, from late Hellenistic / Koine Greek, or medieval 6th century attested oblique cases, dative, δικηγόρῳ), both suitable for a noun in -ήγορος or -ηγόρος, from Ancient Greek δίκη + -ήγορος, the latter suffix from ἀγορεύω”) with ectasis of &lt;α&gt; to &lt;η&gt; in composition), but with stress shift like δημηγόρος. The word was lemmatized by Eustathius and at Suda as δικηγόρος. Diccionario Español correctly lemmatizes δικηγόρος, unlike LSJ which lemmatizes δικήγορος.</div><b>δικογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δικογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>case file, court documents (set of documents used in proceedings in a court case)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δικόγραφ(ο)) + -ία. Compare Ancient Greek δικογραφία.</div><b>δικομανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δικομανής, neuter δικομανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>litigious (inclined to engage in lawsuits)</div><b>δικτάτορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δικτάτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dictator</div><b>δικτατορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δικτατορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dictatorship</div><b>δικτατορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δικτατορική, neuter δικτατορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dictatorial (of or pertaining to a dictator)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dictatorial (in the manner of a dictator)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δικτατορ(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>δικτυωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δικτυωτή, neuter δικτυωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. netlike, netty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meshy, reticulate, latticed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δικτυωτός; see the ancient δίκτυον. By surface analysis, δικτυώ(νω)) + -τός, forming ending -ωτός.
Also, substantivised neuter.</div><b>δικτύωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. networking (the process of meeting new people in a business or social context)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. networking (the use of computer networks)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δικτυώ(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>δικός</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine (feminine δική or δικιά, neuter δικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (followed by possessive pronoun) mine, yours, his, hers, its, ours, theirs</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός είναι <b>δικός</b> μου. - That&#x27;s mine.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. my, your, his, her, its, our, their (emphatic form)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>δικός</b> μου σκύλος - My (own) dog.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δικός, from Ancient Greek ἰδικός, from Ancient Greek ἴδιος.</div><b>διμερής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διμερής, neuter διμερές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. two-part, bipartite, duplex (having two parts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bilateral, bipartite (involving two parties)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διμερής. By surface analysis, δι- (di-) + -μερής</div><b>διμορφοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διμορφοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dimorphotheca</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from translingual Dimorphotheca. By surface analysis, δίμορφος + θήκη.</div><b>δινοφύκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δινοφύκη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dinoflagellate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Dinophyceae. By surface analysis, δίνη + φύκος.</div><b>διοίκηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διοικήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. administration (the act of administering)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: διαχείριση f (diacheírisi)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. administration (a body that administers; a body of administrators)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. administrative headquarters</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διοίκησις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>διοικητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διοικητές, feminine διοικήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chief administrator, governor (person in charge of administration)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διοικητής</b> της κεντρικής τράπεζας - governor of the central bank</div>
  <div class='def'>2. commander</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διοικητής. By surface analysis, διοικώ + -τής.</div><b>διοικητική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διοικητικές, masculine διοικητικός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>administrator (female)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://διοικητικός">διοικητικός</a></div><b>διοικητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διοικητική, neuter διοικητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>administrative, managerial</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural διοικητικοί, feminine διοικητική)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>administrator</div><b>διοικώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to run (eg: a business or other concern)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to administer, manage, supervise</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to command, govern</div><b>διοξίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διοξίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διοξείδιο">διοξείδιο</a></div><b>διοξείδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διοξείδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dioxide</div><b>διοξείδιο του άνθρακα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) carbon dioxide</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι ιδιότητες του <b>διοξειδίου του άνθρακα</b> … - The properties of carbon dioxide …</div><b>διορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to appoint, designate, name</div><b>διορατικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) foresight, insight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> διορατικός + -ότητα. First attested 1884.</div><b>διοργάνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διοργανώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. organization (the act of organizing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. organized event</div>
  <div class='ex'>αθλητική <b>διοργάνωση</b> - sporting event, athletic event</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διοργάνωσις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>διοργανωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διοργανωτές, feminine διοργανώτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>organizer (a person who arranges the details of an event)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from διοργανώ(νω)) + -τής.</div><b>διοργανώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to organise (UK), organize (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διοργανῶ with -ώνω ending. By surface analysis, δι- (di-) + οργανώνω.</div><b>διορθώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fix, correct</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διορθώνω</b> δοκίμια - proof read</div><b>διοχετεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to channel, pipe, conduct, convey, direct the flow</div><b>διπλά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twice as much</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://διπλός">διπλός</a></div><b>διπλάσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διπλάσια, neuter διπλάσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the proportional number two-fold, double, twice the size</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διπλάσιος.</div><b>διπλές στιγμές</b>
