  <div class='def'>disyllabic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δισύλλαβος. By surface analysis, δι- (di-) + συλλαβ(ή)) + -ος (-os)</div><b>διττανθρακικό νάτριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sodium hydrogen carbonate, sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. baking soda</div><b>διττανθρακικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διττανθρακική, neuter διττανθρακικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bicarbonate, hydrogen carbonate</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διττανθρακικό</b> νάτριο - sodium bicarbonate</div><b>διττός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διττή, neuter διττό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two-fold, double</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δισσός.</div><b>διυλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to purify; to refine, distill, crack (crude oil/petroleum)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to purify; to filter, strain, refine (olive oil, vegetable oil)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to purify; to refine (sugar)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διυλίζω.</div><b>διυλιστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural διυλιστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refinery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διυλίζω.</div><b>διφθερίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) diphtheria</div><b>διφθογγισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) diphthongisation</div><b>διφορούμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διφορούμενη, neuter διφορούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>equivocal, ambiguous</div><b>διχασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διχασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>split, division, schism, dichotomy</div><b>διχογνωμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διχογνωμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disagreement, difference of opinion, dissent</div><b>διχοτομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dichotomy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διχοτομία. By surface analysis, διχοτόμος + -ία.</div><b>διχοτομώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to bisect (to divide an angle, line segment, or other figure into two equal parts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to divide in two, to partition (e.g. a country or region)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διχοτομέω.</div><b>διχοτόμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διχοτομήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bisection (divide into two equal parts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dichotomy, split</div><b>διχόνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διχόνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>discord</div><b>διψάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be thirsty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be dry (soil, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek διψάω.</div><b>διψασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine διψασμένη, neuter διψασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thirsty, dry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) eager</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διψασμένος</b> για δόξα - eager for glory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive participle of διψάω, διψώ (“drink”), a verb with no passive voice.</div><b>διψώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://διψάω">διψάω</a></div><b>διψών</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) thirsty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek διψῶν.</div><b>διόδια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. toll</div>
  <div class='def'>2. toll road, turnpike</div>
  <div class='ex'>antonym: phrase δρόμος χωρίς διόδια (&quot;freeway, toll-free road&quot;)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. toll booth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised form of the expression in neuter plural διόδια χρήματα &lt; an adjective διόδιος) by ellipsis of χρήματα n pl. From Ancient Greek δίοδος, from διά in the form of δι- (di-) + ὁδός).</div><b>διόρθωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural διορθώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>correction, proof-reading</div><b>διότι</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>because, for</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek διότι.</div><b>διύλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) purification, refinement</div><b>διώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cyprus form of <a href="bword://δίνω">δίνω</a></div><b>διώκομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prosecuted, persecuted</div><b>διώκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural διώκτες, feminine διώκτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. persecutor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pursuer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διώκτης. By surface analysis, διώκ(ω)) + -της (-tis).</div><b>διώκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to persecute</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to seek</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to prosecute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek διώκω.</div><b>διώξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chasing away</div>
  <div class='def'>2. expulsion, ousting</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dismissal (from employment)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. banishment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From διωξ- (diox-) + -ιμο (-imo).</div><b>διώροφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine διώροφη, neuter διώροφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. two-storey</div>
  <div class='def'>2. double-decker</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>διώροφο</b> λεωφορείο - double-decker bus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek διώροφος. By surface analysis, δι- (di-, from δις) + -ώροφος.</div><b>διώχνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to chase away, to drive away, to send away, to shoo, to get rid of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to expel, to oust, to turf out (to remove from a place)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to expel (to remove from membership)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to fire (to terminate the employment of)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to banish, to chase away, to drive away (to expel from the mind: feeling, pain, thought, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to turn away (to refuse to admit someone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek διώχνω, from Ancient Greek διώκω.</div><b>δογματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δογματική, neuter δογματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dogmatic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δογματικός.</div><b>δοθιήνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δοθιήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boil (accumulation of pus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δοθιήν.</div><b>δοκάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δοκάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beam (horizontal component of building made of wood, concrete, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. goalpost, crossbar; woodwork</div><b>δοκίμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δοκίμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. proof</div>
  <div class='ex'>ασελιδοποίητο <b>δοκίμιο</b> - galley proof</div>
  <div class='def'>2. essay, treatise</div><b>δοκησισοφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δοκησισοφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pedantry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δοκησισοφία.</div><b>δοκιμάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to try, try out</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δοκίμασες</b> ποτέ πίτσα με ανανά; - Have you ever tried pineapple pizza?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to test</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to taste</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to attempt, try</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δοκίμασε</b> να σκαρφαλώσει το βουνό. - He tried to climb the mountain.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δοκιμάζω; By surface analysis, δόκιμος + -άζω.</div><b>δοκιμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δοκιμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trial, test</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>δοκιμή</b> πρέπει να εκτελείται σε αντιπροσωπευτικό δείγμα του λιπάσματος. - The test must be carried out on a representative sample of fertilizer.</div><b>δοκιμασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δοκιμασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ordeal (a painful or trying experience)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strain (a painful experience)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δοκῐμᾰσῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>δοκιμασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine δοκιμασμένη, neuter δοκιμασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tried out, tested</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) experienced</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) who has suffered a lot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of δοκιμάζομαι, passive voice of δοκιμάζω (“try out”).</div><b>δοκιμαστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δοκιμαστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fitting room, changing room (for trying on clothes)</div><b>δοκιμαστικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trying out as a test</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δοκιμαστικός">δοκιμαστικός</a></div><b>δοκιμαστικέ σωλήνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vocative singular of <a href="bword://δοκιμαστικός">δοκιμαστικός</a> <a href="bword://σωλήνας">σωλήνας</a></div><b>δοκιμαστικοί σωλήνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of <a href="bword://δοκιμαστικός">δοκιμαστικός</a> <a href="bword://σωλήνας">σωλήνας</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. vocative plural of <a href="bword://δοκιμαστικός">δοκιμαστικός</a> <a href="bword://σωλήνας">σωλήνας</a></div><b>δοκιμαστικού σωλήνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://δοκιμαστικός">δοκιμαστικός</a> <a href="bword://σωλήνας">σωλήνας</a></div><b>δοκιμαστικούς σωλήνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative plural of <a href="bword://δοκιμαστικός">δοκιμαστικός</a> <a href="bword://σωλήνας">σωλήνας</a></div><b>δοκιμαστικό σωλήνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative singular of <a href="bword://δοκιμαστικός">δοκιμαστικός</a> <a href="bword://σωλήνας">σωλήνας</a></div><b>δοκιμαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δοκιμαστική, neuter δοκιμαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>test, trial</div><b>δοκιμαστικός σωλήνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δοκιμαστικοί σωλήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>test tube</div><b>δοκιμαστικών σωλήνων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of <a href="bword://δοκιμαστικός">δοκιμαστικός</a> <a href="bword://σωλήνας">σωλήνας</a></div><b>δοκός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or masculine (plural δοκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beam (horizontal component of a building, made of wood, concrete, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crossbar</div>
  <div class='def'>3. goalpost</div>
  <div class='def'>4. beam</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δοκός.</div><b>δολάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δολάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dollar (currency and coin)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δολάριο</b> ΗΠΑ - US dollar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English dollar.</div><b>δολιοφθορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δολιοφθορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sabotage (deliberate action aimed at weakening an enemy)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sabotage (acts with intent to obstruct the national defenses of a country)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δόλιος + φθορά.</div><b>δολιοφθορέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δολιοφθορείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saboteur</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δολιοφθορά + -έας.</div><b>δολομίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural δολομίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) dolomite</div><b>δολοφονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δολοφονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>premediated murder, homicide, assassination</div><b>δολοφονημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine δολοφονημένη, neuter δολοφονημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>murdered, assassinated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of δολοφονούμαι, passive voice of δολοφονώ (“I murder”).</div><b>δολοφονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δολοφονική, neuter δολοφονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. murder, murderous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. viciously attacking</div><b>δολοφονούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be assassinated, murdered</div><b>δολοφονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to murder, assassinate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δολοφονῶ, contracted form of δολοφονέω. See δόλος m and φόνος m.</div><b>δολοφόνισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δολοφόνισσες, masculine δολοφόνος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>murderer, murderess</div><b>δολοφόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δολοφόνοι, feminine δολοφόνισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. murderer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. assassin</div>
  <div class='def'>3. killer (something that causes death)</div><b>δολώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bait</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δολόω + suffix -ώνω.</div><b>δομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>structure (social, political, physical, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>δομή</b> του ανθρώπινου σώματος - the structure of the human body</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δομή, from Proto-Indo-European *doméh₂, from the root *dem- (“to build”).</div><b>δομόλιθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δομόλιθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A large buildingstone; a large stone utilized in the architecture of a building.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, δόμος + λίθος.</div><b>δονούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to vibrate, shake</div><b>δονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to make vibrate, induce vibration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δονέω.</div><b>δοξάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to glorify, praise</div><b>δοξάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δοξάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bow (used for playing violin, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δοξάριον.</div><b>δορκάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δορκάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>roe deer</div><b>δορυφορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δορυφορική, neuter δορυφορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) satellite (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δορυφόρ(ος)) + -ικός. Compare Ancient Greek δορυφορικός.</div><b>δορυφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δορυφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>satellite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δορῠφόρος, in the sense of “bodyguard”, equivalent to the Latin satelles. By surface analysis, δόρυ + -φόρος.</div><b>δοσοληψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δοσοληψίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transaction</div>
  <div class='def'>2. commercial relationship</div><b>δοσολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dosage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δόσ(η)) + -ο- (-o-) + -λογία.</div><b>δοτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δοτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dative (case)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek δοτῐκή.</div><b>δουλέμπορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δουλέμποροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slave trader, people trafficker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δούλος + έμπορος.</div><b>δουλεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δουλείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slavery, enslavement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δουλεία.</div><b>δουλειά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δουλειές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. employment, work, job</div>
  <div class='def'>2. task, job</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δουλειά, from Hellenistic Koine Greek δουλεία with synizesis, from δουλεία, from δοῦλος.</div><b>δουλειά δεν είχε ο διάβολος και έπνιγε τα παιδιά του</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) the devil makes work for idle hands, idle hands are the devil&#x27;s workshop (one who is idle will likely come to do evil)</div><b>δουλειά δεν είχε ο διάβολος, γαμούσε τα παιδιά του</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) the devil makes work for idle hands, idle hands are the devil&#x27;s workshop (one who is idle will likely come to do evil)</div><b>δουλεμπόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δουλεμπόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slave trade, human trafficking</div><b>δουλευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δουλευτές or δουλευτάδες, feminine δουλεύτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. worker (especially a good one)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. labourer (UK), laborer (US)</div><b>δουλεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be employed</div><b>δουλεύτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δουλευτές, masculine δουλευτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. worker (especially a good one)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. labourer (UK), laborer (US)</div><b>δουλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to work</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to pull somebody&#x27;s leg</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek δουλεύω, from Ancient Greek δουλόω, from δοῦλος.</div><b>δουλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δουλική, neuter δουλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slavish, servile</div><b>δοχεία απορριμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://δοχείο">δοχείο</a> απορριμάτων</div><b>δοχεία απορριμμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://δοχείο">δοχείο</a> απορριμμάτων</div><b>δοχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δοχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. container, receptacle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pot, urn, vase</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bin, can</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δοχεῖον.</div><b>δοχείο απορριμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δοχεία απορριμάτων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δοχείο">δοχείο</a> απορριμμάτων</div><b>δοχείο απορριμμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δοχεία απορριμμάτων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dustbin (UK), trash can (US)</div><b>δούκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δούκες, feminine δούκισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>duke (male ruler of a duchy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δούκας, form of δούξ, from Koine Greek δούξ, from Latin dux.</div><b>δούκισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δούκισσες, masculine δούκας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>duchess (rank in the nobility; wife of a duke)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δούκας + -ισσα (-issa, “feminine form”)</div><b>δούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δούλες, masculine δούλος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slave (female)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (obsolete) maidservant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δούλος m δουλ- + -α (-a, “suffix for feminine”). Compare to the ancient δούλη.</div><b>δούλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δούλες, masculine δούλος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (as noun, in ecclesiastic phrases) serving God (literally: slave of God) (female)</div>
  <div class='ex'>βαπτίζεται η <b>δούλη</b> του Θεού - the servant of God is baptized</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as adjective, in formal phrases) enslaved</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δούλη</b> χώρα - enslaved country</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The ancient δούλη, used in ecclesiastical or formal phrases. Compare to the modern feminine δούλα (with standard modern feminine suffix -α)</div><b>δούλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δούλοι, feminine δούλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δοῦλος.</div><b>δούρειο ίππο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative singular of δούρειος <a href="bword://ίππος">ίππος</a></div><b>δούρειοι ίπποι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative plural of δούρειος <a href="bword://ίππος">ίππος</a></div><b>δούρειος ίππος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δούρειοι ίπποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek) Trojan horse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Trojan horse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δούρειος ἵππος.</div><b>δούρειου ίππου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of δούρειος <a href="bword://ίππος">ίππος</a></div><b>δούρειους ίππους</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative plural of δούρειος <a href="bword://ίππος">ίππος</a></div><b>δούρειων ίππων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of δούρειος <a href="bword://ίππος">ίππος</a></div><b>δρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://δραχμή">δραχμή</a></div><b>δράκαινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δράκαινες, masculine δράκος or δράκοντας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dragon (legendary serpentine or reptilian creature)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Γάιδαρος στο «Σρεκ» ερωτεύτηκε τη <b>δράκαινα</b>. - Donkey from Shrek fell in love with the dragon.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) dragon, harridan, shrew (an unpleasant woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σωστή <b>δράκαινα</b> αυτή η γυναίκα! - That woman is a right dragon!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dragonet (any of the fish in the family Callionymidae)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δράκαινα, feminine form of δράκων.</div><b>δράκοντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δράκοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δράκος">δράκος</a> (dragon)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δράκων.</div><b>δράκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δράκοι, feminine δράκαινα or δράκισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dragon (legendary serpentine or reptilian creature)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Άγιος Γεώργιος σκότωσε τον <b>δράκο</b>. - St George killed the dragon.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) beast, monster (a serial rapist and/or murderer)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>δράκος</b> των βορείων προαστίων - the beast of the northern suburbs</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (dated, vernacular) A male baby born with a lot of hair (especially on the back), who in older times was thought to grow up to be brave and courageous.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δράκος, from Ancient Greek δράκων with metaplasm -ων to -ος.
Compare Mariupol Greek дъра́кус.</div><b>δράκουλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δράκουλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vampire (the Western &#x27;Dracula&#x27;-based depiction common in films and literature i.e. wearing black cape and having sharp canine teeth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English Dracula.</div><b>δράμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. drama, theatrical play</div>
  <div class='ex'>το αρχαίο <b>δράμα</b> - the ancient drama</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) shocking or disturbing situation or event</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι πόλεις έχουν γεμίσει αυτοκίνητα. <b>Δράμα</b> η κατάσταση! - The cities are full of cars. It&#x27;s a joke of a situation!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δρᾶμα, from δράω.
(For figurative sense): Semantic loan from French drame in its new sense.</div><b>δράμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δράμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (obsolete) drachm, dram, dirham (weight unit equal to ¹⁄₄₀₀ oka, in Greece equal to 3.203 grams)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) smidgen, ounce (small amount)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχει ούτε ένα <b>δράμι</b> μυαλό. - He hasn&#x27;t a smidgen of intelligence.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Reborrowing from the mediaeval δράμι, δράμιον from the Arabic دِرْهَم (dirham), from the ancient Persian 𐭦𐭥𐭦𐭭 (drahm), from the ancient Greek δραχμή f. Αlso see the Hellenistic diminutive δραχμίον n.</div><b>δράπανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δράπανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drill (tool for making holes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δράπανον.</div><b>δράση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>action (of person or object)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek δρᾶσις, or from Koine Greek δρᾶ(σις), “effectiveness”) + -ση (-si).</div><b>δράστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δράστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perpetrator</div><b>δράχνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αδράχνω">αδράχνω</a></div><b>δρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal, rare) to collect fruit</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έδρεψα</b> δάφνες - I collected laurels</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to gain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δρέπω, possibly from Proto-Indo-European *der- (“to tear, pluck”), related to Proto-Slavic *drapati (“to scratch”). Less certain is the relation with Proto-Germanic *trabō (“fringe”) and Medieval Latin drappus.</div><b>δρακόντειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δρακόντεια, neuter δρακόντειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>draconian, oppressive.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> After Δράκων, the harsh Athenian legislator.</div><b>δραματικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δραματικός">δραματικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dramatically</div><b>δραματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δραματική, neuter δραματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dramatic, tragic, exciting, melodramatic</div><b>δραματολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δραματολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dramatics, theatricals (the art of acting)</div><b>δραματοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δραματοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dramatisation (UK):, dramatization (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) exaggerated representation</div><b>δραματοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dramatise, adapt for the theatre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δραματοποιέω. By surface analysis, δράμα + -ποιώ.</div><b>δραματουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δραματουργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the art of the playwright</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the act writing a play</div>
  <div class='def'>3. the body of dramatic works</div><b>δραματουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural δραματουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dramatist, playwright</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) dramaturge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δρᾱματουργός. By surface analysis, δράμα + -ουργός</div><b>δραπέτευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δραπετεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>escape</div><b>δραπέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δραπέτες, feminine δραπέτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>escapee, runaway, absconder, fugitive (person who has escaped confinement eg prison)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>δραπέτες</b> κρύφτηκαν σ’ ένα εγκαταλειμμένο σπίτι. - The escapees hid in an abandoned house.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δραπέτης.</div><b>δραπέτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δραπέτισσες, masculine δραπέτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>escapee, runaway, absconder, fugitive (person who has escaped confinement eg prison)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>δραπέτισσες</b> κρύφτηκαν σ’ ένα εγκαταλειμμένο σπίτι. - The escapees hid in an abandoned house.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δραπέτης + -ισσα (-issa).</div><b>δραπετεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to escape, get away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derived from Ancient Greek δραπέτης.</div><b>δραστήριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δραστήρια, neuter δραστήριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>active (given to action)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δραστήριος.</div><b>δραστηριοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to call to action, to mobilize</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to activate (to encourage development or induce increased activity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δραστήρι(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>δραστηριότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δραστηριότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>activity</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δραστηριότητα μαθηματικών για το νηπιαγωγείο. - Maths activities for the nursery school.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δραστηριότης, equivalent to δραστήριος + -ότητα.</div><b>δραστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. effective, efficacious, potent</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δραστικό</b> συστατικό - active ingredient</div>
  <div class='def'>2. drastic</div><b>δραχμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δραχμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) drachma (former Greek currency 1833-2002)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical, obsolete) drachma (an ancient unit of weight)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δρᾰχμή.</div><b>δρεπάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δρεπάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sickle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. scythe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δρεπάνιον, the diminutive of δρέπανον. See δρεπάνη for more.</div><b>δριμύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δριμεία, neuter δριμύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. harsh, piercing, severe, sharp (cold, winter, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. acrid, pungent, sharp (aroma, taste)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) sharp, severe (criticism, words, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δριμύς.</div><b>δρολάπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δρολάπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rainstorm, downpour, squall (with strong wind)</div><b>δρομάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δρομάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dromedary, Arabian camel (Camelus dromedarius)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δρομάς.</div><b>δρομέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δρομείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. runner</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) ratites (running, flightless birds)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rotor</div>
  <div class='def'>4. cursor (an indicator, often a blinking line or bar and sometimes called a caret, indicating where the next insertion or other edit will take place)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δρομεύς with semantic loan from English cursor.</div><b>δρομολογητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δρομολογητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Internet) router (device connecting local area networks to form a larger internet)</div><b>δρομολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to put into operation, to bring into service, to launch (:means of transport on a given scheduled route)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to set in train, to launch (to start a planned process, to initiate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from δρομολόγιο with -ώ suffix.</div><b>δρομολόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δρομολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. itinerary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. timetable</div><b>δρομόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δρομόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>log (device for measuring a ship&#x27;s speed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κοινό δρομόμετρο (common log)</div><b>δροσίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to refresh</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (impersonal) to be cool (of weather)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek δροσίζω. By surface analysis, δρόσος + -ίζω.</div><b>δροσερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δροσερή, neuter δροσερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cool (of a mildly low temperature)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fresh (invigoratingly cool and refreshing)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of plant material) fresh (still green and not dried)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. fresh (youthful; florid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δροσερός.</div><b>δροσιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δροσιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cool air</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dew</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) freshness (of appearance, artwork, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek δροσιά, from Koine Greek δροσιά, from Ancient Greek δρόσος.</div><b>δροσόπαγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rime, hoarfrost</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δρόσος + πάγος.</div><b>δρούγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>distaff, spindle, bobbin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Serbo-Croatian drȕga /дру̏га, from Proto-Slavic *drǫgъ (“bar, pole”), most likely via Albanian drugë.</div><b>δρυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://δρυς">δρυς</a> (drys)</div><b>δρυμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δρυμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. forest</div>
  <div class='def'>2. park</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δρυμός.</div><b>δρυοκολάπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δρυοκολάπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>woodpecker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δρυοκολάπτης from Ancient Greek δρῦς + κολάπτω, carve, engrave”).</div><b>δρυς</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δρύες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oak tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δρῦς. Compare Albanian dru.</div><b>δρχ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://δραχμή">δραχμή</a></div><b>δρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to act, take action, do something</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to take effect, have an effect on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δρῶ.</div><b>δρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δρόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. roadway, road, street, way</div>
  <div class='def'>2. journey</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *drem-
Proto-Indo-European *-os
Proto-Hellenic *-os
Ancient Greek -ος (-os)
Ancient Greek δρόμος
Greek δρόμος
Inherited from Ancient Greek δρόμος, from Proto-Indo-European *drem- + Ancient Greek -ος (-os)..</div><b>δρύινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δρύινη, neuter δρύινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oak, oaken (made of oak)</div><b>δρύπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δρύπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drupe (stoned fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French drupe.</div><b>δυάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δυάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. two-roomed apartment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. two, deuce</div><b>δυαδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυαδική, neuter δυαδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>binary</div><b>δυνάμεθα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) : &quot;we can&quot;)</div><b>δυνάμεις καταδρομών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://δύναμη">δύναμη</a> καταδρομών</div><b>δυνάστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δυνάστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oppressing ruler, dynast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δυνάστης.</div><b>δυναμίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δυναμίτες, feminine δυναμίτιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dynamite</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dynamiter (of a person explodes dynamite)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) dynamite (of a lively person who achieves)</div><b>δυναμίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυναμίτιδες, masculine δυναμίτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) dynamite (of a lively person who achieves)</div><b>δυναμικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δυναμικός">δυναμικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dynamically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://δυναμικό">δυναμικό</a></div><b>δυναμική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dynamics</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dynamics</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) dynamics (forces that stimulate growth, change, or development)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French dynamique, from Ancient Greek δυναμικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://δυναμικός">δυναμικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>δυναμικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δυναμικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (potential) potential</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) resources</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly nominalized neuter singular of Koine Greek δυναμικός with semantic loan from French potentiel.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://δυναμικός">δυναμικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δυναμικός">δυναμικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>δυναμικό ενέργειας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>action potential</div><b>δυναμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυναμική, neuter δυναμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dynamic, powerful, forceful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δυναμικός.</div><b>δυναμιτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dynamite (detonate/explode dynamite)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to motivate, energise (UK), energize (US)</div><b>δυναμοδότη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυναμοδότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>power take-off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French dynamo) +
δότης</div><b>δυναμό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) dynamo</div><b>δυναστεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυναστείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dynasty</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το 1925, η <b>δυναστεία</b> των Παχλαβί ανήλθαν στην εξουσία στη Περσία - In 1925, the Pahlavi dynasty came to power in Persia.</div><b>δυνατά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δυνατός">δυνατός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strongly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hard (with much force)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Bρέχει <b>δυνατά</b>. - It&#x27;s raining hard.</div><b>δυνατόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, indeclinable) possibility</div>
  <div class='ex'>είναι <b>δυνατόν</b> - it&#x27;s possible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δῠνᾰτόν, singular neuter of δῠνᾰτός.</div><b>δυνατός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυνατή, neuter δυνατό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strong, loud</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strong, powerful</div>
  <div class='def'>3. possible, potential</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δυνατός.</div><b>δυνατότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυνατότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. possibility (the quality of being possible)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. potential (a currently unrealized ability)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. possibility (an option or choice, usually used in context with future events)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural) means, wherewithal (instrument or condition for attaining a purpose; ability required to accomplish some task)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δυνατός + -ότητα, a calque of French possibilité.</div><b>δυνητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>potential, virtual</div><b>δυο</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) two</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δύο.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pair</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δύο.</div><b>δυο δυο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δυο">δυο</a>-<a href="bword://δυο">δυο</a> (dyo-dyo)</div><b>δυο-δυο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two by two, in pairs</div><b>δυοσμαρίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δυοσμαρίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rosemary (Salvia rosmarinus, syn. Rosmarinus officinalis)</div><b>δυσάρεστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσάρεστη, neuter δυσάρεστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unpleasant, displeasing, disagreeable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δυσάρεστος, with semantic loan from French désagréable.</div><b>δυσίατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσίατη, neuter δυσίατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refractory (resistant to treatment, difficult to heal)</div><b>δυσανάγνωστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unreadable, illegible (impossible to decipher)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) unreadable (poorly written)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δυσανάγνωστος.</div><b>δυσανάλογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσανάλογη, neuter δυσανάλογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disproportionate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from δυσ- (dys-) + ανάλογος, a calque of French disproportionné.</div><b>δυσανασχετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to resent, to express resentment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δυσανασχετῶ.</div><b>δυσαρέσκεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσαρέσκειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. resentment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. displeasure</div>
  <div class='def'>3. discontent, disaffection</div><b>δυσαρεστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to displease, to discontent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δυσαρεστῶ.</div><b>δυσαρμονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσαρμονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disharmony</div><b>δυσδιάκριτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσδιάκριτη, neuter δυσδιάκριτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indistinct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δυσδιάκριτος. By surface analysis, δυσ- (dys-) + διάκριτος.</div><b>δυσεντερία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσεντερίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dysentery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δυσεντερία.</div><b>δυσθυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσθυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dysthymia (tendency to be depressed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek δυσθῡμία, from δυσ- (dys-, “bad”) + θῡμός.</div><b>δυσκίνητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσκίνητη, neuter δυσκίνητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ungainly, ponderous, cumbersome, sluggish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) slow in thought and comprehension</div><b>δυσκατέργαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσκατέργαστη, neuter δυσκατέργαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refractory (resistant to great heat)</div><b>δυσκοίλιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσκοίλια, neuter δυσκοίλιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. constipated, costive (unable to defecate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είμαι <b>δυσκοίλιος</b> εδώ και τρεις μέρες. - I&#x27;ve been constipated for the last three days.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. constipating (causing constipation)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δυσκοίλια</b> φρούτα - constipating fruit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δυσκοίλιος, equivalent to δυσ- (dys-, “bad, hard”) + κοιλιά.</div><b>δυσκοιλιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) constipation, costiveness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δυσκοίλιος + -ότητα. First attested 1856.</div><b>δυσκολία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσκολίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>difficulty, inconvenience, trouble</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχα μεγάλη <b>δυσκολία</b> να τον διώξω. - I had great difficulty getting rid of him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δυσκολία, equivalent to δύσκολος + -ία.</div><b>δυσκολεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to made difficult</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to find it difficult to</div><b>δυσκολεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hamper, inconvenience</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to complicate, make difficult</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to find difficult</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δύσκολος δυσκολ- + -εύω</div><b>δυσκολογιάτρευτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσκολογιάτρευτη, neuter δυσκολογιάτρευτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refractory (resistant to treatment, difficult to heal)</div><b>δυσκρασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dyscrasia, blood disorder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δυσ- (dys-, “bad”) + κρασία</div><b>δυσκόλως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) with difficulty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek δυσκόλως.</div><b>δυσλειτουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσλειτουργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>malfunction, dysfunction</div><b>δυσλειτουργικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσλειτουργική, neuter δυσλειτουργικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dysfunctional (functioning incorrectly or abnormally)</div><b>δυσλεξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) dyslexia</div><b>δυσμαί</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural) west, occident</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δυσμαί.</div><b>δυσμηνόρροια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσμηνόρροιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dysmenorrhea</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αντιμέτωπο με την επώδυνη κατάσταση της <b>δυσμηνόρροιας</b> βρίσκεται κάθε μήνα τουλάχιστον το 15% των εφήβων και νεαρών γυναικών, βιώνοντας σοβαρό πόνο και απουσιάζοντας από το σχολείο ή την εργασία. - At least 15% of teenagers and young women face the painful condition of dysmenorrhea every month, experiencing severe pain and being absent from school or work.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French dysménorrhée. First attested in 1876.</div><b>δυσμορφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural δυσμορφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) dysmorphia</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) malformation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δυσμορφίᾱ</div><b>δυσνόητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσνόητη, neuter δυσνόητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obscure, abstruse (hard to understand)</div><b>δυσοσμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσοσμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bad smell, foul odour, fetor, stench, stink</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δυσοσμία</b> του στόματος - bad breath, halitosis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δυσοσμία. By surface analysis, δυσ- (dys-) + οσμή + -ία.</div><b>δυσπεψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) indigestion, dyspepsia</div><b>δυσπιστία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) disbelief, incredulity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) scepticism</div><b>δυσπλασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσπλασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dysplasia</div><b>δυσπροσάρμοστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυσπροσάρμοστη, neuter δυσπροσάρμοστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unadaptable, adaptable with difficulty</div><b>δυσπρόσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) dysprosium</div><b>δυστονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυστονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dystonia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from New Latin dystonia.</div><b>δυστοπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυστοπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dystopia</div><b>δυστροφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυστροφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dystrophy</div><b>δυστυχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυστυχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. misfortune; an undesirable event</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) unhappiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δυστυχία f.</div><b>δυστυχισμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυστυχισμένη, neuter δυστυχισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unhappy (characterised by, feeling or showing unhappiness)</div><b>δυστυχώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unfortunately</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δυστυχῶς, from δυστυχής + -ως, the former component from δυσ- (dus-, “bad”) + τύχη + -ής.</div><b>δυστύχημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δυστυχήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very serious, tragic accident</div><b>δυσφήμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>defamation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δυσφήμισις.</div><b>δυσφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσφορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. discomfort (mental or bodily distress)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. discomfort (something that disturbs one’s comfort; an annoyance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δυσφορία.</div><b>δυσχέρεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυσχέρειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. difficulty, impediment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. distress</div>
  <div class='ex'>αναπνευστική <b>δυσχέρεια</b> - respiratory distress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δυσχέρεια.</div><b>δυτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>westward, westwards</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://δυτικός">δυτικός</a></div><b>δυτικά-βορειοδυτικά</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>west-northwest</div><b>δυτικά-νοτιοδυτικά</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>west-southwest</div><b>δυτικοευρωπαϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυτικοευρωπαϊκή, neuter δυτικοευρωπαϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Western European</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δυτικός + ευρωπαϊκός.</div><b>δυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δυτική, neuter δυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Western, Occidental; belonging to the West; pertaining to the Occident; related to the Western world or to Western Christianity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. west, westward, westerly, western</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δῠτικός. Morphologically δύω + -τικός. In the proper senses, semantic loan from French occidental and English western.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δυτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. westerner</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Western Christian (Roman Catholic or Protestant Christian)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. The West in a sociopolitical sense, i.e. the society, culture, politics of the peoples of Western Europe and North America</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα δόγματα των <b>δυτικών</b> - The doctrines of the West</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δῠτικός. Morphologically δύω + -τικός. In the proper senses, semantic loan from French occidental and English western.</div><b>δυφίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δυφία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>binary digit, bit</div><b>δυφιοαπεικόνιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυφιοαπεικονίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bitmap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δυφίο + απεικόνιση</div><b>δυχατέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυχατέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>daughter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θυγάτηρ, from Proto-Hellenic *tʰúgatēr, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰugh₂tḗr.</div><b>δυόμισι</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (invariable) half past two</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (invariable) two and a half</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δύο + μισό.</div><b>δυόσμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δυόσμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mint, spearmint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἡδύοσμον.</div><b>δω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εδώ">εδώ</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular dependent of <a href="bword://βλέπω">βλέπω</a></div><b>δωδέκατος</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine (feminine δωδέκατη, neuter δωδέκατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twelfth, 12th (ordinal numeral)</div><b>δωδεκάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δωδεκάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twelve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> δωδεκ (dodek, “twelve”) + -άδα</div><b>δωδεκάθεο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek, collective) the twelve gods of Olympus, the twelve Olympians (the major deities of the Greek pantheon)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δωδεκάθεον.</div><b>δωδεκάωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δωδεκάωρη, neuter δωδεκάωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twelve-hour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek δωδεκάωρος. By surface analysis, δώδεκα + ώρ(α)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>δωδεκαδάκτυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) duodenum</div><b>δωδεκαδάκτυλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δωδεκαδάκτυλο">δωδεκαδάκτυλο</a></div><b>δωδεκαδάχτυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://δωδεκαδάκτυλο">δωδεκαδάκτυλο</a></div><b>δωδεκαδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δωδεκαδική, neuter δωδεκαδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>duodecimal</div><b>δωδεκαθεϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural δωδεκαθεϊσμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) Hellenism (the modern-day revival of the polytheistic religious system of Ancient Greece).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δωδεκάθε(ο), “the twelve gods”) + -ισμός. An earlier form, δωδεκαθεΐα, is attested in 1830.</div><b>δωδεκαθεϊστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δωδεκαθεϊστές, feminine δωδεκαθεΐστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Hellenist (a follower and practitioner of Hellenism)</div><b>δωδεκαθεϊστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δωδεκαθεϊστική, neuter δωδεκαθεϊστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Hellenistic (of or relating to Hellenism or Hellenists)</div><b>δωδεκανησιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δωδεκανησιακή, neuter δωδεκανησιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Dodecanese (relating to the island group)</div><b>δωδεκαρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δωδεκαρχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) A small unit of 12 men in the Greek army during the years 1828–1829.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From δωδεκάρχης.</div><b>δωμάτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δωμάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>room (in home or hotel)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek δωμάτιον.</div><b>δωρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to give, donate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δωρίζω, from Ancient Greek δῶρον + -ίζω. By surface analysis, δώρο + -ίζω.</div><b>δωρεά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δωρεές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. donation, gift</div>
  <div class='def'>2. endowment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek δωρεᾱ́.</div><b>δωρεάν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>free, free of charge, gratis, as a gift</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek δωρεᾱ́ν.</div><b>δωροδοκία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δωροδοκίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bribe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bribery</div><b>δωροδοκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bribe</div><b>δόγης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δόγηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doge</div><b>δόγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δόγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dogma, doctrine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. religion, cult</div>
  <div class='def'>3. strategy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δόγμα.</div><b>δόκανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δόκανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trap (device designed to catch or kill animals)</div><b>δόλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δολώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bait</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δόλωμᾰ, with semantic shift &quot;trick, (intellectual) ruse&quot; &gt; &quot;(physical) bait&quot;.</div><b>δόμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δομήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>building, construction (the act or process)</div><b>δόνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δονήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tremor, vibration</div><b>δόντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δόντια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tooth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tusk</div>
  <div class='def'>3. clove (of garlic)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek δόντιν, δόντιον, from Ancient Greek *ὀδόντιον, diminutive of ὀδούς, ὀδόντος, from Proto-Indo-European *h₃dónts (“tooth”).
Compare Mariupol Greek дъонд.</div><b>δόξα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δόξες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. glory, kudos, fame</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pride</div>
  <div class='def'>3. star (of film, pop, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. halo (on religious paintings)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial) rainbow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δόξα.
Compare Mariupol Greek дъо́кса.</div><b>δόξα να &#x27;χει ο Θεός</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thank God</div><b>δόξα σοι ο Θεός</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thank God (literally, “Glory to you, oh God”)</div><b>δόξα τω Θεώ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thank God!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δόξα τω Θεώ</b>, γλιτώσαμε απ’ τον κίνδυνο. - Thank God, we escaped the danger.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> In polytonic script, δόξα τῷ Θεῷ, from δόξα f and dative case τῷ θεῷ m, written with uppercase Θεός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glory to God</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> In polytonic script, δόξα τῷ Θεῷ, from δόξα f and dative case τῷ θεῷ m, written with uppercase Θεός.</div><b>δόρυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δόρατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pike, spear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δόρυ.</div><b>δόση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δόσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dose, instalment, tranche</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dose (of medication)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dose (of radiation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ισοδύναμη <b>δόση</b> - equivalent dose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δόσις. Stem δο- + -σις (-sis), modern -ση (-si), δίδωμι).</div><b>δότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural δότες, feminine δότρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>donor, giver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δοτήρ.</div><b>δότρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δότριες, masculine δότης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>donor, giver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δοτήρ.</div><b>δύναμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυνάμεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. force</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ισχύς ισούται με το γινόμενο της ταχύτητας επί τη <b>δύναμη</b>. - Power equals the velocity multiplied by the force.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. power</div>
  <div class='def'>3. power, force, strength, brawn</div>
  <div class='def'>4. force</div>
  <div class='ex'>στρατιωτικές <b>δυνάμεις</b> - military forces</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek δύναμις. For scientific and modern senses, semantic loan from French puissance or English power.</div><b>δύναμη καταδρομών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δυνάμεις καταδρομών)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commando force (small, elite fighting force)</div><b>δύναμις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://δύναμη">δύναμη</a></div><b>δύο</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) two</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δύο, from Proto-Hellenic *dúwo, from Proto-Indo-European *duwó, *duwéh₃.
Compare Mariupol Greek дъы́я, дъи́я.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) two (playing card)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δύο, from Proto-Hellenic *dúwo, from Proto-Indo-European *duwó, *duwéh₃.
Compare Mariupol Greek дъы́я, дъи́я.</div><b>δύο τελείες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The modern Greek colon ⟨:⟩.</div><b>δύο χιλιάδες</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two thousand</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>δύο χιλιάδες</b> ευρώ - two thousand euros</div><b>δύση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. west</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sunset, sundown</div>
  <div class='def'>3. decline, end</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δύσις.</div><b>δύσθυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δύσθυμη, neuter δύσθυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gloomy, morose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δύσθυμος.</div><b>δύσκολα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δύσκολος">δύσκολος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δύσκολος">δύσκολος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://δύσκολος">δύσκολος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with difficulty</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτόν τον εγκληματία <b>δύσκολα</b> θα τον πιάσουνε. - It will be difficult for them to catch that criminal</div><b>δύσκολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δύσκολη, neuter δύσκολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. difficult, hard, tough, arduous (not easy, requiring much effort)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα Κινέζικα είναι <b>δύσκολη</b> γλώσσα να μάθεις. - Chinese is a difficult language to learn.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. refractory (difficult to treat)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας μου πάσχει από <b>δύσκολη</b> ασθένεια. - My father is suffering from a refractory illness.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of people) difficult, uncooperative, troublesome (hard to manage)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μητέρα μου πάντα ήταν <b>δύσκολος</b> άνθρωπος. - My mother was always a difficult person.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δύσκολος, from Ancient Greek δυσ- (dus-, “bad, hard, unfortunate”) + Ancient Greek -κολος (-kolos, “keeper, tender, watcher”).</div><b>δύσοσμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δύσοσμη, neuter δύσοσμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smelly, fetid, foul-smelling, malodorous, whiffy (having an unpleasant odour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek δύσοσμος. By surface analysis, δύσ- + οσμή + -ος (-os).</div><b>δύσπνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural δύσπνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dyspnea, dyspnoea (difficult respiration)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δῠ́σπνοιᾰ.</div><b>δύστηκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δύστηκτη, neuter δύστηκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refractory (resistant to great heat)</div><b>δύστροπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine δύστροπη, neuter δύστροπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bad-tempered, grumpy, cantankerous</div><b>δύσχρ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of δύσχρηστος: rarely used</div><b>δύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to set, go down (sun, stars, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to decline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δύω.</div><b>δώδεκα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twelve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δώδεκα, from Proto-Indo-European *dwoΗdeḱm̥.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek дъо́дъэка, дъо́дъека.</div><b>δώρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural δώρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gift, donation, present</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bonus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek δῶρον, from Proto-Hellenic *dṓron, from Proto-Indo-European *déh₃rom.</div><b>ε΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ε΄">Ε΄</a></div><b>εάν</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αν">αν</a> (an)</div><b>είδα κι απόειδα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) After many unsuccessful efforts I am resigned to the situation.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έψαχνα για δουλειά για μήνες. <b>Είδα κι απόειδα</b> και αποφάσισα να φύγω για την Αμερική. - I was looking for a job for months. Ι considered [the situation] and reconsidered [with despair] and decided to leave for America.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> είδα, past tense of βλέπω, here &quot;checked, assured myself&quot; and απόειδα past tense of αποβλέπω, here, intensified, &quot;checking, looking once again without any different result&quot;.</div><b>είδηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ειδήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. news item, piece of news, news story</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) newsworthy event</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) news (in newspapers; on TV or radio)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εἴδησις, from εἴδω + -σις (-sis).</div><b>είδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural είδη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kind, form, type</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το θρανίο είναι ένα <b>είδος</b> επίπλου για σχολική χρήση. - The bench is a kind of furniture used in school.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. species</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο σημερινός άνθρωπος ανήκει στο <b>είδος</b> Homo sapiens. - Modern man belongs to the species Homo sapiens.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. article, item</div>
  <div class='ex'>καταστήματα με τουριστικά <b>είδη</b> και τοπικά προϊόντα - shops with tourist items and local products</div>
  <div class='def'>4. style, wear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἶδος.</div><b>είδωλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural είδωλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. idol</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reflection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἴδωλον.</div><b>είθισται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(impersonal, literary) it is customary</div>
  <div class='ex'>ως <b>είθισται</b> - as is customary, as is the custom, as usual</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἴθισται, third-person singular passive perfect form of εἰθίζω, poetic variant of ἐθίζω.</div><b>είκοσι</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twenty, 20</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εἴκοσι.</div><b>είκοσι ένα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twenty-one, 21</div><b>είκοσι δυο</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twenty-two, 22</div><b>είκοσι δύο</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twenty-two, 22</div><b>είκοσι τρία</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twenty-three, 23</div><b>είμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (no past) to be</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Είμαι</b> φίλος της. - I&#x27;m her friend.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, no past) there is, there are, to exist</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Είναι</b> μία γυναίκα στην πόρτα. - There is a woman at the door.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (no past, transitive) to support, be a supporter of (team, political party etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός εκεί <b>είναι</b> ΣΥΡΙΖΑ. - That guy over there is a SYRIZA supporter.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (auxiliary, no past) to be + participles to form alternative passive perfect tenses</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει λυθεί το πρόβλημα, <b>είναι</b> λυμένο. - The problem has been solved, it is [already] solved.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek εἶμαι, from Ancient Greek εἰμί, from Proto-Hellenic *ehmi, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁ésmi (“I am, I exist”).</div><b>είμαι ανοιχτό βιβλίο</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be an open book, wear one&#x27;s heart on one&#x27;s sleeve</div><b>είμεθα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of είμαστε</div><b>είναι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular present of <a href="bword://είμαι">είμαι</a>: &quot;he is, she is, it is&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>το όνομά μου <b>είναι</b> … - my name is …</div>
  <div class='def'>2. third-person plural present of <a href="bword://είμαι">είμαι</a>: &quot;they are&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἶναι, present infinitive of εἰμί.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) being, that which exists</div>
  <div class='ex'>«To Είναι και το Μηδέν» - Being and Nothingness
Title of the French book L&#x27;Être et le Néant by Jean-Paul Sartre.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) one&#x27;s inner world</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν έμαθα την είδηση του θανάτου του, κατέρρευσε όλο μου το <b>είναι</b>. - When I learnt the news of his death, my whole being collapsed.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, indeclinable) everything dear in life</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είσαι το <b>είναι</b> μου, ο έρωτας της ζωής μου. - You are my everything, my life&#x27;s love.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἶναι, present infinitive of εἰμί.</div><b>είναι κινέζικα για μένα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>it&#x27;s all Greek to me (literally, “it&#x27;s Chinese to me”)</div><b>είντα</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Cyprus, indeclinable) synonym of <a href="bword://τι">τι</a> (ti, “what?”), also why</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Είντα</b> κάνεις; - What are you doing? How do you do?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (before nouns) how great a, what kind of</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with adjectives and adverbs) how much</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek εἶντα or εἴντα, from τεῖντα / τείντα, equivalent to a contraction of the phrase τί εἶναι τά &gt; τί εἶν’ τά, equivalent to τι είναι τα.</div><b>είνταμπου</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus, indeclinable) synonym of <a href="bword://τι">τι</a> (ti) or <a href="bword://τι">τι</a> <a href="bword://είναι">είναι</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Είνταμπου</b> συντυχάννεις; - What are you talking about? What are you chatting about?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From είντα + μπου (bou)</div><b>είπα ό,τι είχα να πω</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. I rest my case</div>
  <div class='def'>2. I have said what I have to say.</div><b>είπαν της γριάς να χέσει κι αυτή ξεκωλιάστηκε</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, vulgar) give someone an inch and they&#x27;ll take a mile (concerning those who make excesses)</div><b>είπε ο γάιδαρος τον πετεινό κεφάλα</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the pot calling the kettle black</div><b>είρων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural είρωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ironist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἴρων.</div><b>είσοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural είσοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>entrance, way in</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἴσοδος.</div><b>είσπραξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εισπράξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. collection, receipt (of payments due, taxes, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>είσπραξη</b> (των) φόρων - tax collection</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (plural normally) receipts (a quantity or amount received; takings)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἴσπραξις with semantic loan from French perception. By surface analysis, εισπρά(ττω)) + -ξη (-xi).</div><b>είτε</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>either...or, whether..or</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Είτε</b> κερδίσεις <b>είτε</b> χάσεις, δε με νοιάζει. - Either win or lose, I don&#x27;t care.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἴτε.</div><b>είχανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of έχουν: &quot;they had&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>third-person plural imperfect of έχω (écho)</div><b>εαρινές ισημερίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of εαρινή <a href="bword://ισημερία">ισημερία</a></div><b>εαρινή ισημερία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εαρινές ισημερίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spring equinox, vernal equinox</div><b>εαρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εαρινή, neuter εαρινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spring</div>
  <div class='def'>2. equinoctial (of spring equinox)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εαρινή</b> ισημερία - spring equinox</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Lent, Lenten</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐαρινός.</div><b>εαυτός</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(followed by possessive pronoun) myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, themselves</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρόσεχε τον <b>εαυτό</b> σου! - Take care of yourself!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἑαυτός, from Ancient Greek ἑαυτοῦ.</div><b>εβένινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εβένινη, neuter εβένινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ebony (made of ebony wood)</div><b>εβίβα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheers!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian evviva, from e viva!, from vivere (“live”).</div><b>εβαπορέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) evaporated milk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French évaporé.</div><b>εβδομάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εβδομάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>week</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑβδομάς. Compare Latin hebdomas.</div><b>εβδομήκοντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://εβδομήντα">εβδομήντα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑβδομήκοντα.</div><b>εβδομήντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seventy (70)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑβδομήκοντα, from Proto-Hellenic *hebdmākontə, from Proto-Indo-European *septm̥-ḱómt-h₂, from *septḿ̥(d)ḱomt.</div><b>εβδομαδιαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εβδομαδιαία, neuter εβδομαδιαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>weekly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εβδομάδα. First attested 1856.</div><b>εβδομηκοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εβδομηκοστή, neuter εβδομηκοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seventieth (ordinal form of the number seventy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑβδομηκοστός.</div><b>εβλήθην</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Monotonic spelling of ἐβλήθην, first-person singular simple past indicative of βάλλομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://βάλλω">βάλλω</a></div><b>εβονίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εβονίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ebonite, vulcanite</div><b>εβραϊκά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Hebrew (language)</div><b>εβραϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εβραϊκή, neuter εβραϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Hebrew, Hebraic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek Ἑβραϊκός.</div><b>εγέρθητε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, formal, humorous, sometimes) you (plural) stand up! get up! on your (plural) feet!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγέρθητε, 2nd person plural imperative of the passive aorist (ἠγέρθην) of verb ἐγείρω.</div><b>εγέρθητι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, formal, humorous, sometimes) you (singular)stand up, get up, on your feet (an order to rise or stand)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εγέρθητι</b>, στρατιώτη! - On your feet, soldier!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγέρθητῐ, 2nd person singular imperative of the passive aorist (ἠγέρθην) of verb ἐγείρω.</div><b>εγγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγγίζω">αγγίζω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Ancient Greek ἐγγίζω.</div><b>εγγενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγγενής, neuter εγγενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>native, inherent, innate, inborn</div><b>εγγενώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inherently, innately</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εγγενής.</div><b>εγγλέζικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>English (language)</div><b>εγγλέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγγλέζικη, neuter εγγλέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>English (related to the country, people or language of England)</div><b>εγγλεζομαθημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγγλεζομαθημένη, neuter εγγλεζομαθημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>English in manner, of English upbringing.</div><b>εγγλεζόπουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εγγλεζόπουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>an English child</div><b>εγγονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγγονές, masculine εγγονός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>granddaughter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek εγγονή, from Koine Greek ἐγγόνη with shift of tone from paroxytone to oxytone, synchronically analyzable as the feminine form of εγγονός.</div><b>εγγονός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εγγονοί, feminine εγγονή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grandson</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἔγγονος, with shift of the tone from proparoxytone to oxytone.</div><b>εγγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be registered → and see <a href="bword://εγγράφω">εγγράφω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ἐγγράφομαι</div><b>εγγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to enrol as member of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to record</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to record together with as equally important</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to enter data</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of geometrical shapes) to enclose a shape into another</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * For the sense enrol, a learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐγγράφω and the sense enclose geometric shape, from the Hellenistic sense of the word: Koine Greek ἐγγράφω.</div><b>εγγραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγγραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. registration, enrolment (UK), enrollment (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subscription</div>
  <div class='def'>3. recording</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγγραφή. Morphologically, from εν- (εγ-) (“in”) + γραφή (“script”).</div><b>εγγραφτήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of εγγράφτηκαν, third-person plural simple past of <a href="bword://εγγράφομαι">εγγράφομαι</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://εγγράφω">εγγράφω</a></div><b>εγγυώμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) to guarantee, vouch</div><b>εγγόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγγόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εγγονή">εγγονή</a></div><b>εγγόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εγγόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grandchild</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek εγγόνι, diminutive form in -ι (-i) from εγγονός.</div><b>εγγύηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγγυήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bail, guarantee, pledge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. guarantee, warranty</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι υποχρεωμένοι από το νόμο να έχουν 2 χρόνια <b>εγγύηση</b>. - They are required by law to have a 2-year warranty.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. security deposit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek εγγύη(σις)) + -ση (-si), from the Ancient Greek ἐγγύη. For modern senses, a semantic loan from French garantie and English guaranty.</div><b>εγγύς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. near</div>
  <div class='ex'>στο <b>εγγύς</b> μέλλον - in the near future</div>
  <div class='def'>2. proximal (used as adjective)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εγγύς</b> φάλαγγα - proximal phalange</div><b>εγείρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to raise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to file a suit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐγείρω.</div><b>εγερθήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of εγερθήκαν, third-person plural simple past of εγείρομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://εγείρω">εγείρω</a></div><b>εγερθήτω</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, formal, humorous, sometimes) he/she must stand up! get up, on his/her feet!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγερθήτω, 3nd person singular imperative of the passive aorist (ἠγέρθην) of verb ἐγείρω.</div><b>εγερτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εγερτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reveille (bugle call)</div><b>εγκέφαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εγκέφαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγκέφαλος.</div><b>εγκαίρως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐγκαίρως, from Ancient Greek ἔγκαιρος + -ως.</div><b>εγκαθίσταμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to settle down, move in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be installed</div><b>εγκαθιδρύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be established, installed, put into effect</div><b>εγκαθιδρύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to establish, put into effect, set up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of political systems) to install, bring into force</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εγκαθιδρύθηκε</b> δικτατορικό καθεστώς. - A dictatorial regime was installed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantic loan from French ériger from Ancient Greek ἐγκᾰθιδρῡ́ω (“erect, set up in”). Morphologically, from εν- (εγ-) (“in-”) + κατα- (-καθ-) (“fully”) + ιδρύω (“found”).</div><b>εγκαθιστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to install, put in (equipment, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to install (software)</div><b>εγκαινιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to inaugurate, launch, open</div><b>εγκαινιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inauguration</div><b>εγκατάλειψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) abandonment</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>εγκατάλειψή</b> του από τη μητέρα του τον σημάδεψε βαθιά. - His abandonment by his mother scarred him deeply.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek ἐγκατάλειψις (“residual symptom”) from ἐγκᾰτᾰλείπω. Morphologically, (εν-) εγ- (“in”) + κατα- (“totally”) + λειψ- from verb λείπω.</div><b>εγκατάσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγκαταστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. installation, establishment (act of installing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. residence (permission to reside)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. installation (large equipment)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. installation, settlement, establishment (set of buildings)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. installation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Morphologically from εγ- (“within”) + κατα- (“against-”) + στάση (“position”).</div><b>εγκαταλείπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be abandoned</div><b>εγκαταλείπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to abandon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to forsake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγκαταλείπω. Morphologically, (εν-) εγ- (“in”) + κατα- (“totally”) + λείπω (“leave”).</div><b>εγκατεστημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine εγκατεστημένη, neuter εγκατεστημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. residing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είμαι από την Αθήνα, αλλά τα τελευταία χρόνια είμαι <b>εγκατεστημένος</b> στο Λονδίνο. - I come from Athens, but I reside in London for the last past years.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. installed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>εγκατεστημένα</b> πολλά προγράμματα στον υπολογιστή μου. - I have many programms installed at my computer.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of εγκαθίσταμαι, passive voice of εγκαθιστώ (“install, reside”), a calque of French établi or installé.</div><b>εγκλείομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be shut in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be confined</div><b>εγκλείω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to shut in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to confine (in prison, asylum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγκλείω. Morphologically, from εν- (εγ-) (“en-, in”) + κλείω (“close”).</div><b>εγκληματίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εγκληματίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>criminal, delinquent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the plural root εγκληματ- (egklimat-) of έγκλημα + -ίας.</div><b>εγκληματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγκληματική, neuter εγκληματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>criminal</div><b>εγκληματικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rare) criminality (the state of being criminal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crime, criminality (criminal activity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εγκληματικός + -ότητα.</div><b>εγκληματολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγκληματολογική, neuter εγκληματολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>criminological, forensic</div>
  <div class='ex'>εγκληματολογικό εργαστήριο (forensic laboratory)</div><b>εγκληματολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εγκληματολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>criminologist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έγκλημα.</div><b>εγκλιματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (UK, uncountable) acclimatisation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (US, uncountable) acclimation, acclimatization</div><b>εγκλωβίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to trap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εγ- (eg-) + κλωβ(ός)) + -ίζω.</div><b>εγκλωβισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. caging (the act of placing or trapping something in a cage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. entrapment (the act of entrapping or state of being entrapped)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. confinement, containment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εγκλωβισ-) + -μός.</div><b>εγκολεασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intussusception</div><b>εγκολπώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to adopt, espouse (theories, ideals etc)</div><b>εγκράτεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) abstinence, continence</div><b>εγκρέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εγκρέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egret</div><b>εγκρίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to approve, sanction, endorse, adopt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐγκρίνω. By surface analysis, εν- (εγ-) (en- (eg-), “within”) + κρίνω</div><b>εγκρατής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγκρατής, neuter εγκρατές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abstemious</div><b>εγκυκλοπαίδεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγκυκλοπαίδειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>encyclopaedia, encyclopedia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from New Latin encyclopaedia.</div><b>εγκυκλοπαιδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγκυκλοπαιδική, neuter εγκυκλοπαιδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>encyclopedic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French encyclopédique. By surface analysis, εγκυκλοπαίδ(εια)) + -ικός.</div><b>εγκυμονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be pregnant (with), to carry (to have developing offspring within one&#x27;s body)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in the third person) to carry (with it), to pose (:risk, danger)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐγκυμονῶ, from Ancient Greek ἐγκυμονέω, with semantic loan from French être gros de in the figurative sense.</div><b>εγκυμοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pregnancy</div><b>εγκυρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) validity, the state of being valid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> έγκυρος + -ότητα. First attested 1892.</div><b>εγκύκλιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγκύκλιος or εγκύκλια, neuter εγκύκλιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all-round, general</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐγκύκλιος in the context of ἐγκύκλιος παιδεία.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγκύκλιοι or εγκύκλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. circular (official directive or notice intended for circulation)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εγκύκλιος</b> επιστολή - circular letter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. encyclical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned nominalization of the feminine form of the adjective εγκύκλιος, with semantic loan from French circulaire.</div><b>εγκύπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to examine and study carefully, look into</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγκύπτω. Morphologically, from εγ- (εν-) (“into”) + κύπτω (“bend”).</div><b>εγκώμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εγκώμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>encomium, praise</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εγκώμια</b> Επιταφίου - Encomia Epitafiou</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐγκώμιον, substantivized neuter of Ancient Greek ἐγκώμιος.</div><b>εγρήγορση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alertness, vigilance, wakefulness</div>
  <div class='ex'>εν <b>εγρηγόρσει</b> - on the alert</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐγρήγορσις with -ση (-si) ending and semantic loan from French vigilance.</div><b>εγροικώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γροικώ">γροικώ</a></div><b>εγχέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of liquids) to be poured into, infused, injected</div><b>εγχέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to pour liquid into, infuse, inject</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγχέω. Morphologically, from εγ- (εν-) (“in”) + the ancient χέω (“pour, scatter”).</div><b>εγχείρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγχειρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>operation, surgical procedure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐγχείρησις + -ση.</div><b>εγχείριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εγχειρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εγχείρηση">εγχείρηση</a></div><b>εγχειρίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εγχειρίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dagger</div>
  <div class='def'>2. manual, companion, handbook</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐγχειρίδιον; if inherited, the expected form would be *χειρίδι, *χρίδι.</div><b>εγχύνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εγχέομαι">εγχέομαι</a></div><b>εγχύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εγχέω">εγχέω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εγχέω with metaplasm according to the pattern χέω &gt; χύνω.</div><b>εγχώριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εγχώρια, neuter εγχώριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>domestic (internal to a specific country)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: ντόπιος (ntópios), εσωτερικός (esoterikós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐγχώριος.</div><b>εγωτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εγωτισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egotism</div><b>εγώ</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εγώ</b> πάλεψα με αίμα, δάκρυα και ιδρώτα … - I fought with blood, sweat and tears …</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγώ, from Proto-Hellenic *egṓ, from Proto-Indo-European *eǵóH.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) ego</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει ένα μεγάλο <b>εγώ</b>. - He has a big ego.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) selfishness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐγώ, from Proto-Hellenic *egṓ, from Proto-Indo-European *eǵóH.</div><b>εγώ τα λέω, εγώ τα ακούω</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) I might as well be talking to the wall, like talking to a wall, like talking to a brick wall</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του λέω συνεχώς να φτιάξει το αμάξι του, αλλά <b>εγώ τα λέω, εγώ τα ακούω</b>. - I keep telling him to fix his car but it&#x27;s like talking to the wall.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;I say it, I hear it.&quot;</div><b>εδαφικά νερά</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of εδαφικό <a href="bword://νερό">νερό</a></div><b>εδαφικό νερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εδαφικά νερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>groundwater, ground water</div><b>εδαφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εδαφική, neuter εδαφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. territorial, ground (relating to the land possessed)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ground, soil</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εδαφικό</b> νερό - groundwater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐδαφικός. See the ancient ἔδαφος n.</div><b>εδαφόβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εδαφόβια, neuter εδαφόβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terrestrial, ground-dwelling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έδαφος + βίος + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix).</div><b>εδεσματολόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εδεσματολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>menu (list of dishes)</div><b>εδεσματολόγιον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://εδεσματολόγιο">εδεσματολόγιο</a></div><b>εδωδιμοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εδωδιμοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) grocery store</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation from εδώδιμος + -ο- (-o-) + -πωλείο.</div><b>εδώ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>here</div>
  <div class='ex'>αυτό <b>εδώ</b> - this one</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐδῶ, a metathetic variant of Ancient Greek ὅδε or ὧδε.</div><b>εδώ και</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for the past ..., for ... now, (for the duration of the time mentioned, since)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εδώ και</b> πέντε μέρες δεν έχουν μαζέψει τα σκουπίδια και η γειτονιά αρχίζει να βρομάει. - For the last five days they haven&#x27;t collected the rubbish and the neighbourhood is starting to stink.</div><b>εδώ κι εκεί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://από">από</a> <a href="bword://εδώ">εδώ</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://από">από</a> <a href="bword://εκεί">εκεί</a></div><b>εδώ ο κόσμος καίγεται και το μουνί χτενίζεται</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, vulgar) fiddling while Rome burns, rearrange the deck chairs on the Titanic (concerning those who neglect the important issues and focus on trivial things)</div><b>εδώ που τα λέμε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be honest, if I&#x27;m honest, to tell the truth</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εδώ που τα λέμε</b>, όταν τον πάντρεψε αυτόν ήταν το καλύτερο πράγμα που έκανε ποτέ. - To be honest, marrying him was the best thing she ever did.</div><b>εδώδιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εδώδιμη, neuter εδώδιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>edible (that can be eaten without harm; suitable for consumption)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐδώδιμος.</div><b>εθελοντής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εθελοντές, feminine εθελόντρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>volunteer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐθελοντής, later form of ἐθελοντήρ, from ἐθέλω.</div><b>εθελοντικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εθελοντικός">εθελοντικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voluntarily (in a voluntary manner)</div><b>εθελοντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εθελοντική, neuter εθελοντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voluntary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εθελοντ(ής)) + -ικός.</div><b>εθελοντικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) synonym of <a href="bword://εθελοντικά">εθελοντικά</a> (in a voluntary manner)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εθελοντικ(ός)) + -ώς.</div><b>εθελόντρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εθελόντριες, masculine εθελοντής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>volunteer</div><b>εθιμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εθιμική, neuter εθιμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>customary (in accordance with, or established by, custom or common usage)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εθιμικό</b> δίκαιο - customary law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from έθιμο + -ικός.</div><b>εθισμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εθισμένη, neuter εθισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>addicted (to something)</div><b>εθισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εθισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>addiction</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εθισμός</b> στη νικοτίνη - addiction to nicotine</div><b>εθνάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εθνάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leader of nation or race</div>
  <div class='def'>2. epithet for political leader generally acknowledged as the person who realized the goals of a nation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. title of archbishops of Cyprus, who frequently were also political leaders</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> έθνος + -άρχης from Hellenistic Koine Ancient Greek ἐθνάρχης.</div><b>εθνεγερσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>national revolution for independence</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εθνεγερσία</b> του 1821 - National War of Independence of 1821</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έθνος + έγερση.</div><b>εθνική γιορτή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εθνικές γιορτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>national day</div><b>εθνικίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εθνικίστριες, masculine εθνικιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nationalist (female advocate of nationalism)</div><b>εθνικισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εθνικισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nationalism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εθνικός + -ισμός.</div><b>εθνικιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εθνικιστές, feminine εθνικίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nationalist (advocate of nationalism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> έθνος + -ιστής, calque of English nationalist. First attested 1840.</div><b>εθνικιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εθνικιστική, neuter εθνικιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nationalist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chauvinist</div><b>εθνικοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ετηνικοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nationalisation, taken into public ownership</div><b>εθνικοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to nationalise</div><b>εθνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εθνική, neuter εθνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>national</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εθνικό</b> Θέατρο - National Theatre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐθνικός. By surface analysis, έθνος + -ικός.</div><b>εθνικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εθνικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nationality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εθνικός + -ότητα, calque of French nationalité. First attested 1833.</div><b>εθνικόφρων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εθνικόφρων, neuter εθνικόφρoν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nationalist</div><b>εθνογλωσσολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ethnolinguistics</div><b>εθνογλωσσολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εθνογλωσσολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ethnolinguist</div><b>εθνογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εθνογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ethnographer</div><b>εθνογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εθνογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ethnography</div><b>εθνοκεντρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ethnocentrism</div><b>εθνολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ethnology</div><b>εθνολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εθνολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ethnologist</div><b>εθνόσημο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εθνόσημα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coat of arms</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐθνόσημον. By surface analysis, έθνος + -σημο (-simo, “adhesive stamp”).</div><b>εθολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εθολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ethology</div><b>ειδεχθής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ειδεχθής, neuter ειδεχθές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hateful, heinous</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ειδεχθές</b> έγκλημα - heinous crime</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰδεχθής.</div><b>ειδικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>especially, particularly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ειδικός + -α (-a, “adverbial suffix”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ειδικός">ειδικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ειδικός + -α (-a, “adverbial suffix”).</div><b>ειδικεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to specialize (to focus one&#x27;s study upon a particular skill, field, topic, or genre)</div><b>ειδικεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to specialize (to train (someone) in a speciality)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to specialize (to focus one&#x27;s study upon a particular skill, field, topic, or genre)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ειδικ(ός)) + -εύω, a calque of French spécialiser.</div><b>ειδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ειδική, neuter ειδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. special, particular, specific (thing, person, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. expert, specialist (knowledge, experience, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰδικός, from εἶδος + -ικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ειδικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>specialist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰδικός, from εἶδος + -ικός.</div><b>ειδικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ειδικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>speciality (UK), specialty (US), specialism (that in which one specializes; a chosen expertise or talent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ειδικ(ός)) + -ότητα, a calque of French specialité.</div><b>ειδοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ειδοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. notification, notice, reminder</div>
  <div class='def'>2. warning, alert</div><b>ειδοποιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ειδοποιός, neuter ειδοποιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. specific</div>
  <div class='def'>2. distinguishing (separates one species from another)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ειδοποιός</b> διαφορά - specific difference</div><b>ειδοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to notify, inform</div><b>ειδυλλιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ειδυλλιακή, neuter ειδυλλιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. idyllic (of or pertaining to idylls)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. idyllic (extremely happy, peaceful, or picturesque)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ειδύλλι(ο)) + -ακός.</div><b>ειδωλολάτρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ειδωλολάτρες, feminine ειδωλολάτρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pagan, idolater, heathen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek εἰδωλολάτρης.</div><b>ειδωλολάτρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ειδωλολάτρισσες, masculine ειδωλολάτρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pagan, idolatress, heathen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ειδωλολάτρ + -ισσα (-issa, “feminising”)</div><b>ειδωλολατρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ειδωλολατρική, neuter ειδωλολατρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. idolatrous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pagan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ειδωλολάτρ(ης)) + -ικός.</div><b>ειδύλλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ειδύλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in ancient Greek and Roman literature, a short bucolic poem, usually with romantic theme</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in new times) in ancient Greek and Roman literature, a short bucolic poem, usually with romantic theme; any poem or other literary form of similar character</div>
  <div class='def'>3. romance, love affair</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (ironic) romance, love affair; brief or unexpected friendship or collaboration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The genre from Hellenistic Ancient Greek εἰδύλλιον.
Sense romance, semantic loan from French idylle.</div><b>ειδώλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ειδώλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statuette, figurine</div><b>εικάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to suppose, to guess, to conclude</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰκάζω.</div><b>εικασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εικασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. guess, conjecture, guesswork</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάνω εικασία (make a guess)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conjecture</div>
  <div class='ex'>εικασία του Φερμά - Fermat&#x27;s conjecture</div><b>εικαστικές τέχνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>visual arts</div><b>εικαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εικαστική, neuter εικαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>visual, representative, plastic</div><b>εικονίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εικονίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>icon</div><b>εικονικές πραγματικότητες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of εικονική <a href="bword://πραγματικότητα">πραγματικότητα</a></div><b>εικονική πραγματικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εικονικές πραγματικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>virtual reality</div><b>εικονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εικονική, neuter εικονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. virtual</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bogus, pretend, mock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰκονικός.</div><b>εικονογράφηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εικονογραφήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. illustration (the act of illustrating)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. illustration (a picture designed to decorate a publication, or elucidate a literary work)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. decoration with icons</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εικονογραφη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>εικονογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εικονογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. iconographer, icon painter</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: αγιογράφος m (agiográfos)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. illustrator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek εικονογράφος, from Ancient Greek εἰκονογράφος. By surface analysis, εικόν(α)) + -ο- (-o-) + -γράφος.</div><b>εικονογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εικονογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>iconography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek εικονογραφία, from Ancient Greek εἰκονογραφία. By surface analysis, εικονο- (eikono-) + -γραφία or εικονογράφ(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>εικονογραφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εικονογραφική, neuter εικονογραφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. iconographic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pictographic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εικονογραφ(ία), “iconography”) + -ικός with semantic loan from French pictographique.</div><b>εικονολήπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εικονολήπτες, feminine εικονολήπτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cameraman, camera operator</div><b>εικονολήπτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εικονολήπτριες, masculine εικονολήπτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>camerawoman, camera operator</div><b>εικονολατρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) iconolatry (the use of images as symbols that provide an inspiration and aid to worship, especially in the context of the 8th-9th century Byzantine Iconoclasm)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French iconolâtrie, from Byzantine Greek εικονολάτρης + -ία.</div><b>εικονομαχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>iconomachy, iconoclasm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek εικονομαχία, from εικονομάχ(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>εικονοστάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εικονοστάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>iconostasis</div><b>εικονοστοιχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εικονοστοιχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pixel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εικόνα + στοιχείο</div><b>εικοσάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εικοσάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>group of twenty, a score of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek εικοσάδα, from Koine Greek εἰκοσάς, from Ancient Greek εἴκοσι.</div><b>εικοσάρικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εικοσάρικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, obsolete) twenty-drachma coin / note</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) twenty-euro note</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δυστυχώς, έπλυνα το <b>εικοσάρικο</b> μου μαζί με το παντελόνι. - Sadly, I washed a twenty along with my trousers.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> είκοσι + -άρικο</div><b>εικοσαδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εικοσαδική, neuter εικοσαδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vicesimal, vigesimal</div><b>εικοσιτετράωρο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εικοσιτετράωρος">εικοσιτετράωρος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εικοσιτετράωρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>24 hours, day</div><b>εικοσιτετράωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εικοσιτετράωρη, neuter εικοσιτετράωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twenty-four-hour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εικοσι- (eikosi-) + τετρα- (tetra-) + ώρ(α)) + -ος (-os), a calque of French vingt-quatre heures.</div><b>εικοστός</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine (feminine εικοστή, neuter εικοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twentieth (ordinal numeral)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>εικοστή</b> Μαρτίου του 1872 - the twentieth of March, 1872</div><b>εικοστός πρώτος</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twenty-first (ordinal numeral)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>εικοστό πρώτο</b> αιώνα - the twenty-first century</div><b>εικόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εικόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. picture, image, painting, illustration, portrait</div>
  <div class='def'>2. icon, religious painting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰκών.</div><b>εικόνισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εικονίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>icon, religious painting</div><b>ειλεός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ειλεοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ileum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰλεός.</div><b>ειλημμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ειλημμένη, neuter ειλημμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) taken (emphatically e.g. of decisions, commitments, obligations), undertaken</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην ελπίζεις σε αλλαγές. Οι αποφάσεις της Επιτροπής είναι <b>ειλημμένες</b>. - Do not hope for changes. The Committee&#x27;s decisions are taken [and final].</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formal perfect participle of λαμβάνομαι, passive voice of λαμβάνω. In the fashion of the Hellenistic Koine Greek εἰλημμένος, participle of the ancient λαμβάνω. The spelling with ει of its passive perfect tense εἴλημμαι comes from *σέ‑σληβ‑μαι. Τhe double μμ, from ληβ- + -μ- &gt; λημμ-. Also, semantic loan from French pris.</div><b>ειλικρίνεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) sincerity, frankness</div><b>ειλικρινά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frankly, honestly, sincerely</div><b>ειλικρινής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ειλικρινής, neuter ειλικρινές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sincere, frank</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek εἰλικρινής.</div><b>ειρήνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) peace, tranquility, harmony</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) peaceful nonviolent lifestyle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εἰρήνη.</div><b>ειρηνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ειρηνική, neuter ειρηνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. peaceful (not at war; not disturbed by strife or turmoil; characterized by peace)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. peaceful, pacific, peaceable (inclined to peace; preferring peace by nature; avoiding violence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: ειρηνόφιλος (eirinófilos), φιλειρηνικός (fileirinikós)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. peaceful, pacific (calm, tranquil)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰρηνικός. By surface analysis, ειρήν(η)) + -ικός.</div><b>ειρωνεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ειρωνείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irony</div><b>ειρωνεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ironize (at someone&#x27;s expense), to make fun of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰρωνεύομαι.</div><b>ειρωνικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ειρωνικός">ειρωνικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ironically</div><b>ειρωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ειρωνική, neuter ειρωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ironic, derisive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰρωνικός.</div><b>ειρωνικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ειρωνικά">ειρωνικά</a></div><b>εις</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰς (eis). Doublet of σε (se).</div><b>εις μνήμην</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) in memory of, in memoriam</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned stereotype expression from Katharevousa.
Preposition εἰς &amp; accusative singular of feminine noun Ancient Greek μνήμη.</div><b>εις το επανιδείν</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>until we meet again</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰς (eis, “to”) + ἐπανα- (epana-, “again”) + ἰδεῖν.</div><b>εισάγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to import (goods from abroad)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to introduce (an educational subject)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to introduce (a new product to the market)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to admit (patient to hospital)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to begin, introduce (grammar: a sentence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰσάγω. By surface analysis, εισ- (eis-) + άγω.</div><b>εισέρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to enter, to make one&#x27;s entrance</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εισέρχεστε</b> με δική σας ευθύνη. - You enter at your own risk.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek εἰσέρχομαι. By surface analysis, εισ- (“in”) + έρχομαι (“I come”).</div><b>εισέτι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yet; still</div><b>εισήγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εισηγήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suggestion, recommendation, proposal</div><b>εισαγγελέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εισαγγελείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>district attorney, prosecutor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Modern adaptation (-έας) of Ancient Greek εἰσαγγελεύς, original sense &#x27;bailiff&#x27; / &#x27;court clerk&#x27;, from εἰς (eis, “in, into, to”) + ἀγγέλλω + -εύς. Modern sense acquired in Koine period.</div><b>εισαγγελεύς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://εισαγγελέας">εισαγγελέας</a></div><b>εισαγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εισαγωγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. import (something brought in from an exterior source, especially for sale or trade)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. import, importation (the practice of importing)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. admission (permission to enter, or the entrance itself)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. introduction (explanation of what constitutes the basis of an issue)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. introduction (an initial section of a book or article, which introduces the subject material)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. introduction (the act or process of introducing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰσᾰγωγή with semantic loan from French introduction.</div><b>εισαγωγικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. guillemets, quotation marks, chevrons, duckfoot quotes, the Unicode double angle quotation mark.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. guillemets, quotation marks, chevrons, duckfoot quotes, the Unicode double angle quotation mark.
 (Most commonly in Greek: « or », and in English “ or ”.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter plural form of εισαγωγικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>introductorily</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter plural form of εισαγωγικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εισαγωγικός">εισαγωγικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter plural form of εισαγωγικός.</div><b>εισαγωγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εισαγωγική, neuter εισαγωγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. import (attributive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. admission, entrance (attributive)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εισαγωγικές</b> εξετάσεις - entrance examination</div>
  <div class='def'>3. introductory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εἰσαγωγικός. By surface analysis, εισαγωγή + -ικός.</div><b>εισακούομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to hearkened</div><b>εισακούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to hearken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰσᾰκούω. By surface analysis, εισ- (“in”) + ακούω (“listen”).</div><b>εισβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to invade (to enter by force, usually in order to conquer)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to barge in, to break in, to force one&#x27;s way in, to irrupt, to storm in (to enter forcibly)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰσβάλλω. By surface analysis, εισ- (eis-) + βάλλω.</div><b>εισβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εισβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>invasion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰσβολή.</div><b>εισιτήρια άνευ επιστροφής</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εισιτήριο">εισιτήριο</a> <a href="bword://άνευ">άνευ</a> επιστροφής</div><b>εισιτήρια διάρκειας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εισιτήριο">εισιτήριο</a> διάρκειας</div><b>εισιτήρια διαρκείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εισιτήριο">εισιτήριο</a> διαρκείας</div><b>εισιτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εισιτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ticket for bus, train, etc, or theatre, etc</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek εἰσιτήριον, nominalised neuter of adjective εἰσιτήριος. From εἰσ- (eis-, “in”) + ἰ- (i-) with iota &lt; ι &gt; from &lt; ει &gt; of the verb εἶμι) + -τήριον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of εισιτήριος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of εισιτήριος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form. Also see εἰσιτήριος.</div><b>εισιτήριο άνευ επιστροφής</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εισιτήρια άνευ επιστροφής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>single ticket, one-way ticket</div><b>εισιτήριο διάρκειας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εισιτήρια διάρκειας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εισιτήριο">εισιτήριο</a> διαρκείας</div><b>εισιτήριο διαρκείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εισιτήρια διαρκείας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>season ticket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;duration ticket&quot;.</div><b>εισιτήριο μετ&#x27; επιστροφής</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>return ticket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Contraction of εισιτήριο μετά επιστροφής</div><b>εισπλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to enter port or bay (of ships)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰσπλέω. Morphologically, from εισ- (“to, towards”) + πλέω (“sail”).</div><b>εισπνέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be inhaled</div><b>εισπνέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to breathe in, inhale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰσπνέω.</div><b>εισπράκτορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εισπράκτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ticket collector (UK), guard (UK)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ticket inspector (US), conductor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εἰσπράκτωρ with the accusative case ending -ορα (-ora), from εἰσ- (eis-) + πράκτωρ, with semantic loan from French percepteur.</div><b>εισπράττω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to collect (:payment due, tax, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to receive (:consequences, reaction)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εἰσπράττω, Attic Greek form of εἰσπράσσω.</div><b>εισρέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to flow in, pour into</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰσρέω. Morphologically, from εισ- (“in”) + ρέω (“flow”).</div><b>εισροή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>influx, inflow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek εἰσροή. Morphologically, from εισ- (“in”) + ροή (“flowing”).</div><b>εισφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εισφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contribution</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰσφορά.</div><b>εισόδημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εισοδήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. income, revenue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crop</div><b>εκ</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suggesting source or origin, of, by</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εκ</b> Παρισίων - from Paris</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of of <a href="bword://εκ">εκ</a>. (ek.): million</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Abbreviation of εκατομμύριο.</div><b>εκ μέρους</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. on the part of</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πολύ ευγενικό <b>εκ μέρους</b> σου. - Very kind of you.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. on behalf of</div><b>εκ πρώτης όψεως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. at first glance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. at first sight</div>
  <div class='def'>3. at first blush (rare)</div><b>εκ του προχείρου</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>offhand, off the cuff, off the top of one&#x27;s head</div><b>εκ του σύνεγγυς</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>closely, at close quarters, up close</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύνεγγυς, with semantic loan from French de près.</div><b>εκ των προτέρων</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beforehand, in advance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ex ante, a priori</div><b>εκ των υστέρων</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a posteriori, inductive</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εκ των υστέρων</b>, μετανιώνω - after all [that happened], I regret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of Latin a posteriori. Formal syntax as in Katharevousa, from εκ (ek) / ἐκ (ek, “from”) + genitive plural των (ton) υστέρων: ancient τῶν ὑστέρων. See ύστερος.</div><b>εκ.</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://εκατομμύριο">εκατομμύριο</a>: one million</div><b>εκατομμυριούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εκατομμυριούχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>millionaire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εκατομμύριο + -ούχος</div><b>εκατομμύριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκατομμύρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>million</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A compound of εκατό + μύριοι — literally “hundred myriad”.</div><b>εκατοντάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκατοντάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>group of 100</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εκατό + -άδα</div><b>εκατοντάκις</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a hundred times</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εκατό + -άκις</div><b>εκατονταετία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκατονταετίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>century, 100 years</div><b>εκατονταρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκατονταρχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>century (unit in the Ottoman and Roman armies)</div><b>εκατοστάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκατοστάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κατοστάρι">κατοστάρι</a></div><b>εκατοστάρικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκατοστάρικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κατοστάρικο">κατοστάρικο</a></div><b>εκατοστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκατοστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hundredth (1/100, one of a hundred parts of a whole)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) centimetre (in daily speech)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://εκατοστός">εκατοστός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εκατοστός">εκατοστός</a></div><b>εκατοστόγραμμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκατοστόγραμμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centigram, 1/100 gram</div><b>εκατοστόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκατοστόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centimetre</div><b>εκατοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκατοστή, neuter εκατοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hundredth (the ordinal form of the number one hundred)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑκατοστός.</div><b>εκατό</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hundred, 100</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (excessive, figuratively) a hundred, a thousand (expressing exaggeration)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σου το είπα <b>εκατό</b> φορές, αλλά δεν με ακούς. - I have told you a hundred times, but you won&#x27;t listen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἑκατό, from Ancient Greek ἑκατόν. Compare Mariupol Greek като́.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) the telephone number (i.e. 100) used to call the police, (by extension) the police</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>εκατό</b> ήρθε αμέσως και τον έδιωξε. - The police came immediately and drove them away.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) hundred</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἑκατό, from Ancient Greek ἑκατόν. Compare Mariupol Greek като́.</div><b>εκατόλιτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκατόλιτροα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hectolitre (UK), hectoliter (US) (100 litres)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> French hectolitre, as if εκατό- + λίτρο</div><b>εκατόν</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εκατό">εκατό</a> used to form numbers from 101–199.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εκατόν</b> δύο - one hundred and two</div><b>εκατόνταρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκατόνταρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centurion (commander of unit in the Ottoman and Roman armies)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ἑκατόνταρχος</div><b>εκατόχρονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκατόχρονη, neuter εκατόχρονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pertaining to a century or 100 years</div><b>εκβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to eject (to compel (a person or persons) to leave)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of a river) to empty, to flow, to issue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκβάλλω. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + βάλλω.</div><b>εκβιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκβιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blackmail</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἐκβιασμός.</div><b>εκβιαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine εκβιάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blackmailer</div><b>εκβιομηχάνιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) industrialisation (UK), industrialization (US)</div><b>εκβιομηχανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to industrialise (UK), industrialize (US)</div><b>εκγύμναση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) exercise (physical activity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) training (for athletes, animals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκ + γύμναση, from γυμνάζω.</div><b>εκδέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to flay, skin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to abrade, graze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκδέρω. Doublet of γδέρνω</div><b>εκδήλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκδηλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. manifestation (the act or process of becoming manifest)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. event (a prearranged social activity, function, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εκδηλώνω + -ση (-si) with semantic loan from French manifestation in the sense &#x27;event&#x27;.</div><b>εκδίδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to publish, issue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to extradite (to a country)</div><b>εκδίκηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκδικήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>revenge, vengeance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκδίκησι.</div><b>εκδηλωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκδηλωτική, neuter εκδηλωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (demonstrative) demonstrative</div>
  <div class='def'>2. exuberant</div><b>εκδηλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to manifest, to show, to express (:emotion, intention, interest, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to manifest, to manifest itself, to show itself</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκδηλῶ with -ώνω ending and semantic loan from French (se) manifester. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + δηλώνω.</div><b>εκδικητική πορνογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) revenge porn</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα περιστατικά <b>εκδικητικής πορνογραφίας</b> στην Ελλάδα έχουν αυξηθεί τα τελευταία χρόνια σε ανησυχητικό βαθμό. - The incidents of revenge porn in Greece have increased in recent years to an alarming extent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English revenge porn.</div><b>εκδικούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to avenge oneself, to revenge oneself, to take revenge (on) (take retaliatory action against)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to avenge, to revenge (take vengeance for (a particular harmful action) or on behalf of (its victim))</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκδικοῦμαι, from Ancient Greek ἐκδικέω.</div><b>εκδοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκδοτική, neuter εκδοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>publishing, i.e. in having a purpose or rights to publish work</div><b>εκδοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκδοχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. variant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. version</div><b>εκδρομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκδρομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. excursion (a brief recreational trip)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. road trip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκδρομή from εκ (ek, “from”) + δρόμος. Semantic loan from English excursion.</div><b>εκδυσόζωα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Ecdysozoa.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Ecdysozoa.</div><b>εκδότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκδότες, feminine εκδότρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>publisher</div><b>εκδότρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκδότριες, masculine εκδότης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>publisher</div><b>εκεί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>there</div>
  <div class='ex'>αυτό <b>εκεί</b> - that one</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκεῖ.</div><b>εκείθε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εκείθεν">εκείθεν</a></div><b>εκείθεν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thence, from there</div><b>εκείνος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. that</div>
  <div class='ex'>—Δώσε μου <b>εκείνη</b> την εφημερίδα. —Ποια; —<b>Εκείνη</b> που είναι πάνω στο τραπέζι. Γιατί ετούτη που διαβάζω δε γράφει τίποτα ενδιαφέρον. - —Give me that newspaper. —Which one? —The one (That one) which is on the table. Because the one (this one) which I am reading has (writes) nothing interesting.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) those</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω <b>εκείνα</b> τα βιβλία που είναι στο τελευταίο ράφι. - I want those books which are at the last shelf.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐκεῖνος, from Proto-Hellenic *ékeyenos, from ἐ- (e-, “augment”) + *ḱe (“deictic particle”) + Proto-Indo-European *h₁enos (“that”).</div><b>εκεχειρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκεχειρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cease-fire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκεχειρία.</div><b>εκθέτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to exhibit, display</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε ποια γκαλερί <b>εκθέτει</b> τα έργα του ο νεαρός ζωγράφος; - At which art gallery does this young painter exhibit? (his works)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to expound, report, write up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του <b>εξέθεσα</b> το πρόβλημα με κάθε λεπτομέρεια. - I have described the problem to him in every detail.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to compromise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐκτίθημι.</div><b>εκθειάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to praise, exalt</div><b>εκθετήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκθετήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exhibition space, showroom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εκθέ(τω)) + -τήριο.</div><b>εκκένωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκκενώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evacuation, clearing, emptying</div><b>εκκεντρικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκκεντρικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eccentricity (eccentric behaviour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εκκεντρικός + -ότητα. First attested 1891.</div><b>εκκενώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be evacuated</div><b>εκκενώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to empty</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εκκένωσε</b> το ποτήρι του. - He emptied his glass.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to evacuate, vacate (people from a place)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εκκένωσαν</b> την αίθουσα. - They evacuated the auditorium.</div><b>εκκλησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκκλησίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. church (religious organisation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ορθόδοξη <b>Εκκλησία</b> - the Orthodox Church</div>
  <div class='def'>2. church (everyone, living and dead, in a Christian denomination)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. church, ecclesia (the hierarchy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκκλησία (“assembly”).</div><b>εκκλησίασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) congregation, a gathering of the faithful</div><b>εκκλησιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be a churchgoer (in general), attend mass (one instance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι άνθρωποι δεν <b>εκκλησιάζονται</b> πια αυτές τις μέρες. - People don&#x27;t attend church anymore these days.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκκλησιάζω, mediopassive ἐκκλησιάζομαι.</div><b>εκκλησιαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκκλησιαστική, neuter εκκλησιαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prochurch, ecclesiastical</div><b>εκκολάπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be pecking out of the egg</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to be formed into</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι μεγάλοι επιστήμονες του μέλλοντος <b>εκκολάπτονται</b> σ’ αυτό το πανεπιστήμιο. - The future great scientists are formed in this university.</div><b>εκκολάπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hatch</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to contribute to the conception , formation and development of an idea or a thing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκκολάπτω, from ἐκ- (ek-, “from”) + κολάπτω.</div><b>εκκρεμές</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκκρεμή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pendulum</div><b>εκκρούομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to knock out</div><b>εκκρούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to knock out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to delete one of adjacent vowels of different words</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκκρούω. For sense in linguistics, a semantic loan from German ausstoßen. Morphologically, from εκ- (“counter-”) + κρούω (“strike”).</div><b>εκκωφαντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκκωφαντική, neuter εκκωφαντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deafening, ear-shattering, earsplitting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from unattested stem *εκκωφαν- + -τικός, a calque of French assourdissant. The stem is formed from Ancient Greek εκ- (ek-) + κωφαίνω as a calque of Modern Greek ξεκουφαίνω.</div><b>εκλάπην</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of κλέπτομαι, the passive of κλέπτω, ancient forms of <a href="bword://κλέβομαι">κλέβομαι</a> of <a href="bword://κλέβω">κλέβω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ἐκλάπην</div><b>εκλέγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to elect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκλέγω with sense: &quot;I choose&quot;, extended to &quot;elect&quot;. Also see verbs: επιλέγω, διαλέγω, all preserving the ancient sense from λέγω.</div><b>εκλέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) éclair (small chocolate covered creme-filled pastry puff in a general oblong shape)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French éclair.</div><b>εκλαίρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εκλέρ">εκλέρ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French éclair.</div><b>εκλαμψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eclampsia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French éclampsie.</div><b>εκλείπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκλείπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(astronomy, uncommon) </div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>εκλείπτης</b> φέρει έναν δίσκο απόκρυψης 1,4 μέτρων, σχεδιασμένο να μπλοκάρει το φωτοσφαιρικό ηλιακό φως, δημιουργώντας μια σκιά πλάτους περίπου οκτώ εκατοστών σε απόσταση 150 μέτρων.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκλείπω + -της (-tis).</div><b>εκλείπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to vanish, become extinct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκλείπω.</div><b>εκλεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκλεκτική, neuter εκλεκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>selective, discriminating, choosy</div><b>εκλεκτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκλεκτή, neuter εκλεκτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of people) chosen, elect, select</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of people) chosen, elect, select; chosen one, elect</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of things) choice, fine, select (especially good or preferred)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκλεκτός.</div><b>εκλεπτυσμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine εκλεπτυσμένη, neuter εκλεπτυσμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refined, cultured, subtle, sophisticated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation from the perfect participle of εκλεπτύνω, formed as a calque of French raffiné and calque of English refined.</div><b>εκλεπτύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to make thinner</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to refine, make more sophisticated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκλεπτύνω.</div><b>εκλιπαρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to beg, implore, beg for</div><b>εκλογή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκλογές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the action or result of an election (process of choosing a new leader)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θεωρούσε βέβαιη την <b>εκλογή</b> της στην πρωθυπουργία. - She considered her election as the prime minister a certainty.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in the plural εκλογές: the process of election</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος κέρδισε τις <b>εκλογές</b>; - Who won the election?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκλογή.</div><b>εκλογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκλογική, neuter εκλογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>electoral (of or relating to elections)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εκλογικό</b> σύστημα - electoral system</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εκλογ(ή)) + -ικός, a calque of French électoral.</div><b>εκλογιμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) eligibility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εκλόγιμος + -ότητα. First attested 1840.</div><b>εκλύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to release, to discharge (:energy or matter)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκλύω. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + λύω.</div><b>εκμάθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκμαθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>learning, study</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκμᾰ́θησῐς.</div><b>εκμίσθωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκμισθώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rental, lease</div><b>εκμετάλλευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκμεταλλεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exploitation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκμεταλλεύ(ομαι)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>εκμεταλλεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to exploit, take advantage of (wealth, resources, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκμεταλλεύω of ore”), made deponent by analogy with εργάζομαι, and with semantic loan from French exploiter (une mine).</div><b>εκμεταλλεύσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκμεταλλεύσιμη, neuter εκμεταλλεύσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exploitable (able to be exploited, especially commercially)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εκμεταλλεύ(ομαι)) + -σιμος (-simos).</div><b>εκμισθωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκμισθωτές, feminine εκμισθώτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lessor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Verb εκμισθω-(νω) (“I lease”) + -τής (“suffix to form nouns”)</div><b>εκμισθώτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκμισθώτριες, masculine εκμισθωτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lessor</div><b>εκνευρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to annoy, to irritate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκνευρίζω with semantic loan from French (s&#x27;)énerver. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + νεύρ(ο)) + -ίζω.</div><b>εκνευρισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine εκνευρισμένη, neuter εκνευρισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irritated, nervous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of εκνευρίζομαι, passive voice of εκνευρίζω.</div><b>εκνευρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irritation (state of being irritated)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ενόχληση f (enóchlisi, “annoyance”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek εκνευρισμός with adaptation to modern meaning of εκνευρίζω. By surface analysis, εκνευρίζω + -μός.</div><b>εκνευριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκνευριστική, neuter εκνευριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irritating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the εκνευρισ- stem of εκνευρίζω + -τικός.</div><b>εκοιμήθη</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (euphemistic, formal) (of saints, holy men, nuns, and church officials) died, passed away</div>
  <div class='def'>2. corresponding to Modern third-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://κοιμάμαι">κοιμάμαι</a>: κοιμήθηκε.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from the ancient 3rd person singular ἐκοιμήθη, of passive aorist ἐκοιμήθην of the verb κοιμάω.</div><b>εκούσια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://εκούσιος">εκούσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εκούσιος">εκούσιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voluntarily</div><b>εκούσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκούσια, neuter εκούσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voluntary (done, given, or acting of one&#x27;s own free will)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑκούσιος.</div><b>εκπέμπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be transmitted, broadcasted, emitted</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ἐκπέμπομαι</div><b>εκπέμπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to transmit, broadcast</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εκπέμπω</b> σήμα ΣΟΣ - I send an SOS singal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to radiate, emit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκπέμπω. Morphologically, from εκ- (“from”) + πέμπω (“send”).</div><b>εκπίπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal, intransitive) to extracted discount amount (of money, taxes)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal, intransitive) to lose (rank, position)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (formal, intransitive) to fall into disrepute, fall from grace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐκπίπτω. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + πίπτω</div><b>εκπαίδευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκπαιδεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. training</div>
  <div class='def'>2. drill</div>
  <div class='def'>3. education (compare with <a href="bword://παιδεία">παιδεία</a> the teaching and training of the child)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. instruction</div>
  <div class='def'>5. schooling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εκπαιδεύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>εκπαιδευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκπαιδευτές, feminine εκπαιδεύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trainer, coach, instructor</div><b>εκπαιδευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκπαιδευτική, neuter εκπαιδευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>educational, of education</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εκπαιδευτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teacher</div><b>εκπαιδεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be trained</div><b>εκπαιδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to educate, train</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκπαιδεύω. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + παιδεύω.</div><b>εκπαραθύρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκπαραθυρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. defenestration (act of throwing a person out of a window)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>εκπαραθύρωση</b> της Πράγας - The defenestration of Prague</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) expulsion (possibly with violence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>εκπαραθύρωση</b> του υπουργό αφήνει κενή την έδρα του. - The expulsion of the minister leaves his seat empty.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκ (ek, “out of, de-”) + παράθυρο + -ωση (-osi, “-ation”). Calque of French défenestration.</div><b>εκπατρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκπατρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expatriation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εκπατρίζομαι + -μός.</div><b>εκπλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to set sail, depart</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκπλέω. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-, “from”) + πλέω.</div><b>εκπλήρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fulfilment (the act of fulfilling; the act of consummating a desire or promise)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. discharge, performance (the act of accomplishing an obligation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκπλήρωσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>εκπλήσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to surprise, amaze, leave aghast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκπλήσσω.</div><b>εκπλήττομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of εκπλήσσομαι</div><b>εκπλήττω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εκπλήσσω">εκπλήσσω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκπλήττω.</div><b>εκπληκτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εκπληκτικός">εκπληκτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εκπληκτικός">εκπληκτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εκπληκτικός">εκπληκτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fantastically, wonderfully, greatly, superbly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περάσαμε <b>εκπληκτικά</b> σ’ αυτές τις διακοπές. - We had a fantastic time on this holiday.</div><b>εκπληκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκπληκτική, neuter εκπληκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>that which is astonishing, excellent, wonderful, stunning</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έμεινε έκπληκτη καθώς έριξε μια ματιά στα <b>εκπληκτικά</b> αξιοθέατα. - She was stunned when she gazed at the astonishing sights.</div><b>εκπληρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fulfill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐκπληρ(ῶ)) + modern suffix -ώνω, uncontracted ancient form: ἐκπληρόω. Morphologically, εκ (ek) + πληρώνω</div><b>εκπνέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to exhale, breathe out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to expire, die</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκπνέω.</div><b>εκποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sell off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκποιῶ).</div><b>εκπολιτιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκπολιτιστική, neuter εκπολιτιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cultural</div><b>εκπομπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκπομπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. programme (UK), program (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>τηλεοπτική εκπομπή (television programme)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. broadcast</div>
  <div class='ex'>ζωντανή εκπομπή (live broadcast)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. emission</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐκπομπή.</div><b>εκπροσωπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to represent (to stand or act in the place of; to perform the duties, exercise the rights, or otherwise act on behalf of)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to represent (to act as a representative of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek εκπροσωπώ, with semantic loan from French représenter. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + πρόσωπο + -ώ.</div><b>εκπροσώπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκπροσωπήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>representation (the act of representing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εκπροσωπώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>εκπρόσωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εκπρόσωποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. representative (one who speaks for or acts on behalf of another in a particular (especially official) capacity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: αντιπρόσωπος (antiprósopos), πληρεξούσιος (plirexoúsios)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. representative (one who is taken as typical of a class)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned back-formation from εκπροσωπώ with -ος (-os) ending.</div><b>εκπυρσοκρότηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκπυρσοκροτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. firing, detonation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. discharge, report</div><b>εκρέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of liquids) to flow out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of rivers) to flow down to, run into</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκρέω (“flow out; disappear”). Morphologically, from εκ- (“from”) + ρέω (“flow”).</div><b>εκρήγνυμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) to explode, to blow up, to go off (to fly apart with sudden violent force; to detonate) - literally: &quot;I explode&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η βόμβα είναι έτοιμη να <b>εκραγεί</b>. - The bomb is ready to explode.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, literary) to break out, to erupt (to begin suddenly)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, literary) to explode, to erupt (to make a violent or emotional outburst)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκρήγνυμαι, passive of ἐκρήγνυμι.</div><b>εκρηκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκρηκτική, neuter εκρηκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>explosive</div><b>εκσκαφέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκσκαφείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>digger, excavator (especially mechanical)</div><b>εκσκαφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκσκαφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>excavation (operation, especially mechanical)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek (Hellenistic) ἐκσκαφή.</div><b>εκσκαφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκσκαφική, neuter εκσκαφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>excavation, excavating</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εκσκαφικές</b> μηχάνημα - excavating machine</div><b>εκσπερμάτιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ejaculation (of semen)</div><b>εκσπερμάτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ejaculation (of semen)</div><b>εκσπερματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ejaculate</div><b>εκσπερματώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ejaculate</div><b>εκστρατεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκστρατειές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a military campaign, an expedition</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>εκστρατεία</b> της Χίου για την απελευθέρωση του νησιού άρχισε με σκοπό να φροντίσει να συμπεριληφθεί η Χίος στο νεοσύστατο Ελληνικό κράτος, ύστερα από την σφαγή δεκάδων χιλιάδων Ελλήνων της Χίου από τον Οθωμανικό στρατό. - The campaign of Chios for the liberation of the island commenced with the aim of ensuring that Chios was included in the newly formed Greek state, in the wake of the massacre of tens of thousands of Greeks in Chios by the Ottoman army.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a campaign, crusade; a coordinated team effort to achieve a certain political or social goal</div>
  <div class='ex'>Aντικαπνιστική <b>εκστρατεία</b> - Antismoking campaign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐκστρατεία, akin to verb εκστρατεύω from prefix εκ- (ek-, “out, from”) + στρατός, from Ancient Greek ἐκστρατεύω from ἐκ- (ek-) + στρᾰτεύω; in figurative sense, partially a semantic loan from French campagne.</div><b>εκτάκτως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>on an emergency/extraordinary basis, exceptionally (conducted or convened ad hoc ahead of the normal schedule)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκτάκτως with semantic adaptation to the modern meaning of έκτακτος. By surface analysis, έκτακτ(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>εκτάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκτάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hectare</div><b>εκτέλεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκτελέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. execution, completion (carrying out of an order or an action)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. execution (legal killing of a person)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. execution, performance (manner in which piece is performed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κακή <b>εκτέλεση</b> - bad performance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκτελώ, calque of French exécution. First attested 1840.</div><b>εκτίμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκτιμήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. appreciation, appraisal, assessment, estimation (determination of value; process of making an estimate)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. estimate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. appreciation, esteem, estimation (a positive opinion of a person; favourable regard)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκτίμησις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>εκταμίευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disbursement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εκταμιεύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>εκταμιεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to disburse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκταμιεύομαι. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + ταμείο + -εύω.</div><b>εκταφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκταφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. exhumation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bursting apart</div><b>εκτελέστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκτελέστριες, masculine εκτελεστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. executor (person who carries out some task)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. executrix (someone appointed by a testator to administer a will)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) assassin, murderer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκτελώ, calque of French exécutrice. First attested 1892.</div><b>εκτελεστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκτελεστές, feminine εκτελέστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. executor (person who carries out some task)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. executor (someone appointed by a testator to administer a will)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) assassin, hitman, murderer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκτελώ, calque of French exécuteur. First attested 1812.</div><b>εκτελεστικά αποσπάσματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of εκτελεστικό <a href="bword://απόσπασμα">απόσπασμα</a></div><b>εκτελεστικό απόσπασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εκτελεστικά αποσπάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firing squad</div><b>εκτελεστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκτελεστική, neuter εκτελεστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. executive (concerned with the carrying out of an order or an action)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. executive (concerned with the legal killing of a person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκτελώ, calque of French exécutif. First attested 1796.</div><b>εκτελούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be executed</div><b>εκτελώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to carry out, execute, do (put into effect)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εκτέλεσα</b> τις εντολές του. - I carried out his orders.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to execute, kill (put to death, both by judicial process or unlawfully)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εκτελέστηκε</b> δι’ αποκεφαλισμού. - He was executed by beheading.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to perform, execute (give a performance of)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εκτέλεσαν</b> άψογα το κομμάτι του Μότσαρτ. - They performed Mozart&#x27;s piece brilliantly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκτελέω, from ἐκ- (ek-, “from, out of”) + τέλος and a semantic loan from French exécuter. Sense &quot;perform&quot; developed in Medieval Greek.</div><b>εκτεταμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine εκτεταμένη, neuter εκτεταμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive perfect participle of εκτείνω:; extensive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive perfect participle of εκτείνω:; expansive (comprehensive in scope or extent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκτεταμένος, passive perfect participle of ἐκτείνω.</div><b>εκτιμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εκτιμώ">εκτιμώ</a></div><b>εκτιμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to appreciate, respect</div>
  <div class='ex'>τον <b>εκτιμώ</b> πολύ - I think very highly of him</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to estimate, think, predict</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εκτιμώ</b> ότι θα πουληθεί πολύ ακριβά. - I predict that it will be sold at a very high price.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκτιμῶ, contracted form of ἐκτιμάω, from ἐκ- (ek-) + τιμάω.</div><b>εκτινάσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally, transitive) to throw out, to propel out (to project with force in a outward direction)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to cause to jump, to spike (to cause to increase sharply: price, inflation, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκτινάσσω.</div><b>εκτινάχθηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of εκτινάσσομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://εκτινάσσω">εκτινάσσω</a></div><b>εκτοξεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to launch, to hurl (to throw with force)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to launch (to cause (a rocket, balloon, etc., or the payload thereof) to begin its flight upward from the ground)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to hurl, to throw (:insults, accusations, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκτοξεύω. By surface analysis, εκ- (ek-) + τοξεύω.</div><b>εκτρέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be diverted, led astray</div><b>εκτρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to deflect, divert</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to lead astray, swerve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐκτρέπω. By surface analysis, εκ- (“from, away”) + τρέπω (“turn”).</div><b>εκτροπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκτροπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diversion (the act of diverting)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diversion (removal of water via a canal)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. diversion (detour or rerouting)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. deviation (the act of deviating; wandering off the correct or true path or road)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐκτροπή with semantic loan from French déviation.</div><b>εκτροχιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκτροχιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>derailment</div><b>εκτυπωτές μήτρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εκτυπωτής">εκτυπωτής</a> μήτρας</div><b>εκτυπωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκτυπωτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>printer (used to print text, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English printer. Morphologically, from verb εκτυπώ(νω)) + -τής.</div><b>εκτυπωτής μήτρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκτυπωτές μήτρας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dot matrix printer</div><b>εκτυπώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to print</div><b>εκτυφλωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blinding</div><b>εκτόξευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκτοξεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. launching, launch (the act by which something is launched)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jetting (the application of a jet of liquid)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εκτόξευση</b> νερού - water jet</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hurling (of insults, threats, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) spike, upsurge (a sharp peaking, a sudden upward movement: in price, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εκτοξεύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>εκτός</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. except</div>
  <div class='def'>2. out, outside</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐκτὸς.</div><b>εκφοβιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εκφοβιστική, neuter εκφοβιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intimidating, threatening, menacing</div><b>εκφράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to express</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ξέρω καλά αγγλικά και δυσκολεύομαι να <b>εκφραστώ</b>. - I don&#x27;t know English well and I have difficulty to express myself.</div><b>εκφράσθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formal variant of εκφράστηκα</div>
  <div class='ex'>first-person singular simple past of εκφράζομαι (ekfrázomai), the passive of εκφράζω (ekfrázo)</div><b>εκφωνητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκφωνητές, feminine εκφωνήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>announcer, broadcaster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκφωνώ + -τής.</div><b>εκφωνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to announce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐκφωνῶ.</div><b>εκφύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, intransitive) to grow from</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκφῠ́ομαι (“I am produced from, born from”) of active ἐκφῠ́ω. Morphologically, from εκ- (“from”) + φύομαι.</div><b>εκχέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of liquids) to be poured away</div><b>εκχέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to pour away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐκχέω. Morphologically, from εκ- (“from”) + the ancient χέω (“pour, scatter”).</div><b>εκχιονιστήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εκχιονιστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snow plough (UK), snow plow (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκ (ek, “out”) + χιονίζω + -τήρας.</div><b>εκχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cede, transfer (something to another person)</div><b>εκχύλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εκχυλίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. solvent extraction</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leaching</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκχυλίζω”) + -ση (-si).</div><b>εκχύνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εκχέομαι">εκχέομαι</a></div><b>εκχύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εγχέω">εγχέω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εκχέω with metaplasm according to the pattern χέω &gt; χύνω.</div><b>ελάσσων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελάσσων, neuter έλασσον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. minor (key signature, scale or chord)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. minor, lesser</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐλάσσων.</div><b>ελάτε να δείτε</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έλα">έλα</a> να δεις</div><b>ελάττωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of people) shortcoming, imperfection, deficiency</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of things) malfunction</div>
  <div class='def'>3. any disadvantage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐλάττωμα, from ἐλασσόω, from ἐλάσσων, comparative of μικρός.</div><b>ελάφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελάφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. red deer (Cervus elaphus)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deer, stag, buck, hart</div>
  <div class='def'>3. venison</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐλάφιον, diminutive of ἔλαφος.</div><b>ελάφρωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελαφρώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>respite, relief</div><b>ελάχιστα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of ελάχιστος, the absolute superlative degree of <a href="bword://λίγος">λίγος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of ελάχιστος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. very little, very few</div>
  <div class='def'>2. just a bit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. too little, too few</div><b>ελέγκτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελέγκτριας, masculine ελεγκτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. auditor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inspector</div>
  <div class='def'>3. controller</div><b>ελέγχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be inspected, be audited</div><b>ελέγχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to question, check, test</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to control, check, monitor</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to audit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐλέγχω.</div><b>ελέφαντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ελέφαντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elephant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐλέφας.</div><b>ελαία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελαίαι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ελιά">ελιά</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐλαία.</div><b>ελαίαγνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ελαίαγνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elaeagnus; silverberry, oleaster (Elaeagnus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>ελαίαγνος</b> είναι αειθαλής καλλωπιστικός θάμνος που επιλέγεται συνήθως για το διακοσμητικό φύλλωμά του, αλλά και για τα αρωματικά λευκά μικρά άνθη του. - The elaeagnus is an evergreen ornamental shrub that is usually chosen for its decorative foliage, but also for its fragrant white small flowers.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἐλαίαγνος, with a semantic loan from translingual Elaeagnus.</div><b>ελαιογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελαιογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oil painting (a painting in oil paints, or the technique or process)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ελαιο (elaio, “oil”) + γραφία a calque of French peinture a l&#x27;huile.</div><b>ελαιόδενδρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελαιόδενδρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ελαιόδεντρο">ελαιόδεντρο</a> (olive tree)</div><b>ελαιόδεντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελαιόδεντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>olive tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa ἐλαιόδενδρον, from ἐλαίᾱ + δένδρον.</div><b>ελαιόλαδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελαιόλαδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>olive oil (oil pressed from olives)</div>
  <div class='ex'>έξτρα παρθένο <b>ελαιόλαδο</b> - extra virgin olive oil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐλαιόλαδον, from ἔλαιον + λάδι + -ον (-on).</div><b>ελαιώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ελαιώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>olive grove (area of land where olive trees grow)</div><b>ελατήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελατήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spring (coiled device)</div><b>ελατοτρύγονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελατοτρύγονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oriental turtle dove, Streptopelia orientalis</div><b>ελαφάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελαφάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fawn, young deer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ελάφι + -άκι.</div><b>ελαφίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελαφίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doe, female deer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ελάφι + -ίνα</div><b>ελαφρά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lightly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. quietly</div><b>ελαφραίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to lighten (weight, load, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) to lighten, lessen (make more tolerable)</div>
  <div class='ex'>, less common: ξαλαφραίνω (xalafraíno)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐλαφραίνω from Hellenistic ἐλαφρύνω + -αίνω. Doublet of ελαφρύνω and ελαφρώνω.</div><b>ελαφρομυαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) frivolity, silliness</div><b>ελαφρόμυαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελαφρόμυαλη, neuter ελαφρόμυαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frivolous, scatterbrained</div><b>ελαφρόπετρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελαφρόπετρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pumice</div><b>ελαφρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελαφριά or ελαφρά, neuter ελαφρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. light; light (as a feather), lightweight (clothing, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. light; lighthearted, frivolous</div>
  <div class='def'>3. slight, thin, weak</div>
  <div class='def'>4. weakminded</div>
  <div class='def'>5. trivial, trifling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐλαφρός</div><b>ελαφρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελαφρότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lightness, frivolity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐλαφρότης, equivalent to ελαφρός + -ότητα.</div><b>ελαφρύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελαφριά, neuter ελαφρύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ελαφρός">ελαφρός</a></div><b>ελαφρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lighten (weight, load, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to lighten, lessen (make more tolerable)</div><b>ελαφρώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slightly, somewhat, a little</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐλᾰφρῶς, the adverbial form of ἐλᾰφρός.</div><b>ελβετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελβετική, neuter ελβετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Swiss (relating to Switzerland or its people)</div><b>ελεήμων</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gracious, merciful (compassionate; showing mercy to those who are suffering)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐλεήμων.</div><b>ελεγκτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ελεγκτές, feminine ελέγκτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. auditor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inspector</div>
  <div class='ex'>τελωνειακός <b>ελεγκτής</b> - customs inspector</div>
  <div class='def'>3. controller</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ελεγκτής</b> εναέριας κυκλοφορίας - air traffic controller</div><b>ελεγκτής εναέριας κυκλοφορίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ελεγκτών εναέριας κυκλοφορίας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>air traffic controller</div><b>ελεγκτών εναέριας κυκλοφορίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://ελεγκτής">ελεγκτής</a> εναέριας κυκλοφορίας</div><b>ελεημοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελεημοσύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alms, help (something given to the poor as charity such as money, clothing or food)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ζητιάνος ζητάει <b>ελεημοσύνη</b> από τους περαστικούς. - The beggar begs passers-by for alms.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Ancient Greek ἔλεος
Proto-Indo-European *-eti
Proto-Indo-European *-eyéti
Proto-Indo-European *-esyéti
Proto-Indo-European *-éh₁ti
Proto-Indo-European *-yeti
Proto-Indo-European *-éh₁yeti
Proto-Indo-European *-yeti
Proto-Indo-European *-éyeti
Ancient Greek -έω
Ancient Greek ἐλεέω
Proto-Indo-European *-mṓ
Ancient Greek -μων
Ancient Greek ἐλεήμων
Ancient Greek -συνος (-sunos)
Ancient Greek -σῠ́νη
Ancient Greek ἐλεημοσύνη
Greek ελεημοσύνη
Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐλεημοσύνη, from ἐλεήμων + -σῠ́νη.</div><b>ελευθερία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελευθερίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>freedom, liberty</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η προσωπική <b>ελευθερία</b> είναι απαραβίαστη. - Personal liberty is inviolable.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐλευθερία. Compare the inherited λευτεριά.</div><b>ελευθεροτεκτονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) freemasonry</div><b>ελευθερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to free, to liberate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to untie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐλευθερόω. Doublet of λευτερώνω. By surface analysis, ελεύθερος + -ώνω.</div><b>ελεφαντοστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελεφαντοστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ivory, elephant tusk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ελέφαντας + οστό</div><b>ελεφαντόδοντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελεφαντόδοντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tusk of the elephant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ivory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ελέφαντας + δόντι</div><b>ελεύθερα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ελεύθερος">ελεύθερος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ελεύθερο">ελεύθερο</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>freely</div><b>ελεύθερες αγορές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ελεύθερη <a href="bword://αγορά">αγορά</a></div><b>ελεύθερη αγορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελεύθερες αγορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>free market</div><b>ελεύθερη βούληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>free will</div><b>ελεύθερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελεύθερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. freestyle (swimming event)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. authorisation, permission, freedom to do something</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του έχω δώσει το <b>ελεύθερο</b> να έρχεται όποτε θέλει σπίτι μου. - He can come to my home whenever he wants (because I have told him so)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ελεύθερος">ελεύθερος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ελεύθερος">ελεύθερος</a></div><b>ελεύθερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελεύθερη or ελευθέρα, neuter ελεύθερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>free, single, available</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐλεύθερος. Compare to the inherited λεύτερος.</div><b>ελεύθερος χρόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>free time, time off</div><b>ελιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. olive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. olive tree</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mole, wart (skin blemish)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) gland</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐλαία.</div><b>ελικόπτερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελικόπτερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>helicopter</div><b>ελιξήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελιξήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ελιξίριο">ελιξίριο</a></div><b>ελιξίριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελιξίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elixir</div><b>ελισαβετιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελισαβετιανή, neuter ελισαβετιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Elizabethan, referring to the era of Elizabeth I</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ελισάβετ f + -ιανός.</div><b>ελιόψωμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ελιόψωμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bread with olives in the dough</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ελι(ά) (“olive”) + -ό- (interfix) + -ψωμο (“bread”).</div><b>ελκυστήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ελκυστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tractor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑλκυστήρ, from ἕλκω.</div><b>ελκυστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sexually attractive, charming, winsome</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑλκυστικός.</div><b>ελκύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) to attract, drag</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ελκύω</b> το ενδιαφέρον - attract interest</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to attract, appeal</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με <b>ελκύει</b> πολύ. Είναι πολύ σέξι. - S/he appeals to me very much. S/he is very sexy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Medieval Byzantine Greek ἑλκύω, in analogy to the future ἑλκύσω or aorist εἵλκυσα of Ancient Greek ἕλκω; probably because of similarity to ἐρύω. Unrelated to έλκος.</div><b>ελλαδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελλαδική, neuter ελλαδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Helladic (of the bronze age Greece)</div><b>ελλειπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελλειπτική, neuter ελλειπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. elliptical, elliptic (in a shape of, or reminding of, an ellipse; oval)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. defective (lacking some forms)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. elliptical, elliptic (of, or showing ellipsis; having a word or words omitted)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> In the geometry sense, a learned borrowing from French elliptique, from New Latin ellipticus, from Koine Greek ἐλλειπτικός. In the grammar/linguistic senses, a learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐλλειπτικός.</div><b>ελληνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to Hellenise (UK), Hellenize (US)</div><b>ελληνίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (masculine ελληνιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a female person studying Ancient and Hellenistic Greek literature and language.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a female writer, especially a Jewish or non-Greek one, of the Hellenistic period.</div><b>ελληνικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greek (language)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο άνδρας μιλούσε άπταιστα <b>ελληνικά</b>. - The man spoke fluent Greek.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ελληνικός”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ελληνικός">ελληνικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ελληνικός”).</div><b>ελληνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελληνική, neuter ελληνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greek, Hellenic (related to the country, people or language of Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek Ἑλληνῐκός.</div><b>ελληνιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ελληνιστές, feminine ελληνίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a person studying Ancient and Hellenistic Greek literature and language.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a writer, especially a Jewish or non-Greek one, of the Hellenistic period.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἑλληνιστής, from ἑλληνίζω.</div><b>ελληνιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελληνιστική, neuter ελληνιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Hellenistic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English Hellenistic or German hellenistisch, themselves from Koine Greek Ἑλληνιστής. A reborrowing.</div><b>ελληνοαλβανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελληνοαλβανικη, neuter ελληνοαλβανικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greco-Albanian, Greek-Albanian</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα <b>ελληνοαλβανικά</b> σύνορα - the Greco-Albanian border</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ελληνο- (ellino-) + αλβανικός.</div><b>ελληνοαμερικάνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελληνοαμερικάνη, neuter ελληνοαμερικάνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greek American (of people of Greek ethnicity living in USA)</div><b>ελληνοαμερικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελληνοαμερικανική, neuter ελληνοαμερικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Greco-American, Greek-American (relating to both Greece and America)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Greek-American (relating to Greek Americans)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Sense 1 from ελληνο- (ellino-) + αμερικανικός; sense 2 from Ελληνοαμερικαν(ός), “Greek American”) + -ικός.</div><b>ελληνομάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελληνομάθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>knowledge or study of the Greek language and Greek culture</div><b>ελληνορθόδοξος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελληνορθόδοξη, neuter ελληνορθόδοξο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greek Orthodox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ελληνο- (ellino-, “Greek”) + ορθόδοξος.</div><b>ελληνορρωμαϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελληνορρωμαϊκή, neuter ελληνορρωμαϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ελληνορωμαϊκός">ελληνορωμαϊκός</a></div><b>ελληνορωμαϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ελληνορωμαϊκή, neuter ελληνορωμαϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Graeco-Roman (British), Greco-Roman (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ελληνο- (ellino-) + ρωμαϊκός, a calque of English Graeco-Roman.</div><b>ελνστ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ελληνιστικός">ελληνιστικός</a>: Hellenistic</div><b>ελονοσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελονοσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>malaria</div><b>ελπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ελπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hope, expectation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐλπίς, ἐλπίδα.</div><b>ελπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to hope to, expect to</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to hope, have hope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐλπίζω.</div><b>εμάς</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, strong) us (1st person plural, accusative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal, pronoun, strong) our (1st person plural, genitive)</div><b>εμένα</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, strong) me (1st person singular, accusative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal, pronoun, strong) my (1st person singular, genitive)</div><b>εμένα μου λες</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, informal) you&#x27;re telling me, don&#x27;t I know, you bet.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πω πω, ένας αέρας σήμερα! — <b>Εμένα μου λες</b>! Κόντεψε να με πάρει! - Wow, what a wind today! — You&#x27;re telling me! It nearly took me away!</div><b>εμίρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμίρηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emir (the title of the nominal ruler if an emirate)</div><b>εμαγέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εμαγιέ">εμαγιέ</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εμαγιέ">εμαγιέ</a></div><b>εμαγιέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) enamel, enamelled ware (vitreous coating especially cobalt blue, mainly household wares, often decorative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) smalt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French émaillé (“enamelled”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) enamelled (UK), enameled (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French émaillé (“enamelled”).</div><b>εμβέλεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμβέλειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. range</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) scope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐμβελής, from ἐν (en, “in”) and βέλος.</div><b>εμβατήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμβατήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>march (piece of music written for marching)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι στρατιώτες κάνουν παρέλαση με <b>εμβατήρια</b>. - The soldiers are on parade with marches.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek ἐμβατήριον -with noun μέλος-, neuter adjective of ἐμβατήριος, with stem ἐμβα- of the ancient verb ἐμβαίνω + -τήριον. From (ἐν-) ἐμ- ((en-) em-, “in”) + βαίνω.</div><b>εμβιομηχανική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) biomechanics</div><b>εμβιοτοκίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Embiotocidae or surfperches.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Embiotocidae. By surface analysis, εμβιοτόκος + -ίδες.</div><b>εμβληματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμβληματική, neuter εμβληματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emblematic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εμβληματ-) + -ικός.</div><b>εμβοή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) tinnitus (a jingling or buzzing in the ears)</div><b>εμβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. embolism (an obstruction or occlusion of a blood vessel by an embolus)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ramming attack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμβολή with semantic loan from French embolie, borrowed from the same etymon.</div><b>εμβολίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to ram (one ship into another)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to ram (one vehicle into another)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) to beat someone with a club, etc</div><b>εμβολιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to innoculate (against a disease)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to graft (part of one woody plant onto another)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the more formal εμβόλι(ο)) + -άζω, calque of Greek μπολιάζω.</div><b>εμβολιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμβολιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vaccination</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grafting</div><b>εμβρόντητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμβρόντητη, neuter εμβρόντητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stunned, thunderstruck</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμβρόντητος.</div><b>εμβόλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμβόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vaccine</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βεβαίως υπάρχει ανάγκη να δημιουργηθεί και το <b>εμβόλιο</b> σε περίπτωση πανδημίας όταν παρουσιασθεί ο ιός. - Of course, the vaccine also needs to be created in the event of a pandemic once the virus appears.</div><b>εμείς</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, strong) we (1st person plural, nominative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἡμεῖς.</div><b>εμετικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμετικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emetic</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://εμετικός">εμετικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εμετικός">εμετικός</a></div><b>εμετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμετική, neuter εμετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emetic</div><b>εμετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμετοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vomiting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nausea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔμετος.</div><b>εμιράτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμιράτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emirate (region or country ruled by an emir)</div><b>εμμηνορρυσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) menstruation, period</div><b>εμμηνόπαυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) menopause</div><b>εμμηνόρροια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) menstruation, period</div><b>εμμονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμμονές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adherence (faithful support for some cause)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. persistence, adamance</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fixation, obsession</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμμονή, with adaptation of meaning to εμμένω.</div><b>εμορφιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμορφιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ομορφιά">ομορφιά</a></div><b>εμού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) emu (Dromaius novaehollandiae)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English emu.</div><b>εμπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμπάθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. malice, malevolence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. maliciousness, malevolence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐμπάθεια.</div><b>εμπαίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to mock, tease, ridicule</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐμπαίζω. Morphologically, from (εν-) εμ- (“in”) + παίζω (“play”).</div><b>εμπαθής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμπαθής, neuter εμπαθές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>malevolent, malicious</div><b>εμπαθώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>malevolently, maliciously</div><b>εμπεδώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to consolidate, make permanent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to internalize knowledge of the object</div><b>εμπειρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμπειρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>experience (knowledge or skill gained by practice)</div>
  <div class='ex'>από την προσωπική του <b>εμπειρία</b> - from personal experience</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐμπειρία.</div><b>εμπειρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμπειρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. deciding or acting based on one&#x27;s experience, not theoretical knowledge (sometimes derogatory)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. empiricism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French empirisme.</div><b>εμπειρογνώμονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εμπειρογνώμων">εμπειρογνώμων</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εμπειρογνώμ(ων)) + -ονας (-onas).</div><b>εμπειρογνώμων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμπειρογνώμων, neuter εμπειρογνώμον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expert</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from έμπειρ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + γνώμ(η)) + -ων (-on).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εμπειρογνώμονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expert, connoisseur</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from έμπειρ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + γνώμ(η)) + -ων (-on).</div><b>εμπερικλείομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be contained</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ἐμπερικλείομαι</div><b>εμπερικλείω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to contain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμπερικλείω. Morphologically, from (εν-) εμ- + περικλείω, εμ- + περι- + κλείω.</div><b>εμπιστευτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confidentially</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του μίλησε <b>εμπιστευτικά</b> για την αρρώστια του. - She spoke to him confidentially about his illness.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://εμπιστευτικός">εμπιστευτικός</a></div><b>εμπιστευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμπιστευτική, neuter εμπιστευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confidential</div><b>εμπιστεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to trust, entrust someone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to confide something</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Leanedly, from Ancient Greek ἐμπιστεύομαι, from ἐν (en, “in”) (ἐμ- (em-)) + passive voice of πιστεύω.</div><b>εμπιστοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine singular</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(singular only) trust; confidence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ἐμπιστοστύνη. See Ancient Greek ἐμπιστεύω.</div><b>εμπλέκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to involve (someone in)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐμπλέκω.</div><b>εμπλοκή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμπλοκές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. engagement, enmeshing (gears etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breakdown due to misalignment (gears etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) imbroglio, embroilment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek ἐμπλοκή, from ἐμπλέκω.</div><b>εμπλουτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to enrich (to enhance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to enrich (to adorn, ornate more richly)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to enrich (to cause an increase in the proportion of a given constituent)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to enrich (to increase the amount of one isotope in a mixture of isotopes, especially in a nuclear fuel)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εμ- (em-) + πλούτ(ος)) + -ίζω, a calque of French enrichir.</div><b>εμπνέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be inspired, have an inspiration</div><b>εμπνέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to inspire</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δάσκαλός τους τους είχε <b>εμπνεύσει</b> το ενδιαφέρον για τη λογοτεχνία. - Their teacher had inspired them their interest in literature.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμπνέω. By surface analysis, εμ- (em-) + πνέω.</div><b>εμπνευσμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inspired, with inspiration</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εμπνευσμένος">εμπνευσμένος</a></div><b>εμπνευσμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine εμπνευσμένη, neuter εμπνευσμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of persons) inspired, charismatic, talented</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of work) inspiring, produced by great inspiration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of εμπνέομαι, passive voice of εμπνέω (“inspire”) from Ancient Greek ἐμπνέω (“breathe upon; inspire”). The modern sense, calque of French inspiré.</div><b>εμποδίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prevented</div><b>εμποδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to block, impede, obstruct, be in the way (prevent something or someone from passing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχει μποτιλιάρισμα διότι <b>εμποδίζει</b> αμάξι την δεξιά λωρίδα. - There&#x27;s a traffic jam due to a car blocking the right lane.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to prevent, keep from, stop, hinder, thwart (refrain or cause to refrain)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τί σε <b>εμποδίζει</b>; - What&#x27;s stopping you?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐμποδίζω, from ἐμποδιος (empodios, “obstructive, blocking”), from ἐμποδών, from ἐν (en, “in, on, at”) + ποδῶν, genitive plural of πούς.</div><b>εμπορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) trade, trading</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) trafficking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐμπορία.</div><b>εμπορείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμπορεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated) trading centre (especially a port)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) trading post</div><b>εμπορευματοκιβώτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>container, cargo container, shipping container</div><b>εμπορευματοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμπορευματοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commercialization (UK), commercialisation (US)</div><b>εμπορεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to trade</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to commercialise (UK), commercialize (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμπορεύομαι.</div><b>εμπορεύσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμπορεύσιμη, neuter εμπορεύσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>marketable</div><b>εμπορικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εμπορικός">εμπορικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commercially</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εμπορικός">εμπορικός</a></div><b>εμπορικές αμαξοστοιχίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of εμπορική <a href="bword://αμαξοστοιχία">αμαξοστοιχία</a></div><b>εμπορική αμαξοστοιχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμπορικές αμαξοστοιχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goods train (UK), freight train (US)</div><b>εμπορικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμπορικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shop, store (especially fabrics, fashions and fancy goods)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. haberdashery shop</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://εμπορικός">εμπορικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εμπορικός">εμπορικός</a></div><b>εμπορικό επιμελητήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμπορικά επιμελητήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chamber of commerce</div><b>εμπορικό κέντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμπορικά κέντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shopping centre (UK), shopping center (US), mall, shopping mall</div><b>εμπορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμπορική, neuter εμπορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. commercial, trade</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) of low quality</div><b>εμπορικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) marketability</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εμπορικός + -ότητα. First attested 1891.</div><b>εμποριολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) commerce (the scientific study of trade)</div><b>εμπορομεσίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμπορομεσίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>broker (of commercial transactions)</div><b>εμποροπανήγυρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμποροπανηγύρεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trade fair</div>
  <div class='def'>2. market</div><b>εμποροπανήγυρις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμποροπανηγύρεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://εμποροπανήγυρη">εμποροπανήγυρη</a></div><b>εμποροπλοίαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμποροπλοίαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. merchant ship&#x27;s captain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (obsolete) owner of merchant ship</div><b>εμποροϋπάλληλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εμποροϋπάλληλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shop assistant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εμπόριο + υπάλληλος.</div><b>εμπρήστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμπρήστριες, masculine εμπρηστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>arsonist</div><b>εμπρησμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμπρησμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>arson</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐμπρησμός, from ἐμπίμπρημι + -μός.</div><b>εμπρηστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμπρηστές, feminine εμπρήστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>arsonist</div><b>εμπρός</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ahead, forth, onwards</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προχωρήστε <b>εμπρός</b> παρακαλώ. - Move ahead please.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a preposition) in front of</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (as a preposition) compared to</div>
  <div class='ex'>see Expressions, mostly with μπρος (bros)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. commence, start</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βάζω <b>εμπρός</b> τη μηχανή. - I start the engine.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (with article) ahead, onwards, front</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προχωράω προς <b>τα εμπρός</b> - I move onwards.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (as adjective, with article) ahead, onwards, front</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθησε στην <b>εμπρός</b> βεράντα. - He sat in the balcony out front.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐμπρός from ancient phrase ἐν πρός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (interrogative) hello?</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εμπρός</b>; Λέγετε παρακαλώ; - Hello? Who&#x27;s there?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a command) (usually +imperative) begin to...</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εμπρός</b>, μαρς! - Forward march!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐμπρός from ancient phrase ἐν πρός.</div><b>εμπρόσθιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμπρόσθια, neuter εμπρόσθιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) fore, the one in front</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>εμπρόσθιο</b> και το οπίσθιο τμήμα - the foremost and the hinder section</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐμπρόσθιος, from ἐμ- (em-) &lt; ἐν (en) + πρόσθιος.</div><b>εμπόδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμπόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. obstacle, obstruction, barrier, impediment, hindrance (something preventing passage or success)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δρόμος είναι γεμάτος <b>εμπόδια</b> λόγω κατολισθήσεων. - The road is full of obstructions due to landslides.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hurdle (artificial barrier over which athletes or horses jump in a race)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δρόμος μετ’ <b>εμποδίων</b> - obstacle course</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐμποδιον (empodion), from ἐμποδιος (empodios, “obstructive, blocking”), from ἐμποδών, from ἐν (en, “in, on, at”) + ποδῶν, genitive plural of πούς.</div><b>εμπόλεμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμπόλεμη, neuter εμπόλεμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>belligerent, warring</div><b>εμπόρευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμπορεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>merchandise, commodities, wares</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμπόρευμα, from ἐμπορεύομαι, a verbal formation from ἔμπορος.</div><b>εμπόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εμπόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trade, commerce</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trafficking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμπόριον, neuter of ἐμπόριος, from ἔμπορος.</div><b>εμπόρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμπόρισσες, masculine έμπορας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) merchant, trader, dealer</div><b>εμφάνιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμφανίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. appearance (the act of appearing or coming into sight)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. appearance (the act of appearing in a particular place, or in society, a company, or any proceedings)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. appearance (an instance of someone coming into a court of law to be part of proceedings)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. development (the processing of photographic film so as to bring out the images latent in it)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. appearance (the way in which something or someone appears to others)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐμφάνισις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>εμφανίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to appear, emerge</div><b>εμφανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to present, show</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to print film of a photograph from the negative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐμφανίζω, from ἐμφανής + -ίζω, the former component from ἐμφαίνω.</div><b>εμφιαλομένο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of εμφιαλωμένο</div><b>εμφιαλωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμφιαλωμένη, neuter εμφιαλωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bottled</div>
  <div class='ex'>εμφιαλωμένο νερό (bottled water)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of εμφιαλώνω</div><b>εμφιαλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bottle</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παρασκευάζεται και <b>εμφιαλώνεται</b> από … - Produced and bottled by …</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εν (en, “in”) + φιάλη + -ώνω, calque of French embouteiller. First attested 1892.</div><b>εμφράγματα του μυοκαρδίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://έμφραγμα">έμφραγμα</a> <a href="bword://του">του</a> μυοκαρδίου</div><b>εμφύλιοι πόλεμοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εμφύλιος">εμφύλιος</a> <a href="bword://πόλεμος">πόλεμος</a></div><b>εμφύλιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εμφύλια, neuter εμφύλιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>civil (of disagreement: internal, within a larger whole)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εμφύλιος</b> πόλεμος - civil war</div><b>εμφύλιος πόλεμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εμφύλιοι πόλεμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>civil war</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγγλικός <b>Εμφύλιος Πόλεμος</b> - English Civil War</div><b>εμφύτευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εμφυτεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>implantation (of fertilized egg, artificial tooth; hair transplant)</div><b>εμότζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) emoji</div><b>εμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εξεμώ">εξεμώ</a></div><b>εν</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) in, into, at, within</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐν (en).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, formal) Monotonic script of ἕν</div>
  <div class='ex'>nominative/accusative neuter of ένας (énas) (one)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Ancient Greek ἕν n (“one”) used in set phrases.</div><b>εν αγνοία</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) unbeknownst</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εν αγνοία</b> μου - unbeknownst to me</div><b>εν ακαρεί</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) instantly</div><b>εν γένει</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in general, generally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa ἐν γένει, formed from Ancient Greek ἐν (en) + γένει, dative singular of γένος.</div><b>εν κρυπτώ και παραβύστω</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hole-and-corner, hidden and secret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ancient preposition ἐν (“in”) + singular dative case of κρυπτός (“hidden, secret”) and παράβυστος (“pushed aside in a corner”). See ancient phrase ἐν παραβύστῳ.</div><b>εν μέρει</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in part, partly, partially</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek ἐν (en, “in”) + μέρει, dative singular of μέρος.</div><b>εν οίνω αλήθεια</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in wine, there is truth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of Latin in vīnō vēritās.</div><b>εν ολίγοις</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in short, in a nutshell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐν ὀλῐ́γοις. Literally, “in few (words)”.</div><b>εν πάση περιπτώσει</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) in any case, at any rate, anyhow, regardless</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εν πάση περιπτώσει</b>, αποδείχτηκε ότι ήταν λάθος. - In any case, it was proven wrong.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A calque of French en tout cas. Katharevousa set phrase (polytonic spelling: ἐν πάσῃ περιπτώσει) with preposition ἐν (en, “in”), the feminine singular dative πάσῃ of πᾶς &amp; περίπτωσις -modern περίπτωση-.</div><b>εν παραβύστω</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) in secret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐν παραβύστῳ.</div><b>εν συνεχεία</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subsequently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa, formed from Ancient Greek ἐν (en) + συνεχείᾳ, dative singular of συνέχεια.</div><b>εν τη γενέσει</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) in the beginning, at the moment of the creation of something</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα φαινόμενα βίας πρέπει να αντιμετωπίζονται <b>εν τη γενέσει</b> τους. - Violent incidents must be tackled at their source.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned stereotypical Katharevousa expression. Preposition ἐν (en) &amp; dative singular feminine article τῇ &amp; dative singular of the feminine noun γένεσις.</div><b>εν τούτοις</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) however, nevertheless (see <a href="bword://εντούτοις">εντούτοις</a>)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * see: εντούτοις</div><b>εν τω γίγνεσθαι</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) in progress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned stereotypical Katharevousa expression. Literally, “in the making”.</div><b>εν τω μεταξύ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meanwhile, in the meantime</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐν τῷ μεταξύ.</div><b>εν όψει</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in view, in sight</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κίνδυνος <b>εν όψει</b>! - Danger ahead!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in place of preposition) see Phrase</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐν ὄψει: preposition ἐν (en) &amp; singular dative of feminine noun ὄψις.
For the preposition, semantic loan from French en vue de.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal, with genitive) in view of, in front</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βρισκόμαστε <b>εν όψει</b> εκλογών. - We are expecting elections soon.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) +article in genitive: because of (a cause which will happen in the future)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να προετοιμαστούμε <b>εν όψει</b> των αλλαγών. - We must prepare ourselves in view of the changes [expected].</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐν ὄψει: preposition ἐν (en) &amp; singular dative of feminine noun ὄψις.
For the preposition, semantic loan from French en vue de.</div><b>ενάντια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ενάντιος">ενάντιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with accusative) against</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πλέαμε <b>ενάντια</b> στον άνεμο. - We were sailing against the wind.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adverb formed from ενάντι(ος), “opposing”) + -α (-a, “suffix forming adverbs”). Compare to ancient adverb ἐναντία.</div><b>ενάντιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενάντια, neuter ενάντιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. against</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contrary</div><b>ενάρετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενάρετη, neuter ενάρετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>virtuous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνάρετος. By surface analysis, εν- (en-) + αρετή + -ος (-os).</div><b>ενάρετος κύκλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ενάρετοι κύκλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>virtuous circle</div><b>ενέργεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενέργειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. energy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. action</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνέργεια.</div><b>ενέχυρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενέχυρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pledge, pawn (item given as security on a loan)</div><b>ενήλικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ενήλικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adult</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐνήλιξ).</div><b>ενήλικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενήλικη, neuter ενήλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adult, grown-up (fully grown)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of age</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (noun from verb) adult (a person who has reached the legal age of majority)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐνήλικος with semantic loan from French adulte.</div><b>ενήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aware, informed, cognizant, up-to-date, abreast of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εν- (en-, “in-”) + ημέρα + -ος (-os, “adjectival suffix”), i.e. &quot;aware of the day&#x27;s events&quot;, formed as a calque of French à jour (“up to date”) based on the model of older terms such as ἐφήμερος. Attested since 1871.</div><b>ενίσχυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strengthening</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reinforcement (the act, process, or state of reinforcing or being reinforced)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. amplification</div>
  <div class='def'>4. boost, boosting, enhancement (something that helps, or adds power or effectiveness)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. reinforcement</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (plural normally) reinforcements</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐνίσχυσις. By surface analysis, ενισχύ(ω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>εναέριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εναέρια, neuter εναέριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. aerial (positioned high up; elevated)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. air (attributive), aerial (pertaining to a vehicle which travels through the air)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εθνικός <b>εναέριος</b> χώρος - national airspace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐναέριος.</div><b>εναγωνίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anxiously</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐναγωνίως. By surface analysis, εναγώνι(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>εναλλάκτες θερμότητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εναλλάκτης">εναλλάκτης</a> θερμότητας</div><b>εναλλάκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εναλλάκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inverter, power inverter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. heat exchanger</div><b>εναλλάκτης θερμότητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εναλλάκτες θερμότητας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heat exchanger</div><b>εναλλάξ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternately, by turns</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐναλλάξ.</div><b>εναλλαγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εναλλαγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternation, switching</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐναλλαγή, with semantic loan from French alternance and alternation.</div><b>εναλλακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εναλλακτική, neuter εναλλακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative (other; different from something else)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative (not traditional, outside the mainstream)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐναλλακτικός, with semantic loan from French alternatif and English alternative.</div><b>εναλλασσόμενο ρεύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) alternating current</div><b>εναλλασσόμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εναλλασσόμενη, neuter εναλλασσόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternating</div>
  <div class='ex'>εναλλασσόμενο ρεύμα (alternating current)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rotating</div>
  <div class='ex'>εναλλασσόμενες καλλιέργειες (rotating crops)</div><b>εναντίον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with genitive) against, versus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐναντίον (“opposite to, across”).</div><b>εναντίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>opposition (the act of opposing, of being against something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐναντίωσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>εναρμόνιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αντιστίξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harmonisation (UK), harmonization (US)</div><b>ενασχόληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενασχολήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. engagement (being involved in or occupied by an activity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. activity, pursuit</div>
  <div class='ex'>ψυχαγωγικές <b>ενασχολήσεις</b> - recreational activities/pursuits</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ενασχόλησις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>ενδέκατος</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine (feminine ενδέκατη, neuter ενδωδέκατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eleventh, 11th</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑνδέκᾰτος.</div><b>ενδέχεται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, passive) to be possible</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ενδέχεται</b> να παραιτηθεί η κυβέρνηση. - There is a possibility that the government will resign.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνδέχεται, 3rd singular person of ἐνδέχομαι. By surface analysis, εν- (“in”) + δέχομαι (“receive”).</div><b>ενδίδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to give in, to yield</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνδίδωμι. By surface analysis, εν- (en-) + δίδω.</div><b>ενδεικτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ενδεικτικός">ενδεικτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indicatively, by way of indication</div><b>ενδεικτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενδεικτική, neuter ενδεικτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indicative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐνδεικτικός. By surface analysis, ενδείκ(νυμαι)) + -τικός.</div><b>ενδεχομένως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>possibly, perhaps, conceivably, perchance, potentially</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα βρούμε ποτέ λύση στο πρόβλημα; <b>Ενδεχομένως</b>. - Will we ever find a solution to the problem? Possibly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ενδεχόμενος, itself from ενδέχεται.</div><b>ενδεχόμενο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://ενδεχόμενος">ενδεχόμενος</a>:; accusative masculine singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://ενδεχόμενος">ενδεχόμενος</a>:; nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενδεχόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contingency, eventuality, possibility (something which may or may not happen)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. likelihood, possibility, potential</div><b>ενδεχόμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ενδεχόμενη, neuter ενδεχόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>potential, possible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνδεχόμενον (neuter) or ἐνδεχόμενος, present participle of ἐνδέχομαι, a verb in passive voice.</div><b>ενδιάμεσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενδιάμεση, neuter ενδιάμεσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intermediate (occurring between two extremes, or in the middle of a range)</div><b>ενδιατρίβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to spend time at a certain place</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) to spend time studying a certain subject</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνδιατρίβω. Morphgologically, εν- (“in”) + δια- (“through”) + τρίβω (“grate, grind”).</div><b>ενδιαφέρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be interested in</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ενδιαφέρομαι</b> για τα μαθηματικά. - I&#x27;m interested in mathematics.</div><b>ενδιαφέρον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενδιαφέροντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interest (attention shown)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ενδιαφέρων, the neuter form of the present participle of the verb ενδιαφέρω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ενδιαφέρων">ενδιαφέρων</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ενδιαφέρων, the neuter form of the present participle of the verb ενδιαφέρω.</div><b>ενδιαφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to interest</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν με <b>ενδιαφέρει</b>. - I&#x27;m not interested.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εν- (en-) + διαφέρω.</div><b>ενδιαφέρων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενδιαφέρουσα, neuter ενδιαφέρον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interesting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of the verb ενδιαφέρω.</div><b>ενδογαμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) endogamy</div><b>ενδοκρινής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενδοκρινής, neuter ενδοκρινές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endocrine</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ενδοκρινής</b> αδένας - endocrine gland</div><b>ενδοκρινής αδένας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ενδοκρινείς αδένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endocrine gland</div><b>ενδοκρινολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) endocrinology</div><b>ενδοκρινολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ενδοκρινολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endocrinologist</div><b>ενδοπλασματικό δίκτυο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endoplasmic reticulum, ER</div><b>ενδοσκόπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενδοσκοπήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. endoscopy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. introspection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ενδο- (endo-, “inside”) + -σκόπηση</div><b>ενδοσπέρμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενδοσπέρμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endosperm (inner, starchy part of a grain)</div><b>ενδοφλεβίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intravenously</div><b>ενδοχώρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hinterland (the area of land behind a coastline)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ενδο- (endo-, “within, inside”) + χώρα</div><b>ενδοωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενδοωτική, neuter ενδοωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intra-aural, in the ear.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ενδο- (endo-, “in”) + ωτικός.</div><b>ενδρομίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενδρομίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heavy hand-woven woollen blanket, throw or rug</div><b>ενδυμασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενδυμασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. costume, clothing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. attire, clothing style</div>
  <div class='def'>3. wardrobe, set of clothing</div><b>ενδυναμώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to strengthen, to reinforce</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to empower (to give someone more confidence and/or strength to do something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐνδυναμῶ, contracted form of ἐνδυναμόω, with -ώνω suffix.</div><b>ενδόπλασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) endoplasm</div><b>ενεδρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ambush, waylay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνεδρεύω. By surface analysis, εν- (en-) + εδρεύω.</div><b>ενενήντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ninety</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνενήκοντα, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁néwn̥(d)ḱomt (“nine tens”).</div><b>ενενηκοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενενηκοστή, neuter ενενηκοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ninetieth (ordinal form of the number ninety)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνενηκοστός.</div><b>ενεργ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ενεργητικός">ενεργητικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of ενεργητική <a href="bword://φωνή">φωνή</a></div><b>ενεργειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενεργειακή, neuter ενεργειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>referring to energy sources, to energy efficiency</div>
  <div class='ex'>βελτίωση της <b>ενεργειακής</b> απόδοσης - improvement of energy efficiency</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ενέργει(α), “energy”) + -ακός.</div><b>ενεργητική φωνή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>active voice</div><b>ενεργητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενεργητική, neuter ενεργητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. active (having the power or quality of acting)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. active (given to action)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. active (quick in physical movement; of an agile and vigorous body; nimble)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. energetic (characterised by force or vigour; full of energy; lively, vigorous)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of a homosexual man) active (enjoying a role in anal sex in which he penetrates, rather than being penetrated by his partner)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (of a trade balance) positive</div>
  <div class='def'>7. active (in the active voice)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνεργητικός with semantic loan from French actif in the accounting sense. By surface analysis, ενεργώ + -τικός.</div><b>ενεργοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενεργοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. activation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. using</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ενεργοποιώ + -ση (-si). Also analyzable as ενεργ(ός), “active”) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποίηση.</div><b>ενεργοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to activate, energize, empower</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to put into action, put into use, turn on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ενεργ(ός), “active”) + -ο- (infix) + -ποιώ (“to make”). Attested in 1850, free translation or calque of English activate or French activer.</div><b>ενεργούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to defecate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (active) to act, carry out an action</div><b>ενεργούμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενεργούμενη, neuter ενεργούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>controlled, pawnlike, robotic (describing someone under the control of another)</div><b>ενεργός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενεργός or ενεργή, neuter ενεργό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. active (of a person: given to action)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: δραστήριος (drastírios), ενεργητικός (energitikós)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. active (requiring or implying action or exertion)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. active (emitting hot materials, such as lava, smoke, or steam, or producing tremors)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνεργός.</div><b>ενεργώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to act, carry out an action</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to defecate</div><b>ενεστωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενεστωτική, neuter ενεστωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>present-tense (attributive), present</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ενεστώτ(ας), “present tense”) + -ικός.</div><b>ενεστώτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ενεστώτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(present) present tense</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐνεστώς, perfect active infinitive masculine participle of ἐνίστημι, in the accusative case ενεστῶτα.</div><b>ενετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενετική, neuter ενετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. synonym of <a href="bword://βενετικός">βενετικός</a>, Venetian</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) Enetic, of or related to the ancient Eneti</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (historical) Venetic, of or related to the ancient Veneti</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek Ἐνετικός.</div><b>ενεχυρίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενεχυριάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pawn (instance of pawning)</div><b>ενεχυριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pawn (to deposit an item as security for a monetary loan)</div><b>ενεχυροδανείστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενεχυροδανειστές, masculine ενεχυροδανειστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pawnbroker</div><b>ενεχυροδανειστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενεχυροδανειστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pawnshop, pawn shop</div><b>ενεχυροδανειστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ενεχυροδανειστές, feminine ενεχυροδανείστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pawnbroker</div><b>ενηλικιώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to become an adult (18 years old)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive) to become mature</div><b>ενημέρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενημερώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. information up-to-date, briefing</div>
  <div class='ex'>H <b>ενημέρωση</b> από τα ΜΜΕ πρέπει να είναι αντικειμενική. - The information offered by the media should be objective.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the update, recording of changes</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>ενημερώσεις</b> για το λειτουργικό σύστημα του υπολογιστή σας γίνονται αυτόματα. - The updates for the operating system of your computer, are done automatically.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ενημερώνω + -ση (-si, “suffix for feminine nouns from -σις”).</div><b>ενημερωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενημερωμένη, neuter ενημερωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>well informed, up to date (with information)</div><b>ενημερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to update, fill in, keep someone posted</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to update, upgrade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ενήμερος + -ώνω.</div><b>ενθάρρυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενθαρρύνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. encouragement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incitement</div><b>ενθαλπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enthalpy</div><b>ενθαρρύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to encourage, foster</div><b>ενθουσιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ενθουσιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enthusiasm, excitement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνθουσιασμός. By surface analysis, ενθουσιάζω + -μός.</div><b>ενθρόνιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενθρόνισες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enthronement</div><b>ενθύμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενθυμήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. memory (mostly in the plural)</div>
  <div class='ex'>γλυκιά <b>ενθύμηση</b> - a sweet memory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in philological texts) a reminder note, usually as scholium, often written at the marginal of the page</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐνθύμη(σις), “consideration, esteem; anxiety”) + -ση (-si). Morphologically analysed as εν- (en-, “in”) + θύμηση</div><b>ενθύμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενθύμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>souvenir, memento, keepsake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνθύμιος.</div><b>ενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενική, neuter ενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) referring to the singular number</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ενικός</b> αριθμός - singular number</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural ενικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) singular</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μερικά ουσιαστικά έχουν μόνο <b>ενικό</b>. - Some nouns have singular only (are inflected only in the singular).</div><b>ενισχύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be strengthened</div><b>ενισχύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to strengthen, support, reinforce</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to reinforce, buttress, prop up (building)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to encourage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἐνισχύω.</div><b>εννέα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) nine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably a learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐννέα, from Proto-Hellenic *ennéwə, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁néwn̥.
Compare the inherited εννιά, Mariupol Greek энэ́я.</div><b>εννεακοσιοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εννεακοσιοστή, neuter εννεακοσιοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εννιακοσιοστός">εννιακοσιοστός</a></div><b>εννεακόσα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εννεακόσια">εννεακόσια</a></div><b>εννεακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(obsolete) see: ἐννεακόσια</div><b>εννιά</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) nine, 9</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐννέα, from Proto-Hellenic *ennéwə, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁néwn̥.</div><b>εννιακοσιοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εννιακοσιοστή, neuter εννιακοσιοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nine hundredth</div><b>εννιακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nine hundred</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐννεακόσια, neuter of the plural adjective ἐννεακόσιοι.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of εννιακόσιοι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐννεακόσια, neuter of the plural adjective ἐννεακόσιοι.</div><b>εννοείται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular present indicative of εννοούμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://εννοώ">εννοώ</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of course, it goes without saying</div><b>εννοιολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εννοιολογική, neuter εννοιολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conceptual</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from έννοι(α)) + -ο- (-o-) + -λογ(ία)) + -ικός, formed analogously to σημασιολογικός.</div><b>εννοώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to mean, signify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to understand</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to be decided, have made up one&#x27;s mind</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to be understood, of course</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἐννοέω / ἐννοῶ.</div><b>ενοίκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενοίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rent (payment for accommodation)</div><b>ενοθεϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) henotheism</div><b>ενοικίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενοικιάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>renting, hiring</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ενοικίαση</b> αυτοκινήτων - car rental</div><b>ενοικιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to rent out, to rent (as owner: to grant a lease in return for rent)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to rent (as tenant: to take a lease of premises in exchange for rent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ενοικιάζω. By surface analysis, ενοίκι(ο), “rent”) + -άζω.</div><b>ενοικιάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (masculine ενοικιαστής, feminine ενοικιάστριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hirer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tenant</div>
  <div class='def'>3. leasee</div><b>ενοικιαζόμενος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine ενοικιαζόμενη, neuter ενοικιαζόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>present participle of ενοικιάζομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://ενοικιάζω">ενοικιάζω</a>: rented, rental</div><b>ενοικιαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ενοικιαστές, feminine ενοικιάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hirer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tenant</div>
  <div class='def'>3. leasee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From verb ενοικιάζω, ενοικιασ- + -τής. Also Medieval Greek νοικιαστής, and see Ancient Greek ἐνοίκιος &amp; ἔνοικος.</div><b>ενοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unification, integration (the act of unifying; the state of being unified)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ενοποιώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>ενοποιηθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of ενοποιούμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://ενοποιώ">ενοποιώ</a>:; third-person singular dependent passive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of ενοποιούμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://ενοποιώ">ενοποιώ</a>:; passive nonfinite form</div><b>ενοποιημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ενοποιημένη, neuter ενοποιημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>integrated, merged, united</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ενοποιούμαι, passive voice of ενοποιώ (“integrate, join”).</div><b>ενοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to unify, integrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑνοποιῶ. By surface analysis, ένα + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>ενορχήστρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενορχηστρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. orchestration (arrangement of music for performance by an orchestra)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>ενορχήστρωση</b> του Μότσαρτ είναι ιδιαιτέρως καλή. - Mozart&#x27;s orchestration is particularly good.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) orchestration (control of diverse elements)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ταγματάρχης ήταν υπεύθυνος για την <b>ενορχήστρωση</b> της επίθεσης. - The major was responsible for the orchestration of the attack.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εν- (en-) + ορχήστρα, calque of French orchestration. First attested 1889.</div><b>ενορχηστρώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be orchestrated</div><b>ενορχηστρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to orchestrate (arrange music for performance by an orchestra)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έγραψα το κομμάτι για πιάνο αλλά τώρα που θα δώσω συναυλία, πρέπει να το <b>ενορχηστρώσω</b>. - I wrote the piece for piano but now that I am giving a concert, I have to orchestrate it.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to orchestrate (control diverse elements)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ταγματάρχης ήταν υπεύθυνος για την <b>ενορχήστρωση</b> της επίθεσης. - The major was responsible for the orchestration of the attack.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ορχήστρ(α), “in”) + ορχήστρα + -ώνω, calque of French orchestrer.</div><b>ενοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενοχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guilt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνοχή.</div><b>ενοχλητικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ενοχλητικός">ενοχλητικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>annoyingly, irritatingly (in a bothersome manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χτυπάει <b>ενοχλητικά</b> το τραπέζι με το κουτάλι. - She bangs the table annoyingly with a spoon.</div><b>ενοχλητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενοχλητική, neuter ενοχλητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>annoying, bothersome, irritating, pesky</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή είναι πολύ <b>ενοχλητική</b> γυναίκα. - She&#x27;s a very annoying woman.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ενοχλώ + -τικός. First attested 1889.</div><b>ενοχλητικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενοχλητικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>annoyingness, bothersomeness, peskiness</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>ενοχλητικότητά</b> του είναι γιατί δεν τον αφήνουν να παίξει με τα αλλά παιδιά. - His peskiness is why they don&#x27;t let him play with other children.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ενοχλητικός + -ότητα. First attested 1890.</div><b>ενοχλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to annoy, bother, disturb, pester, trouble</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πότε θα φύγει αυτός ο άντρας, με <b>ενοχλεί</b> παρά πολύ. - When will that man go, he&#x27;s bothering me very much.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to annoy, bother, disturb</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην ρωτάς τέτοιες ερωτήσεις, <b>ενοχλούν</b>. - Don&#x27;t ask such questions, they are bothersome.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνοχλῶ, contracted form of ἐνοχλέω.</div><b>ενρίνως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) in a nasal manner of pronunciation</div><b>ενρινότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενρινότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nasality, the characteristic of nasal voice.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa ἐρρινότης. See έρρινος.</div><b>ενσάρκωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενσαρκώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>embodiment, incarnation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek ἐνσάρκω(σις)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>ενσκήπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to occur as a threat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνσκήπτω (“hurl, fall upon”). Morphologically, from εν- (“in”) + the ancient verb σκήπτω.</div><b>ενσταλάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to pour liquid drop by drop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to instil, infuse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνσταλάζω. For sense instil, semantic loan from French instiller. Also see σταλάσσω and στάζω. Morphologically, from εν- (“in”) + σταλάζω (“drip”).</div><b>ενστερνίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to adopt, embrace (principles, ideals, values, etc)</div><b>ενσυναίσθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) empathy</div><b>ενσωματώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to incorporate, to integrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek ἐνσωματῶ + -ώνω.</div><b>ενσωματώσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενσωματώσιμη, neuter ενσωματώσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>incorporable, integrable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ενσωματώνω + -ιμος (-imos, “-able”).</div><b>εντάξει</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>OK, all right, in order</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είσαι <b>εντάξει</b>; - Are you all right?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of the Katharevousa phrase ἐν τάξει, modelled after Ancient Greek ἐν (en, “in”) τάξει, the latter a dative form of τάξις. Calque of German in Ordnung.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(used adjectivally) all right!, alright, OK, agreed!</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι πολύ <b>εντάξει</b> άνθρωπος. Δε θα σε κλέψει. - He is a very alright man. He will not steal (from) you.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of the Katharevousa phrase ἐν τάξει, modelled after Ancient Greek ἐν (en, “in”) τάξει, the latter a dative form of τάξις. Calque of German in Ordnung.</div><b>εντάσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to put in, to fit in, to include, to integrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐντάσσω. By surface analysis, εν- (en-) + τάσσω.</div><b>εντάχθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of εντάσσομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://εντάσσω">εντάσσω</a></div><b>εντάχθηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of εντάσσομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://εντάσσω">εντάσσω</a></div><b>εντατική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ICU, intensive care unit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μονάδα <b>Εντατικής</b> Θεραπείας - Intensive Care Unit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) any of ICU units</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Common abbreviated form of Μονάδα Εντατικής Θεραπείας, ΜΕΘ (METh).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://εντατικός">εντατικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Common abbreviated form of Μονάδα Εντατικής Θεραπείας, ΜΕΘ (METh).</div><b>εντατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εντατική, neuter εντατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intensive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strenuous</div><b>εντείνεται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular present indicative of εντείνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://εντείνω">εντείνω</a></div><b>εντείνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to intensify</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐντείνω.</div><b>εντελώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intensifier: all, completely, totally</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν <b>εντελώς</b> στην αγάπη. - She was completely in love.</div><b>εντελώς αβέβαιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anyone&#x27;s guess (literally, “completely uncertain”)</div><b>εντολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εντολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. order, command (instruction given to someone more junior)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. command (instruction given to computer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐντολή.</div><b>εντομοκτόνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εντομοκτόνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insecticide</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://εντομοκτόνος">εντομοκτόνος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εντομοκτόνος">εντομοκτόνος</a></div><b>εντομοκτόνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εντομοκτόνος, neuter εντομοκτόνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insecticidal, insecticide</div><b>εντοπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to detect, to spot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to locate, to pinpoint (to find out where something is located)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to localise (UK), localize (US) (to determine where something takes place or is to be found)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εν- (en-) + τόπ(ος)) + -ίζω, with semantic loan from French localiser.</div><b>εντοπίστηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of εντοπίζομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://εντοπίζω">εντοπίζω</a></div><b>εντοπίστηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of εντοπίζομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://εντοπίζω">εντοπίζω</a></div><b>εντοπισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>localisation, localization</div><b>εντούτοις</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) however, nevertheless</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προσπάθησε πολύ· <b>εντούτοις</b> δεν τα κατάφερε. - He tried hard; however, he failed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εν (en) + τούτοις from Ancient Greek οὗτος). Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ἐν τούτοις.</div><b>εντροπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εντροπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://ντροπή">ντροπή</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐντροπή, from Ancient Greek ἐντρέπω, from ἐν (en, “in, on”) + τρέπω.</div><b>εντροπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εντροπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>entropy</div><b>εντσιλάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εντσιλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enchilada</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Spanish enchilada.</div><b>εντυπωσιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to impress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εντύπωση + -άζω.</div><b>εντυπωσιακά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εντυπωσιακός">εντυπωσιακός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impressively</div><b>εντυπωσιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impressive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εντυπωσιάζω + -κός.</div><b>εντωμεταξύ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meanwhile, in the meantime</div><b>εντόλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εντολώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pinkgill, entoloma (Entoloma)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Από τα δηλητηριώδη βρίσκουμε διάφορους αμανίτες (muscaria, pantherina, phalloides) βωλίτες (rhodoxanthus, satanas), κορτινάριους (orellanus, purpureus), εντολώματα (sinuatum), γυρομίτρες (esculenta,gigas) και άλλα πολλά. - Among the poisonous ones we find various amanitas (muscaria, pantherina, phalloides), boletes (rhodoxanthus, satanas), cortinarias (orellanus, purpureus), entolomas (sinuatum), gyromitras (esculenta, gigas) and many others.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Entoloma.</div><b>εντός</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>within</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐντός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with genitive) within</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εντός</b> δέκα ημερών - within ten days</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐντός.</div><b>εντύπωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εντυπώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impression, feeling about something</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐντύπωσις.</div><b>ενυδάτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενυδατώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. moisturising (UK), moisturizing (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hydration</div><b>ενυδατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενυδατική, neuter ενυδατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hydrating</div>
  <div class='def'>2. moisturising (UK), moisturizing (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ενυδατική</b> κρέμα - moisturising cream</div><b>ενυδατώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hydrate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to moisturise (UK), moisturize (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εν- (en-) + Ancient Greek ὑδατ(ῶ), “to be wet, watery”) + -ώνω, a calque of French hydrater.</div><b>ενυδρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενυδρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>otter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐνυδρίς, of ultimately the same Proto-Indo-European origin as the synonym βίδρα.</div><b>ενυδρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενυδρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. aquarium (small, domestic tank)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aquarium (building holding many tanks)</div><b>ενωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ενωμένη, neuter ενωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>united, joined, connected</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of ενώνω.</div><b>ενωτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενωτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hyphen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter of adjective ενωτικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ενωτικός">ενωτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ενωτικός">ενωτικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter of adjective ενωτικός.</div><b>ενωτικό σημείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dash, hyphen</div><b>ενωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ενωτική, neuter ενωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uniting, unionist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἑνωτικός.</div><b>ενόσω</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>while (at the same time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Phrase from Ancient Greek ἐν ὅσῳ.</div><b>ενότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) unity, oneness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unit, section</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑνότης, equivalent to ενός + -ότητα.</div><b>ενόχλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενοχλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>discomfort, complaint, ailment (bodily or mental)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλο παραπονιέται για διάφορα <b>ενοχλήματα</b>. - He&#x27;s always complaining about various discomforts.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐνόχλημα, from ἐνοχλῶ.</div><b>ενόχληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ενοχλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. annoyance, bother, nuisance, trouble, disturbance</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συγγνώμη για την <b>ενόχληση</b>. - Sorry for the disturbance.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ailment, discomfort, complaint (bodily or mental)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλο παραπονιέται για διάφορες <b>ενοχλήσεις</b>. - He&#x27;s always complaining about various ailments.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐνόχληση, from ἐνοχλῶ.</div><b>ενόψει</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εν">εν</a> όψει</div><b>ενύπνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ενύπνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dream</div><b>ενύπνιον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://ενύπνιο">ενύπνιο</a></div><b>ενώ</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. while, as long as, as</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σφύριζε <b>ενώ</b> δούλευε. - He whistled while he worked.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. although, though, whereas, while</div>
  <div class='def'>3. despite, although</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Κώστας βαρέθηκε να περιμένει, <b>ενώ</b> ήξερε πως δεν ήταν ποτέ στην ώρα της. - Costas was bored with waiting, although he knew that she was never on time.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. since, although</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐνῶ, from Ancient Greek ἐν ᾧ.</div><b>ενώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to join, bind, unite, connect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to hyphenate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑνόω / ἑνῶ, from εἷς.</div><b>εξ</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εκ">εκ</a> (ek) (used before a vowel)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εκ δ<b>εξ</b>ιών και <b>εξ</b> ευωνύμων - on the one hand and on the other</div><b>εξάγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to export, smuggle out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to extract (a tooth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξάγω. Morphologically εξ- (ex-) + άγω.</div><b>εξάγωνο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εξάγωνος">εξάγωνος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξάγωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hexagon</div><b>εξάγωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξάγωνη, neuter εξάγωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hexagonal (having six angles)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: (having the form of a hexagon) εξαγωνικός (exagonikós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑξάγωνος. By surface analysis, εξά- + γων(ία)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>εξάδελφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξάδελφοι, feminine εξαδέλφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξάδερφος">ξάδερφος</a></div><b>εξάδερφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξάδερφοι, feminine εξαδέρφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξάδερφος">ξάδερφος</a></div><b>εξάεδρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξάεδρη, neuter εξάεδρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hexahedral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἑξάεδρος.</div><b>εξάλλου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(conjunctive) besides, in addition, moreover</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned univerbation of εξ (ex) + άλλου, a calque of French d&#x27;autre part.</div><b>εξάμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξάμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exametre</div><b>εξάμηνο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of εξάμηνος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of εξάμηνος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξάμηνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. semester (a period or term of six months)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. semester (half of a school year or academic year)</div><b>εξάντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξάντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sextant</div><b>εξάπαντος</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) absolutely, certainly, in any case, definitely</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξάπαντος.</div><b>εξάπλευρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξάπλευρη, neuter εξάπλευρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>six-sided</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἑξάπλευρος. By surface analysis, εξά- + -πλευρος (-plevros).</div><b>εξάποδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Hexapoda.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Hexapoda.</div><b>εξάρτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξαρτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. part, attachment (detachable and serving a specific purpose)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tool, implement</div><b>εξάρτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξαρτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dependence</div><b>εξάρτιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξαρτίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>equipment of a ship, (especially) rigging, sails etc</div><b>εξάρτυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξαρτύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tools of the trade, equipment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. kit, equipment</div><b>εξάσκηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξασκήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>practice (repetition of an activity to improve skill)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εξασκώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>εξάτμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξατμίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. evaporation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. exhaust, exhaust pipe</div><b>εξάχνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural εξαχνώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) sublimation</div><b>εξέγερση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξεγέρσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insurrection, uprising, rebellion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἐξέγερσις.</div><b>εξέδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξέδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. podium, dais, platform</div>
  <div class='def'>2. platform</div>
  <div class='ex'>εξέδρα πετρελαίου (oil platform)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξ (ex) + ἕδρα.</div><b>εξέλιξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξελίξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. evolution</div>
  <div class='def'>2. progress</div><b>εξέταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξετάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. examination, investigation; of plans, accounts, objects, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>2. examination, investigation; examination (of witness)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. examination, investigation; examination (of patient)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. examination, investigation; examination, exam</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἐξέτασις.</div><b>εξήγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξηγήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>explanation, interpretation, exegesis, explication</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξήγησις.</div><b>εξήντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sixty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἑξήντα, from Ancient Greek ἑξήκοντα, from Proto-Indo-European *sweḱsḱomth₂, from the earlier *swéḱs-dḱomt-h₂ (“six-ten”).
Compare Mariupol Greek кси́нда.</div><b>εξήντα εννέα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sixty-nine, 69</div><b>εξήντα οκτώ</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sixty-eight</div><b>εξής</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>following</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ακολουθήστε τις οδηγίες μου ως <b>εξής</b>… - Follow my instructions as follows…</div><b>εξίσου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the same, equal, similar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of εξ ίσου, literally “of the equal”.</div><b>εξίσωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. equalization</div>
  <div class='def'>2. equation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξί̆σωσῐς.</div><b>εξαίρεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξαιρέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. exemption</div>
  <div class='def'>2. exception</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐξαίρεσῐς with -ση (-si) ending and semantic loan from French exception.</div><b>εξαίρετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξαίρετη, neuter εξαίρετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>excellent, fine, first-class; exceptional, unusual, great</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐξαίρετος, from εξ- (ex-) + αἱρῶ + -τος (-tos).</div><b>εξαίρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be praised, emphasized</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ἐξαίρομαι</div><b>εξαίρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) to praise, raise in dignity, exalt</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην ομιλία του <b>εξήρε</b> την προσωπικότητα του απερχόμενου προέδρου. - In his speech he praised the personality of the outgoing president.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) to magnify, emphasize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξαίρω from ἐξ (ex) + ἀείρω, αἴρω. Morphologically, from εξ- + αίρω.</div><b>εξαγνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to purify (to free from guilt or sin)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξ- (ex-) + αγν(ός), “pure”) + -ίζω, a loose calque of French purifier.</div><b>εξαγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξαγωγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. export, exportation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πραγματικά πιστεύω ότι δεν μπορούμε να επιτρέψουμε την <b>εξαγωγή</b> άλλων επιβλαβών για την υγεία τσιγάρων. - I really don&#x27;t think that we can allow ourselves to export more cigarettes which are harmful to one&#x27;s health.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. extraction, influence</div>
  <div class='def'>3. extraction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξαγωγή. Morphologically εξ- (ex-) + αγωγή.</div><b>εξαγωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξαγωνική, neuter εξαγωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hexagonal</div><b>εξαγόμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξαγόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. result</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conclusion (the result of a thought process)</div><b>εξαδέλφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξαδέλφες, masculine εξάδελφος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξαδέρφη">ξαδέρφη</a></div><b>εξαδέλφια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of ξαδέλφια</div><b>εξαδέρφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξαδέρφες, masculine εξάδερφος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξαδέρφη">ξαδέρφη</a></div><b>εξαδέρφια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of ξαδέλφια</div><b>εξαδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξαδική, neuter εξαδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>senary, heximal, seximal</div><b>εξαερισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξαερισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ventilation (the replacement of stale or noxious air with fresh)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ventilation (the mechanical system used to circulate and replace air)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>εξαερισμός</b> δεν λειτουργεί. - The ventilation isn&#x27;t working.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξαερίζω + -μός.</div><b>εξαεριστήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξαεριστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extractor fan, ventilator</div><b>εξαεριωτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξαεριωτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://εξαερωτήρας">εξαερωτήρας</a></div><b>εξαερωτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξαερωτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carburettor (UK), carburetor (US)</div><b>εξαιρετικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εξαιρετικός">εξαιρετικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eminently, exceptionally, outstandingly</div><b>εξαιρετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξαιρετική, neuter εξαιρετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. excellent, fine, first-class; exceptional, unusual, great</div>
  <div class='def'>2. important, extreme</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εξαίρετος + -ικός, modeled after French exceptionnel.</div><b>εξαιρετικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eminently, exceptionally</div><b>εξαιτίας</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with genitive) because of, on account of (implies something negative)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χάσαμε την πτήση μας <b>εξαιτίας</b> σου! - We missed our flight because of you!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of εξ (ex, “from, of, by”) + αιτίας. Calque of French à cause de.</div><b>εξακολουθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to continue (to remain in a given place or condition)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to continue (to do/doing: + να +verb) (to proceed with/prolong an activity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξακολουθῶ. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + ακολουθώ.</div><b>εξακολούθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>continuance, continuation</div>
  <div class='ex'>κατ’ <b>εξακολούθηση</b> - repeatedly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξακολούθησις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>εξακοσιετηρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξακοσιετηρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sexcentenary</div><b>εξακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(noun, numeral) six hundred</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑξακόσια.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of εξακόσιοι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑξακόσια.</div><b>εξαλείφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be effaced, wiped out</div><b>εξαλείφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) efface, wipe out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξαλείφω (“plaster over; wipe out”). Morphologically, from εξ- (εκ-) (“from”) + αλείφω (“spread”).</div><b>εξαναγκάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be forced, compelled</div><b>εξαναγκάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to compel, force</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξαναγκάζω.</div><b>εξαντλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to exhaust, to deplete, to use up (to expend wholly, or until the supply comes to an end)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to exhaust (to discuss thoroughly or completely)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to exhaust (to tire out; to wear out; to cause to be without any energy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξαντλῶ, from Ancient Greek ἐξαντλέω, with semantic loan from French (s&#x27;)épuiser. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + αντλώ.</div><b>εξαπάτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deception</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξαπάτησις. By surface analysis, εξαπάτη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>εξαπατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to deceive, trick, mislead</div><b>εξαπλασιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sextupling</div><b>εξαπολύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be unleashed</div><b>εξαπολύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Ι unleash</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ἐξαπολύω or a learned variant of the inherited ξαπολάω / ξαπολώ from Byzantine Greek ξαπολῶ &lt; ἐξαπολῶ from ἐξ (ex) + the ancient ἀπολύω. Morphologically, from εκ- (εξ-) (ek- (ex-), “from”) + απο- (apo-, “from”) + λύω.</div><b>εξαργυρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cash, redeem (a cheque, a coupon, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξαργυρόω, ἐξαργυρῶ.</div><b>εξαρτημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξαρτημένη, neuter εξαρτημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dependent (relying upon)</div><b>εξαρτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be dependent upon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to owe existence to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐξαρτῶ, contracted form of ἐξαρτάω &lt; ἐξ (ex) + ἀρτάω. Different from the ancient verb ἐξαρτύω and the Hellenistic ἐξαρτίζω.</div><b>εξαρχής</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>originally, from the beginning</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εκείνος <b>εξαρχής</b> ήταν ανθρωποκτόνος και δεν μπόρεσε να σταθεί μέσα στην αλήθεια, γιατί δεν υπάρχει μέσα του τίποτα το αληθινό - He was a murderer from the beginning, and abode not in the truth, because there is no truth in him.</div><b>εξασκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to train (to teach and form someone by practice)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to train, to practise (UK), to practice (US) (to repeat an activity as a way of improving one&#x27;s skill in that activity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐξασκῶ with semantic loan from French exercer. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + ασκώ.</div><b>εξασφαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ensure, to secure, to obtain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξασφαλίζω. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + ασφαλίζω.</div><b>εξατμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to evaporate</div><b>εξατομίκευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξατομικεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>personalisation (UK), personalization (US), individualisation (UK), individualization (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξατομικεύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>εξατομικεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to individualize, to personalize (to modify something to suit an individual)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξ- (ex-) + ατομικ(ός)) + -εύω, a calque of French individualiser.</div><b>εξαφάνιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξαφανίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disappearance, evanescence, extinction, elimination</div><b>εξαφανίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to disappear, vanish</div><b>εξαφανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hide (something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to destroy, obliterate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐξαφανίζω.</div><b>εξεγείρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to excite, stir up, rouse</div><b>εξειδικεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to specialize (to narrow in scope)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξ- (ex-) + ειδικ(ός)) + -εύω, a calque of French spécialiser.</div><b>εξελίσσομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive of εξελίσσω:; to develop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive of εξελίσσω:; to evolve</div>
  <div class='def'>3. passive of εξελίσσω:; to progress</div><b>εξελικτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εξελικτικός">εξελικτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evolutionarily (in an evolutionary manner)</div><b>εξελικτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξελικτική, neuter εξελικτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evolutionary</div><b>εξελληνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to Hellenize, to Greekify (to make Greek)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξελληνίζω. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + ελληνίζω.</div><b>εξεμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to vomit, throw up</div><b>εξεπίτηδες</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επίτηδες">επίτηδες</a></div><b>εξερευνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εξερευνώ">εξερευνώ</a></div><b>εξερευνητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine εξερευνήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>explorer (a person who by means of travel (notably an expedition) searches out new information)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the εξερευνη- stem of εξερευνώ + -τής.</div><b>εξερευνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to explore, investigate</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εξερευνώ</b> όλες τις δυνατότητες. - I explore all the possibilities.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐξερευνῶ &lt; ἐξ (ex) + ἐρευνῶ. A semantic loan from French explorer or from English explore. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-, “from”) + ερευνώ.</div><b>εξετάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be examined, tested</div><b>εξετάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to examine, investigate, question</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐξετάζω, which is composed of ἐξ- (ex-, “from”) + ἐτάζω.</div><b>εξετάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξετάστριες, masculine εξεταστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>examiner</div><b>εξεταστέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξεταστέα, neuter εξεταστέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>examinable</div><b>εξεταστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξεταστές, feminine εξετάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>examiner</div><b>εξευτελίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to debase, to degrade (to lower in quality or value)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to abase, to demean (to lower, as in condition in life, office, rank, etc., so as to cause pain or hurt feelings; to degrade, to depress, to humble, to humiliate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξευτελίζω. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + ευτελίζω.</div><b>εξηγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to clarify, explain, illustrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐξηγῶ, from Ancient Greek ἐξηγέομαι, cognate with English exegesis.</div><b>εξηκοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξηκοστή, neuter εξηκοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sixtieth (the ordinal form of the number sixty)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑξηκοστός.</div><b>εξημέρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξημερώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>domestication, taming</div><b>εξημερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to tame, domesticate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to civilise</div><b>εξηνταδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξηνταδική, neuter εξηνταδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sexagesimal</div><b>εξηρτημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξηρτημένη, neuter εξηρτημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εξαρτημένος">εξαρτημένος</a></div><b>εξισορροπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to balance (to bring to equipoise or equilibrium)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to balance out (to counteract with a contrasting element so as to achieve balance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξ- (ex-) + ισορροπώ.</div><b>εξιστορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to recount, to relate (to tell in a descriptive way)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξιστορῶ (meaning &#x27;to investigate&#x27; in Ancient Greek). By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + ιστορώ.</div><b>εξισωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξισωτική, neuter εξισωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. equalizing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. countervailing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. egalitarian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξισώ(νω)) + -τικός.</div><b>εξισώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to equalize (to make equal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be equalized, to be equal, to equal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to equate [with <a href="bword://με">με</a> (me, + accusative) ‘with’] (to consider equal or equivalent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek ἐξισ(ῶ)) + -ώνω.</div><b>εξοικειώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to become familiarized, to become acquainted</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to familiarize oneself, to acquaint oneself</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to get used to</div><b>εξοικειώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to familiarize, to acquaint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξοικειῶ with -ώνω suffix and semantic loan from French familiariser.</div><b>εξολοθρευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξολοθρευτές, feminine εξολοθρεύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exterminator, terminator</div><b>εξολοθρεύτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξολοθρεύτριες, masculine εξολοθρευτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exterminator, terminator</div><b>εξολοθρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to exterminate</div><b>εξολόθρευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξολοθρεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extermination</div><b>εξομοιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to equate</div><b>εξομολογήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξομολογήτριες, masculine εξομολογητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confessor</div><b>εξομολογητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξομολογητές, feminine εξομολογήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confessor</div><b>εξομολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to confess (to hear or receive a confession of sins from)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (familiar, transitive) to try to obtain a confession from</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξομολογῶ, from Ancient Greek ἐξομολογέω. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + ομολογώ.</div><b>εξομολόγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξομολογήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confession</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξομολόγησις.</div><b>εξοντωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξοντωτική, neuter εξοντωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. destructive, exterminating, cut-throat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. exhausting</div><b>εξοπλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to supply, equip, kit out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to arm</div><b>εξοπλισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine εξοπλισμένη, neuter εξοπλισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>equipped with arms</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of εξοπλίζομαι, passive voice of εξοπλίζω.</div><b>εξοπλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξοπλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. equipment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. equipment, arms</div>
  <div class='def'>3. arming</div><b>εξορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the state of exile or banishment</div><b>εξορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to exile, banish</div><b>εξοργίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to enrage, to incense, to infuriate, to exasperate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐξοργίζω.</div><b>εξοργισμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine εξοργισμένη, neuter εξοργισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>passive perfect participle of εξοργίζομαι and <a href="bword://εξοργίζω">εξοργίζω</a>: enraged, furious, incensed, infuriated, outraged</div><b>εξορκισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξορκισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exorcism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξορκισμός.</div><b>εξουδετερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to neutralize (to make even, inactive or ineffective)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to neutralize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξ- (ex-) + ουδέτερ(ος), “neutral”) + -ώνω, a calque of French neutraliser.</div><b>εξουθενώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to exhaust, to wear out, to take it out of (to cause or contribute to another&#x27;s exhaustion, fatigue, or weariness; to sap the strength or vitality of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξουθενῶ, contracted form of ἐξουθενέω, alternative form of ἐξουδενόω, from οὐδέν. Compare Byzantine Greek εξουθενώ. With -ώνω suffix.</div><b>εξουσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξουσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>authority, power (the enforcement of rules etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐξουσία.</div><b>εξουσιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dominate, to rule (to govern or control by superior authority or power)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐξουσιάζω.</div><b>εξουσιοδοτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to authorize, to empower</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξουσί(α)) + -ο- (-o-) + -δοτώ, a loose calque of French autoriser.</div><b>εξουσιοδότηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξουσιοδοτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>authorisation (UK), authorization (US)</div><b>εξοφλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pay, pay off, repay</div><b>εξοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξοχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. protrusion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. countryside</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξοχή.</div><b>εξοχικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξοχικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>holiday home (a second home probably in the countryside)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://εξοχικός">εξοχικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εξοχικός">εξοχικός</a></div><b>εξοχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξοχική, neuter εξοχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rural, country, in the countryside</div>
  <div class='def'>2. en papillote, cooked in foil, paper, etc</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Morphologically εξοχή + -ικός.</div><b>εξού</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(followed by <a href="bword://και">και</a> (kai) ) hence, hence why</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορώ να κολυμπήσω, <b>εξού</b> και ο φόβος της θάλασσας. - I can&#x27;t swim, hence the fear of the sea.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξ οὓ.</div><b>εξπρές</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) express</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French express, from English express, from French exprès.</div><b>εξτρά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://έξτρα">έξτρα</a> (extra)</div><b>εξυπακούεται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be implied, to be understood</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> third-person singular present of εξυπακούομαι a verb which is in use only as impersonal in 3rd persons. From εξ- (“in”) + υπακούω (“obey”). A calque of French être sous-entendu. The Hellenistic Koine verbal adjective ἐξυπᾰκουστέον had the sense &quot;must be understood, must understand a word&quot;.</div><b>εξυπακούομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be implied, to be understood</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See εξυπακούεται.</div><b>εξυπηρέτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξυπηρετήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. service</div>
  <div class='def'>2. service, favour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐξυπηρέτησις.</div><b>εξυπηρετητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξυπηρετητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. server (hardware or software)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. serviceman</div><b>εξυπηρετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξυπηρετική, neuter εξυπηρετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>helpful, willing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξυπηρετ(ώ)) + -ικός.</div><b>εξυπηρετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to provide service</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to arrange, fit into</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to arrange (affairs, responsibilities)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐξυπηρετῶ. By surface analysis, εξ- (ex-) + υπηρετώ.</div><b>εξυπνάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξυπνάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intelligence, wit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. smartness (with words)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έξυπνος.</div><b>εξυπνοπούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξυπνοπούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smartypants</div>
  <div class='ex'>see: έξυπνος (éxypnos, “intelligent”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έξυπνος, εξυπνο- + πουλί.</div><b>εξυψώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to elevate, raise up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to honour, exalt</div><b>εξωγήινη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξωγήινες, masculine εξωγήινος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alien, extraterrestrial being</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://εξωγήινος">εξωγήινος</a></div><b>εξωγήινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξωγήινη, neuter εξωγήινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extraterrestrial, alien</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έξω + γήινος, calque of English extraterrestrial.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξωγήινοι, feminine εξωγήινη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alien, extraterrestrial being</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έξω + γήινος, calque of English extraterrestrial.</div><b>εξωγήϊνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξωγήϊνη, neuter εξωγήϊνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εξωγήινος">εξωγήινος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξωγήϊνοι, feminine εξωγήϊνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εξωγήινος">εξωγήινος</a></div><b>εξωμήτρια κύηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εξωμήτριος">εξωμήτριος</a> <a href="bword://κύηση">κύηση</a></div><b>εξωμήτριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξωμήτριος or εξωμήτρια, neuter εξωμήτριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extrauterine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έξω + μητέρα + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>εξωμήτριος κύηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ectopic pregnancy</div><b>εξωμότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξωμότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. apostate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. forswearer</div>
  <div class='def'>3. renegade</div><b>εξωπραγματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξωπραγματική, neuter εξωπραγματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unrealistic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξω- (exo-) + πραγματικός, a calque of English unrealistic.</div><b>εξωσυζυγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξωσυζυγική, neuter εξωσυζυγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extramarital, extraconjugal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξω- (exo-) + συζυγικός, a calque of French extraconjugal.</div><b>εξωτερικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εξωτερικός">εξωτερικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>externally, outwardly, on the outside</div><b>εξωτερικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξωτερικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (singular only) overseas</div>
  <div class='def'>2. exterior</div>
  <div class='def'>3. outside, external (filming)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://εξωτερικός">εξωτερικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εξωτερικός">εξωτερικός</a></div><b>εξωτερικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξωτερική, neuter εξωτερικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. outer, external</div>
  <div class='def'>2. foreign</div>
  <div class='def'>3. outpatient</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξωτερικός.</div><b>εξωτερικός ασθενής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>outpatient</div><b>εξωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξωτική, neuter εξωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exotic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξωτικός.</div><b>εξωφρενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξωφρενική, neuter εξωφρενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. preposterous (absurd, or contrary to common sense)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. extravagant, outrageous (transgressing reasonable limits)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek ἔξω φρενῶν + -ικός.</div><b>εξόν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) except</div><b>εξόριστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξόριστες, masculine εξόριστος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exile, an exiled person</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://εξόριστος">εξόριστος</a></div><b>εξόριστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξόριστη, neuter εξόριστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exiled</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξόριστοι, feminine εξόριστη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exile, an exiled person</div><b>εξόστωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξοστώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exostosis</div><b>εξόχως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eminently, extremely, to a great degree</div><b>εξώγαμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εξώγαμη, neuter εξώγαμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of a child) born out of wedlock, illegitimate (born to unmarried parents)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) synonym of <a href="bword://εξωσυζυγικός">εξωσυζυγικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εξω- (exo-) + γάμ(ος), “marriage”) + -ος (-os), a calque of French extraconjugal. A false friend of English exogamous.</div><b>εξώπορτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εξώπορτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>front door</div><b>εξώστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εξώστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. balcony of a building (especially for public speeches)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. balcony</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) people in these seats</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ἐξώστης, from Koine Greek ἐξώστης, from Ancient Greek sense &quot;one who drives out&quot;. From stem ἐξωσ- of the ancient verb ἐξωθέω, ἐξωθῶ.</div><b>εξώφυλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εξώφυλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cover, cover page</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shutter (of a window)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation from εξω- (exo-, “out-”) + φύλλον.</div><b>εορτάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be celebrated</div><b>εορτάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to celebrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑορτάζω, itself the denominal verb of Ancient Greek ἑορτή.</div><b>εορτή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εορτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γιορτή">γιορτή</a></div><b>εορτασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>celebration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑορτασμός.</div><b>εορταστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εορταστική, neuter εορταστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>festive, celebratory (having the atmosphere, decoration, or attitude of a festival, holiday, or celebration)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑορταστικός.</div><b>επάγγελμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επαγγέλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>profession, calling, trade, vocation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπάγγελμα, and semantic loan from French profession. By surface analysis, επ- (ep-, “upon”) + αγγέλλω + -μα (-ma, “-ing”). Calque of Latin professiō (“avowal, public declaration”).</div><b>επάνοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επάνοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. return</div>
  <div class='ex'>, γυρισμός m (gyrismós)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rhetoric) (grammar): antimetabole (figure of speech in which words of consecutive phrases are repeated in reverse order)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένα παράδειγμα <b>επανόδου</b>: «Μη ρωτάτε τι μπορεί να κάνει η χώρα σας για σας. Ρωτήστε τι μπορείτε να κάνετε εσείς για τη χώρα σας.» - An example of an antimetabole is, &quot;Ask not what your country can do for you, ask what you can do for your country.&quot;
By John F. Kennedy, 1961, inauguration speech, Greek translation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπάνοδος. By surface analysis, επαν- (epan-) + οδός.</div><b>επάνω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πάνω">πάνω</a></div><b>επάρκεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) sufficiency, adequacy (quality of enough being available)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λόγω της <b>επάρκειας</b> του φαγητού, ο λιμός δεν μας επηρέασε. - Due to the sufficiency of food, the famine did not affect us.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) competency, proficiency (ability to do something well)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επάρκεια</b> διδασκαλίας - teaching proficiency (teaching certificate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπάρκεια, from Ancient Greek ἐπαρκέω, from ἐπ’ (ep’, “on, onto”) + ἀρκέω.</div><b>επέκταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επεκτάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expansion, extension, spreading</div><b>επέμβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επεμβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intervention</div>
  <div class='def'>2. operation</div><b>επένδυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επενδύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coating, lining (e.g., clothing), background music (e.g., movie score), sheathing, cladding, investment</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ρεζέρβα βρίσκεται συνήθως στο πορτμπαγκάζ, κάτω από την <b>επένδυση</b> του πορτμπαγκάζ. - The spare tire is usually located in the trunk, under the trunk liner.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. investment</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η σταθερότητα στο μέλλον προϋποθέτει <b>επένδυση</b> στο παρόν. - Future stability requires an investment in the present.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επενδύ(ω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>επέρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be approaching/upcoming/oncoming</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπέρχομαι. Morphologically, from επ- (επι-) (“upon, above”) + έρχομαι (“come”).</div><b>επέτειος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επέτειοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anniversary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπέτειος with semantic loan from French anniversaire.</div><b>επήρεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επήρειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) influence</div><b>επί</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with genitive) on, upon</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχω τίποτα να πω <b>επί</b> του θέματος. - I have nothing to say on the matter.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. times</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπί.</div><b>επί τούτοις</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επί">επί</a> τούτω</div><b>επί τούτω</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ad hoc, for this particular purpose</div><b>επίγειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. earthly (of the Earth)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επίγειος</b> παράδεισος - earthly paradise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. earthly (located on land)</div><b>επίγνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>awareness, consciousness (the state or quality of being aware of something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: συναίσθηση f (synaísthisi)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπίγνωσις.</div><b>επίδειξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδείξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. show (of force, wealth, etc.), ostentation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. display, production (of ticket, identification, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. demonstration (of a product)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπίδειξις.</div><b>επίδεσμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επίδεσμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bandage, dressing (applied to a wound)</div><b>επίδικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επίδικη, neuter επίδικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>litigious (of or pertaining to litigation)</div><b>επίδομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιδόματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. benefit, subsidy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. extra wages</div><b>επίδομα ανεργίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδόματα ανεργίας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dole, unemployment benefit</div><b>επίδοση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδόσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. delivery, handing over</div>
  <div class='def'>2. delivery, handing over; service (the serving, or delivery, of a summons or writ)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. performance (someone&#x27;s work output subject to evaluation)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. performance (the amount of useful work accomplished estimated in terms of time needed, resources used, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> In the &#x27;delivery&#x27; sense, a learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπίδοσις, per the meaning of επιδίδω In the &#x27;performance&#x27; sense, a learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπίδοσις.</div><b>επίδραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. effect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. impact (a significant or strong influence or effect)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. influence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επιδρώ + -ση (-si). Alternatively analyzable as επί + δράση.</div><b>επίθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>attack, offensive (in warfare)</div><b>επίθετο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επίθετα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adjective</div>
  <div class='def'>2. surname</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ἐπίθετον</div><b>επίθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suffix</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπίθημα; semantic loan from Latin suffixum or French suffixe.</div><b>επίκαιρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επίκαιρη, neuter επίκαιρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. current (existing or occurring at the moment)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. topical, apropos, relevant (relating to a topic or subject of current interest)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. timely (done at the proper time or within the proper time limits)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπίκαιρος. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + καιρ(ός)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>επίκειται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive, third person) to be imminent, to be about to happen, to be on the way</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Επίκεινται</b> εκλογές. - Elections are imminent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from the 3rd singular person ἐπίκειται of Ancient Greek ἐπίκειμαι -ἐπί + κεῖμαι-, semantic loan from French est imminent. Morphologically analysed as επι- (epi-, “upon”) + κείμαι.</div><b>επίκεντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επίκεντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. epicentre</div>
  <div class='def'>2. center; focal point</div><b>επίλαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (obsolete) the rank of major in a cavalry unit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the rank of major in an armoured unit (NATO grade OF-3 in the British and US armies)</div><b>επίλογος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επίλογοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epilogue</div><b>επίμονα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>persistently, doggedly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://επίμονος">επίμονος</a></div><b>επίμονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επίμονη, neuter επίμονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insistent, persistent, pertinacious, tenacious, dogged, obstinate</div><b>επίνειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επίνεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seaport, haven</div><b>επίπεδη γεωμετρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) plane geometry</div><b>επίπεδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επίπεδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plane, level</div>
  <div class='def'>2. standard, level</div>
  <div class='ex'>διανοητικό επίπεδο (intellectual level)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπίπεδον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://επίπεδος">επίπεδος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://επίπεδος">επίπεδος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>επίπεδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επίπεδη, neuter επίπεδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flat, smooth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. plane</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπίπεδος.</div><b>επίπλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιπλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. furnishing (the act of providing with furniture)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. furnishings, furniture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επιπλώ(νω), “to furnish”) + -ση (-si). Calque of French ameublement.</div><b>επίπονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επίπονη, neuter επίπονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. arduous, laborious, strenuous, toilsome (requiring great effort, hard work)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. painstaking (of work: requiring careful and effortful attention to detail)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπίπονος. By surface analysis, επί- + πόνος.</div><b>επίπρεμνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επίπρεμνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epipremnum (Epipremnum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Epipremnum.</div><b>επίπτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιπτώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>effect, repercussion</div><b>επίρρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιρρήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adverb (lexical category)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το «βεβαίως» είναι ένα <b>επίρρημα</b>. - &quot;Βεβαίως&quot; is an adverb.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπίρρημα.</div><b>επίσημα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://επίσημος">επίσημος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. officially</div>
  <div class='def'>2. formally</div><b>επίσημος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επίσημη, neuter επίσημο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formal, official, authoritative, relating to office or position</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπίσημος.</div><b>επίσης</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>also, too, likewise</div>
  <div class='ex'>Καληνύχτα! — <b>Επίσης</b>! - Goodnight! — Likewise!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of the Ancient Greek phrase ἐπ’ ἴσης, from ἐπί and ἴσης.</div><b>επίσκεψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επισκέψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>visit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σας υπενθυμίζω την <b>επίσκεψη</b> του Δαλάι Λάμα στις 31 Μαΐου στις Βρυξέλλες. - Let me remind you of the Dalai Lama’s visit to Brussels on 31 May.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπίσκεψις.</div><b>επίσκοπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επίσκοποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bishop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπίσκοπος</div><b>επίστεγες αντηρίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of επίστεγη <a href="bword://αντηρίδα">αντηρίδα</a></div><b>επίστεγη αντηρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επίστεγες αντηρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flying buttress</div><b>επίστεγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επίστεγη, neuter επίστεγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capping, crowning, connected with rooves and roofing</div><b>επίστομα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://απίστομα">απίστομα</a></div><b>επίτευγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιτεύγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>achievement, success</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐπίτευγμα.</div><b>επίτηδες</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deliberately, on purpose, intentionally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπίτηδες.</div><b>επίτιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επίτιμη, neuter επίτιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. honorary (with no duties)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. honorary (unpaid)</div><b>επίφαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) appearance, facade, guise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) pretence</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) show</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (uncountable) simulacrum</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, uncountable) veneer</div><b>επίφυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιφύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pineal gland, epiphysis</div><b>επίφυσις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://επίφυση">επίφυση</a></div><b>επίχριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επίχρισης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uniform covering of a surface with a soft material that later hardens; also, the layer of such material</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπίχρισις, with Modern Greek nominal suffix -ση (-si).</div><b>επίχρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιχρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plaster; render, coating applied uniformly to a surface)</div>
  <div class='ex'>~ από ασβεστοκονίαμα</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coating, deposit, layer formed on a bodily surface</div>
  <div class='ex'>~ της γλώσσας</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) superficial or non-essential elements used to conceal what is essential</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κακός άνθρωπος με ένα ~ καλών τρόπων.</div><b>επαγγελματίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural επαγγελματίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. professional, member of a profession or trade</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) professional, expert</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> επάγγελμα + -ίας.</div><b>επαγγελματικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://επαγγελματικός">επαγγελματικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>professionally</div><b>επαγγελματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επαγγελματική, neuter επαγγελματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>professional</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επαγγελματ(ίας)) + -ικός.</div><b>επαγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επαγωγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. induction (thought processes leading to a conclusion)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inductance</div>
  <div class='def'>3. induction (method of proof)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. induction (something being induced)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπᾰγωγή.</div><b>επαγωγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επαγωγική, neuter επαγωγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. deductive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inductive, a posteriori</div><b>επαινετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επαινετική, neuter επαινετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. complementary, appreciative</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flattering</div><b>επαινετός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επαινετή, neuter επαινετό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>praiseworthy, laudable, commendable, rare synonym of <a href="bword://αξιέπαινος">αξιέπαινος</a></div><b>επαινούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be praised</div><b>επαινώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to praise, compliment</div><b>επακολουθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to follow in sequence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to follow as a consequence</div><b>επακόλουθο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επακόλουθα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. consequence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aftermath</div><b>επαλείφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be spread over</div><b>επαλείφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) smear over</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπαλείφω. Morphologically, from επ- (επι-) (“over, on”) + αλείφω (“spread”).</div><b>επαληθεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to check, to verify</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπαληθεύω.</div><b>επαμφοτερίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to vacillate, waver (change opinions frequently)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>επαμφοτερίζουσα</b> στάση της κυβέρνησης. - The wavering government stance.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπαμφοτερίζω, from ἐπι- (epi-, “upon, on”) + ἀμφότερος + -ίζω.</div><b>επανάληψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επαναλήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>repetition</div><b>επανάσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επαναστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>revolution</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επανάσταση</b> του 1821 - Greek War of Independence</div><b>επανέναρξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επανενάρξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reopening, restart of a procedure</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>επανέναρξη</b> της έρευνας γι’ αυτήν την υπόθεση είναι απαραίτητη. - Τhe reopening of the investigation into this case is necessary.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επαν- (επ- ανα-) (epan- (ep- ana-), “re-”) + έναρξη.</div><b>επανένωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) reunification, reunion</div>
  <div class='ex'>η γερμανική επανένωση - the German reunification</div><b>επανέρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to return, to come back</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to resume (to start again after an interruption or pause)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to revert (to return to the possession of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπανέρχομαι. By surface analysis, επαν- (epan-) + έρχομαι.</div><b>επανακάμπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, intransitive) to return</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπανακάμπτω. Morphologically, from (επι-) επ- (“upon”) + ανα- (“re-”) + κάμπτω (“bend”).</div><b>επαναλαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to repeat, do again</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπαναλαμβάνω. By surface analysis, επανα- (epana-) + λαμβάνω.</div><b>επαναπατρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επαναπατρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. repatriation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) return, homecoming</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επαναπατρίζομαι + -μός.</div><b>επαναπροσδιορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. To redetermine.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. To remodify.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επανα- (epana-, “re-, again”) + προσδιορίζω.</div><b>επαναπροωθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>To reimpel, to push forward again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, επανα- (epana-, “re-, again”) + προωθώ.</div><b>επαναστάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επαναστάτες, feminine επαναστάτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insurgent; rebel; revolutionary</div><b>επαναστάτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επαναστάτριες, masculine επαναστάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>revolutionary</div><b>επαναστατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επαναστατική, neuter επαναστατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>revolutionary</div><b>επαναστατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rebel, to revolt</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ξεσηκώνομαι (xesikónomai)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επαναστάτ(ης)) + -ώ.</div><b>επαναφέρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be reset</div><b>επαναφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to reset, restore (to a previous state)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐπαναφέρω, from ἐπανα- (“re-, again”) + φέρω (“bear, carry”).</div><b>επαναφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επαναφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reset, resetting, restoration, return (the act or process of bringing back to a previous state)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rhetoric) epanaphora, anaphora</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tempering</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπαναφορά (from Koine Greek in the rhetorical sense) and with semantic loan from French revenu (“tempering”).</div><b>επαναχρησιμοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επαναχρησιμοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reuse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επανα- (epana-) + χρησιμοποίηση or επαναχρησιμοποιώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>επαναχρησιμοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to reuse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επανα- (epana-) + χρησιμοποιώ.</div><b>επανδρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to man; to crew</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to man; to staff</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπανδρῶ, contracted form of ἐπανδρόω, with semantic loan from English man.</div><b>επανεγκατάσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επανεγκατάστασεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. resettlement, rehoming</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>επανεγκατάσταση</b> των προσφύγων - the resettlement of refugees</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reinstallation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>επανεγκατάσταση</b> των Windows γίνεται μέσα σε λίγα λεπτά. - Reinstallation of Windows is done in minutes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Morphologically from επαν- (“re-”) + εγ- (“within”) + κατα- (“against-”) + στάση (“position”).</div><b>επανειλημμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>repeatedly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω ζητήσει <b>επανειλημμένα</b> να φτιάξεις τη βρύση που στάζει. - I have asked you repeatedly to fix the dripping tap.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the passive perfect participle επανειλημμένος (See etymology) + -α (“suffix for modern adverbs”) or -ως (“suffix for ancient or formal adverbs”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://επανειλημμένος">επανειλημμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>επανειλημμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine επανειλημμένη, neuter επανειλημμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>repeated (persistently)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>επανειλημμένες</b> μου εκκλήσεις δεν εισακούστηκαν. - My repeated appeals were not heard favourably.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formal, perfect tense participle in the fashion of the ancient participles, of the verb επαναλαμβάνομαι, passive voice of modern επαναλαμβάνω or ancient ἐπαναλαμβάνω. Morphologically from επανα- (“again”) + ancient εἰλημμένος or modern ειλημμένος (“who has been taken”). For spelling discussion see Etymology of ειλημμένος. Possibly, also a semantic loan of the English repeated.</div><b>επανειλημμένως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επανειλημμένα">επανειλημμένα</a> repeatedly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κύριε πρόεδρε, έχω ζητήσεi το λόγο <b>επανειλημμένως</b> επί προσωπικού. - Mr Speaker/President, I have repeatedly requested to speak on a personal matter.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the passive perfect participle επανειλημμένος (See etymology) + -ως (“suffix for ancient or formal adverbs”).</div><b>επανεκκίνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επανεκκινήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. restart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reboot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επαν- (epan-) + εκκίνηση.</div><b>επανιδρύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be refounded</div><b>επανιδρύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to refound, reestablish</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Επανιδρύθηκε</b> το κόμμα με νέο όνομα. - The party was refounded under a new name.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncommon) to rebuild</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επαν- (“re-”) + ιδρύω (“found”).</div><b>επανωφόρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επανωφόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πανωφόρι">πανωφόρι</a></div><b>επανόρθωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επανορθώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reparation, redressing, restoration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπανόρθω(σῐς)) + modern ending -ση. Morphologically, from επ- (“re-”) + ανόρθωση + [Term?] (“lifting up”).</div><b>επαπειλούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be threatened</div><b>επαπειλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to threaten (to use threats)</div><b>επαρκής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επαρκής, neuter επαρκές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sufficient, adequate, enough (as much as meets requirements)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχω <b>επαρκείς</b> γνώσεις να διδάξω σ’ αυτό το θέμα. - I don&#x27;t have enough knowledge to teach this subject.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. competent, able, fit (as good as necessary)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο τάδε είναι <b>επαρκής</b> ως δάσκαλος. - So-and-so is competent as a teacher.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπαρκής, from Ancient Greek ἐπαρκέω, from ἐπ’ (ep’, “on, onto”) + ἀρκέω.</div><b>επαρκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be sufficient, be enough (in quantity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be adequate, be suitable, be fit (in quality)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπαρκέω, from ἐπ’ (ep’, “on, onto”) + ἀρκέω. By surface analysis, επ- (ep-) + αρκώ.</div><b>επαρκώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sufficiently, adequately, suitably</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχτισε το σπίτι <b>επαρκώς</b> και έπεσε το ταβάνι. - He didn&#x27;t build the house adequately and the roof fell in.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπαρκώς, from ἐπ’ (ep’, “on, onto”) + ἀρκέω.</div><b>επαρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επαρχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. province</div>
  <div class='def'>2. eparchy, an administrative area in Greece; hundred.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a bishop&#x27;s province</div>
  <div class='def'>4. collectively, the provinces; any region away from the capital</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐπαρχία from Latin provincia. Also, semantic loan from French province, préfecture.</div><b>επαρχιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επαρχιακή, neuter επαρχιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. provincial (of or pertaining to a province)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. eparchial (of or pertaining to an eparchy)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare) synonym of επαρχιώτικος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επαρχί(α)) + -ακός.</div><b>επαφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επαφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. touch, contact</div>
  <div class='ex'>πρέπει τα δύο μέρη να μείνουν σε επαφή έως ότου η κόλλα στεγνώσει - both parts must maintain contact until the glue has set</div>
  <div class='def'>2. electrical contact</div><b>επγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://επισμηναγός">επισμηναγός</a></div><b>επείγων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επείγουσα, neuter επείγον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>urgent, pressing (requiring immediate attention)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επείγον</b> περιστατικό - urgent incident</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From masculine active participle of Ancient Greek ἐπείγω.</div><b>επειγόντως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>urgently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επειγοντ- (epeigont-) + -ως (-os).</div><b>επειδή</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for, because</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐπειδή.</div><b>επεισόδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επεισόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. incident, event</div>
  <div class='def'>2. episode</div>
  <div class='def'>3. scene</div>
  <div class='def'>4. attack, episode</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπεισόδιον.</div><b>επεκταθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular dependent of επεκτείνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://επεκτείνω">επεκτείνω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive nonfinite form of επεκτείνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://επεκτείνω">επεκτείνω</a></div><b>επεκτατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επεκτατική, neuter επεκτατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. expansionary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. expansionist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπεκτατικός with semantic loan from French expansionniste.</div><b>επεκτείνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to expand</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to extend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπεκτείνω.</div><b>επεμβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to interfere, intervene</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπεμβαίνω. By surface analysis, (επί) επ- ep-) + (εν) εμ- ((en) em-) + βαίνω.</div><b>επεμβατισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επεμβατισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interventionism</div><b>επενδυτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επενδυτές, feminine επενδύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>investor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επενδύ(ω)) + -τής.</div><b>επενδυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επενδυτική, neuter επενδυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (relational) investment (attributive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare, relational) coating (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επενδυτ(ής)) + -ικός.</div><b>επενδύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επενδύτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coat, overcoat, cloak</div><b>επενδύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to coat, to cover (with material)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to invest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπενδύω / ἐπενδύνω with semantic loan from French revêtir and investir and English invest. By surface analysis, επ- (ep-) + ενδύω.</div><b>επεξεργασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επεξεργασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. processing, treatment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. editing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. elaboration</div><b>επεξεργαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επεξεργαστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>processor</div><b>επεξεργαστής κειμένου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>word processor</div><b>επερχόμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine επερχόμενη, neuter επερχόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>approaching, oncoming, upcoming</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formal present participle of επέρχομαι, a deponent verb only in the passive voice. Morphologically, from (επι-) επ- (“on”) + ερχόμενος (“coming”).</div><b>επερώτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επερωτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interpellation</div><b>επευφημώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to cheer (to applaud or encourage with cheers or shouts)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπευφημῶ, contracted form of ἐπευφημέω.</div><b>επηρεάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to influence, persuade, try to get someone to do something</div><b>επιβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to impose, force</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιβάλλω. Morphologically επι- (epi-) + βάλλω.</div><b>επιβάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιβάτες, feminine επιβάτιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>passenger (ship, railway, taxi)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιβάτης, from ἐπιβαίνω. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + -βάτης.</div><b>επιβάτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιβάτιδες, masculine επιβάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>passenger</div><b>επιβίβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιβιβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boarding (a ship, aeroplane, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάρτα <b>επιβίβασης</b> - boarding card</div><b>επιβίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιβιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>survival</div><b>επιβαρύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to add to, to aggravate (:problems)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: χειροτερεύω (cheiroterévo), επιδεινώνω (epideinóno)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to burden, to impose on, to weigh down on (to be burdensome for)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to burden, to encumber, to saddle, to weigh down</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to encumber (to burden with a legal claim or other obligation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: χρεώνω (chreóno)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to add to (:debt, price)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιβαρύνω with semantic loan from French aggraver and German belasten. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + βαρύνω.</div><b>επιβατικές αμαξοστοιχίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of επιβατική <a href="bword://αμαξοστοιχία">αμαξοστοιχία</a></div><b>επιβατική αμαξοστοιχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιβατικές αμαξοστοιχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>passenger train</div><b>επιβατικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιβατικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>car passenger</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://επιβατικός">επιβατικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://επιβατικός">επιβατικός</a></div><b>επιβατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιβατική, neuter επιβατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>passenger</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επιβατικός</b> σταθμός - passenger station</div><b>επιβεβαίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιβεβαιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confirmation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιβεβαίωσις, with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>επιβεβαιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to confirm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιβεβαιῶ with -ώνω suffix. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + βεβαιώνω.</div><b>επιβιβάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to board, go aboard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to get in a vehicle, such as a taxi</div><b>επιβιβάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ship, take on board (also for a taxi, train, plane)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιβιβάζω. By surface analysis, επι- (“on”) + βιβάζω (“cause to mount”), βιβάζω being the causative of βαίνω.</div><b>επιβιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to survive, to continue to live or exist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek *ἐπιβι(ῶ) + -ώνω, the former, of -όω conjugation, attested in the aorist form ἐπεβίων of *ἐπιβιόω &quot;to live over&quot;, from ἐπι- (epi-) + βιόω, with semantic loan from French survivre and English survive.</div><b>επιβλέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to supervise, keep an eye on, oversee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> επι- (epi-, “over”) + βλέπω. From Ancient Greek ἐπιβλέπω.</div><b>επιβλαβής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιβλαβής, neuter επιβλαβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harmful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιβλαβής.</div><b>επιβλητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιβλητική, neuter επιβλητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>awesome, grandiose, imposing, impressive, magnificent, majestic, monumental (magnificent)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εννιακόσια χιλιάδες ελαιόδεντρα, ακόμη και αιωνόβια, ξεριζώθηκαν για να ανεγερθεί αυτό το <b>επιβλητικό</b> και άχρηστο έργο. - Nine hundred thousand olive trees, some of them centuries old, have been destroyed to make this impressive but useless structure.</div><b>επιβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. imposition (the act of imposing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. enforcement (the act of enforcing)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επιβολή</b> του νόμου - law enforcement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιβολή with semantic adaptation to επιβάλλω.</div><b>επιβράδυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deceleration, slowing, slowing down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επιβραδύν(ω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>επιβραβεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to reward</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιβραβεύω. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + βραβεύω.</div><b>επιβραδύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to slow, to slow down, to decelerate (to reduce the speed of)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to slow, to delay (to keep from going quickly; to hinder the progress of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: καθυστερώ (kathysteró)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιβραδύνω with semantic loan from French ralentir.</div><b>επιγλωττίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιγλωττίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epiglottis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιγλωττίς, from ἐπί + γλωττίς.</div><b>επιγονατίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. knee pad</div>
  <div class='def'>2. kneecap, patella, rotula</div><b>επιγράμμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strikethrough (thus: struckthrough)</div><b>επιγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to entitled, bear a title</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ἐπιγράφομαι</div><b>επιγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to inscribe, engrave</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to entitle, title</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐπιγράφω, sense &quot;entitle&quot; since Hellenistic times. Morphologically, from επι- (“on”) + γράφω (“write”).</div><b>επιγραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιγραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inscription (especially carved in stone)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. signpost, notice (especially at building entrance)</div><b>επιγραφική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) epigraphy</div><b>επιγραφτήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of επιγραφτήκαν, third-person plural simple past of <a href="bword://επιγράφομαι">επιγράφομαι</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://επιγράφω">επιγράφω</a></div><b>επιδίωξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδιώξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. aim</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cause</div>
  <div class='def'>3. goal</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pursuit</div>
  <div class='def'>5. intent</div><b>επιδείνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) deterioration, worsening, exacerbation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επιδεινώνω + -ση (-si).</div><b>επιδεικνύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to exhibit, to display (to show for others to see)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to exhibit, to display (to demonstrate a quality)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to show off, to make a display of (to demonstrate (a skill, talent or property) for the purpose of bragging or personal exhibitionism)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to show off (to attract attention to for the purpose of bragging or personal exhibitionism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιδεικνύω, from Ancient Greek ἐπιδείκνυμι.</div><b>επιδεινώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to worsen, to make worse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επι- (epi-) + δειν(ός)) + -ώνω, a calque of German verschlechtern.</div><b>επιδειξίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural επιδειξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. show off, swank</div>
  <div class='def'>2. exhibitionist, flasher (one who exposes their genitalia, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επίδειξη + -ας (-as, “-er”), semantic loan from French exhibitionniste.</div><b>επιδειξιομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) exhibitionism (practice or character trait of deliberately drawing attention to oneself)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) exhibitionism (practice or character trait of deliberately displaying one&#x27;s genitals, nipples, or buttocks in public)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επίδειξη + μανία, calque of French exhibitionnisme. First attested 1894.</div><b>επιδερμίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδερμίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. epidermis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) skin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιδερμίς (ἐπί, on top of + δέρμα, skin).</div><b>επιδημία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδημίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epidemic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐπιδημία.</div><b>επιδημιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιδημιακή, neuter επιδημιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επιδημικός">επιδημικός</a></div><b>επιδημικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιδημική, neuter επιδημικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epidemic</div><b>επιδιδυμίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδιδυμίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epididymis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιδιδυμίδα, accusative of ἐπιδιδυμίς.</div><b>επιδιορθώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to repair, to mend, to fix</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιδιορθῶ, from Ancient Greek ἐπιδιορθόω, with -ώνω suffix. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + διορθώνω.</div><b>επιδιόρθωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδιορθώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>repair</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Morphologically επι- (epi-) + διόρθωση.</div><b>επιδιώκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pursue, to seek, to be after (to try to acquire or gain; to strive after; to aim at)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιδιώκω with semantic loan from French poursuivre. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + διώκω.</div><b>επιδοκιμάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to approve, endorse, commend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> επι- (epi-) + δοκιμάζω</div><b>επιδοκιμασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδοκιμασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>approval (the action or the effect of the action)</div><b>επιδρομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδρομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. raid (for attack, arrest or plunder)</div>
  <div class='ex'>αεροπορική <b>επιδρομή</b> - air raid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incursion, invasion</div><b>επιδρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to influence, alter, affect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to have an effect on, act on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιδρῶ, with semantic loan from German einwirken, auswirken. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + δρω (dro).</div><b>επιδόρπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιδόρπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dessert, confection, pudding, sweet</div><b>επιδότηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιδοτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. subsidization</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subsidy, grant (financial support or assistance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the επιδοτη- stem of επιδοτώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>επιεικής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιεικής, neuter επιεικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lenient</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιεικής.</div><b>επιζήμιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιζήμια, neuter επιζήμιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harmful, damaging, inimical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιζήμιος</div><b>επιζώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to survive (to live past a life-threatening event)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to survive, to outlive (to live longer than)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to survive, to live on (of an object or concept, to continue to exist)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιζῶ with semantic loan from French survivre and English survive. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + ζω (zo).</div><b>επιθετικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://επιθετικός">επιθετικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. aggressively, offensively (in a manner characterized by aggression or attack)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aggressively, offensively (in a manner characterized by aggression or attack); offensively (in a manner that is directed towards scoring)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. adjectivally</div><b>επιθετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιθετική, neuter επιθετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. offensive, aggressive, relating to attack</div>
  <div class='def'>2. offensive, aggressive, relating to attack; attacking, offensive (having to do with play directed at scoring)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (adjectival) adjectival</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιθετικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιθετικοί, feminine επιθετική)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. striker, forward</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aggressor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιθετικός.</div><b>επιθετικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιθετικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aggressiveness, aggression, offensiveness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επιθετικός + -ότητα.</div><b>επιθεωρητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine επιθεωρήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inspector (a person employed to inspect something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επιθεωρώ + -τής, with semantic loan from French inspecteur.</div><b>επιθεωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to inspect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιθεωρῶ with semantic loan from French inspecter. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + θεωρώ.</div><b>επιθυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιθυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. desire, yearning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wish</div><b>επιθυμητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιθυμητή, neuter επιθυμητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. desired, wanted</div>
  <div class='def'>2. desirable</div>
  <div class='def'>3. desirable; desirable (of a sexually attractive person)</div><b>επιθυμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to desire, want, wish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to long for, pine for</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπῐθῡμῶ, contracted form of ἐπῐθῡμέω.</div><b>επικίνδυνα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dangerously</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://επικίνδυνος">επικίνδυνος</a></div><b>επικίνδυνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επικίνδυνη, neuter επικίνδυνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dangerous, hazardous, perilous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἐπικίνδυνος. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + κίνδυνος.</div><b>επικαιρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επικαιρότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. current affairs, current events</div>
  <div class='def'>2. topicality</div>
  <div class='def'>3. timeliness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επίκαιρ(ος)) + -ότητα.</div><b>επικαλούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to invoke (to call to mind for some purpose)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to cite (to mention by way of explanation)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to invoke (to appeal for validation to a (notably cited) authority)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to invoke, to call on (to call upon (a person, a god) for help, assistance or guidance)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to invoke (to solicit, petition for, appeal to a favorable attitude)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to appeal to (to call upon a person or an authority to corroborate a statement, to decide a controverted question, or to vindicate one&#x27;s rights)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (rare, transitive) synonym of επονομάζομαι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπικαλοῦμαι, contracted middle/passive voice of ἐπικαλέω.</div><b>επικαλύπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be covered</div><b>επικαλύπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to cover over, overlap (of a surface, sound, subject, authority)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπικαλύπτω. By surface analysis, επι- (“over”) + καλύπτω (“cover”).</div><b>επικαρπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επικαρπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usufruct</div><b>επικείμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine επικείμενη, neuter επικείμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imminent, impending (soon to happen)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of the deponent (passive) verb in 3rd person επίκειται, as is the Ancient Greek ἐπικείμενος of Ancient Greek ἐπίκειμαι.</div><b>επικεντρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to centre (UK), to center (US) (to give (something) a central basis)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to focus (to direct attention, effort, or energy to a particular audience or task)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare, transitive) to find the centre of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επίκεντρ(ο)) + -ώνω.</div><b>επικερδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επικερδής, neuter επικερδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lucrative (producing a surplus; profitable)</div><b>επικεφαλής</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>at the head</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of επί + κεφαλής, a learned calque of French en tête.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) chief, in charge</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επικεφαλής</b> αξιωματικός - chief officer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of επί + κεφαλής, a learned calque of French en tête.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine or neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) head, chief (leader)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επικεφαλής</b> του επιτελείου - chief of staff</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of επί + κεφαλής, a learned calque of French en tête.</div><b>επικοινωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επικοινωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>communication (between two people, machines, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπικοινωνία, with semantic loan from French communication. The Ancient Greek word is from ἐπικοινωνός + -ία, from ἐπι- (epi-, “on, upon”) + κοινωνός, from κοινός.</div><b>επικοινωνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to talk, communicate, contact</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχουμε <b>επικοινωνήσει</b> με το δικηγόρο. - We have contacted the lawyer.</div><b>επικολλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to affix, to glue on, to paste, to stick on (to cause to adhere; to attach with or as if with adhesive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to paste</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επι- (epi-) + κολλώ.</div><b>επικράτεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επικράτειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. state, territory, dominion</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυστραλία, Βόρεια <b>Επικράτεια</b> - Australia, Northern Territory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. domain (the highest taxonomic rank of organisms)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantic loan from French état. From Ancient Greek ἐπικράτεια.</div><b>επικρίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to criticize (to find fault with)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπικρίνω with semantic loan from French censurer. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + κρίνω.</div><b>επικρατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to predominate, prevail</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐπικρατῶ, contracted form of ἐπικρατέω. From επι- (epi-) + κρατέω</div><b>επικροτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to praise</div><b>επικυρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to confirm, affirm, certify, validate (by an official, confirming legal validity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐπικυρ(ῶ)), contracted form of ἐπικυρόω + -ώνω.</div><b>επικόλληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επικολλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the act of pasting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επί + κολλάω.</div><b>επικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἐπικός. By surface analysis, έπος + -ικός.</div><b>επιλέγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be chosen, be selected, be picked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be nicknamed, be so-called</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Αίρυς Β’ Ταργκάρυεν, ο <b>επιλεγόμενος</b> «Παράφρων Βασιλιάς». - Aerys II Targaryen, the so-called ‘Mad King’.</div><b>επιλέγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to choose, select, pick (decide upon from a set of options)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Επέλεξε</b> το πράσινο φόρεμα να φορέσει. - She chose the green dress to wear.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, rare) to conclude (to say in conclusion)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Επιλέγοντας</b>, είπε ότι λυπόταν για ό,τι έκανε. - Concluding, he said he was sorry for what he did.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to επιλεγόμενος: eponymous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιλέγω, from ἐπι- (epi-, “on, onto”) + λέγω.</div><b>επιλαχών</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιλαχούσα, neuter επιλαχόν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>runner-up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>επιλαχών</b> υποψήφιος θα επιλεχθεί για την κενή έδρα. - The runner-up candidate will be selected for the vacant seat.</div><b>επιληπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιληπτική, neuter επιληπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epileptic</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιληπτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A person who has epilepsy, an epileptic</div><b>επιληψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) epilepsy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιληψία.</div><b>επιλογέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιλογείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. selector, chooser, switcher</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dispatcher</div><b>επιλογή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιλογές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>choice, selection, pick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιλογή.</div><b>επιλοχίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural επιλοχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>staff sergeant (the NATO military rank OR-7)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> επι- (epi-, “over”) + λοχίας. First attested 1833.</div><b>επιμέλεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιμέλειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diligence, care</div>
  <div class='def'>2. care, custody</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο δικαστήριο αναθέτει την <b>επιμέλεια</b> των ορφανών σε στενό συγγενή τους. - The court assigns custody of the orphans to close relatives.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. editing (in the context of care for the content or form of that which is edited)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος έκανε την <b>επιμέλεια</b> του βιβλίου; - Who did the book&#x27;s editing?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιμέλεια.</div><b>επιμένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to insist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to persist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιμένω. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + μένω.</div><b>επιμελής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιμελής, neuter επιμελές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>assiduous, diligent, sedulous</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: φιλόπονος (filóponos), εργατικός (ergatikós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιμελής.</div><b>επιμελητήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιμελητήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>representative association/body</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επιμελη(τής)) + -τήριο.</div><b>επιμελητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιμελητές, feminine επιμελήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. one who takes care of a thing, in an official capacity; curator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. one who takes care of a thing, in an official capacity; editor</div>
  <div class='def'>3. one who takes care of a thing, in an official capacity; caretaker, guardian</div><b>επιμονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) insistence, persistence, doggedness, tenacity, obstinacy</div><b>επιμόλυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιμολύνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cross-contamination, cross-infection, contamination, transfection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> επι- (epi-, “mutual”) + μόλυνση</div><b>επιμόνως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://επίμονα">επίμονα</a></div><b>επινεφρίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) adrenaline, epinephrine</div><b>επινοώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to invent, to devise (to design a new process or mechanism)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to invent (to create something fictional for a particular purpose)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπινοῶ, contracted form of ἐπινοέω. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + νοώ.</div><b>επινόηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επινοήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. concept, notion, invention, fabrication (technical, practical)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. invention, fabrication, fiction (of the imagination)</div><b>επιπεφυκίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιπεφυκίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conjunctivitis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> επιπεφυκώς + -ίτιδα</div><b>επιπλέον</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) further, more, extra</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ζήτησε <b>επιπλέον</b> λεφτά για να ολοκληρώσει την δουλειά. - He asked for more money to complete the job.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπιπλέον, equivalent to επί + πλέον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moreover, furthermore, in addition</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μου αρέσει και <b>επιπλέον</b> δεν σου πάει. - I don&#x27;t like it and moreover it doesn&#x27;t suit you.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπιπλέον, equivalent to επί + πλέον.</div><b>επιπλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to float</div>
  <div class='ex'>το ξύλο <b>επιπλέει</b> στο νερό - wood floats on water</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to remain in the upper strata of society</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιπλέω. Morphologically, from επι- (epi-, “above”) + πλέω.</div><b>επιπλοκή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιπλοκές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. complication</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) complication (that which complicates)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιπλοκή with semantic loan from French complications.</div><b>επιπλοποιείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιπλοποιεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>furniture workshop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from έπιπλ(ο)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιείο.</div><b>επιπλοποιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιπλοποιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cabinet maker, cabinetmaker</div><b>επιπλωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine επιπλωμένη, neuter επιπλωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>furnished</div>
  <div class='ex'>ενοικιάζεται <b>επιπλωμένο</b> διαμέρισμα - a furnished flat to rent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of επιπλώνομαι, passive voice of επιπλώνω. See έπιπλο for more.</div><b>επιπροσθέτως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>additionally, in addition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επιπρόσθετ(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>επιπόλαιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιπόλαιη, neuter επιπόλαιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. frivolous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. volatile, fickle</div><b>επιρρηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιρρηματική, neuter επιρρηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adverbial, relating to an adverb</div><b>επιρροή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. influence (the power to affect, control or manipulate something or someone)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. influence (an action exerted by a person or thing with such power on another to cause change)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ασκώ <b>επιρροή</b> σε κάποιον - to exercise/exert influence over somebody</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιρροή with semantic loan from French influence.</div><b>επισήμως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. officially</div>
  <div class='def'>2. formally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπισήμως. By surface analysis, επίσημ(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>επισείομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be brandished as a threat</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ἐπισείομαι</div><b>επισείω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to brandish as a threat, threaten</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπῐσείω (“shake against”).</div><b>επισημάνθηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of επισημαίνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://επισημαίνω">επισημαίνω</a></div><b>επισημαίνεται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular present indicative of επισημαίνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://επισημαίνω">επισημαίνω</a></div><b>επισημαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to spot (to identify)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to mark (to create an indication of (a location))</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to point out, to note (to mention indicatively)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπισημαίνω. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + σημαίνω.</div><b>επισημανθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular dependent of επισημαίνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://επισημαίνω">επισημαίνω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive nonfinite form of επισημαίνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://επισημαίνω">επισημαίνω</a></div><b>επισιτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>food supply</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπισιτισμός.</div><b>επισιτιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επισιτιστική, neuter επισιτιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>food supply, food (attributive)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επισιτιστική</b> ασφάλεια - food security</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly επισιτισ-) + -τικός.</div><b>επισκέπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επισκέπτες, feminine επισκέπτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. visitor, guest</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επισκέπτης</b> καθηγητής - visiting professor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. district nurse</div><b>επισκέπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to visit (to go and meet or see)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπισκέπτομαι (“examine”). Morphologically, from επι- (“over”) + σκέπτομαι (“think”).</div><b>επισκέπτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επισκέπτριες, masculine επισκέπτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. visitor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. district nurse, community nurse</div><b>επισκεπτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επισκεπτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. visiting hour(s)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) visitor(s) (during visitor hours)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. visiting card, card</div><b>επισκευάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to repair, refit, restore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπισκευάζω.</div><b>επισκευή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επισκευές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>repair, refit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπισκευή. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + -σκευή.</div><b>επισκοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επισκοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>episcopacy, diocese</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek ἐπισκοπή, from ἐπίσκοπος.</div><b>επισκοπεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επισκοπείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diocese, bishopric</div><b>επισκόπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επισκοπήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>overview, survey, review (a brief summary; a general view)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπισκόπησις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>επισμηναγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural επισμηναγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-3; squadron leader in the RAF</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-3; major in the USAF</div><b>επιστάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιστάτες, feminine επιστάτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. foreman, chargehand</div>
  <div class='def'>2. superintendent, caretaker, steward</div><b>επιστάτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιστάτριες, masculine επιστάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forewoman, superintendent, caretaker, steward</div><b>επιστήμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιστήμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>science</div>
  <div class='ex'>φυσικές <b>επιστήμες</b> - natural sciences</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ἐπιστήμη.</div><b>επιστήμονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural επιστήμονες, feminine επιστημόνισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scientist</div><b>επιστήμων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://επιστήμονας">επιστήμονας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπῐστήμων.</div><b>επιστημολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιστημολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epistemology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επιστήμη + -λογία.</div><b>επιστημονική φαντασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>science fiction</div><b>επιστημονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιστημονική, neuter επιστημονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scientific</div><b>επιστημοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) the status of a scientist (scientific in method &amp; knowledge)</div><b>επιστημόνισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιστημόνισσες, masculine επιστήμονας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scientist</div><b>επιστολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιστολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. missive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. letter, written communication</div>
  <div class='def'>3. epistle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek ἐπῐστολή.</div><b>επιστράτευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mobilization, placing/putting on a war footing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιστράτευσις. By surface analysis, επιστρατεύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>επιστρέφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be returned</div><b>επιστρέφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to return, come back</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to return, give back</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to pay back</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἐπιστρέφω. Morphologically from επι- (“upon”) + στρέφω (“turn”).</div><b>επιστροφέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιστροφείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epistropheus, axis (one of the cervical vertebrae)</div><b>επιστροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιστροφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. return (ticket)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω ένα εισιτήριο της <b>επιστροφής</b> στην Αθήνα. - I want a return ticket to Athens.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. return (from somewhere)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μετά την <b>επιστροφή</b> του από το Σικάγο - since his return from Chicago</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιστροφή.</div><b>επιστροφεύς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επιστροφέας">επιστροφέας</a></div><b>επιστόμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ακροστόμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mouthpiece (trumpet, clarinet, tobacco pipe, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. muzzle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bung, plug, stopper</div><b>επισυνάπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to attach, affix, append</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐπισυνάπτω. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + συνάπτω.</div><b>επισύναψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επισυνάψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>attachment (a file sent along with an email)</div><b>επιτάφιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιτάφια, neuter επιτάφιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. over or upon a tomb</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Επιτάφιος</b> Θρήνος - lamentation upon the tomb (at Easter)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (capitalized, noun from verb) Epitaphios (embroidered cloth bearing an image of the dead body of Christ)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιτάφιος.</div><b>επιτάχυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acceleration</div>
  <div class='def'>2. induction (of the birth process)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επιταχύνω + -ση (-si), a calque of French accélération. Compare Koine Greek ἐπιτάχυσις.</div><b>επιτέλους</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>at last, finally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a fusion of Ancient Greek ἐπί τέλος and διά τέλους, as a calque of French enfin.</div><b>επιτήδειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιτήδεια, neuter επιτήδειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. skilful, adroit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) cunning, shifty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιτήδειος.</div><b>επιτίθεμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to attack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιτίθεμαι.</div><b>επιταγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιταγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheque (UK), check (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>τραπεζική <b>επιταγή</b> - bank cheque</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπιταγή. For the modern sense of &quot;check&quot;, semantic loan from French mandat.</div><b>επιτακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιτακτική, neuter επιτακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imperative, authoritative, mandatory</div><b>επιτατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιτατική, neuter επιτατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intensive, intensifying (making more intense)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intensive, intensifying (making more intense); intensive (of a word: serving to give emphasis or force)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιτατικός.</div><b>επιταχυνθούν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person plural dependent of επιταχύνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://επιταχύνω">επιταχύνω</a></div><b>επιταχύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to accelerate, to speed up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to hasten (to cause some event to happen earlier than it otherwise would)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιταχύνω with semantic loan from French accélérer.</div><b>επιτηδειότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιτηδειότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skilfulness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> επιτήδειος + -ότητα.</div><b>επιτομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιτομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epitome</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιτομή.</div><b>επιτονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιτονισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intonation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επι- (epi-) + τόν(ος)) + -ισμός, a loose calque of French intonation.</div><b>επιτρέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be allowed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (impersonal) to be allowed</div><b>επιτρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to allow, permit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to enable, let</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned, from Ancient Greek ἐπιτρέπω. By surface analysis, επι- (“on or over”) + τρέπω (“turn”).</div><b>επιτραπέζια αντισφαίριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) table tennis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> επιτραπέζια + αντισφαίριση</div><b>επιτραπέζιοι υπολογιστές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://επιτραπέζιος">επιτραπέζιος</a> <a href="bword://υπολογιστής">υπολογιστής</a></div><b>επιτραπέζιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιτραπέζια, neuter επιτραπέζιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>table, tabletop (attributive) (to be placed or done on a table)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επιτραπέζιος</b> οίνος - table wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιτραπέζιος with semantic loan from French de table. By surface analysis, επι- (epi-) + τραπέζ(ι)) + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>επιτραπέζιος υπολογιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural επιτραπέζιοι υπολογιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>desktop computer</div><b>επιτραχήλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιτραχήλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epitrachelion, stole (vestment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐπιτραχήλιον.</div><b>επιτροπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιτροπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. panel, committee</div>
  <div class='def'>2. commission</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ευρωπαϊκή <b>Επιτροπή</b> - European Commission</div><b>επιτυγχάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal, intransitive) alternative form of <a href="bword://πετυχαίνω">πετυχαίνω</a>; to succeed, accomplish, achieve</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal, transitive) alternative form of <a href="bword://πετυχαίνω">πετυχαίνω</a>; to succeed in</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formally, from Ancient Greek ἐπιτυγχάνω, from ἐπί, επι- (epi-, “upon”) + τυγχάνω.</div><b>επιτυχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιτυχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>success</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιτυχία; by surface analysis, επι- (epi-, “over, above”) + τύχη + -ία.</div><b>επιτυχών</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιτυχούσα, neuter επιτυχόν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>successful</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου έδωσε τη λίστα ονομάτων των <b>επιτυχουσών</b> γυναικών. - He gave me the list of the names of the successful women.</div><b>επιτυχώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>successfully</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιτυχῶς. By surface analysis, επιτυχ(ής)) + -ώς.</div><b>επιτόκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιτόκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interest rate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπιτόκιον.</div><b>επιφάνεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιφάνειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. surface (the overside or up-side of a flat object such as a table, or of a liquid)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. surface (the outside hull of a tangible object)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) surface (outward or external appearance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιφάνεια with semantic loan from French superficie and surface.</div><b>επιφάνεια εργασίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιφάνειες εργασίας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. work surface, desktop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. desktop</div><b>επιφάνειες εργασίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://επιφάνεια">επιφάνεια</a> εργασίας</div><b>επιφανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιφανής, neuter επιφανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a person) eminent, illustrious, prominent (distinguished above others)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπιφανής.</div><b>επιφανειακά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://επιφανειακός">επιφανειακός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>superficially, on the surface</div><b>επιφανειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιφανειακή, neuter επιφανειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. surface (attributive), superficial (existing, occurring, or located on the surface)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επιφανειακή</b> πληγή - surface/superficial wound, flesh wound</div>
  <div class='def'>2. superficial, surface-level (not deep; lacking substance)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>επιφανειακή</b> γνώση - superficial knowledge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from επιφάνει(α)) + -ακός.</div><b>επιφυής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>over the top</div><b>επιφύλαξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιφυλάξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reservation (doubt, hesitation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reservation (of legal rights)</div><b>επιφώνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιφωνήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interjection, exclamation</div><b>επιχαίρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to gloat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιχαίρω. Morphologically, from επι- + χαίρω.</div><b>επιχείρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επιχειρήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>argument, reasoning (reasons in support of a position)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιχείρημα.</div><b>επιχείρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επιχειρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. enterprise, undertaking, attempt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. operation</div>
  <div class='ex'>στρατιωτική <b>επιχείρηση</b> - military operation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. business, enterprise, firm, company</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επίσης, είναι σύνηθες, κυρίως σε υποκαταστήματα που έχουν περιορισμένη ζήτηση για Ενοικίαση με Οδηγό, οι οδηγοί να στέλνονται από την Έδρα της <b>Επιχείρησης</b> στα αρχεία της οποίας βρίσκονται και τα αποδεικτικά για τις προϋποθέσεις που πρέπει να πληρούνται. - It is also common, especially in branches that have limited demand for Rental with a Driver, for drivers to be sent from the Company&#x27;s Headquarters, in whose files are also the evidence of the conditions that must be met.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιχείρησις. For sense business, semantic loan from French entreprise.</div><b>επιχειρηματίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural επιχειρηματίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>businessman, businesswoman</div><b>επιχειρηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιχειρηματική, neuter επιχειρηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enterprising, business, of or related to business</div>
  <div class='ex'>To <b>επιχειρηματικό</b> πλάνο δεν είναι απλά ένα γραπτό κείμενο. - The business plan is not just a simple text.</div><b>επιχειρησιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επιχειρησιακή, neuter επιχειρησιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>business, operational</div><b>επιχειρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to tackle or undertake (a task)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπιχειρέω / ἐπιχειρῶ.</div><b>επιχορήγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grant, subsidy, subvention (financial support)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the επιχορήγη- stem of επιχορηγώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>επιχορηγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fund, to subsidize, to sponsor</div><b>επιχρίω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to coat, cover, or apply a substance uniformly over a surface; to apply a layer (especially by plastering or rendering)</div>
  <div class='ex'>to carry out an επίχριση</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to apply a medicinal substance externally; to smear with an ointment</div>
  <div class='ex'>το φάρμακο επιχρίεται στο δέρμα - the medication is applied to the skin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ἐπιχρίω</div><b>επομένως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consequently, therefore, accordingly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πρόεδρος αρρώστησε· <b>επομένως</b>, δε θα πραγματοποιηθεί η συνεδρίαση. - The president fell ill; consequently, the meeting will not take place.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επόμενος.</div><b>εποποιία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εποποιίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rare) epic poetry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) epic (a series of events considered appropriate to an epic, especially military operations)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French épopée, from Ancient Greek ἐποποιία.</div><b>επουλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to heal (:wound)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to heal (to reconcile, as a breach or difference)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπουλῶ, contracted form of ἐπουλόω, with -ώνω suffix.</div><b>επουσιώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επουσιώδης, neuter επουσιώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inessential (not essential; not of the essence)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. immaterial, minor (lacking in importance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπουσιώδης.</div><b>εποχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εποχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. age, epoch, season</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εποχή</b> του Λίθου - Stone Age</div>
  <div class='def'>2. epoche (the suspension of judgment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐποχή.</div><b>επρόκειτο</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular imperfect of <a href="bword://πρόκειται">πρόκειται</a>:; &quot;was about, concerned&quot;</div>
  <div class='def'>2. third-person singular imperfect of <a href="bword://πρόκειται">πρόκειται</a>:; &quot;was to, should have, was going to, it was destined that&quot;</div><b>επτά</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) seven</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑπτά, from Proto-Hellenic *heptə́, from Proto-Indo-European *septḿ̥.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek эфта́.</div><b>επτά θάλασσες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The seven seas.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επτά + θάλασσες, a calque of English seven seas.</div><b>επτά θανάσιμα αμαρτήματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seven deadly sins</div><b>επτάγωνο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://επτάγωνος">επτάγωνος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επτάγωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heptagon</div><b>επτάγωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επτάγωνη, neuter επτάγωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heptagonal, septangular</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἑπτάγωνος. By surface analysis, επτά + γων(ία)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>επτάπλευρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επτάπλευρη, neuter επτάπλευρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seven-sided, septilateral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑπτάπλευρος. By surface analysis, επτά + -πλευρος (-plevros).</div><b>επτάφωτες λυχνίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://επτάφωτος">επτάφωτος</a> <a href="bword://λυχνία">λυχνία</a></div><b>επτάφωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επτάφωτη, neuter επτάφωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sevenfold light</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From επτά + φως (fos, “light”).</div><b>επτάφωτος λυχνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επτάφωτες λυχνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Judaism) menorah</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;sevenfold-light lamp&quot;.</div><b>επτάχρονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επτάχρονη, neuter επτάχρονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seven-year (seven years old; lasting seven years)</div><b>επταήμερο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of επταήμερος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seven-day period, week (any period of seven consecutive days)</div><b>επταετία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επταετίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seven-year period</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑπταετία. By surface analysis, επτά + έτ(ος), “year”) + -ία.</div><b>επτακοσιοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επτακοσιοστή, neuter επτακοσιοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seven hundredth</div><b>επτακόσα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επτακόσια">επτακόσια</a></div><b>επτακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(noun, numeral) seven hundred</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of επτακόσιοι</div><b>επωδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επωδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>incantation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπῳδή.</div><b>επωδός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επωδοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refrain, chorus</div><b>επωμίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επωμίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epaulette (an ornamentation, worn on the shoulders of a military uniform, as a sign of rank)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπωμίδα, accusative form of ἐπωμίς, with semantic loan from French épaulette.</div><b>επωμίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shoulder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐπωμίζομαι.</div><b>επωνυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural επωνυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nickname, sobriquet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. distinguishing name, trade name</div>
  <div class='def'>3. additional name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπωνυμία.</div><b>επωφελής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επωφελής, neuter επωφελές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beneficial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπωφελής.</div><b>επωφελούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to take advantage of, to avail oneself of [with <a href="bword://από">από</a> or accusative or (learnedly) genitive] (to make beneficial use of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐπωφελοῦμαι, from Ancient Greek ἐπωφελέω, with semantic loan from French profiter.</div><b>επόμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επόμενη, neuter επόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>next, following</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έπομαι, corresponding to Ancient Greek ἑπόμενος, present tense participle of ἕπομαι.</div><b>επώδυνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine επώδυνη, neuter επώδυνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>painful (causing pain or distress, either physical or mental)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐπώδυνος.</div><b>επώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural επώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>surname, family name, last name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπώνυμον, nominal neuter of Ancient Greek ἐπώνυμος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of επώνυμος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of επώνυμος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐπώνυμον, nominal neuter of Ancient Greek ἐπώνυμος.</div><b>ερέθισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερεθίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stimulus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐρέθισμα.</div><b>ερίτιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερίτιμη or ερίτιμος, neuter ερίτιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) highly esteemed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐρίτιμος.</div><b>ερίφιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερίφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kid, young goat</div><b>ερασιτέχνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ερασιτέχνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amateur, dilettante</div><b>εραστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εραστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lover</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐραστής.</div><b>εργάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to work</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εργάστηκε</b> στην τηλεόραση. - He worked in television.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐργάζομαι.</div><b>εργάσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εργάσιμη, neuter εργάσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>working (of a period of time: of or relating to employment; during which work can or must be done)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>εργάσιμη</b> ημέρα - working day, workday</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐργάσιμος.</div><b>εργάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εργάτες, feminine εργάτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. male worker, labourer, hand, servant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. capstan, windlass, winch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐργάτης.</div><b>εργάτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εργάτριες, masculine εργάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female worker, labourer, hand, servant</div><b>εργένης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εργένηδες, feminine εργένισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bachelor, unmarried man</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish اركن (ergen, “unmarried male”).</div><b>εργαζόμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εργαζόμενη, neuter εργαζόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>employed</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εργαζόμενοι, feminine εργαζόμενη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>employed person, worker</div><b>εργαλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εργαλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tool (small, usually hand-held, piece of equipment used to carry out a task)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ξυλουργικό <b>εργαλείο</b> - carpenter&#x27;s tool</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) tool (anything done to accomplish a task)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το λεξικό αυτό είναι ένα πολύτιμο <b>εργαλείο</b> για τις σχολικές εργασίες. - The dictionary is an invaluable tool for schoolwork.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐργαλεῖον.</div><b>εργαλειοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εργαλειοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. toolbox (storage case for tools)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) toolbox (set of routines)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐργαλειοθήκη. Morphologically, εργαλεί(ο) (“tool”) + -ο- + -θήκη (“case, holster”).</div><b>εργασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εργασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. job, profession, work</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα ήθελα να συγχαρώ την Susan για την εξαιρετική <b>εργασία</b> που έχει εκτελέσει. - I would like to congratulate Susan for the excellent work that she has done.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. task</div>
  <div class='def'>3. publication</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην παρούσα εργασία θα μελετηθεί η διαμόρφωση της συλλογικής μνήμης για το Ολοκαυτώματος στην πόλη του Βόλου... - In the present work we will study the formation of the collective memory for the Holocaust in the city of Volos...</div>
  <div class='def'>4. school work, task, assignment</div>
  <div class='def'>5. school work, task, assignment; ellipsis of <a href="bword://εργασία">εργασία</a> <a href="bword://για">για</a> <a href="bword://το">το</a> <a href="bword://σπίτι">σπίτι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐργασία.</div><b>εργαστήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εργαστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. workshop, laboratory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. studio, atelier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐργαστήριον.</div><b>εργαστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εργαστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. workshop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. laboratory</div>
  <div class='def'>3. studio, atelier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐργαστήριον.</div><b>εργατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εργατική, neuter εργατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hard-working</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relating to work/employment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. relating to unions or the labour movement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ἐργᾰτῐκός</div><b>εργατικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) industry, industriousness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) diligence</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) assiduity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εργατικός + -ότητα. First attested 1851.</div><b>εργοδηγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural εργοδηγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foreman, gaffer</div><b>εργοδότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εργοδότες, feminine εργοδότρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>employer</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>εργοδότης</b> είναι υποχρεωμένος να καταβάλει στους υπηκόους τρίτης χώρας όλες τις οφειλόμενες αμοιβές. - The employer is required to pay third-country nationals all outstanding wages.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐργοδότης, Semantic loan from German Arbeitgeber. By surface analysis, εργο- (ergo-, “work”) + -δότης</div><b>εργοθεραπευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εργοθεραπευτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>occupational therapist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έργο + θεραπευτής.</div><b>εργολάβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εργολάβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contractor, entrepreneur</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) lover</div>
  <div class='def'>3. macaroon</div><b>εργολήπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εργολήπτες, feminine εργολήπτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contractor</div><b>εργολήπτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εργολήπτριες, masculine εργολήπτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contractor</div><b>εργοστάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εργοστάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>factory, plant, production facility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εργο- (ergo-) + -στάσιο.</div><b>εργοτάξιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εργοτάξια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. working site, worksite</div>
  <div class='def'>2. construction site, building site</div>
  <div class='def'>3. compound (for work activity or equipment)</div><b>ερείπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερείπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ruin, dilapidated building</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) A person of advanced age, old</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐρείπιον.</div><b>ερεβίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Erebidae.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Erebidae, suffixed with -ίδες.</div><b>ερεβώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερεβώδης, neuter ερεβώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dark</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ἔρεβος.</div><b>ερεθίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to annoy, irritate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to excite</div><b>ερεικώδη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Ericales, an order of plants.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Ericales, from ερείκη + -ώδη.</div><b>ερευνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to search, investigate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ερευν(ώ)) + modern ending -άω. Learnedly from Ancient Greek ἐρευνῶ, contracted from of ἐξερευνάω. See ἔρομαι.</div><b>ερευνήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερευνήτριες, masculine ερευνητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. research worker, researcher</div>
  <div class='def'>2. searcher, detective</div><b>ερευνητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ερευνητές, feminine ερευνήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. research worker, researcher</div>
  <div class='def'>2. searcher, detective</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐρευνητής.</div><b>ερευνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ερευνάω">ερευνάω</a></div><b>ερημίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ερημίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hermit, loner, recluse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐρημίτης.</div><b>ερημιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερημιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wilderness, desolation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. solitude, isolation</div><b>ερημικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερημική, neuter ερημικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. desert</div>
  <div class='def'>2. solitary, isolated, desolate</div><b>ερημοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερημοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>desertification</div><b>ερημώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to desert, to leave deserted</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to become deserted</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to desolate, to devastate, to lay waste</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ερημώνω, from Ancient Greek ἐρημόω with -ώνω suffix.</div><b>εριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εριστική, neuter εριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grumpy, stroppy, quarrelsome, gruff, grouchy, crusty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐριστικός.</div><b>ερμάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερμάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cupboard, armoire</div><b>ερμίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ermine (the animal and its fur), stoat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French hermine.</div><b>ερμαφρόδιτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερμαφρόδιτη, neuter ερμαφρόδιτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hermaphrodite</div>
  <div class='def'>2. androgenous</div><b>ερμηνεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερμηνείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interpretation, construal, explanation (an act of interpreting or explaining something unclear)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interpretation, construal (a sense given by an interpreter; an exposition or explanation given; meaning)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rendition, interpretation (of a role or piece of music by a performer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑρμηνεία with semantic loan from French interprétation in the artistic sense.</div><b>ερμηνεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to interpret, explain, construe, translate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἑρμηνεύω.</div><b>ερμητικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hermetically</div><b>ερμητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερμητική, neuter ερμητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>airtight, hermetic</div><b>ερπετό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερπετά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reptile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑρπετόν.</div><b>ερπυστριοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερπυστριοφόρος, neuter ερπυστριοφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fitted with caterpillar track</div><b>ερπύστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερπύστριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. caterpillar track</div>
  <div class='def'>2. track or conduit (for electrical wiring)</div><b>ερρίνως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) in a nasal manner of pronunciation</div><b>ερρίνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερρινώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://ερρινοποίηση">ερρινοποίηση</a></div><b>ερρινίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to nasalize, make nasal sounds in speech</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ερρινίζει</b>, μιλάει με τη μύτη. - He/she nasalizes, he speaks through the nose.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έρρινος + -ίζω.</div><b>ερρινισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ερρινισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the nasalization, speaking or singing nasally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From verb ερρινίζω.</div><b>ερρινομελής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερρινομελής, neuter ερρινομελές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a cantor) who chants a hymn with a nasal voice</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ψάλτης ήταν <b>ερρινομελής</b> και άμουσος. - The cantor was nasalizing and unmusical.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compound from έρρινος + -μελής).</div><b>ερρινοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερρινοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nasalization, the change of the pronunciation of a phoneme, to nasal</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ερρινοποίηση</b> του [ɡ] σε [ŋɡ] - nasalization of [ɡ] to [ŋɡ]</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French nasalisation. From έρρινος + -ποίηση from -ποίησις.</div><b>ερρινότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερρινότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) nasality, the characteristic of nasal voice.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔρρινον + -της (-tis, suffix for feminine nouns).</div><b>ερυθρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rubella</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ερυθρός">ερυθρός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ερυθρός">ερυθρός</a></div><b>ερυθρά αιμοσφαίρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ερυθρό <a href="bword://αιμοσφαίριο">αιμοσφαίριο</a></div><b>ερυθρίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερυθρίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coral tree (Erythrina)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Erythrina. By surface analysis, ερυθρός + -ίνα.</div><b>ερυθραϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερυθραϊκή, neuter ερυθραϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relating to the territory of Asia Minor where the city of Erythrae was situated</div>
  <div class='ex'>older spelling: Ἐρυθραϊκός (Eruthraïkós)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relating to the suburb of &quot;New Erythraea&quot; near Athens</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Eritrean (relating to the country Eritrea or its people or language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐρυθραϊκός.</div><b>ερυθροκύανος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερυθροκύανη, neuter ερυθροκύανο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, formal, literary) purple, mauve, reddish-blue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ερυθρός + κυανός</div><b>ερυθροφύκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερυθροφύκη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>red alga</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα μεταποιημένα φύκη του γένους Εύχευμα λαμβάνονται από κατεργασία φυσικών στελεχών θαλασσίων φυκών των ειδών Eucheuma cottonii και Eucheuma spinosum της τάξης Rhodophyceae (ερυθροφύκη) με υδατικά διαλύματα αλκαλίων (ΚΟΗ) για την απομάκρυνση των ξένων προσμίξεων, ακολουθούμενη από έκπλυση με καθαρό νερό και ξήρανση, οπότε προκύπτει το τελικό προϊόν. - Processed seaweeds of the genus Eucheuma are obtained by treating natural strains of marine algae of the species Eucheuma cottonii and Eucheuma spinosum of the order Rhodophyceae (red algae) with aqueous alkali solutions (ΚΟΗ) to remove foreign impurities, followed by washing with clean water and drying, resulting in the final product.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ερυθρός + φύκος.</div><b>ερυθρό αιμοσφαίριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερυθρά αιμοσφαίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>erythrocyte, red blood cell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ερυθρό + αιμοσφαίριο</div><b>ερυθρόξυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερυθρόξυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Erythroxylum-genus plant.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Erythroxylum. By surface analysis, ερυθρός + ξύλο.</div><b>ερυθρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερυθρά or ερυθρή, neuter ερυθρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, literary) red</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐρυθρός.</div><b>ερυσίπελας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) erysipelas, St Anthony&#x27;s fire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐρυσίπελας.</div><b>ερχόμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ερχόμενη, neuter ερχόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. one who is coming</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ερχόμενος</b>, συνάντησα τον Γιάννη. - On my way here I met John.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coming, next, forthcoming</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of έρχομαι, a deponent verb only in the passive. From Ancient Greek ἐρχόμενος of verb ἔρχομαι.</div><b>ερωδιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ερωδιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the egret family</div>
  <div class='def'>2. heron</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐρῳδιός.</div><b>ερωμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερωμένες, masculine ερωμένος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lover, sweetheart, mistress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐρωμένη, feminine form of ἐρώμενος.</div><b>ερωμένος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ερωμένοι, feminine ερωμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lover, sweetheart</div><b>ερωτεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fall in love with</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐρωτεύομαι, from ἔρωτ(ας)) + -εύομαι, of active -εύω. ἐρωτ- from the accusative τὸν ἔρωτα of the Ancient Greek noun ὁ ἔρως.</div><b>ερωτημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ερωτημένη, neuter ερωτημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>asked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ερωτώμαι, passive voice of ερωτώ (“ask”).</div><b>ερωτηματικές αντωνυμίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ερωτηματική <a href="bword://αντωνυμία">αντωνυμία</a></div><b>ερωτηματική αντωνυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερωτηματικές αντωνυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(interrogative, pronoun) interrogative pronoun</div><b>ερωτηματικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερωτηματικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>question mark, represented in Greek text by the semicolon ;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ερωτηματικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ερωτηματικός">ερωτηματικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ερωτηματικός">ερωτηματικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ερωτηματικός">ερωτηματικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ερωτηματικός">ερωτηματικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ερωτηματικός.</div><b>ερωτηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερωτηματική, neuter ερωτηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interrogative, questioning</div>
  <div class='ex'>ερωτηματικές προτάσεις - interrogative sentence</div><b>ερωτηματολόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερωτηματολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>questionnaire</div><b>ερωτιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερωτιάρα, neuter ερωτιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. amorous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flirtatious</div><b>ερωτικά βοηθήματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ερωτικό <a href="bword://βοήθημα">βοήθημα</a></div><b>ερωτικά τρίγωνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ερωτικό <a href="bword://τρίγωνο">τρίγωνο</a></div><b>ερωτικό βοήθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sex aid, sex toy</div><b>ερωτικό τρίγωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερωτικά τρίγωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>love triangle</div><b>ερωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ερωτική, neuter ερωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. erotic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. amatory</div>
  <div class='def'>3. amorous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐρωτικός.</div><b>ερωτοδουλειά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερωτοδουλειές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>love affair, affair, liaison</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, έρωτας + -ο- (-o-) + δουλειά. Compare Byzantine Greek ερωτοδουλεία.</div><b>ερωτομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) erotomania</div><b>ερωτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρωτάω">ρωτάω</a>, to ask (a question)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σας <b>ερωτώ</b>, κύριοι δικαστές. - I am asking you [a question], Your Honours.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἐρωτῶ contracted form of ἐρωτάω. Also see ρωτώ.</div><b>ερώτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ερωτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>question (topic under discussion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐρώτημα. By surface analysis, ερωτώ + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ερώτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ερωτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>question (sentence asking for information)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορώ να κάνω μια <b>ερώτηση</b>; - May I ask you a question?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐρώτησις, from ἐρωτάω.</div><b>εσάς</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, strong) you (2nd person plural, accusative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal, pronoun, strong) your (2nd person plural, genitive)</div><b>εσένα</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, strong) you (2nd person singular, accusative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal, pronoun, strong) your (2nd person singular, genitive)</div><b>εσείς</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, strong) you (2nd person plural, nominative)</div><b>εσθονικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Estonian language.</div><b>εσθονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εσθονική, neuter εσθονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Estonian (related to the country, its people or language)</div><b>εσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) swarm (wasps, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, uncountable) crowd (people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑσμός.</div><b>εσπέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εσπέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evening</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑσπέρα</div><b>εσπαντρίγια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εσπαντρίγιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>espadrille</div><b>εσπεράντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Esperanto (language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Esperanto Esperanto.</div><b>εσπερίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εσπερίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soiree</div><b>εσπρέσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) espresso</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian espresso.</div><b>εστέτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) aesthete</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French esthète. Doublet of αισθητής.</div><b>εστία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εστίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fireplace; hearth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. permanent residence</div>
  <div class='def'>3. one&#x27;s homeland</div>
  <div class='def'>4. dorm (student residence)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. goal (the part of a soccer field used for scoring goals)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. focus (of light, heat, pollution etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. focal point</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη γεωμετρία, την παραβολή έχει <b>εστία</b>, όπως έλλειψη ή υπερβολή. - In geometry, the parabola has a focus point, like an ellipse or a hyperbola.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. combustible part of an appliance, or where temperature is developed</div>
  <div class='def'>9. epicenter (of an earthquake)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑστία.</div><b>εστιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to focus (to cause (rays of light, etc) to converge at a single point)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to focus (to adjust (a lens, an optical instrument) in order to position an image with respect to the focal plane)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to focus (to direct attention, effort, or energy to a particular audience or task)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εστί(α), “focus”) + -άζω, a calque of French focaliser.</div><b>εστιατόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εστιατόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>restaurant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἑστιατόριον, from Ancient Greek ἑστίᾶσις, ἑστιάτωρ, ἑστιατορ- + Ancient Greek -ιον, the former component from ἑστιάω, from ἑστία.</div><b>εστραγκόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tarragon (plant and herb)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French estragon.</div><b>εσχάτη προδοσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εσχάτες προδοσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high treason</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French haute trahison.</div><b>εσχατολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) eschatology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French eschatologie</div><b>εσωκλείομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be enclosed</div><b>εσωκλείω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to enclose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εσω- + κλείω. Calque of French inclure.</div><b>εσωτερικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εσωτερικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interior, inside</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://εσωτερικός">εσωτερικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://εσωτερικός">εσωτερικός</a></div><b>εσωτερικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εσωτερική, neuter εσωτερικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. internal, inside, inner</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inland</div>
  <div class='def'>3. domestic (not foreign)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐσωτερικός.</div><b>εσύ</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, strong) you, thou</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εσύ</b> με αγαπάς. - You love me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐσύ, from Ancient Greek σύ, from Proto-Indo-European *túh₂.</div><b>εσώκλειστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εσώκλειστη, neuter εσώκλειστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enclosed (in a mailing)</div><b>εσώρουχο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εσώρουχα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>underwear, undergarment, underclothing (generic term for anything worn under the outside clothes)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γυναίκα μου έχει μαγαζί που πουλάει γυναικεία ρούχα και <b>εσώρουχα</b>. - My wife has a shop that sells women&#x27;s clothes and underwear.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εσώ- + ρούχο. First attested 1888.</div><b>εσώψυχα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εσώψυχος">εσώψυχος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>innermost feelings/thoughts</div><b>εσώψυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εσώψυχη, neuter εσώψυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inner, innermost (relating to somebody&#x27;s private feelings, thoughts, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εσώ- + ψυχ(ή)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>ετάφην</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular simple past of θάπτομαι, the passive of θάπτω</div>
  <div class='def'>2. archaic form of <a href="bword://θάβομαι">θάβομαι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. archaic form of <a href="bword://θάβω">θάβω</a></div><b>ετήσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ετήσια, neuter ετήσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>annual, yearly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From έτος; see Ancient Greek ἐτήσιος.</div><b>εταίρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εταίροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fellow (a member of a learned or professional society)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fellowcraft (a Freemason who has completed the second degree of initiation into Freemasonry)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. partner (someone who is associated with another in a common activity or interest)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (historical) hetairos (a companion of an ancient Greek king, especially as a member of the elite companion cavalry of the Macedonian kings)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑταῖρος with semantic loan from French associé and partenaire and from English partner.</div><b>εταιρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εταιρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εταιρεία">εταιρεία</a></div><b>εταιρεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εταιρείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. association, society</div>
  <div class='def'>2. company</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αξιολόγηση διεξήχθη από την Επιτροπή υποβοηθούµενη από εξωτερική <b>εταιρεία</b> συµβούλων. - The evaluation was performed by the Commission with the assistance of an external consultancy company.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from Ancient Greek ἑταιρεία, from ἑταιρεῖος, from ἑταῖρος, from ἔτης, from Proto-Indo-European *swé (“self”).</div><b>εταιρεία περιορισμένης ευθύνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εταιρείες περιορισμένης ευθύνης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>limited liability company</div><b>εταιρείες περιορισμένης ευθύνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://εταιρεία">εταιρεία</a> περιορισμένης ευθύνης</div><b>εταιρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εταιρική, neuter εταιρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (relational) company (attributive), corporate (of or relating to a company or corporation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical, relational) of or relating to a hetairos</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (historical, relational) of or relating to a hetaera</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἑταιρικός. By surface analysis, εταίρ(ος)) + -ικός.</div><b>ετεροίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ετεροιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apophony, ablaut</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑτεροίωσις.</div><b>ετερογενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ετερογενής, neuter ετερογενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heterogeneous, unhomogeneous</div><b>ετεροφυλόφιλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ετεροφυλόφιλη, neuter ετεροφυλόφιλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of people) heterosexual, straight (attracted to the opposite sex)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ετερο- (etero-, “other, hetero-”) + φύλο + -φιλος (-filos, “-friend, -phile”), calque of English heterosexual.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ετεροφυλόφιλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heterosexual, straight (male heterosexual)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ετερο- (etero-, “other, hetero-”) + φύλο + -φιλος (-filos, “-friend, -phile”), calque of English heterosexual.</div><b>ετερόνευρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Heteroneura.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Heteroneura.</div><b>ετερόχρονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ετερόχρονη, neuter ετερόχρονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>happening at a different time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ετερο- (etero-) + χρόν(ος)) + -ος (-os). Compare Koine Greek ἑτερόχρονος.</div><b>ετοιμάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to get ready, prepare oneself</div><b>ετοιμάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to prepare</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα σας <b>ετοιμάσω</b> την πρόταση, γραπτώς και στα αγγλικά. - I&#x27;ll prepare the proposal for you, in writing and in English.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἑτοιμάζω.</div><b>ετοιμασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ετοιμασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preparation (the act of getting ready)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάναμε πολλές <b>ετοιμασίες</b> για να υποδεχθούμε και να περιποιηθούμε τους καλεσμένους μας. - We made lots of preparations to receive and entertain our guests.</div><b>ετοιματζίδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ετοιματζίδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shop selling off-the-peg, off-the-rack and ready-to-wear clothing</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ετοιματζίδικος">ετοιματζίδικος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ετοιματζίδικος">ετοιματζίδικος</a></div><b>ετοιματζίδικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ετοιματζίδικη, neuter ετοιματζίδικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ready-to-wear, off-the-peg (UK), off-the-rack (US)</div><b>ετοιμόρροπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ετοιμόρροπη, neuter ετοιμόρροπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ready to fall, crumbling, tottering</div><b>ετούτος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine (feminine ετούτη, neuter ετούτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τούτος">τούτος</a></div><b>ετρουσκικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Etruscan (the language of Etruria and its people)</div><b>ετρουσκικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ετρουσκική, neuter ετρουσκικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Etruscan (relating to Etruria or its people or language)</div><b>ετυμηγορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ετυμηγορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>verdict</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐτυμηγορία.</div><b>ετυμολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ετυμολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymology</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα παραδείγματα των <b>ετυμολογιών</b> που πρότεινε είναι τα ακόλουθα ... - Examples of the suggested etymologies are the following...</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐτυμολογία from the ancient ἔτυμ(ος), “true”) + -ο- (-o-) + -λογία, semantic loan from French étymologie or German Etymologie from Latin etymologia.</div><b>ευ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) well</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εὖ.</div><b>ευάερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευάερη, neuter ευάερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>airy, well ventilated</div><b>ευάλωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευάλωτη, neuter ευάλωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vulnerable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. assailable, pregnable</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bribable, corruptable</div><b>ευέξαπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευέξαπτη, neuter ευέξαπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quick-tempered, short-tempered, irascible, combustible</div><b>ευαίσθητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευαίσθητη, neuter ευαίσθητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sensitive, delicate, soft-hearted, touchy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sentient</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐαίσθητος; By surface analysis, ευ (ef, “good, well”) + αισθητός.</div><b>ευαγγέλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ευαγγέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the Gospels, evangel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐαγγέλιον; doublet of βαγγέλιο via Byzantine Greek βαγγέλιον.</div><b>ευαγγελίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bring good news</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐαγγελίζομαι (“bring good news”). See ευ- (“good”) and ἄγγελος.</div><b>ευαισθησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευαισθησίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sensitivity, sensitiveness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sensibility</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λογική και <b>ευαισθησία</b> - Sense and Sensibility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐαισθησία with semantic loan from French sensibilité.</div><b>ευαισθητοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) sensitisation (UK), sensitization (US)</div><b>ευαισθητοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sensitise (UK), to sensitize (US)</div><b>ευγένεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευγένειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used as a vocative honorific.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. politeness, kindness, courtesy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nobility</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tίτλοι <b>ευγενείας</b> ούτε απονέμονται ούτε αναγνωρίζονται σε Ελλάδα. - Titles of nobility are neither awarded nor recognized in Greece.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐγένεια.</div><b>ευγενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευγενής, neuter ευγενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (polite) polite</div>
  <div class='def'>2. noble, aristocratic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐγενής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ευγενείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>noble, aristocrat, peer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐγενής.</div><b>ευγενικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ευγενικός">ευγενικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gracefully, gently, courteously</div><b>ευγενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευγενική, neuter ευγενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(polite) polite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ευγενής + -κός.</div><b>ευγηρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) happiness in old age, good health and easy living in old age</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek εὐγηρία.</div><b>ευγνωμοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gratitude, thankfulness, gratefulness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek εὐγνωμοσύνη. By surface analysis, ευγνώμων + -σύνη.</div><b>ευγνώμων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευγνώμων, neuter ευγνώμον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grateful, thankful</div><b>ευδίαιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευδίαιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scuppers</div><b>ευδαιμονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευδαιμονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bliss</div><b>ευδιάκριτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευδιάκριτη, neuter ευδιάκριτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>distinguishable, discernible, distinct</div><b>ευελιξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flexibility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ευέλικ(τος), “flexible”) + -σία → -ξία.</div><b>ευεξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wellbeing, wellness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐεξία.</div><b>ευεπηρέαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>impressionable, easily influenced</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek εὐεπηρέαστος. Morphologically, from ευ- (ef-) + επηρεάζω + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ευερέθιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευερέθιστη, neuter ευερέθιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irritable (easily exasperated)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: ευέξαπτος (evéxaptos), οξύθυμος (oxýthymos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐερέθιστος. By surface analysis, ευ- (ef-) + ερεθιστ- (stem of ερεθίζω) + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ευερεθιστότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>irritability (the state or quality of being irritable)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ευερέθιστος + -ότητα.</div><b>ευημερία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευημερίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prosperity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. happiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐημερία.</div><b>ευημερώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to prosper</div><b>ευθέως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. immediately, outright, at once</div>
  <div class='def'>2. honestly, directly, straightforwardly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐθέως, adverb of εὐθῠ́ς.</div><b>ευθήρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Eutheria.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Eutheria.</div><b>ευθανασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>euthanasia, mercy killing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek εὐθανασία, from εὐ- (eu-, “good”) + θάνατος</div><b>ευθεία</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ευθύς">ευθύς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in the same direction, straight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From feminine adjective ευθεία, by ellipsis of noun οδός. Also see ευθέως.</div><b>ευθυγραμμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to align (to adjust to a line; to range or form in line; to bring into line)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to align (to organize in a consistent, defined way)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ευθύγραμμ(ος)) + -ίζω.</div><b>ευθυκρισία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural ευθυκρισίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, uncountable, usually) straight judgement, discernment (the ability of correct judgement and thought)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πολιτεύεσθαι είναι τέχνη υψηλή και απαιτεί σοφία και <b>ευθυκρισία</b>, αρετές που δεν κατέχουν όλοι οι πολίτες. - Being a politician is a high art and requires wisdom and sound judgement, virtues that not all citizens possess.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ευθύ(ς), “straight; correct”) + κρίσ(ις), “judgement”) + -ία. First attested in 1825 as εὐθυκρισία.</div><b>ευθυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ευθυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cheerfulness, joy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επικρατούσε μια γενική <b>ευθυμία</b>. Όλοι ήταν χαρούμενοι. - There was a general cheerfulness [in the air]. Everybody was happy.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cheerful mood after drinking wine or spirits</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ποτό αυτό είναι πολύ δυνατό· φέρνει <b>ευθυμία</b>. - This drink is very strong; it brings mirth (makes you jolly).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐθυμία. Morphologically ευ- (ef-, “good”) + θυμός.</div><b>ευθύγραμμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευθύγραμμη, neuter ευθύγραμμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>straight-line (attributive), rectilinear, linear</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ευθύγραμμο</b> τμήμα - line segment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐθύγραμμος.</div><b>ευθύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευθύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. liability</div>
  <div class='def'>2. responsibility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐθύνη, from Ancient Greek εὔθυνα, from εὐθύνω, with semantic loan from French responsabilité.</div><b>ευθύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευθεία, neuter ευθύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. direct, straight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) straightforward, candid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐθύς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>immediately, outright, at once</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐθύς.</div><b>ευκάλυπτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευκάλυπτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eucalyptus</div><b>ευκή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευκές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ευχή">ευχή</a></div><b>ευκίνητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευκίνητη, neuter ευκίνητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>agile, nimble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐκῑ́νητος.</div><b>ευκαιρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευκαιρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. occasion, opportunity, chance</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν είχα η <b>ευκαιρία</b> να ταξιδέψω στην Aμερική. - I didn&#x27;t have the opportunity to travel to America.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bargain sale</div>
  <div class='ex'>see τιμή ευκαιρίας (timí efkairías, “bargain price”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐκαιρία, from εὔκαιρος. See εὖ + καιρός. For sense &quot;bargain&quot;, semantic loan from French occasion.</div><b>ευκαρυωτικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eukaryotes (Eukaryota)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantic loan from translingual Eukaryota, from ευκαρυωτικός.</div><b>ευκατάστατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευκατάστατη, neuter ευκατάστατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>well off (having financial security)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐκατάστατος. By surface analysis, ευ- (ef-) + κατάστα(ση)) + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ευκλείδεια γεωμετρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural ευκλείδειες γεωμετρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) Euclidean geometry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ευκλείδειος + γεωμετρία.</div><b>ευκλείδειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευκλείδειος or ευκλείδεια, neuter ευκλείδειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Euclidean (of, pertaining to, or adhering to the principles of traditional geometry, in which parallel lines are equidistant, as described in Euclid&#x27;s Elements)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ευκλείδεια</b> γεωμετρία - Euclidean geometry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of Euclides (of or pertaining to the eponymous archon of Athens for the year running from July/August 403 BC until June/July 402 BC)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek Εὐκλείδης + -ειος (-eios), a calque of French euclidien.</div><b>ευκολάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ευκολάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, neologism) a piece of cake (of a problem, exercise, recipe)</div><b>ευκολία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευκολίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. easiness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ease, facility (ability, the means to do something; skill, talent)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ease (freedom from worry, concern, embarrassment or awkwardness)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. convenience (the quality of being convenient)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (in plural) conveniences (any object that makes life more convenient; a helpful item)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐκολία, with semantic loan from French facilité. By surface analysis, εύκολος + -ία</div><b>ευκτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευκτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>optative mood</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐκτική, by ellipsis, from the phrase εὐκτική [ἔγκλισις].</div><b>ευλογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευλογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blessing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek εὐλογία.</div><b>ευλογιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευλογιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smallpox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek εὐλογία, used as a euphemistic name for the disease.</div><b>ευλογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to bless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐλογῶ, contracted form of Ancient Greek εὐλογέω.</div><b>ευμετάζωα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Eumetazoa.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Eumetazoa.</div><b>ευνή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, rare) bed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐνή.</div><b>ευνοιοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) favouritism (unfair favouring of one person or group at the expense of another)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εύνοια + -ο- (-o-) + -κρατία. Calque of French favoritisme.</div><b>ευνουχίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to castrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek εὐνουχίζω.</div><b>ευνουχισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευνουχισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>castration, emasculation</div><b>ευνοϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευνοϊκή, neuter ευνοϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. favourable, propitious (advantageous, helpful)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. favourable (expressing or indicating favour or goodwill)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐνοϊκός. By surface analysis, ευ- (ef-) + νο(υς) (no(ys)) + -ικός</div><b>ευνούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευνούχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eunuch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐνοῦχος.</div><b>ευνοώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to prefer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to favour (British), favor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐνοῶ.</div><b>ευπατρίδης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευπατρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Eupatrid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. patrician, aristocrat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐπατρίδης m.</div><b>ευπροσάρμοστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευπροσάρμοστη, neuter ευπροσάρμοστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adaptable</div><b>ευπρόσδεκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευπρόσδεκτη, neuter ευπρόσδεκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. welcome (whose arrival is a cause of joy; received with gladness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. welcome (producing gladness)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐπρόσδεκτος.</div><b>ευρέως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. widely, broadly, extensively (in a wide manner)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. widely (commonly; generally; to a great degree)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ευρέως</b> διαδεδομένος - widespread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐρέως.</div><b>ευρίσκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρίσκω">βρίσκω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek εὑρίσκω.</div><b>ευραπηλιώτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) an east-southeasterly Mediterranean wind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εύρος + απηλιώτης</div><b>ευρασιατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευρασιατική, neuter ευρασιατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Eurasian</div><b>ευρετήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ευρετήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. index (alphabetical listing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. book with alphabetically labelled pages (address book)</div><b>ευρυμάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>breadth of learning, erudition, scholarship (broad knowledge; the quality of being broadly knowledgeable)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ευρυμαθ(ής)) + -εια (-eia), modelled on πολυμάθεια.</div><b>ευρυτίδης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευρυτίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Eurytides-genus swallowtail butterfly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Eurytides.</div><b>ευρωβουλευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ευρωβουλευτές, feminine ευρωβουλευτίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>member of the European Parliament, MEP</div><b>ευρωζώνη</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative letter-case form of <a href="bword://Ευρωζώνη">Ευρωζώνη</a></div><b>ευρωπαϊκά ψαρόνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of ευρωπαϊκό <a href="bword://ψαρόνι">ψαρόνι</a></div><b>ευρωπαϊκές αλκυόνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of ευρωπαϊκή <a href="bword://αλκυόνη">αλκυόνη</a></div><b>ευρωπαϊκές χαλκοκουρούνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of ευρωπαϊκή <a href="bword://χαλκοκουρούνα">χαλκοκουρούνα</a></div><b>ευρωπαϊκή αβοκέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευρωπαϊκές αβοκέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pied avocet (Recurvirostra avosetta)</div><b>ευρωπαϊκή αλκυόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευρωπαϊκές αλκυόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alcedo atthis : kingfisher, common kingfisher</div><b>ευρωπαϊκή καλημάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευρωπαϊκές καλημάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>northern lapwing (Vanellus vanellus)</div><b>ευρωπαϊκή χαλκοκουρούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευρωπαϊκές χαλκοκουρούνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Coracias garrulus: the roller, the European roller, the roller</div><b>ευρωπαϊκοί κορμοράνοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://ευρωπαϊκός">ευρωπαϊκός</a> <a href="bword://κορμοράνος">κορμοράνος</a></div><b>ευρωπαϊκοί μελισσοφάγοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://ευρωπαϊκός">ευρωπαϊκός</a> <a href="bword://μελισσοφάγος">μελισσοφάγος</a></div><b>ευρωπαϊκό ψαρόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ευρωπαϊκά ψαρόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>European starling, common starling, Sturnus vulgaris</div><b>ευρωπαϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευρωπαϊκή, neuter ευρωπαϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>European (pertaining to Europe, its people or the European Union)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ευρωπαϊκή</b> Ένωση - European Union</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ευρωπαίος + -ικός.</div><b>ευρωπαϊκός κορμοράνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευρωπαϊκοί κορμοράνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cormorant (Phalacrocorax carbo including subspecies)</div><b>ευρωπαϊκός μελισσοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευρωπαϊκοί μελισσοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Merops apiaster, the European bee-eater</div><b>ευρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://ευρώ">ευρώ</a></div><b>ευρύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευρεία, neuter ευρύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐρύς.</div><b>ευρύχωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευρύχωρη, neuter ευρύχωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spacious, roomy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. capacious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐρύχωρος.</div><b>ευρώ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) euro (the currency)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Spelling is from Ευρώπη, from Ancient Greek Εὐρώπη, analogously to its equivalents in the other languages of the Union.</div><b>ευρώπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) europium</div><b>ευσέβαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευσέβαστος, neuter ευσέβαστον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, dated) extremely respected</div>
  <div class='ex'>in polytonic script: εὐσέβαστος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εὖ (ev́, “well”) + σεβαστός. Consturcted in analogy to αξιοσέβαστος.</div><b>ευσέβεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευσέβειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>piety, devoutness</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι μοναχοί διακρίνονται για την <b>ευσέβειά</b> τους και τη βαθιά τους πίστη. - Monks are distinguished for their piety and their deep faith.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek εὐσέβεια, from εὐσεβής.</div><b>ευσεβάστως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, dated) with utmost respect, extremely respectfully (dated valediction, close in formal documents)</div>
  <div class='ex'>in polytonic script: εὐσεβάστως</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French respectueusement, attested 1846. From adjective ευσέβαστος. Not to be confused with the Ancient Greek adverb εὐσεβέως and εὐσεβῶς. See adjective εὐσεβής.</div><b>ευσεβής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευσεβής, neuter ευσεβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pious, devout</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *h₁wes-
Proto-Indo-European *h₁wésus?
Proto-Indo-European *h₁es-?
Proto-Indo-European *h₁su-
Proto-Hellenic *ehu-
Ancient Greek ἐΰς
Ancient Greek εὖ
Ancient Greek εὐ- (eu-)
Proto-Indo-European *tyegʷ-der.
Proto-Indo-European *tyégʷ-e-tor
Proto-Hellenic *tségʷomai
Ancient Greek σέβομαι
Ancient Greek -ής
Ancient Greek εὐσεβής
Greek ευσεβής
Inherited from Ancient Greek εὐσεβής, from εὐ- (eu-, “well”) + σέβομαι + -ής.</div><b>ευσεβώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>piously, devoutly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐσεβέως and Attic Greek εὐσεβῶς, adverbs from adjective εὐσεβής.</div><b>ευσπλαχνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compassion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐσπλαγχνία. By surface analysis, εύσπλαχν(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>ευστοχώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, literally) to hit the target</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive) to hit the mark, to succeed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐστοχῶ, from Ancient Greek εὐστοχέω.</div><b>ευσυνείδητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευσυνείδητη, neuter ευσυνείδητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conscientious (thorough, careful, or vigilant in performance of one’s task)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐσυνείδητος with semantic loan from French consciencieux. By surface analysis, ευ- (ef-) + συνείδη(ση)) + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ευτηκτοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευτηκτοειδής, neuter ευτηκτοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eutectoid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English eutectoid.</div><b>ευτοπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευτοπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) eutopia</div><b>ευτυχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) happiness, contentment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εὐτυχία f.</div><b>ευτυχισμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευτυχισμένη, neuter ευτυχισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>happy (characterised by, feeling or showing happiness)</div><b>ευτυχισμένος ο καινούριος χρόνος</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Happy New Year</div><b>ευτυχώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fortunately, luckily</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐτυχῶς, from εὐτυχής + -ως.</div><b>ευτύχημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ευτυχήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fortunate occurrence, good thing, piece of good luck, stroke of luck</div>
  <div class='ex'>είναι <b>ευτύχημα</b> ότι/που […] - it&#x27;s fortunate/a good thing that […]</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐτύχημα. By surface analysis, ευτυχώ + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ευυπόληπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευυπόληπτη, neuter ευυπόληπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. respectable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reputable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ευ- (ef-) + υπολήπ(τομαι)) + -τος (-tos). Compare Ancient Greek εὐϋπόληπτος.</div><b>ευφημισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ευφημισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>euphemism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐφημισμός.</div><b>ευφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) euphoria</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) fertility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εὐφορία.</div><b>ευφορβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευφορβίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>euphorbia (Euphorbia)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Euphorbia.</div><b>ευφραδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευφραδής, neuter ευφραδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fluent, eloquent</div><b>ευφυΐα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) intelligence, cleverness (mental quickness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) intelligence (capacity of mind to understand and learn)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (countable) intellect (intelligent person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐφυΐα with semantic loan from French intelligence.</div><b>ευφυής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευφυής, neuter ευφυές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intelligent, clever, quick-witted</div>
  <div class='ex'>see: έξυπνος (éxypnos, “intelligent”)</div><b>ευφωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) euphony</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐφωνίᾱ. By surface analysis, ευ- (ef-) + φωνή + -ία.</div><b>ευφωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευφωνική, neuter ευφωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>euphonic</div><b>ευχάριστα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ευχάριστος">ευχάριστος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pleasantly, pleasingly, agreeably</div><b>ευχάριστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευχάριστη, neuter ευχάριστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pleasant, pleasing, agreeable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὐχάριστος, with semantic loan from French agréable.</div><b>ευχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. religious blessing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὐχή.</div><b>ευχαρίστηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευχαριστήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pleasure</div><b>ευχαρίστως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gladly, with pleasure (willingly)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὐχαρίστως, with semantic loan from French avec plaisir. By surface analysis, ευχάριστος + -ως (-os).</div><b>ευχαριστία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευχαριστίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thanks</div><b>ευχαριστημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ευχαριστημένη, neuter ευχαριστημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pleased (chiefly used adjectively)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ευχαριστημένος</b> με την απόδοση στο χρηματιστήριο, αγόρασε όλες τις μετοχές και καταστράφηκε. - Being pleased with the performance of the stockmarket, he bought all the shares and was ruined.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ευχαριστιέμαι and ευχαριστούμαι, passive voices of ευχαριστώ (“thank; I please”).</div><b>ευχαριστιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be pleased</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to enjoy, take pleasure</div><b>ευχαριστούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ευχαριστιέμαι">ευχαριστιέμαι</a></div><b>ευχαριστούμε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>we thank you!, thanks! (first-person plural)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person plural present active indicative of <a href="bword://ευχαριστώ">ευχαριστώ</a></div><b>ευχαριστώ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thank you!, thanks! (first-person singular of verb)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek εὐχαριστῶ, contracted form of εὐχαριστέω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to thank</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Αθηνά <b>ευχαρίστησε</b> το Νίκο για το δώρο. - Athena thanked Nikos for the present.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to please</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek εὐχαριστῶ, contracted form of εὐχαριστέω.</div><b>ευχαριστώ πολύ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thank you very much, many thanks</div><b>ευχετήρια κάρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευχετήριες κάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greeting card, greetings card</div><b>ευχετήριες κάρτες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ευχετήρια <a href="bword://κάρτα">κάρτα</a></div><b>ευχετήριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευχετήρια, neuter ευχετήριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with greetings, with good wishes</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έστειλα ένα <b>ευχετήριο</b> τηλεγράφημα. - Ι have sent a greetings telegram.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ευχέ(της), “who wishes”) + -τήριος.</div><b>ευχετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευχετική, neuter ευχετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (desiderative) desiderative</div>
  <div class='def'>2. expressing wishes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ευχέτ(ης), “who wishes”) + -ικός.</div><b>ευωδάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ευωδιάζω">ευωδιάζω</a></div><b>ευωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formal alternative form of <a href="bword://ευωδιά">ευωδιά</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See ευωδιά.</div><b>ευωδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ευωδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fragrance, scent, smell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εὐωδία with synizesis of &lt;ια&gt; to avoid hiatus. From εὐώδης &lt; εὐ (eu, “well”) + -ώδ(ης) &lt; ὄδ-jω).</div><b>ευωδιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to produce a pleasant smell</div><b>ευωδιαστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ευωδιαστή, neuter ευωδιαστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fragrant, sweet smelling.</div><b>ευωδώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ευωδιάζω">ευωδιάζω</a></div><b>εφάπαξ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. once</div>
  <div class='def'>2. one-off (payment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐφάπαξ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lump sum (a one-off payment made or received)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐφάπαξ.</div><b>εφάπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to touch, to be attached upon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek ἐφάπτομαι, mediopassive voice of ἐφάπτω. By surface analysis, εφ- (επι-) (“on”) + the present participle of άπτομαι.</div><b>εφέδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εφέδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ephedra (Ephedra)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ἐφέδρᾱ, with a semantic loan from translingual Ephedra.</div><b>εφέτος</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέτος">φέτος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐφέτος, from Ancient Greek ἐφ’ ἔτος, from ἐφ’ (eph’, “on, at”) + ἔτος.</div><b>εφήβαιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εφήβαια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mons pubis, pubis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of females) mons pubis, pubis; mons veneris</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐφήβαιον.</div><b>εφήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εφήμερη, neuter εφήμερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ephemeral, short-lived</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lasting one day</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐφήμερος, from ἐπ(ί), “upon”, “on”) + ἡμέρᾱ.</div><b>εφίδρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εφιδρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perspiration</div><b>εφαπτομένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εφαπτόμενες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tangent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of εφάπτομαι, a deponent (passive) verb.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of εφαπτόμενος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of εφάπτομαι, a deponent (passive) verb.</div><b>εφαρμογή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εφαρμογές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. implementation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. application (putting or spreading over a surface)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. application, app</div><b>εφαρμόζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to implement, apply, put into effect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fit</div><b>εφεδρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εφεδρική, neuter εφεδρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spare, standby, reserve</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχεις ένα <b>εφεδρικό</b> εξάγωνο κλειδί; - Do you have a spare hex key?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> έφεδρος reserve”) + -ικός.</div><b>εφετείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εφετεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>appeal court, court of appeal</div><b>εφετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εφετική, neuter εφετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. appellate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. appellate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐφετικός.</div><b>εφευρέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εφευρέτες, feminine εφευρέτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inventor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐφευρέτης.</div><b>εφευρίσκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to invent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἐφευρίσκω.</div><b>εφευρετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εφευρετική, neuter εφευρετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inventive (of a person)</div><b>εφεύρεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>invention (process and product)</div><b>εφηβεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εφηβείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adolescence, puberty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐφηβεία.</div><b>εφηβικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εφηβική, neuter εφηβικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adolescent, teenage</div><b>εφημερίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εφημερίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>newspaper, gazette</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the accusative ἐφημερῐ́δᾰ of Koine Greek ἐφημερῐ́ς, ἐφημερῐ́δος from Ancient Greek ἐφήμερος, from ἐπ(ί), “upon”, “on”) + ἡμέρᾱ.</div><b>εφημερεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be on call, be on duty</div><b>εφημερεύων</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine εφημερεύουσα, neuter εφημερεύον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>on call, on duty for the whole day (24 hours)</div>
  <div class='ex'>less formal form for the masculine: εφημερεύοντας (efimerévontas)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formal present participle of εφημερεύω. Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐφημερεύων, active present participle of ἐφημερεύω.</div><b>εφημερόπτερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εφημερόπτερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ephemeropteran</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from εφημερόπτερα, from translingual Ephemeroptera.</div><b>εφιάλτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εφιάλτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nightmare</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Via Ancient Greek ἐφιάλτης) from Ancient Greek ἐπιάλλω, Attic Greek ἐφιάλλω.</div><b>εφιαλτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εφιαλτική, neuter εφιαλτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nightmarish</div><b>εφικτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εφικτή, neuter εφικτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feasible, obtainable, possible, achievable</div><b>εφοδιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be supplied</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εφοδιάστηκα</b> με τρόφιμα και νερό για τρεις μέρες. - I was supplied with food and water for three days.</div><b>εφοδιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to supply</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐφοδιάζω.</div><b>εφοπλίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εφοπλίστριες, masculine εφοπλιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shipowner</div><b>εφοπλιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εφοπλιστές, feminine εφοπλίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shipowner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εφοπλίζω + -ιστής, a calque of French armateur. The first component is synchronically analyzable as επι- (epi-, “on-”) + οπλίζω. First attested 1840.</div><b>εφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tax office</div><b>εφτά</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://επτά">επτά</a></div><b>εφτακοσιοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εφτακοσιοστή, neuter εφτακοσιοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επτακοσιοστός">επτακοσιοστός</a></div><b>εφτακόσα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επτακόσια">επτακόσια</a></div><b>εφτακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επτακόσια">επτακόσια</a></div><b>εφόσον</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as long as, provided that, provided</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εφόσον</b> έχουν καταβληθεί τα πρόστιμα, είναι ελεύθεροι να φύγουν. - As long as the fines are paid, they can go free.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of ἐφ’ ὅσον, a learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐφ’ ὅσον, with semantic loan from English as long as and/or semantic loan from German solange, sofern.</div><b>εχθές</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χθες">χθες</a> (chthes)</div><b>εχθρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εχθρική, neuter εχθρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. enemy (attributive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hostile, inimical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ἐχθρικός. By surface analysis, εχθρ(ός)) + -ικός.</div><b>εχθροπραξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εχθροπραξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hostility (hostile action)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from εχθρ(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + πράξ(η)) + -ία.</div><b>εχθρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εχθροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enemy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐχθρός.</div><b>εχθρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εχθρότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hostility, enmity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> εχθρός + -ότητα. First attested 1843.</div><b>εχινόδερμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Echinodermata.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From translingual Echinodermata.</div><b>εχινόδερμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εχινόδερμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>echinoderm (Echinodermata)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from εχινόδερμα.</div><b>εχινόζωα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Echinozoa, the echinozoans, certain echinoderms.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Echinozoa.</div><b>εψές</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, dialectal) last night, yesterday evening</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Εψές</b>, σαν όλα τα βραδάκια,
Να πάνε κάτου τα φαρμάκια. - Last night, like every other night,
So that the poison goes down.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://χτες">χτες</a> (chtes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὀψές, from Ancient Greek ὀψέ.</div><b>εύγευστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύγευστη, neuter εύγευστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tasty, delicious</div><b>εύζωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εύζωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εύζωνος">εύζωνος</a></div><b>εύζωνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural εύζωνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. evzone, a member of the presidential guard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) the presidential guard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὔζωνος.</div><b>εύθυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύθυμη, neuter εύθυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheerful, cheery, jolly, merry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὔθυμος. By surface analysis, εύ- + θυμός.</div><b>εύκαμπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύκαμπτη, neuter εύκαμπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flexible, pliable (capable of being flexed or bent without breaking)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) flexible (capable of adapting or changing to suit new or modified conditions or situations)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὔκαμπτος.</div><b>εύκολα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εύκολος">εύκολος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>readily; easily</div><b>εύκολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύκολη, neuter εύκολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>easy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὔκολος, from εὐ- (eu-, “well, good”) + a component κολος (kolos) of unknown interpretation and origin.</div><b>εύλογα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://εύλογος">εύλογος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reasonably</div><b>εύλογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύλογη, neuter εύλογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reasonable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὔλογος. By surface analysis, εύ- + -λογος (-logos).</div><b>εύνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural εύνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. favour (UK), favor (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. approval</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εὔνοια.</div><b>εύπεπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύπεπτη, neuter εύπεπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, literally) easily digested, easily digestible, easy to digest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὔπεπτος.</div><b>εύπιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύπιστη, neuter εύπιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>credulous, gullible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὔπιστος.</div><b>εύπορος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύπορη, neuter εύπορο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>affluent, well-to-do, wealthy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὔπορος. By surface analysis, εύ- + πόρος</div><b>εύρη ζώνης</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://εύρος">εύρος</a> ζώνης n</div><b>εύρηκα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eureka</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὕρηκα, past tense of εὑρίσκω. Exclamation attributed to Archimedes. Doublet of βρήκα. Cognate with Sanskrit परीक्षा.</div><b>εύρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ευρήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. find (anything that is found)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. finding (a result of research or an investigation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὕρημα with semantic loan from German Befund and English findings.</div><b>εύρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) width, breadth</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>εύρος</b> του ποταμού - the width of the river</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) the sirocco (a southeasterly Mediterranean wind)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek εὖρος.</div><b>εύρος ζώνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural εύρη ζώνης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bandwidth</div><b>εύρωστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύρωστη, neuter εύρωστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>robust, stalwart</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὔρωστος, from εὖ + ῥώννυμι.</div><b>εύστροφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύστροφη, neuter εύστροφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quickwitted, nimble-witted (mentally keen and alert)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek εὔστροφος.</div><b>εύσωμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine εύσωμη, neuter εύσωμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. big-boned, bulky, heavily built, portly, stocky, stout (of a person, physique: somewhat fat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (euphemistic) big-boned (obese)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek εὔσωμος, in line with the meaning of Ancient Greek εὐσώματος.</div><b>εύφλεκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flammable, inflammable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. combustible</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) excitable, volatile, touchy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εὔφλεκτος.</div><b>εύχευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ευχεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Any seaweed or red alga of the genus Eucheuma.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα μεταποιημένα φύκη του γένους <b>Εύχευμα</b> λαμβάνονται από κατεργασία φυσικών στελεχών θαλασσίων φυκών των ειδών Eucheuma cottonii και Eucheuma spinosum της τάξης Rhodophyceae (ερυθροφύκη) με υδατικά διαλύματα αλκαλίων (ΚΟΗ) για την απομάκρυνση των ξένων προσμίξεων, ακολουθούμενη από έκπλυση με καθαρό νερό και ξήρανση, οπότε προκύπτει το τελικό προϊόν. - Processed seaweeds of the genus Eucheuma are obtained by treating natural strains of marine algae of the species Eucheuma cottonii and Eucheuma spinosum of the order Rhodophyceae (red algae) with aqueous alkali solutions (ΚΟΗ) to remove foreign impurities, followed by washing with clean water and drying, resulting in the final product.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Eucheuma. Equivalent to ευ- (ef-, “good”) + χεύμα.</div><b>εύχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to wish</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σου <b>εύχομαι</b> χρόνια πολλά για τα γενέθλιά σου. - I wish you many years for your birthday.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek εὔχομαι (“pray, wish”), from Proto-Hellenic *eukʰomai, thematized from Proto-Indo-European *h₁é-h₁ugʰ-tor, reduplicated present from *h₁wegʷʰ- (“to promise, to praise”).</div><b>εἰς μνήμην</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Polytonic spelling of <a href="bword://εις">εις</a> μνήμην</div><b>ζ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ζ΄">Ζ΄</a></div><b>ζάπλουτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζάπλουτη, neuter ζάπλουτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, emphatic) very rich</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ζάπλουτος. By surface analysis, ζα- (za-, “very”) + -πλουτος (-ploutos, “rich, wealthy”) from πλούτος m.</div><b>ζάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. die, dice (polyhedron used in game of chance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έριξε δύο <b>ζάρια</b> που του έφεραν εξάρια. - He rolled two dice that got him sixes.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, in plural) dicing, dice game (game in which the outcome is at least partly determined by dice)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχασε το σπίτι του στα <b>ζάρια</b>. - He lost his house playing dice.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζάριν, from ἀζάριον, from Arabic اَلزَّهْر (az-zahr, “the dice”). Cognate with English hazard.</div><b>ζάχαρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζάχαρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sugar, sucrose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *ḱorkeh₂
Proto-Indo-Iranian *ćárkaraH
Proto-Indo-Aryan *śárkaraH
Sanskrit शर्क॑रा
Pali sakkharābor.
Ancient Greek σάκχαρder.
Ancient Greek σάκχαρις
Byzantine Greek ζάχαριςbor.
Greek ζάχαρη
Inherited from Byzantine Greek ζάχαρις, ζάχαρι(ν)), from Ancient Greek σάκχαρις, derived from σάκχαρ which is ultimately from Sanskrit शर्करा via Pali.</div><b>ζάχαρη άχνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>icing sugar</div><b>ζάχαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diabetes</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://σάκχαρο">σάκχαρο</a></div><b>ζέβρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζέβρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>zebra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French zèbr(e) + -α.</div><b>ζέμπρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζέμπρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative pronunciation of <a href="bword://ζέβρα">ζέβρα</a>: zebra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French zèbr(e) + -α. Compare to ζέβρα.</div><b>ζέρδελο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζέρδελα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) apricot</div>
  <div class='ex'>(standard)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζαρταλοῦ, ζαρζαλοῦ and others to be presumed, also already in the last quarter of the fifteenth century ζαρταλούδι, from Old Anatolian Turkish زردالو (zerdalu, “apricot”), from Classical Persian زردآلو.</div><b>ζέσταμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζεστάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. warming, warming up (the act of making warm)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. warming up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ζεστα(ίνω)) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ζέστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) heat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζέω. Cf. also ζεστός. Cognate with Mariupol Greek зест (zjest).</div><b>ζέφυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>zephyr, westerly Mediterranean wind</div><b>ζήλεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζήλειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the medieval spelling of <a href="bword://ζήλια">ζήλια</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ζηλεία. See ζήλια</div><b>ζήλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζήλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jealousy, envy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ζήλια / ζήλεια, from ζηλεία with recessive accent. From ζηλεύω. Also see ζήλος.</div><b>ζήλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) zeal</div>
  <div class='ex'>θρησκευτικός <b>ζήλος</b> - religious zeal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ζῆλος.</div><b>ζήμενς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) siemens (SI unit of conductance/conductivity)</div><b>ζήσε Μάη μου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) that&#x27;ll be the day, that&#x27;ll be the frosty Friday (said in disbelief that something is to happen until a very long time in the future or at all)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Shortened version of ζήσε Μάη μου να φας τριφύλλι; see there for further etymology.</div><b>ζήσε Μάη μου να φας τριφύλλι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) that&#x27;ll be the day, that&#x27;ll be the frosty Friday (said in disbelief that something is to happen until a very long time in the future or at all)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Shortened version of ζήσε μαύρε μου να φας τον Μάη τριφύλλι (literally, “live, my poor (donkey), till May when you can eat clover”), which is an idiom of agricultural origin (i.e. expressing disbelief that on a difficult winter a donkey can survive till May when clover will have sprouted again). Through loss of etymological transparency, the phrase acquired its modern form probably in the 20ᵗʰ c. The shortened forms ζήσε Μάη μου να φας τριφύλλι and ζήσε μαύρε μου να φας τριφύλλι are the most common today.
The older phrase is of Byzantine Greek origin, as it is already recorded by Levinus Warner (mid-17ᵗʰ c.). A calque of this phrase has also been inherited to Modern Turkish as ölme eşeğim ölme yaza yonca bitecek (literally, “don&#x27;t die, my donkey, don&#x27;t die, clover will sprout in summer”) or more rarely ölme eşeğim ölme çayır çimen bitecek, usually shortened to ölme eşeğim ölme.</div><b>ζήτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) zeta, the sixth letter of the modern Greek alphabet, transliterated as Z.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Abbreviation of <a href="bword://ομάδα">ομάδα</a> Ζήτα (a police rapid response motorcycle unit)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, informal, usually) going out begging for money, panhandling</div>
  <div class='ex'>usually in the expression: βγαίνω στη ζήτα (vgaíno sti zíta) with βγαίνω (vgaíno, “come out”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζῆτᾰ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular present imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://ζητάω">ζητάω</a> / <a href="bword://ζητώ">ζητώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>alternative form: ζήταγε (zítage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular simple past perfective imperative of <a href="bword://ζητάω">ζητάω</a> / <a href="bword://ζητώ">ζητώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζήτα</b> μου ό,τι θες! - Ask me for whatever you want!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Verbal form.</div><b>ζήτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζητήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. matter, issue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. question, subject, issue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ζήτημα.</div><b>ζήτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζητήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. demand (the desire to purchase goods and services)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. demand (the amount of a good or service that consumers are willing to buy at a particular price)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο νόμος της προσφοράς και της <b>ζήτησης</b> - the law of supply and demand</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare) demand (requirement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>σε πρώτη <b>ζήτηση</b> - on demand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited or learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ζήτησις, with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>ζήτω</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>long live</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζήτω</b> η Ελλάδα! - Long live Greece!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ζήτω, the third-person imperative of ζῶ.</div><b>ζαβολιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαβολιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cheating</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prank, trick (mischievously deceptive act)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Assibilation of διαβολιά. Compare Ancient Greek διάβολος → ζάβολος, Latin diabolus → zabolus.</div><b>ζαβολιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζαβολιάρα or ζαβολιάρισσα, neuter ζαβολιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cheating (unsporting or underhand)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tricky (adept at using deception)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (noun from verb) cheat, cheater (someone who cheats)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ζαβολ(ιά)) + -ιάρης.</div><b>ζαγάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζαγάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hound, hunting dog</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) inferior person, cur</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish زغر.</div><b>ζακέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζακέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cardigan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French jaquette (“jacket”).</div><b>ζαλάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dizziness, vertigo, loss of balance</div><b>ζαλίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to feel dizzy, be dizzy</div><b>ζαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to make dizzy</div><b>ζαλίζω τ&#x27; αρχίδια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, transitive, vulgar) to twist someone&#x27;s balls, bust someone&#x27;s balls, pester, torment, be a pain in the arse (to annoy someone greatly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε μέρα μου <b>ζαλίζει τ’ αρχίδια</b> να του δανείσω λεφτά. - Every day he’s up my ass trying to get me to lend him money.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “to make dizzy the bollocks”.</div><b>ζαμάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζαμάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) ages</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish زمان, from Classical Persian زمان.</div><b>ζαμπονοτυρόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαμπονοτυρόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ham and cheese pie (Greek pastry made with phyllo and containing ham and cheese, usually feta)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ζαμπόν + τυρόπιτα.</div><b>ζαμπόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ham (cured pork)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French jambon.</div><b>ζαράρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζαράρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>damage, harm, loss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ضرر (zarar).</div><b>ζαργάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαργάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. needlefish, garfish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pike (fish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Earlier σαργώνη, σαργώνος), extended from σαργός, from Ancient Greek σαργός.</div><b>ζαρζαβατικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζαρζαβατικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>any vegetable or herb used for culinary purposes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ζαρζαβάτι + -ικό; the ending -ικό serves to specify that the word refers to a singular/particular vegetable, as the etymological root Ottoman Turkish زرزوات (zarzavat) originally had collective noun status.</div><b>ζαριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>throw / roll of the dice</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχασε όλα τα λεφτά του σε μια <b>ζαριά</b>. - He lost all his money in a roll of the dice.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ζάρι + -ιά.</div><b>ζαρκάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζαρκάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>roe deer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> With vowel leveling from Byzantine Greek ζορκάδιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek ζορκάς, variant of δορκάς. Related to Mariupol Greek зарка́. Lent Albanian sorkadh.</div><b>ζαρωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wrinkly, wrinkled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The passive participle of ζαρώνω.</div><b>ζαρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to wrinkle, crease, crinkle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to crumple, shrivel up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζαρώνω, from an earlier οζαρώνω, probably a verbal formation from Ancient Greek ὄζος (though this is uncertain).</div><b>ζατί</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>because</div><b>ζαφείρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζαφείρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sapphire (gem)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian zaffiro, from Latin sapphirus, from Ancient Greek σάπφειρος.</div><b>ζαφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crocus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. saffron (herb)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Arabic زَعْفَرَان.</div><b>ζαχαροδιαβήτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζαχαροδιαβήτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diabetes, sugar diabetes</div><b>ζαχαροκάλαμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζαχαροκάλαμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sugar cane (Saccharum, esp. Saccharum officinarum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ζάχαρη + κάλαμος + -ο (-o, neuter nominal suffix).</div><b>ζαχαροπλάσταινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαχαροπλάσταινες, masculine ζαχαροπλάστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) confectioner</div><b>ζαχαροπλάστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζαχαροπλάστες, feminine ζαχαροπλάστρια or ζαχαροπλάστισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confectioner</div><b>ζαχαροπλάστισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαχαροπλάστισσες, masculine ζαχαροπλάστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confectioner</div><b>ζαχαροπλάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζαχαροπλάστριες, masculine ζαχαροπλάστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confectioner</div><b>ζαχαροπλαστείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζαχαροπλαστεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cake shop, patisserie</div><b>ζαχαρωτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) boiled sweet, piece of candy, an item of confectionery</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ζαχαρωτός">ζαχαρωτός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ζαχαρωτός">ζαχαρωτός</a></div><b>ζαχαρωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζαχαρωτή, neuter ζαχαρωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baked or boiled with sugar</div><b>ζαχαρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sugar (to coat or sprinkle with sugar)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to crystallise (prepare crystallised fruit, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to sugar (make something palatable)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to court, flirt, spoon</div><b>ζελατίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζελατίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gelatin</div><b>ζεματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to scald, blanch</div><b>ζενίθ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) zenith</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French zénith, from Medieval Latin cenit, ultimately from Arabic سَمْت (samt, “direction, path”).</div><b>ζεσταίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to warm yourself up</div><b>ζεσταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to warm, warm up, heat up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to enliven, excite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ζεστός + -αίνω</div><b>ζεσταμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ζεσταμένη, neuter ζεσταμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>warmed up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ζεσταίνομαι, passive voice of ζεσταίνω.</div><b>ζεστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζεστή, neuter ζεστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. warm, hot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. welcoming, cosy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζεστός, from ζέω.</div><b>ζεττάμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζεττάμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>zettametre (10²¹ metre)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ζεττά- + μέτρο.</div><b>ζευγάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζευγάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of things) couple, pair</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα <b>ζευγάρι</b>: παπούτσια, κάλτσες, γυαλιά - a pair of: shoes, stockings, eyeglasses</div>
  <div class='def'>2. couple, pair; married or in romantic liaison, or acting together (of people)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να σας συστήσω το ζεύγος Παπαδοπούλου. Παντρεύτηκαν πέρσι, αλλά είναι <b>ζευγάρι</b> εδώ και δέκα χρόνια. - May I present Mr and Mrs (the couple) Papadopoulos. They married last year, but they&#x27;ve been together (a pair) for ten years.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of animals) couple, pair; yoke (ie a pair of animals tied together for ploughing)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of animals) couple, pair; a pair of male and female animals</div>
  <div class='def'>5. couple, pair; the other member of a pair (of matching things)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βρήκα το <b>ζευγάρι</b> του σκουλαρικιού μου. - I found my earring&#x27;s partner.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (of cards) couple, pair</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κρατάω <b>ζευγάρια</b> ρηγάδες και άσους. - I hold pairs of kings and aces.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (dialectal) an extent of land; a person or family&#x27;s holding of land</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (dialectal) an extent of land; a unit of land area (variable according to village) of 25-30 hectares</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζευγάρι, ζευγάριν, from ζευγάριον, diminutive of Ancient Greek ζεῦγος. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *yewg- (“to join, tie together”).</div><b>ζεύγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζεύγη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of things) (formal) pair to be used or joined together</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ζεύγος</b> υποδημάτων - pair of shoewear</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of things) (formal) pair to be used or joined together; pair of words with a shared characteristic</div>
  <div class='ex'>See derivatives</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of persons) couple usually married, or acting together</div>
  <div class='ex'>συζυγικό <b>ζεύγος</b> - a married couple</div>
  <div class='def'>4. two animals joined together for ploughing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ζεῦγος. Compare to ζευγάρι.</div><b>ζηλευτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζηλευτή, neuter ζηλευτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enviable, coveted, desirable (desired by virtue of good qualities)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επειδή ήταν <b>ζηλευτό</b> προϊόν, δεν έμεινε στο κατάστημα πολύ ώρα. - Since it was a desirable product, it didn&#x27;t stay for long in the store.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ζηλεύω + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ζηλεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, plural normally, reciprocal) to be jealous of each other (of love jealousy)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζηλεύονται</b> πολύ αυτοί οι δυο. Στο τέλος θα χωρίσουν. - The two of them are very jealous of each other. In the end they will separate.</div><b>ζηλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to envy, be jealous/envious (to feel resentment or wonder at someone for a perceived advantage, material or otherwise)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι ωραίο αμάξι που έχεις, <b>ζηλεύω</b>! - What a nice car you have, I&#x27;m jealous!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to be jealous, suspect (to be suspecting rivalry in love; to be suspicious of a lover or spouse&#x27;s fidelity)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζηλεύει</b> τη γυναίκα του και φοβάται να την αφήσει μόνη τους πουθενά. - He suspects his wife [of cheating] and won&#x27;t let her go anywhere alone.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ζηλεύω, from ζηλόω.</div><b>ζηλιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζηλιάρα, neuter ζηλιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>envious, jealous, possessive</div><b>ζηλοφθονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) envy, jealousy</div><b>ζημιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζημιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. damage, harm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. loss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζημιά, from Ancient Greek ζημία with synizesis of the latter two syllables.</div><b>ζημιογόνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζημιογόνος or ζημιογόνα, neuter ζημιογόνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. damaging, harmful, injurious, prejudicial</div>
  <div class='def'>2. loss-making</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ζημί(α)) + -ο- (-o-) + -γόνος.</div><b>ζημιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to wrong, to disservice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζημιῶ), from ζημία.</div><b>ζητάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to ask for, request</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζήτησα</b> ένα ποτήρι νερό. - I asked for a glass of water.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to seek, look for</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Zητώ</b> μεταχειρισμένο αυτοκίνητο σε καλή κατάσταση. - I’m looking for a used car in good condition.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (informal, passive) to be in demand, sought-after</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πουλάμε πολλές ομπρέλες. <b>Zητιούνται</b> πολύ όταν βρέχει. - We’re selling lots of umbrellas. They are in demand whenever it rains.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern ζητώ + -άω, from Ancient Greek ζητῶ, contracted form of ζητέω.</div><b>ζητημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ζητημένη, neuter ζητημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sought-after</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ζητιέμαι, passive voice of ζητάω, συζητώ (“discuss”) and of ζητούμαι, passive voice of ζητώ.</div><b>ζητιάνευ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of ζητιάνεψε, second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://ζητιανεύω">ζητιανεύω</a></div><b>ζητιάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζητιάνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>male beggar</div><b>ζητιανεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to beg, to panhandle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ζητιάνος (ζητιαν-) + -εύω (“suffix for verbs”).</div><b>ζητούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be in demand, be wanted</div><b>ζητωκραυγάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive, transitive) to cheer (to applaud or encourage with cheers or shouts)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ζητωκραυγ(ή)) + -άζω.</div><b>ζητωκραυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζητωκραυγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a cry or cheer of enthusiasm or victory: hurrah, hurray, banzai</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ζήτω”) + κραυγή.</div><b>ζητώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ζητάω">ζητάω</a></div><b>ζιβάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζιβάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ζιβανία">ζιβανία</a></div><b>ζιβανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζιβανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) zivania, a spirit sometimes flavoured with aniseed or other herbs.</div><b>ζιβελίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://ζιμπελίνα">ζιμπελίνα</a></div><b>ζιζάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζιζάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. weed (unwanted plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cancer, canker</div>
  <div class='def'>3. troublemaker, pest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ζῐζᾰ́νῐον.</div><b>ζιζανιοκτόνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζιζανιοκτόνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>weedkiller, herbicide</div><b>ζιμπάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζιμπάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) rubbish, garbage, trash</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic زبالة.</div><b>ζιμπελίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζιμπελίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stoat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sable (the animal or its fur)</div><b>ζιμπούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζιμπούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ζουμπούλι">ζουμπούλι</a> (hyacinth)</div><b>ζιπούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζιπούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A short jacket or waistcoat (forming part of some Greek folk costumes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζιπούνιν, from ζιπόνιν, from Venetan zipon. Doublet of τζουμπές.</div><b>ζιρκόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) zirconium metal, element 40</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) the mineral zircon (a zirconium nesosilicate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From German Zirkon (“zircon mineral”) via French zircon, formed in the 1780s from Arabic زَرْقُون, from Middle Persian 𐭦𐭫𐭢𐭥𐭭, from Classical Persian زرگون/Middle Persian 𐭦𐭫𐭢𐭥𐭭, from Proto-Iranian *jr̥Hanyam (“gold”). Doublet of ζιργκόν.</div><b>ζλότι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) zloty (Polish currency)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Polish złoty.</div><b>ζουζούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζουζούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. buzzing insect, bug</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) fidgetting child</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>ζουλάπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζουλάπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) wild animal, beast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (offensive, slang) offensive and contemptuous characterisation for a person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Albanian zullap (“wild animal, beast”) probably through Aromanian zulapi. The medieval ζουλάπι(ν)) is different.</div><b>ζουλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to squeeze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ζουλίζω) with change of suffix.</div><b>ζουλούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζουλούμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>injustice, harm, wrong</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Arabic ظُلْمbor.
Ottoman Turkish ظلمbor.
Greek ζουλούμι
Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ظلم, borrowed from Arabic ظُلْم.</div><b>ζουμί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. juice, sap</div>
  <div class='def'>2. broth</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) essence, meaning, sense</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) profit (usually a big)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζουμίν, inherited from Ancient Greek ζωμίον, diminutive of ζωμός.</div><b>ζουμερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>juicy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζουμερός; by surface analysis, ζουμί + -ερός.</div><b>ζουμπάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζουμπάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. punch (tool)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (pejorative, sarcastic) very short man</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish زنبه.</div><b>ζουμπούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) hyacinth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish سنبل, from Classical Persian سنبل (sunbul), from Arabic سُنْبُل (sunbul).</div><b>ζουρνάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζουρνάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shawm, zurna, sorna</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish زورنا (zurna), from Classical Persian زرنا.</div><b>ζοφερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζοφερή, neuter ζοφερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pitch-dark</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bleak, gloomy, grim, dismal (causing cheerlessness or despair)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ζοφερός. By surface analysis, ζόφ(ος)) + -ερός.</div><b>ζοχάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζοχάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, plural normally) pile (informal), haemorrhoid (UK), hemorrhoid (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nervousness</div><b>ζούγκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζούγκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. jungle (a large, undeveloped, humid forest, especially in a tropical region, that is home to many wild plants and animals)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) jungle (a place where people behave ruthlessly, unconstrained by law or morality)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French jungle, from English jungle.</div><b>ζυγά-ζυγά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two by two, in pairs</div><b>ζυγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to weigh</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to weigh up, size up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζυγίζω, from Ancient Greek ζυγός, ζυγόν + -ίζω.</div><b>ζυγαριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζυγαριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scales, balance, pair of scales (for weighing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) balance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ζυγαριά from ζυγαρέα with synizesis. Morphologically, ζυγ(ός) m, “balance, scale”) + -αριά f</div><b>ζυγιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, transitive) to weigh (to determine the weight of an object)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to weigh (to consider)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to balance, to poise (to bring (items) to an equipoise, as the scales of a balance by adjusting the weights)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek ζυγιάζω. By surface analysis, ζυγός + -ιάζω.</div><b>ζυγισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ζυγισμένη, neuter ζυγισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. perfect passive participle of <a href="bword://ζυγίζω">ζυγίζω</a>; weighed, having had its weight measured.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) perfect passive participle of <a href="bword://ζυγίζω">ζυγίζω</a>; Having weighed one&#x27;s thoughts, having been deeply thought about or contemplated.</div><b>ζυγός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζυγή, neuter ζυγό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. even</div>
  <div class='ex'>2, 4, 6 … είναι <b>ζυγοί</b> αριθμοί - 2, 4, 6… are even numbers</div>
  <div class='def'>2. twin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζυγόν, ζυγός, from Proto-Hellenic *dzugón, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *yugóm (“yoke”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζυγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scales, balance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. yoke</div>
  <div class='def'>3. yoke; slavery</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάτω από το ζυγό - under the yoke</div>
  <div class='def'>4. rank or file (of soldiers)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζυγόν, ζυγός, from Proto-Hellenic *dzugón, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *yugóm (“yoke”).</div><b>ζυθοποιείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζυθοποιεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brewery (a building where beer is produced)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ζύθ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιείο.</div><b>ζυμάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζυμάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. yeast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dough</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζυμάριον, from a diminutive form of Ancient Greek ζύμη. Cognate with Pontic Greek ζυμάρι.</div><b>ζυμαρικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζυμαρικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pasta</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ζυμάρι.</div><b>ζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to live</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζει</b> πιά ο κύριος Παναγιώτης; - Is Mr Panagiotis still alive?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to live on, endure (continue to exist)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μνήμη του θα <b>ζει</b> για πάντα στις καρδιές μας. - His memory will live on in our hearts.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to support, sustain (financially)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με τέτοιο μισθό, πώς να <b>ζήσει</b> την οικογένειά του; - With such a salary, how can he support his family?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζῶ, from earlier ζώω, from Proto-Hellenic *ďṓwō, from *gʷyṓwō, from Proto-Indo-European *gʷyéh₃woh₂.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek зу (zu).</div><b>ζωάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζωάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://ζώο">ζώο</a> n</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ζώο + -άκι.</div><b>ζωή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. life</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lifetime</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ζωή.</div><b>ζωή και κότα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) bed of roses, life of Riley (carefree or sweet life) (literally: life and hen)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Από τότε που παντρεύτηκαν, περνούν <b>ζωή και κότα</b> οι δυο τους. - Since they married, their life has been a bed of roses.</div><b>ζωή σε λόγου σας</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>my condolences, I&#x27;m sorry for your loss (said at a funeral to the bereaved)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζωή σε λόγου σας</b> να τον θυμόσαστε! - My condolences, may you live to remember him!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “life to your reason”.</div><b>ζωή σε μας</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>my condolences, I&#x27;m sorry for your loss (said at a funeral to the bereaved)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζωή σε μας</b> να τον θυμόμαστε! - My condolences, may we live to remember him!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “life to us”.</div><b>ζωή σε σας</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>my condolences, I&#x27;m sorry for your loss (said at a funeral to the bereaved)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζωή σε σας</b> να τον θυμόσαστε! - My condolences, may you live to remember him!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “life to you”.</div><b>ζωγράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ζωγράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>painter, artist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ζωγρᾰ́φος.</div><b>ζωγραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζωγραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa pronunciation of <a href="bword://ζωγραφιά">ζωγραφιά</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ζωγρᾰφῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>ζωγραφίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to draw, paint</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to perform a task with virtuosity</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to recount, describe</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ζωγράφισε</b> μια ζοφερή εικόνα της κατάστασης. - He painted a grim picture of the situation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζωγραφίζω, from Ancient Greek ζωγραφῶ, ζωγραφέω with metaplasm of -ίζω onto the stem form ζωγραφησ-. The Ancient Greek term is from ζωγράφος + -έω.</div><b>ζωγραφιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζωγραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>illustration, graphic art</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζωγρᾰφῐ́ᾱ, equivalent of ζωγράφος + -ιά.</div><b>ζωγραφική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζωγραφικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. painting (the production of an artwork)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. painting style</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζωγραφική.</div><b>ζωηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζωηρή, neuter ζωηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lively, energetic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζωηρός from ζωή.</div><b>ζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζωική, neuter ζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (relational) animal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (relational) life (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ζωϊκός. By surface analysis, ζώ(ο), “animal”) + -ικός.</div><b>ζωμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζωμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gravy, broth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζωμός.</div><b>ζωντάνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vitality (energy or vigour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. liveliness, verve, vivacity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from ζωνταν(ός)) + -ια (-ia).</div><b>ζωντανεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to bring to life</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to come to life</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ζωνταν- of ζωντανός + -εύω (“suffix for verbs”).</div><b>ζωντανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζωντανή, neuter ζωντανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alive, living</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) lively</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From stem ζωντ- of the indeclinable medieval participle Byzantine Greek ζῶντα from ancient participle ζῶν of verb ζῶ. With suffix -ανός for adjectives.</div><b>ζωντόβολο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζωντόβολα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated, literally, vernacular) beast, animal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively, offensive) boor, blockhead, lummox (dimwitted and clumsy person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάλι κατόρθωσε να σπάσει τα βάζα μου αυτό το <b>ζωντόβολο</b>! - That big lummox managed to break my vases again!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ζωντόβολον, initially &quot;household pet&quot;, from ζῶ + βόλον, from verb βάλλω.</div><b>ζωοκτονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural ζωοκτονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) animalicide, animalslaughter (the killing of animals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ζῳοκτονία. By surface analysis, ζωο- (zoo-, “zoo-”) + -κτονία.</div><b>ζωολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) zoology</div><b>ζωολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζωολογική, neuter ζωολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>zoological</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ζωολογ(ία) + -ικός.</div><b>ζωολογικός κήπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζωολογικοί κήποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>zoo, zoological garden</div><b>ζωοτομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) vivisection</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) zootomy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French zootomie (“animal anatomy”), from Ancient Greek ζῷον + τέμνω +.</div><b>ζωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζωτική, neuter ζωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vital (alive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) vital, essential (of great importance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζωτικός. Semantic loan from French vital.</div><b>ζωύφιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζωύφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bug (any insect, arachnid, or other terrestrial arthropod that is a pest)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ζῳύφιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek ζῷον.</div><b>ζωώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ζωώδης, neuter ζωώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. like an animal, unlike a human being</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subhuman, animal, bestial, brutish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζῳώδης from ζῷον.</div><b>ζόμπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) zombie</div><b>ζόρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. force, compulsion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. toughness, strain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish زور (zor), from Classical Persian زور.</div><b>ζύγι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζύγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. weight</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα <b>ζύγι</b> του 1 κιλού - 1 kilogram weight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. plumb bob</div><b>ζύθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ζύθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, literary) beer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Koine Greek ζῦθος</div><b>ζύμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζύμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. yeast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dough</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζῡ́μη.</div><b>ζύμωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζυμώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fermentation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fermentation, kneading</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) fermentation, agitation, moulding</div><b>ζώδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζώδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>astrological sign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ζῴδιον, diminutive of ζῷον.</div><b>ζώνες ασφαλείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ζώνη">ζώνη</a> ασφαλείας</div><b>ζώνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. belt, sash, seat belt</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δέσε τη <b>ζώνη</b> σου, σε παρακαλώ - Fasten your seat belt, please</div>
  <div class='def'>2. zone</div>
  <div class='def'>3. band</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ζώνη</b> συχνοτήτων - frequency band</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζώνη.</div><b>ζώνη ασφαλείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ζώνες ασφαλείας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seat belt, safety belt</div><b>ζώνης ασφαλείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://ζώνη">ζώνη</a> ασφαλείας</div><b>ζώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to gird, girdle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ζώνω, from Ancient Greek ζώννυμι via their common past tense stem ζωσ- at ἔζωσα &gt; modern έζωσα. For the stem ζωσ-, also see ζώνη f.</div><b>ζώνων ασφαλείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of <a href="bword://ζώνη">ζώνη</a> ασφαλείας</div><b>ζώο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ζώα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mammal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. animal, beast, creature</div>
  <div class='def'>3. brute, stupid (used as derogatory interjection)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζῷον.</div><b>η Ρώμη δεν χτίστηκε σε μια μέρα</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Rome wasn&#x27;t built in a day</div><b>η γλώσσα κόκαλα δεν έχει μα κόκαλα τσακίζει</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the tongue wounds more than a lance, the pen is mightier than the sword (insults and offensive words hurt more than physical injuries)</div><b>η γριά η κότα έχει το ζουμί</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous) Mature women make better lovers.</div><b>η εκδίκηση είναι ένα πιάτο που τρώγεται κρύο</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>revenge is a dish best served cold (emotional detachment is preferable when taking revenge)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French la vengeance est un plat qui se mange froid.</div><b>η περιέργεια σκότωσε τη γάτα</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curiosity killed the cat</div><b>η σταγόνα που ξεχείλισε το ποτήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic, indeclinable) the straw that broke the camel&#x27;s back, final straw, last straw</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “the drop that made the glass overflow”.</div><b>η΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Η΄">Η΄</a></div><b>ηβηφρένεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hebephrenia</div><b>ηβική τρίχα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pubic hair</div><b>ηβικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ηβική, neuter ηβικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pubic</div><b>ηγέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ηγέτες, feminine ηγέτιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leader (commander, boss)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leader (expert, guide)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἡγέτης, from ἡγοῦμαι.</div><b>ηγέτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηγέτιδες, masculine ηγέτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leader (person in authority)</div><b>ηγήτορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ηγήτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leader</div><b>ηγεμονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηγεμονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hegemony</div>
  <div class='ex'>η σπαρτιατική <b>ηγεμονία</b> - the Spartan hegemony</div>
  <div class='def'>2. principality</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>ηγεμονία</b> του Μονακό - the Principality of Monaco</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἡγεμονία.</div><b>ηγεμονίσκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ηγεμονίσκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>princeling</div><b>ηγεμονεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rule, reign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἡγεμονεύω.</div><b>ηγεμονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ηγεμονική, neuter ηγεμονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. princely</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hegemonic</div><b>ηγεμόνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ηγεμόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prince, sovereign</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leader, ruler</div><b>ηγεσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηγεσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leadership (qualities or position of a leader)</div><b>ηγετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ηγετική, neuter ηγετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leading, leadership</div><b>ηγουμένες</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. misconstruction of ηγούμενες</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (misconstruction) incorrectly accented form of ηγούμενες: nominative, accusative and vocative plural of <a href="bword://ηγουμένη">ηγουμένη</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>see ηγουμένη Declension notes</div><b>ηγουμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηγούμενες, masculine ηγούμενος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbess, prioress, mother superior</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἡγουμένη, feminine noun, as in masculine Koine noun ἡγούμενος, from Ancient Greek present participle of ἡγέομαι / ἡγοῦμαι.</div><b>ηγουμένισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηγουμένισσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbess, prioress, mother superior</div><b>ηγουμενία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηγουμενίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>priorship, abbotship</div><b>ηγουμενείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ηγουμενεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the quarters of an abbot, abbess, prior, etc</div><b>ηγουμενοσυμβούλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ηγουμενοσυμβούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the board of management of an abbey, priory, etc</div><b>ηγούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to lead, be in command of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) to head, head up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek ἡγοῦμαι, contracted form of ἡγέομαι.</div><b>ηγούμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ηγούμενοι, feminine ηγουμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbot, prior, father superior</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἡγούμενος.</div><b>ηδονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηδονές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pleasure, delight (physical/sexual pleasure)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἡδονή.</div><b>ηδονίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to delight in pleasure</div><b>ηδονίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηδονίστριες, masculine ηδονιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hedonist, pleasure seeker, lotus eater</div><b>ηδονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ηδονική, neuter ηδονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voluptuous, sensual, hedonistic</div><b>ηδονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ηδονισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hedonism, pleasure seeking, voluptuousness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ηδονή + -ισμός.</div><b>ηδονιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ηδονιστές, feminine ηδονίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hedonist, pleasure seeker, lotus eater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ηδονή + -ιστής, calque of French hédoniste. First attested 1894.</div><b>ηδονιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ηδονιστική, neuter ηδονιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hedonistic, voluptuous, sensual</div><b>ηδονοβλεψίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ηδονοβλεψίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) voyeur, Peeping Tom, peeper (person who watches another without the other&#x27;s permission and usually without the other&#x27;s knowledge, especially for the purpose of deriving sexual pleasure from the sight)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ηδονή + -ο- (-o-) + βλέπω, semantic loan from French voyeur.</div><b>ηδυπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hedonism, pleasure seeking, voluptuousness</div><b>ηδυπαθής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ηδυπαθής, neuter ηδυπαθές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voluptuous, sensual, hedonistic</div><b>ηδύποτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ηδύποτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>liqueur (sweet alcoholic beverage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἡδύποτον, equivalent to ηδύ- + ποτό.</div><b>ηθ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ηθική">ηθική</a>: ethics</div><b>ηθελημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ηθελημένη, neuter ηθελημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intentional, wilful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of German gewillt. Learnedly but erroneously formed as a passive perfect participle of θέλω, from Ancient Greek ἐθέλω (which has no such participle), by analogy with ημαρτημένος, archaic/learned passive perfect participle of αμαρτάνω, from Ancient Greek ἁμαρτάνω / ἡμᾰρτημένος.</div><b>ηθικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>morally, ethically</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>ηθικά</b> δικαιωμένοι. - It is morally justified.</div><b>ηθική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) morality, ethics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἠθική, feminine nominative/vocative singular of ἠθικός: &quot;of/for/pertaining to morals, moral, expressive of character&quot;.</div><b>ηθικολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ηθικολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>priggishness, moral superiority</div><b>ηθικολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ηθικολογική, neuter ηθικολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relating to ethics</div>
  <div class='def'>2. moralistic</div><b>ηθικολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (no past) to moralise (UK), moralize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (no past) to preach, lecture (correctively about morals)</div><b>ηθικολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ηθικολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. moralist, moraliser</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prig</div><b>ηθικοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) moral education, moralisation</div><b>ηθικοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to uplift morals, improve morally</div><b>ηθικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) morale</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ηθικός">ηθικός</a></div><b>ηθικός</b>
