  <div class='ex'>Βαλτική <b>Θάλασσα</b>, Μεσόγειος <b>Θάλασσα</b> - Baltic Sea, Mediterranean Sea</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sea (all the seas and oceans)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγε στη <b>θάλασσα</b>. - He went to sea.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. storm</div>
  <div class='ex'>πολύ <b>θάλασσα</b> - heavy seas, stormy weather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θάλασσα. Cognate with Tsakonian θάσσα, Mariupol Greek тъа́ласа.</div><b>θάλλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural θάλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) thallium</div><b>θάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to bloom, flower</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to flourish</div><b>θάλπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to make comfortable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal, literally) to warm up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θάλπω (“heat; comfort”).</div><b>θάμνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θάμνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bush, shrub (woody plant of medium size)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θάμνος.</div><b>θάνατος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θάνατοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>death</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θάνατος. Compare Mariupol Greek тъа́натос.</div><b>θάρρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θάρρη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. courage, bravery, valour (UK), valor (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ardour (UK), ardor (US)</div><b>θέα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θέες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>view, sight</div><b>θέαμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θεάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sight (something seen)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spectacle, sight, vision (a special sight)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θέαμα, from the same root as θέᾱ + -μα (-ma).</div><b>θέατρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θέατρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. theatre (UK), theater (US); theatre building, audience</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλο το <b>θέατρο</b> χειροκρότησε δυνατά. - The whole theatre applauded loudly.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. theatre (UK), theater (US); dramatic art</div>
  <div class='def'>3. theatre (UK), theater (US); theatre of war</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θέατρον.</div><b>θέληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θελήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>will, volition</div><b>θέλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to want, desire, will</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>ήθελα</b> δύο μπύρες. - I would like two beers.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to need, require</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νομίζω ότι <b>θέλεις</b> κούρεμα. - I think you need a haircut.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to intend, mean to, want to (have the intention of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συγγνώμη, δεν <b>ήθελα</b> να σε προσβάλω. - I&#x27;m sorry, I didn&#x27;t mean to insult you.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to owe (money, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι σου <b>θέλω</b>; - What do I owe you?</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to have (depict as being)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι φήμες <b>θέλουν</b> να τον είχα φιλήσει. - The rumours have it that I kissed him.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (with two instances in the second person singular θες (thes) ) either ... or</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ήρθε απόψε· <b>θες</b> γιατί δεν αισθανόταν καλά, <b>θες</b> γιατί βρήκε κάτι καλύτερο να κάνει. - He didn&#x27;t come tonight; either he wasn&#x27;t feeling well or he found something better to do.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (transitive) to favour (be lucky at something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν δεν <b>θέλει</b> κάποιον η τύχη, δεν γίνεται τίποτα. - If fortune doesn&#x27;t favour someone, nothing happens.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek θέλω, a parallel form of ἐθέλω.</div><b>θέμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. subject, main topic (of conversation, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. affair</div>
  <div class='def'>3. theme</div>
  <div class='def'>4. stem</div>
  <div class='def'>5. theme, the regional unit of government of the Byzantine empire.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. a matter of [with genitive] (when something is wholly dependent on what noun denotes, e.g., a matter of time)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι μόνο <b>θέμα</b> χρόνου τώρα. - It&#x27;s just a matter of time.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θέμᾰ.</div><b>θέρετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θέρετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. resort</div>
  <div class='ex'>ενα γοητευτικό θέρετρο στη θάλασσα - a charming sea resort</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cottage, holiday accommodation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θέρετρον.</div><b>θέρμανση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θερμάνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heating</div><b>θέρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal, singular only, uncountable) summer, summertime</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (singular only, uncountable) harvest, vintage</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (singular only, uncountable) harvest time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A borrowing from Ancient Greek θέρος, retaining the neuter gender.</div><b>θέση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. position (a place, location)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. job, post</div>
  <div class='def'>3. station, status</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θέσις, from τίθημι.</div><b>θέτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) to place, position</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Θέτεις</b> ένα πολύ σημαντικό θέμα στη συζήτησή μας. - You put/introduce a very important subject in our discussion.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to set</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θέτω</b> σε κίνηση ένα μηχάνημα - I set in motion a device</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (formal) to put (only in set phrases)</div>
  <div class='ex'>see: βάζω (vázo)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek θέτω, from Ancient Greek τίθημι; cf. ἔθετον).</div><b>θήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>case, sheath, holster (close fitting cover or container for something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θήκη.</div><b>θήλαστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θήλαστρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. breast pump</div>
  <div class='def'>2. baby bottle, feeding bottle</div><b>θήλυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of θηλύκι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θῆλυς.</div><b>θήρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) hunting, the hunt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) prey, the hunted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek θήρα.</div><b>θήραμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θηράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. game, prey, quarry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. kill</div><b>θήτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Theta, the 8th letter of the modern Greek alphabet (majuscule Θ, minuscule θ), originally denoting an aspirated t (/tʰ/) but now denoting the voiceless dental fricative (/θ/); usually romanized as &quot;th&quot;.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θῆτᾰ.</div><b>θίασος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θίασοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>theatre company, troupe</div><b>θίγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to hurt, to offend (to affront)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to adversely affect</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in the negative) to touch (:money, property, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to touch on, to touch upon (to mention briefly; to cursorily discuss)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Possibly via a learned borrowing from Koine Greek, from Ancient Greek θιγγάνω, with semantic loan from French toucher.</div><b>θίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dune</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θίς, usually considered Pre-Greek.</div><b>θίξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θίξιμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dent (touch: it action or effect)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. offence</div><b>θαλάμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chamber</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lair of octopus</div><b>θαλάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θαλάμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>underwater lair or nest</div><b>θαλάσσια ανεμώνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θαλάσσιες ανεμώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sea anemone</div><b>θαλάσσια ύδατα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>territorial waters</div><b>θαλάσσιες ανεμώνες</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of θαλάσσια <a href="bword://ανεμώνη">ανεμώνη</a></div><b>θαλάσσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θαλάσσια, neuter θαλάσσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relating to: sea, sailing, seaside</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θαλάσσια</b> ανεμώνη - sea anemone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. maritime, marine</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θαλάσσιο</b> περιβάλλον - marine environment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek θαλάσσιος. By surface analysis, θάλασσ(α), “sea”) + -ιος (-ios, “indicating origin”).
Cognate with Mariupol Greek тъаласи́.</div><b>θαλαμηγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θαλαμηγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. yacht, sailing vessel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small private motorised vessel</div><b>θαλαμηπόλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or masculine (plural θαλαμηπόλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. steward</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flight attendant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θαλαμηπόλος m</div><b>θαλασσής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θαλασσιά, neuter θαλασσί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sea blue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θάλασσ(α) f, “sea”) + -ής.</div><b>θαλασσαετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θαλασσαετοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. white-tailed sea eagle, white-tailed eagle (UK)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sea eagle</div><b>θαλασσινά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seafood</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θαλασσινός, from θάλασσα + -ινός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of θαλασσινό</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θαλασσινός, from θάλασσα + -ινός.</div><b>θαλασσοταραχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rough sea, turbulent sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θάλασσα + -ο- (-o-) + ταραχή.</div><b>θαλπωρή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. warmth, mild and pleasant heat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) warm, familiar, and pleasant atmosphere</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θαλπωρή.</div><b>θαμνοψάλτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θαμνοψάλτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dunnock (Prunella modularis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θάμνος + ψάλτης.</div><b>θαμνώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θαμνώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tough, often prickly shrub</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) such vegetation dominating an area of countryside, maquis</div><b>θαμπός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θαμπή, neuter θαμπό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dim, dull</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blurred, fuzzy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) vague, imprecise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek θαμπός &lt; θαμβός with semantic shift, from Ancient Greek θάμβος.</div><b>θανάσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θανάσιμη, neuter θανάσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fatal, mortal, deadly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: θανατηφόρος (thanatifóros)</div><b>θανατηφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θανατηφόρα, neuter θανατηφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deadly, lethal, mortiferous, death-dealing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: θανάσιμος (thanásimos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θανατηφόρος. By surface analysis, θάνατος + -φόρος.</div><b>θανατοφοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural θανατοφοβίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) thanatophobia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from New Latin thanatophobia. By surface analysis, θάνατος + -φοβία.</div><b>θανατώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to kill</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to execute, to put to death</div><b>θαρραλέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θαρραλέα, neuter θαρραλέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>courageous, brave</div>
  <div class='ex'>μία <b>θαρραλέα</b> πράξη - a courageous deed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θαρραλέος.</div><b>θαρρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to reckon, assume, imagine</div><b>θαυμάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be admired</div><b>θαυμάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to admire, feel admiration for</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to look up to, look at with wonder</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to be impressed by</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θαυμάζω.</div><b>θαυμάσια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wonderfully</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See θαυμάσιος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See θαυμάσιος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://θαυμάσιος">θαυμάσιος</a> (wonderful)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://θαυμάσιος">θαυμάσιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See θαυμάσιος.</div><b>θαυμάσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θαυμάσια, neuter θαυμάσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wonderful, admirable, marvellous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θαυμάσιος; by surface analysis, θαυμάζω + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>θαυμασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θαυμασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>admiration, adoration, appreciation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek.</div><b>θαυμαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine θαυμάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. admirer (one who admires)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. admirer (one who is romantically attracted to someone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θαυμαστής. By surface analysis, θαυμάζω + -τής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive feminine singular of <a href="bword://θαυμαστός">θαυμαστός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>θαυμαστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θαυμαστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exclamation mark (UK), exclamation point (US), the “!”</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://θαυμαστικός">θαυμαστικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative neuter singular of <a href="bword://θαυμαστικός">θαυμαστικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://θαυμαστικός">θαυμαστικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://θαυμαστικός">θαυμαστικός</a></div><b>θαυμαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θαυμαστική, neuter θαυμαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wonderful, marvellous</div><b>θαυμαστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θαυμαστή, neuter θαυμαστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. admirable, wonderful, fine, splendid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic) brave (splendid)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θαυμαστός</b> καινούριος κόσμος - brave new world</div><b>θαυματουργός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θαυματουργή or θαυματουργός, neuter θαυματουργό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. miracle-working, who makes miracles</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θαυματουργή</b> εικόνα της Παναγίας - icon of Holy Mary that makes miracles</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) exceptionally effective or efficient</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θαυματουργό</b> φάρμακο - miracle-working medicine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek θαυματουργός. For icons or saints, inherited from Byzantine Greek θαυματουργός. Morphologically, θαύμα, θαυματ- + -ουργός.</div><b>θαύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θαύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. miracle, supernatural event</div>
  <div class='ex'>Από <b>θαύμα</b>, πέρασες τις εξετάσεις, συγχαρητήρια. - Miraculously, you passed the exam, congratulations.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) marvel, wonder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θαῦμα.</div><b>θεά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θεές, masculine θεός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. goddess</div>
  <div class='def'>2. goddess (adored or idealized woman)</div><b>θεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θείες, masculine θείος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aunt (a sister or sister-in-law of a parent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θείᾱ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://θείος">θείος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://θείος">θείος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θείᾱ.</div><b>θείο χάρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>charisma (extraordinary power granted by the Holy Spirit)</div><b>θείος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θείοι, feminine θεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uncle (a parent’s brother or parent&#x27;s brother-in-law)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θεῖος, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰeh₁(y)-. Compare to θειος (theios).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεία, neuter θείο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>divine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θεῖος, from θεός.</div><b>θεαματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεαματική, neuter θεαματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spectacular, scenic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θέαμα + -ικός. First attested 1883.</div><b>θεαματικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural θεαματικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rare, uncountable, usually) spectacularity (condition of being spectacular)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>θεαματικότητα</b> του νησιού δεν περιγράφεται με λόγια. - The spectacular (views) of the island can&#x27;t be described in words.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) viewership, ratings (number of people who watched a certain programme)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συναντήθηκαν να αποφασίσουν πως να ανεβάσουν την <b>θεαματικότητα</b> της εκπομπής. - They met to decide how to raise the ratings of the show.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θεαματικός + -ότητα. First attested 1825.</div><b>θεατής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θεατές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spectator, observer, viewer, onlooker</div><b>θεατρικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>theatrically</div><b>θεατρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεατρική, neuter θεατρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>theatrical</div><b>θεατρικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://θεατρικά">θεατρικά</a></div><b>θεατρόφιλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεατρόφιλη, neuter θεατρόφιλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. theatre-loving, theatregoing (UK), theater-loving, theatergoing (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) theatre lover, theatregoer (UK), theater lover, theatergoer (US), theatrophile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English theatrophile. By surface analysis, θέατρ(ο)) + -ό- + -φιλος (-filos).</div><b>θεατός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεατή, neuter θεατό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>visible, in sight, seen</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>θεατή</b> πλευρά της Σελήνης - the visible side of the moon</div><b>θειάφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, uncountable) sulphur (UK), sulfur (US) (informal, usually not in chemical literature)</div><b>θειαιθέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θειαιθέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thioether</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θείο + αιθέρας, calque of English thioether.</div><b>θειαφίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dust/treat with sulphur</div><b>θειικό μαγνήσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) magnesium sulphate, Epsom salts</div><b>θειικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sulfuric acid</div><b>θειικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θειική, neuter θειικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sulphate (UK), sulfate (US)</div><b>θειικός ψευδάργυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) zinc sulphate, zinc sulfate</div><b>θειοθειικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θειοθειική, neuter θειοθειικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thiosulfate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θείο + θειικός, calque of English thiosulphate.</div><b>θειοκυανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θειοκυανική, neuter θειοκυανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thiocyanate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θείο + κυάνιο + -ικός, calque of English thiocyanate.</div><b>θειούχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θειούχα, neuter θειούχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sulphurous (UK), sulfurous (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sulphide (UK), sulfide (US)</div><b>θειώδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) sulfur dioxide, SO₂</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) sulfur, a yellowish-green colour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek θειώδης.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://θειώδης">θειώδης</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek θειώδης.</div><b>θειώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θειώδης, neuter θειώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sulfurous, containing or resembling sulfur</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sulfite</div>
  <div class='def'>3. of sulfur (yellowish-green) colour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek θειώδης, from θεῖον + -ώδης.</div><b>θεμέλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θεμέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. foundation, substructure, base</div>
  <div class='def'>2. groundwork</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θεμέλιος.</div><b>θεμελιώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεμελιώδης, neuter θεμελιώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fundamental</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θεμέλιο.</div><b>θεμελιώδης αλληλεπίδραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fundamental interaction</div><b>θεμελιώδης δύναμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fundamental force</div><b>θεμιτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεμιτή, neuter θεμιτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. legitimate (conforming to known principles, or established or accepted rules or standards; valid)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. legitimate (in accordance with the law or established legal forms and requirements)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fair (competition)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θεμιτός.</div><b>θεοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θεοκρατίας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>theocracy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην αρχαία Αίγυπτο επικρατούσε <b>θεοκρατία</b>. - Theocracy was dominant in Ancient Aegypt.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek θεοκρᾰτῐ́ᾱ. Morphologically from θεο- (“god”) + -κρατία (“holding power, ruling”).</div><b>θεολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>theology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek θεολογία.</div><b>θεολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural θεολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>theologian</div><b>θεομηνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θεομηνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disaster from natural causes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek θεομηνία. From θεο- (theo-) + μῆν(ις) f, “wrath”) + -ία</div><b>θεοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. deification (the act of deifying; exaltation to divine honors; apotheosis)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deification (excessive praise)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek θεοποίησις, with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>θεοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to deify</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek θεοποιῶ. By surface analysis, θε(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + ποιώ.</div><b>θεοσεβής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεοσεβής, neuter θεοσεβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>devout, pious, religious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θεοσεβής; θεο- (theo-, “god”) + σέβας.</div><b>θεοσεβούμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεοσεβούμενη, neuter θεοσεβούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>devout, pious</div><b>θεοφοβούμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεοφοβούμενη, neuter θεοφοβούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pious, God-fearing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θεός + φοβούμαι.</div><b>θεούσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θεούσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) churchy woman, religious nut (a woman who is overly religious and often hypocritically so)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο Πράιντ αυτή τη χρόνια, ήταν πάλι μια <b>θεούσα</b> εκεί που τους φώναζε να μετανιώσουν. - At Pride this year, there was a Bible basher there shouting at them to repent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Θεός + -ούσα</div><b>θερίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to reap, cut (cereal crop, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to decimate</div><b>θεραπεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θεραπείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cure, therapy, remedy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θεραπεία.</div><b>θεραπευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θεραπευτές, feminine θεραπεύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. therapist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. healer (one who heals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θεραπευτής. By surface analysis, θεραπεύω + -τής.</div><b>θεραπευτική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://θεραπευτικός">θεραπευτικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>therapeutics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French thérapeutique, from the nominalized feminine of Ancient Greek θεραπευτικός.</div><b>θεραπευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεραπευτική, neuter θεραπευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>therapeutic, curative, healing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θεραπευτικός. By surface analysis, θεραπευτ(ής)) + -ικός.</div><b>θεραπεύσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεραπεύσιμη, neuter θεραπεύσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curable, treatable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the θεραπεύσ- stem of θεραπεύω + -ιμος (-imos).</div><b>θεραπεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cure, to heal, to make better (to restore to health)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cure (to bring (a disease or its bad effects) to an end)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to cure (to remove or cause to be rid of a defect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θεραπεύω.</div><b>θερινή ώρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) summer time</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) daylight saving time, daylight saving (UK), daylight savings (US)</div><b>θερινό ηλιοστάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θερινά ηλιοστάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>summer solstice</div><b>θερινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θερινή, neuter θερινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of or in summer</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα σε αφήσει να τις ξαναδώσεις αν πας <b>θερινό</b> σχολείο. - She&#x27;ll let you retake them if you go to summer school.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θερινός.</div><b>θερισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θερισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harvest (task and season)</div><b>θεριστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θεριστές, feminine θερίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harvester, reaper (person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek θεριστής. By surface analysis, θερίζω + -τής.</div><b>θερμά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>warmly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural neuter of <a href="bword://θερμός">θερμός</a></div><b>θερμάστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θερμάστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire, heater, stove</div><b>θερμίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θερμίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, dated) calorie, used in the past by scientists to measure the quantity of heat.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. kilocalorie used to measure the energy content of food.</div><b>θερμαίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be heated, to be warmed up</div><b>θερμαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to heat</div><b>θερμαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θερμαστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stoker</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fireman</div><b>θερμικές μόνωσεις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of θερμική <a href="bword://μόνωση">μόνωση</a></div><b>θερμική μόνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θερμικές μόνωσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thermal insulation, heat insulation</div><b>θερμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θερμική, neuter θερμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thermal, heat</div><b>θερμοδυναμική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://θερμοδυναμικός">θερμοδυναμικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thermodynamics</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>θερμοδυναμική</b> είναι κλάδος της φυσικής. - Thermodynamics is a branch of physics.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French French thermodynamique. Classical compound from θερμο- + δυναμικός (thermo- + dynamic).</div><b>θερμοδυναμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θερμοδυναμική, neuter θερμοδυναμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>referring to thermodynamics</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θερμοδυναμικός</b> νόμος - thermodynamic law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θερμο- + δυναμικός. See noun θερμοδυναμική (thermodynamics).</div><b>θερμοκήπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θερμοκήπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. greenhouse (garden building)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. glasshouse (large commercial greenhouse)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French serre chaude&quot;”); by surface analysis, θερμο- (thermo-) + κήπος + -ιο (-io). Attested since 1856 in Katharevousa as θερμοκήπιον.</div><b>θερμοκοιτίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θερμοκοιτίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>isolette, infant incubator</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ανησύχησε και αναρωτήθηκε τι να είχε γίνει με την Ιφιγένεια, λυπήθηκε που δεν μπορούσε να είναι κοντά της μια τέτοια στιγμή, πάλι σκέφτηκε το μωρό στη <b>θερμοκοιτίδα</b>, μετά τον Αστέρη και πού να ήταν τώρα.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Rendition of the English incubator. Morphologically from θερμο- (“warm”) + κοιτίδα (“cradle”).</div><b>θερμοκρασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θερμοκρασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. temperature</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θερμοκρασία</b> δωματίου - room temperature</div>
  <div class='def'>2. heat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θερμοκρασία, from θερμός + κρᾶσις. In the modern scientific sense of &quot;temperature&quot;, first attested in 1812 by Koumas.</div><b>θερμομετρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θερμομετρική, neuter θερμομετρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thermometric</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θερμόμετρο + -ικός.</div><b>θερμομονωτικά υλικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of θερμομονωτικό <a href="bword://υλικό">υλικό</a></div><b>θερμομονωτικό υλικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θερμομονωτικά υλικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lagging, thermal insulation material</div><b>θερμομονωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θερμομονωτική, neuter θερμομονωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thermally insulating</div><b>θερμοπίδακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θερμοπίδακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>geyser</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θερμός + πίδακας.</div><b>θερμοπληξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) heatstroke</div><b>θερμοσίφωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θερμοσίφωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>water heater</div><b>θερμοστάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θερμοστάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thermostat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from θερμο- (thermo-) + -στάτης.</div><b>θερμοφόρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θερμοφόρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hot water bottle</div><b>θερμόλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θερμολύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thermolysis, thermal decomposition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from New Latin thermolysis. Morphologically, θερμός + λύση”).</div><b>θερμόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θερμόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thermometer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa Greek θερμόμετρον, first attested in 1766 by Theotokis, borrowed from French thermomètre (“thermometer”), itself composed of Greek elements θερμός + μέτρον.</div><b>θερμόμετρο καραμέλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sugar thermometer (UK), candy thermometer (US)</div><b>θερμός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θερμή, neuter θερμό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. warm, hot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. heated, fervent</div>
  <div class='def'>3. heartfelt, hearty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θερμός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) thermos, vacuum flask, Dewar flask</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θερμός</div><b>θερμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) heat, warmth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) heat</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, uncountable) warmth (of smile, welcome etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θερμότης, equivalent to θερμός + -ότητα.</div><b>θεσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θεσμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>institution (society custom or practice)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γάμος αποτελεί <b>θεσμό</b> που επινόησαν οι κοινωνίες. - Marriage is an institution invented by society.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θεσμός, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰeh₁- (“to put”).</div><b>θεσσαλονικιώτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεσσαλονικιώτικη, neuter θεσσαλονικιώτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Thessalonian (from or related to Thessaloniki)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>θεσσαλονικιώτικη</b> προφορά διαφέρει πολύ από την αθηναϊκή. - The Thessalonian accent differs greatly from the Athenian.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Θεσσαλονίκη.</div><b>θετικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://θετικός">θετικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. positively (in a positive manner)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. affirmatively, in the affirmative</div><b>θετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θετική, neuter θετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reliable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. positive, pragmatic</div>
  <div class='def'>3. assertive</div>
  <div class='def'>4. positive</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θετικός</b> βαθμός του επιθέτου - the positive degree of the adjective</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θετικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θετικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>positive</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>θετικός</b>, ο συγκριτικός και ο υπερ<b>θετικός</b> - the positive, the comparative and superlative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θετικός.</div><b>θετός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adopted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θετός.</div><b>θεωρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θεωρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. theory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contemplation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θεωρία</div><b>θεωρία των συνόλων</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>set theory</div><b>θεωρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θεωρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>box, loge, gallery at a theatre, concert hall, parliament</div>
  <div class='ex'>τιμή εισιτηρίου για πλατεία, εξώστη, <b>θεωρείο</b> - price of ticket for stalls, balcony, box</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θεωρός + -είο. From Koine Greek θεωρεῖον. Compare to θεωρέω.</div><b>θεωρητικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://θεωρητικός">θεωρητικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. theoretically, in theory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. theoretically (in a theoretical manner)</div><b>θεωρητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεωρητική, neuter θεωρητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. theoretical, abstract</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hypothetical, imaginary</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (noun from verb) theorist, theoretician</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θεωρητικός</div><b>θεωρούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be considered</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: θεωροῦμαι (theōroûmai)</div><b>θεωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to consider, regard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to scrutinise</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to validate (a document)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to examine (a text to make corrections.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek θεωρῶ, common form of θεωρέω. Doublet of θωρώ, which was inherited.</div><b>θεϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεϊκή, neuter θεϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>divine, godlike, godly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θεϊκός.</div><b>θεϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural θεϊσμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) theism</div><b>θεόρατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεόρατη, neuter θεόρατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enormous</div><b>θεός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θεοί, feminine θεά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. god (supreme being or a supernatural, typically immortal, being with superior powers)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην αρχαία Ελλάδα, ο Διόνυσος ήταν ο <b>θεός</b> του κρασιού. - In Ancient Greece, Dionysus was the god of wine.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative letter-case form of <a href="bword://Θεός">Θεός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) god, idol (something or someone particularly revered, worshipped, idealized, admired and/or followed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όσον αφορά μουσική, ο Μότσαρτ ήταν ο <b>θεός</b> του. - With regard to music, Mozart was his god.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, figuratively) god, Greek god (exceedingly handsome man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην παραλία ήταν διάφοροι <b>θεοί</b>. - There were several Greek gods on the beach.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θεός.</div><b>θεότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θεότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deity, divinity, godhead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek θεότης, equivalent to θεός + -ότητα.</div><b>θεότρελος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεότρελη, neuter θεότρελο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, emphatic) mad as a hatter, off one&#x27;s rocker, nutty as a fruitcake (insane to the highest degree)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην ακούς τί λέει, είναι <b>θεότρελος</b> ο τύπος. - Don&#x27;t listen to what he&#x27;s saying, the guy is off his rocker.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θεό- + τρελός</div><b>θεόφοβος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θεόφοβη, neuter θεόφοβο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pious, God-fearing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun) God-fearing person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θεός + φόβος.</div><b>θεώρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θεωρήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>theorem</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>θεώρημα</b> του Πυθαγόρα - The theorem of Pythagoras</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θεώρημα.</div><b>θεώρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θεωρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. authentication, endorsement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. outlook, vision</div>
  <div class='def'>3. point of view, notion</div><b>θηκάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θηκάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scabbard, sheath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θήκη, from Ancient Greek θήκη.</div><b>θηλάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to suckle, breast-feed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θηλάζω, from θῆλυς (female, soft, delicate).</div><b>θηλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θηλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nipple, teat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θηλή.</div><b>θηλασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θηλασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>breastfeeding, nursing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θηλασμός.</div><b>θηλαστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θηλαστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mammal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θηλάζω + -στικό</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://θηλαστικός">θηλαστικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://θηλαστικός">θηλαστικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θηλάζω + -στικό</div><b>θηλαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θηλαστική, neuter θηλαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mammalian, milking</div><b>θηλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θηλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loop, noose</div>
  <div class='def'>2. snare (trap)</div><b>θηλυγονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θηλυγονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female birth (used mainly in phrases)</div><b>θηλυκό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θηλυκά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>woman, female</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://θηλυκός">θηλυκός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://θηλυκός">θηλυκός</a></div><b>θηλυκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θηλυκή or θηλυκιά, neuter θηλυκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female (feminine gender)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. feminine (female person or animal)</div><b>θηλυπρεπής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θηλυπρεπής, neuter θηλυπρεπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. effeminate, girly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) camp, gay-acting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θηλυπρεπής &lt; θήλυς + -πρεπής</div><b>θημωνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θημωνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stook</div><b>θηρίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θηρία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wild animal, beast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brute, predator</div>
  <div class='def'>3. large strong man</div>
  <div class='def'>4. giant (vegetable, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θηρίον.</div><b>θηριοδαμαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θηριοδαμαστές, feminine θηριοδαμάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>animal tamer / trainer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θηρίο + δαμάζω + -τής</div><b>θηριοτροφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θηριοτροφεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. menagerie</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) school with undisciplined students</div><b>θηριωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θηριωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ferocity, savagery</div>
  <div class='def'>2. atrocity, cruel act</div><b>θηριωδώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fiercely, ferociously, savagely</div><b>θηριόποδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Theropoda, the theropods.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Theropoda.</div><b>θηριώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θηριώδης, neuter θηριώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. violent and cruel in character and behaviour</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fierce, ferocious, bestial</div>
  <div class='def'>3. monstrous (relating to a mythical monster)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. huge</div><b>θησαυρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θησαυροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. treasure, wealth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thesaurus</div>
  <div class='def'>3. treasury, treasure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θησαυρός.</div><b>θητεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θητείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. national service, military service</div>
  <div class='def'>2. career</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tenure, term of office, period of office</div><b>θλίβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sadden, distress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θλίβω.</div><b>θλίψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θλίψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sorrow, sadness, grief, affliction</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>θλίψη</b> της ήταν μεγάλη για την απώλεια του συζύγου της. - Her sorrow at the loss of her husband was great.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θλῖψις.</div><b>θλιβερά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://θλιβερός">θλιβερός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drearily, sorrowfully</div><b>θλιβερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θλιβερή, neuter θλιβερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dismal, dreary, grievous, lamentable, sad, unfortunate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι καιρός να τελειώσει αυτός ο <b>θλιβερός</b> πόλεμος. - It&#x27;s time to end this unfortunate war.</div><b>θλιμμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sadly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://θλιμμένος">θλιμμένος</a></div><b>θλιμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine θλιμμένη, neuter θλιμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. feeling sad, sorrowful, grieved</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι πάντοτε <b>θλιμμένος</b> από τότε που πέθανε η γυναίκα του. - He is always sad since his wife died.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. showing sadness, gloomy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με κοίταξε μ’ ένα <b>θλιμμένο</b> χαμόγελο. - She looked at me with a sad smile.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of θλίβομαι, passive voice of θλίβω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek unattested *θλιμμένος, as in adverb θλιμμένα. Compare to Ancient Greek τεθλιμμένος.</div><b>θνησιγενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θνησιγενής, neuter θνησιγενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stillborn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) stillborn (of a project which failed from the outset)</div><b>θνησιμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural θνησιμότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) mortality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θνησιμαίος + -ότητα, calque of French mortalité. First attested 1866.</div><b>θνητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θνητή, neuter θνητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mortal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θνητός, from θνῄσκω.</div><b>θολωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θολωτή, neuter θολωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dome-shaped, having a dome roof</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek θολωτός.</div><b>θολός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θολή, neuter θολό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dim, not bright</div>
  <div class='def'>2. muddy, cloudy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek θολός.</div><b>θορυβώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θορυβώδης, neuter θορυβώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>noisy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θορυβώδης. By surface analysis, θόρυβος + -ώδης.</div><b>θούλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural θούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) thulium</div><b>θράκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θράκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hot coal, ember, hot cinder (used to cook food)</div><b>θράσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) effrontery, audacity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) cheek, impudence</div><b>θράφηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of θρέφομαι, the passive of θρέφω</div>
  <div class='ex'>Older form: ἐθράφην (ethráphēn), ἐτράφην (etráphēn)</div><b>θρήνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θρήνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lament, wailing, wail</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek θρῆνος</div><b>θρήσκευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θρησκεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. creed (particular set of religious beliefs)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. denomination</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek θρήσκευμα.</div><b>θρίαμβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θρίαμβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>triumph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek θρίαμβος, which had already acquired the meaning &#x27;triumph&#x27; by semantic loan from Latin triumphus.</div><b>θρίδακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θρίδακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, obsolete, rare) lettuce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θρίδαξ.</div><b>θρίλερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) thriller, sensational news story</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English thriller.</div><b>θρανίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θρανία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>school desk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θρᾱνῐ́ον.</div><b>θρασύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θρασεία, neuter θρασύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cheeky, impudent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. audacious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek θρασύς.</div><b>θραύσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θραύσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fragment, chip, piece, sliver</div>
  <div class='def'>2. potsherd, shard</div>
  <div class='def'>3. shrapnel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θραῦσμα.</div><b>θραύστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θραύστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>breaker, splitter, cracker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θραύστης.</div><b>θρεμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine θρεμμένη, neuter θρεμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fed, nurtured</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fattened up, with sufficient weight</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θρεμμένες</b> αγελάδες - fattened up cows</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τρέφομαι, passive voice of τρέφω &amp; of its variant θρέφομαι of θρέφω.</div><b>θρεονίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural θρεονίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) threonine (an amino acid)</div><b>θρηνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive, transitive) to mourn, lament</div><b>θρησκεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θρησκείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. religion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cult</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θρησκεία.</div><b>θρησκευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θρησκευτική, neuter θρησκευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>religious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θρησκεύω + -τικός.</div><b>θρησκόληπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θρησκόληπτη, neuter θρησκόληπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) zealous; fanatically religious</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βέβαια, όλοι οι <b>θρησκόληπτοι</b> πολιτικοί πήραν τον όρκο μπροστά στους παπάδες. - Of course, all the churchy politicians took the oath in front of the priests.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θρήσκος + -ληπτος (-liptos, “taken, received”). -ληπτος (-liptos) derives from λαμβάνω. First attested 1888.</div><b>θριαμβεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to triumph, prevail</div><b>θρυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θρυλική, neuter θρυλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>legendary (of or pertaining to a legend)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θρύλος + -ικός.</div><b>θρυμματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shatter, smash, break</div><b>θρυπτοφάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural θρυπτοφάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nonstandard spelling of <a href="bword://τρυπτοφάνη">τρυπτοφάνη</a></div><b>θρόμβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θρόμβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blood clot, thrombus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek θρόμβος.</div><b>θρόμβωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θρομβώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thrombosis</div><b>θρόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θρόνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. throne</div>
  <div class='ex'>αυτοκρατορικός, πατριαρχικός, παπικός <b>θρόνος</b> - imperial, patriarchic, papal throne</div>
  <div class='def'>2. monarchy, the royal office</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>θρόνος</b> της Αγγλίας - English monarchy (literally:throne).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θρόνος.</div><b>θρύλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θρύλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. legend (story - usually myth)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. legend, hero (person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek θρῦλος.</div><b>θρύμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θρύμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fragment, shard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crumb</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θρύμμα, from θρύπτω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>θυγατέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θυγατέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, dialectal, literary) daughter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θυγάτηρ, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰugh₂tḗr. Cognate with Tsakonian σάτη.</div><b>θυελλωδώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stormily, thunderously, tempestuously (in a stormy, thunderous or tempestuous manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έφυγε <b>θυελλωδώς</b> από το συνέδριο. - He left the conference stormily.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested 1894.</div><b>θυελλώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θυελλώδης, neuter θυελλώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(also, figuratively) stormy, thundery, tempestuous, thunderous (of or reminiscent of a storm)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Θυελλώδεις</b> βροχές και χαλάζι απόψε στην Αττική. - Thundery downpours and hail tonight in Attica.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek θυελλώδης, from θύελλα.</div><b>θυλακίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θυλακίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thylacine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Thylacinus.</div><b>θυμάμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I remember</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> θυμ(ούμαι)) + ending -άμαι. See θυμούμαι &amp; ενθυμούμαι</div><b>θυμάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θυμάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thyme (herb)</div><b>θυμίαμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θυμιάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. incense resin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incense fumes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θυμίαμα.</div><b>θυμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to remind</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Θύμισέ</b> μου να φέρω τα λεφτά. - Remind me to get the money.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek θυμίζω, active formation from an earlier passive form θυμίζομαι, from Koine Greek ἐνθυμίζομαι, from Ancient Greek ἐνθυμοῦμαι; see θυμάμαι for more.</div><b>θυμιατήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θυμιατήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://θυμιατό">θυμιατό</a></div><b>θυμιατήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θυμιατήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://θυμιατό">θυμιατό</a></div><b>θυμιατήριον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://θυμιατό">θυμιατό</a></div><b>θυμιατίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to burn incense, incense</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to flatter</div><b>θυμιατό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θυμιατά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thurible, censer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incense burner</div><b>θυμικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of θυμικός</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of θυμικός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>affect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned nominalization of the neuter form of θυμικός.</div><b>θυμούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a less common variant of of <a href="bword://θυμάμαι">θυμάμαι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek θυμοῦμαι, from Ancient Greek ἐνθυμοῦμαι, contracted form of ἐνθυμέομαι.</div><b>θυμωμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in an angry way</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://θυμωμένος">θυμωμένος</a></div><b>θυμωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine θυμωμένη, neuter θυμωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>angry, irate, disgruntled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of θυμώνω, a verb without passive voice forms.</div><b>θυμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θυμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anger, rage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θυμός, from Proto-Hellenic *tʰūmós, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰuh₂mós (“smoke”).</div><b>θυμώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to get angry, anger</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Θύμωσα</b> πολύ με τα ψέματα που μου είπε. - I got very angry with the lies s/he told me.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of personal pronoun) to get angry, anger; to become angry with someone</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου <b>θύμωσε</b> γιατί δεν την πήρα τηλέφωνο. Τώρα δε μου μιλάει πια. - She got angry with me because I did not phone her. She won&#x27;t speak to me anymore.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to infuriate, anger, make angry, enrage</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα ψέματά σου με <b>θύμωσαν</b> πολύ. - Your lies made me very angry.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of climate, with the participle) to become stormy</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>θύμωσε</b> ο καιρός – <b>θύμωσε</b> η θάλασσα - the weather became stormy – the sea became stormy</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of skin disease, with the participle) to worsen</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το απόστημα <b>θύμωσε</b>, γι’ αυτό πήρα αντιβιοτικό. - The abscess flared up, so I got an antibiotic.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek θυμώνω, θυμαίνω, from the Ancient Greek θυμόω, θυμῶ + -ώνω. See θυμός for more.</div><b>θυρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θυρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hatch, window, opening (for counter clerk, bank teller, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. box, pigeon hole, mail box (for correspondence, mail, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θυρίς.</div><b>θυρεός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θυρεοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coat of arms, escutcheon</div><b>θυροτηλέφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θυροτηλέφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doorphone, entryphone, intercom (to main entrance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from θύρα + -ο- (-o-) + τηλέφωνο.</div><b>θυροτηλεόραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θυροτηλεοράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The video monitoring system for an entryway, especially of a commercial establishment.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θύρα + τηλεόραση.</div><b>θυρόφυλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>door, specifically door leaf (the moving part, as opposed to the door frame)</div><b>θυσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θυσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sacrifice</div><b>θυσανόποδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θυσανόποδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barnacle</div><b>θυσανόπτερα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Thysanoptera.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Thysanoptera.</div><b>θυσιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sacrifice (to offer to a deity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to sacrifice (to destroy or surrender (something) for the sake of something else regarded as more urgent or valuable)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek θυσιάζω. By surface analysis, θυσία + -άζω.</div><b>θωρακισμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine θωρακισμένη, neuter θωρακισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. armoured (UK), armored (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) ironclad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θωρακίζω.</div><b>θωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(poetic, vernacular) to gaze, examine, look over</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πῶς μᾶς θωρεῖς ἀκίνητος;... Ποῦ τρέχει ὁ λογισμός σου - How do you gaze upon us, still?... And where your thoughts are drifting
(literally, “How do you gaze upon us, immobile?... Where is your thinking running to?”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek θωρῶ, from earlier θιωρῶ, from Ancient Greek θεωρῶ, common variant of θεωρέω / . Doublet of θεωρώ.</div><b>θόλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θόλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dome, vault</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vault, sky</div>
  <div class='def'>3. body cavity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek, thence uncertain, but, according to Beekes, possibly cognate with θάλαμος.</div><b>θόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) thorium</div><b>θόρυβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θόρυβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. noise, racket</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) commotion, stir</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θόρυβος.</div><b>θύελλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θύελλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. storm, tempest, gale, hurricane (strong winds with rain)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ταβάνι κατέρρευσε λόγω της <b>θύελλας</b>. - The ceiling collapsed due to the storm.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) storm, barrage, slew, onslaught (strong amount)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Θύελλα</b> αντιδράσεων προκάλεσαν οι δηλώσεις του Πρωθυπουργού. - The Prime Minister&#x27;s comments caused a barrage of reactions.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θύελλα, from θύω.</div><b>θύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural θύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>victim (of accident; of murder; of violent or malicious act; etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θῦμα.</div><b>θύμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θύμησες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) memory (specific and general)</div><b>θύμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://θυμάρι">θυμάρι</a> (thyme)</div><b>θύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural θύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. door</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a gate in a stadium</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a PC port</div>
  <div class='ex'>θύρα USB (USB port)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θύρα. For senses USB, port, semantic loan from English Universal Serial Bus and port (sense computing).</div><b>θώρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural θώρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chest (thorax)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thorax</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cuirass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek θώραξ.</div><b>ι΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ι΄">Ι΄</a></div><b>ιέρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ιέρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>falcon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἱέραξ.</div><b>ιέρεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιέρειες, masculine ιερέας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>priestess or female minister</div><b>ιαγουάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ιαγουάροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jaguar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English jaguar or French jaguar, from Portuguese jaguar or Spanish yaguar. Doublet of τζάγκουαρ.</div><b>ιαπωνικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Japanese (Japanese language)</div><b>ιαπωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιαπωνική, neuter ιαπωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Japanese, of or relating to Japan.</div><b>ιατρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ιατρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (UK) doctor&#x27;s surgery/clinic/consulting room</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (US) doctor&#x27;s office</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ἰατρεῖον. By surface analysis, ιατρ(ός), “doctor”) + -είο.</div><b>ιατρεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of γιατρεύoμαι</div><b>ιατρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obsolete form of <a href="bword://γιατρεύω">γιατρεύω</a>, old spelling: ἰατρεύω</div><b>ιατρική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ιατρικός">ιατρικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>medicine (academic subject); medical school</div><b>ιατρικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ιατρικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) medication, medicine</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ιατρικός">ιατρικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ιατρικός">ιατρικός</a></div><b>ιατρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιατρική, neuter ιατρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>medical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ιατρός + -ικός.</div><b>ιατρογενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιατρογενής, neuter ιατρογενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>iatrogenic</div><b>ιατροδικαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ιατροδικαστές, feminine ιατροδικαστίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. forensic pathologist (specialist in the use of forensic science to determine cause of death or injury for evidentiary purposes)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. forensic pathologist (specialist in the use of forensic science to determine cause of death or injury for evidentiary purposes); medical examiner, coroner (in North American usage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ιατρ(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + δικαστής, a calque of French médecin légiste or German Gerichtsarzt.</div><b>ιατρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ιατροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γιατρός">γιατρός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἰατρός. Doublet of γιατρός.</div><b>ιβίσκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ιβίσκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hibiscus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἱβίσκος / ἐβίσκος.</div><b>ιβηρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιβηρική, neuter ιβηρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of or relating to Iberia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἰβηρικός from Ἰβηρία.</div><b>ιβουπροφαίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ibuprofen</div><b>ιγκλού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) igloo</div><b>ιγμορίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιγμορίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sinusitis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ιγμόρειο + -ίτιδα.</div><b>ιγνυακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιγνυακή, neuter ιγνυακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>popliteal</div><b>ιγνύα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιγνύες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>popliteus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἰγνύα.</div><b>ιδέα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιδέες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. idea, concept, notion (model in the mind or intellect)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. idea (invention)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. opinion (judgement)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. impression (impression of character)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. ideology, collection or set of ideas (in the plural)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἰδέα, from εἴδω.</div><b>ιδίωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ιδιώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. peculiarity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dialect</div>
  <div class='def'>3. idiom</div>
  <div class='def'>4. habit</div><b>ιδίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>particularly, in particular, especially</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἰδίως.</div><b>ιδανικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ideally, perfectly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ιδανικός">ιδανικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ιδανικό">ιδανικό</a></div><b>ιδανικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ιδανικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ideal, principle</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ιδανικός">ιδανικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ιδανικός">ιδανικός</a></div><b>ιδανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιδανική, neuter ιδανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ideal, model, perfect</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει το <b>ιδανικό</b> βάρος. - He is the ideal weight.</div><b>ιδεαλίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιδεαλίστριες, masculine ιδεαλιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>idealist</div><b>ιδεαλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural ιδεαλισοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) idealism</div><b>ιδεαλιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine ιδεαλίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>idealist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From German Idealist.</div><b>ιδεατός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιδεατή, neuter ιδεατό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ideal (existing only in the mind; conceptual, imaginary)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ιδέα + -τός, erroneously formed by analogy with θεατός. Calque of French idéal.</div><b>ιδεοκινητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιδεοκινητική, neuter ιδεοκινητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ideomotor</div><b>ιδεοληπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιδεοληπτική, neuter ιδεοληπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obsessive (related to or characterised by obsession)</div>
  <div class='ex'>older polytonic spelling, before 1982: ἰδεοληπτικός (ideoliptikós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French obsessionnel. By surface analysis, ιδεο- (ideo-, “ideo-”) + -ληπτικός.
Also substantivised.</div><b>ιδεολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιδεολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ideology</div>
  <div class='def'>2. principles</div><b>ιδεολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ιδεολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ideologue</div><b>ιδεοψυχαναγκαστική διαταραχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) obsessive-compulsive disorder (form of anxiety characterized by an obsessive compulsion to repeatedly perform trivial or meaningless actions)</div><b>ιδεοψυχαναγκαστικής διαταραχής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of ιδεοψυχαναγκαστική <a href="bword://διαταραχή">διαταραχή</a></div><b>ιδεώδες</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ιδεώδης">ιδεώδης</a></div>
