  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιθαγενής, neuter ιθαγενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indigenous, aboriginal</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ιθαγενείς πληθυσμοί της Αυστραλίας (Aboriginal people of Australia)</div><b>ικανοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ικανοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. satisfaction, contentment, fulfilment, gratification (the pleasure obtained from the fulfilment of a need or desire)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. satisfaction, gratification (the act of fulfilling a need or desire)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. satisfaction, compensation, reparation (restitution for an injury or loss)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ικανοποιη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>ικανοποιήθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of ινκανοποιούμαι</div><b>ικανοποιημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ικανοποιημένη, neuter ικανοποιημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>satisfied</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ικανοποιούμαι, passive voice of ικανοποιώ (“to satisfy”).</div><b>ικανοποιητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ικανοποιητική, neuter ικανοποιητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>satisfactory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ικανοποιώ + -τικός.</div><b>ικανοποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be satisfied</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to become satisfied</div><b>ικανοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to satisfy, fulfill, gratify</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἱκανοποιῶ, from marginal Koine Greek ἱκανοποιέω, consisting of ἱκανός + ποιέω, formed as a calque of Latin satisfacio.</div><b>ικανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ικανή, neuter ικανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. able, capable, skilled, able-bodied, competent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fit, battle ready</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἱκανός.</div><b>ικανότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ικανότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ability, capacity, capability, competence, faculty, proficiency</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἱκανότης, equivalent to ικανός + -ότητα.</div><b>ικεμπάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ikebana (Japanese flower arrangemant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Unadapted borrowing from Japanese 生け花 (ikebana, literally “living flowers”).</div><b>ικετεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to beseech, supplicate, beg, implore</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε <b>ικετεύω</b>! Μη μ’ εγκαταλείψεις! - I beg of you! Do not abandon me!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ἱκετεύω, from ἱκέτης, from verb ῑ̔́κω.</div><b>ικρίωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ικριώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scaffold (platform for executions)</div><b>ιλαρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) measles, rubeola (acute highly contagious disease caused by Measles virus of genus Morbillivirus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιος μου δεν πήγε σχολείο αυτή την εβδομάδα λόγω <b>ιλαράς</b>. - My son didn&#x27;t go to school this week because of measles.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἱλαρά, feminine form of Ancient Greek ἱλᾰρός, a euphemistic name for the disease.</div><b>ιλαροτραγωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ιλαροτραγωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tragicomedy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἱλαρός + τραγῳδία.</div><b>ιλλυρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ιλλυρική, neuter ιλλυρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of or relating to the ancient Illyria</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ἰλλυρικός.</div><b>ιμάμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ιμάμης">ιμάμης</a></div><b>ιμάμ μπαϊλντί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imam bayildi (a dish of baked stuffed eggplant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish امام بایلدی. Modern Turkish equivalent imambayıldı (“the priest has fainted”).</div><b>ιμάμης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ιμάμηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imam</div>
