  <div class='def'>5. (US) liquor store</div><b>κάβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cape, headland, promontory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cable, hawser</div><b>κάβουρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάβουρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://καβούρι">καβούρι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. plumber wrench, plumber&#x27;s wrench, Swedish wrench</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάβουρας, from Koine Greek κάβουρος, from Ancient Greek πάγουρος.</div><b>κάγκελο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάγκελα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rail, railing, banister (horizontal bar extending between supports and used for support or as a barrier)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κρατά το <b>κάγκελο</b> για να μην πέσεις. - Hold onto the rail so you don&#x27;t fall.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. baluster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάγκελον, from Latin cancellus.</div><b>κάγκουρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάγκουρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) petrolhead chav (UK), motorhead trailer trash (US) (a boorish and unsophisticated man obsessed with cars, motorbikes and other vehicles; usually also implies a show-off)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα πού πήγε και πάρκαρε ο μαλάκας ο <b>κάγκουρας</b>! - Look where that wanker petrolhead parked!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καγκουρό + -ας (-as).</div><b>κάδμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cadmium</div><b>κάδοι απορριμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://κάδος">κάδος</a> απορριμάτων</div><b>κάδοι απορριμμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://κάδος">κάδος</a> απορριμμάτων</div><b>κάδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bin, bucket, pail, tub, cask</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάδος απορριμμάτων (refuse bin)</div><b>κάδος απορριμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάδοι απορριμάτων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάδος">κάδος</a> απορριμμάτων</div><b>κάδος απορριμμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάδοι απορριμμάτων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in the UK) dustbin, rubbish bin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in the US) trashcan, garbage can</div><b>κάδρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάδρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frame, picture frame</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάδρον, from Venetan quadro, from Latin quadrum.</div><b>κάθαρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθάρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally, obsolete) scrap, rubbish (anything to be cleaned or thrown away)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively, offensive) scum, bastard, son of a bitch, asshole, prick (extremely unpleasant or objectionable person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν θέλω να ξαναδώ αυτό το <b>κάθαρμα</b> μπροστά μου. - I don&#x27;t ever want to see that bastard in front of me again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάθαρμα.</div><b>κάθαρση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθάρσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>catharsis, purging, purification</div><b>κάθε</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) every, each</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάθε</b> υποψήφιος καλείται να … - each candidate is required to …</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) any</div>
  <div class='ex'>με <b>κάθε</b> τίμημα - at any price</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) every, each</div>
  <div class='ex'>δουλεύει <b>κάθε</b> σαββατοκύριακο - he works every weekend</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (derogatory, indeclinable) any</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάθε</b> ανόητος μπορεί να … - any fool can …</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καθέν, neuter form of καθείς, from Ancient Greek καθ’ εἷς, from ἕν καθ’ ἕν.
Compare with Mariupol Greek ка́тъа.</div><b>κάθε καρυδιάς καρύδι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Tom, Dick and Harry</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τη νύχτα συναντάς <b>κάθε καρυδιάς καρύδι</b>. - At night you meet every Tom, Dick and Harry.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “every walnut of the walnut tree”.</div><b>κάθειρξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθείρξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imprisonment</div><b>κάθετα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κάθετος">κάθετος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vertically</div><b>κάθετα δοκάρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of κάθετο <a href="bword://δοκάρι">δοκάρι</a></div><b>κάθετο δοκάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάθετα δοκάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goalpost</div><b>κάθετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κάθετη or κάθετος, neuter κάθετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vertical, steep, sharp</div>
  <div class='def'>2. perpendicular, orthogonal, vertical (at or forming a right angle to)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) unquestionable, straight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κάθετος. Also substantivised. See the ancient verb καθίημι.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάθετοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vertical line, slash, virgule</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pipe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κάθετος. Also substantivised. See the ancient verb καθίημι.</div><b>κάθιδρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κάθιδρη, neuter κάθιδρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extremely sweaty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek κάθιδρος. Morphologically, from καθ- (κατα-) (kath- (kata-), “completely”) + ἱδρώς, ιδρώτας.</div><b>κάθισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a seat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sitting down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek κάθισμα, from Ancient Greek καθίζω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>κάθοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάθοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cathode</div>
  <div class='def'>2. decrease</div>
  <div class='def'>3. descent, route downwards</div>
  <div class='def'>4. route southwards</div>
  <div class='def'>5. participation in an election</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰ́θοδος.</div><b>κάθομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sit, sit down; to remain standing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sit, sit down; to stay, reside</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of activity, of mind) to be occupied with</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of activity, of mind) to remain idle, to be bored, doing nothing, settle</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου <b>κάθησε</b> μουσακάς στον λαιμό. - Moussaka was stuck in my throat.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of activity, of mind) to accept, be patient</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to retreat, to become sedimented (of things)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (idiomatic) to be of less efficiency, worsened results</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (idiomatic) to happen by fluke</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κάθομαι (“I stay, reside”) from Ancient Greek κάθημαι.</div><b>κάθομαι με σταυρωμένα χέρια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to sit on one&#x27;s hands (to be idle when action is needed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι περίμεναν να κάνει κάτι αλλά αυτή <b>καθόταν με σταυρωμένα χέρια</b>. - Everyone expected her to do something but she sat on her hands.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French rester les bras croisés.</div><b>κάιμαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) caiman, cayman</div><b>κάκα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://κακά">κακά</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://κακά">κακά</a></div><b>κάκτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάκτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cactus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from New Latin cactus, from the Ancient Greek κάκτος.</div><b>κάκωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κακώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lesion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάκωσις.</div><b>κάλαμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάλαμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) reed pen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) reed, cane</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) literary art of a writer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κάλαμος; doublet of καλάμι and καλέμι. Also see καλαμάρι.
The figurative sense, a Semantic loan from French plume.</div><b>κάλαντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carol or carols</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Christmas carol</div><b>κάλεσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλέσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>call (to arms, etc.)</div><b>κάλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural κάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) potassium</div>
  <div class='ex'>υδροξείδιο του καλίου - potassium hydroxide</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) alkali</div><b>κάλιον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://κάλιο">κάλιο</a></div><b>κάλλιο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in proverbs set phrases) better, preferable, sooner</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάλλιο</b> αργά παρά ποτέ - better late than never</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάλλιον, comparative form of Ancient Greek καλῶς.</div><b>κάλλιο αργά παρά ποτέ</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>better late than never</div><b>κάλλιο πέντε και στο χέρι παρά δέκα και καρτέρει</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush (It is preferable to have a small but certain advantage than a mere potential of a greater one.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;Better five in the hand than waiting for ten&quot;.</div><b>κάλλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάλλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beauty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάλλος.</div><b>κάλμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) calm seas, dead calm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Twice-borrowed word from Italian calma from Late Latin cauma from Ancient Greek καῦμα (the heat being felt in calm, hot weather)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calm down!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Twice-borrowed word from Italian calma from Late Latin cauma from Ancient Greek καῦμα (the heat being felt in calm, hot weather)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) calm down!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://καλμάρω">καλμάρω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://καλμάρω">καλμάρω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Twice-borrowed word from Italian calma from Late Latin cauma from Ancient Greek καῦμα (the heat being felt in calm, hot weather)</div><b>κάλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>callus, corn, bunion (area of hardened skin)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian callo.</div><b>κάλπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάλπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ballot box</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάλπη f and κάλπις f.</div><b>κάλτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάλτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sock</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stocking</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάλτσες</b> νάιλον - nylon stockings</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian calza (“sock, stocking”).</div><b>κάλυκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάλυκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bullet case, shell case, shell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. calyx, bud</div><b>κάλυμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλύμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cover, hood, lid, cap</div><b>κάλυψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλύψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coverage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. support, protection; protection (law)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. support, protection; camouflage</div>
  <div class='def'>4. support, protection; cover-up</div>
  <div class='def'>5. support, protection; completion (of a task)</div><b>κάλως</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάλωες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cable; thick rope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κάλως; possibly related to Latin colus.</div><b>κάμαρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάμαρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>room, bedroom, chamber</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κάμαρα, a reborrowing from Vulgar Latin camara, from Latin camera, itself from Ancient Greek κᾰμᾰ́ρᾱ.</div><b>κάμερα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάμερες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>camera (usually for film and video)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian camera.</div><b>κάμερα-μαν</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cameraman, camera operator</div><b>κάμνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάνω">κάνω</a></div><b>κάμπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) caterpillar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κάμπη.</div><b>κάμπια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάμπιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caterpillar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κάμπη.</div><b>κάμπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάμποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plain (large, flat lands)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάμπος, from Latin campus.</div><b>κάμποσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κάμποση, neuter κάμποσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enough, sufficient</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καμπόσος, from Ancient Greek κἂν πόσος.</div><b>κάμποχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) enough, a lot</div><b>κάμπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to become curved</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to yield, give in</div><b>κάμπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to bend, to curve</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to bend, to cause to yield (to force to submit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάμπτω</div><b>κάνιστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάνιστρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trug, basket (shallow; made of reed, plastic, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jerrycan (and similar liquid constainers)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bucket (on a hoist)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Ancient Greek κάναστρον, κάνιστρον.</div><b>κάνναβη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cannabis (plant of the genus Cannabis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ινδική <b>κάνναβη</b> - Indian hemp (Cannabis indica)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κάνναβις.</div><b>κάννη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάννες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gun barrel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κάννᾱ.</div><b>κάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to do</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>κάνω</b> ό,τι μου πεις. - I will do whatever you tell me (to do).</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to make</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάνω</b> τοστ - I make a toasted/grilled sandwich.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to cost</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πόσο <b>κάνει</b> η βενζίνη; - How much is the petrol?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to start or found (e.g. a company)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>κάνω</b> μια δική μου επιχείρηση. - I will start my own business.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to take (time)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ταξίδι <b>κάνει</b> τρεις ώρες. - The journey takes three hours.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to be (weather: cloudy, hot, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι καιρό θα <b>κάνει</b> αύριο; - What will the weather be tomorrow?</div>
  <div class='def'>7. to produce, give or yield (crop, produce)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάνω</b> μήλα - produce apples</div>
  <div class='def'>8. to act, impersonate, play a role</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάνω</b> τον βλάκα - play the fool</div>
  <div class='def'>9. to spend (time)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έκανα</b> δύο εβδομάδες στην Αθήνα. - I spent two weeks in Athens.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάμνω.</div><b>κάνω εμετό</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to vomit</div><b>κάνω κακά</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(childish, euphemistic, informal) to poo, poop (to defecate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μωρό <b>έκανε κακά</b> στο πάτωμα. - The baby pooed on the floor.</div><b>κάνω καμάκι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pick up, flirt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;to do/make a spear&quot;.</div><b>κάνω τα στραβά μάτια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to turn a blind eye, to look the other way</div><b>κάνω του κεφαλιού μου</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to do whatever one wants, do anything one wants (usually implies recklessness and lack of consideration)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην προσπαθείς να την πείσεις, αυτή <b>κάνει του κεφαλιού της</b>. - Don&#x27;t try to persuade her, she does whatever she wants.</div><b>κάνω τόπι στο ξύλο</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to beat up, beat to a pulp, beat the crap out of</div><b>κάνω ό,τι μου κατέβει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to do whatever one wants, do anything one wants (usually implies recklessness and lack of consideration)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός πάντα <b>έκανε ό,τι του κατέβει</b>, και κοίτα πως κατάντησε στο τέλος. - He always did whatever he wanted, and look how he ended up in the end.</div><b>κάπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. A shepherd&#x27;s cape</div>
  <div class='def'>2. A woman&#x27;s long sleeveless overcoat or cloak.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. alternative form of <a href="bword://κάππα">κάππα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Late Latin cappa, possibly through Italian cappa.</div><b>κάπαρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάπαρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. caper (plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. caper (flower bud)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. caper (pickled bud)</div><b>κάπνισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) smoking</div>
  <div class='ex'>απαγορεύεται το <b>κάπνισμα</b> - no smoking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καπνίζω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>κάπνιστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάπνιστρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) bee smoker</div><b>κάποιος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. someone, somebody (substantivally; as a pronoun)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κάποιος</b> σε πήρε τηλέφωνο. - Someone phoned you.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. some (adjectivally; as a determiner)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάποιο</b> βιβλίο - some book</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κἄν + ποῖος.</div><b>κάποτε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sometime; formerly, once (of past situations); at some point (of future situations) (an unstated or indefinite time)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κάποτε</b> την αγαπούσα αλλά όχι πια. - I loved her once but not anymore.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sometimes ..., at other times ...; sometimes ..., sometimes ...</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κάποτε</b> μου λέει ότι με αγαπάει, <b>κάποτε</b> όχι. - Sometimes he tells me he loves me, other times he doesn&#x27;t.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάποτε, from κἄν and ποτέ.</div><b>κάπου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. somewhere</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κάπου</b> έβαλα τα κλειδιά και τώρα δεν τα βρίσκω. - I put the keys somewhere and now I can&#x27;t find them.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. about, around</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κάπου</b> τρία κιλά - about three kilos</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κἄν + πού.</div><b>κάπου-κάπου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>now and then</div><b>κάππα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kappa, the tenth letter of the Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάππα.</div><b>κάππαρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάππαρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάπαρη">κάπαρη</a></div><b>κάπρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάπροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wild boar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boar, male pig</div><b>κάπως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. somewhat, kind of, a bit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. somehow, in some way</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of an unattested Byzantine Greek *κάν πως. The *κάν is identical to καν (kan, “even”), and the πως is identical to πως (pos) and used in the sense of the Ancient Greek phrase ἐάν πως.</div><b>κάρβουνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάρβουνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. charcoal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάρβουνον, from Koine Greek κάρβων, from Latin carbo (“carbon”).
Cognate with Mariupol Greek карво́н.</div><b>κάργα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fully, entirely, to the tonsils</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan carga.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) fully, entirely, to the tonsils</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan carga.</div><b>κάργια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάργιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jackdaw</div><b>κάρδαμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάρδαμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cardamom, cardamon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάρδαμον.</div><b>κάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάρυ">κάρυ</a></div><b>κάρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a cart; truck with four wheels</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a cart; truck with four wheels; a vehicle in old and poor condition; a jalopy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) a poorly-made (i.e. ugly) woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κάρρον.</div><b>κάρρυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάρυ">κάρυ</a></div><b>κάρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. card</div>
  <div class='def'>2. credit card, debit card</div>
  <div class='def'>3. postcard</div>
  <div class='def'>4. business card</div>
  <div class='def'>5. birthday card, greeting card</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian carta, from Latin charta, from Ancient Greek χάρτης. Doublet of χάρτης.</div><b>κάρτα γενεθλίων</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάρτες γενεθλίων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>birthday card</div><b>κάρτα επιβίβασης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάρτες επιβίβασης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boarding pass, boarding card</div><b>κάρτες γενεθλίων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of <a href="bword://κάρτα">κάρτα</a> γενεθλίων</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of <a href="bword://κάρτα">κάρτα</a> γενεθλίων</div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of <a href="bword://κάρτα">κάρτα</a> γενεθλίων</div><b>κάρυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) curry (the spice and the dish prepared from it)</div>
  <div class='ex'>σκόνη <b>κάρυ</b> - curry powder</div><b>κάρφωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρφώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nailing, the usage of a nail.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) backstabbing, betrayal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Any point scored by moving the ball downwards, especially if done quickly.; dunk, dunking</div>
  <div class='def'>4. Any point scored by moving the ball downwards, especially if done quickly.; spike, spiking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κάρφωμα. By surface analysis, καρφώνω + -μα (-ma, action of effect).</div><b>κάσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coffin, casket (oblong closed box for burying the dead)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strongbox, coffer, chest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian cassa.</div><b>κάσκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάσκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>helmet (for cycling, motorcycling, police, etc)</div><b>κάστανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάστανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chestnut (fruit of the tree)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάστανον, which is mostly attested in the plural.</div><b>κάστορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κάστορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beaver (aquatic mammal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάστωρ + nominal suffix -ας (-as).</div><b>κάστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάστρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. castle, stronghold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. city walls</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάστρον, from Latin castrum, from Proto-Italic *kastrom.</div><b>κάταγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατάγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fracture, break (bone injury)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάταγμα.</div><b>κάτασπρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κάτασπρη, neuter κάτασπρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(for color/colour) snow-white, lily-white (completely white)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μες σ’ αυτή τη βάρκα είμαι μοναχή,
Κι έχω συντροφιά μου <b>κάτασπρο</b> πουλί. - In this boat, I am all alone,
And I have for company a snow-white bird.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κάτ- + άσπρος.</div><b>κάτι</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) something</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάτι, from κἄν + τι (ti), from Ancient Greek καί + ἄν + τι (ti).</div><b>κάτι τρέχει στα γύφτικα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>so what?; whatever; and I care because?; don&#x27;t care; as if I care; no consequence to me; not my problem; big deal; meh; not impressed; (idiomatic): not my circus, not my monkeys; don&#x27;t give a hoot; that won&#x27;t keep me warm at night; (sarcastic, idiomatic): whoop-dee-do, yay, I almost care</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> literally &quot;there is trouble in the gypsy village&quot;</div><b>κάτισχνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κάτισχνη, neuter κάτισχνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, emphatic, formal) extremely thin, emaciated, scrawny</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κάτισχνος. By surface analysis, κάτ- + ισχνός.</div><b>κάτοικος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κάτοικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inhabitant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάτοικος.</div><b>κάτοπτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάτοπτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reflector</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mirror</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κάτοπτρον. Doublet of καθρέφτης.</div><b>κάτουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κάτουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>urine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κάτουρον, back formation from κατουρῶ, from Ancient Greek κατουρῶ, κατουρέω.</div><b>κάτοχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κάτοχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>holder, owner, keeper (a person who possesses something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ευθύνη που έχει ο <b>κάτοχος</b> ενός αξιώματος θα πρέπει να είναι απολύτως ξεκάθαρη. - The responsibility that a holder of office has should be absolutely clear.</div><b>κάτοχος κάρτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κάτοχοι καρτών)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cardholder</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν ο <b>κάτοχος κάρτας</b> χρειάζεται υγειονομική περίθαλψη κατά τη διάρκεια της παραμονής του, η κάρτα του εξασφαλίζει το δικαίωμα να λάβει αυτή την υγειονομική περίθαλψη από ιατρικό προσωπικό. - If the cardholder is in need of health care during their stay, the card entitles them to receive this care from the medical staff.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adjective κάτοχος m or f -verb κατέχω- and genitive singular of κάρτα f</div><b>κάτω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. down</div>
  <div class='def'>2. below, beneath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάτω.
Compare Mariupol Greek ка́ту.</div><b>κάτω από το ζυγό</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>under the yoke (enslaved)</div><b>κάτω γνάθοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://κάτω">κάτω</a> <a href="bword://γνάθος">γνάθος</a></div><b>κάτω γνάθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάτω γνάθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mandible, lower jawbone</div><b>κάτω σε</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. down (expressing direction towards a lower level)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. along, down (expressing movement)</div><b>κάψα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάψες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. excessive heat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) intense desire</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) intense sexual desire</div>
  <div class='def'>4. capsule; membranous envelope</div>
  <div class='def'>5. capsule; type of simple, dehiscent, dry fruit</div>
  <div class='def'>6. capsule; sporangium</div>
  <div class='def'>7. capsule; small case of gelatine containing a dose of medicine</div>
  <div class='def'>8. button microphone</div>
  <div class='def'>9. fire resistant container</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καίω.</div><b>κάψιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καψίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. burning, burn (the act by which something burns or is burned)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. burn mark (on skin)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) burn (a sensation resembling such an injury)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the καψ- stem of καίω + -ιμο (-imo).</div><b>κάψουλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κάψουλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capsule, small case of gelatine containing a dose of medicine</div><b>κέδρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κέδροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cedar (Cedrus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κέδρος.</div><b>κέικ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) baked cake of various flavours, but not layers of fillings</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unadapted borrowing from English cake, hence the final consonant [k], not encountered in Greek.</div><b>κέλβιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kelvin (measuring absolute or thermodynamic temperature)</div><b>κέλτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κέλτικη, neuter κέλτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Celtic, Gaelic (relating to the Celtic people of Ireland, Scotland and the Isle of Man and their languages and customs)</div><b>κέλυφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κελύφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shell (of mollusks, crustaceans, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. eggshell</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nutshell</div>
  <div class='def'>4. shell, casing (around instrument or mechanism)</div><b>κένταυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κένταυροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek) centaur (mythical beast that is half man, half horse)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Φιρένζε ήταν <b>κένταυρος</b> στη σειρά «Χάρι Πότερ». - Firenze was a centaur in the Harry Potter series.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κένταυρος.</div><b>κέντημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. embroidery</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sting, prick</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) excellent work, work done in a very delicately way</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) work which must be done very precisely or accurately</div><b>κέντρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεντρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stab, prick</div>
  <div class='def'>3. graft</div><b>κέντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κέντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. centre (UK), center (US): the middle of something or somewhere</div>
  <div class='ex'>κέντρο της πόλης (town centre)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. centre (building, institution, club, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κέντρο υγείας (health centre)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. centre</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) centre (the focus or concentrated point)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το κέντρο της πνευματικής ζωής (the focus of intellectual life)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κέντρον, from Proto-Hellenic *kéntron, from Proto-Indo-European *ḱéntrom.</div><b>κέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κέρατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. horn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. horn (wind instrument)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κέρας.</div><b>κέρας της Αμάλθειας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cornucopia, horn of plenty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κέρας of Αμάλθεια</div><b>κέρατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. horn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) adultery</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) protuberance</div><b>κέρδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κέρδη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>profit, gain (material or financial)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κέρδος.</div><b>κέρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κέρινη, neuter κέρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wax (made of wax)</div><b>κέρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κέρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coin</div><b>κέτσαπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (UK, indeclinable, sometimes) ketchup, (especially) tomato ketchup</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (UK, indeclinable, sometimes) tomato sauce</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (US, indeclinable, sometimes) catsup</div><b>κέφαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κέφαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mullet, grey mullet, flathead mullet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek κέφᾰλος.</div><b>κέφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κέφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. high spirits, good humour, gaiety, merriness, merriment</div>
  <div class='ex'>—Ζορμπά [...] Θα τρώμε και θα πίνουμε μαζί. Κι ύστερα θα παίζεις σαντούρι.
—Αν έχω <b>κέφι</b>, ακούς; Αν έχω <b>κέφι</b>. Να σου δουλεύω όσο θες σκλάβος σου! Μα το σαντούρι είναι άλλο πράμα. Είναι θεριό, θέλει λευτεριά. Αν έχω <b>κέφι</b>, θα παίζω· θα τραγουδώ κιόλα. Και θα χορεύω.... - —Zorba … We&#x27;ll eat and drink together, and then, you&#x27;ll play the santouri
—If I&#x27;m in the mood, do you hear? If I&#x27;m in the mood! As far as working for you as much as you want, I&#x27;m your slave. But the santouri is something else and it needs freedom. I&#x27;ll play if I feel like it. I&#x27;ll even sing, and I&#x27;ll dance...</div>
  <div class='def'>2. good mood, disposition</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχω <b>κέφι</b> για δουλειά σήμερα. - I am in no mood for work today.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. good condition (of health), in form</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είμαι σε μεγάλα <b>κέφια</b> σήμερα. - I am in exceptionally good form today.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كیف (keyf, literally “state, disposition”), from Arabic كَيْف (kayf, “state, disposition”).</div><b>κέϊκ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://κέικ">κέικ</a></div><b>κήλεος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>celosia (Celosia)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, a phono-semantic matching of translingual Celosia, borrowed from Epic Greek κήλεος. Seemingly otherwise unrelated to Celosia.</div><b>κήλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hernia, rupture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κήλη.</div><b>κήπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κήποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>garden (especially one around a house)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κῆπος.</div><b>κήρυλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κήρυλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pied kingfisher, Ceryle rudis</div><b>κήρυξη πολέμου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>declaration of war</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See κήρυξη f &amp; genitive singular of πόλεμος m</div><b>κήτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κήτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cetacean: whale, dolphin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sea monster</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Cetus (constellation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κῆτος.</div><b>κίβδηλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κίβδηλη, neuter κίβδηλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>false, fake, counterfeit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κίβδηλος.</div><b>κίμινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κύμινο">κύμινο</a></div><b>κίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cinchona (South American tree)</div>
  <div class='ex'>See synonym κιχγόνη (kichgóni). For the dye, see χένα f (chéna) and κινά n (kiná)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cinchona bark</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English quina, from Spanish quina.</div><b>κίναιδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κίναιδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) homosexual, gay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κί̆ναιδος.</div><b>κίνδυνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κίνδυνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>danger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek κῐ́νδῡνος.</div><b>κίνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κινήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. movement (grouping working towards a common aim)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το συνδικαλιστικό κίνημα (the trades union movement)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mutiny, conspiracy, coup (group intent on radical social change)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κίνημα. Doublet of σινεμά, a reborrowing from French.</div><b>κίνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κινήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. movement (physical motion)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. movement</div>
  <div class='def'>3. traffic</div>
  <div class='def'>4. blink (of eye)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. flow</div>
  <div class='def'>6. activity</div><b>κίνητρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κίνητρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. motivation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incentive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κῑ́νητρον, or else coined independently in modern Greek as κινώ + -τρο (-tro).</div><b>κίονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κίονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>column (with a round cross-section)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κίων, κίονα in the accusative case.</div><b>κίουι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kiwi (bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English kiwi.</div><b>κίσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κίσσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jay, Eurasian jay</div><b>κίτρινη κάρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κίτρινες κάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yellow card</div><b>κίτρινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κίτρινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yellow (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κίτρινος">κίτρινος</a></div><b>κίτρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κίτρινη, neuter κίτρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pale, pallid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. yellow (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κίτρινος with the adjectival suffix -ινος (-inos)), from κίτρον, from Latin citrus, citrum, itself probably via Etruscan ultimately from Ancient Greek κέδρος.</div><b>κίτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κίτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>citron (fruit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Older form: τὸ κίτρον</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κίτρον, from Latin citrum &lt; citrus, itself via Etruscan probably from Ancient Greek κέδρος.</div><b>κίτρου</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Κίτρου (feminine) genitive singular of <a href="bword://Κίτρος">Κίτρος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. <a href="bword://κίτρου">κίτρου</a> (neuter) genitive singular of <a href="bword://κίτρο">κίτρο</a></div><b>κίτρων</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Κίτρων (feminine) genitive plural of <a href="bword://Κίτρος">Κίτρος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. <a href="bword://κίτρων">κίτρων</a> (neuter) genitive plural of <a href="bword://κίτρο">κίτρο</a></div><b>κίχλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κίχλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turdus philomelos: song thrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κίχλη.</div><b>κα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://κυρία">κυρία</a>: Mrs, Ms</div><b>καΐκι</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caique (a small sea-going vessel used for trading or fishing, originally made of wood and powered by sail)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قایق.</div><b>καΐσι</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καϊσί">καϊσί</a></div><b>καίγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to burn, be set on fire, be set alight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of strong) be eager to, anxious to, crave, (idiomatic): be burning (e.g., interest), be itching to</div><b>καίγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καίω">καίω</a></div><b>καίσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) caesium (alkali metal)</div><b>καίω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to burn, burn down, set on fire</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cremate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to singe</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to sterilise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καίω; the form καίγω, from Medieval καίγω, from Proto-Hellenic *kayyō. Also see καύση.</div><b>καβάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (obsolete) equestrianism, horsemanship</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (obsolete) cavalry</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (vulgar) sexual intercourse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καβάλα, borrowed from Venetan cavala or Late Latin caballa. The adverbialization of the noun occurred analogously to γραμμή.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. astride (with one’s legs on either side)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. astride (with one’s legs on either side); horseback, donkeyback (etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καβάλα</b> σε ένα άλογο - astride a horse, on horseback</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καβάλα, borrowed from Venetan cavala or Late Latin caballa. The adverbialization of the noun occurred analogously to γραμμή.</div><b>καβίλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καβίλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wooden dowel, peg, treenail (used in carpentry and shipbuilding)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. marlinspike</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian caviglia (“peg”).</div><b>καβαλάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rider</div>
  <div class='def'>2. saddle (bridge nut)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Late Latin caballārius.</div><b>καβαλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to straddle, sit astride</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to mount, ride</div><b>καβαλάω το καλάμι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, idiomatic) to be too big for one&#x27;s boots, to be too big for one&#x27;s britches, to get above oneself, be full of oneself, to think of oneself as the cat&#x27;s meow, to act all high and mighty, to put on airs, to think the sun comes up just to hear them crow, to be on one&#x27;s high horse, to be all broth and no beans, to be all hat and no cattle, to have a big head, to act puffed up, act stuck up, be on an ego trip, to be a stuffed shirt, to need a slice of humble pie, to think the world revolves around oneself</div>
  <div class='ex'>(act with an undue opinion of one&#x27;s own importance or abilities)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. act haughty, be cocky, be supercilious, be narcissistic, overconfident, hoity-toity, boastful, sanctimonious, pompous, smug, snooty, conceited, to have or act as if having a superiority complex</div><b>καβαλέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καβαλέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. easel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sawhorse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan cavaletto a diminutive form of cavallo (“horse”).</div><b>καβαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καβαλάω">καβαλάω</a></div><b>καβγάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καβγάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>argument, quarrel, row</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish غوغا, from Classical Persian غوغا.</div><b>καβγαδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to argue, to quarrel, to row, to fight (to have a verbal dispute)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the καβγαδ- stem of καβγάς + -ίζω.</div><b>καβουρδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καβουρντίζω">καβουρντίζω</a></div><b>καβουρντίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to brown (food - while cooking)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to roast (coffee beans, meat, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to burn (while sun tanning)</div><b>καβούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καβούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crab (crustacean)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Cf. κάβουρας.</div><b>καγιανάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καγιανάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>omelette</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قایغنه (kaygana).</div><b>καγκελάριος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καγκελάριοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chancellor</div><b>καγκουρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kangaroo</div><b>καδένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καδένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chain at neck, wrist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fob (short chain or ribbon)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan cadena, from Latin catēna.</div><b>καδράρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be framed (of a picture)</div><b>καδράρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to frame (of a picture)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κάδρο (“frame”) + -άρω (“ending for verbs”).</div><b>καζάκικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καζάκικη, neuter καζάκικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καζακικός">καζακικός</a></div><b>καζάχικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καζάχικη, neuter καζάχικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καζακικός">καζακικός</a></div><b>καζίνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καζίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>casino</div><b>καζακικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Kazakh (the language of Kazakhstan and its people)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://καζακικός">καζακικός</a></div><b>καζακικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καζακική, neuter καζακικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Kazakh (relating to Kazakhstan or its people or language)</div><b>καζαμπούμπου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, possibly) black man, negro</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the line αμάν, ο Καζαμπούμπου! in 1962&#x27;s movie Μην είδατε τον Παναή;, exclaimed by the main character after seeing his own face in the mirror blackened by chimney fumes, referring to Joseph Kasa-Vubu who in 1960 had become the first Congolese president after the end of Belgian colonial rule.</div><b>καζαχικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καζακικά">καζακικά</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://καζαχικός">καζαχικός</a></div><b>καζαχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καζαχική, neuter καζαχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καζακικός">καζακικός</a></div><b>καημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καημένη, neuter καημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with an article before) poor (expressing sympathy etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>καημένος</b> ο παππούς δεν αισθάνεται καλά αυτό τον καιρό. - Poor Grandad isn&#x27;t feeling well this weather.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the passive participle of Ancient Greek καίω.</div><b>καημός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καημοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>longing, yearning, heartache</div><b>καθ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of <a href="bword://κατά">κατά</a></div><b>καθ&#x27; ύλην αρμόδιος</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the person (especially the official) relevant or responsible in a matter</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>καθ’ ύλην αρμόδιος</b> υπουργός δεν απάντησε στα ερωτήματά μας. - The responsible minister did not answer our questions.</div><b>καθάριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καθάρια, neuter καθάριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clean, straight</div><b>καθάρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθαρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cleaning, scouring, cleaning up</div>
  <div class='ex'>ανοιξιάτικο <b>καθάρισμα</b> - spring cleaning</div><b>καθέκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθέκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://καρέκλα">καρέκλα</a></div><b>καθέλκομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be launched</div><b>καθέλκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καθελκύω">καθελκύω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καθέλκω.</div><b>καθέναν</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of καθένα</div><b>καθένας</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indefinite) each, each one (separately)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>καθένας</b> μας - each (one) of us</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indefinite) everyone, everybody</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ξέρει ο <b>καθένας</b>! - Everyone knows it!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek καθείς, from Ancient Greek καθ’ εἷς</div><b>καθήκον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθήκοντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>duty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καθήκοντα, from κᾰθήκω.</div><b>καθίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to make to sit down, seat</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καθίζω</b> το μωρό στο καρεκλάκι για να το ταΐσω. - I put the baby in the baby chair to feed it.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) to make to sit down, seat; to make to sit down and listen to directions</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με <b>κάθισε</b> κάτω κι άρχισε να με μαλώνει. - He/she sat me down and started giving instructions.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to settle (sink gradually to a lower level)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το καράβι <b>κάθισε</b> στα ρηχά. - The boat has run aground in the shallows.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καθίζω.</div><b>καθίζω στο σκαμνί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic) to take to court (of law)</div><b>καθίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, dated, literally) chamber pot, potty (a container used for urination and defecation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, offensive) bastard, son of a bitch, asshole, prick (extremely unpleasant or objectionable person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε από ’δω, ρε <b>καθίκι</b>! - Get out of here, you bastard!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καθοίκιν, from κατά + οἶκος, due to the fact that it refers to household chamber pots. The hypercorrect form with καθ- is due to false association with καθίζω.
Due to its actual etymology, the Babiniotis Dictionary uses the alternative form καθοίκι even though in reality it is the less common one encountered.</div><b>καθίσταμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to become, be</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καθίσταται</b> αδύνατο … - it is becoming impossible …; it is impossible …</div><b>καθαρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to clean, to wash</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to peel, to descale</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to cleanse, to purify</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (slang) to kill, do away with, take someone out, eliminate, whack, (idiomatic): clean up one&#x27;s mess</div><b>καθαρίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθαρίστριες, masculine καθαριστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female cleaner, charwoman (dated British)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the καθαρισ- stem of καθαρίζω + -τρια (-tria).</div><b>καθαρεύουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Katharevousa (purist variant of Modern Greek)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine present participle of Ancient Greek καθαρεύω, from καθαρός.</div><b>καθαρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be clean, to be pure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to speak or write in a puristic manner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καθαρεύω.</div><b>καθαρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καθαρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scouring, cleaning up</div>
  <div class='ex'>σκόνη <b>καθαρισμού</b> - scouring powder</div><b>καθαριστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καθαριστές, feminine καθαρίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cleaner (used for all cleaners or a male cleaner)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καθαριστής, equivalent to καθαρ- (kathar-, “to wash”) + -ιστής.</div><b>καθαριότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθαριότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cleaning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cleanliness</div><b>καθαρτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθαρτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the purgatory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καθαρτήριον.</div><b>καθαρτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laxative</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://καθαρτικός">καθαρτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καθαρτικός">καθαρτικός</a></div><b>καθαρτικό άλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(obsolete, uncountable) magnesium sulphate, Epsom salts</div><b>καθαρτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καθαρτική, neuter καθαρτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. laxative</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cleansing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καθαρτικός.</div><b>καθαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καθαρή, neuter καθαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clean, clear</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pure, undefiled</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Judaism) kosher</div>
  <div class='def'>4. perfect (as in perfect fourth)</div><b>καθείς</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καθένας">καθένας</a></div><b>καθελκύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be launched</div><b>καθελκύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to launch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καθέλκω + -ύω. Morphologically, καθ- + ελκύω (“drag”).</div><b>καθεμίαν</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of καθεμία</div><b>καθεμιάν</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of καθεμιά</div><b>καθεξής</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) forth (forward in time, place, degree) used only in the expression: <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://ούτω">ούτω</a> <a href="bword://καθεξής">καθεξής</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καθεξῆς. See the expression και ούτω καθεξής.</div><b>καθεστώς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθεστώτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. regime</div>
  <div class='def'>2. status quo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nominalization of Ancient Greek καθεστώς, passive perfect participle of καθίστημι.</div><b>καθετήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καθετήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>catheter, probe</div><b>καθετί</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) everything, all things</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλει να ξερει <b>καθετί</b>. - He wants to know everything.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) every thing, every single thing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θυμάται με το <b>καθετί</b>. - He remembers every single thing.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κάθε + τι (ti)</div><b>καθηγήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθηγήτριες, masculine καθηγητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. professor, university teacher, lecturer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. secondary school/high school teacher, schoolmistress</div>
  <div class='def'>3. language tutor</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) expert</div><b>καθηγητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καθηγητές, feminine καθηγήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. professor, university teacher, lecturer</div>
  <div class='ex'>αναπληρωτής <b>καθηγητής</b> - associate professor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. secondary school/high school teacher, schoolmaster</div>
  <div class='def'>3. language tutor</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) expert</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καθηγητής, from Ancient Greek καθηγέομαι + -τής, the former component from κατα- (kata-, “down, against”) + ἡγέομαι.</div><b>καθημερινά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://καθημερινός">καθημερινός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>every day, daily</div><b>καθημερινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καθημερινή, neuter καθημερινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. daily, every day</div>
  <div class='def'>2. everyday, ordinary, workaday, commonplace</div>
  <div class='def'>3. working day</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καθημερινός, from καθ’ ἡμέραν, from κατά + ἡμέρα.</div><b>καθημερινότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθημερινότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>daily routine, everyday life</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from καθημεριν(ός)) + -ότητα.</div><b>καθησυχάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to reassure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to calm down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καθησυχάζω. By surface analysis, καθ- (kath-) + ησυχάζω.</div><b>καθησυχαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καθησυχαστική, neuter καθησυχαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reassuring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the καθησυχασ- stem of καθησυχάζω + -τικός.</div><b>καθιδρύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://ιδρύομαι">ιδρύομαι</a>: &quot;to be founded&quot;</div><b>καθιδρύομεν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of καθιδρύομε and καθιδρύουμε</div><b>καθιδρύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://ιδρύω">ιδρύω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κᾰθιδρῡ́ω (“make sit down; establish”). Morphologically, from κατα- (καθ-) (“fully”) + ιδρύω (“found”).</div><b>καθιερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to establish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to consecrate, canonise</div><b>καθιζάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) (of earth) to subside of ground</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) (of particles in a solution) to settle down, as in sedimentation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καθιζάνω.</div><b>καθισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine καθισμένη, neuter καθισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of καθίζω of non-existent passive form of the verb. It is formed from active imperfective (or aorist) stem καθισ- + the ending -μένος for passive perfect participle.</div><b>καθιστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθιστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sitting room, lounge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. slow-moving, bottom dwelling sea organism</div><b>καθιστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to make, to appoint</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καθιστώ</b> (κάποιον) υπεύθυνο (για κάτι) - to make (someone) responsible (for something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καθιστῶ, from Ancient Greek καθίστημι.</div><b>καθοίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθοίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καθίκι">καθίκι</a></div><b>καθοδήγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθοδηγήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guidance, direction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καθοδήγησις. By surface analysis, καθοδήγη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>καθοδηγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to guide (to serve as a guide for someone or something; to lead or direct in a way; to conduct in a course or path)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to guide (to supervise the education or training of someone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καθοδηγῶ. By surface analysis, καθ- (kath-) + οδηγώ.</div><b>καθολικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθολικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. part of a church: space between narthex and sanctuary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. monastery chapel</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (universal) ledger</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://καθολικός">καθολικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καθολικός">καθολικός</a></div><b>καθολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καθολική, neuter καθολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. universal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. catholic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰθολῐκός.</div><b>καθομιλουμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθομιλούμενες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the standard language spoken by the majority of speakers</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the standard urban language of everyday life; colloquial.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of the middle disposition of Hellenistic Koine Greek καθομιλοῦμαι sc. language. From καθομιλέω / καθομιλῶ (“win the favour”) &lt; καθ’ + ὁμιλώ. As a linguistic term, probably a semantic loan from French langue commune.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative feminine singular of καθομιλούμενος: commonly spoken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of the middle disposition of Hellenistic Koine Greek καθομιλοῦμαι sc. language. From καθομιλέω / καθομιλῶ (“win the favour”) &lt; καθ’ + ὁμιλώ. As a linguistic term, probably a semantic loan from French langue commune.</div><b>καθορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to determine, stipulate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek καθορίζω. By surface analysis, καθ- (of κατα- (kata-, “thoroughly”) + ορίζω, the καθ- being harmonized with the old rough breathing of ὁρίζω.</div><b>καθοριστικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://καθοριστικός">καθοριστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>definitively</div><b>καθοριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καθοριστική, neuter καθοριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decisive, determining, crucial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καθοριστικός. By surface analysis, καθορίζω + -ικός.</div><b>καθρέπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καθρέπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καθρέφτης">καθρέφτης</a></div><b>καθρέφτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καθρέφτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mirror</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) looking glass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καθρέφτης, from earlier καθρέπτης, from unattested *κάθρεπτον &lt; *κάθροπτον, from Koine Greek κάθοπτρον, from Ancient Greek κάτοπτρον. Compare the borrowing κάτοπτρο.</div><b>καθρεφτάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καθρεφτάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://καθρέφτης">καθρέφτης</a> m</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καθρέφτ(ης)) + -άκι.</div><b>καθρεφτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to mirror, to reflect (show the image of on its surface by sending light back)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to mirror, to reflect (be an expression or manifestation of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek. By surface analysis, καθρέφτης + -ίζω.</div><b>καθυστέρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καθυστερήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. delay</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lag (bad Internet connection)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa καθυστέρησις, from καθυστερη- (kathysteri-) + -σις (-sis).</div><b>καθυστερημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine καθυστερημένη, neuter καθυστερημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://καθυστερώ">καθυστερώ</a>:; delayed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καθυστερημένη</b> αναχώρηση - delayed departure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://καθυστερώ">καθυστερώ</a>:; backward (underdeveloped)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://καθυστερώ">καθυστερώ</a>:; retarded (having mental retardation; mentally deficient or underdeveloped)</div><b>καθυστερώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to lag, fall short, delay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καθ- (kath-) + υστερώ.</div><b>καθόλου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. at all, a little</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχω <b>καθόλου</b> λεφτά. - I have no money at all.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. by no means</div>
  <div class='def'>3. any</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχεις <b>καθόλου</b> λεφτά; Δεν έχω <b>καθόλου</b>. - Do you have any money? I don&#x27;t have any at all.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καθόλου from Ancient Greek καθ’ (kath’) + ὅλου &quot;on the whole, generally&quot;, genitive of ὅλος.</div><b>καθότι</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>since, inasmuch as (because)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καθότι with semantic loan from French en tant que.</div><b>καθώς</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. as, while</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ευρώ φθίνει <b>καθώς</b> η ευρωπαϊκή κρίση χρεών εξαπλώνεται. - The euro is depreciating as the European debt crisis spreads.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. since, seeing as, because</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν βγήκα απόψε <b>καθώς</b> δεν είχα τα λεφτά. - I didn&#x27;t go out tonight since I didn&#x27;t have the money to.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καθώς, from καθ- (kath-)) + ὡς.</div><b>και</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. and</div>
  <div class='ex'>με γάλα <b>και</b> ζάχαρη - with milk and sugar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. also</div>
  <div class='ex'>ήταν <b>και</b> ο Γιώργος εκεί. - George was also there.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. plus, and</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύο <b>και</b> δύο κάνουν τέσσερα. - Two and two make four.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. past (time)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ώρα είναι δύο <b>και</b> τέταρτο. - The time is a quarter past two.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καί. Cognate with Tsakonian τσαι (tsai) and Italiot Greek ce (“and”).</div><b>και αν</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. and if</div>
  <div class='def'>2. even if</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Και αν</b> ήξερα, δεν θα στο έλεγα. - Εven if I knew, I wouldn&#x27;t tell you.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in a relative clause) “-ever“ ((who)ever, (what)ever, (when)ever, ...)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όπου <b>και αν</b> πάω, με διώχνουν. - Wherever I go, they chase me away.</div><b>και ας</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>even if, even though</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εγώ θα πάω <b>και ας</b> μη με θέλει εκεί. - I&#x27;ll go even if she doesn&#x27;t want me there.</div><b>και βάλε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://και">και</a> (kai), βάλε.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) to or more, if not more (that and even higher)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι σαράντα, μωρέ; Αυτή ήταν εξήντα <b>και βάλε</b>! - Forty? She was sixty if not more!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “and put”.</div><b>και λοιπά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://τα">τα</a> λοιπά</div><b>και λοιπά πολλά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>and many more</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From και (kai, “and”) + λοιπά + πολλά.</div><b>και ούτω καθεξής</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) and so forth, and so on</div>
  <div class='ex'>older spellings: καὶ οὕτω καθεξῆς, καὶ οὕτω καθ’ ἑξῆς</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See και (kai, “and”), ούτω &amp; καθεξής. According to Petrounias, a semantic loan from German und so weiter (usw.).</div><b>και τα λοιπά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>et cetera, etcetera, etc</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From και (kai, “and”) + τα (ta, “the”) + λοιπά.</div><b>καινοζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καινοζωική, neuter καινοζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cenozoic</div><b>καινοτομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καινοτομίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>innovation, novelty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek καινοτομία (“novelty, strangeness”). By surface analysis, καινο- + -τομία from καινός and τομή, τέμνω.</div><b>καινοφανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καινοφανής, neuter καινοφανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) newly-appearing, newly-visible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) unheard of, unprecedented, novel, new-fangled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καινοφᾰνής, from καινός + -φανής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καινοφανείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nova (sudden brightening of a previously inconspicuous star.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καινοφᾰνής, from καινός + -φανής.</div><b>καινούργιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καινούργια, neuter καινούργιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>new; recently acquired or made</div>
  <div class='ex'>αγόρασα <b>καινούργιο</b> αυτοκίνητο - I bought a new car</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymological spelling of the word as in the Hellenistic and mediaeval καινούργιος, from Ancient Greek καινουργός, from καινουργής + -ουργός), + -ιος (-ios).
Also see the modern spelling καινούριος.</div><b>καινούριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καινούρια, neuter καινούριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>modern spelling of <a href="bword://καινούργιος">καινούργιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Byzantine Greek and Hellenistic καινούργιος, without the presence of gamma &lt;γ&gt; since the pronunciation of -ιος for this word does not demand it: /ʝios/ &gt; /ʝos/.
 From Ancient Greek καινουργός, from καινουργής + -ουργός), + -ιος (-ios).
Also see the also commonly used, etymological spelling καινούργιος.</div><b>καιρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καιρική, neuter καιρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) weather (attributive)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καιρικά</b> φαινόμενα - weather phenomena</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καιρικός with semantic loan from English weather. By surface analysis, καιρ(ός)) + -ικός.</div><b>καιροσκόπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural καιροσκόποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>opportunist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καιροσκόπος.</div><b>καιρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καιροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. weather</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καιρού</b> επιτρέποντος - weather permitting (formal expression)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. time, events</div>
  <div class='ex'>από <b>καιρό</b> σε <b>καιρό</b> - from time to time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καιρός. The modern sense, since mediaeval times.</div><b>κακά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>badly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adverbial form of κακός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κακός">κακός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(childish, colloquial, informal, uncountable) poo, poop, crap</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλεις να κανείς <b>κακά</b> σου, παιδί μου; - Do you want to do a poo, my child?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰκκᾰ́ω.</div><b>κακά μάτια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://κακό">κακό</a> <a href="bword://μάτι">μάτι</a></div><b>κακάο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cocoa (raw material, powder and drink)</div><b>κακάσχημος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακάσχημη, neuter κακάσχημο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) butt-ugly, ugly as sin, hideous (exceptionally ugly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή είναι όμορφη αλλά η αδερφή της είναι <b>κακάσχημη</b>. - She&#x27;s beautiful, but her sister is as ugly as sin.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κακός + άσχημος</div><b>κακία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κακίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. evil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. malice, rancour</div>
  <div class='def'>3. grudge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κακία.</div><b>κακίστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κακίστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a woman) spiteful, sinister, vicious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From aorist stem κακισ- of κακίζω + -τρα (-tra).</div><b>κακίστρω</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural κακίστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a woman) synonym of <a href="bword://κακίστρα">κακίστρα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κακίστρ(α) + -ω (-o).</div><b>κακαρέλλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κακαρέλλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common two-banded sea bream (Diplodus vulgaris)</div><b>κακαρίσματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of κακάρισμα</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of κακάρισμα</div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of κακάρισμα</div><b>κακατού</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κακατούα">κακατούα</a></div><b>κακατούα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cockatoo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English cockatoo.</div><b>κακνόσπυρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κακνόσπυρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) corn</div><b>κακογαμημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κακογαμημένη, neuter κακογαμημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) badly-fucked (fucked in an unsatisfying, inadequate way)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή ποτέ δεν χαμογελάει. Πρέπει να είναι <b>κακογαμημένη</b> από τον άντρα της. - She never smiles. She must be being inadequately fucked by her husband.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κακο- (kako-, “bad”) + γαμημένος.</div><b>κακοκαιρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bad weather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κακο- (kako-) + καιρ(ός)) + -ιά.</div><b>κακοκεφιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κακοκεφιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>low spirits, gloominess, misery</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>κακοκεφιά</b> σήμερα. - I have (am in) low spirits today.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κακόκεφ(ος) (“gloomy”) (κακό- (“bad”)+ κέφι n (“mood”)) + -ιά.</div><b>κακολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to defame, malign, blacken</div><b>κακομάζαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακομάζαλη, neuter κακομάζαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ill-fated, miserable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κακομάζαλος.</div><b>κακομαθαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to spoil, pamper, indulge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to become pampered, indulgent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κακο- (kako-, “bad”) + μαθαίνω.</div><b>κακοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κακοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abuse, ill-treatment, maltreatment, mistreatment (cruel or harmful treatment)</div>
  <div class='ex'>παιδική <b>κακοποίηση</b> - child abuse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abuse (forcing of undesired sexual activity by one person on another)</div>
  <div class='ex'>σεξουαλική <b>κακοποίηση</b> - sexual abuse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. abuse (insulting speech)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: βρισιές f pl (vrisiés)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. abuse (misuse; improper use; perversion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κακοποίησις. By surface analysis, κακοποίη-) + -ση (-si) or κακο- (kako-) + -ποίηση.</div><b>κακοποιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακοποιός, neuter κακοποιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>criminal</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κακοποιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. criminal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thug</div><b>κακοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to assault; to knock about, beat up, rough up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to assault; to rape</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to assault; to manhandle</div><b>κακουργηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακουργηματική, neuter κακουργηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>felony (attributive), felonious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the κακουργηματ- stem of κακούργημα + -ικός.</div><b>κακοφανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κακοφανισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. resentment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. offence</div><b>κακούργημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κακουργήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>felony (most serious crime category, punishable by imprisonment of five years to life)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η δολοφονία αποτελεί <b>κακούργημα</b>. - Murder is a felony.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κακούργημα, from κακοῦργος, from κακός + ἔργον.</div><b>κακό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κακά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. evil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) poo, poop, poopies (excrement, faeces)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://κακός">κακός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κακός">κακός</a></div><b>κακό μάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κακά μάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>evil eye</div><b>κακό σκυλί ψόφο δεν έχει</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) a creaking door hangs longest, a creaking door hangs long on its hinges, the good die young (about evil or bad persons who hang on to life and refuse to die)</div><b>κακόβουλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακόβουλη, neuter κακόβουλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>malevolent, malicious, nefarious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κακόβουλος.</div><b>κακόγουστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακόγουστη, neuter κακόγουστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tasteless (lacking delicacy, refinement and good taste)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in bad taste, in poor taste (inappropriate, tactless)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of a person) having bad taste</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κακο- (kako-) + γούστ(ο)) + -ος (-os), a calque of French de mauvais goût.</div><b>κακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακή or κακιά, neuter κακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bad, evil, ill, wicked</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κακοί</b> άνθρωποι - wicked people</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰκός.</div><b>κακότροπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακότροπη, neuter κακότροπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grumpy, stroppy, quarrelsome, gruff, grouchy, cantankerous, curmudgeonly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κακός + τρόπος</div><b>κακότυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακότυχη, neuter κακότυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unfortunate, unlucky, ill-fated (not favoured by fortune)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κακότυχος, from Ancient Greek κακοτυχής. By surface analysis, κακο- (kako-) + τύχ(η)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>κακόφωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κακόφωνη, neuter κακόφωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cacophonous</div><b>καλά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://καλός">καλός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of καλό</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>well</div><b>καλά Χριστούγεννα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Merry Christmas!</div><b>καλά Χριστούγεννα και καλή χρονιά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Merry Christmas and a Happy New Year!</div><b>καλά κρασιά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, sarcastic) yeah, right, as if, whatever, dream on (sarcastic reply to something unlikely or unrelated to discussion)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα παντρευτώ τον Μπραντ Πιτ! — <b>Καλά κρασιά</b>! - I&#x27;m going to marry Brad Pitt! — Dream on!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “good wines”.</div><b>καλά να πάθεις</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>serves you right (you deserve the bad consequences of your actions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κλώτσησες το σκυλί και σε δάγκωσε; <b>Καλά να πάθεις</b>! - You kicked the dog and it bit you? Serves you right!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “well that it happens to you”.</div><b>καλά να πάθετε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>serves you right (you deserve the bad consequences of your actions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ληστέψατε το σπίτι και σας έπιασε η αστυνομία; <b>Καλά να πάθετε</b>! - You robbed a house and the police caught you? Serves you right!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “well that it happens to you”.</div><b>καλά ξεκουμπίδια</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>good riddance!</div><b>καλάθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλάθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. basket</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έβαλε τις φράουλες στο <b>καλάθι</b>. - He put the strawberries into the basket.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. goal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a target area for the ball</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καλάθι(ν)), from Koine Greek καλάθιον, the diminutive of Ancient Greek κάλαθος.</div><b>καλάι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) solder, alloy of tin and (traditionally) lead used to mend, coat, or join –typically small– metal objects.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قالای (kalay, “tin”) (Turkish kalay), from Arabic قَلَعِيّ (qalaʕiyy, “tin”).</div><b>καλάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλάμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reed, plant with hollow stem</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) shin</div>
  <div class='def'>3. telescopic rod (fishing, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καλάμιν, from Koine Greek καλάμιον, a diminutive of Ancient Greek κάλαμος.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek кала́м.</div><b>καλάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to submerge fishing nets</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to lower sails</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to set sail in the open sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan calar (“to sink, submerge”), from Vulgar Latin *calāre (“to loosen and let drop, submerge”), from Latin chalāre, from Ancient Greek χαλάω.</div><b>καλέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masculine vocative singular of <a href="bword://καλός">καλός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vocative singular of <a href="bword://καλός">καλός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) hey, oi (vocative interjection)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καλέ</b> αγόρια, που πάτε; - Hey boys, where are you going?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) will you, won&#x27;t you (request interjection)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγαινε <b>καλέ</b> και φερε το σκυλί για βόλτα! - Go and bring the dog for a walk, won&#x27;t you?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) wow, oh my, jeez, gosh (shock or surprise interjection)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καλέ</b>, κοίτα πόσο ψήλωσε το παιδί! - Oh my, look how tall the child&#x27;s become!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) well, well, well (irony or sarcasm interjection)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καλέ</b> κοίτα ποιος αποφάσισε να ’ρθει! - Well, well, well, look who decided to come!</div><b>καλέμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλέμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cold chisel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قلم (kalem)), whence modern Turkish kalem. Doublet of κάλαμος and καλάμι.</div><b>καλές γιορτές</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Happy Christmas, Merry Christmas, happy holidays</div><b>καλές τέχνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fine arts (purely aesthetic arts)</div><b>καλή Πρωτοχρονιά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Happy New Year</div><b>καλή τύχη</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>good luck!</div><b>καλή χρονιά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Happy New Year</div><b>καλή όρεξη</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bon appétit, enjoy your meal</div><b>καλαίσθητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλαίσθητη, neuter καλαίσθητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tasteful, having good taste</div>
  <div class='def'>2. well-mannered, elegant</div><b>καλαθοσφαίριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλαθοσφαιρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>basketball (formal, rare)</div>
  <div class='ex'>(colloquial, common)</div><b>καλαισθησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) good taste, aesthetic sense</div><b>καλαμάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαμάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small rod</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small wooden skewer</div>
  <div class='def'>3. drinking straw</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Diminutive of: καλάμι n</div><b>καλαμάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαμάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. squid (marine cephalopod)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic) inkwell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καλαμάρι(ο)νn), from Latin calamārius, ultimately from Ancient Greek κάλαμος.</div><b>καλαμίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαμίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fishing tackle, fishing rod</div><b>καλαμαράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (ironic) scholar, philologist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Cyprus) person from Greece</div><b>καλαματιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλαματιανή, neuter καλαματιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to Kalamata, a town in the Peloponnese</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλαματιανοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Kalamatianos, a dance originating in the Peloponnese</div><b>καλαμιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλαμιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reed (grass-like plant)</div><b>καλαμποκάλευρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαμποκάλευρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cornmeal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλαμπόκι + αλεύρι</div><b>καλαμποκέλαιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαμποκέλαια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corn oil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλαμπόκι + έλαιο</div><b>καλαμποκίσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλαμποκίσια, neuter καλαμποκίσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corn, maize</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καλαμποκίσιο</b> ψωμί - cornbread</div><b>καλαμποκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλαμποκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maize plant</div><b>καλαμποκόψωμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαμποκόψωμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cornbread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καλαμπόκ(ι), “maize, corn”) + -ό- (“infix”) + -ψωμο (-psomo, “bread”).</div><b>καλαμπουρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to joke, to pun</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καλαμπούρι + -ίζω.</div><b>καλαμπούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαμπούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joke, rag, pun, gag</div>
  <div class='ex'>διαρκώς κάνει <b>καλαμπούρια</b> - he&#x27;s always making jokes</div><b>καλαμπόκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαμπόκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corn, maize (the plant, the cob or the grain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Uncertain. Three possibilities:</div><b>καλαφάτισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαφατίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. caulking, caulk (the act of caulking or the material used when caulking)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) shag, bang, bonk, screw (an act of sexual intercourse)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλαφατίζω + -μα (-ma), the verb being from Italian calafato, from Arabic كلفت.</div><b>καλαφατικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλαφατικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caulking iron, caulker</div><b>καλδέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλδέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καλντέρα">καλντέρα</a></div><b>καλεσμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine καλεσμένη, neuter καλεσμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>invited as a guest, asked to attend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of καλούμαι, passive voice of καλώ. Also, substantivised.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλεσμένοι, feminine καλεσμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guest (male)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of καλούμαι, passive voice of καλώ. Also, substantivised.</div><b>καλημάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλημάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lapwing, peewit (the family of lapwings and plovers)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. northern lapwing</div><b>καλημέρα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>good morning, hello, good day</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καλημέρα</b>, τι κάνετε; - Good morning, how are you?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of καλή + μέρα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλημέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>good morning, hello</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σας μεταβιβάζω την <b>καλημέρα</b> του ξαδέρφου μας. - I bring our cousin&#x27;s hello.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of καλή + μέρα.</div><b>καλημερούδια</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(endearing, familiar) synonym of <a href="bword://καλημέρα">καλημέρα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καλημέρ(α)) + -ούδι pluralized as -ούδια.</div><b>καληνύκτα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καληνύχτα">καληνύχτα</a></div><b>καληνύχτα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goodnight!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καληνύχτα</b>! και όνειρα γλυκά! - Goodnight! and sweet dreams!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλή + νύχτα</div><b>καλησπέρα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>good evening</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καλή + εσπέρα.</div><b>καλιαρντά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Kaliarda (cant used by the homosexual community in Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Of unknown etymology. Possibly from French gaillard (“strong, sprightly, lively”).</div><b>καλιαρντός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) ugly man</div><b>καλικάντζαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλικάντζαροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gremlin, goblin (creature which according to Greek folklore lives underground and comes up on Christmas Day to cause minor mischief, like putting out fires, and disappearing back into the earth on the feast of the Epiphany)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος έσπασε το πιάτο; Πρέπει να ’ταν ο <b>καλικάντζαρος</b>, ε; - Who broke this plate? It must have been a gremlin, eh?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology unknown.</div><b>καλιφόρνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) californium</div><b>καλκάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλκάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. turbot (fish)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قلقان (kalkan, “shield”), and قلقان بالغی.</div><b>καλλίπυγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλλίπυγη, neuter καλλίπυγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>callipygian, callipygous (with beautifully shaped buttocks)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καλλίπυγος.</div><b>καλλίφωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλλίφωνη, neuter καλλίφωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melodious</div><b>καλλιέπεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) beautiful language, refined turn of phrase</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καλλιέπεια, from καλλι- (kalli-) + ἔπ(ος)) + -εια (-eia).</div><b>καλλιέργεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλλιέργειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. culture</div>
  <div class='ex'>κυττάρων μέσα <b>καλλιέργειας</b> - cell culture media</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) culture, cultivation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>καλλιέργεια</b> των καλύτερων διεθνών σχέσεων - the cultivation of better international relations</div>
  <div class='def'>3. culture, cultivation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καλλιέργεια.</div><b>καλλιγράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural καλλιγράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. calligrapher</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a person with attractive handwriting</div><b>καλλιγραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλλιγραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calligraphy, beautiful writing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καλλιγραφίᾱ.</div><b>καλλιεργήσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλλιεργήσιμη, neuter καλλιεργήσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cultivable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the καλλιεργήσ- stem of καλλιεργώ + -ιμος (-imos).</div><b>καλλιεργώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to grow, cultivate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορούμε να πειραματιζόμαστε άλλο με το τι <b>καλλιεργούμε</b> και το τι καταναλώνουμε. - We have to stop experimenting with what we grow and eat.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καλλιεργῶ, καλλιεργέω, verbal formation from Ancient Greek καλλι- (kalli-, “beautiful”) + ἔργον.</div><b>καλλιστήμονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλλιστήμονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>callistemon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Partial calque of translingual Callistemon. Doublet of Καλλιστήμων.</div><b>καλλιτέχνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλλιτέχνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>artist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek καλλιτέχνης, from Ancient Greek κάλλος + τέχνη + -ης. The modern sense of &quot;artist&quot; is a semantic loan from French artiste.</div><b>καλλιτεχνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλλιτεχνική, neuter καλλιτεχνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>artistic</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καλλιτεχνικός</b> διευθυντής - artistic director</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from καλλιτέχν(ης), “artist”) + -ικός.</div><b>καλλυντικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cosmetic</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://καλλυντικός">καλλυντικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καλλυντικός">καλλυντικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καλλυντικός">καλλυντικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καλλυντικός">καλλυντικός</a></div><b>καλλυντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cosmetic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καλλυντικός, from καλλυντής + -ικός, the former from Ancient Greek καλλύνω.</div><b>καλλωπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to beautify, to make beautiful, to embellish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to groom oneself (to attend to one&#x27;s appearance and clothing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καλλωπίζω.</div><b>καλλωπισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλλωπισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beautification</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grooming (care for one&#x27;s personal appearance, hygiene, and clothing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καλλωπισμός. By surface analysis, καλλωπίζω + -μός.</div><b>καλμάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to make calm, softer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be calm (mostly of the climate, the sea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian calmare.).</div><b>καλντέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλντέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caldera, crater</div><b>καλντερίμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλντερίμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) cobbled street (in towns or villages)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στα <b>καλντερίμια</b> συζητούν ως το πρωί γειτόνοι,
Μα σκοτεινιάζει ο καιρός και στις καρδιές νυχτώνει. - In the cobbled streets, the neighbours chat until morning,
But the weather darkens and night falls in their hearts.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) cobbled mule track (in countryside)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قالدرم.</div><b>καλντεριμιτζού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλντεριμιτζούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prostitute, sex worker</div><b>καλοήθης</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. benign</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη φοβάστε! Είναι <b>καλοήθης</b> όγκος. - Do not fear! It is a benign tumour.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary, rare) good-natured, virtuous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καλοήθης. In the medical sense, semantic loan from French bénin analogically to its antonym κᾰκοήθης. Morphologically, καλο- (kalo-) + -ήθης.</div><b>καλογεροπαίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλογεροπαίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>young monk</div><b>καλογερόπουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλογερόπουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>young monk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καλόγερος + -πουλο (-poulo, diminutive).</div><b>καλοκαίρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλοκαίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>summertime, summer</div>
  <div class='ex'>ντάλα <b>καλοκαίρι</b> - high summer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καλοκαίριν, from Ancient Greek καλοκαίριον. By surface analysis, καλός + καιρός.</div><b>καλοκαιρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>good weather, fine weather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned adaptation of Byzantine Greek καλοκαιριά, influenced by Koine Greek καλοκαιρία. By surface analysis, καλός + καιρός + -ία.</div><b>καλοκαιριάτικα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://καλοκαιριάτικος">καλοκαιριάτικος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, humorous, sometimes) in the summertime, in the summer (usually with reference to something not appropriate or usual for the season)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φοράει παλτό <b>καλοκαιριάτικα</b>; Σίγουρα θα πεθάνει από την ζεστή! - He&#x27;s wearing a coat in summer? He&#x27;ll surely die of heat!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλοκαίρ (“summer”) + -ιάτικα.</div><b>καλοκαιριάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλοκαιριάτικη, neuter καλοκαιριάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>resembling summer, summery</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι 20 Δεκεμβρίου, με <b>καλοκαιριάτικη</b> ζέστη. Μοιάζει με καλοκαιρινή μέρα. - It is the 20th of December, with a summery heat. It feels like a summer day.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλοκαίρ(ι), “summer”) + -ιάτικος.</div><b>καλοκαιρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλοκαιρινή, neuter καλοκαιρινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. summery, summer, summer- (occurring during, suitable for or typical of the season of summer)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καλοκαιρινές</b> διακοπές - summer holidays</div>
  <div class='def'>2. see vernacular expression: <a href="bword://το">το</a> <a href="bword://κάνω">κάνω</a> καλοκαιρινό</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλοκαίρι + -ινός.</div><b>καλομάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλομάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the good mother, the most loved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καλο- (kalo-) + -μάνα from καλός (“good”) and μάνα (“mom, ma”). Cognate with Pontic Greek καλομάνα.</div><b>καλομαθημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine καλομαθημένη, neuter καλομαθημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (used frequently) spoiled</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον φιλοξενήσαμε, τον περιποιηθήκαμε, αλλά είναι <b>καλομαθημένος</b> και δεν του άρεσε τίποτα. - We have invited him to stay, treated him with care, but he is spoiled and nothing was to his liking.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accustomed to an easy life, without deprivations</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν <b>καλομαθημένος</b> άνθρωπος και δεν άντεξε τις κακουχίες του πολέμου. - He was a man accustomed to comfortable life, so, he did not endure the war&#x27;s hardships.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. well-bred, well-mannered</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι ευγενέστατη, με καλούς τρόπους, <b>καλομαθημένη</b> από την οικογένειά της. - She is extremely polite, with good manners, well taught by her family.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of καλομαθαίνω, an active-only verb. The stem μαθη- (mathi-) from the Ancient Greek μανθάνω. From Byzantine Greek καλομαθαίνω: καλο- (“good, well”) + μαθαίνω.</div><b>καλομοίρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλομοίρα, neuter καλομοίρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) favoured by good fortune</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καλομοίρης. Morphologically, καλο- (kalo-, “good”) + μοίρ(α), “fate”) + -ης (-is).</div><b>καλοπέραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) well-being, good time</div><b>καλορίζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλορίζικη, neuter καλορίζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) fortunate, lucky (having good luck)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθαμε για τα <b>καλορίζικα</b>. - We came for good luck.</div><b>καλοριφέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) radiator (finned metal fixture that carries hot water or steam in order to heat a room)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) heating (system)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French calorifère.</div><b>καλοσκέφτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to think about, to think through, to think long and hard about</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καλο- (kalo-) + σκέφτομαι.</div><b>καλοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλοσύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kindness, goodness, benevolence (the state of being kind; disposition to do good)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. kindness, good deed (an instance of kind or charitable behaviour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλός + -οσύνη.</div><b>καλουπώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to construct formwork or shuttering</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cast (concrete etc)</div><b>καλοφάγωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλοφάγωτη, neuter καλοφάγωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (as a blessing) literally: well eaten, a sweet to be eaten with pleasure and satisfaction</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σου έφερα μια τούρτα για τα γενέθλιά σου. <b>Καλοφάγωτη</b>! - I&#x27;ve brought you a brithday cake. May it be eaten with pleasure!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) (of a newly acquired sum of money)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πληρώθηκες τον πρώτο σου μισθό; <b>Καλοφάγωτος</b> να είναι! - You received your first salary? Enjoy spending it!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From verb καλοτρώω from καλός + τρώω, stem of past tense φαγ- έφαγα + -ωτος (-otos, suffix for adjectives).</div><b>καλού-κακού</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>just in case</div><b>καλούδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, in plural) goody, tasty snack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καλός + -ούδι.</div><b>καλούπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλούπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mould (UK), mold (US) (anything in/around which something is formed/shaped)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shuttering, formwork</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> An αντιδάνειο: Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قالب, from Arabic قَالِب, from Koine Greek καλάπους, for which also see καλαπόδι.</div><b>καλπονοθεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλπονοθείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>electoral fraud</div><b>καλσόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tights (UK), pantyhose (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French caleçon.</div><b>καλυτέρευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>improvement, betterment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from καλυτερεύ(ω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>καλυτερεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to improve, to make better</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to improve, to get better</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καλυτερεύω. By surface analysis, καλύτερ(ος)) + -εύω.</div><b>καλωσήρθατε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καλώς">καλώς</a> ήρθατε (welcome)</div><b>καλωσήρθες</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καλώς">καλώς</a> ήρθες (welcome)</div><b>καλωσορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to welcome (a person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καλώς όρ(ισες)) + -ίζω.</div><b>καλωσόρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλωσορίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>welcome</div><b>καλό βράδυ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>good evening</div><b>καλό ταξίδι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bon voyage (have a good journey)</div><b>καλόγερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλόγεροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. monk, friar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, humorous) a solitary, ascetic, monkish person</div>
  <div class='def'>3. coat rack, hatstand</div>
  <div class='def'>4. great tit (bird)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. exposed rock at sea</div>
  <div class='def'>6. boil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καλόγηρος, from καλός + γῆρας.</div><b>καλόγρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλόγριες, masculine καλόγερος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nun, sister (member of a Christian religious community of women who live by certain vows and usually wear a habit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>καλόγριες</b> προσεύχονται κάθε μέρα. - Nuns pray every day.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively, humorous) prude, prig, puritan (woman who is or tries to be excessively proper, especially one who is easily offended by matters of a sexual nature)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Μαριώ δε θα έρθει, είναι σκέτη <b>καλόγρια</b> και δεν της αρέσουν τα πάρτι. - Maria won&#x27;t be coming; she&#x27;s a complete puritan and doesn&#x27;t like parties.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καλόγρια, from καλός + γριά.</div><b>καλόκαρδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλόκαρδη, neuter καλόκαρδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goodhearted, kindhearted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek. By surface analysis, καλο- (kalo-) + καρδ(ιά)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>καλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλή, neuter καλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. good</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nice, likeable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰλός (“beautiful, morally beautiful, of good quality”), from Proto-Indo-European *kal-wo-s, form of *kal- (“beautiful”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καλοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the good man</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>καλοί</b> θα πάνε στον Παράδεισο. - The good will go to Heaven.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. goodie, goody</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sweetheart</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰλός (“beautiful, morally beautiful, of good quality”), from Proto-Indo-European *kal-wo-s, form of *kal- (“beautiful”).</div><b>καλότυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καλότυχη, neuter καλότυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fortunate, lucky (favoured by fortune)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καλότυχος. By surface analysis, καλο- (kalo-) + τύχ(η)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>καλύβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλύβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hut, shack, hovel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cottage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καλύβη</div><b>καλύβη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλύβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καλύβα">καλύβα</a></div><b>καλύπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be covered</div><b>καλύπτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλύπτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. veil, cover</div>
  <div class='def'>2. root cap</div><b>καλύπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cover, coat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cover, provide covering fire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κᾰλῠ́πτω and semantic loan from French couvrir or English cover.</div><b>καλύτερος</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>better, nicer, more likeable. comparative degree of <a href="bword://καλός">καλός</a></div><b>καλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to call, hail (someone by name)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to summon, invite</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to dial, ring (by telephone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καλῶ, contracted form of καλέω.</div><b>καλώδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καλώδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cable, flex, cord</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cable (used in construction of bridges, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καλῴδιον, derived from κάλως.</div><b>καλών τεχνών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of καλές τέχνες</div><b>καλώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>well, all right, alright</div>
  <div class='ex'>έχει <b>καλώς</b> - all is well</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καλῶς. Cognate with Tsakonian καούρ.</div><b>καλώς ήρθατε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>welcome (second person plural, formal ‡) ^†</div><b>καλώς ήρθες</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>welcome (second person singular, familiar) ^†</div><b>καλώς ορίσατε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>welcome (second-person plural or formal ^†)</div><b>καλώς σας βρήκα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pleased to have found you - response to <a href="bword://καλώς">καλώς</a> ορίσατε (second-person plural, formal ^†)</div><b>καλώς σε βρήκα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pleased to have found you - response to <a href="bword://καλώς">καλώς</a> όρισες (second-person singular, informal ^†)</div><b>καλώς όρισες</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>welcome (second person singular or familiar ^†)</div><b>καμάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καμάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. harpoon, spear</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gaff</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) pickup, seduction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καμάκιον, diminutive from Ancient Greek κάμαξ.</div><b>καμάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. arch</div>
  <div class='def'>2. arch (the curved part of the bottom of a foot)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bridge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καμάρα.</div><b>καμήλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμήλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory) old cow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. camel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάμηλος.</div><b>καμίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καμίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. furnace, kiln, oven</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a very hot closed space</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (poetic) burning passion</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) scorcher (hot day)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) harsh conditions requiring great strength</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>καμίνι</b> του πολέμου - the hard conditions of war</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καμίνι(ν) n), from Koine Greek καμίνιον n, diminutive of Ancient Greek κάμινος f, of unknown, probably Pre-Greek origin (as suggested by the typically Pre-Greek suffix -ινος).</div><b>καμαρίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dressing room</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian camerino.</div><b>καμβάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καμβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. canvas (a mesh of loosely woven cotton strands to be decorated with needlepoint, cross-stitch, rug hooking, or other crafts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. canvas (a piece of cloth stretched across a frame on which one may paint)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) canvas (a basis for creative work)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French canevas.</div><b>καμεραμάν</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάμερα">κάμερα</a>-μαν</div><b>καμερουνέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καμερουνέζικη, neuter καμερουνέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cameroonian (relating to Cameroon or its people)</div><b>καμηλοπάρδαλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμηλοπαρδάλεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>giraffe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καμηλοπάρδαλις, from κάμηλος (camelus) + πάρδαλις (see also: cameleopard).</div><b>καμικάζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) kamikaze (suicidal attacker)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, indeclinable, slang) road hog (bad or inconsiderate driver)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English kamikaze, from Japanese 神風(かみかぜ) (kamikaze, “divine wind”).</div><b>καμινάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμινάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>domestic chimney</div><b>καμουφλάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) camouflage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French camouflage.</div><b>καμουφλάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be camouflaged</div><b>καμουφλάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to camouflage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French camoufl(er) + -άρω.</div><b>καμπάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμπάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bell (church bell, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bungalow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καμπάνα, from Late Latin campāna. Cognate: Italiot Greek kampàna.</div><b>καμπάνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμπάνιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>campaign (a series of operations undertaken to achieve a set goal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>προεκλογική <b>καμπάνια</b> - election campaign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian campagna and French campagne.</div><b>καμπίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμπίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ship&#x27;s cabin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. phone box, telephone box, telephone booth, telephone kiosk</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bathing cabin, bathing hut</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian cabina.</div><b>καμπίσια πέρδικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμπίσιες πέρδικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Perdix perdix : grey partridge, English partridge, Hungarian partridge, hun</div><b>καμπίσιες πέρδικες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of καμπίσια <a href="bword://πέρδικα">πέρδικα</a></div><b>καμπίσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καμπίσιοα, neuter καμπίσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of the) plain, plains</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καμπίσια</b> πέρδικα - grey partridge</div><b>καμπαναριό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καμπαναριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bell tower, belfry, campanile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καμπαναρειόν, from καμπαναρείον, derived from καμπανάρης, itself derived from καμπάνα, a borrowing from Late Latin campāna.</div><b>καμπανολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) campanology</div><b>καμπανούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμπανούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>campanula, bellflower (Campanula)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Campanula. By surface analysis, καμπάνα + -ούλα.</div><b>καμπούρα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://καμπούρης">καμπούρης</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> For the noun &quot;hump&quot;: Back-formation from καμπούρ(ης), “hunchback”) + feminine ending -α (-a), inherited from the medieval καμπούρης, from Ottoman Turkish قنبور (kanbur, kambur), possibly from Ancient Greek καμπύλος. Doublet of καμπύλος.
Also, a substantivised feminine adjective of καμπούρης.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμπούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hump, hunch (deformity on back caused by kyphosis, scoliosis, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hump (on a camel)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) hunchback, humpback (female hunched over or suffering from kyphosis, scoliosis, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> For the noun &quot;hump&quot;: Back-formation from καμπούρ(ης), “hunchback”) + feminine ending -α (-a), inherited from the medieval καμπούρης, from Ottoman Turkish قنبور (kanbur, kambur), possibly from Ancient Greek καμπύλος. Doublet of καμπύλος.
Also, a substantivised feminine adjective of καμπούρης.</div><b>καμπούρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καμπούρα, neuter καμπούρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) hunchback, humpback (person hunched over or suffering from kyphosis, scoliosis, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχε την τέντα ξομπλιαστή,
Η βάρκα του <b>καμπούρη</b> Αντρέα. - The awning was decorated with embroidery,
On hunchback Andreas&#x27;s boat.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καμπούρης, from Ottoman Turkish قنبور (kanbur, kambur), possibly from Ancient Greek καμπύλος (in which case, it would be a reborrowing), from the ancient κάμπτω. Doublet of καμπύλος. Also substantivised masculine and feminine.
The neuter form, from καμπούρικος.</div><b>καμπυλωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καμπυλωτή, neuter καμπυλωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from καμπύλ(η)) + -ωτός.</div><b>καμπόσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάμποσος">κάμποσος</a></div><b>καμπύλη</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://καμπύλος">καμπύλος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://καμπύλος">καμπύλος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://καμπύλος">καμπύλος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καμπύλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. curve; curved line</div>
  <div class='def'>2. curve (one-dimensional figure of non-zero length)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) curve (the attractive shape of a woman&#x27;s body)</div><b>καμπύλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καμπύλη, neuter καμπύλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bent; curved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καμπύλος. Doublet of καμπούρα.</div><b>καμωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine καμωμένη, neuter καμωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>made, done</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of κάνω, κάμνω (“do, make”), a verb with no passive forms.
Also, from Byzantine Greek καμωμένος, perfect passive participle of κάμνω Also see the verb καμώνομαι, without active forms, Mediaeval also with active form καμώνω,</div><b>καν</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. even (implying an extreme example in the case mentioned)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ήξερε <b>καν</b> τα βασικά. - He didn&#x27;t even know the basics.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. even, so much as</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ούτε <b>καν</b> με ρώτησε αν ήμουν εντάξει. - He didn&#x27;t even ask me if I was okay.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare) either ... or</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καν</b> χθες καν προχθές με κάλεσε στο τηλέφωνο. - Either yesterday or the day before she called me on the phone.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κἄν, καὶ ἄν, consisting of καί + ἄν.</div><b>κανάκευ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of κανάκεψε, second-person singular simple past active perfective imperative of <a href="bword://κανακεύω">κανακεύω</a></div><b>κανάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κανάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. canal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. channel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin canālis.</div><b>κανάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κανάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jug, pitcher (wide-mouthed vessel with ear handle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καννάτα, from Latin cannata, from Latin canna (“reed”), from Ancient Greek κάννα.</div><b>κανέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κανέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cinnamon (spice)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ξύλο <b>κανέλας</b> - stick of cinnamon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian cannella.</div><b>κανένας</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nobody, no one (not any person; the logical negation of somebody)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Και τελικά <b>δεν</b> ήρθε <b>κανένας</b>. - And finally no one came.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. anyone, anybody</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βλέπετε <b>κανέναν</b>; - Can you see anybody?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κανένας, from Ancient Greek κἄν εἷς.</div><b>κανίβαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κανίβαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cannibal</div><b>κανίφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κανίφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) knife</div><b>καναδέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καναδέζικη, neuter καναδέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://καναδικός">καναδικός</a></div><b>καναδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καναδική, neuter καναδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Canadian</div><b>κανακεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to pamper, pet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to mollycoddle, mother</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Medieval Byzantine Greek κανακεύω, from κανάκι n κανακ- + -εύω.</div><b>καναντέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) waterbomber (firefighting aircraft)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare French canadair, Italian canadair, Serbo-Croatian kanader / канадер, Macedonian канадер (kanader)</div><b>καναπές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καναπέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sofa, couch, settee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Reborrowing from French canapé (“sofa”), from Latin cōnōpēum (“seat with a canopy”) (with various intermediate forms), from Ancient Greek κωνωπεῖον.</div><b>καναπεδάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καναπεδάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small sofa, couch or settee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καναπές + -άκι.</div><b>καναρίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καναρίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>canary (bird)</div><b>κανατάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κανατάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>potter (someone who makes pottery)</div><b>κανελή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative masculine singular of <a href="bword://κανελής">κανελής</a></div><b>κανελής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κανελιά, neuter κανελί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cinnamon (of color/colour)</div><b>κανελί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κανελιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cinnamon (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κανελής">κανελής</a></div><b>κανελλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κανελλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κανελί">κανελί</a></div><b>κανθαρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κανθαρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Spanish fly (beetle)</div><b>κανιβαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to cannibalise (UK), cannibalize (US), eat human meat</div><b>κανιβαλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κανιβαλική, neuter κανιβαλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cannibalistic</div><b>κανιβαλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cannibalism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English cannibalism. By surface analysis, κανίβαλος + -ισμός</div><b>καννάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καννάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κανάτα">κανάτα</a></div><b>καννάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κανάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of κανάτι</div><b>καννέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καννέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κανέλα">κανέλα</a></div><b>καννελής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καννελιά, neuter καννελί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymological speeling of <a href="bword://κανελής">κανελής</a> without simplification of double consonant</div><b>καννόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κανόνι">κανόνι</a></div><b>κανονάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κανονάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small cannon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. qanun, kanun, kanoon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κανόνι + -άκι.</div><b>κανονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to arrange, plan, set a date for</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sort out, determine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰνονῐ́ζω.</div><b>κανονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κανονική, neuter κανονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. regular, usual</div>
  <div class='def'>2. regular (as in regular verb)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek κανονικός from κανών.</div><b>κανονικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κανονικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>regularity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κανονικός + -ότητα.</div><b>κανονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>regulation, bylaw</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κανονισμός.</div><b>κανοντούπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda, indeclinable) kick</div><b>καντάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καντάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>serenade (love song sung in streets with string accompaniment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan cantada (“song”).</div><b>καντάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καντάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (obsolete) kantar, quintal (weight unit equal to 44 okas (οκάδες), in Greece equal to 56.4 kilograms)</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα εκατό καντάρια το ασήμι<b></b> - the hundred kantars of silver</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a moderately large amount</div>
  <div class='ex'>λίγη δράση και πέντε <b>καντάρια</b> έρωτα - some action and a moderately large amount of passion</div>
  <div class='def'>3. scales, balance, especially a vertical spring balance</div>
  <div class='ex'>ηλεκτρονικό <b>καντάρι</b> βαρέως τύπου - high-load electronic balance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καντάρι, a reborrowing (possibly through Ottoman Turkish قنطار (kantar)) from Arabic قِنْطَار”), from Byzantine Greek κεντηνάριον, from Latin centēnārium (“hundredweight”), ultimately from Latin centum (“hundred”). See Arabic قِنْطَار for numerous cognates with the exact same meaning passed via Arabic and Ottoman Turkish throughout the Mediterranean region.</div><b>καντέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καντέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>candela (the SI unit quantifying luminous intensity)</div><b>καντήλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καντήλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oil lamp</div><b>κανταδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κανταδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>serenader (musician who sings love song in streets with string accompaniment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan cantador (“serenader”) or καντάδα + -αδόρος.</div><b>κανό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) canoe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French canot (“canoe”), from Spanish canoa (“canoe”).</div><b>κανόνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κανόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. canon (books of the Bible)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. canon, round</div>
  <div class='def'>3. canon, rule, regulations</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek κανών.</div><b>κανόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cannon</div><b>κανώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κανώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://κανόνας">κανόνας</a></div><b>καουμπόι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) An American cowman, cowboy, cowhand; a buckaroo.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) An Old West figure, a buckaroo or gunslinger, mainly from the literature of the Western genre.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English cowboy.</div><b>καουμπόικο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vocative neuter singular of καουμπόικος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative masculine singular of καουμπόικος</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nominative neuter singular of καουμπόικος</div>
  <div class='def'>4. accusative neuter singular of καουμπόικος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καουμπόικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal) Western (movie genre)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. A movie of the Western genre.</div><b>καούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heartburn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καίω + -ούρα.</div><b>καπάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καπάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lid, top, cap, closure (of container)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قپاق (kapak, “lid; cover; shutter”), from قاپامق (kapamak, “to close, shut”).</div><b>καπάρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καπάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>advance payment, down payment, earnest money, security deposit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian caparra.</div><b>καπέλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καπέλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hat, cap</div>
  <div class='def'>2. something hat-shaped</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) illegal surcharge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian cappello, from Early Medieval Latin cappellus, from Late Latin cappa.</div><b>καπίστρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καπίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>halter, headstall (headgear for animal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Latin capistrum.</div><b>καπαρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to pay in advance; to give a security deposit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to secure (a position, etc.) via backdoor means (to bypass regular or legal procedures)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καπάρο + -ώνω.</div><b>καπετάνιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καπετάνιοι, feminine καπετάνισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. captain (person lawfully in command of a ship or other vessel)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) chieftain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan capetanio.</div><b>καπιταλίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capitalist</div><b>καπιταλίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καπιταλίστριες, masculine καπιταλιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capitalist</div><b>καπιταλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καπιταλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capitalism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French capitalisme.</div><b>καπιταλιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καπιταλιστές, feminine καπιταλίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capitalist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French capitaliste.</div><b>καπνίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be smoked</div><b>καπνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to smoke</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η καμινάδα <b>καπνίζει</b>. - The chimney’s smoking.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to smoke; to smoke (food)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καπνίζουμε</b> το ψάρι πάνω σε ξύλα. - We are smoking the fish over the wood.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to smoke (inhale and exhale smoke) (cigarette, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω να <b>καπνίσω</b> ένα τσιγάρο. - I want to smoke a cigarette.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The transitive sense inherited from Ancient Greek καπνίζω. By surface analysis, καπνός + -ίζω. The sense smoke (cigarette), Calque of French fumer or English smoke.</div><b>καπνίζω σαν φουγάρο</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to smoke like a chimney</div><b>καπνίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καπνίστριες, masculine καπνιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smoker (person who smokes)</div><b>καπνιστήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καπνιστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bee smoker, smoker</div><b>καπνιστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καπνιστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smoking room (room for people to smoke)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. smoker (apparatus for smoking food)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. smokehouse, smokery</div><b>καπνιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καπνιστές, feminine καπνίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smoker (person who smokes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καπν- (kapn-, “to smoke”) + -ιστής. First attested 1873.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive feminine singular of <a href="bword://καπνιστός">καπνιστός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καπν- (kapn-, “to smoke”) + -ιστής. First attested 1873.</div><b>καπνιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καπνιστή, neuter καπνιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smoked</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καπνιστό</b> ζαμπόν - smoked ham</div><b>καπνοδοχοκαθαριστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chimney sweep</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καπνοδόχος + καθαριστής</div><b>καπνοδόχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or masculine (plural καπνοδόχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(feminine usually) chimney, smokestack, funnel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καπνός + δέχομαι.</div><b>καπνοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καπνοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tobacconist&#x27;s (tobacconist&#x27;s shop)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καπνοπώλης + -είο.</div><b>καπνοπώλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καπνοπώλες, feminine καπνοπώλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tobacconist</div><b>καπνοπώλισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καπνοπώλισσες, masculine καπνοπώλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tobacconist</div><b>καπνός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καπνοί m pl or καπνά n pl)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smoke</div>
  <div class='ex'>δεν υπάρχει <b>καπνός</b> χωρίς φωτιά - there is no smoke without fire</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tobacco (plant and its products)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καπνός.</div><b>καποιανής</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of κάποιας</div><b>καποιανού</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of κάποιου</div><b>καποιανών</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of κάποιων</div><b>καπουτσίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καπουτσίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cappuccino (coffee drink)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. capuchin (monkey)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian cappuccino (“cappuccino (coffee drink)”), from Italian cappuccio (“cowl, hood”) (from the hoods of Capuchin monks’ habits) + -ino (diminutive suffix); and cappuccio from cappa (“cowl, hood; cape, cloak; sleeveless coat”) (from Late Latin cappa (“cape; sleeveless coat”); further etymology uncertain, though apparently from Proto-Indo-European *káput (“head”)).</div><b>καππαδοκικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καππαδοκική, neuter καππαδοκικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cappadocian (related to the area, people or dialect of Cappadocia)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Καππαδόκ(ης)) + -ικός.</div><b>καράβι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καράβια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ship, boat, sailing ship</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καράβιν, from Ancient Greek καράβιον, from κάραβος.</div><b>καράβλαχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καράβλαχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yokel, hick, bumpkin, hillbilly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καρά- + βλάχος</div><b>καράτε</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) karate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Unadapted borrowing from Japanese 空手 (“karate”).</div><b>καράτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καράτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carat (weight)</div><b>καράφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καράφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carafe, decanter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian caraffa, probably from Arabic غُرْفَة, from غَرَفَ.</div><b>καράφλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καράφλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of φαλάκρα</div><b>καρέκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρέκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chair</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εκεί που πήγα να κάτσω, κάποιος μου τράβηξε την <b>καρέκλα</b> κι έπεσα στον κώλο μου! - As I was going to sit down, someone pulled the chair out from under me and I fell on my arse!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) seat (membership in an organization, particularly a representative body and/or the power associated therewith)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός μόνο για την <b>καρέκλα</b> του ενδιαφέρεται, όχι για τους ψηφοφόρους του. - He only cares about his seat, not his constituents.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan carega (“chair”), itself ultimately from Ancient Greek καθέδρᾱ. Doublet of καθέδρα.</div><b>καραβάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καραβάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mess tin</div>
  <div class='ex'>παλιά <b>καραβάνα</b> - old hand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Old Italian caravana. Doublet of καραβάνι.</div><b>καραβάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καραβάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. caravan (convoy or procession of travelers, their vehicles and cargo and any pack animals)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>καραβάνι</b> τρέχει μες στη σκόνη,
Και την τρελή σου κυνηγάει σκιά. - The caravan runs through the dust,
And chases after your crazy shadow.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) throng, multitude, crowd (group of people crowded or gathered closely together)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καρβάνι, from Ottoman Turkish كروان (kervan) from Classical Persian کاروان.</div><b>καραβίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καραβίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crayfish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καραβίδα, Koine Greek καραβίς, and Ancient Greek κᾱ́ρᾰβος.</div><b>καραβανσεράι</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caravanserai</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كاروانسَرای (karvanseray), from Classical Persian کاروانسرای.</div><b>καραβοφάναρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καραβοφάναρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lightship, lightvessel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καράβι + φανάρι.</div><b>καραγκιοζιλίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καραγκιοζιλίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) ridiculous act or performance bringing shame on the performer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قرهگوزلق from Ottoman Turkish قرهگوز, the name of the lead character in the traditional Ottoman puppet play of the same name Karagöz, see Karagöz and Hacivat on Wikipedia for more. The play spread across the Ottoman populace becoming popular in Ottoman Greece and becoming known as Karagiozis, see Karagiozis on Wikipedia.Wikipedia for more.</div><b>καραγκιοζλίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καραγκιοζλίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καραγκιοζιλίκι">καραγκιοζιλίκι</a></div><b>καρακάξα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρακάξες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. jay</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) ugly and lying woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καρακάξα, from a Northern dialectal form καρακάκισσα, from Ancient Greek κόραξ, κόρακος + κίσσα. Compare Albanian karakaskë (“magpie”), Sicilian carcarazza (“magpie”).</div><b>καραμέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καραμέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sweet (UK confectionery: toffee, boiled sweet, etc), candy (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. caramel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian caramella.</div><b>καραμέλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καραμέλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>non simplified spelling of <a href="bword://καραμέλα">καραμέλα</a> caramel</div><b>καραμελοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καραμελοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caramelization</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καραμέλα + -ποίηση.</div><b>καραμπίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καραμπίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carbine, rifle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shotgun</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian carabina, from French carabine.</div><b>καραμπογιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καραμπογιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black paint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καρα- (kara-) + μπογιά. Either borrowed wholly from Ottoman Turkish قره بویا (kara boya) or as a combination of both Ottoman Turkish قره (kara, “black”) + Ottoman Turkish بویا (boya, “paint”).</div><b>καραμπόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καραμπόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carom, cannon (shot in which the cue ball strikes two other balls)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) pile-up (traffic accident or collision involving multiple vehicles)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έγινε άσχημη <b>καραμπόλα</b> σήμερα το πρωί στην παραλιακή. - There was a bad pile-up on the coast road today.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French carambole (“red billiard ball”).</div><b>καραπουτάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καραπουτάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) intensified form of <a href="bword://πουτάνα">πουτάνα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καρα- (kara-, intensifying prefix) + πουτάνα</div><b>καρασούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) karasumi</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Japanese カラスミ (karasumi).</div><b>καρατομώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to behead, decapitate, decollate</div><b>καρατσεκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσεκάρω">τσεκάρω</a> (reinforced)</div><b>καρατόμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρατομήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beheading, decapitation, decollation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) removal of a leadership</div><b>καραφλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καραφλή, neuter καραφλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φαλακρός">φαλακρός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Metathesized form of φαλακρός.</div><b>καρβέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρβέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. round loaf</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) the financial means to sustain tolerable standard of living</div>
  <div class='ex'>and see ψωμί n (psomí, “bread”)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) in expressions for duration of life: years</div>
  <div class='ex'>synonyms: expressions with ψωμί n (psomí, “bread”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καρβέλιν, or γαρβέλιν, from Slavic or Aromanian cârveli, plural of cârveale. Cognate to Albanian karabele (“bread for soldiers”).</div><b>καρβίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρβίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carbide</div><b>καρβονικά οξέα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of καρβονικό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of καρβονικό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of καρβονικό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div><b>καρβονικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρβονικά οξέα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carboxylic acid</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αιθανικό οξύ είναι ένα <b>καρβονικό οξύ</b>. - Acetic acid is a carboxylic acid.</div><b>καρβονικόν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of καρβονικό</div><b>καρβονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καρβονική, neuter καρβονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carboxylic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://καρβονικός">καρβονικός</a>: carboxylic</div><b>καρβοξυλικά οξέα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of καρβοξυλικό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of καρβοξυλικό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of καρβοξυλικό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div><b>καρβοξυλικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carboxylic acid</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αιθανικό οξύ είναι ένα <b>καρβοξυλικό οξύ</b>. - Acetic acid is a carboxylic acid.</div><b>καρβοξυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καρβοξυλική, neuter καρβοξυλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carboxylic</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αιθανικό οξύ είναι ένα <b>καρβονικό</b> οξύ. - Acetic acid is a carboxylic acid.</div><b>καρβουνιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to char, burn to a cinder</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to carbonise (UK), carbonize (US)</div><b>καρβουνιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καρβουνιασμένη, neuter καρβουνιασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>charred</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καρβουνιάζω (passive perfect participle).</div><b>καρδερίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρδερίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goldfinch, Eurasian goldfinch, European goldfinch, Carduelis carduelis</div><b>καρδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heart</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, with synizesis to avoid hiatus, from Ancient Greek καρδία, from Proto-Indo-European *ḱḗr.
Cognate with Italiot Greek cardia, Mariupol Greek кардъи́я.</div><b>καρδιαγγειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καρδιαγγειακή, neuter καρδιαγγειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cardiovascular</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καρδιαγγειακή</b> νόσος - cardiovascular disease</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καρδιά + αγγειακός, a calque of English cardiovascular and similar in other languages.</div><b>καρδιακές προσβολές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of καρδιακή <a href="bword://προσβολή">προσβολή</a></div><b>καρδιακή προσβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρδιακές προσβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heart attack</div><b>καρδιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καρδιακή, neuter καρδιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (relational) heart (attributive), cardiac</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) cardiac patient</div>
  <div class='def'>3. synonym of γκαρδιακός</div>
  <div class='ex'>φίλος <b>καρδιακός</b> - bosom friend, intimate friend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καρδιακός. By surface analysis, καρδιά + -ακός.</div><b>καρδινάλιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρδινάλιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cardinal (official)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Byzantine Greek καρδινάλιος, from New Latin cardinālis (“pertaining to a hinge, that on which something turns or depends”).</div><b>καρδιολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural καρδιολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cardiologist</div><b>καρδιοπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρδιοπάθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cardiopathy (cardiovascular disease, heart disease)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καρδιο (kardio, “heart”) + -πάθεια</div><b>καρδιοχειρουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural καρδιοχειρουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cardiac surgeon</div><b>καρδιόσχημος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καρδιόσχημη, neuter καρδιόσχημο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heart-shaped, cordate (having the traditional shape of a heart)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καρδιόσχημο</b> μενταγιόν - heart-shaped locket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καρδιά + σχήμα. First attested 1892.</div><b>καρεκλοκένταυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρεκλοκένταυροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, humorous) ensconced bureaucrat, ensconced politician (public servant or politician who is metaphorically joined to his seat and will never give it up)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός είναι διευθυντής εδώ και τριάντα χρόνια και δεν πρόκειται να παραιτηθεί· είναι <b>καρεκλοκένταυρος</b>. - He&#x27;s been the boss here for thirty years and isn&#x27;t going to quit; he&#x27;s ensconced.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, humorous) obstructive bureaucrat (public servant who inconveniences members of the public rather than assisting them)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν ένας <b>καρεκλοκένταυρος</b> που με έστειλε σε πέντε διαφορετικά γραφία μόνο και μόνο για να μου βάλει μια σφραγίδα. - There was a bureaucrat there that sent me to five different offices just to stamp my document.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καρέκλα + -ο- (-o-) + κένταυρος.</div><b>καρεκλοπόδαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρεκλοπόδαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chair leg</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καρεκλο- (kareklo-) + -πόδαρο</div><b>καριέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καριέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>career</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian carriera.</div><b>καριόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καριόλες, masculine καριόλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, offensive) bitch, whore, trollop, slut, harlot (general term of abuse for a promiscuous woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν ξανάρθει ποτέ εδώ αυτή η <b>καριόλα</b>, θα την πετάξω έξω με τις κλωτσιές! - If that bitch ever comes here again, I&#x27;ll kick her out!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literally, obsolete) old-fashioned wooden bed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καριόλα, from Italian carriola (diminutive of carro).</div><b>καριόλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καριόληδες, feminine καριόλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) asshole, bastard, fucker, douche, wanker (general term of abuse for a man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν ξανάρθει ποτέ εδώ αυτός ο <b>καριόλης</b>, θα τον πετάξω έξω με τις κλωτσιές! - If that asshole ever comes here again, I&#x27;ll kick him out!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καριόλα + -ης (-is, “male ending”)</div><b>καρκίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρκίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cancer (disease of uncontrolled cellular proliferation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crab</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Cancer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καρκίνος</div><b>καρκινικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καρκινική, neuter καρκινικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cancerous, cancer</div><b>καρκινογόνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καρκινογόνος or καρκινογόνα, neuter καρκινογόνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carcinogenic, cancerogenic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from καρκίν(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -γόνος.</div><b>καρμανιόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρμανιόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) guillotine (for capital punishment)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>καρμανιόλα</b> στήνεται, η <b>καρμανιόλα</b> σφάζει,
η <b>καρμανιόλα</b> περπατεί κι’ ο κόσμος κάνει χάζι. - The carmagnole is built, the carmagnole will slit
the carmagnole is walking with all the people watching.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) accident blackspot or a warning sign for one.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η επικίνδυνη στροφή είναι πραγματνική <b>καρμανιόλα</b>. Σκοτώθηκαν πολλοί οδηγοί εδώ. - This turn is a real carmagnole. Many drivers were killed here.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (slang) a trick set up and money won by swindlers</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) a strong saw for trees or iron</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial) a very expensive restaurant</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (historical, obsolete) a French song and dance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French carmagnole (initially a short jacket, later a song and dance of the Terreur period.) from Italian Carmagnola, an Italian city in Piedmont.</div><b>καρμπόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) carbon paper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) carbon copy, a copy made using carbon paper</div><b>καρνάγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρνάγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) boatyard (especially a small repair yard)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian carenaggio.</div><b>καρναβάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρναβάλια, diminutive καρναβαλάκι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carnival</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian carnevale, from Medieval Latin carnem levāre (“to take away/remove meat”).</div><b>καρντάσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρντάσηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brother</div>
  <div class='def'>2. close friend, comrade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قارداش.</div><b>καρντάσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρντάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καρντάσης">καρντάσης</a></div><b>καροτσάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καροτσάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sack truck, sack barrow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncommon) wheelbarrow</div>
  <div class='def'>3. baby buggy, pushchair, stroller</div>
  <div class='def'>4. shopping trolley</div>
  <div class='def'>5. wheelchair</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καρότσα + -άκι.</div><b>καρπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (familiar, literally) to bear fruit, to yield fruit, to fructify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, literary) to bear fruit, to be fruitful (to produce good results)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καρπίζω. By surface analysis, καρπ(ός)) + -ίζω.</div><b>καρπίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρπίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hornbeam (Carpinus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Carpinus, from Latin carpinus.</div><b>καρποφορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, literally) to bear fruit, to yield fruit, to fructify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive) to bear fruit, to be fruitful (to produce good results)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καρποφορῶ. By surface analysis, καρπο- (karpo-) + -φορώ.</div><b>καρπούζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρπούζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>watermelon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قارپوز (karpuz, “melon”), from Classical Persian خربز (xarbuz, “melon”).</div><b>καρπός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρποί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fruit, grain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wrist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰρπός.</div><b>καρπόσωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρποσώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sporocarp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, καρπός + σώμα.</div><b>καρτ-ποστάλ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) postcard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French carte postale (“postcard”).</div><b>καρτέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ambush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek καρτέρι, a back-formation from the verb καρτερῶ καρτερ- + neuter suffix -ι (-i).</div><b>καρτεζίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρτεζίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical, obsolete) kartezini or ~115g.) during Enetocracy in Ionian Islands</div><b>καρτεράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καρτερώ">καρτερώ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern καρτερώ καρτερ- + -άω, from Ancient Greek καρτερῶ, contracted form of καρτερέω.</div><b>καρτερώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) synonym of <a href="bword://περιμένω">περιμένω</a>: wait for (colloquial, in literature)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) synonym of καρτερεύω: suffer patiently (colloquial, in literature)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καρτερῶ, contracted form of καρτερέω, in its mediaeval sense.</div><b>καρυδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρυδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. walnut tree</div>
  <div class='def'>2. walnut (timber, wood)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάρυον.</div><b>καρυδότσουφλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρυδότσουφλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nutshell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) unseaworthy boat or ship</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καρύδι + τσόφλι.</div><b>καρυκεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to add spice, to flavour</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to spice up</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of speech) to embellish with details and nice words</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κᾰρῡκεύω, from κᾰρῡ́κη.</div><b>καρυοθραύστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρυοθραύστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nutcracker (implement for cracking nuts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nutcracker</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (capitalized) the Nutcracker (opera)</div><b>καρυότυπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρυότυποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>karyotype</div><b>καρφί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρφιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nail (metal fastener used for joining wood, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in abbreviated form) rivet</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καρφί</b> για μέταλλα - rivet</div>
  <div class='def'>3. informer, snitch, grass (UK), ratfink (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. spike</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καρφίον, diminutive of κάρφος.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek карфи́.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. straight, directly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. directly, straight away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καρφίον, diminutive of κάρφος.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek карфи́.</div><b>καρφίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρφίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pin (small nail, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pin (part of brooch, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καρφί + -ίτσα.</div><b>καρφώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be nailed</div><b>καρφώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to nail, stab</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to denounce, inform against</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to dunk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καρφώνω, from Koine Greek κάρφω + -ώνω, apparently invoking the meaning of a derivative κᾰ́ρφος &gt; &quot;nail&quot; &gt; &quot;to nail, stab&quot;.</div><b>καρχαρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καρχαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shark</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καρχαρίας, from κάρχαρος. Possibly related to κάρκαρος, but also compare χάραξ and χαράσσω; also Old Armenian կարկառ.</div><b>καρωτίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carotid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carotid artery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καρωτίς.</div><b>καρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) diamond or square decorative pattern</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) with colloquial plural καρά (card games) diamond</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French carreau. Noun, also in adjectival use.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) which is of carreau pattern</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φοράω μια <b>καρό</b> φούστα. - I&#x27;m wearing a skirt with square pattern.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French carreau. Noun, also in adjectival use.</div><b>καρότο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρότα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carrot (root vegetable)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> According to Petrounias inherited from Koine Greek καρῶτα, a feminine noun conceived as neuter plural, a repatriated term from Latin carota, from Koine Greek καρωτόν.
According to Babiniotis, who spells it etymologically καρώτο, a repatriated term from Italian carota conceived as neuter plural from Late Latin carōta, from Koine Greek καρωτόν via plural καρωτά, from the ancient κάρα. He also compares to κεφαλωτόν from κεφαλή.</div><b>καρότσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρότσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trailer (of motor vehicle)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rear part of pickup truck</div>
  <div class='def'>3. four-wheeled carriage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian carrozza.</div><b>καρότσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρότσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wheelbarrow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncommon) baby buggy, pushchair, stroller</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A diminutive of καρότσα.</div><b>καρύδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καρύδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coconut</div><b>καρύδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρύδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. walnut</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pecan</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nut</div>
  <div class='def'>4. Adam&#x27;s apple, laryngeal prominence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καρύδι, from Ancient Greek καρύδιον; diminutive of κάρυον.</div><b>καρύκευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καρυκεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. herb, spice, seasoning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. condiment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) sweetening words</div><b>κασάγια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kasaya</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unadapted borrowing from English kasaya.</div><b>κασέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κασέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cassette (a small flat case containing magnetic tape on two reels, used to record and play back audio and video material)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian cassetta.</div><b>κασεασαύρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Caseasauria or caseasaurians.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Caseasauria.</div><b>κασκαντέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) stuntman, stuntwoman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French cascadeur (“stuntman”).</div><b>κασκόλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) scarf, muffler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French cache-col. See French cacher &amp; col</div><b>κασσίτερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek κασσίτερος.</div><b>καστάνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pawl</div><b>καστανάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καστανάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hot chestnut man, hot chestnut seller</div><b>καστανέρυθρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καστανέρυθρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary, rare) maroon (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter of the adjective καστανέρυθρος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://καστανέρυθρος">καστανέρυθρος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καστανέρυθρος">καστανέρυθρος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter of the adjective καστανέρυθρος.</div><b>καστανέρυθρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καστανέρυθρη, neuter καστανέρυθρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary, rare) of maroon, brownish-red color/colour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κασταν(ός), “brown”) + ερυθρός.</div><b>καστανιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καστανιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chestnut tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κάστανο + -ιά.</div><b>καστανοκίτρινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καστανοκίτρινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fawn (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://καστανοκίτρινος">καστανοκίτρινος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καστανοκίτρινος">καστανοκίτρινος</a></div><b>καστανοκίτρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καστανοκίτρινη, neuter καστανοκίτρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yellow-brown, fawn (of color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καστανός + κίτρινος.</div><b>καστανοκόκκινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καστανοκόκκινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>puce (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://καστανοκόκκινος">καστανοκόκκινος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καστανοκόκκινος">καστανοκόκκινος</a></div><b>καστανοκόκκινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καστανοκόκκινη, neuter καστανοκόκκινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sorrel, puce (of color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From adjectives κασταν(ός), “chestnut brown”) + -ο- + κόκκινος.</div><b>καστανομάλλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brown haired</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καστανο (kastano, “brown”) + μάλλης</div><b>καστανομάτης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καστανομάτα, neuter καστανομάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>browneyed / brown-eyed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καστανο (kastano, “brown”) + μάτης</div><b>καστανό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καστανά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brown, chestnut (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://καστανός">καστανός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καστανός">καστανός</a></div><b>καστανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καστανή, neuter καστανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brown, chestnut (colour)</div><b>κατ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κατά">κατά</a></div><b>κατ&#x27; αντιδιαστολή προς</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. as opposed to, in contrast to, in contradistinction</div>
  <div class='ex'>ευρωπαϊκό δίκαιο <b>κατ’ αντιδιαστολή προς</b> το βρετανικό δίκαιο - European law as opposed to British law</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in opposition to</div><b>κατ&#x27; αρχάς</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first, first of all, at the outset, initially</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek set phrase.</div><b>κατ&#x27; αρχήν</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in principle</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πριν συζητάμε τις λεπτομέρειες πρέπει πρώτα να συμφωνήσουμε <b>κατ’ αρχήν</b>. - Before we discuss the details we must first agree in principle.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. initially, at first</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek set phrase.</div><b>κατ&#x27; εξοχήν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) preeminent, eminent</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχοντας εδραιώσει τη φήμη του ως ο <b>κατ’ εξοχήν</b> θεωρητικός... - Having established his reputation as the dominant theorist...</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατ’ ἐξοχήν.</div><b>κατ&#x27; επέκταση</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>by extension</div><b>κατ&#x27; επανάληψη</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>repeatedly, again and again, time and again</div><b>κατά</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with genitive) versus, against</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έγινε έγκλημα <b>κατά</b> της Ελλάδας! - Crimes were committed against Greece!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with accusative) towards</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το δωμάτιο βλέπει <b>κατά</b> την ανατολή. - The room faces towards the east</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with accusative) during</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κατά</b> τη διάρκεια του ταξιδιού - during the trip</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (with accusative) around, about, close to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα έρθω <b>κατά</b> τις έξι το απόγευμα. - I will come at about six in the evening.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (with accusative) according</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κατά</b> παράδοση - traditionally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek κᾰτά.</div><b>κατά βάθος</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deep down, at bottom (fundamentally; in essence; in reality)</div><b>κατά καιρούς</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>at times, every now and then, every so often, from time to time</div><b>κατά κανόνα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as a rule (in general; most often)</div><b>κατά κράτος</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>completely, totally, utterly (especially: to win, to defeat, to be defeated, etc.)</div><b>κατά κόρον</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to the point of surfeit, ad nauseam</div><b>κατά λάθος</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>by mistake, mistakenly, accidentally, by accident, in error (without intention to do so)</div><b>κατά πάσα πιθανότητα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in all likelihood, probably</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δήμαρχος <b>κατά πάσα πιθανότητα</b> θα παραιτηθεί μετά τα Χριστούγεννα. - The mayor will, in all likelihood, resign after Christmas.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Katharevousa set phrase.</div><b>κατά πολύ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>by far, significantly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ξεπερνάει <b>κατά πολύ</b> τα διεθνή πρότυπα. - It exceeds by far the international standards.</div><b>κατά προσέγγιση</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>approximately</div><b>κατά τόπους</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (adverbial) in places</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (adjectival) local</div><b>κατά φωνή κι ο γάιδαρος</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>speak of the devil, talk of the devil (said when a person mentioned in the current conversation happens to arrive on the scene)</div><b>κατάγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to come from, to hail from (a place)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to descend, to be descended (from a lineage)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to originate (from)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κᾰτᾰ́γομαι, middle/passive of κᾰτάγω.</div><b>κατάδικος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κατάδικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>convict, prisoner</div><b>κατάδοση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταδόσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>informing, denunciation</div><b>κατάδυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταδύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dive, diving (underwater swimming, with or without apparatus)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diving, dive</div><b>κατάθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. testimony (in court of law)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. submission, presentation, registration</div>
  <div class='def'>3. deposit (in bank)</div><b>κατάθλιψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταθλίψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>depression</div><b>κατάκλειστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>completely closed or shut up (house, shop, etc)</div><b>κατάκλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατακλίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) lying down, recumbence, recumbency</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) lying down, recumbence, recumbency; decubitus (the posture of someone in bed, lying down or reclining)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (literary) careening (the act of heaving a ship down on one side so as to expose the other, in order to clean it of barnacles and weed, or to repair it below the water line)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. ellipsis of <a href="bword://έλκος">έλκος</a> κατάκλισης n</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατάκλισις with -ση (-si) ending and possible semantic loan from English decubitus ulcer.</div><b>κατάκτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατακτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conquest</div><b>κατάλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) katal (the derived SI unit of catalytic activity)</div><b>κατάλευκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατάλευκη, neuter κατάλευκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intensive form of <a href="bword://λευκός">λευκός</a>:; all white, pure white, completely white</div>
  <div class='ex'>Και ζήλεψα τη βάρκα τη μικρή τη χιονάτη,
Που της φιλούσε ο γλάρος το <b>κατάλευκο</b> πανί. - And I was jealous of the small snow-white boat,
Whose pure white sail the seagull was kissing.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intensive form of <a href="bword://λευκός">λευκός</a>:; snow-white, lily-white</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek κατάλευκος. By surface analysis, κατά- + λευκός.</div><b>κατάληξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταλήξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ending</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ending, termination</div>
  <div class='def'>3. result or outcome</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Greek κατάληξις.</div><b>κατάληψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταλήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>occupation, capture, takeover</div><b>κατάλληλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατάλληλη, neuter κατάλληλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>appropriate, suitable, fit, right</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατάλληλος corresponding”), from κατα- (kata-) + ἀλλήλων.</div><b>κατάλογος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατάλογοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. catalogue (UK), catalog (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. list</div>
  <div class='def'>3. menu (a list of available dishes in restaurant)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. directory, folder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάλογος &amp; semantic loan from French liste. Analysed as κατά- + -λογος (-logos).</div><b>κατάλυμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καταλύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accommodation, lodging</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατάλυμα.</div><b>κατάλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταλύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>catalysis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κατάλυσις.</div><b>κατάματα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>right at the eyes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κατάματα.</div><b>κατάμαυρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατάμαυρη, neuter κατάμαυρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(for color/colour) jet-black, pitch-black, completely black</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μενεξεδένια ήταν τα μάτια σου,
<b>Κατάμαυρη</b> είναι η μοναξιά. - Violet were your eyes,
Pitch-black is the loneliness.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κατάμαυρος. Morphologically, from κατά- + μαύρος.</div><b>κατάπαυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταπαύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ceasing, cessation</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κατάπαυση</b> των εχθροπραξιών - cessation of hostilities</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατάπαυσις with semantic loan from French cessation. By surface analysis, καταπαύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>κατάπλασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καταπλάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poultice (the treatment or the materials used), fomentation, cataplasm (rare)</div><b>κατάπληκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατάπληκτη, neuter κατάπληκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amazed, surprised, astounded</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek κατάπληκτος, from Ancient Greek κᾰτᾰπλήσσω + -τος (-tos), the former from κατα- (kata-) + πλήσσω.</div><b>κατάποση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swallowing, deglutition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατάποσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>κατάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. curse (the wishing of calamity on someone)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. calamity, disaster</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>damn!</div><b>κατάργηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταργήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abolition, abrogation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάργησις.</div><b>κατάρρευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταρρεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>collapse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek κατάρρευσις with -ση (-si) suffix and adaptation to the modern meaning of καταρρέω.</div><b>κατάρριψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταρρίψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. downing, shooting down (of an aircraft)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rebuttal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. breaking (of a record)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατάρριψις, with -ψη (-psi) ending.</div><b>κατάρτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατάρτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάρτιον.</div><b>κατάσβεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extinguishing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατάσβεσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>κατάσκοπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατάσκοποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spy (person who secretly watches)</div><b>κατάσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. state, condition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. situation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Morphologically from κατα- (“against-”) + στάση (“position or stance”).</div><b>κατάστημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καταστήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) shop, store</div>
  <div class='def'>2. office (for contact with business etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάστημα, from καθίστημι, combined of κατά + ἵστημι.</div><b>κατάστρωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καταστρώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. deck</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deck (class of ship&#x27;s passenger)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάστρωμα.</div><b>κατάσχεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατασχέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confiscation, forfeiture, seizure, sequestration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατάσχεσις with semantic loan from French saisie. By surface analysis, κατασχε-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>κατάσχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to confiscate, seize, sequester, sequestrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Stem κατασχ- from the subjunctive of the second aorist of Ancient Greek κατέσχον of the verb κατέχω, used as indicative present tense. Since 1833, a free translation of French saisir.</div><b>κατάταξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατατάξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>categorization, classification</div><b>κατάφαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>affirmation, confirmation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάφασις.</div><b>κατάφρακτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατάφρακτη, neuter κατάφρακτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to a cataphract</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάφρακτος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατάφρακτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cataphract (a heavily armoured Byzantine military unit; a soldier thus armed; the armour worn)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάφρακτος.</div><b>κατάφυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατάφυτη, neuter κατάφυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lush, verdant, green (lush)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι ο πιο όμορφος και <b>πιο κατάφυτος</b> δρόμος του κόσμου. - It&#x27;s the most beautiful and greenest street in the world.</div><b>κατάφωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατάφωρη, neuter κατάφωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blatant, flagrant (infringement, violation, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: κραυγαλέος (kravgaléos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατάφωρος.</div><b>κατάχαμα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>on the ground</div><b>κατάχρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταχρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abuse of power, alcohol, drugs, etc</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατάχρησις.</div><b>κατέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to possess, to hold (:property)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to occupy (:territory)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to hold, to occupy (:position, rank, role, status, title, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to occupy (to fill space)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to have mastery of, to have mastered, to possess (to have proficiency in or knowledge of: language, skill, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to possess (of an idea, thought, etc.: to dominate (someone&#x27;s mind); to strongly influence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited and learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατέχω. By surface analysis, κατ- (kat-) + έχω.</div><b>κατήγορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accuser</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prosecutor, prosecuting attorney, prosecuting advocate, prosecution counsel</div>
  <div class='ex'>δημόσιος <b>κατήγορος</b> - public prosecutor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατήγορος, from Ancient Greek κᾰτήγορος.</div><b>κατήφορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατήφοροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decline, descent, downhill</div><b>κατίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) A female gossip who is perceived as having poor education and intelligence.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French catin (“harlot, slattern, whore”).</div><b>καταβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to overcome, defeat, humiliate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to exhaust, make an effort</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (formal, transitive) to pay an amount (money)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>καταβάλω</b> το ποσό της αμοιβής σας στην τράπεζα. - I will deposit the amount of your fee at the bank.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταβάλλω, semantic loan from French accabler. By surface analysis, κατα- (“downwards”) + βάλλω (“cast, strike”).</div><b>καταβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. payment, defrayal, defrayment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) foundations, origins</div>
  <div class='ex'>από <b>καταβολής</b> κόσμου - since the world began, since the dawn of time</div>
  <div class='def'>3. exhaustion</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καταβολή</b> δυνάμεων - loss of strength</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταβολή. The &#x27;exhaustion&#x27; sense is derived from the &#x27;exhaust&#x27; sense of καταβάλλω.</div><b>καταβροχθίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to devour, eat greedily</div><b>καταγγέλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to denounce, to terminate (to announce the termination of: agreement)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to denounce, to report (to make a formal or public accusation against; to inform against; to accuse)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to denounce (to criticize or speak out against; to point out as deserving of reprehension, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταγγέλλω with semantic loan from French dénoncer in the sense &#x27;to announce the termination of&#x27;.</div><b>καταγγελία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταγγελίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. complaint</div>
  <div class='def'>2. denunciation, indictment (accusation of wrongdoing; strong criticism)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. notice of termination (of a contract)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταγγελία, with semantic loan from French dénonciation in the &#x27;denunciation&#x27; sense.</div><b>καταγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be recorded, registered</div><b>καταγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to register, list</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to minute (to write minutes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταγράφω. By surface analysis, κατα- (“fully”) + γράφω (“write”). The modern senses are a semantic loan from French enregistrer.</div><b>καταγραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταγραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>writing down, transcription, recording, registering (facts, sounds, values etc.)</div><b>καταγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταγωγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ancestry, descent, lineage, origins</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: γενεαλογία f (genealogía)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. origin (of something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καταγωγή, from Ancient Greek καταγωγή.</div><b>καταδίδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to inform against, denounce</div><b>καταδίκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταδίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sentence, conviction</div><b>καταδίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταδίδω">καταδίδω</a></div><b>καταδικάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to sentence, to condemn (to assign punishment)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to condemn, to damn (to criticize strongly)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to condemn, to doom (to destine to experience bad circumstances; to fix irrevocably the ill fate of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καταδικάζω. By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + δικάζω</div><b>καταδιώκομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prosecuted, persecuted</div><b>καταδιώκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pursue, chase</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταδιώκω as a semantic loan from French poursuivre, persécuter. By surface analysis, κατα- (“against”) + διώκω (“persecute”).</div><b>καταδρομέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταδρομείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commando</div><b>καταδρομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταδρομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>surprise raid (by elite force - commandos, SAS, etc)</div><b>καταδυτικοί κώδωνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://καταδυτικός">καταδυτικός</a> <a href="bword://κώδων">κώδων</a></div><b>καταδυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταδυτική, neuter καταδυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diving</div><b>καταδυτικός κώδων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταδυτικοί κώδωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diving bell</div><b>καταδότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταδότες, feminine καταδότρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. informer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stool pigeon</div><b>καταδότρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταδότριες, masculine καταδότης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. informer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stool pigeon</div><b>καταδύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek καταδύομαι, middle voice of καταδύω. Morphologically, from κατα- (“downward”) + δύομαι of active δύω.</div><b>καταθέτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to register, submit, lodge, file, hand in</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>καταθέσω</b> αγωγή για αποζημίωση. - I&#x27;ll file a lawsuit for damages.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to deposit</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Καταθέτει</b> τα χρήματα σ’ έναν λογαριασμό ταμιευτηρίου. - He deposited the money in a savings account.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to testify, give evidence</div><b>καταθλίβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to depress (to make psychologically depressed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καταθλίβω. By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + θλίβω.</div><b>καταθλιπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταθλιπτική, neuter καταθλιπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. depressing, depressive (causing depression)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. depressive (affected by depression)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pressure (attributive—of a pump, pipe, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the καταθλιπ- stem of καταθλίβω + -τικός, a loose calque of French oppressif.</div><b>καταθρενία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural καταθρενίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταθρηνία">καταθρηνία</a></div><b>καταθρηνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταθρηνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>catathrenia</div><b>καταιγίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταιγίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thunderstorm, storm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καταιγίς.</div><b>κατακάθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατακάθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sediment, dregs</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for Greek coffee) sediment, dregs; dregs, the coffee grounds in Greek coffee</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) a worthless or vulgar person, scum</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) remnants of usually bitter experiences and memories</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from κατακαθίζω.</div><b>κατακίτρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατακίτρινη, neuter κατακίτρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (for color/colour) intensely yellow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of people) extremely pale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατα- (kata-, “intense”) + κίτρινος</div><b>κατακουράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to overtire, excessively wear out</div><b>κατακρατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to detain unlawfully</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to withhold unlawfully</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of a thing) to retain (to hold or keep inside it; to contain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατακρατῶ with semantic loan from French détenir (“to detain”) and retenir (“to retain”). By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + κρατώ.</div><b>κατακριτέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατακριτέα, neuter κατακριτέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reprehensible, blameworthy</div><b>κατακτητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατακτητές, feminine κατακτήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conqueror</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κατακτώ + -τής.</div><b>κατακτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to conquer (to acquire by force of arms, win in war; to become ruler of; to subjugate)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to conquer (to gain, win, or obtain by effort)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to win (to gain by succeeding in competition or contest)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to win (to obtain (someone) by wooing; to make an ally or friend of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατακτῶμαι with semantic loan from French conquérir.</div><b>κατακόκκινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατακόκκινη, neuter κατακόκκινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. all red</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for color/colour) intensely red</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατα- (kata-, “intense”) + κόκκινος</div><b>κατακόμβη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατακόμβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>catacomb</div><b>καταλήγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to end up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (euphemistic) to die</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek καταλήγω &amp; semantic loan from French finir par, aboutir. By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + λήγω.</div><b>καταλαβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to understand, realise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek καταλαβαίνω, from Ancient Greek καταλαμβάνω with metaplasm according to λαμβάνω &gt; λαβαίνω. Morphologically, κατα- (kata-) + λαβαίνω. Doublet of καταλαμβάνω.</div><b>καταλαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to seize, capture, acquire sovereignty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to occupy (space)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταλαμβάνω; compare the inherited doublet καταλαβαίνω.</div><b>καταλανικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Catalan (the language of Catalonia and its people)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://καταλανικός">καταλανικός</a></div><b>καταλανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταλανική, neuter καταλανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Catalan, Catalonian (relating to Catalonia or its people or language)</div><b>καταλυθήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of καταλυθήκαν, third-person plural simple past of <a href="bword://καταλύομαι">καταλύομαι</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://καταλύω">καταλύω</a></div><b>καταλυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταλυτική, neuter καταλυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (general) catalytic (indicating something which accelerates a process or action)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. catalytic</div>
  <div class='def'>3. catalytic</div><b>καταλύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be abolished</div><b>καταλύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταλύτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. catalyst</div>
  <div class='def'>2. catalytic converter</div>
  <div class='def'>3. enabler (person who makes things happen)</div><b>καταλύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to abolish, break down</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to stay (usually for one night)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to catalyze</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to break one&#x27;s fast (resume normal diet after fasting)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταλύω. Morphologically, from κατα- (“fully”) + λύω (“unbind, loosen”).</div><b>καταμαράν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) catamaran (twin-hulled vessel)</div><b>καταμετρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to count, to tally (to determine the number of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταμετρῶ. By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + μετρώ.</div><b>κατανάλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consumption</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατανάλωσις with semantic loan from French consommation. By surface analysis, καταναλώνω + -ση (-si).</div><b>καταναγκάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be forced, compelled</div><b>καταναγκάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to force</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰτᾰνᾰγκᾰ́ζω.</div><b>καταναλωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταναλωτές, feminine καταναλώτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consumer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καταναλώ(νω)) + -τής. Morphologically, κατ- (kat-) + αναλώνω, a form of the ancient ἀναλόω, also see ἀναλίσκω.</div><b>καταναλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to consume</div>
  <div class='ex'>(more formal), αναλώνω (analóno). Also see δαπανώ (dapanó), ξοδεύω (xodévo, “spend”).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek καταναλώνω, a metaplasm of Ancient Greek καταναλίσκω &lt; κατ- (kat-) + ἀναλίσκω. Also see the form ἀναλόω.</div><b>κατανοητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατανοητή, neuter κατανοητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>understandable, comprehensible</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εύκολα έκαναν <b>κατανοητά</b> περίπλοκα θέματα. - They easily demystified complex topics.</div><b>κατανομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατανομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>distribution, allocation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κατανέμω + -η (-i, nominal suffix). Related to but not inherited from Koine Greek κατανομή despite similar construction.</div><b>κατανοώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to understand</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορώ να <b>κατανοώ</b> βασικές λέξεις. - I can understand basic words.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατανοέω / κατανοῶ.</div><b>καταντάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to end up, be reduced to, lower oneself to (to degenerate into an undesired state or condition)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για να θρέψει τα παιδιά της, <b>κατάντησε</b> πόρνη. - In order to feed her children, she was reduced to becoming a prostitute.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make, reduce to, drive (to cause someone or something to degenerate into an undesired state or condition)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον έχει <b>καταντήσει</b> τρελό με τα καμώματά της. - She&#x27;s driven him mad with her antics.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern καταντ(ώ)) + -άω. From the Hellenistic Koine Greek καταντῶ, contracted form of καταντάω, from κατά + ἀντῶ, uncontracted ἀντάω, from ἀντί.</div><b>καταντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταντάω">καταντάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καταντῶ, from κατάντης from κατά + ἀντί. Also see καταντάω.</div><b>κατανόηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατανοήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comprehension, understanding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατανόη(σις), “observation”) + -ση (-si), from κατανοέω + -σις (-sis), with semantic loan from French compréhension, entendement, as well as semantic loan from English understanding.</div><b>καταξοδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to overspend</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to overspend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κατα- (“completely”) + ξοδεύω (“spend”).</div><b>καταξοδιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταξοδεύω">καταξοδεύω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κατα- (“completely”) + ξοδιάζω (“spend”).</div><b>καταπάνω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>straight at, towards, upon, against</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αυτοκίνητο ήρθε <b>καταπάνω</b> του. - The car came towards him.</div><b>καταπίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to swallow (food, drink, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καταπίνω.</div><b>καταπακτή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταπακτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trapdoor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καταπακτή θύρα.</div><b>καταπιέζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to oppress</div><b>καταπλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to arrive at port (of ship)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (ironic) to arrive at port (of ship); to unwanted person arrives</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to sail downstream, downriver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καταπλέω. Morphologically, from κατα- (kata-, “back, against”) + πλέω.</div><b>καταπληκτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fantastic!</div><b>καταπληκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταπληκτική, neuter καταπληκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amazing, fantastic, astounding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καταπληκτικός; by surface analysis, κατάπληκτος + -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>καταπολεμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταπολεμώ">καταπολεμώ</a></div><b>καταπολεμιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταπολεμώ">καταπολεμώ</a></div><b>καταπολεμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fight, combat (against a habit, a situation) to eradicate it</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι κυτταροκίνες σε χαμηλές συγκεντρώσεις βοηθούν τον οργανισμό μας να <b>καταπολεμήσει</b> τις λοιμώξεις. - Cytokines in low concentrations help our body fight infections.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταπολεμῶ, contracted form of καταπολεμέω. By surface analysis, κατα- (“against”) + πολεμώ (“fight”).</div><b>καταπράσινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταπράσινη, neuter καταπράσινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(for color/colour) verdant, lush green, intense green</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατα- (kata-, “intense”) + πράσινος</div><b>καταπραΰνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to soothe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κᾰτᾰπρᾱΰνω, from κατα- (kata-, “down”) + πρᾱΰνω.</div><b>καταπραϋντικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καταπραϋντικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sedative, palliative</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://καταπραϋντικός">καταπραϋντικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καταπραϋντικός">καταπραϋντικός</a></div><b>καταπραϋντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταπραϋντική, neuter καταπραϋντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sedative, soothing, palliative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καταπραΰνω + -τικός.</div><b>καταπόνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταπονήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strain (the act of straining, or the state of being strained)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stress</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fatigue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καταπόνησις with semantic loan from French fatigue. By surface analysis, καταπονη-) + -ση (-si)</div><b>καταπόρφυρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταπόρφυρη, neuter καταπόρφυρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. all purple</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intensely purple</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατα- (kata-, “intense”) + πορφυρός</div><b>καταραμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine καταραμένη, neuter καταραμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>damned, confounded, accursed, cursed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καταραμένη</b> ώρα - cursed moment/hour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of καταριέμαι, a verb without active form. Inherited from Byzantine Greek καταραμένος, from Ancient Greek κατηραμένος, perfect participle of verb καταράομαι / καταρῶμαι.</div><b>καταργούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be abolished, annulled, passive of <a href="bword://καταργώ">καταργώ</a></div><b>καταργώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to abolish, quash, repeal, nullify, negate, annul</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek καταργῶ, contracted form of καταργέω. By surface analysis, κατ- (kat-) + αργώ, from ἀργός / αργός.</div><b>καταριέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to curse (at/about someone/something), rail against</div><b>καταρράκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταρράκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cataract (eye disease)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flood, inundation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. waterfall, cataract, rapids</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καταρράκτης</div><b>καταρράχτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταρράκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταρράκτης">καταρράκτης</a></div><b>καταρρέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to collapse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to break down, collapse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to collapse, go under, fold</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καταρρέω (“fall into ruins”). Morphologically, from κατα- (“fully”) + ρέω (“flow”).</div><b>καταρρίπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to shoot down, to bring down, to down (to make something, especially something flying, fall to the ground)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to rebut (to deny the truth of something, especially by presenting arguments that disprove it)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to refute (to prove (something) to be false or incorrect); to overturn, to topple (:theory)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to refute (to prove (something) to be false or incorrect); to debunk</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, transitive) to break (to do better than (a record), setting a new record)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταρρίπτω with semantic loan from French abattre and battre (“break (a record)”). By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + ρίπτω.</div><b>καταρρακτωδώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>torrentially</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from καταρρακτώδ(ης)) + -ώς.</div><b>καταρρακτώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταρρακτώδης, neuter καταρρακτώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>torrential</div><b>καταρχαιοζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταρχαιοζωική, neuter καταρχαιοζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Hadean</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καταρχαιοζωικός</b> αιώνας - Hadean eon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατα- (kata-, “pre-”) + αρχαιοζωικός</div><b>κατασκήνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατασκηνώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. camp, camping, encampment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. holiday camp, summer camp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κᾰτᾰσκήνωσῐς.</div><b>κατασκευάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to construct (to build or form by assembling parts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to construct (to build (a sentence, an argument, etc.) by arranging words or ideas)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to coin (make up or invent, and establish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατασκευάζω, equivalent to κατα- (kata-, “thorough”) + σκευάζω.</div><b>κατασκευή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατασκευές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. construction, constructing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. structure, design</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κᾰτᾰσκευή. By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + -σκευή</div><b>κατασκευαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατασκευαστές, feminine κατασκευάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maker, manufacturer, constructor</div><b>κατασκηνώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to camp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek κατασκηνόω / κατασκην(ῶ)) + -ώνω. See κατα- (kata-, “down”) + σκηνή.</div><b>κατασκοπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) espionage, spying</div><b>κατασκοπεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κατασκοπία">κατασκοπία</a></div><b>κατασκοπεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to spy</div><b>κατασπαταλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to squander, to waste, to fritter away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κατασπαταλῶ. By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + σπαταλώ.</div><b>καταστηματάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταστηματάρχες, feminine καταστηματάρχισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shopkeeper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατάστημα + -άρχης</div><b>καταστρέφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be ruined</div><b>καταστρέφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to destroy, ruin, wreck</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν φάω κάτι απαγορευμένο, <b>καταστρέφω</b> τη δίαιτα μου; - If I eat something which isn&#x27;t allowed, will I ruin my diet?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to deface</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to destroy (reputation, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταστρέφω. Morphologically from κατα- (“against-”) + στρέφω (“turn”).</div><b>καταστρεπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καταστρεπτική, neuter καταστρεπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>destructive, catastrophic</div><b>καταστροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταστροφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. disaster, catastrophe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. destruction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek καταστροφή.</div><b>καταυλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταυλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. camp, bivouac (tents, bivouacs or their location)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. camp (area of temporary structures for military occupation)</div><b>καταφέρνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) to inflict (a blow)</div><b>καταφέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accomplish, succeed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to persuade, win over, convince</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) synonym of καταφέρω</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατα- (kata-) + φέρνω.</div><b>καταφεύγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to resort to something, have recourse to something</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to take refuge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek καταφεύγω. By surface analysis, κατα- (kata-) + φεύγω.</div><b>καταφθάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to arrive in time</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to arrive unexpectedly (also ironic, or for unpleasant arrivals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καταφθάνω (“fall upon unawares; pay in advance”). The form καταφτάνω, since Mediaeval times. By surface analysis, κατα- (“completely”) + φθάνω (“arrive”). Doublet of καταφτάνω.</div><b>καταφρονημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine καταφρονημένη, neuter καταφρονημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scorned, sneered at</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of καταφρονούμαι and καταφρονιέμαι, passive voice forms of καταφρονώ (“sneer at”).</div><b>καταφρονιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of καταφρονούμαι</div><b>καταφρονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to scorn, be contemptuous of, sneer at</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to spurn, reject</div><b>καταφτάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταφθάνω">καταφθάνω</a> to arrive in time; arrive unexpectedly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καταφτάνω, from Hellenistic Koine Greek καταφθάνω. By surface analysis, κατα- (“completely”) + φτάνω (“arrive”). Doublet of καταφθάνω.</div><b>καταφύγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καταφύγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shelter, refuge</div><b>καταχνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταχνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fog, haze</div><b>καταχρώμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to embezzle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to abuse (to put to a wrong use; to misapply; to use improperly)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>καταχρώμαι</b> την εμπιστοσύνη / υπομονή / καλοσύνη κάποιου - to abuse someone&#x27;s trust / patience / kindness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καταχρῶμαι, contracted form of καταχρᾰ́ομαι.</div><b>καταχωρητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καταχωρητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>processor register</div><b>καταχώρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταχωρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καταχώριση">καταχώριση</a></div><b>καταχώριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καταχωρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. registration (the action of making a record)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. register, entry (a physical record of information)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. publication (of information in a printed form)</div><b>κατεβάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to lower (to cause to descend)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to take down, to bring down (from higher to lower, or from north to south)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to pull down (to make lower by pulling)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to drop off, to let off, to put down (to allow passengers to alight from a vehicle)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to get out (to help, induce or force (someone) to leave)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to depose, to bring down (to remove (a leader) from (high) office)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (transitive) to put forward (:proposal, nomination)</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (transitive) to put away, to down (to consume (food or drink), especially in large quantities)</div>
  <div class='def'>9. (of a lactating female mammal) to give, to produce (:milk)</div>
  <div class='def'>10. (transitive) to shift (:stress to the following syllable)</div>
  <div class='def'>11. (of theatrical performances) to close, to discontinue</div>
  <div class='def'>12. (transitive) to download</div>
  <div class='def'>13. (transitive) to lower (to reduce the degree, intensity, strength, etc., of)</div>
  <div class='def'>14. (transitive) to lower (to reduce (something) in value, amount, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κατεβάζω, with -ε- from κατέβασα, past tense of καταβάζω, from Ancient Greek καταβιβάζω, from κατα- (kata-) + βιβάζω.</div><b>κατεβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to descend, go down</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to disembark, alight, get off, deplane, deboard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κατεβαίνω, from Ancient Greek καταβαίνω.</div><b>κατεδαφίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to demolish, to raze, to level (to destroy (buildings, etc.), especially in a planned or intentional fashion; to destroy by reducing to ground level)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ισοπεδώνω (isopedóno)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek κατεδαφίζω. By surface analysis, κατ- (kat-) + έδαφ(ος)) + -ίζω.</div><b>κατεπείγων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατεπείγουσα, neuter κατεπείγον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>urgent (requiring immediate attention)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: επείγων (epeígon)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From present active participle of Ancient Greek κατεπείγω.</div><b>κατεργάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to process, treat, work, fashion (make, build or construct)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κατεργάζομαι</b> χαλκό - to treat copper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατεργάζομαι.</div><b>κατεργάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατεργάρα, neuter κατεργάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dishonest, roguish</div><b>κατεστημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κατεστημένη, neuter κατεστημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>established</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of καθίσταμαι, passive voice of καθιστώ (“make, appoint”). Created from the stem εστη- of the ancient &#x27;second&#x27; aorist κατέστην + the usual passive participle ending -μένος. The ancient passive perfect participle was καθεστώς.
As a political term, a free translation of English established. Also substantivised: see κατεστημένο.</div><b>κατευθείαν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>directly; straight ahead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κατ’ εὐθεῖαν.</div><b>κατευθύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to give directions; to direct; to lead</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to influence someone by determining their actions; to guide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατευθύνω, from κατ- (kat-) + εὐθύνω, the latter component from εὐθύς.</div><b>κατευνάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to become tranquil, simmer down, quieten</div><b>κατευνάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to placate, pacify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to quieten, make tranquil</div><b>κατεψυγμένα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κατεψυγμένος">κατεψυγμένος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frozen goods</div><b>κατεψυγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κατεψυγμένη, neuter κατεψυγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frozen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of καταψύχομαι, passive voice of καταψύχω. Semantic loan from French surgelé or from English refrigerated. The medieval κατεψυγμένος, with different sense: &quot;destroyed from cold&quot;.</div><b>κατεύθυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατευθύνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>direction, course</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατευθύνω + ση (si).</div><b>κατηγορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατηγορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. category</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άρθρα στην <b>κατηγορία</b> «Ουσιαστικά». - Articles in the category “Nouns”.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusation, charge</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>κατηγορία</b> εναντίον μας ήταν … - The charge against us was …</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (usually) accusation, charge; charges against [with <a href="bword://εναντίον">εναντίον</a>], [with <a href="bword://σε">σε</a> <a href="bword://βάρος">βάρος</a>] or</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰτηγορῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>κατηγορημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κατηγορημένη, neuter κατηγορημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who has been accused</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κατηγορούμαι, passive voice of κατηγορώ (“accuse”).</div><b>κατηγορηματικά</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emphatically, categorically</div><b>κατηγορηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατηγορηματική, neuter κατηγορηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emphatic, categorical</div><b>κατηγορουμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατηγορουμένες, masculine κατηγορούμενος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accused, defendant, charged</div><b>κατηγορούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be accused</div><b>κατηγορούμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατηγορούμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(predicative) predicative</div>
  <div class='ex'>&quot;Ο Γιώργος είναι πονηρός&quot; - Το <b>κατηγορούμενο</b> του υποκειμένου είναι το επίθετο &quot;πονηρός&quot;. - &quot;George is wicked&quot; - The predicative of the subject is the adjective &quot;wicked&quot;.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter of the passive present participle κατηγορούμενος of verb κατηγορώ. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek κατηγορούμενον, substantivised neuter participle of κατηγορῶ, contracted form of κατηγορέω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://κατηγορούμενος">κατηγορούμενος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κατηγορούμενος">κατηγορούμενος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter of the passive present participle κατηγορούμενος of verb κατηγορώ. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek κατηγορούμενον, substantivised neuter participle of κατηγορῶ, contracted form of κατηγορέω.</div><b>κατηγορούμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατηγορούμενοι, feminine κατηγορουμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accused, defendant, charged</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of κατηγορούμαι, passive voice of κατηγορώ. All genders, also substantivised.
As noun, learnedly, as in the substantivised plural masculine participle κατηγορούμενοι of Ancient Greek κατηγορούμενος of verb κατηγορῶ, contracted form of κατηγορέω &amp; semantic loan from French accusé.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine κατηγορούμενη, neuter κατηγορούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>No definition available</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of κατηγορούμαι, passive voice of κατηγορώ. All genders, also substantivised.
As noun, learnedly, as in the substantivised plural masculine participle κατηγορούμενοι of Ancient Greek κατηγορούμενος of verb κατηγορῶ, contracted form of κατηγορέω &amp; semantic loan from French accusé.</div><b>κατηγορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to blame, accuse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to accuse, charge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατηγορῶ, a contracted form of κατηγορέω.</div><b>κατηγόρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατηγορήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>predicate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατηγόρημα. By surface analysis, κατηγορώ + -μα (-ma).</div><b>κατισχύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) to prevail over one&#x27;s opponents, triumph over, overpower</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κατίσχυσε</b> των αντιπάλων του. - He/she prevailed over his/her opponents.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατισχύω.</div><b>κατιτί</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάτι">κάτι</a> (used for stylistic purposes)</div><b>κατιόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατιόντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(physical) cation</div><b>κατιόντες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>descendants, line of descent</div><b>κατοικία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατοικίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. habitation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. residence, home</div>
  <div class='def'>3. house, dwelling</div>
  <div class='def'>4. domicile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek κατοικία.</div><b>κατοικίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατοικίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>domesticated animal, pet</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχετε <b>κατοικίδια</b>; - Do you have any pets?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantive neuter form of κατοικίδιος, from a clipping of κατοικίδιο ζώο.</div><b>κατοικίδιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατοικίδια, neuter κατοικίδιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of animals) domesticated</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο σκύλος και η γάτα είναι <b>κατοικίδια</b> ζώα. - The dog and the cat are domestic animals.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κᾰτοικί̆δῐος.</div><b>κατοικημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κατοικημένη, neuter κατοικημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inhabited, residential</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κατοικούμαι, passive voice of κατοικώ (“inhabit”).</div><b>κατοικώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to inhabit, reside permanently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατοικέω and/or κατοικῶ, from κᾰτᾰ- + οἶκος.</div><b>κατονομάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to name, identify, mention</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλουμε να <b>κατονομάσεις</b> τον πραγματικό ένοχο. - We wish to name the real culprit.</div><b>κατορθωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατορθωτή, neuter κατορθωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>achievable, accomplishable</div><b>κατορθώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to achieve, succeed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατορθῶ, κατορθόω + -ώνω, the former component from κατά- + ὀρθόω.</div><b>κατοστάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατοστάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. 100 metre race</div>
  <div class='def'>2. 100 dirhams, ¹⁄₄ oka</div>
  <div class='def'>3. 100-dirham wine vessel</div>
  <div class='def'>4. 100 grams</div>
  <div class='def'>5. 100 denomination banknote (100 euros, 100 drachmae, etc)</div><b>κατοστάρικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατοστάρικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a one hundred denomination banknote (100 euros, 100 drachmae, etc)</div><b>κατουράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, familiar, intransitive) to pee, wee, piddle, piss (expel urine)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχετε τουαλέτα; Πρέπει να <b>κατουρήσω</b>. - Do you have a toilet? I need to pee.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, familiar, transitive) to pee on, wee on, piss on (soil something or someone with urine)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μωρό με <b>κατούρησε</b> καθώς του άλλαζα την πάνα. - The baby peed on me as I was changing his nappy.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to disregard, scorn, forget (show complete and utter contempt for)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι ασχολείσαι μαζί του; <b>Κατούρα</b> τον. - Why are you bothering with him? Forget him.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, imperative only, transitive) to leave alone, leave in peace (stop bothering)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε παρακαλώ φίλε μου, <b>κατούρα</b> μας. - Please, pal, leave us alone.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κατουρ(ώ)) + -άω, from Ancient Greek κατουρῶ, contracted form of κατουρέω. Equivalent to κατ- (kat-, “down, downwards”) + ουρώ.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek катру́.</div><b>κατουρημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κατουρημένη, neuter κατουρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pissed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) extremely scared</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κατουριέμαι, passive voice of κατουράω, κατουρώ (“piss”).</div><b>κατουριέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, familiar, figuratively, literally) to wet oneself, piss oneself.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αγοράκι <b>κατουρήθηκε</b> και άρχισε να κλαίει. - The little boy wet himself and started to cry.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, familiar, figuratively) to be on the verge of wetting oneself, badly need the toilet.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σας παρακαλώ, βγήτε απ’ την τουαλέτα, <b>κατουριέμαι</b>. - Please come out of the bathroom, I&#x27;m about to wet myself.</div><b>κατουρλιό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατουρλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, plural normally) pee, piss (urine)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έλα εδώ σε παρακαλώ και σφουγγάρισε αυτά τα <b>κατουρλιά</b> που άφησε το σκυλί σου! - Come over here please and mop up the piss your dog left!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) urge to pee, urge to piss</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον έπιασε <b>κατουρλιό</b> από τις πολλές μπίρες. - He got the urge to pee from the many beers he had.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατουρλής + -ιό.</div><b>κατουρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κατουράω">κατουράω</a></div><b>κατοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. possession</div>
  <div class='def'>2. occupation (control of a country by a hostile army)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κατοχή.</div><b>κατούρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, uncountable) peeing, pissing (the act of urinating)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το δημόσιο <b>κατούρημα</b> διώκεται σε μερικές χώρες. - Public urination is prosecuted in some countries.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, uncountable) urge to pee, urge to piss</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την έπιασε <b>κατούρημα</b> κι έψαχνε τουαλέτα. - She got the urge to pee and was looking for a toilet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κατούρημα, equivalent to κατουρώ + -μα (-ma).</div><b>κατούρησα στο πηγάδι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of στο <a href="bword://πηγάδι">πηγάδι</a> κατούρησα</div><b>κατράμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατράμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tar</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χρησιμοποίησαν το ακριβότερο <b>κατράμι</b> για τον καινούργιο δρόμο. - They used the most expensive tar for the new road.</div><b>κατρουλιό</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κατουρλιό">κατουρλιό</a></div><b>κατσέλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατσέλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cyprus form of <a href="bword://αγελάδα">αγελάδα</a></div><b>κατσίκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατσίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nanny goat, female goat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to κατσίκι n; see there for more.</div><b>κατσίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατσίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kid, young goat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Ottoman Turkish كچی. For further etymology, see modern Turkish keçi.</div><b>κατσαβίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατσαβίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>screwdriver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan cazzavide.</div><b>κατσαδιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to bawl out, to dress down, to give someone hell, to give a tongue lashing to, to harangue, to scold loudly (to deliver a loud, hard scolding or lecture to; to reprimand)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κατσάδ(α)) + -ιάζω.</div><b>κατσαπλιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατσαπλιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thief, plunderer, scoundrel, ragamuffin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) an insurgent of the ELAS, the Greek People&#x27;s Liberation Army.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Of unknown etymon.</div><b>κατσαρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατσαρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cockroach (insect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κανθαρίδα; accusative case of κανθαρίς.</div><b>κατσαρολί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατσαρολιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saucepan</div><b>κατσαρομάλλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατσαρομάλλα or κατσαρομαλλούσα, neuter κατσαρομάλλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curly-haired, woolly-haired</div><b>κατσαρόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κατσαρόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. casserole</div>
  <div class='def'>2. saucepan</div>
  <div class='def'>3. stewpot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian cazzarola.</div><b>κατσαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατσαρή, neuter κατσαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curly</div><b>κατσικόδρομος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κατσικόδρομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>desire path, goat track</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κατσίκι + δρόμος.</div><b>κατωιταλιώτικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Italiot Greek</div><b>κατόπιν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. at</div>
  <div class='def'>2. after</div>
  <div class='def'>3. following</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατόπιν.</div><b>κατόρθωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατορθώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. feat, accomplishment, deed, exploit, achievement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tour de force</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κατορθώνω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>κατώτατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατώτατη, neuter κατώτατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lowest</div>
  <div class='def'>2. minimum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κατώτατος. By surface analysis, κάτω + -τατος (-tatos, absolute superlative adjective suffix).</div><b>κατώτατος μισθός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minimum wage</div><b>κατώτερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κατώτερη or κατωτέρα, neuter κατώτερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lower (physical position)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subordinate (rank, social position)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. inferior (quality, qualification)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κατώτερος. A comparative form derived from an adverb: κάτω + -τερος (-teros, comparative suffix).</div><b>κατώφλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κατώφλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. threshold, doorstep</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) threshold (start, point at which something is initiated)</div><b>καυγάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καυγάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καβγάς">καβγάς</a></div><b>καυκασιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καυκασιανή, neuter καυκασιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Caucasian, of the Caucasus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) white (of Caucasian race)</div><b>καυλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καυλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) cock, dick, prick (the penis)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dickhead, knobhead, bell-end (the glans penis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καυλίον, from καυλός.</div><b>καυλιάρα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://καυλιάρης">καυλιάρης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://καυλιάρης">καυλιάρης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://καυλιάρης">καυλιάρης</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καυλιάρες, masculine καυλιάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) horny woman</div><b>καυλιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καυλιάρα, neuter καυλιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very horny, very randy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καυλί.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καυλιάρηδες, feminine καυλιάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) horndog (man fixated on sex)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καυλί.</div><b>καυλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, intransitive, vulgar) to get it up, get a boner, get a hard-on (to get an erection of the penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν είδε το βυζί της, <b>καύλωσε</b>. - When he saw her tit, he got a boner.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, intransitive, vulgar) to be horny, be randy (to be sexually stimulated)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν μου δείξεις τον κώλο σου, θα <b>καυλώσω</b>. - If you show me your ass, I&#x27;ll get turned on.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, transitive, vulgar) to make horny, make randy, turn someone on (sexually stimulate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η φωτογραφία του κώλου σου με <b>καύλωσε</b>. - The photo of your ass turned me on.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καύλα + -ώνω.</div><b>καυστήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καυστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. furnace, combustion chamber</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cauterising iron</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καυστήρ.</div><b>καυστικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://καυστικός">καυστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caustically</div><b>καυστική σόδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) caustic soda (sodium hydroxide)</div><b>καυστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καυστική, neuter καυστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. caustic (capable of burning, corroding or destroying organic tissue)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) caustic (sharp, bitter, cutting, biting, and sarcastic in a scathing way)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καυστικός and from French caustique, from Latin causticus, from Ancient Greek καυστικός.</div><b>καυσόξυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καυσόξυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>piece of firewood, firelog</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from καύση + -ό- + ξύλο.</div><b>καυτερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καυτερή, neuter καυτερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hot, spicy (applied to taste)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καυτός + -ερός.</div><b>καυτηριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to cauterize</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to savage (to criticize vehemently)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek καυτηριάζω.</div><b>καυτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καυτή, neuter καυτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. red hot, boiling, scorching</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boiling, scalding (of water)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. very hot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek καυστός.</div><b>καυχιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to boast, to brag, to vaunt oneself</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek καυχιέμαι, from Ancient Greek καυχῶμαι, contracted form of καυχάομαι.</div><b>καφέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) of brown (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έφυγε το κουμπί της <b>καφέ</b> φούστας μου. - The button of my brown skirt is torn (literally: gone).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French café (“coffee, coffee coloured”) (colour usage).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) brown, coffee (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε μ’ αρέσουν τα <b>καφέ</b>. Προτιμώ το μαύρο από το <b>καφέ</b>. - I don&#x27;t like the browns. I prefer black to brown.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) cafe, coffee shop</div>
  <div class='def'>3. genitive/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://καφές">καφές</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French café (“coffee, coffee coloured”) (colour usage).</div><b>καφέ μπαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) coffee bar</div><b>καφές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καφέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cup of coffee</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω ανάγκη έναν <b>καφέ</b> για να ξυπνήσω. - I need a cup of coffee to wake me up.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coffee</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>καφές</b> και η σοκολάτα έχουν καφεΐνη. - Coffee and chocolate have caffeine.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ground coffee, roast coffee, coffee bean</div>
  <div class='def'>4. coffee plant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish قهوه (kahve, “coffee”) (Turkish kahve), from Arabic قَهْوَة (qahwa).</div><b>καφές της παρηγοριάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καφέδες της παρηγοριάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. The cup of coffee traditionally served and drunk after Greek funerals.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) A cup of coffee drunk after bad news or unfortunate events.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &#x27;coffee of consolation&#x27;</div><b>καφεδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καφεδιά, neuter καφεδί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καφετής">καφετής</a></div><b>καφεμαντεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καφεμαντείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coffee tasseography, coffee tasseomancy, coffee reading (the supposed prediction of the future with the grounds left in a cup of coffee after drinking it)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καφές + -μαντεία</div><b>καφενές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καφενέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) kafenio, cafe</div><b>καφενείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καφενεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kafenio, cafe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) madhouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> καφές + -είο</div><b>καφενείον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://καφενείο">καφενείο</a></div><b>καφενεῖον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://καφενείο">καφενείο</a></div><b>καφενόβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καφενόβια, neuter καφενόβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. describes a frequent customer to the kafenio</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun) a kafenio habitué</div><b>καφετέρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καφετέριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cafeteria</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English cafeteria, from Spanish cafetería, from Spanish cafetero.</div><b>καφετής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καφετιά, neuter καφετί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coffee coloured</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from καφές m</div><b>καφετί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καφετιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (for the colour) close to brown, brownish, coffee (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for the colour) 1,000 drachma note (of that colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indeclinable variant of <a href="bword://καφετής">καφετής</a> for all genders</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://καφετής">καφετής</a></div><b>καφετερία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καφετερίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://καφετέρια">καφετέρια</a></div><b>καφετζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καφετζήδες, feminine καφετζού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coffee shop owner (male)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. waiter (male)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قهوهجی (kahveci), equivalent to καφές + -τζής, both themselves from Ottoman Turkish قهوه (kahve) and Ottoman Turkish ـجی (-ci) respectively.</div><b>καφετζού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καφετζούδες, masculine καφετζής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kafenio owner (female) or owner&#x27;s wife</div>
  <div class='def'>2. waiter (female), waitress</div>
  <div class='def'>3. coffee tasseographer (fortune teller from coffee grounds left in cup)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (derogatory) amateur or unqualified fortune teller</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καφές + -τζού.</div><b>καφετιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καφετιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coffeemaker, coffee pot, cafetière</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French cafetière.</div><b>καφεόδεντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shrub of the tropical genus Coffea, the source of coffee beans</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καφές + δέντρο.</div><b>καψαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to scorch, to singe (to burn the surface of; to burn slightly)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καψάλ(α)) + -ίζω.</div><b>καψούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καψούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κάψουλα">κάψουλα</a></div><b>καψώνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καψώνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hazing (initiation pranks that involve humiliation, harassment or abuse)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι παλιοί βάζουν <b>καψώνια</b> στους νεοσύλλεκτους. - The older ones haze the new recruits.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, figuratively) hardship (difficulty or trouble; hard times)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καψώνω, from Ancient Greek καῦσις, from καίω.</div><b>καϊμάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) foam or crema on freshly prepared Greek coffee</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) clotted cream</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) mastic flavoured ice cream</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, uncountable) elite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قایماق (kaymak).</div><b>καϊσί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καϊσιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>type of apricot, with edible stone</div><b>καύλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καύλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) boner, hard-on, stiffy (erection of the penis)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, vulgar) horniness, hots (sexual excitement)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively, vulgar) Sexually arousing person, person viewed as a sex object.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From καυλί.</div><b>καύση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. combustion, the process of burning.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chemical reaction with the production of energy.</div><b>καύσιμο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://καύσιμος">καύσιμος</a>:; masculine accusative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://καύσιμος">καύσιμος</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative singular</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καύσιμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fuel (energy provider to combustion engine, fire or chemical reaction)</div><b>καύσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine καύσιμη, neuter καύσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>combustible, burnable (able to be used as a fuel due to release of thermal energy when burned)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καύσιμος with semantic loan from French carburant.</div><b>καύσωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural καύσωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heatwave, hot spell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek καύσων.</div><b>καύτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καύτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lit tip of a cigarette</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lit/burnt end of a fuse or wick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek καύστρα, with loss of ⟨σ⟩ as occurred with καυτός from Ancient Greek καυστός.</div><b>καύχημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural καυχήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. boast (something that one brags about)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pride (that of which one is proud; that which excites self-congratulation and self-esteem)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek καύχημα.</div><b>κβάζαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) quasar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of English quasar, from quasi-stellar radio source</div><b>κβάντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κβάντο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quantum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin quantum (“as much, how much”); borrowed in its physics sense via English quantum.</div><b>κβάντουμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κβάντο">κβάντο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Latin quantum (“how much”)</div><b>κβάντωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κβαντώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quantization</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Partial calque of English quantization, suffixed with -ωση (-osi).</div><b>κβαντική ηλεκτροδυναμική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) quantum electrodynamics, QED</div><b>κβαντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κβαντική, neuter κβαντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quantum (relating to)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κβαντική</b> ηλεκτροδυναμική - quantum electrodynamics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κβάντο n, via English quantum (“amount, quantity”) from Latin quantum (“as much, how much”).</div><b>κγ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Greek number representing the number twentythree or 23.</div><b>κείμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κείμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>text (written passage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κείμενον, participle of κεῖμαι.</div><b>κείνη</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of κείνος</div><b>κεδρότσιχλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κεδρότσιχλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fieldfare, Turdus pilaris</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κέδρος + τσίχλα</div><b>κει</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εκεί">εκεί</a></div><b>κειμήλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κειμήλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. heirloom</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sacred relic, holy relic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κειμήλιον.</div><b>κεκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κέικ">κέικ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French cake, from English cake.</div><b>κεκλεισμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine κεκλεισμένη, neuter κεκλεισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://κλεισμένος">κλεισμένος</a>: shut, closed, found only at the expression κεκλεισμένων των θυρών</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek κεκλεισμένος. Formal Perfect participle of κλείνομαι, passive voice of κλείνω. Retains ancient reduplication. See the expression κεκλεισμένων των θυρών.</div><b>κεκλεισμένων των θυρών</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, archaic, literary) behind closed doors, in camera, privately, without audience (mainly for juries)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το δικαστήριο / η επιτροπή συνεδριάζει <b>κεκλεισμένων των θυρών</b> - The jury / the council convenes in camera.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French à huis clos. In genitive plural, from Ancient Greek perfect participle κεκλεισμένος of verb κλείω, and θύρα</div><b>κελί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κελιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cell (in monastery or prison)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κελί</b> φυλακής - prison cell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κελλίν, from Koine Greek κελλίον, from Ancient Greek κέλλα, from Latin cella.</div><b>κελαηδάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of birds) to sing, chirp. cheep, chirrup, tweet</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για μας <b>κελαηδούν</b> τα πουλιά - The birds sing for us (1939 song by Michalis Souyioul)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of a person) to babble, prattle (talk at length happily)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι την ακούς, αυτή την χαζή γυναίκα, που όλο <b>κελαηδάει</b>; - What are you listening to this stupid woman for, who&#x27;s constantly babbling on?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, intransitive) to sing like a bird, sing like a canary (reveal secrets under pressure)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τώρα που τον πιάσανε, το πουλάκι θα <b>κελαηδήσει</b>. - Now that they&#x27;ve got him, he&#x27;ll sing like a canary.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κελαηδ(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω. Inherited from the medieval form Byzantine Greek κελαηδῶ) of κελαδῶ, from Ancient Greek κελαδῶ, contracted form of κελᾰδέω, with diphthongisation of [a] &gt; [ai̯]. The spelling with eta (κελαηδῶ), by false etymology to ἀηδών f, modern αηδόνι n.</div><b>κελαηδότσιχλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κελαηδότσιχλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turdus philomelos: song thrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κελαηδώ”) + τσίχλα</div><b>κελαηδώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κελαηδάω">κελαηδάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κελαηδῶ, and see Etymology at κελαηδάω.</div><b>κελαϊδάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://κελαηδάω">κελαηδάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> For the spelling with iota, see Etymology at κελαηδάω</div><b>κελαϊδώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://κελαηδώ">κελαηδώ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> For the spelling with iota, see Etymology at κελαηδάω</div><b>κελτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κελτική, neuter κελτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κέλτικος">κέλτικος</a></div><b>κεμέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεμέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic, colloquial) money belt (of traditional type)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic, broadly, colloquial) purse</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στης Ανατολής τα μέρη μια φορά και ένα καιρό,
Ήταν άδειο το <b>κεμέρι</b>, μουχλιασμένο το νερό. - In the lands of East once upon a time,
The money purse was empty, the water was mouldy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كمر (kemer, “belt”), from Classical Persian کمر (kamar, “waist”).</div><b>κεμπάπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kebab (oblong minced meat patties, often cooked on skewers)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A Ottoman Turkish كباب (kebab, kebap), from Arabic كَبَاب.</div><b>κενοδοξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vainglory</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: αλαζονεία f (alazoneía), υπεροψία f (yperopsía)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κενοδοξία. By surface analysis, κενός + -ο- (-o-) + δόξα + -ία.</div><b>κενοτάφιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κενοτάφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cenotaph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κενοτάφιον.</div><b>κεντάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to embroider</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sting, prick, spur on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κεντ(ώ)) + -άω, from Ancient Greek κεντῶ, contracted form of κεντέω.</div><b>κεντημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κεντημένη, neuter κεντημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>embroidered</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κεντιέμαι, passive voice of κεντάω, κεντώ (“embroider”).</div><b>κεντρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sting</div><b>κεντρικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κεντρικός">κεντρικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centrally</div><b>κεντρική μονάδα επεξεργασίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>central processing unit</div><b>κεντρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κεντρική, neuter κεντρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>central, pivotal, focal, main</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>κεντρικός</b> διαχειριστής παρέχει υποστήριξη στους εθνικούς διαχειριστές. - The central administrator shall provide support to national administrators.</div><b>κεντροαφρικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κεντροαφρικανική, neuter κεντροαφρικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Central African</div><b>κεντρομόλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κεντρομόλα, neuter κεντρομόλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centripetal</div><b>κεντρόσαυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κεντρόσαυροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kentrosaurus (Kentrosaurus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αλλά αυτός ο <b>Κεντρόσαυρος</b> μάλλον δεν πέθανε από καρκίνο των οστών. Αυτός και χιλιάδες άλλοι <b>Κεντρόσαυροι</b> στο κοπάδι του σκοτώθηκαν από μια καταστροφική πλημμύρα, που πιθανότατα προκλήθηκε από μια τροπική καταιγίδα. - But this Centrosaurus probably didn&#x27;t die of bone cancer. It and thousands of other Centrosauruses in its herd were killed by a catastrophic flood, likely caused by a tropical storm.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Kentrosaurus. By surface analysis, κέντρο + -σαυρος (-savros).</div><b>κεντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κεντάω">κεντάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek κεντῶ.</div><b>κενυατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κενυατική, neuter κενυατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Kenyan (relating to Kenya or its people)</div><b>κενό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κενά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vacuum, void, emptiness</div>
  <div class='ex'>αντλία <b>κενού</b> - vacuum pump</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interstellar space</div>
  <div class='def'>3. gap in space or time (an opening made by breaking or parting, allowing passage, or implying a breach or defect)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. blank (free from writing, printing, or marks; having an empty space to be filled in)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συμπληρώστε τα <b>κενά</b> στην αίτηση. - Fill in the blanks on the application form.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nominalization of adjective κενός (empty, vain)</div><b>κενός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κενή, neuter κενό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>empty, free, vacant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κενός, from Proto-Indo-European *ḱen-.</div><b>κεράσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεράσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cherry (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κεράσι(ν), “cherry”) &lt; Koine Greek κεράσιον a diminutive of Ancient Greek κέρασος, κερασός.</div><b>κεράτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κερατώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) cuckolding, cheating (adultery)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορούσε ποτέ να ξεπεράσει το <b>κεράτωμα</b> της γυναίκας του. - He could never get over his wife&#x27;s cuckolding.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κερατώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>κερήθρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κερήθρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κηρήθρα">κηρήθρα</a></div><b>κερί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wax, beeswax</div>
  <div class='def'>2. candle, taper</div>
  <div class='def'>3. earwax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κηρίον, diminutive of κηρός.</div><b>κεραία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κεραίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. aerial, antenna (for radio, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. horn, antenna, feeler (on insects)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. spar, yard (on sailing ships)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κεραία.</div><b>κεραμίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεραμίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tile (on roofs)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) roof</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχει μια γάτα που όλο νιαουρίζει στα <b>κεραμίδια</b> δίπλα. - There&#x27;s a cat who is constantly meowing on the roof next door.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) house, roof over one&#x27;s head</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μάζεψαν λεφτά για πολλά χρόνια να βάλουν ένα <b>κεραμίδι</b> πάνω απ’ τα κεφάλια τους. - They saved up for many years to get a roof over their heads.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κεραμίδιον.</div><b>κεραμιδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κεραμιδιά, neuter κεραμιδί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terracotta, tile red, brick red (color/colour)</div><b>κεραμιδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεραμιδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terracotta (color/colour)</div><b>κεραμική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κεραμικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ceramic (material)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pottery, ceramics (the craft of making pots)</div><b>κερασένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κερασένια, neuter κερασένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cherry red (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. made of cherry wood</div><b>κερασιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κερασιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cherry tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κεράσι + -ιά.</div><b>κερατάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κερατάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory) cuckold, cuck (man married to an unfaithful wife)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι στο χωριό ξέρουν ότι η γυναίκα του τον απατά αλλά αυτός ο <b>κερατάς</b> δεν το παραδέχεται. - Everyone in the village knows his wife is cheating on him but that cuckold won&#x27;t accept it.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, mildly, offensive) bugger, dope, jerk, schmuck (general purpose disparaging term for someone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι κάθεσαι κι ακούς αυτόν τον <b>κερατά</b> να λέει τις μπούρδες του; - What are you sitting and listening to that bugger spout his crap for?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κερατάς, equivalent to κέρατο + -άς. Compare similar horn-related terms in Italian (cornuto) and Spanish (cornudo).</div><b>κερατοειδής χιτώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κερατοειδείς χιτώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cornea</div><b>κερατώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, transitive) to cuckold, cuck, horn, put horns on (make a cuckold of someone by being unfaithful)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>κεράτωσε</b> η γυναίκα του. - His wife cuckolded him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κερατώνω, from Koine Greek κερατῶ / κεραόω, from κέρας. Compare similar horn-related terms in French (cocufier) and Italian (fare le corna).</div><b>κεραυνοβόλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κεραυνοβόλος or κεραυνοβόλα, neuter κεραυνοβόλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lightning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fast</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unexpected</div><b>κεραυνοβόλος έρωτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>love at first sight</div><b>κεραυνός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κεραυνοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thunderbolt, lightning bolt, lightning strike, lightning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) sudden and unexpected event</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) explosive, aggressive, or forceful actions (often with dramatic or critical intent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek κεραυνός. For the figurative senses, semantic loan from French foudre.</div><b>κεραυνός εν αιθρία</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in negative sense) bolt from the blue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “lightning bolt in a clear sky”. See κεραυνός m and the ancient terms in the phrase ἐν (en, “in”) &amp; the dative αἰθρίᾳ of feminine αἰθρία (by ellipsis of a feminine noun) of adjective αἴθριος.</div><b>κερδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to profit, gain, earn (money)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to gain</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κερδίζω</b> έδαφος - gain ground</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to win (gamble, lottery)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to win (sport, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κερδίζω from κέρδος (“the gain, the profit”) + -ίζω. See the ancient κερδαίνω.</div><b>κερδίζω έδαφος</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to gain ground</div><b>κερδοφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>profitability</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κερδοφόρ(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>κερδοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κερδοφόρα, neuter κερδοφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lucrative, profitable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. winning</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κέρδος + -φόρος</div><b>κερκίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κερκίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. radius (bone of the forearm)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. row of seats in stadium, theatre etc.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. shuttle</div>
  <div class='def'>4. net needle (for nets)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κερκίς, of uncertain origin; possibly Pre-Greek. Also compare Ancient Greek κέρκος.</div><b>κερκολήπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κερκολήπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genus Cercoleptes, kinkajou</div><b>κερνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to treat, offer at one&#x27;s own expense</div><b>κερνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κερνάω">κερνάω</a></div><b>κεσέμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεσέμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκεσέμι">γκεσέμι</a>: ram or billy goat leading the herd</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كوسم + -ι (“ending for neuters”). Αlso variation with alteration of the first letter [k] &gt; [g].</div><b>κετσές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κετσέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. harsh textile made of matted fibres wool, felt</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το δέρμα μου έχει ξεραθεί σαν <b>κετσές</b>. - My skin is dry like felt.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. especially used as a hammam scrub, for pealing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كچه with suffix -ς (-s).</div><b>κεφ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://κεφάλαιο">κεφάλαιο</a> (chapter)</div><b>κεφάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κεφάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) bighead</div><b>κεφάλαιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεφάλαια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chapter (a section of a book)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. capital</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fund</div>
  <div class='ex'>αρχικό <b>κεφάλαιο</b> - principal, initial investment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κεφάλαιον.</div><b>κεφάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεφάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>head</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κεφάλιν, from Hellenistic Koine Greek κεφάλιον, diminutive of the Ancient Greek κεφαλή, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *gʰebʰ-. Doublet of κεφαλή.</div><b>κεφάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κεφάτη, neuter κεφάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joyful, merry</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είμαι <b>κεφάτος</b> σήμερα. - I am in high spirits today.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κέφ-(ι) (“high spirits”) + -άτος (suffix for masculine adjectives).</div><b>κεφίρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kefir</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Russian кефи́р.</div><b>κεφαλάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεφαλάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. headboard, the pillow end of a bed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. capital of column, cornerstone</div>
  <div class='def'>3. spring, fountainhead, headspring (source of water)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. A repeated header or heading</div>
  <div class='def'>5. headband (the woven patch at the top and bottom of the spine of a hardback book)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κεφαλ (kefal, “head”) + -άρι</div><b>κεφαλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κεφαλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) synonym of <a href="bword://κεφάλι">κεφάλι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κεφαλή. Doublet of κεφάλι, inherited from a diminutive form.</div><b>κεφαλαία γράμματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://κεφαλαίο">κεφαλαίο</a> <a href="bword://γράμμα">γράμμα</a> capital or uppercase letters</div><b>κεφαλαίο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://κεφαλαίος">κεφαλαίος</a>:; masculine accusative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://κεφαλαίος">κεφαλαίος</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative singular</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεφαλαία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>majuscule, capital letter, uppercase letter</div><b>κεφαλαίο γράμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεφαλαία γράμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(usually plural) capital letter, block letter, uppercase letter</div><b>κεφαλαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κεφαλαία, neuter κεφαλαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capital (uppercase)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κεφάλαιος. Doublet of κεφαλαιώδης.</div><b>κεφαλαιοκράτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine κεφαλαιοκράτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capitalist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French capitaliste.</div><b>κεφαλαιοκράτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (masculine κεφαλαιοκράτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capitalist</div><b>κεφαλαιοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) capitalism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κεφάλαιο + -κρατία. Calque of French capitalisme. First attested 1887.</div><b>κεφαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κεφαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. headbutt</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: κουτουλιά f (koutouliá)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. header (act of hitting the ball with the head)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κεφάλ(ι)) + -ιά.</div><b>κεφαλομάντηλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a handkerchief worn around the head, especially a Cretan headband</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κεφάλι + μαντήλι; the former from Ancient Greek κεφαλή and the latter from Latin mantelium (“hand towel”).</div><b>κεφαλόρριζο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεφαλόρριζα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cephalorhizum (Cephalorhizum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek κεφαλόρριζον, with a semantic loan from translingual Cephalorhizum. By surface analysis, κεφάλι + ρίζα + -ο (-o, neuter suffix).</div><b>κεφτές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κεφτέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. meatball, kofta</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncommon) meat patty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish كوفته, from Classical Persian کوفته.</div><b>κεχρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεχριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>millet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κέγχροι, plural of κέγχρος.</div><b>κεχριμπάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κεχριμπάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. amber</div>
