  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κιονόκρανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capital (top part of column)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κιονόκρανον from κίων + *κρανον.</div><b>κιοφτές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κιοφτέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κεφτές">κεφτές</a></div><b>κιούριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) curium</div><b>κιρκινέζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κιρκινέζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lesser kestrel (specifically)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small bird of prey (more generally)</div><b>κιρσός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>varicose vein, varicocele</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κιρσός, from κρίκος or κίρκος.</div><b>κις</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) quiche</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κις</b> λορέν - quiche Lorraine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French quiche.</div><b>κιτριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κιτριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>citron tree</div><b>κιτρικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>citric acid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French citrique.</div><b>κιτρινοκαλιακούδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural καλιακούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alpine chough (Pyrrhocorax graculus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, κίτρινος + καλιακούδα.</div><b>κιτρινοσουσουράδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κιτρινοσουσουράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>western yellow wagtail (Motacilla flava)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, κίτρινος + σουσουράδα.</div><b>κιτρινόριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κιτρινόριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turmeric</div><b>κιτς</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kitsch (of questionable aesthetic value; excessively sentimental, overdone or vulgar)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>κιτς</b> φόρεμα που φοράει αυτή σήμερα! - What a kitsch dress she&#x27;s wearing today!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from German Kitsch.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kitsch (art, decorative objects and other forms of representation of questionable artistic or aesthetic value)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν υπάρχει υπερβολή, η τέχνη καταντά <b>κιτς</b>. - When there is exaggeration, art ends up as kitsch.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from German Kitsch.</div><b>κιτσαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κιτσαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kitsch (art, decorative objects and other forms of representation of questionable artistic or aesthetic value)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το άγαλμα είναι σκέτη <b>κιτσαρία</b>! - This statue is pure kitsch!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) kitsch people, kitsch company</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην πας σ’ αυτό το μπαρ, εκεί μαζεύεται όλη η <b>κιτσάρια</b> της Αθήνας! - Don&#x27;t go to that bar, it&#x27;s where the sum of Athenian kitschness goes!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κιτς (kits, “kitsch”) + -ία.</div><b>κιχλίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Cichlidae, the cichlids.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Cichlidae, as Κίχλη + -ίδες.</div><b>κιχώριον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κιχώρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) chicory (Cichorium intybus, Cichorium spinosum, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κιχόριον, diminutive κίχορα, a word of unknown origin.</div><b>κιόλας</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. already (in the context of &#x27;so soon&#x27;)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μόνο είκοσι λεπτά δούλεψες και τελείωσες <b>κιόλας</b>; - You only worked for twenty minutes and you&#x27;re already finished?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. right now, immediately</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περίμενε μια στιγμή και θα σου πω τώρα <b>κιόλας</b> αν πέρασες. - Wait a moment and I&#x27;ll tell you right now if you passed.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. as well, on top of that, even</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν φτάνει που χάλασε τη ρόδα, μου έσπασε και τον καθρέφτη <b>κιόλας</b>. - It wasn&#x27;t enough that he ruined my tyre, he broke my mirror as well.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek καὶ ὅλα.</div><b>κιότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) coward - see: κιοτής</div><b>κκ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of κ (k), Messrs</div><b>κκιάννω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) take, grab, touch</div><b>κκσ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>level tbsp (level tablespoon, as unit of measure)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κοφτό κουτάλι της σούπας</div><b>κλάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>large branch</div><b>κλάδεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλαδέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pruning (the action)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) aggressive tackle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλαδεύω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>κλάδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλάδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) branch, bough</div>
  <div class='ex'>(informal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) branch of organisation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. clade</div>
  <div class='def'>4. industry</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο τουριστικός <b>κλάδος</b> θα χρειαστεί χρόνο για να ανακάμψει. - The tourism industry will need time to recover.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κλάδος. The figurative and scientific senses, semantic loan from French branche and rameau. Also see the inherited doublets κλαδί and κλαρί. Possibly related to ὀκλαδόν.</div><b>κλάμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crying, weeping, cry, weep (act of shedding tears)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κλάμα, from Ancient Greek κλαῦμα.</div><b>κλάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, intransitive) to fart, break wind, blow off</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιός <b>έκλασε</b>; - Who farted?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, slang, transitive) to ignore, disregard, stand up, bail (in an uncaring manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας <b>έκλασε</b> όλους και έκανε ό,τι ήθελε. - She disregarded us all and did what she wanted.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλάω with addition of a nasal infix. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *kelh₂- (“to beat, break”). Related to κλάσμα.</div><b>κλάνω μέντες</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, vulgar) to shit oneself, shit bricks, piss oneself (to be terrified)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν είδα το άλλο αμάξι να ’ρχεται απάνω μου, <b>έκλασα μέντες</b>. - When I saw the other car driving into me, I shat myself.</div><b>κλάνω μαλλί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, vulgar) to shit oneself, shit bricks, piss oneself (to be terrified)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν είδα το άλλο αμάξι να ’ρχεται απάνω μου, <b>έκλασα μαλλί</b>. - When I saw the other car driving into me, I shat myself.</div><b>κλάνω πατάτες</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, vulgar) to shit oneself, shit bricks, piss oneself (to be terrified)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν είδα το άλλο αμάξι να ’ρχεται απάνω μου, <b>έκλασα πατάτες</b>. - When I saw the other car driving into me, I shat myself.</div><b>κλάσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλασίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) farting (the act of farting)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>κλάσιμο</b> μπροστά στους άλλους είναι αγενές. - Farting in front of others is rude.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, slang) bailing, bail</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σόρρυ για <b>κλάσιμο</b> ρε φιλαράκι. - Sorry for bailing on you, buddy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλάνω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>κλάσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fraction</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a small part of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Koine Greek κλᾰ́σμᾰ (“fragment”). From the root of Ancient Greek κλάω. Related to κλάνω.</div><b>κλέβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular present mediopassive indicative of <a href="bword://κλέβω">κλέβω</a>; to be stolen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. first-person singular present mediopassive indicative of <a href="bword://κλέβω">κλέβω</a>; to elope</div><b>κλέβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to steal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to rob</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to cheat</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>κλέβω</b> στο πόκερ. - I don&#x27;t cheat at poker.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in the passive form) <a href="bword://κλέβομαι">κλέβομαι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κλέβω, from Ancient Greek κλέπτω from the aorist stem κλεψ- (kleps-) in the pattern of -ψ- &gt; -β- as in κόπτω, κοψ- &gt; κόβω. The Ancient Greek derives from Proto-Hellenic *klépťō, from Proto-Indo-European *klép-ye-ti, from *klep-.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek клэ́фту.</div><b>κλέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλέη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. renown, fame</div>
  <div class='def'>2. honour (UK), honor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλέος.</div><b>κλέφτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλέφτες, feminine κλέφτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thief, stealer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. klepht (member of an armed group of men who took refuge in the mountains, lived by stealing and fought against the Ottomans)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κλέφτης from the ancient Ancient Greek κλέπτης with [pt] &gt; [ft].</div><b>κλέφτικα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κλέφτικα">κλέφτικα</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in the klepht fashion</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://κλέφτικο">κλέφτικο</a></div><b>κλέφτικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλέφτικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural, rare) kleftiko, the meat of the klephts, or &#x27;kleftiko&#x27; style of cooking meat: marinated, cooked nowadays in parchment paper</div><b>κλέψας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) thief, robber (used chiefly in:)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κλέψας</b> του <b>κλέψαντος</b> - dog eat dog, literally thief of thief</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλέψᾱς, masculine active participle of Ancient Greek κλέπτω.</div><b>κλέψας του κλέψαντος</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic, indeclinable) dog eat dog (regarding cases of widespread lawlessness and impunity, or in which everyone is out to get everyone else)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη περιμένεις να βρεις τίμιο άνθρωπο, εκεί μέσα είναι <b>κλέψας του κλέψαντος</b>. - Don&#x27;t expect to find an honest man, it&#x27;s dog eat dog in there.</div><b>κλήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vine, especially grapevine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλῆμα.</div><b>κλήρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλήροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lot (anything (as a die, pebble, ball, or slip of paper) used in determining a question by chance, or without human choice or will)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου έπεσε ο <b>κλήρος</b>. - It fell to me.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) lot (the part, or fate, that falls to one, as it were, by chance, or without one&#x27;s planning)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lot, allotment (something allotted; a share, part, or portion granted or distributed)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. lot, allotment (something allotted; a share, part, or portion granted or distributed); allotted land</div>
  <div class='def'>5. clergy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ιερατείο n (ierateío)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κλῆρος. The &#x27;clergy&#x27; sense is a learned borrowing from Koine Greek.</div><b>κλήρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κληρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>draw, drawing lots (the procedure by which the result of a lottery is determined)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κλήρωσις. By surface analysis, κληρώ(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>κλήση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. call</div>
  <div class='ex'>υπεραστική <b>κλήση</b> - long-distance call</div>
  <div class='def'>2. summons, subpoena</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ticket, parking ticket (for a traffic violation, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. call (to procedure, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλῆσις, related to καλέω.</div><b>κλίκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clique (small exclusive group)</div><b>κλίμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. climate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) atmosphere, mood</div>
  <div class='def'>3. see, the district or area covered by a Greek Orthodox patriarchate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Κρήτη ανήκει στο <b>κλίμα</b> του Οικουμενικού Πατριαρχείου. - Crete is in the klima of the Ecumenical Patriarchate.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κλίμα.</div><b>κλίμακα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλίμακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scale, mode</div>
  <div class='def'>2. scale</div>
  <div class='def'>3. scale</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κλίμακα</b> Κέλβιν - Kelvin scale (temperature)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. ladder</div>
  <div class='def'>5. stairs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κλῖμᾰξ, with semantic borrowings from French échelle and Italian scala.</div><b>κλίμακα Κελσίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) Celsius scale (temperature)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (UK, informal, uncountable) centigrade scale (temperature)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλίμακα + Κελσίου</div><b>κλίμακα Μποφόρ</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Beaufort scale</div><b>κλίμακας Μποφόρ</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://κλίμακα">κλίμακα</a> Μποφόρ</div><b>κλίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) bed</div>
  <div class='ex'>ξενοδοχείο 300 <b>κλινών</b> - a hotel with 300 beds</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek κλίνη.</div><b>κλίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with no passive) to bend, slant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with no passive) to tend</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (active, intransitive, passive) to inflect, decline, conjugate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλίνω.</div><b>κλίση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inclination, tilt, steepness, slope</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inclination, disposition, tendency</div>
  <div class='def'>3. inflection; declension; conjugation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλίσις.</div><b>κλαίγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κλαίω">κλαίω</a></div><b>κλαίω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cry, weep (shed tears)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το παιδί <b>έκλαιγε</b> γιατί έπεσε κάτω. - The child was crying because he fell down.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of eyes) to water, run (as when cutting onions etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα μάτια <b>κλαίνε</b> όταν καθαρίζει κανείς κρεμμύδια. - The eyes water when one is peeling onions.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to mourn, grieve for (a person or thing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ολόκληρο το χωριό τον <b>έκλαψε</b> όταν πέθανε. - The entire village mourned him when he died.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to feel sorry for (a person or thing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>κλαίω</b> με όλα αυτά που του έγιναν. - I feel sorry for him for all that happened to him.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive) to cry with laughter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλαίω.
Root κλα-. Stems: κλαι- / κλαυσ- &gt; κλαψ- / κλαυθ- &gt; κλαυτ- / κλαυμ &gt; κλαμμ &gt; κλαμ-.</div><b>κλαγγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλαγγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clash, clang (loud sound of metal being hit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλαγγή.</div><b>κλαδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλαδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>branch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κλαδίν, from Koine Greek κλαδίον, with change of accent from κλάδιον, from a diminutive of Ancient Greek κλάδος. Compare κλαρί, as well as the borrowed doublet κλάδος.</div><b>κλαδί ελιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλαδιά ελιάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. branch of an olive tree</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) olive branch (symbol of peace)</div><b>κλαδεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be pruned</div><b>κλαδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to prune, lop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cut back</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek κλαδεύω, from κλάδος m.</div><b>κλαδιά ελιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://κλαδί">κλαδί</a> ελιάς</div><b>κλαμπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) club (association of members)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) nightclub</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English club.</div><b>κλανιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλανιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) fart (intestinal gasses emitted from the anus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλάνω + -ιά.</div><b>κλανιάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλανιάρες, masculine κλανιάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>farter (flatulent person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλανιά + -άρα.</div><b>κλανιάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλανιάρηδες, feminine κλανιάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>farter (flatulent person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλανιά + -άρης.</div><b>κλαρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλαριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stick, twig, small stick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κλαρίν, from Koine Greek κλάριον, from contraction of a hypothetical earlier form *κλαδάριον, from a diminutive of Ancient Greek κλάδος. Compare κλαδί, as well as the borrowed doublet κλάδος.</div><b>κλαρίνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλαρίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clarinet (especially in Greek folk music contexts)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Στάθης παίζει <b>κλαρίνο</b> σε μια παραδοσιακή μπάντα. - Stathis plays the clarinet in a traditional band.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) clarinettist (player of the above instrument, especially in Greek folk music contexts)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Μάνος είναι το καλύτερο <b>κλαρίνο</b> της περιοχής. - Manos is the best clarinettist in the area.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, in plural) folk music venue (a venue that plays folk songs in which clarinets are a prominent instrument)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς μου πάει κάθε Σαββατόβραδο στα <b>κλαρίνα</b>. - My grandfather goes to watch folk music every Saturday night.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian clarino.</div><b>κλαριθρομυκίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural κλαριθρομυκίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) clarithromycin</div><b>κλαρινέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλαρινέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clarinet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian clarinetto.</div><b>κλαρινογαμπρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλαρινογαμπροί, feminine κλαρινογκόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, humorous) metrosexual womanizer, dressed in a vulgar, garish way</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βγαίνεις την Παρασκευή και βλέπεις ένα σωρό <b>κλαρινογαμπρούς</b> στα μπαρ. - You go out on a Friday and see a load of fuckboys in the bars.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλαρίνο + -ο- (-o-) + γαμπρός.</div><b>κλασικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κλασικός">κλασικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>classically</div><b>κλασικές κιθάρες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of κλασική <a href="bword://κιθάρα">κιθάρα</a></div><b>κλασικές μουσικές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of κλασική <a href="bword://μουσική">μουσική</a></div><b>κλασική ελληνική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Classical Greek</div><b>κλασική κιθάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλασικές κιθάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>classical guitar</div><b>κλασική μουσική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλασικές μουσικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>classical music</div><b>κλασικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλασική, neuter κλασικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>classical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French classique + -ικός.</div><b>κλασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κλασμένη, neuter κλασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) farted</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, slang) stoned, baked, high (under the influence of drugs)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κλάνω, a verb without passive forms.</div><b>κλασσικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλασσική, neuter κλασσικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κλασικός">κλασικός</a></div><b>κλαυθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(used in collocation with <a href="bword://οδυρμός">οδυρμός</a>) weeping</div>
  <div class='ex'>(θρήνος,) <b>κλαυθμός</b> και οδυρμός - (lamentation,) weeping and wailing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κλαυθμός.</div><b>κλαψομούνα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κλαψομούνης">κλαψομούνης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κλαψομούνης">κλαψομούνης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κλαψομούνης">κλαψομούνης</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλαψομούνες, masculine κλαψομούνης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, vulgar) crybaby, whinger, whiner, sniveler (someone who takes offense or excessively complains when things aren&#x27;t going well)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω βαρεθεί αυτήν την <b>κλαψομούνα</b> και τα παράπονά της. - I&#x27;ve grown tired of that whinger and her pissing and moaning.</div><b>κλαψομούνης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλαψομούνα, neuter κλαψομούνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, vulgar) moaning, whining, whinging, snivelling (complaining or crying excessively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>κλαψομούνης</b> ο πατέρας μου πάλι κάθεται και κλαίει τη μοίρα του. - My moaning bastard of a father is sitting bemoaning his fate again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλαίω + μουνί with masculine ending.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλαψομούνηδες, feminine κλαψομούνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, vulgar) crybaby, whinger, whiner, sniveler (someone who takes offense or excessively complains when things aren&#x27;t going well)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω βαρεθεί αυτόν τον <b>κλαψομούνη</b> και τα παράπονά του. - I&#x27;ve grown tired of that crybaby and his complaints.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλαίω + μουνί with masculine ending.</div><b>κλείδωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλειδώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joint</div><b>κλείνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to barricade yourself in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be hemmed in</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κλείστηκα</b> στο ασανσέρ. - I was trapped in the lift.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to play defensively</div><b>κλείνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to close, shut, close off</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κλείνω</b> την πόρτα. - I close the door.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to close, shut</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πότε <b>κλείνει</b>; - When does it close?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to turn off, shut down</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έκλεισα</b> την τηλεόραση. - I turned off the television.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to block, trap</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αστυνομία <b>έκλεισε</b> το δρόμο για το αεροδρόμιο. - Police closed the road to the airport.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to book, reserve</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έκλεισα</b> τραπέζι στο εστιατόριο. - I booked a table at the restaurant.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (referring to time or age) to become, have a sum of</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χθες <b>έκλεισα</b> τα τριάντα. - Yesterday I became thirty years old.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. see <a href="bword://κλείνομαι">κλείνομαι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κλείνω, from Ancient Greek κλείω, from Proto-Hellenic *klāyyō, from Proto-Indo-European *kleh₂uyeti, from *kleh₂u-.</div><b>κλείομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κλείνομαι">κλείνομαι</a></div><b>κλείσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλεισίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. closing, closure (the act by which something is closed)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shutting off, turning off (of tap, fluid flow, device, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. close, closing (end or conclusion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the κλεισ- stem of κλείνω + -ιμο (-imo).</div><b>κλείστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλείστρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bolt (a sliding mechanism to chamber and unchamber a cartridge in a firearm)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breechblock (the metal block that closes the breech of a breech-loading gun after insertion of the cartridge)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. shutter (the part of a camera, normally closed, that opens for a controlled period of time to let light in when taking a picture)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κλεῖστρον. By surface analysis, κλείσ- + -τρο (-tro).</div><b>κλείω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κλείνω">κλείνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Ancient Greek κλείω, found in Modern Greek only in compounds.</div><b>κλειδάριθμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλειδάριθμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passkey</div>
  <div class='def'>2. key number, keycode, passcode</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλειδ(ί)) + -ο- (-o-) + αριθμ(ός)) + -ος (-os), a calque of English key number.</div><b>κλειδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλειδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. key (an object that goes into the keyhole and as it turns left or right it locks or unlocks a door, drawer, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. key</div>
  <div class='def'>3. key</div>
  <div class='def'>4. wrench</div>
  <div class='def'>5. keystone (a central wedge-shaped piece of an arch or dome that locks the other pieces in place)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κλειδίν, from Ancient Greek κλειδίον, diminutive of κλείς, from Proto-Hellenic *klāwī́ds, from Proto-Indo-European *kleh₂u- (“nail, pin, hook - instruments, of old use for locking doors”).</div><b>κλειδαράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλειδαράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. locksmith (one who practices locksmithing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. keymaker (one who makes keys)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλειδαρ(ιά)) + -άς.</div><b>κλειδαριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλειδαριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλειδί + -αριά.</div><b>κλειδαρότρυπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλειδαρότρυπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>keyhole</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλειδαρ(ιά), “lock”) + -ό- + τρύπα. Also see the Medieval Greek κλειδότρυπα.</div><b>κλειδούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλειδούχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>switchman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κλειδοῦχος. By surface analysis, κλειδ(ί)) + -ούχος</div><b>κλειδώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to lock (door, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κλειδώνω, from Koine Greek κλειδόω, κλειδῶ, with -ώνω suffix.</div><b>κλεινόν άστυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Athens</div><b>κλεισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κλεισμένη, neuter κλεισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>closed, which has been closed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κλείνομαι, passive voice of κλείνω. Also see κεκλεισμένος.</div><b>κλεισούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stuffiness (the state or quality of being poorly-ventilated and close)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. confinement (the state of being confined or cloistered)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. narrow mountain pass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κλεισούρα, from Late Latin clausūra with folk-etymological conversion of Latin claudō, claus- to κλείνω, κλείσ- + -ούρα.</div><b>κλειστοφοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) claustrophobia</div><b>κλειστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλειστή, neuter κλειστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. closed, shut</div>
  <div class='def'>2. occlusive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive participle of κλείνω.</div><b>κλειτορίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλειτορίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clitoris</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλειτορίς.</div><b>κλεμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κλεμμένη, neuter κλεμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stolen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. who has eloped</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κλέβομαι, passive voice of κλέβω (“steal”), κλέπτω.</div><b>κλεπταποδόχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κλεπταποδόχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fence, receiver (of stolen goods)</div><b>κλεφτρόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλεφτρόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. young thief</div>
  <div class='def'>2. petty thief</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλέφτρα f, feminine of κλέφτης + -όνι (“neuter diminutive”).</div><b>κλεψύδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hourglass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλεψύδρα.</div><b>κλημάτινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλημάτινη, neuter κλημάτινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) vine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κλημάτῐνος. By surface analysis, κλήμα + -ινος (-inos, adjectival suffix).</div><b>κληματίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κληματίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative spelling of κληματσίδα</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clematis (Clematis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κληματίς, with a semantic loan from translingual Clematis for the botanical sense. Further from Ancient Greek κλῆμᾰ.</div><b>κληματαριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κληματαριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A pergola or other shading formed by the branches and leaves of a grapevine.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλήματ- + -αριά</div><b>κληματόκισσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κληματόκισσοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Any plant of the genus Clematicissus.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Clematicissus, hence the intermediate &#x27;o&#x27; instead of &#x27;i.&#x27; Calqued with κλήμα + κισσός.</div><b>κληρικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κληρικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cleric</div><b>κληρονομάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κληρονομώ">κληρονομώ</a></div><b>κληρονομημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κληρονομημένη, neuter κληρονομημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inherited</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>κληρονομημένη</b> ασθένεια. - It is an inherited disease.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κληρονομούμαι and κληρονομιέμαι, passive voices of κληρονομώ, κληρονομάω (“inherit”) respectively.</div><b>κληρονομιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κληρονομιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inheritance, heritage, legacy (that which a person is entitled to inherit, by law or testament)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. heritage (a tradition; a practice or set of values that is passed down from preceding generations through families or through institutional memory)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πολιτιστική <b>κληρονομιά</b> - cultural heritage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek *κληρονομιά from Ancient Greek κληρονομία, with synizesis of the final vowels.</div><b>κληρονομιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κληρονομούμαι">κληρονομούμαι</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>passive of κληρονομάω (klironomáo)</div><b>κληρονομούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be inherited</div><b>κληρονομώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to inherit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κληρονομῶ, contracted form of κληρονομέω. From κλῆρος + νόμος, verb νέμω.</div><b>κληρονόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κληρονόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. heir</div>
  <div class='def'>2. legatee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κληρονόμος. From κλῆρος + νόμος, verb νέμω.</div><b>κλητική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλητικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(vocative) vocative</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κλητική</b> πτώση - vocative case</div><b>κλητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλητή, neuter κλητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) invited, bidden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλητός</div><b>κλικ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable, onomatopoeic) click</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι απλά εικονίδια. Μπορείτε να κάνετε <b>κλικ</b> επάνω τους. - They&#x27;re just icons. You click (make a click) on them.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unadapted borrowing from French clic.</div><b>κλικάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to click (a computer mouse, switch, button, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το λινκ Α <b>κλικαρίστηκε</b> 6 φορές. - Link A was clicked 6 times.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κλικ (klik, “click”) + -άρω.</div><b>κλιμάκωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>escalation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κλιμακώ(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>κλιμακοστάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλιμακοστάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>staircase, stairs, stairway, stairtower</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa form: κλιμακοστάσιον. From Greek κλῖμαξ + -στάσιον.</div><b>κλιμακώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to increase, escalate</div><b>κλιματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλιματική, neuter κλιματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) climate (attributive), climatic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κλιματικός with semantic adaptation to the modern meaning of κλίμα. By surface analysis, κλιματ-) + -ικός.</div><b>κλιματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλιματισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. air conditioning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. air conditioner</div><b>κλιματιστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλιματιστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>air conditioner</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of κλιματιστικός</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of κλιματιστικός</div><b>κλινήρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλινήρης, neuter κλινήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bedridden, confined to bed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κλινήρης.</div><b>κλινική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλινικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clinic (hospital department; a private hospital)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French clinique and English clinic, from Late Latin clīnicus, from Ancient Greek κλῑνικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of κλινικός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>κλινοσκέπασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλινοσκεπάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) bedcover, coverlet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural, literary) bedclothes, bedding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλίνη + -ο- (-o-) + σκέπασμα, a calque of German Bettdecke.</div><b>κλισιόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλισιόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clinometer, inclinometer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alidade</div><b>κλιτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλιτική, neuter κλιτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inflectional</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κλιτικός.</div><b>κλομπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γκλομπ">γκλομπ</a> (gklomp)</div><b>κλοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. theft</div>
  <div class='def'>2. plagiarism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κλοπή.</div><b>κλοτσάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to kick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλοτσώ + -άω.</div><b>κλοτσιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλοτσιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kick (a hit or strike with the leg or foot)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κλοτσιά. By surface analysis, κλοτσ(ώ)) + -ιά.</div><b>κλοτσώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to kick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλότσος + -ώ.</div><b>κλουβί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλουβιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κλουβίν, from earlier κλουβίον, from Koine Greek κλωβίον.</div><b>κλούβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κλούβια, neuter κλούβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. addled (inviable)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for mind) having a mental retardation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κλουβί.</div><b>κλπ.</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>initialism of <a href="bword://και">και</a> λοιπά: etc (an abbreviation of et cetera)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> και (kai, “and”) + λοιπά</div><b>κλυδωνίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rock, sway (as of a ship at sea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κλυδωνίζομαι, from κλύδων.</div><b>κλωνάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κλωνάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>branch, twig</div><b>κλωστή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλωστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thread, cotton, strand, filament</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic) cotton twist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κλωστή, from the feminine of the Ancient Greek adjective κλωστός, from the verb κλώθω. Verbal adjective of κλώθω.</div><b>κλόουν</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) clown (performance artist associated with circus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πολλά άτομα φοβούνται τους <b>κλόουν</b>. - Many people are afraid of clowns.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, indeclinable) clown, buffoon (person who acts in a silly fashion)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πρωθυπουργός είναι ο μεγαλύτερος <b>κλόουν</b> της πολιτικής. - The prime minister is the biggest clown in politics.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English clown.</div><b>κλότσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλότσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a kick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κλότσος, from Medieval Latin calcio, from Latin calx (“heel of the foot”).</div><b>κλύδων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλύδωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rough sea, swell, storm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) unrest on the political or social level</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλύδων.</div><b>κλώθω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to spin (wool, cotton, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλώθω, itself of uncertain origin.</div><b>κλώνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κλώνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strand (of rope)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clone</div>
  <div class='def'>3. slip, cutting</div>
  <div class='def'>4. branch, shoot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κλών</div><b>κλώσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κλώσσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sitter, broody hen (a hen which is sitting on and warming her eggs in order for them to hatch)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>κλώσσα</b> φροντίζει τα κλωσσοπούλια της. - The broody hen is looking after her chicks.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) windbag, chatterbox (a loud and talkative woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην την ακούς, την γριά <b>κλώσσα</b>! - Don&#x27;t listen to her, the old biddy!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κλωσσώ, from Ancient Greek κλώζω.</div><b>κνήμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κνήμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shin, tibia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κνήμη.</div><b>κοάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) koala</div><b>κοίλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοίλη, neuter κοίλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concave, hollow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek adjective κοῖλος.</div><b>κοίλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοιλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hollow, cavity, concavity, dent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek.</div><b>κοίτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. river bed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic, rare) bed (used only in legal expression:)</div>
  <div class='ex'>χωρισμός από τραπέζης και <b>κοίτης</b> - lit.: &quot;separation of table and bed&quot;. Legal separation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek κοίτη.</div><b>κοβάλτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cobalt</div><b>κογιότ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) coyote</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English coyote, from Spanish coyote and Nahuatl coyotl.</div><b>κοιλάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοιλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>valley</div><b>κοιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοιλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stomach, belly, tummy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ventricle (heart)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ventricle (brain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowing from Ancient Greek κοιλία. Compare the inherited doublet κοιλιά.</div><b>κοιλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοιλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abdomen, stomach, belly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, with synizesis to avoid hiatus, from Ancient Greek κοιλία. Compare the borrowed doublet κοιλία.</div><b>κοιλιακή μαρμαρυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ventricular fibrillation</div><b>κοιλιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοιλιακή, neuter κοιλιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abdominal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ventricular</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοιλιακός. By surface analysis, κοιλιά + -ακός.</div><b>κοιλιακός τύφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) typhoid fever, abdominal typhus (the same disease)</div><b>κοιλότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοιλότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cavity, hollow (an unfilled space in something solid)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cavity, hollow (an unfilled space in something solid); cavity (a hollow area within the body)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. locule, loculus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κοιλότητα, accusative singular of κοιλότης. By surface analysis, κοίλ(ος)) + -ότητα</div><b>κοιμάμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sleep (to rest in a state of reduced consciousness)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω να <b>κοιμηθώ</b> αλλά οι διπλανοί κάνουν θόρυβο. - I want to sleep but the next door neighbours are making noise.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to idle, laze, lounge, loaf (to do nothing or to be lazy and unwilling to move)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάνε κάτι και μην <b>κοιμάσαι</b>! - Do something and stop lazing about!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to sleep with (to have sexual intercourse)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άντε τώρα να μετρήσεις με πόσους έχει <b>κοιμηθεί</b> αυτή. - It&#x27;s anyone&#x27;s guess how many she has slept with.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (euphemistic, figurative) ) to repose [in the Lord], to die</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο άγιος <b>εκοιμήθη</b> στα εξήντα του χρόνια. - The saint reposed in the Lord (died) at age sixty.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κοιμοῦμαι, κοιμᾶμαι, from Ancient Greek κοιμῶμαι, contracted form of κοιμάομαι, mediopassive of κοιμάω.</div><b>κοιμάμαι με τις κότες</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://κοιμάμαι">κοιμάμαι</a>, <a href="bword://με">με</a> (me), <a href="bword://τις">τις</a> (tis), κότες.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (humorous, idiomatic) to go to bed with the sun, to go to bed with the chickens (to sleep early, to go to bed early)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Που να τον δεις αυτόν στα κλαμπ, αυτός <b>κοιμάται</b> πάντα <b>με τις κότες</b>! - As if you&#x27;d see him in clubs, he always goes to bed early!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;to sleep with the chickens&quot;. Due to the fact that chickens tend to go to sleep when the sun sets. Compare similar idioms in other languages such as French se coucher avec les poules, Portuguese dormir com as galinhas and Polish chodzić spać z kurami.</div><b>κοιμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to put to bed, put to sleep</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to anaesthetise (UK), anesthetize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to tranquilise (UK), tranquilize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) to quieten, lull</div><b>κοιμητήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοιμητήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) cemetery, graveyard</div><b>κοιμητήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοιμητήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cemetery, graveyard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοιμητήριον. By surface analysis, κοιμάμαι + -τήριο.</div><b>κοιμούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κοιμάμαι">κοιμάμαι</a></div><b>κοινά αηδόνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://κοινό">κοινό</a> <a href="bword://αηδόνι">αηδόνι</a></div><b>κοινά ταμεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://κοινό">κοινό</a> <a href="bword://ταμείο">ταμείο</a></div><b>κοινές τσίχλες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://κοινή">κοινή</a> <a href="bword://τσίχλα">τσίχλα</a></div><b>κοινή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κοινός">κοινός</a> &quot;common&quot;</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) common, standard)</div><b>κοινή λογική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) common sense</div><b>κοινή τσίχλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοινές τσίχλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turdus philomelos: song thrush</div><b>κοινοί κύκνοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://κοινός">κοινός</a> <a href="bword://κύκνος">κύκνος</a></div><b>κοινοβίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοινοβίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cenobite, member of a commune</div>
  <div class='def'>2. that belongs genus Κοινοβίτης (Coenobita) of the family Coenobitidae</div><b>κοινοβιάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοινοβιάτες, feminine κοινοβιάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cenobite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κοινοβιάτης from Koine Greek κοινόβιον, κοινοβιακός.</div><b>κοινοβουλευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοινοβουλευτική, neuter κοινοβουλευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parliamentary</div><b>κοινοβουλευτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parliamentarism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κοινοβουλευτ(ικός)) + -ισμός, a loose calque of French parlementarisme.</div><b>κοινοβούλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοινοβούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parliament</div><b>κοινολεξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοινολεξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saying common, trivial things</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Hellenistic verb Koine Greek κοινολεκτέω / κοινολεκτῶ (“use the language of common life”) and -ία (“suffix for feminine nouns”).</div><b>κοινοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to communicate (to impart or transmit (information or knowledge); to make known)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Internet, transitive) to share</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κοινοποιῶ. By surface analysis, κοινός + -ποιώ</div><b>κοινοπραξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοινοπραξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joint venture, consortium</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek κοινοπραξία, from Koine Greek κοινοπραγία.</div><b>κοινωνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κοινωνώ">κοινωνώ</a></div><b>κοινωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοινωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. community</div>
  <div class='ex'>σοσιαλιστική <b>κοινωνία</b> - socialist community</div>
  <div class='def'>2. society, communion (a group of people with a shared culture)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>κοινωνία</b> της αφθονίας - the affluent society</div>
  <div class='def'>3. communion</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θεία <b>Κοινωνία</b> - Holy Communion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κοινωνίᾱ, from κοινωνός + -ία, from κοινός.</div><b>κοινωνικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κοινωνικός">κοινωνικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socially</div><b>κοινωνική απομάκρυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) social distancing (COVID-19 epidemic precaution)</div><b>κοινωνική τάξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>social class</div><b>κοινωνικοοικονομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοινωνικοοικονομική, neuter κοινωνικοοικονομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socioeconomic, socio-economic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοινωνικός + -ο- (-o-) + οικονομικός.</div><b>κοινωνικοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. socialisation, socialization</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nationalisation, taking into social ownership</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κοινωνικός + -ποιω (-poio, “form, characterise”)</div><b>κοινωνικοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to socialise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to nationalise, take into social ownership</div><b>κοινωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοινωνική, neuter κοινωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. social</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sociable, gregarious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοινωνικός. By surface analysis, κοινωνός + -ικός.</div><b>κοινωνιολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοινωνιολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sociology (study of society, human social interactions, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοινωνί(α), “society”) + -ο- (-o-) + -λογία, a calque of French sociologie.</div><b>κοινωνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to receive communion, communicate, take communion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to give communion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κοινωνώ, from Ancient Greek κοινωνέω, κοινωνῶ, from κοινωνός, from κοινός.</div><b>κοινό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κοινός">κοινός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) public (people in general)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η έκθεση θα είναι ανοιχτή για το <b>κοινό</b> - The exhibition will be open to the public.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) audience</div><b>κοινό αηδόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοινά αηδόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Luscinia megarhynchos: common nightingale</div><b>κοινό ορτύκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοινά ορτύκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common quail, Coturnix coturnix</div><b>κοινό ταμείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοινά ταμεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. common purse, people&#x27;s purse, shared or community fund</div>
  <div class='def'>2. kitty, pot</div><b>κοινόβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοινόβια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coenobium, monastic community</div>
  <div class='def'>2. any community of people living together</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κοινόβιον, neuter of κοινόβιος. Morphologically κοινό- + βί(ος), “life”) + suffix for neuters -ο.</div><b>κοινόι φασιανοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://κοινός">κοινός</a> <a href="bword://φασιανός">φασιανός</a></div><b>κοινόλεκτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοινόλεκτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the common body of a standard language which is spoken by all, including the dialectal speakers of regional or other varieties.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Hellenistic adjective of Koine Greek κοινόλεκτος.</div><b>κοινόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confederation (of city-states with central government)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοινός.</div><b>κοινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοινή, neuter κοινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. common, mutual, shared, joint</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοινή</b> πεποίθηση - common belief</div>
  <div class='def'>2. common, commonplace, ordinary</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>κοινός</b> άνθρωπος - the common man</div>
  <div class='def'>3. public</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοινή</b> γνώμη - public opinion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοινός, from Proto-Indo-European *ḱóm (“with”).</div><b>κοινός κότσυφας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common blackbird, Turdus merula</div><b>κοινός κύκνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοινοί κύκνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mute swan, Cygnus olor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) common swan</div><b>κοινός νους</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) common sense</div><b>κοινός τόπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commonplace</div><b>κοινός φασιανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοινοί φασιανοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common pheasant</div><b>κοινότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοινότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. community (a group sharing common characteristics, such as the same language, law, religion, or tradition)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ευρωπαϊκή Οικονομική <b>Κοινότητα</b> - European Economic Community</div>
  <div class='def'>2. community, commune (administrative division)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κοινότης), with semantic loan from French communauté and commune. By surface analysis, κοινός + -ότητα.</div><b>κοινότοπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοινότοπη, neuter κοινότοπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commonplace, hackneyed, platitudinous, trite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly by back-formation, κοινοτοπ(ία)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>κοινόχρηστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοινόχρηστή, neuter κοινόχρηστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common, communal, shared (for the use of a community; not reserved for private use)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κοινο- (koino-) + χρηστ(ός)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>κοινώνησα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://κοινωνώ">κοινωνώ</a>; Ι received communion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://κοινωνώ">κοινωνώ</a>; I gave communion</div><b>κοιτάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to look at yourself</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κοιτάχτηκε</b> στον καθρέφτη. - She looked at herself in the mirror.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be looked at, have a checkup</div><b>κοιτάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to mind, take care</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) to be interested</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κοιτάζει</b> την τσέπη του. - He is interested in his pocket [his money]</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to look at</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to examine, look over</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to look after an elderly person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κοιτάζω from Ancient Greek κοιτάζω (“put to bed”), from κοίτη. The second meaning (watch), from the habit of guards and nightwatchmen to have their bed near their post.</div><b>κοιτάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to look at</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to look after</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to examine, look over</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοιτάζω, with metaplasm -άζω &gt; -άω.</div><b>κοιτίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοιτίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cradle</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοιτίδα</b> πολιτισμού - cradle of civilization</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From accusative κοιτῐ́δᾰ of Ancient Greek κοιτῐ́ς. Via Katharevousa.</div><b>κοιτιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to look at yourself</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κοιτάχτηκε</b> στον καθρέφτη. - She looked at herself in the mirror.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be looked at, have a checkup</div><b>κοιτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κοιτάω">κοιτάω</a></div><b>κοκ.</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://ούτω">ούτω</a> <a href="bword://καθεξής">καθεξής</a></div><b>κοκαΐνη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cocaine (the drug)</div><b>κοκαλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to freeze (to stop (become motionless) or be stopped due to attentiveness, fear, surprise, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to go stiff</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόκαλ(ο), “bone”) + -ώνω.</div><b>κοκκίνισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reddening</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reddening, blush (redden in the face)</div><b>κοκκινέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοκκινέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>red wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόκκιν(ος), “red”) + -έλι.</div><b>κοκκινίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to blush, redden, colour (UK), color (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek κοκκινίζω, from κόκκινος + -ίζω.</div><b>κοκκινίλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοκκινίλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>redness, rash</div><b>κοκκινιστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοκκινιστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meat (usually beef or pork) braised in tomato sauce.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοκκινιστό</b> με μακαρόνια - meat with tomato sauce and spaghetti</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter of adjective κοκκινιστός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://κοκκινιστός">κοκκινιστός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κοκκινιστός">κοκκινιστός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter of adjective κοκκινιστός.</div><b>κοκκινιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοκκινιστή, neuter κοκκινιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>braised in tomato sauce (sauce made from tomatoes, not ketchup)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοκκινιστό</b> κοτόπουλο - chicken with tomato sauce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοκκινίζω + -τος (-tos, “verbal adjective suffix”). For a semantic parallel, compare Chinese 紅燒 /红烧.</div><b>κοκκινολάχανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοκκινολάχανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>red cabbage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόκκινος + λάχανο.</div><b>κοκκινολαίμης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοκκινολαίμηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>European robin, robin, redbreast (Erithacus rubecula)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόκκινος + λαιμός.</div><b>κοκκινομάλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοκκινομάλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>redhead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κόκκινος + μαλλιά</div><b>κοκκινομάλλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>redhead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κοκκινο- (kokkino-) + -μάλλης</div><b>κοκκινομύτης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοκκινομύτα or κοκκινομυτού, neuter κοκκινομύτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rednosed</div><b>κοκκινοπέρδικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοκκινοπέρδικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alectoris rufa : red-legged partridge, French partridge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κόκκινος + πέρδικα</div><b>κοκκινοχρυσό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://κοκκινοχρυσός">κοκκινοχρυσός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κοκκινοχρυσός">κοκκινοχρυσός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοκκινοχρυσά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bright auburn, titian color</div><b>κοκκινοχρυσός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοκκινοχρυσή, neuter κοκκινοχρυσό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of a reddish-golden color; titian, bright auburn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόκκινος + χρυσός.</div><b>κοκκινότσιχλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοκκινότσιχλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>redwing, Turdus iliacus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κόκκινος + τσίχλα</div><b>κοκορέτσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοκορέτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kokoretsi (a Greek savory Easter dish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Albanian kukurec, which according to Sevan Nişanyan is loaned from Serbo-Croatian kukuruza (“cornbread”)).</div><b>κοκοφοίνικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοκοφοίνικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coconut palm (Cocos nucifera)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compound of Spanish coco + φοίνικας.</div><b>κοκτέιλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) cocktail (mixed drink, usually with alcohol content)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) cocktail party (event where such drinks are drunk)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) mixture of different components</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English cocktail.</div><b>κοκτέιλ μολότοφ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Molotov cocktail</div><b>κοκτέιλ μολότωφ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κοκτέιλ">κοκτέιλ</a> μολότοφ</div><b>κοκόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kokoni (Greek dog breed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Thought to be from κοκόνα.</div><b>κοκότα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοκότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) tart, slut, trollop (woman of loose morals)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic) courtesan (high-class prostitute)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, humorous) a woman excessively concerned with her beauty and appearance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French cocotte.</div><b>κολάρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>collar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κολάρος m, borrowed from Venetan collaro</div><b>κολέγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολέγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. college</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (ironic) holiday camp (place where insufficient discipline and strictness prevail)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English college, from Latin collēgium, with -ιο ending (as in ancient -ιον (-ion) and Latin -ium).</div><b>κολίανδρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κόλιανδρος">κόλιανδρος</a></div><b>κολακεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to flatter, praise excessively, compliment exaggeratedly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κολακεύω.</div><b>κολαστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολαστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hell, a place of suffering and torture.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κολᾰστήρῐον.</div><b>κολατσιό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολατσιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snack, collation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan colazion.</div><b>κολατσό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολατσά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κολατσιό">κολατσιό</a></div><b>κολεόπτερα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Coleoptera, the taxon comprised of all beetles.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Coleoptera.</div><b>κολεός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κολεοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vagina</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sheath</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sheath, scabbard</div>
  <div class='def'>4. coleus (genus of plants)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek κολεός. The botanical sense is a semantic loan from translingual Coleus.</div><b>κολεόσπασμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κολεόσπασμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vaginismus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κολεό + σπασμος (spasmos, “spasm”)</div><b>κολητσίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολητσίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κολλητσίδα">κολλητσίδα</a></div><b>κολιέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) necklace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French collier (“necklace”).</div><b>κολιμπρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) hummingbird</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French colibri (“hummingbird”).</div><b>κολιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κολιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Atlantic chub mackerel (Scomber colias)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κολίας.</div><b>κολλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to glue, attach, affix, stick</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to infect</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to pester</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη μου <b>κολλάς</b>! Με έχεις τρελάνει! - Stop bugging me! You are driving me crazy!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to be glued, be attached, be affixed, be stuck</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, intransitive) to be infected</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern κολλώ + -άω, from Ancient Greek κολλῶ, contracted form of κολλάω.</div><b>κολλέγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολλέγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://κολέγιο">κολέγιο</a></div><b>κολλαριστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κολλαριστή, neuter κολλαριστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. starched, ironed (of clothing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crisp, trim, smart (of personal appearance)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. crisp, fresh (of banknotes, etc)</div><b>κολλημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κολλημένη, neuter κολλημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. glued, stuck</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) with an idée fixe stuck in the mind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κολλιέμαι, passive voice of κολλάω/κολλώ (“I glue”).</div><b>κολλητά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>close together, adjoined</div><b>κολλητήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. soldering iron (tool)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. burdock</div><b>κολλητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κολλητική, neuter κολλητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sticky, adhesive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contagious, infectious, catching (of a disease)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) contagious, infectious (behaviour, laughter, mood, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the κολλη- stem of κολλώ + -τικός. Compare Ancient Greek κολλητικός.</div><b>κολλητσίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολλητσίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) an unwelcome person who attaches themselves to someone else</div>
  <div class='def'>2. burdock, common name of various plants with sticky shoots or seeds</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) bur, (idiomatic): beggar&#x27;s lice, hitchhiker, sticker, sticker bur, (although grammatically incorrect, also written as): burr</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>κολλητσίδα</b> αγριμόνιας. - It&#x27;s an agrimony plant&#x27;s bur.</div><b>κολλητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stuck, glued</div>
  <div class='def'>2. close together, adjoined</div>
  <div class='def'>3. firm, tight, tight-fitting</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine κολλητή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a best friend, bosom buddy</div><b>κολλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κολλάω">κολλάω</a></div><b>κολλώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κολλώδης, neuter κολλώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gummy, sticky, gluy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κολλώδης.</div><b>κολλώδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) collodion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French collodion, from κολλώδης.</div><b>κολοβός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κολοβή, neuter κολοβό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. docked, curtailed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο μύθος του Αισώπου για την <b>κολοβή</b> αλεπού. - Aesop&#x27;s fable of the fox without a tail.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) of which a part is missing (usually the last)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κολοβός</b> στίχος - shortened/incomplete verse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κολοβός.</div><b>κολοκάσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>taro (the Colocasia esculenta plant and its edible tuber)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Cypriot Greek κολοκάσιν, from Byzantine Greek *κολοκάσιν, from Ancient Greek κολοκάσιον, a parallel form of κολοκασία.</div><b>κολοκάσιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολοκάσ̌ια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cyprus form of <a href="bword://κολοκάσι">κολοκάσι</a></div><b>κολοκυθάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολοκυθάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>courgette, zucchini</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κολοκύθι + -άκι</div><b>κολοκυθιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολοκυθιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. squash plant (yielding: marrow, courgette, pumpkin, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a children&#x27;s game</div><b>κολοκυθόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολοκυθόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pumpkin pie, squash pie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κολοκύθι + πίτα</div><b>κολοκύθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολοκύθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pumpkin (vegetable squash)</div><b>κολοκύθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολοκύθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. squash (vegetable family)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. marrow (vegetable), courgette</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κολοκύνθιον, diminutive of κολοκύνθη.</div><b>κολομβιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κολομβιανή, neuter κολομβιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Colombian (relating to Colombia or its people)</div><b>κολονοσκόπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολονοσκοπήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colonoscopy</div><b>κολοτούμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολοτούμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κωλοτούμπα">κωλοτούμπα</a></div><b>κολοχτύπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολοχτύπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mediterranean slipper lobster (Scyllarides latus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, Ancient Greek κόλος + χτυπώ + -α (-a, nominal suffix).</div><b>κολπίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολπίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vaginitis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κόλπος + -ίτιδα</div><b>κολπική μαρμαρυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) atrial fibrillation</div><b>κολπόσπασμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κολπόσπασμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vaginismus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κολπό + σπασμος (spasmos, “spasm”)</div><b>κολυμβητήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>natatorium, swimming pool, facility designed for swimming games or training</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested around 1855. κολυμβητής + -τήριο.</div><b>κολυμβητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κολυμβητές, feminine κολυμβήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swimmer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κολυμβητής. By surface analysis, κολυμπώ + -τής.</div><b>κολυμπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to swim</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου αρέσει να <b>κολυμπάω</b>. - I like to swim.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to swim</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι πολύ πλούσιος. <b>Κολυμπάει</b> στο χρήμα. - He is very rich. He swims in money.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare) to swim; to be swum</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η θάλασσα είναι μολυσμένη. Δεν <b>κολυμπιέται</b> πια. - This sea is polluted. It cannot be swum any more.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From modern κολυμπ(ώ) + -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek κολυμπῶ, from Koine Greek κολυμβῶ pronounced with [mb], uncontracted form of κολυμβάω, said by Beekes to be a denominative verbal derivation of κόλυμβος.</div><b>κολυμπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κολυμπάω">κολυμπάω</a> to swim</div><b>κολχόζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kolkhoz (Soviet farming collective)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Russian колхо́з.</div><b>κολόβωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κολοβώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stumping, truncating, truncation (the act or result of cutting a part off)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stump (what remains after amputation or excision)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From modern verb κολοβώ(νω), “to stump, to truncate”) + -μα (-ma, neuter noun suffix). Morphologically, identical to ancient κολόβωμα.</div><b>κολόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. column, pillar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. post</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian colonna, from Latin columna.</div><b>κολόνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολόνιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eau de Cologne</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian colonia, from French Cologne.</div><b>κολύμβηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) swimming</div><b>κολώνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κολόνα">κολόνα</a></div><b>κολώνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολώνιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κολόνια">κολόνια</a></div><b>κομάντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) commando</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English commando.</div><b>κομίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bring, carry</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κομίζω</b> γλαύκα εις Αθήνας - carry coals to Newcastle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κομίζω.</div><b>κομίζω γλαύκα εις Αθήνας</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carry coals to Newcastle (literally “bring owls to Athens”)</div><b>κομβίον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://κουμπί">κουμπί</a></div><b>κομεντί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) A comedy, specifically a romantic comedy or dramedy (genre of theatre, film, television etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A reborrowing from French comédie, ultimately from Ancient Greek κωμῳδῐ́ᾱ. Doublet of κωμωδία.</div><b>κομητεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κομητείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>county (land ruled by a count or countess)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κόμης + -εία. First attested 1856.</div><b>κομμάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κομμάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. potsherd, shard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. piece, item, unit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. part</div>
  <div class='def'>4. piece (mostly used for one of: Knight, bishop or rook)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κομμάτι(ν)), from Koine Greek κομμάτιον, from Ancient Greek κόμμα. Related to κόπτω.</div><b>κομμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κομμένη, neuter κομμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cut</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sliced, shredded</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) tired, exhausted (of appearance, especially of the face)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε βλέπω <b>κομμένο</b> από το χθεσινό ξενύχτι. - I see that you look exhausted from yesterday&#x27;s all-nighter.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κόβομαι, passive voice of κόβω (“to cut”).</div><b>κομματάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κομματάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://κομμάτι">κομμάτι</a> n: little bit, small piece</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κομμάτ(ι)) + -άκι.</div><b>κομμουνίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κομμουνίστριες, masculine κομμουνιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>communist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French communiste.</div><b>κομμουνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) communism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French communisme.</div><b>κομμουνιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κομμουνιστές, feminine κομμουνίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>communist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French communiste.</div><b>κομμουνιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κομμουνιστική, neuter κομμουνιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>communist</div><b>κομμωτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κομμωτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hairdresser&#x27;s, hairdressing salon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation from κομμωτής + -τήριο.</div><b>κομμωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κομμωτές, feminine κομμώτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hairdresser, hair stylist (for women)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κομμωτής.</div><b>κομμώτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κομμώτριες, masculine κομμωτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hairdresser, hair stylist (for women)</div><b>κομοδίνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κομοδίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nightstand, bedside table</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Italian comodino, from French commode, from Latin commodus (“convenient, comfortable”).</div><b>κομορικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Comorian (the language of Comoros and its people)</div><b>κομορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κομορική, neuter κομορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Comoran, Comorian (relating to Comoros or its people or language)</div><b>κομουνίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κομουνίστριες, masculine κομουνιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κομμουνίστρια">κομμουνίστρια</a></div><b>κομουνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://κομμουνισμός">κομμουνισμός</a></div><b>κομουνιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κομουνιστές, feminine κομουνίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κομμουνιστής">κομμουνιστής</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French communiste.</div><b>κομουνιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κομουνιστική, neuter κομουνιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κομμουνιστικός">κομμουνιστικός</a></div><b>κομπέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) presenter, compere</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French compère.</div><b>κομπίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) scam, racket, racketeering, jobbery, wrangle, (illegal moneymaking scheme)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλα τα λεφτά του με <b>κομπίνες</b> τα έβγαλε. - He made all his money by scams.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French combine.</div><b>κομπιναδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κομπιναδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) fraudster, scammer, racketeer, jobber, swindler (someone who uses illegal schemes to make money)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κομπίνα + -αδόρος</div><b>κομπινεζόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) slip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French combinaison.</div><b>κομπιουτεράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κομπιουτεράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) calculator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κομπιουτερ (kompiouter, “computer”) + -άκι</div><b>κομπιούτερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) computer</div><b>κομπρέσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κομπρέσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compress</div>
  <div class='ex'>κρύα <b>κομπρέσα</b> - cold compress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian compressa.</div><b>κομπρεσέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pneumatic drill (UK), jackhammer (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French compresseur (“compressor”).</div><b>κομψός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κομψή, neuter κομψό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chic, smart, elegant, stylish</div><b>κονίαμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κονιάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mortar</div><b>κονγκολέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κονγκολέζικη, neuter κονγκολέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Congolese (relating to the country or its people)</div><b>κονδυλώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κονδυλώδης, neuter κονδυλώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nodular, noduled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tuberous</div>
  <div class='def'>3. condylar</div><b>κονιάκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cognac, brandy (a distilled beverage)</div><b>κονικλοτροφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rabbit farm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κύνικλος + τροφεῖον.</div><b>κονσέρβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κονσέρβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tin (UK), can (US) - (preserved food container)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. canning, tinning (the process)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) prerecorded television programme</div><b>κονσόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κονσόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>console</div><b>κοντά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. near, nearby</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nearly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κοντά, from Ancient Greek κοντός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κοντός">κοντός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>κοντάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοντάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pole, spar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flagpole</div>
  <div class='def'>3. spear, javelin, pikestaff</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κοντάριν, from Ancient Greek κοντάριον, diminutive of κοντός. Compare -άριον.</div><b>κοντέινερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) container, cargo container, shipping container</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of English container</div><b>κονταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to shorten</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to reduce in height</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοντ(ός)) + -αίνω.</div><b>κοντείνερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κοντέινερ">κοντέινερ</a></div><b>κοντεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be almost over, be almost done, get close</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be about to (do something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κοντεύω, from κοντά + -εύω.</div><b>κοντινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοντινή, neuter κοντινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nearby (adjacent)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. close (for people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοντά + -ινός.</div><b>κοντομάνικο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://κοντομάνικος">κοντομάνικος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κοντομάνικος">κοντομάνικος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοντομάνικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short-sleeved top (but in particular, usually) T-shirt</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φοράς <b>κοντομάνικο</b> χειμωνιάτικα; Είσαι στα καλά σου; - You&#x27;re wearing a T-shirt in winter? Are you mad?</div><b>κοντομάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοντομάνικη, neuter κοντομάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of clothes) short-sleeved</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοντομάνικη</b> φανέλα - short-sleeved shirt/T-shirt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοντός + μανίκι.</div><b>κοντούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοντούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the finish of a design; outline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian contorno (“contour, outline”).</div><b>κοντρολάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be controlled</div><b>κοντρολάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to control</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian controllare.</div><b>κοντσέρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοντσέρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. concerto (for instrument/s and orchestra)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοντσέρτο</b> για πιάνο και ορχήστρα - concerto for piano and orchestra.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. concert, performance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian concerto.</div><b>κοντός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοντή, neuter κοντό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short (in height)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * Sense pole: from Ancient Greek κοντός (“pole”) (noun).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) pole</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * Sense pole: from Ancient Greek κοντός (“pole”) (noun).</div><b>κοπάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοπάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flock (large number of animals associated together in a group)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. herd (of cows, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. school (of fish)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. cattle</div><b>κοπέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοπέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. girlfriend</div>
  <div class='def'>2. young adult woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Initial meaning ‘maid, stepdaughter’, feminine of Byzantine Greek κόπελος, from South Proto-Slavic *kopylъ ‘shoot, sprout; bastard’; cf. also Albanian kopil (“bastard”), Romanian copilă (“child, girl”)).</div><b>κοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cutting, felling, chopping</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοπή. See κόπτω, κόβω.</div><b>κοπανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to beat, thrash, whack, thwack</div><b>κοπερνίκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) copernicium</div><b>κοπετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lamentation, wailing (usually with breast-beating)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: οδυρμός m (odyrmós), θρήνος m (thrínos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κοπετός, from Ancient Greek κοπετός.</div><b>κοπιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to toil, to labour (UK), to labor (US), to work hard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (imperative) to come in</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κοπιάστε</b> στο φτωχικό μας! Καλώς ήρθατε! - Come in to our humble [home/adobe]! Welcome!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited and learned borrowing from Koine Greek κοπιάζω. See the ancient verb κοπιόω. By surface analysis, κόπ(ος)) + -ιάζω.</div><b>κοπιαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοπιαστική, neuter κοπιαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>arduous, laborious, strenuous, toilsome (requiring great effort, hard work)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek κοπιαστικός. By surface analysis, κοπιασ-) + -τικός.</div><b>κοπρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοπρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stray dog, cur</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) layabout, scum, cur</div><b>κοπριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοπριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dung, manure, muck</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fertilizer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόπρος.</div><b>κοπτάτσια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cooption, assignment of an (elected) office to a person by higher authorities without an election</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Entered Greek political discourse from Russian кооптация (kooptacija, “cooption”), ultimately from Latin cooptatio (“cooption”).</div><b>κοπτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοπτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>incisor</div><b>κορ ανγκλέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cor anglais, English horn (a woodwind instrument)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French cor anglais (literally “English horn”).</div><b>κοράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. raven, common raven (Corvus corax)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crow (a member of the corvid family)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) undertaker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek κοράκιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek κόραξ.</div><b>κοράλλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοράλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coral (the organism and the structures it creates)</div><b>κορέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορέοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κοριός">κοριός</a></div><b>κορίανδρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κόλιανδρος">κόλιανδρος</a></div><b>κορίτσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κορίτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. girl, daughter</div>
  <div class='ex'>έκανε <b>κορίτσι</b> - she gave birth to a girl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. girlfriend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κορίτσι, diminutive of Ancient Greek κόρη + -ίτσι, form of -ίτσιν.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek кури́ц.</div><b>κορακίστικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (childish) A form of wordplay used by children to disguise their sentences, similar to pig Latin, either by inserting nonsense syllables (such as <a href="bword://κα">κα</a>) between each syllable of the original phrase or by reversing the order of the syllables.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) gibberish, gobbledygook, mumbo jumbo (any confusing or meaningless speech)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άσε τα <b>κορακίστικα</b> και μιλά καθαρά! - Stop speaking gibberish and speak properly!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively, humorous) Katharevousa (the form of Greek more similar to the Ancient language, replaced in 1982)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόραξ.</div><b>κοραλλί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) coral (the colour)</div><b>κοραλλιογενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοραλλιογενής, neuter κοραλλιογενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coralligenous, coralliferous (bearing coral)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοράλλι + -γενής.</div><b>κορασίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κορασίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, dated) girl, miss, maiden (young unwed woman, approximately aged 15 to marriage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κορασίς, a diminutive of κόρη.</div><b>κορατσίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κορατσίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) crow</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κρά! Κρά! Κορατσίνα! // Χαρτί σου πέμπ’η μάνα σου, // Χαρτί τσαι χαρτουλλίνα. - Caw! Caw! Crow! // Your mum sends you a letter, // letter and postcard.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Various forms ultimately from a confusion of κόρακας, κορώνα and possibly even Proto-Slavic *gavornъ (“raven”).</div><b>κορβανάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορβανάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. safe, strongbox, coffer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fund, coffers</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο κρατικός <b>κορβανάς</b> είναι άδειος και τρύπιος. - The national coffers are empty and leaky.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. kitty, pot</div><b>κορεσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. saturation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. satiation, satiety, repletion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the κορεσ- stem of κορεννύω + -μός.</div><b>κοριός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοριοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bug, bedbug</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) insect</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bug (electronic listening device)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόρις.</div><b>κορμί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κορμιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>body</div>
  <div class='ex'>Και σιδερένιο πλάσε και άξιο το <b>κορμί</b>. - And make the body worthy and ironlike.</div><b>κορμοράνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορμοράνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cormorant (Phalacrocorax carbo including subspecies)</div><b>κορμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trunk of tree</div>
  <div class='def'>2. torso</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trunk (main part of body) of animal</div>
  <div class='def'>4. main body of something</div>
  <div class='def'>5. dessert in a log shape</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κορμός.</div><b>κορν φλέικς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) corn flakes</div><b>κορν-μπίφ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) corned beef</div><b>κορνάρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κορναρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beeping, honking, tooting (the act of blowing a vehicle&#x27;s horn)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με ενοχλεί να ζω δίπλα στην εθνική οδό, που έχει πάντα <b>κορναρίσματα</b>. - It bothers me living next to the highway, where there&#x27;s always beeping.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κορνάρω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>κορνάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to beep, honk, toot, blow (use the horn)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι διάολο <b>κορνάρει</b> αυτός από πίσω; - What the hell is that guy behind beeping for?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian cornare, equivalent to κόρνα + -άρω.</div><b>κορνίζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κορνίζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. picture frame, frame</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cornice</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pelmet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan cornise.</div><b>κορνφλάουρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cornflour (UK), cornstarch (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English cornflour.</div><b>κορνφλέικς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) corn flakes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of English corn flakes</div><b>κορομηλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κορομηλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cherry plum tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κορόμηλο + -ιά.</div><b>κοροναϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοροναϊός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κορωνοϊός">κορωνοϊός</a></div><b>κορονοϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορονοϊός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κορωνοϊός">κορωνοϊός</a></div><b>κοροϊδευτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κοροϊδευτικός">κοροϊδευτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mockingly, derisively</div><b>κοροϊδευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοροϊδευτική, neuter κοροϊδευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mocking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοροϊδεύ(ω)) + -τικός.</div><b>κοροϊδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to mock, ridicule, tease, make fun of</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν της άρεσε να πηγαίνει σχολείο επειδή την <b>κοροϊδεύανε</b>. - She didn&#x27;t like going to school because they teased her.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dupe, fool, deceive</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορεί αυτόν να τον <b>κορόιδεψες</b>, αλλά εγώ είμαι πολύ πιο έξυπνος. - You may have fooled him but I am a lot smarter.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κορόιδο + -εύω.</div><b>κορσές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορσέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. corset, girdle (woman&#x27;s garment)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (obsolete) stays (woman&#x27;s garment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French corset (“corset”).</div><b>κορτάκιας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορτάκηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a man who likes to flirt and make amorous advances on many women; a wolf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόρτε + -άκιας, from Italian corte (“courtyard”).</div><b>κορυνοβακτηρίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κορυνοβακτηρίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corynebacterium (Corynebacterium)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>κορυνοβακτηρίδιο</b> της διφθερίτιδας (Corynebacterium diphtheriae) είναι ένα μικρό Gram-θετικό βακτήριο. - Klebs-Löffler bacillus (Corynebacterium diphtheriae) is a small Gram-positive bacterium.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Corynebacterium, as κορύνη + βακτηρίδιο.</div><b>κορυφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. peak, summit, top, crest</div>
  <div class='def'>2. apex, vertex</div>
  <div class='def'>3. maxima</div>
  <div class='def'>4. ridge</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) top person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κορυφή.</div><b>κορυφαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κορυφαία, neuter κορυφαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>top (of the highest quality or rank)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κορυφαῖος. By surface analysis, κορυφή + -αίος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine κορυφαία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) corypheus (the conductor or leader of the dramatic chorus in Ancient Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κορυφαῖος. By surface analysis, κορυφή + -αίος.</div><b>κορυφώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>make or cause to peak, to reach a peak, to reach a high point</div><b>κορωναϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορωναϊός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κορωνοϊός">κορωνοϊός</a></div><b>κορωνοϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κορωνοϊοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coronavirus</div><b>κορόιδευ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of κορόιδεψε, second-person singular simple past active perfective imperative of <a href="bword://κοροϊδεύω">κοροϊδεύω</a></div><b>κορόιδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κορόιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dupe, sucker</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fall guy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an unattested earlier *κουρόγιδο, itself from κουρεύω + γίδι, probably in reference to Byzantine soldiers who were shaved with a goat&#x27;s shears.
An alternative theory takes *κουρόγιδο as a borrowing from Latin corrideo (“to laugh together or aloud”).</div><b>κορόμηλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κορόμηλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cherry plum (fruit)</div><b>κορόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κορόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κορώνα">κορώνα</a></div><b>κορόνα ή γράμματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κορώνα">κορώνα</a> ή γράμματα</div><b>κορώνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κορώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crown</div>
  <div class='def'>2. krona (currency of Sweden)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. krone (currency of Denmark)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Latin corōna, from Ancient Greek κορώνη. Doublet of κουρούνα.</div><b>κορώνα ή γράμματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) heads or tails</div><b>κορώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to burn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to excite, enflame</div><b>κος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Mr, Mr.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Abbreviation of κύριος m</div><b>κοσμάκης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(collective, singular only) (pejorative) poor, simple people</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι φταίει ο κακομοίρης ο <b>κοσμάκης</b>; Φταίνε οι πολιτικοί. - Is it the fault of the poor people? It is the politicians&#x27; fault.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κόσμ(ος), “people”) + -άκης.</div><b>κοσμηματοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοσμηματοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jeweller&#x27;s, jewellery shop (UK), jeweler&#x27;s, jewelry store (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοσμηματ- (kosmimat-) + -ο- (-o-) + -πωλείο.</div><b>κοσμηματοπώλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοσμηματοπώλες, feminine κοσμηματοπώλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jewelryseller, jeweler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόσμημα + -πώλης.</div><b>κοσμητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοσμητική, neuter κοσμητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of an adjective) That has the characteristics of a modifier; modifying</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (ironic) insulting, derogatory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κοσμητικός, derived from κοσμητής, derived from κοσμέω, derived from κόσμος.</div><b>κοσμητολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κοσμητολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beautician, cosmetologist</div><b>κοσμιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοσμιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decency; propriety; seemliness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κοσμιότης, equivalent to κόσμιος + -ότητα.</div><b>κοσμοδρόμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοσμοδρόμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cosmodrome (space launch site)</div><b>κοσμοθεωρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοσμοθεωρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>worldview, world view</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόσμος + θεωρία a calque of German Weltanschauung.</div><b>κοσμοκράτωρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοσμοκράτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cosmocrat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κοσμοκράτωρ</div><b>κοσμολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cosmology</div><b>κοσμοναύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοσμοναύτες, feminine κοσμοναύτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cosmonaut, astronaut</div><b>κοσμοπολίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοσμοπολίτες, feminine κοσμοπολίτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a cosmopolite; one who has traveled to many countries or is familiar with many cultures</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) a cosmopolitan (literally “world citizen”) who rejects loyalty to any particular nation; an internationalist or antinationalist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κοσμοπολίτης.</div><b>κοσμοσυρροή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>large crowd, throng</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κοσμο- (kosmo-) + συρροή, a loose calque of French affluence.</div><b>κοστίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cost (involve expenditure)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to cost, come as a blow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κόστος.</div><b>κοσταρικανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοσταρικανή, neuter κοσταρικανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Costa Rican</div><b>κοστούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοστούμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suit, costume</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian costume.</div><b>κοτέτσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοτέτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. henhouse, chicken coop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory) hovel (a small and meager house or cottage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Slavic котац (kɔ̌tat͡s: pigsty) + -ι (-i), from Proto-Slavic *kotьcь (“nest, warehouse”).</div><b>κοτομπουκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοτομπουκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chicken nugget (piece of chicken breast covered with breadcrumbs or batter)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κοτο- (koto-, “of chicken”) + μπουκιά.</div><b>κοτσάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοτσάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stalk, peduncle, stem (of leaf, flower, etc)</div><b>κοτσάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (familiar, idiomatic, transitive) to impose, put, add something which is regarded excessive or unfair</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to hitch (connect a trailer to another vehicle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian cozzare.</div><b>κοτσαδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοτσαδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tow bar, tow hitch (ball or hook attached to rear of vehicle for towing another vehicle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κοτσάρω + -αδόρος</div><b>κοτσυφίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοτσυφίνες, masculine κότσυφας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hen blackbird, female blackbird, Turdus merula</div><b>κοτσύφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοτσύφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blackbird</div><b>κοτυληδόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοτυληδόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cotyledon</div><b>κοτόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοτόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chicken pie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κότα + -ό- + πίτα.</div><b>κοτόπουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοτόπουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chicken, pullet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chicken meat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κότ(α), “hen”) + -όπουλο; see -πουλος (-poulos) for the latter component.</div><b>κουάρκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) quark (subatomic particle)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα <b>κουάρκ</b> θεωρούνται σήμερα βασικοί τύποι των στοιχειωδών σωματιδίων. - Quarks are now considered to be one of the main types of elementary particles</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English quark.</div><b>κουαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cyprus dialectal form of <a href="bword://κουβαλώ">κουβαλώ</a>: carry, transfer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουβαλώ, with silencing of intervocalic [v].</div><b>κουαφέζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κουαφέζα">κουαφέζα</a></div><b>κουαφέζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουαφέζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) hairdresser</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French coiffeuse.</div><b>κουβάλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουβαλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carrying</div><b>κουβάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουβάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ball of yarn, skein ball</div>
  <div class='def'>2. creased clothes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουβάρι(ν)), from Koine Greek κουβάριον, diminutive of κουβαρίς</div><b>κουβάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bucket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουβάς, from Ottoman Turkish قوغه (Turkish kova) + -ς (-s)</div><b>κουβέντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουβέντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chat, conversation, talk</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχαμε μια σύντομη <b>κουβέντα</b>. - We had a short chat.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. word, phrase</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κομβέντον/κομβέντος, from Latin conventus.</div><b>κουβέρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουβέρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blanket</div>
  <div class='def'>2. deck</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pretext</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan coverta, from Latin cooperta. Compare standard Italian coperta, French couvert(ur)e.</div><b>κουβαλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal) to carry</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κουβάλησα</b> τρεις βαριές σακούλες με ψώνια. - I carried three heavy shopping bags.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to bring along uninvited or irritating people</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας έκανε επίσκεψη, μας <b>κουβάλησε</b> και την πεθερά της. - She payed us a visit; she brought along her mother-in-law too.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουβαλ(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουβαλῶ, a metaplasm of Hellenistic Koine Greek κοβαλεύω.</div><b>κουβαλημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κουβαλημένη, neuter κουβαλημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carried (informal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κουβαλιέμαι, passive voice of κουβαλάω, κουβαλώ (“carry”).</div><b>κουβαλιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be carried</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>κουβαλιέται</b>. Είναι πολύ βαρύ. - It cannot be carried. It is too heavy.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to visit where to be not wanted, to gatecrash</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κουβαλιέται</b> στο σπίτι μας κάθε Κυριακή, μαζί με τα παιδιά και την πεθερά της. - She drags herself to our house every Sunday, with her kids and her mother-in-law.</div><b>κουβαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less common variant of <a href="bword://κουβαλάω">κουβαλάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουβαλῶ, a metaplasm of Hellenistic Koine Greek κοβαλεύω and see κουβαλάω</div><b>κουβανέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κουβανέζικη, neuter κουβανέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://κουβανικός">κουβανικός</a></div><b>κουβανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κουβανική, neuter κουβανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cuban</div><b>κουβεντιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to chat (to be engaged in informal conversation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to talk about (to discuss)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (familiar, transitive) to talk about, to gossip about (to spread information about third parties in conversation, often negatively)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουβέντ(α)) + -ιάζω.</div><b>κουβεντούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουβεντούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chat (informal conversation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουβεντ (kouvent, “conversation”) + -ούλα</div><b>κουδουνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ring, jingle, jangle</div><b>κουδουνίστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rattle (baby&#x27;s toy designed to make sound)</div><b>κουδούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουδούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. doorbell, buzzer</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια παχουλή γυναίκα πενήντα και βάλε άνοιξε την πόρτα αμέσως μετά τον ήχο του <b>κουδουνιού</b>. Με κοίταξε με μια αγενή έκφραση απορίας. Της πούλησα ένα παραμύθι διαφορετικό από εκείνο που είχα πασάρει μερικά λεπτά πριν στην ένοικο του ημιυπόγειου διαμερίσματος. - A chubby woman, fifty or more, opened the door immediately after the sound of the doorbell. She regarded me with a rude questioning expression. I gave her some tall tale, different from the one I had given the tenant of the basement apartment a few minutes beforehand.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουδούνι(ν)), inherited through Koine Greek from Ancient Greek κωδώνιον, a diminutive of κώδων.</div><b>κουζίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουζίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kitchen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cooker</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κουζίνα</b> ρυζιού - rice cooker</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cuisine, cookery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan cusina, from Late Latin cocīna, from earlier coquīna.</div><b>κουκί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουκιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>broad bean (UK), fava bean (US)</div><b>κουκαμπούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουκαμπούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(also) kookaburra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English kookaburra.</div><b>κουκλοθέατρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>puppet theatre, puppet theater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κούκλα + θέατρο.</div><b>κουκλόσπιτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουκλόσπιτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dollhouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κούκλ(α), “doll”) + -ό- + σπίτ(ι), “house”) + -ο (-o).</div><b>κουκουβάγια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουκουβάγιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>owl</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably of onomatopoeic origin; similar words seem to be found in other regional languages: compare Bulgarian кукумявка (kukumjavka), Albanian kukuvajkë, Italian coccoveggia, Ladino kukuvaya, Romanian cucuvea.</div><b>κουκουλοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κουκουλοφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A person (often a criminal) who wears a mask or has a hood covering their face in order to conceal their identity.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άγνωστοι <b>κουκουλοφόροι</b> λήστεψαν την τράπεζα. - Unknown hooded people robbed the bank.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουκούλα + -φόρος</div><b>κουκουλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hood, cover up, muffle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to conceal, camouflage</div><b>κουκουρίκου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cock-a-doodle-doo</div><b>κουκούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουκούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hood, cowl</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φορούσε μια <b>κουκούλα</b>, ώστε να μη δεις το πρόσωπό της. - She was wearing a hood so you could not see her face.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin cuculla. Doublet of κούκλα.</div><b>κουκούτσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουκούτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stone, pit (of plum, olive, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pip (of orange, lemon, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. kernel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουκούτσιν, borrowed from Italian cucuzza (“pumpkin”), with semantic shift &quot;pumpkin&quot; &gt; &quot;seeds of the pumpkin&quot; &gt; &quot;seeds of other fruit (stone, pit, pip, kernel)&quot;.</div><b>κουλάκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουλάκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kulak (prosperous peasant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Russian from кула́к.</div><b>κουλάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal, intransitive) to cool it, become calm, cool down</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal, transitive) to make calm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English cool.</div><b>κουλλούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουλλούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κουλούρα">κουλούρα</a></div><b>κουλουριάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to curl up</div>
  <div class='ex'>H γάτα <b>κουλουριάστηκε</b> κοντά στη φωτιά. - The cat curled up near the fire.</div><b>κουλουριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to coil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to curl up</div><b>κουλούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουλούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. general term for ring like objects</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coil (of rope, wire, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. life jacket</div>
  <div class='def'>4. bread roll in shape of a ring, bagel like roll</div>
  <div class='def'>5. zero mark at school</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively) marriage, marriage wreath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κολλύρα.</div><b>κουλούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουλούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>koulouri, simit (ring shaped bread roll covered with sesame seeds)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κολλύριον, diminutive of Ancient Greek κολλύρα (roll or loaf of bread ). Cognate with Sicilian cuḍḍura. Unrelated to Northern Kurdish kilor (“koulouri, simit”).</div><b>κουλροφοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουλροφοβίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coulrophobia (fear of clowns)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English coulrophobia.</div><b>κουλτούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουλτούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>culture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin cultūra.</div><b>κουλόμπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) coulomb (units of electricity)</div><b>κουλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>handless, having one or no hands at all</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουλός, from Ancient Greek κυλλός”).</div><b>κουμ κουάτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κουμκουάτ">κουμκουάτ</a></div><b>κουμ-κουάτ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κουμκουάτ">κουμκουάτ</a></div><b>κουμάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουμάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal, slang) gambling, any instance of a game of chance involving a wager</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small, traditionally ceramic pitcher for serving water</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قمار (qumar, “gambling”) (compare Turkish kumar), from Arabic قِمَار.</div><b>κουμαριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουμαριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strawberry tree, Arbutus unedo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουμαρέα, from Byzantine Greek κούμαρον, from Koine Greek κόμαρον via [o&gt;u], from Ancient Greek κόμαρος (whence also Sicilian cacumbaru) transformed to neutral gender under the semantic influence of other fruit names. See κόμαρος for further etymology, perhaps ultimately Pre-Greek.</div><b>κουμκουάτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kumquat</div><b>κουμπάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουμπάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://κουμπί">κουμπί</a> n</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουμπί + -άκι.</div><b>κουμπάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουμπάροι, feminine κουμπάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (broadly) A close friend, especially a close family friend.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. A folksy term of address for an unknown man of approximately the same age as the speaker.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς σε λένε <b>κουμπάρε</b>; - What&#x27;s your name mate?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. One&#x27;s best man, koumbaros: the primary attendant (to the couple) in a wedding ceremony.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. The godfather of one&#x27;s child (customarily the same person as the koumbaros).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan compare (“godfather”), conpare, from Latin compater, compatrem, from cum (“with”) + pater (“father”).</div><b>κουμπί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουμπιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. button (fastener for clothing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. button (instrument control)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουμπίον, from Ancient Greek κομβίον with o &gt; u raising in the first syllable due to labial influence of the succeeding consonant, from κόμβος + -ιον (-ion, “diminutive suffix”).</div><b>κουμπότρυπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουμπότρυπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>buttonhole</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουμπ(ί), “button”) + -ό- + τρύπα</div><b>κουμπώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to buckle (belt, shoe, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to button, button-up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουμπί + -ώνω</div><b>κουνάβι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ferret</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) one of the mustelids</div><b>κουνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κουνώ">κουνώ</a></div><b>κουνέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female rabbit, doe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, pejorative) woman who had many births</div><b>κουνέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουνέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rabbit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian coniglio (dialectal cunelo, plural cuneli). Hellenistic Koine noun κύνικλος m,). Also see Latin cunīculus. Compare English cony.</div><b>κουνιάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουνιάδες, masculine κουνιάδος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sister-in-law, specifically spouse&#x27;s sister</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan cognada, cugnada, from Latin cognāta.</div><b>κουνιάδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουνιάδοι, feminine κουνιάδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brother-in-law, specifically spouse&#x27;s brother</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η νύφη έχει κουνιάδο και ο αδερφός του συζύγου της νύφη.<b></b> - The bride has a brother-in-law and her hushand&#x27;s brother has a sister-in-law.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan cognado, cugnado, from Latin cognātus.</div><b>κουνιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to get a move on, stir oneself</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to swing, wobble</div><b>κουνουπίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουνουπίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cauliflower</div>
  <div class='ex'>Greek taxonomic binominal: Βρασική η λαχανώδης ποικ. η Βοτρύτης</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουνουπίδιν (12th century), probably from a noun *κανωπίδιον, diminutive of Hellenistic κάνωπον. See city of Κάνωπος.
Also see the dialectal synonym καρναπίδι / καρναμπίκι / καρναμπίτι, ultimately from κράμβη with influence from (Ottoman) Turkish, as well as Albanian kunipidhë, from Ottoman Turkish قرنابیت (karnabit), قنبیط (kunnebit), from Arabic قَرْنَبِيط, قُنَّبِيط, itself from Ancient Greek κραμβίδιον, a diminutive of κράμβη. Doublet of καρναμπίτι.</div><b>κουνουπιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουνουπιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mosquito net</div><b>κουνούπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουνούπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mosquito</div>
  <div class='ex'>τσίμπημα <b>κουνουπιού</b> - mosquito bite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουνούπιον, from Koine Greek κωνώπιον, from Ancient Greek κώνωψ, possibly ultimately from Egyptian ḫnws (“a kind of stinging insect”).</div><b>κουντεπιέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) instep</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French cou-de-pied.</div><b>κουντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot dialect form of <a href="bword://σκουντώ">σκουντώ</a> (push)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Clipping of σκουντώ.</div><b>κουνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shake, swing, nod</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουνώ, from Ancient Greek κινῶ, κινέω, with sound change -ι- (-i-) &gt; -ου- (-ou-) via influence of the initial κ- (k-) in conjunction with medial -ν- (-n-).</div><b>κουπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) hairstyle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French coupe.</div><b>κουπί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουπιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oar, paddle, scull</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κώπη.</div><b>κουρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>haircutting (work done by barber or shearer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κουρά.</div><b>κουράγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) courage, valour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan coragio. Compare Italian coraggio.</div><b>κουράδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουράδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of κουράδα</div>
  <div class='def'>2. herd</div><b>κουράδω</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (masculine κουράδας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, offensive) turd, prick, jerk, asshole (a bad-natured or contemptible woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην ασχολείσαι μ’ αυτήν την <b>κουράδω</b>. - Don&#x27;t bother with that turd.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουράδα + -ω (-o)</div><b>κουράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be tired, be worn out</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κουράζομαι</b> να κάνω την ίδια δουλειά τόσα χρόνια. - I am tired of doing the same job all these years.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to wear out yourself</div><b>κουράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to tire, wear out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to annoy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουράζω, from Ancient Greek κουρ(ά) f, “cropping of the hair”) + -άζω.</div><b>κουρέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουρείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barber, men&#x27;s hairdresser</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουρεύω + -έας</div><b>κουρέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουρέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rag, piece of cloth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) person wrecked by illness or suffering</div><b>κουραδομηχανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουραδομηχανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) lazybones, lazy ass, loafer, lazy shit (lazy person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σήκω πάνω και κάνε κάτι, μωρή <b>κουραδομηχανή</b>! - Get up and do something will you, you lazy shit!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουράδα + μηχανή.</div><b>κουραδόμαγκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουραδόμαγκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) fake tough guy (man who pretends to be tough but isn&#x27;t)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύο <b>κουραδόμαγκες</b> προσπάθησαν να μας δείρουν αλλά στο τέλος νικήσαμε. - Two fake tough guys tried to beat us up but in the end, we beat them.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουράδα + μάγκας.</div><b>κουραμπιές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουραμπιέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>qurabiya, an almond flavoured, crisp bun similar to a macaroon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قرابیه (kurabiye), غرابیه (gurabiye).</div><b>κουρασάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουρασάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high quality lime mortar which also includes brick dust and natural pozzolana</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish خراسانی, from Persian خراسان”). Doublet of Χορασάν.</div><b>κουρασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κουρασμένη, neuter κουρασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tired, worn out</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είμαι <b>κουρασμένος</b>, κατάκοπος από την πολλή δουλειά. - I am tired, totally worn out from too much work.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) having run out of ideas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κουράζομαι, passive voice of κουράζω.</div><b>κουραστικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κουραστικός">κουραστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tiringly</div><b>κουραστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κουραστική, neuter κουραστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tiring</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trying</div>
  <div class='def'>3. heavy going</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Stem κουράσ-”) + -τικός</div><b>κουραφέξαλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) hogwash, bullshit (nonsense)</div><b>κουρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barbershop, barber&#x27;s shop, hairdresser&#x27;s</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek κουρεῖον.</div><b>κουρελιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to rip apart, tear to pieces</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to humble</div><b>κουρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cut hair</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουρεύω, from the mediopassive Hellenistic Koine Greek κουρεύομαι from κουρά.</div><b>κουρκουμάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουρκουμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turmeric</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Spanish cúrcuma or Catalan cúrcuma, from Arabic كُرْكُم (kurkum). See كُرْكُم (kurkum) for more cognates. Doublet of κρόκος.</div><b>κουρκουρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://γουργουρίζω">γουργουρίζω</a></div><b>κουρκούμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουρκούμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κουρκουμάς">κουρκουμάς</a></div><b>κουρκούτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουρκούτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>batter (mixture of flour with water and/or milk)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قورقوت. Ultimately from Old Armenian կորկոտ (korkot), կորկուտ (korkut).</div><b>κουρούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουρούνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crow (bird of the genus Corvus); hooded crow (Corvus cornix)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crow (bird of the genus Corvus); carrion crow (Corvus corone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κορώνη. Doublet of κορώνα.</div><b>κουρσάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουρσάροι, feminine πειρατίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corsair, pirate, sea robber</div><b>κουρσεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pillage, to plunder</div><b>κουρτίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουρτίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curtain (window covering)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian cortina.</div><b>κουσβαράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) coriander</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately akin to Arabic كُزْبَرَة (kuzbara), compare Ottoman Turkish كسبره.</div><b>κουσούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shortcoming (usually for a person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قصور (kusur), from Arabic قُصُور.</div><b>κουστουμιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουστουμιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) suit, costume</div><b>κουστούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουστούμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://κοστούμι">κοστούμι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Egyptiot) swimsuit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French costume.</div><b>κουτά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κουτός">κουτός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κουτός">κουτός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κουτός">κουτός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κούτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>box</div><b>κουτάβι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουτάβια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>puppy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουτάβιον, of uncertain further origin. Perhaps from an element *kut- of Slavic origin) + a suffix -άβιον. Likely not related to κόττᾰβος”).</div><b>κουτάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουτάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, figuratively) gaining illegal benefit when in a position of power</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ladle, large spoon, scoop (wood or metal)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. scapula</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουτάλι + -α (-a, “augmentative suffix”).</div><b>κουτάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουτάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spoon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) spoon (metal lure for fishing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουτάλιν, from Koine Greek κουτάλιον and κωτάλιον, diminutives of Koine Greek κώταλις, of unclear origin. Cognate with Pontic Greek κουτάλιν, κουτάλι, κουτάλ’.
Compare Ancient Greek κοτύλη; Old Georgian კუტალი, კუტალაჲ, Chechen кӏудал (kʼudal), Ingush кӏудал (kʼudal), Bats კუტალ; Armenian գդալ (gdal), կոտալ (kotal); Turkish gedal /gıdal /gidal /gödel /güdel /güdül /kıtal /kotalı /kutal /kutalı; Serbo-Croatian kȕtao, Bulgarian кутел (kutel).</div><b>κουτί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουτιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. packet, carton</div>
  <div class='def'>2. can, canister</div>
  <div class='def'>3. box, caddy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κυτίον, from Ancient Greek κυτίδιον, diminutive of κύτος.</div><b>κουταλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουταλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small spoon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially) teaspoon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουτάλι + -άκι</div><b>κουταλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουταλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spoonful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουτάλι + -ιά.</div><b>κουταμάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουταμάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stupidity, idiocy (the quality of being stupid)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>κουταμάρα</b> της δεν υποφέρεται. - Her stupidity is insufferable.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nonsense, stupidity, idiocy, rubbish (stupid behaviour or talk)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι να γελάς με αυτές τις <b>κουταμάρες</b> που λέει. - You can&#x27;t help but laugh at the nonsense he says.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουτός + -μάρα.</div><b>κουτεντές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουτεντέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>idiot, a stupid or gullible person.</div><b>κουτορνίθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουτορνίθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. simpleton</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ninny, nincompoop, goose</div><b>κουτουλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to headbutt; to hit one&#x27;s head (on something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to meet (unexpectedly)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to be very tired and have one&#x27;s head nod off and bobble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably from κούτελο + -άω.</div><b>κουτουράδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stupidity, foolishness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουτουρού.
2 Possibilities exist:</div><b>κουτσή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κουτσός">κουτσός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κουτσός">κουτσός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κουτσός">κουτσός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουτσές, masculine κουτσός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lame/crippled woman/girl, cripple</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δώσε τη θέση σου σ’ αυτήν την <b>κουτσή</b>! - Give up your seat to that crippled woman!</div><b>κουτσαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to limp, hobble</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be lame</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in the perfective) to make lame</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουτσαίνω. By surface analysis, κουτσ(ός)) + -αίνω.</div><b>κουτσοβλαχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Aromanian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Kουτσόβλαχος + -ικός, from Ottoman Turkish كوچوك افلاق, a name used to distinguish it from the greater Wallachia on the other side of the Danube, i.e. Romania. Influenced and reanalyzed with the similar sounding κουτσός, hence literally, “lame Vlach”.</div><b>κουτσομπολεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to gossip, to tittle-tattle, (to talk about third parties, often negatively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλο το χωριό <b>κουτσομπολεύει</b> τη Μαρία που κλέφτηκε με το Γιάννη. - The whole village is gossipping about Maria who eloped with John.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουτσο- (koutso-)) + an element μπολ- (bol-) + -εύω.
The element μπολ- (bol-) is most likely from the verb μπολιάζω, i.e. &quot;to graft lame (speech)&quot; &gt; &quot;to gossip&quot;. Alternatively, the element is from an earlier μπολεύω, from an unattested Hellenistic Koine Greek *ἐμπολεύω, from ἐν- (en-) + a contamination-driven blend of πολεύω and ἐμπολάω.</div><b>κουτσομπολιό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουτσομπολιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gossip, tittle-tattle, idle talk (the act of talking about third parties, often negatively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>κουτσομπολιό</b> είναι γι’ αυτούς που δεν έχουν άλλο να κάνουν. - Gossip is for those who have nothing else to do.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gossip, tale, rumour, story (stories and comments made while gossiping, often telling lies or stretching the truth)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να μην πιστεύεις τα <b>κουτσομπολιά</b>. - Don&#x27;t believe the rumours.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουτσομπολεύω + -ιό.</div><b>κουτσομπόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουτσομπόλες, masculine κουτσομπόλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gossip, gossiper (one who talks about third parties, often negatively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Μαριώ είναι η μεγαλύτερη <b>κουτσομπόλα</b> στο χωριό μας. - Maria is the biggest gossip in our village.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουτσομπόλης + -α (-a).</div><b>κουτσομπόλευ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of κουτσομπόλεψε, second-person singular simple past active perfective imperative of <a href="bword://κουτσομπολεύω">κουτσομπολεύω</a></div><b>κουτσομπόλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουτσομπόληδες, feminine κουτσομπόλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gossip, gossiper (one who talks about third parties, often negatively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην ακούς τις ιστορίες του Αλέκου, είναι <b>κουτσομπόλης</b>. - Don&#x27;t listen to Alekos&#x27;s stories, he&#x27;s a gossiper.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουτσομπολεύω + -ης (-is).</div><b>κουτσουλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of birds) to crap on, to poop on, to shit on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουτσουλ(ιά)) + -άω.</div><b>κουτσουλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of birds) to crap on, to poop on, to shit on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουτσουλ(ιά)) + -ίζω.</div><b>κουτσουλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουτσουλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) birdshit, bird crap (bird droppings)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε πρωί το αμάξι μου είναι γεμάτο <b>κουτσουλιές</b>. - Every morning, my car is full of bird crap.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) any small item or person</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι μια <b>κουτσουλιά</b> άνθρωπος και μας το παίζει μάγκας. - He&#x27;s a little chicken shit man and he&#x27;s playing it tough.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοττοτσιλιά, from κόττος + τσίρλα.</div><b>κουτσουπιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Judas tree (Cercis siliquastrum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Of unclear origin. One theory derives the word from κουτσούπι + -ιά (as the Judas tree&#x27;s flowers tend to blossom very close to its trunk), the former of apparently disputed origin:</div><b>κουτσός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κουτσή, neuter κουτσό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of people and animals) lame, crippled (unable to walk properly because of a problem with the feet or legs)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας μου είναι <b>κουτσός</b> λόγω ασθένειας. - My father is lame because of illness.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of objects with legs) broken</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην κάθεσαι σ’ αυτήν την <b>κουτσή</b> καρέκλα. Θα πέσεις. - Don&#x27;t sit in that broken chair. You&#x27;ll fall.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) crooked</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το κείμενο αυτό έχει <b>κουτσές</b> αράδες. Διόρθωσέ το! - This article has crooked lines. Fix it!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουτσός, of uncertain etymology. Likely from Middle Bulgarian коуцъ, from Proto-Slavic *kucъ (“crippled, tailless”), of expressive origin. Another possibility is derivation from late Koine Greek κοξός, borrowed from Late Latin coxus (“lame, crippled”), from Latin coxa (“hip, thigh”), from Proto-Indo-European *koḱs-.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουτσοί, feminine κουτσή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lame/crippled man/boy, cripple</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δώσε τη θέση σου σ’ αυτόν τον <b>κουτσό</b>! - Give up your seat to that crippled man!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουτσός, of uncertain etymology. Likely from Middle Bulgarian коуцъ, from Proto-Slavic *kucъ (“crippled, tailless”), of expressive origin. Another possibility is derivation from late Koine Greek κοξός, borrowed from Late Latin coxus (“lame, crippled”), from Latin coxa (“hip, thigh”), from Proto-Indo-European *koḱs-.</div><b>κουτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κουτή, neuter κουτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stupid, dumb, idiotic, silly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κοττόμυαλος, which derives from Ancient Greek κόττος. Doublet of κότα f.</div><b>κουτόφραγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κουτόφραγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, humorous) stupid European (a gullible or naive person from Western Europe who is seen as easily being tricked or made a fool of by a Greek)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε χρόνια μας έρχονται στο ξενοδοχείο κάτι <b>κουτόφραγκοι</b> και πάντα τους χρεώνουμε παραπάνω από τους Έλληνες. - Every year some stupid Europeans come to our hotel and we always charge them more than the Greeks.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουτός + Φράγκος.</div><b>κουφάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουφάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (referring to animals) corpse, carcass, carrion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hull</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κουφάριν, from Ancient Greek κοῦφος.</div><b>κουφαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to deafen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make hollow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κουφαίνω, from Koine Greek κωφαίνω, with vowel alteration per κωφός → κουφός. By surface analysis, κουφ(ός)) + -αίνω</div><b>κουφιοκέφαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κουφιοκέφαλη, neuter κουφιοκέφαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>empty-headed</div><b>κουφοξυλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κουφοξυλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elder, elderberry (tree)</div><b>κουφός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κουφή, neuter κουφό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deaf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κωφός, which, if related to κηφήν, is possibly from Proto-Indo-European *k/gʰ(e/o)h₂bʰ-, but without any other IE cognates; both could be from Pre-Greek.</div><b>κοφτά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κοφτός">κοφτός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abruptly, bluntly, brusquely, curtly, sharply (of speech)</div><b>κοφτερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sharp, cutting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κοπτερός, from κόπτω.</div><b>κοφτό μακαρονάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοφτά μακαρονάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>macaroni</div><b>κοφτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κοφτή, neuter κοφτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cut up)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοφτό</b> μακαρονάκι - macaroni</div>
  <div class='def'>2. level</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοφτό</b> κουταλάκι - level spoonful</div>
  <div class='def'>3. abrupt, swift</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κοφτή</b> κίνηση - abrupt movement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κόβω + -τος (-tos).</div><b>κοχενίλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κοχενίλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cochineal (type of scale insect)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι κάμπιες των λυκαινιδών ζουν σε συμβίωση με τα μυρμήγκια, ενώ οι κάμπιες μερικών νυκτιιδών είναι σαρκοφάγες και είναι ωφέλιμες γιατί παρασιτούν σε επιβλαβείς <b>κοχενίλες</b>. - The caterpillars of lycaenids live in symbiosis with ants, while the caterpillars of some noctuids are carnivorous and are beneficial because they parasitize harmful cochineals.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cochineal (dye and color thereof produced by the insect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Disputed. See the etymology of English cochineal.</div><b>κοχλίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κοχλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. screw (threaded fastener)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. worm gear</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cochlea</div>
  <div class='def'>4. Archimedes&#x27; screw</div>
  <div class='def'>5. snail</div>
  <div class='def'>6. helix</div>
  <div class='def'>7. volute (spiral on Ionic column)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοχλίας, from κόχλος, probably related to Ancient Greek κόγχη, κόγχος.</div><b>κοχύλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοχύλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shell, seashell (of a mollusc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κογχύλιον. Cognates include English cockle.</div><b>κοψίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κοψ (kops, “cut”) + -ίδι.</div><b>κούγκαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) puma, cougar, mountain lion</div><b>κούκκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κούκκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κούκος">κούκος</a></div><b>κούκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κούκλες, masculine κούκλος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. doll, puppet, marionette (toy in the form of a human, usually of a baby or girl)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το κοριτσάκι παίζει με τις <b>κούκλες</b>. - The little girl plays with the dolls.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mannequin, dummy (life-size model of the human body, used for the fitting or displaying of clothes)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίταγε τις <b>κούκλες</b> στην βιτρίνα. - She was looking at the mannequins in the display.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) doll, dish, peach, beauty, babe, bombshell (very attractive woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα κάτι <b>κούκλες</b> στην παραλία, θεέ μου! - Look at those babes on the beach, my god!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, endearing) darling, sweetheart, babycakes (term of affection for young woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να ’σαι καλά, <b>κούκλα</b> μου! - Take care of yourself, sweetheart!</div>
  <div class='def'>5. skein (length of cotton yarn put up together)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χρειάζεσαι πέντε <b>κούκλες</b> για να φτιάξεις πουλόβερ. - You need five skeins to make a jumper.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. corncob</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin cuculla (“hood”). Doublet of κουκούλα.</div><b>κούκλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κούκλοι, feminine κούκλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally, rare) male doll</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) stud, hunk, Adonis (very handsome man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα τι <b>κούκλος</b> είναι, παναγιά μου! - Look what a hunk he is, my god!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin cucullus (“hood”).</div><b>κούκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κούκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cuckoo (Cuculus canorus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare Ancient Greek κόκκῡξ. Cognate with Sicilian cuccu.</div><b>κούμαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κούμαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cane apple, the fruit of the strawberry tree (Arbutus unedo)</div>
  <div class='ex'>(dialectal, Euboea)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an alcoholic spirit made from the distillation of strawberry tree fruit</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κούμαρο</b> ο κόσμος δυνατό θα πίνει απ’ τα παγούρια - the people will be drinking strong strawberry tree spirit from the flasks</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κούμαρον, from Koine Greek κόμαρον via [o&gt;u], from Ancient Greek κόμαρος (whence also Sicilian cacumbaru) transformed to neutral gender under the semantic influence of other fruit names. See κόμαρος for further etymology, perhaps ultimately Pre-Greek.</div><b>κούνελος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κούνελοι, feminine κουνέλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rabbit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουνέλ(ι)) + -ος (-os, augmentative suffix).</div><b>κούνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κούνιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. swing (usually children&#x27;s playground apparatus)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cradle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from Koine Greek κοῦνα singular of κοῦναι from Latin cunae (“cradle”).</div><b>κούπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κούπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mug, large cup (drinking vessel)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trophy cup</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hearts (suit of playing cards)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. liquid measure used in cookery</div>
  <div class='def'>5. cup (part of a brassiere)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κοῦπα, from Ancient Greek κοῦπα, from Late Latin cuppa, from Latin cūpa.</div><b>κούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) treatment (of the sick)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κούρα, from Italian cura.</div><b>κούραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tiredness</div><b>κούρεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουρέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. haircut</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shearing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mowing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουρεύω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>κούριερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) courier (company that delivers goods or messages usually by collecting them from sender)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) courier (person working in collection or delivery on the above company)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of English courier</div><b>κούρνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κούρνιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perch, roost (for bird)</div><b>κούρσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κούρσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. road race</div>
  <div class='def'>2. race; competition</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (dated) a small passenger car</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French course + -α (-a).</div><b>κούτελο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (familiar) forehead</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) honour, esteem</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω καθαρό <b>κούτελο</b> και το κεφάλι ψηλά. - I have a clear honour and I am an honest/proud person.
literally: I have a clean forehead and my head high.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κούτελο(ν)), from Ancient Greek κότυλος, from κοτύλη.</div><b>κούτσα-κούτσα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) with a limp, limpingly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γριά ήρθε <b>κούτσα-κούτσα</b> στην πόρτα. - The old woman came to the door limping.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) slowly, haltingly, bit by bit, little by little</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός ο άνθρωπος <b>κούτσα-κούτσα</b> κάνει την δουλειά του και είναι πολύ ενοχλητικό. - This person is doing his job bit by bit and it&#x27;s very annoying.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κουτσός.</div><b>κούφιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κούφια, neuter κούφιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hollow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rotten, decayed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κοῦφος.</div><b>κούφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frivolous, foolish, impractical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κοῦφος.</div><b>κούφωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κουφώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wall aperture (for a window or door); window aperture</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wall aperture (for a window or door); doorway</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) frame (for a window or door); window frame</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (broadly) frame (for a window or door); door frame</div>
  <div class='def'>5. recess (in a wall, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κούφωμα. By surface analysis, κουφώ(νω)) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>κπν.</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of κάποιον: κάποιον</div><b>κράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of birds, without passive forms) to caw, croak, crow (make shrill or harsh sound, especially as of a crow, raven or rook)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο κόρακας <b>κράζει</b> όλη νύχτα, ενοχλώντας αυτούς που προσπαθούν να κοιμηθούν. - The crow caws all night long, annoying those who are trying to sleep.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of humans, without passive forms) to roar, bellow, holler, yell, screech (shout out in a loud and/or annoying voice)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>κράζει</b> πάλι αυτή η χαζή δίπλα; - What&#x27;s that silly one next door hollering about again?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (without passive forms) to call over, cry out for</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με <b>έκραξε</b> από απέναντι νά ’ρθω. - He called me over from opposite.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (with passive forms and participle) to rip to shreds, shout down, boo (criticize severely)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλες οι εφημερίδες βγήκαν να τον <b>κράξουν</b> γι’ αυτό που είπε. - All the papers came out and ripped him to shreds over what he said.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κράζω.</div><b>κράκερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter or masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cracker (food item)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English cracker.</div><b>κράμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mixture</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alloy</div><b>κράμβη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κράμβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. turnip</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brassica, the cabbage family</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κράμβη. Doublet of κραμπί.</div><b>κράνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κράνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>helmet (for cycling, motorcycling, police, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρᾰ́νος.</div><b>κράση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. constitution or health of a person&#x27;s body.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. constitution, make up or analysis of a mixture or alloy.,</div>
  <div class='def'>3. crasis (contraction of a vowel at the end of a word with the start of the next word)</div><b>κράταιγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κράταιγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hawthorn, crataegus (Crataegus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek κράταιγος.</div><b>κράτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρατήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. booking (reservation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εάν χρειαστεί να τροποποιήσετε μία <b>κράτηση</b> … - if you need to change a booking …</div>
  <div class='def'>2. confinement, custody, imprisonment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κράτησις, from Ancient Greek κρατέω, ultimately from κράτος and Proto-Indo-European *kret- (“insight, strength”).</div><b>κράτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κράτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>state, sovereign country</div>
  <div class='ex'>αδέσμευτα <b>κράτη</b> - non-aligned states</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κράτος, from Proto-Indo-European *kret-.</div><b>κράτος πρόνοιας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>welfare state</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably a calque of French État-providence.</div><b>κρέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρέατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meat</div>
  <div class='ex'>πρόβειο <b>κρέας</b> - mutton</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρέας, from Proto-Hellenic *kréwas, from Proto-Indo-European *kréwh₂s.</div><b>κρέμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρέμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cream (the fatty dairy product from milk)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cream (cosmetic or medical product for the skin)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ενυδατική <b>κρέμα</b> - moisturising cream</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cream dessert</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian crema, from French crème.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular present active imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://κρεμάω">κρεμάω</a> and <a href="bword://κρεμώ">κρεμώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative form: κρέμαγε (krémage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular active perfective imperative of <a href="bword://κρεμάω">κρεμάω</a> and <a href="bword://κρεμώ">κρεμώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative form: κρέμασε (krémase)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>κρέμα γάλακτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρέμες γάλακτος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cream, dairy cream</div><b>κρέμες γάλακτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://κρέμα">κρέμα</a> γάλακτος</div><b>κρήνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of κρήνη</div><b>κρίκετ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cricket</div><b>κρίκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρίκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. link, ring (attached to or part of a chain)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. earring</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) link (connection between two people, subjects, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρίκος; ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *(s)ker- (“bend, turn”).</div><b>κρίμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Indicates a wrong or unjustified action or a misfortune:a misdeed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρίμα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Indicates pity or shame</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρίμα.</div><b>κρίνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lily</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρίνον.</div><b>κρίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to judge, assess, decide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κρίνω, from Proto-Hellenic *kríňňō, from Proto-Indo-European *kri-n-ye-, from *krey-.</div><b>κρίπερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) creeper (a villain from Minecraft)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of English creeper.</div><b>κρίση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. judgment, opinion, verdict</div>
  <div class='def'>2. judgement, discretion</div>
  <div class='def'>3. crisis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρίσις.</div><b>κρίσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κρίσιμη, neuter κρίσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>critical, crucial</div><b>κραγιόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) lipstick</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) crayon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French crayon (“pencil”).</div><b>κραγιόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κραγιόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rare spelling of <a href="bword://κραγιόν">κραγιόν</a></div><b>κραγμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine κραγμένη, neuter κραγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a gay man) flaming, extremely camp (exhibiting typical assumed homosexual behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι οι <b>κραγμένοι</b> γκέι της Αθήνας βγήκαν για το Πράιντ. - All the flamers of Athens came out for Pride.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κράζομαι, passive voice of κράζω (“to caw”).</div><b>κραδασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κραδασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vibration (act of vibrating)</div><b>κραιπάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κραιπάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>binge, bender</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κραιπᾰ́λη.</div><b>κρακεράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρακεράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small cracker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κράκερ + -άκι.</div><b>κραμβολάχανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κραμβολάχανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κραμπολάχανο">κραμπολάχανο</a></div><b>κραμπολάχανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κραμπολάχανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Savoy cabbage</div><b>κρανίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρανία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cranium, skull</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρανίον.</div><b>κρανιοθήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>headhunter (rare)</div><b>κρασί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρασιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κρασίον, from Ancient Greek κρᾶσις, literally because in ancient Greece wine was usually drunk mixed with water. Compare with οίνος m. Cognate with Mariupol Greek красы́ and Sicilian crascì.</div><b>κρασοπότηρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρασοπότηρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wine glass</div><b>κρατάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with passive voice) <a href="bword://κρατιέμαι">κρατιέμαι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with passive voice) <a href="bword://κρατιέμαι">κρατιέμαι</a>; to hold, hold back</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Κράτα</b> γερά, μην πέσεις! - Hold firmly, so that you will not fall down!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with passive voice) κρατούμαι</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (with passive voice) κρατούμαι; to imprison, retain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κρατώ, from Ancient Greek κρατῶ, from κρατέω.</div><b>κρατήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρατήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crater</div>
  <div class='def'>2. krater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρατήρ.</div><b>κραταιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κραταιή or κραταιά, neuter κραταιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mighty (very strong, possessing might)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κραταιός.</div><b>κρατητήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρατητήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cells area in police station</div><b>κρατιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to hold on</div><b>κρατικοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρατικοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nationalisation (UK), nationalization (US)</div><b>κρατικοποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be nationalised (UK), be nationalized (US)</div><b>κρατικοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to nationalise (UK), nationalize (US) (take into public ownership)</div><b>κρατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κρατική, neuter κρατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>state, government</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κράτ(ος)) + -ικός, a calque of French étatique and German staatlich.</div><b>κρατιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρατιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statist (a supporter of statism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κράτος + -ιστής, calque of French étatiste. The Byzantine Greek κρατιστής is unrelated to the modern sense.</div><b>κρατουμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρατουμένες, masculine κρατούμενος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κρατούμενη">κρατούμενη</a></div><b>κρατούμενη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρατούμενες, masculine κρατούμενος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prisoner, detainee</div><b>κρατούμενος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine κρατούμενη, neuter κρατούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://κρατάω">κρατάω</a> and <a href="bword://κρατώ">κρατώ</a>: held; detained</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρατούμενοι, feminine κρατούμενη or κρατουμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prisoner, detainee</div><b>κρατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κρατάω">κρατάω</a></div><b>κραυγάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shout loudly, yell, yell out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κραυγάζω.</div><b>κραυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κραυγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scream, shout, yell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κραυγή.</div><b>κραυγαλέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κραυγαλέα, neuter κραυγαλέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. garish, gaudy, loud (so colourful as to be in bad taste)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blatant, glaring, screaming (obvious)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: κατάφωρος (katáforos), προκλητικός (proklitikós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κραυγ(ή)) + -αλέος.</div><b>κρεατοελιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρεατοελιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wart</div><b>κρεατομηχανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meatgrinder (literally or figuratively)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κρεατο- (kreato-, “meat”) + μηχανή.</div><b>κρεατοσφαιρίδια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meatballs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κρέας + σφαιρίδιο, probably a calque of English meatball.</div><b>κρεατοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κρεατοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carnivore, meat eater</div><b>κρεατόμυγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρεατόμυγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bluebottle, greenbottle, blowfly (flies of the family Calliphoridae that lay their eggs in rotting meat, dung, or open wounds)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φερε μου τη μυγοσκοτώστρα να σκοτώσω αυτήν την <b>κρεατόμυγα</b>. - Bring me the flyswatter so I can kill that bluebottle.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κρεατό- + -μυγα (-myga, “fly”).</div><b>κρεβάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρεβάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. A marriage custom, usually some days before the marriage, during which friends and relatives throw money onto the marriage bed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κρεβάτι(ο)νn), from Koine Greek κραβ(β)άτιον (krab(b)átion), from a diminutive of Ancient Greek κράββατος.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek крева́ть.</div><b>κρεβατοκάμαρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρεβατοκάμαρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bedroom</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) bedroom furniture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κρεβάτ(ι), “bed”) + -ο- (-o-) + κάμαρα.</div><b>κρεβατομουρμούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) curtain lecture (scolding or grumbling by a wife to her husband in bed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γυναίκα του δεν τον αφήνει να κοιμηθεί με την <b>κρεβατομουρμούρα</b> κάθε βράδυ. - His wife doesn&#x27;t let him sleep; she gives him a curtain lecture every night.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κρεβάτι + μουρμούρα.</div><b>κρεμ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cream (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French crème (“cream”)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cream (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French crème (“cream”)</div><b>κρεμάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρεμάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gallows, gallows tree</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hangman (word guessing game)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κρεμώ + -άλα</div><b>κρεμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hang, hang upon, suspend</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to hang (execute)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern κρεμ(ώ) + -άω, from Ancient Greek κρεμῶ, contracted form of κρεμάω &lt; κρέμαμαι, κρίμνημι of uncertain etymon. Also see κρέμομαι. Cognate with Mariupol Greek кримазу (krimazu).</div><b>κρεμασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κρεμασμένη, neuter κρεμασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hung, suspended</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hanged (executed by suspension by neck)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) dependent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κρεμιέμαι, passive voice of κρεμάω / κρεμώ.</div><b>κρεμαστάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρεμαστάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. something hanging</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (often) hanging fruit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) something tempting but unreachable</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (derogatory, figuratively) sagging breasts</div><b>κρεμαστές γέφυρες</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of κρεμαστή <a href="bword://γέφυρα">γέφυρα</a></div><b>κρεμαστή γέφυρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρεμαστές γέφυρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suspension bridge</div><b>κρεμαστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κρεμαστή, neuter κρεμαστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hanging, suspended</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κρεμαστή</b> γέφυρα - suspension bridge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κρεμάω + -τος (-tos)</div><b>κρεμιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be hung, to hang</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to be dependent upon</div><b>κρεμμύδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρεμμύδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. onion (plant, bulb and vegetable)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) shallot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κρεμμύδιν, from Koine Greek κρέμμυον, from Ancient Greek κρόμμυον. Cognate with Pontic Greek κρομμύδ’.</div><b>κρεμμύδι χλωρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρεμμύδια χλωρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spring onion, scallion</div><b>κρεμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κρεμάω">κρεμάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρεμῶ, uncontracted form of κρεμάω. Also see the modern κρεμάω.</div><b>κρεολός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρεολοί, feminine κρεολή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) Creole (white descendants, usually male, of Spanish settlers in South America)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) Creole (people, usually male, of mixed parentage born in the Caribbean and South America)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French créole.</div><b>κρεοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) butcher&#x27;s, butcher&#x27;s shop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κρεοπωλεῖον. By surface analysis, κρέας + -πωλείο.</div><b>κρεοπώλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρεοπώλες, feminine κρεοπώλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) butcher (a person who prepares and sells meat)</div><b>κρεοπώλισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρεοπώλισσες, feminine κρεοπώλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>butcher (a person who prepares and sells meat)</div><b>κρεπερί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) creperie</div><b>κρετίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κρετίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cretin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. idiot, cretin</div><b>κρι-κρι</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kri-kri, Cretan ibex, feral Cretan goat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic</div><b>κριάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κριάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ram</div><b>κριθάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κριθάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barley</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κριθάριον, diminutive of κρῑθή.</div><b>κριθαράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κριθαράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. orzo pasta</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stye</div><b>κριθαρότο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) a risotto-style dish made of orzo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Blend of κριθαράκι + ριζότο.</div><b>κριθαρόψωμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κριθαρόψωμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barley bread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κριθάρι(ι) (“barley”) + -ό- (“infix”) + -ψωμο (“bread”).</div><b>κρικετόμυς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρικετομύες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hamster (a small Euroasian rodent of the subfamily Cricetinae)</div><b>κρικητός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρικητοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hamster (a small Euroasian rodent of the subfamily Cricetinae)</div><b>κριλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) krill (Euphausiacea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English krill.</div><b>κρινοειδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Crinoidea or crinoids.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Crinoidea.</div><b>κριτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κριτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>criterion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κριτήριον.</div><b>κριτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κριτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. judge (a person who decides the fate of someone or something that has been called into question)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. judge (a person officiating at a sports event, a contest, or similar)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κριτής. By surface analysis, κρί(νω)) + -τής.</div><b>κριτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κριτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>critique, criticism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French critique &amp; German Kritik, from Ancient Greek κριτική (τέχνη), “art of judging”), substantivised feminine of adjective κριτικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κριτικός">κριτικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French critique &amp; German Kritik, from Ancient Greek κριτική (τέχνη), “art of judging”), substantivised feminine of adjective κριτικός.</div><b>κριτική κειμένου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>textual criticism (literally: criticism/judgement of text)</div><b>κριτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κριτική, neuter κριτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>critical (expressing comment or judgement)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κριτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>critic</div>
  <div class='ex'>κριτικός θεάτρου (theatre critic)</div><b>κριτσίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κριτσίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>breadstick, grissino</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian grissini, plural form of grissino (“breadstick”), from Piedmontese grissin, ghërsin, diminutive of ghersa (“line, row”).</div><b>κριός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κριοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ram, male sheep</div>
  <div class='def'>2. battering ram</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κριός.</div><b>κροατικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Croatian (the language of Croatia and its people)</div><b>κροατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κροατική, neuter κροατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Croatian (related to the country, people or language)</div><b>κροκόδειλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κροκόδειλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mistaken spelling of <a href="bword://κροκόδιλος">κροκόδιλος</a></div><b>κροκόδιλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κροκόδιλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crocodile (amphibious reptile)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Koine Greek [Term?] from Ancient Greek κροκόδιλος</div><b>κροκός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κροκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κρόκος">κρόκος</a></div><b>κρομμύδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρομμύδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κρεμμύδι">κρεμμύδι</a></div><b>κροταλίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κροταλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rattlesnake, rattler</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) untrustworthy and violent man, a sidewinder</div><b>κροτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to make sudden and strong rattling sound, crack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κροτέω, κροτῶ.</div><b>κρουαζιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρουαζιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cruise, leisure voyage</div><b>κρουαζιερόπλοιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρουαζιερόπλοια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cruise liner, cruise ship</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κρουαζιέρα + πλοίο</div><b>κρουασάν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) croissant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French croissant.</div><b>κρουστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κρουστή, neuter κρουστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of or related to percussion instruments</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tightly woven</div>
  <div class='def'>3. full</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek κρουστός, and a semantic loan from German Schlaginstrumente or French instruments à percussion.</div><b>κρούομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be struck</div><b>κρούσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρούσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. case (an instance of a specific condition or set of symptoms)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. case, instance (single occurrence of an undesirable phenomenon)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κροῦσμα, from Ancient Greek κροῦμα.</div><b>κρούσταλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρούσταλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of κρύσταλλο</div><b>κρούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to strike</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The Ancient Greek κρούω.</div><b>κρυμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κρυμμένη, neuter κρυμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hidden, latent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κρύβομαι, passive voice of κρύβω (“to hide”).</div><b>κρυολογημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κρυολογημένη, neuter κρυολογημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who has caught a cold, a chill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of κρυολογώ, a verb without passive forms.</div><b>κρυολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to have a cold, catch a cold, feel unwell</div><b>κρυολόγημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρυολογήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a cold, a sniffle</div><b>κρυοπάγημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρυοπαγήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) frostbite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Either from stem κρυοπαγη- of Katharevousa verb κρυοπαγῶ + -μα (-ma), or a free translation of English frostbite. For the combining forms, see κρύο n and πάγος m.</div><b>κρυπτανάλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cryptanalysis</div><b>κρυπτογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cryptography</div><b>κρυπτογραφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to encode, encipher, encrypt</div><b>κρυπτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) krypton</div><b>κρυστάλλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρυσταλλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crystallisation (UK), crystallization (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>νερό <b>κρυστάλλωσης</b> - water of crystallisation</div><b>κρυσταλλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κρυσταλλική, neuter κρυσταλλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crystalline, crystallised (UK), crystallized (US)</div><b>κρυφά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in secret</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κρυφός">κρυφός</a></div><b>κρυφτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hide and seek (UK), hide and go seek (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter form of Byzantine Greek κρυφτός, from Ancient Greek κρυπτός.</div><b>κρυφός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κρυφή, neuter κρυφό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hidden, secret</div><b>κρυψορχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cryptorchidism</div><b>κρυωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κρυωμένη, neuter κρυωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>such that has a cold, a sniffle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> perfect passive participle of κρυώνω, a verb without passive forms.</div><b>κρυώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to feel cold.</div><b>κρόκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρόκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. yolk, egg yolk</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κρόκος</b> αβγού - egg yolk</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crocus</div>
  <div class='def'>3. saffron</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρόκος, of ultimately Semitic origin. Doublet of κουρκουμάς.</div><b>κρόταφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρόταφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>temple</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρόταφος.</div><b>κρότος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρότοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sudden and strong rattling sound, crack</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι ήταν αυτός ο <b>κρότος</b>; Τρόμαξα πολύ. - What was this sound (crack)? I was very frightened.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρότος.</div><b>κρύβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to hide, cover</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek κρύβω, from the ancient κρύπτω (“hide”) from the perfective stem κρυψ- in the pattern of -ψ- &gt; -β- as in κλέπτω, κλεψ- &gt; κλέβω.</div><b>κρύο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρύα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cold, cold weather, etc</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρύος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://κρύος">κρύος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κρύος">κρύος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρύος.</div><b>κρύος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κρύα, neuter κρύο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cold, chilled, chilly, cool</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρύος.
Compare Mariupol Greek кри́ю.</div><b>κρύπτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κρύπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hidden secret place</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crypt</div>
  <div class='def'>3. crypt</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>κρύπτες</b> των αμυγδαλών - crypts of the tonsils</div><b>κρύπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κρύβω">κρύβω</a></div><b>κρύσταλλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κρύσταλλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crystal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κρύσταλλος.</div><b>κρύωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κρυώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cold</div><b>κρώζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of birds) to caw, to croak, to crow (to make a harsh or shrill cry)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of a person) to squawk (to yell, scream, or call out shrilly)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κρώζω.</div><b>κσ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to tbsp (tablespoon, as unit of measure)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κουτάλι της σούπας</div><b>κτένι</b>
