  <div class='def'>2. farm</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) land, farmland</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κτῆμα, from κτάομαι.</div><b>κτήνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κτήνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. domestic animal, farm animal, beast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. beast, animal, mammal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) wild person, brute, beast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κτῆνος.</div><b>κτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κτίριο">κτίριο</a></div><b>κτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χτίζω">χτίζω</a></div><b>κτίριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κτίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>building</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκητήριον.</div><b>κτίσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κτίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. building, construction</div>
  <div class='def'>2. creation</div><b>κτίστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κτίστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://χτίστης">χτίστης</a> (builder)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. God</div><b>κταπόδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κταπόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://χταπόδι">χταπόδι</a></div><b>κτενοφόρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κτενοφόρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ctenophore (Ctenophora)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Ctenophora. By surface analysis, Ancient Greek κτείς + -φόρο.</div><b>κτηματομεσίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κτηματομεσίτες, feminine κτηματομεσίτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>realtor, real estate agent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation from κτήμα + -ο- (-o-) + μεσίτης.</div><b>κτηνάνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κτηνάνθρωπος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brute, pig (abusive, brutish man)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κτήνος + άνθρωπος.</div><b>κτηνίατρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κτηνίατροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vet, veterinary surgeon</div><b>κτηνοβάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κτηνοβάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bestialist (person that has sexual relations with non-human animals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κτηνοβάτης, from κτῆνος + -βάτης, from verb βαίνω.</div><b>κτηνοβασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κτηνοβασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beastiality (sexual activity between a human and another animal species)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κτῆνος + -βασία, from verb βαίνω.</div><b>κτηνοτροφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) husbandry, animal husbandry, livestock rearing</div><b>κτηνοτρόφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural κτηνοτρόφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stock farmer, stockman, stock breeder</div><b>κτηνώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κτηνώδης, neuter κτηνώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. like an animal in form and behaviour</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brutal, brutish, animal, beastly</div><b>κτητικές αντωνυμίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of κτητική <a href="bword://αντωνυμία">αντωνυμία</a></div><b>κτητική αντωνυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κτητικές αντωνυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(possessive, pronoun, without noun) possessive pronoun</div><b>κτητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κτητική, neuter κτητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (possessive) possessive</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει <b>κτητικές</b> τάσεις. - He has possessive tendencies.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (possessive) possessive</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κτητική</b> αντωνυμία - possessive pronoun</div><b>κτλ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>initialism of <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://τα">τα</a> λοιπά: etc (an abbreviation of et cetera)</div><b>κτυπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χτυπάω">χτυπάω</a></div><b>κτυπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χτυπάω">χτυπάω</a></div><b>κτύπημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κτυπήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χτύπημα">χτύπημα</a></div><b>κτύπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κτύποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χτύπος">χτύπος</a></div><b>κυανοκόρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυανοκόρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cyanocorax-genus bird.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from translingual Cyanocorax. By surface analysis, κυανός + κόρακας.</div><b>κυανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυανή, neuter κυανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (contemporary, formal) cyan or sky blue</div>
  <div class='ex'>blue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as noun) see: κυανό</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κύανος, also see the Mediaeval κυανός m, from Ancient Greek κῠᾰνοῦς/κῠᾰ́νεος from κύανος. Cognate with Mycenaean Greek 𐀓𐀷𐀜 (ku-wa-no) and Tsakonian κουβάνε.</div><b>κυβέρνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυβερνήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>government, cabinet</div><b>κυβίστηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυβιστήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) somersault</div><b>κυβερνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κυβερνώ">κυβερνώ</a></div><b>κυβερνήτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυβερνήτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. governor (leader of a region or state)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. captain, skipper</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pilot (of an aircraft)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κυβερνήτης.</div><b>κυβερνημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κυβερνημένη, neuter κυβερνημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>governed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κυβερνιέμαι, or κυβερνώμαι, passive voice forms of κυβερνώ (“govern”).</div><b>κυβερνητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυβερνητική, neuter κυβερνητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>governmental</div><b>κυβερνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to rule, govern</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to captain</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to navigate</div><b>κυβεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to play dice, gamble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κῠβεύω.</div><b>κυβισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) cubism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) cubic capacity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French cubisme.</div><b>κυβοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυβοειδής, neuter κυβοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cuboid (resembling a cube)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek κυβοειδής. By surface analysis, κύβος + -ειδής.</div><b>κυδωνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυδωνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quince tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See κυδώνι; both are ultimately from Κυδωνία.</div><b>κυδώνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυδώνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. quince</div>
  <div class='def'>2. warty venus (Venus verrucosa)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κυδώνιον, shortening of μηλοκυδώνιον, ultimately from Κυδωνία.</div><b>κυκεώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυκεώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kykeon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mess, jumble</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bewilderment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κυκεών, from Ancient Greek κυκεών.</div><b>κυκλαδίτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυκλαδίτικη, neuter κυκλαδίτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cycladic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Κυκλάδ(ες)) + -ίτικος.</div><b>κυκλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυκλική, neuter κυκλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. circular, round</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cyclical, cyclic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κυκλικός. By surface analysis, κύκλος + -ικός.</div><b>κυκλοθυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cyclothymia</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mood swings</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from German Zyklothymie, from Ancient Greek. By surface analysis, κυκλο- (kyklo-) + θυμ(ός)) + -ία.</div><b>κυκλοθυμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυκλοθυμική, neuter κυκλοθυμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cyclothymic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. moody (given to sudden or frequent changes of mood)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κυκλοθυμ(ία)) + -ικός, a calque of German zyklothym.</div><b>κυκλοπροπάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cyclopropane</div><b>κυκλοφοράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) (colloquial) alternative form of <a href="bword://κυκλοφορώ">κυκλοφορώ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κυκλοφορ(ώ), “circulate”) + -άω.</div><b>κυκλοφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) circulation</div>
  <div class='ex'>σε κυκλοφορία (in circulation; on the market)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) traffic</div><b>κυκλοφορημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κυκλοφορημένη, neuter κυκλοφορημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>circulated</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το γραμματόσημο είναι <b>κυκλοφορημένο</b> από πέρσι. - The stamp is circulated since last year.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κυκλοφορούμαι, passive voice of κυκλοφορώ (“circulate”).</div><b>κυκλοφορητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυκλοφορητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>circulator (mechanism that circulates fluid around a system)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κυκλοφορώ + -τής, a calque of English circulator.</div><b>κυκλοφορούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rare) (equivalent of the intransitive active voice) <a href="bword://κυκλοφορώ">κυκλοφορώ</a>; to be published</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) (equivalent of the intransitive active voice) <a href="bword://κυκλοφορώ">κυκλοφορώ</a>; to be in circulation</div><b>κυκλοφορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to publish, issue, circulate, release(book, newspaper, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, transitive) to circulate, go around</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive, transitive) to be around, be available</div>
  <div class='ex'>δεν <b>κυκλοφορεί</b> κανείς - there’s no one around</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive, transitive) to drive around (in a car, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from κυκλοφορ(ία), “circulation”) + -ώ. The sense of the ancient mediopassive κυκλοφορέομαι was revolve. By surface analysis, κυκλο- (kyklo-, “circle (κύκλος)”) + -φορώ</div><b>κυκλωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυκλωτική, neuter κυκλωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>encircling</div>
  <div class='ex'>μια <b>κυκλωτική</b> κίνηση - an encircling movement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κύκλω(ση)) + -τικός.</div><b>κυκλώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυκλώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cyclone</div><b>κυκλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to surround, to encircle, to encompass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κυκλώνω, from Ancient Greek κυκλόω. By surface analysis, κύκλ(ος)) + -ώνω.</div><b>κυλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to roll</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to flow</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to go by</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From earlier κυλώ + -άω, inherited from Byzantine Greek κυλῶ, from Ancient Greek κῠλῐ́ω.</div><b>κυλινδρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυλινδρική, neuter κυλινδρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cylindrical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κυλινδρικός. By surface analysis, κύλινδρ(ος)) + -ικός.</div><b>κυλινδροπίστονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυλινδροπίστονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cylinder and piston (together)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κύλινδρος + πιστόνι</div><b>κυλινδρόμυλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυλινδρόμυλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>roller mill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κύλινδρος + μύλος</div><b>κυματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to wave, flutter, flap (handkerchief, skirt, flag, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι σημαίες <b>κυμάτιζαν</b> στον αέρα. - The flags were waving in the breeze.</div><b>κυματιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυματιστή, neuter κυματιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wavy, undulating, rippling (rising or swelling in waves)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wavy (having wave-like shapes on its border or surface; waved)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κυματιστά</b> μαλλιά - wavy hair</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from κυματίζω + -τός, a loose calque of French ondulé.</div><b>κυματοθραύστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυματοθραύστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A breakwater which protects a harbour or coastline.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κύματο + θραύστης.</div><b>κυνήγι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυνήγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hunting, shooting, the chase (the search for prey and its killing, for food or pleasure)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prey (the animal hunted)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κυνήγιν, from Koine Greek κυνήγιον, from Ancient Greek κυνηγέσιον, from κυνηγός, from κύων.</div><b>κυναίλουρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheetah</div><b>κυνηγάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to chase, hunt, pursue, follow</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με έστειλε να <b>κυνηγήσω</b> την ουρά μου. - He sent me to chase my tail.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to stalk, track (an animal)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stalk, harass (a person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι ήταν κάτι περισσότερο από πρόθυμοι να⁠ παίζουν το αγαπημένο παιχνίδι του Ντάντλι: να <b>κυνηγούν</b> τον Χάρι! - They were all more than happy to play Dudley&#x27;s favorite game: to harass Harry.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κυνηγώ + -άω. From Ancient Greek κῠνηγῶ, contracted form of κῠνηγέω. See κῠνηγός, κύων.</div><b>κυνηγημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine κυνηγημένη, neuter κυνηγημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hunted, chased</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of κυνηγιέμαι, passive voice of κυνηγάω / κυνηγώ (“to hunt”).</div><b>κυνηγητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυνηγητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hunt, chase, pursuit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μετά τη ληστεία, άρχισε το <b>κυνηγητό</b>. - After the robbery, he began the chase.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tag</div>
  <div class='def'>3. manhunt</div><b>κυνηγιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be hunted</div><b>κυνηγοί κεφαλών</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://κυνηγός">κυνηγός</a> κεφαλών</div><b>κυνηγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυνηγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hunter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. seeker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κῠνηγός. See κύων.</div><b>κυνηγός κεφαλών</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυνηγοί κεφαλών)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>headhunter</div><b>κυνηγόσκυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυνηγόσκυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hunting dog, hunter (a dog used in hunting)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κυνήγ(ι)) + -ο- (-o-) + σκυλ(ί)) + -ο (-o).</div><b>κυνηγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κυνηγάω">κυνηγάω</a></div><b>κυνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυνική, neuter κυνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cynical</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυνικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cynic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dog</div><b>κυνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) cynicism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) Cynicism</div><b>κυνοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυνοειδής, neuter κυνοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>canine (relating to dogs or canids)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κυνοειδής.</div><b>κυνόδοντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυνόδοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>canine tooth; cuspid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek κῠνόδους.</div><b>κυοφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυοφορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gestation</div><b>κυοφορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to gestate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek κυοφορῶ, contracted form of κυοφορέω &lt; κύω</div><b>κυπαρίσσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυπαρίσσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cypress tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κυπάρισσος.</div><b>κυπαρισσόμηλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cypress cone</div><b>κυπελλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυπελλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small bowl for yoghurt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. little cup, sippy cup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * κύπελλο + -άκι.</div><b>κυπρίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυπρίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carp</div><b>κυπραίικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal variant of <a href="bword://κυπριακά">κυπριακά</a>: the Cypriot Greek dialect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter plural of κυπραίικος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κυπραίικος">κυπραίικος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>κυπραίικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυπαραίικη, neuter κυπραίικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κυπριακός">κυπριακός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek Κυπραῖ(ος))) + -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>κυπριακά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot (the dialect of Cyprus and its people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter plural of κυπριακός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κυπριακός">κυπριακός</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>(both, less formal or idiomatic)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>κυπριακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυπριακή, neuter κυπριακός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot (relating to the country of Cyprus, its people or its language)</div><b>κυπριώτικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot (the dialect of Cyprus and its people)</div><b>κυρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) lady of the island (when there is only one)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lady of the house, mistress of the house</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κυρά.</div><b>κυράτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυράτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, humorous) snob, social climber (woman who tries to mimic the behaviours and manners of those on a higher social standing than her)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, derogatory, humorous) gossip (woman who gossips, usually negatively, about others)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μαζεύτηκαν όλες οι <b>κυράτσες</b> της γειτονιάς να κουτσομπολέψουν. - All the gossipy hens of the neighbourhood have gathered to gossip.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κυράτσα.</div><b>κυρήθρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυρήθρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κηρήθρα">κηρήθρα</a></div><b>κυρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. madam</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lady</div>
  <div class='def'>3. teacher</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κῡρῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>κυρίαρχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυρίαρχη, neuter κυρίαρχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dominant (predominant, common, prevalent, of greatest importance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sovereign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek with semantic loan from French souverain.</div><b>κυρίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mainly, chiefly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κυρίως, from Ancient Greek κύριος.</div><b>κυριακάτικα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κυριακάτικος">κυριακάτικος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, humorous, sometimes) on a Sunday, of a Sunday (usually with reference to something not appropriate or usual for the day)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί δουλεύεις <b>κυριακάτικα</b>; Η Κυριακή είναι αργία. - Why are you working on a Sunday of all days? Sunday is a day off.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Κυριακ(ή) (“Sunday”) + -άτικα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Sunday best (person&#x27;s finest clothing, especially the clothes one reserves to wear to church on Sunday)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φόρεσε τα <b>κυριακάτικά</b> της για να δεχτεί το βραβείο. - She wore her Sunday best to receive the award.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Κυριακ(ή) (“Sunday”) + -άτικα.</div><b>κυριακάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυριακάτικη, neuter κυριακάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of Sunday</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>κυριακάτικος</b> τύπος - the Sunday&#x27;s press</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (adverb) see <a href="bword://κυριακάτικα">κυριακάτικα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Κυριακή + -άτικος.</div><b>κυριακή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of κυριακός (in expressions)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κυριακή</b> αργία - the day off of Sunday</div><b>κυριαρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. domination, dominion, rule (the exercise of power in ruling)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sovereignty (of a nation or other polity: the state of being able to govern itself without the coercion or concurrence of other polities)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dominance, predominance, preponderance, supremacy (being in a position of power, authority or ascendancy over others)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κυριαρχία.</div><b>κυριαρχώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dominate (to govern, rule or control by superior authority or power)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dominate (to exert an overwhelming guiding influence over something or someone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek κυριαρχῶ (of the -έω conjugation) with a contemporary influence of a semantic loan from French dominer. From Koine Greek κυρίαρχ(ος)) + -ῶ &lt; Koine Greek κυριαρχία f.</div><b>κυριεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to capture, to take possession of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to conquer</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of a feeling) to overcome, to overwhelm, to take hold of, to take possession of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κυριεύω.</div><b>κυριλέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) posh, affected, chic</div><b>κυριολεκτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κυριολεκτικός">κυριολεκτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>literally (word for word, not figuratively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τώρα, μιλάς μεταφορικά ή <b>κυριολεκτικά</b>; - Are you speaking figuratively or literally?</div><b>κυριολεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυριολεκτική, neuter κυριολεκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>literal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek κυριόλεκτος + -ικός.</div><b>κυριολεξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυριολεξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>literal meaning, literal sense</div>
  <div class='ex'>στην <b>κυριολεξία</b> - in the literal sense, literally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κυριολεξία.</div><b>κυριότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ownership, possession (the state of having complete legal control of something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κυριότητα, accusative of κυριότης, with semantic loan from French propriété. By surface analysis, κύρι(ος)) + -ότητα.</div><b>κυρογενιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυρογενιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the grandmother&#x27;s family.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κυρούλα + γενιά.</div><b>κυρτοδάκτυλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυρτοδάκτυλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Kotschy&#x27;s gecko (Mediodactylus kotschyi)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) Any Mediodactylus species.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, κυρτός + Ancient Greek δᾰ́κτῠλος. Compare translingual Cyrtodactylus.</div><b>κυρτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυρτή, neuter κυρτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>convex</div><b>κυρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to ratify</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κυρῶ, contracted form of κυρόω, with -ώνω suffix.</div><b>κυστίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυστίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vesicle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κύστη + -ίδιο.</div><b>κυστεΐνη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cysteine</div><b>κυστική ίνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cystic fibrosis</div><b>κυστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cystic (relating to a cyst)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cystic relating to the bladder or gall bladder.</div><b>κυτταρίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural κυτταρίνεσ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) cellulose (carbohydrate plant material)</div><b>κυτταρίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυτταρίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cellulite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κύτταρ(ο), “cell”) + -ίτιδα, a calque of French cellulite.</div><b>κυτταρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κυτταρική, neuter κυτταρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cellular (relating to cells)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from κύτταρο n</div><b>κυτταρομεγαλοϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κυτταρομεγαλοϊοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cytomegalovirus (Cytomegalovirus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κύτταρο + μεγάλος + ιός. Calque of translingual Cytomegalovirus.</div><b>κυψέλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυψέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beehive, hive</div><b>κωδεΐνη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) codeine</div><b>κωδικοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to codify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to codify</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to codify, organise (data)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to encode, encipher, encrypt</div><b>κωδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κωδική, neuter κωδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of or relating to code; secret</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κωδικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>access code, password</div><b>κωδικός πρόσβασης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κωδικοί πρόσβασης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>password, access code</div><b>κωδωνοκρούστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κωδωνοκρούστες, feminine κωδωνοκρούστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bell ringer / bell-ringer / bellringer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κώδων + -ο- + -κρούστης of κρούω. In analogy to the Hellenistic κυμβᾰλοκρούστης.</div><b>κωδωνοστάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωδωνοστάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bell tower, belfry, campanile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κώδων + -ο- (-o-) + -στάσιο</div><b>κωλοδάχτυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωλοδάχτυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, offensive) middle finger, one-finger salute, the finger (obscene gesture made by holding only the middle finger of a hand erect while the rest of the fingers are in a fist)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, vulgar) rectal fingerbang (insertion of one or more digits into another person&#x27;s anus for sexual pleasure)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλο- (kolo-, “arse, ass”) + δάχτυλο.</div><b>κωλομέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωλομέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) arsecheek, asscheek, butt cheek, nether cheek (a buttock)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτές τις μέρες οι άντρες φοράνε τα παντελόνια τους τόσο χαμηλά που φαίνονται τα <b>κωλομέρια</b> τους. - These days, guys wear their trousers so low you can see their arsecheeks.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλο- (kolo-, “arse, ass”) + μέρος.</div><b>κωλοπαίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωλοπαίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κωλόπαιδο">κωλόπαιδο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλο- (kolo-, “arse, ass”) + παιδί.</div><b>κωλοπηλάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κωλοπηλάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, humorous) The type of running or sprinting someone does when they need to defecate urgently (for example, with diarrhoea).</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχε διάρροια και έκανε μεγάλη <b>κωλοπηλάλα</b> να βρει τουαλέτα. - He got an attack of diarrhoea and had to run quickly to find a toilet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) A great rush or desire for quick action.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τους έπιασε <b>κωλοπηλάλα</b> να τελειώσουν γρήγορα την δουλειά. - They were rushing a lot to finish the job.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλο- (kolo-, “arse, ass”) + πηλάλα.</div><b>κωλοτούμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολοτούμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. somersault</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) U-turn, sudden turnabout, volte-face, backflip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλο- (kolo-, “arse, ass”) + τούμπα.</div><b>κωλοτρυπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κωλοτρυπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) arsehole, asshole (the anus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πιο παράξενο πράγμα που είδα ήταν ένας ζωγράφος που έφτιαχνε έργα με την <b>κωλοτρυπίδα</b> του. - The weirdest thing I saw was an artist who made works with his arsehole.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλο- (kolo-, “arse, ass”) + τρύπα + -ίδα.</div><b>κωλοφυλλάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωλοφυλλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) rag (low-quality newspaper or magazine)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλο- (kolo-, “arse, ass”) + φυλλάδα.</div><b>κωλοφωτιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κωλοφωτιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) firefly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλο- (kolo-, “arse, ass”) + φωτιά.</div><b>κωλόγερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κωλόγεροι, feminine κωλόγρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, offensive) old bastard, old asshole, old fart (abusive term for elderly man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πέθανε επιτελούς αυτός ο <b>κωλόγερος</b>. - That old bastard finally died.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλό- + γέρος.</div><b>κωλόγρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κωλόγριες, masculine κωλόγερος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, offensive) old bitch, old cunt, old fart (abusive term for elderly woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η εκκλησία είναι πάντα γεμάτη <b>κωλόγριες</b> την κυριακή. - The church is always full of old bitches every Sunday.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλό- + γριά.</div><b>κωλόμουνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωλόμουνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, offensive) cunt, cuntbitch (term of abuse against woman)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, vulgar) boy pussy, boy cunt, man cunt (male&#x27;s anus in gay contexts)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ψάχνω κάποιον να μου γαμήσει το <b>κωλομούνο</b>. - I&#x27;m looking for someone to fuck my boy pussy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλό- + μουνί.</div><b>κωλόπαιδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωλόπαιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) little shit, little brat, little asshole (badly behaved child or young person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>κωλόπαιδα</b> της γειτονιάς πάλι μου έσπασαν το παράθυρο. - Those little shits from the neighbourhood broke my window again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλό- + παιδί.</div><b>κωλότσεπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hip-pocket</div><b>κωλόχαρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κωλόχαρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) bumfodder, asswipe (toilet paper)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory) Any paper deemed to be worthless and without value such as university degrees or any other official documents.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κωλό- + χαρτί.</div><b>κωλύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be hindered</div><b>κωλύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to prevent by putting an obstacle, hinder, preclude</div>
  <div class='ex'>(usually in the passive) I am unable to</div>
  <div class='def'>2. perfective tenses: for legal expressions</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η διάταξη αυτή <b>κωλύει</b> την εφαρμογή της εθνικής ρυθμίσεως. - That rule precludes the application of national legislation. (eur‑lex, 1993)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κωλύω of unknown etymon. Possibly related to the ancient κολούω or to *κῶλ-ος.</div><b>κωμειδύλλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωμειδύλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comic opera, play usually including songs, musical theatre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From κωμωδ(ία), “comedy”) + ειδύλλιο. Semantic loan from French comédie-vaudeville.</div><b>κωμωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κωμωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comedy (play or film)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κωμῳδία.</div><b>κωμόπολη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κωμοπόλεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small town</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κωμόπολις. By surface analysis, κώμ(η)) + -ό- + πόλη.</div><b>κωνάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωνάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pineal gland</div><b>κωνάριον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωνάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://κωνάριο">κωνάριο</a></div><b>κωνίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωνία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cone cell (part of the retina)</div><b>κωνοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κωνοειδής, neuter κωνοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pineal, conical; having the shape of a cone.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conoid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, κώνος + -ειδής.</div><b>κωνοφόρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κωνοφόρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conifer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivization of κωνοφόρος.</div><b>κωνσταντινουπολίτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κωνσταντινουπολίτικη, neuter κωνσταντινουπολίτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Constantinopolitan</div><b>κωπήποδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Copepoda</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from taxonomic name Copepoda, from Ancient Greek κώπη + Ancient Greek πούς.</div><b>κωπηλάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κωπηλάτες, feminine κωπηλάτισσα or κωπηλάτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oarsman, rower</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κωπηλάτης.</div><b>κωφός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κωφή, neuter κωφό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κουφός">κουφός</a></div><b>κόβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Ι cut, chop, divide, carve, slice (into parts)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πότε θα <b>κόψουμε</b> την πρωτοχρονιάτικη πίτα; - When shall we cut the New Year pie?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Ι cut, trim, prune (reduce by cutting)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το κείμενο είναι πολύ μεγάλο, να <b>κόψουμε</b> κάτι. - There is too much text, we need to cut something.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to take a shortcut, cut</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to cut (pack of playing cards)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to cut, quit (cigarettes, gambling)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to drop out, quit (school, course)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έκοψε</b> τάξεις του. - He cut his classes.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. to strike, mint (coins and medals)</div>
  <div class='def'>8. to interrupt, cut short, cut off (conversation)</div>
  <div class='def'>9. to separate (for sauces etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κόβω, from the Ancient Greek κόπτω from the perfective stem κοψ- in the pattern of -ψ- &gt; -β- as in κλέπτω, κλεψ- &gt; κλέβω. Also see κόπος. Doublet of κόπτω.</div><b>κόθρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόθροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) crusty layer around a pie or bread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Possibly inherited from Byzantine Greek κόδρα, from Latin quadra (“morsel”), with an unexpected devoicing of δ (d).</div><b>κόκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coca (the cultivated South American shrub Erythroxylum coca and its dried leaf which is the source of cocaine)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) coke (cocaine)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Coke, coke (cola-based soft drink, especially Coca-Cola)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from New Latin coca, with the &#x27;coke&#x27; (cocaine) sense influenced by English coke.</div><b>κόκαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κόκαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bone</div>
  <div class='ex'>Kρέας χωρίς <b>κόκαλα</b> για μαγείρεμα. - Meat without bones for cooking.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shoehorn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κόκκαλον n, from Ancient Greek κόκκαλος.</div><b>κόκκινη</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κόκκινος">κόκκινος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόκκινες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quaffle</div>
  <div class='ex'>… όχι, όχι, η <b>κόκκινη</b> είναι τώρα στα χέρια των Σλίθεριν. - … no, no, the quaffle [lit. &quot;red&quot;] is now in the hands of Slitherin.</div><b>κόκκινη κάρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόκκινες κάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>red card</div><b>κόκκινη παλίρροια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) red tide</div><b>κόκκινο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://κόκκινος">κόκκινος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://κόκκινο">κόκκινο</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κόκκινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. red (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>κόκκινο</b> είναι το χρώμα του πάθους. - Red is the colour of passion.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) red, red light</div>
  <div class='ex'>περνάω με <b>κόκκινο</b> - to run a red light</div><b>κόκκινο πάντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κόκκινα πάντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>red panda (Ailurus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, κόκκινος + πάντα.</div><b>κόκκινο πανί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic, uncountable) red rag (something that will enrage another particular person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>κόκκινο πανί</b> γι’ αυτήν είναι οι άνθρωποι που δέρνουν ζώα. - The red rag for her is people who beat animals.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English red rag.</div><b>κόκκινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κόκκινη, neuter κόκκινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>red (colour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δύο <b>κόκκινα</b> τριαντάφυλλα - two red roses</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κόκκινος, from κόκκος. The modern sense came from grains of holly, which were used to make red dyes. Displaced earlier Ancient Greek ἐρυθρός.</div><b>κόκκορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόκκορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://κόκορας">κόκορας</a></div><b>κόκκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόκκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>speck, grain, particle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόκκος.</div><b>κόκορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόκορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cock (male chicken)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cock (the hammer of a firearm trigger mechanism)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) a man with vigorous sex life</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Of onomatopoeic origin.</div><b>κόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sheet of paper</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κόλα</b> χαρτί - sheet of paper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) cola (soda drink)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) kola, cola (Cola, plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Possibly a back-formation from πρωτόκολλο reinterpreted as “first sheet of paper”.</div><b>κόλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flatterer, bootlicker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόλαξ</div><b>κόλαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κολάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hell, inferno, a place of anguish and suffering</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek κόλαση, form of κόλασις, from Ancient Greek κόλασις, thus &quot;the place of punishment&quot;.</div><b>κόλατζης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) goalkeeper</div><b>κόλαφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόλαφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slap (to the face)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόλαφος. Doublet of κόλπο.</div><b>κόλιανδρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) coriander (the spice or the plant)</div><b>κόλιαντρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κόλιανδρος">κόλιανδρος</a></div><b>κόλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. glue, gum</div>
  <div class='ex'>see κόλλα on the Greek Wikipedia.Wikipedia ᵉˡ</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sizing, size</div>
  <div class='def'>3. flour paste, starch paste, starch glue</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular simple past active perfective imperative of <a href="bword://κολλάω">κολλάω</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://κολλώ">κολλώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>alternative form: κόλλησε (kóllise)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular imperfect active perfective imperative of <a href="bword://κολλάω">κολλάω</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://κολλώ">κολλώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>alternative form: κόλλαγε (kóllage)</div><b>κόλλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. action or result of fixing, bonding two things together with some third material:; soldering</div>
  <div class='def'>2. action or result of fixing, bonding two things together with some third material:; gluing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fixation (state of mind)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) sexual harassment</div><b>κόλον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) colon (last part of the digestive system)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κόλον.</div><b>κόλπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόλποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bosom</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bay, gulf</div>
  <div class='def'>3. vagina</div>
  <div class='def'>4. atrium (of the heart)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κόλπος. Doublet of κόρφος.</div><b>κόμβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόμβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interchange, hub, circus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. knot (speed of one nautical mile per hour)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. node</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κόμβος with semantic loan from French nœud for &#x27;hub&#x27; and &#x27;node&#x27; and from English knot in the nautical sense. Doublet of κόμπος.</div><b>κόμης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόμητες, feminine κόμισσα or κόμησσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>count, earl (rank in the nobility)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κόμης, from Latin comes.</div><b>κόμησσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόμησσες, masculine κόμης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κόμισσα">κόμισσα</a></div><b>κόμικς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) comics (artistic medium consisting of series of drawings to tell a story)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English comics, the plural of comic, ultimately derived from Greek elements. Doublet of κωμικός.</div><b>κόμισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόμισσες, masculine κόμης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>countess (rank in the nobility)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κόμης + -ισσα (-issa, “feminine form”)</div><b>κόμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κόμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. political party</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a typographical symbol: “,”; the comma (used as a punctuation mark)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a typographical symbol: “,”; the decimal point</div>
  <div class='ex'>5<b>,</b>3 - five point three</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόμμα.</div><b>κόμμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κόμμεα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rubber latex</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόμμι, from Egyptian qmy (“anointing oil”), N29-W19-i-i*t-N33:Z2 qmyt (“acanthus resin, gum”).</div><b>κόμμωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κομμώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hairstyle, hairdo, coiffure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κόμμω(σις), “sophisticated treatment”) + modern suffix -ση (-si). The traditional connection to κόμη appears to be folk etymology.</div><b>κόμπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόμποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. knot, looping of a piece of string</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δένει <b>κόμπο</b>. - He ties a knot.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. node of plant stem</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κόμβος. Doublet of κόμβος.</div><b>κόμπρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόμπρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cobra (venomous snake)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Portuguese cobra, from Latin colubra.</div><b>κόνδυλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόνδυλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tuber</div>
  <div class='def'>2. condyle</div><b>κόπανος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόπανοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dolly, washing beetle, battledore</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flail</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pestle</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (UK) numbskull, (US) jerk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόπανον.</div><b>κόπιτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόπιτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hook and eye (fastener)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قوپچه.</div><b>κόπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. toil, hard work, effort</div>
  <div class='def'>2. suffering</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fatigue</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pay, wages (for such work)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόπος (“striking; fatigue”). See κόπτω.</div><b>κόπρανα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>excrement, faeces (UK), feces (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόπρος.</div><b>κόπρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) faeces (UK), feces (US), shit, manure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) dirt</div>
  <div class='def'>3. stray dog</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόπρος.</div><b>κόπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόπτες, feminine κόπτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cutter (occupation of craftsman who cuts out leather, textiles etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cutter (tool or machine for cutting leather, textiles etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. wire cutters (hand tool)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) university teacher who selects students for rejection</div><b>κόπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to show excessive selfish interest in something</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κόπτομαι (“beat oneself, mourn”), mediopassive voice of κόπτω. Also see the modern verb κόβω.</div><b>κόπτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόπτριες, masculine κόπτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cutter (occupation of craftswoman who cuts out leather, textiles etc)</div><b>κόπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κόβω">κόβω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> This form was a learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κόπτω found in Katharevousa; compare the inherited doublet κόβω.</div><b>κόπωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fatigue, weariness (exhaustion, tiredness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fatigue (weakening and eventual failure of material)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κόπωση</b> των μετάλλων - metal fatigue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek κόπωσις, with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>κόρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crust (of bread, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. scab (of wound)</div><b>κόρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>raven, Corvus corax.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόραξ.</div><b>κόρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. daughter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. maiden, girl</div>
  <div class='def'>3. kore (the modern term for an ancient Greek statue of a young woman)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pupil (of the eye)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κόρη.</div><b>κόρνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κόρνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>horn (type of instrument)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian corno.</div><b>κόρυμβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόρυμβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cluster of flowers with a flat or convex top</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French corymbe, from Latin corymbus, from Ancient Greek κόρυμβος.</div><b>κόρφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόρφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dialectal) chest, breast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) chest as a place to embrace someone</div><b>κόσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scythe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Proto-Slavic *kosa.</div><b>κόσμημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κοσμήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jewel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόσμημᾰ.</div><b>κόσμιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κόσμια, neuter κόσμιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decent; seemly; proper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κόσμιος.</div><b>κόσμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόσμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Universe, cosmos</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) world; planet Earth; an imaginary world</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) world; planet Earth; one&#x27;s own, inner world</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ζει σε άλλον <b>κόσμο</b>! - He lives in another world!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (collective) society, the people, the masses</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν φταίει ο <b>κόσμος</b>, φταίνε οι πολιτικοί. - It is not the fault of the people, it is the politicians&#x27; fault.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (collective) people (multiple persons)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπήρχε πολύς <b>κόσμος</b> στη συναυλία. - There were a lot of people at the concert.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. a group of people (geographically, historically, socially)</div>
  <div class='ex'>O Ρωμαϊκός <b>κόσμος</b> - The Roman world (the Romans, the Roman civilization)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. cosmos (Cosmos sp. plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>8. world; planet Earth</div>
  <div class='ex'>See related expressions</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κόσμος. Cognate with Mariupol Greek ко́смос.</div><b>κόστος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κόστη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cost</div>
  <div class='def'>2. money or effort expended in achieving something</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cost price</div>
  <div class='def'>4. rate</div>
  <div class='def'>5. consequences</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian costo.</div><b>κότα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hen, chicken (female adult Gallus gallus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>κότα</b> και ο κόκορας ζευγαρώνουν. - The hen and the rooster are mating.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) chicken, coward (person who lacks courage)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη φοβάσαι, μωρή <b>κότα</b>. Δε θα σου κάνει τίποτα το σκυλί. - Don&#x27;t be afraid, you chicken. The dog won&#x27;t do anything (bad) to you.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, derogatory) ditz, airhead, bimbo (feather-brained or pretentious woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα τη φοράει αυτή η <b>κότα</b>! - Look what that bimbo is wearing!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From feminine form of Ancient Greek κόττος. Doublet of κουτός.</div><b>κότερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κότερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cutter, yacht</div><b>κότσια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of κότσι</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (familiar) guts, nerve, balls (courage; determination)</div><b>κότσυφας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κότσυφες, feminine κοτσυφίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blackbird, Turdus merula</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek κόσσυφος</div><b>κόττα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόττες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κότα">κότα</a></div><b>κόφτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόφτες, feminine κόφτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κόπτης">κόπτης</a></div><b>κόφτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κόφτριες, masculine κόφτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κόπτρια">κόπτρια</a></div><b>κόχλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κόχλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) eyeliner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic كحل.</div><b>κόψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κόψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the edge, view of a sword</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κόψις</div><b>κύβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κύβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cube</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κύβος.</div><b>κύημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the foetus, embryon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κύημα.</div><b>κύηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gestation, pregnancy, gravidity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek κύη(σις), “pregnancy”) + modern ending -ση (-si), with ancient sense &quot;conception&quot;.</div><b>κύκλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κύκλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. series, cycle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. circle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. period, cycle</div>
  <div class='def'>4. circuit, course, track</div>
  <div class='def'>5. course</div>
  <div class='def'>6. turnover</div>
  <div class='def'>7. clique, coterie, circle, set (of people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κύκλος.</div><b>κύκλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κυκλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. circuit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. network</div>
  <div class='def'>3. circle, ring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> κύκλος + -ωμα (-oma)</div><b>κύκνεια άσματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of κύκνειο <a href="bword://άσμα">άσμα</a></div><b>κύκνειο άσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κύκνεια άσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swan song (a final performance)</div><b>κύκνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κύκνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swan, Cygnus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κύκνος.</div><b>κύλινδρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κύλινδροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cylinder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κύλινδρος.</div><b>κύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wave, breaker, roller</div>
  <div class='ex'>παλιρροϊκό <b>κύμα</b> - tidal wave</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) surge, wave, influx</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το δεύτερο <b>κύμα</b> της πανδημίας Covid-19 πλήττει με μεγάλη σφοδρότητα τη χώρα μας. - The second wave of the COVID-19 pandemic is hitting our country extremely hard.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κῦμα. Cognate with Mariupol Greek ки́ма.</div><b>κύμβαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κύμβαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cymbal, cymbals</div><b>κύμινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cumin (spice)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κύμινον</div><b>κύπελλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κύπελλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cup, bowl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. goblet (drinking vessel)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trophy cup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κύπελλον.</div><b>κύρια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://κύριος">κύριος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://κύριος">κύριος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mainly</div><b>κύρια άρθρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of κύριο <a href="bword://άρθρο">άρθρο</a></div><b>κύρια πιάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of κύριο <a href="bword://πιάτο">πιάτο</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of κύριο <a href="bword://πιάτο">πιάτο</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of κύριο <a href="bword://πιάτο">πιάτο</a></div><b>κύρια σελίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κύριες σελίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>home page (literally, “initial page”)</div><b>κύριε</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vocative singular of <a href="bword://κύριος">κύριος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Sir!, Boss!</div>
  <div class='ex'>—«Μου τα υπογράφετε, σας παρακαλώ, <b>κύριε</b>;»
—«Κάνε μου μια χάρη. Μη με αποκαλείς <b>κύριε</b>, τουλάχιστον όταν είμαστε μόνοι.» - —&quot;Won&#x27;t you sign, please, Sir?&quot;
—&quot;Do me a favor. Do not call me Sir, at least when we&#x27;re alone.&quot;</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) see: Κύριε</div><b>κύριο άρθρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κύρια άρθρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leader, lead story, leading article</div><b>κύριο πιάτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κύρια πιάτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>main course</div><b>κύριο όνομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proper noun</div><b>κύριος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κύριοι, feminine κυρία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mister (title conferred on an adult male)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. master (someone who has control over something or someone)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sir (an address to any male)</div>
  <div class='ex'>formal spelling: Κύριος (Kýrios)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κύριος (noun and adjective).
For the &quot;mister (term of address)&quot; sense, from Koine Greek κύριος, and a semantic loan from French monsieur or Italian signore.
Compare Mariupol Greek ки́риос.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine κύρια, neuter κύριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>main, principal, most important</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>κύριο</b> άρθρο εφημερίδας - main article of a newspaper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κύριος (noun and adjective).
For the &quot;mister (term of address)&quot; sense, from Koine Greek κύριος, and a semantic loan from French monsieur or Italian signore.
Compare Mariupol Greek ки́риос.</div><b>κύρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) influence due to authority</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) prestige, good reputation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) reliability, validity</div>
  <div class='ex'>δίχως επιστημονικό <b>κύρος</b> - without scientific validity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κῦρος, semantic loan from French autorité.</div><b>κύρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κυρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ratification</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sanction (an approval, by an authority, generally one that makes something valid)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sanction, penalty (punishment or coercive measure intended to ensure compliance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>διοικητική <b>κύρωση</b> - administrative sanction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κύρωσις with semantic loan from English sanction. By surface analysis, κυρώ(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>κύστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κύστεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cavity, pouch, bag, sac</div>
  <div class='ex'>ουροδόχος <b>κύστη</b> - urinary bladder</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cyst (sac which develops in the natural cavities of an organ)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κύστις.</div><b>κύτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κύτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vessel (vase, jug, ewer, urn)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hold (of ship or aeroplane)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κύτος.</div><b>κύτταρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κύτταρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cell (basic unit of a living organism)</div><b>κώδικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κώδικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (countable) code</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (countable) codex (an early manuscript book)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) source code</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek κῶδιξ, ultimately from Latin codex.</div><b>κώδικας Μορς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Morse code</div><b>κώδικας Μπράιγ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) braille</div><b>κώδων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κώδωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bell)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek κώδων.</div><b>κώλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural κώλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, mildly, vulgar) bumhole, butthole, arsehole, asshole (the anus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπήκε μια κάμερα στον <b>κώλο</b> του για να κάνει εξέταση. - A camera went up his bumhole to examine.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, informal) arse, ass, bum, bottom (the buttocks)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φόρεσε κάτι πιο σεμνό, φαίνεται ο <b>κώλος</b> σου. - Wear something more modest, your arse is showing.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) seat (of clothes)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>κώλος</b> του παντελονιού θέλει ράψιμο. - The seat of the trousers needs stitching.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, figuratively) rear (of a vehicle)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial, figuratively) hole (in anything)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>κώλος</b> της βελόνας - the eye of a needle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κῶλον.</div><b>κώλος και βρακί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, familiar, idiomatic, indeclinable) hand in glove (very close, very intimate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι δυο τους είναι <b>κώλος και βρακί</b> από τα παιδικά τους χρόνια. - The two of them have been hand in glove from their childhood.</div><b>κώμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) coma</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek κῶμα.</div><b>κώφωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural κωφώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deafness</div><b>λ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://λέξη">λέξη</a>: word</div><b>λ.χ.</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>e.g., for example</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from λόγου χάρη</div><b>λ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Λ΄">Λ΄</a></div><b>λάβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lava (molten rock)</div><b>λάβαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάβαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. banner, standard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flag</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek λάβαρον, from Latin labarum.</div><b>λάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. olive oil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek (ἐ)λάδιν ((e)ládin), from Ancient Greek ἐλᾴδιον, a diminutive form of ἔλαιον, from ἐλαία.
Compare Mariupol Greek ладъ.</div><b>λάθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>error, mistake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek λάθος, with stem λαθ-, from the aorist of Ancient Greek λανθάνω, from *leh₂- (“to be hidden, be covered”).</div><b>λάιμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lime</div><b>λάλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) honest friend</div><b>λάλλαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) heat, heatwave</div><b>λάμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λάμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. metal sheet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fine chisel</div>
  <div class='def'>3. blade</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (indeclinable) llama</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (indeclinable) lama, Tibetan Buddhist leader</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin lamina (“sheet”).</div><b>λάμβδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λάμδα">λάμδα</a></div><b>λάμδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lambda, the 11th letter of the Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λᾰ́βδᾰ.</div><b>λάμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λάμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lamp, electric or oil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. light bulb</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian lampa (“lamp”), from Latin lampas (“lamp”), from Ancient Greek λαμπάς, from Ancient Greek λάμπω.</div><b>λάμπαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) milk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic لبن (laban).</div><b>λάμπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λάμπω.</div><b>λάμψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λάμψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gleam, glow, shine, glint</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) glow (as of a healthy face)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) radiation (of a geographical culture, outwards)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek λάμψις.</div><b>λάπαθο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάπαθα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λάπατο">λάπατο</a></div><b>λάπατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάπαθα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sorrel</div><b>λάρικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λάρικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>larch (Larix)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek λάριξ.</div><b>λάρυγγας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λάρυγγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>larynx</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek λάρυγξ.</div><b>λάσπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λάσπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mud, silt, mire</div>
  <div class='def'>2. slime</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mortar, cement</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) depression</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) corruptness, turpitude</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λάσπη, of unclear origin. Has been connected to Koine Greek ἑλεσπίς, Ancient Greek θλᾰ́σπῐς, and λάπη. It appears feasible that the Modern term originated as a colloquial blend of these aforementioned terms, in particular, an aphetic form of ἑλεσπίς influenced by the vowel pattern of θλᾰ́σπῐς.</div><b>λάστιχο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάστιχα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rubber</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tyre (UK), tire (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hose</div>
  <div class='def'>4. elastic</div>
  <div class='def'>5. condom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Repatriated loanword from Italian elastico (“elastic (band)”) with loss of initial ε (e) and consonant shift κ (k) &gt; χ (ch), perhaps via influence from folk Tuscan dialects. Doublet of ελαστικός; see also English elastic.</div><b>λάτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>house cleaning</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λατρεύω.</div><b>λάτρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λάτρεις, feminine λάτρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. male worshipper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) lover</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λάτρης</b> του αθλητισμού - sports lover</div><b>λάτρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λάτρισσες, masculine λάτρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female worshipper</div><b>λάφτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λαούτο">λαούτο</a></div><b>λάφυρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάφυρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. item of loot or plunder</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) spoils, spoils of war</div><b>λάχανα βρυξελλών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://λάχανο">λάχανο</a> βρυξελλών</div><b>λάχανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάχανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cabbage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λάχανον.</div><b>λάχανο βρυξελλών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λάχανα βρυξελλών)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Brussels sprout, sprout</div><b>λέαινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λέαινες, masculine λέων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lioness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) brave woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λέαινα λέων, λε(οντ)- m-, “lion”) + -αινα (-aina).</div><b>λέβητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λέβητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boiler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λέβης.</div><b>λέγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be called</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς <b>λέγεστε</b>; - What are you called?</div><b>λέγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λέω">λέω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λέγω (λέγω, sense: &#x27;say&#x27;).</div><b>λέιζερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) laser</div><b>λέμβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λέμβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dinghy, small boat, rowing boat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passenger basket of balloon, airship gondola</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λέμβος.</div><b>λέμφωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεμφώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lymphoma</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Α calque of French lymphome.</div><b>λέξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λέξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>word, term (linguistic unit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λέξις.</div><b>λέπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scale (of fish or reptile)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flake (of skin)</div><b>λέσχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λέσχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. club (association of members)</div>
  <div class='ex'>φοιτητική <b>λέσχη</b> - (university)-students&#x27; club</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gambling club</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λέσχη in sense &quot;public building as meeting place&quot;. Cognate words with stems λεχ-, λοχ-: see: λεχώνα, λοχεία, λόχος, and λόχμη.</div><b>λέτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) letter, document</div><b>λέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to say, tell</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο παιδί <b>είπε</b> την πρώτη του λέξη. - The child said his first word.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to discuss, converse</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>λένε</b> μεταξύ τους. - They are discussing.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to talk (with one another)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Καιρό έχουμε να τα <b>πούμε</b>. - It&#x27;s been a while since we talked.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to recite, tell, recount, sing (a poem, song, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το παιδάκι <b>είπε</b> ένα τραγούδι. - The child sang a song.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to suppose, imagine (a hypothetical scenario)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λέμε</b> τώρα, αν γινόταν πόλεμος. - We&#x27;re supposing now, if there were a war.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (with <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia) ) refer to, talk about</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λες</b> για τον φίλο σου τώρα; - Are you talking about your friend now?</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (transitive) to mean, say (to clarify etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω να <b>πω</b> ότι δεν είναι τόσο απλά τα πράγματα. - I mean that things aren&#x27;t that simple.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (figuratively, intransitive) to remind of, mean something to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το όνομα δε μου <b>λέει</b> τίποτα. - That name means nothing to me.</div>
  <div class='def'>9. (figuratively, intransitive) to be any good, be worth anything</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λέει</b> τίποτα αυτό το κομπιούτερ; - Is this computer any good?</div>
  <div class='def'>10. (intransitive) to suggest, advise</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λέω</b> να πάμε μια βόλτα. - I say that we should go for a walk.</div>
  <div class='def'>11. (transitive) to call (name someone or something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με <b>λένε</b> Γιώργο. - I am called Giorgos.</div>
  <div class='def'>12. used with δεν (den), indicates something is slow to come</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η μέρα δε <b>λέει</b> να τελειώσει. - This day doesn&#x27;t want to end.</div>
  <div class='def'>13. (with να (na) ) think (something will happen)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λες</b> να μας προδώσει; - Do you think he&#x27;ll betray us?</div>
  <div class='def'>14. (colloquial, transitive) to read, explain (fortell using cards etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου <b>είπε</b> τον καφέ. - He/she told me the coffee. (phrase used to describe Turkish coffee fortune telling)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λέγω, with suppletive inflections such as the simple past είπα deriving from the aorist εἶπον, εἶπα.</div><b>λήγουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λήγουσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ult (last syllable of a word)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nominalised (by ellipsis of συλλαβή) present participle of λήγω.</div><b>λήγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to terminate, end, expire</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αγώνας <b>έληξε</b> ισόπαλος. - The match ended in a draw.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to end</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα ρήματα που <b>λήγουν</b> σε -ίζω γράφονται με γιώτα. - Verbs ending in -ízo are written with an iota.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λήγω.</div><b>λήθη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) oblivion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek λήθη.</div><b>λήμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λήμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lemma (the chief form of a word found in dictionaries and encyclopedias)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το λεξικό μου έχει πάνω από 80.000 <b>λήμματα</b>. - My dictionary has over 80,000 entries.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lemma (proved theorem used in the proof of another)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek λῆμμᾰ, from *λῆβ-μα. For stem ληβ-, see verb λαμβάνω.</div><b>λήξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) expiration</div>
  <div class='ex'>ημερομηνία <b>λήξης</b> - expiration date</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) termination, end</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο διαιτητής σφύριξε τη <b>λήξη</b> του αγώνα. - The referee signalled the end of the game.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λῆξις.</div><b>λήψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. taking, making (of decisions)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. taking, adoption (of measures)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. taking (of medication)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. receipt (of a communication, message, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. taking (of photographs)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. filming, shooting (of video)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. reception</div>
  <div class='def'>8. assumption</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λῆψις with -ψη (-psi) suffix.</div><b>λίαν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. very</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι ένα <b>λίαν</b> καλό. - It&#x27;s a very good thing.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. much</div>
  <div class='def'>3. exactly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λῑ́ᾱν.</div><b>λίβας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) the libecchio (a southwesterly Mediterranean wind)</div><b>λίβρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίβρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pound (unit of weight)</div><b>λίγκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) language</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian lingua.</div><b>λίγο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://λίγος">λίγος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://λίγος">λίγος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a little</div><b>λίγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λίγη, neuter λίγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a little, a few, a bit of</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα ήθελα <b>λίγη</b> ζάχαρη, παρακαλώ. - I would like a little sugar, please.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λίγος, form of ὀλίγος from the ancient Ancient Greek ὀλίγος, with omission of ⟨ο⟩, perceived as an article (ὁ). Also see ολίγος.</div><b>λίθινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λίθινη, neuter λίθινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stone, of stone</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λίθινη</b> Εποχή - Stone Age</div><b>λίθιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural λίθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) lithium</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From New Latin lithium.</div><b>λίθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural λίθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. masculine form:; stone (building material)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο θεμέλιος <b>λίθος</b> - the foundation stone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) masculine form:; stone, calculus</div>
  <div class='def'>3. masculine form:; Εποχή <a href="bword://του">του</a> Λίθου (“Stone Age”)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. feminine form (in compounds referring to special stones):; λυδία <a href="bword://λίθος">λίθος</a> (“touch stone”)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. feminine form (in compounds referring to special stones):; <a href="bword://φιλοσοφική">φιλοσοφική</a> <a href="bword://λίθος">λίθος</a> (“philosopher&#x27;s stone”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek λῐ́θος m or f.</div><b>λίμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. file, rasp</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nail file, emery board</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) synonym of <a href="bword://πείνα">πείνα</a>, extreme hunger, gluttony</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian lima.</div><b>λίμνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίμνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λίμνη.</div><b>λίμπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot, indeclinable) pumpkin seed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic لب (libb).</div><b>λίμπρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίμπρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λίβρα">λίβρα</a></div><b>λίπανση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιπάνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lubrication</div><b>λίπασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιπάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fertilizer</div>
  <div class='ex'>φυσικό <b>λίπασμα</b> - natural fertilizer</div><b>λίπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λίπη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fat, tallow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λίπος. For sense substance with fat, a semantic loan from French graisse or English fat.</div><b>λίρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pound, lira (various countries)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λίρα</b> στερλίνα - pound sterling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian lira.</div><b>λίστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>list</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λίστα</b> με τα ψώνια - shopping list</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian lista.</div><b>λίστα αναμονής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίστες αναμονής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>waiting list</div><b>λίστα επιθυμιών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίστες επιθυμιών)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wish list, bucket list</div><b>λίστες αναμονής</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://λίστα">λίστα</a> επιθυμιών f</div><b>λίστες επιθυμιών</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://λίστα">λίστα</a> επιθυμιών f</div><b>λίτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λίτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>litre (UK), liter (US)</div><b>λίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leerfish, garrick (Lichia, Lichia amia)</div><b>λα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) la</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) A (sixth note or submediant in the C major scale)</div><b>λαίδη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lady (British title held by women or the wife of a lord)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English lady.</div><b>λαίμαργος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λαίμαργη, neuter λαίμαργο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gluttonous, greedy (given to excessive eating; prone to overeating)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) gluttonous, greedy, voracious (having a great appetite for anything; eager)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λαίμαργος.</div><b>λαβή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. handle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hilt</div>
  <div class='def'>3. grip, handgrip, handhold, grasp</div><b>λαβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λαμβάνω">λαμβάνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek [Term?], from Ancient Greek λαμβάνω. Doublet of λαμβάνω.</div><b>λαβύρινθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαβύρινθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. maze, labyrinth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. labyrinth (part of inner ear)</div><b>λαγάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαγάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hare hunter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λαγός.</div><b>λαγίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαγίνες, masculine λαγός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λαγουδίνα">λαγουδίνα</a></div><b>λαγγόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαγγόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pygmy cormorant (Microcarbo pygmaeus)</div><b>λαγνεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lust, sexual desire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λαγνεία, derived from the verb λαγνεύω.</div><b>λαγοκοιμάμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sleep lightly, be in halfsleep</div><b>λαγοπόδαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) rabbit&#x27;s paw</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rabbit&#x27;s foot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λαγο- (lago-) + -πόδαρο</div><b>λαγουδίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαγουδίνες, masculine λαγός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female hare, doe hare</div><b>λαγούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>narrow underground tunnel, burrow (especially: as dug by a small creature)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish لغم. See it for more.</div><b>λαγούτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαγούτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λαούτο">λαούτο</a></div><b>λαγωνικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hound (dog)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> According to folk etymology, from Ancient Greek λακωνικός κύων. Most probably from Ancient Greek λαγώς. Compare also Albanian langua (“id.”).</div><b>λαγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαγοί, feminine λαγουδίνα or λαγίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hare, male hare</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jackrabbit (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λαγώς, from λαγωός, from Proto-Indo-European *(s)leg (“to droop, be slack, languid”) in the sense of drooping ears; see also Latin langueo and languidis, Ancient Greek [Term?] (“shrunken, slack”) and [Term?] (“lascivious, lustful”).</div><b>λαδάδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαδάδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, dated) oil factory, oil shop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λάδι + -άδικο</div><b>λαδάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαδάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. olive oil merchant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. oiler (person responsible for lubrication)</div><b>λαδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λαδιά, neuter λαδί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. olive (of color/colour); khaki (with a dull green or green-brown, the colour of olives)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. olive (of color/colour); greenish-yellow (with the colour of olive oil)</div><b>λαδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) olive (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indeclinable variant of <a href="bword://λαδής">λαδής</a> for all genders</div>
  <div class='def'>2. genitive/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://λαδής">λαδής</a></div><b>λαδερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαδερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oil dispenser (used in kitchens or at the table)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dish cooked with oil usually collectively in the plural: λαδερά</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter of adjective λαδερός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://λαδερός">λαδερός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://λαδερός">λαδερός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter of adjective λαδερός.</div><b>λαδερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λαδερή, neuter λαδερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oily, rich in oil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prepared with olive oil</div><b>λαδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oil stain</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πουκαμίσου ήταν γεμάτο <b>λαδιές</b>. - The shirt was full of oil stains.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. olive crop</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) deceit, trick (unethical or illegal act)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μάλλον έχει κάνει την <b>λάδια</b> του, εξού και το ένοχο ύφος. - He&#x27;s probably done his deceit, hence the guilty look.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λάδι + -ιά.</div><b>λαδικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαδικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oil dispenser (used in kitchens or at the table)</div><b>λαδόχαρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greaseproof paper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λάδι + χαρτί</div><b>λαζαρέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαζαρέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a leprosarium or quarantine hospital</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan lazareto (“leper hospital”); cf. Italian lazzaretto.</div><b>λαζαρέττο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαζαρέττα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less common spelling of <a href="bword://λαζαρέτο">λαζαρέτο</a></div><b>λαθρέμπορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαθρέμποροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smuggler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λαθραίος + έμπορος.</div><b>λαθραίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λαθραία, neuter λαθραίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. illegal, smuggled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stealthy, clandestine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λαθραῖος.</div><b>λαθρεμπόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (countable and uncountable, plural λαθρεμπόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) smuggling, trafficking</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (countable, uncountable) contraband</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, λαθραίος + εμπόριο.</div><b>λαιμαργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) gluttony</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) gluttony, greed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λαιμαργία.</div><b>λαιμαριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>collar (for animals)</div><b>λαιμητόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαιμητόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guillotine (for capital punishment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λαιμός + τομή.</div><b>λαιμοδέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαιμοδέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>necktie, tie</div><b>λαιμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαιμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neck, throat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λαιμός, probably a Pre-Greek word; compare λαῖπος, λαιφάσσω, and λαιφός.</div><b>λακκούβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λακκούβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pothole, puddle, hole road</div><b>λακτόζη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lactose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French lactose (“lactose”).</div><b>λακωνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to laconize; speak laconically, in a terse or brief manner; to speak with brevity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λακωνίζω. By surface analysis, Λᾰ́κων + -ίζω.</div><b>λακωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λακωνική, neuter λακωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Laconian, of or pertaining to Laconia</div>
  <div class='def'>2. laconic, concise, terse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. taciturn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Λακωνικός.</div><b>λαλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λαλώ">λαλώ</a></div><b>λαλημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine λαλημένη, neuter λαλημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sounded, spoken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of λαλούμαι and λαλιέμαι, passive forms of λαλώ and λαλάω respectively.</div><b>λαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. speech, voice</div>
  <div class='ex'>μου κόβερται η <b>λαλιά</b> - my voice has failed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. language</div>
  <div class='ex'>η ελληνική <b>λαλιά</b> - the Greek language</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek λαλιά</div><b>λαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to talk, speak</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of musical instruments) to sound</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, idiomatic) to go crazy, to lose one&#x27;s mind from exhaustion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λαλῶ, contracted form of λαλέω.</div><b>λαμαρίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαμαρίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sheet metal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) baking sheet, baking tin, baking pan</div><b>λαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to get, receive</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η τηλεόρασή μου δεν <b>λαμβάνει</b> σήμα πολύ καλά. - My tv set does not receive a signal very well.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to take</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to understand, get</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λαμβάνω. Doublet of λαβαίνω.</div><b>λαμιώδη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Lamiales, an order of plants.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Lamiales, as λάμιον + -ώδη.</div><b>λαμπραντόρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) labrador (dog)</div><b>λαμπροβούτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαμπροβούτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Gavia arctica : black-throated diver (UK), black-throated loon (US)</div><b>λαμπρυντικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαμπρυντικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rinse aid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λαμπρύνω.</div><b>λαμπρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bright, illuminated, light, luminous, lambent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λαμπρός.</div><b>λαμπρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brilliance, brilliancy (the quality of being exceptionally effulgent (giving off light))</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brightness, luminance</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) splendour (UK), splendor (US) (magnificent appearance, display or grandeur)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λᾰμπρότητᾰ, accusative singular of λᾰμπρότης. By surface analysis, λαμπρ(ός)) + -ότητα.</div><b>λαμπρύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to brighten, polish, beautify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to polish, pump up (someone&#x27;s image)</div><b>λανθάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lanthanum</div><b>λανθάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be latent, be dormant, be underlying</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek λανθάνω; doublet of λαθαίνω.</div><b>λανθάνων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λανθάνουσα, neuter λανθάνον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>latent, hidden, underlying</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λανθάνουσα</b> θερμότητα - latent heat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek λανθάνων, present active participle of λανθάνω.</div><b>λανθανίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lanthanides</div><b>λανθανίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λανθανίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lanthanide</div><b>λανθασμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>incorrectly, wrongly (in an incorrect manner)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λανθασμένος, from λάθος.</div><b>λανθασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine λανθασμένη, neuter λανθασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>incorrect, erroneous, wrong</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of λανθάνω, a morphologically incorrect participle formation based on the present tense.</div><b>λανθασμένως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>incorrectly, wrongly (in an incorrect manner)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λανθασμένος, from λάθος.</div><b>λαντάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαντάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lantana (Lantana)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Lantana.</div><b>λαογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural λαογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>folklorist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λαός + -γράφος. First attested 1891.</div><b>λαογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) folklore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λαός + -γραφία, calque of German Volkskunde. Modern sense has completely replaced the Koine Greek sense of &quot;census&quot;.</div><b>λαούτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαούτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laouto, lute (Greek or Cypriot instrument of the lute family)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan lauto.</div><b>λαπαροσκόπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαπαροσκοπήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laparoscopy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French laparoscopie, from Ancient Greek λαπάρα + -σκόπησις.</div><b>λαπαροσκόπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαπαροσκόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laparoscope</div><b>λαρδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαρδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lard, pork fat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λαρδί(ο)νn, “lard”), diminutive of Koine Greek λάρδος, from Latin lārdum/lāridum (“bacon fat”), perhaps borrowed from Ancient Greek λαρινός.</div><b>λαρυγγίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαρυγγίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laryngitis</div><b>λαρυγγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λαρυγγική, neuter λαρυγγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guttural, laryngeal, throaty</div><b>λασπωτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λασπωτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mudguard</div><b>λατέρνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λατέρνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barrel piano</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Latin lanterna (“lantern, torch”) likely through Ottoman Turkish [Term?], itself possibly through lanterna (“lighthouse, lantern”).</div><b>λατίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lateen sail</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small sailing boat with that rig</div><b>λατινικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Latin (language of the ancient Romans)</div><b>λατινοελληνικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greeklish</div><b>λατομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quarry</div><b>λατρεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adoration, loving devotion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. darling, the person whom one adores</div>
  <div class='def'>3. worship</div>
  <div class='def'>4. a cult</div><b>λατρευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λατρευτική, neuter λατρευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>devotional, adorational, of worship</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek λατρευτικός, from Koine Greek λατρευτικός.</div><b>λατρεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be adored, worshipped</div><b>λατρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to worship, adore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λατρεύω.</div><b>λατσολίθαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λατσολίθαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) diamond</div><b>λαχανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λαχανιά, neuter λαχανί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of lime green color/colour</div>
  <div class='ex'>also indeclinable λαχανί (lachaní) for all genders</div><b>λαχανί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indeclinable variant of <a href="bword://λαχανής">λαχανής</a> for all genders</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://λαχανής">λαχανής</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λαχαν(ής), “literally: in the colour of a cabbage”) + -ί</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) the lime green (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λαχαν(ής), “literally: in the colour of a cabbage”) + -ί</div><b>λαχανίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαχανίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greens, leaf vegetables</div><b>λαχανιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pant, gasp, puff, be out of breath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀναχανιάζω -also form ἀχανιάζω, with deletion of initial unstressed vowel and dissimilation of /n-n/ &gt; /l-n/. from the Hellenistic use of Ancient Greek ἀναχαίνω, form of ἀναχάσκω &lt; ἀνα- (ana-) + χάσκω + -ιάζω. Italiot Greek αναχαννίζω.</div><b>λαχανιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine λαχανιασμένη, neuter λαχανιασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gasping, panting, being out of breath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of λαχανιάζω, a verb without passive voice forms.</div><b>λαχανικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαχανικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vegetable (edible plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vegetables (in general); greens</div>
  <div class='def'>3. vegetables (in general); greenstuff</div>
  <div class='def'>4. vegetables (in general); herbs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λάχανο + the neuter form of -ικός.</div><b>λαχανόκηπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαχανόκηποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vegetable garden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λάχανο + κήπος.</div><b>λαχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lottery, lottery ticket, lottery prize</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) windfall (sudden large benefit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French loterie, using the Ancient Greek λαγχάνω</div><b>λαχούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λαχούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. A kind of embroidered silk or woolen fabric with a characteristic decorative pattern.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. A women&#x27;s shawl made of the above fabric.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish لاهوری, see there for more.</div><b>λαχτάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαχτάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. longing, yearning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. craving, hankering</div>
  <div class='def'>3. eager anticipation</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pang of anxiety</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λακτάρα.</div><b>λαϊκ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://λαϊκός">λαϊκός</a>: colloquial, informal</div><b>λαϊκά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>folk song, folk music</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://λαϊκός">λαϊκός</a></div><b>λαϊκή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λαϊκές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>street market</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λαϊκή</b> αγορά - street market</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://λαϊκός">λαϊκός</a></div><b>λαϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λαϊκή, neuter λαϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. popular, people&#x27;s (people’s) (relating to the people)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λαϊκή</b> Δημοκρατία της Κίνας - People&#x27;s Republic of China</div>
  <div class='def'>2. folk (customs, dance, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λαϊκά</b> τραγούδια - folk songs</div>
  <div class='def'>3. working class, lower class</div>
  <div class='def'>4. vulgar, cheap (goods, clothing, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. colloquial, common (words, language)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. lay, secular (not ecclesiastical, nonclergy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λᾱϊκός, from λᾱός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαϊκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>layman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λᾱϊκός, from λᾱός.</div><b>λαϊκός στρατός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαϊκοί στρατοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>people&#x27;s army</div><b>λαϊκότροπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λαϊκότροπη, neuter λαϊκότροπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. folksy (characteristic of the common people)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. folksy (relating to an informal vernacular)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, λαϊκός + τρόπος + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix).</div><b>λαός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λαοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>people, the mass of a community as distinguished from a special class (elite); the commonalty; the populace; the vulgar; the common crowd; the citizens.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λαός.</div><b>λγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://λοχαγός">λοχαγός</a></div><b>λεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prey</div>
  <div class='def'>2. booty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεία.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://λείος">λείος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://λείος">λείος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεία.</div><b>λείος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεία, neuter λείο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smooth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sleek</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεῖος.</div><b>λείπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be absent, to be missing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to lack</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έλειψαν</b> οι πρωτοβουλίες από τους αρμοδίους. - The authorities lacked the initiative.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λείπω.</div><b>λείψανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λείψανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. human remains, corpse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of food) leftovers, remains, leavings, scraps</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek λείψανον, from λείπω + -ανον (-anon)</div><b>λεβάντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεβάντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lavender (plant, flower and herb)</div><b>λεβάντες</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine singular</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the Levant, levanter (an easterly Mediterranean wind)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://Λεβάντες">Λεβάντες</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian levante + -ς (-s).</div><b>λεβέντης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λεβέντες or λεβέντηδες, feminine λεβέντισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. manly, strong young lad</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) brave (young) man</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (ironic) one with the fortitude to endure prison</div>
  <div class='ex'>Της φυλακής τα σίδερα είναι για τους λεβέντες. - The prison&#x27;s irons are for the brave.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λεβέντης, from Ottoman Turkish لوند (levend), from Classical Persian لوند (lawand)
A connection with Italian leventi is unlikely.</div><b>λεβαντίνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεβαντίνη, neuter λεβαντίνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Levantine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from Italian levante</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λεβαντίνοι, feminine λεβαντίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Levantine (a person from the Levant or of Levantine ethnicity).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from Italian levante</div><b>λεβεντιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεβεντιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the taken-together properties of a <a href="bword://λεβέντης">λεβέντης</a> m).</div><b>λεβιστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεβιστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lovage (herb)</div><b>λειαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to make smooth; to iron (cloth)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to make smooth; to sand, plane (wood)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to make smooth; to grind (to paste)</div><b>λειμώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λειμώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meadow, pasture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λειμών.</div><b>λειτουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λειτουργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. liturgy, Mass, Eucharist, divine service</div>
  <div class='def'>2. function, operation, service, working(s)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ώρες <b>λειτουργίας</b> - working hours</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λειτουργία, from λειτουργός + -ία.</div><b>λειτουργιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λειτουργιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Judaism) shewbread</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Eucharistic bread</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) liturgy</div><b>λειτουργικά συστήματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of λειτουργικό <a href="bword://σύστημα">σύστημα</a> (operating systems)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of λειτουργικό <a href="bword://σύστημα">σύστημα</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of λειτουργικό <a href="bword://σύστημα">σύστημα</a></div><b>λειτουργικό σύστημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λειτουργικά συστήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>operating system</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το Linux είναι δημοφιλές <b>λειτουργικό σύστημα</b>. - Linux is a popular operating system.</div><b>λειτουργικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λειτουργική, neuter λειτουργικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. operational, operating, running</div>
  <div class='def'>2. functional</div><b>λειτουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural λειτουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>public official</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λειτουργός.</div><b>λειτουργώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to function, to operate, to work</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to perform the Divine Liturgy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of churchgoers) to attend the Divine Liturgy</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of churches) to hold the Divine Liturgy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λειτουργῶ) with semantic loan from French fonctionner and the ecclesiastical senses borrowed learnedly from Koine Greek.</div><b>λειψανοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λειψανοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reliquary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λείψαν(ο) (“relic”) + -ο- + -θήκη (“case, holster”).</div><b>λειψυδρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λειψυδρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>water shortage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek λειψυδρία.</div><b>λειώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λιώνω">λιώνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See Etymology at λιώνω.</div><b>λεκάνες απορροής</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://λεκάνη">λεκάνη</a> απορροής</div><b>λεκάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεκάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. any large bowl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. washbasin, handbasin, sink</div>
  <div class='def'>3. toilet bowl, toilet pan</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pelvis</div>
  <div class='def'>5. basin, catchment (An area of water that drains into a river)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεκάνη.</div><b>λεκάνη απορροής</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drainage basin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λεκάνη + απορροή</div><b>λεκές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stain, splatter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish لكه (leke), from Classical Persian لکه (laka, lakka).</div><b>λεκιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dirty, to smear, to stain</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λέκιασε</b> την υπόληψη του. - It tarnished his reputation.</div><b>λεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεκτική, neuter λεκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. verbal (of or relating to words)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. verbal (concerned with the words, rather than the substance of a text)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. verbal (consisting of words only)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λεκτική</b> επικοινωνία - verbal communication</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λεκτικός. By surface analysis, λέγω + -τικός.</div><b>λεμονάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεμονάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lemonade (non-carbonated variety)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan łimonada, with the /e/ taken from λεμόνι.</div><b>λεμονής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεμονιά, neuter λεμονί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) of lemon (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λεμόν(ι), “lemon”) + -ής suffix for colours</div><b>λεμονί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lemon (color; especially a very bright tone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>— Ποιο είναι το αγαπημένο σου χρώμα;
— To <b>λεμονί</b>. Μ’ αρέσουν όλα τα κίτρινα και τα <b>λεμονί</b>. Τα αγαπημένα μου είναι τα λεμονιά. (χρώματα) - &quot;What is your favourite colour?&quot;
&quot;Lemon. I like all yellows and lemon. My favourites are the lemon&quot; (colours).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λεμόν(ι)) + -ί.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. indeclinable form of <a href="bword://λεμονής">λεμονής</a> for all genders</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω πολλές <b>λεμονί</b> μπλούζες και δύο <b>λεμονί</b> παντελόνια. Εσύ έχεις πολλές λεμονιές φούστες και μερικά λεμονιά παντελόνια. - I have lots of lemon⁽ᶠᵉᵐⁱⁿⁱⁿᵉ⁾ blouses and two lemon^((neuter) You have many lemon(feminine)) skirts and some lemon⁽ⁿᵉᵘᵗᵉʳ⁾ trousers.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://λεμονής">λεμονής</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λεμόν(ι)) + -ί.</div><b>λεμονίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεμονίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lemonade (carbonated variety with real lemon juice)</div><b>λεμούριος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λεμούριοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lemur</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English lemur, from Latin lemures.</div><b>λεμόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεμόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lemon (fruit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lemon yellow, lemon (colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λεμόνι from the form λιμόνι, from Italian limone, from Arabic لَيْمُون, from Classical Persian لیمو.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek лемо́н.</div><b>λενινισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural λενινισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) Leninism</div><b>λεξικογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lexicography</div><b>λεξικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεξικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dictionary, lexicon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek λεξικόν.</div><b>λεξιλογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεξιλογική, neuter λεξιλογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lexic, lexical</div><b>λεξιλόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεξιλόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vocabulary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. glossary</div>
  <div class='def'>3. terminology</div><b>λεξιπλασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεξιπλασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nonce word</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> (neologism, end of the 20th century) From λεξι- (“lexi-, of a word”) + -πλασία (“-plasia (mainly medical term)”). Literally: word-making.</div><b>λεξούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεξούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>little word</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λέξη + -ούλα</div><b>λεοντάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεοντάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λιοντάρι">λιοντάρι</a></div><b>λεοντή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεοντές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lionskin</div><b>λεοπάρδαλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεοπαρδάλεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leopard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεόπαρδος, from λέων + πάρδος, from earlier πάρδαλις.</div><b>λεπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεπίδα, the accusative singular of λεπίς.</div><b>λεπρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεπρή, neuter λεπρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leprous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leper (when used as a noun)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεπρός.</div><b>λεπτά πριόνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://λεπτό">λεπτό</a> <a href="bword://πριόνι">πριόνι</a></div><b>λεπτοδείκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λεπτοδείκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minute hand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from λεπτ(ό)) + -ο- (-o-) + δείκτης, a calque of German Minutenzeiger.</div><b>λεπτομέρεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεπτομέρειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>detail</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δείτε παρακάτω όλες τις <b>λεπτομέρειες</b>. - Look below for all the details.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek λεπτομέρεια, from Ancient Greek λεπτομερής, from λεπτός + μέρος.</div><b>λεπτό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://λεπτός">λεπτός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://λεπτός">λεπτός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεπτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. minute (unit of time equal to sixty seconds)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περίμενε δύο <b>λεπτά</b>, παρακαλώ. - Please wait a moment.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) lepton (hundredth of a drachma, or an ancient coin)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cent (hundredth of a euro)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα ρέστα σας είναι σαράντα <b>λεπτά</b>. - Your change is forty cents.</div><b>λεπτό έντερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) small intestine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λεπτός + έντερο.</div><b>λεπτό πριόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεπτά πριόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jigsaw (fine/thin power saw)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λεπτός + πριόνι</div><b>λεπτό χαρτί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tissue paper</div><b>λεπτόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεπτόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lepton</div><b>λεπτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεπτή, neuter λεπτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thin, skinny, slim</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flimsy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. delicate, refined</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεπτός.</div><b>λερώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to become dirty, to stain oneself</div><b>λερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dirty, stain, besmirch</div><b>λες και</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as if, as though, like</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περπατούσε <b>λες και</b> είχε χαλίκια στο παπούτσι. - She was walking as if she had pebbles in her shoe.</div><b>λεσβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεσβίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lesbian, gay woman</div><b>λεσβιάζουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεσβιάζουσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lesbian, gay woman</div><b>λεσβιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, intransitive, vulgar) to turn lesbian, become lesbian</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Τάνια πράγματι <b>λεσβίασε</b>; - Did Tanya really go gay?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεσβῐάζω, from Λέσβος + -άζω.</div><b>λετονικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Latvian, Lettish (the language of Latvia)</div><b>λετονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λετονική, neuter λετονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Latvian, Lett (of Latvia, its people or its language)</div><b>λετσόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λετσόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) lesson</div><b>λεττονικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λετονικά">λετονικά</a></div><b>λεττονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεττονική, neuter λεττονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λετονικός">λετονικός</a></div><b>λευκίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leucine</div><b>λευκαδίτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pertaining to the island of Lefkada or to Leucadians</div><b>λευκαντικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λευκαντικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bleach</div><b>λευκοί νάνοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://λευκός">λευκός</a> <a href="bword://νάνος">νάνος</a></div><b>λευκοπίνακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λευκοπίνακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whiteboard (white board that can be written upon with marker)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο μαθητής γράφει με μαρκαδόρο στον <b>λευκοπίνακα</b>. - The student writes with a marker on the whiteboard.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λευκο- (lefko-, “white”) + πίνακας, calque of English whiteboard.</div><b>λευκορωσικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Belarusian (the language of Belarus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Λευκορωσία + -ικός</div><b>λευκορωσικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λευκορωσική, neuter λευκορωσικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Belarusian (relating to Belarus, its people or language)</div><b>λευκοσίδηρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) tin-plate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, uncountable) tin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λευκός + σίδηρος</div><b>λευκοτσικνιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λευκοτσικνιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>little egret</div><b>λευκωματίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λευκωματίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>albumin</div><b>λευκό αιμοσφαίριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λευκά αιμοσφαίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leukocyte, white blood cell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λευκό + αιμοσφαίριο</div><b>λευκόιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λευκόια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snowdrop (plant, flower)</div><b>λευκόουρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine λευκόουρη, neuter λευκόουρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>white-tailed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λευκόουρο</b> ελάφι - white-tailed deer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, λευκός + ουρά + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix).</div><b>λευκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λευκή, neuter λευκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) white</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λευκό</b> μάρμαρο, σύννεφο, χαμόγελο - white marble, cloud, smile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in set phrases) see Expressions and see Λευκός</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>Λευκός</b> Οίκος βρίσκεται στην 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue. - The White House is located at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of animals) pure, blank, clean</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λευκός</b> καρχαρίας - white shark</div>
  <div class='def'>4. see <a href="bword://λευκός">λευκός</a> as noun</div>
  <div class='def'>5. and see λευκό</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λευκός, from Proto-Indo-European *lewk- (“white; light; bright”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λευκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a white person</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φυλετικές διακρίσεις των <b>λευκών</b> εναντίον των μαύρων. - Racial discriminations of the white against the black.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λευκός, from Proto-Indo-European *lewk- (“white; light; bright”).</div><b>λευκός νάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λευκοί νάνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>white dwarf</div><b>λευκόχρυσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) platinum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λευκός + χρυσός</div><b>λευτερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://ελευθερώνω">ελευθερώνω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (common, literary) to give birth (of women)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λευτερώνω, ἐλευτερώνω, λευθερώνω, from Ancient Greek ἐλευθερόω. Doublet of ελευθερώνω. By surface analysis, λεύτερος + -ώνω.</div><b>λευχαιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λευχαιμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leukemia, leukaemia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French leucémie, from Ancient Greek λευκός + αἷμα.</div><b>λεφτάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λεφτάδες, feminine λεφτού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rich person, plutocrat, moneybags</div><b>λεφτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεφτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://λεπτό">λεπτό</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, rare) money, usually in the plural λεφτά</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See λεπτό.</div><b>λεχώνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a woman who has just given birth or, at most, fortysome days ago</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λεχώνα, from Ancient Greek λεχώ.</div><b>λεωφορείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεωφορεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bus, omnibus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λεώς, an archaic Attic variant of λαός, and φορείο from the verb φέρω. First coined in 1863 in order to translate the French bus or omnibus. The choice of the obsolete variant λεώς instead of the common λαός was due to the Ancient Greek word λεωφόρος of the same etymology still being in use.</div><b>λεωφορειούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λεωφορειούχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bus owner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λεωφορείο + -ούχος</div><b>λεωφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεωφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wide city street or highway</div>
  <div class='def'>2. avenue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λεωφόρος, clipping of λεωφόρος ὁδός &quot;people-leading street&quot;, thus of feminine gender.</div><b>λεϊμονιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεϊμονιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of λεμονιά</div><b>λεϊμόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λεϊμόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λεμόνι">λεμόνι</a></div><b>λεόπαρδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λεόπαρδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obsolete form of <a href="bword://λεοπάρδαλη">λεοπάρδαλη</a> (leopard)</div><b>λεύκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λεύκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poplar (tree)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Ancient Greek λεύκη.</div><b>λεύκουρη καλημάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>white-tailed lapwing, white-tailed plover (Vanellus leucurus)</div><b>λεύκουρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεύκουρη, neuter λεύκουρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>white-tailed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λευκός + οὐρά.</div><b>λεύκωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λευκώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. album (for photographs, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. albumen, egg white</div>
  <div class='def'>3. protein</div>
  <div class='def'>4. proteinuria (colloquial)</div><b>λεύτερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λεύτερη, neuter λεύτερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ελεύθερος">ελεύθερος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λεύτερος, from forms ἐλεύτερος, λεύθερος, from Ancient Greek ἐλεύθερος with omission of unaccented first vowel /e/ and dissimilation of [fθ] &gt; [ft]. Compare to ελεύθερος.</div><b>ληξιαρχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ληξιαρχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>civil register office</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ληξιαρχείον, from λῆξις (&lt; λαγχάνω) + ἀρχεῖον (&lt; ἄρχω).</div><b>ληξιπρόθεσμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ληξιπρόθεσμη, neuter ληξιπρόθεσμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mature</div><b>λησμονάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λησμονώ">λησμονώ</a></div><b>λησμονιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λησμονιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>state of forgetfulness, oblivion</div><b>λησμονιούνται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person plural present of λησμονιέμαι</div>
  <div class='def'>2. third-person plural subjunctive of λησμονιέμαι</div><b>λησμονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, literary, poetic) to forget, be forgetful</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη παρακαλώ σας, μη
<b>Λησμονάτε</b> τη χώρα μου! - Please, oh please don&#x27;t
forget my country!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek λησμονῶ, from λήσμων, from λάθμων, from verb λανθάνω</div><b>ληστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ληστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. robber, thief, bandit, burglar (one who steals another person&#x27;s property by force)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αδίστακτοι <b>ληστές</b> εδειραν και λήστεψαν 90-χρονη. - Ruthless thieves beat and robbed a 90-year-old.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) profiteering</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (historical) highwayman, bandit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα βουνά ήταν κρησφύγετα των <b>ληστών</b>. - The mountains were hiding places of the robbers.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λῃστής / ληϊστής, from λεία.</div><b>ληστεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ληστείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>robbery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λῃστείᾱ.</div><b>ληστεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to rob, mug</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fleece, cheat</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to pillage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λῃστεύω.</div><b>λιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dry in the sun</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sunbathe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἡλιάζω.</div><b>λιάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>liana (vine)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English liana, from French liane.</div><b>λιακάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιακάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sunshine, sun (the light and warmth which is received from the sun, especially in spring, autumn or winter)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λιακό + -άδα, from Byzantine Greek ἡλιακόν, itself a neuter substantive from Koine Greek ἡλῐᾰκός.</div><b>λιακωτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιακωτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. solarium, sun lounge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. suntrap, sunny spot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἡλιακόν + suffix -ωτό, the former a neuter substantive of Koine Greek ἡλῐᾰκός.</div><b>λιανεμπόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιανεμπόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>retail outlet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λιανικός + εμπόριο.</div><b>λιανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιανική, neuter λιανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>retail</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>λιανικός</b> κλάδος κατέγραψε στο σύνολο του απώλειες 2% στον S&amp;P 500. - The retail sector recorded, in total, a loss of 2% on the S&amp;P 500.</div><b>λιβάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιβάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meadow, pasture, lea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λιβάδιον.</div><b>λιβάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιβάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>incense, frankincense</div><b>λιβανέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιβανέζικη, neuter λιβανέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lebanese (related to the country, people or Lebanon)</div><b>λιβανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιβανική, neuter λιβανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lebanese (related to the country, people or Lebanon)</div><b>λιβελούλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιβελούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dragonfly</div><b>λιβεριανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιβεριανή, neuter λιβεριανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Liberian (of or pertaining to Liberia or its people)</div><b>λιβερμόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) livermorium</div><b>λιβυκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιβυκή, neuter λιβυκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Libyan (relating to Libya or its people)</div><b>λιγνίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιγνίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lignin</div><b>λιγνιτωρυχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιγνιτωρυχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lignite mine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from λιγνίτης + ορυχείο.</div><b>λιγοστεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to reduce, decrease, lessen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λιγοστεύω, form of ὀλιγοστεύω, from ὀλιγοστός) + -εύω.</div><b>λιγοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιγοστή, neuter λιγοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scarce, meagre, short</div>
  <div class='ex'>κι είναι καιρός να πούμε τα λιγοστά μας λόγια γιατί η ψυχή μας αύριο κάνει πανιά. - and it&#x27;s time to say our few words because tomorrow our soul sets sail.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λιγοστός, form of ὀλιγοστός from the Ancient Greek ὀλιγοστός, with omission of initial ⟨ο⟩, perceived as the article ὁ (ho). Also see λίγος.</div><b>λιγούστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>privet, ligustrum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin ligustrum.</div><b>λιδοκαΐνη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) lidocaine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English lidocaine.</div><b>λιθογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιθογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lithography (method of print creation using metal or stone)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lithograph (print produced using lithography)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lithography (technical method for industrial printing)</div><b>λιθουανικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lithuanian, the language of Lithuania.</div><b>λιθουανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιθουανική, neuter λιθουανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lithuanian (related to the country, people or language of Lithuania)</div><b>λιθρίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιθρίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λυθρίνι">λυθρίνι</a></div><b>λιθόστρωτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιθόστρωτa)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cobblestone, cobble</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://λιθόστρωτος">λιθόστρωτος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://λιθόστρωτος">λιθόστρωτος</a></div><b>λιθόστρωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιθόστρωτη, neuter λιθόστρωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cobbled, paved with cobblestones</div><b>λιθόσφαιρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lithosphere</div><b>λικέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) liqueur (sweet-flavoured alcoholic beverage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French liqueur.</div><b>λιλά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lilac, mauve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French lilas, from Old French lilac.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lilac, mauve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French lilas, from Old French lilac.</div><b>λιμάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιμάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. harbour, quay, port</div>
  <div class='ex'>ασφαλές <b>λιμάνι</b> - safe harbour</div>
  <div class='def'>2. port (town or city with a dock or harbour)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) safe harbor, safe haven</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Reborrowing from Ottoman Turkish لیمان (liman), or λιμνιώνας, diminutive λιμενάρι), from Hellenistic Koine Greek λῐμένιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek λῐμήν.</div><b>λιμένας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λιμένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, literary) port, dock, harbour</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οργανισμός <b>Λιμένος</b> Πειραιώς - Piraeus Port Authority</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λιμήν.</div><b>λιμένος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of λιμένα, genitive singular of <a href="bword://λιμένας">λιμένας</a> “of the port”</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οργανισμός <b>Λιμένος</b> Πειραιώς - Piraeus Port Authority</div><b>λιμενάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural λιμενάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harbour master (UK), harbormaster (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λιμενάρχης.</div><b>λιμεναρχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιμεναρχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A port authority, the administration of control over a port.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, λιμενάρχης + -είο.</div><b>λιμνάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stagnate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to become stagnant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λιμνάζω.</div><b>λιμνούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιμνούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pond</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) any small body of water</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λίμνη + -ούλα.</div><b>λιμοκτονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. famine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. starvation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λιμοκτονία. By surface analysis, λιμός + -κτονία.</div><b>λιμοκτονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to starve, famish, die of hunger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λῑμοκτονέω. By surface analysis, λιμός + κτείνω.</div><b>λιμουνάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λεμονάδα">λεμονάδα</a></div><b>λιμπίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of food) to fancy, crave, one&#x27;s mouth waters for (have an immense appetite for)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είδα την τούρτα και την <b>λιμπίστηκα</b>. - I saw the cake and my mouth watered for it.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of people) to fancy, lust after (have an immense sexual desire for)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παρά την ηλικία του, <b>λιμπίζεται</b> τις μικρούλες. - Despite his age, he lusts after young ones.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λιμβίζω, from Koine Greek λιμβός.</div><b>λιμπεραλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λιμπεραλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>liberalism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French libéralisme.</div><b>λιμπρετίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λιμπρετίστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>librettist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian librettista with -ς (-s) suffix. By surface analysis, λιμπρέτο + -ίστας.</div><b>λιμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λιμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>famine, starvation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λιμός.</div><b>λινάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λινάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flax (plant and fibres)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λινάριν, from Hellenistic Koine Greek λινάριον, diminutive of the ancient λίνον.</div><b>λινό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λινά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linen</div><b>λινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λινή, neuter λινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linen (made of flax fibres)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λινός, from Ancient Greek λινοῦς with metaplasm of the ending, contracted form of λίνεος, from λίνον.</div><b>λιοντάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιοντάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λιοντάρι(ν)), from earlier λεοντάριν, from Koine Greek λεοντάριον, diminutive of Ancient Greek λέων.</div><b>λιονταράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιονταράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lion cub, young lion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λιοντάρι + -άκι</div><b>λιονταρίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιονταρίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lioness</div><b>λιονταρόψαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιονταρόψαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lionfish (Pterois)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, λιοντάρι + ψάρι + -ο (-o, neuter suffix). Compare English lionfish.</div><b>λιοψημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιοψημένη, neuter λιοψημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ηλιοψημένος">ηλιοψημένος</a></div><b>λιπαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lubricate, oil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to manure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek λίπα.</div><b>λιπαντής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λιπαντές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greaser, oiler (worker or crew in charge of lubrication)</div><b>λιπαντικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιπαντικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lubricant</div><b>λιπαντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιπαντική, neuter λιπαντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lubricating</div><b>λιπαρά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://λιπαρός">λιπαρός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form. In the noun sense, a semantic loan from French matière grasse and English fat.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fat content</div>
  <div class='ex'>γιαούρτι με χαμηλά <b>λιπαρά</b> - low-fat yoghurt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form. In the noun sense, a semantic loan from French matière grasse and English fat.</div><b>λιπαρά οξέα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of λιπαρό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div><b>λιπαρό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιπαρά οξέα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fatty acid</div><b>λιπαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιπαρή, neuter λιπαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fatty, greasy, oily (containing oil or fat)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λιπαρό</b> δέρμα - greasy/oily skin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λιπαρός.</div><b>λιπιδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lipidic</div><b>λιποβαρής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιποβαρής, neuter λιποβαρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>underweight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λιπο- (lipo-) + βάρος + -ής.</div><b>λιποθυμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to faint, pass out</div><b>λιποθυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιποθυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. faint, syncope</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blackout</div><b>λιποθυμιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιποθυμιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λιποθυμία">λιποθυμία</a></div><b>λιποθυμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λιποθυμάω">λιποθυμάω</a></div><b>λιπόθυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιπόθυμη, neuter λιπόθυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>faint, lightheaded</div><b>λιπώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιπώδης, neuter λιπώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fatty, adipose, fat</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λιπώδης</b> ιστός - adipose tissue</div><b>λιτανεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λιτανείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>procession of supplication, litany</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λιτανείᾱ.</div><b>λιτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιτή, neuter λιτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. simple; frugal; economical; thrifty; spartan; no frills; no bells and whistles; not fancy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. laconic; terse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λῑτός.</div><b>λιτότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>austerity, frugality, frugalness, thrift</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λιτότης, equivalent to λιτός + -ότητα.</div><b>λιόγερμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιογέρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, literary) sunset</div><b>λιόδεντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιόδεντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>olive tree</div><b>λιόκαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιόκαλη, neuter λιόκαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beautiful, handsome (as the sun)</div><b>λιόκαμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιοκάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ηλιόκαμα">ηλιόκαμα</a></div><b>λιόφως</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ηλιόφως">ηλιόφως</a></div><b>λιόφωτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ηλιόφωτο">ηλιόφωτο</a></div><b>λιόφωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιόφωτοςη, neuter λιόφωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ηλιόφωτος">ηλιόφωτος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://λιόφως">λιόφως</a></div><b>λιόχαρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λιόχαρη, neuter λιόχαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ηλιόχαρος">ηλιόχαρος</a></div><b>λιόψωμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λιόψωμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ελιόψωμο">ελιόψωμο</a></div><b>λιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to melt (make something liquid by heat)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φάε το παγωτό σου πριν <b>λιώσει</b>. - Eat your ice cream before it melts.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, transitive) to dissolve (make something blend into liquid)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το χάπι <b>έλιωσε</b> στο ποτήρι. - The pill dissolved in the glass.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to crush, mash (reduce to a soft pulpy state by beating or pressure)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μηχανή για να <b>λιώσεις</b> σταφύλια. - A machine that crushes grapes.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to crush, destroy (completely eradicate someone/something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν δεν παραιτηθείς, θα σε <b>λιώσω</b>. - If you don&#x27;t give up, I will destroy you.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) to wear out (break down or destroy by overuse)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>λιώσει</b> πολλά παπούτσια. - I&#x27;ve worn out many shoes.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively, intransitive) to waste away (become very thin and weak through illness etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η καημένη η γιαγιά <b>έλιωσε</b> από την αρρώστια. - Poor Granny has been wasting away from the illness.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (figuratively, intransitive) to be exhausted (through overwork, exertion etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λιώνω</b> από την δουλειά τις περασμένες εβδομάδες. - I&#x27;m drowning in work these past few weeks.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (figuratively, intransitive) to burn up (usually through love or infatuation)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λιώνω</b> γι’ αυτόν αλλά αυτός ούτε με ξέρει. - I&#x27;m burning up over him but he doesn&#x27;t even known me.</div>
  <div class='def'>9. (of a corpse) to decompose, rot</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν <b>λιώσει</b> το σώμα, τότε θα κάνουμε την εκταφή των οστών. - When the body decomposes, then we&#x27;ll exhume the bones.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * Common spelling λιώνω, according to Evangelos Petrounias: From Byzantine Greek λιώνω from Ancient Greek λειῶ, from λεῖος, with simplification of spelling.</div><b>λοίμωξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λοιμώξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>infection</div>
  <div class='ex'>λοίμωξη του ουροποιητικού συστήματος (urinary tract infection)</div><b>λοίσθιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λοίσθια, neuter λοίσθιος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>at the end</div><b>λοβός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λοβοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lobe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λοβός m, from Proto-Indo-European *leh₂b- (“to hang loosely”).</div><b>λογάριθμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λογάριθμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>logarithm</div><b>λογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>charitable collection</div><b>λογίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be considered</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λογίζομαι (“reckon”).</div><b>λογίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογίστριες, masculine λογιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accountant, book-keeper</div><b>λογαριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to account for, to calculate, to reckon (to compute an account of; to make a reckoning of)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to count, to factor in (to reckon in; to include in a calculation)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to take account of, to take into account (to take into consideration)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to count, to reckon (to consider something as an example of something or as having some quality; to account, to regard as)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to plan [with να (na, + verb) ‘to do’] (to intend)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to be counted, to be reckoned (to be considered or esteemed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λογαριάζω, from Ancient Greek λογάρι(ον), diminutive of λόγος; also &quot;account&quot;) + -άζω.</div><b>λογαριασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λογαριασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bill (UK); tab, check (US) (account at restaurant, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορώ να έχω τον <b>λογαριασμό</b>; - Can I have the bill please?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. invoice, account</div>
  <div class='def'>3. account (at bank, with company, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. financial report</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λογαριασμός, from the aorist stem λογαριασ- of the verb λογαριάζω + -μός, the verb from Ancient Greek λογάριον + -ᾰ́ζω, from the neuter diminutive of masculine λόγος.</div><b>λογεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λογία">λογία</a></div><b>λογικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://λογικός">λογικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>logically</div><b>λογική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://λογικός">λογικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) logic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) common sense</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σκοντάφτουνε στη Λογική και στα ραβδιά των άλλων - They have to face the Common Sense and sticks of others</div><b>λογικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) reason, mind</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://λογικός">λογικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://λογικός">λογικός</a></div><b>λογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λογική, neuter λογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. logical, rational</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reasonable</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sensible (Acting with or showing good sense; able to make good judgements based on reason)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a sane person</div><b>λογισμικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λογισμικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>software</div><b>λογισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λογισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. calculation, calculus</div>
  <div class='ex'>see λογισμός on the Greek Wikipedia.Wikipedia ᵉˡ</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thought, reflection</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) concern, worry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> For sense &quot;thought, reflection&quot;, inherited from Ancient Greek λογισμός. See λογίζομαι and λόγος.
For mathematics, learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λογισμός and semantic loan from French calcul.</div><b>λογιστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λογιστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accounting department</div><b>λογιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λογιστές, feminine λογίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accountant, book-keeper</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εγώ δουλεύω σαν <b>λογιστής</b>. - I work as an accountant.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λογιστής, equivalent to λογ- (log-, “to calculate”) + -ιστής.</div><b>λογιστική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://λογιστικός">λογιστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) accounting</div><b>λογιστικό φύλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λογιστικά φύλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spreadsheet</div><b>λογιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λογιστική, neuter λογιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accounting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λογιστικός.</div><b>λογιότ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of λογιότερος: more learned</div><b>λογοδιάρροια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογοδιάρροιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) logorrhea, verbal diarrhea, garrulousness, uncontrollable chatter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek λογοδιάρροια. By surface analysis, λόγος, λογο- + διάρροια</div><b>λογοκλοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογοκλοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plagiarism, literary piracy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from λογοκλοπία, from (Koine) λογο- (logo-, “word”) + κλέπτω</div><b>λογοκρίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to censor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λογοκρισία f λογο(κρισία) + κρίνω.</div><b>λογοκρίτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογοκρίτριες, masculine λογοκριτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>censor</div><b>λογοκρισία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογοκρισίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>censorship</div><b>λογοκριτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λογοκριτές, feminine λογοκρίτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>censor</div><b>λογοπαίγνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λογοπαίγνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>play on words, wordplay, pun</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from λογο- (logo-) + Ancient Greek παίγνιον, a calque of French jeu de mots.</div><b>λογοτέχνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural λογοτέχνες, feminine λογοτέχνιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>writer, author</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λογοτέχνης, from Ancient Greek λογοτέχνης.</div><b>λογοτέχνιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογοτέχνιδες, masculine λογοτέχνης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female writer, author</div><b>λογοτεχνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λογοτεχνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. literature (written works)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. literature (the study of written works)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λογοτεχνία, from λογοτέχνης + -ία.</div><b>λογοτεχνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λογοτεχνική, neuter λογοτεχνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. literary (relating to literature)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. literary (relating to writers, or the profession of literature)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. literary (appropriate to literature rather than everyday writing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from λογοτεχν(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>λογχίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to spear, bayonet</div><b>λογχοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λογχοφόρα, neuter λογχοφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>armed with a spear or lance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λόγχη + -φόρος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λογχοφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spearman, lancer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λόγχη + -φόρος</div><b>λογότυπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λογότυπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λογότυπος">λογότυπος</a></div><b>λογότυπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λογότυποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>logo, logotype</div><b>λοιμοκαθαρτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λοιμοκαθαρτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quarantine hospital</div><b>λοιμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λοιμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>epidemic, plague</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Περικλής πέθανε στον «<b>Λοιμό</b> των Αθηνών», που το 430 σκότωσε πάνω από 75.000 Αθηναίους. - Pericles died during the Plague of Athens in 430 (BCE) which took the lives of more than 75,000 Athenians.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λοιμός.</div><b>λοιμώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λοιμώδης, neuter λοιμώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>infectious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λοιμώδης.</div><b>λοιπόν</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. so, therefore, right, well, then (introduces a logical conclusion)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχουμε λεφτά· <b>λοιπόν</b>, δεν θα πάμε διακοπές φέτος. - We have no money; so we won&#x27;t go on holiday this year.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. so, anyway (changing the subject or introducing a new topic)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λοιπόν</b>, ας περάσουμε στο επόμενο θέμα. - Anyway, let&#x27;s move on to the next topic.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) so, then</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λοιπόν</b>; - So what? / Well then?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek λοιπόν, from Ancient Greek λοιπός.</div><b>λοιπός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λοιπή, neuter λοιπό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>remaining, the rest of, other</div>
  <div class='ex'>και τα <b>λοιπά</b> - and the rest, et cetera</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λοιπός.</div><b>λοκ άουτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lockout</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English lockout.</div><b>λοκάντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λοκάντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) inn, restaurant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian locanda; now largely displaced by εστιατόριο, a learned borrowing from Koine Greek.</div><b>λοκατζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λοκατζήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commando</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Λ.Ο.Κ. (L.O.K.) + -τζής. Λ.Ο.Κ. is an acronym of Λόχοι Ορεινών Καταδρομών.</div><b>λολ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Internet) LOL</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English lol, written with Greek letters.</div><b>λονδρέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λονδρέζικη, neuter λονδρέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) London; of London, from London</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγε κόκκινο <b>λονδρέζικο</b> λεωφορείο. - He left on a red London bus.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Λονδρέζ(ος)) + -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>λοξοδρομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λοξοδρομίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhumb line, rhumb, loxodrome</div><b>λοξός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λοξή, neuter λοξό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slanting; sloping; oblique</div>
  <div class='def'>2. screwy; odd</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λοξός.</div><b>λοξότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λοξότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obliquity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λοξότης, equivalent to λοξός + -ότητα.</div><b>λορένσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λωρένσιο">λωρένσιο</a></div><b>λοστρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λοστρόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boatswain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian nostromo with dissimilation of consonants [n]-[m] &gt; [l]-[m], from Catalan nostramo or Spanish nuestramo &lt; nuestro (“our”) amo (“master”), used in 16th century slave galleys as &quot;overseer&quot;.</div><b>λουθηρανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λουθηρανές, masculine λουθηρανός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lutheran (a Christian of that persuasion)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://λουθηρανός">λουθηρανός</a></div><b>λουθηρανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λουθηρανική, neuter λουθηρανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lutheran</div><b>λουθηρανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λουθηρανή, neuter λουθηρανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lutheran</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λουθηρανοί, feminine λουθηρανή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lutheran (a Christian of that persuasion)</div><b>λουκάνικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λουκάνικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sausage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, sometimes) kit bag, duffel bag</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λουκάνικον, λουκάνικα, from Latin lūcānica.</div><b>λουκέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λουκέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>padlock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian lucchetto.</div><b>λουκανικόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λουκανικόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sausage roll, pigs in blankets (Greek pastry made with phyllo wrapped around a sausage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λουκάνικο + πίτα.</div><b>λουκουμάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λουκουμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loukoumas (a small doughnut, often flavoured with honey and cinnamon)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish لقمه (lukma, lokma), from Arabic لُقْمَة (luqma).</div><b>λουκούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λουκούμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turkish delight, locoum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish لوقوم (lokum), from Arabic رَاحَة الْحُلْقُوم al-ḥulqūm, “throat comfort”).</div><b>λουλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λουλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bluing for whitening, laundry blue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λουλάκιν, from Arabic لِيلَك, from Classical Persian لیلک.</div><b>λουλάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λουλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the bowl of a hookah or narghile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish لوله.</div><b>λουλακής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λουλακιά, neuter λουλακί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of indigo (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>also, indeclinable λουλακί (loulakí, adjective) for all genders</div><b>λουλακί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indeclinable variant of <a href="bword://λουλακής">λουλακής</a> for all genders</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Indeclinable adjective and substantivised neuter of the adjective λουλακής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) indigo (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Indeclinable adjective and substantivised neuter of the adjective λουλακής.</div><b>λουλούδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λουλούδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flower</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Various theories have been proposed:</div><b>λουμπάρδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λουμπάρδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) bombard, cannon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Spanish lombarda, itself altered by contamination with the region of Lombardy. Doublet of μπομπάρδα</div><b>λουξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) lux (SI Unit of illuminance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in older times) a lamp with petrol oil</div>
  <div class='ex'>λάμπα <b>λουξ</b> - lamp &quot;lux&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from German Lux, from Latin lux (“light”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) luxurious, lux</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French luxe (pronounced as Latin lux), from Latin luxus</div><b>λουξεμβουργιανά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Luxembourgish (the language of Luxembourg and its people)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://λουξεμβουργιανός">λουξεμβουργιανός</a></div><b>λουξεμβουργιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λουξεμβουργιανή, neuter λουξεμβουργιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Luxembourgian (relating to Luxembourg or its people or language)</div><b>λουράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λουράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>watchstrap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λουρί + -άκι</div><b>λουρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λουριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strap, belt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rein, lead, leash</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λῶρος.</div><b>λουρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λουρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λωρίδα">λωρίδα</a></div><b>λουτέτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λουτήτιο">λουτήτιο</a> (rare)</div><b>λουτέτσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λουτήτιο">λουτήτιο</a></div><b>λουτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λουτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) bathtub, bath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek λουτῆρᾰ, accusative of λουτήρ.</div><b>λουτήτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lutetium</div><b>λουτρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λουτρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bathroom</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λουτρόν; see there for further etymology. Cognate with Breton laouer, English lather and Icelandic löður.</div><b>λουφάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, intransitive) to skive, shirk, loaf (to avoid working due to laziness)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτόν διάλεξες για εργάτη; Όλο <b>λουφάρει</b>. - You chose him as an employee? All he does is skive.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λούφα + -άρω.</div><b>λουφαδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λουφαδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) skiver, shirker, loafer (someone who avoids work due to laziness)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λούφα + -αδόρος</div><b>λοφάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λοφάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hillock, small hill</div>
  <div class='def'>2. down, rolling grassland</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λόφος + -άκι.</div><b>λοφίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λοφία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crest, plume</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λοφίον, from Ancient Greek λόφος.</div><b>λοφίσκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λοφίσκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hillock, small hill</div><b>λοφιοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λοφιοφόρος, neuter λοφιοφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crested, plumed, having a crest or plume (birds, military uniforms, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λοφιο (lofio, “crest, plume”) + -φόρος</div><b>λοφώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λοφώδης, neuter λοφώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hilly (which resembles or is in some way similar to a hill)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hilly (of a region, containing many hills)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λοφώδης, from λόφος + -ώδης. Equivalent to λόφος + -ώδης.</div><b>λοχίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural λοχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sergeant (the NATO military rank OR-6)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λόχος + -ίας. First attested 1833.</div><b>λοχαγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural λοχαγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the rank of captain in the British and US armies with the NATO grade OF-2</div><b>λούγκρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λούγκρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, slang) faggot, queer, homo (male homosexual)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε από ’δω, μωρή <b>λούγκρα</b>! - Get out of here, you faggot!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Kaliarda, colloquial, slang) wickedness, evil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology unknown. Sense 2 probably emerged first in Kaliarda, then was absorbed into general population lexicon.</div><b>λούζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to shampoo, wash hair</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to endure something annoying</div><b>λούζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to wash, shampoo, bathe (especially hair)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>λούσεις</b> τα μαλλιά σου; - Will you wash your hair?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to soak, bathe (in liquids like sweat, blood, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λούζομαι</b> στον ιδρώτα. - I am soaked in sweat.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to bathe in</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το δωμάτιο <b>λούζεται</b> στο φως. - The room is bathed in light.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) to reprimand, tell off; heap abuse on</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>έλουσα</b> για καλά μπροστά σ’ όλους. - I told him off in front of everyone.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial) to suffer through (endure someone or something boring or annoying)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>λούστηκα</b> για δύο ώρες. Τι βαρετός άνθρωπος! - I suffered through him for two hours. What a boring man!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λούζω, from Ancient Greek λούω, from Proto-Indo-European *lewh₃-.</div><b>λούκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λούκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gutter (on roof)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish اولق (oluk).</div><b>λούμεν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lumen (the derived SI unit of luminous flux)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Latin lumen.</div><b>λούμπεν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lumpen, relating to the lumpenproletariat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From German Lumpen.</div><b>λούμπεν προλεταριάτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lumpenproletariat</div><b>λούνα παρκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) luna park, amusement park</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English luna park.</div><b>λούστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. polish (substance used to polish)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) shining (exceptional merit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian lustro (“shining”).</div><b>λούτσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λούτσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pike, luce (fish)</div><b>λούφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) skiving, shirking, loafing (the action of avoiding work due to laziness)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λούφα</b> και Παραλλαγή - Loafing and Camouflage (1984 Greek film by Nikos Perakis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λωφῶ.</div><b>λούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λούζω">λούζω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λούω.</div><b>λυγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bend, flex</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λύγος.</div><b>λυγαριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λυγαριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wicker, one of various plants with flexible twigs, used for basketry etc</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) osier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λυγαρέα, equivalent to λυγίζω + -αριά.</div><b>λυγιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λυγιστή, neuter λυγιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flexible, bendable, bendy</div><b>λυγμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λυγμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sob (a cry with a short, sudden expulsion of breath)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κλαίω με <b>λυγμούς</b> - to sob</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek λυγμός.</div><b>λυγξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λύγκας">λύγκας</a></div><b>λυδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λυδική, neuter λυδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Lydian, a person of or from Lydia.</div><b>λυθρίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λυθρίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common pandora, red seabream (Pagellus erythrinus)</div><b>λυκάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λυκάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wolf cub</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λύκος + -άκι</div><b>λυκάνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λυκάνθρωποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. werewolf (person who is transformed or can transform into a wolf)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μόνο πράγμα που μπορεί να σκοτώσει τον <b>λυκάνθρωπο</b> είναι μια ασημένια σφαίρα. - The only thing that can kill a werewolf is a silver bullet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) person suffering from lycanthropy (delusion in which they believe they are a wolf)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λύκος + άνθρωπος.</div><b>λυκαινίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Lycaenidae, the lycaenids or gossamer-winged butterflies.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Holly blue είναι μικρή πεταλούδα και ανήκει στην οικογένεια <b>Λυκαινίδες</b> (Lycaenidae) και τη συναντάμε μόνο σε ορεινές περιοχές του Τροόδους. - The Holly blue is a small butterfly belonging to the family Lycaenidae (Lycaenidae) and is found only in mountainous areas of Troodos.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From translingual Lycaenidae, suffixed with -ίδες. Influenced by the family’s etymological cognate, Greek λύκαινα.</div><b>λυκανθρωπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) lycanthropy (delusion in which a person believes they are a wolf)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) lycanthropy (act of shapeshifting between the form of a human being and a wolf)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From λύκος + άνθρωπος + -ία.</div><b>λυκόφως</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) twilight, gloaming</div><b>λυμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine λυμένη, neuter λυμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. untied</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λυμένος</b> κόμπος - untied knot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. solved</div>
  <div class='def'>3. and see <a href="bword://λύνω">λύνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of λύνομαι, passive voice of λύνω (“untie, solve”).</div><b>λυμαντρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λυμαντρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lymantria (Lymantria)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Lymantria.</div><b>λυοφιλοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lyophilisation (UK), lyophilization (US), freeze-drying</div><b>λυπάμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be sorry</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λυπάμαι</b> που δεν μπορούσα να έρθω. - I&#x27;m sorry that I couldn&#x27;t come.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to feel sorry for, to pity, to take pity on</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γυναίκα <b>λυπήθηκε</b> τον ζητιάνο. - The woman felt sorry for the beggar.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive form of λυπώ.</div><b>λυπημένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in a sad way</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://λυπημένος">λυπημένος</a></div><b>λυπημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine λυπημένη, neuter λυπημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sorrowful, sad, melancholy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of λυπάμαι/λυπούμαι, passive voice forms of λυπώ.</div><b>λυπηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λυπηρή, neuter λυπηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sad, saddening (causing feelings of sadness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. regrettable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λυπηρός. By surface analysis, λυπη-) + -ρός.</div><b>λυπητερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λυπητερή, neuter λυπητερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sad, saddening (causing feelings of sadness)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λυπητερός. By surface analysis, λυπη-) + -τερός.</div><b>λυπούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λυπάμαι">λυπάμαι</a>, passive of <a href="bword://λυπώ">λυπώ</a></div><b>λυπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sadden, distress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λυπῶ, contracted form of λυπέω.</div><b>λυροπετεινός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λυροπετεινοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black grouse (Lyrurus tetrix)</div><b>λυσίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lysine</div><b>λυσεργικού οξέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of λυσεργικό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div><b>λυσεργικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lysergic acid</div><b>λυσιτελές</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of λυσιτελής</div><b>λυσιτελή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine/feminine singular of λυσιτελής</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vocative masculine singular of λυσιτελής</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of λυσιτελής</div><b>λυσιτελείς</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative masculine/feminine plural of λυσιτελής</div><b>λυσιτελούς</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive masculine/feminine/neuter singular of λυσιτελής</div><b>λυσιτελών</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive masculine/feminine/neuter plural of λυσιτελής</div><b>λυσσάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of animals and sometimes humans) to go rabid, be afflicted with rabies</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of humans and natural phenomena) to go mad, be furious, rage</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of humans) to be obsessed with, be manic about</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek λυσσῶ) + the modern ending -άω, from λύσσα. Also see the mediaeval λυσσιάζω.</div><b>λυσσασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine λυσσασμένη, neuter λυσσασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. who has rabies</div>
  <div class='def'>2. obsessed with, craving for</div>
  <div class='def'>3. enraged</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of λυσσάω (“get rabies”), a verb without passive forms.</div><b>λυσσώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λυσσάω">λυσσάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λυσσῶ, from λύσσα. See λυσσάω.</div><b>λυτρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ransom, redeem (release in exchange for payment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λυτρόω with nasal infix.</div><b>λυτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λυτή, neuter λυτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undone, loose, untied</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λῠτός.</div><b>λυχνάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oil lamp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λυχνάριν, ultimately from Ancient Greek λύχνος.</div><b>λυχνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λυχνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lamp</div><b>λυώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://λιώνω">λιώνω</a> and <a href="bword://λειώνω">λειώνω</a></div><b>λχ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of λ.χ. (l.ch.)</div><b>λωποδύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λωποδύτες, feminine λωποδύτισσα or λωποδύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thief, robber, pickpocket, pilferer (one who steals, usually petty objects)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έρχονται μες στα σκοτάδια σαν τους <b>λωποδύτες</b> - They come in the dark like thieves.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) crook, cheat, trickster (person who steals, lies, cheats or does other dishonest or illegal things)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λωποδύτης, from λέπω + δύω. The word originally referred to a thief who stole clothes, for example from those bathing or travelling.</div><b>λωρένσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lawrencium</div><b>λωρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λωρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strip (long, narrow piece of a material)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lane (division of roadway)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λωρίδα</b> κυκλοφορίας - traffic lane</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rank</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a Katharevousa form λωρίς + suffix -ίδα, the former from Koine Greek λῶρος.</div><b>λωτός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λωτοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lotus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. persimmon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λωτός.</div><b>λόγ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://λόγιος">λόγιος</a>: learned</div><b>λόγγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λόγγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dense and bushy forest, grove</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek λόγγος, from Proto-Slavic *lǫ̑gъ.</div><b>λόγια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>words</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Alternative plural of λόγος</div><b>λόγιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine λόγια, neuter λόγιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scholarly, learned</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λόγιος.</div><b>λόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. word (word of honour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. speech, language</div>
  <div class='def'>3. speech, oration</div>
  <div class='def'>4. ratio</div>
  <div class='def'>5. reason, causation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχει βάσιμος <b>λόγος</b> να τηρείται το απόρρητο. - There is a good reason to keep something confidential.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λόγος.</div><b>λόγος διαστάσεων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) aspect ratio</div><b>λόγχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λόγχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spear, lance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λόγχη.</div><b>λόγω</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, with genitive) because of, due to, owing to, on account of (as a consequence of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ματς ακυρώθηκε <b>λόγω</b> καταιγίδας. - The match was cancelled due to a storm.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λόγῳ, dative singular of λόγος, a calque of French en raison de.</div><b>λόξιγγας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λόξιγκας">λόξιγκας</a></div><b>λόξιγκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, uncountable) hiccups</div><b>λόξυγγας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λόξιγκας">λόξιγκας</a></div><b>λόξυγκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://λόξιγκας">λόξιγκας</a></div><b>λόρδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λόρδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>English lord or nobleman</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λόρδος Βύρων (Lord Byron)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English lord.</div><b>λόφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λόφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λόφος.</div><b>λύγκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lynx</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λύγξ,).</div><b>λύεται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) equivalent of standard Modern Greek λύνεται: he/she/it is untied, solved</div>
  <div class='ex'>used in the set phrases like</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λύεται.</div><b>λύκαινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λύκαινες, masculine λύκος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>she-wolf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> λύκος + -αινα (-aina, “feminising”)</div><b>λύκειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λύκεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. secondary school (UK), high school (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lyceum</div>
  <div class='def'>3. organisation with educational and cultural objectives</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Λύκειον (the name of a gymnasium, or athletic training facility, near Athens where Aristotle established his school), from Λύκειος.</div><b>λύκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λύκοι, feminine λύκαινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wolf</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wolfdog</div>
  <div class='def'>3. aggressive and bloodthirsty person</div>
  <div class='def'>4. lupus</div>
  <div class='def'>5. cock of old hunting gun</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λύκος</div><b>λύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural λύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wastewater</div>
  <div class='ex'>διαχείριση αστικών <b>λυμάτων</b> - management of urban wastewater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λῦμα.</div><b>λύνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular present passive indicative of <a href="bword://λύνω">λύνω</a>; to break loose, untie oneself</div>
  <div class='def'>2. first-person singular present passive indicative of <a href="bword://λύνω">λύνω</a>; to be untied</div>
  <div class='def'>3. first-person singular present passive indicative of <a href="bword://λύνω">λύνω</a>; to be solved (of problems etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to relax, loosen</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to paralyse (UK), paralyze (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν άκουσα την είδηση του θανάτου του, <b>λύθηκαν</b> τα πόδια μου και λιποθύμησα. - When I heard the new of his death, my legs paralysed and I fainted.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to burst into laughter, laugh to tears</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λύθηκα</b> στα γέλια. - I pissed myself laughing / I died laughing / I burst out laughing</div><b>λύνοντας</b>
  <div><i>participle</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) solving</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πέρασα την ώρα μου <b>λύνοντας</b> σταυρόλεξα. - I spent time solving crosswords.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) untying</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) and see <a href="bword://λύνω">λύνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of λύνω. It functions as an adverb.</div><b>λύνουνε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of λύνουν, first-person plural present active indicative of <a href="bword://λύνω">λύνω</a></div><b>λύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to untie, to unbind, to undo, to unbuckle</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λύνω</b> τα κορδόνια μου. - I untie my laces.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to release</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έλυσα</b> το σκύλο. - I released the dog.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to solve</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω να <b>λύσω</b> την εξίσωση, αλλά είναι πολύ δύσκολη. - I want to solve the equation, but it is very difficult.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to break, to terminate, to end</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Λύθηκαν</b> τα μάγια. - Τhe spell was broken.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to dismantle, to strip down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λύνω, from Ancient Greek λύω.</div><b>λύουμε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of λύομεν first-person plural present active indicative of <a href="bword://λύω">λύω</a></div><b>λύπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λύπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sorrow, sadness, unhappiness, suffering</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pity, compassion</div>
  <div class='def'>3. regret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λύπη.</div><b>λύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lyre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λύρα.</div><b>λύση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>answer (to problem), solution</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek λύσις.</div><b>λύσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) rabies (animal and human disease caused by species of Lyssavirus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε μακριά απ’ αυτό το σκυλί· έχει <b>λύσσα</b> και αν σε δαγκώσει, τελείωσες. - Get away from that dog; it has rabies and if it bites you, you&#x27;re finished.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) rage, fierceness, fury</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η θάλασσα χτυπούσε με <b>λύσσα</b> πάνω στα βράχια. - The sea bashed the rocks with fury.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (followed by <a href="bword://με">με</a> (me) or <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia)) obsession with/for, mania with/for</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>λύσσα</b> για τον τζόγο. - I am obsessed with gambling.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (uncountable) overly salty food</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτά τα μπιφτέκια είναι <b>λύσσα</b>. - These burgers are way too salty.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (uncountable) great hunger</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ας βρούμε κάνα εστιατόριο, έχω <b>λύσσα</b>. - Let&#x27;s find a restaurant, I&#x27;m famished.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λύσσα, from Proto-Indo-European *wĺ̥kʷos (“wolf”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular present imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://λυσσάω">λυσσάω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative form: λύσσαγε (lýssage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular simple past perfective imperative of <a href="bword://λυσσάω">λυσσάω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative form: λύσσαξε (lýssaxe)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λύσσα, from Proto-Indo-European *wĺ̥kʷos (“wolf”).</div><b>λύτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ransom, payment made for someone&#x27;s release</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek λύτρα, plural of λύτρον.</div><b>λύτρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural λυτρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>redemption, salvation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek λύτρωσι.</div><b>λύχνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural λύχνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oil lamp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek λῠ́χνος.</div><b>λύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to terminate, adjourn, end (especially in passive form)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>λύεται</b> η συνεδρίαση - (especially of court) the session is adjourned</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) to dismantle, disassemble → see participle λυόμενος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek λῡ́ω. Also see λύνω.</div><b>μ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contraction of <a href="bword://με">με</a> (me, “with”)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contraction of <a href="bword://με">με</a> (me, “me”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με μισεί … <b>μ’</b> αγαπά! - She hates me … she loves me!</div><b>μ.Χ.</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A.D., AD, initialism of <a href="bword://μετά">μετά</a> Χριστόν</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Initialism of μετά Χριστόν.</div><b>μ.μ.</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abbreviation of <a href="bword://μετά">μετά</a> <a href="bword://το">το</a> <a href="bword://μεσημέρι">μεσημέρι</a> (post meridiem)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abbreviation of <a href="bword://μετά">μετά</a> μεσημβρίαν (post meridiem)</div><b>μ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Μ΄">Μ΄</a></div><b>μΧ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of μ.Χ. (m.Ch.)</div><b>μάγγο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://μάνγκο">μάνγκο</a></div><b>μάγειρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μάγειρες, feminine μαγείρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cook, chef</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μάγειρας, from Ancient Greek μάγειρ or μάγειρος.</div><b>μάγειρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μάγειροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μάγειρας">μάγειρας</a></div><b>μάγια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (accusative) spell (magic and witchcraft)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μου έκανε <b>μάγια</b> - (s)he cast a spell [on me]</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) the language of Maya</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) a dancer&#x27;s leotard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μάγια, substantivised neuter adjective of the Hellenistic Koine Greek μάγιος, or from the Ancient Greek μαγεία f with a change of gender and movement of the accent. From μάγος.</div><b>μάγισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μάγισσες, masculine μάγος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>witch, sorceress</div><b>μάγκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μάγκες, feminine μάγκισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (broadly, colloquial, derogatory) tough guy, macho man (person who acts tough and talks in a particular heavy way, the implication usually being they are anything but)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν ένας τύπος <b>μάγκας</b> που μάλωνε με οποιονδήποτε τον κοιτούσε στραβά. - There was a tough guy who was picking fights with anyone who looked at him wrong.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in positive contexts) tiger, daredevil, lionheart (smart or cunning person who is exceptionally able and resourceful)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί δεν περιμένεις στη σειρά; Πιστεύεις ότι είσαι πιο <b>μάγκας</b> από μας; - Why won&#x27;t you wait your turn in the queue? Do you think you&#x27;re braver than us?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, informal) dude, man, bro, guy (term of address between friends)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λοιπόν, <b>μάγκες</b>, είμαστε έτοιμοι; - Right, guys, are we ready?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (historical, obsolete) mangas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish مانقه (manka, manga, “squad”).
According to Nisanyan, from Italian banco, originally a naval term used to refer to a bank or row of sailors, later becoming a group or assembly of sailors (often with the additional meaning of a mess or meal assembly), then, according to Nisanyan, a non-naval term by the 20th century used by the ground forces.</div><b>μάγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μάγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magma</div><b>μάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μάγοι, feminine μάγισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. magician</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wizard, sorcerer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μάγος.</div><b>μάγουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μάγουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheek</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from late Koine Greek μάγουλον, itself from Late Latin magulum.</div><b>μάζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μάζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mass (quantity of matter cohering together to make one body)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ατομική <b>μάζα</b> - atomic mass</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, plural normally) mass, throng, multitude (group of people crowded or gathered closely together)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>μάζες</b> κατέβηκαν στους δρόμους για να διαδηλώσουν. - The masses have taken to the streets to protest.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The form of the word continues Ancient Greek μᾶζα, but the sense &quot;mass&quot; of the modern Greek word is semantic loan from French masse (18th century), derived from Latin massa (English mass, German Masse, etc.)</div><b>μάζευ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of μάζεψε, second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://μαζεύω">μαζεύω</a></div><b>μάης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μάηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the garland of flowers of the 1st of May</div>
  <div class='def'>2. and see <a href="bword://Μάης">Μάης</a></div><b>μάθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lesson (a period set aside for tuition)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subject (an area of knowledge to be taught)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lesson, experience</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαθαίνω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>μάθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>learning (the process of acquiring knowledge)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μάθησις.</div><b>μάλαμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαλάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) gold, something made of gold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) somebody or something with exceptional good or valuable qualities</div>
  <div class='ex'>καρδιά μάλαμα - heart of gold</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μάλαγμα, from Ancient Greek μάλαγμα, a noun form of μαλάσσω. Doublet of αμάλγαμα.</div><b>μάλιστα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. especially, particularly, in particular</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα λαχανικά και <b>μάλιστα</b> τα φρούτα είναι υγιεινές τροφές. - Vegetables and especially fruits are healthy foods.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. even</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το κρασί είναι <b>μάλιστα</b> πιο εξαιρετικό. - This wine is even more excellent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μάλιστα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yes, certainly, OK, of course, right you are, okey-dokey (acknowledging that a request has been heard and understood)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μάλιστα.</div><b>μάλλινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>woollens (UK), woolens (US)</div><b>μάλλινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μάλλινη, neuter μάλλινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>woollen, woolly (UK); woolen (US)</div><b>μάλλον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rather</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rather, more precisely</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ήθελα να φύγω. Ή <b>μάλλον</b>, ήθελα, αλλά όχι μόνος μου. - I didn’t want to leave. Or rather I did, just not alone.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rather, somewhat fairly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το πεπόνι είναι <b>μάλλον</b> άγευστο. - This melon is rather tasteless.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (for the first person singular of the present tense) I guess</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είμαι ο μόνος εδώ <b>μάλλον</b> που δεν του αρέσει η ροκ μουσική. - I am the only one here who doesn’t like rock music, I guess.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μᾶλλον).</div><b>μάλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>argument, scolding, telling off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μάλωμα, equivalent to μαλώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>μάμμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μάμμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) grandmother</div><b>μάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (familiar) mum, mom, ma, mama</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bank</div>
  <div class='def'>3. the checker on the ace- or one-point</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (familiar) used to refer to wife or mistress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μάμμη.</div><b>μάνατζερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) manager</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English manager.</div><b>μάνατζμεντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) management</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English management.</div><b>μάνγκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mango (fruit and tree)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English mango.</div><b>μάννα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative spelling of <a href="bword://μάνα">μάνα</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) manna</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μάμμη.</div><b>μάνταλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μάνταλα)</i></div>
