  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of μέρεψε, second-person singular simple past active perfective imperative of <a href="bword://μερεύω">μερεύω</a></div><b>μέρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μερίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dividend</div><b>μέρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μέρη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. part (a fraction of a whole)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αγγλικό Βικιλεξικό αποτελεί <b>μέρος</b> ενός πολυεθνικού διαδικτυακού εγχειρήματος. - English Wiktionary is part of an online multinational project.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. place (an area; somewhere within an area)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σ’ αυτό το <b>μέρος</b> είναι κρυμμένος ένας θησαυρός. - There&#x27;s treasure hidden at this place.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. movement, part</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το τρίτο <b>μέρος</b> της συμφωνίας. - The third movement of the symphony.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. party, side (group of people forming one side in a given dispute)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα ενδιαφερόμενα <b>μέρη</b> κατέληξαν σε συμβιβαστική λύση. - The interested parties reached a compromise.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (euphemistic) loo, privy, commode, lavatory (toilet)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πάω στο <b>μέρος</b> - to go to the loo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μέρος.</div><b>μέσ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μέσα">μέσα</a></div><b>μέσ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μεσαιωνική ελληνική”)</div><b>μέσα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μέσος">μέσος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) inner</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>μέσα</b> κόσμος μου - my inner world (soul)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Plural neuter of adjective μέσος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in, inside (something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>μέσα</b> στο δωμάτιο. - He/she/it is inside the room.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in, through (a situation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έζησα <b>μέσα</b> στις κακουχίες. - I lived through hardship.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. in, through (involvement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>μέσα</b> στην ομάδα μας. - She is in our team.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (with prepositions) as affirmative response, certainly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα από <b>μέσα</b>! - Look at the inside!</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (as preposition, of place) into</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βάλε τις αποσκευές <b>μέσα</b> στο αυτοκίνητο. - Put the luggage in the car.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (as preposition, of place) between</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια μονοκατοικία <b>μέσα</b> σε πολυκατοικίες. - A house between apartment buildings.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (as preposition, of time) within, before</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα σε πληρώσω <b>μέσα</b> σε ένα μήνα. - I will pay you within a month.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (as preposition, of time) during</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μέσα</b> στον επόμενο μήνα - during the next month</div>
  <div class='def'>9. (in space, as preposition, of way) through</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το λεωφορείο δεν περνάει <b>μέσα</b> από το κέντρο της πόλης. - The bus does not pass through the centre of the city.</div>
  <div class='def'>10. (in manner, as preposition, of way) through, with</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πέτυχα <b>μέσα</b> από πολλή προσπάθεια. - I succeeded through considerable effort.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Plural neuter of adjective μέσος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μέσο and <a href="bword://μέσον">μέσον</a>; the media; means</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μέσο and <a href="bword://μέσον">μέσον</a>; the middle parts (of space or time)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μέσο and <a href="bword://μέσον">μέσον</a>; connections</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (indeclinable) the inside, inner part</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αφαιρούμε το <b>μέσα</b> της ντομάτας προσεκτικά. - We remove the inner part of the tomato carefully.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Plural neuter of adjective μέσος.</div><b>μέσα μαζικής ενημέρωσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μέσο μαζικής ενημέρωσης: mass media</div><b>μέσα μαζικής επικοινωνίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μέσο μαζικής επικοινωνίας: mass media</div><b>μέση</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://μέσος">μέσος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the feminine of Ancient Greek μέσος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μέσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. middle, midpoint</div>
  <div class='def'>2. waist, waistband</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lower back (the part of the human trunk between the ribs and the buttocks)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the feminine of Ancient Greek μέσος.</div><b>μέσν.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μεσαιωνική ελληνική”)</div><b>μέσο μαζικής ενημέρωσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μέσα μαζικής ενημέρωσης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>medium, mass medium</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See μέσο, μαζικής (feminine genitive) of adjective μαζικός (“mass”), ενημέρωσης (genitive singular) of noun ενημέρωση f (“information up-to-date”).</div><b>μέσο μαζικής επικοινωνίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μέσα μαζικής επικοινωνίας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>medium, mass medium</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See μέσο, μαζικής (feminine genitive) of adjective μαζικός (“mass”), επικοινωνίας (genitive singular) of noun επικοινωνία f (“communication”).</div><b>μέσον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μέσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of μέσο</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intermediary</div><b>μέσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μέση, neuter μέσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. middle, mid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. average, mean</div>
  <div class='def'>3. compromise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μέσος, from Proto-Hellenic *métsos, Proto-Indo-European *médʰyos (“between”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μέσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. average, mean</div>
  <div class='ex'>αριθμητικός <b>μέσος</b> - arithmetic mean</div>
  <div class='def'>2. midfielder</div>
  <div class='def'>3. middle finger</div>
  <div class='def'>4. middle toe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μέσος, from Proto-Hellenic *métsos, Proto-Indo-European *médʰyos (“between”).</div><b>μέσου μαζικής ενημέρωσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of μέσο μαζικής ενημέρωσης</div><b>μέσου μαζικής επικοινωνίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of μέσο μαζικής επικοινωνίας</div><b>μέσω</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with genitive) via (by way of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η πτήση είναι <b>μέσω</b> Λονδίνου. - The flight is via London.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with genitive) via, through (by means of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αγώνας θα μεταδοθεί ζωντανά <b>μέσω</b> δορυφόρου. - The match will be broadcast live via satellite.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μέσῳ, dative singular of μέσος, a semantic loan from German mittels.</div><b>μέσων μαζικής ενημέρωσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of μέσο μαζικής ενημέρωσης</div><b>μέσων μαζικής επικοινωνίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of μέσο μαζικής επικοινωνίας</div><b>μέταλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μέταλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metal</div><b>μέτοικος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural μέτοικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) metic (Ancient Greece)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. immigrant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnely, from Ancient Greek μέτοικος.</div><b>μέτρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. counting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. measurement</div><b>μέτριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μέτρια, neuter μέτριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. medium, mediocre, ordinary, mild</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένας άνδρας <b>μετρίου</b> αναστήματος - a man of medium height</div>
  <div class='def'>2. <a href="bword://α">α</a> coffee with medium sugar</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένα <b>μέτριο</b> παρακαλώ! - A medium sweet coffee please!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) unexaggerated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μέτριος.</div><b>μέτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μέτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. measure, measurement</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ίντσα είναι <b>μέτρο</b> μήκους. - An inch is a measure of length.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rule, ruler</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα <b>μέτρο</b> του ποδιού - a one-foot ruler</div>
  <div class='def'>3. measure, action</div>
  <div class='ex'>αντιπλημμυρικά <b>μέτρα</b> - flood prevention measures</div>
  <div class='def'>4. metre (universal), meter (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. metre (UK), meter (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. moderation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μέτρον. The modern sense is reinforced by French mètre.</div><b>μέτωπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μέτωπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. forehead</div>
  <div class='def'>2. front (weather front)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. front line</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μέτωπον.</div><b>μέχα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) in, inside</div><b>μέχρι</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. until, till, til, &#x27;til</div>
  <div class='ex'>θα μείνω <b>μέχρι</b>… - I will stay until…</div>
  <div class='def'>2. until, as far as</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μέχρι</b> τέλους - as far as the end</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μέχρι.</div><b>μέχρι τα μπούνια</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>full to the brim, (lit) to the scuppers</div><b>μέχρις</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μέχρι">μέχρι</a></div><b>μήκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μήκη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>length (the distance between the two ends of an object)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μῆκος, from Proto-Hellenic *mā́kos, from Proto-Indo-European *méh₂ḱos.</div><b>μήκων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural μήκωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poppy, papaver</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>Μήκων</b> η υπνοφόρος είναι είδος παπαρούνας από το οποίο παρασκευάζεται το όπιο. - The Papaver somniferum (opium poppy) is a type of Papaver from which opium is produced.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μήκων. Cognate with German Mohn.</div><b>μήλα του Αδάμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μήλο">μήλο</a> <a href="bword://του">του</a> <a href="bword://Αδάμ">Αδάμ</a></div><b>μήλινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μήλινη or μηλίνη, neuter μήλινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of or made from the apple-tree and its fruit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. originating from apples</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μήλινος.</div><b>μήλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μήλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. apple</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) dodgeball</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μῆλον.</div><b>μήλο του Αδάμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μήλα του Αδάμ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Adam&#x27;s apple, laryngeal prominence</div><b>μήνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>month</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μήνας</b> μπαίνει, <b>μήνας</b> βγαίνει - month in, month out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μήν, from Proto-Indo-European *mḗh₁n̥s.
Compare Mariupol Greek ми́на.</div><b>μήνιγγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μήνιγγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meninx</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ΚΝΣ για να προστατεύεται περιβάλλεται από τρία μεμβρανώδη περιβλήματα που λέγονται οι <b>μήνιγγες</b>. - To protect itself, the CNS is surrounded by three membranes called the meninges.</div><b>μήνις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rage, wrath, anger</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>μήνις</b> του Αχιλλέα - the rage of Achilles</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μῆνις.</div><b>μήνυμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μηνύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. message</div>
  <div class='ex'>συλλυπητήριο <b>μήνυμα</b> - message of condolence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. email; text, SMS</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) message</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>μήνυμα</b> του χριστιανισμού - the message of Christianity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μήνῡμα, from μηνύω + -μα (-ma, “suffix that forms neuter nouns”).</div><b>μήνυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μηνύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>complaint, case, lawsuit</div><b>μήπως</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. if, by any chance, perhaps, maybe</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μήπως</b> θέλεις να βγούμε; - By any chance would you like to go?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. as if, didn&#x27;t? (forming rhetorical questions that indicate the opposite of the question is true)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μήπως</b> δεν τον βοήθησα τόσες φορές; - As if I didn&#x27;t help him so many times.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. in case, for fear that, lest</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φοβήθηκα <b>μήπως</b> του έγινε κάτι, αργεί πολύ. - I&#x27;m worried in case something happened to him, he&#x27;s very late.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek [Term?] phrase μή πως.</div><b>μήτε</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. neither … nor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nor even, not even</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μήτε.</div><b>μήτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μήτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. womb</div>
  <div class='def'>2. matrix</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mold (anything around or on which something is formed or shaped)</div><b>μίζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. starter motor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ignition key</div>
  <div class='def'>3. kickback, sweetener (bribe)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French mise, and cognate mise en marche.</div><b>μίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μίλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μίλι(ν)), from Koine Greek μίλιον, from Latin mīlia.</div><b>μίμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mime</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μῖμος.</div><b>μίνι</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mini</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mini, miniskirt</div><b>μίνι φούστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μίνι φούστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>miniskirt</div><b>μίνι φούστες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μίνι">μίνι</a> <a href="bword://φούστα">φούστα</a></div><b>μίξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μίξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a mixing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η <b>μίξη</b> των χρωμάτων του ουρανού και της θάλασσας είναι ταυτόχρονα καταπραϋντική και σαγηνευτική. - This mixing of the colours of the sky and the sea is both soothing and captivating.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μίξις.</div><b>μίσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μίση)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hatred, hate, enmity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μῖσος.</div><b>μίσχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μίσχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>petiole, stalk, stem (of plant)</div><b>μα</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>but</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω να βγω απόψε <b>μα</b> δεν μπορώ. - I want to go out tonight, but I can&#x27;t.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μά, from Old Italian ma, from Latin magis.</div><b>μα την Παναγία</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>on God, I swear to God (vigorously affirm the truth to a statement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ήμουνα εγώ, <b>μα την Παναγία!</b>. - It wasn&#x27;t me, I swear to Holy Mary!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μα (ma, oath particle) + accusative case as the singular την (tin) Παναγία.</div><b>μα το Θεό</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) in the name of God</div>
  <div class='ex'>— Η Ελένη παντρεύτηκε. —Τι μου λες; — Αλήθεια σου λέω. Στ’ ορκίζομαι, <b>μα το θεό</b>. - &quot;Helen got married.&quot; &quot;What are you telling me?&quot; (cannot believe it) &quot;I speak the truth. I swear, in the name of God.&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μα (ma, oath particle) + accusative case as the singular τον (ton) θεό. Also see Ancient Greek μὰ τὼ θεώ with dual number for θεός, referring to Demeter and Persephone.</div><b>μαΐστρος</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mistral, northwesterly Mediterranean wind</div><b>μαέστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://μαέστρος">μαέστρος</a></div><b>μαέστρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαέστροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conductor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian maestro, from Latin magister (“master”).</div><b>μαία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>midwife</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μαῖα.</div><b>μαίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rage (act in an angry or mad manner)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μαίνομαι.</div><b>μαβής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαβιά, neuter μαβί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated, poetic) azure, navy blue, dark blue (literary use especially in reference to the sky, sea, or eyes)</div>
  <div class='ex'>θάλασσας του πρωιού κι ανέφελου ουρανού λαμπρά <b>μαβιά</b> - bright azures of morning sea and cloudless sky</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) violet, mauve, dark purple</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish ماوی (mavi, “blue, watery”), ultimately from Arabic ماء.</div><b>μαβί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated, indeclinable, poetic) azure, dark blue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, indeclinable) mauve, dark purple</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish ماوی (mavi, “blue”), from Arabic مَاوِيّ, ultimately from Arabic مَاء.</div><b>μαγαζί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαγαζιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shop, store, retail outlet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. office (for contact with business etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Arabic خَزَنَder.
Arabic مَخْزَنder.
Arabic مَخَازِنbor.
Venetan magazénbor.
Byzantine Greek μαγαζίον
Greek μαγαζί
Inherited from Byzantine Greek μαγαζί(ον), μαγαζίν, magazín, “warehouse, shop”), from Venetan magasín with the deletion of [n], from Arabic مَخَازِن pl, plural of مَخْزَن.</div><b>μαγγάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) manganese</div><b>μαγεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magic, sorcery, witchcraft</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαγείᾱ, equivalent of μάγος + -εία.</div><b>μαγείρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγείρισσες, masculine μάγειρας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cook, chef</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μαγείρ + -ισσα (-issa, feminine suffix).</div><b>μαγειρίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγειρίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traditional soup eaten on Holy Saturday</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαγειριά + -ίτσα.</div><b>μαγειρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαγειρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kitchen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) kitchens, cookhouse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) place to eat</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) folksy, cheap restaurant</div><b>μαγειρειό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαγειρειά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαγειρείο">μαγειρείο</a></div><b>μαγειρεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be cooked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be cooked up</div><b>μαγειρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cook</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to cook up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek μαγειρεύω.</div><b>μαγειριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγειριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capacity (of a cooking vessel)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαγειρ- (mageir-, “relating to cooking”) + -ιά. See μάγειρος for more.</div><b>μαγειρικά σκεύη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kitchenware, cookware</div><b>μαγειρική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγειρικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cookery</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cooking</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cuisine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek μαγειρική.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://μαγειρικός">μαγειρικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek μαγειρική.</div><b>μαγειρική σόδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) baking soda (sodium bicarbonate)</div><b>μαγειρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαγειρική, neuter μαγειρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>culinary, cooking</div><b>μαγερειό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαγερειά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαγειρείο">μαγειρείο</a></div><b>μαγεριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγεριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαγειριά">μαγειριά</a></div><b>μαγεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cast a spell (on), to bewitch, to charm, to enchant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to charm, to captivate, to enchant, to spellbind</div><b>μαγιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. yeast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) seed (of a something for future growth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish مایه (maya), from Classical Persian مایه. Cognate to Macedonian маја (maja).</div><b>μαγικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μαγικός">μαγικός</a></div><b>μαγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαγική, neuter μαγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magic, magical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαγικός. By surface analysis, μάγος + -ικός.</div><b>μαγιονέζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγιονέζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mayonnaise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French mayonnaise.</div><b>μαγιό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) A swimsuit, a swimming costume, swimming trunks.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French maillot (“swimsuit, jersey”).</div><b>μαγκάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαγγάνιο">μαγγάνιο</a></div><b>μαγκανοπήγαδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαγκανοπήγαδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a well windlass</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) monotonous work; treadmill; everyday life&#x27;s routine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαγκάνι + πηγάδι</div><b>μαγκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory) tough guy behaviour/attitude, macho man behaviour/attitude</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταματά τις <b>μαγκιές</b> και κάτσε κάτω. - Stop with the tough guy attitude and sit down.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, often, sarcastic) great achievement, cool act/thing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κατάφερες να τον νικήσεις; Μωρέ, <b>μαγκιά</b> σου! - You managed to defeat him? Well done you!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μάγκας + -ιά.</div><b>μαγματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαγματική, neuter μαγματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magmatic</div><b>μαγνήσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural μαγνήσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) magnesium</div><b>μαγνήτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαγνήτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magnet</div><b>μαγνησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) magnesium oxide, magnesia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαγνησίᾱ, from the city Μαγνησία.</div><b>μαγνητίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to magnetize</div><b>μαγνητική ροή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magnetic flux</div><b>μαγνητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαγνητική, neuter μαγνητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. magnetic, of or pertaining to magnetism.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μαγνητική</b> πυξίδα - magnetic compass</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) mesmerising, attractive (personality)</div><b>μαγνητισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαγνητισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magnetism</div><b>μαγνητόφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tape recorder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Reborrowing from English magnetophone, from μαγνητο- (magnito-) + -φωνο (-fono); for the respective components, see μαγνήτης + φωνή.</div><b>μαγνόλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγνόλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magnolia (Magnolia)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Magnolia.</div><b>μαγουλάδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mumps (contagious disease)</div><b>μαγούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαγούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Uncertain. See Romanian măgură for more.</div><b>μαδέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) madeira (wine)</div><b>μαζί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (place) together, with (takes the genitive case)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχεις τα κλειδιά <b>μαζί</b> σου; - Do you have the keys with you?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. together, along with, at the same time</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ελένη έφτασε <b>μαζί</b> με τις αδελφές της. - Eleni arrived along with her sisters.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek μαζίν, from the Ancient Greek μαζίον, diminutive of μᾶζα f.</div><b>μαζδαϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Mazdaism</div><b>μαζεμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μαζεμένη, neuter μαζεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. collected, gathered</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shrunk</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) very shy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μαζεύομαι, passive voice of μαζεύω (“gather; shrink”).</div><b>μαζεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to gather, congregate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cower</div><b>μαζεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to collect, gather, get in, harvest</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to collect, pick up</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to furl, gather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μαζεύω from Hellenistic Ancient Greek ὁμαδεύω, from ἡ ὁμάς, from ὁμός. Adapted to μαζί and μᾶζα by folk etymology.</div><b>μαζικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μαζικός">μαζικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>en masse</div><b>μαζικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαζική, neuter μαζικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mass (collectively, en masse)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μαζική</b> υστερία - mass hysteria</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mass, massic (related to weight)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μαζικός</b> αριθμός - mass number</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mass, bulky</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French en masse. Morphologically from μάζα (“mass”) + -ικός (“suffix for adjectives”).</div><b>μαζοχίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαζοχίστριες, masculine μαζοχιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masochist</div><b>μαζοχιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαζοχιστές, feminine μαζοχίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masochist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French masochiste.</div><b>μαζούν</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) matzoon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Armenian մածուն (macun).</div><b>μαθήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαθήτριες, masculine μαθητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pupil, student, schoolgirl</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μανθάνω.</div><b>μαθαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to learn; to learn, acquire knowledge</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μαθαίνω</b> γαλλικά, πιάνο και χορό. - I learn (take lessons) French, piano and dancing.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to learn; to learn, to be informed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έμαθα</b> τα νέα. - I heard the news.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to become accustomed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δυστυχώς, <b>έχω μάθει</b> στο κάπνισμα και δεν μπορώ να το κόψω. - Regrettably, I&#x27;ve got the habit of smoking and I cannot give up.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to teach</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μάθε</b> μου να παίζω πιάνο! - Teach me to play the piano!</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to teach; to teach a lesson (in order to warn or punish)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα σε <b>μάθω</b> εγώ να λες ψέματα· για τιμωρία, δε θα πας στο πάρτυ. - I&#x27;ll teach you a lesson for lying; as a punishment, you won&#x27;t go to the party.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μαθαίνω, form of μανθάνω from the past tense stem μαθ‑ (ἔμαθον) of ancient Ancient Greek verb μανθάνω and metaplasm ‑αίνω.</div><b>μαθεύεται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular present of <a href="bword://μαθεύομαι">μαθεύομαι</a>:; becoming known (of news)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το νέο <b>μαθεύεται</b>, διαδίδεται γρήγορα, αλλά δεν το είπανε ακόμη στις ειδήσεις. - The news story is becoming known, it spreads fast, but it has not been announced yet on the news.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (impersonal) third-person singular present of <a href="bword://μαθεύομαι">μαθεύομαι</a>:; it is known, publicised, it is rumoured</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Mαθεύεται</b> ότι παραιτήθηκε ο πρόεδρος - It is becoming known that the president resigned.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * See etymology of μαθεύομαι.</div><b>μαθεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to transpire, become known</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα νέα <b>μαθεύονται</b> γρήγορα. - News become known (spread) fast.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially, impersonal, past, third person) to become known, publicised, to be rumoured</div>
  <div class='ex'>see: μαθεύεται (mathévetai), μαθεύτηκε (mathéftike)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the stem μαθ- of μαθαίνω + suffix -εύομαι.</div><b>μαθεύτηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://μαθεύομαι">μαθεύομαι</a>:; became known (of news)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το νέο <b>μαθεύτηκε</b>, διαδόθηκε γρήγορα, αλλά δεν το είπανε ακόμη στις ειδήσεις. - The news story became known, it has spread fast, but it has not been announced yet on the news.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (impersonal) third-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://μαθεύομαι">μαθεύομαι</a>:; it has been known, publicised, it was rumoured</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Mαθεύτηκε</b> ότι παραιτήθηκε ο πρόεδρος - It became known that the president resigned.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * See etymology of μαθεύομαι.</div><b>μαθημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μαθημένη, neuter μαθημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. which has been learnt</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το κεφάλαιο 2 είναι διαβασμένο και <b>μαθημένο</b> καλά· θα πάρω καλό βαθμό στις εξετάσεις. - Chapter 2 is read and well learnt; I&#x27;ll get a good grade at the exams.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. which has been learnt; who has learnt</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to be accustomed to something</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είμαι <b>μαθημένος</b> στη ζέστη· δεν αντέχω τα χιόνια. - I am accustomed to warm [climate]; I cannot bear snow.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μαθαίνομαι, passive voice of μαθαίνω.
Stem μαθη- + suffix for passive perfect -μένος from ancient verb μανθάνω.</div><b>μαθηματικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mathematics</div><b>μαθηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαθηματική, neuter μαθηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mathematical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαθηματικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mathematician</div>
  <div class='def'>2. maths teacher (UK), math teacher (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαθηματικός.</div><b>μαθητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαθητές, feminine μαθήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pupil, student, schoolboy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. disciple</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαθητής.</div><b>μαθητεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαθητείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apprenticeship</div><b>μαθητευόμενη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαθητευόμενες, masculine μαθητευόμενος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apprentice</div><b>μαθητευόμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαθητευόμενοι, feminine μαθητευόμενη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apprentice</div><b>μαιευτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαιευτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maternity hospital</div><b>μακάβριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>macabre, ghoulish, ghastly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν βλέπω ποτέ ταινίες τρόμου, είναι πολύ <b>μακάβριες</b> για μένα. - I never watch horror films, they are too macabre for me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French macabre. First attested 1897.</div><b>μακάκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>macaque</div><b>μακάριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακάρια, neuter μακάριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blissful (extremely happy; full of joy; experiencing, indicating, causing, or characterized by bliss)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μακάρια</b> άγνοια - blissful ignorance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) blessed (having divine aid, or protection, or other blessing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μακάριος.</div><b>μακάρτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) sour milk, curdled milk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Armenian մակարդ (makard).</div><b>μακαρθισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural μακαρθισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(US, historical, uncountable, usually) McCarthyism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English McCarthyism; by surface analysis, Μακάρθι + -ισμός.</div><b>μακαρονάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μακαρονάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spaghetti dish (with cheese or sauce, especially)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μακαρόνι + -άδα</div><b>μακαρονοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακαρονοειδής, neuter μακαρονοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>macaronic, sesquipedalian</div><b>μακαρόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μακαρόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a piece of tubular pasta (macaroni)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a piece of spaghetti</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan macaroni.</div><b>μακεδονίτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακεδονίτικη, neuter μακεδονίτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μακεδονικός">μακεδονικός</a></div><b>μακεδονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακεδονική, neuter μακεδονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Macedonian (relating to Macedonia or its people or language)</div><b>μακελάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μακελάρηδες, feminine μακελάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) butcher, slaughterer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μακελλάριος, from Latin macellarius (“butcher or meat seller”).</div><b>μακελλάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μακελάρης">μακελάρης</a></div><b>μακιγιάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) makeup, cosmetics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French maquillage.</div><b>μακιγιέζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable, masculine μακιγιέρ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cosmetician, makeup artist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French maquilleuse.</div><b>μακιγιέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or neuter (indeclinable, feminine μακιγιέζ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cosmetician, make-up artist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French maquilleur.</div><b>μακραίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to grow longer, to grow out, to let grow (:hair)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of hair) to grow longer</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to move away</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to prolong, to lengthen (to extend the duration of)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to lengthen, to make longer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μακραίνω, from Ancient Greek μακρ(ύνω)) + -αίνω.</div><b>μακραίωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακραίωνη, neuter μακραίωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centuries-long, centuries-old, age-old</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μακραίων, from μακρός + αἰών.</div><b>μακρηγορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) verbosity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek μᾰκρηγορῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>μακρηγορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to expatiate, speak at length</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be verbose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μακρηγορῶ, contracted form of μακρηγορέω.</div><b>μακριά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. far</div>
  <div class='def'>2. afar</div>
  <div class='def'>3. away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μακρύς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μακρύς">μακρύς</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://μακρύς">μακρύς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μακρύς.</div><b>μακρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακρινή, neuter μακρινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. distant, far off, faraway, remote</div>
  <div class='def'>2. long (of journey, trip, expedition, etc)</div><b>μακροβιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) longevity, long life</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μακροβιότης, equivalent to μακρόβιος + -ότητα.</div><b>μακρογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) macrography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French macrographie. By surface analysis, μακρο- (makro-) + -γραφία.</div><b>μακροζωία</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) longevity, having long life</div><b>μακροημέρευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) longevity, long life</div><b>μακροθυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) forbearance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek μᾰκροθῡμῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>μακροκατάσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μακροκαταστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>macrostate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μακρο- (makro-, “macro”) + κατάσταση</div><b>μακροοικονομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακροοικονομική, neuter μακροοικονομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>macroeconomic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French macroéconomique. By surface analysis, μακροοικονομ(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>μακροπρόθεσμα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μακροπρόθεσμος">μακροπρόθεσμος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>long-term, in the long term, in the long run</div><b>μακροπρόθεσμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακροπρόθεσμη, neuter μακροπρόθεσμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>long-term</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μακρός + προθεσμία, calque of English long-term.</div><b>μακροχρόνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακροχρόνια, neuter μακροχρόνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. long-lasting, long (persisting or enduring for a long time; having great duration)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. long-term (extending over a relatively long time period)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. long-standing (having existed for a long time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μακροχρόνιος. By surface analysis, μακρο- (makro-) + χρόν(ος)) + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>μακρυμάλλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακρυμάλλα, neuter μακρυμάλλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. longhaired</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) longhaired person, longhair</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μακρυ- (makry-) + -μάλλης.</div><b>μακρόστενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>long and narrow, oblong</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μακρό- + στενός.</div><b>μακρόχρονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακρόχρονη, neuter μακρόχρονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. long (of a vowel, of a sound)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. synonym of <a href="bword://μακροχρόνιος">μακροχρόνιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μακρός + χρόνος.</div><b>μακρύ χέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see μακρύ, <a href="bword://χέρι">χέρι</a>.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, idiomatic, uncountable) long arms, sticky fingers (inclination to steal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχε <b>μακριά χέρια</b> αλλά κάποτε πιάστηκε. - He had sticky fingers but he was caught one time.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory, idiomatic, uncountable) Roman hands and Russian fingers (tendency towards unwanted sexual touching)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν ήταν νέος, είχε <b>μακρύ χέρι</b> αλλά τώρα συμμορφώθηκε. - When he was young, he had Roman hands and Russian fingers but now he&#x27;s cleaned up his act.</div><b>μακρύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μακριά, neuter μακρύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>long</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μακρύς, from Ancient Greek μακρός, from Proto-Hellenic *makrós, from Proto-Indo-European *mh₂ḱrós, from *meh₂ḱ- (“to increase”).</div><b>μαλάγρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαλάγρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chum (groundbait for sea fishing)</div><b>μαλάκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mollusc (UK), mollusk (US) (soft-bodied invertebrate of the phylum Mollusca, typically with a hard shell of one or more pieces)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The plural is inherited from Ancient Greek μαλάκια. The singular is a learned back-formation from this.</div><b>μαλάκω</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (masculine μαλάκας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive, rare) bitch, asshole, jerk (general term of abuse for a female)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα τη <b>μαλάκω</b> τι έκανε! - Look what that bitch did!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαλάκας + -ω (-o).</div><b>μαλάρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) malaria</div><b>μαλαγασικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Malagasy (language)</div><b>μαλακά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gently</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μαλακός">μαλακός</a></div><b>μαλακία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαλακίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, literally, vulgar) masturbation, wanking</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>μαλακία</b> σε κάνει τυφλό. - Wanking makes you blind.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, vulgar) stupidity, idiocy, nonsense, bullshit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλο κάθεται και λέει <b>μαλακίες</b>. - All he does is sit around and talk bullshit.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαλακία, from Ancient Greek μαλακός.</div><b>μαλακίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally, offensive, vulgar) to wank, wank off, jerk off, jack off (masturbate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταματά να <b>μαλακίζεσαι</b>, θα τυφλωθείς! - Stop jacking off, you&#x27;ll go blind!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, vulgar) to talk or do nonsense</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, vulgar) to waste time; fuck around</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη <b>μαλακιστείς</b>! - Don&#x27;t fuck around!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαλακίζομαι, from Ancient Greek μαλακός.</div><b>μαλακισμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine μαλακισμένη, neuter μαλακισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, literally, vulgar) drowsy/lethargic from excessive masturbation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (used like a noun) fucking, cunting, bloody; imbecilic, moronic, braindead</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι θέλει αυτός ο <b>μαλακισμένος</b> και μας ενοχλεί συνεχώς; - What does that fucking idiot want, always bothering us?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μαλακίζομαι, a passive deponent verb. From Ancient Greek μαλακίζομαι.</div><b>μαλακτικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of μαλακτικός</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of μαλακτικός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαλακτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. softener</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hair conditioner</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fabric softener</div>
  <div class='def'>4. conditioner</div><b>μαλακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαλακή or μαλακιά, neuter μαλακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. soft</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meek, compliant</div>
  <div class='def'>3. gentle, mild, mild-mannered, mellow</div>
  <div class='def'>4. malleable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαλακός.</div><b>μαλακώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to soften (to make something soft or softer)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to soften (to become soft or softer)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to mollify, to soften (to make less intense, harsh, painful, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to mollify (to appease, pacify)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, intransitive) to become soft, to go soft, to soften (to become gentle or meek in character)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μαλακώνω, from Koine Greek μαλακῶ, with -ώνω suffix.</div><b>μαλαματένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαλαματένια, neuter μαλαματένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, literary, poetic) gold, golden (made of gold)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) golden, tender (of heart, words, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μαλαματένια</b> λόγια στο μαντήλι,
Τα βρήκα στο σεργιάνι μου προχθές - Golden words in the handkerchief,
I found them on my walk the day before yesterday.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μάλαμα.</div><b>μαλαπέρδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαλαπέρδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) cock, prick, dick (penis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unknown</div><b>μαλαχίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) malachite</div><b>μαλαχώδη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Malvales.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μαλάχη + -ώδη, a calque of translingual Malvales.</div><b>μαλγασικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Malagasy (the language spoken in Madagascar)</div><b>μαλγασικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαλγασική, neuter μαλγασικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Madagascan (relating to Madagascar or its people)</div><b>μαλινέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαλινέζικη, neuter μαλινέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Malian (of or pertaining to Mali or its people)</div><b>μαλλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mall, shopping mall</div><b>μαλλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαλλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wool</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) all the hairs of the head</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μαλλίν, from Ancient Greek μαλλίον, diminutive of μαλλός.</div><b>μαλλί της γριάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαλλιά της γριάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>candy floss (UK), cotton candy (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαλλί γριά</div><b>μαλλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hair (the hairs of the head collectively)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the plural of μαλλί.</div><b>μαλλιά της γριάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μαλλί">μαλλί</a> <a href="bword://της">της</a> γριάς</div><b>μαλτέζικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Maltese, the language of Malta.</div><b>μαλτέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαλτέζικη, neuter μαλτέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Maltese (relating to Malta or its people or language)</div><b>μαλτεζικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαλτεζική, neuter μαλτεζικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαλτέζικος">μαλτέζικος</a></div><b>μαλωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μαλωμένη, neuter μαλωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fighting, quarreling, not on speaking terms</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας της κι αυτή είναι χρόνια <b>μαλωμένοι</b>. - She and her father have been quarreling for years now.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of μαλώνω, a verb with no passive forms.</div><b>μαλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to tell off, scold, rebuke, chide, give out (criticise harshly)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μάλωσα</b> τον γιο μου επειδή έβαλε ένα πιρούνι στην πρίζα. - I told my son off for putting a fork into the socket.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to argue, quarrel, fight (have a verbal dispute)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε φορά που <b>μαλώνουν</b> οι διπλανοί τους ακούει όλη η γειτονιά. - Every time the people next door argue the whole neighbourhood hears.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly, intransitive) to not be on speaking terms (after an argument)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εγώ και ο πατέρας μου είμαστε τώρα δύο χρόνια <b>μαλωμένοι</b>. - My father and I haven&#x27;t been on speaking terms for two years now.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μαλώνω &lt; *ὁμαλώνω &lt; *ὁμαλόω, from Ancient Greek ὁμαλός.</div><b>μαμά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (familiar) mom, mum, ma</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, rare, slang) a vehicle with unmodified factory-built equipment.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μάμμα with one &lt; μ &gt; (simplification of spelling) and recessive accent as in the French maman. From the Ancient Greek μάμμη</div><b>μαμίσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαμίσια, neuter μαμίσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, slang) unmodified, factory-built (for vehicles or vehicle equipment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derivative of μαμά + -ίσιος</div><b>μαμμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαμμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) midwife</div><b>μαμμόθρεφτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαμμόθρεφτη, neuter μαμμόθρεφτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a child but also of adults) pampered, mollycoddled, spoilt (treat with excessive care, attention or indulgence and thus unable to function without help)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι περιμένεις; Στη σημερινή μέρα, όλοι οι νέοι είναι <b>μαμμόθρεφτοι</b> και εντελώς ανίκανοι. - What do you expect? Nowadays, all young people are mollycoddled and completely incompetent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek μαμμόθρεπτος, initially &quot;one who is fed by grandmother&quot;, from μάμμη + θρεπτός, verbal adjective from τρέφω. Cognate with English mammothrept.</div><b>μαμούθ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mammoth</div><b>μανάβης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μανάβηδες, feminine μανάβισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greengrocer (person who sells fresh vegetables and fruit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγα στο <b>μανάβη</b> να μου δώσει φρέσκες φράουλες. - I went to the greengrocer so he could give me fresh strawberries.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish مناو (manav), of unknown origin.</div><b>μανάβικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανάβικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greengrocer&#x27;s, greengrocer&#x27;s shop, greengrocery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μανάβ(ης), “greengrocer”) + -ικο (-iko), neuter singular of -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>μανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mania</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rage, frenzy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. obsession</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μανία.</div><b>μανίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sleeve</div><b>μανδαρίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μανδαρίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mandarin, Chinese court official</div><b>μανδαϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Mandaism/Mandaeism</div><b>μανδύας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μανδύες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cloak, cape, mantle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mantle (of the Earth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μανδύας.</div><b>μανεκέν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mannequin, dummy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French mannequin.</div><b>μανιάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μανιάτικη, neuter μανιάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Maniot, of or relating to the Mani</div><b>μανιακός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μανιακοί, feminine μανιακή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maniac</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μανιακός</div><b>μανιβέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μανιβέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crank (bent piece of an axle or shaft used to impart rotation)</div><b>μανικιούρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) manicure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French manucure, coming from Latin manus &#x27;hand&#x27; and cūra &#x27;care&#x27;.</div><b>μανιοκατάθλιψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μανιοκαταθλίψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>manic depression</div><b>μανιτάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανιτάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mushroom (the fruiting body of a fungus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek mushroom name ἀμᾱνί̄της, from the mountain range Ᾰ̓́μᾱνος with the provenience suffix -ῑ́της, plus the later colloquial suffix + -άρι.</div><b>μανιφέστο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανιφέστα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>manifesto</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian manifesto.</div><b>μανιχαϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Manichaism/Manichaeism</div><b>μανουάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανουάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A large floor standing candlestick, often with a sand-lined area for votive candles.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μανουάλι(ο)νn), from Latin manuale (“candelabrum”).</div><b>μανουσάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανουσάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (regional) Narcissus tazetta</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (regional) violet (Viola odorata)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Middle Armenian մանուշակbor.
Byzantine Greek μανουσιάκων
Greek μανουσάκι
Already as Byzantine Greek μανουσιάκων, from Old Armenian մանուշակ. Cognate with Pontic Greek μανουσ̌άκ’. Doublet of μενεξές.</div><b>μανούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cream cheese</div><b>μανσέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μανσέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cuff (the end of a shirt sleeve that covers the wrist)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French manchette, with -α (-a) suffix.</div><b>μαντάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to darn (woven repair to cloth)</div><b>μαντέμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cast iron</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Arabic عَدَنَ (ʕadana)
Arabic مَعْدِن (maʕdin)bor.
Ottoman Turkish معدن (maden)bor.
Greek μαντέμι
Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish معدن (maden), borrowed from Arabic مَعْدِن (maʕdin), from عَدَنَ (ʕadana).</div><b>μαντήλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαντήλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>large mantilla or headscarf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μαντήλη, μανδίλιον, μαντίλιον, μανδήλη, from Latin mantēle, mantēlium.</div><b>μαντήλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαντήλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. handkerchief</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small mantilla or headscarf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin mantelium or mantilium (“napkin”).</div><b>μαντίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαντίλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαντήλι">μαντήλι</a></div><b>μανταρίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανταρίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tangerine, mandarin orange</div><b>μανταρινιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μανταρινιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mandarin orange tree, tangerine tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μανταρίν(ι)) + -ιά.</div><b>μαντείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαντεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oracle (a shrine dedicated to some prophetic deity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>μαντείο</b> των Δελφών - the oracle of Delphi</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μαντεῖον.</div><b>μαντεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be foretold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be guessed</div><b>μαντεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to prophesy, foretell, predict</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to guess</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek μαντεύω, from mediopassive Ancient Greek μαντεύομαι.</div><b>μαντζαφλάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαντζαφλάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) widget, doodah (UK), doodad (UK), thingummy, thingy (something with unknown name)</div><b>μαντζουράνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαντζουράνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>marjoram</div><b>μαντική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://μαντικός">μαντικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>divination (the apparent art of discovering secrets or the future by preternatural means)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ellipsis of μαντική τέχνη.</div><b>μαντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαντική, neuter μαντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oracular (of or relating to an oracle)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. divinatory (pertaining to divination)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μαντικός. By surface analysis, μάντ(ης)) + -ικός.</div><b>μαντολάτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαντολάτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nougat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan mandolato. In Italian, mandorlato.</div><b>μαντολίνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαντολίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mandolin</div><b>μαντούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαντούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>floghera (Cretan wind instrument)</div><b>μαντράσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαντράσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) school</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic مدرسة (madrasa).</div><b>μαντρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαντριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pen, fold, enclosure (for animals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μαντρί, from μανδρίον; diminutive of Ancient Greek μάνδρα.</div><b>μαντρότοιχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαντρότοιχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wall, fence (enclosing a yard, pen, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μάντρα + τοίχος</div><b>μανόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μανόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>manometer</div><b>μαξιλάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαξιλάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bolster, large cushion</div><b>μαξιλάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαξιλάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pillow</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Άννα κοιμάται με δύο <b>μαξιλάρια</b>. - Anna sleeps with two pillows.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cushion</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορείς να μου δώσεις το <b>μαξιλάρι</b> από τον καναπέ, παρακαλώ; - Could you please hand me the cushion off the couch?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μαξιλάριν, from earlier μαξιλάριον, μαξιλλάριον, derived with a diminutive suffix from Latin māxillāris (“of the jaw”) or māxilla, māxillārum.</div><b>μαξιλαροθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαξιλαροθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pillowcase</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μαξιλάρ(ι)) + -ο- (-o-) + -θήκη.</div><b>μαοϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Maoism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French maoïsme.</div><b>μαοϊστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine μαοΐστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(male) Maoist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Μάο”) + -ιστής.</div><b>μαούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαούνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barge, lighter</div><b>μαρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>picarel (Spicara smaris)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σμαρίς, via the accusative singular σμᾰρῐ́δᾰ.</div><b>μαραίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to wither, shrivel, wilt</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το λουλούδι <b>μαραίνεται</b>. - The flower is withered.</div><b>μαραίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to wither, shrivel</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ήλιος <b>μαραίνει</b> το λουλούδι. - The sun withers the flower.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to eat away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μᾰραίνω.</div><b>μαραγκός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαραγκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) carpenter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan marangon (“carpenter”), from a Medieval Latin development of Latin mergus (“diver, loon”); see there for more.</div><b>μαραθικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαραθική, neuter μαραθικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Marathi</div><b>μαραθωνοδρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural μαραθωνοδρόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>marathon runner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μαραθών(ιος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -δρόμος.</div><b>μαραθόριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαραθόριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Florence fennel</div><b>μαραθώνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαραθώνια, neuter μαραθώνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of or relating to Marathon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) during exhaustive or long and tedious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Μαραθών + -ιος (-ios).
Sense “marathon” is a calque of French marathon, ultimately from the same source.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαραθώνιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. marathon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, figuratively) marathon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Μαραθών + -ιος (-ios).
Sense “marathon” is a calque of French marathon, ultimately from the same source.</div><b>μαρασκίνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαρασκίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maraschino (sweet liqueur made from marasca cherries)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian maraschino.</div><b>μαραφέτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαραφέτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. widget, doodah (UK), doodad (UK), thingummy, thingy (something with forgotten or unknown name)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gizmo, contraption (clever or complicated mechanism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish معرفت, from Arabic مَعْرِفَة (maʕrifa).</div><b>μαργαρίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαργαρίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>margarine, spread</div><b>μαργαρίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαργαρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. daisy (flower)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. daisywheel, print-wheel</div><b>μαργαριτάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαργαριτάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pearl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, ironic) a serious mistake in grammar or syntax</div>
  <div class='ex'>synonyms: linguistic terms: βαρβαρισμός (varvarismós) for grammar mistakes, σολοικισμός (soloikismós) for syntax mistakes</div><b>μαργράβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαργράβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) margrave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French margrave + -ος (-os), from Middle Dutch marcgrāve.</div><b>μαριονέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαριονέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>puppet, marionette</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French marionnette (“puppet, marionette”).</div><b>μαριονέττα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαριονέττες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαριονέτα">μαριονέτα</a></div><b>μαριχουάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) marijuana</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Spanish marihuana.</div><b>μαρκάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be marked, be branded, be ticked (off)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το χαρτί <b>μαρκαρίστηκε</b> από τον δάσκαλο. - The paper was marked by the teacher.</div><b>μαρκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of a surface) to mark, stamp, tick (with a writing implement or stamp)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δάσκαλος <b>μάρκαρε</b> την εξέταση. - The teacher marked the exam.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of an animal) to brand</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αγρότης <b>μαρκάρει</b> τις αγελάδες του. - The farmer brands his cows.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to mark (follow an opposing player to prevent scoring or passing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>μαρκάρισε</b> και πήρε την μπάλα. - He marked him and took the ball.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to spot, pick out (in a crowd)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας <b>μαρκάρει</b> την κόρη του στο πλήθος. - The father picks out his daughter in the crowd.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian marcare.</div><b>μαρκαδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαρκαδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>felt-tip pen, marker, marker pen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Spanish marcador (“marker”) or μαρκάρω + -αδόρος.</div><b>μαρκαρισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μαρκαρισμένη, neuter μαρκαρισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stamped, marked, ticked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μαρκάρομαι, passive voice of μαρκάρω.</div><b>μαρμαρυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαρμαρυγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scintillation, shining, shimmering</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fibrillation, palpitation</div><b>μαρμελάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαρμελάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jam; marmalade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French marmelade.</div><b>μαρμότα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαρμότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>marmot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed either from French marmotte or Italian marmotta.</div><b>μαρξίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαρξίστριες, masculine μαρξιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Marxist</div><b>μαρξικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαρξική, neuter μαρξικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Marxian (relating to Karl Marx)</div><b>μαρξισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural μαρξισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) Marxism</div><b>μαρξισμός-λενινισμός</b>
  <div><i>name</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Marxism-Leninism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Russian марксизм-ленинизм (marksizm-leninizm). By surface analysis, μαρξισμός + λενινισμός.</div><b>μαρξιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαρξιστές, feminine μαρξίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Marxist</div><b>μαρξιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαρξιστική, neuter μαρξιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Marxist (relating to Marxism)</div><b>μαροκινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαροκινή, neuter μαροκινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Moroccan (of or pertaining to Morocco or its people)</div><b>μαρουλοσαλάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαρουλοσαλάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lettuce salad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαρούλι + σαλάτα.</div><b>μαρουλόφυλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαρουλόφυλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lettuce leaf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαρούλι + φύλλο</div><b>μαρούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαρούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lettuce (vegetable)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μαρούλιον.</div><b>μαρσάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to rev, to rev up, to gun (to press the accelerator of a motor vehicle hard)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μαρσάρισε</b> σκληρά, ώσπου ένα σύννεφο σκόνης ξεσηκώθηκε και τον τύλιξε. - He revved hard, until a cloud of dust rose up and coiled around him.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to make a revving/vrooming noise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French march(er) + -άρω.</div><b>μαρτυράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαρτυρώ">μαρτυρώ</a></div><b>μαρτυρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαρτυρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. testimony</div>
  <div class='def'>2. account</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μᾰρτῠρῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>μαρτυρημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μαρτυρημένη, neuter μαρτυρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. testified</div>
  <div class='def'>2. betrayed, revealed</div>
  <div class='def'>3. attested</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μαρτυρούμαι and μαρτυριέμαι, passive forms of μαρτυρώ and μαρτυράω respectively.</div><b>μαρτυρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to bear witness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to attest, reveal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to testify, give evidence</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to inform against, tell, betray (mostly for the form <a href="bword://μαρτυράω">μαρτυράω</a>)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (for <a href="bword://μαρτυρώ">μαρτυρώ</a>) to be martyred</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μαρτυρῶ, contracted form of μαρτυρέω.</div><b>μαρτύριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαρτύρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. torture, agony</div>
  <div class='def'>2. martyrdom</div><b>μας</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, possessive, pronoun, weak) our (1st person plural, genitive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal, possessive, pronoun, weak) us (1st person plural, accusative)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (personal, possessive, pronoun, weak) our (belonging to us)</div><b>μασάει η κατσίκα ταραμά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, figuratively, humorous) no one can deceive me, I was not born yesterday (literally does the goat chew roe?)</div><b>μασάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) massage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French massage.</div><b>μασάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to chew, masticate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) to squander (money)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μασώ, from Byzantine Greek μασῶ, μασίζω, from a blend of Ancient Greek μασάομαι and μαστάζω.</div><b>μασέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μασέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dentures, false teeth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dentition</div><b>μασίς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French macis.</div><b>μασιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μασιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire tongs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ماشه, from Classical Persian ماشه.</div><b>μασκάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μασκάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μασχάλη">μασχάλη</a></div><b>μασκαράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μασκαράδες, feminine μασκαρού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. masquerader</div>
  <div class='def'>2. buffoon</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trickster</div><b>μασκοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masked (wearing a mask)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μασκ (mask) + -ο- (-o-) + -φόρος</div><b>μασούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μασούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek, Modern, Standard) yarn or thread rolled in the shape of a cylinder</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Greek, Modern, Standard) a shape of tube of any objects (informal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα <b>μασούρι</b> λίρες - a tube of pound coins</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Egyptiot) pipe, tube, duct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μασούριον, from Ottoman Turkish ماسوره (masura).
The Egyptiot Greek word, borrowed from Egyptian Arabic ماسورة.</div><b>μαστίγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαστίγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μάστιξ, from the accusative singular μάστιγα.</div><b>μαστίγωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαστιγώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whipping; flagellation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek μαστίγωσις, from Ancient Greek μάστιξ.</div><b>μαστίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be scourged, infested</div><b>μαστίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to scourge, plague, infest</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια επιδημία <b>μαστίζει</b> τον πληθυσμό. - A plague is hitting the populace.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαστίζω.</div><b>μαστίχα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαστίχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mastic (resin and flavouring)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chewing gum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μαστίχα, from Koine Greek μαστίχη.</div><b>μασταμπάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μασταμπάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mastaba</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish مصطبه (mastaba).</div><b>μαστιγώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, literally) to flog, to whip</div><b>μαστιχιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαστιχιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mastic tree, Pistacia lentiscus.</div><b>μαστιχόδενδρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαστιχόδενδρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαστιχόδεντρο">μαστιχόδεντρο</a></div><b>μαστιχόδεντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαστιχόδεντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mastic tree, Pistacia lentiscus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαστίχα + δέντρο</div><b>μαστογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural μαστογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) mammography, mastography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, μαστός + -γραφία.</div><b>μαστροπός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαστροποί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pimp (prostitution)</div><b>μαστρωπός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://μαστροπός">μαστροπός</a></div><b>μαστός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαστοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. breast (mammary gland)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. udder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μαστός.</div><b>μασχάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μασχάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>armpit, axilla</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μασχάλη.</div><b>μασώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μασάω">μασάω</a></div><b>ματάκιας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ματάκηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peeping tom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μάτι + -άκιας</div><b>ματαίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in vain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ματαίως. By surface analysis, μάται(ος)) + -ως (-os)</div><b>ματαίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ματαιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cancellation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thwarting, stymieing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ματαιώ(νω)) + -ση (-si). Compare Koine Greek ματαίωσις.</div><b>ματαιοδοξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ματαιοδοξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vanity, vainglory, conceit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ματαιόδοξ(ος)) + -ία, a calque of French vaine gloire or Italian vanagloria, from Medieval Latin vāna glōria, itself a calque of Koine Greek κενοδοξία.</div><b>ματαιόδοξος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ματαιόδοξη, neuter ματαιόδοξο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vain, conceited, vainglorious</div><b>ματαιότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of ματαιότητα</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ματαιότης</b> <b>ματαιοτήτων</b>, τα πάντα <b>ματαιότης</b> - Vanity of vanities; all is vanity. (Ecclesiastes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ματαιότης.</div><b>ματαιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cancel (to invalidate or annul: planned activity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to thwart, foil, frustrate, stymie (a plan of intended action)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek ματαιῶ + -ώνω, with semantic loan from French rendre vain.</div><b>ματζέντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) magenta (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English magenta.</div><b>ματζουράνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ματζουράνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαντζουράνα">μαντζουράνα</a></div><b>ματζόρε</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) major (key signature or scale)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian maggiore.</div><b>ματιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ματιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>look, glance</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ρίξε μια <b>ματιά</b> σ’ αυτό το φάκελο! - Have a look at this folder!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μάτι.</div><b>ματιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to give or put the evil eye</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ματιάζω. By surface analysis, μάτ(ι), “eye”) + -ιάζω.</div><b>ματοβαμμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine ματοβαμμένη, neuter ματοβαμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αιματοβαμμένος">αιματοβαμμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αιματοβαμμένος by aphesis.</div><b>ματοκυλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αιματοκυλίζω">αιματοκυλίζω</a></div><b>ματοκυλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to slaughter, massacre</div><b>ματοκύλισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ματοκυλίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αιματοκύλισμα">αιματοκύλισμα</a></div><b>ματοτσίνορο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ματοτσίνορα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eyelash</div><b>ματοτσίνουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ματοτσίνουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ματοτσίνορο">ματοτσίνορο</a></div><b>ματς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) match (usually football match)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English match.</div><b>ματωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ματωμένη, neuter ματωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bloody, bloodstained, bleeding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Participle of ματώνω</div><b>ματόκλαδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ματόκλαδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal) eyelash</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (used in the plural) eyelashes</div><b>ματώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cut, bleed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to stain with blood</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From αιματώνω with aphesis.</div><b>μαυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (active, transitive) to blacken, make black</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (active, intransitive) to blacken, become black</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (active, intransitive) to tan, suntan</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (for passive voice) to vote against (for a person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μαυρίζω from Ancient Greek μαῦρος. Sense vote against from an old way of voting with small spherical &#x27;votes&#x27;, white (positive) and black (negative).</div><b>μαυρίζω στο ξύλο</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to beat up, beat to a pulp, beat the crap out of, beat black and blue</div><b>μαυραγορίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαυραγορίτες, feminine μαυραγορίτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black marketeer</div><b>μαυραγορίτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαυραγορίτισσες, masculine μαυραγορίτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black marketeer</div><b>μαυρικιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαυρικιανή, neuter μαυρικιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mauritian (of or pertaining to Mauritius or its people)</div><b>μαυρισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μαυρισμένη, neuter μαυρισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blacked, blackened</div>
  <div class='def'>2. suntanned</div>
  <div class='def'>3. voted against (with a black ballot)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μαυρίζομαι, passive voice of μαυρίζω.</div><b>μαυριτανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαυριτανική, neuter μαυριτανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mauritanian (of or pertaining to Mauritania or its people)</div><b>μαυροβουνιακά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Montenegrin (language)</div><b>μαυροβουνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαυροβουνιακή, neuter μαυροβουνιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Montenegrin (relating to Montenegro or its people or language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Μαυροβούνιο + -ακός.</div><b>μαυρομάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαυρομάνικη, neuter μαυρομάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of an object) black-handled; having a black handle or grip.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μαυρομάνικος. By surface analysis, μαυρο- (mavro-, “black-”) + μανίκι + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix), literally “black-sleeved”.</div><b>μαυροπίνακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαυροπίνακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blackboard (black board that can be written upon with chalk)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο μαθητής γράφει με κιμωλία στον <b>μαυροπίνακα</b>. - The student writes with chalk on the blackboard.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαυρο- (mavro-, “black”) + πίνακας, calque of French tableau noir. First attested 1838.</div><b>μαυροπεριστερόκοτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαυροπεριστερόκοτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black-bellied sandgrouse (Pterocles orientalis)</div><b>μαυροπούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαυροπούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) starling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μαύρος + πουλί.</div><b>μαυροσταχτάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαυροσταχτάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common swift (Apus apus)</div><b>μαυροτσικλιτάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαυροτσικλιτάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) black woodpecker</div><b>μαυροψάρονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαυροψάρονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spotless starling, Sturnus unicolor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαύρος + ψαρόνι</div><b>μαυρόλαιμη τσίχλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαυρόλαιμες τσίχλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turdus atrogularis: the black-throated thrush</div><b>μαυρόλαιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαυρόλαιμη, neuter μαυρόλαιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black-throated, black-necked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαυρός + λαιμός</div><b>μαυρόχωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαυροχώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>humus (soil component)</div><b>μαυρόψαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαυρόψαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://μελανούρι">μελανούρι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, μαυρο- (mavro-, “black”) + ψάρι + -ο (-o), literally “blackfish”.</div><b>μαυσωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαυσωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mausoleum (large tomb)</div><b>μαφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mafia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian mafia.</div><b>μαφιόζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαφιόζικη, neuter μαφιόζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relating to a mafia</div><b>μαφιόζος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαφιόζοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mafioso, gangster, mobster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian mafioso.</div><b>μαφόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαφόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. veil, head-dress of women and monks (ecclesiastical use)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a thin, usually purple, veil that covers the head and shoulders of the Virgin Mary and other female saints</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μαφόριον. Cognate with Latin mafortium (“woman’s head-dress, veil”), Latin maforte, Latin mafors, Koine Greek μαφόρτης.</div><b>μαχαίρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαχαίρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. large knife; augmentative of <a href="bword://μαχαίρι">μαχαίρι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. butcher&#x27;s knife</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably a derivative of μαχαίρι, ultimately from Ancient Greek μάχαιρα.</div><b>μαχαίρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαχαίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. knife</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dagger</div>
  <div class='def'>3. guillotine (for cutting paper)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek μαχαίριον, diminutive of Ancient Greek μάχαιρα. Doublet of μάχαιρα.
Compare Mariupol Greek маше́р.</div><b>μαχαιρίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαχαιρίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scalpel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small knife</div><b>μαχαιροπήρουνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαχαιροπήρουνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαχαιροπίρουνο">μαχαιροπίρουνο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Dvandva compounds of μαχαίρι + -ο- (-o-) + πιρούνι.</div><b>μαχαιροπίρουνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαχαιροπίρουνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (usually) knife and fork</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, usually) cutlery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Dvandva compounds of μαχαίρι + -ο- (-o-) + πιρούνι.</div><b>μαχαιρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to stab, knife</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μαχαίρι.</div><b>μαχαιρώνωμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be stabbed</div><b>μαχητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαχητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>warrior, fighter, combatant</div><b>μαϊμού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαϊμούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) knockoff</div>
  <div class='def'>2. monkey</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μαϊμού, from Ottoman Turkish میمون (maymun), from Arabic مَيْمُون.</div><b>μαϊντανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαϊντανοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. parsley (both the plant and herb)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μαϊντανός</b>, φασκόμηλο, δεντρολίβανο και θυμάρι. - Parsley, sage, rosemary and thyme.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, humorous) rent-a-gob (someone who regularly appears in the media, especially to stir controversy or self-aggrandize)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αμάν πιά μ’ αυτόν τον <b>μαϊντανό</b>, τον έχει βαρεθεί η ψυχή μου να τον βλέπω συνεχώς στα κανάλια να λέει τις μπούρδες του! - Enough with that rent-a-gob, I am sick to the back teeth of seeing him on the TV channels spouting his crap!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish معدنوس (maydanos), from Arabic مَعْدُونِس; ultimately from Byzantine Greek μακεδονήσιον.</div><b>μαϊστράλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>light, gentle mistral</div><b>μαϊστροτραμουντάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) north-northwesterly Mediterranean wind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μαΐστρος + τραμουντάνα</div><b>μαϊτνέριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) meitnerium</div><b>μαόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mahogany (red-brown wood of the tree genus Swietenia)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) mahogany furniture or fittings</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κρεβατοκάμαρα της ήταν όλο <b>μαόνι</b>. - Her bedroom was all in mahogany.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French mahagoni.</div><b>μαύρα μάτια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μαύρο">μαύρο</a> <a href="bword://μάτι">μάτι</a></div><b>μαύρες αγορές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μαύρη <a href="bword://αγορά">αγορά</a></div><b>μαύρες τρύπες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μαύρη <a href="bword://τρύπα">τρύπα</a></div><b>μαύρη αγορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαύρες αγορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black market</div><b>μαύρη τρύπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μαύρες τρύπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black hole</div><b>μαύρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαυρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. suntan, suntanning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blackening</div>
  <div class='def'>3. blemish (on fruit)</div><b>μαύρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαύρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. black (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) negative vote</div>
  <div class='ex'>ρίχνω <b>μαύρο</b> - I vote (literally: throw in) black.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (slang) hashish</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://μαύρος">μαύρος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://μαύρος">μαύρος</a></div><b>μαύρο μάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μαύρα μάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black eye</div><b>μαύρο ψωμί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brown bread</div><b>μαύρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαύρη, neuter μαύρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of black (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>for the color/colour, see μαύρο n (mávro, noun)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) gloomy, dark, grim</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) depressed, miserable</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) grim, terrible, evil</div>
  <div class='def'>5. bold</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek μαῦρος, from Ancient Greek ἀμαυρός. Also substantivised.</div><b>μαύρος δρυοκολάπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαύροι δρυοκολάπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black woodpecker</div><b>μαύρος κι άραχνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μαύρη κι άραχνη, neuter μαύρο κι άραχνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) dreary, bleak, miserable</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα βλέπει όλα <b>μαύρα κι άραχνα</b>. - He sees everything in a miserable way.</div><b>μαύρος φραγκολίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μαύροι φραγκολίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black francolin, Francolinus francolinus</div><b>με</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with accusative) with</div>
  <div class='ex'>μίλησε <b>με</b> την Ελένη - he talked with Eleni</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with accusative) by</div>
  <div class='ex'>πουλάει <b>με</b> το κιλό - she sells by the kilo</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with accusative) by, on</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>με</b> το αυτοκίνητο - by car</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a metanalysis of the Byzantine Greek phrase μεταῦτα + ταῦτα), from Ancient Greek μετά.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, weak) me (1st person singular, accusative)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Με</b> μισεί … <b>μ’</b> αγαπά! - She hates me … she loves me!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek με (me, “me”).</div><b>με ευχέρεια</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fluent, with ease</div><b>με λένε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. they call me … (literal translation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. my name is …</div><b>με λίγα λόγια</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in short, in a few words, in a nutshell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> με (me, “with”) + λίγα + λόγια</div><b>με νύχια και με δόντια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) tooth and nail (with all one&#x27;s strength or power)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάλεψε <b>με νύχια και με δόντια</b> για να σώσει το παιδί από τη θάλασσα. - He fought tooth and nail to save the child from the sea.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English tooth and nail.</div><b>με πήρε το ποτάμι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>he was financially ruined</div><b>με πιάνει αμόκ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to run amok, run amuck</div><b>με πορδές δεν βάφονται αβγά</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous) A task can&#x27;t be completed correctly if one doesn&#x27;t work hard or do all that is necessary.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;With farts, eggs can&#x27;t be/aren&#x27;t dyed&quot;.</div><b>με τα πόδια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>on foot</div><b>με το αζημίωτο</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. for a consideration</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in expectation of a profit</div><b>μείγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μείγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mixture, a combination of materials</div><b>μείγμα πέντε καρυκευμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>five-spice powder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μείγμα + πέντε + καρυκευμάτων</div><b>μείγμα πέντε μπαχαρικών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>five-spice powder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μείγμα + πέντε + μπαχαρικών</div><b>μείζων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μείζων, neuter μείζον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. major, greater, more important</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μείζων</b> καταθλιπτική διαταραχή - major depressive disorder</div>
  <div class='def'>2. major (key signature, scale, chord or interval)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μείζων</b> κλίμακα - major key</div><b>μείον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minus (less)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μεῖον.</div><b>μείωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μειώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. decrease, reduction: the process, the rate of that process or its result</div>
  <div class='def'>2. decrease</div>
  <div class='ex'>colloquial abbreviation μει (mei)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. meiosis, reduction division of a cell</div>
  <div class='def'>4. humiliation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μείωσις.</div><b>μεγάκυκλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεγάκυκλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>megacycle</div><b>μεγάλα ονόματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μεγάλο <a href="bword://όνομα">όνομα</a></div><b>μεγάλε</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masculine vocative singular of <a href="bword://μεγάλος">μεγάλος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Expresses approval, affirmation, recognition</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μεγάλε</b>! Τα κατάφερες! - Dude! You did it!</div><b>μεγάλες ωτίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of μεγάλη <a href="bword://ωτίδα">ωτίδα</a></div><b>μεγάλη ωτίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεγάλες ωτίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great bustard, Otis tarda</div><b>μεγάλο όνομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεγάλα ονόματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>surname, last name, family name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally: μεγάλο + όνομα n</div><b>μεγάλοι τελικοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μεγάλος">μεγάλος</a> <a href="bword://τελικός">τελικός</a></div><b>μεγάλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεγάλη, neuter μεγάλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. big, large, great (of greater than average size)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιαγιά, τι <b>μεγάλα</b> δόντια που έχεις. - Grandma, what big teeth you have.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tall, high (of greater than average height)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έκοψαν τα <b>μεγάλα</b> δέντρα. - They cut down the tall trees.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. big, great (of greater than average intensity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο πρόσωπό του φαινόταν <b>μεγάλη</b> χαρά. - One could see great happiness on his face.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. long (of greater than average length of time)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το καλοκαίρι οι μέρες είναι <b>μεγάλες</b> ενώ τον χειμώνα είναι μικρές. - In the summer the days are long, while in the winter they&#x27;re short.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. adult, grown-up (having reached the adult age)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο κύριο τραπέζι, κάθονται μόνο οι <b>μεγάλοι</b>. - Only the adults sit at the main table.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. old, elderly (having reached an advanced age)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας μου είναι <b>μεγάλος</b> άνθρωπος και δεν περπατάει καλά. - My father is an old man and doesn&#x27;t walk very well.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. big, great (important, superior or famous)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αύριο είναι η <b>μεγάλη</b> μέρα. - Tomorrow is the big day.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (of letters) uppercase, capital</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το όνομά μου να γράφει με <b>μεγάλα</b> γράμματα. - My name is to be written in capital letters.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μεγάλος, from Ancient Greek μέγας from its stem μεγαλ-, from Proto-Indo-European *méǵh₂s (“big, great”).</div><b>μεγάλος τελικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεγάλοι τελικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cup Final</div><b>μεγάλος τσικνιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεγάλοι τσικνιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great egret (bird - Ardea alba)</div><b>μεγάφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεγάφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loudspeaker, speaker</div>
  <div class='def'>2. megaphone</div><b>μεγααιώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεγααιώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μεγα- (mega-, “large”) + αιώνας</div><b>μεγαβάτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) megawatt</div><b>μεγαλακρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) acromegalia, acromegaly</div><b>μεγαλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεγαλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. greatness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grandeur, magnificence, majesty, splendour (UK), splendor (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) splendours (UK), splendors (US), trappings of greatness (indications of elevated status, wealth, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μεγαλεῖον, nominalized neuter form from Ancient Greek μεγαλεῖος, with semantic loan from French grandeurs in the plural usage.</div><b>μεγαλειώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεγαλειώδης, neuter μεγαλειώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grand, grandiose, magnificent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μεγαλείο + -ώδης, a calque of French grandiose.</div><b>μεγαλομάρτυρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural μεγαλομάρτυρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great martyr</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek μεγαλομάρτυρα, accusative singular of μεγαλομάρτυς. By surface analysis, μεγαλο- (“great”) + μάρτυρας (“martyr; witness”).</div><b>μεγαλομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεγαλομανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>megalomania</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French mégalomanie, a Greek classical compound from mégalo- + manie (μεγαλο- (“megalo-”) + μανία (“mania”)).</div><b>μεγαλοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to exaggerate, to overstate, to blow out of proportion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μεγαλοποιῶ, contracted form of μεγαλοποιέω. By surface analysis, μεγαλο- (megalo-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>μεγαλοπρέπεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magnificence, grandeur, majesty, splendour (UK), splendor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεγαλοπρέπεια. By surface analysis, μεγαλοπρεπ(ής)) + -εια (-eia).</div><b>μεγαλοπρεπής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεγαλοπρεπής, neuter μεγαλοπρεπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magnificent, grandiose, majestic, splendid (grand and impressive in appearance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: επιβλητικός (epivlitikós), μεγαλειώδης (megaleiódis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεγαλοπρεπής.</div><b>μεγαλούτσικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεγαλούτσικη or μεγαλούτσικια, neuter μεγαλούτσικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. biggish, largish, big enough, somewhat big (but not very big)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. old enough, oldish, somewhat old (but not very old) (of a person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είδες τον καινούριο φίλο της Μαρίας; <b>Μεγαλούτσικος</b> δεν είναι; - Have you seen Mary&#x27;s new boyfriend? He is rather old, isn&#x27;t he?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μεγάλος + -ούτσικος</div><b>μεγαλόδοντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεγαλόδοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>megalodon (Otodus megalodon)</div><b>μεγαλόφωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεγαλόφωνη, neuter μεγαλόφωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of a person) loud, loud-voiced</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of an utterance or exclamation) loud, booming</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεγαλόφωνος. By surface analysis, μεγάλος + -ό- + φωνή + -ος (-os).</div><b>μεγαλόψυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεγαλόψυχη, neuter μεγαλόψυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magnanimous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεγαλόψυχος.</div><b>μεγαλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to enlarge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to magnify</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to increase, to make bigger</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to increase, to get bigger, to grow</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of days) to get longer</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to bring up, raise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μεγαλώνω. By surface analysis, μεγάλος + -ώνω.</div><b>μεγαχέρτζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) megahertz</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μεγα- (mega-, “mega-”) + χερτζ (chertz, “hertz”).</div><b>μεγεθυντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεγεθυντική, neuter μεγεθυντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. magnifying</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μεγεθυντικός</b> φακός - magnifying glass</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (augmentative) augmentative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μεγεθύν(ω)) + -τικός, a calque of English magnifying and German Vergrößerungs- (“magnifying”) and of Medieval Latin augmentativus (“augmentative”).</div><b>μεγεθύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to enlarge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to magnify</div><b>μεδούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) bone marrow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μεδούλιον, from μεδούλη, from Latin medulla.</div><b>μεζές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεζέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. snack, titbit, meze, tapas</div>
  <div class='def'>2. savoury</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish مزه (mezze, meze), from Classical Persian مزه (mazza, maza).</div><b>μεζονέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) a small house, flat or apartment</div><b>μεζουζά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Judaism, indeclinable) mezuzah</div><b>μεθ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of <a href="bword://μετά">μετά</a> (used before a word which had rough breathing in Ancient Greek)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of <a href="bword://μετά">μετά</a> (used before a word which had rough breathing in Ancient Greek)</div><b>μεθάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>methane</div><b>μεθάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μεθώ">μεθώ</a></div><b>μεθανάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) formaldehyde, methanal</div><b>μεθανόλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) methanol, methyl alcohol</div><b>μεθαύριο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>day after tomorrow</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μεθαύριο</b> θα πάμε διακοπές. - The day after tomorrow, we&#x27;ll go on holidays.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek μεθαύριον, equivalent to μεθ- (meth-, “after”) + αύριο.</div><b>μεθιονίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>methionine</div><b>μεθοδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεθοδική, neuter μεθοδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>methodical, orderly, businesslike, systematic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek μεθοδῐκός.</div><b>μεθοδολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεθοδολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>methodology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French méthodologie, from méthode (“method”) and -logie (“-logy”); finally from Ancient Greek μέθοδος and λέγω.</div><b>μεθυλένιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεθυλένια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>methylene</div><b>μεθυλική αλκοόλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>methanol, methyl alcohol</div><b>μεθυλομάδα</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine (plural μεθυλομάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>methyl group</div><b>μεθυσμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μεθυσμένη, neuter μεθυσμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drunk, intoxicated, inebriated, drunken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of μεθάω / μεθώ, a verb without passive forms.
The perfective stem μεθυσ- (methys-) as in simple past μέθυσα. From the active Ancient Greek μεθύω, μεθῶ where the μεθυσ- stem is supplied from verb μεθύσκω.</div><b>μεθυστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεθυστική, neuter μεθυστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intoxicating, liking drunkenness</div><b>μεθύλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεθύλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>methyl</div>
  <div class='ex'>ισοκυανικό μεθύλιο (methyl isocyanate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> French méthyle, from Ancient Greek μέθυ.</div><b>μεθύσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεθύσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drunkenness</div><b>μεθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to get drunk, become intoxicated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μεθύω.
Compare Mariupol Greek метъу́.</div><b>μεικτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεικτή, neuter μεικτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μικτός">μικτός</a></div><b>μειονέκτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μειονεκτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. handicap, drawback, disadvantage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. imperfection, defect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μειονεκτέω + ἔχω) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>μειονοψηφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μειονοψηφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less frequent form of of <a href="bword://μειοψηφία">μειοψηφία</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>and see πλειοψηφία (pleiopsifía)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French minorité, with μειονο- (meiono-)) + ψήφ(ος)) + -ία, created in analogy to the antonym πλειονοψηφία.</div><b>μειονότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μειονότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. minority group, minority (a group that forms only a small part of the population, whether it be for ethnic or other reasons)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εθνοτική <b>μειονότητα</b> - ethnic minority</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) minority (any subgroup that does not form a numerical majority)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μείον + -ότητα), calque of French minorité. Created in analogy to πλειονότητα.</div><b>μειοψηφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μειοψηφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minority (any subgroup that does not form a numerical majority)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μειο- (meio-) + ψήφ(ος)) + -ία, modelled on πλειοψηφία. A calque of French minorité.</div><b>μεις</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εμείς">εμείς</a></div><b>μειχτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μειχτή, neuter μειχτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A rare form of <a href="bword://μεικτός">μεικτός</a></div><b>μειωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μειωμένη, neuter μειωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lower</div>
  <div class='def'>2. humiliated, fallen</div>
  <div class='def'>3. reduced, lessened</div><b>μειωτ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μειωτικός: derogatory</div><b>μειωτέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μειωτέοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minuend</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην αφαίρεση &quot;10 - 8 = 2&quot;, το &quot;10&quot; είναι ο <b>μειωτέος</b>. - In the subtraction &quot;10 - 8 = 2&quot;, &quot;10&quot; is the minuend.</div><b>μειόκαινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μειόκαινη, neuter μειόκαινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Miocene</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>μειόκαινη</b> εποχή - the Miocene epoch</div><b>μειώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to reduce, diminish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cut back, downsize</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to lower, diminish</div><b>μελάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μελάνι">μελάνι</a></div><b>μελάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μελάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ink</div>
  <div class='ex'>δοχείο μελανιού (ink pot, ink cartridge)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ink (sepia coloured ink from cuttlefish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μελάνιον.</div><b>μελάνωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελανώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ink stain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. melanoma</div><b>μελάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μελάτη, neuter μελάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. soft-boiled (of an egg (literally) honey like)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) very sweet</div>
  <div class='def'>3. A type of sponge in its natural habitat</div><b>μελένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μελένια, neuter μελένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. containing honey, honeyed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. honey-coloured</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) sugary, over-sweet</div><b>μελέτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>study (all senses)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μελέτη.</div><b>μελίγκρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελίγκρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aphid, greenfly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μελίκηρα.</div><b>μελίσσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μελίσσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bee colony, beehive</div><b>μελαγχολία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. melancholia, melancholy (great sadness or depression, especially of a thoughtful or introspective nature)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. melancholia, melancholic depression</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μελαγχολία.</div><b>μελαγχολώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be/become/feel melancholic</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μελαγχολώ</b>, μελαγχολείς - I feel melancholic, youˢⁱⁿᵍᵘˡᵃʳ feel melancholy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make melancholic, to put in a melancholic mood</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με <b>μελαγχολεί</b> ο βροχερός καιρός. - Rainy weather depresses me / makes me melancholy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μελαγχολῶ, as a contracted form of its Late Hellenistic Koine conjugation in -έω, μελαγχολέω, departing from the ancient -άω conjugation μελαγχολάω.</div><b>μελανές οπές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μελανή <a href="bword://οπή">οπή</a></div><b>μελανή οπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελανές οπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black hole</div><b>μελανίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) melanin</div><b>μελανιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine μελανιασμένη, neuter μελανιασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perfect perfect participle of μελανιάζω</div><b>μελανοκύτταρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melanocyte</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μελανο- (melano-, “black”) + κύτταρο</div><b>μελανούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μελανούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saddled seabream (Oblada melanura)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μελανούρι, from Ancient Greek μελάνουρος.</div><b>μελανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μελανή, neuter μελανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. black, dark, blue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bruised</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μέλας.</div><b>μελαχρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>one who has black hair and/or eyes, swarthy; brunet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μελαχρινός, from Ancient Greek μελάχρους.</div><b>μελεαγρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελεαγρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Numida meleagris, helmeted guineafowl</div><b>μελετάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to study</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μελετάει</b> πάρα πολύ, είναι μελετηρός. - Ηe practices a lot, he is very studious.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to practise (UK), practice (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μελετάει</b> πιάνο τρεις ώρες τη μέρα. - She practices piano three hours per day.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to contemplate, deliberate</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to research, to investigate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σχέδιο <b>μελετάται</b> από επιτροπή του υπουργείου. - The project is examined by a ministry&#x27;s committee.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From modern μελετώ, contracted form of μελετάω) + modern suffix -άω</div><b>μελετημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μελετημένη, neuter μελετημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>studied, well prepared</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μελετιέμαι &amp; μελετώμαι, passive voices of μελετάω / μελετώ (“I study”).</div><b>μελετηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μελετηρή, neuter μελετηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>studious (given to reading and learning)</div><b>μελετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μελετάω">μελετάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μελετῶ, contracted form of μελετάω. Also see modern μελετάω.</div><b>μελισσοκομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beekeeping, apiculture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μέλισσα + -κομία. First attested 1889.</div><b>μελισσοκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μελισσοκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apiary (place where bees and their hives are kept)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μέλισσα + -κομείο.</div><b>μελισσοκόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural μελισσοκόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beekeeper, apiarist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μέλισσα + -κόμος.</div><b>μελισσοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μελισσοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bee-eater, (especially) European bee-eater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μέλισσα + -φαγος (-fagos, “eater”)</div><b>μελιτζάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελιτζάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aubergine (UK), eggplant (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μελιντζάνα under the influence of Italian melanzana, both from Arabic بَاذِنْجَان, from Classical Persian بَاذِنْگَان, بَاتِنْگَان, ultimately from Dravidian. Doublet of βαζάνι and πατλιτζάνι.</div><b>μελιτζανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μελιτζανιά, neuter μελιτζανί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aubergine (UK), eggplant (US) (color/colour)</div><b>μελιτζανί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) aubergine (UK), eggplant (US) (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) aubergine (UK), eggplant (US) (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μελιτζανής">μελιτζανής</a></div><b>μελιτζανοσαλάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελιτζανοσαλάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aubergine purée</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μελιτζάνα + σαλάτα</div><b>μελλοντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μελλοντική, neuter μελλοντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>future, to come</div><b>μελοδραματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μελοδραματική, neuter μελοδραματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melodramatic</div><b>μελομακάρονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μελομακάρονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melomakarono, a honey flavoured dessert</div><b>μελοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to set to music, to melodize (to write music for existing text)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μελοποιῶ.</div><b>μελτέμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μελτέμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meltemi, etesian (a dry, northerly, Aegean, seasonal wind)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish ملتم (meltem).</div><b>μελωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μελωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. melody, tune</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carol, tune</div><b>μελωδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μελωδική, neuter μελωδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melodic, melodious, tuneful</div><b>μελόδραμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μελοδράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melodrama</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French mélodrame (“indivisible”).</div><b>μελόντικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melodica</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English melodica.</div><b>μεμβράνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεμβράνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. membrane</div>
  <div class='def'>2. film (thin layer)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tegument</div>
  <div class='def'>4. vellum</div><b>μεμψίμοιρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεμψίμοιρη, neuter μεμψίμοιρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(often) complaining, griping</div><b>μεν</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>used in expressions</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μεν</b> … αλλά - yes … but; it&#x27;s true … but</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μέν.</div><b>μενίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μενίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Certain freshwater otocephalan fish of the family Leuciscidae, especially Squalius keadicus and others found near or in Laconia, Greece.</div>
  <div class='ex'>χρυσή <b>μενίδα</b> - Tropidophoxinellus spartiaticus</div><b>μενεξές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μενεξέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>violet (plant &amp; flower)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish منكشه, from Classical Persian بنفشه.</div><b>μενεξεδή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative masculine singular of <a href="bword://μενεξεδής">μενεξεδής</a></div><b>μενεξεδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μενεξεδιά, neuter μενεξεδί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>violet, purple, mauve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μενεξές.</div><b>μενεξεδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μενεξεδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>violet, mauve</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://μενεξεδής">μενεξεδής</a></div><b>μενουέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μενουέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minuet (dance and its musical accompaniment)</div><b>μενού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) menu (list of dishes on offer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French menu.</div><b>μενσεβίκικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μενσεβίκικη, neuter μενσεβίκικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μενσεβικικός">μενσεβικικός</a></div><b>μενσεβίκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μενσεβίκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Menshevik</div><b>μενσεβικικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μενσεβικική, neuter μενσεβικικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Menshevik</div><b>μενταγιόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pendant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French médaillon (“medallion, cameo”).</div><b>μεντελέβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) mendelevium</div><b>μεντεσές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεντεσέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hinge (as for a door)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish منتشه, itself a vulgarization of Classical Persian بندگشا.</div><b>μεξικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεξικανική, neuter μεξικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mexican</div><b>μεράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Passion: intense desire or sorrow.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Zeal for the actualization of a desire, or the emotion associated with such a zeal.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (plural normally) Joy, good mood.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish مراق (merak, “desire, enthusiasm”).</div><b>μερίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μερίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. portion, part (fraction of a whole)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια <b>μερίδα</b> της κοινής γνώμης δε συμφωνεί με τον πρωθυπουργό. - A portion of the public doesn&#x27;t agree with the prime minister.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) portion, helping, share, ration (an allocated amount, usually in food contexts)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια <b>μερίδα</b> τηγανητές πατάτες και μια κόκα κόλα, παρακαλώ. - A portion of chips and a coke, please.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μερίς, from Ancient Greek μέρος.</div><b>μερίδα του λέοντος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μερίδες του λέοντος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lion&#x27;s share</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French la part du lion. see: μερίδα f, ancient μερίς &amp; λέων m in the genitive singular.</div><b>μερίδες του λέοντος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μερίδα">μερίδα</a> <a href="bword://του">του</a> λέοντος</div><b>μερίδιο αγοράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>market share</div><b>μερακλής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine μερακλού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>devotee, enthusiast (one who has a passionate interest in something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish مراقلی, with -ς (-s) suffix.</div><b>μεραρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεραρχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>division</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οδός Ογδόης Μεραρχίας (8th Division Street)</div><b>μερεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ημερεύω">ημερεύω</a></div><b>μεριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>side</div><b>μερικά αθροίσματα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of μερικό <a href="bword://άθροισμα">άθροισμα</a></div><b>μερικοί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative masculine plural of <a href="bword://μερικός">μερικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron, masculine (feminine μερικές, neuter μερικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>few, some (an indeterminate small number)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μερικά</b> φλιτζάνια καφέ - a few cups of coffee</div><b>μερικό άθροισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μερικά αθροίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subtotal</div><b>μερικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μερική, neuter μερικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. partial, part, sub</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μερικό</b> άθροισμα - subtotal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. part-time</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural, pronoun) some, a few</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μέρος + -ικός.</div><b>μερικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>partly, partially, in part</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μερικῶς. By surface analysis, μερικ(ός)) + -ώς.</div><b>μερμήγκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μερμήγκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μυρμήγκι">μυρμήγκι</a></div><b>μεροκάματο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεροκάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) a day&#x27;s pay</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) a day&#x27;s work</div><b>μεροληπτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be biased, partial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μέρος + -ο- (-o-) + λαμβάνω, via form ληπτός.</div><b>μεροληψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) bias, partiality</div><b>μες</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μέσα">μέσα</a></div><b>μεσ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μεσαιωνική ελληνική</div><b>μεσάζοντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεσάζοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://μεσάζων">μεσάζων</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεσάζων, accusative μεσάζοντα. Doublet of μεσάζων.</div><b>μεσάζων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεσάζοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>middleman, intermediary, facilitator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεσάζων, active present participle of μεσάζω, with semantic loan from French intermédiaire. Doublet of μεσάζοντας.</div><b>μεσάνυχτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>midnight</div><b>μεσίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεσίτες, feminine μεσίτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>broker, mediator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μεσίτης.</div><b>μεσαίο δάχτυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>middle finger</div><b>μεσαίωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεσαίωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Middle Ages see: <a href="bword://Μεσαίωνας">Μεσαίωνας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See Μεσαίωνας. With lowercase first letter, when referring to medieval periods or styles in general.</div><b>μεσαιωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσαιωνική, neuter μεσαιωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. medieval, mediaeval</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) retarded, backward</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of torture) retarded, backward; harsh</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μεσαίωνας m μεσαιων- + -ικός</div><b>μεσαιωνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) medievalism, mediaevalism</div><b>μεσαιωνοδίφης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural μεσαιωνοδίφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>medievalist, mediaevalist</div><b>μεσημέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεσημέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>midday</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μέση + ημέρα.</div><b>μεσημβρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεσημβρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) noon, midday</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεσημβρία.</div><b>μεσημβρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσημβρινή, neuter μεσημβρινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. midday, related to noon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. facing the south, southern</div>
  <div class='def'>3. meridian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μεσημέρι.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεσημβρινοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meridian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μεσημέρι.</div><b>μεσημεριανό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεσημεριανά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lunch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μεσημεριανός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://μεσημεριανός">μεσημεριανός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://μεσημεριανός">μεσημεριανός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μεσημεριανός.</div><b>μεσημεριανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσημεριανή, neuter μεσημεριανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>midday, related to noon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μεσημέρι.</div><b>μεσν.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μεσαιωνική ελληνική”)</div><b>μεσοαρχαιοζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσοαρχαιοζωική, neuter μεσοαρχαιοζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mesoarchean</div><b>μεσογειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσογειακή, neuter μεσογειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mediterranean</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το εστιατόριο προσφέρει μεσογειακή κουζίνα. - The restaurant offers Mediterranean cuisine.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Μεσόγει(ος)) + -ακός.</div><b>μεσοζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσοζωική, neuter μεσοζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mesozoic</div><b>μεσοκάθετος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεσοκάθετοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perpendicular bisector</div><b>μεσολαβήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεσολαβήτριες, masculine μεσολαβητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mediator (negotiator between parties seeking agreement)</div><b>μεσολαβητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεσολαβητές, feminine μεσολαβήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mediator (negotiator between parties seeking agreement)</div><b>μεσολαβώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to mediate, to intercede (to act as a mediator)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to intervene (to come between, or to be between, persons or things)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to intervene, to intercede (to occur, fall, or come between, points of time, or events; to pass between)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μεσολαβῶ, from Ancient Greek μεσολαβέω.</div><b>μεσολιθικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσολιθική, neuter μεσολιθικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mesolithic, pertaining to the Middle Stone Age</div><b>μεσονυχτίς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>at around midnight</div><b>μεσονύκτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεσονύκτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) midnight</div><b>μεσονύκτιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσονύκτια, neuter μεσονύκτιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>midnight</div><b>μεσονύχτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεσονύχτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) midnight</div><b>μεσοπαθ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://μεσοπαθητικός">μεσοπαθητικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μεσοπαθητική <a href="bword://φωνή">φωνή</a></div><b>μεσοπαθητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσοπαθητική, neuter μεσοπαθητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in the mediopassive voice or middle voice</div><b>μεσοπολεμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσοπολεμική, neuter μεσοπολεμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interwar (relating to the time between two wars)</div><b>μεσοπροτεροζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσοπροτεροζωική, neuter μεσοπροτεροζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mesoproterozoic</div><b>μεσοπρωτεροζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://μεσοπροτεροζωικός">μεσοπροτεροζωικός</a></div><b>μεσοστρατίς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, literary, poetic) in the middle of theroad, midway</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ξεπρόβαλε <b>μεσοστρατίς</b>, το χέρι να σου δώσω. - Appear in the middle of the road, so that I can give you my hand.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μεσοστρατίς, from μέσος + στράτα.</div><b>μεσοφωνηεντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεσοφωνηεντική, neuter μεσοφωνηεντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intervocalic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μεσο- (meso-) + φωνηεντικός, a calque of French intervocalique.</div><b>μεστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεστή, neuter μεστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ripe (cereal crops)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tough (peas, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. full</div>
  <div class='def'>4. mature</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μεστός.</div><b>μεστώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to ripen, mature (fruit, seeds, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to mature (people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μεστός.</div><b>μεσώροφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεσώροφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://ημιώροφος">ημιώροφος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μεσ- (mes-, “middle”) + -ώροφος, from όροφος.</div><b>μετ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contraction of <a href="bword://μετά">μετά</a> (used with a following vowel)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contraction of <a href="bword://μετά">μετά</a> (used with a following vowel)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εισιτήριο <b>μετ’</b> επιστροφής - return ticket, ticket with return</div><b>μετά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. afterwards, later</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα το συζητήσουμε <b>μετά</b>. - We will discuss it later.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with <a href="bword://από">από</a> and accusative) after</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μετά</b> από πέντε λεπτά, ήρθε το λεωφορείο. - After five minutes the bus came.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>so, therefore (for consecutive statements)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας έχει ξεγελάσει τόσες φορές. <b>Μετά</b>, πώς να του έχουμε εμπιστοσύνη; - We have been deceived so often. So how can we have confidence in him?</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. after</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μετά</b> το δείπνο, έπεσα για ύπνο. - After dinner I went to sleep.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. with (accompanying)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήλθε <b>μετά</b> της συζύγου του. - He came with his wife</div>
  <div class='def'>3. with (manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μετά</b> τιμής - yours sincerely</div><b>μετά βαΐων και κλάδων</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with rapturous acclaim, with a hero&#x27;s welcome</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι πρωταθλητές έγιναν δεκτοί <b>μετά βαΐων και κλάδων</b>. - The champions were received with rapturous acclaim / were given a hero&#x27;s welcome.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “with (palm) branches and branches”, a reference to the use of palm branches in antiquity to symbolize triumph, notably in the Gospel of John&#x27;s account of Jesus&#x27;s triumphal entry into Jerusalem.</div><b>μετά θάνατον ζωή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>afterlife, life after death</div><b>μετά μεσημβρίαν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>post meridiem, after noon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English post meridiem (p.m.), from Latin post (“after”) merīdiem, accusative singular of merīdiēs (“noon, midday”).</div><b>μετά το μεσημέρι</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>post meridiem, after noon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Expression, equivalent to the formal μετά μεσημβρίαν.</div><b>μετάβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. going across, passage across, crossover</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transition, changeover, shift, switchover (the process of change from one form, state, style or place to another)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μετάβασις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>μετάγγιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταγγίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transfusion</div><b>μετάδοση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταδόσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transmission</div>
  <div class='def'>2. broadcast</div>
  <div class='def'>3. contagion, transmission (of disease)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek μετάδο(σις)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>μετάθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (especially) transposition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transfer (of an employee)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. redeployment (of service personnel)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. metathesis</div>
  <div class='def'>5. deferment, deferral, postponement</div>
  <div class='def'>6. moving, rescheduling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μετάθεσις with, in the sense &#x27;transfer&#x27;, semantic loan from German Versetzung and French déplacement. By surface analysis, μεταθέτω + -ση (-si).</div><b>μετάλλευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεταλλεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ore</div><b>μετάλλινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μετάλλινη, neuter μετάλλινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metal, metallic</div><b>μετάλλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (feminine μετάλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>medal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Italian medaglia f (“medal”), treated as a neuter noun by folk-etymological association with Ancient Greek μέταλλον. Attested since 1871 in Katharevousa as μετάλλιον.</div><b>μετάνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετάνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. change of mind, new mind</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) prostration, genuflection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετάνοια.</div><b>μετάξι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μετάξια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>silk (fibre, textile)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετάξιον, diminutive of μέταξα.</div><b>μετάπτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταπτώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sudden change of situation or a property, fluctuation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο καιρός έχει <b>μεταπτώσεις</b>: πότε κρύο, πότε ζέστη. - The weather keeps changing: sometimes cold, sometimes heat.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ablaut, vowel gradation. For Greek language: change during derivation or composition of a word, or specifically of a vowel or diphthong in a root, or stem, or an affix, either qualitative (short ↔ short), or quantitive (short ↔ long)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ποσοτική <b>μετάπτωση</b>: λ<b>έ</b>γω - λ<b>ό</b>γος - quantitive ablaut: lĕgo (“I say”) - lŏgos (“speech, reason”)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. changes in a quantum system</div>
  <div class='def'>4. precession</div>
  <div class='def'>5. transition metal, in the term</div>
  <div class='ex'>στοιχεία <b>μετάπτωσης</b> - transition metals</div>
  <div class='def'>6. precession</div>
  <div class='def'>7. characterizes rifts on the Earth&#x27;s crust</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετᾰ́πτωσῐς (“change”) + πτῶσῐς). Sense in linguistics, since Hellenistic Koine.</div><b>μετάσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metastasis</div><b>μετάφραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταφράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. translation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. translation (the discipline or study of translating written language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετάφρασις, from μεταφράζω, from μετά + φράζω.</div><b>μετέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to participate, take part in</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην ανθρωπιστική αποστολή <b>μετείχαν</b> πολλοί εθελοντές. - Many volunteers participated in the humanitarian mission.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μετέχω. By surface analysis, μετ- (“after”) + έχω (“I have”)</div><b>μετέωρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μετέωρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. meteor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shooting star (informal)</div><b>μεταβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to change, to alter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεταβάλλω. By surface analysis, μετα- (meta-) + βάλλω.</div><b>μεταβίβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταβιβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transfer, passing on, conveyance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. devolution</div><b>μεταβατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταβατική, neuter μεταβατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transitive</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό είναι ένα <b>μεταβατικό</b> ρήμα. - This is a transitive verb.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transition</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μεταβατικό</b> μέταλλο - transition metal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. transitional</div><b>μεταβλητή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://μεταβλητός">μεταβλητός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταβλητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>variable a symbol which can be assigned an arbitrary value.</div><b>μεταβλητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταβλητή, neuter μεταβλητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>changeable, variable, mutable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μεταβάλλω.</div><b>μεταβλητότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural μεταβλητότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) changeableness, variability, volatility</div><b>μεταβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. change, alteration</div>
  <div class='def'>2. about-turn (turn of 180 degrees)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεταβολή.</div><b>μεταβολισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεταβολισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metabolism</div><b>μεταγενέστερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταγενέστερη, neuter μεταγενέστερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Koine Greek (the Greek language approximately between 300 BCE and 600 AD)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. later, subsequent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Comparative of Ancient Greek μεταγενής.</div><b>μεταγλωττίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to dub (replace the soundtrack of a film with a translation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek μεταγλωττίζω, from μετα- (meta-) + Attic Greek γλώττ(α)) + -ίζω.</div><b>μεταγλωττίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταγλωττίστριες, masculine μεταγλωττιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dubber (person who dubs films)</div><b>μεταγλωττιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεταγλωττιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. translator (person who translates speech, whether in film or otherwise)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. compiler (computer program)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μεταγλωττ- (metaglott-, “to translate”) + -ιστής. Computing term is a semantic loan from English compiler.</div><b>μεταγλώττιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταγλωττίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of text) transcribing or translating to a simpler or a different form of language</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μεταγλώττιση</b> των όρων της καθαρεύουσας στην κοινή νεοελληνική - transferring of the Katharevousa terms to koine Neo-Hellenic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dubbing</div>
  <div class='ex'>See μεταγλώττιση on the Greek Wikipedia.Wikipedia ᵉˡ</div>
  <div class='def'>3. compilation, compiling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From verb μεταγλωττί(ζω)) + -ση (-si). Compare to the medieval μεταγλώττισμα n. See μετα- (meta-) and the ancient γλῶττα, Attic form of γλῶσσα.</div><b>μεταγραμματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεταγραμματισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transliteration</div><b>μεταγραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταγραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transcription</div><b>μεταδίδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to transmit, broadcast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to transmit, communicate, impart, convey (information)</div><b>μεταδοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταδοτική, neuter μεταδοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contagious, infectious, catching</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contagious, infectious</div>
  <div class='ex'>O ενθουσιασμός είναι μεταδοτικός . - The enthusiasm is contagious.</div><b>μεταθέτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to transfer, to shift, to transpose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μετατίθημι with adaptation of τίθημι to θέτω. By surface analysis, μετα- (meta-) + θέτω.</div><b>μετακίνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετακινήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. repositioning, redeployment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. move, movement</div>
  <div class='def'>3. transport, transportation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μετα- (meta-) + κίνηση</div><b>μετακινούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to move (to go, in any manner, from one place or position to another)</div><b>μετακινώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to move, to shift (to change the position of)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to move (to go, in any manner, from one place or position to another)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μετακινῶ. By surface analysis, μετα- (meta-) + κινώ.</div><b>μετακομίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to move house</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μετακομίζω, from μετα- (meta-) + κομίζω.</div><b>μετακόμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετακομίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moving house, getting out, removal</div><b>μεταλλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεταλλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ore mine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μεταλλεῖα n pl.</div><b>μεταλλικό νερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεταλλικά νερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mineral water</div>
  <div class='ex'>φυσικό <b>μεταλλικό νερό</b> - natural mineral water</div><b>μεταλλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταλλική, neuter μεταλλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metal, metallic</div><b>μεταλλουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταλλουργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metallurgy</div><b>μεταλλουργείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) metal works, metal factory</div><b>μεταλλοχύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεταλλοχύτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>founder, iron worker</div><b>μεταλλωρυχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεταλλωρυχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ore mine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μεταλλωρύχ(ος)) + -είο.</div><b>μεταμορφώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to transform, to metamorphose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μεταμορφῶ with -ώνω suffix.</div><b>μεταμόρφωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metamorphosis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μεταμόρφωσις.</div><b>μεταμόσχευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transplantation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grafting</div>
  <div class='def'>3. grafting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μεταμόσχευσις in the horticultural sense, with semantic loan from English transplantation in the surgical sense. By surface analysis, μεταμοσχεύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>μετανάστευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>migration: immigration, emigration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μεταναστεύω.</div><b>μετανάστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μετανάστες, feminine μετανάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>migrant: immigrant, emigrant</div><b>μετανάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετανάστριες, masculine μετανάστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>migrant: immigrant, emigrant</div><b>μετανιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to change one&#x27;s mind, regret</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to repent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Potentially analysable as μετα- (“after”) + νοέω (“perceive, notice, think”).</div><b>μετανοών</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μετανούσα, neuter μετανούν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>penitent, remorseful</div><b>μεταξωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταξωτή, neuter μεταξωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of silk; made of silk.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγόρασα ένα νέο <b>μεταξωτό</b> φόρεμα. - I bought a new silk dress.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μετάξι + -τος (-tos, suffix forming adjectives).</div><b>μεταξύ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. between, amongst, amid (place)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. between, meanwhile (time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μεταξύ, from Ancient Greek μεταξύ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>between</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχουν πτήσεις <b>μεταξύ</b> αυτής της πόλης και εκείνης της πόλης - There are flights between this city and that city.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μεταξύ, from Ancient Greek μεταξύ.</div><b>μεταπείθω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dissuade, bring round</div><b>μεταπλασμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine μεταπλασμένη, neuter μεταπλασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. perfect passive participle of μεταπλάθω</div>
  <div class='def'>2. perfect passive participle of μεταπλάθω; reshaped, transformed</div><b>μεταπνευμονοϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metapneumovirus</div><b>μεταπτυχιακό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεταπτυχιακά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. master&#x27;s degree</div>
  <div class='def'>2. postgraduate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μετα- (meta-, “post”) + πτυχίο.</div><b>μεταρηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταρηματική, neuter μεταρηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deverbal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μετα- (meta-, “change in position or condition”) + ρηματικός.</div><b>μεταρρυθμίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be reformed</div><b>μεταρρυθμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to reform</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to rearrange, modify</div><b>μεταρρυθμίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταρρυθμίστριες, masculine μεταρρυθμιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reformer, reformist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. follower of the Reformation</div><b>μεταρρυθμιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεταρρυθμιστές, feminine μεταρρυθμίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reformer, reformist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. follower of the Reformation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μεταρρυθμ- (metarrythm-, “to reform”) + -ιστής, religious term is a calque of German Reformator. First attested 1871.</div><b>μεταρρυθμιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταρρυθμιστική, neuter μεταρρυθμιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reforming, reformative</div><b>μεταρρύθμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταρρυθμίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reformation, reform</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (capitalized) the Protestant Reformation</div><b>μετασχηματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μετασχηματισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transformation, remodelling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transformation</div><b>μετασχηματιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μετασχηματιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transformer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μετασχηματ- (metaschimat-, “to transform”) + -ιστής, calque of French transformateur.</div><b>μετατοπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, sometimes, transitive) to move, to shift, to displace (to change the position of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μετα- (meta-) + τόπ(ος)) + -ίζω, a calque of French déplacer.</div><b>μετατρέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be changed, converted</div><b>μετατρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to convert, change into</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετᾰτρέπω. By surface analysis, μετα- (“post-, after”) + τρέπω (“turn”).</div><b>μετατροπέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μετατροπείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inverter, converter, adapter, transducer, reactor, exciter</div><b>μετατρόχιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μετατρόχια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>track, wheelbase</div><b>μεταφέρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be transported</div><b>μεταφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to transfer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek μεταφέρω. By surface analysis, μετα- (suffix for movement) + φέρω (“carry”).</div><b>μεταφιλοσοφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) metaphilosophy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μετα- (meta-) + φιλοσοφία</div><b>μεταφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rhetoric) metaphor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transport, transportation, (by ext, in plural) trucking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μεταφορά.</div><b>μεταφορέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεταφορείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carrier, conveyor, transporter, haulage contractor, haulier</div><b>μεταφορικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>figuratively, metaphorically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of μεταφορικός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fare, fares, cost of transportation (of persons, items, goods etc.)</div><b>μεταφρ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://μεταφραστικός">μεταφραστικός</a></div><b>μεταφράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be translated</div><b>μεταφράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to translate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μεταφράζω. By surface analysis, μετα- (meta-) + φράζω.</div><b>μεταφράστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταφράστριες, masculine μεταφραστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>translator, interpreter</div><b>μεταφρασιολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>form of μεταφρασεολογία: traductology, translation studies</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σκέφτομαι πάντως ότι στις μέρες μας, όπου η <b>μεταφρασιολογία</b> δίνει ένα ζωτικό παρών, θα ήταν ευχής έργον ν’ αναληφθεί μια συνολική έρευνα για τις μεταφραστικές αντιλήψεις και πρακτικές του 19ου αιώνα. - Still, I&#x27;ve been thinking that in our times, with translation studies being vivedely present, it would be desirable to take up an overall research of the 19th century&#x27;s preceptions and practices of translation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μετάφραση + -λογία.</div><b>μεταφραστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μεταφραστές, feminine μεταφράστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>translator, interpreter</div><b>μεταφραστικό δάνειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεταφραστικά δάνεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calque, loan translation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Free translation of French calque, or a calque of German Lehnübersetzung or English loan translation. See σημασιολογικό δάνειο.</div><b>μεταφραστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταφραστική, neuter μεταφραστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>translation</div><b>μεταφυτεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be transplanted</div><b>μεταφυτεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to transplant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek μεταφυτεύω. By surface analysis, μετα- (“meta-, post-”) + φυτεύω (“I plant”).</div><b>μεταφόρτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transshipment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. download</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare) upload</div><b>μεταφύτευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεταφυτεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transplanting</div><b>μεταχειρισμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μεταχειρισμένη, neuter μεταχειρισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>used, second hand</div><b>μεταϋλικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μεταϋλικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metamaterial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μετα- (meta-, “after, meta-”) + υλικό, calque of English metamaterial.</div><b>μετβ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://μεταβατικός">μεταβατικός</a>: transitive (verb)</div><b>μετεγχειρητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>postoperative</div><b>μετενσάρκωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετενσαρκώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reincarnation</div><b>μετεξεταστέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μετεξεταστέα, neuter μετεξεταστέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pupil or student who must be re-examined</div><b>μετεωρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μετεωρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meteorite</div><b>μετεωριτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μετεωριτική, neuter μετεωριτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. meteoritic (relating to meteors)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Relating to the Meteora.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μετεωρίτης + -ικός.</div><b>μετεωρολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) meteorology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French météorologie, from Ancient Greek μετεωρολογία, from μετέωρᾰ + -λογία.</div><b>μετεωρολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μετεωρολογική, neuter μετεωρολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meteorological</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French météorologique. By surface analysis, μετεωρολογ(ία)) + -ικός. Compare Koine Greek μετεωρολογικός.</div><b>μετονομάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to rename</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μετονομάζω. By surface analysis, μετ- (met-) + ονομάζω.</div><b>μετονομάστηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of μετονομάζομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://μετονομάζω">μετονομάζω</a></div><b>μετονομασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετονομασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>renaming</div><b>μετουσίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετουσιώωσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transformation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transubstantiation</div><b>μετουσιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to transform</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to transubstantiate</div><b>μετοχή</b>
