  <div class='def'>1. participle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. share, stock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετοχή.</div><b>μετράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to count, measure, include</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to count, matter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετρέω, from μέτρον.</div><b>μετρημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μετρημένη, neuter μετρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. counted, measured</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) moderate, careful in speech and manners, never exaggerating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μετριέμαι, passive voice of active μετράω/μετρώ (“I count, measure”).</div><b>μετρητά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cash, money in distinction to valuables, property, investments etc.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήρε την προίκα σε <b>μετρητά</b>. - She took the dowry in cash.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μετρητός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μετρητός">μετρητός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μετρητός.</div><b>μετρητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μετρητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. meter, counter (instrument which measures size, speed, consumption, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meter reader (person who records readings)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive feminine singular of <a href="bword://μετρητός">μετρητός</a></div><b>μετρητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μετρητή, neuter μετρητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>measurable, of that which can be measured</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μετρητός, from the past stem μετρη- of μετράω / μετρώ + -τος (-tos).</div><b>μετριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dampen, to bate, to temper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετριάζω. By surface analysis, μέτριος + -άζω.</div><b>μετριέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to compare, be estimated</div><b>μετριοπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) moderation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μετριο (metrio, “medium”) + -πάθεια</div><b>μετριοφροσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετριοφροσύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>modesty, humility</div><b>μετριότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μετριότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mediocrity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μετριότης, equivalent to μέτριος + -ότητα.</div><b>μετριόφρων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μετριόφρων, neuter μετριόφρον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>modest, unassuming, unpretentious</div><b>μετρονόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μετρονόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. metronome (device used to mark time-tempo by means of regular ticks)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. metrologist (one who studies or practices metrology)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French métronome, from Ancient Greek μέτρον + νόμος.</div><b>μετρούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μετριέμαι">μετριέμαι</a>, passive of <a href="bword://μετράω">μετράω</a>/<a href="bword://μετρώ">μετρώ</a></div><b>μετρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) underground railway, subway, the metro</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French métro, from métropolitain (“metropolitan”).</div><b>μετρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μετράω">μετράω</a></div><b>μετωνυμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μετωνυμική, neuter μετωνυμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>metonymic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek μετωνυμικός, from μετωνυμία. By surface analysis, μετωνυμία + -ικός.</div><b>μετωπιαίος λοβός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μετωπιαίοι λοβοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frontal lobe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, μετωπιαίος + λοβός.</div><b>μετόχι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>outlying farm or shrine of a monastery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μετόχιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek μετοχή.</div><b>μεφίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μεφίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. skunk (Mephitidae)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Any skunk of the genus Mephitis.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Mephitidae, from Mephitis, from Latin mephītis (“noxious gas”).</div><b>μεϊκάπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) make-up, makeup, cosmetics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English make-up.</div><b>μη με λησμόνει</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) forget-me-not (small 5-petalled blue flower from the genus Myosotis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of German Vergissmeinnicht.</div><b>μη στάξει και μη βρέξει</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic, indeclinable) pampered, spoiled, mollycoddled (excessively well-cared for and/or sheltered)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μάνα της την είχε πάντα <b>μη βρέξει και μη στάξει</b> και επομένως δεν ήταν έτοιμη να αντιμετωπίσει τον κόσμο. - Her mother had always pampered and spoiled her so therefore she wasn&#x27;t ready to face the world.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “don&#x27;t let it drip or get wet”.</div><b>μη χειρότερα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>what next, dear me, good grief, sweet Jesus (expression of shock and/or hope worse doesn&#x27;t happen)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μη χειρότερα</b>! Να δούμε τι άλλο θα μας συμβεί τώρα! - Good grief! Let&#x27;s see what else will happen to us!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “not worse”.</div><b>μηδέν</b>
  <div><i>num, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. zero</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nothing, nought, nil</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cipher</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μηδέν, neuter nominative/accusative of Ancient Greek μηδείς.</div><b>μηδέν άγαν</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nothing in excess</div>
  <div class='def'>2. avoid extremes</div>
  <div class='def'>3. behave correctly</div><b>μηδενίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be set to zero</div><b>μηδενίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to set to zero; to zero</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μηδένισε</b> το οδόμετρο - he zeroed the odometer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to set to zero; to allocate zero marks</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο λογοκλόπος <b>μηδενίζεται</b> - the plagiarist had his marks set to zero</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to cancel, cancel out</div><b>μηδενικό</b>
  <div><i>num, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. zero, nought, nil.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nobody, nonentity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek μηδέν.</div><b>μηδενισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μηδενισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cancelling out (UK), canceling out (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the fact of being given zero marks</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nihilism (extreme scepticism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μηδέν + -ισμός, a calque of French nihilisme. First attested 1820.</div><b>μηδενιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μηδενιστική, neuter μηδενιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nihilistic</div><b>μηλίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μηλίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cider</div><b>μηλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μηλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apple tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μηλέᾱ. By surface analysis, μήλο + -ιά.</div><b>μηλόκρασο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cider</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μήλο + κρασί</div><b>μηνιαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μηνιαία, neuter μηνιαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>monthly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μήνας.</div><b>μηνιγγιτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μηνιγγιτική, neuter μηνιγγιτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meningitic, pertaining to meningitis</div><b>μηνοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μηνοειδής, neuter μηνοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crescent, crescent-shaped, lunate, meniscus (shaped)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μηνοειδές</b> σίγμα - lunate sigma</div><b>μηνύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be sued</div><b>μηνύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to sue; file a lawsuit against somebody</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είπαν ότι θα τον <b>μηνύσουν</b>. - They said they were going sue him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μηνύω.</div><b>μηρυκάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ruminate, chew the cud</div><b>μηρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μηροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thigh</div>
  <div class='def'>2. haunch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek μηρός.</div><b>μητέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μητέρες, masculine πατέρας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mother</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>μητέρα</b> του μαθητή - the student&#x27;s mother</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) mother (the first example of something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>μητέρα</b> των κοινοβουλίων - the mother of parliaments</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) mother (something that is the greatest of its kind)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν <b>μητέρα</b> των μαχών. - It was the mother of battles.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μήτηρ, from Proto-Hellenic *mā́tēr, from Proto-Indo-European *méh₂tēr.</div><b>μητριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μητριές, masculine πατριός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stepmother (the wife of a biological father who is not the biological mother)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μητρυιά with simplified spelling. Also see πατριός.</div><b>μητριαρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) matriarchy (system of control by women)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μητρι- (mitri-, “mother”) + -αρχία</div><b>μητρική γλώσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μητρικές γλώσσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mother tongue</div><b>μητρική εταιρεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μητρικές εταιρείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parent company</div><b>μητρική κάρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μητρικές κάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>motherboard</div><b>μητρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μητρική, neuter μητρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maternal, motherly</div><b>μητροπολίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μητροπολίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Μητροπολίτης">Μητροπολίτης</a> (usually spelt with a capital)</div><b>μητρυιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymological, not simplified spelling of <a href="bword://μητριά">μητριά</a></div><b>μητρόπολη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μητροπόλεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. capital city, metropolis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with capital letter) cathedral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μητρ- (mitr-, “mother”) + πόλη</div><b>μητρώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μητρώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mother&#x27;s name</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) mother&#x27;s surname</div><b>μητρώο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μητρώα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>register, record (official)</div><b>μηχάνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μηχανήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>machine, gadget, device</div><b>μηχανές αναζήτησης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μηχανή">μηχανή</a> αναζήτησης</div><b>μηχανές εσωτερικής καύσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://μηχανή">μηχανή</a> εσωτερικής καύσης</div><b>μηχανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μηχανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. machine, engine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. locomotive, the power unit of a train which does not carry passengers or freight itself</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) motorcycle, motorbike</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) a way to deceive people</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μηχᾰνή.</div><b>μηχανή αναζήτησης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μηχανές αναζήτησης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>search engine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μηχανή + αναζήτηση</div><b>μηχανή εσωτερικής καύσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μηχανές εσωτερικής καύσης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>internal combustion engine</div><b>μηχανική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) mechanics</div>
