  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) microbiology</div><b>μικρογραμμάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικρογραμμάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microgram</div><b>μικροκινητικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>micromobility</div><b>μικροκλίμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικροκλίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μικρόκλιμα">μικρόκλιμα</a></div><b>μικροκύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικροκύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microwave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μικρός + κύμα</div><b>μικροοικονομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μικροοικονομική, neuter μικροοικονομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microeconomic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French microéconomique. By surface analysis, μικροοικονομ(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>μικροοργανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μικροοργανισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microorganism</div><b>μικροπλαστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικροπλαστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) microplastic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Internationalism. By surface analysis, μικρο- (mikro-, “micro-”) + πλαστικό.</div><b>μικροσκοπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μικροσκοπική, neuter μικροσκοπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microscopic, minute</div><b>μικροσκόπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικροσκόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microscope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μικρός + σκοπέω, borrowed from French microscope.</div><b>μικροφίλμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) microfilm</div><b>μικρούλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μικρούλα, neuter μικρούλι or μικρούλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (diminutive) sweet, sweet and small</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in the first neuter forms shown) something very small/sweet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μικρ-ός + -ούλης</div><b>μικρούτσικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μικρούτσικη or μικρούτσικια, neuter μικρούτσικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tiny, wee, very small</div>
  <div class='def'>2. smallish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μικρός + -ούτσικος</div><b>μικρό γράμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικρά γράμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small or lowercase letter</div><b>μικρό δάχτυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικρά δάχτυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>little finger (UK), pinkie (US)</div><b>μικρό δαχτυλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικρά δαχτυλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. little finger (UK), pinkie (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small finger (diminutively)</div><b>μικρό όνομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικρά ονόματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>given name, first name, forename, Christian name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally: μικρό + όνομα n</div><b>μικρόβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικρόβια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microbe, germ</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μικρός + βίος, literally “a small living organism”.</div><b>μικρόκλιμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικροκλίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microclimate</div><b>μικρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μικρή or μικρά, neuter μικρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small, little</div>
  <div class='def'>2. short (in length)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα καλοκαίρια μας <b>μικρά</b>,
κι ατέλειωτοι οι χειμώνες. - Our summers short,
and our winters endless.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. young</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μῑκρός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μικροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. little finger</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μῑκρός.</div><b>μικρόφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μικρόφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microphone</div><b>μικτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μικτή, neuter μικτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mixed</div><b>μιλάνε όλοι, μιλάν κι οι κώλοι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, vulgar) Of persons who try and offer an opinion in areas in which they are unqualified or unknowledgable</div><b>μιλάς αγγλικά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) Do you speak English? ^†</div><b>μιλάτε αγγλικά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, in plural) Do you speak English? ^†</div><b>μιλάτε πιο αργά, παρακαλώ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>please speak more slowly</div><b>μιλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to speak, talk</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ας <b>μιλάμε</b> στον ενικό! - Let&#x27;s talk in the singular!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to have friendly relations, be on speaking terms</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για κάποιο λόγο, δε <b>μιλιούνται</b> μεταξύ τους. - For some reason, they don&#x27;t have friendly relations.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μιλώ + -άω, inherited from Byzantine Greek ὁμιλῶ, μιλῶ, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ὁμῑλέω, ancient sense &quot;be in company with&quot;, from ὅμῑλος.</div><b>μιλιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be spoken to, be spoken</div><b>μιλιταρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) militarism</div><b>μιλκ σέικ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μιλκσέικ">μιλκσέικ</a></div><b>μιλκσέικ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) milkshake</div><b>μιλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a more formal variant of <a href="bword://μιλάω">μιλάω</a></div><b>μιλώ στον ενικό</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>address / speak informally, thou someone rather than <a href="bword://εσείς">εσείς</a>, usually as a sign of familiarity; similar concept to German duzen or French tutoyer)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορούμε να <b>μιλήσουμε στον ενικό</b>; - May we speak informally?</div><b>μιμητισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μιμητισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mimicry, mimetism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μιμητής + -ισμός.</div><b>μιμόδραμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μιμοδράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mime, mime show</div><b>μινί φούστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μινί φούστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μίνι">μίνι</a> <a href="bword://φούστα">φούστα</a></div><b>μινί φούστες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of μινί <a href="bword://φούστα">φούστα</a></div><b>μιναρές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μιναρέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minaret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish مناره (minare), from Arabic مَنَارَة.</div><b>μινουέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μινουέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μενουέτο">μενουέτο</a> minuet</div><b>μινωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μινωική, neuter μινωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Of the Minoan people, culture or language</div><b>μινόρε</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) minor (key signature or scale)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian minore.</div><b>μισάωρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μισάωρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>half-hour, half an hour</div><b>μισακάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μισακάρηδες, feminine μισακάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) a farmer who cultivates part of the land of another (usually a large) landowner in return for a share of the crop (usually half); tenant farmer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μισακ(ός), “divided into two halves”) + -άρης.</div><b>μισαλλοδοξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μισαλλοδοξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bigotry</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αγώνας μεταξύ ελευθερίας και <b>μισαλλοδοξίας</b> άρχισε από τα πρώτα χρόνια της ιστορίας της Αμερικής. - The fight between liberty and bigotry began from the first years of the history of America.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μισαλλόδοξος, derived from μισῶ + ἀλλόδοξος. In turn from ἄλλος + δόξα. First attested in 1826.</div><b>μισελληνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) anti-Hellenism</div><b>μισημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μισημένη, neuter μισημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who is hated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μισιέμαι and of μισούμαι, passive voice forms of μισώ (“I hate”).</div><b>μισθοδοσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μισθοδοσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pay, payment of salaries or wages</div>
  <div class='def'>2. payroll</div><b>μισθοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μισθοφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mercenary soldier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μισθοφόρος. By surface analysis, μισθός + -φόρος.</div><b>μισθωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μισθωτές, feminine μισθώτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lessee, renter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μισθωτής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive feminine singular of <a href="bword://μισθωτός">μισθωτός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μισθωτής.</div><b>μισθωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μισθωτή, neuter μισθωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paid, salaried</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μισθωτός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μισθωτοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wage earner, salaried or paid employee</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νέο «ψαλίδι» στα εισοδήματα βλέπει το 38% των <b>μισθωτών</b>. - New &#x27;cut&#x27; to incomes hits 38% of employees.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μισθωτός.</div><b>μισθός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μισθοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>salary, pay, wage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μισθός.</div><b>μισθώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to rent, lease from someone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to hire, employ, take on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μισθοῦμαι, contracted middle diathesis of Ancient Greek μισθόω, with metaplasm with -ώνω.</div><b>μισθώτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μισθωτές, masculine μισθωτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lessee, renter</div><b>μισιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to hate each other</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτοί οι δύο <b>μισιούνται</b>. - These two hate each other.</div><b>μισοκράτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μισοκράτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Marxism) semi-state, the socialist state which is withering away.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Από την πρώτη στιγμή της συγκρότησής της, η δικτατορία του προλεταριάτου, το σοσιαλιστικό κράτος είναι &quot;<b>μισοκράτος</b>&quot;, όπως το χαρακτήριζε ο Ένγκελς, είναι &quot;όχι κράτος καθ’ αυτό&quot;. - From the first moment of its formation, the dictatorship of the proletariat, the socialist state is a “semi-state”, according to Engels; it is not a “state per se”.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μισο- (miso-, “semi-”) and κράτος.</div><b>μισοσκόταδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μισοσκόταδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>half-light, gloaming, semidarkness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μισο- (miso-, “half”) and σκοτάδι.</div><b>μισούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be hated (by someone else)</div><b>μιστωτός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μιστωτοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μισθωτός">μισθωτός</a></div><b>μισό</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>half, ¹⁄₂</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μισό</b> κιλό κρασί - half a kilo of wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μισός, from Byzantine Greek ἥμισος, from Ancient Greek ἥμισυς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://μισός">μισός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://μισός">μισός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μισός, from Byzantine Greek ἥμισος, from Ancient Greek ἥμισυς.</div><b>μισός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μισή, neuter μισό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>half</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μισή</b> ώρα - half an hour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἥμισος, from Ancient Greek ἥμισυς.</div><b>μισώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to hate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μισῶ, contracted form of μισέω.</div><b>μιτοχόνδριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μιτοχόνδρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mitochondrion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from New Latin mitochondrium.</div><b>μιχτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μιχτή, neuter μιχτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A rare form of <a href="bword://μικτός">μικτός</a></div><b>μιόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μιόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>muon</div><b>μμ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of μ.μ. (m.m.)</div><b>μνήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μνήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grave, sepulchre, tomb</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μνῆμα.</div><b>μνήμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μνήμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. memory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. remembrance</div>
  <div class='def'>3. reminiscence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek μνήμη.</div><b>μνεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μνείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. allusion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mention</div>
  <div class='def'>3. point</div>
  <div class='def'>4. reference</div>
  <div class='def'>5. suggestion</div><b>μνημείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μνημεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>memorial, monument</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μνημείο</b> του πολέμου - war memorial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μνημεῖον.</div><b>μνημονιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μνημονιακή, neuter μνημονιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. something that pertains to the Memorandum of Understanding between the EC/ECB/IMF troika and Greece on the Greek economy.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. one who supports the legality of, or the measures implemented by the above memorandum.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μνημόνιο and -ακός.</div><b>μνημόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μνημόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>memorandum, memo</div><b>μνημόσυνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μνημόσυνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>memorial event (in memory of a deceased person), commemoration</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο <b>μνημόσυνο</b> παραβρέθηκε πλήθος κόσμου. - A large number of people attended the memorial service</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek μνημόσυνον, from Ancient Greek μνημόσυνον, from Ancient Greek μνημοσύνη.</div><b>μοίρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μοίρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fate, destiny</div>
  <div class='def'>2. portion, share</div>
  <div class='def'>3. degree (unit of angle)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. squadron</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μοῖρα and the three Μοῖραι.</div><b>μοίρασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sharing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μοίρασμα. By surface analysis, μοιρασ-) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>μοβ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mauve, violet, purple</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French mauve, from Latin malva (“mallow”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mauve, violet, purple</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French mauve, from Latin malva (“mallow”).</div><b>μοβόρικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μοβόρη, neuter μοβόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αιμοβόρικος">αιμοβόρικος</a></div><b>μοβόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μοβόρα, neuter μοβόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αιμοβόρος">αιμοβόρος</a></div><b>μοδάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μοδάτη, neuter μοδάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fashionable, up-to-date, in fashion</div><b>μοδίστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tailor (female)</div><b>μοζαμβικανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μοζαμβικανή, neuter μοζαμβικανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mozambican (of or pertaining to Mozambique or its people)</div><b>μοιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to look like, resemble, take after (to be like or similar to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το παιδί σου σού <b>μοιάζει</b>. - Your child looks like you.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia) ) look like, be taken for (by somebody, in the genitive)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σου <b>μοιάζω</b> για ηλίθιος; - Do you take me for an idiot?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with να (na) ) look like, seems like</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μοιάζει</b> να βρέξει. - It looks like it&#x27;s going to rain.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὁμοιάζω, from ὅμοιος.</div><b>μοιράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to share</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η οικογένεια <b>μοιράστηκε</b> με τον ξένο το φτωχικό φαγητό. - The family shared the scarce food with the stranger.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to divide (between people)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η πρωτοχρονιάτικη πίτα <b>μοιράστηκε</b> σε οχτώ κομμάτια. - The New Year&#x27;s pie was divided into eight pieces.</div><b>μοιράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to divide, cut up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to divide, share out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μοιράζω, from Koine Greek μοιράζω. See Ancient Greek μοῖρᾰ for more.</div><b>μοιρασμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in divided way</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μοιρασμένος">μοιρασμένος</a></div><b>μοιρασμένος</b>
