  <div class='def'>mukhtar</div><b>μοχθηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μοχθηρή, neuter μοχθηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>malicious, malevolent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μοχθηρός.</div><b>μοχθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to labour (UK), to labor (US), to toil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μοχθῶ, contracted form of μοχθέω.</div><b>μοχλός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μοχλοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lever</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μοχλός.</div><b>μούδιασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>numbness (absent or reduced sensitivity to cutaneous stimulation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the μούδιασ- stem of μουδιάζω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>μούμια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μούμιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mummy</div><b>μούντζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μούντζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moutza, mountza (a rude, insulting and contemptuous gesture made with the hands)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Byzantine Greek μούντζα, μούτζα.</div><b>μούρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, often) mug, face</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταμάτα ή θα σου δώσω μια στη <b>μούρη</b>! - Stop or I&#x27;ll box you one in the face!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) guy, person (usually of cunning, adept or dexterous person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα <b>μούρη</b> εκεί! - Look at that guy over there!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) front bumper, fender (of a vehicle, especially a car)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τρακάραμε με τη <b>μούρη</b>. - We crashed at the front.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μούρη, from Italian murri, plural of murro, from Vulgar Latin *murrum.</div><b>μούρλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>madness, insanity, wackiness</div><b>μούρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μούρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mulberry (fruit)</div><b>μούσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μούσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. muse (source of inspiration)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Muse</div><b>μούσκλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin muscus (“moss”)</div><b>μούσκλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin muscus (“moss”)</div><b>μούσλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>muesli</div><b>μούσμουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μούσμουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loquat (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μούσπουλον, from μέσπουλον, from Ancient Greek μέσπιλον.</div><b>μούστος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) must (wine juice, before or during fermentation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μοῦστος, from Latin mustum.</div><b>μούτζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μούτζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μούντζα">μούντζα</a></div><b>μούτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μούτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, often, plural normally) mug, face</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έπεσε με τα <b>μούτρα</b> στη λάσπη. - She fell face first into the mud.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) guy, person (usually of cunning, adept or dexterous person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι μεγάλο <b>μούτρο</b>! Άκου τι σκαρφίστηκε! - He&#x27;s a very cunning guy! Listen to what he came up with!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μοῦτρον, of uncertain origin. Possibly from Italian mutria (“haughty expression”) or from Italian mostra (“exhibition, show”).</div><b>μούχλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) mould (UK), mold (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μοῦχλα, borrowed from (Western?) Aramaic מוּגְלָא / ܡܘܓܠܐ (“pus, water under a blister”; in the usually attested Eastern language forms instead not actually recorded as “mould”, apparently then transferred from an idea of “damaging liquid”), when also around the same time Middle Armenian forms the compound մգլահոտ (mglahot, “smelling like mould; smell of mould”), as otherwise the present term would have to be from Ancient Greek ὀμίχλη and the Armenian term մգլել (mglel, “to mould”) parallelly from մէգ; prompt to conjecture native etymologies, Indo-Europeanists sport the leading opinion even that the Bulgarian and Macedonian terms in the descendants section below would rather hail from Proto-Slavic *mъxъ (“moss”) + *-ъlъ.</div><b>μπάγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπάγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πάγκος">πάγκος</a></div><b>μπάζο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπάζα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rubble, debris (especially from demolition)</div><b>μπάιτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) byte</div><b>μπάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ball</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bauble</div>
  <div class='def'>3. football</div>
  <div class='def'>4. bale (rounded bundle or package of goods)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan bala (or Italian balla).</div><b>μπάλσαμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπάλσαμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βάλσαμο">βάλσαμο</a></div><b>μπάλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. A patching, a mending.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) A patch.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπαλώνω + -μα (-ma). The computing sense is a semantic loan from English patch.</div><b>μπάμια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπάμιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>okra, ladies&#x27; fingers, gumbo (vegetable and plant, Abelmoschus esculentus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>μπάμιες</b> λαδερές ήταν νόστιμες. - The okra cooked in olive oil was delicious.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بامیه (bamya)</div><b>μπάμπουρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπάμπουρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bumblebee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * Either onomatopoeic, or from Ancient Greek βομβυλιός, ultimately from βόμβος.</div><b>μπάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bath (act of bathing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μετά το <b>μπάνιο</b> χαλάρωσα πολύ. - I relaxed a lot after the bath.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) bathroom (room for bathing and usually with a toilet)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπήκε μέσα στο <b>μπάνιο</b> πριν μια ώρα. Τι κάνει τόση ώρα άραγε; - He went into the bathroom an hour ago. What could he possibly be doing?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) swim (instance of swimming or bathing, usually in the sea)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πόσα <b>μπάνια</b> έκανες το καλοκαίρι; - How many swims did you go for in the summer?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (broadly) bath, bathtub (tub or pool used for bathing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>μπάνιο</b> είναι γεμάτο σαπουνάδα. - The bath is full of suds.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian bagno, from Vulgar Latin *balneum, from Latin balneum, from Ancient Greek βαλανεῖον.</div><b>μπάντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπάντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. side (all location senses)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πλοίο έγειρε από τη δεξιά <b>μπάντα</b>. - The ship leaned on its right side.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. band (part of the radio spectrum)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα αεροπλάνα συνομιλούν στη <b>μπάντα</b> αεροναυτιλίας 118–136. - The planes converse on the air navigation band of 118–136.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. marching band (group of instrumental musicians in woodwind and brass families who generally perform outdoors)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>μπάντα</b> θα παίξει στην εγκαίνια του δημάρχου. - The band will play at the inauguration of the mayor.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian banda.</div><b>μπάντμιντον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) badminton</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English badminton.</div><b>μπάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bar (rigid oblong object)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the bar in a bar</div>
  <div class='def'>3. barbell</div>
  <div class='def'>4. a securing bar in a door or gate</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (informal) bar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian barra.</div><b>μπάρκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπάρκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) barque</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) voyage, tour (period of time spent serving on a ship)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. The onboarding of things onto a ship.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian barca, from Latin barca.</div><b>μπάρμαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) barman, bartender</div><b>μπάρμπα-Σαμ</b>
  <div><i>name</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Uncle Sam</div><b>μπάρμπας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαρμπάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) uncle (brother of one&#x27;s father or mother)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω έναν <b>μπάρμπα</b> στη Νέα Υόρκη. - I have an uncle in New York.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) pops, uncle, dad, old timer (any older man in general)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύο <b>μπαρμπάδες</b> καθόντουσαν και παίζανε σκάκι στο καφενείο. - Two old timers were sitting playing chess in the coffee shop.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μπάρμπας, from Venetan barba (“uncle”).</div><b>μπάρμπεκιου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) barbecue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English barbecue, from Spanish barbacoa</div><b>μπάσκετ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) basketball (the less formal, but much more common term)</div>
  <div class='ex'>(the formal term), μπάσκετ-μπολ f (básket-bol) (not common)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Shortening of μπάσκετ-μπολ, from English basketball</div><b>μπάσκετ-μπολ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) basketball (the shortened form is more usual)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English basketball.</div><b>μπάσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μπάσα, neuter μπάσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bass</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μπάσο</b> σαξόφωνο - bass saxophone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian basso.</div><b>μπάσταρδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μπάσταρδη, neuter μπάσταρδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (derogatory) bastard (who is considered an illegitimate descendant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, noun from verb) bastard (a person who was born out of wedlock, and hence often considered an illegitimate descendant)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory) bastard (who is considered a contemptible person)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (derogatory, noun from verb) bastard (a contemptible person)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπάσταρδε</b>! - Bastard!</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of things) bastard, bastardized (of abnormal, irregular or otherwise inferior qualities)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek (with stress shift) from Italian bastardo.</div><b>μπάτσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπάτσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slap, biff</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) cop, copper (police officer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian bazza.</div><b>μπάφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπάφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joint, spliff, marijuana cigarette</div><b>μπέιζμπολ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) baseball</div><b>μπέικιν πάουντερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) baking powder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of English baking powder</div><b>μπέικον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bacon, cured pork</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of English bacon</div><b>μπέρδεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπερδέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. confusion, tangle, jumble, mix-up</div>
  <div class='ex'>το μπέρδεμα των σκοινιών (the tangle of cords)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) trouble</div><b>μπέρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπέρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. baggy overcoat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cloak, cape, mantle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian berta &lt; German Bertha.</div><b>μπέσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) oath, vow of mutual assistance or friendship; alliance, agreement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) trust, reliability, trustworthiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian besë.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(slang, uncountable) an expression like trust (as an interjection) and honest (as an adverb); really.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα του σπάσω τα δόντια όταν τον δω, <b>μπέσα</b>. - I&#x27;ll break his teeth when I see him, trust.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian besë.</div><b>μπέϊκιν πάουντερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of μπέικιν πάουντερ</div><b>μπίρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπίρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beer, ale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan bira (“beer”), from Old High German bior (“beer”), or borrowed from Italian birra, which replaced cervogia. from German Bier.</div><b>μπα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) wow (interjection denoting surprise)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπα</b>, για κοίτα ποιος ήρθε! - Wow, look who came!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) nope, nah, bah (casual interjection denoting &#x27;no&#x27; or &#x27;not&#x27;)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νομίζεις ότι θα έρθει; <b>Μπα</b>, σιγά μην έρθει! - Do you think she&#x27;ll come? Nah, as if she will!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) an interjection denoting indignation or discomfort</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπα</b>, τον παλιάνθρωπο, τι μου έκανε! - The bugger, see what he did to me!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) an interjection denoting irony, often preceded with <a href="bword://α">α</a> (a)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι εγκληματίας; Α <b>μπα</b>, ιδέα σου! - So he&#x27;s a criminal? No, you think!?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic. Compare to Italian ba.</div><b>μπαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to enter, go in, come in, get in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to shrink (cloth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from medieval Byzantine Greek ἐμπαίνω, omitting the first vowel, from Ancient Greek ἐμβαίνω (pronounced with [mb]), from εμ- (em-) + βαίνω.</div><b>μπαγαπόντης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαγαπόντηδες, feminine μπαγαπόντισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hustler, trickster, spiv</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian vagabondo (“vagabond”)</div><b>μπαγιάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μπαγιάτικη, neuter μπαγιάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stale (having lost its freshness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fusty (smelling stale or moldy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بایات (bayat) ( + -ικος (-ikos)), from Arabic بَائِت. Compare Byzantine Greek μπαγιάτι.</div><b>μπαγιονέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπαγιονέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bayonet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French baïonnette.</div><b>μπαγκέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπαγκέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. baton (of conductor)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. drum stick</div>
  <div class='def'>3. baguette</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French baguette.</div><b>μπαγλαμάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαγλαμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baglamas, stringed instrument similar to a lute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish باغلامه.</div><b>μπαινοβγαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to come and go, come in and go out</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι κότες <b>μπαινοβγαίνουν</b> από το πορτάκι. - The hens come and go through the little door.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Dvandva compound of μπαίνω + βγαίνω</div><b>μπακάλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπακάληδες, feminine μπακάλισσα or μπακάλαινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grocer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بقال (bakkal), from Arabic بَقَّال.</div><b>μπακάλικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grocer&#x27;s shop, grocery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπακάλης + -ικο (-iko, “shop, etc”)</div><b>μπακαλάος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπακαλάοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://βακαλάος">βακαλάος</a></div><b>μπακαλιάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπακαλιάροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cod (marine fish of the family Gadidae)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian baccalà and Portuguese bacalhau.</div><b>μπακλαβάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπακλαβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baklava</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα κομμάτι <b>μπακλαβά</b> - a piece of baklava</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish باقلوا (baklava).</div><b>μπακουνινιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural μπακουνινιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bakuninist</div><b>μπαλάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαλάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) tile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic بلاط.</div><b>μπαλένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπανέλα">μπανέλα</a></div><b>μπαλέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαλέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ballet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian balletto (“ballet”).</div><b>μπαλαίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπανέλα">μπανέλα</a></div><b>μπαλαδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαλαδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) footballer (a skilled or exceptionally able one)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπάλα + -αδόρος</div><b>μπαλαλάικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπαλαλάικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>balalaika</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Russian балала́йка.</div><b>μπαλαντέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) joker</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) an all-rounder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French baladeur (“wanderer”).</div><b>μπαλαρίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπαλαρίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ballerina</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian ballerina (“ballerina”).</div><b>μπαλκόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαλκόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>balcony</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian balcone.</div><b>μπαλλέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαλλέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπαλέτο">μπαλέτο</a></div><b>μπαλλαρίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπαλλαρίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπαλαρίνα">μπαλαρίνα</a></div><b>μπαλονάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. endearment or diminutive form for: <a href="bword://μπαλόνι">μπαλόνι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. stent (slender tube inserted into a blood vessel)</div><b>μπαλτάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαλτάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cleaver, chopper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بالطه (balta).</div><b>μπαλόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαλόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>balloon (child’s toy)</div><b>μπαλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to patch, mend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐμπαλώνω, from Ancient Greek ἐμβάλλω retaining pronunciation [mb] of &lt;μβ&gt; + -ώνω (suffix for verbs).</div><b>μπαμπάκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαμπάκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endearing form of <a href="bword://μπαμπάς">μπαμπάς</a> m: daddy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπαμπ(άς)) + -άκας.</div><b>μπαμπάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαμπάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βαμβάκι">βαμβάκι</a></div><b>μπαμπάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Bambara (the language of Mali)</div><b>μπαμπάς</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dad, father (informal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. baba (bun, usual soaked in rum often with cream)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بابا (baba) + ending -ς (-s) or onomatopoeic child word. The ancient hypocoristic for πατήρ was πάππας. Unrelated is the medieval βάβω / μπάμπω, from Slavic *babo.</div><b>μπαμπέσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαμπέσηδες, feminine μπαμπέσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cunning, deceptive, untrustworthy person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian i pabesë, with progressive assimilation of the /b/.</div><b>μπαμπουίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαμπουίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baboon</div><b>μπαμπού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bamboo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English bamboo, from Dutch bamboe, from Portuguese bambu, from Malay bambu, from Kannada ಬಮ್ಬು (bambu).</div><b>μπαμπούλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαμπούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bogeyman, bugbear (menacing, ghost-like monster in children&#x27;s stories)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγαινε να κοιμηθείς, πριν έρθει ο <b>μπαμπούλας</b> να σε φάει! - Go to sleep before the bogeyman comes to eat you!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bogeyman, bugbear (imaginary source of dread or fear)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>μπαμπούλας</b> του κομμουνισμού - the bogeyman of communism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek μπαμπούλας, of uncertain origin. Possibly onomatopoeic.</div><b>μπανάλ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) banal</div><b>μπανάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπανάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>banana</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Portuguese banana.</div><b>μπανάνα σπλιτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) banana split</div><b>μπανέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. baleen (plates in mouth of baleen whale)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. whalebone (corset component)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian balena, from Ancient Greek φάλαινα.</div><b>μπανανιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπανανιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>banana tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπανάνα + -ιά.</div><b>μπανανόφλουδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>banana peel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπανάνα + φλούδα.</div><b>μπανγκαλόου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bungalow</div><b>μπανιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπανιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bathtub, bath (large container for holding water in which a person may bathe)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Καθάρισε σε παρακαλώ την <b>μπανιέρα</b>, είναι γεμάτη σαπουνάδες. - Please clean the bathtub, it&#x27;s full of suds.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπάνιο + -ιέρα</div><b>μπανιερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπανιερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) swimsuit, swimming costume, swimming trunks</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπάνιο + -ερό</div><b>μπανιστήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) voyeurism (sexual satisfaction by watching people secretly, especially when those being watched are undressed or engaging in sexual activity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπανισ- (banis-) + τήρι</div><b>μπανιστηρτζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπανιστηρτζήδες, feminine μπανιστηρτζού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) peeping tom, peeper, voyeur (person who watches another without the other&#x27;s permission and usually without the other&#x27;s knowledge, especially for the purpose of deriving sexual pleasure from the sight of the other)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπανιστηρτζής</b> συνελήφθη στην παραλία. - A peeping tom was arrested on the beach.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπανιστήρ(ι), “voyeurism”) + -τζής.</div><b>μπανιστηρτζού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπανιστηρτζούδες, masculine μπανιστηρτζής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) peeping tom, peeper, voyeur (female person who watches another without the other&#x27;s permission and usually without the other&#x27;s knowledge, especially for the purpose of deriving sexual pleasure from the sight of the other)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπανιστηρτζού</b> συνελήφθη στην παραλία. - A peeping tom was arrested on the beach.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From masculine μπανιστηρτζ(ής)) + feminine ending -ού. Morphologically, μπανιστήρ(ι), “voyeurism”) + -τζού.</div><b>μπαντούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπαντούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μαντούρα">μαντούρα</a></div><b>μπαξές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαξέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>garden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish باغچه, from Classical Persian باغچه, diminutive of باغ.</div><b>μπαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) bar (business where drinks are sold)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) bar (piece of furniture over which drinks are taken)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) bar (measure of atmospheric pressure)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration from English bar</div><b>μπαράζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) barrage, outcry (overwhelming outburst)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπήρξε <b>μπαράζ</b> αντιδράσεων στις δηλώσεις του πρωθυπουργού. - There was a barrage of reactions to the Prime Minister&#x27;s statements.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) barrage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French barrage.</div><b>μπαργούμαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable, masculine μπάρμαν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) barwoman, barmaid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English barwoman.</div><b>μπαρδόν</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(slang) I am sorry, I apologize, excuse me (UK), pardon me (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λοιπὸννν νά... σάς ζητώ συγγνώμη ρὲ παιδιά. Μπαρδόν. .. . . . Τη μπάτισα τη φλούδα... Έπεσα έξω. Τι θὰ γίνει τώρα... θα - . . . . .. .. μου πάρετε το κεφάλι; Λοιπόν μπαρδόν. - Welllll... let me apologize, you guys. Bardon .... I blew it... I miscalculated. What is there to be done... You will... chop off my head? So: bardon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρντόν, phonetic transfer of French pardon with [d] &gt; [ð] in an attempt to make it sound more formal and [p] &gt; [b].</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable, slang) apology, pardon in the expression</div>
  <div class='ex'>με το <b>μπαρδόν</b> - with my apologies</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρντόν, phonetic transfer of French pardon with [d] &gt; [ð] in an attempt to make it sound more formal and [p] &gt; [b].</div><b>μπαρκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to embark on a ship</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἰμπαρκάρω, from Italian imbarcare</div><b>μπαρμπέρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαρμπέρηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barber, men&#x27;s hairdresser</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian barbiere from Latin barba.</div><b>μπαρμπέρικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαρμπέρικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barbershop, barber&#x27;s shop, hairdresser&#x27;s</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπαρμπέρης + -ικο (-iko, “shop, etc”)</div><b>μπαρμπούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαρμπούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mullet (striped red), Mullus surmuletus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gurnard</div>
  <div class='def'>3. borlotti (bean)</div><b>μπασιά</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) entrance</div><b>μπασκέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπασκέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>basket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπάσκετ.</div><b>μπασκίνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) cop, copper (police officer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish باصقین + -ας (-as).</div><b>μπαστουνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπαστουνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a hit with a cane</div><b>μπαστούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαστούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cane, walking stick</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spade</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (idiomatic) trouble</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα βρήκα <b>μπαστούνια</b> - I met with trouble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian bastone (“stick, cane”), from Latin bastum (“stick”). Compare Sicilian vastuni.</div><b>μπατανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπατανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) blanket</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Egyptiot) blanket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بطانیه (battaniye) from Arabic بَطَّانِيَّة.</div><b>μπαταρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπαταρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>battery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French batterie.</div><b>μπαχάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαχάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>allspice (spice)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بهار (bahar), from Arabic بَهَار. Doublet of έαρ.</div><b>μπαχαρικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπαχαρικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπαχάρι + -ικό”).</div><b>μπαχτσές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπαχτσέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπαξές">μπαξές</a></div><b>μπεζ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) beige</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French beige.</div><b>μπεκερέλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) becquerel (the derived SI unit quantifying the activity of radioactive materials)</div><b>μπεκιάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπεκιάρηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bachelor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بكار, from Arabic بِكَر (bikar, “virgin”).</div><b>μπεκρής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπεκρήδες, feminine μπεκρού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) drunkard (male)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بكری (Turkish bekri) + -ς (-s).</div><b>μπεκρού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπεκρούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drunkard (female)</div><b>μπελάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπελάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) trouble, bother (difficulty, problem, condition, or action contributing to such a situation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα έχεις <b>μπελάδες</b> με την μάνα σου αν το κάνεις. - You&#x27;ll have trouble with your mother if you do it.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) nuisance, pain (person causing annoyance or inconvenience)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>μπελάς</b> είναι αυτό το παιδί! - What a nuisance that child is!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بلا, from Arabic بَلَاء.</div><b>μπελαντόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπελαντόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. belladonna, deadly nightshade (Atropa belladonna)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. belladonna (alkaloid)</div><b>μπελούγκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπελούγκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beluga</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English beluga.</div><b>μπενζίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπενζίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βενζίνη">βενζίνη</a></div><b>μπεντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) egg</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic بيض.</div><b>μπεντόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπεντόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπιτόνι">μπιτόνι</a></div><b>μπερές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπερέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French béret.</div><b>μπερδεμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in a confusing manner</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μπερδεμένος">μπερδεμένος</a></div><b>μπερδεμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μπερδεμένη, neuter μπερδεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tangled, knotty, muddled (hair, string, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. confused (thought, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tricky, complicated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μπερδεύομαι, passive voice of μπερδεύω.</div><b>μπερδεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to get confused, to get muddled up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to become involved</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to get tangled, to get caught</div><b>μπερδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to tangle (hair, string, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to muddle up, to mess up (to mix together, to bring into disorder: papers, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ανακατεύω (anakatévo, “to mix, to mix up”)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to confuse, to muddle up, to mix up (to fail to correctly distinguish between)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to confuse, to perplex (to create confusion in the mind of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: συγχύζω (synchýzo)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to complicate (to make complex)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐμπερδεύω &lt; ἐμπερδένω) as in the participle ἐμπερδεμένος). From (ἐν-) ἐμ- ((en-) em-, “in”) + (περι-) περ- ((peri-) per-, “around”) + the ancient δέω.</div><b>μπερκέλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) berkelium</div><b>μπερντές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπερντέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) curtain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the screen used in shadow puppetry</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) a performance of shadow puppetry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پرده (perde, “curtain, screen, vail”), from Classical Persian پرده (parda).</div><b>μπεσαλής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπεσαλήδες, feminine μπεσαλού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trustworthy person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπέσα + -αλής.</div><b>μπεσαμέλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) béchamel</div><b>μπετονιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπετονιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concrete mixer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bétonnière (“concrete mixer”) + feminine noun suffix -α (-a).</div><b>μπετό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπετά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπετόν">μπετόν</a></div><b>μπετόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable) concrete</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French béton (“concrete”), from Latin bitūmen (“asphalt”).</div><b>μπετόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπετόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπιτόνι">μπιτόνι</a></div><b>μπιγκουντί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπικουτί">μπικουτί</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bigoudi (“hair roller”).</div><b>μπιγκόνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπιγκόνιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bignonia (Bignonia-genus plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Bignonia.</div><b>μπιζάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ask for an encore (in concerts, recitals, performances)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>μπιζάρισαν</b> δέκα φορές! - They asked for an encore ten times!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπιζ (biz) + -άρω. From the French bis! Also see the Italian verb bissare.</div><b>μπιζέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπιζέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pea (fresh green)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pea (plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian piselli, plural of pisello, from Vulgar Latin *pisellum, diminutive of Latin pisum, itself borrowed from Ancient Greek πίσον, πίσος.</div><b>μπιζελιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπιζελιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pea plant</div><b>μπικίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bikini</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bikini.</div><b>μπικουτί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable) hair roller</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bigoudi (“hair roller”).</div><b>μπιλιάρδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπιλιάρδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>billiards, pool</div><b>μπιλιέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπιλιέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. note, billet (short informal letter)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. card, note (attached to present, bouquet, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. visiting card, card</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian biglietto, from Middle French billette (“schedule”).</div><b>μπιμπερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) baby bottle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French biberon.</div><b>μπινές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπινέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory) faggot, queer, homo (male homosexual)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, offensive) prick, dickhead, jerk (general term of abuse for a male)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish ابنه (ibne), from Ottoman Turkish ابنت (ibnet), from Arabic اِبْنَة (ibna).</div><b>μπινελίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπινελίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, literally) gay behaviour, acting like a fag (male homosexual)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, plural normally) bollocking, cursing out (severe reproach)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αφεντικό του τον άρχισε στα <b>μπινελίκια</b>. - His boss started to give him a bollocking.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) delicacy, tasty snack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish ابنهلك (ibnelik), composed of ابنه (ibne) + ـلك (lik), the former leading to μπινές.</div><b>μπιντές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπιντέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bidet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French bidet.</div><b>μπιντόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπιντόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπιτόνι">μπιτόνι</a></div><b>μπιρμπίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) nightingale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بلبل, from Classical Persian بلبل (bulbul, “nightingale”).</div><b>μπισκότο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπισκότα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>biscuit, cookie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μπισκόττιν, from Italian biscotto, from Latin bis- (“twice”) + coquō (“to bake”).</div><b>μπιτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bit</div><b>μπιτς βόλεϊ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) beach volleyball</div><b>μπιτόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπιτόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a metal or plastic container for storing and transporting liquids</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jerrycan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French bidon.</div><b>μπιφτέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπιφτέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patty, burger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bifteck, from English beefsteak.</div><b>μπιχλιμπίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπιχλιμπίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trinket, knick-knack, bric-a-brac, bauble (small showy ornament or piece of jewelry)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γριά φορούσε διάφορα <b>μπιχλιμπίδια</b> μικρής αξίας. - The old woman was wearing various cheap trinkets.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, figuratively) trinket, trifle (anything of little importance or worth)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή πάντα κουβαλάει ένα σωρό <b>μπιχλιμπίδια</b> στην τσάντα της. - She always carries around a load of trinkets in her bag.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek λεμπλεμπίδι, from Ottoman Turkish لبلبی (leblebi, “roasted chickpea; jumbled or mashed chickpea”), from Arabic لَبْلَابِيّ.</div><b>μπλάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to spread</div><b>μπλάκης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) black</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English black.</div><b>μπλάντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda, indeclinable) blood</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *bʰleh₃-?
Proto-Germanic *blōþą
Proto-West Germanic *blōd
Old English blōd
Middle English blood
English bloodbor.
Greek μπλάντι
Borrowed from English blood.</div><b>μπλέκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to tangle, entangle, involve</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπλέχτηκαν</b> τα μαλλιά μου στο κουμπί. - My hair were caught at the button.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐμπλέκω (&lt; ἐμ- (em-) &lt; ἐν- (en-) + πλέκω) with deletion of the arctic unstressed vowel.</div><b>μπλε</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) blue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun) blue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bleu.</div><b>μπλε μαρέν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) of navy blue color/colour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French bleu marine, pronounced as marin.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) navy blue (the color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French bleu marine, pronounced as marin.</div><b>μπλε μαρίν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) different pronunciation (similar to French) of <a href="bword://μπλε">μπλε</a> μαρέν</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French bleu marine.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) different pronunciation (similar to French) of <a href="bword://μπλε">μπλε</a> μαρέν</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French bleu marine.</div><b>μπλιαχ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ew, yuck, ugh, blegh (expression of disgust or nausea)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπλιαχ</b>, πάτησα σκατά σκύλου. - Ew, I stepped in dog shit.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic</div><b>μπλοκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to block (to fill or obstruct (something) so that it is not possible to pass)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to block (to prevent (something or someone) from passing)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to block (to prevent (something) from happening or (someone) from doing something)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to block (to impede (an opponent or opponent’s play))</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to block (to bar (a message or communication), or bar connection with)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adapted borrowing of Italian bloccare + -ω (-o).</div><b>μπλου τζιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπλουτζίν">μπλουτζίν</a></div><b>μπλουζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) blues</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English blues.</div><b>μπλουζόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) blouson, blouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French blouson.</div><b>μπλουτζίν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) blue jeans</div><b>μπλούζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπλούζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blouse, top</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French blouse.</div><b>μποέμικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μποέμικη, neuter μποέμικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bohemian (unconventional)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μποέμ + -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>μπογάζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπογάζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπουγάζι">μπουγάζι</a></div><b>μπογιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπογιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paint, dye</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بویا (boya).</div><b>μπογιατίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to paint</div><b>μπογιατζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπογιατζήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. painter (laborer)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dauber</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Either borrowed wholly from Ottoman Turkish بویاجی or created using μπογιά + -τζής, in either case from Ottoman Turkish.</div><b>μποδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εμποδίζω">εμποδίζω</a></div><b>μποζόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μποζόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boson</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>μποζόνιο</b> Χιγκς - the Higgs boson</div><b>μποκ τσόι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pak choi, Chinese cabbage</div><b>μπολ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bowl (container used for ornament or storage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bol, from English bowl.</div><b>μπολιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to graft (a plant or tree, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπόλι + -ιάζω.</div><b>μπολονέζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bolognese</div><b>μπολσεβίκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπολσεβίκες, masculine μπολσεβίκος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bolshevik; bolshie (derogatory, slang)</div><b>μπολσεβίκικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μπολσεβίκικη, neuter μπολσεβίκικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπολσεβικικός">μπολσεβικικός</a></div><b>μπολσεβίκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπολσεβίκοι, feminine μπολσεβίκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bolshevik, Bolshevist; bolshie (derogatory, slang)</div><b>μπολσεβικικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μπολσεβικική, neuter μπολσεβικικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bolshevik; bolshie (derogatory, slang)</div><b>μπολσεβικισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπολσεβικισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bolshevism</div><b>μπομπάρδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπομπάρδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bombard, (ancient cannon)</div><b>μπομπότα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπομπότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cornbread, hoecake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Uncertain. Probably borrowed from</div><b>μπονάτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπονάτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπουνάτσα">μπουνάτσα</a></div><b>μπονατσάρει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπουνατσάρει">μπουνατσάρει</a></div><b>μποράγκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μποράγο">μποράγο</a></div><b>μποράγο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) borage</div><b>μποράντζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μποράγο">μποράγο</a></div><b>μπορδέλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπορδέλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπορντέλο">μπορντέλο</a></div><b>μπορεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>may</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>μπορεί</b>! - It is not possible!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> third-person singular present of μπορώ</div><b>μπορντέλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπορντέλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπουρδέλο">μπουρδέλο</a></div><b>μπορντέλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπορντέλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μπουρδέλο">μπουρδέλο</a></div><b>μπορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. I can, be able</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπορείς</b> αύριο; - Can you [do it] tomorrow?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. I may</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μπορώ</b>; <b>Μπορώ</b>! - May I? I may!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (third person) <a href="bword://μπορεί">μπορεί</a> it is possible, it may, maybe, perhaps it… (impersonal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>μπορεί</b>! - Ιt&#x27;s not possible!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐμπορῶ, ἠμπορῶ, ultimately from Ancient Greek εὐπορέω).</div><b>μποτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) boat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) bot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English boat.</div><b>μποτίλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μποτίλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bottle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian bottiglia (“bottle”).</div><b>μποτιλιάρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μποτιλιαρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a traffic jam</div><b>μποτσουανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μποτσουανή, neuter μποτσουανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Botswanan (relating to Botswana or its people)</div><b>μπουάπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουάπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) gatehouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic بوابة.</div><b>μπουάπης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπουάπηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) doorman, gatekeeper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic بواب.</div><b>μπουγάζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουγάζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strait</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a narrow pass between two mountains</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) gust of wind from narrow straits</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بوغاز.</div><b>μπουγάτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουγάτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cream pie (a type of pie made from phyllo pastry and usually containing semolina custard, but also sometimes cheese or mince meat)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πογάτσα, from Late Latin focācium, derived from Latin focus (“hearth”). Compare Serbo-Croatian pogača.</div><b>μπουγιαμπέσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουγιαμπέσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bouillabaisse (fish soup from Provence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bouillabaisse, from Occitan bolhabaissa, possibly a compound of bolhir (“to boil”) + abaissar (“to lower [temperature]”).</div><b>μπουζί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spark plug, sparking plug</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French bougie.</div><b>μπουζοκαλώδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουζοκαλώδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spark plug lead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπουζί + καλώδιο.</div><b>μπουζούκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουζούκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bouzouki, stringed musical instrument similar to a lute</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) nightclub</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بوزوق (bozuk).</div><b>μπουζόκλειδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουζόκλειδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spark plug spanner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπουζί + κλειδί.</div><b>μπουκάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουκάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>augmentative of <a href="bword://μπουκάλι">μπουκάλι</a> n: large bottle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπουκάλ(ι)) + -α (-a, augmentative suffix).</div><b>μπουκάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουκάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bottle</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα ποτήρι και ένα <b>μπουκάλι</b> κρασί - a glass and a bottle of wine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the contents of a bottle</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πόσα <b>μπουκάλια</b> μπίρα ήπιατε χθες; - How many bottles of beer did you^([V-form]) drink yesterday?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A modern or mediaeval αντιδάνειο, inherited from Byzantine Greek μπουκάλιν, from Venetan bocal -Italian boccale (“jug, bottle”), or otherwise from Sicilian buccali (“jug, bottle”)-, from Latin baucalus, from Koine Greek βαύκαλις; probably of Central Atlas Tamazight or Egyptian origin.</div><b>μπουκέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουκέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bunch of flowers, bouquet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) punch, bunch of fives, beating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Germanic *buskaz
Frankish *buskbor.
Early Medieval Latin boscus
Old French boisder.
Old French bochet
French bouquetbor.
Greek μπουκέτο
Borrowed from French bouquet.</div><b>μπουκαδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπουκαδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>burglar</div><b>μπουκαλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://μπουκάλι">μπουκάλι</a> n: small bottle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπουκάλι + -άκι.</div><b>μπουκαμβίλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουκαμβίλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bougainvillea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From New Latin bougainvillea, from French Bougainville.</div><b>μπουκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mouthful</div><b>μπουλέτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bullet (singular form of μπουλεθιά, used in firearms)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. projectile (any object propelled into the air, intended to strike a target)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From dialectal Greek.</div><b>μπουλντόζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουλντόζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bulldozer</div><b>μπουμπουνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (impersonal, intransitive, sometimes) to rumble (to make a low, heavy, continuous sound)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to get rumbling (:a fire, by adding more fuel)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (familiar, transitive) to slug, to wallop (to hit very hard; to strike heavily)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>μπουμπουνητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουμπουνητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rumble, rumbling (of thunder), thunder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπουμπουν(ίζω)) + -ητό.</div><b>μπουμπούκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dear, darling, honey, sweetheart</div><b>μπουμπούκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουμπούκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bud (of a plant&#x27;s shoot or flower)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unknown. Possibly related to Ancient Greek βόμβυξ.</div><b>μπουνάτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουνάτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lull, dead calm, calm sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *melh₂-der.
Ancient Greek μᾰλᾰκός
Proto-Indo-European *-h₂
Proto-Indo-European *-éh₂
Proto-Indo-European *-i-eh₂
Proto-Hellenic *-íā
Ancient Greek -ῐ́ᾱ
Ancient Greek μᾰλᾰκῐ́ᾱbor.
Latin malacia
▲
Latin bonusinflu.
Vulgar Latin *bonacia
Venetan bonazzabor.
Greek μπουνάτσα
Borrowed from Venetan bonazza. Doublet of μαλακός and μαλακία.</div><b>μπουνατσάρει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to be dead calm (chiefly of sea, generally of good weather)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπουνάτσα (“calm seas”) + -άρω (“ending for verbs”), here only in 3rd person singular. See the Italian bonaccia.</div><b>μπουνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπουνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. punch</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pugno (“punch”), from Latin pugnus (“fist, punch”).</div><b>μπουντρούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουντρούμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dungeon (an underground prison or vault)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> An αντιδάνειο: Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پودروم (bodrum, podrum, “basement”), either from Koine Greek ὑπόδρομος or from Byzantine Greek ἱππόδρομος”) at the Hippodrome. Less likely is the reduction from the Hellenistic ὑπόδρομος or a contamination of the name Πέτρος, from which the Turkish placename Bodrum, from the fort of the Knights Hospitaller at Halicarnassus.</div><b>μπουράντζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μποράγο">μποράγο</a></div><b>μπουρέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουρέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boureki, börek, pasty, small pie (with a savoury or sweet filling)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μπουρέκιον, from Ottoman Turkish بورك.</div><b>μπουρδέλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουρδέλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brothel, bordello</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian bordello.</div><b>μπουρμπουάρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πουρμπουάρ">πουρμπουάρ</a></div><b>μπουρνούζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπουρνούζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bathrobe, cotton robe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish برنس (burnus, burnuz), from Arabic بُرْنُس (burnus).</div><b>μπουρουντιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μπουρουντιανή, neuter μπουρουντιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Burundian (relating to Burundi or its people)</div><b>μπουφάν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) puffer jacket, down jacket</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, indeclinable) jacket, bomber jacket, anorak</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French bouffant.</div><b>μπουχέσας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπουχέσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, offensive) scaredy cat, chickenshit, wuss (someone who is easily scared)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σιγά μην τολμήσει να πει κάτι αυτός ο <b>μπουχέσας</b>! - As if that wuss would dare to say anything!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, offensive) fatso, landwhale, tub of guts (fat and dull person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην φοβάσαι, αυτός ο <b>μπουχέσας</b> δε μπορεί να μας πιάσει. - Don&#x27;t worry, that fatso can&#x27;t catch us.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μπου (bou, “boo, scaring sound”) + χέζω.</div><b>μποφόρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μποφόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Beaufort</div>
  <div class='ex'>κλίμακα <b>Μποφόρ</b> - Beaufort scale</div><b>μποϊκοτάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be boycotted</div><b>μποϊκοτάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to boycott</div><b>μπούκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπούκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lock, tuft, curl (of hair)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French boucle. Compare Turkish bukle and Romanian buclă, also borrowed from French and with the same meaning.</div><b>μπούκοβο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chili flakes, crushed red pepper, red pepper flakes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Named after the village of Bukovo in North Macedonia, credited with the invention of this type of seasoning.</div><b>μπούμερανγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) boomerang</div><b>μπούνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scuppers (deck drainage ports/holes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian bugna.</div><b>μπούτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπούτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thigh (human or animal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leg</div>
  <div class='ex'>αρνί <b>μπούτι</b> με πατάτες - leg of lamb with potatoes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بوت (but).</div><b>μπούφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπούφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. eagle owl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) fool, stupid person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μποῦφος, from Late Latin būfus (“owl”). Or further from Ancient Greek βοῦφος. A borrowing is necessary because of the well-established bilabial spirantization, which is missing in the term. With the seldom occurrence of the Ancient Greek word, a derivation becomes less likely.</div><b>μπράβο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bravo, well done</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian bravo.</div><b>μπράντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) brandy, cognac (distilled beverage)</div><b>μπράτσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπράτσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. upper arm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the arm of a chair</div>
  <div class='def'>3. type of inflatable armband used as a life preserver to facilitate swimming, typical of small children</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian braccio.</div><b>μπρίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπρίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. briki (small metal coffee pot)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://βρίκιον">βρίκιον</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Middle Persian *ābrēkbor.?
Parthian *ābrēkbor.?
Classical Syriac ܐܒܪܝܩܐbor.?
Arabic إِبْرِيقbor.
Ottoman Turkish ابریقbor.
Greek μπρίκι
Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ابریق.</div><b>μπρίκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπρίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://βρίκιον">βρίκιον</a></div><b>μπρελόκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) keyring, fob, keyfob</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French breloque.</div><b>μπριάμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) a vegetable stew, typically with such ingredients as eggplant, onion, potatoes, tomato and zucchini, similar to ratatouille</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بریان (biryan), from Classical Persian بریان.</div><b>μπριζόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπριζόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steak</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan brisiola, from French brisolée, from Italian braciola.</div><b>μπροκαντέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Egyptiot, indeclinable) seller of antiques</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Egyptiot, indeclinable) antiquarian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French brocanteur.</div><b>μπροκαντερί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot, indeclinable) antique shop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French brocanteur + -ερί.</div><b>μπρος</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εμπρός">εμπρός</a></div><b>μπρος γκρεμός και πίσω ρέμα</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>between a rock and a hard place</div><b>μπρος πίσω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to and fro, back and forth</div><b>μπροστά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in front, in front of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ahead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From εμπρός.</div><b>μπροστινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μπροστινή, neuter μπροστινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fore, forward, front, in front (located at the front)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek εμπροστινός minus the initial unstressed vowel. By surface analysis, μπροστά + -ινός.</div><b>μπρούτζος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μπρούτζοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brass</div><b>μπρόκολο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπρόκολα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>broccoli</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian broccolo.</div><b>μπόγιας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dog catcher, dog warden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian boia.</div><b>μπόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μπόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. graft</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) vaccine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐμβόλιον, with aphesis of the initial ἐ and change of spelling -μβ- (-mv-) &gt; μπ- (b-) in order to preserve the original of the initial Ancient pronunciation.</div><b>μπόλικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μπόλικη, neuter μπόλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plenty, lots, galore, abundant (enough or more than necessary)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην ανησυχείς, έχουμε <b>μπόλικο</b> φαγητό για όλους. - Don&#x27;t worry, we have plenty of food for everyone.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of clothes) loose, full, ample (bigger than necessary)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φορούσε μια <b>μπόλικη</b> φούστα με μπιχλιμπίδια. - She was wearing an ample skirt with trinkets.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بول (bol) with adjective ending -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>μπόμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπόμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://βόμβα">βόμβα</a> (bomb)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. adulterated alcoholic drink</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Dagwood sandwich</div><b>μπόνους</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bonus (extra amount of money given as a premium)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English bonus, from Latin bonus.</div><b>μπόντι μπίλντερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bodybuilder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English bodybuilder.</div><b>μπόντι μπίλντερς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of μπόντι μπίλντερ</div><b>μπόντι μπίλντινγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bodybuilding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English bodybuilding.</div><b>μπόντιμπίλντερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of μπόντι μπίλντερ</div><b>μπόουλινγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) bowling (tenpin, etc)</div><b>μπόρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπόρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cloudburst, downpour, rainstorm, rain squall</div>
  <div class='def'>2. storm, tempest</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) storm, tempest (intense period of commotion or adversity)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μπόρα</b> είναι και θα περάσει - the storm will pass; it will blow over</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan bora (“north-eastern wind”), from Latin boreas (“north wind”), from Ancient Greek Βορέας. Compare Turkish bora (“squall”).</div><b>μπόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) bohrium</div><b>μπότα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. boot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bass drum</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) occupation army</div><b>μπόχα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπόχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stench, stink, reek (strong foul smell)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Possibly from *απόχα, with rebracketing of initial α and voicing of π → μπ influenced by the preceding feminine indefinite article forms μια (mia) and την (tin) respectively.</div><b>μπύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μπύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a popular spelling of <a href="bword://μπίρα">μπίρα</a> with upsilon, an expressive letter with no etymological basis</div><b>μσν.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μεσαιωνική ελληνική</div><b>μτβ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://μεταβατικός">μεταβατικός</a>: transitive (verb)</div><b>μτγν.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μεταγενέστερη ελληνική: Koine Greek (the Greek used between c. 300 BC - 600 AD)</div><b>μτφρδ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of μεταφραστικό <a href="bword://δάνειο">δάνειο</a>: calque, translated loanword, loan translation</div><b>μτχ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://μετοχή">μετοχή</a></div><b>μυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://μυς">μυς</a> (mys)</div><b>μυαλό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυαλά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) brains (used as food)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. brain (person providing intellectual input)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. mind</div>
  <div class='def'>5. head</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μυαλόν, from alteration of earlier μυαλός, from Koine Greek μυαλός, from Ancient Greek μυελός.</div><b>μυγαλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shrew (small mammal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek μῡγᾰλῆ, μῡγᾰλέη.</div><b>μυγδαλάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μυγδαλάτη, neuter μυγδαλάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αμυγδαλάτος">αμυγδαλάτος</a></div><b>μυγδαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυγδαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αμυγδαλιά">αμυγδαλιά</a></div><b>μυγιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of animals) to be bothered by flies</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το άλογο <b>μυγιάζεται</b>. - The horse is being bothered by flies.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, intransitive) to take offense, take umbrage, take something personally (feel or show resentment because one believes that something said is about them)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη <b>μυγιάζεσαι</b>, δεν εννοούσα εσένα! - Don&#x27;t get all offended, I didn&#x27;t mean you!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μύγα f + -ιάζομαι</div><b>μυγοπαγίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυγοπαγίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flytrap (trap for catching flies)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κρέμασα <b>μυγοπαγίδες</b> για να τις διώξω. - I hung up flytraps to get rid of them.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μύγα + παγίδα.</div><b>μυγοσκοτώστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυγοσκοτώστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) flyswatter (hand-held device for killing flies or other insects)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φερε μου τη <b>μυγοσκοτώστρα</b> να διώξω αυτές τις μύγες. - Bring me the flyswatter so I can get rid of these flies.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μύγα + -ο- (-o-) + σκοτώστρα.</div><b>μυελοπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural μυελοπάθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) myelopathy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, Ancient Greek μυελός) + -πάθεια.</div><b>μυθιστοριογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural μυθιστοριογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>novelist, writer, fictionist</div><b>μυθιστόρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυθιστορήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>novel (work of prose fiction)</div><b>μυθολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυθολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mythology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μυθολογία.</div><b>μυθοπλασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fiction (imaginative storytelling not recounting real facts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mythmaking</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fiction, fabrication (verbal or written account that is not based on actual events (often intended to mislead))</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μύθ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -πλασία, a corruption of Koine Greek μυθοπλαστία (same meaning).</div><b>μυκτήρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μυκτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, archaic, formal) nostril (either of the two orifices located on the nose)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μυκτήρ, from μύσσομαι. Related to μύξα.</div><b>μυλόπετρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυλόπετρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>millstone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μυλόπετρα. By surface analysis, μύλος + πέτρα.</div><b>μυξινάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυξινάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>golden grey mullet (Liza aurata, ayn. Mugil auratus)</div><b>μυοκάρδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) myocardium</div><b>μυρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be smelt, be smelled (by yourself or by others)</div><b>μυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to smell, sense a smell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to smell, have a particular smell</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to smell bad or good</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μυρίζω.</div><b>μυριάποδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυριάποδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>myriapod</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from μυριάποδα, from translingual Myriapoda, from Ancient Greek μῡρῐᾰ́ς + Ancient Greek πούς.</div><b>μυρμήγκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυρμήγκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ant (insect)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a hard-working, diligent person</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) an insignificant person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μυρμήγκιν, from Koine Greek μυρμήκιον, diminutive of the Ancient Greek μύρμηξ, from Proto-Indo-European *morwi-.</div><b>μυρμηγκίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Formicidae.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μυρμήγκι + -ίδες, a calque of translingual Formicidae.</div><b>μυρμηγκοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μυρμηγκοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anteater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μυρμήγκι + φαγάς</div><b>μυρμηκικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formic acid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μυρμηκικός + οξύ</div><b>μυρμηκικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μυρμηκική, neuter μυρμηκικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>antlike, related to an ant or ants</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μυρμήγκι n.</div><b>μυροβλυσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The exudation of the Oil of Saints.</div>
  <div class='ex'>also form: μυρόβλυσις (muróblusis)</div><b>μυροβλύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μυροβλύτες, feminine μυροβλύτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(usually) myroblyte</div>
  <div class='ex'>άγιος Δημήτριος ο <b>Μυροβλύτης</b> - saint Demetrius the Myroblyte</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek μυροβλύτης (Late Koine &amp; Medieval), from the ancient μῠ́ροPν)) + βλύ(ζω)) + -της (-tis). The epithet for saint Demetrius, since the 11th century.</div><b>μυροβλύτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυροβλύτισσες, masculine μυροβλύτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>myroblyte</div><b>μυρσίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυρσίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>myrtle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μυρσίνη.</div><b>μυρτιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυρτιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>common myrtle, Myrtus communis</div><b>μυρωδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυρωδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smell, scent, odour (UK), odor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μυρωδία.</div><b>μυρωδικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυρωδικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. herb</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aromatic</div><b>μυρωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine μυρωμένη, neuter μυρωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fragrant, anointed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of μυρώνομαι, passive voice of μυρώνω.</div><b>μυς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μυς or μύες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. muscle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) mouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μῦς, from Proto-Hellenic *mū́s, from Proto-Indo-European *múHs.</div><b>μυστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mystery</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sacrament</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μυστήριον.</div><b>μυστηριώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μυστηριώδης, neuter μυστηριώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mysterious</div><b>μυστικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>secretly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μυστικός">μυστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of <a href="bword://μυστικό">μυστικό</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of <a href="bword://μυστικό">μυστικό</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of <a href="bword://μυστικό">μυστικό</a></div><b>μυστικοπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) secretiveness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) secretive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μυστικο (mystiko, “secret”) + -πάθεια</div><b>μυστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. secret</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mystery</div><b>μυστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μυστική, neuter μυστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mystic, secret, clandestine, hidden, private</div><b>μυστρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυστριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trowel (mason’s tool, used in spreading and dressing mortar)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η δουλειά κάνει τους άντρες,
Το γιαπί, το πηλοφόρι, το <b>μυστρί</b>. - Hard work makes the man,
The building site, the mortarboard, the trowel.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek μυστρίον, from Ancient Greek μύστον, of unknown derivation.</div><b>μυταράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μυταράδες, feminine μυταρού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) bignose (someone with an unpleasantly large nose)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βλέπεις αυτόν το <b>μυταρά</b>; - Do you see that big-nosed guy?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μύτη + -αράς</div><b>μυταρού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυταρούδες, masculine μυταράς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) bignose (someone with an unpleasantly large nose)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βλέπεις αυτήν τη <b>μυταρού</b>; - Do you see that big-nosed lady?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μύτη + -αρού</div><b>μυτερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μυτερή, neuter μυτερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sharp, pointed, nosed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μύτη.</div><b>μυτοτσίμπιδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μυτοτσίμπιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>needlenose pliers</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μύτη + τσιμπίδα</div><b>μυωπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μυωπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>myopia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μυωπίᾱ, from μύωψ, from μύω + ὤψ.</div><b>μυωπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μυωπική, neuter μυωπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>myopic, short-sighted</div><b>μυώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μυώδης, neuter μυώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>muscular, musclebound (having large, well-developed muscles)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μυώδης. By surface analysis, μυ(ς) (my(s), “muscle”) + -ώδης.</div><b>μχ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of μ.χ. (m.ch.)</div><b>μωαμεθανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Mohammedanism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from μωαμεθαν(ός), “Mohammedan”) + -ισμός, a calque of French mahométisme.</div><b>μωβ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μοβ">μοβ</a> (mov)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μοβ">μοβ</a> (mov)</div><b>μωρέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vocative masculine singular of <a href="bword://μωρός">μωρός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, informal) you, dude, mate (a sort of vocative familiar call to a (male) friend or acquaintance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πότε θα μας επισκεφτείς, <b>μωρέ</b>; - When will you be visiting us, dude?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, informal) hey, jeez, gosh, yikes, oh my (expressing surprise, bewilderment, admiration, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Μωρέ</b> κοίτα τι γίνεται στην πλατεία! - Hey, look what&#x27;s happening in the square!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek μωρέ, from Ancient Greek μωρέ, already used as interjection in Hellenistic Koine, vocative singular of μωρός, initially as ὦ μῶρε, often in Aristophanes, vocative of form μῶρος. Compare the vernacular ωρέ. For the mediaeval forms μρέ, μπρέ, βρέ, see Etymology at the modern βρε (vre).
The interjection seems to be etymologically identical to the vocative adjective, from Byzantine Greek μωρέ, via the semantic shift “fool” → “buddy, mate, man”. Likely a doublet of βρε (vre) and ρε (re), which may be clippings from the same original word. As similar interjections are found across the Balkan sprachbund (cf. Albanian bre and more), this has led some linguists to speculate about substrate origin, but the more popular assumption is that all are eventually from Greek.</div><b>μωρή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://μωρός">μωρός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj, feminine (masculine μωρέ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, informal, offensive, sometimes) you (a sort of vocative familiar call to a (female) friend or acquaintance, or to a male when used before a feminine noun)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε από τη μέση, <b>μωρή</b> κλώσσα! - Get out of the way, you silly hen!</div><b>μωρομάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μωρομάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) a mother with an infant child</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From μωρo- (moro-) + -μάνα from μωρό and μάνα.</div><b>μωρομάντηλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baby wipe</div><b>μωρό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://μωρός">μωρός</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://μωρός">μωρός</a>:; masculine accusative singular</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μωρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. baby, infant</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>μωρό</b> θέλει τάισμα. - The baby needs feeding.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (followed by <a href="bword://μου">μου</a>) baby (term of address for partner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σ’ αγαπώ, <b>μωρό</b> μου. - I love you, baby.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μωρόν, from Ancient Greek μωρός, as babies were seen as &quot;stupid morons without knowledge&quot;.</div><b>μωρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μωρή, neuter μωρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stupid, silly, daft, simple</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν θέλω να κάθομαι να ακούω <b>μωρές</b> κουβέντες. - I don&#x27;t want to sit and listen to stupid conversations.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μωρός.</div><b>μόδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μόδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fashion, mode</div>
  <div class='ex'>της <b>μόδας</b> - fashionable, à la mode</div>
  <div class='def'>2. craze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French mode and/or Italian moda (“fashion”).</div><b>μόλις</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barely</div>
  <div class='def'>2. just</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μόλις.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as soon as, once</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μόλις.</div><b>μόλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μόλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jetty, mole</div><b>μόλυβδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek μόλυβδος, of Anatolian origin, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *merkʷ- (“dark”).</div><b>μόλυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μολύνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. infection</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contagion</div>
  <div class='def'>3. contamination, pollution</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μόλυνσις with -ση (-si) ending and semantic loan from English pollution.</div><b>μόνιμα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>permanently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adverbial form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://μόνιμος">μόνιμος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See μόνιμος.</div><b>μόνιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μόνιμη, neuter μόνιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. regular, standing (of an army)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. permanent, constant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μόνιμος; equivalent to μένω + -ιμος (-imos).</div><b>μόνο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. only</div>
  <div class='def'>2. just</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μόνο</b> και <b>μόνο</b> - just, only, solely</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μόνον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>but</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μόνον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://μόνος">μόνος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://μόνος">μόνος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μόνον.</div><b>μόνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine μόνη, neuter μόνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. solitary, alone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unmarried</div>
  <div class='def'>3. solo, unaccompanied</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>μόνος</b> μου - by myself</div>
  <div class='def'>4. unique, singular</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (used with an article) only</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Κώστας ήταν ο <b>μόνος</b> που έτρεξε να βοηθήσει. - Kostas was the only one to run for help.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek μόνος.
Compare Mariupol Greek мо́нос, мо́ну.</div><b>μόνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μονώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insulation</div><b>μόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. molecule</div>
  <div class='def'>2. particle, speck (a very small piece of matter)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. part (of the body)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. part (of the body); genitalia, parts</div>
  <div class='def'>5. particle</div>
  <div class='def'>6. point (in an evaluative scale)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μόριον.</div><b>μόρσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μόρσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncommon) tenon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian morso, from Latin morsus, participle form of the verb mordere (“to bite”)</div><b>μόρτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μόρτηδες, feminine μόρτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical, literally) gravedigger (someone immune to plague who was paid to bury the bodies of those affected)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) lowlife, lout, bum, ragamuffin, scoundrel (untrustworthy, despicable or disreputable person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πλαστό το πασαπόρτι,
σαν και την καρδιά σου, <b>μόρτη</b>. - The forged passport,
Is like your heart, you lowlife.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably derived, through Turkish, from Italian morti, plural of morto (“dead”).</div><b>μόρτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μόρτισσες, masculine μόρτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical, literally) gravedigger (woman immune to plague who was paid to bury the bodies of those affected)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) lowlife, lout, bum, ragamuffin, scoundrel (untrustworthy, despicable or disreputable female person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> μόρτης + -ισσα (-issa)</div><b>μόρφωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) education</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) erudition, learning</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) shaping, form</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για τη <b>μόρφωση</b> άποψης έγινε αυτοψία. - To form a view an autopsy was performed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine μόρφωσις.</div><b>μόσχευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μοσχεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transplant, graft, cutting (a transplanted plant piece)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transplant, graft (a transplanted organ or tissue)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek μόσχευμα.</div><b>μότο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) motto</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian motto, from Latin muttum (“mutter”).</div><b>μόχθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μόχθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toil, drudgery, hard work, labour(UK), labor(US)</div><b>μύγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μύγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fly, housefly (any insect of the order Diptera or Muscidae)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για σκέπασε το κρέας, μπας και πλακώσουν <b>μύγες</b>! - Cover up the meat, in case flies swarm onto it!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μύγα, μυῖγα, μύγια, from Ancient Greek μυῖα.</div><b>μύγδαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μύγδαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αμύγδαλο">αμύγδαλο</a></div><b>μύδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μύδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mussel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μύδιν, from Ancient Greek μύδιον, the diminutive of μῦς.</div><b>μύθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μύθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fable, tale, story, myth, fairy tale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Ancient Greek μῦθοςder.?
Greek μύθος
 From Ancient Greek μῦθος.</div><b>μύκητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μύκητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fungus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μύκης.</div><b>μύλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μύλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mill (for grinding flour, etc; the building housing it)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αλέθω κόκκους καφέ με τον μύλο μου. - I grind coffee beans with my mill.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the cylinder of a revolver</div>
  <div class='def'>3. the merry-go-round, roundabout (playground equipment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μύλη</div><b>μύξα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μύξες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, familiar) snot, booger, bogey (nasal mucus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταματά να τρως τις <b>μύξες</b> σου. - Stop eating your snot.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, humorous) snot, slob (any slovenly person who causes disgust)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην τρως το φαΐ που έφτιαξε αυτή η <b>μύξα</b>. - Don&#x27;t eat the food that slob made you.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μύξα, which shares an origin with μύκης, from Proto-Indo-European *mew-k- (“slip, slime”). Cognates include Latin mucus.</div><b>μύριοι</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. numerous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ten thousand, myriad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> In sense &quot;ten thousand&quot;, learned borrowing from Ancient Greek μύριοι. In sense &quot;numerous&quot;, inherited from Ancient Greek μύριοι.</div><b>μύρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μύρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. perfume, scent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. oil of saints, aromatic oil</div>
  <div class='def'>3. myrrh</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μῠ́ρον.</div><b>μύρρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μύρρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μύρο">μύρο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek μύρρα, from a Semitic root M-R-R meaning bitter.</div><b>μύρτιλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural μύρτιλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bilberry, whortleberry (Vaccinium myrtillus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Μυρτίλος.</div><b>μύτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural μύτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nose</div>
  <div class='def'>2. point, pencil lead</div>
  <div class='def'>3. toe (of shoe)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. sense of smell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek μύτη, from Ancient Greek μύτις. Displaced earlier Ancient Greek ῥίς.</div><b>μώλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural μώλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μόλος">μόλος</a></div><b>ν΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ν΄">Ν΄</a></div><b>νάζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νάζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, often) mincing manner, affectation, airs and graces, coquetry (unnatural, cutesy or put on behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή εκεί πέρα όλο κάνει <b>νάζια</b> για να βρει άντρα. - That one over there is always playing cute so she can find a man.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, in plural) acting up, carrying on (stubborn behaviour in order to get something one desires)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μικρή κάνει <b>νάζια</b> επειδή δεν της αγόρασα το παιχνίδι που ήθελε. - My young girl is acting up because I didn&#x27;t buy her the toy she wanted.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ناز (naz), from Classical Persian ناز.</div><b>νάιλον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nylon</div><b>νάμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spring, fount, source of clean water</div>
  <div class='def'>2. communion wine, sacramental wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νᾶμα.</div><b>νάμπου</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://είνταμπου">είνταμπου</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Νάμπου</b> κάμνεις; - What are you doing? How do you do?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Clipping of είνταμπου.</div><b>νάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νάνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dwarf (person of short stature, often one whose limbs are disproportionately small in relation to the body)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Τύριον Λάννιστερ στο «Game of Thrones» είναι <b>νάνος</b>. - Tyrion Lannister from Game of Thrones is a dwarf.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (attributive) dwarf (animal, plant or other thing much smaller than the usual of its sort)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νάνος</b> φοίνικας - dwarf palm tree</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dwarf, dwarf star (star of relatively small size)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. dwarf (member of a race of beings, usually depicted as having some sort of supernatural powers and being skilled in crafting and metalworking)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νᾶνος.</div><b>νάρδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural νάρδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>valerian, nard, spikenard (Nardostachys grandiflora).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νάρδος.</div><b>νάρκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νάρκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. torpor, stupor, lethargy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. numbness</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mine, landmine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νάρκη. The &quot;mine, landmine&quot; sense is a loose semantic loan from French torpille (“electric ray; torpedo”), whence also τορπίλη.</div><b>νάρκισσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νάρκισσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. narcissus, daffodil (plant, flower)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. narcissist (person)</div><b>νάτριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural νάτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) sodium (alkali metal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>υδροξείδιο του <b>νατρίου</b> - sodium hydroxide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from New Latin natrium</div><b>νάφθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νάφθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>naphtha (variable mixture of liquid hydrocarbons with boiling point of 35–200 °C)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νάφθα. Doublet of νέφτι.</div><b>νέα ελληνικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Modern Greek, Neo-Hellenic language</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νέος &amp; ελληνικά neuter plural, substantivized from ελληνικός.</div><b>νέα ελληνική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of νέα <a href="bword://ελληνικά">ελληνικά</a>: Modern Greek, Neo-Hellenic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine singular substantivized from adjectives νέος &amp; ελληνικός by ellipsis of feminine noun γλώσσα.</div><b>νέγρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νέγρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a black woman</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a negress</div><b>νέγρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νέγροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a black person, especially a black man</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a negro</div><b>νέκρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>necrosis</div><b>νέκταρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek, uncountable) nectar (the drink of the gods)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, uncountable) an especially delicious drink</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νέκταρ.</div><b>νέο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://νέος">νέος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://νέος">νέος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νέα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>news</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχει κανένα <b>νέο</b>; - Is there any news?</div><b>νέον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) neon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English neon, from Ancient Greek νέον.</div><b>νέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νέα, neuter νέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. young, youthful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. modern, new</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek νέος, from Proto-Hellenic *néwos, from Proto-Indo-European *néwos. Compare to the inherited Demotic synonym νιος (nios).</div><b>νέρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) black</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian nero, from Latin niger.</div><b>νέφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νέφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smog</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cloud</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cloud</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νέφος.</div><b>νέφτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νέφτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. turpentine, volatile essential oil from distillation of terebinth or pine tree resin, used as a solvent and paint thinner.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mineral turpentine, white spirit, volatile transparent liquid derived from paraffin, used as a solvent and paint thinner, with similar applications but very different chemical consistency from resin-derived wood turpentine.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish نفت (neft, “spirit of turpentine”), from Classical Persian نفت (naft). Doublet of νάφθα.</div><b>νέφωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεφώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cloud cover, cloudiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek νέφωσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>νέων ελληνικών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive of νέα <a href="bword://ελληνικά">ελληνικά</a></div><b>νήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. yarn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. filament</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Internet) thread (series of messages)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νῆμα.</div><b>νήσος Αγίας Ελένης</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Saint Helena (an island and constituent part of the British overseas territory of Saint Helena, Ascension and Tristan da Cunha, located in the South Atlantic Ocean)</div><b>νήσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νήσσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, humorous) synonym of the modern <a href="bword://πάπια">πάπια</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Occurs only in the quasi-Katharevousa set phrase of unknown origin:</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νῆσσα, Ionic form of νῆττα.</div><b>νίκελ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://νικέλιο">νικέλιο</a></div><b>νίκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>victory</div><b>νίπτω τας χείρας μου</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to wash one&#x27;s hands of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From The Bible&#x27;s account of Pontius Pilate washing his hands and refusing to condemn Jesus. Matthew Ch.27:24.</div><b>νίτρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nitration</div><b>να είσαι καλά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Mostly used to express gratitude. Thank you!, Thank you very much!.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Literally, May you be well !</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From second person subjunctive form of είμαι + καλά.</div><b>να μη με βρει το βράδυ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>if I&#x27;m lying, I&#x27;m dying, cross my heart and hope to die (I am absolutely telling the truth)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Να μη με βρει το βράδυ</b> αν σου λέω ψέματα! - If I&#x27;m lying, I&#x27;m dying.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally &quot;that night won&#x27;t find me&quot;.</div><b>να φάνε και οι κότες</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic, indeclinable, postpositional) galore, in abundance, to spare (literally: so that even the chickens eat)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν κέρδισε το λαχείο, είχε λεφτά <b>να φάνε και οι κότες</b>. - When he won the lottery, he had money galore.</div><b>ναΐφ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) naive, inexperienced</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) naive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French naïf.</div><b>ναβάχο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Navajo</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Navajo (language)</div><b>ναδίρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) nadir</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Either a Learned borrowing from French nadir, or borrowed from Spanish nadir, ultimately from Arabic نَظِير, implying the &#x27;opposite of&#x27; zenith.</div><b>ναζί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Nazi</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from German Nazi.</div><b>ναζίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ναζίστριες, masculine ναζιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Nazi (female follower of Nazism)</div><b>ναζιάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ναζιάρες, masculine ναζιάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) mincing girl/woman, simpering girl/woman (female who exhibits unnatural, cutesy or put on behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι αυτή μια <b>ναζιάρα</b>! - Isn&#x27;t she a mincing hussy!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νάζι + -άρα.</div><b>ναζιάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναζιάρηδες, feminine ναζιάρα or ναζού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) mincing man, simpering man (male who exhibits unnatural, cutesy or put on behaviour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νάζι + -άρης.</div><b>ναζισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Nazism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from German Nazismus.</div><b>ναζιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναζιστές, feminine ναζίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Nazi (male follower of Nazism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From German Nazist.</div><b>ναζιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ναζιστική, neuter ναζιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Nazi, Nazist</div><b>ναζού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ναζούδες, masculine ναζιάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) mincing girl/woman, simpering girl/woman (female who exhibits unnatural, cutesy or put on behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι αυτή μια <b>ναζού</b>! - Isn&#x27;t she a mincing hussy!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νάζι + -ού.</div><b>ναι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναί.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) yes</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ναι</b> σε όλα! - Yes to all!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναί.</div><b>νανομελισσοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νανομελισσοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Merops orientalis, the green bee-eater, little green bee-eater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νανο- (nano-, “little”) + μελισσοφάγος</div><b>νανούρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νανουρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lullaby, cradle song</div><b>νανόκυκνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νανόκυκνοςοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Bewick&#x27;s swan, tundra swan, Cygnus cygnus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νανό- + κύκνος</div><b>νανόπτερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νανόπτερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>American cormorant (Nannopterum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Nannopterum. By surface analysis, νάνος + Ancient Greek πτερόν.</div><b>ναργιλές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναργιλέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hookah, narghile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish نارگیله (nargile), from Classical Persian نارگیله, ultimately from Sanskrit नारिकेल.</div><b>ναρκοληψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) narcolepsy</div><b>ναρκομανής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ναρκομανείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drug addict (esp. addicted to opiates)</div><b>ναρκοπέδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ναρκοπέδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. minefield</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) minefield (a dangerous situation requiring care)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νάρκη + πεδίο.</div><b>ναρκωτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ναρκωτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. narcotic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. drug (affecting the central nervous system)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ναρκωτικός">ναρκωτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ναρκωτικός">ναρκωτικός</a></div><b>ναρκωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ναρκωτική, neuter ναρκωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>narcotic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναρκῶ.</div><b>νασιοναλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νασιοναλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nationalism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νασιοναλ (nasional) + -ισμός.</div><b>ναυάγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ναυάγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shipwreck (the wreck of a ship)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shipwreck (the wrecking of a ship)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. breakdown, crash</div>
  <div class='def'>4. bankruptcy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναυᾱ́γιον, from ναῦς + ἄγνῡμι.</div><b>ναυαγοσώστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναυαγοσώστες, feminine ναυαγοσώστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. male lifeguard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lifeboatman, male member of lifeboat crew</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ναυαγοσωστικός.</div><b>ναυαγοσώστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναυαγοσώστριες, masculine ναυαγοσώστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female lifeguard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. female lifeboat crew member</div><b>ναυαγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sink (of a ship; of a passenger on a sunk ship)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to result in utter failure</div><b>ναυαρχίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flagship</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ναυαρχίς by ellipsis of Ancient Greek ναῦς f, from the accusative singular τὴν ναυαρχίδα.</div><b>ναυπηγείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ναυπηγεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shipyard</div><b>ναυς</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νήες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic, historical, obsolete) an ancient ship</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic, figuratively, obsolete) nave (part of a church)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νᾱῦς, from Proto-Indo-European *néh₂us. Cognate with Italian nave, Persian ناو.</div><b>ναυσιπλοΐα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) navigation</div><b>ναυτία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ναυτίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nausea, seasickness, motion sickness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. disgust</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναύτης, ναῦς.</div><b>ναυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ναυτική, neuter ναυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. marine, nautical</div>
  <div class='def'>2. naval</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to Ancient Greek ναῦς</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναυτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seaman, mariner, sailor, seafarer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to Ancient Greek ναῦς</div><b>ναυτιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shipping, navigation (traffic or travel by vessel, especially commercial shipping)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shipping (especially commercial: the body of ships belonging to one nation, port or industry; ships collectively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εμπορική <b>ναυτιλία</b> - merchant shipping</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ναυτιλία.</div><b>ναυτοδικείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αεροδικεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>naval court martial</div><b>ναυτολόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ναυτολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ship&#x27;s articles</div><b>ναφθαλίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) naphthalene</div><b>ναϊάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ναϊάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>naiad (a river nymph)</div><b>ναϊάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ναϊάδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ναϊάδα">ναϊάδα</a></div><b>ναός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>place of worship: church, temple, mosque, synagogue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναός.</div><b>ναύαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ναύαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>admiral (appointed commander of a navy; NATO grade OF-9 or equivalent ranks in other navies)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναύαρχος.</div><b>ναύλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fare (the cost of a ticket)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ναύλος.</div><b>ναύλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναύλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fee (for chartering a ship or aeroplane)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carriage (charge carrying goods)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fare)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναῦλος.</div><b>ναύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ναύτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sailor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ναύτης.</div><b>νεάργυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νεάργυροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nickel silver</div><b>νεαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νεαρή, neuter νεαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>young, youthful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek νεᾰρός.</div><b>νεβρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεβρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fawn skin (especially as a garment of Dionysus)</div><b>νεκρή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://νεκρός">νεκρός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://νεκρός">νεκρός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://νεκρός">νεκρός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκρές, masculine νεκρός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dead woman, decedent</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η σορός της <b>νεκρής</b> μεταφέρεται στο κοιμητήριο. - The deceased&#x27;s body is being transferred to the cemetery.</div><b>νεκρική ακαμψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rigor mortis</div><b>νεκρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νεκρική, neuter νεκρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. death</div>
  <div class='def'>2. funeral</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) deathly, dead</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νεκρική</b> σιγή - deathly silence</div><b>νεκροθάλαμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νεκροθάλαμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>morgue, mortuary (building or room where dead bodies are kept before burial or cremation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πτώμα του έμεινε μια βραδιά στο <b>νεκροθάλαμο</b> και την επόμενη μέρα τον θάψανε. - His body spent one night in the morgue and the next day he was buried.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “dead”) + θάλαμος. First attested 1887.</div><b>νεκροθάφτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νεκροθάφτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gravedigger (person employed to dig graves)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>νεκροθάφτης</b> καλύπτει το φέρετρο με χώμα. - The gravedigger is covering the coffin with dirt.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) ender, wrecker, ruiner (someone or something that puts an end to something else)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Εμφυλιος Πόλεμος ήταν ο <b>νεκροθάφτης</b> της εθνικής ενότητας. - The civil war put an end to national unity.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek νεκροθάπτης, equivalent to νεκρο- (nekro-, “death”) + θάβω.</div><b>νεκροκεφαλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκροκεφαλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skull, death&#x27;s-head (symbol for danger of death)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “death”) + κεφαλή</div><b>νεκροκρέβατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νεκροκρέβατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. deathbed (bed on which someone dies)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, rare) coffin, casket (oblong closed box in which a dead person is buried)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “death”) + κρεβάτι.</div><b>νεκρολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκρολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obituary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “death”) + -λογία</div><b>νεκρομαντεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκρομαντείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>necromancy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek νεκρομαντεία. By surface analysis, νεκρο- (nekro-, “necro-, dead”) + -μαντεία</div><b>νεκροστάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νεκροστάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>morgue, mortuary, viewing room (room where dead bodies are kept for viewing before burial or cremation, often next to a chapel)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συγκινήθηκε πολύ όταν τον είδε στο <b>νεκροστάσιο</b>. - He was very moved when he saw him in the mortuary.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “dead”) + -στάσιο. First attested 1883.</div><b>νεκροταφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νεκροταφεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. graveyard, cemetery (place where the dead are buried)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η διάσημη ηθοποιός θα ταφεί αύριο στο Πρώτο <b>Νεκροταφείο</b> Αθηνών. - The famous actress will be laid to rest tomorrow in the First Cemetery of Athens.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, humorous) deathly silent place</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για βάλε λίγη μουσική, είναι σκέτο <b>νεκροταφείο</b> εδώ μέσα! - Put on some music will you, it&#x27;s like a graveyard in here!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly, figuratively) graveyard, junkyard, scrapheap (final storage place for things that are no longer useful or useable)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το παλιό μου αμάξι πήγε στο <b>νεκροταφείο</b> αυτοκινήτων. - My old car went to the car junkyard.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “dead”) + τάφ(ος), “grave”) + -είο. First attested 1833.</div><b>νεκροτομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκροτομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>autopsy, necrotomy, necropsy (dissection performed on a corpse to find cause of death)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επειδή πέθανε νέος και ξαφνικά, χρειάστηκε να γίνει <b>νεκροτομή</b>. - Because he died young and suddenly, an autopsy needed to take place.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “dead”) + τομή. First attested 1852.</div><b>νεκροτομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νεκροτομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>morgue, mortuary (specifically, ones in which autopsies are carried out to determine cause of death), autopsy room</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι φοιτητές ιατρικής διενεργούν νεκροτομή στο <b>νεκροτομείο</b>. - The medical students are carrying out an autopsy in the morgue.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκροτομή + -είο. First attested 1876.</div><b>νεκροφάγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νεκροφάγος, neuter νεκροφάγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>necrophagous, scavenging, carrion feeding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νεκρο- (nekro-, “carrion”) + -φάγος.</div><b>νεκροφυλακείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νεκροφυλακεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>morgue, mortuary (building or room where dead bodies are kept before burial or cremation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πτώμα του έμεινε μια βραδιά στο <b>νεκροφυλακείο</b> και την επόμενη μέρα τον θάψανε. - His body spent one night in the morgue and the next day he was buried.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “dead”) + φυλάσσω + -είο. First attested 1833.</div><b>νεκροφόρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκροφόρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hearse (vehicle for carrying coffins to the cemetery)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νεκρο- (nekro-, “dead”) + φέρω</div><b>νεκροψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκροψίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>necropsy, autopsy</div><b>νεκρόπολη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεκροπόλεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>necropolis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νεκρόπολῐς.</div><b>νεκρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νεκρή or νεκρά, neuter νεκρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dead (no longer living)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας μου είναι <b>νεκρός</b> εδώ και πέντε χρόνια. - My father has been dead for five years.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) dead, lifeless (lacking energy or life)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το πάρτι ήταν εντελώς <b>νεκρό</b>. Καλύτερα να εμένα σπίτι. - That party was completely dead. It would have been better to stay home.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) dead (completely inactive; broken, inoperable)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν σήκωσα το τηλέφωνο, ήταν <b>νεκρό</b>. - When I picked up the phone, it was dead.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) dead (no longer used or required)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νεκρός</b> νόμος - dead law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νεκρός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νεκροί, feminine νεκρή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dead man, decedent</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η σορός του <b>νεκρού</b> μεταφέρεται στο κοιμητήριο. - The deceased&#x27;s body is being transferred to the cemetery.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νεκρός.</div><b>νεκταρίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νεκταρίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nectarine</div><b>νενέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) grandmother</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Turkish nine (“grandmother”)
: is attested from the 1st century AD.)</div><b>νεοαρχαιοζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νεοαρχαιοζωική, neuter νεοαρχαιοζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Neoarchean</div><b>νεοβιταλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neovitalism</div><b>νεογέννητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νεογέννητη, neuter νεογέννητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>newborn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek νεογέννητος with semantic loan from French nouveau-né in non-literal contexts. By surface analysis, νεο- (neo-) + γεννη-) + -τος (-tos).</div><b>νεογενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νεογενής, neuter νεογενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic) new, recent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic) newborn</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Neogene</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek νεογενής.
Sense 3 is a semantic loan from English Neogene.</div><b>νεογραμματικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Neogrammarian / neogrammarian</div><b>νεοδαμώδης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νεοδαμώδεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>An emancipated helot in Ancient Sparta</div><b>νεοδαρβινισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neo-Darwinism</div><b>νεοδύμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) neodymium</div><b>νεοελληνικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of νέα <a href="bword://ελληνικά">ελληνικά</a>: Modern Greek, Neo-Hellenic language</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter plural substantivized from adjective νεοελληνικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of νεοελληνικός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>νεοελληνική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of νέα <a href="bword://ελληνικά">ελληνικά</a>: Modern Greek, Neo-Hellenic language</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine singular substantivized from adjective νεοελληνικός by ellipsis of feminine noun γλώσσα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of νεοελληνικός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>νεοζηλανδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νεοζηλανδική, neuter νεοζηλανδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) of New Zealand</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νεοζηλανδικά</b> κρασιά - New Zealand wines</div><b>νεοκλασικισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural νεοκλασικισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) neoclassicism</div><b>νεοκύστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neobladder</div><b>νεολαία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεολαίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>youth (young people, collectively)</div><b>νεολιθική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://νεολιθικός">νεολιθικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://νεολιθικός">νεολιθικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://νεολιθικός">νεολιθικός</a></div>
