  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νεράντζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bitter orange, Seville orange (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek νεράντζι, form of νεράντζιον, from Venetan naranza or Arabic or Persian origin.</div><b>νεραντζιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νεραντζιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Seville orange tree, bitter orange tree</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι δρόμοι της Αθήνας είναι γεμάτοι <b>νεραντζιές</b>. - The streets of Athens are full of bitter orange trees.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek νεραντζέα, from νεράντζιον + -έα. By surface analysis, νεράντζ(ι)) + -ιά.</div><b>νεροαρουραίος</b>
