  <div class='def'>1. sink, washbasin</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>νεροχύτης</b> της κουζίνας - kitchen sink</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) of someone who speaks vulgarly</div>
  <div class='ex'>έχω ένα στόμα <b>νεροχύτη</b> - to have a mouth like a sewer (literally &quot;like a sink&quot;)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νεροχύτης, from νερό + χυ- (khu-)) + -της.</div><b>νερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. water (liquid H₂O)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μαλακό <b>νερό</b> - soft water</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) glass or bottle of water</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φέρε μου ένα <b>νερό</b>, σε παρακαλώ. - Bring me a glass of water, please.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) water bill (utility bill for water)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πλήρωσα το <b>νερό</b>. - I paid the water bill.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) wash (a cycle in a washing machine)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε βγήκε ο λεκές στο <b>νερό</b>. - The stain didn&#x27;t come out in the wash.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νερό(ν)), from Koine Greek νηρόν, from νηρός, from Ancient Greek νεαρός, from the term νεαρὸν ὕδωρ. Cognate with Italiot Greek nerò, Pontic Greek νερόν. Displaced earlier Ancient Greek ὕδωρ almost entirely, except in compound terms and formal language. Sometimes misattributed to unrelated Etruscan 𐌍𐌄𐌓𐌉 (neri, “water”) or even Sanskrit नीर.</div><b>νερόμυλος</b>
