  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νηφάλια, neuter νηφάλιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. calm, astute, sober, collected, dispassionate</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νηφάλια</b> σκέψη - a sober thought</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sober, not drunk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νηφάλιος.</div><b>νηφαλίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. soberly (not drunkenly)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. calmly</div><b>νηφαλιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sobriety</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νηφαλιότης, equivalent to νηφάλιος + -ότητα.</div><b>νι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) nu, the 13th letter of the modern Greek alphabet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable, symbol) tick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νῦ.</div><b>νιάου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(onomatopoeic) meow (sound of a cat)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Νιάου νιάου</b>, βρε γατούλα,
Με τη ροζ μυτούλα,
Γατούλα μου μικρή. - Meow, meow, little cat,
With the little pink nose,
My little cat.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) meow (sound a cat makes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>νιάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. youth (the quality or the time of being young)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να ’χα τα <b>νιάτα</b> σου, θα τα μπορούσα όλα! - If I had your youth, I could do anything!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (collective) youth, young people (the young as a group)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα οργισμένα <b>νιάτα</b> απαιτούσαν δωρεάν παιδεία. - The angry youths demanded free education.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νεάτα, from Ancient Greek νεότης.</div><b>νιαουρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to meow, miaow (the cry of a cat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to whine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νιάου.</div><b>νιγηρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νιγηρική, neuter νιγηρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Nigerien (of or pertaining to Niger or its people)</div><b>νικάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to win, prevail</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to defeat, conquer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νικώ + -άω, from Ancient Greek νικῶ, contracted form of νικάω. From νίκη f.</div><b>νικέλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nickel</div><b>νικήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νικήτριες, masculine νικητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>winner, victor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νῑκήτρῐᾰ. By surface analysis, νικητής + -τρια (-tria).</div><b>νικαραγουανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νικαραγουανή, neuter νικαραγουανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Nicaraguan</div><b>νικημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine νικημένη, neuter νικημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>defeated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of νικιέμαι, passive voice of active νικάω/νικώ (“I win, I prevail”).</div><b>νικητήριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νικητήρια, neuter νικητήριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>winning, relating to victory</div><b>νικητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νικητές, feminine νικήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>winner, victor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νικητής.</div><b>νικηφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νικηφόρος or νικηφόρα, neuter νικηφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>victorious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νῑκηφόρος, equivalent to νίκη + -φόρος.</div><b>νικοτίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nicotine</div><b>νικώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://νικάω">νικάω</a></div><b>νινί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baby, infant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νηνίον, from Ancient Greek νῆνις.</div><b>νινυδρίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νινυδρίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ninhydrin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from German Ninhydrin, suffixed with -ίνη.</div><b>νιοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νιοστή, neuter νιοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nᵗʰ, representing an unspecified ordinal number, usually the terminal item in a series.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Που αντιστοιχεί στο σωστό <b>νιοστό</b> όρο. - Corresponding to the correct nᵗʰ term.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νι (ni, “n”) + -οστός.</div><b>νιούτον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) newton (units of force)</div><b>νιπτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νιπτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>washbasin, sink</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowing from the accusative plural form of Ancient Greek νιπτήρ.</div><b>νιρβάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nirvana</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Sanskrit निर्वाण. First attested 1867.</div><b>νισάφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pity, self-restraint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish إنصاف with metathesis [in]&gt;[ni], ultimately from Arabic إِنْصَاف.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enough!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish إنصاف with metathesis [in]&gt;[ni], ultimately from Arabic إِنْصَاف.</div><b>νισαντήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ammonium chloride, sal ammoniac, an inorganic salt used as a flux for cleaning metal surfaces by reacting with the oxides on their surface</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish نشادر, from Classical Persian نشادر.</div><b>νισεστές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νισεστέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>starch, cornstarch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish نشاسته, possibly with [a] &gt; [e], from Classical Persian نشاسته.</div><b>νιτρικό αμμώνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ammonium nitrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νιτρικός + αμμώνιο.</div><b>νιτρικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nitric acid</div><b>νιτρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νιτρική, neuter νιτρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nitric</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νιτρικό</b> οξύ - nitric acid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) rotgut (drink)</div><b>νιτροβενζόλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural νιτροβενζόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) nitrobenzene</div><b>νιτρογόνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncommon, uncountable) nitrogen</div><b>νιτροκυτταρίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nitrocellulose</div><b>νιτροποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νιτροποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nitrification</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νίτρο + -ποίηση, calque of French nitrification.</div><b>νιφάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νιφάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. snowflake</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flake</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νιφάδες</b> καλαμποκιού - corn flakes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek νιφάς.</div><b>νιχόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nihonium</div><b>νιόβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) niobium</div><b>νιώθω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to feel</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νιώθω</b> ντροπή - I feel ashamed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νιώθω, probably from γνώθω influenced by the aorist form ἔγνωσα with influence from the aorist ενιωσα &lt; ενόησα), from Ancient Greek νοέω. Alternatively, the Byzantine term may stem from a form *ἐννοιῶ, from Ancient Greek ἔννοια).</div><b>νοήμων</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) intelligent (having at least a similar level of brain power to humankind)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>νοήμων</b> ζωή - intelligent life</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) intelligent (of high or especially quick cognitive capacity, bright)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: έξυπνος (éxypnos), ευφυής (effyís)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (literary, noun from verb) intelligence (an entity that is intelligent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νοήμων.</div><b>νοηματική γλώσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sign language</div><b>νοημοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural νοημοσύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) intelligence as the capacity of mind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, since 1856 from νοήμων, νοημ(ον)--, “intelligent”) + -οσύνη</div><b>νοητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νοητή, neuter νοητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) conceivable, perceptible, intelligible, comprehensible (able to be imagined in the mind)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν είναι <b>νοητό</b> να διακινδυνεύσεις τη ζωή σου γι’ αυτήν. - It&#x27;s not conceivable for you to risk your life for her.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, formal) imaginary, notional (that which exists only in the mind)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>νοητός</b> άξονας της Γης - the Earth&#x27;s notional axis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νοητός, from Ancient Greek νοέω.</div><b>νοιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to care for</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to take care of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐννοιάζομαι, ἐγνοιάζομαι, γνοιάζομαι, also with active forms, from Ancient Greek ἔννοι(α)) + -άζομαι.</div><b>νοικιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ενοικιάζω">ενοικιάζω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek νοικιάζω, from the Hellenistic Ancient Greek ἐνοικιάζω. Doublet of ενοικιάζω.</div><b>νοικοκυρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νοικοκυρές, masculine νοικοκύρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) housewife, female head of household, landlady</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νοικοκυρά, from Ancient Greek οἶκος + κυρά.</div><b>νοικοκυριό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νοικοκυριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. household, family within a single domicile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. housekeeping, housework, chores</div>
  <div class='def'>3. that which is necessary for a household: furniture, appliances, etc.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek νοικοκυριό, from νοικοκυρεύω + -ιό.</div><b>νοικοκύρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νοικοκύρηδες or νοικοκυραίοι, feminine νοικοκυρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. head of household, householder</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (familiar) master (someone who has control (in a locality))</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (familiar) landlord (a person that leases real property; a lessor)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. man of means</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Rebracketing of τον οικοκύρης with prothesis of ν to οικοκύρης, from οίκ(ος), “house, home”) + -ο- (-o-) + κύρης.</div><b>νοιώθω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://νιώθω">νιώθω</a></div><b>νομάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νομάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nomarch, department governor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νομός + -άρχης</div><b>νομίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to think, consider, opine</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Νομίζω</b> ότι η μητέρα του ζει. - I think that his mother is alive.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νομίζω.</div><b>νομικό πρόσωπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νομικά πρόσωπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>legal person</div><b>νομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νομική, neuter νομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pertaining to the law, legal</div>
  <div class='ex'>νομικός σύμβουλος (legal advisor)</div><b>νομισματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νομισματική, neuter νομισματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. monetary, of or related to currency</div>
  <div class='def'>2. numismatic</div><b>νομισματοκοπείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νομισματοκοπεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mint (coin production unit)</div><b>νομισματολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural νομισματολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coin collector, numismatist</div><b>νομισματοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>monetization</div><b>νομοθέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νομοθέτες, feminine νομοθέτις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. legislator</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σόλων ο νομοθέτης (Solon the legislator)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. legislature</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νομοθέτης.</div><b>νομοθεσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νομοθεσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. legislature, legislation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. code of laws</div><b>νομολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νομολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. legal precedent, all previous decisions made by the law courts as they apply to a case; case law, law as set by precedent (in English law this is common law)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially) judgements made by courts of appeal on points of law</div>
  <div class='ex'>H νομολογία του Aρείου Πάγου. - The judgments of the Supreme Court</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French nomologie (“nomology, the study of law related to precedent”).</div><b>νομομαθής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νομομαθής, neuter νομομαθές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. learned in law</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) jurist, legal expert, legal scholar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νομομαθής. By surface analysis, νομο- (nomo-) + -μαθής.</div><b>νομοσχέδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νομοσχέδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bill (draft law)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from νομο- (nomo-) + σχέδιο, a calque of German Gesetzentwurf.</div><b>νομοταγής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νομοταγής, neuter νομοταγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>law-abiding</div><b>νομπέλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nobelium</div><b>νομπελίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νομπελίστες, feminine νομπελίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Nobel Prize awardee, Nobel Prize winner</div><b>νομός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νομοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nome, department, county, prefecture</div>
  <div class='ex'>O <b>νομός</b> Λακωνίας είναι ο νοτιότερος <b>νομός</b> της Πελοποννήσου. - Laconia prefecture is the southernmost prefecture of the Peloponnese.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νομός.</div><b>νονά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νονές, masculine νονός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>godmother</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine of νονός.</div><b>νονός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νονοί, feminine νονά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. godfather, godparent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. godfather (mafia leader)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νονός, from Koine Greek νόννος, either of onomatopoetic origin or perhaps borrowed from Late Latin nonnus.</div><b>νοοτροπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νοοτροπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mentality, mindset</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from νοο- (noo-) + τρόπ(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>νορβηγικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Norwegian (the language of Norway and its people)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://νορβηγικός">νορβηγικός</a></div><b>νορβηγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νορβηγική, neuter νορβηγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Norwegian (related to the Norway, its people or language)</div><b>νορεπινεφρίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) norepinephrine</div><b>νοσηλεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νοσηλείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>care, treatment, hospitalisation</div><b>νοσηλευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νοσηλευτές, feminine νοσηλεύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>male nurse (especially a scientifically trained one)</div><b>νοσηλεύτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νοσηλεύτριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nurse (especially one scientifically trained)</div><b>νοσηλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to treat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νοσηλεύω.</div><b>νοσοκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νοσοκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hospital</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek νοσοκομεῖον. By surface analysis, νόσος + -κομείο.</div><b>νοσοκόμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νοσοκόμες, masculine νοσοκόμος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nurse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νοσοκόμος + -α (-a).</div><b>νοσοκόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νοσοκόμοι, feminine νοσοκόμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>male nurse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νόσος + -κόμος, or directly from Ancient Greek νοσοκόμος.</div><b>νοσοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disease bearer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νόσος + -φόρος”)</div><b>νοστίμευ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of νοστίμεψε, second-person singular simple past active perfective imperative of <a href="bword://νοστιμεύω">νοστιμεύω</a></div><b>νοσταλγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural νοσταλγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) nostalgia, homesickness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) nostalgia (bittersweet yearning for the things of the past)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French nostalgie, from New Latin nostalgia; a neoclassical compound coined from Ancient Greek νόστ(ος), “journey”) + ἄλγ(ος), “pain”) + suffix -ία.</div><b>νοσταλγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νοσταλγική, neuter νοσταλγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nostalgic</div><b>νοσταλγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural νοσταλγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>person who is homesick or nostalgic</div><b>νοσταλγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to feel nostalgic, be homesick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned back-formation from νοσταλγία, with -ώ.</div><b>νοστιμάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νοστιμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. taste, flavor, piquancy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. savoriness, palatability</div>
  <div class='def'>3. prettiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek νοστιμάδα; by surface analysis, νόστιμος + -άδα.</div><b>νοστιμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of food) I make tasty, season</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to make pretty</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of food) to become tasty</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, intransitive) to become pretty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek νοστιμίζω from νόστιμ(ος)) + -ίζω.</div><b>νοστιμεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fancy, find to one&#x27;s taste</div><b>νοστιμεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of food) to make tasty, season</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to make pretty, elegant</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, intransitive) to become pretty</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of food) to become tasty</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to fancy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek νοστιμεύω, verbal formation from νόστιμος, also + -ομαι (-omai, suffix for passive voice), forming passive voice νοστιμεύομαι with different meaning.</div><b>νοστιμούτσικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νοστιμούτσικη, neuter νοστιμούτσικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A little tasty, but not very.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νόστιμος + -ούτσικος.</div><b>νοσώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to ail, to be ailing, to be ill, to be sick</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive) to be ailing (of an economy or social institution)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νοσῶ, contracted form of νοσέω.</div><b>νοτίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>south</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from νότι(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>νοτιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νοτιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the ostro (a southerly Mediterranean wind)</div><b>νοτιοαμερικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νοτιοαμερικανική, neuter νοτιοαμερικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>South American</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νότιος + αμερικανικός</div><b>νοτιοανατολικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>southeast</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://νοτιοανατολικός">νοτιοανατολικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>southeast</div><b>νοτιοανατολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νοτιοανατολική, neuter νοτιοανατολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>southeast, southeasterly</div><b>νοτιοαφρικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νοτιοαφρικανική, neuter νοτιοαφρικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>South African (relating to South Africa or its people)</div><b>νοτιοδυτικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>southwest</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://νοτιοδυτικός">νοτιοδυτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>southwest</div><b>νοτιοδυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νοτιοδυτική, neuter νοτιοδυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>southwest, southwesterly, southwestwards, southwestern</div><b>νοτιοδυτικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>southwest</div><b>νουγκατίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νουγκατίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nougatine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French nougatine.</div><b>νουκλεΐνη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nuclein</div><b>νουκλεοπρωτεΐνη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nucleoprotein</div><b>νουνός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νουνοί, feminine νουνά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://νονός">νονός</a></div><b>νους</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νόες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mind, brains</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νοῦς.</div><b>νούμερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νούμερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>numeral, number</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From late Byzantine Greek νούμερος, borrowed from Italian numero, from Latin numerus.</div><b>νούφαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νούφαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lotus, water lily</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νούφαρο, from Arabic نُوفَر, a reduced form of نِلُوفَر, نِينُوفَر, from Middle Persian nylw(k)pl, from Sanskrit नीलोत्पल.</div><b>ντάγκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντάγκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(slang) A state of comfort and satisfaction of having consumed too much (food, alcohol, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήπιαμε και βαρέσαμε <b>ντάγκλες</b>. - We drank and then we got wasted.</div><b>ντάλα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) found in phrases</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ντάλα</b> μεσημέρι - high noon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish دال (dal) + -α (-a), suffix for adverbs.</div><b>ντάλα καλοκαίρι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high summer</div><b>ντάλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντάλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dahlia</div><b>ντάμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντάμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the queen in a pack of playing cards.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. draughts; checkers.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. female companion on a dance (or any event)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian dama (“queen (playing card), king (draughts)”, literally “lady”). Displaced Ancient Greek πεσσεία.</div><b>ντάμπου</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://είνταμπου">είνταμπου</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ντάμπου</b> κάμνεις; - What are you doing? How do you do?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Clipping of είνταμπου.</div><b>ντέρτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντέρτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. longing, passion</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το είχα <b>ντέρτι</b> να ταξιδέψω στη θάλασσα. - I had the longing to travel (sail) at sea.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sadness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish درد (derd, dert), from Classical Persian درد (dard).</div><b>ντέφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντέφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tambourine</div><b>ντήζελ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ντίζελ">ντίζελ</a></div><b>ντίγκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) dingo</div><b>ντίζελ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) diesel (engine and fuel)</div><b>νταβάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νταβάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gadfly, horsefly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://ταβάνι">ταβάνι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νταβάνι, ταβάνι, from Venetan tavan. The initial voicing is due to the preceding neuter definite article. The masculine forms in -ος (-os) are back-formations.</div><b>νταβάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νταβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ταβάς">ταβάς</a></div><b>νταβατζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νταβατζήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) pimp (man who arranges clients for prostitutes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish دعواجی (davaci), from Ottoman Turkish دعوى (dava), دعوا (dava) from Arabic دَعْوَى + Ottoman Turkish ـجی (ci).</div><b>νταμάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) quarry</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εδώ είναι Αττική, φαιό <b>νταμάρι</b>. - This is Attica, a dull grey quarry.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish طمر (damar, “vein, blood vessel”). See there for further etymology and cognates.</div><b>νταντά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νταντάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar) nanny, nursemaid (a woman or girl employed to care for children)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: τροφός f (trofós), γκουβερνάντα f (gkouvernánta)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ﺩﺍﺩﻯ, from Classical Persian دادا.</div><b>ντανταϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) dadaism</div><b>νταούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νταούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small drum, tupan, davul (folk musical instrument)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish طاول (davul, “drum”), from Arabic طَبْل.</div><b>νταπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda, indeclinable) masturbation</div><b>νταραβερίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, familiar) to hobnob with, have dealings with (be involved with financially)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Νταραβέριζεται</b> με διάφορες εταίρες. - He has dealings with different companies.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) to go out with, carry on with (be involved with romantically/sexually)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Νταραβέριζεται</b> με τον γαλατά. - She&#x27;s carrying on with the milkman.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νταραβέρι n + -ίζομαι</div><b>νταρμστάντιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) darmstadtium</div><b>ντατούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντατούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>datura</div><b>νταϊκόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) daikon, mooli</div><b>ντεκόρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) decor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French décor.</div><b>ντελίβερι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) delivery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration and unadapted borrowing from English delivery.</div><b>ντελίριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντελίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (familiar) delirium</div>
  <div class='def'>2. delirium (wild, frenzied excitement or ecstasy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian delirio.</div><b>ντελαπάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ντεραπάρω">ντεραπάρω</a></div><b>ντελβές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ντελβέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coffee grounds, sediments of Turkish coffee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish تلوه (telve, “coffee grounds”).</div><b>ντελιβεράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ντελιβεράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a delivery person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ντελίβερι + -άς. Since the beginning of the 21st century. From English deliveryman.</div><b>ντεμέκ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, informal) allegedly, supposedly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish دیمك (demek, “so, that is to say”).</div><b>ντεντέκτιβ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ντετέκτιβ">ντετέκτιβ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English detective.</div><b>ντεραπάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of vehicles) to skid over, turn upside-down</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύο αμάξια <b>ντεραπάρισαν</b> αλλά δεν υπήρξαν θύματα. - Two cars skidded over but there were no victims.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian derapare + -άρω. Also with [r] &gt; [l], ντελαπάρω.</div><b>ντετέκτιβ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) detective (police officer who looks for evidence as part of solving a crime)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έβαλε <b>ντετέκτιβ</b> να παρακολουθεί τη γυναίκα του. - He hired a detective to watch his wife.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English detective.</div><b>ντετερμινισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>determinism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From German Determinismus.</div><b>ντισπανσέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) dispensary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French dispensaire.</div><b>ντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) C (first note or tonic in the C major scale)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ντο</b> ματζόρε - C major</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) do</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian do.</div><b>ντοκιμαντέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) documentary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Unadapted borrowing from French documentaire.</div><b>ντολμάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ντολμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dolma, stuffed leaf (especially vine leaves)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish طولمه (dolma).</div><b>ντομάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντομάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tomato</div>
  <div class='ex'>αγγούρια και <b>ντομάτες</b> σαλάτα - cucumber and tomato salad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Nahuan *toma-tl
Classical Nahuatl tomatlbor.
Spanish tomate
Greek ντομάτα
From Spanish tomate.</div><b>ντοματάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντοματάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tomato, especially cherry tomato</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ντομάτα + -άκι.</div><b>ντοματίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντοματίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cherry tomato</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ντομάτα + -ίνι.</div><b>ντοματιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντοματιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tomato plant</div><b>ντοματοσαλάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντοματοσαλάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tomato salad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ντομάτα + σαλάτα</div><b>ντοσιέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) dossier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French dossier.</div><b>ντουέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>duet</div><b>ντουί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) lamp holder, lamp socket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French douille (“electrical socket”).</div><b>ντουζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ντους">ντους</a> (ntous)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French douche.</div><b>ντουζίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντουζίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twelve, dozen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Neapolitan duzzina (“dozen”), either from Occitan dotzena or French douzaine (or the corresponding ancestor languages), ultimately from Vulgar Latin *dōdeci and Latin duodecim.</div><b>ντουλάπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντουλάπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wardrobe (UK), closet (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) fat and clumsy woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ντουλάπι + -α (-a, augmentative suffix).</div><b>ντουλάπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντουλάπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cupboard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish دولاب (dolab) (whence Turkish dolap), from Classical Persian دولاب.</div><b>ντουμάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smoke, roke, especially of very thick consistency</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish دومان (duman, “smoke”).</div><b>ντουμπελέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντουμπελέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τουμπελέκι">τουμπελέκι</a></div><b>ντουμπλάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be replaced by a double (actor)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be dubbed</div><b>ντουμπλάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stand in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dub</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French doubler.</div><b>ντουνιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ντουνιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>world</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish دنیا, from Arabic دُنْيَا. With -ς (-s) suffix.</div><b>ντουντούκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>megaphone (chiefly the hand-held electric megaphone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish دودوك.</div><b>ντους</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) shower (device for bathing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Καθάρισε το <b>ντους</b> πριν βρωμίσει! - Clean the shower before it becomes dirty!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) shower (an instance of washing under the above)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάνε κανένα <b>ντους</b>, γιατί βρωμάει ο ιδρώτας! - Have a shower, will you, your sweat stinks!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French douche.</div><b>ντουφέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντουφέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τουφέκι">τουφέκι</a>: rifle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τουφέκι with voicing of initial [t] &gt; [d], as in τομάτα &gt; ντομάτα f.</div><b>ντούμπνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) dubnium</div><b>ντούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) corn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic درة (dura).</div><b>ντρέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be shy, be bashful</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ντρέπομαι</b> να μιλήσω στα κορίτσια. - I&#x27;m too shy to talk to girls.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be ashamed, feel shame</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ντράπηκα</b> με τον τρόπο που μίλησε στην μητέρα του. - I was ashamed of the way he spoke to his mother.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare, transitive) to respect</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ντρεπόταν</b> τον καθηγητή. - He respected the professor.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ντρέπομαι, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐντρέπομαι of active Ancient Greek ἐντρέπω, from ἐν (en, “in, on”) + τρέπω.</div><b>ντροπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ντροπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shyness, bashfulness (quality of being shy, a fear of social interactions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν πήγε να δώσει ομιλία, κοκκίνισε από <b>ντροπή</b>. - When she went to give a speech, she blushed from shyness.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shame, disgrace (painful feeling due to recognition or consciousness of impropriety, dishonor or other wrong)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο κατηγορούμενος χαμήλωσε το βλέμμα από <b>ντροπή</b>. - The accused lowered his gaze out of shame.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) shameful/disgraceful behaviour, shameful/disgraceful act</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>ντροπή</b> να κοροϊδεύεις τους ανάπηρους. - It is shameful to mock the disabled.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐντροπή. Cognate with English entropy.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for shame, shame</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει καταντήσει αηδία η κατάσταση, <b>ντροπή</b> πια! - The situation has ended up ridiculous, for shame!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐντροπή. Cognate with English entropy.</div><b>ντροπή σου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shame on you</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ντροπή σου</b> να βρίζεις γέρον άνθρωπο. - Shame on you for swearing at an elderly person.</div><b>ντροπαλά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ντροπαλός">ντροπαλός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ντροπαλός">ντροπαλός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ντροπαλός">ντροπαλός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shyly, bashfully, diffidently, sheepishly, timidly</div>
  <div class='ex'>«Ποιος είναι;» ρώτησε <b>ντροπαλά</b>. - “Who is it?” he asked sheepishly.</div><b>ντροπαλοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) shyness, bashfulness, diffidence</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχει πολλούς φίλους λόγω <b>ντροπαλοσύνης</b>. - She doesn&#x27;t have many friends due to shyness.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ντροπαλός + -οσύνη.</div><b>ντροπαλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ντροπαλή, neuter ντροπαλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shy, bashful, diffident, sheepish</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι πολύ <b>ντροπαλή</b> και δεν υψώνει ποτέ την φωνή. - She&#x27;s very shy and never raises her voice.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐντροπαλός, from Ancient Greek ἐντρέπω, from ἐν (en, “in, on”) + τρέπω.</div><b>ντροπαλότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) shyness, bashfulness, diffidence</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχει πολλούς φίλους λόγω <b>ντροπαλότητας</b>. - He doesn&#x27;t have many friends due to shyness.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ντροπαλός + -ότητα.</div><b>ντροπιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to feel ashamed, feel embarrassed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ντροπιάστηκα</b> όταν δεν μπορούσα να θυμηθώ τ’ όνομά του. - I was embarrassed when I couldn&#x27;t remember his name.</div><b>ντροπιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to shame, bring shame to, humiliate, disgrace, mortify, embarrass</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ντρόπιασες</b> την οικογένειά μας μ’ αυτό που έκανες. - You brought shame to our family with what you did.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐντροπιάζω, equivalent to ντροπή + -ιάζω.</div><b>ντροπιασμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ashamed, in a state of shame</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ντροπιασμένος">ντροπιασμένος</a></div><b>ντροπιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ντροπιασμένη, neuter ντροπιασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ashamed, embarrassed, shamefaced (feeling intense shame)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι παίκτες ήταν βαθιά <b>ντροπιασμένοι</b> για την ήττα τους. - The players were deeply ashamed of their defeat.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ντροπιάζομαι, passive voice of ντροπιάζω.</div><b>ντροπιαστικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ντροπιαστικός">ντροπιαστικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ντροπιαστικός">ντροπιαστικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ντροπιαστικός">ντροπιαστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shamefully, embarrassingly, mortifyingly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πόσο που μας πρόσφερε ήταν <b>ντροπιαστικά</b> μικρό. - The amount he offered us was embarrassingly small.</div><b>ντροπιαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ντροπιαστική, neuter ντροπιαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shameful, embarrassing, mortifying (causing intense shame)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι στο σχολείο είδαν την <b>ντροπιαστική</b> γυμνή μου φωτογραφία. - Everyone in school saw my embarrassing naked photo.</div><b>ντυμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ντυμένη, neuter ντυμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clad, dressed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. covered (of surfaces, objects)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ντύνομαι, passive voice of ντύνω (“I dress”).</div><b>ντόντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) dodo (Raphus cucullatus)</div><b>ντόπιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ντόπια, neuter ντόπιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of people) local, native, indigenous (pertaining to an area and living/present there also)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>ντόπιοι</b> ποτέ δεν βγαίνουν το βράδυ επειδή δεν είναι ασφαλές. - The natives never go out in the evening as it&#x27;s not safe.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of items) local, homegrown (from an area and being used/consumed there also)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>ντόπια</b> λεμόνια είναι πιο καλά από τα εισαγόμενα. - Homegrown lemons are better than imported ones.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐντόπιος, from ἐν (en, “in, on”) + τόπος.</div><b>ντύνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dress oneself</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dress up, wear a disguise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive form of ντύνω.</div><b>ντύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to dress someone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to cover, upholster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐνδύνω, from Ancient Greek ἐνδύω.</div><b>ντύσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ντυσίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. outfit, clothing, rig out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dressing, getting dressed</div>
  <div class='def'>3. upholstery, covering</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ντύνω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>νυκτερινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νυκτερινή, neuter νυκτερινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://νυχτερινός">νυχτερινός</a></div><b>νυκτόβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νυκτόβιa, neuter νυκτόβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nocturnal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νύξ + βίος.</div><b>νυμφαλίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Nymphalidae; the brush-footed butterflies.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Nymphalidae, suffixed with -ίδες.</div><b>νυμφευμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νυμφευμένη, neuter νυμφευμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>married, wed</div><b>νυν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) now, currently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νῦν.</div><b>νυν και αεί</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>now and forever, for ever and ever</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek νῦν καὶ ἀεὶ.</div><b>νυστάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be sleepy, feel tired</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μωρό <b>νυστάζει</b>. - The baby is tired.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make sleepy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η φωνή του με <b>νύσταξε</b>. - His voice made me sleepy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νυστάζω.</div><b>νυστέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νυστέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scalpel, lancet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish نیشتر, from Classical Persian نیشتر, with the spelling influenced by Ancient Greek νύσσω.</div><b>νυσταγμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nystagmus, miner&#x27;s disease</div><b>νυφίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νυφίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. weasel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ferret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a diminutive of νύφη. For similar semantic developments, compare Italian donnola and Spanish comadreja.</div><b>νυφικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νυφικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wedding dress, bridal gown</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of νυφικός</div><b>νυχθημερόν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for a day and a night continuously</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νυχθήμερον.</div><b>νυχοκόπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νυχοκόπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nail clipper, nail trimmer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from νύχι + -ο- (-o-) + κοπ- (kop-, “to cut, trim”) + -της (-tis).</div><b>νυχτερίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νυχτερίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bat (small flying mammal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νυκτερίς, from νύκτερος, from νύξ.</div><b>νυχτερινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νυχτερινή, neuter νυχτερινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. night</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Nυχτερινό</b> σχολείο - Night school, evening class</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nocturnal</div><b>νυχτοκόρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νυχτοκόρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>black-crowned night heron (Nycticorax nycticorax)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Nycticorax, as νύχτα + κόρακας. Contrast Ancient Greek νυκτικόραξ.</div><b>νυχτοπεταλούδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νυχτοπεταλούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νύχτα + πεταλούδα</div><b>νυχτοφοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nyctophobia.</div><b>νυχτωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νυχτωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary, poetic) nocturne (song or piece suggestive of night)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποια <b>νυχτωδία</b> το φως σου έχει πάρει;
Και σε ποιο γαλαξία να σε βρω; - What nocturne has taken away your light?
And in which galaxy should I find you?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νύχτα + ωδή + -ία.</div><b>νυχτόβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νυχτόβιa, neuter νυχτόβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://νυκτόβιος">νυκτόβιος</a></div><b>νχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ναύαρχος">ναύαρχος</a></div><b>νωθρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νωθρή, neuter νωθρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sluggish, languid, lazy (having little motion, disinclined to move)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Απάνω στο γιατάκι σου φίδι <b>νωθρό</b> κοιμάται,
Και φέρνει βόλτες ψάχνοντας τα ρούχα σου η μαϊμού. - On your bunk bed, a sluggish snake is sleeping,
And the monkey goes about, searching through your clothes.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of a mind) dull, slow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νωθρός, from νωθής.</div><b>νωθρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νωθρότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sloth, sluggishness, indolence, idleness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νωθρότης, equivalent to νωθρός + -ότητα.</div><b>νωπογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νωπογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fresco (wall painting and the technique for its production)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From νωπός + γράφω + -ία, semantically influenced by Italian affresco.</div><b>νωπός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νωπή, neuter νωπό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fresh</div>
  <div class='ex'>προϊόντα νωπά (fresh produce)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of clothes) damp</div>
  <div class='def'>3. recent, new</div><b>νωρίς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. early (before the expected time)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. soon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νωρίς, modified from Ancient Greek ἔνωρος, from a univerbation of ἐν ὥρᾳ, literally “in to (a) period of time”.</div><b>νωρίτερα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>earlier</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα ξεκινήσει δύο ώρες <b>νωρίτερα</b>. - It will start two hours earlier.</div><b>νωτιαίος μυελός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νωτιαίοι μυελοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spinal cord</div><b>νωχελικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νωχελική, neuter νωχελικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lazy (characterised by idleness)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from νωχελ(ής)) + -ικός.</div><b>νωχελικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νωχελικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sluggishness, indolence, idleness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> νωχελικός + ότητα.</div><b>νόημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νοήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sense, meaning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gesture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νόημα, with semantic loan from German Noem, itself from the Ancient Greek term, and Wink (“sign, wave”).</div><b>νόηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cognition, noesis, intellect (the process of knowing, of acquiring knowledge and understanding through thought and through the senses; the cognitive faculty)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νόησις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>νόθος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νόθα or νόθη, neuter νόθο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. illegitimate, bastard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mongrel</div>
  <div class='def'>3. false, spurious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νόθος.</div><b>νόκτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νόκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) joke</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic نكتة (nukta).</div><b>νόμιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νόμιμη, neuter νόμιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. legal (allowed by law)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. legitimate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νόμιμος. By surface analysis, νόμος + -ιμος (-imos).</div><b>νόμισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νομίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a coin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the basic currency of a country</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nomisma</div>
  <div class='def'>4. money circulating in the form of notes and coin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νόμισμα, from νομίζω, hence &quot;to make common use of&quot;, from νόμος, from νέμω.</div><b>νόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. law, rule, code</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο λόγος της ήταν <b>νόμος</b> - Her word was law.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nome (type of musical composition in ancient Greece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek νόμος.</div><b>νόμος των εφαπτομένων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) law of tangents</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, νόμος + των pl (ton, “of”) + εφαπτομένων f pl</div><b>νόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) godmother</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from Italian nonna (“grandmother”), from Latin nonna (“grandmother”).</div><b>νόσημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νοσήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disease</div>
  <div class='ex'>σεξουαλικώς μεταδιδόμενο <b>νόσημα</b> - sexually transmitted disease</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek νόσημα. By surface analysis, νοση-) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>νόσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νόσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disease</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νόσος.</div><b>νόστιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νόστιμη, neuter νόστιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tasty (food, drink, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nice, attractive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek νόστιμος, Hellenistic sense &quot;wholesome, productive&quot;, mediaeval sense &quot;delicious, tasty&quot;. See νόστος.</div><b>νότα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) (music) note (written or printed character)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) note (sound of a note)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) nuance</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (literally) note (diplomatic missive or written communication)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin nota.</div><b>νότια-νοτιοανατολικά</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>south-southeast</div><b>νότια-νοτιοδυτικά</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>south-southwest</div><b>νότιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine νότια, neuter νότιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>south, southerly (toward the south, from the south)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νότιος. By surface analysis, νότος”) + -ιος (-ios).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural νότιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) the ostro (a southerly Mediterranean wind)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νότιος. By surface analysis, νότος”) + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>νότος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) south</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) south wind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νότος.</div><b>νύκτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νύκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://νύχτα">νύχτα</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. Cretan dialect form of <a href="bword://νύχτα">νύχτα</a></div><b>νύμφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νύμφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bride</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Greek) nymph, female sprite, female nature spirit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nymph, larva</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pupa, chrysalis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νύμφη.</div><b>νύστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νύστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tiredness, drowsiness, sleepiness</div><b>νύφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νύφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bride</div>
  <div class='def'>2. daughter-in-law (the wife of someone&#x27;s son)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sister-in-law (the wife of someone&#x27;s brother)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek νύμφη.</div><b>νύχι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural νύχια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nail, fingernail, toenail</div>
  <div class='def'>2. claw</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hoof (of horses, donkeys, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek νύχιν, inherited from Ancient Greek ὀνύχιον, diminutive of ὄνυξ, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *h₃negʰ-.</div><b>νύχτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural νύχτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>night</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek νύχτα, form of νύκτα from Ancient Greek νύξ, as in the accusative singular νύκτα, from Proto-Indo-European *nókʷts.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek ны́хта.</div><b>ξ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ξ΄">Ξ΄</a></div><b>ξάγρυπνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξάγρυπνη, neuter ξάγρυπνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>awake all night, wakeful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξάγρυπνος, alternative form of ξαγρυπνός, from ξ‑ (x‑) + ἄγρυπνος.</div><b>ξάδελφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξάδελφοι, feminine ξαδέλφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξάδερφος">ξάδερφος</a></div><b>ξάδερφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξάδερφοι, feminine ξαδέρφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cousin (male)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the form ξάδελφος, from εξάδελφος, from Koine Greek ἐξάδελφος, from Ancient Greek ἐξ- (ex-) + ἀδελφός.</div><b>ξάστερα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clearly</div><b>ξέβρασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεβράσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jetsam (cargo thrown overboard at sea)</div><b>ξέγνοιαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξέγνοιαστη, neuter ξέγνοιαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carefree, unworried, untroubled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. easy-going</div><b>ξέκωλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξέκωλη, neuter ξέκωλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of a woman) scantily clad (wearing revealing or too little clothes)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλες οι <b>ξέκωλες</b> βγήκαν απόψε στα μπαρς. - All the barely dressed girls are out tonight in the bars.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) bottomless (having no bottom part)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην βάζεις τίποτα εκεί μέσα, είναι <b>ξέκωλο</b> πιθάρι. - Don&#x27;t put anything in there, it&#x27;s a bottomless jar.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (xe-, “un-, -less”) + κώλος with adjective ending.</div><b>ξένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) foreign lands, overseas, abroad (usually in the context of emigration)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη μου θυμώνεις μάτια μου
 που φεύγω για <b>τα ξένα</b>
 πουλί θα γίνω και θα ’ρθω
 πάλι κοντά σε σένα - Do not be angry with me, my dearest,
 that I am leaving for foreign lands;
 I will turn into a bird and come
 back close to you again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξένος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ξένος">ξένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξένος.</div><b>ξένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξένες, masculine ξένος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foreigner, alien</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ξένος">ξένος</a></div><b>ξένο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) xenon</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ξένος">ξένος</a> (foreign)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ξένος">ξένος</a></div><b>ξένοιαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξένοιαστη, neuter ξένοιαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carefree, unworried, untroubled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. easy-going</div><b>ξένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξένη, neuter ξένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. foreign</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strange, unfamiliar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξένος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξένοι, feminine ξένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. foreigner, alien</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stranger</div>
  <div class='def'>3. guest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξένος.</div><b>ξέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to know of, be familiar with (a fact, a person or a language)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ήξερα</b> ότι ήταν γιατρός. - I knew he was a doctor.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia) ) know someone as, know someone to be</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εγώ, τουλάχιστον, την <b>ξέρω</b> για άνθρωπο πολύ χαλαρό. - I, at least, know her as a very calm person.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξέρω, ἐξέρω, from Ancient Greek ἐξεῦρον, aorist of ἐξευρίσκω, from ἐξ- (ex-, “ex-”) + εὑρίσκω).</div><b>ξίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vinegar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὀξίδιον, from Ancient Greek ὄξος.
Compare Mariupol Greek ксидъ.</div><b>ξίφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sword</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ξίφος.</div><b>ξαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to card, comb (stage in fibre production)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξαίνω, from Proto-Indo-European *ksen- (“to scratch; to comb, card”), from *késr̥ (“comb”), from the root *kes-. Possible cognates include Latin sentis (“thorn”), Irish cíor (“comb”), English hair.</div><b>ξαγναντεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αγναντεύω">αγναντεύω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From (ξε-) ξ- (“totally”) + αγναντεύω (“gaze from far above”).</div><b>ξαδέλφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξαδέλφες, masculine ξάδελφος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξαδέρφη">ξαδέρφη</a></div><b>ξαδέλφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξαδέλφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξαδέρφι">ξαδέρφι</a></div><b>ξαδέρφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξαδέρφες, masculine ξάδερφος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cousin (female)</div><b>ξαδέρφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξαδέρφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cousin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See εξάδελφος.</div><b>ξακουστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξακουστή, neuter ξακουστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>famous</div><b>ξαλεγράρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αλεγράρω">αλεγράρω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξ- (ξε-) (“prefix for emphasis”) + αλεγράρω (“cheer up”)</div><b>ξαλμυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to desalt, freshen (remove salt from saltbeef, bacalao, etc)</div><b>ξαμολάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αμολάω">αμολάω</a></div><b>ξανά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>again</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξανά- (from ἐξ (ex) + ἀνά), taken from verbs such as ἐξαναπληρόω or ἐξανευρίσκω.</div><b>ξαναβλέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to see each other again</div><b>ξαναβλέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to see again</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to see again, regain sight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξανα- (xana-, “again”) + βλέπω</div><b>ξαναγεννηθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular dependent of ξαναγεννιέμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://ξαναγεννώ">ξαναγεννώ</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive nonfinite form of ξαναγεννιέμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://ξαναγεννώ">ξαναγεννώ</a></div><b>ξαναγεννώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (active) to give birth again</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to be reborn, to feel reborn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξαναγεννώ, from Koine Greek ἐξαναγεννῶμαι. By surface analysis, ξανα- (xana-) + γεννώ.</div><b>ξαναγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be rewritten</div><b>ξαναγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rewrite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐξαναγράφω. Morphologically, from ξανα- (xana-, “again”) + γράφω.</div><b>ξαναδιαβάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to reread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξανα- (xana-) + διαβάζω.</div><b>ξαναλέγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξαναλέω">ξαναλέω</a></div><b>ξαναλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rehearse, say again</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξανα- (xana-) + λέω</div><b>ξαναμοιράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be divided again, redistributed</div><b>ξαναμοιράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to redistribute, share again</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξαναμοιράζει</b> τα βιβλία στους μαθητές. - She redistributes the books to the students.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξανα- (xana-, “again”) + μοιράζω.</div><b>ξαναπαίρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to take again</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to call again, to call back (to telephone someone again)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξαναπαίρνω. By surface analysis, ξανα- (xana-) + παίρνω.</div><b>ξαναπερνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξαναπερνώ">ξαναπερνώ</a></div><b>ξαναπερνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to repass, pass again</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξανα- (xana-, “again”) + περνώ</div><b>ξανασυμβεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular dependent of ξανασυμβαίνω and ξανασυμβαίνει</div>
  <div class='def'>2. active nonfinite form of ξανασυμβαίνω and ξανασυμβαίνει</div><b>ξανασυναντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to meet again, remeet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξανα- (xana-, “again”) + συναντώ</div><b>ξαναφτιάχνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to remake (to make again)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to rebuild (to build again or anew)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (familiar, transitive) to patch up (to repair relations)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξανα- (xana-) + φτιάχνω.</div><b>ξανθή σταφίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sultana (dried grape)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Form of ξανθός + σταφίδα</div><b>ξανθομάλλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξανθομάλλα or ξανθομαλλού or ξανθομαλλούσα, neuter ξανθομάλλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flaxen-haired, fair-haired</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξανθός + μαλλιά + -ης (-is, adjectival ending)</div><b>ξανθός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξανθή or ξανθιά, neuter ξανθό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blonde, blond, fair, flaxen, tawny</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ξανθά</b> μαλλιά, όπως το μέλι - blonde hair like honey</div>
  <div class='def'>2. golden</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ξανθό</b> στάχυ - golden ear (of corn)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξανθός.</div><b>ξαπλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to lie down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξαπλώνω, from earlier εξαπλώνω with aphesis, from Ancient Greek ἐξαπλόω + -ώνω, from ἐξ- (ex-) + απλόω, for which see απλώνω for more.</div><b>ξαπλώστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξαπλώστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sunlounger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the ξαπλώσ- stem of ξαπλώνω + -τρα (-tra).</div><b>ξαποσταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar) to rest, pause</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἐξαποσταίνω from ἐξ- (ex-) &gt; ξ- + αποσταίνω &lt; ancient ἀποσταίνω. For the rare spelling with epsilon ξαποστένω see αποσταίνω.</div><b>ξαραχνιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be cleaned up from cobwebs</div><b>ξαραχνιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to clean up the cobwebs</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σπίτι δεν <b>ξαραχνιάστηκε</b> εδώ και ένα χρόνο. - The house has not been cleaned of webs for a year now.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξ(ε)- (x(e)-, “de-”) + αραχνιάζω.</div><b>ξαρμυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξαλμυρίζω">ξαλμυρίζω</a></div><b>ξασπρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to whiten, clean white clothes</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to fade (lose color or brightness)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξ(ε)- (x(e)-, “prefix for opposite result”) + ασπρίζω, with both combining forms medieval: Byzantine Greek ἀσπρίζω as well as the prefix ἐξ-.</div><b>ξαφνιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to startle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to surprise, to take by surprise, to take aback</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξαφν(ίζω)) + -ιάζω.</div><b>ξαφνικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suddenly, all of a sudden</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ξαφνικός">ξαφνικός</a></div><b>ξαφνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξαφνική, neuter ξαφνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sudden, unexpected, unannounced</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξαφνικός, from έξαφνος + -ικός, from άξαφνος.</div><b>ξαφρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to purloin, pilfer, steal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to skim (clear a liquid from scum or substance floating)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξαφρίζω, inherited from Ancient Greek ἐξαφρίζω.</div><b>ξείδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξείδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vinegar</div><b>ξεβιδώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to unscrew</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξε- (xe-, “un-, de-”) + βιδώνω.</div><b>ξεβουλωτήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεβουλωτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plunger (for unblocking sinks, drains, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unblocker</div>
  <div class='def'>3. something to uncork bottles</div><b>ξεβράκωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεβράκωτη, neuter ξεβράκωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) pantsless (not wearing underwear)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι θέαμα ήταν κι αυτό, όταν εμφανίστηκε μπροστά μας <b>ξεβράκωτος</b>! - What a sight that was, when he appeared in front of us pantsless!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) dirt-poor, destitute (so poor metaphorically as to not even have money for underwear)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι πας και παντρεύεσαι αυτόν τον <b>ξεβράκωτο</b>; - What are you marrying that dirt-poor man for?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of a woman) dowryless (not rich enough to bring any dowry to a marriage)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγε και παντρεύτηκε μια <b>ξεβράκωτη</b>. - He went and married a dowryless woman.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of a woman) scantily clad (wearing revealing or too little clothes)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βγαίνεις το σαββατοκύριακο και βλέπεις παντού ένα σωρό <b>ξεβράκωτες</b> στις παραλίες. - You go out on a weekend and see a whole load of barely dressed women on the beaches.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (xe-, “un-”) + βρακί with adjective ending.</div><b>ξεβράκωτος στ&#x27; αγγούρια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, idiomatic, indeclinable) caught with one&#x27;s pants down; unprepared</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχει καμία ιδέα τι κάνει, σαν <b>ξεβράκωτος στ’ αγγούρια</b>. - He has no idea what he&#x27;s doing; he&#x27;s completely unprepared.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “pantsless among the cucumbers”.</div><b>ξεγελάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to fool, to trick (to deceive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ἐκγελάω”)). By surface analysis, ξε- (xe-) + γελάω.</div><b>ξεγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be erased, removed, struck out</div><b>ξεγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to strike out, erase, rub out (remove from being written)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν θα λάβει μέρος. Τον <b>ξέγραψα</b> από τον κατάλογο των διαγωνιζομένων. - He will not participate. I erased him (his name) from the list of contestants.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to write off, scrub from memory, no longer count as, no longer consider</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγε μετανάστης στην Αμερική και δεν ξαναγύρισε. Τον <b>ξεγράψαμε</b>. - He went as an immigrant to America and never came back. We&#x27;ve written him off.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of health) to lose every hope, give up on</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι γιατροί τον έχουν <b>ξεγράψει</b>. - Doctors have given him up for dead.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Medieval Byzantine Greek (ἐ)ξεγράφω ((e)xegráphō) from the Ancient Greek ἐκγρᾰ́φω. The sense &quot;strike out&quot; since Hellenistic times. With ξε- (xe-, “un-”) + γράφω.</div><b>ξεδοντιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) To pull teeth, to detooth or defang.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) To defang; to render harmless.</div><b>ξεθυμαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cool down, abate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to blow over (go away; settle or calm down)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to fizzle out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek (ἐ)ξεθυμαίνω ((e)xethumaínō), from Ancient Greek ἐκθυμαίνω.</div><b>ξεκάθαρα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ξεκάθαρος">ξεκάθαρος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clearly, unambiguously (in a clear manner; in a manner that is not ambiguous; leaving no doubt)</div><b>ξεκάθαρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεκάθαρη, neuter ξεκάθαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clear, unambiguous (free of ambiguity or doubt; easily understood)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clear (able to perceive clearly)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. plain (honest and without deception)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεκάθαρος. By surface analysis, a back-formation from ξεκαθαρίζω + -ος (-os).</div><b>ξεκίνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεκινήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>start, outset</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξεκινώ + -μα (-ma)</div><b>ξεκαθαρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to make (something) clear, to clear misunderstandings and questions, to clarify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to become clear</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to clear out and clean up (i.e. to keep a specific set of useful things and discard the rest)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεκαθαρίζω &lt; ἐξεκαθαρίζω, from Koine Greek ἐκκαθαρίζω (ἐκ- (ek-) &gt; ξε- (xe-) + καθαρίζω).</div><b>ξεκαλοκαιριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to summer, to spend the summer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξε- (xe-) + καλοκαίρ(ι)) + -ιάζω. Compare Byzantine Greek ξεκαλοκαιρεύω.</div><b>ξεκαρφώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to unnail</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (xe-, “un-”) + καρφώνω</div><b>ξεκινάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to start out, start off, set off, pull away</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to start, commence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξεκιν(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek (ἐ)ξεκινῶ ((e)xekinô) from the Ancient Greek ἐκκινῶ with ἐκ- &gt; (ἐ)ξε- + κινέω. Morphologically, from ξε- (xe-) + κινώ.</div><b>ξεκινώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a more formal variant of <a href="bword://ξεκινάω">ξεκινάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek (ἐ)ξεκινῶ ((e)xekinô); and see: ξεκινάω for more information.</div><b>ξεκλειδώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to unlock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξε- (xe-) + κλειδώνω.</div><b>ξεκουράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rest and see <a href="bword://ξεκουράζω">ξεκουράζω</a></div><b>ξεκουράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to rest (give rest to)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξεκούρασα</b> τα πόδια μου γιατί πονούσαν. - I rested my feet because they hurt.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to die (usually in the third person, past tense)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξεκουράστηκε</b> ο καημένος, μετά από τόσο μεγάλη ασθένεια. - He rested, poor soul, after such a long disease.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεκουράζω, with ξε- (xe-, “un-, undo”) + κουράζω.</div><b>ξεκούμπωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεκούμπωτη, neuter ξεκούμπωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unbuttoned, not buttoned</div><b>ξεκούραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rest, relaxation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξε- (xe-) + κούραση or ξεκουράζω + -ση (-si).</div><b>ξεκωλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξεκωλώνω">ξεκωλώνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek ξεκωλιάζω, equivalent to ξε- (xe-, “un-, de-”) + κώλος with verb ending.</div><b>ξεκωλιάρα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ξεκωλιάρης">ξεκωλιάρης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ξεκωλιάρης">ξεκωλιάρης</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ξεκωλιάρης">ξεκωλιάρης</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξέκωλος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξεκωλιάρες, masculine ξεκωλιάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) slut, slapper, skank, tart, whore (sexually promiscuous woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η <b>ξεκωλιάρα</b> έχει πηδήξει ολόκληρο το χωριό. - That slut has fucked the entire village.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξέκωλος.</div><b>ξεκωλιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεκωλιάρα, neuter ξεκωλιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) lucky devil, lucky dog, having devil&#x27;s luck, lucky bastard (exceptionally lucky, usually in games)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νίκησε πάλι το παιχνίδι αυτός ο <b>ξεκωλιάρης</b> ο νεαρός. - That lucky little fucker won the game again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξέκωλος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξεκωλιάρηδες, feminine ξεκωλιάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) jerk, asshole, prick (general term of abuse for a man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου ’σπασες το αμάξι, ρε <b>ξεκωλιάρη</b>! - You broke my car, you motherfucker!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξέκωλος.</div><b>ξεκωλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, transitive) to bugger, sodomize (especially violently or roughly)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive, vulgar) to exhaust, tire, wear out</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο προϊστάμενός μας μας έχει <b>ξεκωλώσει</b> στη δουλειά. - Our manager has worn us the fuck out with work.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek ξεκωλώνω, equivalent to ξε- (xe-, “un-, de-”) + κώλος + -ώνω</div><b>ξελαρυγγίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shout oneself hoarse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξε- (xe-) + λαρύγγ(ι), “throat”) + -ίζομαι.</div><b>ξελογιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be seduced, be led astray</div><b>ξελογιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to tempt, seduce, entice (to attract by arousing desire or hope)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξελόγιασε</b> την γκόμενα του φίλου του. - He seduced his friend&#x27;s girlfriend.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to lead astray, lead asunder, corrupt (influence to have bad habits or to behave improperly or illegally)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάποιοι νεαροί <b>ξελογιασαν</b> την κόρη της. - Some young guys led her daughter astray.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξελογιάζω, from ξε- (xe-, “un-, de-”) + λογιάζω, from λόγος.</div><b>ξελογιάστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξελογιάστρες, masculine ξελογιαστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(also, figuratively) seductress, temptress (alluring woman who seduces or exploits)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είσαι το καμάρι της καρδιάς μου,
Θεσσαλονίκη όμορφη, γλυκιά,
Κι αν ζω στην <b>ξελογιάστρα</b> την Αθήνα,
Για σένα τραγουδώ κάθε βραδιά. - You&#x27;re the pride of my heart,
Beautiful, sweet Thessaloniki,
And even if I live in Athens, that seductress
For you I sing each night.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξελογιάζω.</div><b>ξελογιαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξελογιαστές, feminine ξελογιάστρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seducer, tempter (alluring man who seduces or exploits)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξελογιάζω.</div><b>ξελύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to untie, unbind, undo, unbuckle</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξελύνω</b> τα μαλλιά μου. - I untie my hair.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Morphologically ξε- (xe-, “un-”) + λύνω. From Ancient Greek ἐξέλῡσᾰ, aorist of ancient verb ἐκλῡ́ω.</div><b>ξεμέθυστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεμέθυστη, neuter ξεμέθυστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sobered up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the ξεμεθυσ- stem of ξεμεθώ + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ξεμένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be left over</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to run out</div><b>ξεμανίκωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεμανίκωτη, neuter ξεμανίκωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sleeveless</div><b>ξεματιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to remove the evil eye from, to unjinx</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξε- (xe-) + ματιάζω.</div><b>ξεμεθάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξεμεθώ">ξεμεθώ</a></div><b>ξεμεθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sober up, sober</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (xe-, “un-”) + μεθώ</div><b>ξεμουδιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to revive from numbness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to recover from numbness</div><b>ξεμπέρδεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεμπερδέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disentanglement, untangling</div>
  <div class='ex'>Καλά <b>ξεμπερδέματα</b>! - Good luck with sorting that out!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξεμπερδεύω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ξεμπερδεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be disentangled</div><b>ξεμπερδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to untangle (hair, string, difficult problem)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to finish up, settle an affair</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξεμπέρδεψα</b> με τις εξετάσεις. Θα πάω διακοπές. - I finished the exams. I&#x27;ll go on vacation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεμπερδεύω, ξεμπερδένω. Prefix ξε- (xe-, “un-”) + μπερδεύω.</div><b>ξεμπλέκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to tease (separate the fibers of clothing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to get disinvolved, get rid of a chore or situation</div><b>ξενέρωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξενέρωτη, neuter ξενέρωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) sober (not drunk)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of people) boring, dull, unfun, uncool (completely uninteresting and no fun at all)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αδελφός της ο <b>ξενέρωτος</b> θα έρθει· μόνο ελπίζω να μην καταστρέψει το βράδυ. - Her boring brother will be coming; I only hope he doesn&#x27;t ruin the night.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (xe-, “un-”) + νερό with adjective ending.</div><b>ξενία</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξενίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hospitality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξενία.</div><b>ξεναγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ξεναγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guide, tour guide</div><b>ξεναγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to show around, give a tour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ξεναγῶ.</div><b>ξενερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to sober up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, transitive) to disgust</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to let down, disillusion, frustrate</div><b>ξενικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural ξενικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) foreignness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξενικός + -ότητα.</div><b>ξενιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξενιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>host (an animal, plant or cell harbouring another organism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ξενιστής, equivalent to ξενίζω + -ιστής.</div><b>ξενιτειά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξενιτειές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymological spelling of <a href="bword://ξενιτιά">ξενιτιά</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymological spelling of ξενιτιά, according to the ancient ξενιτεία.</div><b>ξενιτειές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymological spelling of ξενιτιές, nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ξενιτειά">ξενιτειά</a></div><b>ξενιτειών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymological spelling of ξενιτιών, genitive plural of <a href="bword://ξενιτειά">ξενιτειά</a></div><b>ξενιτιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξενιτιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foreign lands, foreign parts, abroad, overseas where one is obliged to settle by necessity</div>
  <div class='ex'>Της <b>Ξενιτιάς</b> (Φεγγάρι μάγια μου ’κανες) - Of Living Abroad (Moon, you bewitched me)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξενιτιά (also with suffixes -ειά, -εία), from Ancient Greek ξενιτεία with spelling simplification. The ancient spelling with ⟨ει⟩ is supported by Babiniotis..</div><b>ξενοδοχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξενοδοχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hotel (establishment that provides accommodation and other services for paying guests)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ξενοδοχείο</b> μας ήταν ακριβώς δίπλα στη θάλασσα. - Our hotel was right beside the sea.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ξενοδοχεῖον, equivalent to ξενοδόχος + -είο.</div><b>ξενοδοχειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξενοδοχειακή, neuter ξενοδοχειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hotel-, hotel</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ξενοδοχειακές</b> εγκαταστάσεις - hotel facilities</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξενοδοχεῖον. First attested 1889.</div><b>ξενοδοχουπάλληλος</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://ξενοδοχοϋπάλληλος">ξενοδοχοϋπάλληλος</a></div><b>ξενοδοχοϋπάλληλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ξενοδοχοϋπάλληλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hotel worker, hotel employee (person who works in a hotel)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φέτος προσλάβαμε δέκα νέους <b>ξενοδοχοϋπαλλήλους</b>. - This year, we hired ten new hotel employees.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξενοδοχ(είο), “hotel”) + -ο- (infix) + υπάλληλος.</div><b>ξενοδόχα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξενοδόχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) hotelier (person who runs or owns a hotel)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>ξενοδόχα</b> χαιρετά τους επισκέπτες. - The hotelier greets the guests.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξενοδόχος + -α (-a).</div><b>ξενοδόχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ξενοδόχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hotelier (person who runs or owns a hotel)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>ξενοδόχος</b> αποφάσισε να χτίσει καινούργια πισίνα. - The hotelier decided to build a new pool.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξενοδόχος, from ξένος + δέχομαι.</div><b>ξενολατρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξενολατρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) worship of foreign civilisations or lifestyle</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάποιος πρέπει να γράψει κάποτε το παράξενο ρομάντζο της ελληνικής <b>ξενολατρίας</b> και ξενοφοβίας - Somebody has to write at some point the strange romance of Greek xenolatry and xenophobia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξενο- (xeno-, “xeno-, foreign”) + -λατρία, from ξένος m and λατρεύω.</div><b>ξενομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξενομανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>xenomania</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Equivalent to ξένος + μανία.</div><b>ξενοφιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) xenophilia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξένος + -φιλία.</div><b>ξενοφοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) xenophobia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English xenophobia, from Ancient Greek ξένος + New Latin phobia, from Ancient Greek φόβος.</div><b>ξεντύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to undress (to remove the clothing off)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξε- (xe-) + ντύνω.</div><b>ξενυχτάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stay up all night, stay awake, keep someone up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to keep vigil</div><b>ξενυχτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to maintain a vigil (by the sick or the dead)</div><b>ξενυχτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξενυχτάω">ξενυχτάω</a></div><b>ξενόποδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξενόποδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clawed frog (Xenopus)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξενόποδες</b> (Xenopus laevis και Xenopus tropicalis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Xenopus, from Ancient Greek ξένος + Ancient Greek πούς.</div><b>ξενύχτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξενύχτηδες, feminine ξενύχτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>night owl, night person</div><b>ξενύχτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξενύχτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all-nighter (something that lasts throughout the whole night: study session, party, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from ξενυχτώ, with -ι (-i) suffix.</div><b>ξενώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξενώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. guest room (accommodation set aside for guests)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hostel, guesthouse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. guesthouse (in monastery)</div><b>ξενώνας νεότητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξενώνες νεότητας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>youth hostel</div><b>ξενώνες νεότητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ξενώνας">ξενώνας</a> νεότητας</div><b>ξεπίτηδες</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://επίτηδες">επίτηδες</a></div><b>ξεπερνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to exceed, to surpass (to go beyond: amount, expectations, limit, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to overcome, to surmount</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to exceed, to surpass, to overtake (to be better than)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (xe-) + περνάω</div><b>ξεπερνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξεπερνάω">ξεπερνάω</a></div><b>ξεπληρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pay back, to repay (debt)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek. By surface analysis, ξε- (xe-) + πληρώνω.</div><b>ξεποδαριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar, transitive) to exhaust / tire out with walking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξε- (xe-) + ποδάρ(ι)) + -ιάζω.</div><b>ξεπούλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεπούλημα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clearance sale</div>
  <div class='def'>2. selling out; betrayal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξεπουλάω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ξεπροβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to appear gradually, pop up, spring into existence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (“little by little”) + προβάλλω (“appear; present”).</div><b>ξεραίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be dry, desiccate, dry up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be fast asleep, be unconscious</div><b>ξεραίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to dry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, idiomatic) to hurt very much</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με πάτησε στο πόδι και με <b>ξέρανε</b>. - S/he stepped on my toe and s/he really hurt me.</div><b>ξερασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ξερασμένη, neuter ξερασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vomited</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ξερνιέμαι, passive voice of ξερνάω, ξερνώ (“vomit”).</div><b>ξεριζώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to uproot, deracinate, extirpate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to make homeless, eradicate (of a people)</div><b>ξερνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, familiar) to vomit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐξεράω.</div><b>ξερνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξερνάω">ξερνάω</a></div><b>ξερογλείφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to lick one&#x27;s lips when observing a desired food or yearning for something</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξερο- (xero-, “dry, dehydrated”) + γλείφομαι</div><b>ξεροκέφαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεροκέφαλη, neuter ξεροκέφαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stubborn, obstinate, pigheaded, mulish, bloody-minded</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξερο- (xero-, “dry”) + κεφάλ(ι), “head”) + -ος (-os). Compare Koine Greek ξηροκέφαλος.</div><b>ξεροκεφαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stubbornness, obstinacy, pigheadedness, mulishness, bloody-mindedness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξεροκέφαλ(ος)) + -ιά.</div><b>ξερολιθιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξερολιθιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dry-stone wall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ξηρόλιθος, from ξηρός + λίθος.</div><b>ξεροψημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεροψημένη, neuter ξεροψημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crisp, crusty (of bread, etc.)</div><b>ξερόλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξερόλες, feminine ξερόλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) know-it-all, know-all, clever dick, smart ass, smarty pants (someone who pretends to know everything or has an opinion to offer on all subjects)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φυσικά, ο άνδρας της, ο <b>ξερόλας</b>, μας είπε πώς να κάνουμε τη δουλειά σωστά. - Of course, her husband, the know-it-all, told us how to do the job properly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξέρω + όλα + -ας (-as, “-er”).</div><b>ξερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξερή, neuter ξερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dry, dried, arid</div>
  <div class='ex'>το κλίμα είναι πολύ <b>ξερό</b> - the climate is very dry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dry, not sweet</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ξερό</b> κρασί - dry wine</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dry, laconic, perfunctory</div>
  <div class='ex'>μου είπε ένα <b>ξερό</b> ‹καλημέρα› - I said a dry ‘good morning’</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) incapacitated, unconscious</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) dead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξερός, from Ancient Greek ξηρός, with shift -ηρ- &gt; -ερ- (-er-).</div><b>ξερόψωμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξερόψωμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dry, stale bread</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bread not accompanied by any other food, poor lunch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξερ(ός) (“dry”) + -ό- (“interfix”) + -ψωμο (“bread”) from ψωμί.</div><b>ξεσκάτισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεσκατίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) wiping, cleaning (of the faeces from the bottom of a baby, an ill person or an infirm person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ξεσκάτισμα</b> και το πλύσιμο για τους καρκινοπαθείς γίνεται κάθε μέρα στις έξι το απόγευμα. - The cleaning of bottoms and bathing of the cancer patients happens every day at six in the evening.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) cleaning, cleaning up (a dirty area or place)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ξεσκάτισμα</b> του μπάνιου δεν ήταν εύκολο. - The cleaning up of the bathroom wasn&#x27;t easy at all.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξεσκατώνω + -ισμα (-isma).</div><b>ξεσκάτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεσκατώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) wiping, cleaning (of the faeces from the bottom of a baby, an ill person or an infirm person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ξεσκάτωμα</b> του μωρού είναι το πιο αηδιαστικό πράγμα για τους νέους γονείς. - The cleaning of the baby&#x27;s bottom is the most disgusting thing for new parents.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) cleaning up, fixing (of someone else&#x27;s problem or metaphorical mess)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το να τα κανείς όλα θάλασσα εύκολο ήταν· το <b>ξεσκάτωμα</b> μετά όμως, καθόλου. - Messing everything up was easy; fixing it up after, not at all.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξεσκατώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ξεσκέπαστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεσκέπαστη, neuter ξεσκέπαστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not covered, uncovered</div><b>ξεσκίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to shred, lacerate, tear apart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to copulate vigorously with, fuck</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to slave, work for low pay</div><b>ξεσκατίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, transitive, vulgar) to wipe, clean (the faeces from the bottom of a baby, an ill person or an infirm person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι δουλειά κι αυτή, να <b>ξεσκατίζει</b> τους γέρους. - What a job that is, wiping old people&#x27;s asses.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, transitive) to clean, clean up (a dirty area or place)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα μια βρωμιά που μ’ άφησαν να <b>ξεσκατίσω</b>! - Look at the fucking mess they left for me to clean up!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek ξεσκατίζω, equivalent to ξε- (xe-, “un-, de-”) + σκατό with verb ending.</div><b>ξεσκατώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, transitive, vulgar) to wipe, clean (the faeces from the bottom of a baby, patient, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ως νοσοκόμα, κάθε μέρα πρέπει να <b>ξεσκατώνω</b> τους αρρώστους. - As a nurse, every day I have to wipe the patients&#x27; asses.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, transitive) to clean up, fix (someone else&#x27;s problem or metaphorical mess)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα έκανες όλα σκατά και αφίσες εμένα να <b>ξεσκατώσω</b> μετά. - You fucked it all up and left me to clean up after you.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek ξεσκατώνω, equivalent to ξε- (xe-, “un-, de-”) + σκατό + -ώνω</div><b>ξεσκεπάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to uncover, take the lid off, expose</div><b>ξεσκονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dust, remove dust</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to brush up, restudy, dust off, bone up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να <b>ξεσκονίσω</b> λίγο τα αγγλικά μου. - I have to brush up on my English a bit.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to suck up to, flatter, brownnose</div><b>ξεσπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to break out, to erupt (to begin suddenly and forcefully)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξέσπασε</b> πόλεμος. - War broke out.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to break into, to burst into, to burst out [with <a href="bword://σε">σε</a> (se, + accusative)] (to display sudden powerful emotion)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ξεσπάω σε</b> κλάματα - to break into tears, to burst into tears</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to lash out (at), to bite one&#x27;s head off, to go off on</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάντα <b>ξεσπά</b> στα παιδιά, ακόμα κι αν δεν φταίνε. - She always bites the kids&#x27; heads off, even if it&#x27;s not their fault.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to erupt (to release an outburst of anger)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην <b>ξεσπάς</b> τα νεύρα σου πάνω μου! - Don&#x27;t take your frustrations out on me!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεσπώ, from Ancient Greek ἐκσπῶ. By surface analysis, ξε- (xe-) + σπάω.</div><b>ξεσπίτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεσπιτώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>throwing out, eviction (the act of being expelled from or deprived of a house)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έξι μήνες χωρίς ενοίκιο πριν το <b>ξεσπίτωμά</b> τους. - Six months without rent before their eviction.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξεσπιτώνω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>ξεσπιτώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be thrown out, be evicted</div><b>ξεσπιτώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, transitive) to throw out, evict (force to leave a home)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν πλήρωσαν το ενοίκιο και ο ιδιοκτήτης τους <b>ξεσπίτωσε</b>. - They didn&#x27;t pay the rent and so the owner threw them out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (xe-) + σπιτώνω.</div><b>ξεσχίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξεσκίζω">ξεσκίζω</a></div><b>ξεφαντώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to have a blast, to live it up, to revel (to enjoy oneself at a festive gathering)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεφαντώνω, from εξεφαντώνω, from Koine Greek *ἐκφαντῶ, from ἔκφαντος.</div><b>ξεφεύγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to flee, to get away, to escape</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to get away (to slip from one&#x27;s control)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stray, to veer (to wander, as from a direct course)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to escape, to elude (to evade the observation or notice of; to not be seen or remembered by)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεφεύγω, from εξεφεύγω, from Ancient Greek ἐκφεύγω. By surface analysis, ξε- (xe-) + φεύγω.</div><b>ξεφλουδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to peel, hull, husk, shell, shuck</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to debark</div><b>ξεφορτώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to unload, to discharge (to remove the load from)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to unburden (:person or animal)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to get rid of, to rid oneself of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεφορτώνω, from εξεφορτώνω, from εκφορτ(ώ)) + -ώνω. By surface analysis, ξε- (xe-) + φορτώνω.</div><b>ξεφτέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεφτέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Eurasian sparrowhawk (Accipiter nisus)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, informal) bright spark, quickwitted, intelligent</div>
  <div class='ex'>see: έξυπνος (éxypnos, “intelligent”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεφτέριν &lt; ξυπτέριν &lt; ἐξυπτέριον, from Koine Greek ὀξυπτέριον n with assimilation of pronunciation [pt] &gt; [ft] and with the influence of ξε- (xe-). a diminutive of Ancient Greek ὀξύπτερος &lt; ὀξύς + πτερ(όν) n, “wing”) + -ος (-os). Compare to ὠκύπτερος and the etymologies of ὀξύς and ὠκύς.
Cognate with Pontic Greek ξυφτέριν and akin to τσίφτης and Albanian qift. Probably, not related to the Latin accipiter from acus.</div><b>ξεφτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, literally) to fray</div><b>ξεφυτρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sprout, burgeon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to shoot up, spring into existence</div><b>ξεφωνητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξεφωνητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scream (loud exclamation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. call (cry or shout)</div><b>ξεχέζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to rail, chide, tell off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξε- (xe-, “un-, de-”) + χέζω</div><b>ξεχασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ξεχασμένη, neuter ξεχασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forgotten</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ξεχνιέμαι, passive voice of ξεχνάω, ξεχνώ (“forget”).</div><b>ξεχειλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to overflow, bubble over, run over</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to fill to the brim</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to erupt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek (ἐ)ξεχειλίζω ((e)xekheilízō). Equivalent to ξέχειλος (literally ξε- (xe-, “over-”) the χείλη) + -ίζω.</div><b>ξεχειμωνιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to winter, to spend the winter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεχειμωνιάζω. By surface analysis, ξε- (xe-) + χειμών(ας)) + -ιάζω.</div><b>ξεχνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I forget</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξεχν-ώ + -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξεχνῶ and ξεχάνω, itself from ξε- (xe-, “un-, de-, out-”) + χάνω.</div><b>ξεχνιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be forgotten</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to become forgetful, to drift while thinking or doing something</div>
  <div class='ex'>Διάβαζα ένα βιβλίο και <b>ξεχάστηκα</b>! Έχασα το ραντεβού. - I was reading a book and forgot [the time]! I missed my rendezvous.</div><b>ξεχνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξεχνάω">ξεχνάω</a> to forget</div><b>ξεχωρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to divide, separate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to distinguish, tell the difference</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to stand out, stick out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Byzantine Greek infinitive ἐξεχώρισα, from Koine Greek ἐκχωρίζω, from ἐκ- (ek-) + χωρίζω.</div><b>ξεχωριστά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ξεχωριστός">ξεχωριστός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>separately</div><b>ξεχωριστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξεχωριστή, neuter ξεχωριστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. separate, distinct</div>
  <div class='def'>2. exceptional, distinctive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξεχωρίζω + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ξεψυχάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (euphemistic) to pass away, pass on, expire, draw one&#x27;s last breath, bite the dust (to die)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γνωστός ηθοποιός <b>ξεψύχησε</b> σήμερα το πρωί. - The well-known actor passed away this morning.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to lose one&#x27;s power, spark or liveliness (due to work, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξεψύχησα</b> από την δουλειά σήμερα. - Work tired me out today.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to torture someone, to give them hell (put them through stress etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το παιδί με <b>ξεψυχάει</b>. - That child will be the death of me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern ξεψυχ(ώ) (literally, “the soul comes out of me”) + -άω. From the mediaeval Byzantine Greek ξεψυχῶ &lt; ἐξεψυχῶ, from Koine Greek ἐκψύχω. The ἐκ- (ek-) &gt; ξε- (xe-). See ψυχή.</div><b>ξεψυχισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ξεψυχισμένη, neuter ξεψυχισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. faint, toneless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. faint, toneless; hardly heard (of voice)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of ξεψυχάω, ξεψυχώ, a verb without passive voice forms. Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek (ἐ)ξεψυχισμένος ((e)xepsukhisménos).</div><b>ξεψυχώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξεψυχάω">ξεψυχάω</a></div><b>ξεύρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξέρω">ξέρω</a></div><b>ξημέρωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξημερώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dawn, sunrise, daybreak</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξημερώνω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ξημερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (perfective, usually) to stay up until dawn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with third person subject) to dawn (to begin to brighten with daylight)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξημερώνω, from earlier ἐξημερώνω, from ἐξ- (ex-) + ἡμέρα + -ώνω.</div><b>ξηρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>land (not sea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ξηρά, by ellipsis of the noun γῆ, &quot;dry land&quot;, substantivised feminine of the adjective ξηρός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ξηρός">ξηρός</a>, formal or dated variant of of ξηρή</div>
  <div class='ex'>used in set phrases like ξηρά τροφή f (xirá trofí)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ξηρός">ξηρός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form, learnedly, from Ancient Greek ξηρά, feminine adjective of ξηρός.</div><b>ξηρασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξηρασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drought</div><b>ξηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξηρή or ξηρά, neuter ξηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) dry, dried, arid</div>
  <div class='ex'>το κλίμα είναι πολύ <b>ξηρό</b> - the climate is very dry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) dry, not sweet</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ξηρός</b> οίνος - dry wine</div><b>ξηρός καρπός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξηροί καρποί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dried nuts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξηρός + καρπός.</div><b>ξηρός πάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) dry ice, cardice (solid carbon dioxide)</div><b>ξι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) xi, the name for the 14th letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξεῖ.</div><b>ξινάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξινάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pickaxe, pick, pickax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀξίνη, from Latin ascia.</div><b>ξινίλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξινίλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sour taste, sourness, acidity</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχει λίγο <b>ξινίλα</b>. Μήπως έχει χαλάσει το γάλα; - There&#x27;s a sour taste. Has the milk gone off perhaps?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) acid reflux, heartburn (sour taste from indigestion)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>ξινίλα</b> επειδή έφαγα πριν κοιμηθώ. - I have heartburn because I ate before I slept.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξινός + -ίλα.</div><b>ξινό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ξινός">ξινός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ξινός">ξινός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) citric acid</div><b>ξινόγαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whey</div><b>ξινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξινή, neuter ξινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sour, tart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. acid, bitter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξινός, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ὄξινος, from ὄξος.</div><b>ξιφίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξιφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swordfish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξῐφῐ́ᾱς.</div><b>ξιφίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξιφίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dagger</div><b>ξιφιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξιφιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξιφίας">ξιφίας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξιφιός, Late Hellenistic form ξίφιος, from the Ancient Greek ξιφίας. Doublet of ξιφίας.</div><b>ξιφολόγχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξιφολόγχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bayonet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξίφος + λόγχη</div><b>ξιφομάχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξιφομάχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. swordsman</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fencer</div><b>ξιφομαχώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fight with swords</div><b>ξιφουλκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to draw a sword (remove from scabbard)</div><b>ξιφοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξιφοφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. swordsman, sword carrier</div>
  <div class='def'>2. swordtail (Xiphophorus hellerii)</div><b>ξοδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to spend, expend (money)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε βράδυ <b>ξοδεύει</b> μια περιουσία. - He spends a fortune every night.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to spend (time)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξόδεψα</b> όλη τη ζωή μου για σένα. - I spent all my life for you.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to spend, use up, consume (anything consumable)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξοδέψαμε</b> όλο το ζεστό νερό. - We&#x27;ve used up all the hot water.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐξοδεύω, from Koine Greek ἐξοδεύω, ἔξοδος.</div><b>ξοδιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξοδεύω">ξοδεύω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξοδιάζω, ἐξοδιάζω, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ἐξοδιάζω. Also see ξοδεύω.</div><b>ξομολογητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξομολογητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εξομολογητής">εξομολογητής</a></div><b>ξουράφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξουράφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξυράφι">ξυράφι</a></div><b>ξουτ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) shoo (used to get rid of animals, insects and undesirable people)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξουτ</b> από ’δω, μύγες. - Shoo, flies.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeia.</div><b>ξούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) bullshit, bushwah</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) shave, shaving (the action or outcome of having a shave)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξούρας.</div><b>ξυλάγγουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξυλάγγουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Armenian cucumber</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, idiomatic) A joyless person who can&#x27;t stand up and speak in front of others</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξύλο + αγγούρι</div><b>ξυλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξυλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stick</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spill</div>
  <div class='def'>3. chopstick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξύλο + -άκι.</div><b>ξυλαγγουριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξυλαγγουριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Armenian cucumber plant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξύλο + αγγουριά</div><b>ξυλεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) timber, lumber</div><b>ξυλοκάρφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξυλοκάρφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξυλόκαρφο">ξυλόκαρφο</a></div><b>ξυλοπόδαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) wooden leg</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stilt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξυλο- (xylo-) + -πόδαρο</div><b>ξυλουργική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) carpentry, woodwork</div><b>ξυλουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ξυλουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carpenter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξυλουργός, from ξύλον + -ουργός; equivalent to ξύλο + -ουργός.</div><b>ξυλόκαρφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξυλόκαρφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wooden dowel, peg, treenail</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξύλο + καρφί</div><b>ξυλόφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξυλόφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>xylophone</div><b>ξυπνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to awake, wake up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αύριο θα <b>ξυπνήσω</b> νωρίς. - Tomorrow I shall wake up early.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to awaken, wake</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>ξυπνήσεις</b> το μωρό με τις φωνές σου. - You&#x27;ll wake the baby with your talking.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to become aware, wake up, awaken</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξύπνα</b> επιτέλους, η ζωή αλλάζει! - Wake up already; life changes!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek ξυπνῶ, from Koine Greek ἐξυπνῶ, from Ancient Greek ἐξ (ex, “out”) and ὕπνος. Also see ἔξυπνος.</div><b>ξυπνητήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alarm clock</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ξυπνητήρι</b> μου χρειάζεται νέες μπαταρίες. - My alarm clock needs new batteries.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξύπνημα + -τήρι.</div><b>ξυπνητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξυπνητή, neuter ξυπνητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. awake, awakening</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conscious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξυπνώ + -τός.</div><b>ξυπνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ξυπνάω">ξυπνάω</a></div><b>ξυπόλητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://ξυπόλυτος">ξυπόλυτος</a></div><b>ξυπόλυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξυπόλυτη, neuter ξυπόλυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barefoot, barefooted, shoeless</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη μπαίνεις στο υπόγειο <b>ξυπόλυτος</b>, έχει σπασμένο γυαλί! - Don&#x27;t go into the basement barefoot, there&#x27;s broken glass!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) dirt-poor, destitute</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι πας και παντρεύεσαι αυτόν τον <b>ξυπόλυτο</b>; - What are you marrying that dirt-poor man for?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐξυπόλυτος, from Koine Greek ἐξυπολύω, from ἐξ (ex, “out of”) + ὑπό + λύω.</div><b>ξυπόλυτος στ&#x27; αγκάθια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic, indeclinable) unprepared</div>
  <div class='ex'>Που πας <b>ξυπόλυτος στ’ αγκάθια</b>; Πρέπει να προετοιμαστείς. - Where do you think you&#x27;re going unprepared? You must prepare yourself.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “barefoot into the thorns”.</div><b>ξυράφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξυράφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>razor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξυράφιον, from a diminutive derivative of Ancient Greek ξυρόν.</div><b>ξυρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shave yourself</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξυρίστηκε</b> και ντύθηκε. - He shaved and dressed.</div><b>ξυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shave</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ξυρίσε</b> τα πόδια της. - She shaved her legs.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek ξυρίζω, from Ancient Greek ξυρέω, ξυρῶ + -ίζω.</div><b>ξωτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξωτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elf, sprite, imp, pixie, gnome (any from a race of mythical, supernatural beings resembling but seen as distinct from human beings)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιος μου ντύθηκε <b>ξωτικό</b> για τις Απόκριες. - My son dressed as an elf for Halloween.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐξωτικόν, with aphesis. Doublet of εξωτικός.</div><b>ξόανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξόανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wooden statue, effigy of a god</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) stupid or insensitive person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξόανον.</div><b>ξόρκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξόρκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incantation</div><b>ξύδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξύδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>More common spelling of <a href="bword://ξίδι">ξίδι</a>, vinegar</div><b>ξύλα κοπής</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ξύλο">ξύλο</a> κοπής</div><b>ξύλινα πνευστά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>woodwind (orchestral section)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ξύλινα n pl + πνευστά pl</div><b>ξύλινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξύλινη, neuter ξύλινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wooden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξύλινος.</div><b>ξύλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξύλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wood (material forming the structure of trees)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stick</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ξύλο</b> κανέλας - stick of cinnamon</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (slang) beating, thrashing (physical assault)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάποιος του έδωσε <b>ξύλο</b>. - Someone beat him up.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ξύλον.
Compare Mariupol Greek кси́лу.</div><b>ξύλο κοπής</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ξύλα κοπής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chopping board, cutting board</div><b>ξύνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be scratched</div><b>ξύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to scratch, to scrape</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ξύνω, from Ancient Greek ξύω with addition of a nasal infix based on the common aorist stem ξυσ-.</div><b>ξύπνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>awakening</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξυπνώ + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ξύπνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ξύπνια, neuter ξύπνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. awake, conscious, wide-awake</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alert, smart</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ξυπνός, from Koine Greek ἔξυπνος, from Ancient Greek ἐξ (ex) + ὕπνος. Doublet of έξυπνος.</div><b>ξύρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shave, shaving (action or result of shaving)</div><b>ξύστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ξύστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pencil sharpener</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ξύνω, past tense stem ξυσ- + -τρα (-tra). Compare to ξυστρί, and Ancient Greek ξύστρα.</div><b>ο</b>
  <div><i>article, masculine singular</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the definite masculine singular article. the</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὁ (ho).</div><b>ο κώλος σου να φέξει</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(humorous, mildly, vulgar) a meaningless phrase said after the number <a href="bword://έξι">έξι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “may your ass shine”.</div><b>ο λύκος κι αν εγέρασε κι άσπρισε το μαλλί του, μήτε τη γνώμη άλλαξε, μήτε την κεφαλή του</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) the wolf may lose his teeth but never his nature; the fox may grow grey but never good; a leopard cannot change its spots (one&#x27;s nature cannot change no matter how much time passes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * see: λύκος, κι αν, και αν (ki an, kai an, “even if”), εγέρασε, doler form of γέρασε past tense of γερνάω, άσπρισε past tense of ασπρίζω, μήτε, γνώμη f, άλλαξε past tense of αλλάζω, κεφαλή f: demotic form of κεφάλι n</div><b>ο μη γένοιτο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) God forbid, touch wood, perish the thought (don&#x27;t let it be)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εάν <b>ο μη γένοιτο</b> χάσεις το πορτοφόλι σου, τι θα κάνεις; - If, God forbid, you lose your wallet, what will you do?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek set phrase consisting of ὅ + μή + γένοιτο.</div><b>ο΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ο΄">Ο΄</a></div><b>οίδημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>edema</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἴδημα, from οἰδέω.</div><b>οίκε ευγηρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vocative singular of <a href="bword://οίκος">οίκος</a> ευγηρίας</div><b>οίκο ευγηρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative singular of <a href="bword://οίκος">οίκος</a> ευγηρίας</div><b>οίκοι ανοχής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://οίκος">οίκος</a> ανοχής</div><b>οίκοι ευγηρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of <a href="bword://οίκος">οίκος</a> ευγηρίας</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vocative plural of <a href="bword://οίκος">οίκος</a> ευγηρίας</div><b>οίκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οίκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic, formal) settlement, tent, temple, dwelling, home (structure built or serving as an abode of human beings)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λευκός <b>Οίκος</b> - White House</div>
  <div class='def'>2. family (a dynasty with its ancestors and descendants, especially a royal or noble one)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Οίκος</b> των Κομνηνών - House of Komnenos</div>
  <div class='def'>3. house, firm (place of business)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εκδοτικός <b>οίκος</b> - publishing house</div>
  <div class='def'>4. strophe, stanza (of the kontakion)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. manor (lord&#x27;s residence in feudal times)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. house (one astrological division)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἶκος.</div><b>οίκος ανοχής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οίκοι ανοχής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, euphemistic) brothel, house of ill repute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French maison de tolérance. Compare also to Italian term casa di tolleranza.</div><b>οίκος ευγηρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οίκοι ευγηρίας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(euphemistic, formal) nursing home, old people&#x27;s home (hospital and care facility for the elderly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς ζει εδώ και ενάμιση χρόνο στον <b>οίκο ευγηρίας</b>. - Grandad has been living in a nursing home for the last year and a half.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;house of happy old age&quot;.</div><b>οίκου ευγηρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of <a href="bword://οίκος">οίκος</a> ευγηρίας</div><b>οίκους ευγηρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative plural of <a href="bword://οίκος">οίκος</a> ευγηρίας</div><b>οίκτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οίκτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pity, mercy, compassion</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προκαλεί φθόνο παρά <b>οίκτο</b>. - He provokes envy rather than pity.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derived from Ancient Greek οἶκτος.</div><b>οίκων ευγηρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of <a href="bword://οίκος">οίκος</a> ευγηρίας</div><b>οίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, literary) wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek οἶνος.</div><b>οβάλ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ovoid, oval</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French ovale.</div><b>οβίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οβίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shell (projectile fired from a siege mortar or a cannon)</div><b>οβελίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οβελίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skewer</div><b>οβελίσκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οβελίσκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. obelisk</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dagger (text character: † )</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὀβελίσκος. See οβελός.</div><b>οβελιστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rotisserie (shop or restaurant)</div><b>ογδοήκοντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://ογδόντα">ογδόντα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ὀγδοήκοντα.</div><b>ογδοηκοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ογδοηκοστή, neuter ογδοηκοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eightieth (ordinal form of the number eighty)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀγδοηκοστός.</div><b>ογδόντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eighty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀγδοήκοντα, from Proto-Indo-European *oktōḱomt, from earlier *oḱto(w)-dḱomt (“eight-ten”).</div><b>ογκανέσσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) oganesson</div><b>ογκολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) oncology</div><b>ογκομέτρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ογκομετρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>titration</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ογκομέτρηση</b> εξουδετέρωσης - acid-base titration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From όγκος + μέτρηση, from μετράω, a form of ογκομετρία, calque of French volumétrie. See the ancient ὄγκος and μέτρον for further etymology.</div><b>ογκομετρική φιάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ογκομετρικές φιάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>volumetric flask</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ογκομετρικός + φιάλη.</div><b>ογκώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ογκώδης, neuter ογκώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bulky</div>
  <div class='def'>2. large in number</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀγκώδης. By surface analysis, όγκ(ος)) + -ώδης.</div><b>οδήγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδηγήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>driving</div><b>οδαλίσκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδαλίσκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>odalisque</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish اوطهلق, from اوده (oda, “room”).</div><b>οδηγάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://οδηγώ">οδηγώ</a></div><b>οδηγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδηγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) instruction, command, directive</div><b>οδηγημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine οδηγημένη, neuter οδηγημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>driven, led</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of οδηγούμαι, passive voice of οδηγάω / οδηγώ.</div><b>οδηγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural οδηγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. driver (of a vehicle)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. guide, leader (person who shows the way)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. guide (book, etc with instructions)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. driver</div>
  <div class='def'>5. driver, guide (mechanism used to force a specified move or motion)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. Girl Guide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὁδηγός, from Ancient Greek ὁδός and ἡγέομαι from Proto-Indo-European *séh₂gyeti (“to seek out, look for, track”).</div><b>οδηγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to drive (direct a vehicle)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to drive, guide, lead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁδηγῶ, contracted form of ὁδηγέω; see Greek οδηγός for more.</div><b>οδικοί χάρτες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://οδικός">οδικός</a> <a href="bword://χάρτης">χάρτης</a></div><b>οδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οδική, neuter οδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>road, street (both attributive) (of or on the road or street)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>οδικός</b> χάρτης - road map, street map</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from οδ(ός)) + -ικός, a calque of French routier.</div><b>οδικός χάρτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οδικοί χάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>road map, roadmap</div><b>οδοκαθαριστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οδοκαθαριστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>streetcleaner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οδός + καθαριστής.</div><b>οδομετρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural οδομετρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) odometry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) hodometry (obsolete)</div><b>οδοντίατρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural οδοντίατροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dentist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From οδοντ- (odont-, “tooth”) + ιατρός, a calque of German Zahnarzt.</div><b>οδοντίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dentin</div><b>οδοντιατρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οδοντιατρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dental clinic, dental practice, dental surgery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from οδοντ- (odont-) + ιατρείο.</div><b>οδοντιατρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οδοντιατρική, neuter οδοντιατρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) dental (of or concerning dentistry)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>οδοντιατρική</b> κλινική - dental clinic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From οδοντίατρος + -ικός.</div><b>οδοντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οδοντική, neuter οδοντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dental, concerning the teeth</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: (pertaining to dentistry) οδοντιατρικός (odontiatrikós)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dental</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>οδοντικά</b> σύμφωνα είναι τα ⟨τ⟩, ⟨δ⟩, ⟨θ⟩ και ⟨ντ⟩ - The dental consonants are ⟨τ⟩, ⟨δ⟩, ⟨θ⟩ and ⟨ντ⟩.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀδοντικός. See δόντι and -ικός.
Compare Mariupol Greek дъонды́.</div><b>οδοντογένεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>odontogenesis</div><b>οδοντογιατρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural οδοντογιατροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://οδοντίατρος">οδοντίατρος</a></div><b>οδοντογλυφίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδοντογλυφίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toothpick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οδοντο- (odonto-, “dental”) + γλυφίδα</div><b>οδοντογονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>odontogeny, odontogenesis</div><b>οδοντοστοιχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδοντοστοιχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dentures, false teeth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dentition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οδοντο- (odonto-, “tooth”) + στοίχος</div><b>οδοντοφατνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dentoalveolar, dentialveolar</div><b>οδοντόβουρτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδοντόβουρτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toothbrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οδοντό- + βούρτσα.</div><b>οδοντόκρεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδοντόκρεμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toothpaste</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οδοντό- + κρέμα</div><b>οδοντόλιθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>odontolith</div><b>οδοντόπαστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδοντόπαστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toothpaste</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οδοντό- + πάστα</div><b>οδοστρωτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οδοστρωτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. road roller</div>
  <div class='def'>2. steamroller, steam roller</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) person who forces someone to do something</div><b>οδυρμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lamentation, wailing, loud weeping</div>
  <div class='ex'>(θρήνος,) κλαυθμός και <b>οδυρμός</b> - (lamentation,) weeping and wailing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀδυρμός, from ὀδύρομαι. By surface analysis, οδύρ(ομαι)) + -μός.</div><b>οδόντωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>odontoma</div><b>οδός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. street, road, way</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει αξιοποιηθεί η περιοχή απ’ όπου περνούσε η παλαιά <b>οδός</b>. - The area which the old road passed through is being developed.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. route</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ασφαλώς, κανείς δεν ισχυρίζεται ότι η νομοθετική <b>οδός</b> είναι πάντα η καλύτερη. - Of course, no one would argue that the legislative route is always the best.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁδός.</div><b>οδόφραγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οδοφράγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barricade (a barrier constructed across a road)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from οδ(ός)) + -ό- + φράγμα.</div><b>οδύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οδύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>literally: extreme pain of the soul; sorrow, grief, anguish, unhappiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὀδύνη. Stem ὀδ- related to the ancient ἔδω, as the pain &quot;devours&quot; us.</div><b>οθωμανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οθωμανική, neuter οθωμανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Ottoman</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Οθωμανική Αυτοκρατορία (The Ottoman Empire)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang, vulgar) οθωμανικό. Anal sex. (Similar to Greek #7)</div><b>οθόνες αφής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://οθόνη">οθόνη</a> αφής</div><b>οθόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οθόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. monitor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. screen (cinema etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. canvas (for oil paint)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀθόνη, probably from a Semitic language), ultimately from Egyptian jdmj (“fine red linen”).</div><b>οθόνη αφής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οθόνες αφής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>touch screen</div><b>οικία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οικίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>home, residence</div><b>οικείος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οικεία, neuter οικείο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>familiar, personal, intimate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκεῖος.</div><b>οικειοθελώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of one&#x27;s own will, voluntarily, readily</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From οικείος + θέλω + -ώς.</div><b>οικειότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οικειότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. familiarity, intimacy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. knowledge, acquaintance, familiarity with something</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκειότης, equivalent to οικείος + -ότητα.</div><b>οικιακά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>housework</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://οικιακός">οικιακός</a></div><b>οικιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οικιακή, neuter οικιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>domestic</div>
  <div class='ex'>οικιακή οικονομία (home economics)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek οἰκιακός, an adjectival formation from Ancient Greek οἰκία. The modern sense of &quot;domestic&quot; is a semantic loan from French domestique.</div><b>οικισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οικισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. settlement, locality, subdivision (of homes)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. housing estate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκισμός.</div><b>οικογένεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οικογένειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. family</div>
  <div class='def'>2. family</div>
  <div class='def'>3. household</div>
  <div class='def'>4. kin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκογεν(ής), “born in the house; homebred slave”) + -εια (-eia) or as in Koine Greek οἰκογένεια with different sense &quot;the state or verification of being a homebred slave&quot;. For Medieval Greek, see φαμελιά.</div><b>οικογενειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οικογενειακή, neuter οικογενειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>family, familial</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>οικογενειακός</b> γιατρός - family doctor</div><b>οικογενειοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nepotism (favoring of relatives or personal friends because of relationship rather than ability)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λόγω <b>οικογενειοκρατίας</b>, ο πρόεδρος της εταιρείας διόρισε την σύζυγο του ως διευθύντρια. - Because of nepotism, the president of the company appointed his wife as director.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οικογένεια + -κρατία.</div><b>οικοδέσποινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οικοδέσποινες, masculine οικοδεσπότης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hostess</div>
  <div class='def'>2. housewife</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκοδέσποινα.</div><b>οικοδεσπότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οικοδεσπότες, feminine οικοδέσποινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>host (typically in a personal home, one who hosts guests)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek οἰκοδεσπότης.</div><b>οικοδιακοπές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>staycation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From οικο- (oiko-, “house”) + διακοπές pl, a calque of English staycation.</div><b>οικοδομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οικοδομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. building under construction</div>
  <div class='def'>2. construction, building (process, occupation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek οἰκοδομή.</div><b>οικοδομικά τετράγωνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of οικοδομικό <a href="bword://τετράγωνο">τετράγωνο</a></div><b>οικοδομικές άδειες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of οικοδομική <a href="bword://άδεια">άδεια</a></div><b>οικοδομική άδεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οικοδομική άδεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>planning permission, construction permit, building permit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See οικοδομικός, άδεια f.</div><b>οικοδομικής άδειας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of οικοδομική <a href="bword://άδεια">άδεια</a></div><b>οικοδομικό τετράγωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οικοδομικά τετράγωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>block, city block</div><b>οικοδομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οικοδομική, neuter οικοδομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>building, construction (relating to buildings, their design or their construction)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek οἰκοδομῐκός (“suitable for building”), from ancient sense “capable to build”.</div><b>οικοδομικών αδειών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of οικοδομική <a href="bword://άδεια">άδεια</a></div><b>οικοδομώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to build, construct</div><b>οικοδόμημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οικοδομήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. building, construction, edifice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) something achieved by teamwork</div><b>οικοδόμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οικοδομήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>act or process of building; construction.</div><b>οικοδόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οικοδόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>builder, constructor</div><b>οικολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ecology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from German Ökologie or French écologie. By surface analysis, οικο- (oiko-) + -λογία.</div><b>οικολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οικολογική, neuter οικολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ecological</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from German ökologisch and French écologique. By surface analysis, οικολογία + -ικός.</div><b>οικονομάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal) to get (money, supplies, work, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Οικονόμησα</b> καλά λεφτά απ’ τη δουλίτσα αυτή! - Got some good money from this little job!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. and see: <a href="bword://οικονομώ">οικονομώ</a>:; to save (money, time, space, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. and see: <a href="bword://οικονομώ">οικονομώ</a>:; to economise (UK), economize (US) in general</div>
  <div class='def'>4. and see: <a href="bword://οικονομώ">οικονομώ</a>:; to economies elsewhere have enabled extra expenditure</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη νέα έκδοση του βιβλίου έχουν <b>οικονομηθεί</b> επιπλέον πληροφορίες και παραρτήματα. - With the new edition of the book extra information and appendices have been included (in adequate space).</div><b>οικονομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οικονομίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. economy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. economics</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (countable, plural normally) saving</div>
  <div class='def'>4. thrift</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek οἰκονομία.</div><b>οικονομιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal) passive of <a href="bword://οικονομάω">οικονομάω</a>; to be acquired (of money, goods), passive senses of <a href="bword://οικονομάω">οικονομάω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) passive of <a href="bword://οικονομάω">οικονομάω</a>; <a href="bword://από">από</a>: to manage to acquire sufficiently something</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Οικονομήθηκα</b> από ρούχα φέτος! Μου χάρισε πολλά η θεία μου. - (I am supplied) Got myself enough clothes this year! My aunt gave me as gift plenty.</div><b>οικονομικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://οικονομικός">οικονομικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. economically (from the perspective of economics or an economy)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. economically (in an economical manner; not wastefully; not extravagantly)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>economics (science)</div><b>οικονομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οικονομική, neuter οικονομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. economic, financial, fiscal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thrifty, economical</div><b>οικονομολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural οικονομολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>economist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from οικονομ(ία)) + -ο- (-o-) + -λόγος, a loose calque of French économiste, from Ancient Greek οἰκονομ(ία)) + -ιστής.</div><b>οικονομοτεχνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οικονομοτεχνική, neuter οικονομοτεχνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>technoeconomic</div><b>οικονομώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to save (money, time, space, etc), economise (UK), economize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. and see: <a href="bword://οικονομάω">οικονομάω</a> (informal): get (money, supplies, work, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκονομέω, οἰκονομῶ, verbal derivation from οἰκονόμος.</div><b>οικονόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (feminine οικονόμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. housekeeper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. steward</div>
  <div class='def'>3. frugal or thrifty person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκονόμος, from οἶκος + νόμος.</div><b>οικοπεδούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural οικοπεδούχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plotholder, site owner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from οικόπεδ(ο)) + -ούχος.</div><b>οικοσκευή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(collective) household effects, household goods</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek οἰκοσκευή. By surface analysis, οικο- (oiko-) + -σκευή. See οίκος m and σκεύος n.</div><b>οικοσοσιαλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural οικοσοσιαλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) ecosocialism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, οικο- (oiko-, “eco-”) + σοσιαλισμός.</div><b>οικοσύστημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οικοσυστήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ecosystem</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French écosystème (“ecosystem”). By surface analysis, οικο- (oiko-) + σύστημα.</div><b>οικοτοξικολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ecotoxicology</div><b>οικοτροφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boarding house</div><b>οικουμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) world, ecumene (the whole inhabited Earth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek οικουμένη.</div><b>οικουμενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οικουμενική, neuter οικουμενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. global, universal (concerning all parts of the world; common to all society; worldwide)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ecumenical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek οἰκουμενικός. By surface analysis, οικουμέν(η)) + -ικός.</div><b>οικόπεδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οικόπεδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plot, parcel of land for building on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰκόπεδον,&lt; οἶκος + πέδον.</div><b>οινολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oenology</div><b>οινομαγειρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οινομαγειρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A restaurant which serves food and wine.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From οινο- (oino-, “oeno-”) + μαγειρείο.</div><b>οινοπνευματώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οινοπνευματώδης, neuter οινοπνευματώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alcoholic (containing alcohol)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>οινοπνευματώδες</b> ποτό - alcoholic drink</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the οινοπνευματ- stem of οινόπνευμα + -ώδης, a loose calque of French alcoolique.</div><b>οινοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οινοφόρα or οινοφόρος, neuter οινοφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>viniferous, wine-bearing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From οίνος + -φόρος.</div><b>οινοχόος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οινοχόοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sommelier</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cupbearer</div>
  <div class='def'>3. barman, bartender</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰνοχόος, from οἶνος + χέω.</div><b>οινόπνευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οινοπνεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. surgical spirit, rubbing alcohol</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ethyl alcohol</div>
  <div class='def'>3. alcohol (intoxicating drink)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From οίνος + πνεύμα, a calque of French esprit-de-vin, itself calquing Latin spiritus vini. First attested 1831.</div><b>οισοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οισοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oesophagus (UK), esophagus (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek οἰσοφάγος.</div><b>οισοφαγίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οισοφαγίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>esophagitis</div><b>οιστρογόνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οιστρογόνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oestrogen (UK), estrogen (US)</div><b>οιωνός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οιωνοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>omen, portent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οἰωνός.</div><b>οκά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οκάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oka (weight unit equal to 400 dirhams, in Greece equal to 1.282kg)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish اوقه (okka), which is perhaps from Arabic أُوقِيَّة.</div><b>οκάπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) okapi (Okapia johnstoni)</div><b>οκαπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rare spelling of <a href="bword://οκάπι">οκάπι</a></div><b>οκνηρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οκνηρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) laziness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὀκνηρία, following the meaning of οκνηρός</div><b>οκνηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οκνηρή, neuter οκνηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) lazy</div><b>οκτάγωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οκτάγωνη, neuter οκτάγωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>octagonal, octangular</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὀκτάγωνος, from Ancient Greek ὀκτάγωνον. By surface analysis, οκτά- + γων(ία)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>οκτάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οκτάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>octane</div><b>οκτάπλευρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οκτάπλευρη, neuter οκτάπλευρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eight-sided, octolateral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὀκτάπλευρος. By surface analysis, οκτά- + -πλευρος (-plevros).</div><b>οκτάπους</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οκτάποδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://χταπόδι">χταπόδι</a> literally: &quot;with eight feet&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>older spelling: ὀκτάπους (oktápous)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὀκτάπους (adjective, also substantivised), from ὀκτα- (okta-, “eight-”), ὀκτώ, modern spelling οκτώ and πούς m.</div><b>οκταδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οκταδική, neuter οκταδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>octal</div><b>οκτακοσιοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οκτακοσιοστή, neuter οκτακοσιοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eight hundredth</div><b>οκτακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(noun, numeral) eight hundred</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of οκτακόσιοι</div><b>οκταπόδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οκταπόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://χταπόδι">χταπόδι</a></div><b>οκτώ</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) eight, 8</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ώρα είναι <b>οκτώ</b> το βράδυ. - It is eight in the evening.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀκτώ, from Proto-Hellenic *oktṓ, from Proto-Indo-European *oḱtṓw.
Compare Mariupol Greek ухто́.</div><b>ολάκερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολάκερη, neuter ολάκερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ολόκληρος">ολόκληρος</a> (frequently used in literature)</div><b>ολίγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολίγη, neuter ολίγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://λίγος">λίγος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα ήθελα έναν καφέ με <b>ολίγη</b>. Με λίγη ζάχαρη, όχι πολλή. - I would like a coffee with a little. With a little sugar, not too much.
 (expression: καφές με ολίγη)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. see masculine plural: οι ολίγοι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀλίγος. Also see λίγος.</div><b>ολημερίς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) all day</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ὁλημερίς, from ὅλος + ἡμέρα + -ίς (adverbializing suffix, analogical to νωρίς etc.).</div><b>ολιβίνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural ολιβίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) olivine</div><b>ολιγαρκής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολιγαρκής, neuter ολιγαρκές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frugal, self-denying</div><b>ολιγαρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ολιγαρχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oligarchy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀλιγαρχία.</div><b>ολιγαρχική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ολιγαρχικές, masculine ολιγαρχικός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oligarch</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ολιγαρχικός">ολιγαρχικός</a></div><b>ολιγαρχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολιγαρχική, neuter ολιγαρχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oligarchic</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ολιγαρχικοί, feminine ολιγαρχική)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oligarch</div><b>ολιγοπώλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ολιγοπώλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oligopoly</div><b>ολιγόκαινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολιγόκαινη, neuter ολιγόκαινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Oligocene</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>ολιγόκαινη</b> εποχή - the Oligocene epoch</div><b>ολιγόλογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολιγόλογη, neuter ολιγόλογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>taciturn, laconic, terse, untalkative</div><b>ολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολική, neuter ολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>total</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From όλος + -ικός.</div><b>ολισθαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to slide slip</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to drift</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to lapse</div><b>ολλανδέζικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Dutch (language)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ολλανδέζικος">ολλανδέζικος</a></div><b>ολλανδέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολλανδέζικη, neuter ολλανδέζικος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Dutch (related to the Netherlands, its people or language)</div><b>ολλανδικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Dutch (language)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ολλανδικός">ολλανδικός</a></div><b>ολλανδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολλανδική, neuter ολλανδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) of the Netherlands; Dutch</div><b>ολοήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all-day, whole-day</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὁλοήμερος. By surface analysis, όλος + -ο- (-o-) + ημέρα + -ος (-os).</div><b>ολογράφως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in full, in whole words (of writing: without abbreviations or numerals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek ὁλόγραφ(ος), “written in full”) + -ως (-os).</div><b>ολοζώντανος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολοζώντανη, neuter ολοζώντανο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intensive form of <a href="bword://ζωντανός">ζωντανός</a>:; very much alive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intensive form of <a href="bword://ζωντανός">ζωντανός</a>:; very lively, full of life</div>
  <div class='def'>3. intensive form of <a href="bword://ζωντανός">ζωντανός</a>:; very vivid, intensely lifelike (of a description, memory, representation, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ολοζώντανος. By surface analysis, ολο- (olo-) + ζωνταν(ός)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>ολοκαίνουργιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολοκαίνουργια, neuter ολοκαίνουργιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brand new (unused)</div><b>ολοκαίνουργος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολοκαίνουργα, neuter ολοκαίνουργο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ολοκαίνουριος">ολοκαίνουριος</a></div><b>ολοκαίνουριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολοκαίνουρια, neuter ολοκαίνουριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brand new</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ολο- (olo-) + καινούριος.</div><b>ολοκαύτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ολοκαυτώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. burnt offering</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sacrifice</div>
  <div class='def'>3. genocide, extermination</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (capitalized) the Holocaust</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁλόκαυστος.</div><b>ολοκλήρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ολοκληρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. completion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. integration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ολοκληρώνω + -ση (-si) with semantic loan from French intégrale in the calculus sense.</div><b>ολοκληρωμένα κυκλώματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ολοκληρωμένο <a href="bword://κύκλωμα">κύκλωμα</a></div><b>ολοκληρωμένο κύκλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ολοκληρωμένα κυκλώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>integrated circuit</div><b>ολοκληρωτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ολοκληρωτικός">ολοκληρωτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>completely, totally, utterly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η πόλη καταστράφηκε <b>ολοκληρωτικά</b>. - The town was completely destroyed.</div><b>ολοκληρωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολοκληρωτική, neuter ολοκληρωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. complete, total, utter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. totalitarian</div>
  <div class='def'>3. integral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ολοκληρώ(νω)) + -τικός with semantic loan from French total, totalitaire and (calcul) intégral.</div><b>ολοκληρώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be completed</div><b>ολοκληρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to complete, to finish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ὁλοκληρ(ῶ)) + -ώνω. See ολόκληρος for more.</div><b>ολομέλεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ολομέλειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plenary session, plenum</div><b>ολομελής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολομελής, neuter ολομελές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) plenary</div><b>ολομόναχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολομόναχη, neuter ολομόναχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, emphatic) completely alone, isolated</div><b>ολονυχτίς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) all night</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ὁλονυχτίς, from ὅλος + νύχτα + -ίς (adverbializing suffix, analogical to νωρίς etc.).</div><b>ολοσχερώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>completely, totally, utterly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το εργοστάσιο καταστράφηκε <b>ολοσχερώς</b>. - The factory was completely destroyed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὁλοσχερῶς. By surface analysis, ολοσχερ(ής)) + -ώς</div><b>ολοφύρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) to lament, to wail (to weep mournfully)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀλοφύρομαι.</div><b>ολούθε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) all around, from all sides, everywhere</div><b>ολυμπιάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ολυμπιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>olympiad</div><b>ολυμπιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολυμπιακή, neuter ολυμπιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Olympic (of or pertaining to the Olympic Games)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ολυμπιακοί</b> Αγώνες - Olympic Games</div>
  <div class='def'>2. for the sports club and its fans, see Ολυμπιακός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek Ὀλυμπιακόν ἔτος with semantic loan from French olympique in relation to the Olympic Games. By surface analysis, Ολύμπια + -ακός.</div><b>ολυμπιονίκης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ολυμπιονίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Olympic champion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Olympic medallist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀλυμπιονίκης.</div><b>ολόασπρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολόασπρη, neuter ολόασπρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(for color/colour) snow-white, lily-white (completely white)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ολό- + άσπρος.</div><b>ολόγιομος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολόγιομη, neuter ολόγιομο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(emphatic) completely full, full to the brim</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ολόγιομο</b> φεγγάρι - full moon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Variant of ολόγεμος with [e] → [o] change influenced by labial stop [m].</div><b>ολόγραμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ολογράμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hologram</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French hologramme &lt; Ancient Greek ὅλος + γράμμα n</div><b>ολόκαινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολόκαινος or ολόκαινη, neuter ολόκαινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Holocene</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>ολόκαινος</b> εποχή - the Holocene epoch</div><b>ολόκληρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολόκληρη, neuter ολόκληρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whole, entire, complete</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὁλόκληρος.</div><b>ολόμαυρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολόμαυρη, neuter ολόμαυρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jet-black, pitch-black (completely black)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὁλόμαυρος, equivalent to ολό- + μαύρος.</div><b>ολόφωτο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ολόφωτος">ολόφωτος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ολόφωτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a device for collecting light with lenses and directing it with reflectors</div><b>ολόφωτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολόφωτη, neuter ολόφωτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(poetic) filled with light, projecting light</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ολόφωτο</b> φεγγάρι - bright-shining moon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ὁλόφωτος. Morphologically, from (όλος) ολο- olo-, “holo- whole, entire”) + (φως) φωτ- ((fos) fot-, “light”) + -ος (-os).</div><b>ολόχαρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολόχαρη, neuter ολόχαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>full of joy</div><b>ολόχρυσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολόχρυση, neuter ολόχρυσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. made entirely out of gold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intensely golden in color</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of hair shade) golden blond, blonde, flaxen, fair</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα <b>ολόχρυσα</b> μαλλιά της ανέμιζαν στον αέρα. - Her golden blonde hair was blowing in the wind.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὁλόχρυσος. Equivalent to όλος + χρυσός.</div><b>ολόψυχα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wholeheartedly</div><b>ολύμπιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ολύμπια or ολυμπία, neuter ολύμπιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Olympian (relating to the Greek gods and Mount Olympus)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) Olympian (of heroic proportions)</div><b>ομάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. group, bunch, gang (of people or friends)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. panel (interview panel, panel of judges)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. team</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>ομάδα</b> μας έχασε στον ημιτελικό. - Our team lost in the semifinal.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. group in the periodic table</div>
  <div class='def'>5. squad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὁμάς with accusative case ending -άδα, in some cases a semantic loan from French équipe.</div><b>ομάδα Ζήτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>police rapid response motorcycle unit</div><b>ομήγυρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομηγύρεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) small gathering, small group of people</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὁμήγυρις.</div><b>ομίχλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fog</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀμίχλη, from Proto-Hellenic *omíkʰlā, from Proto-Indo-European *h₃migʰleh₂. Compare Albanian mjegull, Polish mgła, Latvian and Lithuanian migla (“fog”). Compare also Armenian մեգ (meg), Persian میغ, Sanskrit मेघ from a related root.</div><b>ομαδοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομαδοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grouping (a collection of things or people united as a group)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grouping (combining into groups)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ομάδ(α), “group”) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποίηση, a loose calque of French groupement.</div><b>ομαδοποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be grouped</div><b>ομαδοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to group</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ομάδ(α), “group”) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>ομαλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομαλή, neuter ομαλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. regular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. smooth, even, regular</div>
  <div class='def'>3. normal, regular</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὁμαλός.</div><b>ομελέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομελέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>omelette</div><b>ομηρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) the state of being a hostage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμηρεία.</div><b>ομιλήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομιλήτριες, masculine ομιλητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. speaker (one who speaks)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. speaker (one who uses a particular language)</div>
  <div class='ex'>φυσικός <b>ομιλήτρια</b> - native speaker</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lecturer, speaker</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>ομιλήτρια</b> ανέπτυξε το θέμα. - The speaker developed the theme.</div><b>ομιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομιλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. speech</div>
  <div class='def'>2. discourse, talk</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lecture, homily</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμιλία.</div><b>ομιλητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ομιλητές, feminine ομιλήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. speaker (one who speaks)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. speaker (one who uses a particular language)</div>
  <div class='ex'>φυσικός <b>ομιλητής</b> - native speaker</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lecturer, speaker</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>ομιλητής</b> ανέπτυξε το θέμα … - the speaker developed the theme …</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation from ομιλώ + -τής, with semantic loan from French orateur and semantic loan from German Redner. Related to, but not derived from, the phonetically identical Ancient Greek ὁμιλητής.</div><b>ομιλητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομιλητική, neuter ομιλητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>talkative, loquacious, garrulous, chatty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὁμιλητικός, with semantic influence from the related ομιλητής.</div><b>ομιλούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://μιλιέμαι">μιλιέμαι</a></div><b>ομιλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://μιλώ">μιλώ</a></div><b>ομοίωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ομοιώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>effigy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀμοίωμα.</div><b>ομοίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>likeness</div>
  <div class='ex'>κατ’ εικόνα και καθ’ <b>ομοίωση</b> - in the image and likeness (of someone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὁμοίωσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>ομοαξονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομοαξονική, neuter ομοαξονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coaxial</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ομοαξονικό</b> καλώδιο - coaxial cable</div><b>ομοβροντία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>burst, volley, fusillade (of gunfire)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ομο- (omo-, “same, homo-”) + βρόντος</div><b>ομογένεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) homogeneity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) people of any nation (especially the Greeks) living in a foreign country, taken collectively</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμογένεια.</div><b>ομογενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομογενής, neuter ομογενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>homogeneous</div><b>ομογνωμοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) unanimity, agreement</div><b>ομοιογενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομοιογενής, neuter ομοιογενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ομογενής">ομογενής</a></div><b>ομοιοκατάληκτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομοιοκατάληκτη, neuter ομοιοκατάληκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhyming</div><b>ομοιομορφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομοιομορφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uniformity (state of being uniform)</div><b>ομοιοπαθητική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομοιοπαθητικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>homeopathy</div><b>ομοιοπολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομοιοπολική, neuter ομοιοπολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>covalent, homeopolar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From όμοιος + πολικός.</div><b>ομοιωματικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ditto</div><b>ομοιόβαθμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coequal in rank</div><b>ομοιόμορφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομοιόμορφη, neuter ομοιόμορφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>similar, uniform</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὁμοιόμορφος.</div><b>ομοιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομοιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>similarity</div><b>ομολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (usually) confession, admission, avowal (open admittance of having done something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν περιμένεις από αυτήν να παραδεχτεί λάθος, θα περιμένεις πολύ· δεν έχει δώσει ποτέ της <b>ομολογία</b>. - If you&#x27;re waiting for her to admit wrongdoing, you&#x27;ll be waiting a long time; she has never given a confession.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. profession, creed (reading or statement of belief that summarizes the faith it represents)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ομολογία</b> πίστεως - profession of faith</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bond, debenture (documentary obligation to pay a sum or to perform a contract)</div>
  <div class='ex'>λαχειοφόρα <b>ομολογία</b> - premium bond</div>
  <div class='def'>4. homology (correspondences and relationships between various things)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>ομολογία</b> χεριού και ποδιού - the homology of arms and legs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμολογία, equivalent to ομο- (omo-, “same, homo-”) + -λογία. The present legal, religious and economic senses date from the Koine Greek era.</div><b>ομολογημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ομολογημένη, neuter ομολογημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confessed, admitted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ομολογούμαι, passive voice of ομολογώ (“confess, admit”).</div><b>ομολογητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ομολογητές, feminine ομολογήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. confessor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. confessor (official designation of a person who acknowledges their Christian faith, especially when this risks their life)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θεοφάνης Ομολογητής (Theophanes the Confessor)</div><b>ομολογιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://ομολογούμαι">ομολογούμαι</a></div><b>ομολογιέται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of ομολογείται</div><b>ομολογουμένως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>admittedly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σενάριο αυτό –ομολογουμένως εφιαλτικά ενδιαφέρον, από πολλές απόψεις– … - The scenario, admittedly of nightmarishly interest in many respects …</div><b>ομολογούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to confess, be confessed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to have to admit, admittedly</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ομολογείται</b> ότι το συγκεκριμένο τραγούδι δεν ήταν πολύ καλό αλλά έχει θαυμαστές. - One must admit that the song in question wasn&#x27;t very good but it has admirers.</div><b>ομολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to confess, admit (concede the truth)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο κατηγορούμενος <b>ομολόγησε</b> την ενοχή του. - The accused confessed his guilt.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, transitive) to profess (declare one&#x27;s adherence to a religion)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ομολογώ</b> την πίστη μου - I profess my faith</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμολογῶ, contracted form of ὁμολογέω, equivalent to ομο- (omo-, “same”) + λόγος.</div><b>ομορφαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to become beautiful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to beautify, to make beautiful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ομορφαίνω. By surface analysis, όμορφ(ος)) + -αίνω.</div><b>ομορφιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομορφιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beauty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> όμορφος + -ιά</div><b>ομοσπονδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομοσπονδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>federation, confederation, confederacy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ὁμοῦ + σπονδή. Calque of French fédération. First attested 1836.</div><b>ομοσπονδιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομοσπονδιακή, neuter ομοσπονδιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>federal, federative</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>Ομοσπονδιακή</b> Δημοκρατία της Γερμανίας - Federal Republic of Germany</div><b>ομοταξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ομοταξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>class</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from New Latin homotaxia, from ομο- (omo-, “same”) + τάξις + -ία.</div><b>ομοφυλοφιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) homosexuality (sexual orientation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ομόφυλος + -ο- (-o-) + -φιλία.</div><b>ομοφυλόφιλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομοφυλόφιλη, neuter ομοφυλόφιλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of people) homosexual, gay (attracted to the same sex)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αδερφή μου είναι <b>ομοφυλόφιλη</b>. - My sister is gay.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from ομοφυλοφιλ(ία)) + -ος (-os). By surface analysis, ομο- (omo-, “same, homo-”) + φύλο + -φιλος (-filos, “-friend, -phile”), calque of German homosexuell.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ομοφυλόφιλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>homosexual, gay man (male homosexual)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη Σαουδική Αραβία, βάζουν τους <b>ομοφυλόφιλους</b> στη φυλακή. - In Saudi Arabia, they put gay people in prison.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from ομοφυλοφιλ(ία)) + -ος (-os). By surface analysis, ομο- (omo-, “same, homo-”) + φύλο + -φιλος (-filos, “-friend, -phile”), calque of German homosexuell.</div><b>ομοφωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομοφωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unanimity, agreement,</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consensus</div>
  <div class='def'>3. homophony</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ομο- (omo-, “same, homo-”) + φωνή</div><b>ομοφώνως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unanimously</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὁμοφώνως. By surface analysis, ομόφωνος + -ως (-os).</div><b>ομπρέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομπρέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>umbrella</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian ombrella.</div><b>ομπρελοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομπρελοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>umbrella stand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ομπρέλα + θήκη.</div><b>ομφαλός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. navel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. omphalos</div>
  <div class='def'>3. belly button</div>
  <div class='def'>4. hilum</div>
  <div class='def'>5. umbilicus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀμφαλός. Doublet of αφαλός.</div><b>ομόηχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομόηχη, neuter ομόηχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. homophonous, same-sounding (of words: having the same pronunciation but different meanings)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural, noun from verb) homophones</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὁμόηχος. By surface analysis, ομό- + ήχος.</div><b>ομόλογη</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ομόλογος">ομόλογος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ομόλογος">ομόλογος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ομόλογος">ομόλογος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομόλογες, masculine ομόλογος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of people) counterpart, equivalent, opposite number (one which has corresponding functions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Υπουργός Δικαιοσύνης συναντήθηκε με την Αμερικανή <b>ομόλογή</b> του. - The Justice Minister met with his American counterpart.</div><b>ομόλογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομόλογη, neuter ομόλογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. corresponding (have a similar relationship or proportion)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ομόλογα</b> σχήματα - corresponding shapes</div>
  <div class='def'>2. homologous (showing a degree of correspondence or similarity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η βάλανος είναι <b>ομόλογη</b> με την κλειτορίδα. - The glans penis is homologous with the clitoris.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμόλογος, equivalent to ομό- + -λογος (-logos, “reason, mind, -logous”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ομόλογοι, feminine ομόλογη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of people) counterpart, equivalent, opposite number (one which has corresponding functions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Υπουργός Εξωτερικού συναντήθηκε με τον Αμερικανό <b>ομόλογό</b> του. - The Foreign Minister met with his American counterpart (ie, the Secretary of State).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμόλογος, equivalent to ομό- + -λογος (-logos, “reason, mind, -logous”).</div><b>ομόνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ομόνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concord, harmony</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>Ομόνοια</b> είναι η μικρή στρογγυλή πλατεία <b>Ομονοίας</b> στην Αθήνα. - Omonoia is the small round square of Concord in Athens.</div><b>ομότεχνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομότεχνη, neuter ομότεχνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fellow (of someone following the same craft or trade)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun) fellow-craftsman, fellow-craftswoman</div><b>ομόφυλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομόφυλη, neuter ομόφυλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of the same race</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of the same sex, same-sex</div>
  <div class='def'>3. of the same religion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμόφῡλος, with semantic loan from English and French homophile, which were coined from Greek elements (albeit a different root for the second element).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ομόφυλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. co-religionist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. same-sex</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁμόφῡλος, with semantic loan from English and French homophile, which were coined from Greek elements (albeit a different root for the second element).</div><b>ομόφωνα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ομόφωνος">ομόφωνος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unanimously</div><b>ομόφωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομόφωνη, neuter ομόφωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. homophonous, homophone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unanimous</div><b>ομόψυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ομόψυχη, neuter ομόψυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unanimous, of one mind, as one</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ομόφωνος (omófonos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὁμόψυχος. By surface analysis, ομό- + ψυχ(ή)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>ον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>being, creature</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄν, neuter gender of present participle ὤν of the verb εἰμί.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of ων (on) “being”</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄν, neuter gender of present participle ὤν of the verb εἰμί.</div><b>ον-λάιν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ονλαιν">ονλαιν</a> (onlain)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ονλαιν">ονλαιν</a> (onlain)</div><b>ον-λαίν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ονλαιν">ονλαιν</a> (onlain)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ονλαιν">ονλαιν</a> (onlain)</div><b>ον-λαιν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ονλαιν">ονλαιν</a> (onlain)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ονλαιν">ονλαιν</a> (onlain)</div><b>ον.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'><a href="bword://ονομαστική">ονομαστική</a> (nominative case)</div><b>ονείρωξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wet dream</div><b>ονειρεμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dreamily</div>
  <div class='def'>2. idyllically</div><b>ονειρεμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ονειρεμένη, neuter ονειρεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fantastic, idyllic, undreamed of</div><b>ονειρευτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ονειρευτή, neuter ονειρευτός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dreamlike</div><b>ονειρεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to dream, dream about</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>ονειρεύτηκες</b> εψές; - What did you dream about last night?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to daydream</div><b>ονειρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ονειρική, neuter ονειρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dream, dreamy</div><b>ονειροκρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ονειροκρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interpreter of dreams, oneiromancer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dream book</div><b>ονειρολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ονειρολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interpreter of dreams, oneiromancer</div><b>ονειρομάντης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interpreter of dreams, oneiromancer</div><b>ονειροπαρμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ονειροπαρμένη, neuter ονειροπαρμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>starry-eyed, living in a dream world</div><b>ονειροπολώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to daydream, fantasise (UK), fantasize (US)</div><b>ονλαιν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) online</div>
  <div class='ex'>καζίνο ονλαιν (online casino)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>online</div><b>ονομ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ονομαστική">ονομαστική</a></div><b>ονομάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be named</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) one&#x27;s name is</div><b>ονομάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to give a name to, call by name, name</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to pronounce, formally declare</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek ὀνομάζω.</div><b>ονομασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ονομασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>naming, appellation, designation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προστατευόμενη <b>Ονομασία</b> Προέλευσης - Protected Designation of Origin</div><b>ονομαστική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(nominative) nominative case</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το υποκείμενο του ρήματος βρίσκεται πάντα σε <b>ονομαστική</b> πτώση. - The subject of the verb is always in the nominative case.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ονομαστικός">ονομαστικός</a></div><b>ονομαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ονομαστική, neuter ονομαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominal, titular</div><b>ονοματεπώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ονοματεπώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>full name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ονοματ- (onomat-) + επώνυμο.</div><b>ονοματοποιία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ονοματοποιίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>onomatopoeia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀνοματοποιία.</div><b>οντότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οντότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>entity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὀντότητα, accusative of ὀντότης, with semantic loan from French entité and essence. By surface analysis, οντ- (ont-) + -ότητα.</div><b>ονόματι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>called, named</div><b>οξαλίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οξαλίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wood sorrel, any plant belonging to the family Oxalidaceae</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὀξαλίς.</div><b>οξαλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οξαλική, neuter οξαλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oxalic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French oxalique, from Oxalis (“wood sorrel”) (due to the plant&#x27;s high oxalate content), from Ancient Greek ὀξαλίς.</div><b>οξεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οξείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ´ (a strong stress accent used in polytonic Greek script e.g. in Ancient Greek and sometimes Modern Greek)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. acute (angle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀξεῖᾰ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://οξύς">οξύς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>οξείδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οξείδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oxide</div><b>οξείδιο του αργιλίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) aluminium oxide, alumina</div><b>οξειδοαναγωγή</b>
