  <div class='def'>scanner (optical)</div><b>οπτιμιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οπτιμιστές, feminine οπτιμίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>optimist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transfer or calque of French optimiste, from Latin optimus (“best”) + (-iste) -ιστής.</div><b>οπτόπλινθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οπτόπλινθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) mudbrick</div><b>οπτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οπτή, neuter οπτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fired</div><b>οπωροπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οπωροπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greengrocer&#x27;s shop, greengrocer&#x27;s, greengrocery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation from οπωροπώλ(ης)) + -είον.</div><b>οπωροπώλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οπωροπώλες, feminine οπωροπώλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fruiterer, greengrocer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὀπωροπώλης.</div><b>οπωσδήποτε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anyway, anyhow, in any case, definitely</div><b>οπόσουμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) opossum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (US, indeclinable) possum</div><b>οπότε</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. so</div>
  <div class='def'>2. therefore, consequently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὁπότε. Compare to the inherited όποτε.</div><b>ορίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be defined</div>
  <div class='ex'>Jean Paul Sartre
Ο φασισμός δεν ορίζεται από τον αριθμό των θυμάτων, αλλά από τον τρόπο που τα σκοτώνει.
O fasismós den orízetai apó ton arithmó ton thymáton, allá apó ton trópo pou ta skotónei.
Fascism is not defined by the number of its victims, but by the way it kills them.</div><b>ορίζοντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>horizon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from the accusative singular ὁρίζοντα of the Ancient Greek ὁρίζων, demarcating cycle)
Substantivised neuter of the active participle ορίζων of verb ορίζω -ancient: ὁρίζω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Present participle of <a href="bword://ορίζω">ορίζω</a>: defining</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Active participle of ορίζω.</div><b>ορίζουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>determinant</div><b>ορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to define, designate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to decide</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to arrive, reach</div>
  <div class='ex'>καλώς ορίσατε! - welcome!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁρίζω.</div><b>ορίστε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (sarcastic, sometimes) here&#x27;s, there&#x27;s, here you are, here you go, there you are, there you go (said when handing something over to someone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Που είναι το παλτό μου; <b>Ορίστε</b>! - Where&#x27;s my coat? Here you are!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. yes? (said when someone is addressing you, to whom you owe respect)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κύριε Γιάννη; <b>Ορίστε</b>! - Mr Yianni? Yes?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. yes?, go ahead (said when asking someone to do something or say something)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ορίστε</b>, σας ακούω. - Go ahead, I can hear you.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. hello?, hi (said on answering the telephone)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ορίστε</b>, ποιος είναι; - Hello, who is it?</div>
  <div class='def'>5. pardon?, excuse me?, sorry? (said on requesting someone to repeat due to not having heard them)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ορίστε</b>, τι είπατε; - Sorry, what was that?</div>
  <div class='def'>6. pardon?, excuse me?, sorry? (said on hearing something shocking or confusing)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ορίστε</b>; Συμβαίνουν όντως τέτοια πράγματα; - Excuse me? Do such things really happen?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The interjection sense comes from the verb form of ορίζω (see below), literally &quot;set out!&quot; or &quot;define!&quot;.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second-person plural perfective imperative of <a href="bword://ορίζω">ορίζω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The interjection sense comes from the verb form of ορίζω (see below), literally &quot;set out!&quot; or &quot;define!&quot;.</div><b>οραγκουτάγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οραγκουτάγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ουρακοτάγκος">ουρακοτάγκος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English orangutan, from Malay orang utan.</div><b>οραματίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to envisage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) to see visions [defs 3, 4, 6]; hallucinate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὁρᾱματίζομαι (“look”) from ὅρᾱμᾰ.</div><b>ορατός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορατή, neuter ορατό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>visible, in sight, seen</div><b>ορατότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>visibility (the degree to which things may be seen)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ορατ(ός)) + -ότητα, a calque of French visibilité.</div><b>οργάνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οργανώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>organization</div><b>οργή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rage, anger, wrath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ὀργή.</div><b>οργίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be enraged, to be angry</div><b>οργίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to infuriate, anger, make angry, enrage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to antagonise (UK), antagonize (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀργίζω.</div><b>οργανικές ενώσεις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative plural of <a href="bword://οργανική">οργανική</a> <a href="bword://ένωση">ένωση</a></div><b>οργανική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>instrumental case</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://οργανικός">οργανικός</a></div><b>οργανική ένωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οργανικές ενώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>organic compound</div><b>οργανική χημεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) organic chemistry</div><b>οργανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οργανική, neuter οργανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. organic</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>οργανική</b> χημεία - organic chemistry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (instrumental) instrumental</div><b>οργανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οργανισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. organism (living thing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε ζωντανός <b>οργανισμός</b> αλληλεπιδρά με άλλους <b>οργανισμούς</b>. - Every living thing interacts with other organisms.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. organisation (UK), organization (US), board</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ελληνικός <b>Οργανισμός</b> Τουρισμού - Greek Tourism Board</div><b>οργανογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>organography</div><b>οργανοληπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οργανοληπτική, neuter οργανοληπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>organoleptic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French organoleptique.</div><b>οργαντίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>organdy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From οργκαντίνα, from Italian organtina.</div><b>οργανώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to organize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French organiser. By surface analysis, όργανο + -ώνω.</div><b>οργασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οργασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orgasm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀργασμός, from ὀργάω.</div><b>ορδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. horde</div>
  <div class='def'>2. swarm</div><b>ορείχαλκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ορείχαλκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bronze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀρείχαλκος.</div><b>ορειβάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ορειβάτες, feminine ορειβάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mountaineer, mountain climber</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ορει(βασία)) + -βάτης. Compare Ancient Greek ὀρειβάτης.</div><b>ορειβασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mountaineering, mountain climbing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὀρειβασία with semantic loan from English mountaineering or German Bergsteigen.</div><b>ορεινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορεινή, neuter ορεινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mountainous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρεινός</div><b>ορειχάλκινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορειχάλκινη, neuter ορειχάλκινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bronze, of bronze</div><b>ορεκτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορεκτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>starter, appetiser, entrée, hors d&#x27;oeuvre, antipasto</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ορεκτικός">ορεκτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ορεκτικός">ορεκτικός</a></div><b>ορεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορεκτική, neuter ορεκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>appetising, tasty</div><b>ορθάνοιχτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορθάνοιχτη, neuter ορθάνοιχτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, emphatic) wide open</div><b>ορθές γωνίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative plural of ορθή <a href="bword://γωνία">γωνία</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative plural of ορθή <a href="bword://γωνία">γωνία</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative plural of ορθή <a href="bword://γωνία">γωνία</a></div><b>ορθή γωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορθές γωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>right angle</div><b>ορθογένεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) orthogenesis</div><b>ορθογναθικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορθογναθική, neuter ορθογναθικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orthognathic (referring to the straightening of the jaw or surgical efforts pertaining thereto)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English orthognathic, suffixed with -ικός. By surface analysis, ορθός + γνάθος + -ικός.</div><b>ορθογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) spelling (act, practice, ability, or subject of forming words with letters)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το να γράφει κανείς «γυνέκα» δεν είναι σωστή <b>ορθογραφία</b>. - For anyone to write &#x27;wuman&#x27; (sic) is not correct spelling.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, uncountable) spelling (as a school subject)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Στέλιος παίρνει πάντα άριστα στην <b>ορθογραφία</b>. - Stelios always gets an excellent score in spelling.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) orthography (aspect of language study concerned with letters and their sequences in words)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρθογραφία, equivalent to ορθο- (ortho-, “correct, ortho-”) + -γραφία.</div><b>ορθογωνιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orthogonality</div><b>ορθογώνιο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ορθογώνιος">ορθογώνιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ορθογώνιος">ορθογώνιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορθογώνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ορθογώνιο">ορθογώνιο</a> <a href="bword://παραλληλόγραμμο">παραλληλόγραμμο</a></div><b>ορθογώνιο παραλληλόγραμμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορθογώνια παραλληλόγραμμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rectangle, right-angled quadrilateral</div><b>ορθογώνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορθογώνια, neuter ορθογώνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orthogonal, right-angled, rectangular</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρθογώνιος.</div><b>ορθοδοντική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orthodontics</div><b>ορθοδοξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορθοδοξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. orthodoxy (conventionality in doctrine and belief)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conforming behaviour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ὀρθός + δόξα.</div><b>ορθοδόξως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) orthodoxly, orthodoxally (in an orthodox manner)</div><b>ορθοσίφωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ορθοσίφωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orthosiphon (Orthosiphon)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from translingual Orthosiphon.</div><b>ορθωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ορθωμένη, neuter ορθωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who is raised, erect, lifted</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γάτα όρμησε με <b>ορθωμένες</b> τις τρίχες της. - The cat sprang with its hair erect.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ορθώνομαι, passive voice of ορθώνω (“lift”).</div><b>ορθό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορθά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rectum</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ορθός">ορθός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ορθός">ορθός</a></div><b>ορθόδοξα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ορθόδοξος">ορθόδοξος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ορθόδοξος">ορθόδοξος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ορθόδοξος">ορθόδοξος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orthodoxly, orthodoxally (in an orthodox manner)</div><b>ορθόδοξη</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ορθόδοξος">ορθόδοξος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ορθόδοξος">ορθόδοξος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ορθόδοξος">ορθόδοξος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρθόδοξος, equivalent to ορθό- + δόξα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορθόδοξες, masculine ορθόδοξος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Orthodox (follower of any of the Eastern Orthodox churches)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μάνα μου είναι <b>ορθόδοξη</b> αλλά ο πατέρας μου είναι καθολικός. - My mother is an Orthodox but my father is a Catholic.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρθόδοξος, equivalent to ορθό- + δόξα.</div><b>ορθόδοξος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορθόδοξη, neuter ορθόδοξο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Orthodox (relating to any Orthodox movement or ideology; on its own, usually refers to Greek Orthodox in Greek)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το να έχει γένια ο παπάς είναι <b>ορθόδοξη</b> παράδοση. - For the priest to have a beard is an Orthodox tradition.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. orthodox (following convention or tradition)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο επιστήμονας δοκιμάζει όλες τις <b>ορθόδοξες</b> μεθόδους. - The scientist is trying all the conventional methods.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρθόδοξος, equivalent to ορθό- + δόξα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ορθόδοξοι, feminine ορθόδοξη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Orthodox (follower of any of the Eastern Orthodox churches)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Από την στιγμή που τον βάλανε στην κολυμπήθρα, ο Νίκος ήταν <b>ορθόδοξος</b>. - From the minute they put him in the font, Nikos was an Orthodox.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρθόδοξος, equivalent to ορθό- + δόξα.</div><b>ορθός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορθή, neuter ορθό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. upright, erect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. straight</div>
  <div class='def'>3. correct, right, sound</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρθός , from Proto-Hellenic *ortʰwós, from Proto-Indo-European *h₃r̥dʰwós, from *h₃erdʰ- (“upright”). Cognate with Latin arduus, Sanskrit ऊर्ध्व.</div><b>ορθότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. correctness (freedom from error)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. correctness (conformity to the truth or to fact)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀρθότηητα, accusative of ὀρθότης. By surface analysis, ορθ(ός)) + -ότητα.</div><b>ορθώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lift, erect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to raise</div><b>οριζόντια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://οριζόντιος">οριζόντιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>horizontally</div><b>οριζόντια δοκάρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of οριζόντιο <a href="bword://δοκάρι">δοκάρι</a></div><b>οριζόντιο δοκάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οριζόντια δοκάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crossbar (of goalposts)</div><b>οριζόντιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οριζόντια or οριζόντιος, neuter οριζόντιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>horizontal</div><b>ορισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ορισμένη, neuter ορισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>defined</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ορίζομαι, passive voice of ορίζω (“define”).</div><b>ορισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ορισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. definition (the process of defining)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. definition, designation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. clue, definition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὁρισμός.</div><b>οριστικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://οριστικός">οριστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>definitively, at the last stage</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η σχέση μας έληξε <b>οριστικά</b>. - Our affair has ended definitively.</div><b>οριστικά άρθρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of οριστικό <a href="bword://άρθρο">άρθρο</a></div><b>οριστική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://οριστικός">οριστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οριστικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indicative mood</div><b>οριστικό άρθρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οριστικά άρθρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(article, definite) definite article</div><b>οριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οριστική, neuter οριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. definitive (conclusive or decisive; not subject to subsequent alteration)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. final, in a final stage</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (definite) definite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁριστικός.</div><b>ορκίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to swear, be sworn in</div><b>ορκίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to swear, to affirm something with an oath (literally or metaphorically)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to swear (someone in), swear in</div><b>ορκωμοσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορκωμοσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swearing-in, oathtaking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὁρκωμοσία.</div><b>ορμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. momentum, impetus, force</div>
  <div class='def'>2. momentum</div>
  <div class='def'>3. urge</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, in plural) libido</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχει <b>ορμές</b>, είναι σεξουαλικά ανίκανος. - He lacks urges, he is impotent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὁρμή.</div><b>ορμαθίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://αρμαθιάζω">αρμαθιάζω</a></div><b>ορμητικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορμητικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. impetuosity, momentum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rush</div><b>ορμιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορμιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fishing line</div><b>ορμόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορμόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hormone</div><b>ορνιθολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ornithology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄρνις and -λογία).</div><b>ορνιθολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ορνιθολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ornithologist</div><b>ορνιθόγαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορνιθόγαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>star-of-Bethlehem (Ornithogalum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρνιθόγαλον, with a semantic loan from translingual Ornithogalum.</div><b>ορνιθώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ορνιθώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hennery, poultry farm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. henhouse, chicken coop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> όρνιθα + -ώνας</div><b>ορνιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορνιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wild fig tree</div><b>οροβάγχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οροβάγχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>broomrape (Orobanche-genus plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀροβάγχη. Semantic loan from translingual Orobanche.</div><b>οροθετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to delimit, to demarcate</div><b>ορολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terminology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from όρ(ος), “term”) + -ο- (-o-) + -λογία, a calque of French terminologie.</div><b>οροσειρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οροσειρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mountain range, mountain chain, sierra, ridge</div><b>οροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οροφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ceiling, roof</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀροφή.</div><b>ορρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ορροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ορός">ορός</a></div><b>ορτανσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορτανσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hortensia, hydrangea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French hortensia, from translingual Hortensia, an obsolete genus, now Hydrangea.</div><b>ορτύκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορτύκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quail (game bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὀρτύκιον, diminutive of ὄρτυξ.</div><b>ορυγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ορυγμένη, neuter ορυγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, uncommon) dug</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ορύσσομαι, passive voice of ορύσσω, an archaic verb. See the ancient ὀρύσσω.</div><b>ορυκτολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) mineralogy</div><b>ορυκτολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ορυκτολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mineralogist</div><b>ορυχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορυχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mine (source of coal, silver, etc)</div><b>ορφάνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) orphanhood (the state of being an orphan)</div><b>ορφανοτροφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orphanage</div><b>ορφανό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορφανά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orphan</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ορφανός">ορφανός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ορφανός">ορφανός</a></div><b>ορφανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορφανή, neuter ορφανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. orphan</div>
  <div class='def'>2. orphaned</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρφανός, from Proto-Indo-European *h₃órbʰos.</div><b>ορχήστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορχήστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. orchestra, band</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jazz band</div>
  <div class='def'>3. orchestra (The space in a theatre where the chorus danced and sang.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὀρχήστρα.</div><b>ορχηστρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ορχηστρική, neuter ορχηστρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orchestral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὀρχηστρικός; by surface analysis, ορχήστρα + -ικός.</div><b>ορχιδέα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορχιδέες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orchid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French orchidée, from Latin orchis, from Ancient Greek ὄρχις.</div><b>ορός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. serum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρός; see also Sanskrit सर (sara, “flowing”), सरित् (sarit, “river, brook”) and Latin serum (“whey”).</div><b>ορόσημο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορόσημα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. landmark (an object that marks the boundary of a piece of land)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) landmark, milestone (an important event)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from όρ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -σημο (-simo), a calque of English landmark.</div><b>ορύσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, dated, literary) synonym of <a href="bword://σκάβω">σκάβω</a>: dig</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὀρύσσω. Also in Mediaeval Greek, with alternative form ὀρύγω.</div><b>οσιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οσιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>godliness, piety, holiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὁσιότης.</div><b>οσμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οσμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smell, odour (UK), odor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀσμή.</div><b>οσμίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to smell something</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to guess, understand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οσμ(ή), “smell”, noun) + -ίζομαι.</div><b>οσοσδήποτε</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (pronoun, universal) no matter how many</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (pronoun, universal) no matter how much</div><b>οστεάλευρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bone meal</div><b>οστεοβλάστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>osteoblast</div><b>οστεορραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>osteosuture, osteorrhaphy</div><b>οστεοσάρκωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οστεοσαρκώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>osteosarcoma</div><b>οστεοσκλήρυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>osteosclerosis</div><b>οστεοσκλήρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>osteosclerosis</div><b>οστεοφυλάκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οστεοφυλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ossuary (building for holding the bones of the dead)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν εκταφεί ο παππούς, τα κόκαλα θα πάνε στο <b>οστεοφυλάκιο</b>. - When grandad is exhumed, his bones will be brought to the ossuary.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οστό + φυλάσσω. First attested 1889.</div><b>οστεώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οστεώδης, neuter οστεώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skin and bones, bony; malnourished to the point of frailty.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀστεώδης. By surface analysis, οστό + -ώδης.</div><b>οστομάχιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οστομάχια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ostomachion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ὀστομάχιον, from ὀστέον + μάχη + -ιον (-ion, nominal suffix). The original Ancient Greek term is often said to be coined by Greek mathematician and physicist (c. 287 – c. 212 BC) Archimedes.</div><b>οστούν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://οστό">οστό</a></div><b>οστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bone (part of skeleton)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀστέον, from Proto-Indo-European *h₃esth₁-, *h₃osth₁-.
Compare Mariupol Greek студъ.</div><b>οσφυαλγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οσφυαλγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lumbago</div><b>οσφυολαγόνιος</b>
