  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οὐρανός, with the sense of &quot;roof&quot; taken as a semantic loan or calque from French.
Compare Mariupol Greek урано́с, урано́.</div><b>ουρηθροσκοπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural ουρηθροσκοπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) urethroscopy</div><b>ουρηθροσκόπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural ουρηθροσκοπήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) urethroscopy</div><b>ουρική αρθρίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gout, podagra</div><b>ουρλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to yell, scream</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to howl (animal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ουριάζω, with epenthetic -λ- from Italian urlare (“to scream, howl”), from an earlier ούρυοῡμοι + -ιάζω, ultimately from Ancient Greek ὠρύομαι.</div><b>ουρλιαχτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ουρλιαχτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. howl (esp. animal, wind)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>Ουρλιαχτό</b> είναι το ποίημα του αμερικανού Άλλεν Γκίνσμπεργκ. - Howl is the poem by the American Allen Ginsberg.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cry, yell</div><b>ουροδοχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ουροδοχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bedpan, bed urinal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ούρο + δοχείο</div><b>ουροδόχοι κύστες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ουροδόχος <a href="bword://κύστη">κύστη</a></div><b>ουροδόχος κύστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ουροδόχοι κύστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>urinary bladder</div><b>ουροθήλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural ουροθήλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) urothelium</div><b>ουρολιθίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>urolithiasis</div><b>ουροσκοπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>uroscopy</div><b>ουρόλιθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>urolith</div><b>ουρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to urinate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek οὐρῶ, contracted form of οὐρέω.</div><b>ους</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) ear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οὖς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>οὕς accusative masculine plural of ὅς</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Monotonic spelling of ancient pronoun form οὕς.</div><b>ουσ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ουσιαστικό">ουσιαστικό</a>: noun</div><b>ουσάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ουσάροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hussar (light cavalry of European armies)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Serbo-Croatian husar.</div><b>ουσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ουσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. being</div>
  <div class='def'>2. substance</div>
  <div class='def'>3. meaning, sense</div>
  <div class='def'>4. essence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οὐσία.</div><b>ουσιαστικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ουσιαστικός">ουσιαστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. essentially</div>
  <div class='def'>2. substantially</div>
  <div class='def'>3. substantively</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ουσιαστικό">ουσιαστικό</a></div><b>ουσιαστικοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ουσιαστικοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominalization, substantivization</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ουσιαστικό + ποίηση.</div><b>ουσιαστικοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to substantivise, nominalise (UK), substantivize, nominalize (US)</div><b>ουσιαστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ουσιαστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>noun, substantive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantic loan from French substantif.</div><b>ουσιαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ουσιαστική, neuter ουσιαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. substantial, real (true, actual)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. essential (necessary)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ουσία + -τικός, calque of French substantiel. First attested 1890. There is a single attestation in a Koine Greek document for οὐσιαστικός but its meaning is unknown and it is not related to the current word.</div><b>ουσιώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ουσιώδης, neuter ουσιώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. essential (of the essence)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. substantial, material</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ουσιώδεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. material, substance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. essential (a necessary thing)</div><b>ουστ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) shoo (used to get rid of animals and undesirable people)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ουστ</b>, βρωμόσκυλο. - Shoo, dirty dog.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Possibly from Ottoman Turkish اوش اوشت or only the latter اوشت likely with multiple dialectal variants leading to the Turkish hoşt.
Highly likely, some kind of onomatopoeic sound, possibly conflated with the Turkish term. Compare French ouste, German husch, Hungarian hess and Irish hois, all onomatopoeic used to shoo an animal. For a similar phenomenon see English puss, Turkish pisi and Arabic بَسَّة (bassa), all likely onomatopoeic from the sound used to attract a cat.</div><b>ουτοπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ουτοπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>utopia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English Utopia, the fictional place in the book Utopia (1516) by Sir Thomas More. Coined from Ancient Greek οὐ (ou, “not, no”) + τόπος.</div><b>ουτοπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ουτοπική, neuter ουτοπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>utopian, visionary</div><b>ουφολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ufology (pseudoscience)</div><b>οφείλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to owe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be obliged to</div><b>οφειλέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οφειλέτες, feminine οφειλέτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>debtor</div><b>οφειλέτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οφειλέτριες, masculine οφειλέτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>debtor</div><b>οφθαλμίατρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oculist (US), ophthalmologist (UK)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> οφθαλμ- (ofthalm-) + -ίατρος; see οφθαλμός and γιατρός, respectively, for the components.</div><b>οφθαλμαπάτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οφθαλμαπάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>optical illusion, mirage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from οφθαλμ- (ofthalm-) + απάτη, a calque of English optical illusion.</div><b>οφθαλμοκινητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oculomotor</div><b>οφθαλμοκινητικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oculomotion</div><b>οφθαλμοπληγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural οφθαλμοπληγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) ophthalmoplegia</div><b>οφθαλμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οφθαλμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in medical context) eye</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bud, eye (of a plant&#x27;s shoot or flower)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀφθαλμός.</div><b>οφιοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οφιοειδής, neuter οφιοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snakelike, ophidian, anguineous, serpentine, sinuous, meandering</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι δρόμοι της παλιάς πόλης ήταν <b>οφιοειδείς</b>. - The old town&#x27;s streets were sinuous.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὀφῐοειδής. Ancient Greek ὀφιώδης.</div><b>οφιολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ophiology</div><b>οφσάιντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) offside (in an illegal position ahead of the ball)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English offside.</div><b>οχι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://όχι">όχι</a></div><b>οχιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οχιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adder, viper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) an evil, cunning person</div><b>οχλοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οχλοκρατίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ochlocracy, mob rule, mobocracy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὀχλοκρατία, equivalent to όχλος + -κρατία.</div><b>οχτακοσιοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine οχτακοσιοστή, neuter οχτακοσιοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://οκτακοσιοστός">οκτακοσιοστός</a> (less common)</div><b>οχτακόσα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://οκτακόσια">οκτακόσια</a></div><b>οχτακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://οκτακόσια">οκτακόσια</a></div><b>οχταπόδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οχταπόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χταπόδι">χταπόδι</a></div><b>οχτώ</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://οκτώ">οκτώ</a> (eight, 8)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ώρα είναι <b>οχτώ</b> το βράδυ. - It is eight o&#x27;clock in the evening.</div><b>οψιανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οψιανοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://οψιδιανός">οψιδιανός</a></div><b>οψιδιανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural οψιδιανοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obsidian</div><b>ούζο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ούζα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ouzo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Disputed, possibly from Italian, see ouzo.</div><b>ούλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ούλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gingiva, gum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek οὖλον</div><b>ούλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ούλη, neuter ούλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://όλος">όλος</a></div><b>ούπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) wall plug (UK), screw anchor (US)</div><b>ούρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ούρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) urine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οὖρον, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *h₂wers- (“to rain, drip”), *weh₁r-.</div><b>ούτε</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. neither, nor</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ούτ’</b> εγώ - neither do I -ούτ(ε) followed by vowel-</div>
  <div class='def'>2. neither … nor</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ούτε</b> έφαγα, <b>ούτε</b> ήπια. - I have neither eaten nor drunk.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nor even, not even</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν είχε αυτοκίνητο, <b>ούτε</b> καν ένα ποδήλατο. - He had no car, not even a bicycle.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek οὔτε.</div><b>ούτε γάτα ούτε ζημιά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as if nothing had happened (literally: neither cat nor damage)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τη Δευτέρα πέρασε να με δει· <b>ούτε γάτα ούτε ζημιά</b>. - He came to see me on Monday as if nothing had happened.</div><b>ούτος</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>he</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek οὗτος.</div><b>ούτω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ούτως">ούτως</a> (used before a consonant): thus, like this, like that</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek οὕτω. Also see οὕτως.</div><b>ούτως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in set phrases) thus, so, like this, like that</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ούτως</b> ή άλλως - one way or another</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek οὕτως.</div><b>ούτως ή άλλως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) one way or another (somehow, but certainly)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ούτως ή άλλως</b>, θα φτάσουμε εκεί. - One way or another, we&#x27;ll get there.</div><b>ούφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) UFO, an unidentified flying object</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> English ufo</div><b>π.Κ.Ε.</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>BCE, initialism of <a href="bword://προ">προ</a> Κοινής Εποχής</div><b>π.Χ.</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>B.C., BC, initialism of <a href="bword://προ">προ</a> Χριστού</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Initialism of προ Χριστού.</div><b>π.μ.</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://προ">προ</a> μεσημβρίας (ante meridiem)</div><b>π.χ.</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>e.g., for example</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from παραδείγματος χάριν</div><b>π΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Π΄">Π΄</a></div><b>πΧ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of π.Χ. (p.Ch.)</div><b>πά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://απά">απά</a></div><b>πάγκαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πάγκαλη, neuter πάγκαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very nice</div><b>πάγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πάγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shop counter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. workbench</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bench (long seat)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (usually) booth, stall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πάγκος/μπάγκος, from Italian banco.</div><b>πάγκρεας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pancreas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παγ- (pag-) + κρέας; (παγ- (pag-) from παν- (pan-)).</div><b>πάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ice (frozen water)</div>
  <div class='ex'>με <b>πάγο</b> - with ice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) cold person, unfriendly person (one metaphorically cold like ice)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η γυναίκα δε χαμογελάει ποτέ· είναι σκέτος <b>πάγος</b>. - That woman never smiles — she&#x27;s a complete ice queen.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πάγος.</div><b>πάγουρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hermit crab</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) crab</div><b>πάγωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. freezing (the act or process whereby something becomes frozen)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. freeze (a halt of a regular operation; a prevention of change or movement)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. freeze (a block on recruitment, pay increases, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πάγωμαν. By surface analysis, παγώ(νω)) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>πάθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>disease, malady</div><b>πάθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πάθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pathos</div>
  <div class='def'>2. empathy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. disease, misfortune, suffering, loss, grief</div>
  <div class='def'>4. passion, affection</div>
  <div class='def'>5. animosity</div>
  <div class='def'>6. grudge</div>
  <div class='def'>7. occurrence, accident</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πάθος.</div><b>πάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wrestling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fight, struggle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πάλη.</div><b>πάλι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. again</div>
  <div class='def'>2. on the other hand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πάλιν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Pali</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Sanskrit पालि, पालिभाषा, but probably with some other European language (English Pali?) serving as intermediate.</div><b>πάλι καλά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) just as well, luckily, good thing (that), thank God (an occurrence, or situation is not only fortunate, but that on the contrary, it could have been a lot worse)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πάλι καλά</b> που πήρες μαζί τον φακό, αλλιώς θα χανόμασταν. - Just as well you took a torch with you, otherwise we would have been lost.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “again good”.</div><b>πάλλευκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πάλλευκη, neuter πάλλευκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (for color/colour) snow-white, lily-white (completely white)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) immaculate, undefiled, unsullied (morally clear and pure)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πάλλευκος, equivalent to (παν-) πάλ- (“all, completely”) + λευκός.</div><b>πάλσαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pulsar (rotating neutron star)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English pulsar.</div><b>πάμμαυρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πάμμαυρη, neuter πάμμαυρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (for color/colour) completely black, all black</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for color/colour) jet-black, pitch-black (extremely black)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> (παν-) πάμ- (“all”) + μαύρος with regressive assimilation of ‑ν- to ‑μ- and recessive accent.</div><b>πάμφθηνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πάμφθηνη, neuter πάμφθηνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dirt cheap, very cheap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παμ- (pam-) + φθηνός.</div><b>πάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nappy (UK), diaper (US)</div><b>πάνελ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) panel, committee</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) panel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English panel.</div><b>πάνθεο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πάνθεον">πάνθεον</a></div><b>πάνθεον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pantheon (all the gods of a religion; all the select members of a group)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Latin Pantheum, itself a borrowing from Ancient Greek Πάνθειον; thus a reborrowing. By surface analysis, παν- (pan-, “all”) + θεός.</div><b>πάνθηρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πάνθηρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>panther</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πάνθηρ.</div><b>πάνινα παπούτσια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. canvas shoes</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sandshoes (Scotland, Australia, New Zealand)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. plimsolls (England), pumps (N England)</div><b>πάνινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πάνινη, neuter πάνινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>made from fabric or cloth</div><b>πάνσοφα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πάνσοφος">πάνσοφος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very wisely, omnisciently</div><b>πάνσοφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πάνσοφη, neuter πάνσοφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. omniscient, all-knowing, pansophic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. very learned or wise</div><b>πάντα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>always</div>
  <div class='ex'>για <b>πάντα</b> - for ever</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πάντα, from Ancient Greek πάντα, accusative of πᾶς, from Proto-Indo-European *peh₂nts; cf. also παν (pan). Cognate with Pontic Greek πάντα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://παν">παν</a> (pan)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) panda</div>
  <div class='def'>3. side (of body)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. band</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πάντα, from Ancient Greek πάντα, accusative of πᾶς, from Proto-Indo-European *peh₂nts; cf. also παν (pan). Cognate with Pontic Greek πάντα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>det</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://πας">πας</a> (pas):; masculine accusative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://πας">πας</a> (pas):; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πᾶς.</div><b>πάντοτε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>always</div><b>πάντως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in any case, at any rate, anyway</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πάντως, an adverbial formation from πᾶς.</div><b>πάνω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>above, on, up, upstairs</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>πάνω</b> όροφος - the upper floor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐπάνω.</div><b>πάνω από το πτώμα μου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>&quot;over my dead body&quot; (absolutely not)</div><b>πάνω κάτω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. all over, everywhere, up and down</div>
  <div class='ex'>near-synonyms: πέρα δώθε (péra dóthe), παντού (pantoú)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. confused, muddled, disorderly</div>
  <div class='def'>3. roughly, approximately, about, more or less, give or take</div>
  <div class='def'>4. from top to bottom</div><b>πάπαλα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>no more, end (of something)</div><b>πάπας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πάπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pope (the bishop of Rome)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Either Inherited from Byzantine Greek πάπας, from Medieval Latin papa from Late Koine Greek πάπας, which derives from Ancient Greek πάππας, an imitative/nursery word (See French papa).
...or, a modern αντιδάνειο from Medieval Latin papa, from Late Koine Greek πάπας.
The etymology of πάπας -and παπᾶς- is discussed by Eustathius at Opuscula 38.line 58 &amp; 39.50.</div><b>πάπια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πάπιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. duck (aquatic bird)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια <b>πάπια</b> μα ποια <b>πάπια</b>, μια <b>πάπια</b> με παπιά. - A duck, but which duck? A duck with ducklings.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bed urinal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πάπια, perhaps onomatopoeic. Compare Italian papera (“duck”).</div><b>πάπλωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quilt, duvet</div><b>πάππος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πάπποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grandfather</div><b>πάπρικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πάπρικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paprika (spice powder)</div><b>πάρεργο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πάρεργα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bywork, sideline (secondary occupation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hobby</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πάρεργον.</div><b>πάριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πάριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baloney, boloney (seasoned sausage typically made from beef, pork or chicken)</div><b>πάρκινγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) parking</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) parking space</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English parking.</div><b>πάρκινσον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Parkinson&#x27;s disease, Parkinson’s (neurological disorder)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English Parkinson, from James Parkinson (1755-1824).</div><b>πάρκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πάρκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. park</div>
  <div class='ex'>αιολικό <b>πάρκο</b> - wind farm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. garden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian parco, from Old French parc.</div><b>πάροδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πάροδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. byroad, byway, bypass (secondary road)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. parodos (a side entrance to an Ancient Greek theater, affording access to the stage or orchestra)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. parodos (the ode sung by the chorus as it enters the orchestra, typically following the play&#x27;s prologue)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (literary) passage, passing (of time)</div>
  <div class='ex'>με την <b>πάροδο</b> του χρόνου - with the passage of time, with the passing of time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πάροδος, with semantic loan from French passage and Greek πέρασμα in the sense &#x27;passage/passing (of time)&#x27;.</div><b>πάσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pass (bar dividing a kitchen, where the finished dishes are passed)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pass (permission or license to pass)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. screw thread</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pitch of screw thread</div><b>πάσπαλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πασπάλη">πασπάλη</a></div><b>πάστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πάστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. layered cake with cream</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pasta (spaghetti, macaroni, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. paste</div>
  <div class='ex'>οδοντό<b>παστα</b> - toothpaste</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) nature, quality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pasta f (“cake, tart”), from Late Latin pasta f, from Ancient Greek παστά n pl, from Ancient Greek παστός, from Ancient Greek πάσσω.</div><b>πάστα φλόρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πάστα φλόρες or πάστες φλόρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crostata</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pasta frolla.</div><b>πάστορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πάστορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Protestant priest, pastor, minister</div><b>πάσχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to suffer [with <a href="bword://από">από</a> ‘from’] (to feel pain or illness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to suffer (to undergo hardship)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πάσχω.</div><b>πάτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πάτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bottom, base; seabed, riverbed, lakebed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bottom, base; of a bottle, container, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bottom, base; buttocks</div>
  <div class='def'>4. bottom, base; of boot or shoe, insole</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) bottom (of the class, test, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πάτος.</div><b>πάτσι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) quits, even</div><b>πάτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πατώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. floor of a room, elevator etc.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. floor of a building</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) wooden floor of a room</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πατώνω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>πάχνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rime, hoarfrost</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek πάχνη</div><b>πάχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πάχη or πάχια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thickness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. corpulence, fatness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾰ́χος, from πᾰχῠ́ς.</div><b>πάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://πηγαίνω">πηγαίνω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πάω</b> στην Αθήνα. - I am going to Athens.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to go, attend</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πας</b> στα ελληνικά; - Do you go to Greek (classes)?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to go, elapse (for a time)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πάνε</b> χρόνια πίσω - they go back years</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to go</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πως τα <b>πας</b>; - How are things going? How are you getting on?</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to go with, have sex with</div>
  <div class='def'>6. first-person singular dependent active of <a href="bword://πηγαίνω">πηγαίνω</a> and <a href="bword://πάω">πάω</a>; to depart, leave</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι ώρα να <b>πάμε</b>. - It is time to leave.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. first-person singular dependent active of <a href="bword://πηγαίνω">πηγαίνω</a> and <a href="bword://πάω">πάω</a>; to be going to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σήμερα θα <b>πάω</b> να ψηφίσω. - Today I&#x27;m going to vote.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πάγω, from Ancient Greek ὑπάγω. See πηγαίνω.</div><b>πέδιλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πέδιλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sandal, open shoe.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flipper</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in a motor car) pedal especially its flattened end.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. base of spread footing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέδιλον.</div><b>πέλαγο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πέλαγα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πέλαγος">πέλαγος</a></div><b>πέλαγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πελάγη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sea (Adriatic, Aegean, Ionian, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέλαγος.</div><b>πέλαο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πέλαγος">πέλαγος</a></div><b>πέλεκυς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πελέκεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. battle axe, double-headed axe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a force that punishes severely</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βαρύς θα είναι ο <b>πέλεκυς</b> τού υπουργείου για τις κλινικές που δεν συμμορφώνονται με τον κανονισμό. - The ministry&#x27;s penalty will be severe for clinics that do not conform to the regulation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέλεκυς, cognate with Sanskrit परशु, which would reflect Proto-Indo-European *peleḱús if reconstructed back to PIE. Beekes suggested a Pre-Greek origin. Compare also German Beil (“axe”) as well as Hungarian and Turkish balta, both meaning &quot;axe.&quot;</div><b>πέλμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πέλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sole (of foot)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sole (of shoe)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέλμᾰ.</div><b>πέμπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be sent</div><b>πέμπτη</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πέμπτος">πέμπτος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέμπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fifth as in fifth form, gear, grade. etc; overdrive gear.</div><b>πέμπτον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fifthly</div><b>πέμπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πέμπτη, neuter πέμπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fifth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πέμπτος.</div><b>πέμπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to send</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέμπω.</div><b>πένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πέννα">πέννα</a></div><b>πέναλτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) penalty</div><b>πέναλτυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πέναλτι">πέναλτι</a></div><b>πένθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πένθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mourning, grief, bereavement (personal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>πένθος</b> είναι μία μακροχρόνια και συναισθηματικά επώδυνη διαδικασία. - Grief is a long and emotionally painful process.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mourning (clothing and other signs of bereavement)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mourning (period of public respect paying)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek πένθος.</div><b>πέννα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέννες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plectrum (for playing guitar, bouzouki, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pen nib</div>
  <div class='def'>3. penny (subdivision of English pound)</div><b>πένσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πένσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Linesman&#x27;s or screw pliers</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pleat, tuck</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan pensa.</div><b>πέντε</b>
  <div><i>num, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) five, 5</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πέντε</b> αισθήσεις - five senses</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέντε, from Proto-Hellenic *pénkʷe, from Proto-Indo-European *pénkʷe.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek пе́нды.</div><b>πέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πέη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>penis</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>πέος</b> είναι το έξω γεννητικό όργανο του άρρενος τόσο στον άνθρωπο όσο και στα ζώα. - The penis is the external sexual organ of the male in both humans and animals.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέος.</div><b>πέπων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πέπονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://πεπόνι">πεπόνι</a></div><b>πέρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beyond</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέρᾱ.</div><b>πέρα δώθε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to and fro</div><b>πέραν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beyond</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://πέρα">πέρα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέραν.</div><b>πέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πέρατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>end</div>
  <div class='ex'>στα πέρατα της γης/του κόσμου (to the ends of the earth/the world)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πέρας.</div><b>πέρασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passage, passing (of time, weather, illness, events)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο <b>πέρασμα</b> του φασισμού από την Eυρώπη. - The passing of fascism from Europe.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passage, leg (part of a journey)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. passing, threading (ring on finger, thread through a needle)</div><b>πέργκολα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέργκολες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pergola</div>
  <div class='def'>2. arbour (UK), arbor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pergola, from Latin pergula.</div><b>πέργολα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέργολες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πέργκολα">πέργκολα</a></div><b>πέρδικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέρδικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>partridge (game bird of the genera Perdix or Alectoris)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέρδιξ.</div><b>πέρδομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to flatulate, break wind, pass wind (emit gasses from the anus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέρδομαι.</div><b>πέριξ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) around, near, about</div>
  <div class='ex'>also substantivised, as neuter plural: τα πέριξ (ta périx)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) around, near, about (in the vicinity of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>«Επικίνδυνη ζώνη»: κάθε ζώνη εντός ή/και <b>πέριξ</b> μηχανήματος, όπου ένα πρόσωπο διατρέχει κίνδυνο για την υγεία του ή την ασφάλειά του. - ‘Danger zone’: any zone in and/or around machinery in which a person is subject to a risk to their health or safety.</div><b>πέρκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέρκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perch (fish)</div><b>πέρμιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πέρμια, neuter πέρμιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Permian</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>πέρμια</b> περίοδος - the Permian period</div><b>πέρσι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πέρυσι">πέρυσι</a></div><b>πέρυσι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>last year (the year before the present one)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέρυσι, from Proto-Hellenic *péruti, from Proto-Indo-European *péruti.</div><b>πέστο</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pesto</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pesto.</div><b>πέστροφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέστροφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trout</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Bulgarian пъстърва.</div><b>πέταλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πέταλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. horseshoe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. petal</div>
  <div class='def'>3. U-shaped road</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέταλον.</div><b>πέταμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>throwing out, throwing away (the act of getting rid of something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το λάστιχο αυτό είναι εντελώς φθαρμένο και θέλει <b>πέταμα</b>. - That tyre is completely worn out and needs throwing out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πετώ + -μα (-ma). Doublet of πέταγμα</div><b>πέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lapel</div><b>πέτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rock, stone (mass or fragments)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) rock (something hard and stable)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rock (rocky hazard)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέτρα.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek пе́тра.</div><b>πέτρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πέτρινη, neuter πέτρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of stone, of rock</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πέτρινη</b> σκάλα - stone staircase</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) stone</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πέτρινη</b> καρδιά - heart of stone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πέτρινος. By surface analysis, πέτρ(α)) + -ινος (-inos).</div><b>πέτρωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετρώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rock</div>
  <div class='ex'>ιζηματογενή <b>πετρώματα</b> - sedimentary rock</div><b>πέτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rind</div>
  <div class='def'>2. skin, crust (on baked dish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pezza (“patch of cloth”).</div><b>πέττεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πέττειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>petteia (Ancient Greek board game)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέττεια.</div><b>πέφτουλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πέφτουλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) a player, a seducer</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εδώ σου λέει ο καβαλιέρε τις μίσθωνε με την ντουζίνα τις ανήλικες στην Ιταλία, δεν δικαιούμεθα κι εμείς μία ελαφρώς σιτεμένη, Δικαιούμεθα! Όχι όμως και να τη ρεζιλέψει στον κοινωνικό της περίγυρο ο <b>πέφτουλας</b>! - The Cavalier tells you that he rented young girls by the dozen in Italy, aren&#x27;t we entitled to a lightly matured one, we are! But not to have her ridiculed in the public sphere by that player!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πέφτω.</div><b>πέφτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fall, fall off, drop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to hang</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to come down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πέφτω, from Ancient Greek πίπτω with regular dissimilation [pt] &gt; [ft] and [i] &gt; [e] based on the aorist stem πεσ- (pes-).</div><b>πέφτω απ&#x27; τα σύννεφα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) come down to earth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “fall from the clouds”.</div><b>πέψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) digestion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέψις.</div><b>πήδημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πηδήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bound, leap, jump</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) sexual intercourse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πήδημα.</div><b>πήλινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πήλινη, neuter πήλινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>made of clay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πήλῐνος.</div><b>πήχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πήχεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. forearm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cubit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bar (in high jump)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πῆχυς.</div><b>πήχυς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πήχεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://πήχης">πήχης</a></div><b>πίεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pressure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pressure, stress (mental)</div><b>πίεση αίματος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιέσεις αίματος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blood pressure</div><b>πίεση του αίματος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιέσεις του αίματος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blood pressure</div><b>πίθηκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πίθηκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ape, monkey</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an uncivilised person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πίθηκος.</div><b>πίθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πίθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) large jar, cask</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for monotonous, impossible tasks) the jar of the Daughters of Danaus</div>
  <div class='ex'>o <b>πίθος</b> των Δαναΐδων - the jar of the Danaids/daughters of Danaus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πῐ́θος.</div><b>πίκολο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>piccolo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian piccolo (“small”). The Italian word for piccolo flute is ottavino.</div><b>πίκρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πίκρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bitterness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bitterness, embitterment, gall, sadness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from Ancient Greek πικραίνω.</div><b>πίλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πίλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hat, cap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πῖλος.</div><b>πίνακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πίνακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. table of values, chart, diagram</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blackboard, chalkboard, dry erase board, whiteboard</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dashboard</div>
  <div class='def'>4. matrix</div>
  <div class='def'>5. painting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πῐ́ναξ.</div><b>πίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to drink (water, coffee, beers, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to toast (drink to someone&#x27;s health)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to drink (to be a drinker of alcoholic drinks)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) to smoke tobacco, use drugs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πῑ́νω.</div><b>πίπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πίπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tobacco pipe (used for inhaling the smoke of burning tobacco)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς καπνίζει την <b>πίπα</b> του κάθε βράδυ. - Grandad smokes his pipe every evening.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, vulgar) blowjob, head (act of fellatio)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια κοπέλα μου πήρε μια <b>πίπα</b> χτες. - A girl gave me head yesterday.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pipa.</div><b>πίπερμαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) A certain mint-flavored liquor.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English peppermint.</div><b>πίσον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πίσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the plant Pisum sativum, pea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Katharevousa term for botany, as in Koine τὸ πίσον, Ancient Greek πίσος.</div><b>πίσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πίσσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tar, pitch, coal tar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. black</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Cyprus) thrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πίσσα.</div><b>πίστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πίστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. piste</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ice rink</div>
  <div class='def'>3. circus ring</div>
  <div class='def'>4. dance floor</div>
  <div class='def'>5. racecourse, race track</div>
  <div class='def'>6. track, player track (e.g., the looping track of spaces used for advancement in some board games)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French piste.</div><b>πίστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πίστεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>faith, creed, belief</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πίστις.</div><b>πίστομα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://απίστομα">απίστομα</a></div><b>πίστωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιστώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>credit</div><b>πίσω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>behind, back</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πίσω, from an aphetic form of Ancient Greek ὀπίσω.</div><b>πίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pita, pie (type of pastry, flat unleavened bread usually used for making pies)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) pie (expressing a whole available for distribution)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα μεγαλύτερο κομμάτι από την <b>πίτα</b> του εθνικού εισοδήματος - a bigger share of the pie of national income</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (slang) very drunk, stoned</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γίνομαι <b>πίτα</b> στο μεθύσι. - I become stoned from drinking.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πίτα.</div><b>πίτουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πίτουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bran (outside layer of a grain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πίτυρον.</div><b>πίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pizza</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pizza (“pizza”).</div><b>παΐδι</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) rib</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παγίδιον, from παγίς + -ῐ́δῐον, the former from Ancient Greek πάγη, owing to the ribcage&#x27;s resemblance to a metal trap.</div><b>παίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to play (with toys, to have fun)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα παιδιά <b>έπαιζαν</b> στο σοκάκι. - Children were playing in the street.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to play</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παίζουν</b> ποδόσφαιρο. - They are playing football.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to play</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έμαθε μόνη της να <b>παίζει</b> κιθάρα. - She taught herself to play the guitar.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to act, play</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Ρίτσαρντ Μπάρτον <b>έπαιξε</b> το ρόλο του. - Richard Burton played the part.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to gamble, play, bet</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παίζουν</b> στη ρουλέτα. - They play roulette.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to play, fiddle, fidget</div>
  <div class='ex'>Mην <b>παίζεις</b> τα ψιλά στην τσέπη σου. - Do not play with the change in your pocket.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. to play, move (a game, a card, a piece, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ταμπλώ όπου <b>παίζεται</b> το τάβλι χωρίζεται σε τέσσερα τμήματα. - The board on which backgammon is played has four sections.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παίζω.</div><b>παίκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παίκτες, feminine παίκτρια or παίχτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>player, sportsman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παίκτης.</div><b>παίκτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παίκτριες, masculine παίκτης or παίχτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>player, sportswoman</div><b>παίρνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be taken</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένα δάνειο <b>παίρνεται</b> εύκολα. - A loan is easily taken.</div><b>παίρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to take, get, move</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παίρνω</b> λαχανικά από το σουπερμάρκετ. - I get vegetables from the supermarket.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to gain</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παίρνω</b> 1 κιλό το μήνα. - I put on 1 kilo a month.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to call (someone) (on the telephone)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παίρνω</b> τηλέφωνο τη μητέρα μου - I make a phone [call] [to] my mother</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παίρνω, aphetic form of ἐπαίρνω, which comes from Ancient Greek ἐπαίρω with addition of a nasal infix.</div><b>παίρνω με τ&#x27; αβγά</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) to egg (to throw eggs at someone or something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι διαδηλωτές <b>πήραν</b> τον πρωθυπουργό <b>με τ’ αβγά</b> καθώς έβγαινε απ’ τ’ αμάξι. - The protesters egged the Prime Minister as he was exiting the car.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) to disapprove strongly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν το προτείνουμε αυτό, θα μας <b>πάρουν με τ’ αβγά</b>. - If we suggest that, they&#x27;ll hurl tomatoes at us.</div><b>παίρνω πίπα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) to fellate, blow, give head</div><b>παίρνω χαμπάρι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, idiomatic) to get wind of (to hear or learn about)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>πήρα χαμπάρι</b> ότι είχε πεθάνει. - I didn&#x27;t get wind that he had died.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, idiomatic) to notice, be wise to (to understand or realise something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δυστυχώς δεν τους <b>πήρε χαμπάρι</b> καθώς του έκλεβαν το σπίτι. - Unfortunately, he didn&#x27;t notice them robbing his house.</div><b>παίχτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παίχτες, feminine παίκτρια or παίχτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παίκτης">παίκτης</a> (player, sportsman)</div><b>παίχτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παίχτριες, masculine παίκτης or παίχτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παίκτρια">παίκτρια</a> (player, sportswoman)</div><b>παγάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ice cube</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πάγος + -άκι</div><b>παγίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παγίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trap, gin trap, snare</div><b>παγίδευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trapping, ensnaring, entrapment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παγιδεύ(ω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>παγετώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παγετώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glacier</div><b>παγιδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to trap, ensnare</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to frame, fit up, falsely incriminate</div><b>παγιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to consolidate, to establish, to fix, to solidify (to make stronger or more solid; to make stable or firm)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek παγιῶ + -ώνω, with semantic loan from French affermir and consolider.</div><b>παγκοσμίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>globally, worldwide, all over the world</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: διεθνώς (diethnós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παγκοσμίως. By surface analysis, παγκόσμιος + -ως (-os).</div><b>παγκοσμιοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παγκοσμιοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) globalisation (UK), globalization (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παγκόσμι(ος), “global”) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποίηση</div><b>παγκράτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pankration</div><b>παγκόσμια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>globally, all over the world</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παγκόσμιος">παγκόσμιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://παγκόσμιος">παγκόσμιος</a></div><b>παγκόσμιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παγκόσμια, neuter παγκόσμιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>global, worldwide, universal, world</div>
  <div class='ex'>Β΄ <b>Παγκόσμιος</b> Πόλεμος - World War ΙΙ</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παγκόσμιος; by surface analysis, παγ- (pag-) + κόσμος + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>παγοβούτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγοβούτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Gavia immer : great northern diver (UK), common loon (US)</div><b>παγοδρομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παγοδρομίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ice skating</div><b>παγοδρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural παγοδρόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ice skater, skater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πάγ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -δρόμος, a calque of German Eisläufer.</div><b>παγοθραυστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγοθραυστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>icebreaker</div><b>παγοκρύσταλλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παγοκρύσταλλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>icicle</div><b>παγοκύστη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ice pack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πάγος + κύστη, calque of English ice bag.</div><b>παγούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flask</div>
  <div class='def'>2. water bottle (UK), canteen (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πάγουρος, perhaps due to the shape of the container. Cognate with Cappadocian Greek παγρί.</div><b>παγωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine παγωμένη, neuter παγωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. iced, chilled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. frosty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> passive perfect participle of παγώνω, a verb without passive forms.</div><b>παγωνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παγωνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frost, freeze (intense cold weather)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γειτονιά, ο δρόμος σου στενός,
<b>Παγωνιά</b> και γκρίζος ουρανός. - Neighbourhood, your road narrow,
Frost and grey sky.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παγώνω + -ιά.</div><b>παγωτατζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παγωτατζήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) ice cream vendor, ice cream man (selling ice cream with a cart)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παγωτό + -τζής.</div><b>παγωτατζίδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγωτατζίδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ice cream parlor (US), ice cream parlour (UK), ice cream shop</div><b>παγωτιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παγωτιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ice cream maker (machine for making ice cream)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παγωτό + -ιέρα</div><b>παγωτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγωτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ice cream</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the stem παγ- (pag-)) and the suffix -ωτό, cf. -ωτός.</div><b>παγόβουνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγόβουνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>iceberg</div>
  <div class='ex'>η κορυφή του <b>παγόβουνου</b> - the tip of the iceberg</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πάγος + βουνό. Calque of English iceberg.</div><b>παγόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παγώνι">παγώνι</a></div><b>παγώνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παγώνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peacock, peahen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παώνιν, from Koine Greek παών, borrowed from Latin pavo.</div><b>παγώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to freeze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παγώνω, from παγόω, from Ancient Greek πήγνυμι. By surface analysis, πάγος + -ώνω.</div><b>παζάρεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παζαρέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>haggling, bargaining, negotiation over price, etc</div><b>παζάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παζάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an open market (organised, often periodic, trading event); fair; bazaar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. market place</div>
  <div class='def'>3. negotiation, haggling, bargaining</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پازار (pazar) / بازار (bazar), from Classical Persian بازار.</div><b>παζαρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to haggle, bargain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παζάρι n παζαρ- + -εύω</div><b>παζλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) puzzle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) jigsaw puzzle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English puzzle.</div><b>παθ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of παθητικός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of παθητική <a href="bword://φωνή">φωνή</a></div><b>παθαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, rare, transitive) to happen to (something negative), be wrong with, suffer, undergo</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>έπαθες</b>; - What happened to you? / What&#x27;s wrong with you?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be afflicted, suffer (something medical and negative)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>πάθουν</b> τα μάτια σου αν κάθεσαι τόσο κοντά! - Your eyes will suffer if you sit that close!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to be overcome (with an intense emotion, such as love, lust, etc), be moved, be deeply touched</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παθαίνω</b> για σένα! - I&#x27;m mad about you!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, intransitive) to be astounded, be flabbergasted (be intensely shocked)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έπαθα</b> όταν τον συνάντησα εκεί. - I was flabbergasted when I met him there.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παθαίνω, from Ancient Greek ἔπαθον the aorist of πάσχω.</div><b>παθαίνω αμόκ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to run amok, run amuck</div><b>παθολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pathology</div><b>παθός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(vernacular) someone who experienced, suffered or underwent something</div><b>παιδάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παιδάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a small child)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παιδ(ί), “child”) + -άκι</div><b>παιδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παιδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>child, kid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παιδίν, from Ancient Greek παιδίον, diminutive of παῖς.
Cognate with Italiot Greek pedì, Mariupol Greek пидъы́.</div><b>παιδίατρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural παιδίατροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pediatrician (US), paediatrician (UK)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French pédiatre, from Ancient Greek παῖς + ἰατρός. First attested 1886.</div><b>παιδαγωγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παιδαγωγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pedagogy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παιδαγωγία.</div><b>παιδαγωγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παιδαγωγική, neuter παιδαγωγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>educational, of education, pedagogical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek παιδαγωγικός.</div><b>παιδαγωγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural παιδαγωγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. educationalist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. guide, guru</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παιδαγωγός.</div><b>παιδαγώγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) education, erudition, learning (the product of teaching, training, etc)</div><b>παιδαριώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παιδαριώδης, neuter παιδαριώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. childish</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>παιδαριώδης</b> δικαιολογία! - What a childish excuse!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. puerile, naive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παιδάριον + -άριον) + -ώδης.</div><b>παιδεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) education (the teaching and training of the child, compare with <a href="bword://εκπαίδευση">εκπαίδευση</a>)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπουργείο Παιδείας (Ministry of Education)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) learning, culture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek παιδείᾱ.</div><b>παιδεράστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παιδεράστριες, masculine παιδεραστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paedophile (UK); pedophile (US); pædophile (obsolete)</div><b>παιδεραστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παιδεραστές, feminine παιδεράστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pedophile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pederast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek παιδεραστής.</div><b>παιδεραστία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παιδεραστίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pederasty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) sodomy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παιδεραστίᾱ.</div><b>παιδεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be pestered, chastened</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be trying hard</div><b>παιδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated) to train</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to harass, pester, chasten</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to torture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παιδεύω.</div><b>παιδιάστικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παιδιάστικη, neuter παιδιάστικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>childish, childlike, immature</div><b>παιδιατρική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural παιδιατρικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) pediatrics</div><b>παιδικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παιδικός">παιδικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun, in retail context) children&#x27;s items, childrenswear, toys</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in a child&#x27;s manner</div>
  <div class='def'>2. childishly</div><b>παιδική χαρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>playground</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παιδική + χαρά</div><b>παιδικόν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of παιδικό</div><b>παιδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παιδική, neuter παιδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. belonging or referring to a child, children&#x27;s</div>
  <div class='def'>2. childish</div><b>παιδοβούβαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παιδοβούβαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, offensive) fat child</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, derogatory, offensive) fat man</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθε αυτός ο <b>παιδοβούβαλος</b> να μας πει τι να κάνουμε και του ’πα να πάει να γαμηθεί. - That fat pig of a man tried to tell us what to do and I told him to go fuck himself.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παιδο- (paido-, “child”) + βούβαλος</div><b>παιδοκτονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παιδοκτονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>infanticide, pedicide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παιδοκτονία, equivalent to παιδο- (paido-, “child”) + -κτονία.</div><b>παιδότοπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παιδότοποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>playground (large open space for children to play on, usually having dedicated play equipment)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλα τα παιδιά της γειτονιάς μαζεύτηκαν στον <b>παιδότοπο</b>. - All the kids in the neighbourhood gathered in the playground.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παιδό- + τόπος.</div><b>παιχνίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παιχνίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. toy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. game, match</div>
  <div class='def'>3. television game show</div>
  <div class='def'>4. trick, deceit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παιχνίδι(ν)), from Koine Greek παιχνίδιον, from a diminutive of Ancient Greek παίγνιον, from παίζω.</div><b>παιχνιδιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παιχνιδιάρα, neuter παιχνιδιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>playful, sporty</div><b>παιχνιδομηχανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παιχνιδομηχανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>video game console</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παιχνίδι + μηχανή</div><b>πακέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. package (a physical container)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. package</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (slang) big disappointment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * (box): Borrowed from Italian pacchetto.</div><b>πακετάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be packed</div><b>πακετάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to packet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to pack (e.g., luggage), box up, fill, package</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πακέτο (“packet”) + -άρω (“ending for verbs”).</div><b>πακιστανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πακιστανική, neuter πακιστανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Pakistani</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Πακιστάν + -ικός.</div><b>παλάβρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παλάβρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) boastful talk, usually insubstantial (empty words)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) nonsense talk, hogwash</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ladino פאלאברה/palavra (“word; boastful talk”), from Old Spanish parabla (“word; story, rumour”), from Latin parabola (“comparison; later, speech”), from Ancient Greek παραβολή.</div><b>παλάμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παλάμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. palm, hand</div>
  <div class='def'>2. decimetre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παλάμη.</div><b>παλάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παλάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>palace</div><b>παλίρροια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παλίρροιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek *παλίρροια, from πάλιν + ῥοία.</div><b>παλαβός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλαβή, neuter παλαβό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crazy, foolish, nuts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παλαβός.</div><b>παλαιά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>once (formerly)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://παλαιός">παλαιός</a>:; feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://παλαιός">παλαιός</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural</div><b>παλαιά νορβηγικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Old Norse (Germanic language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πᾰλαιός + νορβηγικά n pl.</div><b>παλαιοαρχαιοζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλαιοαρχαιοζωική, neuter παλαιοαρχαιοζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Paleoarchean</div><b>παλαιογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παλαιογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paleography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, παλαιός + -γραφία.</div><b>παλαιοζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλαιοζωική, neuter παλαιοζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Paleozoic</div><b>παλαιολιθική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Paleolithic (Old Stone Age)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://παλαιολιθικός">παλαιολιθικός</a></div><b>παλαιολιθικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλαιολιθική, neuter παλαιολιθικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paleolithic, pertaining to the Old Stone Age</div><b>παλαιοπροτεροζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλαιοπροτεροζωική, neuter παλαιοπροτεροζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Paleoproterozoic</div><b>παλαιοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παλαιοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>antique shop, antique store</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: παλιατζίδικο n (paliatzídiko)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παλαιο- (palaio-) + -πωλείο.</div><b>παλαιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παλαιστές, feminine παλαίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wrestler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παλαιστής.</div><b>παλαιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλαιά, neuter παλαιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) long established, old, former, not modern, worn out</div><b>παλαμάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παλαμάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mooring rope</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, vulgar) penis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παλαμάρι(ον)), probably from Italian palamara, from Medieval Latin palamarius, probably from Ancient Greek παλάμη. Alternatively, inherited from Byzantine Greek παλαμάρι(ον)), from Ancient Greek παλάμη + -άριον. Other descendants of the medieval term include Italian palamaro, Turkish palamar, Albanian pallamar, Romanian pălămar.</div><b>παλαμίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παλαμίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Atlantic bonito (fish of the species Sarda sarda)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παλαμίδα, from Koine Greek παλαμίς, from Ancient Greek πηλαμύς.</div><b>παλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fight (to engage in physical combat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to wrestle (to contend, with an opponent, by grappling and attempting to throw, immobilize or otherwise defeat him)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to fight, to struggle, to wrestle (to strive for or against something)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to be fought over</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παλεύω. By surface analysis, πάλ(η)) + -εύω.</div><b>παλιά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://παλιός">παλιός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παλιός">παλιός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in the old / olden days, back then (referring to former times)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παλιά</b>, οι άνθρωποι ταξίδευαν με άλογα. - In the olden days, people used to travel by horse.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. for a long time</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γνωριζόμαστε από <b>παλιά</b>. - We have known each other for a long time.</div><b>παλιάνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παλιάνθρωποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) jerk, jackass, douchebag, dick, douche (rude, obnoxious, unpleasant, or cruel person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα τι έκανες στο πουκάμισό μου, ρε <b>παλιάνθρωπε</b>! - Look what you&#x27;ve done to my shirt, you jerk!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παλιο- (palio-, “old”) + άνθρωπος.</div><b>παλιάτσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παλιάτσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clown (performance artist associated with the circus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το τσίρκο έχει πολλούς <b>παλιάτσους</b>. - The circus has a lot of clowns.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) clown, buffoon, fool (person who acts in a silly fashion)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η υπουργός είναι ο μεγαλύτερος <b>παλιάτσος</b> της πολιτικής. - The minister is the greatest buffoon in politics.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pagliaccio.</div><b>παλιατζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παλιατζήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>junk dealer (small trader who buys old and used items for scrapping or reselling)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παλιά + -τζής.</div><b>παλιατζούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παλιατζούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. old, worn out and useless thing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. junk (collectively), old and useless things, knickknacks</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παλιατζής + -ούρα.</div><b>παλικάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παλικάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. boy, young man</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brave young man</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παλικάριν, diminutive of Ancient Greek πάλλαξ. Cognate with Pontic Greek παλληκάρι.</div><b>παλινόρθωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παλινορθώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>restoration (of a monarchy)</div><b>παλιομοδίτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλιομοδίτικη, neuter παλιομοδίτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>old-fashioned, outdated, out of fashion</div><b>παλιούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παλιούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Christ&#x27;s thorn (Paliurus spina-christi)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παλιούριον, diminutive of Ancient Greek πᾰλίουρος.</div><b>παλιρροιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλιρροιακή, neuter παλιρροιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tidal, related to the tides</div><b>παλιρροϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλιρροϊκή, neuter παλιρροϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tidal, related to the tides</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παλιρροϊκό</b> κύμα - tidal wave</div><b>παλιόγερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παλιόγεροι, feminine παλιόγρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, offensive) old bastard, old asshole, old fart (abusive term for elderly man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πες αυτού του <b>παλιόγερου</b> να πα να γαμηθεί! - Tell that old bastard to go fuck himself!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παλιό- + γέρος.</div><b>παλιόγρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παλιόγριες, masculine παλιόγερος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, offensive) old hag, old crone, old fart (abusive term for elderly woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πες αυτής της <b>παλιόγριας</b> να πα να γαμηθεί! - Tell that old bitch to go fuck herself!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παλιό- + γριά.</div><b>παλιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παλιά, neuter παλιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. old, former, long established, not modern</div>
  <div class='def'>2. worn out (of objects)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. long established, with long experience (especially in a profession) (of people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παλιός, from Ancient Greek παλαιός.</div><b>παλλάδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) palladium</div><b>παλμογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παλμογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oscilloscope</div><b>παλμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παλμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pulse, heartbeat, palpitation (of the heart)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. throb, throbbing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. oscillation, vibration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παλμός &amp; semantic loan from French oscillation.</div><b>παλούκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παλούκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stake, pole, pale (archaic)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) stinker, poser (difficult problem, test paper, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (vulgar) penis</div><b>παλτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παλτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coat, overcoat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French paletot.</div><b>παμπ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pub.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English pub.</div><b>παμφάγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παμφάγος or παμφάγα, neuter παμφάγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. omnivorous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun) omnivore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παμφάγος. By surface analysis, (παν-) παμ- (“all”) + -φάγος.</div><b>παμψυχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare, uncountable) panpsychism</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αλλά εκτός από τις μαθηματικές και φιλοσοφικές αυτές θεωρίες περί Παμψυχίας, περί Καθάρσεως, περί Ευδαιμονίας της Ψυχής. - But apart from mathematical and philosophical theories on Panpsychism, on Purification, on Happiness of the Spirit.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the French panpsychisme.</div><b>παμψυχισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) panpsychism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the French panpsychisme.</div><b>παν</b>
  <div><i>det</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://πας">πας</a> (pas)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾶν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πάντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the universe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the most important thing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. everything (usually plural)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾶν.</div><b>πανάθεμα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dammit!</div><b>πανάκριβος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανάκριβη, neuter πανάκριβο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very expensive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παν- (pan-) + ακριβ(ός)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>πανάρχαιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανάρχαιη or πανάρχαια, neuter πανάρχαιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>extremely old, very ancient</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παν- (pan-, “very”) + αρχαίος.</div><b>πανέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) panéed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French pané.</div><b>πανέλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πάνελ">πάνελ</a></div><b>πανί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πανιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cloth, fabric</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sail</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin pannus (“cloth; rag”).</div><b>πανίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fauna</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πανίς, from Πάν, the Greek god of nature.</div><b>πανίσχυρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανίσχυρη, neuter πανίσχυρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>almighty, all-powerful, supremely powerful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πανίσχυρος. By surface analysis, παν- (pan-) + ισχυρός.</div><b>πανακότα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανακότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>panna cotta</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian panna cotta.</div><b>παναμαϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παναμαϊκή, neuter παναμαϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Panamanian</div><b>πανανθρώπινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανανθρώπινη, neuter πανανθρώπινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>panhuman, universal (applying to all human beings; common to all humankind)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παν- (pan-) + ανθρώπινος, possibly a calque of English panhuman.</div><b>παναραβισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pan-Arabism</div><b>πανδαιμόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pandemonium</div><b>πανδημία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανδημίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pandemic</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η εικόνα της <b>πανδημίας</b> δεν είναι αυτή που ανέμεναν η κυβέρνηση. - The picture of the pandemic is not what the government expected.</div><b>πανδημικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανδημική, neuter πανδημικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pandemic (widespread epidemic)</div><b>πανδοχέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πανδοχείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>innkeeper</div><b>πανδοχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πανδοχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inn, guesthouse</div><b>πανελλήνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter singular</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the Greek people, the Greek nation</div>
  <div class='ex'>also spelling: Πανελλήνιο</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek Πανελλήνιον temple of Πᾰνελλήνιος Ζεύς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πανελλήνιος">πανελλήνιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πανελλήνιος">πανελλήνιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>πανελλήνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανελλήνια, neuter πανελλήνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Panhellenic</div>
  <div class='ex'>το νέο Πανελλήνιο ρεκόρ (the new Panhellenic record)</div><b>πανεπιστήμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πανεπιστήμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>university</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πανεπιστήμιο</b> Θεσσαλίας - University of Thessaly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παν- (pan-, “everything”) + επιστήμη, a calque of Medieval Latin universitas.</div><b>πανεπιστήμιον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πανεπιστήμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://πανεπιστήμιο">πανεπιστήμιο</a></div><b>πανεπιστημιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανεπιστημιακή, neuter πανεπιστημιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) university (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πανεπιστήμι(ο)) + -ακός, a calque of French universitaire.</div><b>πανευρωπαϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανευρωπαϊκή, neuter πανευρωπαϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Pan-European</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παν- (pan-, “pan-”) + Ευρώπη.</div><b>πανευτυχής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανευτυχής, neuter πανευτυχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>overjoyed (very happy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek πανευτυχής. By surface analysis, παν- (pan-) + ευτυχής.</div><b>πανεύκολα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πανεύκολος">πανεύκολος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From adjective πανεύκολος, the neutral plural becomes adjective.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very easily, extremely easily</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From adjective πανεύκολος, the neutral plural becomes adjective.</div><b>πανεύκολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανεύκολη, neuter πανεύκολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, emphatic) very easy, extremely easy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παν- (“very”) + εύκολος (“easy”).</div><b>πανηγυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to celebrate (to honour by rites, by ceremonies of joy and respect, or by refraining from ordinary business; to observe duly)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to celebrate (to engage in joyful activity in appreciation of an event)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πανηγυρίζω.</div><b>πανηγύρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πανηγύρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. saint&#x27;s day, feast day (especially in a rural setting and involving prayers, food, song and dancing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο <b>πανηγύρι</b> του Προφήτη Ηλία συμμετέσχε ολόκληρο το χωριό. - The whole village took part in the feast day for the Prophet Elijah.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) village fair, feast, fete (with food, song and dancing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν παντρεύτηκε η Σοφία, έγινε μεγάλο <b>πανηγύρι</b> μετά. - When Sophia got married, there was a great feast afterwards.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, in plural) celebrating, merrymaking, festivities</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>πανηγύρια</b> άρχισαν ακόμα και πριν τελειώσει ο αγώνας. - The festivities began even before the match ended.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, humorous) quarrel, row, fight, rumpus, barney</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχαμε πάλι <b>πανηγύρια</b> στο σπίτι μας απόψε. - We had another row at home tonight.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) fair (a large market held occasionally, mainly selling agricultural, homemade items and crafts)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς αγόρασε ένα τρακτέρ στο <b>πανηγύρι</b>. - Grandad bought a tractor at the fair.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πανήγυρις.</div><b>πανθεϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pantheism (belief that the universe is divine)</div><b>πανικοβάλλομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be panic stricken</div><b>πανικοβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cause panic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πανικ(ός) (“panic”) + -ο- (infix) + βάλλω (“attack, throw”), calque of English panic, throw into panic.</div><b>πανκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter or masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) punk, punk rock</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) punk, punk rock fan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English punk.</div><b>πανομοιότυπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πανομοιότυπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. facsimile, replica</div>
  <div class='def'>2. autotype, image</div><b>πανομοιότυπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανομοιότυπη, neuter πανομοιότυπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>identical</div><b>πανοπλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανοπλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. armour, armor (tanks, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. suit of armour, armour, armor</div><b>πανοραμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πανοραμική, neuter πανοραμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>panoramic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English panoramic. By surface analysis, πανόραμ(α)) + -ικός.</div><b>πανούκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανούκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plague</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (specifically) disease of pigs</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) plague (of locusts; of Nazis)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) troublesome woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Latin panicula (“swelling, tumour”).
The figurative sense &quot;troublesome woman&quot; has been extracted from the phrase έξω κούκλα, μέσα πανούκλα.</div><b>πανσέληνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανσέληνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>full moon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πανσέληνος, παν- (pan-) + σελήνη.</div><b>πανσές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πανσέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pansy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French pensée.</div><b>πανσιόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pension, guesthouse (small hotel)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French pension.</div><b>πανσοφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανσοφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>omniscience</div><b>πανσόφως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very wisely, omnisciently</div><b>πανταχού</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, formal) everywhere (at all places)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πανταχού</b> παρών - omnipresent, ubiquitous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πανταχοῦ, from πᾶν + -αχ- (-akh-, “palatal interfix”) + -οῦ.</div><b>πανταχού παρών</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρούσα, neuter παρόν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) omnipresent, ubiquitous (being everywhere at once)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Θεός είναι <b>πανταχού παρών</b>. - God is omnipresent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of Medieval Latin omnipraesens.</div><b>πανταχούσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανταχούσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://απανταχούσα">απανταχούσα</a></div><b>παντελόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παντελόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trousers (UK), pants (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pantaloni.</div><b>παντεσπάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παντεσπάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sponge cake, madeira cake, pound cake (US) (as an ingredient in trifles or other desserts)</div>
  <div class='ex'>«αν δεν έχει ο λαός ψωμί, ας φάει <b>παντεσπάνι</b>» - &quot;If the people have no bread, let them eat cake&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پان دیسپانیا (pandispanya), from Italian pandispagna (“bread from Spain”).</div><b>παντζάμπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Punjabi (language)</div><b>παντζάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παντζάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beetroot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish پانجار (pancar, “beetroot”), from Armenian բանջար, from Old Armenian բանջար.</div><b>παντζούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shutter (on a window)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Turkish pancur, panjur + -ι (-i). Nishanyan derives the Turkish from a French pare-jour (“light-organizing; lattice cage”, literally “adorn-daylight”); however, the French term apparently lacks attestation in texts, in addition to the term bearing strong resemblance to Persian پَنْجَرِه (panjere, “window, lattice”) (which was borrowed into Ottoman Turkish as پنجره (pencere)). Other theories consider the Turkish as borrowed from French abat-jour (“lampshade”)); however, this is phonetically less likely.</div><b>παντογνώστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παντογνώστες, feminine παντογνώστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a person who is all-knowing, omniscient</div><b>παντοδυναμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>omnipotence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παντοδύναμ(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>παντοδύναμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παντοδύναμη, neuter παντοδύναμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>almighty, omnipotent</div><b>παντοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παντοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grocer&#x27;s shop, grocer&#x27;s, grocery, grocery store</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παντοπωλεῖον, from παντο- (panto-, “pan-, all”) + πωλέω + -εῖον.</div><b>παντοπώλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παντοπώλες, feminine παντοπώλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grocer</div><b>παντοπώλισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παντοπώλισσες, masculine παντοπώλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grocer (female)</div><b>παντοτινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παντοτινή, neuter παντοτινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>everlasting</div><b>παντοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://απαντοχή">απαντοχή</a></div><b>παντού</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>everywhere, all over the place (at all places)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοιτάξαμε <b>παντού</b> αλλά δεν βρήκαμε τίποτα. - We looked everywhere but found nothing.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παντοῦ, from πᾶν + -οῦ.</div><b>παντούφλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παντούφλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a less common form of <a href="bword://παντόφλα">παντόφλα</a></div><b>παντρεμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παντρεμένες, masculine παντρεμένος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wife, spouse</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://παντρεμένος">παντρεμένος</a></div><b>παντρεμένος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παντρεμένοι, feminine παντρεμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>husband, spouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of παντρεύομαι, passive voice of παντρεύω. Also substantivised.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine παντρεμένη, neuter παντρεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>married, wed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of παντρεύομαι, passive voice of παντρεύω. Also substantivised.</div><b>παντρεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to marry, wed (to take a husband or wife)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive of παντρεύω.</div><b>παντρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to marry off, wed (arrange or take part in a marriage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to match, combine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παντρεύω or ὑπανδρεύω, from Hellenistic ὕπανδρος, from ὑπό + ἀνδρός.</div><b>παντόφλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παντόφλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slipper, mule (footwear)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pantofola.</div><b>πανωφόρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πανωφόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coat, overcoat, jacket</div><b>πανό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) banner, placard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French panneau (“sign”).</div><b>πανώλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πανώλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plague</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πανώλης.</div><b>παξιμάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παξιμάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rusk (twice-baked piece of bread)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nut (to fit to a bolt)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Koine Greek παξαμᾶς</div><b>παπάγια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παπάγιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>papaya</div><b>παπάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παπάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) ball, bollock, nut, nad (the testicle)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πράγμα που πονάει περισσότερο στον κόσμο είναι να σε κλωτσήσει κάποιος στα <b>παπάρια</b>. - The thing that hurts the most in the world is for someone to kick you in the balls.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) gizmo (an annoying gadget or item)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς λειτουργεί αυτό το <b>παπάρι</b>; - How does this bloody thing work?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παπάρα f.</div><b>παπάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παπάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. A term of address or Honorific for an Eastern Orthodox or Roman Catholic priest.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. king card in a pack of playing cards</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek παπάς / πάπας honorific for priests, or Byzantine Greek παπᾶς, from Ancient Greek πάππας. Doublet of πάπας Also see παππούς.</div><b>παπαγάλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παπαγάλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. parrot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a person who repeats uncritically what has been said by others</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pappagallo, from Byzantine Greek παπαγᾶς, from Arabic بَبَّغَاء, of onomatopoeic origin.</div><b>παπαγαλία</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>by rote</div><b>παπαγαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to parrot, repeat parrot fashion</div><b>παπαδίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παπαδίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tit (especially great tit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) ladybird</div><b>παπαδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παπαδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The wife of a priest.</div>
  <div class='ex'>formal synonym: πρεσβυτέρα (presvytéra)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παπαδιά, from Koine Greek παπαδία, with synizesis ία &gt; ιά. By surface analysis, παπάς + -ιά.</div><b>παπαρδέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παπαρδέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chatter, twaddle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a type of pasta</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) popcorn</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (derogatory) a talkative woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pappardella (“a type of pasta; an argument”).</div><b>παπαρούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παπαρούνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poppy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Latin papaver. Compare Romanian paparoană, paparuna and Sicilian paparina.</div><b>παπιγιόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) bow tie, bowtie</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) papillon (breed of dog)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French papillon (“butterfly”).</div><b>παπιόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παπιγιόν">παπιγιόν</a></div><b>παπλωματοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παπλωματοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>duvet cover, quilt cover</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the παπλωματ- stem of πάπλωμα + -ο- (-o-) + -θήκη.</div><b>παπουτσής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παπουτσήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) shoemaker, cobbler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παπούτσι.</div><b>παπουτσοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shoe storage, rack, cabinet, organizer etc.</div><b>παπούτσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παπούτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shoe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) aubergine, eggplant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پاپوش, from Classical Persian پاپوش.</div><b>παππάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παππάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymological spelling of <a href="bword://παπάς">παπάς</a>.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See παπάς</div><b>παππούς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παππούδες, feminine γιαγιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grandfather, grandad, grandpa (father of one&#x27;s parents)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>παππούς</b> μου πολέμησε στον Β’ Παγκόσμιο Πόλεμο. - My grandpa fought in World War II.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) gramps, pops, oldtimer (to address any old man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρόσεξε, <b>παππού</b>, μη σε πατήσει κανένα αμάξι καθώς περνάς το δρόμο. - Careful, gramps, that a car doesn&#x27;t run you over as you cross the road.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παππούς, from Ancient Greek παππίας, diminutive form of πάππας.</div><b>παρ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παρά">παρά</a></div><b>παρ&#x27; ολίγο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παραλίγο">παραλίγο</a></div><b>παρ&#x27; όλ&#x27; αυτά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of παρ’ <a href="bword://όλα">όλα</a> <a href="bword://αυτά">αυτά</a></div><b>παρ&#x27; όλα αυτά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nevertheless, despite this, in spite of this</div><b>παρά</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with accusative) despite, in spite of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with accusative) less (minus; not including)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with accusative) less (minus; not including); to (preceding the next whole hour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι έξι <b>παρά</b> είκοσι πέντε. - It&#x27;s twenty-five to six</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρά</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. but</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (comparative) than</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρά</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://παράς">παράς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>παρά θίν&#x27; αλός</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) by the sea, by the seaside</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρά θῖν’ ἁλὸς, from Homer&#x27;s Iliad.</div><b>παράβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. offence, transgression, violation, infraction, contravention (breach of law, rules, agreements, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. part of theatrical performance</div><b>παράβλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραβλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fender</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek παράβλημα, from Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰβάλλω.</div><b>παράβυστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, obsolete) secret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράβυστος.</div><b>παράγκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παράγκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shack, shanty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian baracca.</div><b>παράγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prepared from, be derived from</div><b>παράγοντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παράγοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>factor (that which contributes to a result)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ψυχολογικός <b>παράγοντας</b> παίζει σπουδαίο ρόλο στην προετοιμασία των αθλητών. - The psychological factor plays an important part in the preparation of athletes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παράγ(ων)) + -οντας (-ontas).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>active present participle of <a href="bword://παράγω">παράγω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>παράγραφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παράγραφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. paragraph</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clause (of document)</div><b>παράγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to produce, generate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράγω. Morphologically παρ- (par-) + άγω.</div><b>παράδειγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραδείγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. example, instance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. paradigm</div>
  <div class='def'>3. warning</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek παράδειγμα.</div><b>παράδεισος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παράδεισοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paradise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράδεισος.</div><b>παράδοξο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://παράδοξος">παράδοξος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://παράδοξος">παράδοξος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παράδοξα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. paradox (a counterintuitive conclusion or outcome)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. paradox</div><b>παράδοξος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράδοξη, neuter παράδοξο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strange, paradoxical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράδοξος.</div><b>παράδοση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραδόσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. delivery (of goods, parcels, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tradition</div>
  <div class='def'>3. surrender</div>
  <div class='def'>4. lecture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek παράδοσις + modern ending -ση (-si), thus παρά- + δόση f.
The Triantafyllidis Dictionary suggests that the sense of &quot;tradition&quot; is influenced by semantic loan from French tradition and German Überlieferung, which are themselves from traditio, which in had its sense of &quot;tradition&quot; influenced by the Koine Greek παράδοσις</div><b>παράθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. apposition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. quotation</div><b>παράθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ending, desinence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. affix</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of New Latin affixum or French affixe, on the basis of επίθημα.</div><b>παράθυρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παράθυρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. window</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) window, view</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παράθυρο</b> στον κόσμο - window on the world</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) loophole</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα <b>παραθυράκια</b> του νόμου - loopholes of the law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παράθυρον, from Koine Greek παράθυρος, from Ancient Greek παρα- (para-) + θυρίς.</div><b>παράκαμψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρακάμψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>detour, diversion, bypass</div><b>παράκλητος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παράκλητοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comforter (one who comforts)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰ́κλητος</div><b>παράκτιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράκτια, neuter παράκτιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>littoral</div><b>παράλειψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραλείψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. omission</div>
  <div class='def'>2. neglect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράλειψις.</div><b>παράλια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://παράλιος">παράλιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παράλιος">παράλιος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coastal areas, coasts, coastline</div><b>παράλιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράλια, neuter παράλιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coastal</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: παραθαλάσσιος (parathalássios), παράκτιος (paráktios)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παράλιος, from παρα- (para-) + ἅλς + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>παράλληλα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παράλληλος">παράλληλος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. parallelly; parallelwise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. at the same time</div><b>παράλληλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράλληλη, neuter παράλληλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parallel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from παρά + ἄλληλος, along each other</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παράλληλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parallel, line of latitude</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from παρά + ἄλληλος, along each other</div><b>παράλογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράλογη, neuter παράλογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>illogical, unreasonable, absurd</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράλογος. By surface analysis, παρα- (para-) + λόγος.</div><b>παράλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραλύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, literally) paralysis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek παράλυ(σις)) + -ση (-si) &amp; semantic loan from French paralysie. Doublet of παραλυσία.</div><b>παράμεσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παράμεσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ring finger</div><b>παράμετρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παράμετροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parameter</div><b>παράνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παράνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. paranoia</div>
  <div class='def'>2. insanity</div>
  <div class='def'>3. suspiciousness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek παράνοια.</div><b>παράνομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (obsolete) surname</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) nickname</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παράνομος">παράνομος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>illegally</div><b>παράνομος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράνομη, neuter παράνομο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. illegal, illicit, unlawful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. underground</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράνομος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παράνομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>outlaw, a person denied legal rights.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράνομος.</div><b>παράνυμφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural παράνυμφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bridesmaid, best man</div><b>παράνυφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural παράνυφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παράνυμφος">παράνυμφος</a></div><b>παράξενα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παράξενος">παράξενος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παράξενος">παράξενος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://παράξενος">παράξενος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strangely, oddly, weirdly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν ένας τύπος εκεί που μας κοίταζε <b>παράξενα</b>. - There was a guy there who was looking at us strangely.</div><b>παράξενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράξενη, neuter παράξενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of things) strange, weird, odd, bizarre (deviating from the normal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο καιρός είναι πολύ <b>παράξενος</b> αυτές τις μέρες. - The weather is very strange these days.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of people) strange, weird, odd, peculiar, whacko, oddball (having an unusually strange character or behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Από τότε που έγινε το ατύχημα, έχει γίνει πολύ <b>παράξενος</b>. - Since the accident happened, he&#x27;s become very weird.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παράξενος, equivalent to παρά- + ξένος.</div><b>παράπονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παράπονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>complaint, grievance</div><b>παράπτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραπτώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minor crime, misdeed, misdemeanour, transgression</div><b>παράρτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραρτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. appendix, annexe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. branch (location of an organization or business)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek παράρτημα and a semantic loan from French supplément or annexe. From παραρτάω, παρ- (par-) + ἀρτάω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>παράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) money (dosh, dough, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پاره (pare, para), from Classical Persian پاره.</div><b>παράσημο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παράσημα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. order, medal, decoration (award, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. medal (physical object)</div><b>παράσιτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παράσιτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. parasite</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) parasite, freeloader</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivization of the Ancient Greek παράσιτος by calque of the French parasite.</div><b>παράσπονδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράσπονδη, neuter παράσπονδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perfidious, treacherous, wrong-doing</div><b>παράσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. performance</div>
  <div class='ex'>θεατρική <b>παράσταση</b> - theatrical performance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. presentation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. representation</div>
  <div class='ex'>αριθμητική <b>παράσταση</b> - arithmetical representation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παράστασις, from Ancient Greek παρίστημι, from παρα- (para-) + ῐ̔́στημῐ.</div><b>παράταιρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράταιρη, neuter παράταιρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mismatched; discrepant; incongruous; odd</div><b>παράταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρατάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. extension, prolongation (enlargement in time)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παράταση</b> της ζωής - life extension</div>
  <div class='def'>2. extension (allowance of further time granted to a debtor to pay a debt)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. extra time, overtime</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παράτασις with -ση (-si) suffix and semantic loan from French prolongation (“extra time, overtime”).</div><b>παράτριμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) gall (sore caused by chafing of the skin)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) nappy rash</div><b>παράφωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παράφωνη, neuter παράφωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. out of tune, dissonant, discordant</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια παλιά <b>παράφωνη</b> κιθάρα. - An old out-of-tune guitar.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of a singer) out of tune, cacophonous</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί την αφήνεις να τραγουδήσει, είναι <b>παράφωνη</b>; - Why do you let her sing, she&#x27;s out of tune?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παραφωνία.</div><b>παρέα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρέες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>company of friends, fellowship, party</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ladino parea, ultimately from Late Latin pariculus, diminutive of Latin pār. Compare Spanish pareja (“couple”).</div><b>παρέκκλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρέκκλισες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. deviation, departure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aberration</div><b>παρέλαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρελάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. march-past, parade</div>
  <div class='def'>2. parade (non-military)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. march (protest march)</div><b>παρέλλαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) parade</div><b>παρέμβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρεμβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intervention (the action of intervening; interfering in some course of events)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παρεμβαίνω + -ση (-si), a calque of French intervention.</div><b>παρένθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρενθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parenthesis (either or each of the brackets enclosing a word or words; or the enclosed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>σε παρένθεση
in parenthesis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρένθεσις.</div><b>παρέρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to go (pass) by, glide away</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of time) to elapse</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι γενιές έρχονται και <b>παρέρχονται</b>. - Generations come and go.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to omit, suppress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾰρέρχομαι. Morphologically, from παρα- (para-, “by, near”) + έρχομαι.</div><b>παρέσυρα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular simple past active of παρασέρνω</div>
  <div class='def'>2. first-person singular imperfect active of παρασύρω</div>
  <div class='def'>3. first-person singular simple past active of παρασύρω</div><b>παρέσυρε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular simple past active of παρασέρνω</div>
  <div class='def'>2. third-person singular imperfect active of παρασύρω</div>
  <div class='def'>3. third-person singular simple past active of παρασύρω</div><b>παρέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to give, provide</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to offer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek παρέχω, from παρά + ἔχω</div><b>παραέβαλα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of παραβάζω</div><b>παραίσθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραισθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. illusion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hallucination</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παραίσθησις with semantic loan from French hallucination. By surface analysis, παρ- (par-) + αίσθηση.</div><b>παραίτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραιτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. resignation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abdication</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παραίτησις.</div><b>παραβάλλομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be compared, collated</div><b>παραβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to compare</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to parallel, collate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰβᾰ́λλω. By surface analysis, παρά- + βάλλω.</div><b>παραβάλτε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second-person plural simple past perfective imperative of παραβάζω</div><b>παραβάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παραβάτες, feminine παραβάτιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. offender, transgressor, violator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. apostate (as a pejorative nickname for the Roman emperor Julian)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ιουλιανός ο <b>Παραβάτης</b> - Julian the Apostate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παραβάτης.</div><b>παραβίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραβιάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>act or result of a violation, breach, infringement</div><b>παραβατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραβατική, neuter παραβατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. delinquent (failing in or neglectful of a duty or obligation; guilty of a misdeed or offense)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. infringing, offending, transgressing, unlawful (behaviour, conduct)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παραβατικός. By surface analysis, παραβάτ(ης)) + -ικός.</div><b>παραβιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to violate, breach, force, invade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰβῐάζω, from πᾰρᾰ- + βῐάζω.</div><b>παραβλέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be overlooked, ignored intentionally</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to met with someone too often</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to was seen very well</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Sense overlook from Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰβλέπω.</div><b>παραβλέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to turn a blind eye to, shut one&#x27;s eyes to (to deliberately overlook)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to see or meet very often</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to see very well (in the phrase)</div>
  <div class='ex'>βλέπω και <b>παραβλέπω</b> - I see very well indeed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παραβλέπω (Hellenistic sense: overlook).</div><b>παραβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. parabola</div>
  <div class='def'>2. parable</div>
  <div class='def'>3. comparison</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek παραβολή.</div><b>παραβύστω</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, dative, obsolete) in secret; monotonic script of <a href="bword://παραβύστῳ">παραβύστῳ</a></div><b>παραβύστῳ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dative, obsolete) in secret</div>
  <div class='ex'>Monotonic spelling: παραβύστω (paravýsto)</div><b>παραγάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>longline</div><b>παραγίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, literally) to become too much of</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παράγινε</b> το κακό! - The bad [situation] has become too much! [to bear]</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρα- (para-, “too much, over-”) + γίνομαι. The ancient παραγί(γ)νομαιnomai) has a different meaning: stand beside someone.</div><b>παραγγέλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to order, pass on, send a message</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰγγέλλω. Morphologically, from παρα- (“through, near”) + αγγέλλω (“announce”).</div><b>παραγγέλνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παραγγέλλω">παραγγέλλω</a></div><b>παραγγελία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραγγελίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. message, word</div>
  <div class='def'>2. order; request a product or service</div>
  <div class='def'>3. order; the product or service delivered</div><b>παραγιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παραγιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. A young male apprentice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. An adopted son or foster son</div>
  <div class='def'>3. A male live-in servant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρά + γιος (gios, “son”)</div><b>παραγκούπολη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραγκουπόλεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slum, shantytown</div><b>παραγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be struck out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be written in excess</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to be written in with exaggeration</div><b>παραγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to erase, strike out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to write too much</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to write for too long (expresses fatigue)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to exaggerate, overstate when writing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned, from Hellenistic Koine Greek παραγράφω (“change an entry”). Morphologically, from παρα- (“beside”) + γράφω (“write”).</div><b>παραγραφήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of παραγραφήκαν, third-person plural simple past of <a href="bword://παραγράφομαι">παραγράφομαι</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://παραγράφω">παραγράφω</a> (at Etymology 1)</div><b>παραγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραγωγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. production (the act of producing, making or creating something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. production (the act of being produced)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. production (the total amount produced)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. manufacturing</div>
  <div class='def'>5. deduction (a process of reasoning that moves from the general to the specific, in which a conclusion follows necessarily from the premises presented)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παραγωγή with semantic loan from French production. By surface analysis, παρα- (para-) + αγωγή.</div><b>παραγωγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραγωγική, neuter παραγωγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fertile, fecund</div>
  <div class='def'>2. productive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παραγωγ(ή)) + -ικός, a calque of French productif and déductif and English derivational.</div><b>παραγωγικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) productivity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παραγωγικός + -ότητα, a calque of French productivité. First attested 1868.</div><b>παραγωγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>producer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παραγωγός, from παράγω.</div><b>παραδάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>money, dosh, dough etc.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παραδ (parad, “money”) + -άκι</div><b>παραδέχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to admit, accept, concede, allow (confess to be true or to be the case, typically with reluctance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επιτέλους <b>παραδέχτηκε</b> ότι την αγαπάει. - At last he admitted that he loved her.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to recognise (as able or skilled), take one&#x27;s hat off to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη μαγειρική τον <b>παραδέχομαι</b>· είναι αξεπέραστος. - As far as cooking is concerned, I take my hat off to him; he&#x27;s unsurpassed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παραδέχομαι, equivalent to παρα- (para-) + δέχομαι.</div><b>παραδίδομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be delivered (of things, lectures, lessons)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to surrender</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to indulge in</div><b>παραδίδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to hand over, transfer, entrust</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to hand over, surrender</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to give (a lesson, transfer knowledge)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παραδίδω, from Ancient Greek παραδίδωμι.</div><b>παραδίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://παραδίδω">παραδίδω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. to give too much (and spoil)</div><b>παραδείγματος χάριν</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for example, e.g., eg</div><b>παραδεισένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραδεισένια, neuter παραδεισένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Edenic (of or suggesting Eden, the paradise of the Bible)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. paradisiac, paradisiacal, paradisical (of or resembling paradise)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) heavenly (strongly or sublimely beautiful or pleasurable)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παράδεισ(ος)) + -ένιος.</div><b>παραδεκτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραδεκτή, neuter παραδεκτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>acceptable, accepted, admissible (worthy, decent, sure of being accepted)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παραδεκτό</b> επιχείρημα - accepted argument</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παραδεκτός, equivalent to παρα- (para-) + δεκτός.</div><b>παραδοξότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραδοξότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) paradoxicalness, paradoxicality</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) strangeness, oddity, oddness</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (countable) oddity (an odd or strange thing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παραδοξότητα, accusative of παραδοξότης. By surface analysis, παράδοξ(ος)) + -ότητα.</div><b>παραδοσιακά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://παραδοσιακός">παραδοσιακός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traditionally</div><b>παραδοσιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραδοσιακή, neuter παραδοσιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traditional</div><b>παραδοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραδοχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acceptance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. confession</div><b>παραδόξως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paradoxically</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παραδόξως. By surface analysis, παράδοξ(ος)) + -ως (-os).</div><b>παραδόπιστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραδόπιστη, neuter παραδόπιστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>money-loving, money-worshipping; corrupt or venal.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παράς + πίστη + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix).</div><b>παραθύρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραθύρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) window</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άνοιξ’ <b>το παραθύρι</b> σου
ξανθέ βασιλικέ μου
και με γλυκό χαμόγελο
μια καληνύχτα πες μου - Open your window,
My golden basil,
And with a sweet smile,
Say goodnight to me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παράθυρο + -ι (-i)</div><b>παραιτούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to resign, quit (job or position)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to abandon, quit (task or activity)</div><b>παρακάμπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be skipped, overriden</div><b>παρακάμπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bypass, skip, override</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παρακάμπτω. By surface analysis, παρα- (“near”) + κάμπτω (“bend”).</div><b>παρακάτω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>further down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρα- (para-) + κάτω.</div><b>παρακέντηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paracentesis</div><b>παρακαλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παρακαλώ">παρακαλώ</a></div><b>παρακαλώ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. please</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φέρε μου, σε <b>παρακαλώ</b>, έναν καφέ. - Bring me some coffee, please.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. you&#x27;re welcome, it&#x27;s nothing (response to being thanked)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (interrogative) can I help you?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (interrogative) hello? excuse me?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek παρακαλῶ, ancient sense: &quot;invite&quot;, contracted form of Ancient Greek παρακαλέω. By surface analysis, παρα- (para-) + καλώ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) request, beg, plead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek παρακαλῶ, ancient sense: &quot;invite&quot;, contracted form of Ancient Greek παρακαλέω. By surface analysis, παρα- (para-) + καλώ.</div><b>παρακεί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a bit further</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρα- and (ε)κεί.</div><b>παρακείμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(perfect, present) perfect, present perfect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰκείμενος. Present participle of παράκειμαι, an ancient middle voice verb. The noun is a substantivization in Hellenistic Koine with ellipsis of χρόνος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine παρακείμενη, neuter παρακείμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) lie beside or before, left or right</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χορήγηση οικοδομικής άδειας για την αποθήκη και τον <b>παρακείμενο</b> χώρο. - Planning permission for the storeroom and the neighbouring area.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰκείμενος. Present participle of παράκειμαι, an ancient middle voice verb. The noun is a substantivization in Hellenistic Koine with ellipsis of χρόνος.</div><b>παρακινώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to egg on, to encourage, to urge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to motivate (of circumstances: to animate; to propel; to cause to take action)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρακινῶ, contracted form of παρακινέω, with semantic loan from French inciter. By surface analysis, παρα- (para-) + κινώ.</div><b>παρακλινικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρακλινική, neuter παρακλινικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paraclinical</div><b>παρακμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρακμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decay, decadence, decline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek παρακμή.</div><b>παρακοιμάμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to oversleep (to sleep for longer than planned)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Πέτρος <b>παρακοιμήθηκε</b> και έχασε την πτήση του. - Peter overslept and missed his flight.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to sleep in (to sleep for longer than necessary)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παρακοιμήθηκες</b>! Μεσημέριασε πια! - You slept in! It&#x27;s already past noon!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρα- (para-, “expressing excessive”) + κοιμάμαι. Of different sense are the Mediaeval παρακοιμοῦμαι and the Hellenistic παρακοιμῶμαι, contracted form of παρακοιμάομαι, whose participle παρακοιμώμενος was in use as an official&#x27;s title in the Byzantine Empire.</div><b>παρακοιμούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παρακοιμάμαι">παρακοιμάμαι</a></div><b>παρακολουθάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παρακολουθώ">παρακολουθώ</a></div><b>παρακολουθιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of παρακολουθούμαι</div>
  <div class='ex'>passive of παρακολουθάω (parakoloutháo)</div><b>παρακολουθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to watch, spectate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to surveille, keep watch on</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to follow, trail</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρακολουθῶ, from παρα- (para-, “para-, beside”) + ἀκολουθῶ; for the latter component, see ακολουθώ.</div><b>παρακολούθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρακολουθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. surveillance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spectating</div>
  <div class='def'>3. monitoring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρακολούθησις, with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>παρακούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to mishear, hear wrongly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to disobey</div><b>παρακωλύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be hindered</div><b>παρακωλύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to hinder, impede, obstruct, prevent</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το εμπόδιο <b>παρακωλύει</b> τη διέλευση των αυτοκινήτων. - The barrier prevents the passing of cars (cars from passing).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρα- + κωλύω (“hinder”).</div><b>παρακόρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρακόρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. An adopted daughter or foster daughter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. A female live-in servant</div>
  <div class='ex'>[...] πιοτὸ θεϊκό, ποὺ μὲς στὸ σπίτι / σκλάβα καμιὰ δὲν τό ’ξερε μήτε ἄλλη <b>παρακόρη</b> / ἐξὸν αὐτός, τὸ ταίρι του καὶ μιὰ ἐπιστάτρα μόνη. - … a drink of the gods, of which in the household / not a single slave nor housemaid knew of it / other than he, his partner and one butler only.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρά + κόρη</div><b>παραλήγουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραλήγουσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>penult (next to last syllable of a word)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰλήγουσᾰ. By surface analysis, παρά + λήγουσα.</div><b>παραλήπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παραλήπτες, feminine παραλήπτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>recipient, consignee, receiver</div><b>παραλήπτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραλήπτριες, masculine παραλήπτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>recipient, consignee, receiver</div><b>παραλήρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραληρήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. delirium, brain fever</div>
  <div class='def'>2. frenzy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παραληρώ + -μα (-ma)</div><b>παραλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beach, seashore</div>
  <div class='def'>2. seaside</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παραλίᾱ, from παρα- (para-) + ἅλς.</div><b>παραλίγο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(used with the subjunctive tense) nearly, almost, near miss, close but no cigar, hair&#x27;s breadth, just (used to indicate something that almost or nearly happened)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παραλίγο</b> να τρακάρουμε. - We almost crashed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρά + λίγο</div><b>παραλαβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παραλαμβάνω">παραλαμβάνω</a></div><b>παραλαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to receive, take delivery of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to collect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek παραλαμβάνω.</div><b>παραλείπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to omit, leave out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to neglect</div><b>παραλιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραλιακή, neuter παραλιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>littoral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παραλί(α)) + -ακός.</div><b>παραλλαγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραλλαγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. variant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. variation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παραλλαγές Ντιαμπέλλι - Diabelli Variations</div><b>παραλλακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parallactic</div><b>παραλληλόγραμμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραλληλόγραμμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parallelogram</div><b>παραλογίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be out of one&#x27;s mind, lose one&#x27;s mind (lose all sense of logic)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην τον ακούς, <b>παραλογίζεται</b> λόγω θλίψης. - Don&#x27;t listen to him, he’s out of his mind due to grief.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to rant, rave, talk through one&#x27;s hat (to invent or fabricate facts)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παραλογίζεσαι</b> μ’ αυτά που λες, δεν το καταλάβαινες; - You&#x27;re talking through your hat, can&#x27;t you understand it?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰλογῐ́ζομαι (“cheat; reason falsely”).</div><b>παραλυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραλυτική, neuter παραλυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paralysed (UK), paralyzed (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παραλυτικός.</div><b>παραλύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to paralyse (UK), paralyze (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to bring to a halt</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to be numb, be paralysed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek παραλύω, as in the passive form παραλύομαι &amp; semantic loan from French paralyser. From παρα- (para-) + λύω.</div><b>παραμένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to remain, stay (at a location)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to remain, stay (unchanged)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stay, remain at a place for too long, more than expected</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek παραμένω, from παρά + μένω</div><b>παραμεθαύριο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>day after day after tomorrow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρα- (para-) + μεθαύριο.</div><b>παραμερίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to step aside</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to push, place aside</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) (figuratively) brush aside, set aside</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παραμερίζω.</div><b>παραμικρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραμικρή, neuter παραμικρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smallest, slightest, minutest, tiniest</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχω την <b>παραμικρή</b> αμφιβολία. - I haven&#x27;t the slightest doubt.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρα- (para-) + μικρός.</div><b>παραμονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abidance, staying, remaining</div>
  <div class='def'>2. eve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παραμονή, from παραμένω.</div><b>παραμύθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παραμύθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fairy tale (folktale featuring fantasy characters)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αγοράκι πρέπει να ακούσει <b>παραμύθι</b> να κοιμηθεί. - The boy needs to be told a fairy tale to go to sleep.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) fairy tale, tall tale (unrealistic story)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας έλεγε <b>παραμύθια</b> για τα μέρη που δήθεν είδε. - He was telling us fairy tales about the places he supposedly saw.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παραμύθιν, from Ancient Greek παραμύθιον + μῦθος + -ιον (-ion)).</div><b>παρανομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρανομίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crime, wrongdoing, transgression</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unlawfulness, lawlessness</div><b>παρανομιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to nickname, to give someone a nickname</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Greek παρα- (para-, “more, substitute”) + ονομάζω.</div><b>παρανοϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρανοϊκή, neuter παρανοϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paranoid</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρανοϊκοί, feminine παρανοϊκή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>person with paranoia</div><b>παρανοώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to misunderstand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρανοῶ, contracted form of παρανοέω.</div><b>παραξενεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (active, transitive) to seem strange to, to surprise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to find it strange, to be surprised</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (active, intransitive) to become strange</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παράξεν(ος)) + -εύω.</div><b>παραξενιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραξενιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a person) oddness, weirdness, eccentricity, quirk, idiosyncrasy, oddity (state of being different from the usual in behaviour; something odd)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι οι άνθρωποι έχουν <b>παραξενιές</b> όταν γεράσουν. - All people have quirks when they get old.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παράξενος + -ιά.</div><b>παραπάνω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. above, over, higher up (at or to a higher point)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη σταματήσεις στον δεύτερο όροφο, ανέβα <b>παραπάνω</b>. - Don&#x27;t stop on the second floor, go up above.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. beyond, further (at a more distant point)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σπίτι του είναι σ’ ένα στενό <b>παραπάνω</b>. - His house is up a lane beyond.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. more, more than, over (in excess of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή πρέπει να είναι <b>παραπάνω</b> από εξήντα τώρα. - She must be above sixty now.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek παραπάνω, equivalent to παρα- (para-) + πάνω.</div><b>παραπέμπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be referred</div><b>παραπέμπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to refer (direct to a source for help or information)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παραπέμπω.</div><b>παραπαίω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stagger</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to be unsteady, at the point of collapsing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παραπαίω (“hit wrongly; be foolish, loose my mind”). Morphologically, from παρα- (para-) + παίω.</div><b>παραπλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sail near, by</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παραπλεύσαμε</b> το ακρωτήριο. - We sailed by the cape.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παραπλέω. Morphologically, from παρα- (para-, “beside, near”) + πλέω.</div><b>παραπλανητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παραπλανητική, neuter παραπλανητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misleading</div><b>παραπλανώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to mislead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παραπλανῶ, active voice of παραπλανῶμαι. By surface analysis, παρα- (para-) + πλανώ.</div><b>παραπομπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραπομπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. referral (the act or process of transferring someone or something to another, of sending by reference, or referring)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reference (the act of referring)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. reference, citation (a short written identification of a previously published work which is used as a source)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εκκρεμεί <b>παραπομπή</b> - citation needed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παραπομπή with semantic loan from French renvoi.</div><b>παραπονιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to complain, whine, grumble</div><b>παραπονούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παραπονιέμαι">παραπονιέμαι</a></div><b>παραπόταμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παραπόταμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tributary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, παρα- (para-, “beside, aside”) + ποταμός. Compare German Nebenfluss.</div><b>παρασημοφορήθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of παρασημοφορούμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://παρασημοφορώ">παρασημοφορώ</a></div><b>παρασημοφορήθηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of παρασημοφορούμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://παρασημοφορώ">παρασημοφορώ</a></div><b>παρασημοφορηθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular dependent of παρασημοφορούμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://παρασημοφορώ">παρασημοφορώ</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive nonfinite form of παρασημοφορούμαι</div><b>παρασημοφορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to decorate (to honour by providing a medal, ribbon, or other adornment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παράσημ(ο)) + -ο- (-o-) + -φορώ.</div><b>παρασιτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρασιτική, neuter παρασιτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) parasitic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρασιτικός. By surface analysis, παράσιτος + -ικός.</div><b>παρασκήνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρασκήνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in plural, often) backstage</div>
  <div class='ex'>στο <b>παρασκήνιο</b> - behind the scenes, behind the curtain, backstage, offstage; (by extension) in the background</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) behind-the-scenes dealings, backroom dealings</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παρασκήνιον with semantic loan from French coulisses. Compare English parascenium.</div><b>παρασκευάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prepared, produced</div><b>παρασκευάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to prepare</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to make, concoct</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to cook</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρασκευάζω. By surface analysis, παρα- (para-) + σκευάζω.</div><b>παρασκευάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρασκευάστριες, masculine παρασκευαστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. laboratory technician or assistant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. preparer</div><b>παρασκευή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρασκευές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. preparation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. for &quot;Friday&quot;, see <a href="bword://Παρασκευή">Παρασκευή</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πᾰρᾰσκευή. Morphologically παρα- (para-) + -σκευή.</div><b>παρασκευαστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρασκευαστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laboratory, preparation room</div><b>παρασκευαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρασκευαστές, feminine παρασκευάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. laboratory technician or assistant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. preparer</div><b>παρασκεύασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρασκευάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. preparation (that which is prepared)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. preparation (that which is prepared); preparation (a substance, especially a remedy, that is prepared)</div><b>παρασπονδυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paraspinal</div><b>παραστάθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular simple past of παραστέκομαι</div>
  <div class='def'>2. first-person singular simple past of παρασταίνομαι</div><b>παραστέκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>t<a href="bword://ο">ο</a> help, support</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου <b>παραστάθηκε</b> στις δύσκολες στιγμές μου. - He/She supported me in difficult times of mine.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παραστέκω: παρα- (para-) + στέκω. Literally, “to be at the side of someone”.</div><b>παραστένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obsolete spelling of <a href="bword://παρασταίνω">παρασταίνω</a> (demotic spelling of 19th-20th century)</div><b>παρασταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παριστάνω">παριστάνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρίστημι with -αίνω metaplasm of the imperfective stem παραστησ-.</div><b>παρασύρει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular dependent active of παρασέρνω</div>
  <div class='def'>2. active nonfinite form of παρασέρνω</div>
  <div class='def'>3. third-person singular present active indicative of παρασύρω</div>
  <div class='def'>4. third-person singular dependent active of παρασύρω</div>
  <div class='def'>5. active nonfinite form of παρασύρω</div><b>παρατάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to abandon, desert, give up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to drop, dump, quit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε θα τα <b>παρατήσω</b>! - I will not quit!</div><b>παρατήρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρατηρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. comment, observation, remark</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα συνοψίσω τις <b>παρατηρήσεις</b> μου για τον Όμιλο των 8 με μια <b>παρατήρηση</b> γενικότερης φύσεως. - I am going to conclude my remarks concerning the G8 with an observation of a more general nature.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rebuke, reprimand</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tου έκαναν <b>παρατήρηση</b>, γιατί ήταν εκπρόθεσμος. - He was reprimanded because he was out of time.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. surveillance, reconnaissance, systematic monitoring of the enemy&#x27;s positions and movements</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το στρατιωτικό αεροπορία εξελίχτηκε αρχικά σε μέσο ως αποστολές <b>παρατήρησης</b> των βολών του πυροβολικού και πτήσεις αναγνώρισης (Νικολουδάκης, 2015). - Military aviation initially evolved into a means of artillery surveillance missions and reconnaissance flights (Nikoloudakis, 2015).</div><b>παρατατικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρατατικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imperfect, past continuous, past progressive, imperfective past (tense)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παρατατικός, from Ancient Greek παρατείνω, perhaps via a participle form παρατετακώς.</div><b>παρατεταμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine παρατεταμένη, neuter παρατεταμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive perfect participle of παρατείνω: extended</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prolonged, protracted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρατεταμένος, passive perfect participle of παρατείνω.</div><b>παρατηρητήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρατηρητήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. observation post, lookout, observatory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crow&#x27;s nest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρατηρώ + -τήριο, with semantic loan from French observatoire.</div><b>παρατηρητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρατηρητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>observer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek παρατηρητής.</div><b>παρατηρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to observe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to notice</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to note</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρατηρῶ, contracted form of παρατηρέω, from παρα- (para-) + τηρέω. The sense &quot;observe&quot; is a semantic loan from French observer.</div><b>παρατράγουδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρατράγουδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) debacle, fiasco (shocking or untoward event)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν έβλεπες τα <b>παρατράγουδα</b> που γινόντουσαν εδώ κάθε σαββατόβραδο! - If only you could see the debacle that go on here every Saturday night!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρα- (para-, “excessive”) + τραγούδι.</div><b>παρατσούκλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρατσούκλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nickname, sobriquet</div><b>παρατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παρατάω">παρατάω</a></div><b>παραφρενία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paraphrenia</div><b>παραφωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dissonance, atonality</div>
  <div class='def'>2. disagreement, discord</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French paraphonie.</div><b>παραχαϊδεμένος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παραχαϊδεμένοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mollycoddle (person who is pampered and overprotected)</div><b>παραχαϊδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to caress, extendedly or for a long time</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to pamper, mollycoddle, cosset</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρα- (“over- excessively”) + χαϊδεύω (“fondle”).</div><b>παραχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to grant, to concede (to give, confer, make concession of: land, place, right, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: χορηγώ (chorigó)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παραχωρῶ, from Ancient Greek παραχωρέω, with semantic loan from French concéder. By surface analysis, παρα- (para-) + χωρώ.</div><b>παραχώρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παραχωρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>allowance, concession, grant</div><b>παρβοϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parvovirus</div><b>παρδαλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dappled, pinto</div>
  <div class='def'>2. motley</div><b>παρειά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρειές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) cheek</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, formal) bent side wall (of vessels, ships, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρειά.</div><b>παρεισφρέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to infiltrate, inveigle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to creep in, worm your way in</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek παρεισφρέω. The ancient verb φρέω was only found in compounds. Morphologically, from παρ- + εισ-+ φρέω.</div><b>παρεκκλήσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρεκκλήσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chapel belonging to an individual or institution</div><b>παρεκκλίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to depart, deviate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to deflect</div><b>παρεκτρέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be diverted, led astray</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to go astray, exceed the bounds, behave improperly</div><b>παρεκτρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lead astray, swerve aside</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to go astray, exceed the bounds, behave improperly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned from Ancient Greek πᾰρεκτρέπω. By surface analysis, παρ- (“near”) + εκ- (“from, away”) + τρέπω (“turn”).</div><b>παρεκτραπήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of παρεκτραπήκαν, third-person plural simple past of <a href="bword://παρεκτρέπω">παρεκτρέπω</a></div><b>παρελθοντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρελθοντική, neuter παρελθοντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. past, of the past</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παρελθοντικά</b> γεγονότα - past events</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (past) past</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παρελθοντικός</b> χρόνος - past tense</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παρελθοντ- (parelthont-) + -ικός.</div><b>παρελθόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. past, time past</div>
  <div class='ex'>το ιστορικό <b>παρελθόν</b> - the historical past</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) a past full of sexual experience</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι γυναίκα με <b>παρελθόν</b>. - She is a woman with a past.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρελθόν, with semantic loan from French passé.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://παρελθών">παρελθών</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>παρελθόν</b> έτος - the past year</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παρελθόν, with semantic loan from French passé.</div><b>παρελθών</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine παρελθούσα, neuter παρελθόν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) one who passed, past, last</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο νέος νόμος ανακοινώθηκε την <b>παρελθούσα</b> εβδομάδα. - The new law was announced last week.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek παρελθών, past participle of παρέρχομαι, see παρῆλθον and ἐλθών. Found mostly as an adjective, and substantivised as the neuter noun παρελθόν.</div><b>παρεμβατισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) interventionism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρέμβαση + -ισμός, calque of French interventionnisme.</div><b>παρεμπιπτόντως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>by the way, incidentally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the present active participle stem παρεμπιπτοντ- (parempiptont-) of Koine Greek παρεμπίπτω, + -ως (-os), a calque of French incidemment.</div><b>παρεμποδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to block, hinder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek παρεμποδίζω. Morphologically, from παρ- + εμποδίζω (“obstruct”).</div><b>παρενέργεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρενέργειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(usually) side effect, adverse effect</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το φάρμακο δεν έχει καθόλου <b>παρενέργειες</b>. - This medicine has no side effects.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρ- (par-) + ενέργεια, calque of German Nebenwirkung.</div><b>παρενδυσιομανής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρενδυσιομανείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transvestite (person with that psychological disorder)</div><b>παρενδυσιομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) transvestism (psychological disorder)</div><b>παρενόχληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρενοχλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harassment, vexation, annoyance</div><b>παρεξήγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρεξηγήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misunderstanding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρα- (para-) + εξήγηση.</div><b>παρεξηγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to misunderstand, misinterpret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρα- (para-, “in opposition to”) and εξηγώ.</div><b>παρερμηνεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to misinterpret, to misconstrue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παρερμηνεύω. By surface analysis, παρ- (par-) + ερμηνεύω.</div><b>παρεστιγμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρεστιγμένη, neuter παρεστιγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of notes) dotted (with a dot after, increasing the value of the duration by half of the basic note)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παρεστιγμένη</b> παύση - dotted rest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρα- (para-, “extra”) + ἐστιγμένος, perfect subjunctive/optative passive singular of στίζω.</div><b>παρετυμολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρετυμολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>folk etymology (false etymology that incorrectly explains the origin of a word)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παρ- (par-) + ετυμολογία, calque of German Volksetymologie. First attested 1874.</div><b>παρευρίσκομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to attend, to be in attendance (to be present at an event)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παρ- (par-) + ευρίσκομαι, a calque of French y être.</div><b>παρηγοράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) (uncommon) alternative form of <a href="bword://παρηγορώ">παρηγορώ</a></div><b>παρηγορημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine παρηγορημένη, neuter παρηγορημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comforted, consoled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of παρηγορούμαι and παρηγοριέμαι, passive voices of παρηγορώ, παρηγοράω (“console”) respectively.</div><b>παρηγορητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρηγορητική, neuter παρηγορητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comforting, consoling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παρηγορητικός. By surface analysis, παρηγορη-) + -τικός.</div><b>παρηγοριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρηγοριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consolation, solace, comfort</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα λόγια σας μου ήταν <b>παρηγοριά</b> όταν έχασα τον πατέρα μου. - Your words were a comfort to me when I lost my father.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek παρηγοριά, from Ancient Greek παρηγορία with synizesis to -ιά, from παρήγορος, παρ- (par-) + the ancient stem ηγορ- of ἀγορά with lengthening for the initial 〈α〉 + -ία</div><b>παρηγορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to comfort, console</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek παρηγορῶ, contracted form of παρηγορέω, from παρήγορος.</div><b>παρθένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρθένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. virgin, maiden (person who has never had sexual intercourse)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. girl, young woman, maid, maiden</div><b>παρθένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρθένα or παρθένος, neuter παρθένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. virgin</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>Παρθένος</b> Μαρία - the Virgin Mary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pure, unadulterated</div>
  <div class='ex'>έξτρα παρθένο ελαιόλαδο - extra virgin olive oil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρθένος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρθένοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Virgo (someone born under that zodiacal sign)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. virgin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρθένος.</div><b>παρθενικό όνομα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(poetic) expression by Ioannis Gryparis, at his translation of the ancient phrase παρθένων ἐπώνυμον, referring to Parthenopeus (Παρθενοπαῖος)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ὁ δ’ ὠμόν, οὔτι παρθένων ἐπώνυμον,
[…] Παρθενοπαῖος Ἀρκάς: ὁ δὲ τοιόσδ’ ἀνὴρ
ho d’ ōmón, oúti parthénōn epṓnumon,
[…]Parthenopaîos Arkás: ho dè toiósd’ anḕr
But (now he makes his advance with ...) a terrifying look, not at all like the maidens he&#x27;s named for.
[…] he is Parthenopaeus of Arcadia. Such is the man
English translation (1926) by Herbert Weir Smyth @perseus.tufts.edu
The Modern Greek translation by Gryparis @greek-language.gr</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See παρθενικός &amp; όνομα n</div><b>παρθενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρθενική, neuter παρθενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. virginal, maiden</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pure, immaculate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) Being a first occurrence or event</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) Being a first occurrence or event; maiden, inaugural</div><b>παρθενικός υμένας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hymen (the membrane occluding the vagina)</div><b>παρθενογένεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) parthenogenesis (production of seed without fertilization)</div><b>παρισιάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρισιάνικη, neuter παρισιάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Parisian</div><b>παρισινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρισινή, neuter παρισινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Parisian</div><b>παριστάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to describe or represent something as it is, or as something else</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι μουσικοί ήχοι <b>παριστάνονται</b> με τις νότες. - Musical sounds are represented with notes.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to describe or represent something as it is, or as something else; to depict</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το άγαλμα <b>παριστάνει</b> την Ελευθερία. - The statue represents Liberty.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to pretend, play a role</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Παριστάνει</b> τον έξυπνο, αλλά είναι κουτός. - He pretends to be clever, but he is stupid.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παριστάνω from Ancient Greek παρίστημι.</div><b>παριστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παριστάνω">παριστάνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παριστάω / παριστῶ.</div><b>παρκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to park (a vehicle)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγαινε να βρεις κάπου να <b>παρκάρεις</b>. - Go and find somewhere to park.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian parcare from French parquer (“to park”), from parc (“park, pen”).</div><b>παρκέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παρκάρω">παρκάρω</a></div><b>παρκαδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρκαδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) car parker, valet car parker (someone who parks cars in carparks)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παρκάρω + -αδόρος</div><b>παρκαρισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine παρκαρισμένη, neuter παρκαρισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parked (of a vehicle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of παρκάρομαι, passive voice of παρκάρω.</div><b>παρκόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρκόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parking meter</div><b>παρμπρίζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) windscreen (UK), windshield (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French pare-brise (“windscreen”).</div><b>παρνάσσιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρνάσσιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Any butterfly of the genus Parnassius, including the Apollo.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>παρνάσσιος</b> Απόλλων - Parnassius apollo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Parnassius, from Ancient Greek Παρνάσσῐος.</div><b>παροιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παροιμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proverb, saying, adage, maxim (phrase expressing a basic truth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παροιμία.</div><b>παροιμιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παροιμιακή, neuter παροιμιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proverbial, related to a proverb</div><b>παροιμιώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παροιμιώδης, neuter παροιμιώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>legendary, proverbial, famous</div><b>παρομοίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρομοιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>simile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρομοίωσις.</div><b>παρομοιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to liken, to compare [with <a href="bword://με">με</a> (me, + accusative) ‘to’] (to regard or state that (someone or something) is like another person or thing; to declare two things to be similar in some respect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παρομοιάζω. By surface analysis, παρόμοιος + -άζω.</div><b>παρονομαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρονομαστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>denominator, the lower part of a fraction (thus 2 in ½).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French dénominateur.</div><b>παροξυσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παροξυσμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. paroxysm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spasm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παροξυσμός.</div><b>παροξύτονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παροξύτονη, neuter παροξύτονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paroxytone, (of a word) having a stressed penultimate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek.</div><b>παροτρύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to encourage, urge</div><b>παρουσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρουσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>presence (being present)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρουσία.</div><b>παρουσίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρουσιάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. presentation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε <b>παρουσίαση</b> είναι μοναδική. Δεν έχει σημασία αν κάθε φορά λες τα ίδια πράγματα. - Each presentation is unique. It doesn&#x27;t matter if you say the same thing every time.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. introduction</div><b>παρουσιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to appear, be shown</div><b>παρουσιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to present, introduce, show, submit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek παρουσιάζω, with some modern semantic influence from French présenter.</div><b>παρουσιάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρουσιάστριες, masculine παρουσιαστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>presenter, compere, newsreader</div><b>παρουσιαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural παρουσιαστές, feminine παρουσιάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>presenter, compere, newsreader</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from the past stem παρουσιασ- (parousias-) of παρουσιάζω + -τής, formed as a calque of French présentateur.</div><b>παροχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παροχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. provision (the act of providing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. benefit (a payment made in accordance with an insurance policy or a public assistance scheme)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. supply (of fluid resource through a pipeline)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. flow, flowrate, flow rate (the amount of a fluid that moves or the rate of fluid movement)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek παροχή with semantic loan from English supply.</div><b>παρτέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παρτέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flowerbed, raised bed, parterre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French parterre.</div><b>παρτιτούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>score, sheet music</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian partitura.</div><b>παρφουμάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be perfumed</div><b>παρφουμάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, passive, usually) to perfume oneself in excess</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French parfumer or Italian perfumare + endings -άρω or the less common -ίζω.</div><b>παρφουμαρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be perfumed</div><b>παρφουμαρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παρφουμάρω">παρφουμάρω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French parfumer or Italian perfumare + endings -άρω or the less common -ίζω.</div><b>παρωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parody</div><b>παρωνυχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρωνυχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whitlow, paronychia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρωνυχία.</div><b>παρωνυχίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρωνυχίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hangnail</div>
  <div class='def'>2. least important issue or problem</div><b>παρωπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρωπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blinker (UK):, blinder (US): (usually in the plural; worn by horses)</div><b>παρόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παρόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) pompous and silly, stupid word or saying</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dialectal, regional) word, speech, synonym of <a href="bword://λόγος">λόγος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A Modern Greek αντιδάνειο. Borrowed from Italian parola, from French parole (or see Etymology at Italian parola) &lt; Late Latin parabola &lt; Koine Greek παραβολή, ancient sense: &quot;analogy&quot;.
Or, Inherited from Byzantine Greek παρόλα, a Medieval Greek αντιδάνειο.</div><b>παρόλο</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(followed by <a href="bword://που">που</a> or <a href="bword://ότι">ότι</a>) even though, although</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A learned univerbation of παρά + όλο, a calque of French malgré tout.</div><b>παρόμοιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρόμοια, neuter παρόμοιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>similar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πᾰρόμοιος.</div><b>παρόν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
