  <div class='def'>impulse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παρόρμησις.</div><b>παρότρυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παροτρύνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>encouragement</div><b>παρών</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παρούσα, neuter παρόν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(not comparable) present, in attendance (located in the immediate vicinity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δια του <b>παρόντος</b> - with this, herewith</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From masculine active participle of Ancient Greek πάρειμι.</div><b>πας</b>
  <div><i>det, masculine (feminine πάσα, neuter παν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic) every, any</div>
  <div class='ex'>κατά <b>πάσα</b> πιθανότητα - in all likelihood</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic) whole, all</div>
  <div class='ex'>δια <b>παντός</b> - forever</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πᾶς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular present of <a href="bword://πάω">πάω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular dependent of <a href="bword://πάω">πάω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. second-person singular dependent of <a href="bword://πηγαίνω">πηγαίνω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ὑπάγω.</div><b>πασαλίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πασαλίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pashalik</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish پاشالق.</div><b>πασαλείβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πασαλείφω">πασαλείφω</a></div><b>πασαλείφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be daubed, done hastily, and see <a href="bword://πασαλείφω">πασαλείφω</a></div><b>πασαλείφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to smear hastily, offhand</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to daub, do something in a hasty and offhand manner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Koine Greek πισσᾰλοιφέω, πισσαλοιφῶ with assimilation [i-a &gt; a-a] (πισσ-α &gt; πασ-α) + αλείφω.</div><b>πασαπόρτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πασαπόρτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, informal) passport (official document used for international journeys)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δίνω <b>πασαπόρτι</b> - to give passport, figuratively to give the boot, to get rid of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian passaporti, plural of passaporto (“passport”).</div><b>πασιέντζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πασιέντζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patience</div><b>πασιέντσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πασιέντσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πασιέντζα">πασιέντζα</a></div><b>πασκάλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pascal (units of pressure)</div><b>πασπάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, uncountable) flour, powder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πασπάλη</div><b>πασπαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sprinkle, powder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πασπάλη.</div><b>πασπατεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to grope, fumble (exploratory)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to grope, fumble (sexual)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πασπατεύω, of uncertain origin.</div><b>παστέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παστέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pasteli (confection made from sesame seeds and honey)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pastello.</div><b>παστίλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παστίλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pastille, lozenge, drop</div><b>παστίτσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παστίτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pastichio, pastitsio (a Greek dish made with cheese, chopped meat and pasta, resembling lasagna)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pasticcio, from Vulgar Latin *pasticium, from *pastīcius (“of dough”), from Late Latin pasta (“paste; pasta”), from Ancient Greek παστά, from παστός.</div><b>παστερίωση</b>
