  <div class='ex'>πασχαλινό αβγό (Easter egg)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from Πάσχα</div><b>πατ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) stalemate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) fly (piece of cloth covering an opening, zip, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French pat, from Italian patta, from Latin pacta.</div><b>πατάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πατάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loft, attic (or similar space used for storage in an apartment)</div><b>πατάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πατάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. potato (vegetable, plant or dish)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) chips (UK), fries (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) mistake, error</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) something boring, uninteresting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Spanish patata, from Taíno batata.</div><b>πατάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to tread, tread on, trample, trample on, step on</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς <b>πατάει</b> τα σταφύλια στα αμπελοχώραφά του. - Grandad is trampling the grapes in his fields.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to press, depress (exert weight or force against)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πάτα</b> το κουμπί για να σταματήσει το λεωφορείο. - Press the button to stop the bus.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, transitive) to iron (pass an iron over in order to remove creases)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορείς να μου <b>πατήσεις</b> το πουκάμισο; - Can you iron my shirt?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to conquer, defeat (overcome in combat or sports)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι Ρωμαίοι ποτέ δεν <b>πάτησαν</b> την Ιρλανδία. - The Romans never conquered Ireland.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of vehicles) to run over (drive over, causing injury or death)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>πάτησε</b> λεωφορείο και πέθανε. - A bus ran him over and he died.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively, transitive) to violate (an oath, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πάτησε</b> την συμφωνία χωρίς να νοιάζεται. - He violated the agreement without caring.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (with a number) to reach, hit, become (an age)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τώρα που <b>πάτησα</b> τα πενήντα, αρχίζει να χειροτερεύει η όρασή μου. - Now that I&#x27;ve hit fifty, my eyesight is starting to get worse.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (with a noun) to indicate that an excess or a lot of that noun is being done or used</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πατάω</b> δουλειά - to get hard to work</div>
  <div class='def'>9. (intransitive) to stand, step, walk, tread</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρόσεχε εκεί που <b>πατάς</b>, το σανίδιο έχει σαπίσει. - Be careful stepping there, the board has rotted.</div>
  <div class='def'>10. (of vehicles) to step on it, put pedal to the metal (drive fast)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πάτα</b> να τους προλάβουμε! - Step on it so we can catch them!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πατ(ώ)) + -άω. Inherited from Ancient Greek πατῶ, contracted form of πατέω.</div><b>πατέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πατέρες or πατεράδες, feminine μητέρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with double plural) father; a male parent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with plural πατέρες) Father (form of address for monk or priest)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with plural πατέρες) founder of a science</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (with plural πατέρες) ancestors</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πατέρας, from Ancient Greek πατήρ through the accusative singular πατέρα, from Proto-Hellenic *patḗr, from Proto-Indo-European *ph₂tḗr. Doublet of πατήρ.</div><b>πατήρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://πατέρας">πατέρας</a>, father</div>
  <div class='def'>2. God the Father</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (literary) father (form of address for monk or priest)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek πᾰτήρ. Doublet of πατέρας.</div><b>πατίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πατίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scooter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. roller skate</div><b>πατατάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πατατάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>potato crisp (UK), potato chip (US).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πατατ (patat, “potato”) + -άκι</div><b>πατζάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πατζάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://παντζάρι">παντζάρι</a></div><b>πατινάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(for sense, of adding patina) to become patinated, develop a patina</div><b>πατινάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to skate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to slip (clutch)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to patinate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πατίνι + -άρω. From the French patiner.</div><b>πατισάχ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Padishah</div><b>πατούσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sole (of foot)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular imperfect of <a href="bword://πατάω">πατάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>πατρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πατρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. homeland, country of birth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. birthplace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the accusative of Ancient Greek πατρίς. Compare Mariupol Greek патридъа.</div><b>πατρίκιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πατρίκιοι, feminine πατρικία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. patrician (Roman and Byzantine social class)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. upper class</div><b>πατριάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πατριάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. patriarch (rank in church hierarchy)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. patriarch (character in the Old Testament)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. patriarch (clan chief)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πατριάρχης.</div><b>πατριαρχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πατριαρχική, neuter πατριαρχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. paternalistic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. patriarchal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek πατριαρχικός</div><b>πατριδογνωσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>homeland knowledge (subject formerly taught in Greek primary schools which imparted basic knowledge about one&#x27;s country and local region)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πατρίδ(α)) + -ο- (-o-) + -γνωσία. Compare German Heimatkunde, Hungarian honismeret, Turkish yurt bilgisi.</div><b>πατρικά ονόματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πατρικό">πατρικό</a> <a href="bword://όνομα">όνομα</a></div><b>πατρικία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πατρικίες, masculine πατρίκιος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. patrician (Roman and Byzantine social class)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. upper class</div><b>πατρικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πατρικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>family home</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πατρικός">πατρικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πατρικός">πατρικός</a></div><b>πατρικό όνομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πατρικά ονόματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maiden name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πατρικό + όνομα n</div><b>πατρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πατρική, neuter πατρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fatherly, paternal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πατρικός. By surface analysis, πατέρας + -ικός.</div><b>πατριωτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patriotically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πατριωτικός">πατριωτικός</a></div><b>πατριωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πατριωτική, neuter πατριωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patriotic</div><b>πατριωτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πατριωτισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patriotism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πατριώτης + -ισμός.</div><b>πατριός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πατριοί, feminine μητριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stepfather (a husband of a biological mother who is not the biological father)</div>
  <div class='ex'>with no simplification of spelling: πατρυιός (patruiós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek πατρυιός with simplication of spelling. From πατήρ m, stem πατρ-. The ancient spelling with ⟨υι⟩ in analogy to the feminine μητρυιά.</div><b>πατριώτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πατριώτες, feminine πατριώτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patriot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πατριώτης.</div><b>πατριώτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πατριώτισσες, masculine πατριώτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patriot</div><b>πατροκτονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πατροκτονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patricide (the crime of killing one&#x27;s father)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πατρο- (patro-, “father”) + -κτονία</div><b>πατροκτόνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πατροκτόνος, neuter πατροκτόνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patricidal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πατροκτόνος. By surface analysis, πατήρ + κτόνος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πατροκτόνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patricide (a person who murders his/her father)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πατροκτόνος. By surface analysis, πατήρ + κτόνος</div><b>πατρωνυμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πατρωνυμική, neuter πατρωνυμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patronymic</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patronymic</div><b>πατρώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πατρώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. father&#x27;s name, patronym</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) patronym, father&#x27;s surname</div><b>πατσάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πατσάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tripe and trotters (prepared as meat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tripe soup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پاچه with -ς (-s) suffix, from Classical Persian پاچه.</div><b>πατσαβουρόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πατσαβουρόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cake or pie made with squashed phyllo, with any flavour added</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πατσαβούρ(α), “swab”) + -ό- + -πιτα (πίτα), “pie”)</div><b>πατσαβούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πατσαβούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a swab, rag, any dirty cloth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. any squashed piece of cloth or paper</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) stupid or promiscuous woman</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) disreputable printed medium (newspaper, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan spazzadura with deletion of [s] and [d&gt;ð&gt;v], from Italian spazzatura, spazzare, from Latin spatior, spatium.</div><b>πατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πατάω">πατάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πατῶ, contracted form of πατέω.</div><b>πατώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to floor (cover or furnish with a floor)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρώτα απ’ όλα, θα <b>πατώσουν</b> το σπίτι και μετά θα χτίσουν τους τοίχους. - First of all, they’ll floor the house and then they&#x27;ll build the walls.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to bottom (furnish with a bottom)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο βαρελάς <b>πατώνει</b> το βαρέλι. - The cooper is bottoming the barrel.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to tamp down (compact a substance until it is flat)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς <b>πατώνει</b> τον καπνό στην πίπα του. - Grandad is tamping down the tobacco in his pipe.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of the sea or rivers) to touch the bottom, touch the ground (be able to touch the seabed or riverbed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>πατώνω</b> εδώ! - I’m not touching bottom out here!</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of exams) to fail, come last</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σ’ αυτές τις εξετάσεις σίγουρα θα <b>πατώσω</b> επειδή δεν μελέτησα. - I’m surely going to fail these exams since I didn&#x27;t study.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πατώνω, from πάτος.</div><b>παυσίπονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παυσίπονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>painkiller, analgesic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παύση + πόνος.</div><b>παυσίπονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παυσίπονη, neuter παυσίπονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>analgesic, painkilling</div><b>παχαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to get fat, to fatten</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make fat, to fatten</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek παχ(ύνω)) + -αίνω.</div><b>παχνί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παχνιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>manger, crib</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πανθίον, a diminutive of πάνθη, which is itself a variant of φάτνη, via a metathesis of /tn/ &gt; /θn/ &gt; /xn/.</div><b>παχυντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παχυντική, neuter παχυντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fattening</div><b>παχυσαρκία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παχυσαρκίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obesity, fatness</div><b>παχύ έντερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) large intestine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παχύς + έντερο.</div><b>παχύρρευστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παχύρρευστη, neuter παχύρρευστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>viscous, thick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from παχύ- + ρευστ(ός)) + -ος (-os), a calque of German dickflüssig.</div><b>παχύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παχιά, neuter παχύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fat, a formal synonym of <a href="bword://χοντρός">χοντρός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. incrassate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. overweight</div>
  <div class='def'>4. thick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πᾰχῠ́ς.</div><b>παχύσαρκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παχύσαρκη, neuter παχύσαρκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obese, corpulent, overweight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From παχύς + σάρκα.</div><b>παχύσωμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine παχύσωμη, neuter παχύσωμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fat, obese, overweight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πᾰχῠ́ς.</div><b>παϊδάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural παϊδάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chop, cutlet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> παΐδι + -άκι (suffix for diminutive neuters).</div><b>παϊράκιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) flag, banner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish بیراق (bayrak) + -ιν (-in).</div><b>παύλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παύλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dash</div>
  <div class='ex'>διπλή παύλα ― diplí pávla (two dashes used instead of parentheses)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. quotation dash</div><b>παύση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural παύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pause, hiatus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cessation, discontinuation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. termination (the process of firing an employee)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. rest (a pause of a specified length in a piece of music)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. rest, pause (a written symbol indicating such a pause in a musical score such as in sheet music)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek παῦσις with semantic loan from French pause in the musical senses. By surface analysis, παύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>παύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stop, cease</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έπαψα</b> να πηγαίνω - I stopped going</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to pause</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to depose, relieve of duties</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πρωθυπουργός <b>έπαυσε</b> τον υπουργό. - The prime minister removed the minister.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek παύω.</div><b>πβ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of παράβαλε</div><b>πβ.</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of παράβαλε</div><b>πείθομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be persuaded, to be convinced</div><b>πείθω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to persuade, convince, induce (in 1st person singular present)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πείθω.</div><b>πείνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πείνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hunger, famine, starvation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πεῖνα.</div><b>πείρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) experience</div>
  <div class='ex'>πρακτική <b>πείρα</b> - practical experience</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πεῖρα.</div><b>πείραγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πειράγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teasing, ribbing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the πειρακ- stem of πειράζω + -μα (-ma) with assimilation of [km] to [ɣm].</div><b>πείραμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πειράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>experiment (test under controlled conditions)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Either inherited from Byzantine Greek or learned borrowing from Koine Greek πείραμα, from Ancient Greek πειρῶμαι + -μα (-ma)), with semantic loan from English experiment.</div><b>πείσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πείσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stubbornness, obstinacy, mulishness</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάνω <b>πείσματα</b> - to act up, to throw tantrums</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πείσμα, from Koine Greek πεῖσμα.</div><b>πεδίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεδία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. field, domain, range, plain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ground</div>
  <div class='def'>3. field, plane</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πεδίον.</div><b>πεδιάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεδιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plain (large, flat lands)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πεδιάς + accusative suffix -άδα, from πεδίον + -ιάς.</div><b>πεδικουλαρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεδικουλαρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Pedicularis-genus plant.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from translingual Pedicularis.</div><b>πεδιλοδοκός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεδιλοδοκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strap beam, a concrete beam that connects two footings</div><b>πεδινές πέρδικες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of πεδινή <a href="bword://πέρδικα">πέρδικα</a></div><b>πεδινή πέρδικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεδινές πέρδικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Perdix perdix: grey partridge, English partridge, Hungarian partridge, hun</div><b>πεδινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεδινή, neuter πεδινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of the) plain, plains</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πεδινή</b> πέρδικα - grey partridge</div><b>πεζά γράμματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of πεζό <a href="bword://γράμμα">γράμμα</a> small or lowercase letters</div><b>πεζά στοιχεία</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative plural of πεζό <a href="bword://στοιχείο">στοιχείο</a></div><b>πεζογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) prose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πεζο- (pezo-, “prosaic, pedestrian”) + γραφή</div><b>πεζοδρόμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεζοδρόμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>footpath, footway, pavement (UK), sidewalk (US) (a paved path, for the use of pedestrians, located at the side of a road)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from πεζο- (pezo-) + δρόμος + -ιον (-ion), formed as a calque of German Fußgängerweg (“pedestrian path”).</div><b>πεζοδρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πεζοδρόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>walker, rambler, hiker, tramper (NZ)</div><b>πεζομαχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεζομαχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>infantry battle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πεζομαχίᾱ. By surface analysis, πεζο- (pezo-, “foot”) + μάχη.</div><b>πεζοναύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πεζοναύτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>marine (member of the Royal Marines, Marine Corps, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πεζο- (pezo-, “pedestrian”) + ναύτης</div><b>πεζοπορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεζοπορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hiking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πεζοπορία. By surface analysis, πεζοπορ(ώ)) + -ία.</div><b>πεζοπορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεζοπορική, neuter πεζοπορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hiking, walking; hikers&#x27;, walkers&#x27;</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πεζοπορικός</b> χάρτης - hiking map</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πεζο- (pezo-, “pedestrian”) + πορεία</div><b>πεζοπόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πεζοπόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>walker, rambler, hiker, tramper (NZ)</div><b>πεζό γράμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεζά γράμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(usually plural) small or lowercase letter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πεζό + γράμμα</div><b>πεζό στοιχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεζά στοιχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small/lowercase letter</div><b>πεζόδρομος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πεζόδρομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traffic-free street, pedestrian area, pedestrian mall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πεζό- + δρόμος</div><b>πεζός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεζή, neuter πεζό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. on foot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prosaic, prose</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mundane</div>
  <div class='def'>4. lowercase</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πεζοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pedestrian</div><b>πεθαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to die</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άκουσες ότι <b>πέθανε</b> ο κύριος Βαγγέλης; - Did you hear that Mr Vangelis died?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to kill (either directly or indirectly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτά τα φάρμακα τον <b>πέθαναν</b> στο τέλος. - These medicines killed him in the end.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to kill, torture (cause suffering to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα με <b>πεθάνεις</b> με τη ζήλια σου! - Your jealousy will be the death of me!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to pain, hurt, kill (cause pain to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το δόντι μου με <b>πεθαίνει</b>. - My tooth is killing me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Backformation from Ancient Greek ἀπέθανε the third person singular aorist of ἀποθνῄσκω.</div><b>πεθαμένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar, in plural, informal) the dead (implied: relatives) used in condolence phrases</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να συχωρεθούν τα <b>πεθαμένα</b> σας - May your dead [relatives] be forgiven.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter plural of participle πεθαμένος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πεθαμένος">πεθαμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>πεθαμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πεθαμένη, neuter πεθαμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dead, deceased, (English euphemisms): departed, late, passed away</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) lost, perished</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>πεθαμένες</b> ελπίδες - the dead hopes</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, idiomatic) exhausted</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γύρισα στο σπίτι <b>πεθαμένος</b> από τη δουλειά - I returned home, exhausted from work.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (neutral plural) see <a href="bword://πεθαμένα">πεθαμένα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> passive perfect participle of πεθαίνω, a verb without passive forms. Inherited from Byzantine Greek ἀπεθαμένος from stem ἀπεθαν- with assimilation [nm &gt; mm] and simplification of [mm &gt; m]. See Ancient Greek aorist ἀπέθᾰνον of ἀποθνῄσκω.</div><b>πεθερά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεθερές, masculine πεθερός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mother-in-law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See πενθερά.</div><b>πεθερικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in-laws, parents-in-law (spouse&#x27;s mother and father and rarely their close family)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) in-laws&#x27; house</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάμε αύριο στα <b>πεθερικά</b> μας. - Tomorrow, we&#x27;re going to our in-laws’.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From adjective πεθερικός.</div><b>πεθερός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πεθεροί, feminine πεθερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>father-in-law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πενθερός.</div><b>πειθαναγκάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be forced, compelled</div><b>πειθαναγκάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to compel, force</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek πειθᾰνάγκη + -άζω (“suffix for verbs”).</div><b>πειθόμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πειθόμενη, neuter πειθόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) being convinced, being persuaded</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek πειθόμενος. Present participle of πείθομαι, passive voice of πείθω.</div><b>πειθώ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) persuasion (the act of persuading)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) persuasion, persuasiveness (one&#x27;s ability or power to influence someone&#x27;s opinions or feelings)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πειθώ.</div><b>πεινάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be hungry, be famished</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πεινάω.</div><b>πεινασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πεινασμένη, neuter πεινασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hungry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of πεινάω, πεινώ, a verb with no passive voice.</div><b>πεινώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πεινάω">πεινάω</a></div><b>πεινών</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) hungry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πεινῶν.</div><b>πειράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be annoyed, irritated or teased</div><b>πειράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to annoy, bother, irritate, vex</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με <b>πειράζει</b> πολύ η φασαρία εδώ. - The noise here is annoying me greatly.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) matter, mind, bother</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>πειράζει</b>, έχω άλλο. - It doesn&#x27;t matter, I have another one.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to hurt, bother, offend</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα λόγια του με <b>πείραξαν</b> πολύ. - His words hurt me very much.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to harass, bother (sexually)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορεί να πάει πουθενά χωρίς να την <b>πειράζουν</b> άνδρες ανελέητα. - She can&#x27;t go anywhere without men relentlessly harassing her.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to tease, make fun of, joke, rib, kid, banter</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην ταράζεσαι, απλά σε <b>πειράζει</b>. - Don&#x27;t get upset, he&#x27;s only joking with you.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to move, touch, tamper with (so as to annoy someone by doing so)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος <b>πείραξε</b> τους δίσκους μου; - Who touched my records?</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (transitive) to affect (negatively), trouble, bother, not agree with (of food)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η μπριζόλα που έφαγε τον <b>πείραξε</b> αργότερα. - The steak he ate didn&#x27;t agree with him later on.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (as an adjective) to modify, change, tweak (something so it is better or more suited)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αμάξι μου είναι <b>πειραγμένο</b>. - My car is modified.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πειράζω.</div><b>πειραματίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to experiment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πειραματ- (πείραμα), “experiment”, stem from the genitive) + -ίζομαι.</div><b>πειραματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πειραματική, neuter πειραματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>experimental</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the πειραματ- stem of πείραμα + -ικός, a calque of English experimental.</div><b>πειραματόζωο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πειραματόζωα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. experimental animal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) guinea pig (ie experimental animals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πειραματ- (“stem of πείραμα, experiment”) + -ο- (“infix”) + ζώο (“animal”).</div><b>πειρασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πειρασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>temptation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πειρασμός.</div><b>πειρατής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πειρατές, feminine πειρατίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pirate, corsair</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πειρατές</b> του Αιγαίου - Pirates of the Aegean Sea</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) copier of copyright materials</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πειρατής.</div><b>πειρατίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πειρατίνες, masculine πειρατής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pirate, sea robber</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) copier of copyright materials</div><b>πειρατεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πειρατείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. piracy (robbery at sea)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) piracy (copying of copyright materials)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Kάνει πειρατεία με κασέτες. - Make piracy tapes</div><b>πεισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πεισμένη, neuter πεισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>convinced, persuaded</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>πεισμένη</b> ότι θα την παντρευτεί. Εγώ όμως είμαι απολύτως πε<b>πεισμένη</b> ότι είναι ψεύτης. - She is induced to believe that he will marry her. But I am absolutely convinced that he is a liar.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of πείθομαι, passive voice of πείθω.</div><b>πεισματάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεισματάρα, neuter πεισματάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stubborn, obstinate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: αδιάλλακτος (adiállaktos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πεισματάρης. By surface analysis, from the πεισματ- stem of πείσμα + -άρης.</div><b>πεισμώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be stubborn, to dig one&#x27;s heels in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make stubborn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πείσμ(α)) + -ώνω.</div><b>πειστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πειστική, neuter πειστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>persuasive, convincing</div><b>πειστικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πειστικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>persuasiveness, convincingness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πειστικός + -ότητα.</div><b>πεκάν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pecan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French pacane.</div><b>πεκινουά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Pekingese (dog)</div><b>πελάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πελάτες, feminine πελάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. customer, client, patient</div>
  <div class='def'>2. patron, customer, guest (of hotel, cafe, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πελάτης.</div><b>πελάτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πελάτισσες, masculine πελάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female customer, client, patient</div><b>πελέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πελέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>axe (tool or weapon)</div><b>πελαργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πελαργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stork (bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek.</div><b>πελατεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) custom (customers)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) practice (medical, law, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) patronage, clientele</div><b>πελεκάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πελεκάνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pelican</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πελεκάν, related to Πέλεκας and πέλεκυς.</div><b>πελώριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πελώρια, neuter πελώριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>huge, massive, enormous, gigantic, humongous</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ψαράς έπιασε ένα <b>πελώριο</b> ψάρι. - The fisherman caught a huge fish.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πελώριος, from πέλωρ, of unknown origin. Perhaps from Proto-Indo-European *kʷer-ōr modified by dissimilation, given variant forms such as τέλωρ and τελώριος which point to an original initial *kʷ, though this derivation is problematic.</div><b>πενήντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fifty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πεντήκοντα, from Proto-Indo-European *penkʷēḱomth₂, from earlier *pénkʷedḱomt (“fifty”).</div><b>πενηντάρικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πενηντάρικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, obsolete) fifty-drachma coin / note</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) fifty-euro note</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τρελάθηκες; Μην δίνεις το <b>πενηντάρικο</b> στον ζητιάνο! - Are you mad? Don&#x27;t give that fifty to the beggar!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πενήντα + -άρικο</div><b>πενθήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πενθήμερη, neuter πενθήμερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>five-day (lasting five days)</div><b>πενθερά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πενθερές, masculine πενθερός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πεθερά">πεθερά</a></div><b>πενθερικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πεθερικά">πεθερικά</a></div><b>πενθερός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πενθεροί, feminine πενθερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πεθερός">πεθερός</a></div><b>πενικιλίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) penicillin</div><b>πεντάγραμμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεντάγραμμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>staff, stave (the set of five horizontal and parallel lines on and between which musical notes are written or pointed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πεντάγραμμον with semantic loan from Italian pentagramma, itself borrowed from the same etymon. By surface analysis, πεντά- + γραμμ(ή)) + -ο (-o).</div><b>πεντάγωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεντάγωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pentagon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter adjective πεντάγωνος by ellipsis of the noun σχήμα. Morphologically from πεντα- (“penta-”) + -γωνο (“-gon”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of πεντάγωνος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of πεντάγωνος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter adjective πεντάγωνος by ellipsis of the noun σχήμα. Morphologically from πεντα- (“penta-”) + -γωνο (“-gon”).</div><b>πεντάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pentane</div><b>πεντάπλευρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεντάπλευρη, neuter πεντάπλευρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>five-sided, quinquelateral (having five sides)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adjectivized learned borrowing from Koine Greek πεντάπλευρον. By surface analysis, πεντά- + -πλευρος (-plevros).</div><b>πεντάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεντάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>five-lepta/cent coin</div><b>πεντάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεντάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. five (describing a set or group with four elements)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. five-roomed apartment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. five</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πέντ(ε)) + -άρι.</div><b>πεντάωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεντάωρη, neuter πεντάωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) five hours, lasting five hours</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πεντα- + ώρα + -ος (-os)</div><b>πεντέμισι</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. five and a half</div>
  <div class='def'>2. half past five</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πέντε + -μισι (-misi).</div><b>πεντήκοντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://πενήντα">πενήντα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek πεντήκοντα.</div><b>πενταήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>five-day (lasting five days)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πεντα- (penta-) + ημέρ(α)) + -ος (-os)</div><b>πενταδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πενταδική, neuter πενταδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quinary, pentimal</div><b>πεντακάθαρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεντακάθαρη, neuter πεντακάθαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(adjective, emphatic) spotless, spick-and-span, clean as a whistle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πεντα- (penta-) + καθαρός.</div><b>πεντακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(noun, numeral) five hundred</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of πεντακόσιοι</div><b>πεντανόστιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεντανόστιμη, neuter πεντανόστιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very tasty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πεντα- (penta-) + νόστιμος.</div><b>πεντασύλλαβος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεντασύλλαβη, neuter πεντασύλλαβο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of five syllables, pentasyllabic</div><b>πεντηκοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεντηκοστή, neuter πεντηκοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fiftieth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πεντηκοστός.</div><b>πεντικιούρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pedicure</div><b>πεολειξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεολειξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fellatio (oral stimulation of the penis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πέος + λείχω.</div><b>πεολειχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πεολειχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πεολειξία">πεολειξία</a></div><b>πεπειραμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine πεπειραμένη, neuter πεπειραμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a person) experienced, seasoned (having experience)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πεπειραμένος, passive perfect participle of πειράω.</div><b>πεπεισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πεπεισμένη, neuter πεπεισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, literary) convinced, persuaded</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι πεισμένη ότι θα την παντρευτεί. Εγώ όμως είμαι απολύτως <b>πεπεισμένη</b> ότι είναι ψεύτης. - She is induced to believe that he will marry her. But I am absolutely convinced that he is a liar.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek πεπεισμένος. Formal perfect participle of πείθομαι, passive voice of πείθω. It retains the ancient reduplication πε-π...-.</div><b>πεποίθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεποιθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. conviction, belief</div>
  <div class='def'>2. confidence</div><b>πεπρωμένο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεπρωμένα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>destiny, fate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο θάνατος είναι το κοινό <b>πεπρωμένο</b> όλων μας. - Death is the common destiny of us all.</div><b>πεπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πεπτική, neuter πεπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) digestive, peptic (of, relating to, or functioning in digestion)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πεπτικά</b> υγρά - digestive/peptic fluids/juices</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πεπτικός with semantic loan from French digestif.</div><b>πεπόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεπόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melon (fruit and plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πεπόνιον, a diminutive of Ancient Greek πέπων.</div><b>περήφανος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περήφανη, neuter περήφανο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proud</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ὑπερήφανος.</div><b>περί</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. about, approximately</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>περί</b> τις πεντακόσιες δραχμές - about five hundred drachmas</div>
  <div class='def'>2. about, concerning</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ήξερα <b>περί</b> τίνος πρόκειται. - I didn&#x27;t know what it was about.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περί.</div><b>περίβολος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περίβολοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. walled garden</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wall around a garden</div><b>περίδρομος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περίδρομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abdominal pain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in the phrase <a href="bword://τρώω">τρώω</a>/<a href="bword://κατεβάζω">κατεβάζω</a> <a href="bword://τον">τον</a> περίδρομο) too much food</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Either an ellipsis of περίδρομος δαίμων, a supposed cause of gluttony, or borrowed from Ancient Greek περίδρομος, referring to the drawstring on a bag of food.</div><b>περίεργα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curiously, strangely</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://περίεργος">περίεργος</a></div><b>περίεργος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περίεργη, neuter περίεργο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. curious (eager to learn; inquisitive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strange, peculiar, curious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περίεργος, from περι- (peri-, “peri-, around”) + ἔργον.</div><b>περίθαλψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>care (the treatment of those in need (especially as a profession))</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: μέριμνα f (mérimna), φροντίδα f (frontída)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek περίθαλψις. By surface analysis, περιθάλπω + -ση (-si).</div><b>περίληψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιλήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>summary, synopsis, summing up, brief</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περίληψις.</div><b>περίμετρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περίμετροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. circumference</div>
  <div class='def'>2. perimeter</div><b>περίνεο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perineum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περίς through influence from Ancient Greek πηρίς.</div><b>περίοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περίοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. period, phase, epoch</div>
  <div class='ex'>πρώιμη <b>περίοδος</b> - early period</div>
  <div class='def'>2. period, season</div>
  <div class='ex'>καλοκαιρινή <b>περίοδος</b> - summer period</div>
  <div class='def'>3. period in the periodic table</div>
  <div class='def'>4. period, menstruation</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (rhetoric) period of sentences</div>
  <div class='def'>6. period of phrases</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek περῐ́οδος.</div><b>περίοικος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περίοικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. neighbour (UK), neighbor (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (obsolete) perioecus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περίοικος.</div><b>περίπατος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περίπατοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. walk, hike, stroll (journey on foot)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. walk, way (recognised route for such a walk)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. opening (type of move)</div><b>περίπλοκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περίπλοκη, neuter περίπλοκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>complex, complicated</div><b>περίπολος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περίπολοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patrol</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περίπολος.</div><b>περίπου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. approximately, about</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nearly</div>
  <div class='def'>3. circa</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Univerbation of περί + που (pou, “that”).</div><b>περίπτερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περίπτερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kiosk, newsstand</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coffee stall</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pavilion with colonnades</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek περίπτερον, neuter of περίπτερος, from περί + πτερόν, likely referring to the awning covering such a shop.</div><b>περίπτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιπτώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. case (an actual event, situation, or fact)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σ’ αυτήν την <b>περίπτωση</b>, όντως έλεγε την αλήθεια. - In this case, he really was telling the truth.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. case (a person being treated for illness or a customer being served in a shop etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω δει διάφορες παρόμοιες <b>περιπτώσεις</b> ως ψυχίατρος. - As a psychiatrist, I’ve seen several similar cases.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, derogatory) oddball, weirdo (a person who behaves strangely)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή ήταν μοναδική <b>περίπτωση</b>! - She was a genuine oddball!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial) cream of the crop, crème de la crème (an outstanding or excellent case)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρόκειται για <b>περίπτωση</b> γιατρού και ανθρώπου. - As a doctor and a human being, he was the cream of the crop.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περίπτωσις, from περιπίπτω.</div><b>περίσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. situation, occasion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. instance, circumstance</div><b>περίστροφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περίστροφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>revolver (a handgun with revolving chamber)</div><b>περίττωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιττώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural) excrement, excreta, faeces (UK), feces (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περίττωμα.</div><b>περίφημα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://περίφημος">περίφημος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>splendidly, excellently, great</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα πήγες <b>περίφημα</b>. - You did splendidly.</div><b>περίφημος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περίφημη, neuter περίφημο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>famous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περίφημος; by surface analysis, περί- + φήμη + -ος (-os).</div><b>περίφραξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιφράξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fence, fencing, enclosure</div><b>περίφραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιφράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>circumlocution</div><b>περαιτέρω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beyond that point</div>
  <div class='def'>2. further</div>
  <div class='ex'>μη <b>περαιτέρω</b> - no further</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περαιτέρω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) next steps, the rest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περαιτέρω.</div><b>περασμένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural) the past years</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θυμάμαι τα <b>περασμένα</b>. - I am reminiscing the past.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter plural of participle περασμένος by ellipsis of nouns referring to &quot;past&quot; χρόνια, συμβάντα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://περασμένος">περασμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>περασμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine περασμένη, neuter περασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passed (through, over, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δαχτυλίδι <b>περασμένο</b> στο δάχτυλο - a ring placed/passed on the finger</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of the past</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>περασμένες</b> γενιές - the past generations</div>
  <div class='def'>3. of the past; old</div>
  <div class='ex'>άνθρωπος <b>περασμένης</b> ηλικίας - a man (someone) of old age</div>
  <div class='def'>4. of the past; previous</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>περασμένο</b> Σάββατο - the previous Saturday</div>
  <div class='def'>5. registered</div>
  <div class='ex'>όνομα <b>περασμένο</b> στον κατάλογο - a name added at the list</div>
  <div class='def'>6. and see Expressions</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (for time) past</div>
  <div class='ex'>είναι <b>περασμένες</b> τρεις - it is three past [not specified how long]</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (gender neutral) see <a href="bword://περασμένα">περασμένα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of περνιέμαι, passive voice of περνάω, περνώ (“I pass”).</div><b>περαστικά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>get well soon</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://περαστικός">περαστικός</a></div><b>περαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περαστική, neuter περαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passer-by</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passing, of passage</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Περαστικά</b> πουλιά φαίνεται πως απαντούν την άνοιξη. - Birds of passage are seen in spring.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ephemeral, brief</div>
  <div class='def'>4. casual</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From περνάω, περνώ + -τικός.</div><b>περβόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περβόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://περιβόλι">περιβόλι</a></div><b>περγαμηνή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περγαμηνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parchment (writing material)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Πέργαμον.</div><b>περγαμοντιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περγαμοντιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bergamot tree</div><b>περγαμόντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περγαμόντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bergamot (fruit and essence)</div><b>περεστρόικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) perestroika</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Russian перестро́йка.</div><b>περηφάνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) pride</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) arrogance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπερηφανία by procope, from ῠ̔πέρ + φᾱνός from φαίνω from Proto-Hellenic *pʰáňňō from Proto-Indo-European *bʰeh₂- (“to shine”). Equivalent to περήφανος + -ία.</div><b>περιάνθιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perianth</div><b>περιέργεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) curiosity, inquisitiveness</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>περιέργεια</b> σκότωσε τη γάτα. - Curiosity killed the cat.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περιεργία.</div><b>περιέργως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curiously, strangely</div><b>περιέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to contain (to hold inside)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to contain (to include as a part)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περιέχω. By surface analysis, περι- (peri-) + έχω.</div><b>περιήγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιηγήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tour (guided visit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sightseeing, ramble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περιήγησις, from περι- (peri-) + ἥγησῐς.</div><b>περιήλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιήλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perihelion</div><b>περιαγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>roaming (the ability to use a cell phone outside of its original registering zone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>διεθνής <b>περιαγωγή</b> - international roaming</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περιαγωγή. By surface analysis, περι- (peri-) + αγωγή.</div><b>περιαυτολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to speak highly of oneself, to boast, to brag, to blow one&#x27;s own trumpet, to toot one&#x27;s own horn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek περιαυτολογῶ, contracted form of περιαυτολογέω.</div><b>περιβάλλον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιβάλλοντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. environment (conditions, social factors around something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ambient conditions</div><b>περιβαλλοντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιβαλλοντική, neuter περιβαλλοντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>environmental (pertaining to one&#x27;s environment)</div><b>περιβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>attire, dress, garb, raiment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek περιβολή.</div><b>περιβόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιβόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vegetable garden, orchard garden, allotment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek περιβόλιν, from Koine Greek περιβόλιον, from a diminutive ofAncient Greek περίβολος, from περιβάλλω. From περί- (peri-, “around”) + βάλλω.</div><b>περιγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be described</div><b>περιγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to write an account, describe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to describe, give an account of (orally)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περῐγρᾰ́φω. By surface analysis, περι- (“around”) + γράφω (“write”).</div><b>περιγραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιγραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. description, account</div>
  <div class='def'>2. delineation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> περι- (peri-, “around”) + γράφω</div><b>περιδέραιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιδέραια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>necklace</div><b>περιεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιεκτική, neuter περιεκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concise, compact</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek περιεκτικός, from the combining form περιεκ- (periek-) of περιέχω + -τικός.</div><b>περιεχόμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιεχόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. content (that which is contained)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contents, table of contents (of a book, etc.)</div><b>περιηγήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιηγήτριες, masculine περιηγητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. traveller (UK), traveler (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tourist, sightseer</div><b>περιηγητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περιηγητές, feminine περιηγήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tourist, sightseer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. traveller (UK), traveler (US)</div><b>περιηγητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιηγητική, neuter περιηγητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tourist, touristic, sightseeing</div><b>περιηγούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to travel, tour, go on a tour</div><b>περιθάλπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be nursed</div><b>περιθάλπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to look after, care for, shelter, help (persons in need)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Περιθάλπει</b> τους πρόσφυγες. - She shelters the refugees.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to nurse, attend to, treat (care for in a medical context)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η νοσοκόμα <b>περιθάλπει</b> τους αρρώστους. - The nurse treats the patients.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περῐθᾰ́λπω (“warm all round, cherish”). By surface analysis, περι- (“around”) + θάλπω (“provide comfort”).</div><b>περιθώριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιθώρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. margin (the edge of the paper, typically left blank when printing but sometimes used for annotations, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. margin (the edge or border of any flat surface)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) margin, room, leeway (a permissible difference; allowing some freedom to move within limits)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>περιθώριο</b> σφάλματος - margin of error</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) sidelines (the outside or perimeter of any activity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>βάζω στο <b>περιθώριο</b> - to put on the sidelines, to leave to one side</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) fringe (a group of people situated on the periphery of a larger community)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>περιθώριο</b> της κοινωνίας - the fringes of society</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Possibly a learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek *περιθεώριον, from Koine Greek περιθεωρῶ, from Ancient Greek περιθεωρέω, with semantic loan from French marge.</div><b>περικάρπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περικάρπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pericarp, pod, husk, shell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the part of the hand around the wrist</div>
  <div class='def'>3. wristband</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περῐκᾰ́ρπῐον.</div><b>περικλείομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be encircled, surrounded</div><b>περικλείω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to enclose, to encircle, to encompass, to surround, to fence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περικλείω. Morphologically, from περι- + κλείω.</div><b>περικοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περικοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. quotation, extract, citation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cutback, abridgement</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pericope</div><b>περικυκλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(often, transitive) to surround, to encircle (on all sides)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περικυκλόω with -ώνω ending. By surface analysis, περι- (peri-) + κυκλώνω.</div><b>περικόβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be cropped, shortened</div><b>περικόβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) synonym of <a href="bword://περικόπτω">περικόπτω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> περι- (“around”) + κόβω (“cut”) from the ancient form κόπτω. See περικόπτω.</div><b>περικόπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be cropped, shortened</div><b>περικόπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to crop, trim, shorten, clip</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to slash (prices, salaries)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περικόπτω. By surface analysis, περι- (“from”) + κόπτω (“cut”).</div><b>περιλαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to contain, include</div><b>περιμένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wait, wait for, expect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek περιμένω &lt; περι- (peri-) + μένω.</div><b>περιμετρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιμετρική, neuter περιμετρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perimetric</div><b>περινεύριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perineurium</div><b>περιοδεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιοδείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tour (a journey through a given list of places, such as by an entertainer performing concerts)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek περιοδεία.</div><b>περιοδεύων</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine περιοδεύουσα, neuter περιοδεύον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>itinerant, travelling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek περιοδεύων. Formal active present participle of περιοδεύω.</div><b>περιοδικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://περιοδικός">περιοδικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>periodically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://περιοδικό">περιοδικό</a></div><b>περιοδικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://περιοδικός">περιοδικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://περιοδικός">περιοδικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * (Adjective): inflectional form of περιοδικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιοδικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magazine, periodical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * (Adjective): inflectional form of περιοδικός.</div><b>περιοδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιοδική, neuter περιοδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>periodic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek περιοδικός, περιοδικῶς</div><b>περιοδικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) periodicity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> περιοδικός + -ότητα, calque of French périodicité. First attested 1841.</div><b>περιορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to confine, restrict, curb</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το υπουργείο επιθυμεί να <b>περιορίσει</b> την άνοδο του πληθωρισμού. - The ministry wishes to restrict the rise in inflation.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to limit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to restrain</div><b>περιορισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine περιορισμένη, neuter περιορισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>limited</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of περιορίζομαι, passive voice of περιορίζω.</div><b>περιορισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περιορισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. restriction, limitation, constraint</div>
  <div class='def'>2. house arrest, confinement</div><b>περιοριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιοριστική, neuter περιοριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>restrictive, limiting, constrictive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek περιοριστικός with semantic loan from French limitatif. By surface analysis, περιορίζω + -τικός.</div><b>περιουσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιουσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. property, possessions, wealth (personal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fortune, estate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περιουσία.</div><b>περιοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιοχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. region, district, area</div>
  <div class='def'>2. domain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περιοχή.</div><b>περιπέτεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιπέτειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adventure, escapade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περιπέτεια, from περί + the stem of πίπτω.</div><b>περιπετειώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιπετειώδης, neuter περιπετειώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adventuresome, eventful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. adventurous, eager for adventure</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dangerous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From περιπέτεια + -ώδης. Calque of French aventureux.</div><b>περιπλάνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιπλανήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wandering (travelling without preset route; roaming)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) wandering (aimless thought)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek περιπλάνησις, with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>περιπλέκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to complicate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to entangle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek περιπλέκω. By surface analysis, περι- (“around”) + πλέκω (“knit; intertwine”).</div><b>περιπλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sail round, circumnavigate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περιπλέω. Morphologically, from περι- (peri-, “round”) + πλέω.</div><b>περιπλεχθήκαν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>variation of of περιπλέχθηκαν, third-person plural simple past of περιπλέκομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://περιπλέκω">περιπλέκω</a></div><b>περιπλεχτήκαν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>variation of περιπλέχτηκαν, third-person plural simple past of περιπλέκομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://περιπλέκω">περιπλέκω</a></div><b>περιπλεχτήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of <a href="bword://περιπλεχτήκαν">περιπλεχτήκαν</a>, third-person plural simple past of περιπλέκομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://περιπλέκω">περιπλέκω</a></div><b>περιποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>looking after, care, attention, treatment</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>περιποίηση</b> ομορφιάς για ένα λείο δέρμα. - Beauty treatment for a smooth skin.</div><b>περιποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to entertain, tend, look after, take care of</div><b>περιπολικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιπολικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patrol car</div><b>περιπολώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to patrol</div><b>περιπτεράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περιπτεράδες, feminine περιπτερού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kiosk owner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from περίπτερο</div><b>περιπτερού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιπτερούδες, masculine περιπτεράς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kiosk owner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from περίπτερο</div><b>περισπωμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περισπωμένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>circumflex ( ͂ ) (a diacritical mark, used in the polytonic script of Greek)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised feminine of the ancient participle περισπώμενος. (See the ancient περισπωμένη).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of περισπώμενος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised feminine of the ancient participle περισπώμενος. (See the ancient περισπωμένη).</div><b>περισπώμενες</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine plural of περισπώμενος</div><b>περισσεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be left over, to be to spare</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μου <b>περισσεύει</b> χρόνος. - I have no time to spare.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be superfluous</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to be more than enough, to be in surfeit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek περισσεύω.</div><b>περιστέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιστέρες, masculine περιστέρι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rock dove, rock pigeon (female)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) pigeon (term of endearment for a woman)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) dove (politician supporting peace)</div><b>περιστέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιστέρια, feminine περιστέρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rock dove (IOC preferred term), rock pigeon, Columba livia (usually used for male birds or both sexes generally)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) dove, pigeon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Ancient Greek περιστέριον, diminutive of περιστερά.</div><b>περιστασιακά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>now and then, occasionally, sometimes, every so often</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://περιστασιακός">περιστασιακός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://περιστασιακός">περιστασιακός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://περιστασιακός">περιστασιακός</a></div><b>περιστασιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιστασιακή, neuter περιστασιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>occasional</div><b>περιστασιακώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) now and then, occasionally, sometimes, every so often</div><b>περιστατικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιστατικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. event, incident</div>
  <div class='def'>2. case</div><b>περιστερά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιστερές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) pigeon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the dove (representing the Holy Spirit; the bird released by Noah)</div><b>περιστεριώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περιστεριώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://περιστερώνας">περιστερώνας</a></div><b>περιστεροτροφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιστεροτροφεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pigeon loft</div><b>περιστερόκοτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιστερόκοτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sandgrouse</div><b>περιστερώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural περιστερώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pigeonhouse, dovecote</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pigeonhole</div><b>περιστρέφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to revolve</div><b>περιστρέφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to rotate, spin, pivot, revolve</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Περιστρέφω</b> τη βίδα. - I turn the screw.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περιστρέφω. Morphologically from περι- (“around-”) + στρέφω (“turn”).</div><b>περιστροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιστροφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>revolution, a turn on an axis</div><b>περιστύλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιστύλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cloister.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek περίστῦλον.</div><b>περιτομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιτομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>circumcision</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek περιτομή.</div><b>περιτονίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peritonitis</div><b>περιτριγυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to go round, to surround</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek περιτριγυρίζω. By surface analysis, περι- (peri-) + τριγυρίζω.</div><b>περιτριγύρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>encirclement; surrounding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From περιτριγυρίζω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>περιττός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιττή, neuter περιττό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unnecessary, needless, superfluous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. odd</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περιττός, the Attic dialectal form of περισσός odd”).</div><b>περιτόναιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peritoneum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περιτόναιον.</div><b>περιφάνεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://περηφάνια">περηφάνια</a></div><b>περιφέρεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιφέρειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. circumference</div>
  <div class='def'>2. region, territory</div>
  <div class='def'>3. periphery, region (an administrative unit of a state)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. waistline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περιφέρεια, from περιφέρω, περι- (peri-, “around, surrounding”) + φέρω + -εια (-eia); verbal root from Proto-Hellenic *pʰérō (“to bear”) from Proto-Indo-European *bʰéreti.</div><b>περιφερειακή ενότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περιφερειακές ενότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>regional unit; the third-level administrative unit of Greece.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From περιφερειακός + ενότητα.</div><b>περιφερειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιφερειακή, neuter περιφερειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. regional</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>περιφερειακό</b> συμβούλιο - regional council</div>
  <div class='def'>2. circumferential</div>
  <div class='def'>3. peripheral</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pertaining to the peripheral nervous system</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>Περιφερειακό</b> Νευρικό Σύστημα - The Peripheral Nervous System</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From περιφέρει(α), “region, territory; periphery”) + -ακός from Ancient Greek περιφέρω, περι- (peri-, “around, surrounding”) + φέρω. Semantic loan from French périphérique.</div><b>περιφερειακός δρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ring road</div><b>περιφερικό νευρικό σύστημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περιφερικά νευρικά συστήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peripheral nervous system</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, περιφερικός + νευρικός + σύστημα.</div><b>περιφράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://περιφράσσω">περιφράσσω</a></div><b>περιφράσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to enclose, fence</div><b>περιφραστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περιφραστική, neuter περιφραστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>periphrastic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περιφραστικός.</div><b>περιφρονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>despise, contemn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek περιφρονέω.</div><b>περιφρόνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) scorn, contempt, disdain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περῐφρόνησῐς.</div><b>περιωπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) standing, status</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) character, reputation, image</div><b>περνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to pass, go past</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to outrun, go past, overtake</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to pass through, penetrate, thread, go through</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to pass through, penetrate, thread, go through</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to put on (clothing)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to coat (paint, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. to while (to pass time idly), spend (time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> περν-ώ + -άω. From Byzantine Greek and Koine Greek περνῶ, from Ancient Greek περάω.</div><b>περνιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be passed</div>
  <div class='ex'>H κλωστή δεν <b>περνιέται</b> στη βελόνα. - The thread does not pass through the needle.</div><b>περνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://περνάω">περνάω</a></div><b>περουβιανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περουβιανή, neuter περουβιανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Peruvian (relating to Peru or its people)</div><b>περούκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περούκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wig, peruke</div><b>περπάτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural περπατήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. walking</div>
  <div class='def'>2. walk (a manner of walking; a person&#x27;s style of walking)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: βάδισμα (vádisma)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek περπάτημα. By surface analysis, περπατώ + -μα (-ma).</div><b>περπατάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to walk, stroll</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern περπατώ περπατ- + -άω, inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek περπατῶ, from the Ancient Greek περιπατῶ/περιπατέω &lt; περι- (peri-) + πατῶ, contracted form of πατέω.</div><b>περπατημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine περπατημένη, neuter περπατημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) walked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) experienced in too many love affairs, who has been around</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of περπατιέμαι, passive voice of περπατάω, περπατώ (“walk”).</div><b>περπατιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be walked</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός ο δρόμος δεν <b>περπατιέται</b>. Είναι γεμάτος χώμα και πέτρες. - This road cannot be walked. It is full of dirt and stones.</div><b>περπατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://περπατάω">περπατάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek περπατῶ, and see περπατάω.</div><b>περσικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Persian (language)</div><b>περσικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περσική, neuter περσικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Persian (pertaining to Persia, its people or language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Περσίς.</div><b>περσινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine περσινή, neuter περσινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of last year (occurring during or concerning the previous year)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>περσινή</b> παράσταση ήταν καλύτερη από τη φετινή. - Last year&#x27;s performance was better than this year&#x27;s.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek περυσινός, from πέρυσι + -ηνός.</div><b>περφορατέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) perforator, hole punch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> French perforateur</div><b>περόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural περόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fibula (a bone in the leg)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pin (used for fastening cloth etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pin (used in mechanisms)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τράβηξε την <b>περόνη</b> της χειροβομβίδας και την πέταξε. - He pulled the grenade&#x27;s pin and threw it.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably a borrowing from Ancient Greek περόνη. Cf. the inherited diminutive πιρούνι.</div><b>πεσιμιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πεσιμιστές, feminine πεσιμίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pessimist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transfer or calque of French pessimiste, from Latin pessimus (“worst”) + -iste &lt; -ιστής</div><b>πεσκίρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεσκίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(vernacular) towel for the face</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پیشگیر from Classical Persian پیشگیر + suffix for neuter nouns -ι (-i).</div><b>πεστιμάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεστιμάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) apron</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پشتمال, from Classical Persian پشتمال. For semantics compare Albanian peshtamall (“apron”).</div><b>πετάγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to jump, jump up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to dart, nip, dash (move quickly and possibly unexpectedly)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Alternative passive of πετάω.</div><b>πετάγομαι σαν την πορδή</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, informal) to stick one&#x27;s oar in, stick one&#x27;s nose into (to join a conversation or get involved without having been asked)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ξαφνικά, <b>πετάχτηκε</b> η Μαριώ <b>σαν την πορδή</b> και άρχισε να λέει τα δικά της. - Suddenly, Mario stuck her oar in and started talking about her own stuff.</div><b>πετάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pedal</div><b>πετάμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετάμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>petametre</div><b>πετάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to fly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to throw</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to throw away, drop</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to waste</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to thrown away; drop by</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to thrown away; drop by; see: <a href="bword://πετάγομαι">πετάγομαι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πετ(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω. From Koine Greek πετῶ, contracted form of πετάω from Ancient Greek πέταμαι, from Proto-Indo-European *péth₂eti (“to fly”) from the root *peth₂- (literally “to spread one’s wings”).</div><b>πεταλίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεταλίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (broadly) barnacle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. limpet</div><b>πεταλούδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεταλούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. butterfly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. butterfly stroke</div>
  <div class='def'>3. wing nut, butterfly nut</div>
  <div class='def'>4. crucian carp</div>
  <div class='def'>5. butterfly knife; balisong</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πεταλούδα, of uncertain further origin. Probably from an earlier unattested *πεταλίδα with change of suffix to -ούδα, from the root underlying Ancient Greek πέτᾰλον + -ίδα, with possible influence from the marginally-attested πετηλίς. Alternatively, from Ancient Greek πετάννυμι, which stems from the same Indo-European root as πέτᾰλον above.</div><b>πεταλωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πεταλωτήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>farrier</div><b>πεταλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shoe (a horse)</div><b>πετεινός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πετεινοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(poetic, uncommon) cock, rooster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πετεινός from πέτομαι from Proto-Indo-European *péth₂eti from the root *peth₂- (literally “to spread one’s wings”).</div><b>πετιμέζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετιμέζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. very sweet grape syrup, made by boiling the must</div>
  <div class='def'>2. something excessively sweet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پكمز (pekmez), پتمز (petmez), from Classical Persian بگماز.</div><b>πετοσφαίριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) volleyball</div><b>πετράδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετράδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>precious or semi-precious stone, gem</div><b>πετρέλαιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετρέλαια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>petroleum, oil</div><b>πετρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πετρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peregrine falcon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πέτρα + -της (-tis).</div><b>πετραρχικό σονέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετραρχικά σονέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Petrarchan sonnet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Named after Italian scholar, poet and humanist Francesco Petrarca (1304–1374), who did not invent but made large use of this form in his authorship.</div><b>πετραρχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πετραρχική, neuter πετραρχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Petrarchan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Πετράρχης + -ικός</div><b>πετραρχισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Petrarchism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Πετράρχης + -ισμός</div><b>πετρελαιαγωγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πετρελαιαγωγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oil pipeline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πετρέλαι(ο)) + αγωγός.</div><b>πετρογκάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) a small bottled gas cooking stove</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πετρο- (petro-, “petroleum”) + γκάζι</div><b>πετροδολάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετροδολάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>petrodollar</div><b>πετροκότσυφας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πετροκότσυφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Monticola saxatilis: the common rock thrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πετρο- (petro-, “rock”) + κότσυφας</div><b>πετροπέρδικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πετροπέρδικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alectoris graeca : rock partridge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πετρο- (petro-, “rock”) + πέρδικα</div><b>πετροσέλινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) parsley</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πετροσέλῑνον.</div><b>πετσέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πετσέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. towel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. napkin, serviette</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pezzetta, from pezza (“patch of cloth”).</div><b>πετσί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πετσιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. skin, pelt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. leather</div><b>πετσετέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) with the texture of towelling (of cloth)</div><b>πετυχαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to succeed, accomplish, achieve</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πέτυχα</b> τον στόχο μου - I achieved my goal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to succeed in</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πέτυχα</b> στις εξετάσεις! - I succeeded in the exams!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) come across, find by coincidence</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πέτυχα</b> στο ίντερνετ ένα βίντεο - I came across a video on the internet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πετυχαίνω, α colloquial form of Ancient Greek ἐπῐτῠγχᾰ́νω.</div><b>πετυχημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πετυχημένη, neuter πετυχημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://πετυχαίνω">πετυχαίνω</a>:; successful (of a person: having succeeded; having achieved success)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://πετυχαίνω">πετυχαίνω</a>:; successful (resulting in success; assuring, or promoting, success; having the desired effect)</div><b>πετόσφαιρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πετόσφαιρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ball used in volleyball</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://πετοσφαίριση">πετοσφαίριση</a></div><b>πετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πετάω">πετάω</a></div><b>πευκοβελόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πευκοβελόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pine needle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek: πεύκη + βελόνη.</div><b>πεφτάστρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πεφταστέρι">πεφταστέρι</a></div><b>πεφταστέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεφταστέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shooting star, meteor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πέφτω + αστέρι</div><b>πεχάμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pH meter</div><b>πεύκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πεύκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πεύκο">πεύκο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πεύκη.</div><b>πεύκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πεύκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pine tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πεύκη.</div><b>πηγάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πηγάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>well (water source)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πηγάδι, from Koine Greek πηγάδιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek πηγή.</div><b>πηγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πηγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. source, origin (person, place, thing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πλουτοπαραγωγικές <b>πηγές</b> - sources of wealth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. water source, spring</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο ποτάμι έχει τις <b>πηγές</b> του στο βουνό. - The river has its source in the mountains.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πηγή.</div><b>πηγαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to go</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to take something somewhere, transport</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορείς να με <b>πας</b> στο αεροδρόμιο; - Can you take me to the airport?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπάγω in its Hellenistic sense &quot;go&quot;, through its imperfect tense ὑπῆγον &gt; mediaeval ὑπῆγα which was wrongly analysed as ὑ- + πῆγα (instead of ὑπ- +) and a formation of a new mediaeval present tense + -αίνω, πηγαίνω, in the familiar pattern of aorist - present like ἔμαθα - μαθαίνω. The form πάω, with silenced [ɣ] for &lt;γ&gt; of mediaeval ὑπάγω.
Also see the imperative άμε!, parallel form of πήγαινε! and the mediaeval types ὑπαγαίνω, ἀπηγαίνω.</div><b>πηγαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πηγαία, neuter πηγαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. <a href="bword://ο">ο</a>f or from a well</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of or relating to source</div>
  <div class='def'>3. original</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πηγαίο</b> χιούμορ - an original sense of humour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πηγαῖος.</div><b>πηγαίος κώδικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πηγαίοι κώδικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) source code</div><b>πηγαινέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable) back and forth, toing and froing (movement forward followed by a return to the same position)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν πολύ <b>πηγαινέλα</b> στα γραφεία για να βγάλω αυτό το διαβατήριο. - There was a lot of back and forth between offices for me to get this passport.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πήγαινε + έλα</div><b>πηγούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πηγούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πιγούνι">πιγούνι</a></div><b>πηδάλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πηδάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rudder, helm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. A part of a watch used to control the spring.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek πηδάλιον</div><b>πηδάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to jump, leap (all senses)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πήδηξα</b> στη θάλασσα. - I jumped into the sea.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive, vulgar) to fuck, screw, shag, bang (have sexual intercourse)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έπαιζε την καλή γυναίκα ενώ <b>πηδούσε</b> τον άλλον. - She pretended to be a good wife while fucking the other guy.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to skip over, omit (in writing, etc, due to time constraint)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με την τρεχάλα, <b>πήδηξε</b> δύο παραγράφους. - In his haste, he skipped over two paragraphs.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to skip, jump ahead (a class or level in school due to exceptional ability)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πήδηξε</b> κατευθείαν από την τρίτη στην πέμπτη. - She skipped directly from third grade to fifth grade.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πηδῶ or πηδάω.</div><b>πηδηγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πηδηγμένη, neuter πηδηγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fucked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. skipped, omitted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of πηδιέμαι, passive voice of πηδάω, πηδώ (“I jump, skip; fuck”).</div><b>πηδημένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine πηδημένη, neuter πηδημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πηδηγμένος">πηδηγμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of πηδιέμαι, passive voice of πηδάω, πηδώ (“I jump, skip; fuck”).</div><b>πηδιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be leaped, to be skipped</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fuck</div><b>πηδώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πηδάω">πηδάω</a></div><b>πηκτινάση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pectinase</div><b>πηλίκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) quotient (the number resulting from division)</div><b>πηλοφόρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πηλοφόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hawk, mortarboard (plasterer&#x27;s tool, made of a flat surface with a handle below, used to hold an amount of plaster prior to application to the wall or ceiling being worked on)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η δουλειά κάνει τους άντρες,
Το γιαπί, το <b>πηλοφόρι</b>, το μυστρί. - Hard work makes the man,
The building site, the mortarboard, the trowel.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πηλοφόριον, diminutive of Koine Greek πηλοφόρος, from Ancient Greek πηλοφορέω / πηλοφορῶ, from πηλός + φέρω.</div><b>πηλός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πηλοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πηλός, of uncertain origin; possibly Pre-Greek.</div><b>πηνίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πηνία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coil, choke</div><b>πηρούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πηρούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πιρούνι">πιρούνι</a></div><b>πηχτή</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πηχτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brawn (UK), head cheese (US) (a meat jelly made with pork)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek πηκτή.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πηχτός">πηχτός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>πηχτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πηχτή, neuter πηχτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thick (having a viscous consistency)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: πυκνός (pyknós)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coagulated, congealed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πηκτός.</div><b>πι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) Letter pi</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) Used in &quot;στο <a href="bword://πι">πι</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> <a href="bword://φι">φι</a>&quot; meaning at the drop of a hat.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πεῖ.</div><b>πιάνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>piano</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quietly</div><b>πιάνο με ουρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιάνα με ουρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grand piano</div><b>πιάνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be caught</div><b>πιάνομαι στα χέρια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to come to blows (to fight physically)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι διαδηλωτές <b>πιάστηκαν στα χέρια</b> με τους αστυνόμους. - The protesters came to blows with the police.</div><b>πιάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (negative, usually) to acquire, fill with, catch</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έπιασε</b> ψείρες. - She got lice.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to make an income, earn, profit, collect</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πούλησα κι <b>έπιασα</b> καλά λεφτά. - I sold it and made good money.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (literally) with one&#x27;s hands: hold, catch, grab, take, handle, hold</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πιάνω</b> το σφυγμό της. - I&#x27;m taking her pulse.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to adhere, stick, catch, anchor</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι ρόδες του αυτοκινήτου δεν μπορούν να <b>πιάσουν</b> στην άμμο. - The car&#x27;s wheels can&#x27;t get traction in the sand.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial) to rent</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to occupy, fill</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχεις <b>πιάσει</b> όλο το κρεβάτι με τις ποδάρες σου. - You&#x27;re taking up the entire bed with your big feet.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (of signals perceived by the senses) to catch, get, perceive, understand</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με την κεραία της τηλεόρασης, <b>πιάνουμε</b> τα τοπικά κανάλια. - With the TV antenna, we get local channels.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. to take over, occur to oneself, happen to oneself, feel</div>
  <div class='ex'>Mε <b>έπιασε</b> φαγούρα. - I&#x27;m itchy.</div>
  <div class='def'>9. to attempt, tackle, start, take up, act effectively, get results</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έριξε μια ιδέα αλλά δεν <b>έπιασε</b>. - He threw out an idea, but it didn&#x27;t catch on.</div>
  <div class='def'>10. to reach</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πιάνει</b> τα 100 χιλιόμετρα σε δέκα δευτερόλεπτα. - It can go 100 km in 10 seconds.</div>
  <div class='def'>11. to bribe</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα <b>έπιασε</b> ο διαιτητής. - The referee was bribed.</div>
  <div class='def'>12. (passive) get help, assistance</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου δεν είχα από πού να <b>πιαστώ</b>. - I didn&#x27;t have anyone to help me.</div>
  <div class='def'>13. (passive) to fight, to be involved, quarrel, get into a fight</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πιάνεται</b> με όλο τον κόσμο. - He fights with everyone.</div>
  <div class='def'>14. (passive) to have muscular stiffness and pain</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πιάστηκε</b> το χέρι μου από το γράψιμο. - My hand was sore from writing.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πιάνω, from earlier πιάζω by metaplasm, with reference to the past stem πιασ- (pias-) and by analogy with the unrelated verb φθάνω and its past stem φθασ- (fthas-), from Ancient Greek πιέζω.</div><b>πιάσ&#x27; τ&#x27; αβγό και κούρευ&#x27; το</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous) try to get blood from a stone (try to do something difficult, frustrating, or pointless)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι να του πάρει η ασφάλεια για την ζημιά; <b>Πιάσ’ τ’ αβγό και κούρευ’ το!</b> - What can the insurance possibly take off him? [It would be like] trying to get blood from a stone!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally &quot;catch the egg and shave it&quot;.</div><b>πιάτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιάτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plate (tableware)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) dishes (tableware that is to be or is being washed after being used to prepare, serve and eat a meal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πλένω τα <b>πιάτα</b> - I wash the dishes; I do the washing up</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) plate (the contents of a plate)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (metonymically) dish (a specific type of prepared food)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. course (a stage of a meal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πρώτο <b>πιάτο</b> - first course</div>
  <div class='def'>6. dish (a type of antenna with a similar shape to a plate or bowl)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δορυφορικό <b>πιάτο</b> - satellite dish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πιάτο from Italian piatto, from Vulgar Latin *plattum, accusative of *plattus from Ancient Greek πλατύς, a repatriated word).</div><b>πιάτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιάτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. market, marketplace</div>
  <div class='def'>2. square, area, spot</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πιάτσα</b> των ταξί - taxi rank</div><b>πιέζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) to be pressed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to be under pressure, be stressed</div><b>πιέζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to press, squeeze</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in the passive) to be under pressure, stressed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πιέζω. Doublet of πιάνω.</div><b>πια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. any more, no more, no longer, any longer (from a given time onwards)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε σε θέλω <b>πια</b>. - I don&#x27;t want you any more.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. already, now</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Γιώργος έχει μεγαλώσει <b>πια</b>. - George has now grown up.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. already (as an intensifier), at last.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ησύχασε <b>πια</b>! - Be quiet already!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Originally a synezesis to avoid linguistic hiatus, from an earlier form πλια (plia), inherited from Ancient Greek πλέα, the plural of πλέον.</div><b>πιανίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πιανίστες, feminine πιανίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pianist, piano player</div><b>πιανίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιανίστριες, masculine πιανίστας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pianist, piano player</div><b>πιατίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιατίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cymbal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian piattino (“cymbal”).</div><b>πιγγ πογγ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of πινγκ πονγκ (pingk pongk)</div><b>πιγκ πογκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ping pong, table tennis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English ping pong.</div><b>πιγκουίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πιγκουίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>penguin</div><b>πιγούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιγούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πιγούνιν, from πουγούνιν, from Koine Greek πωγώνιον, a diminutive of Ancient Greek πώγων.</div><b>πιεζοηλεκτρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πιεζοηλεκτρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>piezoelectricity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English piezoelectricity.</div><b>πιεστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιεστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. press</div>
  <div class='def'>2. printing press</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek πιεστήριον. By surface analysis, πιέζω + -τήριο.</div><b>πιθάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιθάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pot, large jar usually made of clay</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of Diogenes who lived in such a cask</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>πιθάρι</b> του Διογένη - the jar of Diogenes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek or Byzantine Greek πιθάριον, diminutive of Ancient Greek πίθος.</div><b>πιθανολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to conjecture, to hypothesize, to speculate, to suppose, to think likely/probable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πιθανολογῶ. By surface analysis, πιθανός + -λογώ.</div><b>πιθανόν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. probably (in all likelihood)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. possibly (perhaps)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πιθανόν. See πιθανώς for more.</div><b>πιθανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πιθανή, neuter πιθανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>likely, possible, potential, probable</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>πιθανός</b> ο πυρηνικός πόλεμος Ινδίας – Πακιστάν. - Nuclear war between India and Pakistan is a real possibility.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πιθανός.</div><b>πιθανότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιθανότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. possibility (the quality of being possible)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. probability, likelihood (relative chance of an event happening)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. probability (a number, between 0 and 1, expressing the precise likelihood of an event happening)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πιθανότης, equivalent to πιθανός + -ότητα.</div><b>πιθανώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. probably (in all likelihood)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. possibly (perhaps)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πιθανῶς. By surface analysis, πιθανός + -ώς.</div><b>πικ απ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πικάπ">πικάπ</a></div><b>πικάπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) electrical device for reproducing music and other sound</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Κατίνα έσκυψε και ξανάβαλε την πρίζα του <b>πικάπ</b> πίσω. - Katina bent down and plugged her record player back in.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) electrical device for reproducing music and other sound; turntable</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) electrical device for reproducing music and other sound; record player (UK)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (indeclinable) electrical device for reproducing music and other sound; phonograph (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (indeclinable) electrical device for reproducing music and other sound; gramophone (UK, dated)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English pickup.</div><b>πικράθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of πικραίνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://πικραίνω">πικραίνω</a></div><b>πικραίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to embitter, to make bitter (cause someone to feel bitterness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to sadden, to upset</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πικραίνω.</div><b>πικραλίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chicory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dandelion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Hellenistic Koine Greek πικραλίς, from the accusative singular &quot;τὴν πικραλίδα.</div><b>πικρικού οξέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of πικρικό <a href="bword://οξύ">οξύ</a></div><b>πικρικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>picric acid</div><b>πικροδάφνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πικροδάφνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oleander</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πικρο- (pikro-, “bitter”) + δάφνη</div><b>πικρομάρουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πικρομάρουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kind of chicory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πικρο- (pikro-, “bitter”) + μαρούλ(ι), “lettuce”) + -ο (-o, suffix, neuter)</div><b>πικρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πικρή, neuter πικρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bitter (taste)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bitter, sorrowful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πικρός.</div><b>πικρός καφές της παρηγοριάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πικροί καφέδες της παρηγοριάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>More elaborate form of <a href="bword://καφές">καφές</a> <a href="bword://της">της</a> παρηγοριάς.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &#x27;bitter coffee of consolation&#x27;</div><b>πιλάφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιλάφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pilaf, pilau</div>
  <div class='ex'>ατζέμ <b>πιλάφι</b> - meat pilaf</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (diminutive, slang) naval NCO</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) something meaningless</div><b>πιλίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιλίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pileus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πιλίδιον, diminutive of πῖλος.</div><b>πιλοτάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be piloted</div><b>πιλοτάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pilot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pilotare + -ω (“ending for verbs”).</div><b>πιλότος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πιλότοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pilot, aviator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pilot</div><b>πινέλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πινέλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πινέλο">πινέλο</a></div><b>πινέλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πινέλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. artist&#x27;s or decorator&#x27;s brush</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shaving brush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pennello (“brush”).</div><b>πινακίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πινακίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plaque, nameplate, shingle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. number plate, license plate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. road sign, traffic sign</div>
  <div class='def'>4. tablet (slab of rock)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek πινακίς, a diminutive of Ancient Greek πίναξ. By surface analysis, πίνακας + -ίδα.</div><b>πινακοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πινακοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. art gallery</div>
  <div class='def'>2. art collection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πίνακ(ας), “tablet, painting”) + -ο- (-o-) + -θήκη. For the modern senses, a reborrowing from German Pinakothek from Latin pinacotheca in its modern sense.</div><b>πινγκ πόνγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of πινγκ πονγκ (pingk pongk)</div><b>πινγκ-πονγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of πινγκ πονγκ (pingk pongk)</div><b>πινελιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πινελιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brushstroke (the action and its result)</div>
  <div class='ex'>βάζω τις τελευταίες <b>πινελιές</b> - to add the finishing touches</div><b>πιο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>more</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πλιό, from πλέο, from Ancient Greek πλέον. Doublet of πλέον.</div><b>πιπέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιπέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pepper (spice)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέπερι.</div><b>πιπέρι καγιέν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιπέρια καγιέν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cayenne pepper (spice)</div><b>πιπί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (childish, colloquial, uncountable) wee wee, peepee (urine)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλεις να κανείς <b>πιπί</b>, παιδί μου; - Do you want to go wee wee, my child?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (childish, colloquial, uncountable) willy, peter, peepee (penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άσε το <b>πιπί</b> σου ήσυχο! - Leave your willy alone!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>πιπίλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dummy (UK), pacifier (US) (artificial teat for babies)</div><b>πιπίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιπίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pigeon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) lolita, nymphette, jailbait</div><b>πιπεριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιπεριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pepper (fruit and plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Ancient Greek πέπερι.</div><b>πιπερόριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιπερόριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ginger (spice)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ginger (plant)</div><b>πιπερόρριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιπερόρριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πιπερόριζα">πιπερόριζα</a></div><b>πιπώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) to blow, go down on, give head, suck off (to fellate the penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το φετίχ του ήταν να βλέπουν άλλοι όταν τον <b>πίπωναν</b>. - His fetish was to have others watch when he was getting his dick sucked.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πίπα + -ώνω.</div><b>πιροσκί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pirozhki (type of crispy pastry roll, made from fried dough and containing either minced beef or sausage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Russian пирожки́.</div><b>πιρουνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιρουνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forkful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πιρούνι + -ιά.</div><b>πιρούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιρούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fork</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πιρούνιν and περούνιν, from Koine Greek περόνιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek περόνη.</div><b>πιρόγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιρόγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pirogue (canoe of shallow draft made by hollowing a log)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με μια <b>πιρόγα</b> φεύγεις και γυρίζεις,
Τις ώρες που αγριεύει η βροχή. - You set off and come back in a pirogue,
At times when the rain grows fiercer.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French pirogue.</div><b>πισίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πισίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swimming pool</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian piscina.</div><b>πισθάγκωνα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with the hands in the back (in a way that somehow his/her elbows are attached to each other)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀπισθάγκωνα.</div><b>πισινά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>buttocks, bottom</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πισινός">πισινός</a></div><b>πισινό φανάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>taillight, taillamp</div><b>πισινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πισινή, neuter πισινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>back, rear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὀπισινός with apheresis of the initial ὀ- (o-), from Ancient Greek ὀπίσω + -ῐνος. By surface analysis, πίσω + -ινός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πισινοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>behind, rear end, derriere, bum, butt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὀπισινός with apheresis of the initial ὀ- (o-), from Ancient Greek ὀπίσω + -ῐνος. By surface analysis, πίσω + -ινός.</div><b>πιστή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιστές, feminine πιστός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>believer (usually in the plural)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πιστός">πιστός</a></div><b>πιστεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be believed</div><b>πιστεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to believe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to believe; to believe in, to be a believer</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to think, assume</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πιστεύω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) convictions, beliefs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πιστεύω.</div><b>πιστολάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small gun)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dummy gun used as toy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hair-dryer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πιστολ- (pistol-, “gun”) + -άκι</div><b>πιστοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιστοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. certification (the act of certifying)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. certification, certificate (a professional qualification that certifies a person&#x27;s ability)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πιστοποίηση</b> Ελληνομάθειας - Certificate of Attainment in Greek</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πιστοποίησις. By surface analysis, πιστοποιώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>πιστοποιητικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of πιστοποιητικός</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of πιστοποιητικός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιστοποιητικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. credential</div>
  <div class='def'>2. certificate</div><b>πιστοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to certify, attest</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to vouch for</div><b>πιστωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πιστωτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>creditor</div><b>πιστωτικές κάρτες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of πιστωτική <a href="bword://κάρτα">κάρτα</a></div><b>πιστωτική κάρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιστωτικές κάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>credit card</div><b>πιστωτικοί συνεταιρισμοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πιστωτικός">πιστωτικός</a> <a href="bword://συνεταιρισμός">συνεταιρισμός</a></div><b>πιστωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πιστωτική, neuter πιστωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>credit (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πιστωτικός, from πιστόω + -τικός, from πιστός.</div><b>πιστωτικός συνεταιρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πιστωτικοί συνεταιρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>credit union</div><b>πιστόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιστόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pistol, handgun</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Czech píšťala (“firearm”, literally “tube, pipe”).</div><b>πιστόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιστόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>piston</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French piston.</div><b>πιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πιστή, neuter πιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. faithful, true</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένας πιστός φίλος (a faithful friend)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (perfect) perfect</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα πιστό αντίγραφο (a perfect copy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πιστός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πιστοί, feminine πιστή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>believer (usually in the plural)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πιστός.</div><b>πισωγλέντης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πισωγλέντηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, offensive) faggot, queer, fudgepacker (male homosexual, especially one who practices anal sex)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός είναι <b>πισωγλέντης</b>, δεν τον ενδιαφέρουν οι γυναίκες. - He&#x27;s a faggot, women don&#x27;t interest him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πίσω + γλεντώ</div><b>πισωκολλητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πισωκολλητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) doggy style</div>
  <div class='ex'>Της γυναίκας μου της αρέσει το <b>πισωκολλητό</b>. - My wife likes doing it doggy-style.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πίσω + κολλητός</div><b>πιτσαδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πιτσαδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pizza maker, pizza deliverer (job titles)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πίτσα + -αδόρος</div><b>πιτσαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πιτσαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pizzeria</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian pizzeria, equivalent to πίτσα + -αρία.</div><b>πιτσιρίκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://πιτσιρίκος">πιτσιρίκος</a></div><b>πιτσιρίκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πιτσιρίκοι, feminine πιτσιρίκα, neuter πιτσιρίκι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. little boy; nipper, kid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. little boy; poppet, moppet (endearing)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. little boy; brat (naughty)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Uncertain. Possibly from a common root to Sicilian picciriḍḍu, readapted with the Greek dimuntive ending -ίκος. Cognate with Italian piccirillo and Neapolitan piccerillo.</div><b>πιτσουνάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιτσουνάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a small pigeon)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (endearing) my darling</div>
  <div class='def'>3. and see: πιτσουνάκια (the plural)</div><b>πιτσούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. squab (baby pigeon or dove)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. squab (meat of a squab)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. endearing term, also the diminutive <a href="bword://πιτσουνάκι">πιτσουνάκι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian piccion(e) (as a dish) + ending -ι (-i). The change [o] &gt; [u]</div><b>πιωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πιωμένη, neuter πιωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drunk, intoxicated, inebriated, drunken</div><b>πιόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) pawn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French pion (“pawn”).</div><b>πιόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πιόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pion, alternative form of <a href="bword://πι">πι</a>-μεσόνιο.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English pion. Morphologically from πι or πεῖ for pi + -ιο (as in ancient -ιον (-ion) and New Latin -ium).</div><b>πλάγια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://πλάγιος">πλάγιος</a>:; nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://πλάγιος">πλάγιος</a>:; nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obliquely, indirectly</div><b>πλάγιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλάγια, neuter πλάγιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oblique, slanting, indirect, roundabout</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλάγιος.</div><b>πλάθω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to create, form, shape</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πλάθει</b> ψωμάκια, κεφτέδες, κτλ - she forms bread rolls, meatballs, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fabricate, make up</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έπλασε</b> ιστορίες - she created stories</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλάσσω.</div><b>πλάκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλάκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slab, bar, sheet, tablet (piece of material with uniform cross-section)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. paving slab, flagstone</div>
  <div class='def'>3. slab of concrete forming horizontal component of a building</div>
  <div class='def'>4. tectonic plate (part of the lithosphere)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. plaque</div>
  <div class='def'>6. joke</div><b>πλάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fallacy (erroneous opinion or belief)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spiritual delusion, deception, conceit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. plane (woodworking tool)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek πλάνη, from πλανάω.</div><b>πλάση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary, uncountable) creation (all which exists)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πλάση, from Ancient Greek πλάσις.</div><b>πλάσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blood plasma</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>πλάσμα</b> είναι το ρευστό μέρος του αίματος. - Plasma is the fluid part of blood.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. plasma (ionised gaseous phase)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πλάσμα</b> συναντάται στον Ήλιο και στους αστέρες. - Plasma is found in the Sun and the stars.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. creature, animate being</div>
  <div class='ex'>όλα τα πλάσματα του Θεού - all God&#x27;s creatures</div>
  <div class='def'>4. creature (fictional or invented)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αποκαλύφθηκε πως το <b>πλάσμα</b> ήταν ένα άσχημο μεγάλο τέρας. - The creature was revealed to be a huge ugly monster.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial) A beautiful woman.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλάσμα.</div><b>πλάστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. builder, creator, maker</div>
  <div class='def'>2. moulder, shaper (worker)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rolling pin (utensil)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλάστης.</div><b>πλάτανος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλάνατοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plane tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλάτανος, from πλατύς</div><b>πλάτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλάτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>width, breadth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλάτος.</div><b>πλέγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλέγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grid (a rectangular array of squares or rectangles)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) network</div>
  <div class='def'>3. synonym of σύμπλεγμα n</div>
  <div class='def'>4. plexus</div>
  <div class='def'>5. mesh, lattice, network, braid (a structure made of connected strands)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πλέγμα with semantic loan from French réseau (“network”) and German Komplex (“complex”).</div><b>πλέθρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλέθρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) plethron</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλέθρον.</div><b>πλέκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to knit, plait, weave</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to tangle, intertwine</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to praise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλέκω, from Proto-Hellenic *plékō, from Proto-Indo-European *pleḱ-.</div><b>πλένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to wash (your body, dishes, clothes, car, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πλένω</b> τα πιάτα - I wash the dishes; I do the washing up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to brush teeth</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to wash up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλύνω, from Proto-Indo-European *plew- (“to wash”).</div><b>πλέξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλεξίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>knitting (the process and activity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>της αρέσει το πλέξιμο (she likes knitting)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πλέκω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>πλέον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated, formal) more, -er (forms comparatives)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>πλέον</b> γνωστός ηθοποιός. - The more known actor.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. already</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>πλέον</b> αργά. - It&#x27;s already late.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. from now on</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υποσχέθηκε να είναι <b>πλέον</b> πιστός στην φιλενάδα του. - He promised to be faithful to his girlfriend from now on.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. more than</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πλέον</b> των διακοσίων χιλιάδων βγήκαν να ψηφίσουν. - More than two hundred thousand came out to vote.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλείων, comparative of πολύς. Doublet of πιο (pio).</div><b>πλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sail (in a boat)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to float</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to have something in abundance, in expressions like</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πλέω</b> σε πελάγη ευτυχίας - I sail in seas of happiness, I am happy</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) to be too large (of shoes, clothing, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλέω.
Compare Mariupol Greek плэ́ву.</div><b>πλήγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλήγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blow (an instance of forceful striking or hitting)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) blow (a damaging occurrence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πλῆγμα.</div><b>πλήθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλήθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a lot, large number, masses</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mob, crowd</div>
  <div class='def'>3. The masses</div>
  <div class='def'>4. number</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο <b>πλήθος</b> των επισκεπτών αυξάνει καθημερινά. - The number of visitors is increasing daily.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πλῆθος.</div><b>πλήκτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλήκτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. key (of keyboard instrument)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. plectrum (for playing guitar, bouzouki, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. key (of computer, typewriter, calculator, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. spur, claw on rear of fowl&#x27;s leg</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλῆκτρον.</div><b>πλήμνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hub (of a wheel)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλήμνη.</div><b>πλήξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) boredom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πλῆξις, from πλήσσω</div><b>πλήρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλήρης, neuter πλήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>complete, full</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πλήρες</b> ωράριο λειτουργίας - full operation time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλήρης, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *pleh₁-, pleh₁-r-. Compare Latin plērus.</div><b>πλήρως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fully, completely</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πλήρης + -ως (-os, “ly”)</div><b>πλήρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πληρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. filling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. satisfaction</div><b>πλήττω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be bored</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal, transitive) to strike, smite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πλήττω, Attic form of πλήσσω</div><b>πλίθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πλίνθος">πλίνθος</a></div><b>πλίθινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλίνθινη, neuter πλίνθινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πλίνθινος">πλίνθινος</a></div><b>πλίθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλίθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πλίνθος">πλίνθος</a></div><b>πλίθρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πλίνθος">πλίνθος</a></div><b>πλίνθινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλίνθινη, neuter πλίνθινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>built of mudbricks or adobe</div><b>πλίνθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλίνθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mudbrick</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) brick</div><b>πλαίσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλαίσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. frame, framework, casing (of construction, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) context, scope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλαίσιον.</div><b>πλαγιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slope of a landform that extends above the surrounding terrain:; mountainslope</div>
  <div class='def'>2. slope of a landform that extends above the surrounding terrain:; hillside</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλαγία, feminine of πλάγιος.</div><b>πλαγιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to tilt, lean (oneself)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to lie, to lie down</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to tilt, lean (something)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to sail close to the wind</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to use indirect speech</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek πλαγιάζω. The sense &quot;lean&quot;, since Medieval times.</div><b>πλαδαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλαδαρή, neuter πλαδαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flabby (also figuratively)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek πλαδαρός. The figurative sense, a semantic loan from French flasque.</div><b>πλαζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) beach (especially large and with facilities)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French plage (“beach”), from Italian piaggia (“sloped terrain”).</div><b>πλακέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) flat</div><b>πλακί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) a savoury dish of beans (or sometimes fish) cooked with oil in a flat dish in an oven</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλακίον, a diminutive of πλάξ.</div><b>πλακατζής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλακατζού, neuter πλακατζίδικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. used to describe:; a prankster</div>
  <div class='def'>2. used to describe:; a joker</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλακατζήδες, feminine πλακατζού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joker, prankster</div><b>πλακατζού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλακατζούδες, masculine πλακατζής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joker, prankster</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πλακατζής">πλακατζής</a></div><b>πλακομούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλακομούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) tribbing, tribadism, scissoring (sex act in which two women intertwine their legs and rub their vulvas against each other)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν μπήκα στο σπίτι της θειάς μου και είδα <b>πλακομούνι</b> στο κρεβάτι, έφυγα αμέσως. - When I went into my aunt&#x27;s house and saw them scissoring on the bed, I left right away.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πλακ(ώνω), “to press down, to push down on”) + -ο- (infix) + μουνί.</div><b>πλακούντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλακούντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. placenta, afterbirth (vascular organ in mammals during gestation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>πλακούντας</b> αποβάλλεται μετά τον τοκετό. - The placenta is expelled after birth.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. placenta</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a type of pie or pastry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πλακοῦς, -οῦντος, from πλάξ. Sense 1 and sense 2 are semantic loans from New Latin placenta.</div><b>πλακώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to fall down on, crash down, come down on (collapse so that the remains are on top of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η στέγη <b>πλάκωσε</b> τους εργάτες. - The roof crashed down on the workers.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) to fall, come on (to progress, to develop, to arrive)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθε η νύχτα και μας <b>πλάκωσε</b> το σκοτάδι. - Night came and darkness fell on us.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to press down, squash, crush, smother, oppress (put pressure on)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Σπύρος <b>πλάκωσε</b> τον μικρότερο αδελφό του. - Spyros crushed his younger brother (by sitting or falling on him).</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (familiar, figuratively, transitive) to depress (make depressed or sad)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα κλειστά παράθυρα του <b>πλάκωναν</b> την ψυχή. - The closed windows were depressing his soul.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive) to stuff one&#x27;s face with (to eat or drink excessively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθαν στο εστιατόριο και <b>πλάκωσαν</b> τα κρέατα και τα κρασιά. - They came to the restaurant and stuffed their faces with meat and wine.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (of males) to bonk, shag, screw (to have sexual intercourse with)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πόσες γυναίκες έχει <b>πλακώσει</b> τώρα ο Αλέκος; - How many women has Alekos fucked now?</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (colloquial, figuratively, intransitive, transitive) to beat up; beat the shit out of (to give a severe beating to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν δεν σταματήσεις, θα σε <b>πλακώσω</b> στις μπουνιές! - If you don&#x27;t stop, I&#x27;ll beat the shit out of you!</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (figuratively, intransitive) to sweep down on, arrive (usually in great numbers or unexpectedly)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πλάκωσαν</b> όλοι οι τουρίστες στην παραλία και την καταστρέψανε. - All the tourists swooped down on the beach and ruined it.</div>
  <div class='def'>9. (figuratively, intransitive) to get stuck into (become occupied with or immersed in)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν θέλεις να περάσεις φέτος, κανόνισε να <b>πλακώσεις</b> τα βιβλία. - If you want to pass this year, make sure you get stuck into the books.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πλακώνω, from Ancient Greek πλακόω, from πλάξ.</div><b>πλανήτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλανήτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>planet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλᾰνήτης.</div><b>πλανόδιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλανόδια, neuter πλανόδιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>itinerant</div><b>πλασιέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) salesman, representative, rep</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French placier (“representative, canvasser”).</div><b>πλαστικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (countable and uncountable, plural πλαστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) plastic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Internationalism. By surface analysis, πλαστικός + -ο (-o, neuter suffix).</div><b>πλαστογράφηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forgery, counterfeiting (the act or crime)</div><b>πλαστογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλαστογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the act of forgery or fabrication</div><b>πλαστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλαστή, neuter πλαστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. invented, artificial</div>
  <div class='def'>2. false, spurious, fictitious, counterfeit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλαστός. By surface analysis, πλάθω + -τός.</div><b>πλατάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλατάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πλάτανος">πλάτανος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a diminutive form of Ancient Greek πλάτανος.</div><b>πλατίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλατίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. platinum</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>πλατίνα</b> είναι ένα πολύτιμο μέταλλο. - Platinum is a precious metal.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contact breaker (electrical switch in internal combustion engines)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>πλατίνες</b> έχουν φθαρεί. - The contact breakers have been damaged.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian platina or from French platine Ultimately from Spanish platina (“platinum”).</div><b>πλατεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλατείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. town square</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the place with seats in front and about the same level as the stage</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) people in these seats</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From feminine adjective of Ancient Greek πλατεῖα: &quot;wide street&quot;) of πλατύς.
# (town square) Semantic loan from French place from Latin platea from Ancient Greek πλατεῖα of πλατύς. Also see the colloquial synonym πιάτσα from Italian.
# (theater stalls) Semantic loan from Italian platea from Latin platea from Ancient Greek plural neuter πλατέα of πλατύς
Both, repatriated loanwords.</div><b>πλατεῖα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) polytonic spelling: nominative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πλατύς">πλατύς</a>: wide</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Polytonic spelling of <a href="bword://πλατεία">πλατεία</a></div><b>πλατιά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πλατύς">πλατύς</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Older form of nominative: πλατεῖα</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πλατύς">πλατύς</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Older form of nominative: πλατέα</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(absolute, no superlative) widely</div>
  <div class='ex'>Older form: πλατέως</div><b>πλατομαντήλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>burdock</div><b>πλατυέλμινθας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλατυέλμινθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. platyhelminth, flatworm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) Platyhelminthes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from translingual Platyhelminthes, as πλατύς + έλμινθα + -ας (-as, masculine nominative suffix).</div><b>πλατφόρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλατφόρμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. platform, podium</div>
  <div class='def'>2. platform (in railway station)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trailer platform (to transport goods)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. platform (position on policy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French plate-forme.</div><b>πλατωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλατωνική, neuter πλατωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Platonic (relating to Plato)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Platonic (of a personal relationship: not sexual)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek Πλατωνικός, from Ancient Greek Πλάτων.</div><b>πλατό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλατά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stage, set</div>
  <div class='def'>2. studio, stage, set</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A reborrowing from French plateau (“stage, set”), from Latin *plattus (“flattened”), from Ancient Greek πλατύς</div><b>πλατύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wide, broad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλατύς.</div><b>πλατύφυλλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλατύφυλλα, neuter πλατύφυλλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>broad-leaved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πλατύφυλλος.</div><b>πλαφόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) ceiling, cap (the upper limit of an object or action)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) ceiling (the maximum permitted level in a financial transaction)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French plafond.</div><b>πλείστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλείστη, neuter πλείστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) many, most</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλεῖστος, superlative of πολύς.</div><b>πλειονοψηφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλειονοψηφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less frequent form of of <a href="bword://πλειοψηφία">πλειοψηφία</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>and see μειοψηφία (meiopsifía)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek πλειονοψηφία,), from πλείων (stem πλειονο- (pleiono-)) + ψῆφ(ος)) + -ία</div><b>πλειονότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>majority</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>πλειονότητα</b> των μαθητών σε αυτό το σχολείο παίζει ποδόσφαιρο. - The majority of the students in this school play football.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλειονότης.</div><b>πλειοψηφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλειοψηφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. majority of votes</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the one who has the majority of votes (e.g. at parliament)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο αρχηγός της <b>πλειοψηφίας</b> - the majority leader</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (general) majority</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>πλειοψηφία</b> των μαθητών σε αυτό το σχολείο παίζει ποδόσφαιρο. - The majority of the students in this school play football.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek πλειοψηφία, form of πλειονοψηφία, from πλείων (stem πλειο- (pleio-)) + ψῆφ(ος)) + -ία</div><b>πλειστάκις</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) often, many times</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλειστάκις.</div><b>πλειστηριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to auction, sell by auction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλειστηριάζω (“raise the price”).</div><b>πλειστηριασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλειστηριασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>auction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek πλειστηριασμός (“increase of price”).</div><b>πλειστοκαινικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλειστοκαινική, neuter πλειστοκαινικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Pleistocene</div><b>πλειστόκαινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλειστόκαινη or πλειστόκαινος, neuter πλειστόκαινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Pleistocene</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>πλειστόκαινη</b> εποχή - the Pleistocene epoch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See Πλειστόκαινο.</div><b>πλειόκαινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλειόκαινη or πλειόκαινος, neuter πλειόκαινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Pliocene, Pleiocene</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>πλειόκαινη</b> εποχή - the Pleiocene epoch</div><b>πλεμόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλεμόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πνευμόνι">πνευμόνι</a></div><b>πλεονάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be superfluous, more than enough</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be in the majority</div><b>πλεονέκτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλεονεκτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. profit, gain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. advantage, boon (something offering the chance of gain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλεονέκτημα, from πλεονεκτέω + -μα (-ma), from πλέον + ἔχω.</div><b>πλεονέκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλεονέκτες, feminine πλεονέκτρια or πλεονέκτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greedy person, grasping person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πλεονέκτης.</div><b>πλεονεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>advantageous</div><b>πλεονεκτικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) acquisitiveness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πλεονεκτικός + -ότητα.</div><b>πλεονεξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) greed, avarice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πλεονεξία.</div><b>πλευρονήκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλευρονήκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flatfish, (plaice, flounder, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πλευρό + νήκτης</div><b>πλευρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλευρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rib, flank, side of a torso</div>
  <div class='def'>2. side</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλευρόν.</div><b>πληγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πληγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wound (an injury, such as a cut, stab, or tear, to a (usually external) part of the body)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) wound</div>
  <div class='ex'>ξύνω παλιές <b>πληγές</b> - to reopen old wounds</div>
  <div class='def'>3. plague (a widespread affliction, calamity or destructive influx, especially when seen as divine retribution)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πληγή.</div><b>πληγώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to wound, to hurt, to injure</div><b>πληθ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://πληθυντικός">πληθυντικός</a></div><b>πληθυντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πληθύνω.</div><b>πληθυσμιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πληθυσμιακή, neuter πληθυσμιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) population (attributive)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πληθυσμιακή</b> ομάδα - population group</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πληθυσμ(ός)) + -ιακός.</div><b>πληθυσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πληθυσμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>population</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πληθυσμός, from Ancient Greek πληθυσμός.</div><b>πληθωρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πληθωρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inflation</div><b>πληθωριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πληθωριστική, neuter πληθωριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inflationary</div><b>πληθώρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) plethora, abundance, profusion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πληθώρα.</div><b>πληκτρολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to key in, type, use a keyboard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πλῆκτρον”), λόγος.</div><b>πληκτρολόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πληκτρολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. keyboard (of computer, typewriter, calculator, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. keyboard (of piano, accordion, etc)</div><b>πλημμέλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλημμελήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misdemeanour (crime category, punishable by fines and/or imprisonment of ten days to five years or by incarceration in a reformatory for underage criminals)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κλοπή αμαξιού αποτελεί <b>πλημμέλημα</b>. - Stealing a car is a misdemeanour.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλημμελής, from πλήν + μέλος. First attested (modern sense) 1833.</div><b>πλημμυρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλημμυρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high tide, flood tide</div><b>πλημμυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to flood</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to swarm</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to be flooded, to be inundated</div><b>πλημμυρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλημμυρική, neuter πλημμυρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flood</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πλημμυρικός</b> κίνδυνος - flood danger</div><b>πλημμύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλημμύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flood, inundation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) flood, superabundance</div><b>πλημυρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλημυρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πλημμυρίδα">πλημμυρίδα</a></div><b>πλημύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλημύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πλημμύρα">πλημμύρα</a></div><b>πλην</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) minus sign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλήν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minus (less)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλήν.</div><b>πληροφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πληροφορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>information</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek πληροφορία, from πλήρης + φέρω. For the modern sense of &quot;information&quot;, semantic loan from French renseignement or enseignement.</div><b>πληροφορική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>computer science, informatics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πληροφορία. Calque of French informatique.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of πληροφορικός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πληροφορία. Calque of French informatique.</div><b>πληροφοριοδότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πληροφοριοδότες, feminine πληροφοριοδότρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>informer, informant</div><b>πληροφοριοδότρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πληροφοριοδότριες, masculine πληροφοριοδότης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>informer, informant</div><b>πληροφορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to inform</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πληροφορῶ, with semantic loan from French renseigner and enseigner.</div><b>πληροφόρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>information (the act of informing or imparting knowledge)</div>
  <div class='ex'>προς <b>πληροφόρησή</b> σας - for your information</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πληροφόρησις with semantic adaptation to the modern meaning of πληροφορώ. By surface analysis, πληροφόρη) + -ση (-si).</div><b>πληρωμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πληρωμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>payment, defrayment</div><b>πληρωτέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πληρωτέα, neuter πληρωτέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>payable (due to be paid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πληρωτέον n with semantic loan from French and English payable. By surface analysis, πληρώ(νω)) + -τέος.</div><b>πληρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) to fulfil (UK), to fulfill (US), to meet (to satisfy; to comply with: conditions, requirements, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ανταποκρίνομαι (antapokrínomai)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πληρῶ).</div><b>πληρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to pay (give money for work done or goods received)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to pay, sacrifice something in exchange for a benefit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to pay (suffer) for past action</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πληρόω. For semantic development, compare Hebrew שילם / שִׁלֵּם.</div><b>πλησίον</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) fellow man</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>near, nearby</div><b>πλησιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(not common passive, with special meaning) to be approached, contacted</div><b>πλησιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to approach, bring, move closer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to approach (to bribe or persuade)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to approach (for sexual purposes)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. <a href="bword://πλησιάζομαι">πλησιάζομαι</a>: I am approached, contacted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλησιᾰ́ζω.</div><b>πλησιέστερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλησιέστερη, neuter πλησιέστερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with definite article) closest, nearest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πλησιέστερος, comparative degree of πλησίος, with semantic loan from French le plus proche.</div><b>πλιάτσικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλιάτσικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plundering, looting, theft</div>
  <div class='def'>2. loot, booty, plunder</div>
  <div class='def'>3. theft, stealing (in general)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian plaçkë.</div><b>πλιατσικολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to plunder, loot, pillage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πλιάτσικο + -λογώ.</div><b>πλιγούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλιγούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bulgur (coarsely ground wheat)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish بلغور.</div><b>πλιθάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πλίνθος">πλίνθος</a></div><b>πλινθοδομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>building of brick</div><b>πλινθοδομώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) To build using bricks.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, πλίνθος + δομώ. Compare πλινθοδομή.</div><b>πλινθοκτίστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bricklayer</div><b>πλοήγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>navigation (the act of navigating)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: (navigation by sea) ναυσιπλοΐα (nafsiploḯa)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πλοηγώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>πλοία πολεμικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of πολεμικό <a href="bword://πλοίο">πλοίο</a></div><b>πλοίαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πλοίαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>captain in the Royal Navy and in the US navy, a rank with the NATO grade OF-5</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πλοίο + -αρχος (-archos), the latter component from άρχω.</div><b>πλοίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλοία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ship, large boat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλοῖον, from the o-grade of the root of πλέω + -ιον (-ion), or inherited directly from Proto-Indo-European *plówyom (“floating vessel”).</div><b>πλοηγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πλοηγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pilot</div><b>πλοκάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tentacle</div><b>πλουραλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pluralism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pluralism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French pluralisme (“pluralism”), suffixed with -ισμός.</div><b>πλουτοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) plutocracy (rule by the wealthy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλουτοκρατία. Equivalent to πλούτος + -κρατία.</div><b>πλουτοκρατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλουτοκρατική, neuter πλουτοκρατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plutocratic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πλουτοκρατία + -ικός.</div><b>πλουτώνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) plutonium</div><b>πλούσια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lavishly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πλούσιος">πλούσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πλούσιος">πλούσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πλούσιος">πλούσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. nominative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πλούσιος">πλούσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>5. accusative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πλούσιος">πλούσιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>6. vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πλούσιος">πλούσιος</a></div><b>πλούσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πλούσια, neuter πλούσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rich, wealthy, luxuriant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλούσιος.</div><b>πλούτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πλούτος">πλούτος</a> m</div><b>πλούτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πλούτη n pl or πλούτια n pl)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wealth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. affluence, richness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πλοῦτος.</div><b>πλυντήρια πιάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πλυντήριο">πλυντήριο</a> πιάτων</div><b>πλυντήρια ρούχων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πλυντήριο">πλυντήριο</a> ρούχων</div><b>πλυντήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλυντήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. washing machine, washer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dishwasher, washer</div>
  <div class='def'>3. laundry, laundry room (facility for laundering)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. car wash</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter substantive of Ancient Greek πλυντήριος, from πλύνω + -τήρῐος, with semantic loan from English washing machine.</div><b>πλυντήριο πιάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλυντήρια πιάτων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dishwasher</div><b>πλυντήριο ρούχων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλυντήρια ρούχων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>washing machine (for clothing, etc)</div><b>πλωτάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πλωτάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the rank of lieutenant commander in the Royal Navy and US navy with the NATO grade OF-3</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek πλωτάρχης, from the Ancient Greek πλωτ(ός), “navigable, floating”) + -άρχης.</div><b>πλύσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλυσίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>washing, cleaning (using water, etc to clean something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πλένω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>πλύσιμο σόδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) washing soda (sodium bicarbonate)</div><b>πλώρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πλώρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bow, prow</div>
  <div class='ex'>βάζω <b>πλώρη</b> για - to set course for (destination, objective)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πλώρα with change of -α to -η by analogy with πρύμη, from Ancient Greek πρῷρα. Doublet of πρώρα.</div><b>πμ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of π.μ. (p.m.)</div><b>πνέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to blow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πνέω.</div><b>πνίγηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of πνίγομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://πνίγω">πνίγω</a></div><b>πνίγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of creatures) to drown, smother, choke (to asphyxiate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δέκα άτομα <b>πνίγηκαν</b> εξαιτίας του τσουνάμι. - Ten people drowned because of the tsunami.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prevent growth</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα ζιζάνια <b>έπνιξαν</b> τα λουλούδια. - Weeds smothered the flowers.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) prevent manifestation, hide expression</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο παχύ χαλί <b>έπνιγε</b> τον ήχο των βημάτων τους. - The thick carpet muffled the sound of their footsteps.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) overwhelm, fill, suffocate</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πνιγήκαμε</b> στη σκόνη. - We suffocated from the dust.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) suffocate, choke, cause intense mental discomfort, tension, distress</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορώ να κάτσω μέσα στο σπίτι· <b>πνίγομαι</b>! - I can&#x27;t sit inside the house; I&#x27;m suffocating!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πνῑ́γω.</div><b>πνευματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πνευματική, neuter πνευματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mental (relating to the mind)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ghostly</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pneumatic</div>
  <div class='def'>4. spiritual</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πνευματικός.</div><b>πνευμονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πνευμονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pneumonia</div><b>πνευμονική εμβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πνευμονικές εμβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pulmonary embolism</div><b>πνευμονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πνευμονική, neuter πνευμονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pulmonary</div><b>πνευμονογαστρικό νεύρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vagus nerve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, πνευμονογαστρικός + νεύρο.</div><b>πνευμονολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pulmonology</div><b>πνευμονολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πνευμονολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pneumologist, pulmonologist</div><b>πνευμονοπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πνευμονοπάθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lung disease, pulmonary disease</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πνευμονικός + -πάθεια</div><b>πνευμονοϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pneumovirus</div><b>πνευμόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πνευμόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lung as sold meat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://πνεύμονας">πνεύμονας</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πνευμόνιον.</div><b>πνευστά όργανα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πνευστό">πνευστό</a> <a href="bword://όργανο">όργανο</a></div><b>πνευστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πνευστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wind, blown (instrument)</div><b>πνευστό όργανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πνευστά όργανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wind instrument</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πνευστο</b> μουσικό <b>όργανο</b> - wind instruments</div><b>πνεύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πνεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spirit, the (soul)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spirit, the immaterial element of human being in contrast to the body</div>
  <div class='def'>3. spirit (a spiritual being)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. spirit (the special character or meaning of something)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. intellect</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι τιτάνες της τέχνης και του <b>πνεύματος</b> - the titans of art and spirit (intellect/culture)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. humor</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάνω <b>πνεύμα</b> - I tell a joke</div>
  <div class='def'>7. a diacritic used in the polytonic writing system of Greek language indicating a smooth or rough breathing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek πνεῦμα.</div><b>πνεύμονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πνεύμονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lung</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) area with clean fresh air</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πνεύμων.</div><b>πνικτίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πνικτίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pnictogen, a member of the nitrogen group of elements</div><b>πνοή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. breath, waft, whiff (small amount of wind; a light gust of air)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: φύσημα n (fýsima)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in set expressions) breath (a single act of breathing in or out; a breathing of air)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μέχρι την τελευταία του <b>πνοή</b> - until his last breath</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) impetus</div>
  <div class='ex'>δίνω <b>πνοή</b> σε - to breathe life into, to give impetus to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πνοή with semantic loan from French haleine in the figurative &#x27;impetus&#x27; sense.</div><b>πο-πό</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) frequently with repetition of the syllable πο (po, “po”) in a variety of intonations; wow!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πό πο-πό</b>! Τι κορίτσαρος είν’ αυτός! - Wow! What a hot girl she is!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) frequently with repetition of the syllable πο (po, “po”) in a variety of intonations; yikes!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πο-πό</b>! Βρωμάει η αποχέτευση! - Yikes! The sewage stinks.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) frequently with repetition of the syllable πο (po, “po”) in a variety of intonations; oh dear! oh no!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ποοοο-πόο</b>! Έπαθε έμφραγμα, σαράντα ετών! - Oh dear! He had a heart attack, at age forty!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Uncertain. Probably from Ancient Greek interjection πόποι! (onomatopoeia).</div><b>ποίημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποιήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poem, piece of verse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποίημα.</div><b>ποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) poetry, verse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary, uncountable) poesy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποίησις, from ποιέω.</div><b>ποίησις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://ποίηση">ποίηση</a>, poetry, verse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Katharevousa) fabrication, creation, production</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Katharevousa) magical procedure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποίησις, ποιέω.</div><b>πογκρόμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pogrom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Russian погро́м.</div><b>ποδάγρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποδάγρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gout, podagra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποδᾰ́γρᾱ; gout (of human beings)”), from πούς + ἄγρᾱ.</div><b>ποδάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποδάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, familiar) leg (also mockingly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάρ’ τα <b>ποδάρια</b> σου από δω. - Μοve your legs &#x27;from here&#x27; (a bit further away)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ποδάρι, from Ancient Greek ποδάριον, diminutive of πούς.</div><b>ποδήλατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποδήλατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pedalo</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bicycle, cycle; bike (informal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πούς + ἐλαύνω + -τος (-tos).</div><b>ποδαρίλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, uncountable) foot smell, foot odour</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>ποδαρίλα</b> όταν έβγαλε τα παπούτσια του κόντεψε να μας πνίξει. - The smell of his feet when he took off his shoes nearly choked us.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ποδάρι + -ίλα.</div><b>ποδαρόδρομος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποδαρόδρομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>walk, slog (a distance walked, especially a relatively long distance that is time-consuming and/or tiring to walk)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ποδάρ(ι)) + -ό- + δρόμος.</div><b>ποδηλάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποδηλάτες, feminine ποδηλάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cyclist, bicyclist</div><b>ποδηλάτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποδηλάτισσες, masculine ποδηλάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cyclist</div><b>ποδηλασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποδηλατοδρόμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cycling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cycling, cycle racing</div><b>ποδηλατοδρομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποδηλατοδρομίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cycle race</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ποδήλατ(ο)) + -ο- (-o-) + -δρομία.</div><b>ποδηλατοδρόμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποδηλατοδρόμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>velodrome</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ποδήλατ(ο)) + -ο- (-o-) + -δρόμιο, a calque of French vélodrome.</div><b>ποδηλατοδρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποδηλατοδρόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>racing cyclist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ποδήλατ(ο)) + -ο- (-o-) + -δρόμος.</div><b>ποδηλατόδρομος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποδηλατόδρομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cycle path, cycle lane</div><b>ποδηλατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cycle</div><b>ποδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apron, pinafore</div><b>ποδοσφαιρίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποδοσφαιρίστριες, masculine ποδοσφαιριστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>footballer, football player, soccer player (one who plays soccer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ποδόσφαιρο + -ίστρια, calque of English footballer.</div><b>ποδοσφαιρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ποδοσφαιρική, neuter ποδοσφαιρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to football, soccer</div><b>ποδοσφαιριστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποδοσφαιριστές, feminine ποδοσφαιρίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>footballer, football player, soccer player (one who plays soccer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ποδόσφαιρο + -ιστής, calque of English footballer. First attested 1896.</div><b>ποδοσφαιροποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποδοσφαιροποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) footballisation (UK), footballization (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ποδόσφαιρο + -ποίηση, calque of English footballization.</div><b>ποδόγυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποδόγυροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hem of a skirt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) female sex, women</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ποδό- + γύρος.</div><b>ποδόσφαιρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποδόσφαιρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>football, soccer, association football</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English football with ποδο- (podo-, “from Ancient Greek πούς, ποδός”) + -σφαιρο”). First attested 1895.</div><b>ποζιτρόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποζιτρόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>positron, antielectron</div><b>ποθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to desire, to crave</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to lust after</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ποθῶ, contracted form of ποθέω.</div><b>ποιήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποιήτριες, masculine ποιητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female poet, poetess</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ποιήτρια.</div><b>ποιηματάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποιηματάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://ποίημα">ποίημα</a> n</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the ποιηματ- stem of ποίημα + -άκι.</div><b>ποιητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποιητές, feminine ποιήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. poet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in ecclesiastic texts as in the Credo of the Nicene Creed) maker: see the ancient <a href="bword://ποιητής">ποιητής</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Πιστεύω εἰς ἕνα Θεόν, Πατέρα, Παντοκράτορα, <b>ποιητὴν</b> οὐρανοῦ καὶ γῆς - I believe in one God, the Father Almighty, the maker^((accusative singular)) of heaven and earth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ποιητής.</div><b>ποιητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ποιητική, neuter ποιητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poetic, poetical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποιητικός, from ποιέω.</div><b>ποικίλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to variegate (to change the appearance of something, especially by covering with patches or streaks of different colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to vary (to institute a change in, from a current state; to modify)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to vary (to display differences)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ποικίλλω.</div><b>ποικίλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ποικίλη, neuter ποικίλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. varied</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ποικίλη</b> ύλη - with variety of material</div>
  <div class='def'>2. variegated, motley</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lavishly ornamented</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ποικίλος.</div><b>ποικίλως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, literary) in various ways</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ποικίλως.</div><b>ποικιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποικιλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. variety, selection</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το καλοκαίρι βλέπεις στις παραλίες μια <b>ποικιλία</b> ανθρώπων. - You see a variety of people on the beach in summer.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a selection of snacks, appetizers or other tidbits, served as a dish (menu item)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παραγγείλαμε μια <b>ποικιλία</b> για τρία άτομα. - We ordered a selection for three people.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. variety</div>
  <div class='ex'>μια ιταλική <b>ποικιλία</b> ντομάτας - an Italian variety of tomato</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποικίλος.</div><b>ποικιλότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποικιλότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diversity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ποικίλος + -ότητα.</div><b>ποιμένας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποιμένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) shepherd</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pastor; priest (shepherd of the people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποιμήν.</div><b>ποιμενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ποιμενική, neuter ποιμενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shepherd&#x27;s, pastoral (of or pertaining to shepherds)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ποιμενικός. By surface analysis, ποιμένας + -ικός.</div><b>ποινή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποινές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>punishment, sentence, penalty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποινή.</div><b>ποινικοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποινικοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>criminalisation (UK), criminalization (US)</div><b>ποινικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ποινική, neuter ποινικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>criminal, penal, punitive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ποινή + -ικός. Calque of French pénal.</div><b>ποιος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine (feminine ποια, neuter ποιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. who</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ποιος</b> είναι ο επισκέπτης; - Who is the caller?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. which</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ποιο</b> βιβλίο θέλεις; - Which book do you want?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in accusative case) who, whom</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε <b>ποιον</b> το έδωσες; - To whom did you give it?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in genitive case) whose</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ποιανού</b> είναι η πετσέτα; - Whose is the towel?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποῖος, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷís.</div><b>ποιος θα φυλάξει τους φύλακες</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who watches the watchers, literally: who will guard the guardians</div><b>ποιοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ποιοτική, neuter ποιοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. qualitative</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ποιοτικός</b> έλεγχος’’ - quality control</div>
  <div class='def'>2. of good quality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ποιότης + -ικός. Calque of French qualitatif.</div><b>ποιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quality; attribute (differentiating property)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κακής <b>ποιότητας</b> - shoddy, of poor quality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ποιότης, from the accusative singular &quot;τὴν ποιότητα&quot;, from ποιός, from ποῖος &amp; semantic loan from French qualité. Equivalent to ποι(ός)) + -ότητα.</div><b>ποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) to make, synonym of <a href="bword://κάνω">κάνω</a>, used mostly in compounds -<a href="bword://ποιώ">ποιώ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ποιῶ, contracted form of ποιέω.</div><b>πολίτευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. form of government</div>
  <div class='def'>2. polity (organizational structure and governance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολίτευμα.</div><b>πολίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πολίτες, feminine πολίτισσα or πολίτις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. citizen, resident of town or city</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αμερικανός πολίτης (American citizen)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. civilian, not of the church or armed forces</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολίτης.</div><b>πολίτικη λύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολίτικες λύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kamancheh</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πολίτικη + λύρα.</div><b>πολίτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολίτικη, neuter πολίτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Constantinopolitan</div>
  <div class='def'>2. evocative of the style or spirit of Constantinople (Istanbul)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πολίτικοι μεζέδες περιμένουν να τους απολαύσετε. - Istanbul-style mezes are waiting to be enjoyed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adjectival form of Πόλη, short for Κωνσταντινούπολη</div><b>πολίτις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολίτιδες, masculine πολίτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) citizen, resident of town or city</div><b>πολειφάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πολειφάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://απολειφάδι">απολειφάδι</a></div><b>πολεμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to engage in war, fight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to oppose, struggle against</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (for <a href="bword://πολεμάω">πολεμάω</a>) to try very hard to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern πολεμώ + -άω, from Ancient Greek πολεμῶ, contracted form of πολεμέω.</div><b>πολεμίστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>battlement, embrasure and/or merlon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πολεμίστρα, from πολεμῶ.</div><b>πολεμικό πλοίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πλοία πολεμικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>warship</div><b>πολεμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολεμική, neuter πολεμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. warlike, bellicose</div>
  <div class='def'>2. polemic, disputatious, argumentative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολεμικός. By surface analysis, πόλεμος + -ικός.</div><b>πολεμιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πολεμιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>warrior, fighter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολεμιστής.</div><b>πολεμοκάπηλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολεμοκάπηλη, neuter πολεμοκάπηλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(also) warmonger</div><b>πολεμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πολεμάω">πολεμάω</a></div><b>πολεοδομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. urban planning (the conscious design of towns and cities)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. urban planning (the science of such design)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πολεοδόμ(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>πολικές αρκούδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of πολική <a href="bword://αρκούδα">αρκούδα</a></div><b>πολική αρκούδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολικές αρκούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polar bear (Ursus maritimus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πολικός + αρκούδα.</div><b>πολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολική, neuter πολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polar, Arctic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French polaire (“polar”).</div><b>πολιορκία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολιορκίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>siege, military blockade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολιορκίᾱ.</div><b>πολιορκητικοί κριοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πολιορκητικός">πολιορκητικός</a> <a href="bword://κριός">κριός</a></div><b>πολιορκητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολιορκητική, neuter πολιορκητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>besieging, siege</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πολιορκητική</b> μηχανή - siege engine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολῐορκέω (ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *tpelH- (“city; fortification”)) + ἕρκος)) + -τῐκός.</div><b>πολιορκητικός κριός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πολιορκητικοί κριοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>battering ram</div><b>πολιορκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lay siege to, siege, besiege</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to mob, beset</div><b>πολιούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patron saint (of a city)</div><b>πολισμάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, dated) policeman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English policeman.</div><b>πολιτεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολιτείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. state</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι 50 <b>πολιτείες</b> των ΗΠΑ - the 50 states of the USA</div>
  <div class='def'>2. city, place</div>
  <div class='ex'>η χαμένη <b>πολιτεία</b> του Μάτσου Πίκτσου - the lost city of Machu Picchu</div>
  <div class='def'>3. government, republic</div>
  <div class='def'>4. body politic, polity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολιτεία.</div><b>πολιτειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολιτειακή, neuter πολιτειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. constitutional, state</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun) state</div><b>πολιτικά κόμματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of πολιτικό <a href="bword://κόμμα">κόμμα</a></div><b>πολιτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολιτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>politics, policy</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πολιτικός">πολιτικός</a></div><b>πολιτική ανυπακοή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) civil disobedience</div><b>πολιτικό κόμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πολιτικά κόμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>political party</div><b>πολιτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολιτική, neuter πολιτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>political, civic, civilian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολῑτῐκός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πολιτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>politician (one engaged in politics)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Μελίνα Μερκούρη ήταν πρώτη Ελληνίδα <b>πολιτικός</b> που ... - Melina Mercouri was the first (female) Greek politician who ...</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολῑτῐκός.</div><b>πολιτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πολιτισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>culture, civilisation, way of life</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο αρχαίος ελληνικός πολιτισμός (ancient Greek civilisation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πολιτισμός, from Ancient Greek πολίτης + -ισμός.</div><b>πολιτιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολιτιστική, neuter πολιτιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cultural</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε χρόνο μία ευρωπαϊκή πόλη γίνεται η <b>πολιτιστική</b> πρωτεύουσα της Ευρώπης. - Every year one European city becomes the cultural capital of Europe.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English cultural. Morphologically from πολιτι(σμός), “civilisation”) + -τικός.</div><b>πολιτογράφηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολιτογραφήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. naturalization (the action of naturalizing somebody; act of granting citizenship)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. naturalization (the admission or adoption of foreign words or customs into general use)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πολιτογραφώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>πολιτογραφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to naturalize (to grant citizenship to someone not born a citizen)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to naturalize (to make (a word) a natural part of the language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πολιτογραφῶ.</div><b>πολλά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πολύς">πολύς</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πολύς">πολύς</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot dialect form of <a href="bword://πολύ">πολύ</a></div><b>πολλάκις</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>often, many times</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολλάκις. By surface analysis, πολύς + -άκις.</div><b>πολλαπλά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πολλαπλός">πολλαπλός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in multiple ways</div><b>πολλαπλασιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to increase, multiply</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to multiply</div><b>πολλαπλασιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πολλαπλασιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. proliferation, amplification</div>
  <div class='def'>2. multiplication</div><b>πολλαπλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολλαπλή, neuter πολλαπλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>multiple, manifold</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολλαπλοῦς, from πολλᾰ́ + -πλόος.</div><b>πολλοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the majority; most people</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the masses</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative masculine plural of <a href="bword://πολύς">πολύς</a></div><b>πολυάνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυάνθρωπη, neuter πολυάνθρωπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. populous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crowded</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (rare) large, numerous (group of people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολῠάνθρωπος. By surface analysis, πολυ- (poly-) + άνθρωπος.</div><b>πολυάσχολος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυάσχολη, neuter πολυάσχολο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>busy with many things</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: απασχολημένος (apascholiménos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πολυάσχολος. By surface analysis, πολυ- (poly-) + ασχολ(ία)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>πολυέλαιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πολυέλαιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chandelier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πολύς + ἔλαιον</div><b>πολυαγαπημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πολυαγαπημένη, neuter πολυαγαπημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beloved, darling, dearest (much loved, dearly loved)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα γράμματά του πάντα άρχιζαν με τη φράση «<b>Πολυαγαπημένη</b> μου μητέρα». - His letters always started with the phrase &quot;My much-beloved mother&quot;.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of πολυαγαπιέμαι, passive voice of παλυαγαπάω/πολυαγαπώ. Morphologically, πολυ- (poly-, “very”) + αγαπημένος, passive perfect participle of αγαπάω/αγαπώ.</div><b>πολυβραβευμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυβραβευμένη, neuter πολυβραβευμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>multi-award-winning</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πολυ- (poly-) + βραβευμένος.</div><b>πολυβόλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πολυβόλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>machine gun</div><b>πολυδιαβασμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυδιαβασμένη, neuter πολυδιαβασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. much-read, widely read (book, author, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. well-read (person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: μορφωμένος (morfoménos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πολυ- (poly-) + διαβασμένος.</div><b>πολυεθνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυεθνική, neuter πολυεθνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. multinational</div>
  <div class='def'>2. multiethnic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πολυ- (poly-) + έθν(ος)) + -ικός, a calque of English multinational.</div><b>πολυεθνοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυεθνοτική, neuter πολυεθνοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plurinational, polyethnic</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>Πολυεθνοτικό</b> Κράτος της Βολιβίας - the Plurinational State of Bolivia</div><b>πολυεπίπεδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυεπίπεδη, neuter πολυεπίπεδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. multilayered</div>
  <div class='def'>2. multilevel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πολυ- (poly-) + επίπεδ(ο)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>πολυετής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυετής, neuter πολυετές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perennial, lasting for many years</div><b>πολυθρόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυθρόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>armchair</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian poltrona (“armchair”), reanalyzed folk etymologically as πολυ- (poly-, “poly-, many”) + θρόνος.</div><b>πολυκαλλιέργεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυκαλλιέργειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polyculture (the planting of two or more crops in the same place)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πολυ- (poly-) + καλλιέργεια; calque of French polyculture.</div><b>πολυκατοικία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυκατοικίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apartment building, block of flats</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πολυ- (poly-) + κατοικία.</div><b>πολυκύμαντος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυκύμαντη, neuter πολυκύμαντο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of a body of water) turbulent, choppy (swelling with many waves)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) turbulent, eventful, with many ups and downs (life, period, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πολυ- (poly-) + the κυμαν- stem of κυμαίνω + -τος (-tos). Compare Koine Greek πολυκύματος.</div><b>πολυλεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυλεκτική, neuter πολυλεκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polylectic</div><b>πολυλογάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πολυλογάδες, feminine πολυλογού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chatterbox</div><b>πολυλογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυλογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. verbosity, wordiness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. verbiage (overabundance of words)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολυλογία.</div><b>πολυλογού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυλογούδες, masculine πολυλογάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chatterbox</div><b>πολυμάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polymathy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολυμάθεια. By surface analysis, πολυμαθ(ής)) + -εια (-eia).</div><b>πολυμέρεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυμέρειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>multilateralism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πολυ- (poly-, “multi”) + μέρος + -εια (-eia, “-ism”).</div><b>πολυμήχανος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυμήχανη, neuter πολυμήχανο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ingenious, cunning</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολυμήχανος.</div><b>πολυμερής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυμερής, neuter πολυμερές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. multilateral, multipartite</div>
  <div class='def'>2. polymeric</div>
  <div class='def'>3. multisided, multi-parted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολυμερής with semantic loan from French polymère. By surface analysis, πολυ- (poly-) + -μερής.</div><b>πολυμορφισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πολυμορφισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polymorphism</div><b>πολυνευροπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polyneuropathy</div><b>πολυπαλλόμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πολυπαλλόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πολύ + παλλόμενος, passive participle of πάλλομαι. Nominalised by ellipsis of σύμφωνο.</div><b>πολυπληθής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυπληθής, neuter πολυπληθές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>numerous, multitudinous (consisting of a large number of individuals or parts)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πολυπληθής. By surface analysis, πολυ- (poly-) + πλήθ(ος)) + -ής.</div><b>πολυπόθητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυπόθητη, neuter πολυπόθητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coveted, longed-for, much-desired</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek πολυπόθητος, By surface analysis, πολυ- (poly-) + πόθητος.</div><b>πολυσυζητημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πολυσυζητημένη, neuter πολυσυζητημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>overdiscussed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of πολυσυζητιέμαι, passive voice of πολυσυζητάω, -συζητώ (“discuss”). Analysed as πολυ- (“much”) + συζητημένος (“talked about”).</div><b>πολυσύλλαβος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυσύλλαβη, neuter πολυσύλλαβο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polysyllabic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πολυσύλλαβος. By surface analysis, πολυ- (poly-) + συλλαβ(ή)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>πολυσύχναστος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυσύχναστη, neuter πολυσύχναστο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frequented, much frequented, busy</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πολυσύχναστη</b> ταβέρνα - busy taverna</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πολυ- (poly-, “poly”) + συχνάζω + -τος (-tos, “verbal adjective suffix”). Attested since 1844.</div><b>πολυτάραχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυτάραχη, neuter πολυτάραχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turbulent, tumultuous, checkered (life, period, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πολυτάραχος. By surface analysis, πολυ- (poly-) + ταραχή + -ος (-os).</div><b>πολυτέλεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυτέλειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>luxury, indulgence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολυτέλεια, with semantic loan from French luxe.</div><b>πολυταξιδεμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυταξιδεμένη, neuter πολυταξιδεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>well-travelled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πολυ- (poly-) + ταξιδεμένος, passive perfect participle of ταξιδεύω.</div><b>πολυτελής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυτελής, neuter πολυτελές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. luxurious</div>
  <div class='def'>2. extravagant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολυτελής. From πολυ- + τέλος + -ής.</div><b>πολυτονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυτονική, neuter πολυτονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polytonic (the diacritic system used in Greece before 1982)</div><b>πολυφωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυφωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polyphony</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολυφωνία.</div><b>πολυχρωμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολυχρωμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colourfulness (UK), colorfulness (US)</div><b>πολυόμματος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολυόμματη, neuter πολυόμματο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>many-eyed; having many eyes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολυόμμᾰτος. The noun sense is a semantic loan from translingual Polyommatus.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πολυόμματοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Polyommatus-genus butterfly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολυόμμᾰτος. The noun sense is a semantic loan from translingual Polyommatus.</div><b>πολυώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πολυώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polynomial</div><b>πολωνέζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολωνέζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. polonaise; Polish dance in triple time</div>
  <div class='def'>2. polonaise; music for this dance.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French polonaise (“dance of Poland”).</div><b>πολωνικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πολωνικός">πολωνικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Polish (the language of Poland and its people)</div><b>πολωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολωνική, neuter πολωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Polish (relating to Poland or its people or language)</div><b>πολωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολωτική, neuter πολωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polarising (UK), polarizing (US)</div><b>πολύ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πολύς">πολύς</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πολύς">πολύς</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πολύς">πολύς</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολύ. Cognate with Mariupol Greek пулы́.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very, too, long</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Ελένη είναι <b>πολύ</b> χαρούμενη. - Eleni is very happy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολύ. Cognate with Mariupol Greek пулы́.</div><b>πολύγραφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>copygraph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From German Polygraph.</div><b>πολύγωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πολύγωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polygon</div><b>πολύγωνο κλειδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πολύγωνα κλειδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ring spanner (UK), ring wrench (US)</div><b>πολύγωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>polygonal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολύγωνος.</div><b>πολύπαθος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολύπαθη, neuter πολύπαθο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>long-suffering</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πολύ- + πάθ(ος)) + -ος (-os) or Ancient Greek πολυπαθ(ής)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>πολύπλευρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολύπλευρη, neuter πολύπλευρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. many-sided, multilateral (having many sides)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) many-sided, multifaceted (having many aspects)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πολύπλευρος. By surface analysis, πολύ- + -πλευρος (-plevros)</div><b>πολύπλοκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολύπλοκη, neuter πολύπλοκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>complex, intricate, complicated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πολῠ́πλοκος.</div><b>πολύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολλή, neuter πολύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. much, many, a lot of</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγόρασα <b>πολύ</b> λάδι. - I bought a lot of oil.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. too much</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έβαλες <b>πολλή</b> ζάχαρη στον καφέ. - You put too much sugar in the coffee.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πολύς.</div><b>πολύτεκνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολύτεκνη, neuter πολύτεκνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>having many children</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πολύτεκνος. By surface analysis, πολύ- + τέκνο + -ος (-os).</div><b>πολύτιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολύτιμη, neuter πολύτιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>valuable, precious, invaluable, priceless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Analyzed as πολυ- (“much”) + τιμή (“price”).</div><b>πολύφωτο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of πολύφωτος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πολύφωτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small chandelier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized neuter adjective πολύφωτος (“with many lights”).</div><b>πολύχρονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολύχρονη, neuter πολύχρονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. multiyear, yearslong, lasting many years</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: μακροχρόνιος (makrochrónios)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in wishes of long life) long-lived</div>
  <div class='ex'>(Να είσαι) <b>πολύχρονος</b>/<b>πολύχρονη</b>! - Long may you live! Many happy returns!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πολύχρονος, a later form for Ancient Greek πολυχρόνιος. By surface analysis, πολύ- + χρόν(ος)) + -ος (-os, adjective-forming suffix).</div><b>πολύχρωμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολύχρωμη, neuter πολύχρωμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colourful (UK), colorful (US)</div><b>πολύωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πολύωρη, neuter πολύωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of many hours, multihour, hours-long (lasting multiple hours)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πολύ- + ώρ(α)) + -ος (-os). Compare Ancient Greek πολύωρος.</div><b>πολώνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) polonium</div><b>πομοντόρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πομοντόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plum tomato</div><b>πομπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. procession (group of people or things moving along in an orderly, stately, or solemn manner)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) disgrace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πομπή.</div><b>πομπός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πομποί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transmitter (radio, etc)</div><b>πομφόλυξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πομφόλυγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, formal) blister</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek πομφόλῠξ</div><b>πομφός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πομφοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) blister</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πομφός.</div><b>πονάει το δόντι μου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I have a toothache</div><b>πονάει το κεφάλι μου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I have a headache</div><b>πονάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to ache</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πονώ + -άω, from Ancient Greek πονῶ, from πονέω.</div><b>πονέντες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πουνέντες">πουνέντες</a></div><b>πονηρά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πονηρός">πονηρός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cunningly; trickily</div><b>πονηριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πονηριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) cunning, guile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (countable) ruse, wile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πονηριά, from Ancient Greek πονηρία, with synizesis. By surface analysis, πονηρ(ός)) + -ιά</div><b>πονηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πονηρή, neuter πονηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tricky, cunning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. evil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πονηρός.</div><b>πονοκέφαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πονοκέφαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. headache</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) headache (problem)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πόνος + κεφάλι</div><b>πονοψυχιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πονοψυχιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) mercy, compassion, pity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πόνος + ψυχή + -ιά.</div><b>ποντάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to wagered</div><b>ποντάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bet, wager</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from old Italian pontare + verb ending -ω.</div><b>ποντίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποντίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mouse (rodent)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strong muscles, especially biceps</div>
  <div class='def'>3. meat from the lower part of the leg of a calf</div>
  <div class='def'>4. mouse</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (slang) recruit, newbie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ποντίκιν, ποντίκιον, diminutives of ποντικός, from the Ancient Greek Ποντικός μῦς, that is a μῦς from Pontus; Ποντικός. The computing term is a semantic loan from English mouse.</div><b>ποντίφικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποντίφικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pontiff</div><b>ποντικοπαγίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποντικοπαγίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mousetrap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ποντικός + παγίδα</div><b>ποντικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποντικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ποντικός, from the Ancient Greek Ποντικός μῦς, that is a μῦς from Pontus. Also see the Mediaeval diminutives ποντίκιν, ποντίκιον, Modern Greek ποντίκι.</div><b>πονόδοντος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πονόδοντοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toothache</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πόνος + δόντι</div><b>πονόλαιμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πονόλαιμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sore throat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πόνος + λαιμός</div><b>πονόματος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πονόματοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sore eyes, eye ache</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πόνος + μάτι</div><b>πονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πονάω">πονάω</a></div><b>ποπ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) pop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun) pop (music)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derived from English pop music.</div><b>ποπ αρτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pop art</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of the English</div><b>ποπ κορν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) popcorn</div><b>ποπκόρν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ποπ">ποπ</a> κορν (pop korn)</div><b>ποποπό</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of πο-πό</div><b>ποπό</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of πο-πό</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative singular of <a href="bword://ποπός">ποπός</a></div><b>ποπός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποποί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(childish, colloquial, euphemistic) bum, butt, hiney, tush (the buttocks or anus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη δείχνεις τον <b>ποπό</b> σου, είναι αγενές. - Don&#x27;t show your bum, it&#x27;s rude.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>πορδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) fart (intestinal gasses emitted from the anus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έφαγε φασολάδα και μας τάραξε στις <b>πορδές</b>. - He ate bean soup and he drove us mad with his farts.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) little fart, little shit (a child, usually in the context of impudence or cheek)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιος σου είναι μια σταλιά <b>πορδή</b> και αντιμιλάει. - Your son is only a small little fart of a child and he&#x27;s talking back.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) twit, fart (unimportant person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Νίκος είναι μια <b>πορδή</b>, μην τον ξεσυνερίζεσαι. - Nikos is a little twit, don&#x27;t even bother with him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέρδομαι.</div><b>πορδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, intransitive) to fart, break wind, blow off</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορώ να ανεχτώ τον θείο της, όλο <b>πορδίζει</b> και κανέναν δεν υπολογίζει. - I can&#x27;t bear her uncle; he farts all the time and doesn&#x27;t give a damn about anyone else.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πορδή + -ίζω.</div><b>πορδίλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, uncountable, vulgar) fart smell (odour of flatlence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άνοιξε το παράθυρο να φύγει η <b>πορδίλα</b>! - Open the window so the fart stink goes out!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πορδή + -ίλα.</div><b>πορεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>course, track, direction of movement</div><b>πορθμείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορθμεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ferry, ferryboat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πορθμεῖον, from πορθμός, from περάω/περῶ, from πέρα, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *per- (“to carry forth”).</div><b>πορθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πορθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strait, channel, narrows</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πορθμός.</div><b>πορνίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορνίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (derogatory) whore, hooker, dirty whore</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fucking slut, dirty slut</div><b>πορνεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) prostitution</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πορνεία.</div><b>πορνείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορνεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brothel, bordello, house of ill repute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πορνεῖον.</div><b>πορνογράφημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορνογραφήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pornographic writing, pornography</div><b>πορνογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πορνογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pornographer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πορνογράφος.</div><b>πορνογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορνογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pornography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French pornographie, itself coined from Koine Greek πορνογράφος. First attested in 1893. See πόρνη, πόρνος &amp; -γραφία.</div><b>πορνογραφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πορνογραφική, neuter πορνογραφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pornographic</div><b>πορνόγερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πορνόγεροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, offensive) dirty old man (middle-aged or elderly man who acts in a lecherous or lewd manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένας <b>πορνόγερος</b> προσπάθησε να μου βάλει χέρι και τον χαστούκισα. - A dirty old man tried to grope me and I slapped him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πόρνος + γέρος.</div><b>πορσελάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορσελάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. porcelain (material)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. An object made of porcelain.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian porcellana.</div><b>πορτιέρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine πορτιέρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. doorman, doorkeeper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bouncer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian portiere.</div><b>πορτμπαγκάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) trunk (of a car)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French porte-bagages.</div><b>πορτογαλικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Portuguese (the language of Portugal and its people)</div><b>πορτογαλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πορτογαλική, neuter πορτογαλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Portuguese (relating to Portugal or its people or language)</div><b>πορτοκάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορτοκάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orange (fruit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>φρεσκοστυμμένος χυμός <b>πορτοκαλιού</b> - freshly squeezed orange juice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian portogallo (“orange”) or whichever cognate in the languages of Italy. Mediation via Ottoman Turkish پورتقال (portokal) is also possible.</div><b>πορτοκαλάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορτοκαλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. orange juice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. orangeade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πορτοκάλι + -άδα</div><b>πορτοκαλάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορτοκαλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A small orange,)</div><b>πορτοκαλής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πορτοκαλιά, neuter πορτοκαλί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orange (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πορτοκάλι.</div><b>πορτοκαλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orange (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) orange (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πορτοκαλής">πορτοκαλής</a></div><b>πορτοκαλεώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πορτοκαλεώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orange grove, orange plantation, orangery</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη νότια Ελλάδα υπάρχουν πολλοί <b>πορτοκαλεώνες</b>. - In southern Greece there are many orange groves.</div><b>πορτοκαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορτοκαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orange tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πορτοκάλι + -ιά. Cf. Πορτογαλία.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πορτοκαλής">πορτοκαλής</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πορτοκαλής">πορτοκαλής</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πορτοκάλι + -ιά. Cf. Πορτογαλία.</div><b>πορτοκαλόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορτοκαλόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a cake made with orange zest and juice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πορτοκάλ(ι), “orange”) + -ό- + -πιτα (πίτα), “pie”)</div><b>πορτορικάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πορτορικάνικη, neuter πορτορικάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Puerto Rican</div><b>πορτορικανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πορτορικανική, neuter πορτορικανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πορτορικάνικος">πορτορικάνικος</a></div><b>πορτουλάκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορτουλάκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>portulaca, purslane (Portulaca)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Portulaca, from Latin portulāca.</div><b>πορτοφόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορτοφόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wallet, purse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian portafoglio.</div><b>πορτρέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορτρέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a portrait (of a face)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a profile or description of a person</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a description of a situation, era, mood etc</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French portrait.</div><b>πορτραίτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορτραίτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πορτρέτο">πορτρέτο</a></div><b>πορφυρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πορφυρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Tyrian purple, royal purple (the colour of this dye)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the adjective πορφυρός; cf. Ancient Greek πορφύρα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πορφυρός">πορφυρός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πορφυρός">πορφυρός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the adjective πορφυρός; cf. Ancient Greek πορφύρα.</div><b>πορφυρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πορφυρή, neuter πορφυρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Tyrian purple, royal purple (having this colour; dyed with this dye)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πορφύρεος.</div><b>πορφύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πορφύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. murex (the mollusc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Tyrian purple, royal purple (the dye, and its colour, obtained from the mollusc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πορφύρα.</div><b>ποσέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) poached</div>
  <div class='ex'>αβγό <b>ποσέ</b> - poached egg</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French poché.</div><b>ποσειδώνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) neptunium (element)</div><b>ποσοστιαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ποσοστιαία, neuter ποσοστιαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>percentage (attributive), percentual (of, pertaining to, or expressed as a percentage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ποσοστ(ό)) + -ιαίος.</div><b>ποσοστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποσοστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>percentage</div>
  <div class='ex'>το ελάχιστο ποσοστό (tiny percentage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ποσός + -στός.</div><b>ποσοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ποσοτική, neuter ποσοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quantitative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ποσότης + -ικός. Calque of French quantitatif.</div><b>ποσό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποσά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amount, quantity, sum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποσόν.</div><b>ποσότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποσότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quantity, amount</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μία σχετικά μικρή <b>ποσότητα</b> πετρελαίου, 10.000 λίτρα, χύθηκε στη θάλασσα. - A relatively small quantity of oil, 10,000 litres, ended up in the sea.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποσότης, equivalent to πόσος + -ότητα.</div><b>ποτάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποτάμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. river, stream</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) stream, flow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from Koine Greek, from Ancient Greek ποτάμιον, diminutive of ποταμός.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek пута́м.</div><b>ποτέ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ever, at any time (emphatic)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα σταματήσεις <b>ποτέ</b> αυτή τη φασαρία; - Will you ever stop this trouble?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not ever, never (when used with the negative)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Δεν</b> τρώει <b>ποτέ</b> κρέας. - He never eats meat.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποτέ.</div><b>ποτήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glass (drinking vessel, usually made of glass)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποτήριον. Cognate with Tsakonian ποκήρι.</div><b>ποτήρι κρασιού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποτήρια κρασιού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ποτήρι">ποτήρι</a> <a href="bword://του">του</a> κρασιού (wine glass)</div><b>ποτήρι του κρασιού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποτήρια του κρασιού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wine glass</div><b>ποτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to give a drink to, water</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ιπποκόμος <b>πότισε</b> τα άλογα - The groom watered the horses.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to water, irrigate</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πότισε</b> ντοματιές του. - He watered his tomato plants.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to soak, steep</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποτῐ́ζω.</div><b>ποταμοπλοΐα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) riverboating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ποταμόπλοιο + -ία.</div><b>ποταμόπλοιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποταμόπλοια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>riverboat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ποταμός + πλοίο.</div><b>ποταμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποταμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ποτάμι">ποτάμι</a> (river)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποταμός.</div><b>ποταμόχοιρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ποταμόχοίροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bush pig (Potamochoerus sp.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ποταμόχοιρος των θάμνων - bush pig, Potamochoerus larvatus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Potamochoerus. By surface analysis, ποτάμι + χοίρος.</div><b>ποτεντίλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ποτεντίλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>potentilla (Potentilla)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Potentilla.</div><b>ποτιστήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποτιστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>watering can</div><b>ποτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ποτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beverage, drink</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποτόν, noun use of the neuter form of ποτός, from Proto-Hellenic *potós, from Proto-Indo-European *ph₃tós (“(having been) drunk; having drunk”), derived from the root *peh₃- (“to drink”). Cognate with Italian poto.</div><b>που</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>that</div>
  <div class='ex'>χαίρομαι <b>που</b> σε βλέπω ξανά - I am glad to see you again</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πού, from Ancient Greek ὅπου.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) that, who, whom, which</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο άνθρωπος <b>που</b> είδα στο δρόμο ήταν ένας παλιός μου φίλος. - The man whom I saw on the road was an old friend.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) where</div>
  <div class='ex'>το μέρος <b>που</b> μεγάλωσα - the place where I grew up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πού, from Ancient Greek ὅπου.</div><b>που να μη σε βρει το βράδυ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(offensive) drop dead, I hope you die, may you be struck dead (general curse hoping for someone&#x27;s swift death)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Που να μη σε βρει το βράδυ</b>, ρε καθίκι! - Drop dead, you bastard!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally &quot;that night won&#x27;t find you&quot;.</div><b>πουαντιγισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pointillism</div><b>πουαντιλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pointillism</div><b>πουαντιλιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πουαντιλιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pointillist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French pointilliste. Suffixed with -ιστής. By surface analysis, πουαντιλισμός + -τής.</div><b>πουδράρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to powder oneself</div><b>πουδράρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to powder (apply powder)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια ώρα <b>πουδράρουν</b> την ηθοποιό. - They&#x27;ve been powdering the actress for an hour now.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French poudrer, equivalent to πούδρα + -άρω.</div><b>πουδραρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πουδράρομαι">πουδράρομαι</a>, passive of <a href="bword://πουδράρω">πουδράρω</a></div><b>πουδριέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πουδριέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>powder compact, powder box (small box which holds powder)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έβγαλε ένα σωρό <b>πουδριέρες</b> και καλλυντικά από την τσάντα της. - She took out a load of powder boxes and cosmetics from her bag.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French poudrier, equivalent to πούδρα + -ιέρα.</div><b>πουθενά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. somewhere, anywhere (in or to any place)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε θα πάμε <b>πουθενά</b> φέτος για διακοπές. - We won&#x27;t be going anywhere on holiday this year.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nowhere, anywhere (in no place)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έψαξα παντού, αλλά δεν σε βρήκα <b>πουθενά</b>. - I looked everywhere, but didn&#x27;t find you anywhere.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πουθενά, from πόθεν + -νά.
Compare with Mariupol Greek пудъи́на.</div><b>πουκάμισο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουκάμισα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shirt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shed snakeskin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πουκάμισον, from earlier ὑποκάμισον, from ὑπο- (hupo-) + καμίσιον, the latter part from Medieval Latin or Late Latin camisia (“shirt”), of Gaulish and perhaps ultimately Germanic origin.</div><b>πουλάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πουλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pullet, young hen</div><b>πουλάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουλάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foal (infant or young horse, donkey or mule)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πουλάριν, from Ancient Greek πωλάριον, diminutive form of πῶλος, from Proto-Indo-European *péh₂uros, from *peh₂w-.</div><b>πουλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sell out, betray</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πουλ(ώ)) + -άω, inherited from Byzantine Greek πουλῶ, from Ancient Greek πωλῶ with [o] &gt; [u], contracted form of πωλέω.</div><b>πουλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bird</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (childish, colloquial, euphemistic) willy, peter, wee-wee, pee-pee (the penis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πουλλίν, from Koine Greek πουλλίον), itself either from Latin pullus or alternatively from Ancient Greek πῶλος.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek плы (ply).</div><b>πουλακίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πουλακίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πουλάδα">πουλάδα</a></div><b>πουλερικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουλερικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. poultry, domestic fowl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. poultry meat</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάνω δίαιτα με <b>πουλερικά</b> και λαχανικά. - I am on a diet of poultry and vegetables.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πουλ(ί), “bird”) + -ερικό. Petrunias associates it to neuter noun αερικό a word reminding of air and flying. Babiniotis, to σιδερ-ικό or πεθερ-ικό (but both words with ‑ερ- in their stems, suffixed with -ικό).</div><b>πουλητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πουητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πωλητής">πωλητής</a></div><b>πουλιέμαι σα νερό</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) sell like hot cakes (literally: I am selling like water. Usually in the 3rd person.)</div><b>πουλλίν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουλλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) bird</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πουλλίν, from Koine Greek πουλλίον), itself either from Latin pullus or alternatively from Ancient Greek πῶλος.</div><b>πουλμανάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουλμανάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shuttle, minibus</div><b>πουλόβερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pullover, jumper, sweater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English pullover.</div><b>πουλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less common variant of <a href="bword://πουλάω">πουλάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πουλῶ, from Ancient Greek πωλῶ with regular development ω &gt; [u], contracted form of πωλέω.</div><b>πουνέντες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) zephyr, a light westerly Mediterranean wind</div><b>πουρμπουάρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tip (money left for waiter, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French pourboire.</div><b>πουρνάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουρνάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kermes oak, prickly oak (Quercus coccifera)</div><b>πουρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) senile person (physically or mentally fragile)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πουρός">πουρός</a></div><b>πουρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πουρή, neuter πουρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory) doddering, physically senile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory) gaga, mentally senile</div><b>πουστάκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of πουστάκος</div><b>πουσταριό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουσταριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, vulgar) gays, queers, fags (gay men as a group in general)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχουν Πράιντ σήμερα και θα μαζευτεί όλο το <b>πουσταριό</b> της Αθήνας. - They have Pride today, and all the fags in Athens are gonna get together.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πούστης + -ριό.</div><b>πουστεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, intransitive, vulgar) to turn gay, become gay</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγε στη Νέα Υόρκη και <b>πούστεψε</b> ο καημένος! - He went to New York and became a faggot, poor guy!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, transitive, vulgar) to turn gay, make gay</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταματά να του βάζεις κραγιόν, θα το <b>πουστέψεις</b> το παιδί! - Stop putting lipstick on him, you&#x27;ll turn the boy into a queer!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πούστ(ης), “gay/fag/queer”) + -εύω.</div><b>πουστιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πουστιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) rude act, rude behaviour, discourtesy.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν μεγάλη <b>πουστιά</b> του να μ’ αφήσει να πληρώσω μόνος μου τον λογαριασμό. - It was really fucking rude of him to leave me to pay the bill on my own.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πούστης + -ιά.</div><b>πουστοφέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, vulgar) to behave or talk like a gay person, mince, act camp, shit rainbows</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έτσι που <b>πουστοφέρνει</b>, πώς να μην το ήξερες ότι ήταν γκέι; - The way he prances around, how could you not know he was gay?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πούστης + φέρνω.</div><b>πουτάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πουτάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, vulgar) whore, hooker (prostitute)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Αλέξανδρος πάει συχνά στις <b>πουτάνες</b>. - Alexander sees hookers a lot.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, derogatory, offensive) whore, bitch, slut, trollop (term for a woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άντε φύγε από ’δω, μωρή <b>πουτάνα</b>! - Get outta here, stupid bitch!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πουτάνα, from Italian puttana.
Ultimately from Old French putain, from Latin puta.</div><b>πουτανίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πουτανίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncommon, vulgar) little whore, prostitute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πουτάν(α), “whore”) + -ίδιο</div><b>πουτανιάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (derogatory, vulgar) A man who frequents brothels, a whoremaster.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, vulgar) (by extension) A man who has an intense and promiscuous love life; a player.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πουτάνα + -ιάρης.</div><b>πού</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(interrogative) where</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πού</b> είναι η γυναίκα μου; - Where is my wife?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ποῦ, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷos.</div><b>πού είναι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. where is?</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πού είναι</b> η τράπεζα, παρακαλώ; - Where is the bank, please?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. where are?</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πού είναι</b> τα καταστήματα, παρακαλώ; - Where are the shops, please?</div><b>πού είναι η τουαλέτα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>where is the toilet?</div><b>πού και πού</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>now and again, now and then, every so often, occasionally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;where and where&quot;.</div><b>πούδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πούδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>powder (mixture of fine dry, sweet-smelling particles applied to the face or other body parts, to reduce shine or to alleviate chaffing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φορούσε <b>πούδρα</b> ώστε να μη φαίνονται τα σπυριά της. - She was wearing powder so that her spots wouldn&#x27;t be visible.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French poudre.</div><b>πούθε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (interrogative) whence?, from where?</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πούθε ήρθε;</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (interrogative) whence?, from what source?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πόθεν.</div><b>πούλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sale (an exchange of goods or services for currency or credit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>για <b>πούλημα</b> - for sale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πώλημα with vowel change per πωλώ → πουλώ.</div><b>πούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>checker (playing piece)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پول (pul), possibly from Classical Persian پول, itself possibly from Ancient Greek ὀβολός. The semantic sense of a stamp or game piece in backgammon or checkers is an Ottoman innovation possibly deriving from the originally borrowed meaning of a (small) coin. Doublet of πούλια.</div><b>πούλμαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) coach, bus (with one deck)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Named after George Pullman, compare English Pullman.</div><b>πούμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) puma, cougar, mountain lion</div><b>πούντρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πούντρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://πούδρα">πούδρα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French poudre.</div><b>πούπουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πούπουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soft feather, down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan pùpoƚa.</div><b>πούρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πούρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cigar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Spanish puro (“cigar”).</div><b>πούστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πούστηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory) faggot, queer, homo (male homosexual)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αδερφός της φιλενάδας μου είναι <b>πούστης</b>. - My girlfriend’s brother is a queer.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, informal) dude, man, bro (term of address between friends)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Που πας τέτοια ώρα, ρε <b>πούστη</b>; - Where you off to this late, dude?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, offensive) prick, dickhead, jerk (general term of abuse for a male)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα τι πήγε και έκανε ο <b>πούστης</b>! - Look what that prick went and did!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پوشت, from Classical Persian پشت.</div><b>πούστικα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πούστικος">πούστικος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πούστικος">πούστικος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πούστικος">πούστικος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, vulgar) in a gay manner, in a queer manner (in the manner of a homosexual)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί μιλάς <b>πούστικα</b>; - Why are you talking like a queer?</div><b>πούστικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πούστικη or πούστικια, neuter πούστικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, vulgar) gay, faggoty, queer (relating to homosexuals)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όχι, δεν με ενδιαφέρουν οι <b>πούστικες</b> ταινίες. Μου αρέσουν οι γυναίκες. - No, I&#x27;m not interested in faggoty films. I like women.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πούστης.</div><b>πούτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πούτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(vulgar) penis</div><b>πούτσος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πούτσοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) cock, dick, prick (the penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθα μέσα καθώς ντυνόταν και είδα τον <b>πούτσο</b> του. - I came in as he was getting dressed and I saw his cock.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Uncertain, possibly from Proto-Slavic *buca (“bulge, protrusion”). Compare Proto-Germanic *putōną (“to stick, stab”), which is of uncertain origin. Possibly ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *bud- (“to shoot, sprout”), itself speculated to be of substrate origin.</div><b>πράγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πράγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thing, entity, object</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι είναι αυτό το <b>πράγμα</b> στον κουβά; - What is that thing in the bucket?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stuff</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (euphemistic) thingy (penis, vagina, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κλείσε το φερμουάρ – φαίνεται το <b>πράγμα</b> σου! - Close your zipper – your thingy is showing!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρᾶγμα.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek пра́ма.</div><b>πράγματι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indeed, really, actually</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Fossilized (adverbial-instrumental) dative of πράγμα.</div><b>πράκτορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πράκτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>agent</div><b>πράμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus, Early Modern Greek) <a href="bword://πράγμα">πράγμα</a></div><b>πράξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πράξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. act, deed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. action, praxis</div>
  <div class='def'>3. operation</div>
  <div class='def'>4. act</div>
  <div class='def'>5. transaction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek πρᾶξις.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek пра́кси.</div><b>πράος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πράα, neuter πράο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mild, gentle, placid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. benign</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bland</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρᾷος.</div><b>πράσινα άλογα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>my eye, my foot, rubbish, nonsense; literally: green horses</div><b>πράσινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πράσινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the green color/colour</div>
  <div class='ex'>πράσινο on the Greek Wikipedia.Wikipedia ᵉˡ</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) greenery</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) lawn, grass</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην πατάτε το <b>πράσινο</b>. - Do not step on the greenery.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (broadly) green light</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περνάμε με <b>πράσινο</b>, σταματάμε με κόκκινο. - literally: We pass with green, we stop with red.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πράσινος">πράσινος</a> (green)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πράσινος">πράσινος</a></div><b>πράσινο φως</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://πράσινο">πράσινο</a>, <a href="bword://φως">φως</a> (fos).</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic, uncountable) green light (approval or permission to proceed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δήμαρχος τους έδωσε το <b>πράσινο φως</b> να χτίσουν την πολυκατοικία. - The mayor gave them the green light to build the block of flats.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English green light.</div><b>πράσινοι νανομελισσοφάγοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://πράσινος">πράσινος</a> <a href="bword://νανομελισσοφάγος">νανομελισσοφάγος</a></div><b>πράσινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πράσινη, neuter πράσινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of green color/colour (colour of growing foliage)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πράσινα</b> μάτια - green eyes</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of plants and fruit) green, unripe (not ready to be picked)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πράσινη</b> ντομάτα - green/unripe tomato</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) green (environmentally friendly)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. for the color/colour, see <a href="bword://πράσινο">πράσινο</a> n</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πράσινος, from πράσον. Displaced earlier Ancient Greek χλωρός.</div><b>πράσινος μελισσοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πράσινοι μελισσοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Merops persicus, the blue-cheeked bee-eater</div><b>πράσινος νανομελισσοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πράσινοι νανομελισσοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Merops orientalis, the green bee-eater, little green bee-eater</div><b>πράσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πράσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leek</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πράσον.</div><b>πράσσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πράσσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πράσο">πράσο</a></div><b>πρέζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρέζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pinch (small amount of powder or granules, such that the amount could be held between fingertip and thumb tip)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η συνταγή θέλει μια <b>πρέζα</b> ζάχαρης. - The recipe needs a pinch of sugar.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) hit (dose of an illegal or addictive drug)</div>
  <div class='ex'>παίρνω <b>πρέζα</b> - take a hit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly, colloquial, figuratively) drug (illegal or addictive psychoactive substance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον σκότωσε η <b>πρέζα</b>. - Drugs killed him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian presa (“pinch”).</div><b>πρέπει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, with genitive) to be fit for, to be worthy of, deserve</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του <b>πρέπουν</b> μεγάλες τιμές· πρέπει να πάρει βραβείο Νόμπελ. - Great honours are fitting for him; he must receive a Nobel prize.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (singular only) must, have to, ought</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρέπει</b> να πω πως… - I must say that…</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from third-person singular πρέπει of Ancient Greek πρέπω.</div><b>πρέπων</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πρέπουσα, neuter πρέπον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) appropriate, fitting, proper, suitable</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: δέων (déon, “appropriate”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formal present participle of πρέπει. Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρέπων, present active participle of πρέπω.</div><b>πρέσβειρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρέσβειρες, masculine πρέσβυς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ambassador</div><b>πρέσβης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πρέσβεις, feminine πρέσβειρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ambassador</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πρέσβυς + -ης (-is).</div><b>πρέσβυς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρέσβεις, feminine πρέσβειρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://πρέσβης">πρέσβης</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρέσβυς.</div><b>πρήζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to become swollen, become bloated, swell up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το χέρι της <b>πρήστηκε</b> όταν την τσίμπησε η μέλισσα. - Her hand became swollen when the bee stung her.</div><b>πρήζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rare, transitive) to swell, bloat (cause to become bigger or engorged)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα κουνούπια του <b>έπρηξαν</b> το πρόσωπο με τα τσιμπήματά τους. - The mosquitoes caused his face to swell with their bites.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive) to pester, badger, vex (annoy persistently)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιος μου με <b>πρήζει</b> να του αγοράσω ποδήλατο. - My son is pestering me to get him a bicycle.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἔπρησα, aorist of πρήθω.</div><b>πρήζω τ&#x27; αρχίδια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, transitive, vulgar) to twist someone&#x27;s balls, bust someone&#x27;s balls, pester, torment, be a pain in the arse (to annoy someone greatly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε μέρα μου <b>πρήζει τ’ αρχίδια</b> να του δανείσω λεφτά. - Every day he’s up my ass trying to get me to lend him money.</div><b>πρήζω το συκώτι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, transitive) to pester, torment, be a pain (to annoy someone greatly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας <b>έχει πρήξει το συκώτι</b> με τις ερωτήσεις του. - He&#x27;s tormented us with his questions.</div><b>πρήξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρηξίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. swelling, edema (abnormally swollen part of the body)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει <b>πρήξιμο</b> στο πρόσωπο από τσίμπημα σφήκας. - He has a swelling on the face from a wasp sting.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bloating (swelling caused by overeating or drinking)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>πρήξιμο</b> διότι παραφάγα. - I&#x27;m bloated since I overate.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) pain, pain in the neck (something very annoying or frustrating)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν μεγάλο <b>πρήξιμο</b> να βγάλω διαβατήριο. - It was a huge pain in the neck to get a passport.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρήζω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>πρίγκιπας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρίγκιπες, feminine πριγκίπισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prince, title for the male children of a king and sometimes the extended royal family.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. title for the ruler of a small country</div>
  <div class='def'>3. senior title of the nobility</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) A person living in luxury.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Zει σαν <b>πρίγκιπας</b>. - He lives like a prince.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Ancient Greek πρῖγκιψ, from Latin prīnceps.</div><b>πρίζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρίζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wall socket (for an electrical plug)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French prise (“(electrical) socket, wall socket”).</div><b>πρίον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) prion</div><b>πρίσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. point of view</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρίσμα. Sense 2 is a semantic loan from French prisme.</div><b>πραγματεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πραγματείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>treatise, discourse, dissertation</div><b>πραγματικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>really, truly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πραγματικός">πραγματικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πραγματικός">πραγματικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πραγματικός">πραγματικός</a></div><b>πραγματικοί αριθμοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πραγματικός">πραγματικός</a> <a href="bword://αριθμός">αριθμός</a></div><b>πραγματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πραγματική, neuter πραγματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>real, factual, actual (having physical existence), (having existence or substance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πραγματικός.</div><b>πραγματικός αριθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πραγματικοί αριθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>real number</div><b>πραγματικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πραγματικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reality, actuality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πραγματικός + -ότητα. First attested 1787.</div><b>πραγματοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πραγματοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>realization (the act of making real)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πραγματοποιώ + -ση (-si). Also analyzable as πραγματ- (pragmat-) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποίηση.</div><b>πραγματοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to make real, to realize, to fulfil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to carry out, to effect, to effectuate, to perform</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πραγματ- (pragmat-) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>πρακτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πρακτικός">πρακτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>practically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. minutes (a written record of a meeting)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πρακτικό">πρακτικό</a></div><b>πρακτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρακτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>practice (a customary action, habit, or behaviour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρᾱκτική, substantivized feminine of πρᾱκτικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://πρακτικός">πρακτικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>πρακτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρακτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. written report</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) minutes of a meeting</div><b>πρακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρακτική, neuter πρακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. practical (being likely to be effective and applicable to a real situation; able to be put to use)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. practical (relating to, or based on, practice or action rather than theory or hypothesis)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. practical (of a person, having skills or knowledge that are practical)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρακτικός with semantic loan from French pratique.</div><b>πρακτορείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρακτορεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bureau, agency</div><b>πραμάτεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πραμάτειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>merchandise, goods (especially that sold door to door)</div><b>πραματευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πραματευτάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peddler, hawker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πραγματευτής.</div><b>πραξικόπημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πραξικοπήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coup d&#x27;état, coup, putsch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πράξη + κόπτω, calque of French coup d&#x27;État.</div><b>πρασεοδύμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πρασινοδύμιο">πρασινοδύμιο</a></div><b>πρασινίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to become green, to go green, to turn green</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρασινίζω</b> από τη ζήλια μου - to turn green with envy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make green, to turn green, to green</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to green (to add green spaces to)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρασινίζω. By surface analysis, πράσιν(ος)) + -ίζω.</div><b>πρασινοδύμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) praseodymium</div><b>πρασινούλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρασινούλα, neuter πρασινούλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greenish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πράσινος + -ούλης</div><b>πρασινωπός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρασινωπή, neuter πρασινωπό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>greenish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πράσιν(ος), “green”) + -ωπός</div><b>πρασουλίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρασουλίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncommon) chives (herb), chive (plant)</div><b>πρασόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρασόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leek pie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πράσο + -ό- + πίτα.</div><b>πρατήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρατήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. filling station</div>
  <div class='def'>2. baker&#x27;s shop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πρατήριον, from πρατήρ.</div><b>πραχτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πραχτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πρακτική">πρακτική</a></div><b>πρβ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of παράβαλε</div><b>πρβ.</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of παράβαλε</div><b>πρβλ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of παράβαλε</div><b>πρβλ.</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of παράβαλε</div><b>πρεζάκιας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρεζάκηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) junkie, drug addict, drug fiend (person with a chemical or psychological dependency on a drug)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη πας σ’ αυτό το πάρκο, είναι όλο <b>πρεζάκηδες</b> εκεί! - Don&#x27;t go to that park, it&#x27;s full of junkies!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρέζα + -άκιας</div><b>πρεζάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, intransitive) to shoot up, toke, use (take drugs)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει καιρό να <b>πρεζάρει</b> και έχει συμπτώματα στέρησης. - It&#x27;s been a while since she used and she has withdrawal symptoms.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρέζα + -άρω</div><b>πρεζόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρεζόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) junkie, drug addict, drug fiend (person with a chemical or psychological dependency on a drug)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήρθε ένα <b>πρεζόνι</b> που μου ζητούσε λεφτά. - A junkie came to me and asked me for money.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρέζα + -όνι</div><b>πρεμιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρεμιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>premiere, première</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French première.</div><b>πρεσάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be pressed, forced</div><b>πρεσάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) to press, oppress, force</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian pressare.</div><b>πρεσβεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρεσβείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. embassy, delegation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) honours or privileges granted to elderly persons</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρεσβεία, and see πρέσβυς.</div><b>πρεσβευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρεσβευτές, feminine πρεσβευτίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ambassador</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρεσβευτής, from πρεσβεύω + -τής.</div><b>πρεσβευτίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρεσβευτίνες, masculine πρεσβευτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) ambassadress, female ambassador</div><b>πρεσβυωπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) presbyopia, farsightedness, longsightedness</div><b>πρηνής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρηνής, neuter πρηνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) prone, face down</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρανής.</div><b>πρησμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πρησμένη, neuter πρησμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swollen, bloated (distended)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είδα το <b>πρησμένο</b> πρόσωπο και κατάλαβα ότι είχε αλλεργία. - I saw her swollen face and realized she had an allergy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of πρήζομαι, passive voice of πρήζω.</div><b>πριαπισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πριαπισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>priapism</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πριαπισμός</b> είναι πάθηση που εκδηλώνεται ως μη υποχωρούσα, παρατεταμένη επώδυνη στύση, η οποία δε σχετίζεται απαραίτητα με σεξουαλική διέγερση. - Priapism is a condition that manifests as a persistent, prolonged and painful erection which is not necessarily associated with sexual arousal.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πριαπισμός / Πριαπισμός from verb πριαπίζω, πριαπισ- + -μός, from Πρίᾱπος. By surface analysis, Πρίαπ(ος)) + -ισμός.</div><b>πριγκίπισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πριγκίπισσες, masculine πρίγκιπας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>princess (daughter of a monarch)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derived from Latin prī̆ncipissa (“princess”), By surface analysis, πρίγκιπας + -ισσα (-issa).</div><b>πριμιτίφ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) primitive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French primitiv, from Latin prīmitīvus.</div><b>πριν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ago</div>
  <div class='def'>2. before</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρίν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>before</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρίν.</div><b>πρινάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρινάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πουρνάρι">πουρνάρι</a></div><b>πριονίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sawdust</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πριον- (prion-, “saw”) + -ίδι.</div><b>πριονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to saw (to cut with a saw)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to saw off (to cut off with a saw)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πριονίζω</b> το κλαδί στο οποίο/που κάθομαι - to saw off the branch one is sitting on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πριονίζω. By surface analysis, πριόν(ι)) + -ίζω.</div><b>πριτσίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πριτσίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rivet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish پرچین.</div><b>πριτσομπαλίτσας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a children&#x27;s toy, &quot;gas out&quot;</div><b>πριόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πριόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saw (cutting tool)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρίων, from the same root as πείρω.</div><b>προ</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with genitive) before</div><b>προ μεσημβρίας</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ante meridiem, before noon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English ante meridiem (a.m.), from Latin ante (“before”) merīdiem, accusative singular of merīdiēs (“noon, midday”).</div><b>προάγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to promote, to advance, to further (to advocate or urge on behalf of, or otherwise create positive conditions for)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to promote (to raise (someone) to a more important, responsible, or remunerative job or rank)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προάγω with semantic loan from French promouvoir.</div><b>προάστιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προάστια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suburb, outskirts</div><b>προέλευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προελεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>origin</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ελληνική γλώσσα εμπλουτίζεται με πολλές λέξεις γαλλικής <b>προέλευσης</b>. - The Greek language is enriched by many words of French origin.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek προέλευσῐς, with semantic shift &quot;procession&quot; &gt; &quot;origin&quot; via semantic loan from French provenance.</div><b>προέρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to originate, spring from, come from</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προέρχομαι (“arrive first, go before”). Morphologically, from προ- (“from”) + έρχομαι (“come”). The modern sense: semantic loan from French provenir.</div><b>προήρθα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of προήλθα</div><b>προίκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dowry (cash, goods or property exchanged at time of marriage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) capital necessary to properly operate something</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προίξ.</div><b>προίξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προίκα">προίκα</a></div><b>προαίσθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προαισθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>premonition, foreboding, hunch, presentiment (a strong intuition that something is about to happen)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προ- (pro-) + αίσθημα, a calque of French pressentiment.</div><b>προαγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προαγωγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. promotion, advancement, furtherment (act of moving forward or proceeding in a course)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>προαγωγή</b> των εθνικών συμφερόντων - the promotion of national interests</div>
  <div class='def'>2. promotion (advancement in rank or position)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν πήρα ακόμη <b>προαγωγή</b> στη δουλειά αν και δουλεύω εκεί πέντε χρόνια τώρα. - I still haven&#x27;t had a promotion at work even though I&#x27;ve worked there for five years now.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek προαγωγή, from Ancient Greek προάγω.</div><b>προαγωγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προαγωγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pimp, procurer (prostitution)</div><b>προαιρετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προαιρετική, neuter προαιρετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>optional</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προαιρετικός with semantic loan from French facultatif.</div><b>προαισθάνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to forebode, have a foreboding, know in advance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προαισθάνομαι.</div><b>προαλείφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prepared (of persons: to take office, of developments: to happen)</div><b>προαλείφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary, passive, present, usually) prepare (of persons: for assuming office)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προαλείφω (“cover beforehand”). In the passive, Hellenistic Koine Greek προαλείφομαι (“be anointed before -e.g. wrestling-”). Morphologically, from προ- (“before”) + αλείφω (“spread”).</div><b>προανάκριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προανακρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preliminary investigation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προανακρί(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>προανάφλεξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αναφλέξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preignition, premature ignition, premature combustion, pinking, knocking (internal combustion engine)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προ- (pro-, “pre”) + ανάφλεξη</div><b>προαναγγέλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to announce in advance, to preannounce</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to herald, to be a harbinger of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προαναγγέλλω. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + αναγγέλλω.</div><b>προαναφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to mention beforehand/previously, to aforemention</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προαναφέρω. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + αναφέρω.</div><b>προαστιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προαστιακή, neuter προαστιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suburban</div><b>προαύλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προαύλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yard (enclosed area by a public building)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>προαύλιο</b> της φυλακής - the prison courtyard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek προαύλιον, from πρό + Ancient Greek αὐλή.</div><b>προβάδισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προβαδίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>precedence, superiority, lead, priority</div><b>προβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to present itself</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to project (to cast (an image or shadow) upon a surface; to throw or cast forward; to shoot forth)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to promote (to attempt to popularize)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to put forward, to present, to raise (:argument, objection, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to project</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to project (to assume qualities or mindsets in others based on one&#x27;s own personality)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προβάλλω, with learned semantic loan from French projeter and English project. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + βάλλω.</div><b>προβατίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προβατίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ewe</div><b>προβιομηχανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προβιομηχανική, neuter προβιομηχανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preindustrial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προ- (pro-, “pre”) + βιομηχανικός</div><b>προβλέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to foresee, predict</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to organise (UK), organize (USA) in advance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek προβλέπω. For sense organise in advance, semantic loan from French prévoir. By surface analysis, προ- (“pre-”) + βλέπω (“see”).</div><b>προβλήτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προβλήτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jetty, pier (dock or wharf extending from the shore)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προβλῆτα, accusative of προβλής.</div><b>προβληματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to concern, to trouble</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be concerned, to have concerns, to be troubled</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to wonder (to ponder; to feel doubt and curiosity; to query in the mind)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The active voice is a back-formation from Byzantine Greek προβληματίζομαι. By surface analysis, προβληματ-) + -ίζω.</div><b>προβληματικοί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masculine nominative/vocative plural of προβλημᾰτῐκός</div><b>προβληματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προβληματική, neuter προβληματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>problematic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προβληματικός and semantic loan from French problématique from which also the feminine προβληματική as noun. By surface analysis, πρόβλημα, προβληματ- + -ικός.</div><b>προβολέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προβολείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spotlight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. projector (an optical device)</div>
  <div class='ex'>προβολέα διαφανειών (slide projector)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in genitive) headlight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προβάλλω + -έας</div><b>προβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. projection, screening</div>
  <div class='def'>2. projection</div>
  <div class='def'>3. promotion, publicising</div>
  <div class='def'>4. play (a button that causes media to be played, e.g., press play)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. lunge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προβολή with semantic loan from French and English projection.</div><b>προγαμιαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προγαμιαία, neuter προγαμιαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>premarital, premarriage, prematrimonial, prenuptial</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προγαμιαία</b> συμφωνία - prenuptial agreement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek προγαμιαίος. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + γάμ(ος)) + -ιαίος.</div><b>προγεννητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prenatal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προ- (pro-, “pre-”) + γεννητικός, calque of French prénatal.</div><b>προγεστερόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προγεστερόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>progesterone</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η χορήγηση <b>προγεστερόνης</b> σε γυναίκες που παρουσιάζουν αιμορραγία στην αρχή της εγκυμοσύνης και έχουν ιστορικό αποβολών, θα μπορούσε να αποτρέψει πολλές χιλιάδες αποβολές κάθε χρόνο. - Giving progesterone to women who bleed early in pregnancy and have a medical history of miscarriage could prevent many thousands of miscarriages each year.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French progestérone.</div><b>προγευματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to have breakfast, breakfast</div><b>προγιαγιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προγιαγιάδες, masculine προπάππος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great-grandmother</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προ- (pro-, “before”) + γιαγιά</div><b>προγονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προγονές, masculine προγονός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stepdaughter (the daughter of a spouse by a previous marriage)</div><b>προγονός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προγονοί, feminine προγονή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stepson (the son of a spouse by a previous marriage)</div><b>προγραμματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to plan, schedule, organise (UK), organize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to programme (UK), program (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to program</div><b>προγραμματίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προγραμματίστριες, masculine προγραμματιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>programmer (software writer)</div><b>προγραμματισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προγραμματισμένη, neuter προγραμματισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://προγραμματίζω">προγραμματίζω</a>:; planned, scheduled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://προγραμματίζω">προγραμματίζω</a>:; programmed</div><b>προγραμματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προγραμματισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. planning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. programming</div><b>προγραμματιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προγραμματιστές, feminine προγραμματίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>programmer (software writer)</div><b>προδίδομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fail</div><b>προδίδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to betray, give away, (tattletale): tattle, tell on, double-cross, compromise, (idiomatic): roll over, (idiomatic): rat out, (idiomatic, vulgar): fuck over</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to reveal, give away (e.g., one&#x27;s position)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προδίδωμι.</div><b>προδίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προδίδω">προδίδω</a></div><b>προδιάθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προδιαθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. predisposition (the state of being predisposed or susceptible to a health condition)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. predisposition (the state of being predisposed to a behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: τάση f (tási), ροπή f (ropí), κλίση f (klísi)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. prior attitude, disposition in advance (before an event, experience, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προδιάθεσις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>προδοσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προδοσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. betrayal, treachery</div>
  <div class='def'>2. treason</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προδοσίᾱ.</div><b>προδοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προδοτική, neuter προδοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traitorous, treacherous, treasonous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, προδότης + -ικός.</div><b>προδότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προδότες, feminine προδότρια or προδότρα or προδότισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. traitor, betrayer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. informer, grass (colloquial (UK))</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek προδότης.</div><b>προεδρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προεδρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>presidency (position and office)</div><b>προεδρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προεδρική, neuter προεδρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>presidential (pertaining to a president or presidency)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πρόεδρ(ος)) + -ικός, a calque of French présidentiel.</div><b>προειδοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προειδοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>warning, notice</div><b>προειδοποιημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προειδοποιημένη, neuter προειδοποιημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>warned, who has been warned</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of προειδοποιούμαι, passive voice of προειδοποιώ (“I warn”).</div><b>προειδοποιητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προειδοποιητική, neuter προειδοποιητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>warning</div><b>προειδοποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be forewarned</div><b>προειδοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to forewarn, warn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to inform in advance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From προ- (pro-, “pre-”) + ειδοποιώ.</div><b>προεκλογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προεκλογική, neuter προεκλογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preelection, preelectoral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προ- (pro-) + εκλογικός, a calque of French pré-électoral.</div><b>προεντατήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pretensioner</div><b>προετοιμάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prepared, ready</div><b>προετοιμάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to coach, train, prepare beforehand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek προετοιμάζω. By surface analysis, προ- (“pre-”) + ετοιμάζω (“prepare”).</div><b>προετοιμασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προετοιμασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preparation beforehand (the act of getting ready)</div><b>προζύμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προζύμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>starter, starter dough, levain (a yeast culture, or a medium containing one, used to start a fermentation process)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek προζύμιον.</div><b>προηγμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προηγμένη, neuter προηγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>advanced (at or close to the state of the art)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προηγμένες</b> τεχνολογίες - advanced technologies</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προηγμένος, passive perfect participle of προάγω.</div><b>προηγουμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προηγούμενες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the previous day</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tην Κυριακή έκανε κρύο, γιατί την <b>προηγουμένη</b> είχε χιονίσει. - It was cold on Sunday, because it had snowed the day before.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine of the present participle προηγούμενος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://προηγούμενος">προηγούμενος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned inflectional form of the present participle προηγούμενος.</div><b>προηγουμένως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>previously, beforehand</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σας μεταδίδουμε την ομιλία του προέδρου του συνδικάτου, που έγινε <b>προηγουμένως</b>. «Σύντροφοι, όπως είπε <b>προηγούμενα</b> και ο αντιπρόεδρος, πρέπει να προχωρήσουμε σε απεργία.» - We broadcast the speech of the Union&#x27;s president, given just previously. “Comrades, as the vice president said before, we must start a strike.”</div><b>προηγούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lead, to go first, to go in front (to proceed before others)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to lead, to be ahead (to be more advanced than others: in technology, business, a contest, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to precede, to antecede (to happen prior to other things)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Kανένας δεν στερείται την ιδιοκτησία του, παρά μόνο για δημόσια ωφέλεια που έχει αποδειχθεί με τον προσήκοντα τρόπο, όταν και όπως ο νόμος ορίζει, και πάντοτε αφού <b>προηγηθεί</b> πλήρης αποζημίωση, που να ανταποκρίνεται στην αξία την οποία είχε το απαλλοτριούμενο κατά το χρόνο της συζήτησης στο δικαστήριο για τον προσωρινό προσδιορισμό της αποζημίωσης. - No one shall be deprived of his property except for public benefit which must be duly proven, when and as specified by statute and always following full compensation corresponding to the value of the expropriated property at the time of the court hearing on the provisional determination of compensation.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προηγοῦμαι. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + ηγούμαι.</div><b>προηγούμενα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(nonstandard) previously, beforehand</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σας μεταδίδουμε την ομιλία του προέδρου του συνδικάτου, που έγινε <b>προηγουμένως</b>. «Σύντροφοι, όπως είπε <b>προηγούμενα</b> και ο αντιπρόεδρος, πρέπει να προχωρήσουμε σε απεργία.» - We broadcast the speech of the Union&#x27;s president, given just previously. “Comrades, as the vice president said before, we must start a strike.”</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://προηγούμενος">προηγούμενος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) see προηγούμενο for meanings</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προηγούμενος">προηγούμενος</a></div><b>προηγούμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προηγούμενοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the preceding, the previous one</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>προηγούμενος</b> είπε ότι οι ερωτήσεις της προφορικής εξέτασης ήταν δύσκολες. - The previous one [student] said that the questions of the oral test were difficult.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of προηγούμαι, a verb without active forms. Also substantivised. From Ancient Greek προηγούμενος, present participle of προηγέομαι / προηγοῦμαι.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προηγούμενη, neuter προηγούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preceding, previous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of προηγούμαι, a verb without active forms. Also substantivised. From Ancient Greek προηγούμενος, present participle of προηγέομαι / προηγοῦμαι.</div><b>προημιτελικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προημιτελική, neuter προημιτελικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quarterfinal (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προ- (pro-) + ημιτελικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προημιτελικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quarterfinal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προ- (pro-) + ημιτελικός.</div><b>προθέρμανση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προθερμάνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. preheating, prewarming</div>
  <div class='def'>2. warming up, warm-up, warmup (a regime of gentle exercise undertaken before some strenuous exercise or sport)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προθέρμανσις + -ση (-si), with semantic loan from English warming up and German Anwärmen.</div><b>προθείος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προθείοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great-uncle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προ- (pro-, “pre-”) + θείος. Also see the Koine πρόθειος.</div><b>προθεσμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προθεσμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deadline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προθεσμία.</div><b>προθυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) willingness, readiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προθῡμίᾱ. By surface analysis, πρόθυμος + -ία.</div><b>προκάτοχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural προκάτοχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>predecessor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned derivation from Ancient Greek προκατέχω, modelled on κατέχω → κάτοχος.</div><b>προκαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to challenge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to provoke</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to call forth, to cause</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, fom Ancient Greek προκαλῶ, contracted form of προκαλέω. Morphologically, προ- (pro-, “before”) + καλώ.</div><b>προκαρκινικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προκαρκινική, neuter προκαρκινικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>precancerous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, προ- (pro-, “pre-, before”) + καρκινικός.</div><b>προκατάληψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προκαταλήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bias, prejudice</div>
  <div class='ex'>«Περηφάνια και <b>προκατάληψη</b>» είναι ο τίτλος μυθιστορήματος της Τζέιν Όστεν. - &quot;Pride and Prejudice&quot; is the title of a novel by Jane Austen.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek προκατάλη(ψις)) + -ψη (-psi) &amp; semantic loan from French prévention.</div><b>προκαταβάλλομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prepaid</div><b>προκαταβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to pay (especially pay in advance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to prepay, pay a deposit, make an advance payment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek προκᾰτᾰβᾰ́λλω. Morphologically, from προ- (“before”) + καταβάλλω (“pay; overcome; exhaust;”).</div><b>προκαταβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προκαταβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deposit, prepayment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προκαταβολή.</div><b>προκαταβολικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beforehand, in advance</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural neuter of <a href="bword://προκαταβολικός">προκαταβολικός</a></div><b>προκαταβολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προκαταβολική, neuter προκαταβολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>advance, upfront</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προκαταβολική</b> πληρωμή - advance/upfront payment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προκαταβολ(ή)) + -ικός.</div><b>προκείμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προκείμενη, neuter προκείμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in front, who is set before us</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in question, at hand, at issue (currently receiving attention; under discussion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προκείμενος, passive present participle for verb πρόκειμαι.</div><b>προκειμένου</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. <a href="bword://προκειμένου">προκειμένου</a> να: in order to, in order for, so that</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έγινε αλλαγή στο κείμενο, <b>προκειμένου να</b> αποκρυβεί η αλήθεια. - An alteration was applied on the text in order to hide the truth.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. since, because</div>
  <div class='def'>3. provided</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προκειμένου, genitive singular, neuter προκείμενον of passive present participle προκείμενος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of προκείμενο</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προκειμένου, genitive singular, neuter προκείμενον of passive present participle προκείμενος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive neuter singular of <a href="bword://προκείμενος">προκείμενος</a>, (also of masculine)</div>
  <div class='ex'>standard genitive form: προκείμενου (prokeímenou)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προκειμένου, genitive singular, neuter προκείμενον of passive present participle προκείμενος</div><b>προκλητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προκλητική, neuter προκλητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. provocative (serving or tending to elicit a strong, often negative sentiment in another person)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. defiant</div>
  <div class='def'>3. provocative (serving or tending to excite, stimulate or arouse sexual interest; sexy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προκλητικός with semantic loan from French provocant in the sexual sense. By surface analysis, προκλη-) + -τικός.</div><b>προκοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προκοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. success, prosperity, progress, advancement</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κανείς δεν βλέπει <b>προκοπή</b> χωρίς δουλειά. - No one has success without work.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. industry, industriousness, hard work, diligence</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>προκοπή</b> νικάει τη φτώχια. - Diligence beats poverty.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) The money or other rewards that come from industry or hard work.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τόσα χρόνια δούλευε σαν σκυλί ο πατέρας μου αλλά <b>προκοπή</b> καμία. - My father worked so many years like a dog but got no reward.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προκοπή.</div><b>προκριματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προκριματική, neuter προκριματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preliminary, test</div><b>προκυμαία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προκυμαίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wharf, quay, jetty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προκυμία, reanalyzed with nominal suffix -αία.</div><b>προκόβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to progress, thrive, succeed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προκόπτω with metaplasm as in κόπτω &gt; κόβω. Prefix προ- (pro-).</div><b>προκύπτει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal) to be produced (of a result)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal) to occur suddenly</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (impersonal) to be drawn (of a conclusion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Third persons from Ancient Greek προκύπτω. Morphologically, from προ- (“forwards”) + κύπτει.</div><b>προκύπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>see: <a href="bword://προκύπτει">προκύπτει</a> (only in 3rd persons) to emerge, arise, result</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προκύπτω.</div><b>προλίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) proline</div><b>προλαβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to have time to do something, catch up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βιάζομαι! Δεν <b>προλαβαίνω</b>! - I&#x27;m in a hurry. I don&#x27;t have time!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to avert, ward off</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to avoid, obviate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προλαμβάνω.</div><b>προλαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(passive, rare, transitive) to forestall, anticipate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative form for this sense only: προλαβαίνω (prolavaíno)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προλαμβάνω. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-, “before”) + λαμβάνω</div><b>προλετάριος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προλετάριοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proletarian, prole</div><b>προλεταριάτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) proletariat, working class, lower class</div>
  <div class='ex'>λούμπεν <b>προλεταριάτο</b> - lumpenproletariat</div><b>προλεταριακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προλεταριακή, neuter προλεταριακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proletarian</div><b>προληπτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in a preventive, precautionary way</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προληπτικός">προληπτικός</a></div><b>προληπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προληπτική, neuter προληπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. preventing, precautionary - sense as in <a href="bword://προλαμβάνω">προλαμβάνω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. superstitious</div><b>προμήθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προμήθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. procurement, purveyance, supply</div>
  <div class='def'>2. commission</div><b>προμήθειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προμήθιο">προμήθιο</a></div><b>προμήθιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) promethium</div><b>προμαχώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προμαχώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bastion</div><b>προμηθευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προμηθευτές, feminine προμηθεύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supplier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προμηθευτής, with semantic loan from French pourvoyeur. By surface analysis, προμηθεύω + -τής.</div><b>προμηθευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προμηθευτική, neuter προμηθευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) supply, supplier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From προμηθευτής + -ικός.</div><b>προμηθεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be provided, supplied</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek προμηθεύομαι.</div><b>προμηθεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to provide, supply</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η οργάνωση <b>προμήθευε</b> καθαρό νερό στα παιδιά για δεκαετίες. - The organisation provided clean water to the children for decades.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the passive form προμηθεύομαι for more.</div><b>προμηνύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be forewarned, preannounced</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (third person) to be imminent (mostly unpleasant)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>προμηνύονται</b> δυσάρεστες εξελίξεις. - Νο developments are imminent.</div><b>προμηνύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to preannounce, forewarn, demonstrate indications of, presage</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα σύννεφα <b>προμηνύουν</b> βροχή. - The clouds presage rain.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (third person) to be imminent (mostly unpleasant)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Προμηνύεται</b> καταιγίδα. - A storm is imminent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προμηνύω. Morphologically, from προ- (pro-, “before”) + μηνύω.</div><b>προμνησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προμνησίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) déjà vu</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neologism of the 21st century, free translation of the French déjà vu which is frequently used in the original French. Created in the pattern of αμνησία from προ- (“pre, before”) + μνη- + -σία (suffix for abstract nouns).</div><b>προνύμφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προνύμφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>larva</div><b>προξενείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προξενεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consulate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρόξενος + -είο.</div><b>προξενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consular</div><b>προξενώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to cause</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προξενῶ.</div><b>προοδευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προοδευτική, neuter προοδευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. progressive, forward</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gradual</div><b>προοδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to progress, to advance (to develop positively)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Προοδεύουν</b> αλματωδώς. - They are progressing by leaps and bounds.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προοδεύω with semantic loan from French progresser.</div><b>προοπτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προοπτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. perspective (the technique of representing three-dimensional objects on a two-dimensional surface)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prospect (the potential things that may come to pass, often favorable)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. outlook (expectation for the future)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προοπτική, nominalized feminine singular of προοπτικός. In the &#x27;perspective&#x27; sense, a calque of French perspective.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://προοπτικός">προοπτικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>προοπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προοπτική, neuter προοπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perspective (of, in or relating to perspective)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προοπτική</b> σχεδίαση - perspective drawing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from προοπτική with -ικός suffix.</div><b>προορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to decide or determine in advance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προορίζω, from προ- (pro-) + ὁρίζω.</div><b>προορισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προορισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. destination</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mission, aim, purpose</div><b>προπάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>propane</div><b>προπάππος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προπάπποι, feminine προγιαγιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great-grandfather</div><b>προπένιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>propene</div><b>προπαγάνδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προπαγάνδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>propaganda</div><b>προπαραλήγουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προπαραλήγουσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the antepenultimate syllable of a word</div><b>προπαροξύτονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προπαροξύτονη, neuter προπαροξύτονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proparoxytone, (of a word) having a stressed antepenultimate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προπαροξύτονος.</div><b>προπενικό οξύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) propenoic acid (synonym of acrylic acid)</div><b>προπληρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to prepay</div><b>προπομπός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προπομποί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forerunner</div><b>προπονήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προπονήτριες, masculine προπονητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coach, trainer (female)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Female of προπονητής + -τρια (-tria).</div><b>προπονητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προπονητές, feminine προπονήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coach, trainer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested around 1896. From προπονώ + -της (-tis).</div><b>προπονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to coach, train</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προπονέω ~ προπονῶ.</div><b>προπυλένιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>propylene</div><b>προπόνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προπονήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. training</div>
  <div class='def'>2. workout</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From προπονώ (“I train”) + -ση (-si) from ancient -σις.</div><b>προπύργιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προπύργια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bastion (a well-fortified position; a stronghold or citadel)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bastion (a person, group, or thing, that strongly defends some principle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προπύργιον. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-, “fore-”) + πύργ(ος), “tower”) + -ιο (-io).</div><b>προς</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with accusative) towards, toward, to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προχώρησε <b>προς</b> το σχολείο. - She walked to school</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (temporal, with accusative) towards</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Προς</b> το βράδυ το γάλα λιγοστεύει σε ποσότητα. - Towards evening the quantity of milk diminishes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρός, from Proto-Indo-European *próti ~ *préti.</div><b>προς Θεού</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for God&#x27;s sake, for Christ&#x27;s sake, for heaven&#x27;s sake (exclamation of anger, surprise, or impatience)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όχι, δεν θα του πω τίποτα, <b>προς Θεού</b>! - No, of course I won&#x27;t tell him anything, for God&#x27;s sake!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “towards God”.</div><b>προς τα πίσω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>backwards, rearward (direction)</div><b>προς το παρόν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for the moment, for the time being</div><b>προσάρτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσαρτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. annexation (incorporation of territories)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. attachment (process or means by which something is physically attached)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: σύναψη f (sýnapsi), σύνδεση f (sýndesi)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προσάρτησις with semantic loan from French annexion. By surface analysis, προσάρτη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>προσέγγιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσεγγίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. approximation, approach</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rapprochement</div><b>προσέλευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσελεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. attendance (the act of attending; the state of being present)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. turnout (the number or proportion of people who attend or participate in an event or are present at a venue)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προσέλευσις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>προσέρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to arrive, show up (formal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο μάρτυρας δεν <b>προσήλθε</b> στο δικαστήριο. - The witness did not come to court.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσέρχομαι. Morphologically, from προσ- (“toward”) + έρχομαι (“come”).</div><b>προσέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to watch, observe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to watch out, be careful</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to take care of someone</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to take care</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προσέχω. See προς (pros, “towards”) and έχω.</div><b>προσήλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσηλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fixation (maintaining the gaze on a single location)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fixation (a state of mind involving obsession with a particular person, idea or thing)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. attachment, commitment, adherence (a strong psychological bonding to something; being bound emotionally or intellectually to a course of action, cause, values, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προσήλωσις. By surface analysis, προσηλώ(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>προσήνεμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσήνεμη, neuter προσήνεμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>windward</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσήνεμος, from πρός + ἄνεμος.</div><b>προσανατολίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to orientate, orient</div><b>προσανατολισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προσανατολισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orientation</div><b>προσαρμογή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσαρμογές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adjustment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. adaptation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προσαρμογή, with semantic loan from French adaptation.</div><b>προσαρμόζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to get used to, conform</div><b>προσαρμόζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fit, adjust</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to adapt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek προσαρμόζω. For adapt, semantic loan from French adapter. By surface analysis, προσ- (“towards”) + αρμόζω (“fit together, join”).</div><b>προσαρμόσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσαρμόσιμη, neuter προσαρμόσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adaptable</div><b>προσαρτημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προσαρτημένη, neuter προσαρτημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. annexed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προσαρτημένη</b> περιοχή - annexed region</div>
  <div class='def'>2. appended</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προσαρτημένα</b> έγγραφα - attached documents</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of προσαρτώμαι, passive voice of προσαρτώ (“annex”).</div><b>προσαρτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to annex</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to append, affix</div><b>προσβάσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσβάσιμη, neuter προσβάσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accessible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Possibly from πρόσβασ(η)) + -ιμος (-imos).</div><b>προσβασιμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) accessibility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προσβάσιμος + -ότητα.</div><b>προσβλέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to look forward to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσβλέπω. By surface analysis, προσ- (pros-, “towards”) + βλέπω.</div><b>προσβλητικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προσβλητικός">προσβλητικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insultingly, offensively</div><b>προσβλητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσβλητική, neuter προσβλητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insulting, offensive</div><b>προσβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. insult, offense (deliberatedly intended to be rude)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. insult (thing causing offense by being of unacceptable quality)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. onset, offense, attack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προσβολή.</div><b>προσγείωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσγειώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. landing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) coming down to earth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προς (pros, “towards”) + γη (gi, “earth”)</div><b>προσγειώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to land</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to disillusion, bring down to earth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek πρόσγειος + -ώνω, a calque of French atterrir, with semantic loan from English bring down to earth. By surface analysis, προσ- (pros-, “towards”) + γη (gi, “earth”) + -ώνω.</div><b>προσγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be attributed in addition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be adscript</div><b>προσγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) to attribute in addition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of ancient Greek) to write immediately after another character as adscript</div>
  <div class='ex'>see passive perfect participle προσγεγραμμένος (prosgegramménos), and the noun προσγεγραμμένη</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσγρᾰ́φω. Also, semantic loan from German zuschreiben. Morphologically, from προσ- (“toward”) + γράφω (“write”).</div><b>προσδιορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to determine (to ascertain definitely; to figure out, find out, or conclude by analyzing, calculating, or investigating)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to determine (to set the boundaries or limits of)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to modify (to qualify the meaning of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσδιορίζω. By surface analysis, προσ- (pros-) + διορίζω.</div><b>προσδοκία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσδοκίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expectation (that which is expected or looked for)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μεγάλες <b>Προσδοκίες</b> - Great Expectations</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσδοκία.</div><b>προσδοκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to hope for, expect</div><b>προσδοκώμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to hoped for, expected</div><b>προσεγγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to draw near, come near, approach</div><b>προσεγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προσεγμένη, neuter προσεγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carefully done/made, neat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of προσέχω.</div><b>προσεισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foreshock</div><b>προσεκτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προσεκτικός">προσεκτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προσεκτικός">προσεκτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προσεκτικός">προσεκτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carefully, closely</div><b>προσεκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσεκτική, neuter προσεκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>careful, cautious, prudent, meticulous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσεκτῐκός, from προσέχω + -τικός.</div><b>προσελκύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be attracted</div><b>προσελκύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to attract</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσέλκω + -ύω. Morphologically, from προς + ελκύω (“drag”).</div><b>προσεταιριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσεταιριστική, neuter προσεταιριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>associative (algebraic property of an operator)</div><b>προσευχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσευχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prayer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek προσευχή. Morphologically from προσ- (“toward”) + ευχή (“wish”).</div><b>προσεχής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσεχής, neuter προσεχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>approaching, next, forthcoming, upcoming (upcoming)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νομίζω ότι είναι πολύ νωρίς να πούμε τι θα κάνει η <b>προσεχής</b> διακυβερνητική διάσκεψη. - I think that it is too early to say what the upcoming intergovernmental conference will produce.</div><b>προσεχτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσεχτική, neuter προσεχτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προσεκτικός">προσεκτικός</a></div><b>προσεύχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pray</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek προσεύχομαι. Morphologically, προσ- (“towards”) + εύχομαι (“wish”).</div><b>προσηνέμως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>windward</div><b>προσθέτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to add, append, extend</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to add</div><b>προσθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. addition, extension, affix</div>
  <div class='def'>2. addition, inclusion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσθήκη. Morphologically, from προσ- + -θήκη (“case, holster”).</div><b>προσιτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσιτή, neuter προσιτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accessible, approachable, friendly (person)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accessible (location)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. accessible, understandable (book, play, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. affordable (price)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσιτός, derived from πρόσειμι, with semantic loan from French accessible.</div><b>προσκήνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προσκήνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in an ancient theater) proscenium (the stage area immediately in front of the scene building)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in an modern theater) proscenium (the stage area between the curtain and the orchestra)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) forefront, foreground</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προσκήνιον. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + σκηνή + -ιο (-io).</div><b>προσκαλεσμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προσκαλεσμένη, neuter προσκαλεσμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who has been invited</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of προσκαλούμαι, passive voice of προσκαλώ (“invite”).</div><b>προσκαλούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be invited</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be summoned</div><b>προσκαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to invite</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to summon, call for</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσκαλῶ, contracted form of προσκαλέω. By surface analysis, προσ- (pros-, “toward”) + καλώ.</div><b>προσκεκλημένος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προσκεκλημένοι, feminine προσκεκλημένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) guest, synonym of <a href="bword://καλεσμένος">καλεσμένος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>έφτασαν οι <b>προσκεκλημένοι</b> μας - our guests have arrived</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formal perfect participle of προσκαλούμαι, passive voice of προσκαλώ (“invite”). Retains the ancient reduplication κε-κ...-. Compare to the standard προσκαλεσμένος. Also substantivised.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προσκεκλημένη, neuter προσκεκλημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) who has been invited as a guest, form of προκαλεσμένος</div>
  <div class='ex'>synonym of καλεσμένος (kalesménos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formal perfect participle of προσκαλούμαι, passive voice of προσκαλώ (“invite”). Retains the ancient reduplication κε-κ...-. Compare to the standard προσκαλεσμένος. Also substantivised.</div><b>προσκοπίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσκοπίνες, masculine πρόσκοπος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Girl Guide, Girl Scout</div><b>προσκοπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσκοπική, neuter προσκοπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scout (related to scouts and scouting)</div><b>προσκοπιμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prefeasibility</div><b>προσκοπισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) Scouting, the Scout Movement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) scouting (activities of Scouting)</div><b>προσκρούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to knock against, have a collision</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πλοίο <b>προσέκρουσε</b> στην προβλήτα του λιμανιού. - The ship hit the pier of the port.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to face an obstacle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to face an obstacle; to be contrary to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο νόμος αυτός <b>προσκρούει</b> στις επιταγές του συντάγματος. - This law is contrary to the orders of the constitution.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσκρούω. Morphologically, from προσ- (“towards”) + κρούω (“strike”).</div><b>προσκυνήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προσκυνήτριες, masculine προσκυνητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pilgrim</div><b>προσκυνητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προσκυνητές, feminine προσκυνήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pilgrim (usually male)</div><b>προσκυνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to genuflect, to kneel down, to kneel (in worship or reverence)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to worship</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (historical) to bend the knee, to genuflect (to swear fealty or allegiance; to acknowledge one&#x27;s submission or servitude)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) to bend the knee, to bow, to kowtow, to prostrate oneself (to show undue deference, obedience)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προσκυνῶ, contracted form of προσκυνέω.</div><b>προσκόλληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσκολλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dedication</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cohesiveness, adhesion</div><b>προσκόπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) stumble, meet with difficulties</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η προσπάθειά μου <b>προσέκοψε</b> στην άρνησή του. - My effort stumbled upon his denial.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσκόπτω. By surface analysis, προσ- (“toward”) + κόπτω (“cut”).</div><b>προσκύνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προσκυνήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pilgrimage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From προσκυνώ + -μα (-ma, “noun”).</div><b>προσλαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to take on, to hire, to recruit (to employ, to give a job to)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to take on (to begin to have or exhibit: characteristic)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσλαμβάνω with semantic loan from French acquérir.</div><b>προσληπτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσληπτή, neuter προσληπτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hirable, employable, recruitable</div><b>προσομοίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>simulation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προσομοιω(τής), “simulator”) + -ση (-si), a calque of English simulation.</div><b>προσοχή</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. attention, be careful, watch out</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Προσοχή</b>! Το δέντρο πέφτει. - Watch out! The tree is falling!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. attention (drill order)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσοχή.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) attention, focus</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην του αποσπάς την <b>προσοχή</b> όταν οδηγάει. - Don&#x27;t distract his attention while he&#x27;s driving.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) attention, care</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι δίσκοι μου χρειάζονται <b>προσοχή</b> και φροντίδα. - My records need attention and care.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable) attention (upright pose with hands rigidly at side)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσοχή.</div><b>προσπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσπάθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>attempt, try; go (informal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε ανταγωνιστής επιτρέπεται τρεις <b>προσπάθειες</b>. - Each competitor is allowed three attempts.</div><b>προσπέλαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσπελάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>access, approaching</div><b>προσπαθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to attempt, try</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προσπαθῶ, προσπαθέω, from προσπαθής + -έω, from Ancient Greek πρός + πάθος + -ής.</div><b>προσποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pretend, feign, affect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσποιοῦμαι, past participle of προσποιέομαι, passive form of προσποιέω.</div><b>προσροφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to adsorb</div><b>προστάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dictate, to enjoin (to direct with authority)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) to command, to order (to do: να + verb)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to order</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προστάσσω. By surface analysis, προσ- (pros-) + τάζω.</div><b>προστάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προστάτες, feminine προστάτιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. protector</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pimp</div>
  <div class='def'>3. prostate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προστάτης</div><b>προστάτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προστάτιδες, masculine προστάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>protector</div><b>προστακτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προστακτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imperative mood</div><b>προστασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προστασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>protection, keeping safe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προστασία, from προστάτης, with semantic loan from French protection.</div><b>προστατευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προστατευτική, neuter προστατευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. protectionist, protective</div>
  <div class='def'>2. patronising</div><b>προστατευόμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προστατευόμενοι, feminine προστατευομένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>protégé</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of προστατεύομαι, passive of προστατεύω. Also substantivized as a calque of French protégé.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προστατευόμενη, neuter προστατευόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>protected</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Προστατευόμενη</b> Ονομασία Προέλευσης - Protected Designation of Origin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of προστατεύομαι, passive of προστατεύω. Also substantivized as a calque of French protégé.</div><b>προστατεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to protect, safeguard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to give patronage to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προστᾰτεύω, from προστάτης + -εύω.</div><b>προσφάτως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) recently</div><b>προσφέρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to offer yourself</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be suitable for</div><b>προσφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to offer, propose</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to give, present</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσφέρω. By surface analysis, προσ- (pros-, “toward”) + φέρω.</div><b>προσφεύγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to turn to, to resort (to consult for help; to have recourse to)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to appeal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προσφεύγω. By surface analysis, προσ- (pros-) + φεύγω.</div><b>προσφιλής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσφιλής, neuter προσφιλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dear, beloved, close to one&#x27;s heart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cherished (thing, ideal, pastime, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προσφιλής.</div><b>προσφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. offer, offering</div>
  <div class='def'>2. proposal, tender</div>
  <div class='def'>3. contribution (to joint work)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. prosphora, holy bread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προσφορά.</div><b>προσφυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσφυγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. recourse, resorting, appeal (the use of (someone or something) as a source of help in a difficult situation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rhetoric) appeal (a call to, or the use of, a principle or quality for purposes of persuasion)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προσφυγή</b> στην αυθεντία - appeal to authority</div>
  <div class='def'>3. appeal (an application to a superior court or judge for a decision or order by an inferior court or judge to be reviewed and overturned)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προσφυγή with semantic loan from French recours.</div><b>προσφυγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσφυγική, neuter προσφυγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) refugee (attributive)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προσφυγική</b> κρίση - refugee crisis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πρόσφυγ(ας)) + -ικός.</div><b>προσφύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, intransitive) to be fully attached</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the middle voice of Ancient Greek προσφῠ́ομαι (“I am attached, I cling”) of active προσφῠ́ω (“cause to grow”). Morphologically, from προσ- (“towards”) + φύομαι.</div><b>προσχέδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προσχέδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. draft (an early version of a written work or drawing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. draft (a preliminary sketch or outline for a plan)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. blueprint</div>
  <div class='def'>4. maquette</div>
  <div class='def'>5. preliminary sketch, cartoon</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: σκίτσο n (skítso)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προ- (pro-) + σχέδιο, a calque of French avant-projet.</div><b>προσχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to join, go over, accede</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Προσχώρησαν</b> στη συμμαχία. - They joined the alliance.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to adhere, adopt</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Προσχωρεί</b> στο σοσιαλισμό. - He adheres to socialism.</div><b>προσωνυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσωνυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>name, title</div>
  <div class='ex'>«Ηρακλής νικητής», <b>προσωνυμία</b> του Ηρακλή στην αρχαία Ρώμη. - &quot;Hercules Victor&quot; was the title of Heracles in ancient Rome.</div><b>προσωπικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προσωπικός">προσωπικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>personally</div><b>προσωπικές αντωνυμίες</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of προσωπική <a href="bword://αντωνυμία">αντωνυμία</a></div><b>προσωπική αντωνυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσωπικές αντωνυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>personal pronoun</div><b>προσωπικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://προσωπικός">προσωπικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://προσωπικός">προσωπικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter singular</i></div>
  <div class='def'>personnel, staff</div><b>προσωπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσωπική, neuter προσωπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. personal, intimate, private</div>
  <div class='def'>2. personal</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προσωπική</b> αντωνυμία - personal pronoun</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek προσωπικός, from πρόσωπον + -ικός.</div><b>προσωπικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσωπικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. character, personality</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει ισχυρή προσωπικότητα. - He has a strong personality.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. personality (in show business, sport, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο κόσμος συγκεντρώθηκε να δει τις προσωπικότητες από το Hollywood. - People gathered to see the personalities from Hollywood</div>
  <div class='def'>3. influential person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From προσωπικός + -ότητα. Calque of French personnalité. First attested 1848.</div><b>προσωπογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσωπογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>portrait (painting, photograph etc of a person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa πρόσωπον &quot;face&quot; + -γραφία, from Ancient Greek γράφω &quot;I write, draw. paint&quot;.</div><b>προσωρινά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προσωρινός">προσωρινός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. temporarily</div>
  <div class='def'>2. provisionally</div><b>προσωρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προσωρινή, neuter προσωρινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>temporary, provisional</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Composed of προσ- (pros-) + ώρα + -ινός.</div><b>προσόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προσόντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. qualification (eg: diploma, degree, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. asset</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) the equipment needed for a task or project</div><b>προτίμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προτιμήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>preference</div><b>προτείνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to suggest, propose</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to propose, nominate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (archaic) to stretch forth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek προτείνω, from προ- (pro-) + τείνω.</div><b>προτελευταίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προτελευταία, neuter προτελευταίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>penultimate, last but one, next to last</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek προτελευταῖος. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-, “before”) + τελευταίος.</div><b>προτεραιοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to prioritize</div><b>προτεραιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προτεραιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. priority (the quality of being earlier or coming first compared to another thing; the state of being prior)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. priority, right of way (the right to pass first)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. priority (an item&#x27;s relative importance)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. priority (a goal of a person or an organisation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek προτεραῖος + -ότητα, a calque of French priorité.</div><b>προτεροζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προτεροζωική, neuter προτεροζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Proterozoic</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προτεροζωικός</b> αιώνας - Proterozoic eon</div><b>προτιμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to prefer, like more</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προτιμώ</b> να περπατήσω - I prefer to walk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προ- (pro-) + τῑμάω.</div><b>προτιμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προτιμάω">προτιμάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa προτιμῶ, from Ancient Greek προτιμάω, from προ- (pro-) + τιμάω.</div><b>προτομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προτομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bust (a sculptural portrayal of a person&#x27;s head and shoulders)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προτομή.</div><b>προτού</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>before</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek προτοῦ.</div><b>προτρέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be urged to</div><b>προτρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to urge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προτρέπω. By surface analysis, προ- (“before”) + τρέπω (“turn”).</div><b>προτροπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προτροπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. urging, exhortation, encouragement</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: παρακίνηση f (parakínisi)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prompt (a sequence of characters that is displayed to indicate that a computer is ready to receive input)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προτροπή.</div><b>προφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pronounce, to enunciate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προφέρω, with semantic loan from French prononcer. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + φέρω.</div><b>προφήτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προφήτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prophet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προφήτης.</div><b>προφίλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) profile (the side view of a subject)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, indeclinable) One&#x27;s character, style, or manner of behavior.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French profil, from Italian profilo, profilare (“to spin forth, to draw forth”), filare (“to spin yarn”), from Late Latin fīlō (“to weave”), Latin fīlum (“thread”).</div><b>προφανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προφανής, neuter προφανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obvious, evident</div><b>προφανώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. obviously, of course</div>
  <div class='ex'>—Δυσαρεστήθηκε ο πελάτης;
—<b>Προφανώς</b>!’’ έφυγε από το κατάστημα. - —Was the customer displeased?
—Obviously! He left the shop.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. obviously, apparently, evidently</div>
  <div class='ex'>—Γιατί ζήτησε μεγάλο ποτήρι;
—<b>Προφανώς</b> το ήθελε για τη μπίρα! - —Why did (s)he ask for a large glass?
—Apparently (s)he needed it for the beer.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek προφανῶς, adverb of the Ancient Greek προφανής.</div><b>προφασίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pretend, sham, feign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προφᾰσίζομαι (“pretend by excuse”). With prefix προ-.</div><b>προφητεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προφητείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prophecy, prediction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προφητείᾱ.</div><b>προφητεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be prophesied, predicted</div><b>προφητεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to prophesy, predict</div><b>προφητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προφητική, neuter προφητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prophetic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προφητικός.</div><b>προφθάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προφταίνω">προφταίνω</a></div><b>προφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pronunciation, articulation, enunciation (manner in which sounds are pronounced)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>προφορά</b> μερικών ήχων στα Ελληνικά είναι δύσκολη για τους ξένους. - The pronunciation of some of the sounds of Greek is difficult for foreigners.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accent (manner in which a certain language is pronounced depending on location and other factors)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορούσα να καταλάβω την Κρητική <b>προφορά</b> του. - I couldn&#x27;t understand his Cretan accent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προφορά.</div><b>προφορικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oral exams</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προφορικός">προφορικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προφορικός">προφορικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προφορικός">προφορικός</a></div><b>προφορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προφορική, neuter προφορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oral, spoken, verbal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προφορικός. By surface analysis, προφορά + -ικός.</div><b>προφτάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προφταίνω">προφταίνω</a></div><b>προφταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to catch, to get to in time, to make it in time for (to be in time for)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to catch up (with) (to reach something that had been ahead)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to make it in time</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to have time</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to be around at the same time as</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προφθάνω with -αίνω ending and -φθ- → -φτ- change.</div><b>προφυλάσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to protect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προφυλάσσω. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + φυλάσσω.</div><b>προφυλακτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προφυλακτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bumper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (usually) protector</div><b>προφυλακτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. protectively</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carefully</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://προφυλακτικός">προφυλακτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://προφυλακτικό">προφυλακτικό</a></div><b>προφυλακτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προφυλακτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>condom</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://προφυλακτικός">προφυλακτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://προφυλακτικός">προφυλακτικός</a></div><b>προφυλακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προφυλακτική, neuter προφυλακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prophylactic; protecting, protective</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prophylactic; contraceptive</div>
  <div class='def'>3. careful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek προφυλακτικός, an adjectival formation from Ancient Greek προφυλάσσω, from προ- (pro-) + φυλάσσω, with semantic loan from French préservatif.</div><b>προφυλαχτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προφυλαχτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προφυλακτήρας">προφυλακτήρας</a></div><b>προφυλαχτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προφυλακτικά">προφυλακτικά</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://προφυλαχτικό">προφυλαχτικό</a></div><b>προφυλαχτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προφυλαχτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προφυλακτικό">προφυλακτικό</a></div><b>προφυλαχτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προφυλαχτική, neuter προφυλαχτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προφυλακτικός">προφυλακτικός</a></div><b>προφυματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pretuberculous, pretubercular</div><b>προφύλαξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προφυλάξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. guard, protection, shelter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prophylaxis</div><b>προχθές</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two days ago, on the day before yesterday</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From προ (pro, “before”) + χθές.</div><b>προχοΐδα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>burette, glass laboratory instrument used for titration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Katharevousa learned borrowing from Koine Greek προχοΐς, diminutive of Ancient Greek πρόχους by semantic influence of the latter; itself from προ- (pro-) + χοῦς, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰew-.</div><b>προχτές</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προχθές">προχθές</a></div><b>προχωράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to move forward, advance, progress, proceed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with <a href="bword://σε">σε</a> (se) ) start to do</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>προχωρήσουμε</b> στην υλοποίηση του έργου. - We will move forward with the realisation of the project.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προ- (pro-) + χωρώ + -άω.</div><b>προχωρημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine προχωρημένη, neuter προχωρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>advanced (in a late stage of development or education; having greatly progressed beyond an initial stage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of προχωρώ.</div><b>προχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προχωράω">προχωράω</a></div><b>προψές</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://προχθές">προχθές</a></div><b>προωθητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προωθητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pusher, mover, impeller</div><b>προωθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to push forward, impel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προωθῶ, contracted form of προωθέω. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + ωθώ.</div><b>προϊστάμενη</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine for <a href="bword://προϊστάμενος">προϊστάμενος</a> (past participle of προΐσταμαι)</div><b>προϊστάμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προϊσταμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>superior, senior (in a hierarchy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προϊστάμενος, passive present participle of προΐστημι.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προϊστάμενοι, feminine προϊσταμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. superior (the one that has the higher rank in a division of a corporation, organization, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. supervisor, foreman, manager</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek προϊστάμενος, passive present participle of προΐστημι.</div><b>προϊστορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προϊστορική, neuter προϊστορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prehistoric</div><b>προϊόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προϊόντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. product, produce, yield</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (plural normally) goods, products</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προϊόντα</b> ψυγείου - refrigerated goods</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter form of adjective προϊών, semantic loan from French produit.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://προϊών">προϊών</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>προϊών</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine προϊούσα, neuter προϊόν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. progressive, gradual, advancing (increasing in severity)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προϊούσα</b> πνευμονία - progressive pneumonia</div>
  <div class='def'>2. progress, increasing, mounting</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>προϊούσα</b> εξέλιξη - progress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek προϊών, present active participle of From Ancient Greek πρόειμι, from πρό + εἶμι.</div><b>προϋπηρεσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προϋπηρεσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>work experience, professional experience</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχετε <b>προϋπηρεσία</b> σε αυτό το επάγγελμα; - Do you have work experience in this profession?</div><b>προϋπολογισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural προϋπολογισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. budget</div>
  <div class='def'>2. estimate</div><b>προϋπόθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προϋποθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. presupposition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. condition, precondition, prerequisite</div>
  <div class='def'>3. premise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From προ- (pro-, “before”) + υπόθεση. Calque of French présupposition.</div><b>προώθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προωθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. advancing, forwarding (of goods, troops, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. promotion, advancement (elevation of a person in rank or position)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. promotion (dissemination of information in order to increase its popularity)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. promotion</div>
  <div class='def'>5. expediting, fast-tracking (of a process)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προωθώ + -ση (-si) with semantic loan from French promotion.</div><b>πρυτανεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρυτανείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rectorship, deanship</div><b>πρωί</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>morning</div>
  <div class='ex'>Also, πρωία (proḯa) (ironic or Katharevousa)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek πρωΐ, from Ancient Greek πρωΐ, an adverb reanalysed as a neuter noun. The inflected forms properly belong to the adjective πρωινός.</div><b>πρωί πρωί</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. too early in the morning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bright and early</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Repetition of πρωί, literally &quot;morning morning&quot;</div><b>πρωθυπουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρωθυπουργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>premiership, office of prime minister</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πρωθυπουργός + -ία. First attested 1839.</div><b>πρωθυπουργόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://πρωθυπουργός">πρωθυπουργός</a></div><b>πρωθυπουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πρωθυπουργοί, feminine πρωθυπουργίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prime minister, premier (head of a Westminster-style government)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πρωθ- (proth-, “first, prime”) + υπουργός, calque of English prime minister. First attested 1839.</div><b>πρωινό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρωινά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. morning</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breakfast, breakfast time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρωί, cf. πρωΐ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πρωινός">πρωινός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πρωινός">πρωινός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρωί, cf. πρωΐ.</div><b>πρωινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωινή, neuter πρωινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>morning</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρωινή</b> εφημερίδα - morning paper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρωΐ + -ινός.</div><b>πρωκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωκτική, neuter πρωκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anal (related to, or through the anus)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρωκτικός</b> σωλήνας - anal tube</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωκτός + -ικός.</div><b>πρωκτός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρωκτοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>anus</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δυστυχώς, οι εξετάσεις δείχνουν ότι πάσχετε από καρκίνο του <b>πρωκτού</b>. - Unfortunately, the tests show that you are suffering from cancer of the anus.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρωκτός.</div><b>πρωτάθλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρωταθλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>championship (competition)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωτ- (prot-, “first”) + άθλημα</div><b>πρωταγωνιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρωταγωνιστές, feminine πρωταγωνίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>protagonist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρωταγωνιστής, equivalent to πρωτ- (prot-, “first”) + αγωνιστής.</div><b>πρωταγωνιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωταγωνιστική, neuter πρωταγωνιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leading, protagonistic</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παίζει <b>πρωταγωνιστικό</b> ρόλο. - (He/she/it) plays a leading role.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πρωταγωνιστ(ής)) + -ικός.</div><b>πρωταγωνιστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively, intransitive) to play the leading role, to be the main figure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to play the leading role, to star</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρωταγωνιστῶ.</div><b>πρωταθλήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρωταθλήτριες, masculine πρωταθλητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>champion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωτ- (prot-, “first”) + αθλητής + -τρια (-tria). First attested 1896.</div><b>πρωταθλητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρωταθλητές, feminine πρωταθλήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) champion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πρωτ- (prot-, “first”) + αθλητής, first attested in 1839. The medieval or Late Koine πρωταθλητής with different sense &quot;first martyr of faith&quot;, also see πρώταθλος.</div><b>πρωτακτίνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) protactinium, protoactinium</div><b>πρωταρχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωταρχική, neuter πρωταρχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. primordial (first, earliest or original)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. primary, prime (main; principal; chief; placed ahead of others)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πρώτ(η)) + αρχ(ή)) + -ικός, a calque of French primordial.</div><b>πρωτεΐνη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. protein (class of food)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. protein (molecular structure)</div><b>πρωτευουσιάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρωτευουσιάνες, masculine πρωτευουσιάνος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, humorous) Athenian (female citizen or inhabitant of Athens)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωτεύουσα + -ιάνα.</div><b>πρωτευουσιάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρωτευουσιάνοι, feminine πρωτευουσιάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, humorous) Athenian (male citizen or inhabitant of Athens)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωτεύουσα + -ιάνος, first attested 1887.</div><b>πρωτεύουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρωτεύουσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capital, capital city</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πρωτεύων">πρωτεύων</a></div><b>πρωτεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to rank in first place, to come first</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (third person) to take precedence, be particularly important</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρωτεύω, from πρῶτος + -εύω.</div><b>πρωτεύων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτεύουσα, neuter πρωτεύον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. main, primary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. premier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See πρωτεύω, with Semantic loan from French principal.</div><b>πρωτοανακαλύπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to was first discovered</div><b>πρωτοανακαλύπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to discover for the first time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωτο- (“first”) + ανα- (“re-”) + καλύπτω (“cover”) (πρωτο- (proto-) + ανακαλύπτω)</div><b>πρωτοβάθμιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτοβάθμια, neuter πρωτοβάθμιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of the first rank</div>
  <div class='def'>2. primary (first or earliest in a group or series)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρωτοβάθμια</b> εκπαίδευση - primary education</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of an equation) first-degree</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρωτοβάθμια</b> εξίσωση - first-degree equation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πρωτο- (proto-) + βαθμ(ός)) + -ιος (-ios), a calque of French de premier degré.</div><b>πρωτοβουλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρωτοβουλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>initiative</div>
  <div class='ex'>παίρνω την πρωτοβουλία να - to take the initiative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωτο- (proto-, “first”) + βουλή + -ία. First attested 1871.</div><b>πρωτοβρόχι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρωτοβρόχια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first rain of autumn</div><b>πρωτογλώσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proto-language</div><b>πρωτοελλαδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>early Helladic</div><b>πρωτοελληνική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Proto-Greek</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, πρωτο- (proto-, “proto-”) + ελληνική.</div><b>πρωτοετής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτοετής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of a student) first-year</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) first-year student</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωτο- (proto-) + -ετής.</div><b>πρωτοκολλημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine πρωτοκολλημένη, neuter πρωτοκολλημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>registered and numbered in a logbook</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αίτησή μου είναι <b>πρωτοκολλημένη</b> με αριθμό πρωτοκόλλου 145. - My application is registered with protocol number 145.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of πρωτοκολλώμαι, passive voice of πρωτοκολλώ (“register”).</div><b>πρωτοκολλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to register on the official logbook that keeps records of incoming and outgoing documents</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αίτησή σας <b>πρωτοκολλήθηκε</b> με αριθμό πρωτοκόλλου 145. - Your application has been registered with protocol number 145.</div><b>πρωτομάρτυρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πρωτομάρτυρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>protomartyr</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek πρωτομάρτυρα, accusative singular of πρωτομάρτυς. By surface analysis, πρωτο- (“first”) + μάρτυρας (“martyr; witness”).</div><b>πρωτομαγιάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>connected with 1st of May</div><b>πρωτομηνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρωτομηνιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar) first day of the month</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek *πρωτομηνία), modelled after Ancient Greek νουμηνία. By surface analysis, πρωτο- (proto-) + μήν(ας)) + -ιά.</div><b>πρωτοπορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρωτοπορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vanguard, innovator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πρώτος + πορεία, a calque of French avant-garde.</div><b>πρωτοποριακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτοποριακή, neuter πρωτοποριακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pioneering, groundbreaking, innovative, original</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πρωτοπορί(α)) + -ακός, a loose calque of French avant-gardiste.</div><b>πρωτοπόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pioneer</div><b>πρωτοσέλιδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτοσέλιδη, neuter πρωτοσέλιδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>front-page (which appears on the front page of a publication)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρωτοσέλιδος</b> τίτλος - front-page headline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πρωτο- (proto-) + σελίδ(α)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>πρωτοστάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρωτοστάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ringleader, prime mover</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρωτοστάτης.</div><b>πρωτοστατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to play a leading part</div><b>πρωτοστόμια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Protostomia.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Protostomia.</div><b>πρωτοτόκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτοτόκος or πρωτοτόκα, neuter πρωτοτόκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a mother) first-time, having her firstborn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρωτοτόκος.</div><b>πρωτοφανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτοφανής, neuter πρωτοφανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>very unusual, unprecedented</div><b>πρωτόγαλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural πρωτογάλατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) colostrum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρωτό- + γαλα (gala, “milk”)</div><b>πρωτόγονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτόγονη, neuter πρωτόγονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. primitive, savage, barbaric</div>
  <div class='def'>2. uncivilised, uncultivated</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (as a noun) savage, primitive person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρωτόγονος. By surface analysis, πρωτό- + γόνος.</div><b>πρωτόζωα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Protozoa.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Protozoa.</div><b>πρωτόκολλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρωτόκολλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. protocol, diplomatic document about the relationships and agreements between states and their representatives</div>
  <div class='def'>2. logbook recording the issue and return of documents</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πρωτόκολλον, equivalent to πρωτό- + κόλλα.</div><b>πρωτόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρωτόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>proton</div><b>πρωτότοκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτότοκη, neuter πρωτότοκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. firstborn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) firstborn (child)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρωτότοκος.</div><b>πρωτότυπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρωτότυπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prototype</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek πρωτότῠπον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πρωτότυπος">πρωτότυπος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πρωτότυπος">πρωτότυπος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>πρωτότυπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρωτότυπη, neuter πρωτότυπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prototype</div>
  <div class='def'>2. original</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρωτότυπες</b> ιδέες - original ideas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρωτότυπος, equivalent to πρωτό- + τύπος. Semantic loan from French original for which also see its synonym prototype.</div><b>πρόβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρόβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trial (test of a product or procedure)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fitting, try-on (the act of trying on clothes to inspect or adjust the fit)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rehearsal (the practising of something which is to be performed before an audience)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian prova.</div><b>πρόβατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρόβατα, feminine προβατίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sheep</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) sheep (someone who is easily deceived by others)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) lamb (a very gentle person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόβατον. Cognates include Mariupol Greek про́вату.</div><b>πρόβειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρόβεια, neuter πρόβειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sheep&#x27;s, ovine</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρόβειο</b> κρέας - mutton</div><b>πρόβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρόβια, neuter πρόβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πρόβειος">πρόβειος</a></div><b>πρόβλεψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προβλέψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. forecasting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. forecast, prediction</div>
  <div class='def'>3. provision (the act of providing, or making previous preparation)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. provision (money set aside for a future event)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from προβλέπω + -ση (-si), a calque of French prévision and provision.</div><b>πρόβλημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προβλήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. problem, complicated question or subject</div>
  <div class='def'>2. problem, sum</div>
  <div class='def'>3. difficulty, trouble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek πρόβλημᾰ. For sense &quot;difficulty&quot;, semantic loan from French problème or English problem.</div><b>πρόγευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προγεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>breakfast (first meal of the day)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρό- + γεύμα</div><b>πρόγνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προγνώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. precognition, forecast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prognosis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόγνωσις.</div><b>πρόγονος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρόγονοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ancestor</div><b>πρόγραμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προγράμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. schedule (timed plan of events)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. program (of events)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. calendar (future events for theatre etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. broadcast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόγραμμα.</div><b>πρόδρομος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πρόδρομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forerunner, precursor, pioneer</div><b>πρόεδρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πρόεδροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chairman</div>
  <div class='def'>2. president</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρόεδρος. By surface analysis, προ- (pro-) + Ancient Greek ἕδρᾱ.</div><b>πρόθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. preposition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intent, intention, purpose</div>
  <div class='def'>3. prosthesis (an artificial replacement for a body part)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. prosthetic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόθεσις.</div><b>πρόθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prefix</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of New Latin praefixum or French préfixe, on the basis of επίθημα.</div><b>πρόθυμα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πρόθυμος">πρόθυμος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>willingly, readily</div><b>πρόθυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρόθυμη, neuter πρόθυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>willing, ready</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόθυμος</div><b>πρόκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρόκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tack, brad</div>
  <div class='def'>2. metal or wooden headless shoe nail, sparable</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) hint</div>
  <div class='ex'>πετάω <b>πρόκες</b> - to throw out hints</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian brocca; likely back-formed in Greek from accusative singular την πρόκα which was rebracketed from earlier τη μπρόκα.</div><b>πρόκειται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (followed by <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia) /<a href="bword://περί">περί</a>) it&#x27;s about, it concerns</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να σας μιλήσω. <b>Πρόκειται</b> για τον γιό σας. - I have to speak to you. It&#x27;s about your son.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (followed by να) to be going to, be to (with regard to something likely, though not certain)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>πρόκειται</b> να τον ξαναδείς ποτέ. - You&#x27;re (probably) never going to see him again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From third-person singular πρόκειται of Ancient Greek πρόκειμαι. See πρό- and κείμαι, ancient κεῖμαι.</div><b>πρόκληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προκλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>provocation, challenge, defiance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρόκλησις with -ση (-si) and semantic loan from French provocation.</div><b>πρόκριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>qualification (the act or process of qualifying for a position, achievement, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρόκρισις. By surface analysis, προκρί(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>πρόληψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προλήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prevention</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>πρόληψη</b> είναι καλύτερη από τη θεραπεία. - Prevention is better than cure.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. superstition</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: δεισιδαιμονία f (deisidaimonía)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. prolepsis (a construction that consists of placing an element in a syntactic unit before that to which it would logically correspond)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (rhetoric) prolepsis, anticipation (the anticipation of an objection to an argument)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρόληψις with -ση (-si) ending and semantic loan from French prévention, préjugé and prolepse.</div><b>πρόλοβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόλοβος.</div><b>πρόλογος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prologue, preface</div><b>πρόξενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πρόξενοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. consul</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cause</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προ- (pro-, “for”) + ξένος</div><b>πρόοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρόοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>progress, moving forward</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόοδος.</div><b>πρόπερσι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>two years ago, during the year before last</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> προ- (pro-) + πέρσι</div><b>πρόποδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foot (of a mountain)</div><b>πρόποση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toast (proposed salutation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόποσις.</div><b>πρόσβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>access, approach</div><b>πρόσημο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρόσημα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sign, the symbol to differentiate a positive or negative number.</div><b>πρόσθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. addition (the act of adding anything)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. addition (the arithmetic operation of adding)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόσθεσις.</div><b>πρόσκαιρα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πρόσκαιρος">πρόσκαιρος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>temporarily, pro tempore</div><b>πρόσκαιρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρόσκαιρη, neuter πρόσκαιρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. temporary, lasting a short time</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ending soon</div><b>πρόσκληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσκλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. call</div>
  <div class='def'>2. invitation</div><b>πρόσκομμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προσκόμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obstacle</div><b>πρόσκοπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρόσκοποι, feminine προσκοπίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scout, Boy Scout</div><b>πρόσληψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσλήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. employment, hiring, recruitment, taking on (of employee)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intake (of nutrient)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρόσληψις, semantically adapted to the modern meaning of προσλαμβάνω.</div><b>πρόσοψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προσόψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. facade, front, frontage (the face of a building, especially the front view or elevation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. facade, front (a deceptive or insincere outward appearance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρόσοψις + -ψη (-psi).</div><b>πρόστιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fine (payment for breaking the law)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόστιμον.</div><b>πρόστυχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρόστυχη, neuter πρόστυχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. low, ignoble</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lewd, lascivious</div>
  <div class='def'>3. vile, vulgar, nasty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek προστυχής.</div><b>πρόσφατα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>recently</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πρόσφατος">πρόσφατος</a></div><b>πρόσφατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>recent</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρόσφατες</b> αλλαγές - recent changes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόσφατος.</div><b>πρόσφυγας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρόσφυγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refugee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρόσφυξ.</div><b>πρόσφυμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προσφύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>affix</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek πρόσφυμα with semantic loan from New Latin affixum.</div><b>πρόσχαρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρόσχαρη, neuter πρόσχαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheerful, jovial, jolly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek πρόσχᾰρης, with metaplasm of -ης (-is) &gt; -ος (-os) akin to that seen in δύστυχος &lt; δῠστῠχής, from Ancient Greek προσχαίρω + -ης, from προσ- (pros-, “towards, pro-”) + χαίρω.</div><b>πρόσχημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural προσχήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pretext, pretense (a false, contrived, or assumed purpose or reason)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρόσχημα.</div><b>πρόσωπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρόσωπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. face</div>
  <div class='def'>2. character, person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πρόσωπον.</div><b>πρόσωπου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of προσώπου, genitive singular of <a href="bword://πρόσωπο">πρόσωπο</a></div><b>πρόταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural προτάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sentence, clause, proposition</div>
  <div class='ex'>κύρια <b>πρόταση</b> - main clause, independent clause</div>
  <div class='def'>2. proposal, proposition (something which is proposed, or offered for consideration or acceptance)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. motion (formal request put to a court, meeting, parliament, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρότᾰσῐς, with semantic loan from French proposition in the grammatical sense.</div><b>πρότυπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρότυπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. template, model, pattern (used for later copies)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. standard</div><b>πρόφαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pretext</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρόφασις.</div><b>πρόχειρα φαγητά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πρόχειρο">πρόχειρο</a> <a href="bword://φαγητό">φαγητό</a></div><b>πρόχειρο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πρόχειρος">πρόχειρος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πρόχειρος">πρόχειρος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρόχειρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) clipboard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Internet) draft, an early version of an e-mail</div>
  <div class='def'>3. scrap paper</div><b>πρόχειρο φαγητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρόχειρα φαγητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snack</div><b>πρόχειρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρόχειρη, neuter πρόχειρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slapdash, shoddy (produced or carried out hastily or carelessly)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rough, sketchy (approximate; not finished; roughly or hastily laid out; intended for later refinement)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πρόχειρη</b> εκτίμηση - rough estimate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rough and ready, quick and dirty (crude or unpolished, but still fit for use)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. makeshift</div>
  <div class='def'>5. casual (clothes)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. handy, ready, at hand, to hand (nearby, within reach; offering itself at once; opportune; convenient)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πρόχειρος, from πρό- + χείρ + -ος (-os).</div><b>πρόωρα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://πρόωρος">πρόωρος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prematurely</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ahead of time, early</div><b>πρόωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρόωρη, neuter πρόωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. premature</div>
  <div class='def'>2. early (before the appointed or expected time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρόωρος with semantic loan from French précoce and prématuré.</div><b>πρύμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρύμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar) stern</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πρύμνη with loss of ⟨ν⟩. Doublet of πρύμνη.</div><b>πρύμνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πρύμνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stern</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρύμνη. Doublet of πρύμη.</div><b>πρύτανης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πρυτάνεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rector, dean (holder of office in institution)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) elder statesman (term of respect for elderly and prominent person)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. prytanis (holder of office in ancient Athens)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρύτανις.</div><b>πρώην</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) former, ex, onetime, sometime</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πρώην.</div><b>πρώην Γιουγκοσλαβική Δημοκρατία της Μακεδονίας</b>
  <div><i>name, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) former Yugoslav Republic of Macedonia (former long form of North Macedonia: a country in Southeast Europe, on the Balkan Peninsula; used in diplomatic circles before the 2019 agreement that produced the name North Macedonia due to the naming dispute with Greece over the former name of the country, Macedonia)</div><b>πρώιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρώιμη, neuter πρώιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forward, precocious, early, premature</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek πρώϊμος</div><b>πρώτα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πρώτος">πρώτος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in the old days</div><b>πρώτα ονόματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of πρώτο <a href="bword://όνομα">όνομα</a></div><b>πρώτες βοήθειες</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first aid</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιατρός παρέχει τις <b>πρώτες βοήθειες</b> στον τραυματία. - The doctor is providing first aid to the injured person.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English first aid.</div><b>πρώτο όνομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πρώτα ονόματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first name, forename, Christian name</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πρώτο + όνομα n</div><b>πρώτοι αριθμοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πρώτος">πρώτος</a> <a href="bword://αριθμός">αριθμός</a></div><b>πρώτον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first; firstly; in the first place</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρῶτον, from πρῶτος.</div><b>πρώτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πρώτη, neuter πρώτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(numeral, ordinal) first</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>Πρώτος</b> Παγκόσμιος Πόλεμος - the First World War</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πρῶτος.</div><b>πρώτος αριθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πρώτοι αριθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prime number</div><b>πρώτων βοηθειών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of πρώτες βοήθειες</div><b>πτέραρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πτέραρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-9; air chief marshal in the RAF</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-9; general in the USAF</div><b>πτέρνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πτέρνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φτέρνα">φτέρνα</a></div><b>πτέρυγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πτέρυγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) wing (of a bird)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wing (a part of something that is lesser in size than the main body, such as an extension from the main building)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. wing (a faction of a political movement)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. wing (an organizational grouping in a military aviation service)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πτέρῠγᾰ, accusative of πτέρῠξ, with semantic loan from French aile for other senses.</div><b>πτήση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flight, the act of flying</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πτῆσῐς, from πέτομαι + -σις (-sis).</div><b>πταίσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πταίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. infraction, (minor) offence (least serious crime category, only punishable by fines and/or maximum imprisonment of one month)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η παράνομη στάθμευση είναι <b>πταίσμα</b>. - Illegal parking is an infraction.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) blunder, slip (small mistake)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό που έκανα εγώ ήταν <b>πταίσμα</b> μπροστά σ’ αυτό που έκανε εκείνη. - What I did was a minor slip compared to what she did.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πταῖσμα, from πταίω. Related to φταίω.</div><b>πταίω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to nudge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to crash into</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stumble</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to err</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to have bad luck</div>
  <div class='def'>6. alternative form of <a href="bword://φταίω">φταίω</a>, as in the expression</div>
  <div class='ex'>τις πταίει; (tis ptaíei?, “who&#x27;s to blame?”) (article in 1874 by politician Charilaos Trikoupis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πταίω. Doublet of φταίω.</div><b>πταισματοδίκης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πταισματοδίκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magistrate, justice of the peace (presiding judge that tries minor offences/infractions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>πταισματοδίκης</b> τον έβαλε δέκα μέρες στη φυλακή. - The magistrate put him in jail for ten days.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πταίσμα + -δίκης. First attested 1833.</div><b>πταισματοδικείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πταισματοδικεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>magistrates&#x27; court (court of first instance that tries minor offences/infractions)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έπρεπε να πάω <b>πταισματοδικείο</b> επειδή πέρασα με κόκκινο. - I had to go to the magistrates&#x27; court because I ran a red light.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πταίσμα + -δικείο. First attested 1833.</div><b>πτερύγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πτερύγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fin, flipper (of fish)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) mechanical part resembling a fin</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) blade of a propeller</div>
  <div class='def'>4. auricle (of the ear), pinna</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πτερύγιον, diminutive of πτέρυξ).</div><b>πτηνοτροφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) aviculture</div><b>πτηνό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πτηνά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bird</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πτηνός, from πέτομαι, itself from Proto-Indo-European *péth₂eti (“to fly”) from the root *peth₂- (literally “to spread one’s wings”).
 Beware Slavic false cognates such as Bulgarian пти́ца, Macedonian птица (ptica), Russian пти́ца, Serbo-Croatian пти̏ца/ptȉca, Ukrainian пти́ця, all meaning bird from Proto-Slavic *pъtica. True cognates are Old English feþer (English feather), Latin petō, Russian перо́, Sanskrit पतति, Avestan 𐬞𐬀𐬙𐬀𐬌𐬙𐬌 (pataⁱti), Old Armenian թիռ.</div><b>πτυελοδοχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πτυελοδοχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spittoon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πτύελο + δοχείο</div><b>πτυχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πτυχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fold, bend, crease</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fold</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πτυχή.</div><b>πτυχίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πτυχία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>academic degree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πτύχιον, from Ancient Greek πτυχή.</div><b>πτχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://πτέραρχος">πτέραρχος</a></div><b>πτωματοφάγο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πτωματοφάγα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scavenger, carrion eater</div><b>πτωχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πτωχικόή, neuter πτωχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φτωχικός">φτωχικός</a></div><b>πτωχοκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πτωχοκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poorhouse, almshouse (a charitable institution where poor or homeless people are lodged)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πτωχ(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + -κομείο.</div><b>πτώμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πτώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cadaver, corpse, dead body</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carcass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πτῶμα.</div><b>πτώση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πτώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. case</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το υποκείμενο του ρήματος βρίσκεται πάντα σε ονομαστική <b>πτώση</b>. - The subject of the verb is always in the nominative case.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fall, drop, reduction (in position, quantity, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fall, downfall, overthrow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πτῶσις.</div><b>πτώχευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πτωχεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bankruptcy</div><b>πυγμάχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πυγμάχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>boxer, (formal, dated) pugilist</div><b>πυγμαχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural πυγμαχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) boxing, (formal, dated) pugilism</div><b>πυγολαμπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυγολαμπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firefly, firebug</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πυγολαμπίς</div><b>πυελογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural πυελογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) pyelography</div><b>πυκνοκατοικημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυκνοκατοικημένη, neuter πυκνοκατοικημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>densely populated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πυκν(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + κατοικημένος.</div><b>πυκνωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυκνωτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>capacitor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested around 1870. From πυκνώνω + -της (-tis), calque of French condensateur.</div><b>πυκνός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυκνή, neuter πυκνό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dense (having relatively high density)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dense, compact (crowded together; closely packed)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. dense, thick (difficult to penetrate)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. fine-toothed (comb)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. fine-meshed (sieve)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. heavy (of great force, power, or intensity)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. compact (of prose: brief and pithy; not verbose)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πυκνός.</div><b>πυκνότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυκνότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>density</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πυκνότης, equivalent to πυκνός + -ότητα.</div><b>πυλώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυλώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pillar, pylon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gatehouse, portal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πυλών.</div><b>πυξίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυξίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compass</div>
  <div class='ex'>μαγνητική <b>πυξίδα</b> - magnetic compass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πυξίς, from πυξός.</div><b>πυορροώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suppurate</div><b>πυρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πυρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fire, the firing of weapons</div>
  <div class='ex'>ανοίγω <b>πυρ</b> - open fire</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) hostile attitude</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πυρά</b> κατά της κυβέρνησης - firing against (attacking) the government</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fire</div>
  <div class='ex'>ασφάλεια <b>πυρός</b> - fire insurance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πῦρ.</div><b>πυρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) the stake (a place of execution)</div>
  <div class='ex'>θάνατος επί της <b>πυράς</b> - death at the stake</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) pyre</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>πυρά</b> του βωμού - the pyre of the altar</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (formal, in plural) firing of arms, nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://πυρ">πυρ</a> (pyr)</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα <b>πυρά</b> των όπλων - the firing of arms</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πυρά.</div><b>πυράκανθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυράκανθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firethorn, pyracantha (Pyracantha)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From translingual Pyracantha. By surface analysis, πυρ (pyr, “fire”) + άκανθος.</div><b>πυρήνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυρήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. core, stone, pit (of fruit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nucleus (of an atom)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. nucleus (of cell)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. kernel (of an operating system)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. core (of the Earth)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively) core, kernel (centrally important element or group of elements in a system, collective etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πυρήν.</div><b>πυρίμαχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυρίμαχη, neuter πυρίμαχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire resistant, fireproof</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πυριμάχος with a faulty recessive accent in the pattern of αξιόμαχος. By surface analysis, πυρί- + -μαχος (-machos). See πυρ n (pyr, “fire”) (formal) &amp; μάχη f.</div><b>πυρίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gunpowder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Koine Greek πυρίτης m + -ιδα (-ida, feminine suffix), with feminization influenced by French poudre.</div><b>πυρίτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural πυρίτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) silicon</div><b>πυραγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πυραγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire captain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πυρ- (pyr-) + -αγός, in the pattern of λοχαγός. See πυρ (pyr) and άγω.</div><b>πυραμίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυραμίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pyramid (solid figure)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pyramid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πυραμίς.</div><b>πυραμιδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυραμιδική, neuter πυραμιδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pyramidal, pyramidical, pyramid-shaped</div><b>πυραυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυραυλική, neuter πυραυλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to rockets as missile</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πυραυλικά</b> όπλα - rocket weapons</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πύραυλ(ος), “rocket”) + -ικός.</div><b>πυρετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυρετοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fever, pyrexia</div>
  <div class='ex'>κίτρινος <b>πυρετός</b> - yellow fever</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) frenzy, fever</div>
  <div class='ex'>απεργιακός <b>πυρετός</b> - strike fever</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πυρετός.</div><b>πυρεῖον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πυρεῖα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) a match, a matchstick (a small stick tipped with a substance susceptible to ignition by friction)</div>
  <div class='ex'>In Strandard Modern Greek: σπίρτο n (spírto)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πῠρεῖον, only for Katharevousa.</div><b>πυρηνική χημεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nuclear chemistry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πυρηνικός + χημεία.</div><b>πυρηνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυρηνική, neuter πυρηνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nuclear</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα επηρεάσει επίσης και ολόκληρες πόλεις, όπου ο <b>πυρηνικός</b> σταθµός είναι γενικά ο κύριος εργοδότης. - It would also affect whole towns where the nuclear power plant is generally the main employer.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πυρήνας + -ικός. Calque of French nucléaire and English nuclear.</div><b>πυρηνικός αντιδραστήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυρηνικοί αντιδραστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nuclear reactor</div><b>πυρκαγιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυρκαγιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conflagration, fire (large and destructive - of building etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πυρκαϊά.</div><b>πυροβασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire walking</div><b>πυροβολικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) artillery, ordnance</div><b>πυροβολισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυροβολισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shot, gunshot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) gunfire</div><b>πυροβολώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to shoot, fire</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιόρταζαν την επιτυχία τους πίνοντας και <b>πυροβολώντας</b>. - They celebrated their success by drinking and shooting.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to fire at, shoot at</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πυροβολῶ. By surface analysis, πυρο- (pyro-, “pyro-”) + -βολώ for more on the latter component).</div><b>πυροβόλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gun, cannon, artillery</div><b>πυροβόλο όπλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πυροβόλα όπλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firearm</div><b>πυροδοτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to fire, pull the trigger</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to detonate, to set off, to touch off (to cause to explode)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to initiate, to set off, to touch off, to trigger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πυρο- (pyro-) + -δοτώ.</div><b>πυροκυτταρίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pyrocellulose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, πυρο- (pyro-, “pyro-, fire”) + κυτταρίνη.</div><b>πυροκότσυφας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυροκότσυφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Monticola saxatilis: the common rock thrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πυρο- (pyro-, “fire”) + κότσυφας</div><b>πυρομανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυρομανής, neuter πυρομανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pyromaniacal</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural πυρομανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pyromaniac</div><b>πυρομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pyromania</div><b>πυρομαχικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(collective, noun) ammunition</div><b>πυροσβέστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυροσβέστες, feminine πυροσβέστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fireman, firefighter (male)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πυρο- (pyro-, “pyro-, fire”) + stem σβεσ- as in ancient σβέννυμι [see modern σβήνω] + -της (-tis). Attested since 1855.</div><b>πυροσβέστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυροσβέστριες, masculine πυροσβέστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firefighter (female)</div><b>πυροσβεστήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πυροσβεστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire extinguisher</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πυρο- (pyro-) + Koine Greek σβεστήρ + -ας (-as), a calque of English fire extinguisher.</div><b>πυροσβεστική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυροσβεστικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire brigade (UK), fire department (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάρε αμέσως τηλέφωνο στο 199, την <b>πυροσβεστική</b>! - Phone immediately 199 the [Hellenic] Fire Brigade!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ellipsis of πυροσβεστική υπηρεσία f.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πυροσβεστικός">πυροσβεστικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>πυροσβεστική υπηρεσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυροσβεστικές υπηρεσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire service literally: &quot;fire extinguishing service&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>see tautonym πυροσβεστικό σώμα (pyrosvestikó sóma, “literally: firefighting body”) (official)</div><b>πυροσβεστικό σώμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πυροσβεστικά σώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire service, fire brigade, literally: &quot;fire extinguishing corps&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>«<b>Πυροσβεστικό Σώμα</b> Ελλάδος» είναι ο επίσημος τίτλος της πυροσβεστικής υπηρεσίας στην Ελλάδα. - &quot;Hellenic Fire Service&quot; [literally: Fire extinguishing Corps of Greece] is the official title of the fire service in Greece.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> πυροσβεστικό, neuter of adjective πυροσβεστικός &amp; the neuter noun σώμα (&quot;body&#x27;, in sense &quot;corps, organisation&quot;.</div><b>πυροσβεστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυροσβεστική, neuter πυροσβεστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire extinguishing, firefighting (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from πυροσβέστ(ης)) + -ικός.</div><b>πυροτέχνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πυροτεχνήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firework, pyrotechnic (device using gunpowder and other elements which explodes with light and sound when lit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το Πάσχα στην Ελλάδα, συνηθίζεται να εκτοξεύουν <b>πυροτεχνήματα</b> τα μεσάνυχτα. - On Easter in Greece, it is customary to set off fireworks at midnight.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πυρο- (pyro-, “fire”) + τέχνημα, a calque of English firework. First attested 1840.</div><b>πυροφάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πυροφάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fishing light attractor (fishing aid which uses lights attached to structure above water or suspended underwater to attract fish)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έφυγε με το <b>πυροφάνι</b> κι ούτε πια πίσω δεν εφάνει - He left with the fishing light and never more appeared again.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) The method of fishing that uses such a fish attractor.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ψαράς πήγε για <b>πυροφάνι</b>. - The fisherman went fishing with an attractor.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) A type of hazing where lit paper is inserted between the toes of a sleeping soldier.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From πῦρ + φᾱνῐ́ον, derived from φαίνω.</div><b>πυρπολικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πυρπολικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fireship</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πυρπολικός">πυρπολικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πυρπολικός">πυρπολικός</a></div><b>πυρπολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυρπολική, neuter πυρπολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>incendiary</div><b>πυρόλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυρολύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pyrolysis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from New Latin pyrolysis. By surface analysis, πυρό- + λύση”), see the ancient λύσις.</div><b>πυρόπληκτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine πυρόπληκτη, neuter πυρόπληκτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firestricken (victimized by a fire)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, πυρο- (pyro-, “fire”) + -πληκτος (-pliktos, “-stricken, -struck”).</div><b>πυρότουβλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firebrick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Translation of English firebrick</div><b>πυτιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πυτιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rennet</div><b>πυώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πυώδης, neuter πυώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>purulent</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον είδα σα στοιχειό ξερασμένο με αηδία απ’ τα έγκατα της γης, εγωκεντρικό και ματαιόδοξο, μ’ ένα κομμάτι σίδερο στη θέση της καρδιάς· με <b>πυώδη</b> λύμφη στις αρτηρίες, αντι για αίμα·[...]</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πυώδης</div><b>πχ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of π.χ. (p.ch.)</div><b>πχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://πλωτάρχης">πλωτάρχης</a></div><b>πχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://πλοίαρχος">πλοίαρχος</a></div><b>πωλήσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πωλήσιμη, neuter πωλήσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saleable</div><b>πωλήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πωλήτριες, masculine πωλητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seller, vendor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. saleswoman, shopworker, shop assistant, shopgirl</div><b>πωλητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πωλητές, feminine πωλήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seller, vendor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. salesman, shopworker, shop assistant</div>
  <div class='def'>3. salesman, representative, rep</div><b>πωλούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to be being sold</div><b>πωλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, literary) to sell, alternative form of <a href="bword://πουλάω">πουλάω</a> and <a href="bword://πουλώ">πουλώ</a>, especially used in advertisements</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πωλείται</b> διαμέρισμα. - Flat for sale</div><b>πωπώ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as alternative form of πο-πό</div><b>πως</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>that (introducing a clause)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νόμιζα <b>πως</b> είχες φύγει ήδη. - I thought (that) you&#x27;d already left.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πῶς.</div><b>πως είσαι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πώς">πώς</a> είσαι</div><b>πως είστε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πώς">πώς</a> είστε</div><b>πως σε λένε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) what is your name?, what&#x27;s your name?, what are you called?</div><b>πό-πο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of πο-πό</div><b>πόα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πόες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>herb, herbacious plant (plant whose stem isn&#x27;t woody and which dies every year)</div><b>πόδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leg (portion of the lower human appendage from the thigh to the ankle)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έσπασε το <b>πόδι</b> του παίζοντας ποδόσφαιρο. - He broke his leg playing football.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. foot</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του έκοψαν το <b>πόδι</b> επειδή είχε μολυνθεί το δάχτυλο του ποδιού. - They cut his foot off because the toe had become infected.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) leg, foot (of a piece of furniture)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το τραπέζι έχει μόνο τρία <b>πόδια</b>. - This table only has three legs.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) finger (of land)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Χαλκιδική έχει τρία <b>πόδια</b>. - Chalkidiki has three fingers.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. foot (unit of measure equal to twelve inches or one third of a yard)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αεροσκάφος πετάει στα τριάντα χιλιάδες <b>πόδια</b>. - The aircraft is flying at thirty thousand feet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek πόδιν, from Ancient Greek πόδιον, diminutive of πούς, ποδός, from Proto-Indo-European *póds, *pṓds.</div><b>πόδι του αθλητή</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) athlete&#x27;s foot</div><b>πόθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. desire, yearning (the feeling of desiring; an eager longing for something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. desire, lust (strong sexual attraction)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: λαγνεία f (lagneía)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pothos</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πόθος.</div><b>πόκερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) poker</div><b>πόλεμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πόλεμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>war</div>
  <div class='ex'>εμφύλιος <b>πόλεμος</b> - civil war</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πόλεμος.</div><b>πόλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πόλεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. town; city</div>
  <div class='def'>2. townspeople</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek πόλις, from Proto-Hellenic *ptólis, from Proto-Indo-European *tpólHis, from *tpelH-.</div><b>πόλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πόλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pole (extreme of an axis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βόρειος Πόλος (North Pole)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. post (pole in a battery)</div><b>πόλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πολώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. polarisation (UK), polarization (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. polarity</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) polarisation (UK), polarization (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πολιτική πόλωση - political polarisation</div><b>πόμολο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πόμολα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>doorknob, knob</div><b>πόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pony (small horse)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English pony.</div><b>πόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πόνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pain, ache</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πόνος.</div><b>πόνος στο αυτί</b>
  <div><i>phrase, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>earache (literally: ache in the ear)</div><b>πόντος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πόντοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. point (the unit of scoring in a game or competition)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. centimeter</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ladder (length of unravelled fabric in a knitted garment, especially in nylon stockings)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. stitch</div>
  <div class='def'>5. open sea, large sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan pont (“point, end”).</div><b>πόπο</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of πο-πό</div><b>πόρδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πόρδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πορδή">πορδή</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πέρδομαι.</div><b>πόρνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. harlot; prostitute; whore</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a term of abuse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πόρνη.</div><b>πόρνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πόρνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rent boy, gigolo (male prostitute)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. whoremaster (man who uses the services of prostitutes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πόρνος.</div><b>πόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pore, duct</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ιδρώτας έτρεχε από κάθε <b>πόρο</b> του δέρματος μας. - Sweat ran from every pore of our skin.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) resources, (financial) means</div>
  <div class='ex'>φυσικοί <b>πόροι</b> - natural resources</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πόρος.</div><b>πόρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πόρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>door (to house or room)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from late Koine Greek πόρτα, itself borrowed from Latin porta (“gate, entrance”).</div><b>πόση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drinking (the act by which drink is consumed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πόσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://πόσος">πόσος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>πόσθη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) foreskin, prepuce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πόσθη.</div><b>πόσο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>how much, how many</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://πόσος">πόσος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://πόσος">πόσος</a></div><b>πόσο κάνει</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. How much?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. How much is it?</div><b>πόσο κοστίζει</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>how much does it cost?</div><b>πόσο μάλλον</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>let alone, to say nothing of</div><b>πόσο χρονών είσαι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) how old are you?</div><b>πόσο χρονών είσαστε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) how old are you?</div><b>πόσο χρονών είστε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) how old are you?</div><b>πόσος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine (feminine πόση, neuter πόσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. how much? (questions of quantity and extent)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πόσο</b> κάνει; - How much?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) how many? (questions of quantity)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πόσοι</b> θέλετε καφέ; - How many want coffee?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πόσος, from Proto-Hellenic *kʷotsos, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷóti, adverb from *kʷos.</div><b>πόσουμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://οπόσουμ">οπόσουμ</a></div><b>πότε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>when</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πότε</b> πάτε στην Αγγλία; - When are you going to England?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πότε, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷokʷe, from *kʷos + *-kʷe, equivalent to πο- (po-) + τε (te).</div><b>πότε ο Γιάννης δεν μπορεί, πότε ο κώλος του πονεί</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, vulgar) Of lazy people who feign illness whenever it&#x27;s convenient. Lazy people always have an excuse.</div><b>πότε πότε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>occasionally, now and again, now and then, sometimes, every so often</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “when when”.</div><b>πότε-πότε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πότε">πότε</a> <a href="bword://πότε">πότε</a></div><b>πότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πότες, feminine πότρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. drinker (usually to excess)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. toper, sot, drunkard</div><b>πότρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πότες, masculine πότης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. drinker (usually to excess)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. toper, sot, drunkard</div><b>πύθωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πύθωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>python</div><b>πύλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πύλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gate, portal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. portal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πύλη.</div><b>πύον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pus (substance composed of dead white blood cells and dead bacteria found in bacterial infections)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η πληγή του μάζευε <b>πύον</b> και έπρεπε να την τρυπήσει ο γιατρός. - His wound was collecting pus and the doctor had to puncture it.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πύον, from Ancient Greek πύθω / πύθομαι, from *puH-.
Cognate with Sanskrit पूयति, Latin pus, Gothic 𐍆𐌿𐌻𐍃 (fuls, “foul”), Old English fūl (“foul”) and Latin puteō.</div><b>πύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, uncountable) heat (radiation from fire)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) flush (redness of the face)</div><b>πύραυλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πύραυλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. projectile, rocket, missile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cone, cornet</div>
  <div class='ex'>παγωτό πύραυλος (ice cream cone)</div><b>πύργος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πύργοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tower</div>
  <div class='ex'>κεντρικός <b>πύργος</b> - central tower, castle keep</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rook</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πύργος.</div><b>πύρινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine πύρινη, neuter πύρινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fiery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek πύρινος. By surface analysis, πυρ (pyr) + -ινος (-inos).</div><b>πώγων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πώγωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) see <a href="bword://πώγωνας">πώγωνας</a> in Modern Greek</div><b>πώγωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural πώγωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) beard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, formal) chin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek πώγων. Also see πιγούνι, inherited from a diminutive derivative.</div><b>πώληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural πωλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sale, sales</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>πώληση</b> όπλων - arms sales</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πώλησις.</div><b>πώμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural πώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. screwcap, cap</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stopper, bung</div><b>πώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(interrogative) how (in what way or manner; in what state; to what extent)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Πώς</b> είστε; - How are you?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πῶς.</div><b>πώς είπατε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>What did you say? (formal, plural, V-form) ^†</div><b>πώς είπες</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>What did you say? (informal, singular, T-form) ^†</div><b>πώς είσαι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) How are you? (singular^†)</div><b>πώς είστε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) How are you? (plural^†)</div><b>πώς σας λένε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>what is your name?, what&#x27;s your name?, what are you called?</div><b>πώς σε λένε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) what is your name?, what&#x27;s your name?, what are you called?</div><b>ρ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ρήμα">ρήμα</a>: verb</div><b>ρ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ρ΄">Ρ΄</a></div><b>ράβδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ράβδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rod, stick, baton</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ingot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥάβδος.</div><b>ράβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be sewn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be a customer of a tailor</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πού <b>ράβεσαι</b>; - Where do you have your clothes made?</div><b>ράβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sew</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ραύγω, ράβγω is the mediaeval form. From the ancient ῥάπτω from the perfective stem ραψ- in the pattern of -ψ- &gt; -β- as in κλέπτω, κλεψ- &gt; κλέβω.</div><b>ράγισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραγίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fissure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fissuring</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ραγίζω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>ράγκμπυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) rugby, rugby football</div><b>ράδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) roadstead (open waters outside a harbour, suitable as an anchorage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian rada (“roadstead”), from French rade (“harbour”), from Middle English rade, from Old English rad (“journey”).</div><b>ράδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ράδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. radio, wireless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) radium, the element symbol Ra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French radio.</div><b>ράμφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ράμφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beak, bill (of a bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ῥάμφος.</div><b>ράπισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραπίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slap (to the face)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ῥάπισμα.</div><b>ράπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, no past) to simplified spelling of the ancient ῥάπτω, in Standard Modern Greek: <a href="bword://ράβω">ράβω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥάπτω.</div><b>ράσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ράσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>habit (clothing worn by a monk, friar, nun, etc)</div><b>ράτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ράτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. race, a breed or strain of domesticated animal</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ιδιοσυγκρασία του Γκόλντεν Ριτρίβερ είναι χαρακτηριστικό γνώρισμα της <b>ράτσας</b>. - The Golden Retriever&#x27;s temperament is characteristic of the breed.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) race (of human races)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian razza (“race”).</div><b>ράφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ράφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shelf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish راف (raf), from Arabic رَفّ (raff).</div><b>ράφτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ράφτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tailor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ῥάπτης; by surface analysis, ράπτω + -ης (-is, “nominal suffix”).</div><b>ράχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ράχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. back, spine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. back, rear</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ridge, crest</div>
  <div class='def'>4. spine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥάχις.</div><b>ρέβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρέβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turnip</div><b>ρέγγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρέγγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>herring (fish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan renga.</div><b>ρέγκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρέγκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρέγγα">ρέγγα</a></div><b>ρέματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ρέμα</div><b>ρέματος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of ρέμα</div><b>ρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to tend, lean</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥέπω.</div><b>ρέστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>change (money)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πάντα ζητούσα τα <b>ρέστα</b> - always ask for the change</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Italian resto</div><b>ρέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to flow, run (of liquids)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥέω.</div><b>ρήγας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρηγάδες, feminine ρήγισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. king</div>
  <div class='def'>2. king (in a pack of playing cards)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ῥήξ, from Latin rēx (“king”).
The second meaning is a semantic loan from Italian re.</div><b>ρήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>verb</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ῥῆμα.</div><b>ρήνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rhenium</div><b>ρήον το βαρβαρικόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhubarb</div><b>ρήση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saying, statement (significant phrase uttered by someone)</div><b>ρήτορας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρήτορες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. orator, rhetorician (skilled and eloquent public speaker)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. speaker (maker of a speech to an audience)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥήτωρ.</div><b>ρίγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρίγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. line, stripe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) straightedge, rule, ruler</div><b>ρίγανη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρίγανες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oregano (herb and plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἀρίγανη from Ancient Greek ὀρίγανος f.</div><b>ρίγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρίγη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) chill, chills, shiver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῥῖγος.</div><b>ρίζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρίζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. root (of a plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. root</div>
  <div class='def'>3. root</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥίζα.
Cognates include Mariupol Greek ри́за and Tsakonian σχίντα.</div><b>ρίζωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ριζώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhizome</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῥίζωμα.</div><b>ρίπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) simplified spelling of the ancient ῥίπτω; in Standard Modern Greek: <a href="bword://ρίχνω">ρίχνω</a></div><b>ρίσκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρίσκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>risk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Old Italian risco.</div><b>ρίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) sea urchin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably from Sicilian rizza.</div><b>ρίχνει καντάρια</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rain cats and dogs (to rain very heavily)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally: &quot;to pour kantars [of rain]&quot;</div><b>ρίχνει καρεκλοπόδαρα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rain cats and dogs (to rain very heavily)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally: &quot;to pour chairlegs&quot;</div><b>ρίχνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to drop (anchor)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ρίχνω</b> άγκυρα - to drop anchor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to drop, lower (price)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to shed (a load)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to throw</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to knock over</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to shoot down</div>
  <div class='def'>7. to sprinkle, strew</div>
  <div class='def'>8. to make a pass (at)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ῥίχνω, from a dissimilation of an unattested *ρίφνω, from a metaplasm of *ρίφτω, from Ancient Greek ῥίπτω.</div><b>ρίχνω φως</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://ρίχνω">ρίχνω</a>, <a href="bword://φως">φως</a> (fos).</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ρίξε</b> λίγο <b>φως</b> για να δω που πάω! - Throw some light so I can see where I&#x27;m going!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic, transitive) to shed light on, shed light on (to illuminate or to make clear)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η έρευνα <b>έριξε φως</b> σε πολλές από τις λεπτομέρειες της εξαφάνισης. - The investigation shed a lot of light on the details of the disappearance.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English shed light on.</div><b>ρίψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρίψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. drop (an act or instance of dropping)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. throw (the act of throwing something)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. throw (the flight of a thrown object)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῥῖψις, with -ψη (-psi) ending.</div><b>ραβάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amble (gait in which the horse used both legs on one side, and then the other)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish راهوان (rahvan).</div><b>ραβέντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραβέντια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhubarb</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish راوند (ravent), from Classical Persian ریوند.</div><b>ραβίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ραβίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ραββίνος">ραββίνος</a></div><b>ραββί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Judaism, indeclinable) rabbi (minister of religion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ῥαββί, from Hebrew רבי (rabbi, “my master”).</div><b>ραββίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ραββίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Judaism) rabbi (minister of religion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian rabbino.</div><b>ραβδί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραβδιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rod, stick, pointer, cane</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wand (of magic, of fairy tales)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ῥαβδίν, inherited from Ancient Greek ῥαβδίον, a diminutive of ῥάβδος.</div><b>ραβδίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραβδία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://ραβδί">ραβδί</a>: small rod</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rod cell (part of the retina)</div><b>ραγάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ραγάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stretch mark</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ῥαγάς.</div><b>ραγιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ραγιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rayah</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, derogatory) vassal, subordinate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish رعایا (re&#x27;aya), from Arabic رَعَايَا, plural of رَعِيَّة (raʕiyya, “herd, flock”).</div><b>ραγισματιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ραγισματιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crack, fissure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the ραγισματ- stem of ράγισμα + -ιά.</div><b>ραδίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραδίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>collective name for vegetables of the genus Cichorium especially chicory (a group of edible, often bitter, leaves)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ραδίκι, variant of ραδίκιον, from Italian radicchio. The form ραδίκι may have been borrowed from the Italian plural radicchi, taken in Greek as a singular neuter. Doublet of ραντίτσιο, a more recent borrowing.</div><b>ραδερφόρντιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rutherfordium</div><b>ραδιενέργεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) radioactivity</div><b>ραδιενεργά απόβλητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radioactive waste</div><b>ραδιενεργά νέφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ραδιενεργό <a href="bword://νέφος">νέφος</a></div><b>ραδιενεργό νέφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραδιενεργά νέφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nuclear fallout</div><b>ραδιενεργός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ραδιενεργός or ραδιενεργή, neuter ραδιενεργό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radioactive</div><b>ραδιοσυχνότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ραδιοσυχνότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radio frequency</div><b>ραδιοτηλεοπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ραδιοτηλεοπτική, neuter ραδιοτηλεοπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radio and television, broadcasting</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ραδιοτηλεοπτικός</b> φορέας - (radio and television) broadcaster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ραδιο(φωνικός)) + τηλεοπτικός.</div><b>ραδιοφωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ραδιοφωνική, neuter ραδιοφωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) radio (attributive)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ραδιοφωνικός</b> σταθμός - radio station</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French radiophonique. By surface analysis, ραδιοφων(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>ραδιόφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραδιόφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radio</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English radiophone.</div><b>ραδόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) radon</div><b>ραθυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) indolence, idleness</div><b>ρακή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρακές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>raki (spirit sometimes flavoured with aniseed or other herbs)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish راقی. Compare Mariupol Greek рахи́.</div><b>ρακί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρακή">ρακή</a></div><b>ρακοσυλλέκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine ρακοσυλλέκτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ragpicker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested around 1829. Calque of English ragpicker: ρακο- (rako-) + συλλέκτης</div><b>ρακούν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) raccoon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English raccoon.</div><b>ραμολιμέντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραμολιμέντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) senile dementia (deterioration in mind and body often accompanying old age)</div>
  <div class='ex'>παθαίνω <b>ραμολιμέντο</b> - to go senile, to lose one&#x27;s marbles</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, derogatory) senile person</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ασ’ τον να μιλάει, είναι <b>ραμολιμέντο</b> πια και δεν ξέρει τί λέει. - Ignore what he&#x27;s saying, he&#x27;s senile now and doesn&#x27;t know what&#x27;s saying anymore.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian rammollimento (“softening”).</div><b>ρανίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρανίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drop (of liquid)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μέχρι τελευταίας <b>ρανίδος</b> του αίματος - to the last drop of blood (until death)</div><b>ρανίς</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://ρανίδα">ρανίδα</a> (drop)</div><b>ραντάρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) radar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English radar.</div><b>ραντίτσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραντίτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radicchio, the “Italian chicory” (form of chicory)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neologism. Italian radicchio, with Italian /k/ ⟨ch⟩ mistakenly read as /tʃ/ and hence adapted into Greek as /ts/ (cf. κοντσέρτο &lt; concerto). The much rarer variant ραντίκιο reflects the Italian pronunciation. Doublet of ραδίκι.</div><b>ραντεβού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) appointment, rendezvous</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχε <b>ραντεβού</b> στο γιατρό. - He/She had an appointment with the doctor.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) date (romantic meeting or outing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω ένα <b>ραντεβού</b> απόψε. - I have a date tonight.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French rendez-vous.</div><b>ραντό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot, indeclinable) platform in the sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French radeau.</div><b>ραπάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραπάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Koine Greek ῥαπάνιον</div><b>ραπανάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραπανάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ραπάνι + -άκι.</div><b>ραπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ραπτική, neuter ραπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) sewing (attributive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ῥαπτικός. By surface analysis, ράπτ(ω)) + -ικός.</div><b>ρατσίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρατσίστριες, masculine ρατσιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>racist</div><b>ρατσισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρατσισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>racism</div><b>ρατσιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρατσιστές, feminine ρατσίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>racist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ράτσα + -ιστής, calque of Italian razzista.</div><b>ρατσιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρατσιστική, neuter ρατσιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>racist</div><b>ραφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ραφεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tailor shop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ραφεῖον.</div><b>ραχοκοκαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ραχοκοκαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spine, backbone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ράχη + κόκαλο.</div><b>ρε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, informal) hey, eh, dude (informal address to a friend or acquaintance, usually male)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ρε</b> Γιάννη, τι γίνεται εκεί πέρα; Ακούω φωνές. - Hey Gianni, what&#x27;s going on over there? I can hear shouting.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, informal, offensive) you, blud (informal address to strangers and unfamiliar people to deliberately offend)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε από δω, <b>ρε</b> αλήτη. - Get out of here, you bum.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, informal) jeez, gosh, yikes, my (expressing surprise or bewilderment)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αχ <b>ρε</b> τι πάθαμε. - Oh jeez, look what happened to us.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὠρέ, from Ancient Greek μωρέ, the vocative form of μωρός. Doublet of βρε (vre) and μωρέ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) re</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) D (second note or supertonic in the C major scale)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ρε</b> ματζό<b>ρε</b> - D major</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian re.</div><b>ρεβέντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεβέντια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ραβέντι">ραβέντι</a></div><b>ρεβίθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεβίθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chickpea (food/seed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a Byzantine Greek *ρεβίθιν, from Ancient Greek ἐρεβίνθιον, from a diminutive of Ancient Greek ἐρέβινθος.</div><b>ρεβιθάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρεβιθάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baked chickpeas, chickpea bake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ρεβίθι f.</div><b>ρεβιθιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρεβιθιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chickpea plant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from ρεβίθι n</div><b>ρεβιθοκεφτές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρεβιθοκεφτέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρεβυθοκεφτές">ρεβυθοκεφτές</a></div><b>ρεβιθοσαλάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρεβιθοσαλάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chickpea dip, chickpea paste, revithosalata</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ρεβίθι + σαλάτα</div><b>ρεβυθάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρεβυθάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρεβιθάδα">ρεβιθάδα</a></div><b>ρεβυθοκεφτές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρεβυθοκεφτέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chickpea ball</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ρεβίθι + κεφτές</div><b>ρεβυθοσλάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρεβυθοσαλάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρεβιθοσαλάτα">ρεβιθοσαλάτα</a></div><b>ρεβύθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεβύθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρεβίθι">ρεβίθι</a></div><b>ρεζέρβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρεζέρβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. reserve, spare (all senses)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spare tyre, spare wheel</div>
  <div class='def'>3. emergency fuel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian riserva.</div><b>ρεζίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεζίλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laughing stock, object of ridicule.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έγινα <b>ρεζίλι</b> μπροστά σε όλους. - I was a laughing stock in front of everyone.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish رذیل (rezil), from Arabic رَذِيل.</div><b>ρεζιλεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be humiliated, made to look ridiculous</div><b>ρεζιλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to humiliate, make the laughing stock, make to look ridiculous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ρεζίλι (from Ottoman Turkish رذیل (rezil)) + -εύω.</div><b>ρεκόρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) record (a supreme achievement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το νέο Πανελλήνιο ρεκόρ. - the new Panhellenic record</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French record.</div><b>ρελές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρελέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relay</div><b>ρεμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of ρέμα</div><b>ρεμπέτικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεμπέτικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rebetiko (type of Greek urban folk music)</div><b>ρεντίκολο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεντίκολα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. laughing stock</div>
  <div class='def'>2. butt (of a joke)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian ridicolo.</div><b>ρεντγκένιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) roentgenium</div><b>ρεπάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεπάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ραπάνι">ραπάνι</a></div><b>ρεπερτόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεπερτόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>repertoire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian repertorio.</div><b>ρεπεσάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) repechage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French repêchage.</div><b>ρεπορτάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) reportage, reporting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French reportage.</div><b>ρεπό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable) day off, rest day, one day holiday</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>ρεπό</b> κάθε δεύτερη Tρίτη. - I have a day off every second Tuesday.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French repos (“rest”).</div><b>ρεπόρτερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) reporter, journalist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English reporter.</div><b>ρεσεψιονίστ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) receptionist (especially in a hotel)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French réceptionniste.</div><b>ρεσεψιόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) reception, reception desk (especially in a hotel)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French réception.</div><b>ρεσιτάλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) recital</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French récital.</div><b>ρετάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρετάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. remnant, the remaining fabric at the end of the bolt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) an unimportant or weakly person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian ritaglio (“remnant fabric”) or the dialectal form thereof retaglio.</div><b>ρετινάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>retinal</div><b>ρετινόλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>retinol</div><b>ρετιρέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) penthouse, top floor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French retiré.</div><b>ρετροϊός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρετροϊοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>retrovirus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ρετρο- (retro-, “retro-”) + ιός, calque of English retrovirus.</div><b>ρετσίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>retsina, a type of resinated wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ῥετσίνα, from Italian resina (“resin”). Doublet of ρετσίνι and ρητίνη.</div><b>ρετσινάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρετσινάτη, neuter ρετσινάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unresinated, non-resinated</div><b>ρευματοδότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρευματοδότες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socket, electrical socket, wall socket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ρεύμα + δότης.</div><b>ρευστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρευστή, neuter ρευστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>liquid, fluid</div><b>ρευστότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fluidity (the state of being fluid rather than viscous)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fluidity (a measure of the extent to which something is fluid)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. liquidity (the state of being liquid)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) fluidity (the quality of being fluid, free-flowing or in flux: of a situation, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. liquidity (the property of being easily convertible to cash)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. liquidity (availability of cash in the short term)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ρευστ(ός)) + -ότητα.</div><b>ρεφρέν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) refrain, chorus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) repetitious (something repeated verbally to the point of tedium)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French refrain (“refrain, chorus”).</div><b>ρεφραίν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρεφρέν">ρεφρέν</a></div><b>ρεύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. air current</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ρεύμα</b> θερμού αέρος - a warm air current</div>
  <div class='def'>2. current, stream, torrent</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι επικίνδυνο να κολυμπάς εδώ: έχει πολύ ισχυρά ρεύματα.<b></b> - It is dangerous to swim here: there are extremely strong currents.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) electric current</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) a trend, tendency to agree or support an idea, movement</div>
  <div class='ex'>καλλιτεχνικό <b>ρεύμα</b> - art movement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ῥεῦμα, from ῥέω, from Proto-Indo-European *srew- (“to flow”). Compare to ρέμα n.</div><b>ρεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to burp, belch</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη <b>ρεύεσαι</b> έτσι δυνατά! - Don&#x27;t burp so loudly!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἐρεύγομαι through the aorist ἐρεύχθην to the Byzantine Greek ἐρεύτην and present tense with -ομαι.</div><b>ρητίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρητίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. resin (a viscous hydrocarbon secretion of many plants, particularly coniferous trees)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. resin (any of various yellowish viscous liquids or soft solids of plant origin; used in lacquers, varnishes and many other applications)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. resin (any synthetic compound of similar properties)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῥητίνη. Doublet of ρετσίνι and ρετσίνα.</div><b>ρητορική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhetoric (the art of using language, especially public speaking, as a means to persuade)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῥητορική.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://ρητορικός">ρητορικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>ρητορικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρητορική, neuter ρητορικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhetorical, rhetoric, oratorical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ῥητορῐκός</div><b>ρητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dictum, maxim, saying (short phrase expressing a basic truth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ῥητόν, from Ancient Greek ῥητός, with semantic loan from French dicton.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of ρητός</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of ρητός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>ρηχός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρηχή or ρηχιά, neuter ρηχό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shallow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adjectival formation from ρηχαίνω, a verbal formation derived from Ancient Greek ῥηχίη, the Ionic variant of ῥᾱχῐ́ᾱ. The sense development would be &quot;to become like a tide&quot; &gt; &quot;to become shallow&quot;.</div><b>ριγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) rink</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) boxing ring</div><b>ριγωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ριγωτή, neuter ριγωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>striped, striated, lined, ruled (e.g., paper)</div><b>ριγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shiver, tremble</div><b>ριζά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foot (of a mountain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ρίζα.</div><b>ριζικάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ριζικάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small object used in the &#x27;klidonas&#x27; folk custom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ριζικό + -άρι.</div><b>ριζικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ριζική, neuter ριζικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (relational) root (attributive), radical (of or pertaining to a root)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. radical (thoroughgoing; far-reaching)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ῥιζικός with semantic loan from French radical. By surface analysis, ρίζ(α)) + -ικός.</div><b>ριζοβολώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. To take root, to enroot (to establish roots)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. To take root (to become established, to take hold)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ῥιζοβολέω / ῥιζοβολῶ, from ῥίζα + βάλλω.</div><b>ριζοβούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ριζοβούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foot (of a mountain)</div><b>ριζοσπάστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ριζοσπάστες, feminine ριζοσπάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radical</div><b>ριζοσπάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ριζοσπάστες, masculine ριζοσπάστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radical</div><b>ριζοσπαστικοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) radicalization</div><b>ριζοσπαστικοποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be radicalised (UK), be radicalized (US)</div><b>ριζοσπαστικοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to make radical, radicalise (UK), radicalize (US)</div><b>ριζοσπαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ριζοσπαστική, neuter ριζοσπαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>radical</div><b>ριζότο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) risotto</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian risotto, from riso (“rice”).</div><b>ριζώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to take root</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See ρίζα.</div><b>ριμπάουντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) rebound</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English rebound.</div><b>ρινική κοιλότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nasal cavity</div><b>ρινολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhinology</div><b>ρινόκερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρινόκεροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rhinoceros</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥινόκερως, from ῥίς + κέρας.</div><b>ρινόκερως</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρινόκερος">ρινόκερος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥινόκερως, from ῥίς and κέρας.</div><b>ρινόπλαξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) helmeted hornbill (Rhinoplax vigil)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Rhinoplax.</div><b>ρισκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to risk, to put at risk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ρίσκ(ο)) + -άρω.</div><b>ριψοκινδυνεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to risk, to take a chance</div><b>ρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) rho, the 17th letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥῶ.</div><b>ροή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flow</div>
  <div class='ex'>διάγραμμα <b>ροής</b> - flow chart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flow, flux</div>
  <div class='ex'>μαγνητική <b>ροή</b> - magnetic flux</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) flux, change</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥοή. See there for further etymology and cognates.</div><b>ροβίθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροβίθια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρεβίθι">ρεβίθι</a></div><b>ροδάκινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροδάκινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. peach (fruit)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ξεφλουδίζω ένα <b>ροδάκινο</b>. - I peel a peach.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) soft skin. In expression</div>
  <div class='ex'>μάγουλο <b>ροδάκινο</b> - cheek (like) peach</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * Inherited from Byzantine Greek ῥοδάκινον from Late Koine Greek or medieval δωράκινον (with metathesis of [d] [r] and vowel change [u&gt;o]) or δωρακινὸν μῆλον. From Latin duracinum.</div><b>ροδάκινον</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Cypriot dialect form of <a href="bword://ροδάκινο">ροδάκινο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ῥοδάκινον, from Ancient Greek δωράκινον.</div><b>ροδακινί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) peach (color/colour)</div><b>ροδακινιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ροδακινιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peach tree</div><b>ροδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ροδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pomegranate tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ρόδι + -ιά.</div><b>ροδοδάφνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ροδοδάφνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oleander</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥοδοδάφνη. Equivalent to ροδο- (rodo-, “rhodo-, rose coloured”) + δάφνη.</div><b>ροδοπελεκάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ροδοπελεκάνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great white pelican (Pelecanus onocrotalus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ρόδο + πελεκάνος.</div><b>ροδόκηπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ροδόκηποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rose garden</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ρόδο + κήπος</div><b>ροδόνερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροδόνερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rose water, rosewater</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ρόδο + νερό</div><b>ροδόσταγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροδοστάγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rosewater</div><b>ροδόσταμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροδόσταμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rosewater</div><b>ροδώδη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Rosales, an order which includes many flowering plants, like the typic roses.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ρόδο + -ώδη n pl, a calque of translingual Rosales.</div><b>ροδώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ροδώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rose garden</div><b>ροζ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pink</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French rose, from Latin rosa, most probably from Ancient Greek ῥόδον, making the word a doublet of ρόδο.</div><b>ροζέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) rosé</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ροζέ</b> κρασί - rosé wine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French rosé.</div><b>ροκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) rock</div><b>ροκ εντ ρολ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) rock and roll</div><b>ροκάνισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροκανίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A gnawing, a nibbling. (process or result of gnawing or nibbling)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ροκανίζω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ροκφόρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Roquefort</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French roquefort.</div><b>ρολά αφρού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://ρολό">ρολό</a> αφρού</div><b>ρολό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρολά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. roll (paper, fabric, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. roller (paint, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hair roller</div>
  <div class='def'>4. rolled food (meat, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (as plural) roller blind, roller shutter, roller door</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French rouleau (“roll, roller”).</div><b>ρολό αφρού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρολά αφρού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foam roller</div><b>ρολόι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clock, timepiece (instrument that measures or keeps track of time)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ρολόι</b> σταμάτησε να δουλεύει και χρειάζεται επισκευή. - The clock stopped working and needs repair.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. watch, wristwatch (portable or wearable timepiece)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το λουρί του <b>ρολογιού</b> μου έχει σπάσει. - The strap of my watch is broken.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. clock tower (tower with clock with several faces and/or bells)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ρολόι</b> στο Λονδίνο ονομάζεται «Μπιγκ Μπεν». - The clock tower in London is called &#x27;Big Ben&#x27;.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) meter (device that measures things)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το <b>ρολόι</b> του ηλεκτρικού - the electricity meter</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (rare) passion flower (genus Passiflora)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὡρολόγιον, with aphesis.</div><b>ρομάντζο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρομάντζα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>romance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian romanzo.</div><b>ρομαντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρομαντική, neuter ρομαντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. romantic (connected with love and romance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μια <b>ρομαντική</b> συνάντηση - a tryst, a romantic encounter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Romantic (connected with that period of art, literature, music)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>ρομαντική</b> εποχή - the Romantic period</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> English romantic</div><b>ρομπαμπέκια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot, indeclinable) junk dealer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian roba vecchia.</div><b>ρομποτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) robotics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English robotics.</div><b>ρομπότ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) robot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Czech robot.</div><b>ροπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inclination</div>
  <div class='def'>2. torque</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) trend, tendency</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥοπή, from Ancient Greek ῥέπω.</div><b>ροσόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροσόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rose-flavoured liqueur (an alcoholic liqueur flavoured with extract of rose)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τ’ αγόρι μου είναι πλάσμα αξιοζήλευτο
το βλέμμα του, το γέλιο του ροσόλι. - My boy is an enviable creature,
His gaze, his laugh are rose liqueur.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian rosolio.</div><b>ρουαντέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρουαντέζικη, neuter ρουαντέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Rwandan (of or pertaining to Rwanda or its people)</div><b>ρουβίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rubidium</div><b>ρουθήνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ruthenium</div><b>ρουθούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρουθούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nostril (either of the two orifices located on the nose)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ῥωθώνιον, from a diminutive form of Ancient Greek ῥώθων.</div><b>ρουκέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρουκέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rocket (projectile)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) projectile vomit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan rocheta.</div><b>ρουμάνικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://ρουμανικά">ρουμανικά</a></div><b>ρουμάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρουμάνικη, neuter ρουμάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρουμανικός">ρουμανικός</a></div><b>ρουμανικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Romanian language.</div><b>ρουμανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρουμανική, neuter ρουμανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Romanian (relating to Romania or its people or language)</div><b>ρουμπίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρουμπίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ruby</div><b>ρουσφέτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρουσφέτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>favour (UK), favor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish رشوت, from Arabic رُشْوَة, رِشْوَة.</div><b>ρουτίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρουτίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>routine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French routine, with -α (-a) ending.</div><b>ρουφάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to swallow, gulp, slurp, drain, sniff, guzzle (obnoxious sounds made when swallowing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sip</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to suck, breathe in</div>
  <div class='ex'>(of blood) exploit</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to soak up, absorb (of knowledge, learning)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (idiomatic, transitive) to suck (it) up</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (vulgar) to blow, to suck off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ρουφ-ώ + -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek ρουφῶ, from Ancient Greek ῥοφῶ, contracted form of ῥοφέω.</div><b>ρουφηγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ρουφηγμένη, neuter ρουφηγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sipped and see <a href="bword://ρουφάω">ρουφάω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. hollow (of lips, cheeks)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ρουφηγμένα</b> χείλη, <b>ρουφηγμένα</b> μάγουλα - hollow lips, hollow cheeks</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ρουφιέμαι, passive voice of ρουφάω, ρουφώ (“I sip”).</div><b>ρουφιάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρουφιάνοι, feminine ρουφιάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pander, procurer, pimp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian ruffiano, roffia (“dirt”), from hrŭf (“eschar, crust”). Cognate with Icelandic hrufla (“to scratch”), English ruffle.</div><b>ρουφιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be sipped</div><b>ρουφούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) nostril, naris</div><b>ρουφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρουφάω">ρουφάω</a></div><b>ροφός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ροφοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grouper (fish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὀρφώς.</div><b>ροχάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροχάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phlegm, spittle</div><b>ροχάλισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροχαλίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snoring (act of someone breathing loudly in sleep)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ροχάλισμα</b> συνδέεται με την παχυσαρκία. - Snoring is associated with obesity.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ροχαλίζω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>ροχαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to snore (breathe during sleep with harsh snorting noises)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορούσα να κοιμηθώ χθες το βράδυ, έτσι που <b>ροχάλιζε</b>. - I couldn&#x27;t sleep last night, the way he was snoring.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ῥοχαλίζω, from Koine Greek ῥογχαλίζω, from ῥέγχω.</div><b>ροχαλητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ροχαλητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. snoring (act of someone breathing loudly in sleep)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>ροχαλητό</b> συνδέεται με την παχυσαρκία. - Snoring is associated with obesity.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. snore (an instance of snoring)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>ροχαλητά</b> του ήταν πολύ δυνατά. - His snores were very loud.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ροχαλίζω, following the ending of κυνηγητό as a model.</div><b>ρούβλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρούβλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rouble, ruble</div><b>ρούγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρούγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, dialectal) street, alley</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, dialectal) small village square</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin ruga.</div><b>ρούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρούμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rum</div><b>ρούχο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρούχα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. garment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) clothes, clothing, dress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ροῦχον, borrowed from a Slavic language; see Proto-Slavic *ruxo (“garments, clothing”) (whence Serbo-Croatian ruho, Polish rucho) for more. Despite superficially similar phonetics and semantics, not related to Late Latin roccus (“ecclesiastical vestment”) (the latter whence English rochet), which instead derives from Proto-Germanic *hrukkaz (“tunic, robe”).</div><b>ρυάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρυάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stream, brook, rivulet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥύαξ.</div><b>ρυθμίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be adjusted, be sorted, be fixed, be sorted out</div><b>ρυθμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to adjust, set, modify, tune, regulate (to change something, usually so as to correct it)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να <b>ρυθμίσω</b> το ρολόι μου. - I have to adjust my watch.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) to organise, arrange, settle, sort out, fix (to correct something)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ρυθμίζω</b> τη ζωή μου - to sort out one&#x27;s life</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥυθμίζω, from Ancient Greek ῥυθμός.</div><b>ρυθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρυθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rhythm (music, dance, poetry, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rate, pace</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) style</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥυθμός.</div><b>ρυμουλκώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to tow, drag</div><b>ρυτίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρυτίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wrinkle, line, furrow (on face, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥυτίς</div><b>ρυτιδόσπορο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρυτιδόσπορα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Any plant of the genus Rhytidosporum.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Rhytidosporum, by surface analysis, ρυτίδα + σπόρος.</div><b>ρω</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρο">ρο</a> (ro).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥῶ.</div><b>ρωγμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρωγμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crack (thin space opened in a previously solid material)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) gap (discontinuity, e.g. in a narrative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῥωγμή.</div><b>ρωμαίικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρωμαίικη or ρωμαίικια, neuter ρωμαίικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greek, of the Greek people</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ῥωμαίικος”), from Koine Greek Ῥωμαῖος. Compare ρωμαϊκός.</div><b>ρωμαϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρωμαϊκή, neuter ρωμαϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Roman (of or from Rome)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ρωμαϊκό</b> Δίκαιο - Roman Law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Ῥωμαϊκός.</div><b>ρωσικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the Russian language</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ρωσικός">ρωσικός</a></div><b>ρωσικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρωσική, neuter ρωσικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Russian (relating to Russia or its people or language)</div><b>ρωτάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to question</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>ρώτησα</b> για την δουλειά του. - I questioned him about his job.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to ask (about)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σε <b>ρωτώ</b> μία ερώτηση. - I am asking you a question.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to enquire (UK), inquire (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ρωτώ + -άω. From Byzantine Greek (ἐ)ρωτῶ ((e)rōtô, “ask”) + -άω, from Ancient Greek ἐρωτῶ contracted form of ἐρωτάω.</div><b>ρωτημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ρωτημένη, neuter ρωτημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>asked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ρωτιέμαι, passive voice of ρωτάω, ρωτώ (“ask”).</div><b>ρωτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρωτάω">ρωτάω</a></div><b>ρόγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρόγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rare) payment to farmer (especially for keeping animals for grazing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. misspelling of <a href="bword://ρώγα">ρώγα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ῥόγα from verb ῥογεύω from Latin ērōgō.</div><b>ρόδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pomegranate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ρόιδι, with assimilation of the όι &gt; ό, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ῥοΐδιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek ῥοιά, variant of ῥόα.</div><b>ρόδινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ρόδινη, neuter ρόδινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) pink</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rosy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ruddy</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (informal) optimistic</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (literary) roseate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῥόδινος.</div><b>ρόδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rhodium</div><b>ρόδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρόδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rose (flower)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥόδον.</div><b>ρόζος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρόζοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. callus (hard patch of skin)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. knot (deformity in wood)</div><b>ρόιδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρόιδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρόδι">ρόδι</a></div><b>ρόιδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρόιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρόδι">ρόδι</a></div><b>ρόκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρόκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rocket, arugula (a salad leaf)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) distaff (device for holding fibres to be spun)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek ῥόκα, from Italian ruca</div><b>ρόλεϊ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable) hair roller</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English roller.</div><b>ρόλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρόλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. role, part</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) role</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French rôle.</div><b>ρόμβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρόμβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rhombus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diamond (colloquially)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. point (one of 32 divisions on a compass)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῥόμβος.</div><b>ρόμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρόμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dressing gown</div>
  <div class='def'>2. robe, gown</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) fool, idiot</div>
  <div class='ex'>γίνομαι ρόμπα - to be made to look a fool</div><b>ρόμπολο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Bosnian pine (Pinus heldreichii)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Scots pine (Pinus sylvestris)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian rrobull.</div><b>ρόπαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρόπαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bat, club, truncheon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥόπαλον.</div><b>ρόχαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρόχαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ροχάλα">ροχάλα</a></div><b>ρύγχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρύγχη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snout, the nose of an animal, muzzle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ῥύγχος.</div><b>ρύζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρύζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὀρύζιον.</div><b>ρύθμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρυθμίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. regulation (the act of regulating or the condition of being regulated)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. arrangement, disposition</div>
  <div class='def'>3. setting (a level or placement that a knob or control is set to)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ρύθμισις. By surface analysis, ρυθμίζω + -ση (-si).</div><b>ρύπανση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρυπάνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pollution, contamination (vitiation of the environment by harmful substances)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ρύπανση</b> της ατμόσφαιρας - air pollution</div>
  <div class='def'>2. soiling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ῥύπανσις.</div><b>ρύπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρύποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. filth, dirt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contamination, impurity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ῥύπος.</div><b>ρώγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ρώγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. berry (of a grape) (small round fruit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nipple</div>
  <div class='def'>3. finger tip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῥώξ, accusative: ῥῶγα, a variant of ῥάξ.</div><b>ρώθων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ρώθωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρουθούνι">ρουθούνι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ῥώθων. Doublet of the inherited ρουθούνι.</div><b>ρώσικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ρωσικά">ρωσικά</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of ρώσικος</div><b>ρώτημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ρωτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>question, query</div><b>σ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contraction of <a href="bword://σε">σε</a> (se, “to, at”)</div><b>σ&#x27; αγαπώ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contraction of <a href="bword://σε">σε</a> <a href="bword://αγαπώ">αγαπώ</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable, poetic) saying &quot;I love you&quot;, the declaration or expression of one&#x27;s romantic love.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένας κόσμος τραγούδια, / ένα δάσος <b>“σ’ αγαπώ”</b> / μες στα μάτια σου παίζουνε κρυφτό. - A world of songs, / a forest of &quot;I love you&quot;&#x27;s / play hide-and-seek in your eyes.</div><b>σ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://σελίδα">σελίδα</a> (page)</div><b>σ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Σ΄">Σ΄</a></div><b>σάβανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σάβανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shroud (cloth for wrapping the dead)</div><b>σάκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σάκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bag (of paper or plastic; for shopping, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sack (of paper or plastic; for storing/packing loose materials)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bin bag, waste bag</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pouch, wallet (for documents)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σάκκος, most likely from Semitic.</div><b>σάκχαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σάκχαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sugar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diabetes</div>
  <div class='def'>3. blood sugar concentration</div><b>σάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shawl</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian scialle, from French châle, from Hindi शाल, from Classical Persian شال.</div><b>σάλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) saliva, spit</div><b>σάλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strong movement of the sea</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) unrest</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παραιτήθηκε ο πρωθυπουργός. Έγινε <b>σάλος</b>. - The prime minister resigned. There was an uproar.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σάλος.</div><b>σάλπιγγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σάλπιγγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bugle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. oviduct, Fallopian tube, salpinx</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σάλπιγξ.</div><b>σάλπιγξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σάλπιγγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Katharevousa, archaic, formal) war-trumpet, trump</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Katharevousa, archaic, formal) trumpet-call</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σάλπιγξ.</div><b>σάλτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σάλτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jump, leap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian salto.</div><b>σάλτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σάλτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sauce, salsa</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian salsa.</div><b>σάμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) samba</div><b>σάντουιτς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sandwich</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English sandwich.</div><b>σάντουϊτς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σάντουιτς">σάντουιτς</a></div><b>σάουνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σάουνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sauna</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Finnish sauna.</div><b>σάπιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σάπια, neuter σάπιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rotten, decayed, putrid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. morally corrupted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σάπιος, from σαπίζω, from σήπω.</div><b>σάπων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σάπωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σαπούνι">σαπούνι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek σᾱ́πων.</div><b>σάρκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σάρκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flesh</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pulp, flesh</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σάρκα, from the accusative singular σάρκα of Ancient Greek σάρξ.</div><b>σάτιρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σάτιρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>satire</div><b>σάχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σάχηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shah</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο Σάχης Περσίας - The Shah of Persia</div><b>σέβας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σέβη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. respect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) a respectful greeting</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) <a href="bword://τα">τα</a> σέβη <a href="bword://μου">μου</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σέβας. For the expression &#x27;τα σέβη μου&#x27;, semantic loan from French respects.</div><b>σέβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to respect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *tyegʷ-der.
Proto-Indo-European *tyégʷ-e-tor
Proto-Hellenic *tségʷomai
Ancient Greek σέβομαι
Greek σέβομαι
Inherited from Ancient Greek σέβομαι, from Proto-Hellenic *tségʷomai, from Proto-Indo-European *tyégʷ-e-tor, from *tyegʷ-.</div><b>σέγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σέγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jigsaw</div><b>σέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. saddle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. saddle (for bicycle, etc)</div><b>σέλερι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable, rare) celery</div><b>σέλινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σέλινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>celery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σέλινον.</div><b>σέλφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) selfie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English selfie.</div><b>σένα</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εσένα">εσένα</a></div><b>σέντερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) centre (player position in centre of field)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English centre.</div><b>σέντερ μπακ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) centre-back (player who plays in the centre of defence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English centre-back.</div><b>σέντερ φορ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) centre forward (most central of the strikers)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English centre-forward.</div><b>σέντρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kick-off</div>
  <div class='def'>2. center spot</div>
  <div class='def'>3. chip shot (when the ball is kicked from underneath with accuracy)</div><b>σέξι</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sexy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή είναι πολύ <b>σέξι</b>. - She is very sexy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English sexy.</div><b>σέπαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σέπαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sepal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from New Latin sepalum.</div><b>σέρβικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σερβικά">σερβικά</a></div><b>σέρβικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σέρβικη, neuter σέρβικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σερβικός">σερβικός</a></div><b>σέρνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to drag oneself, crawl</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι φαντάροι <b>σέρνονται</b> χάμω για να μην εντοπηθούν. - The soldiers crawl in order not to be located.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dodder, totter, stagger (walk unsteadily, eg due to old age)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γερος <b>σερνόταν</b> από την πόρτα στην αυλή. - The old man was staggering out the door into the garden.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of diseases) to be going around, be widespread</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτές τις μέρες, <b>σέρνεται</b> μια επικίνδυνη γρίπη. - These days, a dangerous flu has been going around.</div><b>σέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to pull, drag, haul, pull along</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα παιδιά <b>σέρνουν</b> το καρότσι με τον αδερφό τους. - The children pull the wagon with their brother.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, imperative, intransitive, regional) to go</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σύρε</b> να φέρεις νερό. - Go and get some water.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σέρνω / σύρνω, from Ancient Greek σύρω.</div><b>σέρνω το χορό</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, idiomatic) to lead a dance line (especially in traditional Greek circle dances)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to be a ringleader</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “drag the dance”.</div><b>σέσκουλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σέσκουλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Swiss chard, chard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σεῦκλον or σευκλογούλιν, from Ancient Greek τεῦτλον, Ionic σεῦτλον.</div><b>σέσουλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σέσουλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>food scoop, flour scoop</div>
  <div class='ex'>με τη <b>σέσουλα</b> - by the bucketload, hand over fist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian sessola.</div><b>σήκωσε κι η μύγα κώλο να χέσει τον κόσμο όλο</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) Said of those who have risen above their station, often in an arrogant manner.</div><b>σήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trademark, logo</div>
  <div class='def'>2. insignia</div>
  <div class='def'>3. signature tune</div>
  <div class='def'>4. signal (analogue or digital)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. signal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῆμα.</div><b>σήμα κατατεθέν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σήματα κατατεθέντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) registered trademark</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) hallmark, trademark (distinguishing characteristic)</div><b>σήματα κατατεθέντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://σήμα">σήμα</a> κατατεθέν</div><b>σήμερα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>today</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σήμερ(ον)) + -α (-a), with change of ending by analogy with τώρα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) the present, today</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σήμερ(ον)) + -α (-a), with change of ending by analogy with τώρα.</div><b>σήραγγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tunnel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῆραγξ.</div><b>σήριαλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σίριαλ">σίριαλ</a></div><b>σήτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σήτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sieve (a tool with a fine mesh used to sift flour, sand, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. window screen (a mesh set in a frame used to keep foreign debris and insects out of a room without blocking fresh airflow)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nominal formation probably cognate with, though apparently not derived from, Ancient Greek σήθω of σῆστρον) under folk-etymological influence of the ancient σητάνιος / σητάνειος, a form surviving in some modern Greek dialects, as the South Italiot Greek.
Τhe theory taking the word as borrowed from Slavic) is considered improbable.</div><b>σήψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sepsis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. decay, decomposition</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) corruption</div><b>σίβερτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sievert (the derived SI unit of equivalent dose)</div><b>σίγα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second-person singular simple past imperative of σιγάω: &quot;keep quiet&quot;</div><b>σίγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sigma, the 18th letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῖγμᾰ.</div><b>σίγμα τελικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Final sigma, ς, the lowercase form of the letter used in the terminal position.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σίγμα + τελικό.</div><b>σίγουρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>certainly, inescapably, inevitably, unavoidably</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σίγουρος">σίγουρος</a></div><b>σίγουρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σίγουρη, neuter σίγουρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>secure, certain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan seguro, siguro.</div><b>σίδερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σίδερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. iron (the metal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. iron, smoothing iron, flat iron</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>σίδερο</b> είναι μια οικιακή συσκευή. - The iron is a domestic appliance.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σίδερον, from Ancient Greek σίδηρος. Doublet of σίδηρος.</div><b>σίδερο σιδερώματος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flat iron, iron</div><b>σίδηρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural σίδηροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) iron (chemical element)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal, uncountable, usually) iron: Εποχή <a href="bword://του">του</a> Σιδήρου f</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σίδηρος, whence also the vernacular forms Byzantine Greek σίδερον n and σίδερο (an etymological doublet). See σίδηρος for the ultimately disputed etymology.</div><b>σίελος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, uncountable) saliva (mouth secretion)</div><b>σίκαλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rye</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σίκαλη, from Latin sēcale (“rye”).</div><b>σίριαλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) serial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English serial.</div><b>σίριαλ κίλερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) serial killer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English serial killer.</div><b>σίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σήτα">σήτα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See σήτα.</div><b>σίφουνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σίφουνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tornado</div>
  <div class='def'>2. U-bend</div><b>σίφωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σίφωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σίφουνας">σίφουνας</a></div><b>σα δε ντρέπεσαι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shame on you (literally: as if you aren&#x27;t ashamed)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σα δε ντρέπεσαι</b> να βρίζεις την γριούλα! - Shame on you for cursing at a little old lady!</div><b>σαΐνι</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hawk</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially) Levant sparrowhawk, Accipiter brevipes</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) bright spark, genius</div>
  <div class='ex'>see: έξυπνος (éxypnos, “intelligent”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish شاهین, with deletion of [h] from Classical Persian شاهین.</div><b>σαΐτα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. arrow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. paper aeroplane, paper airplane</div>
  <div class='def'>3. shuttle (of a loom)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. Dahl&#x27;s whip snake (Platyceps najadum)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (archaic) slingshot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σαγίτα, σαγίττα, σαΐτα, σαΐττα, from Koine Greek σαγίττα, from Latin sagitta.</div><b>σαβάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαβάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>savanna, savannah</div><b>σαβαρέν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) baba (bun, usual soaked in rum often with cream)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Named after the French writer Brillat-Savarin</div><b>σαββατοκύριακο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαββατοκύριακα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spelling with lowercase of <a href="bword://Σαββατοκύριακο">Σαββατοκύριακο</a></div><b>σαβούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) ballast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) dregs, trash</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σαβούρα, from Late Latin saburra. Compare Italian zavorra.</div><b>σαγάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαγάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>double-handled frying pan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish صحن, from Arabic صَحْن. Compare Greek τηγάνι and Ancient Greek τήγανον or τάγηνον.</div><b>σαγανάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαγανάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saganaki (small double-handled frying pan or a fried cheese dish cooked in it)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Diminutive form of σαγάνι, from Ottoman Turkish صحن, from Arabic صَحْن. Compare Greek τηγάνι and Ancient Greek τήγανον or τάγηνον.</div><b>σαγηνευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σαγηνευτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seducer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σᾰγηνευτής, i.e. having the alluring power of a fish net.</div><b>σαγηνευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαγηνευτική, neuter σαγηνευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beguiling, tempting, seductive, alluring, glamorous, magnetic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the noun σαγηνευτής + -ικός.</div><b>σαγηνεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) to captivate, fascinate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μουσική αυτή <b>σαγηνεύει</b> τον ακροατή. - This music captivates the listener.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σᾰγηνεύω, with figurative semantic shift &quot;to catch fish with a net&quot; &gt; &quot;to catch people&#x27;s attention&quot;.</div><b>σαγιονάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαγιονάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flip-flop, thong, jandal (sandal, usually of rubber, secured to the foot by two straps mounted between the big toe and its neighbour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην παραλία φοράω <b>σαγιονάρες</b>. - I wear flip-flops at the beach.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English sayonara, from Japanese さようなら or its shorter, less traditional form さよなら (sayonara). Use for footwear comes from first seeing this footwear in the 1957 film Sayonara. Cognate with Spanish sayonara (“flip-flop, thong, jandal”).</div><b>σαγόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαγόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jaw, chin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably from Katharevousa σαγόνιον, from Ancient Greek σιαγόνιον, diminutive of σιαγών.</div><b>σαδισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) sadism</div><b>σαδιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαδιστική, neuter σαδιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sadistic</div><b>σακάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σακάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jacket</div><b>σακάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σακάτηδες, feminine σακάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) cripple (person with impaired physical abilities because of deformation, injury, or amputation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο νάνος, ο σακάτης και η μητέρα της τρέλας. - The dwarf, the cripple and the mother of madness.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish سقط, from Arabic سَقَط.</div><b>σακί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σακιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σακίν, σακκίν, from Ancient Greek σᾰκκῐ́ον, diminutive of σᾰ́κκος.</div><b>σακίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σακίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. haversack</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rucksack, backpack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Diminutive of σάκος.</div><b>σακαράκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σακαράκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>old car, jalopy, banger, bucket, wreck</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian carcassa (“hulk, carcass”).</div><b>σακκίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σακκίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σακίδιο">σακίδιο</a></div><b>σακούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σακούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bag (made of plastic or paper)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σάκος + -ούλα.</div><b>σακούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σακούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small sack (especially made of cloth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σακούλιον, from Ancient Greek σάκκος. By surface analysis, σάκ(ος)) + -ούλι.</div><b>σαλάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>salami</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian salami.</div><b>σαλάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαλάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. salad (a dish, usually served cold, prepared usually from vegetables)</div>
  <div class='ex'>αγγούρια και ντομάτες <b>σαλάτα</b> - cucumber and tomato salad</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) (untidy) mess</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan salata, ultimately derived from Latin sal (“salt”).</div><b>σαλίγκαρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σαλίγκαροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σαλιγκάρι">σαλιγκάρι</a> (snail, especially a large one)</div><b>σαλαμούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brine (a saltwater solution for food preservation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Old Italian or Venetan salamora, with [o] → [u] change influenced by preceding labial stop [m]. Compare Late Latin and Medieval Latin salimuria and descendants.</div><b>σαλβάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαλβάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) shalwar, harem pants (loose trousers traditionally worn by both men and women)</div>
  <div class='ex'>γυναίκες όλων των φυλών και των εθνικοτήτων / με νυκτικά, ποκάμισα, <b>σαλβάρια</b> και πασούμια - women of all races and nationalities / with nightgowns, shirts, shalwars and slippers</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish شالوار, from Classical Persian شلوار. Ultimately a doublet of Ancient Greek σαράβαρα.</div><b>σαλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to move slightly, move up and down</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive) to go crazy</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive, uncommon) to cause to rock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek σαλεύω. See σάλος.</div><b>σαλιγκάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαλιγκάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snail</div><b>σαλοτραπεζαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαλοτραπεζαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>living room, lounge diner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σαλόνι + τραπεζαρία.</div><b>σαλούβαρδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σαλούβαρδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forkbeard (Phycis phycis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Kαλυτερα να μην τους βαραμε γιατι συνηθως &quot;κρατανε&quot; ψαρια κοντα τους(π.χ. το καλοκαιρι σε μια τρυπα με εναν <b>σαλουβαρδο</b> που ειχα σταμπαρει πηρα δυο καλους σαργους και αφησα και για αλλες φορες).</div><b>σαλπάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to set sail, weigh anchor, depart by sea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian salpare (“to weigh anchor”).</div><b>σαλπιγγίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) salpingitis, inflammation of the oviducts</div><b>σαλπιγγογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαλπιγγογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>salpingography, a salpingogram, x-ray of the oviducts</div><b>σαλτάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to jump</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) to go crazy, go mad, flip</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian saltare.</div><b>σαλτικίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Salticidae, the salticids or jumping spiders.</div>
  <div class='ex'>3. το θηλ. ως ουσ. ζωολ. η ηλιοφανής
 αραχνίδιο τής οικογένειας <b>σαλτικίδες</b>. - 3. fem. as a noun, zool. or ηλιοφανής
 arachnid of the family Salticidae.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From translingual Salticidae, suffixed with -ίδες.</div><b>σαλόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαλόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lounge, living room, reception room</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian salone.</div><b>σαμάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σαμάνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shaman</div><b>σαμάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>packsaddle, bat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σαμάριν, from earlier σαγμάριον, a diminutive, via Koine Greek, of Ancient Greek σάγμα.</div><b>σαμάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) samarium</div><b>σαμιαμίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gecko</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σαμαμίθιον, a diminutive of σαμιάμινθος.</div><b>σαμιαμίθι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σαμιαμίδι">σαμιαμίδι</a></div><b>σαμούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) sable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish سمور (samur), from Classical Persian سمور.</div><b>σαμπάνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαμπάνιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>champagne</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French champagne.</div><b>σαμπί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαμπιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sampi (obsolete Greek alphabetical character - Ϡ ϡ)</div><b>σαμπανιζέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sparkling, champagne-like</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French champanisé.</div><b>σαμποδρόμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαμποδρόμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) sambadrome (exhibition place for samba schools in Brazil)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σάμπα + -δρόμιο, based on Portuguese sambódromo.</div><b>σαμποτάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sabotage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French sabotage.</div><b>σαμποτέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) saboteur</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French saboteur.</div><b>σαμπουάν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) shampoo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French shampooing.</div><b>σαμπούκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαμπούκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>elder, elderberry (tree)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian sambuco (“elder”).</div><b>σαμπρέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σαμπρέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bladder (liner used contain air, eg within a football)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inner tube (used contain air within tyre)</div><b>σαμπρέλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σαμπρέλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σαμπρέλα">σαμπρέλα</a></div><b>σαν</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. like, as, as if</div>
  <div class='ex'>να ζήσει <b>σαν</b> βασιλιάς - to live like a king</div>
  <div class='def'>2. because, as, when</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταμάτησε να μιλάει <b>σαν</b> να κατάλαβε το λάθος που έκανε. - He stopped talking when he realized the mistake he had made.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Ancient Greek ὡσάν, merged form of ὡς + ἄν.</div><b>σαν δεν ντρέπεσαι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>see: σα <a href="bword://δε">δε</a> ντρέπεσαι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “as if you aren&#x27;t ashamed”.</div><b>σαν τα κρύα τα νερά</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) full of vigor and exceptional beauty (used mostly for young women)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στα νιάτα της ήταν <b>σαν τα κρύα τα νερά</b>. - In her youth, she was exceptionally pretty.</div><b>σαν τα χιόνια</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Said to somebody who appears after a long absence: Long time no see!, Well look who it is!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “like the snows”.</div><b>σαν ταύρος σε υαλοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>like a bull in a china shop</div><b>σαν την άδικη κατάρα</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) like a headless chicken, like a blue-arsed fly (frantically, hurriedly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλο τρέχει από μέρος σε μέρος <b>σαν την άδικη κατάρα</b>. - He&#x27;s always running around from place to place like a blue-arsed fly.</div><b>σαν την κότα στον μύλο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic, indeclinable) readily available, at one&#x27;s fingertips, on tap (literally: like a hen in the mill)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το χρυσάφι στην Αφρική ήταν μια φορά <b>σαν την κότα στον μύλο</b>. - Gold was readily available in Africa at one time.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Due to the fact that grain (for the chicken to eat) is available in abundance in mills.</div><b>σανίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σανίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plank; board (piece of wood)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bean pole (a thin person)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (capitalized) Sanida, a Cypriot village</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σανίς.</div><b>σανατόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σανατόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sanatorium</div><b>σανδάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σανδάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) sandal</div>
  <div class='ex'>less formal, SMG: σαντάλι (santáli)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σανδάλιον”).</div><b>σανδαράχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σανδαράχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. realgar, sandarac, red sulfide of arsenic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. orpiment, yellow sulfide of arsenic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σανδαράχη, variant of σανδαράκη, traditionally considered an oriental loanword from an unknown source.</div><b>σανταλόξυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σανταλόξυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sandalwood</div><b>σαντιγί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) whipping cream (thick cream that has had air incorporated into it by rapid beating)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τρώει μηλόπιτα με <b>σαντιγί</b>. - She&#x27;s eating apple pie with whipped cream.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French chantilly, from French crème chantilly (“Chantilly cream, whipped cream”).</div><b>σαντιγύ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) non-simplified spelling of <a href="bword://σαντιγί">σαντιγί</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Οlder, disused spelling which attempts to match the vowel ⟨y⟩ in the original French chantilly. See more at σαντιγί</div><b>σαντούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαντούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>santur, folk stringed musical instrument, a type of hammered dulcimer played with small mallet-like hammers</div>
  <div class='ex'>—Ζορμπά [...] Θα τρώμε και θα πίνουμε μαζί. Κι ύστερα θα παίζεις σαντούρι.
—Αν έχω κέφι, ακούς; Αν έχω κέφι. [...] το σαντούρι είναι άλλο πράμα. Είναι θεριό, θέλει λευτεριά. Αν έχω κέφι, θα παίζω.
—Zormpá [...] Tha tróme kai tha pínoume mazí. Ki ýstera tha paízeis santoúri.
—An écho kéfi, akoús? An écho kéfi. [...] to santoúri eínai állo práma. Eínai therió, thélei lefteriá. An écho kéfi, tha paízo. - —Zorba … We&#x27;ll eat and drink together, and then, you&#x27;ll play the santouri.
—If I&#x27;m in the mood, do you hear? If I&#x27;m in the mood! … the santouri is a wild beast and it needs freedom. I&#x27;ll play if I feel like it.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish سانطور (santur), from Classical Persian سنتور.</div><b>σανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or neuter plural (plural σανά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From dialectal Bulgarian сяно (sjano), from Proto-Slavic *sěno.</div><b>σαξόφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαξόφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>saxophone</div><b>σαπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to rot (to suffer decomposition due to biological action, especially by fungi or bacteria)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to rot (to make putrid; to cause to putrefy)</div><b>σαπιοκοιλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαπιοκοιλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (derogatory, offensive) A large and flabby belly.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κατέβασε το φανελάκι, φαίνεται η <b>σαπιοκοίλια</b> σου. - Pull down your shirt, your fat belly is showing.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, derogatory, offensive) A fat or obese person with such a belly; fatso, fat-ass, fatty.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μπορούσα να κινηθώ στο λεωφορείο σήμερα με τις <b>σαπιοκοιλιές</b>. - I couldn&#x27;t move on the bus today with all the fatties.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σάπιος + κοιλιά</div><b>σαπουνάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαπουνάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suds, soapsuds, lather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σαπούν(ι), “soap”) + -άδα</div><b>σαπουνίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be washed with soap</div><b>σαπουνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to soap, wash with soap</div><b>σαπουνόπερα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαπουνόπερες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soap opera</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σαπούνι + όπερα, a calque of English soap opera.</div><b>σαπούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαπούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σαπούνιν, inherited from Koine Greek σᾱπώνιον, a diminutive of σᾱ́πων, borrowed from Latin sāpō (“soap”).</div><b>σαπωνοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) saponification</div><b>σαράντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(cardinal, numeral) forty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σαράντα, from Ancient Greek τεσσαράκοντα, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷetwr̥̄ḱomt, from earlier *kʷetwr̥-dḱomt (“four-ten”). The first three letters were mistaken for the article τὲς in Medieval times, hence the loss. The memorial service is derived from the belief that Jesus Christ was resurrected for that period of time before ascending to Heaven.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Orthodox memorial service in the fortieth day</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σαράντα, from Ancient Greek τεσσαράκοντα, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷetwr̥̄ḱomt, from earlier *kʷetwr̥-dḱomt (“four-ten”). The first three letters were mistaken for the article τὲς in Medieval times, hence the loss. The memorial service is derived from the belief that Jesus Christ was resurrected for that period of time before ascending to Heaven.</div><b>σαραντίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to become forty days old (babies)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to reach the fortieth day after childbirth (mothers)</div><b>σαρανταποδαρούσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαρανταποδαρούσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. centipede</div>
  <div class='def'>2. millipede</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σαράντα + ποδάρι + -ούσα</div><b>σαρδάμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) nonexistent words of phrases resulting from transposition of syllables; spoonerism ; metathesis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Coined by Αχιλλέας Μαδράς (spelling his name backwards), an actor known for transposing syllables.</div><b>σαρδέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαρδέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sardine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian sardella.</div><b>σαρδέλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαρδέλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://σαρδέλα">σαρδέλα</a></div><b>σαρδόνια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sardonically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://σαρδόνιος">σαρδόνιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σαρδόνιος">σαρδόνιος</a></div><b>σαρδόνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαρδόνια, neuter σαρδόνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sardonic, scornfully mocking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σαρδόνιος, alternative form of σαρδάνιος.</div><b>σαρκασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σαρκασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sarcasm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σαρκασμός.</div><b>σαρκαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαρκαστική, neuter σαρκαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sarcastic, taunting, mordant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French sarcastique, which was formed from sarcasme.</div><b>σαρκικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαρκική, neuter σαρκικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carnal, fleshly (especially sensual)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σαρκικός. By surface analysis, σάρκα + -ικός.</div><b>σαρκοβόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαρκοβόρα, neuter σαρκοβόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carnivorous, meat-eating</div><b>σαρκοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαρκοφάγος, neuter σαρκοφάγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carnivorous, meat-eating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σαρκοφάγος. By surface analysis, σάρκα + -φάγος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or masculine (plural σαρκοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sarcophagus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carnivore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σαρκοφάγος. By surface analysis, σάρκα + -φάγος.</div><b>σαρξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σάρκα">σάρκα</a>, flesh, meat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σάρκα">σάρκα</a>, matter (things not spiritual)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (Katharevousa) The seat of animalistic/immoral desires and thoughts, such as lust</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σάρξ.</div><b>σαρωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σαρωτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scanner</div><b>σαρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sweep (with broom)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to scan</div><b>σας</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal) your (2nd person plural, genitive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal) you (2nd person plural, accusative)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (possessive) your (belonging to you)</div><b>σαστίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be bewildered, surprised, confused</div><b>σατέν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) satin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French satin, Borrowed from Arabic زَيْتُون</div><b>σατανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σατανισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Satanism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cunning person, devil</div><b>σατιρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to satirize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French satiriser. By surface analysis, σάτιρ(α)) + -ίζω.</div><b>σαφής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαφής, neuter σαφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clear, explicit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σᾰφής.</div><b>σαφώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. clearly, distinctly (in a clear manner)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clearly (to a degree clearly discernible)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σαφῶς. By surface analysis, σαφής + -ώς.</div><b>σαχάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαχάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σαγάνι">σαγάνι</a></div><b>σαχάριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σαχάρια, neuter σαχάριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Saharan (relating to the Sahara desert)</div><b>σαχανάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σαχανάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σαγανάκι">σαγανάκι</a></div><b>σαχλαμάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαχλαμάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nonsense, inanity, rubbish (foolish talk)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταματά να λες <b>σαχλαμάρες</b> και μιλά σοβαρά! - Stop with the nonsense and start speaking seriously!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nonsense, stupidity, idiocy, rubbish (foolish behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πως να μάθω στο παιδί μου να μην κάνει <b>σαχλαμάρες</b> όλη μέρα; - How can I teach my child not to do stupid things all day?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σαχλός + -μάρα.</div><b>σαύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σαύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lizard</div><b>σβάστικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σβάστικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swastika</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From German Swastika, from Sanskrit स्वस्तिक.</div><b>σβέλτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σβέλτη, neuter σβέλτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quick, well coordinated, agile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian svelto.</div><b>σβέρκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σβέρκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nape (of neck)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Albanian zverk.</div><b>σβήνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fade, be erased</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to disappear, get lost</div><b>σβήνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Ι put out, extinguish (candle, fire, cigarette)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Ι blow out (candle)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to turn off, switch off (light, radio, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to erase, rub out (error)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to slake, quench (thirst)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to stall (car)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (euphemistic) to die, expire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σβήνω, from Ancient Greek σβέννυμι through its aorist 3rd plural ἔσβησαν creating the new verb σβήνω in the pattern of ἔλυσα I untied”) &gt; modern λύνω. The proscribed spelling σβύνω, from a dated etymology through σβεννύω, a variant of σβέννυμι.</div><b>σβήστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σβήστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σβηστήρι">σβηστήρι</a></div><b>σβηστήρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σβηστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σβηστήρι">σβηστήρι</a></div><b>σβηστήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σβηστήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σβηστήρι">σβηστήρι</a></div><b>σβηστήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σβηστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eraser, rubber (UK)</div><b>σβουνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dung, manure of cows or other large animals</div><b>σβούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σβούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spinning top, top</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) live wire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare Serbo-Croatian zvȓk /звр̑к.</div><b>σγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://σμηναγός">σμηναγός</a></div><b>σγουρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σγουρή, neuter σγουρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curly, frizzy</div><b>σε</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, weak) you (2nd person singular, accusative)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς <b>σε</b> λένε; - What is your name?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σέ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to, at, by, in, on, unto, upon; for time, duration</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα επιστρέψω <b>σε</b> δύο ημέρες. - I&#x27;ll come back in two days.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to, at, by, in, on, unto, upon; for space or position</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βάζω το μολύβι <b>σε</b> κάποιο τραπέζι. - I put the pencil on a certain table.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to, at, by, in, on, unto, upon; other senses</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έδωσαν το βραβείο <b>σε</b>/<b>σ’</b> έναν νέο ποιητή. - They gave the prize to a young poet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek εἰς (eis). Doublet of εις (eis).</div><b>σε αγαπώ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I love you</div><b>σε απευθείας σύνδεση</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>online</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σε (se, “in, on”) + απευθείας + σύνδεση</div><b>σείω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σείω.</div><b>σεβασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σεβασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>respect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σέβομαι.</div><b>σεβντάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σεβντάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. infatuation, strong love</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strong desire, passion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish سودا, from Arabic سَوْدَاء.</div><b>σεβρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) kidskin, kid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French chevreau.</div><b>σεζλόνγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) chaise longue (UK), chaise lounge (US)</div><b>σεζόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) season (part of year)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French saison.</div><b>σειέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of σείομαι, passive of <a href="bword://σείω">σείω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>in phrase σειέται και λυγιέται (seiétai kai lygiétai)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive form of verb σειώ, a variation of σείω.</div><b>σειρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σειρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. turn</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι η <b>σειρά</b> μου να μιλήσω. - It&#x27;s my turn to speak.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. series; serial; TV series</div>
  <div class='def'>3. order (arrangement, disposition, sequence; the state of being well arranged)</div>
  <div class='ex'>αλφαβητική <b>σειρά</b> - alphabetical order</div>
  <div class='def'>4. class (military; persons subject to the same intake)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. line (of text)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σειρά. The sense “TV series” is a semantic loan from French série or from English serial.</div><b>σειρήνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σειρήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. siren, alarm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Greek) siren, one of a group of nymphs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Σειρήν.</div><b>σεις</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εσείς">εσείς</a></div><b>σεισμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σεισμική, neuter σεισμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seismic (relating to earthquakes)</div><b>σεισμογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σεισμογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seismograph</div><b>σεισμολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seismology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French séismologie. By surface analysis, σεισμ(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + -λογία.</div><b>σεισμολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σεισμολογική, neuter σεισμολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seismological</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French séismologique (alternative form of sismologique). By surface analysis, σεισμολογ(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>σεισμολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σεισμολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seismologist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French séismologue, a variant of sismologue. By surface analysis, σεισμός + -λόγος.</div><b>σεισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σεισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>earthquake, tremor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σεισμός.</div><b>σεκόια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σεκόιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sequoia (Sequoia)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Sequoia.</div><b>σελήνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) selenium</div><b>σελίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σελίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. page, leaf (in a book)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Internet) web page</div>
  <div class='ex'>κύρια σελίδα (main page)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σελίς.</div><b>σελίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σελίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shilling (previous or current coin of the United Kingdom, Cyprus and various African countries)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Cyprus) small coin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian scellini.</div><b>σεληνάκατος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σεληνάκατοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lunar module</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σελήνη + άκατος.</div><b>σεληνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σεληνιακή, neuter σεληνιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lunar</div>
  <div class='ex'>σεληνιακό φως, (&quot;moonlight&quot;)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σεληνιακός.</div><b>σελιδοδείκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σελιδοδείκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bookmark</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σελίδα + δείκτης</div><b>σελινόριζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σελινόριζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>celeriac</div><b>σεμίδαλις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σιμιγδάλι">σιμιγδάλι</a></div><b>σεμινάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seminar</div><b>σεμνός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σεμνή, neuter σεμνό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. modest, decorous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unassuming, unpretentious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σεμνός, from σέβομαι, from Proto-Indo-European *tyegʷ- (“avoid, yield to”).</div><b>σενάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σενάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>script, scenario</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian scenario, and ultimately a doublet of σκηνή.</div><b>σεναριογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σεναριογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scriptwriter, screenwriter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σενάρι(ο)) + -ο- (-o-) + -γράφος, a calque of English scriptwriter.</div><b>σενεγαλέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σενεγαλέζικη, neuter σενεγαλέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Senegalese (relating to Senegal or its people)</div><b>σεντόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σεντόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sheet, bedsheet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σεντόνι(ν)), from Koine Greek σινδόνιον, diminutive of the ancient Ancient Greek σινδών. Also see the Hellenistic feminine σινδόνη.</div><b>σεξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sexual intercourse, sex</div><b>σεξισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural σεξισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) sexism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English sexism, suffixed with -ισμός.</div><b>σεξουαλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σεξουαλική, neuter σεξουαλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sexual</div><b>σεξουαλικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sexuality</div><b>σερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (capitalized, indeclinable, often) Sir (the titular prefix given to a knight or baronet)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) sir (a respectful term of address to a man)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English Sir.</div><b>σερίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>serine</div><b>σερίφης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σερίφηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sheriff</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French shérif plus -ης (-is) ending, from English sheriff.</div><b>σεραπιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σεραπιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Any orchid of the genus Serapias.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek σερᾱπῐᾰ́ς, with a semantic loan from translingual Serapias.</div><b>σεραφίμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σεραφείμ">σεραφείμ</a></div><b>σεραφείμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) seraph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σεραφείμ n or m, from Biblical Hebrew שְׂרָפִים, the plural form of שָׂרָף.</div><b>σερβίρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be served</div><b>σερβίρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wait tables, wait on tables</div>
  <div class='def'>2. serve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σερβίρω, borrowed from Italian servire (“to serve”).</div><b>σερβιέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σερβιέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sanitary napkin, sanitary pad, sanitary towel (pad of absorbent material worn by women to absorb the menstrual flow)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>σερβιέτα</b> χρησιμοποιείται για την απορρόφηση της έμμηνης ρύσης. - A sanitary napkin is used for the absorption of the menstrual flow.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French serviette (“napkin”).</div><b>σερβικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Serbian (the language of Serbia and its people)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σερβικός">σερβικός</a></div><b>σερβικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σερβική, neuter σερβικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Serbian (relating to Serbia, its people or language)</div><b>σερβιρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σερβίρω">σερβίρω</a></div><b>σερβιτόρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σερβιτόρες, masculine σερβιτόρος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female waiter, waitress</div>
  <div class='def'>2. barmaid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Feminine form of σερβιτόρος.</div><b>σερβιτόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σερβιτόροι, feminine σερβιτόρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. male waiter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. barman, bartender</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian servitore.</div><b>σερβοκροάτικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σερβοκροατικά">σερβοκροατικά</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σερβοκροάτικος">σερβοκροάτικος</a></div><b>σερβοκροάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σερβοκροάτικη, neuter σερβοκροάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σερβοκροατικός">σερβοκροατικός</a></div><b>σερβοκροατικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Serbo-Croatian, Serbo-Croat (the language)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σερβοκροατικός">σερβοκροατικός</a></div><b>σερβοκροατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σερβοκροατική, neuter σερβοκροατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Serbo-Croatian</div><b>σεργιάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σεργιάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) walk, stroll, often with the scope of finding girls or boys</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish سیران (seyran).</div><b>σερμαγιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σερμαγιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(common) capital</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish سرمایه (sermaye), from Classical Persian سرمایه.</div><b>σερσένι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σερσένια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hornet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a Slavic language, Proto-Slavic *sьrxy.</div><b>σεσημασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine σεσημασμένη, neuter σεσημασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) branded (of criminals)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι <b>σεσημασμένος</b> κακοποιός, γνωστός στην αστυνομία. - He is a branded criminal, known by the police.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek σεσημᾰσμένος. Formal perfect participle of σημαίνομαι, passive voice of σημαίνω. It retains the ancient reduplication σε-σ...-. Semantic loan from French marqué.</div><b>σετ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) set, group (of similar people or things)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγόρασα ένα <b>σετ</b> εργαλείων. - I bought a set of tools.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) set</div><b>σηκωθήκανε τ&#x27; αγγούρια να γαμήσουν το μανάβη</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) Said of cases where those of a lower standing rise up against their superiors - implies failure or lack of strength.</div><b>σηκωμάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σηκωμάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) boner, stiffy (erect penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχα <b>σηκωμάρες</b> όταν με φίλησε η φιλενάδα μου. - I got a boner when my girlfriend kissed me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σηκώνω + -μάρα.</div><b>σηκώθηκαν τα πόδια να χτυπήσουν το κεφάλι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Said of cases where those of a lower standing rise up against their superiors - implies failure or lack of strength.</div><b>σηκώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stand up, rise (from a sitting position)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to get up, arise, be up and about</div><b>σηκώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to raise, put up, lift, heave</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σηκώνω</b> άγκυρα - to weigh/raise anchor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σηκώνω, from Koine Greek σηκῶ, from Ancient Greek σηκόω + -ώνω.</div><b>σημάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σημάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mark, sign</div>
  <div class='def'>2. scar, birthmark</div>
  <div class='def'>3. omen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σημάδιον, from diminutive of Ancient Greek σῆμα.</div><b>σημαία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σημαίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flag</div>
  <div class='def'>2. banner</div>
  <div class='def'>3. standard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σημαίᾱ, variant of σημείᾱ.</div><b>σημαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to mean, signify</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αλλά αυτό δεν <b>σημαίνει</b> ότι γυρνάμε στο «business as usual». - But this doesn&#x27;t mean that we&#x27;re going back to “business as usual”.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to ring, sound</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to signal</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to be marked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σημαίνω, from σῆμα n.</div><b>σημαδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to aim</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to scar</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to mark (visibly impressing or signing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σημαδεύω.</div><b>σημαιοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σημαιοφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a naval rank with the NATO grade OF-1; midshipman in the Royal Navy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a naval rank with the NATO grade OF-1; ensign in the US Navy</div><b>σημαντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σημαντική, neuter σημαντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>important, significant, considerable, outstanding, notable</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο καλός ύπνος είναι <b>σημαντικός</b> για την απώλεια βάρους. - Good sleep is important for weight loss.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σημαντικός.</div><b>σημασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σημασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. meaning, sense (single conventional use of a word)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο ρήμα «τρέχω» έχει πολλές <b>σημασίες</b>. - The verb “trécho” has many meanings.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. significance, importance, consequence (extent to which something matters)</div>
  <div class='ex'>έχει <b>σημασία</b> - it matters</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σημασία.</div><b>σημασιολογικό δάνειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σημασιολογικά δάνεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>semantic loan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantic loan from German Lehnübersetzung and French emprunt. Also see μεταφραστικό δάνειο.</div><b>σηματοδοτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to signalize (to furnish (a road intersection or pedestrian crossing) with a traffic signal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to signal (to indicate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σηματο- (simato-) + -δοτώ, a calque of French signaliser.</div><b>σημείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σημεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sign, mark</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spot, place</div>
  <div class='def'>3. point</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, derived from Ancient Greek σημεῖον.</div><b>σημείωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σημειώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>note, memo, missive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σημείωμα.</div><b>σημείωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σημειώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>note, marginal comment, footnote, endnote</div><b>σημειολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) semiotics (the study of cultural sign processes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French séméiologie.</div><b>σημειωθήκαν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of σημειώθηκαν of passive verb <a href="bword://σημειώνομαι">σημειώνομαι</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>see at σημειώνω</div><b>σημειωθήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of σημειώθηκαν of passive verb <a href="bword://σημειώνομαι">σημειώνομαι</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>see at σημειώνω</div><b>σημειωματάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σημειωματάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>notebook (book for notes and memoranda)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σημείωμα + -άριο</div><b>σημειωτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σημειωτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>semiotics (the study of cultural sign processes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French séméiotique.</div><b>σημειωτόν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in gymnastics) slow pace</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of progress) extremely slow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Free translation of French marquer le pas. Neuter singular, from the Hellenistic Koine adjective σημειωτός (“shown, indicated by a mark”) from verb σημειόω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in gymnastics) pacing slowly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, indeclinable) progressing extremely slowly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Free translation of French marquer le pas. Neuter singular, from the Hellenistic Koine adjective σημειωτός (“shown, indicated by a mark”) from verb σημειόω.</div><b>σημειώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to mark, and see <a href="bword://σημειώνω">σημειώνω</a></div><b>σημειώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to put a mark as recognizing sign or a reminder</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σημειώνω</b> χι στη σωστή απάντηση. - I mark the correct answer with an X.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to write down a note</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σημείωσα</b> το τηλέφωνό σου. - I wrote down your phone number.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to pay attention, consider seriously</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σημείωσε</b> τα λόγια μου! - Mark my words!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to pay attention, consider seriously; to note, to add emphasis</div>
  <div class='ex'>αξίζει να <b>σημειωθεί</b> - it is worth noting</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (third person) to occur</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε <b>σημειώθηκαν</b> επεισόδια στον χθεσινό αγώνα. - Νο incidents occurred at yesterday&#x27;s match.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to achieve an outcome (positive or negative)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σημειώνω</b> επιτυχία - achieve success</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σημει(ῶ), “put a mark”) / σημειόω + -ώνω, a rare active form of the more common middle disposition verb σημειοῦμαι. Ultimately from Ancient Greek σημεῖον.</div><b>σημερινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σημερινή, neuter σημερινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>today&#x27;s, happening today</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek σημερινός. By surface analysis, σήμερα + -ινός.</div><b>σημιτικός</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A supporter or adherent of the pro-European vision and general management of political life introduced by Costas Simitis.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πόσο <b>σημιτικός</b> είναι τελικά ο Μητσοτάκης;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Σημίτης + -ικός.</div><b>σημυδόσπιζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σημυδόσπιζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. redpoll</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially) common redpoll, Acanthis flammea (sometimes kept as a caged bird)</div><b>σηπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σηπτική, neuter σηπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>septic</div><b>σηψαιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σηψαιμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>septicemia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σήψη + αίμα + -ία, a calque of New Latin sēpticaemia, from the same Greek roots.</div><b>σθένος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. moral or emotional strength, might, power</div>
  <div class='def'>2. valence, valency</div>
  <div class='def'>3. valency (number of grammatical arguments a verb can take)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek σθένος.</div><b>σι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) si, ti</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) B (seventh note or leading tone in the C major scale)</div><b>σιέστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) siesta, afternoon nap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Spanish siesta.</div><b>σιαγόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σιαγόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. jawbone (mandible or maxilla)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the jaws of a tool or instrument</div>
  <div class='ex'>H μέγγενη έχει δύο <b>σιαγόνες</b>. - The vice has two jaws.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σιαγών.</div><b>σιγά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slowly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. quietly</div>
  <div class='def'>3. as if (used in phrases denoting irony in response to threats or to something deemed highly unlikely)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός θα με δείρει; <b>Σιγά</b>, αυτός ούτε ένα παιδάκι δεν μπορεί να δείρει! - He&#x27;ll beat me up? As if, he couldn&#x27;t even beat a little child!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σιγῇ.</div><b>σιγά-σιγά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gradually, carefully, very slowly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σιγά.</div><b>σιγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural σιγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) silence, quiet</div><b>σιγαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σιγαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>absence of sounds in the environment, quiet, silence</div><b>σιγανά ποτάμια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of σιγανό <a href="bword://ποτάμι">ποτάμι</a></div><b>σιγανό ποτάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιγανά ποτάμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) dark horse</div><b>σιγανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σιγανή, neuter σιγανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quiet, gentle, soft</div><b>σιγοβράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to simmer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σιγά + βράζω</div><b>σιγουρεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to make sure</div><b>σιγουρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to secure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to make sure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σίγουρ(ος)) + -εύω.</div><b>σιδεράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιδεράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blacksmith, ironsmith</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from σίδερο n</div><b>σιδερένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σιδερένια, neuter σιδερένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. iron (made of iron)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) iron, of iron, of steel (strong, inflexible)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σιδερένια</b> θέληση - iron will</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σιδερένιος. By surface analysis, σίδερ(ο)) + -ένιος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>get well soon, good recovery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σιδερένιος. By surface analysis, σίδερ(ο)) + -ένιος.</div><b>σιδερίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιδερίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Greek grapevine (of species Vitis vinifera), a late ripening variety bearing reddish hardy berries</div>
  <div class='def'>2. formal term, synonym of <a href="bword://τσάι">τσάι</a> <a href="bword://του">του</a> βουνού: ironwort, mountain tea (genus Sideritis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek σῐδηρῖτῐς, feminine of Ancient Greek σῐδηρῑ́της, with change of ⟨ηρ⟩ &gt; ⟨ερ⟩ [er] -see: σίδερο n- and modern masculine suffix -ίτης. Doublet of σιδηρίτης.
The sense for herb, since Byzantine Greek σιδερίτης, alternative form for σιδηρῖτις f and σιδηρίτης m.</div><b>σιδερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to iron, press (clothing)</div><b>σιδηρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιδηρίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>siderite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σῐδηρῑ́της or learned borrowing from French sidérite, from Latin sideritis &lt; Ancient Greek σῐδηρῖτῐς, feminine of σῐδηρῑ́της. By surface analysis, σίδηρ(ος)) + -ίτης. Doublet of σιδερίτης.</div><b>σιδηροδρομικοί σταθμοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://σιδηροδρομικός">σιδηροδρομικός</a> <a href="bword://σταθμός">σταθμός</a></div><b>σιδηροδρομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σιδηροδρομική, neuter σιδηροδρομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rail, connected to railways, etc</div>
  <div class='ex'>σιδηροδρομικός σταθμός (railway station)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σιδηρόδρομος m.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιδηροδρομικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>railwayman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σιδηρόδρομος m.</div><b>σιδηροδρομικός σταθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιδηροδρομικοί σταθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>railway station (UK), train station (US)</div><b>σιδηροδρομικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>by rail</div><b>σιδηροπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιδηροπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ironmonger&#x27;s, hardware shop, hardware store</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σίδηρος + -πωλείο</div><b>σιδηροσωλήνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιδηροσωλήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>iron pipe, iron tube</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σίδηρος + σωλήνας.</div><b>σιδηροτροχιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σιδηροτροχιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rail (the metal bar forming part of the track for a railroad)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σιδηρο- (sidiro-) + τροχιά, a calque of German Eisenbahn.</div><b>σιδηρουργείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιδηρουργεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smithy, forge, blacksmith&#x27;s workshop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ironworks</div><b>σιδηρουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιδηρουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blacksmith, ironsmith, smith</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σιδηρουργός, from σίδηρος + -ουργός; equivalent to σίδερο + -ουργός.</div><b>σιδηρόδρομος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιδηρόδρομοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>railway (UK), railroad (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σίδηρος + δρόμος</div><b>σιιτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σιιτική, neuter σιιτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Shiite, Shi&#x27;ite, Shiitic, Shi&#x27;itic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English Shiitic, Shi&#x27;itic. By surface analysis, σιίτ(ης)) + -ικός.</div><b>σικελικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σικελική, neuter σικελικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Sicilian (relating to Sicily or its people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Σικελικός. By surface analysis, Σικελία + -ικός</div><b>σικελιώτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σικελιώτικη, neuter σικελικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σικελικός">σικελικός</a></div><b>σικορέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) chicory (leaf vegetable)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) chicory (used in coffee)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neologism from French chicorée.</div><b>σιλδεναφίλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sildenafil</div><b>σιμίτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot, indeclinable) koulouri, simit (ring shaped bread roll covered with sesame seeds)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic سميد. Doublet of σιμίτι, loaned from Ottoman Turkish. Also see Ancient Greek σεμίδαλις.</div><b>σιμίτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιμίτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>simit, bagel or ring doughnut shaped bread roll covered with sesame seeds</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish سمید (simit, “simit, semolina”), from Classical Persian سمید, ultimately from Akkadian 𒆠𒅔𒆠𒅔𒄯𒄯.</div><b>σιμιγδάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιμιγδάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>semolina</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek σιμιγδάλι. From the Hellenistic noun σεμιδάλιν, from Ancient Greek σεμίδαλις</div><b>σιμπόργκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) seaborgium</div><b>σινάπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mustard (plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σίναπι.</div><b>σινάφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σινάφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a class of people practicing the same craft or sharing the same occupation (dated, vernacular)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) people of the same social class or group</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish صنف, from Arabic صِنْف. Compare Turkish sınıf (“class, classroom”).</div><b>σιναϊτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σιναϊτική, neuter σιναϊτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Sinaitic</div><b>σινεμά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cinema (film/movie theatre)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French cinéma, clipping of cinématographe, a compound from Ancient Greek κίνημα, stem κινηματο- + -γράφος from γράφω. Doublet of κίνημα and the vernacular masculine σινεμάς.</div><b>σινικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σινική, neuter σινικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Chinese, Sinic (related to China, its people or language)</div>
  <div class='ex'>–Δάσκαλε, θαυμάζω τη <b>σινική</b> ευρετικότητα του σαδισμού σας! - –Master, I admire the sinic inventiveness of your sadism!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Medieval Latin Sinae + -ικός</div><b>σιντριβάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιντριβάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fountain (artificial water feature)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish شادروان, from Classical Persian شادروان. Contaminated with συντρίβω.</div><b>σιροκολεβάντες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) an east-southeasterly Mediterranean wind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σιρόκος + λεβάντες</div><b>σιρόκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) the sirocco (a warm southeasterly Mediterranean wind)</div><b>σιρόπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιρόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>syrup</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σιρόπι</b> σφενδάμου - maple syrup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian sciroppo (“syrup”).</div><b>σιτάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιτάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wheat (plant and grain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σιτάρι, from Ancient Greek σιτάριον.</div><b>σιταποθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σιταποθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>granary (cereal grain storage warehouse)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σιτάρι + αποθήκη</div><b>σιτηρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cereal (a member of that class of grains)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῖτος.</div><b>σιτοβολώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σιτοβολώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. granary, (barn, silo for storing grain)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breadbasket, granary</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Μανιτόμπα είναι ο <b>σιτοβολώνας</b> του Καναδά. - Manitoba is the granary of Canada.</div><b>σιτσιάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σιτσιάνικη, neuter σιτσιάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σικελικός">σικελικός</a></div><b>σιφινιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σιφινιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σιφονιέρα">σιφονιέρα</a></div><b>σιφονιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σιφονιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chest of drawers</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French chiffonnier (“chest of drawers”).</div><b>σιφόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Greek, Modern, Standard, indeclinable) siphon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Egyptiot, indeclinable) siphon, cistern, flusher</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) the textile chiffon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unadapted borrowing from French siphon.</div><b>σιφόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σιφόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>U-bend</div><b>σιχαίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to detest, hate, despise, loathe (due to finding someone or something disgusting and/or distasteful)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όχι, ευχαριστώ, δεν θέλω παγωτό. <b>Σιχαίνομαι</b> τις φράουλες. - No, thanks, I don&#x27;t want any ice cream. I hate strawberries.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σιχαίνομαι, from Hellenistic σικχαίνω + -ομαι (-omai, ending for passive verbs), from σικχός.</div><b>σιχτίρ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) fuck, for fuck&#x27;s sake, damn it, shit, crap (expressing dismay, anger or frustration)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το σκυλί γαβγίζει όλη νύχτα… <b>Σιχτίρ</b> πιά! - That dog&#x27;s been barking all night… Fucking hell!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish سیكدیر (sikdir).</div><b>σιχτίρι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σιχτίρ">σιχτίρ</a></div><b>σιωπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σιωπώ">σιωπώ</a></div><b>σιωπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σιωπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>silence, quiet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σιωπή.</div><b>σιωπηλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σιωπηλή, neuter σιωπηλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>silent, quiet</div><b>σιωπηρά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tacitly, silently</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σιωπηρός">σιωπηρός</a></div><b>σιωπηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σιωπηρή, neuter σιωπηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tacit, silent, unsaid</div><b>σιωπηρώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tacitly, silently</div><b>σιωπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to remain silent, keep silent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fall silent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σιωπῶ, contracted form of σιωπάω.</div><b>σκάβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dig</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to excavate, wear away</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to carve</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from Koine Greek, from Ancient Greek σκᾰ́πτω from the perfective stem σκαψ- in the pattern of -ψ- &gt; -β- as in κλέπτω, κλεψ- &gt; κλέβω.</div><b>σκάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to burst, explode, alternative form of <a href="bword://σκάω">σκάω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σκάζει</b> η βόμβα - the bomb explodes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σκάζω, from Ancient Greek σχάζω, σχάω with [sx] &gt; [sk].</div><b>σκάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) chess</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (countable) chessboard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian scacchi, from Arabic شَاه, from Classical Persian شاه.</div><b>σκάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ladder, rung</div>
  <div class='def'>2. staircase, stairs, stairway</div>
  <div class='def'>3. escalator</div>
  <div class='def'>4. gangway</div>
  <div class='def'>5. pier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκάλα, from Latin scāla.</div><b>σκάνδαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκάνδαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scandal (incident or event that disgraces or damages the reputation of the persons or organization involved)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο υπεύθυνος της εταιρίας παραιτήθηκε μετά το <b>σκάνδαλο</b>. - The head of the company resigned after the scandal.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek σκάνδαλον. Doublet of σκάνταλο (inherited).</div><b>σκάνδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scandium</div><b>σκάνερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) scanner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English scanner.</div><b>σκάνταλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκάνταλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rascal, scamp (mischievous child)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek σκάνδαλον with -νδ- → -ντ- alteration. Doublet of σκάνδαλο.</div><b>σκάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σχάρα">σχάρα</a></div><b>σκάρτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκάρτη, neuter σκάρτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shoddy, of poor quality</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λόρδος άνθρωπος και να ’χει <b>σκάρτο</b> ρολόι! - A man [like] a lord! and he has a shoddy watch!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σκάρτος, from Italian scarto + ending -ς (-s).
Also see the Italian scartare and carta from Ancient Greek χάρτης, indicating a reborrowing. A possible influence of the Italian scartare in its sense &quot;discard&quot; and its relation to the French écarter; see the verb ξεσκαρτάρω.</div><b>σκάρφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκάρφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Certain species of the genus Helleborus, a genus of ranunculaceous flowers.</div><b>σκάσε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://σκάω">σκάω</a> and <a href="bword://σκάζω">σκάζω</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) shut up, shut it, be quiet</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκάσε</b> πια! Μας έχεις πρήξει το συκώτι με τα ανόητα τραγούδια σου! - Shut up already! You&#x27;ve driven us mad with your stupid songs!</div><b>σκάφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκάφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. craft, ship</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hull</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκάφος.</div><b>σκάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to burst, explode</div>
  <div class='ex'>See the alternative form: σκάζω (skázo)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to burst</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>έσκασε</b> στα γέλια. - He/she burst into laughter.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to feel very hot, to get too hot</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκάω</b> απ’ τη ζέστη. - I feel very hot.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) to announce (sensationally, suddenly)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) to anger, become angry, fulminate</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (colloquial, idiomatic, impolite) to shut up (cease talking)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έσκασε</b> επιτέλους αυτός ο παλιόγερος; - Has that old fart shut up yet?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκάζω, from the mediaeval σκάζω, from Ancient Greek σχάζω, σχάω with [sx] &gt; [sk].</div><b>σκέιτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) skateboard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Shortening of σκέιτμπορντ, borrowed from English skateboard</div><b>σκέιτμπορντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) skateboard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English skateboard.</div><b>σκέιτμπορντινγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) skateboarding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English skateboarding.</div><b>σκέλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>legs (of human, or of quadruped (chiefly hind legs))</div>
  <div class='ex'>βάζω την ουρά κάτω από τα <b>σκέλια</b> - to put one&#x27;s tail between one&#x27;s legs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκέλ(ος)) + -ια (-ia), analogously to πόδι / πόδια.</div><b>σκέλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leg</div>
  <div class='def'>2. part (distinct element of anything consisting of alike elements)</div><b>σκέπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formal or dated form of <a href="bword://σκέφτομαι">σκέφτομαι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σκέπτομαι. Compare to the inherited σκέφτομαι.</div><b>σκέτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκέτη, neuter σκέτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plain, pure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. neat, straight, undiluted (for alcoholic drinks)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unsweetened, black (for coffee drinks)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. an intensifier for abstract nouns</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σκέτη</b> ανοησία - sheer nonsense</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian schietto, of Germanic origin.</div><b>σκέφτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to think, to ponder, to consider</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκεφτόμουν</b> τι μπορούσαμε να κάνουμε. - I was thinking what we could do.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκέπτομαι, from Ancient Greek σκέπτομαι.</div><b>σκέψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκέψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thought, mental activity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκέψις.</div><b>σκήπτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκήπτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sceptre</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκῆπτρον. Doublet of σκούπα.</div><b>σκίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to secede, break away from</div><b>σκίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of water) to traverse, cross, go across</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το καράβι <b>σκίζει</b> τα κύματα. - The boat slits the waves.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) to bust one&#x27;s butt, try very hard</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκίστηκα</b> να τους περιποιηθώ. - I busted my butt to entertain them.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to slit, tear, cleave, sliver, split</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, figuratively) to win, triumph</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκίζει</b> η ομάδα! - The team is winning!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σκίζω, from Ancient Greek σχίζω. Doublet of σχίζω.</div><b>σκίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σχίνος">σχίνος</a></div><b>σκίουρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκίουροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>squirrel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκίουρος.</div><b>σκίρων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) mistral, northwest Mediterranean wind</div><b>σκίτσο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκίτσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sketch, cartoon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian schizzo, from Latin schedium, scheda (“stroke of papyrus”).</div><b>σκαθάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκαθάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beetle</div><b>σκαιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκαιά or σκαιή, neuter σκαιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rough</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brusque</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rude, impolite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *skeh₂iwo-. Cognate with Latin scaevus.</div><b>σκακιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκακιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chessboard</div><b>σκακιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκακιστική, neuter σκακιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) chess</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκακιστής + -ικός.</div><b>σκαλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκαλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rung (of a ladder)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. step, stair</div><b>σκαλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dig, to dig up, to hoe, to till (:soil)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dig around, to rummage (to search inside with disregard for the way in which things were arranged)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to carve (to cut a design into a hard surface)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek σκαλίζω, from Ancient Greek σκάλλω.</div><b>σκαληνός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκαληνή, neuter σκαληνό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scalene, having unequal sides</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκαληνός.</div><b>σκαλμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκαλμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκαρμός">σκαρμός</a></div><b>σκαλωσιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκαλωσιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scaffolding</div><b>σκαλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to catch on something protruding and get stuck in</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to encounter obstacles</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an unattested Byzantine Greek *σκαλώνω, from σκάλα + -ώνω.</div><b>σκαμνί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκαμνιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stool</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκαμνί, σκαμνίον, diminutive of Koine Greek σκάμνον, borrowed from Latin scamnum.</div><b>σκαμπίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκαμπίλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slap</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cardgame</div><b>σκανδάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκανδάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trigger (finger-operated lever used to fire a gun)</div>
  <div class='ex'>με το δάχτυλο στη <b>σκανδάλη</b> - with one&#x27;s finger on the trigger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σκανδάλη, changed gender form of Ancient Greek σκάνδαλον.</div><b>σκανδαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκανδαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκανταλιά">σκανταλιά</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned -ντ- → -νδ- variant of σκανταλιά, perhaps influenced by σκάνδαλο.</div><b>σκανδιναβικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκανδιναβική, neuter σκανδιναβικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Scandinavian</div><b>σκανταλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκανταλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (countable) mischief (a playful annoying action)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (countable, in plural) mischief (a playful annoying action); mischief (conduct that playfully causes petty annoyance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκάνταλ(ο)) + -ιά.</div><b>σκαντζόχοιρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκαντζόχοιροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hedgehog</div>
  <div class='ex'>1904, Folktale from Thrace in Nikolaos G. Polites, Studies of the life and languages of the Greek people, vol. 2, part 1, page 193.
Μία μάννα εἶχε τέσσερα παιδιά, τὸν σκαντζόχοιρο, τὴν ἀράχνη, τὴ χελῶνα καὶ τὴ μέλισσα. Ἦρθε καιρός καὶ ἀρρώστησε καὶ ἔστειλε νὰ φωνάξῃ τὸν σκαντζόχοιρο […]. Ό σκαντζόχοιρος ὅμως εἶπε […] πῶς ἔχει νὰ βάλῃ φράχταις ’ς τἀμπέλι του καὶ δὲν ἔχει καιρό. Ἡ μάννα του τὸν καταράστη τότε· «Νὰ του καθήσουν οἱ τσαλιαίς ’ς τὴ ῥάχη».
Mía mánna eíche téssera paidiá, ton skantzóchoiro, tin aráchni, ti chelóna kai ti mélissa. Írthe kairós kai arróstise kai ésteile na fonáxei ton skantzóchoiro […]. O skantzóchoiros ómos eípe […] pos échei na válei fráchtes &#x27;s tampéli tou kai den échei kairó. I mánna tou ton katarásti tóte; «Na tou kathísoun oi tsaliés &#x27;s ti ráchi». - A mother had four children, a hedgehog, a spider, a turtle and a bee. There came a time when she got sick and she summoned the hedgehog […]. But the hedgehog said […] that he had to put fences at his vineyard and had no time. Then, his mother cursed him: &quot;May the thorns [of the stakes for the fence] sit on his back.&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κανθόχοιρος, from Ancient Greek ἀκανθόχοιρος, from ἄκανθος + χοῖρος. Doublet of ακανθόχοιρος.</div><b>σκαπάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκαπάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pickaxe, pick, pickax</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hoe</div>
  <div class='def'>3. archaeological dig</div><b>σκαρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκαρία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stocks, slipway (upon which a ship is built)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) hull (of a ship)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) temperament (of a person)</div><b>σκαραβαίος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκαραβαίοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scarab beetle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κάραβος.</div><b>σκαραβαιίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Scarabaeidae, the scarabaeids or scarabs.</div>
  <div class='ex'>1. ζωολ. γένος και γενική ονομασία τών κολεόπτερων εντόμων της οικογένειας <b>σκαραβαιίδες</b> και κυρίως τών κοπροφάγων εκπροσώπων της οικογένειας αυτής, γνωστών και με τη λόγια ονομασία κάνθαρος και με την κοινή ονομασία μπούρμπουλας - 1. zool. genus and common name of the coleopteran insects of the family Scarabaeidae and mainly of the dung-eating representatives of this family, also known by the colloquial name kántharos and by the common name búrmpulas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From translingual Scarabaeidae, suffixed with -ίδες.</div><b>σκαρλάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκαρλάτη, neuter σκαρλάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crimson, scarlet</div><b>σκαρμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκαρμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rowlock, thole, tholepin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκαλμός.</div><b>σκαρφαλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to climb, clamber, scale (up a steep surface)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perhaps a blend of καρφώνω + σκαλώνω, with the σ- (s-) of σκαλώνω switched to the beginning of καρφώνω.</div><b>σκασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine σκασμένη, neuter σκασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. burst, exploded</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αυτοκίνητο έχει <b>σκασμένο</b> λάστιχο. - The car has a flat tyre.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) worried, annoyed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect passive participle of σκάω / σκάζω, a verb with no passive forms.</div><b>σκασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suffocation or asphyxia caused by overindulgence, usually of food, chiefly in set phrases</div>
  <div class='ex'>τρώω του <b>σκασμού</b> - I eat so much I can&#x27;t breathe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκάζω / σκάω, stem σκασ- as in past tense έσκασα, + -μός. Compare to σκασίλα f, σκάσιμο n. The medieval σκασμός, of different sense.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) shut up, shut it, be quiet</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκασμός</b>! Ο κόσμος προσπαθεί να κοιμηθεί! - Shut up! People are trying to sleep!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκάζω / σκάω, stem σκασ- as in past tense έσκασα, + -μός. Compare to σκασίλα f, σκάσιμο n. The medieval σκασμός, of different sense.</div><b>σκατ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) skat</div><b>σκατά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable, vulgar) shit, crap, lousy (of bad quality)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(vulgar) shit, fuck, damn it, crap</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://σκατό">σκατό</a> (shit, feces)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. genitive/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://σκατάς">σκατάς</a> (horrible man, asshole)</div><b>σκατά στα μούτρα σου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive, vulgar) go fuck yourself, get fucked, bugger off, get screwed (to someone who has done something wrong or morally despicable)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκατά στα μούτρα σου</b>, παλιάνθρωπε! - Go fuck yourself, you piece of shit!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “shit on your face”.</div><b>σκατάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκατάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, offensive) prick, jerk, asshole (a bad-natured or contemptible man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας τους είναι <b>σκατάς</b>. - Their father is an arsehole.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκατό + -άς.</div><b>σκατής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκατιά, neuter σκατί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) shit-brown, shit-coloured (brown like faeces)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι πολύ άσχημη αυτή η <b>σκατιά</b> γραβάτα που φοράει. - That shit-brown tie he&#x27;s wearing is very ugly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκατό.</div><b>σκατίλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, uncountable, vulgar) shitty smell, smell of shit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χάλασε ο βόθρος και μύρισε ο τόπος <b>σκατίλα</b>. - The cesspit broke and a smell of shit filled the air.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκατό + -ίλα.</div><b>σκατολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκατολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scatology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκατο- (skato-, “shit”) + -λογία.</div><b>σκατολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκατολογικη, neuter σκατολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scatological</div><b>σκατουλής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκατουλιά, neuter σκατουλί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) shit-brown, shit-coloured (brown like faeces)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί φοράς αυτό το <b>σκατουλί</b> πουκάμισο; - Why are you wearing that shit-coloured shirt?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκατό.</div><b>σκατό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκατά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, plural normally, vulgar) shit, shite, crap (faeces)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος άφησε τα <b>σκατά</b> στη τουαλέτα πάλι; - Who left their shit in the toilet again?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, vulgar) crap, dirt (anything foul-smelling and dirty in general)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήξαμε στο <b>σκατό</b> σ’ αυτή τη γειτονιά. - We&#x27;re drowning in crap in this neighbourhood.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively, plural normally, vulgar) shit, shite, crap, nonsense, rubbish (anything low quality and worthless)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί σπαταλά τα λεφτά της σ’ αυτά τα <b>σκατά</b>; - Why does she waste her money on that shit?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) little shit, little fart (small and insolent child)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιος σου είναι ένα τόσο δα <b>σκατό</b> και βγάζει γλώσσα. - Your son is only a little shit and he&#x27;s talking smack.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial, figuratively, humorous) any small item, in a negative light.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι πλήρωσες τόσα λεφτά για αυτό το <b>σκατό</b>; - Why&#x27;d you pay so much money for that piece of crap?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from σκάτα, from the plural of Ancient Greek σκῶρ.</div><b>σκατόγερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκατόγεροι, feminine σκατόγρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, offensive) old bastard, old shit, old asshole (abusive term for elderly man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πιάσανε ακόμα αυτόν τον <b>σκατόγερο</b> τον παιδεραστή; - Did they catch that old bastard of a paedophile yet?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκατό- + γέρος.</div><b>σκατόγρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκατόγριες, masculine σκατόγερος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, offensive) old bitch, old cunt, old fart (abusive term for elderly woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πότε θα πεθάνει η <b>σκατόγρια</b> η πεθερά μου; - When will my old bitch of a mother-in-law die?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκατό- + γριά.</div><b>σκατόπαιδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκατόπαιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) little shit, little brat, little asshole (badly behaved child or young person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ανιψιός μου είναι σκέτο <b>σκατόπαιδο</b> και τον μισούν τα αλλά παιδιά. - My nephew is a real little brat and the other kids hate him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκατό- + παιδί.</div><b>σκελίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκελίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clove (of garlic)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σκελίδα</b> σκόρδου - clove of garlic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκελίς, variant of σχελίς.</div><b>σκελετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκελετοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. skeleton</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) frame of any structure</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>σκελετός</b> του κτιρίου - the frame of the building</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σκελετός, ancient sense: &quot;dried up, dried up body, withered&quot;, from σκελε- (skele-), from Proto-Indo-European *(s)kelh₁- (“to parch, wither”)) + -ετός. Compare Greek σκληρός.</div><b>σκεπάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to cover (put a cover over)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to cover up, to cover (be a cover over)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πολλά μέτρα χιόνι <b>σκέπασαν</b> την αυλή - several feet of snow covered the yard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σκεπάζω.</div><b>σκεπάρνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκεπάρνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adze (chiefly carpenter&#x27;s)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σκεπάρνιον, diminutive of ancient σκέπαρνον.</div><b>σκεπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκεπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>roof (of building)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκεπάζω.</div><b>σκεπτικίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκεπτικίστριες, masculine σκεπτικιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sceptic</div><b>σκεπτικισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκεπτικισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scepticism (UK), skepticism (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. disbelief</div><b>σκεπτικιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκεπτικιστές, feminine σκεπτικίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sceptic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French scepticiste. First attested 1887.</div><b>σκεπτικιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκεπτικιστική, neuter σκεπτικιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sceptical</div><b>σκεπτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκεπτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rationale, reasoning</div><b>σκεπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκεπτική, neuter σκεπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contemplative, meditative, thoughtful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκεπτικός.</div><b>σκερπάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκερπάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκεπάρνι">σκεπάρνι</a></div><b>σκευοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκευοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cupboard, credenza, ambry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκευοθήκη. Morphologically, from σκεύος (“appliance, implement, utensil”) + -θήκη (“case, encasement, slip”).</div><b>σκευοφυλάκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sacristy (room in a church where sacred vessels, books, vestments, etc. are kept)</div><b>σκεφτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκεφτική, neuter σκεφτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκεπτικός">σκεπτικός</a></div><b>σκεύος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκεύη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. utensil, gadget, appliance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sacred vessel at the Eucharist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek σκεῦος (as in English skeuomorph).</div><b>σκηνή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκηνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. skene</div>
  <div class='def'>2. scene, stage</div>
  <div class='def'>3. incident, scene (where an incident occurred)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>σκηνή</b> εγκλήματος. - the crime scene.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. tent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκηνή.</div><b>σκηνικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκηνικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scenery, set, backdrop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. scene (embarrassing exhibition of passion)</div><b>σκηνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκηνική, neuter σκηνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stage, stagey, scene</div><b>σκηνοθέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκηνοθέτες, feminine σκηνοθέτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>director</div><b>σκηνοθέτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκηνοθέτριες, masculine σκηνοθέτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>director</div><b>σκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) skiing, water skiing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) ski - worn on the feet for winter or water skiing</div><b>σκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) shadow, shade; dark area cast by light source</div>
  <div class='ex'>θέατρο <b>σκιών</b> - shadow theatre</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) shadow, shade; black area</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ύποπτος χάθηκε μέσα στις <b>σκιές</b> της νύχτας. - The suspect disappeared into the shadows of the night.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) shadow, shade; follower, person following another, tail</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) shadow, shade; shadow effects (in typography, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σκιά</b> ματιών - eye shadow</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) shadow, shade; faded, weakened person</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σκιά</b> του παλιού του εαυτού - shadow of his former self</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (Kaliarda) secret gay life, double life</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκιά; Doublet of ίσκιος.</div><b>σκιά ματιών</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) eye shadow</div><b>σκιάχτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a scarecrow, a straw man</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a very ugly person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκιάζω + -τρο (-tro, “instrument suffix”)</div><b>σκιοφωτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκιοφωτισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chiaroscuro</div><b>σκλάβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκλάβες, masculine σκλάβος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slave</div><b>σκλάβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκλάβοι, feminine σκλάβα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek Σκλάβος; see that entry for more.</div><b>σκλαβιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκλαβιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slavery, servitude (emotive term)</div><b>σκλαβοπάζαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκλαβοπάζαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slave market</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκλάβος + παζάρι.</div><b>σκληρά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://σκληρός">σκληρός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hard (with much force or effort)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. harshly</div><b>σκληροί δίσκοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://σκληρός">σκληρός</a> <a href="bword://δίσκος">δίσκος</a></div><b>σκληροί χιτώνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://σκληρός">σκληρός</a> <a href="bword://χιτώνας">χιτώνας</a></div><b>σκληρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκληρή, neuter σκληρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fierce, rough, cruel, harsh</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκληρός.</div><b>σκληρός δίσκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκληροί δίσκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hard disk</div><b>σκληρός χιτώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκληροί χιτώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sclera, white of the eye</div><b>σκληρότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σκληρότητα">σκληρότητα</a></div><b>σκληρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hardness, severity, cruelty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκληρότης, equivalent to σκληρός + -ότητα.</div><b>σκοινάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκοινάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σχοινάκι">σχοινάκι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * diminutive of σκοινί</div><b>σκοινένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκοινένια, neuter σκοινένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rope (consisting of or related with)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σκοινένια</b> σκάλα - rope ladder</div><b>σκοινί</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκοινιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σχοινί">σχοινί</a></div><b>σκοινοποιείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκοινοποιεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σχοινοποιείο">σχοινοποιείο</a></div><b>σκολιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκολιά, neuter σκολιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) bent; crooked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σκολιός.</div><b>σκολόπενδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκολόπενδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centipede</div><b>σκολόπεντρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκολόπεντρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκολόπενδρα">σκολόπενδρα</a> (centipede)</div><b>σκολύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκολύτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Scolytus-genus weevil.</div><b>σκονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cover with dust</div><b>σκοντάβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκοντάφτω">σκοντάφτω</a></div><b>σκοντάφτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to stumble, knock against</div><b>σκονόγαλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural σκονογάλατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) milk powder, powdered milk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκόνη + γάλα</div><b>σκοπευτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shooting range</div><b>σκοπεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to aim, take aim</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to aim, intend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκοπεύω, from σκοπέω. For the sense of &quot;to aim&quot;, semantic loan from French viser.</div><b>σκοπιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκοπιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. watchtower, sentry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. viewpoint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκοπιᾱ́, from σκοπός.</div><b>σκοπός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκοποί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. goal (aim, desired result)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. guard (occupation)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tune, melody</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκοπός.</div><b>σκορ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) score</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> English score</div><b>σκοράρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to score (to earn points in a game)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκορ (skor) + -άρω.</div><b>σκορδάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκορδάτη, neuter σκορδάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>garlicky, flavoured with or tasting of garlic</div><b>σκορδαλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκορδαλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skordalia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκόρδο + αλιάδα</div><b>σκορδόψωμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκορδόψωμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>garlic bread</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκόρδ(ο), “garlic”) + -ό- (infix) + -ψωμο (-psomo, “bread”).</div><b>σκορπιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκορπιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scorpion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Scorpio</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκορπίος</div><b>σκοτάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκοτάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>darkness, dark</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From mediaeval or Hellenistic *σκοτάδιον, diminutive of the ancient σκότος σκοτ- + -άδιον &gt; modern -άδι.</div><b>σκοτία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scotia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκοτία.</div><b>σκοτεινή ενέργεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dark energy</div><b>σκοτεινιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to darken, become dark, behave darkly</div><b>σκοτεινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκοτεινή, neuter σκοτεινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dark, mysterious, shady</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dim</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) untrustworthy, shady</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκότος.</div><b>σκοτεινότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκοτεινότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. obscurity (visual)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. darkness (quantified)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκοτεινότης, equivalent to σκοτεινός + -ότητα.</div><b>σκοτούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dizziness, giddiness, vertigo</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) trouble, worry</div><b>σκοτωμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκοτωμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. killing (act of ending someone&#x27;s life)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>σκοτωμός</b> του δεν είχε νόημα. - There was no sense to his killing.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) massacre, slaughter, carnage (killing of a considerable number)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>σκοτωμός</b> στα Καλάβρυτα το 1943 έγινε από τους ναζιστές. - The slaughter in Kalavryta in 1943 was carried out by the Nazis.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) crush, scramble, scuffle (violent crowding)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γινόταν <b>σκοτωμός</b> για το τελευταίο εισιτήριο. - There was a mad scramble for the last ticket.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκοτωμός, from Ancient Greek σκοτόω.</div><b>σκοτώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to kill, murder, slay</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκότωσα</b> μια αράχνη την άλλη μέρα. - I killed a spider the other day.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to hurt, injure severely</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σκότωσα</b> το χέρι μου. - I hurt my hand badly.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to ruin (a piece of music, performance, language, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός ο πιανίστας <b>σκότωσε</b> το κομμάτι εντελώς. - That pianist ruined the piece entirely.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) to sell (goods at a price lower than their worth)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήρα κάτι παλιά CD που έχω και τα <b>σκότωσα</b> στη λαϊκή. - I took some old CDs that I have and sold them for cheap at the market.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σκοτώνω, from Ancient Greek σκοτόω, itself from σκότος.</div><b>σκοτώστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκοτώστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) swatter (hand-held device used to swat insects)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φέρε μου τη <b>σκοτώστρα</b> να διώξω αυτές τις σφήκες. - Bring me the flyswatter so I can get rid of these wasps.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) deathtrap (extremely dangerous vehicle, usually due to poor maintenance or age)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ακόμα κυκλοφορεί μ’ αυτή τη <b>σκοτώστρα</b>. - He&#x27;s still driving around in that deathtrap.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) unsportsmanlike, dangerous player (usually of a defender)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκοτώνω + -τρα (-tra).</div><b>σκουλήκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκουλήκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. worm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. larva, silkworm</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) a sly or slimy person</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) a description of jealousy etc</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>σκουλήκι</b> της ζήλειας τον καταβροχθίζει. - The worm of jealousy gnaws at him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκουλήκι &lt; σκουλήκιν from σκουλήκιον from Ancient Greek σκωλήκιον, a diminutive of σκώληξ. Compare σκούληκας and σκώληκας.</div><b>σκουλαρίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκουλαρίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>earring</div><b>σκουμπρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκουμπριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mackerel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκομβρίς, from σκόμβρος. Cognate to English scomber.</div><b>σκουντάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to nudge, jog, push</div><b>σκουντηγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine σκουντηγμένη, neuter σκουντηγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nudged, pushed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of σκουντιέμαι, passive voice of σκουντάω, σκουντώ (“nudge, push”).</div><b>σκουντημένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine σκουντημένη, neuter σκουντημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκουντηγμένος">σκουντηγμένος</a>: nudged, pushed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of σκουντιέμαι, passive voice of σκουντάω, σκουντώ (“nudge, push”).</div><b>σκουντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκουντάω">σκουντάω</a></div><b>σκουπίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκουπίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rubbish, garbage, trash</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) sweepings</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκουπίζω + -ίδι, referring to the result of sweeping.</div><b>σκουπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sweep, vacuum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to clean, wipe, polish</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to wipe, dry (tears, sweat)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκούπ(α)) + -ίζω.</div><b>σκουπιδαριό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκουπιδαριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trash heap, garbage dump</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, σκουπίδι + -αριό.</div><b>σκουπιδιάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκουπιδιάρηδες, feminine σκουπιδιάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dustman (UK), bin man (UK), garbage collector (US), garbo (Australia)</div><b>σκουπιδιάρικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκουπιδιάρικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dust cart (UK), garbage truck (US)</div><b>σκουπιδιάρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκουπιδιάρισσες, masculine σκουπιδιάρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refuse (UK), garbage collector (US), garbo (Australia)</div><b>σκουπιδοτενεκές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκουπιδοτενεκέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in the UK) dustbin, rubbish bin</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in the US) trashcan, garbage can</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκουπίδ(ι)) + -ο- (-o-) + τενεκές.</div><b>σκουριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκουριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rust</div>
  <div class='def'>2. metallic corrosion, especially surface oxidation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκωρία with accent shifting.</div><b>σκουροκόκκινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκουροκόκκινη, neuter σκουροκόκκινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dark red</div><b>σκουρόχρωμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκουρόχρωμη, neuter σκουρόχρωμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dark-coloured (UK), dark-colored (US)</div><b>σκούπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκούπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. broom, brush</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vacuum cleaner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκοῦπᾰ, from Latin scōpa (“broom”). Doublet of σκήπτρο.</div><b>σκούρο μπλε</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) dark blue, navy blue</div><b>σκούρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκούρα, neuter σκούρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dark (generally used with shades and colours)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σκούρο</b> μπλε - dark blue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) tough; gloomy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκοῦρος, itself from Venetan or Italian scuro, from earlier oscuro, from Latin obscūrus.</div><b>σκούτερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) motorscooter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) scooter</div><b>σκούφια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beanie, knit cap (US), woolly hat (UK)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκούφια, from Italian scuffia.</div><b>σκούφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκούφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. knitted cap or hat, beanie, toque</div>
  <div class='def'>2. skullcap, yarmulke, kippah</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bathing cap</div><b>σκυθρωπός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκυθρωπή, neuter σκυθρωπό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>morose, glum, sullen, gloomy, melancholy</div><b>σκυλάδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκυλάδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory) A Greek nightclub where low quality singing and entertainment takes place.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι ξέρει αυτός από μουσική; Μόνο στα <b>σκυλάδικα</b> πάει. - What does he know about music? He only goes to the crappy nightclubs.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory) The songs, usually played on bouzoukis and concerning love, that are played in the above venues.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αγόρασε ένα CD με <b>σκυλάδικα</b>. - He bought a CD with skyladika songs.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκύλος + -άδικο. Probably so-named because the singers can be imagined to be &#x27;howling&#x27; like dogs.</div><b>σκυλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκυλιά, masculine σκύλος, feminine σκύλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dog (Canis lupus familiaris) (general term for both sexes)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο πάρκο βλέπεις πάντα πολλά <b>σκυλιά</b>. - You always see loads of dogs in the park.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) beast, brute (violent person with wild behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην αφήσεις αυτό το <b>σκυλί</b> κοντά στα παιδιά σου! - Don&#x27;t let that beast close to your kids!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) hard worker (someone who works tirelessly and with great success)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή είναι <b>σκυλί</b> στη δουλειά της. - She is a tirelessly hard worker.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκυλίον, a diminutive of from Ancient Greek σκύλος; cf. σκύλαξ.</div><b>σκυλίσια ζωή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) dog&#x27;s life</div><b>σκυλίσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκυλίσια, neuter σκυλίσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. doggy (suggesting of, or in the manner of a dog)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. canine</div><b>σκυλοβαριέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be bored to death, to be bored out of one&#x27;s mind, to be bored to tears</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκυλο- (skylo-) + βαριέμαι.</div><b>σκυλοβρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar, transitive) to give dog&#x27;s abuse to, to shower abuse on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σκυλο- (skylo-) + βρίζω.</div><b>σκυλολόι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκυλολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, literally) pack of dogs (usually stray)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάλι ήρθε αυτό το <b>σκυλολόι</b> και τριγυρίζει στο πάρκο. - That pack of dogs is back again and is wandering around the park.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) mob, rabble (group of loud and usually rowdy people)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μετά το ματς, ξεχύθηκαν στο δρόμο όλα τα <b>σκυλολόγια</b>. - After the match, the mobs rushed out into the street.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σκυλο- (skylo-, “dog”) + λέγω.</div><b>σκυλόψαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκυλόψαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dogfish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Byzantine Greek, σκυλό- + ψάρι.</div><b>σκυρόδεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural σκυροδέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) concrete</div><b>σκυτάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκυτάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. baton (in relay races)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) scytale (simple Spartan cryptographic device)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκῠτάλη</div><b>σκυταλοδρομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκυταλοδρομίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relay, relay race</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σκυτάλ(η)) + -ο- (-o-) + -δρομία.</div><b>σκωληκοειδίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) appendicitis (inflammation of the appendix)</div><b>σκωρίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκωριάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rust</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rust fungus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σκωριάζω.</div><b>σκωτσέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκωτσέζικη, neuter σκωτσέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Scottish, Scots (relating to Scotland or its people)</div><b>σκόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>powder, dust</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σκόνη</b> κάρυ - curry powder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκόνη, from Ancient Greek κόνῐς with a prothetic σ- (s-).</div><b>σκόνη ψησίματος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baking powder</div><b>σκόπιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σκόπιμη, neuter σκόπιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. purposeful, intentional, deliberate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. expedient, advisable, appropriate (suitable to effect some desired end or the purpose intended)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σκόπιμος.</div><b>σκόρδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σκόρδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>garlic (plant, bulb)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σκόρδον, from Ancient Greek σκόροδον.</div><b>σκόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moth, especially clothes moth</div><b>σκύβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lean</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to bend over, bend down</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stoop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σκύφτω, with metaplasm of -βω (-vo) replacing -φτω by analogy of τρίβω and its past stem τριψ- (trips-), from Ancient Greek κύπτω with an epenthetic σ- (s-).</div><b>σκύλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σκύλες, masculine σκύλος, neuter σκυλί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bitch (female dog, or Canis lupus familiaris)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>σκύλα</b> γέννησε κουτάβια. - The bitch gave birth to puppies.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively, offensive) bitch, cow, hellcat (despicable or disagreeable woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η <b>σκύλα</b> μου έχει καταστρέψει τη ζωή. - This bitch has destroyed my life.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σκύλαξ.</div><b>σκύλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκύλοι, feminine σκύλα, neuter σκυλί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dog (Canis lupus familiaris) (general term for both sexes or more specifically, a male)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα γυρίσω σε λίγο, πρέπει να πάω βόλτα τον <b>σκύλο</b> μου. - I&#x27;ll be back in a while, I have to go walk my dog.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) beast, brute (violent person with wild behaviour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην αφήσεις αυτόν τον <b>σκύλο</b> κοντά στα παιδιά σου! - Don&#x27;t let that beast get close to your kids!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) hard worker (someone who works tirelessly and with great success)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός είναι <b>σκύλος</b> στη δουλειά του. - He is a tirelessly hard worker.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. dogfish (any of various small sharks)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έπιασα έναν <b>σκύλο</b> στο δίχτυ μου. - I caught a dogfish in my net.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek and Koine Greek σκύλος, from a root σκυλ- (skul-) also found in Ancient Greek σκύλαξ + -ος (-os).</div><b>σκύλος που γαβγίζει δεν δαγκώνει</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barking dogs seldom bite (people who make threats rarely carry them out)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην τον φοβάσαι, δεν θα κάνει τίποτα· <b>σκύλος που γαβγίζει δεν δαγκώνει</b>! - Don&#x27;t be afraid of him, he won&#x27;t do anything; barking dogs seldom bite!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;A dog that barks doesn&#x27;t bite&quot;.</div><b>σκώρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σκώροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκόρος">σκόρος</a></div><b>σλαβικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σλαβική, neuter σλαβικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Slavic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Σλάβ(ος)) + -ικός.</div><b>σλιπολούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σλιπολούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) swimming trunks</div><b>σλοβάκικα</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σλοβακικά">σλοβακικά</a></div><b>σλοβάκικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σλοβακικός">σλοβακικός</a></div><b>σλοβένικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σλοβενικά">σλοβενικά</a></div><b>σλοβένικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σλοβενικός">σλοβενικός</a></div><b>σλοβακικά</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Slovak (the language of Slovakia and its people)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σλοβακικός">σλοβακικός</a></div><b>σλοβακικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σλοβακική, neuter σλοβακικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Slovakian (relating to Slovakia or its people or language)</div><b>σλοβενικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Slovene, the language of Slovenia.</div><b>σλοβενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σλοβενική, neuter σλοβενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Slovenian, Slovene</div><b>σλόγκαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) slogan (a distinctive phrase of a person or group of people, such as a movement or political party)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) slogan (a catchphrase associated with a product or service being advertised)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English slogan.</div><b>σμάλτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σμάλτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. enamel (fused ceramic coating)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tooth enamel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian smalto, ultimately from Frankish *smalt.</div><b>σμέουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σμέουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) raspberry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Possibly from μούρο</div><b>σμέρνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σμέρνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moray eel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek σμύραινα.</div><b>σμήναρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σμήναρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-5; group captain in the RAF</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-5; colonel in the USAF</div><b>σμήνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σμήνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. group of animals or things:; bevy, flock (birds)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. group of animals or things:; swarm (bees)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. group of animals or things:; cluster (stars)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. group of animals or things:; squadron, flight (aircraft)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σμῆνος / σμᾶνος.</div><b>σμίλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σμίλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chisel</div><b>σμαράγδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σμαράγδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emerald</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σμαράγδι(ν)), from Koine Greek σμαράγδιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek σμάραγδος, of Semitic origin.</div><b>σμεουριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σμεουριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) raspberry plant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σμέουρο + -ιά.</div><b>σμηναγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σμηναγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-2; flight lieutenant in the RAF</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-2; captain in the USAF</div><b>σμρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://σημαιοφόρος">σημαιοφόρος</a></div><b>σμυρναλκυόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σμυρναλκυόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>white-throated kingfisher, Halcyon smyrnensis</div><b>σμχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://σμήναρχος">σμήναρχος</a></div><b>σμόκιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) dinner jacket (UK), tuxedo (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English smoking (“smoking jacket”).</div><b>σνακ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) a snack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English snack.</div><b>σοβάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σοβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plaster (wall coating)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish صیوا.</div><b>σοβαρολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to be serious, be for real (talk seriously and not be joking)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα μας αφήσεις εδώ μόνους μας; <b>Σοβαρολογείς</b> τώρα; - You&#x27;re going to leave us here on our own? Are you serious?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σοβαρός + λέγω. First attested 1894.</div><b>σοβαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σοβαρή, neuter σοβαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reliable, serious, humourless, grave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek σοβαρός and semantic loan from Italian serioso.</div><b>σοβαρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seriousness, gravity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. severity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σοβαρότητα, accusative singular of σοβαρότης with semantic adaptation to the modern meaning of σοβαρός and semantic loan from English seriousness. By surface analysis, σοβαρ(ός)) + -ότητα.</div><b>σοβιέτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) soviet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Russian совет (sovet)</div><b>σοβινισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σοβινισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jingoism, chauvinism, exaggerated nationalism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French chauvinisme.</div><b>σοβινιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σοβινιστική, neuter σοβινιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jingoistic, chauvinistic</div><b>σοβχόζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sovkhoz</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Russian совхо́з.</div><b>σοδειά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σοδειές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harvest (crop gathered in and accumulated)</div><b>σοδομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) sodomy</div><b>σοδομίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σοδομίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sodomite</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sod (slang)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Σόδομα; cf. sodomite.</div><b>σοδομισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) sodomy</div><b>σοκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) shock (a sudden or violent mental or emotional disturbance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) shock (something so surprising that it is stunning)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) shock (a medical emergency characterized by the inability of the circulatory system to supply enough oxygen to meet tissue requirements)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French choc.</div><b>σοκάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σοκάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>back street, back alley, back alleyway, alley (small and narrow street in a neighbourhood)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα παιδιά παίζουν κρυφτό στα <b>σοκάκια</b> της γειτονιάς. - The children play hide and seek in the back alleys of the neighbourhood.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish سوقاق (sokak), from Arabic زُقَاق.</div><b>σοκάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be shocked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive form of σοκάρω.</div><b>σοκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shock someone emotionally (with intense words and/or actions)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σοκ (sok) + -άρω.</div><b>σοκολάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σοκολάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chocolate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cocoa (hot drink)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French chocolat.</div><b>σοκολατάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σοκολατάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fancy chocolates, confectionery chocolate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. box of chocolates</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σοκολατ (sokolat, “chocolate”) + -άκι</div><b>σοκολατί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chocolate colour</div><b>σολ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) sol</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) G (fifth note or dominant in the C major scale)</div><b>σολάνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σολάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>solanum, nightshade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Solanum.</div><b>σολοικισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>write or speak with mistakes in syntax, solecism</div>
  <div class='ex'>for mistakes in grammar, see βαρβαρισμός m (varvarismós, “barbarism -linguistics-”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek σολοικισμός.</div><b>σολομός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>salmon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Latin salmō.</div><b>σομαλικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Somali (the language spoken in Somalia)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σομαλικός">σομαλικός</a></div><b>σομαλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σομαλική, neuter σομαλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Somali (relating to Somalia or its people)</div><b>σονέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σονέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sonnet</div><b>σορμπέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sorbet (dessert of frozen sweetened water with flavouring)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Arabic شَرِبَder.
Arabic شَرْبَةbor.
Classical Persian شَرْبَتbor.
Ottoman Turkish شربتbor.
Italian sorbettobor.
Middle French sorbet
French sorbetbor.
Greek σορμπέ
Borrowed from French sorbet, from Middle French sorbet, borrowed from Italian sorbetto, borrowed from Ottoman Turkish شربت, borrowed from Classical Persian شَرْبَت, borrowed from Arabic شَرْبَة, from شَرِبَ.</div><b>σορόπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σορόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σιρόπι">σιρόπι</a></div><b>σορός</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σοροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. corpse, cadaver, dead body</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coffin</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σορός, from Proto-Indo-European *tworHos, from *twerH- (“to enclose”).</div><b>σος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sauce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French sauce.</div><b>σοσιαλίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σοσιαλίστριες, masculine σοσιαλιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socialist</div><b>σοσιαλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σοσιαλισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socialism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French socialisme. First attested 1852.</div><b>σοσιαλιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σοσιαλιστές, feminine σοσιαλίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socialist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French socialiste. First attested 1852.</div><b>σοσιαλιστικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socialistically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σοσιαλιστικός">σοσιαλιστικός</a></div><b>σοσιαλιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σοσιαλιστική, neuter σοσιαλιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socialist, socialistic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σοσιαλιστής m.</div><b>σοσιαλιστικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>socialistically</div><b>σου</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indirect object, personal) you</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σου</b> είπε την αλήθεια. - He told you the truth.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (possessive) your</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αδερφός <b>σου</b> ήρθε. - Your brother came.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σοῦ, σύ.</div><b>σουίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σουίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. suite (group of connected rooms, usually separable from other rooms by means of access)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έκλεισα μια <b>σουίτα</b> στο ξενοδοχείο «Χίλτον». - I booked a suite at the Hilton Hotel.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. suite (both musical senses)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>σουίτα</b> «Σεχραζάντ» του Ρίμσκυ-Κόρσακοφ - Rimsky-Korsakov&#x27;s “Scheherazade” suite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French suite.</div><b>σουαρέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) soiree</div><b>σουαχίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Swahili</div><b>σουβέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) coaster, drinks mat</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σουβέρ</b> μπίρας - beer mat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French sous-verre.</div><b>σουβενίρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) souvenir</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French souvenir.</div><b>σουβλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σουβλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. awl, bradawl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bodkin</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncommon) skewer</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural) twinge (sharp, darting pain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σουβλί, σουβλίον, σουβλίν, σούβλα, σοῦβλα, σούβλη, σούγλη, from Latin sūbula (“awl”).</div><b>σουβλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to skewer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to impale</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to have a sudden, sharp, local pain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σουβλίζω. By surface analysis, σούβλ(α)) + -ίζω.</div><b>σουβλατζίδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>souvlaki shop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σουβλάκι + -τζίδικο.</div><b>σουγιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σουγιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. penknife, pocketknife (UK)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jack-knife, pocketknife (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unknown</div><b>σουηδικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Swedish, the language of Sweden</div><b>σουηδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σουηδική, neuter σουηδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Swedish (relating to the country of Sweden, its people or its language)</div><b>σουλτάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σουλτάνες, masculine σουλτάνος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sultana (wife or mother of a sultan)</div><b>σουλτάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σουλτάνοι, feminine σουλτάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sultan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σουλτᾶνος, from Ottoman Turkish سلطان, from Arabic سُلْطَان.</div><b>σουλφίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σουλφίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inorganic sulfide, mineral sulfide</div>
  <div class='def'>2. organic sulfide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English sulphide.</div><b>σουμάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σουμάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sumac (spice)</div><b>σουξέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) success (the fact of getting or achieving respect or fame)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) hit (something very successful, such as a song, film, or video game, that receives widespread recognition and acclaim)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unadapted borrowing from French succès.</div><b>σουπερμάρκετ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) supermarket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English supermarket.</div><b>σουπιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σουπιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cuttlefish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σηπία.</div><b>σουπιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σουπιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tureen (serving dish for soup or stew)</div><b>σουρεαλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) surrealism (artistic movement and aesthetic philosophy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French surréalisme (“surrealism”).</div><b>σουρεαλιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σουρεαλιστική, neuter σουρεαλιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>surreal, surrealistic</div><b>σουρικάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σουρικάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meerkat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Suricata.</div><b>σουριναμέζικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σουριναμέζικη, neuter σουριναμέζικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Surinamese (relating to Paraguay or its people)</div><b>σουρουπώνει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the sun sets</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Aromanian soare apune (“sun sets”).</div><b>σουρούπωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σουρουπώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dusk, sunset</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σουρουπώνει + -μα (-ma).</div><b>σουρρεαλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σουρεαλισμός">σουρεαλισμός</a></div><b>σουρωτήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σουρωτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strainer, sieve, colander</div><b>σουρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to strain, drain, filter</div><b>σουσάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σουσάμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sesame (seed)</div><b>σουσαμιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σουσαμιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sesame (plant)</div><b>σουσουράδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σουσουράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>white wagtail (Motacilla alba)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek σουσουράδα.</div><b>σουτ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) shh, hush, shush (requesting silence)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σουτ</b>! Μη μιλάς! - Shush! Don&#x27;t talk!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) shot (act of launching a ball or similar object toward a goal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παίκτης <b>ρίχνει σουτ</b> στο τέρμα. - The player shoots at the goal.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English shoot.</div><b>σουτάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be shot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fired, expelled</div><b>σουτάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to shoot, strike (fire a ball at target)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σούταρε</b> από απόσταση και δεν έβαλε γκολ. - He shot from a distance and didn&#x27;t get a goal.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive) to boot out, kick out (expel someone or something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την <b>σουτάραν</b> από την δουλειά μετά απ’ αυτό που έκανε. - They booted her out of the job after what she did.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σουτ (sout, “shoot”) + -άρω.</div><b>σουτζουκάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σουτζουκάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>meatball</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: κεφτές (keftés)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish صوجوق (sucuk) + -άκι or σουτζούκι + -άκι.</div><b>σουτζούκ λουκούμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) A sweet jelly prepared from wine must, which has been enriched with walnuts and formed into a roll.</div><b>σουτζούκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σουτζούκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. soutzouki; A dry, low fat, spicy sausage.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. soutzouki; A jelly prepared from wine must, which has been enriched with walnuts and formed into a roll.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish صوجوق (sucuk).</div><b>σουτιέν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) brassiere, bra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French soutien.</div><b>σοφά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σοφός">σοφός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wisely</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://σοφάς">σοφάς</a>: of the sofa</div><b>σοφάς</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative feminine plural of <a href="bword://σοφός">σοφός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form of Katharevousa, in the ancient fashion.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σοφάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>low sofa, usually a built construction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish صفه (sofa) + ending -ς (-s), from Arabic صُفَّة.</div><b>σοφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σοφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wisdom</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural, ironic) denoting a speaker&#x27;s statements as anything but smart, important, etc.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σοφῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>σοφίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σοφίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>attic (space, often unfinished and with sloped walls, directly below the roof)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French soffite (“soffit”), from Italian soffitto, from Vulgar Latin *suffīctus, perfect passive participle of Latin suffīgō (“to fasten or fix onto”).</div><b>σοφός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σοφή, neuter σοφό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σοφός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σοφοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a wise man, a sage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σοφός.</div><b>σούβλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σούβλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spit, skewer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σούβλα, borrowed from Latin sūbula.</div><b>σούδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gutter (open domestic drain for waste water)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σούδα.</div><b>σούπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σούπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French soupe.</div><b>σούπερ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) super, wonderful, awesome</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin super.</div><b>σούπρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σούπρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supra (traditional Georgian feast)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Georgian სუფრა (supra).</div><b>σούρβο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σούρβα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sorb (fruit of Sorbus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σοῦρβον, from Latin sorbum and sorbus.</div><b>σούρνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) to be dragged</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) to be exhausted, to crawl</div><b>σούρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σέρνω">σέρνω</a></div><b>σούρουπα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dialectal) at sunset, at dusk, by nightfall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σουρουπώνει + -α (-a).</div><b>σούρουπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σούρουπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dusk, twilight, gloaming</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from σουρουπώνει.</div><b>σούστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σούστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spring (coiled evice)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Greek dance (dance involving leaping/bouncing movements)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. two-wheeled horse-drawn cart</div><b>σπάγγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπάγγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. string, line, packthread</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cord, thin rope</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) skinflint, mean person</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) curmudgeon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σπάγγος, from Italian spago. The figurative &#x27;skinflint&#x27; sense is derivable from σπαγγ(οραμμένος)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>σπάγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπάγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπάγγος">σπάγγος</a></div><b>σπάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to break</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to break (into two or more pieces that cannot easily be reassembled)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπῶ / σπάω + -άζω, probably ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *(s)peh₂- (“to draw, set in motion”). Also see σπάω.</div><b>σπάνια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rarely, seldom, infrequently</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://σπάνιος">σπάνιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σπάνιος">σπάνιος</a></div><b>σπάνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπάνια, neuter σπάνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rare, exceptional, infrequent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπάνιος.</div><b>σπάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπάζω">σπάζω</a></div><b>σπάργανα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. swaddling clothes</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) infancy</div><b>σπάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπάροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. annular seabream (Diplodus annularis)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, humorous) lazybones</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σπάρος. The figurative sense is probably from this.</div><b>σπάρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπάρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spartium, gorse, furze, whin, (Ulex)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπάρτος, variant of σπάρτον.</div><b>σπάσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπασίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. breaking, breakage (the act by which something is broken)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. break, breakage, fracture (an instance of being broken)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) plate smashing</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (familiar) pain (an annoyance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the σπασ- stem of σπάζω + -ιμο (-imo).</div><b>σπάτουλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπάτουλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spatula (flat, blunt implement used for scraping and spreading in kitchen)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spatula (thin, flat implement used in laboratory)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. palette knife (used by artists)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Late Latin spatula, probably through a Venetan intermediate spatoła.</div><b>σπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) alternative form of <a href="bword://σπάζω">σπάζω</a> (active voice only); to irritate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου τη <b>σπάει</b> πολύ αυτός ο καθηγητής. - This professor really gets on my nerves.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) alternative form of <a href="bword://σπάζω">σπάζω</a> (active voice only); to break</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) alternative form of <a href="bword://σπάζω">σπάζω</a> (active voice only); to break (into two or more pieces that cannot easily be reassembled)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (slang) alternative form of <a href="bword://σπάζω">σπάζω</a> (active voice only); to split, to leave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σπῶ / σπάω, from Ancient Greek σπάω, of Proto-Indo-European origin. Also see σπάζω.</div><b>σπάω στο ξύλο</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to beat up, beat to a pulp, beat the crap out of</div><b>σπάω τον πάγο</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://σπάω">σπάω</a>, <a href="bword://τον">τον</a> (ton), πάγο.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σπάσε τον πάγο</b> και φτιάξε παγάκια! - Break the ice and make some ice cubes!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) to break the ice (to start to get to know people to avoid social awkwardness and formality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι δύσκολο για μερικούς να <b>σπάσουν τον πάγο</b> σε νέα δουλειά. - It is difficult for some to break the ice in a new job.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French briser la glace.</div><b>σπέρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπέρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seed, the seed of plants.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. human or animal seed, semen, sperm</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) the origin, the source, the germ, a primeval element</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) offspring, descendants, race, issue.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σπέρμα.</div><b>σπέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to disperse seeds, sow, seed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σπέρνω, from Ancient Greek σπείρω with metaplasm in the pattern of φέρω &gt; φέρνω, or σύρω &gt; σέρνω.</div><b>σπίθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπίθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spark, sparkle (short or small burst of electricity or fire)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια <b>σπίθα</b> μπορεί να κάψει ολόκληρο το δάσος. - A spark can burn the entire forest.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) spark, glimmer (small amount of something that has the potential to become something greater)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>σπίθα</b> του πολέμου - the spark of war</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) flame (burning zeal, passion, imagination, excitement, or anger)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>σπίθα</b> του πάθους στον γάμο τους - the flame of passion in their marriage</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) bright spark (person who is intelligent, clever or quick-witted)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σκέτη <b>σπίθα</b> αυτός ο νεαρός! - A real bright spark, that kid!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σπίθα, from Ancient Greek σπιθίζω.</div><b>σπίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>finch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπίνος, σπίγγος.</div><b>σπίρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπίρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. match (stick)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) very clever</div>
  <div class='ex'>expressions: σπίρτο μοναχό! (spírto monachó!, “truly clever! absolute genius!”)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) spirit, surgical spirit as of liquor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian spirito with silencing of the unaccented [i], from Latin spiritus.</div><b>σπίτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπίτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (adverbial, also) house, home (structure built or serving as an abode of human beings)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα πάμε στο <b>σπίτι</b> του φίλου μου. - We will go to my friend&#x27;s house.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) household, house (all the persons who live in a given house)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλο το <b>σπίτι</b> μετακόμισε τον χειμώνα σε πιο ζεστά μέρη. - The entire household moved in winter to warmer climes.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (euphemistic, figuratively) brothel (house used by prostitutes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σπίτιν, from earlier or Koine Greek ὁσπίτιον, from Latin hospitium (“lodgings”). Compare Albanian shtëpi and Sicilian spizziu. Displaced earlier Ancient Greek οἶκος.</div><b>σπίτι μου, σπιτάκι μου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>home sweet home, there&#x27;s no place like home (expressing comfort or joy in one&#x27;s home)</div><b>σπίτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπιτώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>housing, putting up (the act of being provided with a house)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι πρόσφυγες απαιτούν <b>σπίτωμα</b>. - The refugees are demanding housing.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σπιτώνω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>σπαγγέτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) spaghetti (pasta)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian spaghetti.</div><b>σπαγκέτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπαγγέτι">σπαγγέτι</a></div><b>σπαθί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπαθιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sword</div>
  <div class='def'>2. club (suit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σπαθίν, from Ancient Greek σπαθίον, a diminutive of σπάθη.</div><b>σπανάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπανάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spinach (the vegetable Spinacia oleracea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σπινάκιον, σπανάκιν, σπινάκι, ultimately from Classical Persian اسپناخ see more there.</div><b>σπανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to be rare, to be scarce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σπανίζω. By surface analysis, σπάνιος + -ίζω.</div><b>σπανίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rarely, seldom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σπᾰνῐ́ως. By surface analysis, σπάνι(ος)) + -ως (-os)</div><b>σπανακόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπανακόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spanakotiropita, spinach and cheese pie</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spanakopita, spinach pie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σπανάκι + πίτα</div><b>σπανιόλικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ισπανικά">ισπανικά</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σπανιόλικος">σπανιόλικος</a></div><b>σπανιόλικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπανιόλικη, neuter σπανιόλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ισπανικός">ισπανικός</a></div><b>σπανιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπανιότητα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rarity, rareness (quality)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rarity</div>
  <div class='ex'>Eίναι <b>σπανιότητα</b> στα μικρότερα νησιά. - They are a rarity on the smaller islands.</div><b>σπανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπανή, neuter σπανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hairless</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σπανός, from the first component of Koine Greek σπανοπώγων for the second component), further from σπάνιος. Alternatively, directly from σπάνιος, with the same formation pattern seen in ὀρθός &lt; ὄρθιος.
Despite similar phonetics and semantics, not related to σπαρνός or Latvian spārns (“wing”). However, compare Romanian spân (“hairless”), Aromanian spãn, Sicilian spanu, which may derive from the same Ancient Greek root, possibly via a Vulgar Latin intermediate.</div><b>σπαράγγι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπαράγγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>asparagus (wild plant or domesticated vegetable plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἀσπάραγος.</div><b>σπαράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to tear to pieces, to rend, to lacerate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to convulse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to break (to be crushed, or overwhelmed with sorrow or grief)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to break, to rend (:heart, soul)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σπαράζω, from Ancient Greek σπαράσσω.</div><b>σπαρτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπαρτή, neuter άσπαρτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seeded, sown (of land or seed)</div><b>σπασίκλας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπασίκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nerd</div><b>σπασμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine σπασμένη, neuter σπασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. broken, ruptured, fractured</div>
  <div class='def'>2. broken (by age and bad health)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) pissed off, annoyed, fed up</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of speech) spoken with foreign accent</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μιλούσε <b>σπασμένα</b> ελληνικά.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of σπάζομαι, passive voice of σπάζω (“Ι break”). Also perfect passive participle of σπάω, an active parallel form with no passive forms.</div><b>σπασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spasm, convulsion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπασμός, related to the verb σπάω.</div><b>σπαστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπαστή, neuter σπαστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. broken, cracked</div>
  <div class='def'>2. split, folding, hinged, pivoted</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σπαστό</b> ποδήλατο - folding bicycle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. wavy</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σπαστά</b> μαλλιά - wavy hair</div><b>σπατάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπατάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>waste, wastefulness, extravagance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σπατάλη.</div><b>σπαταλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to waste, squander (money)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πως θα συντηρήσω την περιουσία μου αν <b>σπαταλώ</b> τον χρόνο μου σε ανοησίες - How would I maintain my wealth if I waste my time with nonsense</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπᾰτᾰλᾰ́ω.</div><b>σπαταλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπαταλάω">σπαταλάω</a></div><b>σπείρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coil, ring, scroll</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spiral (on a snail&#x27;s shell)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. thread, spiral groove (on a screw or bolt)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) band, cabal, gang</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπεῖρα, from Proto-Indo-European *sper- (“to twist, turn”).</div><b>σπείρωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπειρώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thread, spiral groove (on screw, nut or bolt)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σπείρα + -ωμα (-oma)</div><b>σπειροειδές κλιμακοστάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπειροειδή κλιμακοστάσιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spiral staircase</div><b>σπειροειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπειροειδής, neuter σπειροειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spiral, coiled (in that form)</div><b>σπερματοζωάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπερματοζωάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spermatozoon, a single sperm cell.</div><b>σπεύδω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to hasten, to hurry, to rush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σπεύδω.</div><b>σπηλαιογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπηλαιογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) cave painting (cave painting and the technique for its production)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σπήλαιο + γράφω + -ία.</div><b>σπηλαιολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) speleology</div><b>σπηλαιολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σπηλαιολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. speleologist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. caver, potholer</div><b>σπηλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπήλαιον.</div><b>σπιθίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sparkle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of wines) to be sparkling</div><b>σπιλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to tarnish, besmirch (reputation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to shame</div><b>σπιούνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπιούνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) snitch, mole, spy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian spione.</div><b>σπιρτόκουτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπιρτόκουτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>matchbox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σπίρτο + κουτί.</div><b>σπιτήσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπιτήσια, neuter σπιτήσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπιτικός">σπιτικός</a>: house, home, homely (of or concerning the home / house)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σπίτι + -ήσιος.</div><b>σπιτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπιτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>house, household (all the persons who live in a given house)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλο το <b>σπιτικό</b> μετακόμισε τον χειμώνα σε πιο ζεστά μέρη. - The entire household moved in winter to warmer climes.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://σπιτικός">σπιτικός</a></div><b>σπιτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπιτική, neuter σπιτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. house, home, homely (of or concerning the home / house)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σπιτική</b> ατμόσφαιρα - homely atmosphere</div>
  <div class='def'>2. homemade (made in the home)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σπιτικό</b> φαγητό - homemade food</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σπίτι + -ικός.</div><b>σπιτοσπουργίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπιτοσπουργίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Passer domesticus, house sparrow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σπίτι + σπουργίτης.</div><b>σπιτοσπουργίτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπιτοσπουργίτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπιτοσπουργίτης">σπιτοσπουργίτης</a></div><b>σπιτόγατος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπιτόγατοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>homebody (person who doesn&#x27;t like leaving the home, who infrequently goes out)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Παύλος είναι <b>σπιτόγατος</b> και δεν πάει συχνά σε πάρτυ. - Paul is a homebird and doesn&#x27;t often go to parties.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σπίτι + -ό- + γάτος.</div><b>σπιτώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be put up, be housed</div><b>σπιτώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, transitive) to house, take in, shelter (provide with a home)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σπίτωσαν</b> τους σύριους σε στρατόπεδο προσφύγων. - They housed the Syrians in a refugee camp.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, transitive) to shack up, put up (to provide with a home, especially of an unmarried couple)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει <b>σπιτώσει</b> έναν αλλοδαπό. - She&#x27;s shacked up with a foreigner.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σπίτι + -ώνω.</div><b>σπλάγχνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπλάγχνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπλάχνο">σπλάχνο</a></div><b>σπλάχνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπλάχνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in plural) guts, entrails</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) One&#x27;s child</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπλάγχνον.</div><b>σπλαχνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπλαχνική, neuter σπλαχνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. compassionate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. visceral</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek σπλαγχνικός in the &#x27;compassionate&#x27; sense, learned borrowing from Koine Greek (same etymon) in the anatomical sense. By surface analysis, σπλάχνο + -ικός.</div><b>σπογγαλιεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) sea sponge aquaculture, spongiculture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested in 1886. From Greek σπόγγος + ἁλιεία</div><b>σπονδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπονδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>libation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπένδω.</div><b>σπονδείος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπονδείοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spondee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπονδεῖος.</div><b>σπονδυλικές στήλες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of σπονδυλική <a href="bword://στήλη">στήλη</a></div><b>σπονδυλική στήλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπονδυλικές στήλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spine, spinal column</div><b>σπονδυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπονδυλική, neuter σπονδυλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vertebral (relating to vertebras)</div><b>σπονδυλωτά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σπονδυλωτός">σπονδυλωτός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Vertebrata, the vertebrates.</div><b>σπονδυλωτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπονδυλωτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vertebrate (animal with vertebras)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://σπονδυλωτός">σπονδυλωτός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://σπονδυλωτός">σπονδυλωτός</a></div><b>σπονδυλωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπονδυλωτή, neuter σπονδυλωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vertebrate (relating to animals with vertebras)</div><b>σπορ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) sport</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) sports</div>
  <div class='ex'>σπορ αυτοκίνητο - sports car</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of French sport, itself borrowed from English sport.</div><b>σπορτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπορ">σπορ</a> (spor)</div><b>σπουδάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to study a subject at university</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σπουδάζω.</div><b>σπουδάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπουδάστριες, masculine σπουδαστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. schoolgirl, pupil, student (female; secondary education level)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. person systematically engaged in a field of study</div><b>σπουδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπουδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. study</div>
  <div class='def'>2. study, endgame study</div>
  <div class='def'>3. eagerness, haste</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural) education, studies</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σπουδή.</div><b>σπουδαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σπουδαία, neuter σπουδαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>great, important, remarkable, great (also used ironically)</div>
  <div class='ex'>αυτό είναι πολύ <b>σπουδαίο</b> νέο! - this is very important news!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek σπουδαῖος.</div><b>σπουδαστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπουδαστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>study room</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the σπουδασ- stem of σπουδάζω + -τήριο, a loose calque of German Studierzimmer.</div><b>σπουδαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπουδαστές, feminine σπουδάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. schoolboy, pupil, student (male; secondary education level)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. person systematically engaged in a field of study</div><b>σπουργίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπουργίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπουργίτι">σπουργίτι</a></div><b>σπουργίτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπουργίτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sparrow</div><b>σπούδασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπουδάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>education</div><b>σπριντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sprint (includes: running, cycling, canoeing, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English sprint.</div><b>σπρώχνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to push</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to drive</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to push</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σπρώχνω, from Ancient Greek προωθῶ / προωθέω by liaison with pronouns, or from an unattested ancient *εἰσπροωθῶ. See προωθώ for more.</div><b>σπυράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπυράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pimple,)</div><b>σπυρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σπυριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grain (as of rice)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pimple</div>
  <div class='def'>3. boil</div><b>σπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπάω">σπάω</a></div><b>σπόνδυλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπόνδυλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vertebra</div><b>σπόντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σπόντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cushion on a snooker table</div><b>σπόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σπόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seed (of a plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sperm, seed</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) seed (of an idea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σπόρος.</div><b>σσ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of σελίδες (pages)</div><b>στ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contraction of στο (sto, “in the, at the, to the”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αύριο, θα πάμε <b>στ’</b> άλλο εστιατόριο. - Tomorrow we’ll go to the other restaurant.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contraction of στα (sta, “in the, at the, to the”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι θα πεις <b>στ’</b> άλλα αγόρια; - What will you say to the other boys?</div><b>στ&#x27; αρχίδια μου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) I don&#x27;t give a fuck, I don&#x27;t give a shit, I don&#x27;t give a damn (literally: at my balls)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Στ’ αρχίδια μου</b> άμα θα αργήσεις εσύ ή όχι! - I don’t give a fuck if you’re late or not!</div><b>στ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ΣΤ΄">ΣΤ΄</a></div><b>στάβλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στάβλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stable (a building, wing or dependency set apart and adapted for lodging and feeding ungulates)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) pigsty (a dirty or very untidy place)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek στάβλος m, from στάβλον n, from Latin stabulum.</div><b>στάδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stadium, sports venue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Ancient unit of length (184.9 meters)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cable (the unit of length = 608 feet = 185 m = ¹⁄₁₀ nautical mile)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. stage (a segment of time, one of a series representing a stage of development)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) real or virtual space for an activity (stage, theatre)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στάδιον.</div><b>στάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) to be stained by dripping</div><b>στάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to drip, dribble, trickle (fall one drop at a time or slowly steadily)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έσταξε</b> λίγο κονιάκ στο τσάι της. - She dripped a little cognac into her tea.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to leak, drip, (of nose, eyes etc) be runny</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να καλέσουμε τον υδραυλικό, <b>στάζει</b> ο νεροχύτης. - We&#x27;ll have to call the plumber, the sink is leaking.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, idiomatic) to cross someone&#x27;s palm, cough up (pay for)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Στάξε</b> μου πριν σου πω πληροφορίες! - Cough up before I tell you any information!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στάζω.</div><b>στάθμευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταθμεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>parking</div><b>στάθμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) level</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>στάθμη</b> της θάλασσας - the level of the sea</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) level (of education, sophistication etc)</div><b>στάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. drop (small quantity of liquid)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) drop (small quantity of anything)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>στάλα στάλα</b> - drop by drop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from σταλ(άζω)) + -α (-a).</div><b>στάλπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στάρπη">στάρπη</a></div><b>στάμνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στάμνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pitcher, urn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From στάμνί + -α (-a, an augmentative suffix).</div><b>στάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pen (fenced area for animals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek στάνη, from Proto-Slavic *stanъ (“lodging”). Compare Koine Greek βουστάνη.</div><b>στάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σιτάρι">σιτάρι</a></div><b>στάρπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) curd, curds</div><b>στάσεις λεωφορείων</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://στάση">στάση</a> λεωφορείου</div><b>στάση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. position, attitude, stance (physical or mental)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stop, bus stop</div>
  <div class='def'>3. frame</div>
  <div class='def'>4. stop (coming to a halt)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. mutiny, rebellion</div>
  <div class='def'>6. stasis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στάσις.</div><b>στάση λεωφορείου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στάσεις λεωφορείων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bus stop</div><b>στάτους κβο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) status quo</div><b>στάχτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στάχτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ash, ashes (of a fire)</div><b>στάχυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στάχυα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ear, spike (fruiting body of a grain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek στάχυον, from Ancient Greek στάχυς.</div><b>στέγαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στεγάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>housing</div><b>στέγη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στέγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. roof (of house, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) house</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στέγη.</div><b>στέκει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular present of <a href="bword://στέκω">στέκω</a>:; he/she/it stands</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Στέκει</b> ακίνητος. - He stands motionless.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. third-person singular present of <a href="bword://στέκω">στέκω</a>:; is true, is correct</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η θεωρία δεν <b>στέκει</b>. - This theory is not correct (does not make sense).</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (impersonal) third-person singular present of <a href="bword://στέκω">στέκω</a>:; not acceptable</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην εποχή μας δε <b>στέκει</b> να παντρεύεσαι σε ηλικία 14 ετών. - In our age, it is not correct to get married, age 14.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * See etymology of στέκω.</div><b>στέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>haunt, stamping ground, hangout (place where one is regularly found)</div><b>στέκομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stand, stand up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to stay, stop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From στέκω (“I stand”).</div><b>στέκω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, intransitive, no past) to stand up, stand still</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Στέκω</b> ακίνητος. - I stand motionless.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, no past, third person) to be true, be correct</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι θεωρίες σου δεν <b>στέκουν</b>. - Your theories are not correct (do not make sense).</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (impersonal, intransitive, no past) to be unacceptable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek στέκω, from Hellenistic Koine Greek στήκω &lt; Ancient Greek ἕστηκα, perfect of verb ῐ̔́στημῐ.</div><b>στέλεχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στελέχη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. official</div>
  <div class='def'>2. counterfoil (of cheque etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. stalk, stem</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στέλεχος.</div><b>στέλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>* see: -<a href="bword://στέλλω">στέλλω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the combining form -στέλλω and stems στελ-, σταλ-, στολ-, the Ancient Greek στέλλω and the Modern στέλνω.</div><b>στέλνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to send</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the mediaeval Byzantine Greek στέλνω, from Ancient Greek στέλλω with metaplasm to -νω (-no) in the pattern for φέρω &gt; φέρνω. Furthermore, from Proto-Hellenic *stéľľō, from Proto-Indo-European *stel- (“to set”).</div><b>στέμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στέμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crown (royal, imperial or princely headdress)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. corona (of the sun)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στέμμα.</div><b>στέπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στέπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steppe</div><b>στέρεο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) stereo (audio system)</div><b>στέρεος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στέρεη, neuter στέρεο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στερεός">στερεός</a></div><b>στέρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στερήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deprivation, privation, lack</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η παιδική μου ηλικία ήταν γεμάτη <b>στερήσεις</b>. - My childhood was full of deprivations. (much deprived)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek στέρησις.</div><b>στέριωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στερέωμα">στερέωμα</a></div><b>στέρνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στέρνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sternum, breastbone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chest</div><b>στέφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be crowned</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be wreathed on one&#x27;s head</div><b>στέφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to crown (a monarch, a beauty pageant or competition winner)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in marriage ceremony) synonym of <a href="bword://στεφανώνω">στεφανώνω</a>; to place crown on the heads of the bride and bridegroom</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of an activity) synonym of <a href="bword://στεφανώνω">στεφανώνω</a>; to successfully conclude</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η προσπάθειά μου <b>στέφθηκε</b> με επιτυχία. - My effort was crowned with success.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στέφω, from Proto-Hellenic *stékʷʰō, from Proto-Indo-European *stegʷʰ-. For &#x27;crowning a monarch&#x27; semantic loan from French couronner.</div><b>στήθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στήθη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chest, thorax</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bust, breasts</div>
  <div class='def'>3. heart (seat of emotions and passion)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. breast meat (of poultry)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στῆθος.</div><b>στήλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στήλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. column, stele</div>
  <div class='def'>2. file</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στήλη.</div><b>στήμονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στήμονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stamen</div><b>στήνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to put up, to erect (to assemble or build into an upright position)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to put up, to erect (to assemble or build into an upright position); to pitch (:a tent)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to stand up (to set in an standing position)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to set up (to start: a business, scheme, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (familiar, transitive) to stand, to put (someone somewhere)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (familiar, transitive) to stand up (to avoid a prearranged meeting, especially a date, with (a person) without prior notification; to jilt)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (familiar, transitive) to fix, to rig, to set up (to dishonestly arrange for an outcome in advance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek στήνω, from Koine Greek ἱστάνω, from Ancient Greek ἵστημι.</div><b>στήριγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στηρίγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>support (support: physical, emotional or psychological)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στήριγμα.</div><b>στήριξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στηρίξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. supporting, support (the act of keeping something/someone from falling)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) support (help, backing)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) support (evidence)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) basing, resting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from στηρίζω, stem στηρικ- (stirik-) + -ση (-si) &gt; -ξη (-xi), or from Ancient Greek στήριξις.</div><b>στίβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στίβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. athletics track</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) athletics; track and field</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) arena; field; theater (the realm in which particular events take place)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στίβος, with a semantic loan from English track (“path or course laid out for a race”).</div><b>στίβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στύβω">στύβω</a></div><b>στίγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στίγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stigma (mark of infamy)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. disgrace</div>
  <div class='def'>3. scar, birthmark</div>
  <div class='def'>4. stigma (Ϛ and ϛ: a ligature of the Greek letters lunate sigma and tau)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στίγμα.</div><b>στίξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στίξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>punctuation (the symbols and their use)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στίξις, from Ancient Greek στίζω. Compare Greek στιγμή or στίγμα, English stigma.</div><b>στίφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στίφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>swarm, army, host, horde</div><b>στίχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στίχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a line of text, usually verse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. verse, poetry</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) lyrics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στίχος.</div><b>στα καλά καθούμενα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all of a sudden, just like that, out of nowhere, out of the blue (suddenly and unexpectedly; without warning)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: ξαφνικά (xafniká)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “(with things) sitting well”.</div><b>στα παπάρια μου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) I don&#x27;t give a fuck, I don&#x27;t give a shit, I don&#x27;t give a damn</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν σου αρέσει αυτή η ταινία; <b>Στα παπάρια μου</b>! Εμείς αυτό θα δούμε! - You don&#x27;t like this film? I don&#x27;t give a shit! That&#x27;s what we&#x27;re seeing!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “at my balls”.</div><b>σταγόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταγόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drop, blob</div>
  <div class='ex'>σταγόνα βροχής (raindrop)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the singular accusative σταγόνα of Ancient Greek σταγών.</div><b>σταδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>level staff, stadia</div><b>σταδιακά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σταδιακός">σταδιακός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gradually</div><b>σταδιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σταδιακή, neuter σταδιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gradual</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek στάδιον + -ακός.</div><b>σταδιοδρομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταδιοδρομίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>career (especially advancement in a profession)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σταδιοδρομία with semantic loan from French carrière.</div><b>σταθερά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταθερές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constant</div>
  <div class='ex'>σταθερά του Πλανκ (Plank&#x27;s constant)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steadily</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://σταθερός">σταθερός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σταθερός">σταθερός</a></div><b>σταθερά Αβογκάντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Avogadro constant, Avogadro&#x27;s number</div><b>σταθεροποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταθεροποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stabilisation (UK), stabilization (US)</div><b>σταθεροποιητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταθεροποιητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stabilizer (any substance added to something in order to stabilize it)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σταθεροποιώ + -τής, a calque of French stabilisateur.</div><b>σταθεροποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to stabilize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σταθεροποιῶ. By surface analysis, σταθερός + -ποιώ.</div><b>σταθερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σταθερή, neuter σταθερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constant, steady</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σταθερός.</div><b>σταθερότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σταθερότητα">σταθερότητα</a></div><b>σταθερότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) stability, steadiness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σταθερότης, equivalent to σταθερός + -ότητα.</div><b>σταθμάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταθμάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stationmaster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σταθμός + -άρχης.</div><b>σταθμεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to park</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to halt, stop</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to halt (for rest or overnight)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek σταθμεύω, from σταθμός + -εύω, with semantic loan from French stationner.</div><b>σταθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. station, terminus, terminal (bus, railway)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σταθμός</b> λεωφορείων - bus station</div>
  <div class='def'>2. station, facility, centre</div>
  <div class='ex'>παιδικός <b>σταθμός</b> - kindergarten</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σταθμός, from Proto-Indo-European *sth₂-dʰ-mós.</div><b>στακάτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) staccato</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian staccato.</div><b>σταλάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to drip (to let fall in drops)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to drip, to drop (to fall in drops or droplets)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σταλάζω, from Ancient Greek σταλάσσω.</div><b>σταλαγμίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stalagmite</div><b>σταλακτίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stalactite</div><b>σταλινικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σταλινική, neuter σταλινικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Stalinist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, Στάλιν + -ικός.</div><b>σταλμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine σταλμένη, neuter σταλμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στέλνομαι, passive voice of στέλνω</div><b>σταματάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to halt, stop, stop off (movement)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cease, stop (action, activity)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stop, terminate (come to an end)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to drop out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern σταματώ σταματ- + -άω, inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek σταματῶ, a metaplasm of σταματίζω, from the neuter noun στάμα, from stem στα- of the Ancient Greek ἵσταμαι, passive of ἵστημι.</div><b>σταματημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine σταματημένη, neuter σταματημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>halted, stopped, motionless</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι μποτιλιάρισμα! Όλα τα αυτοκίνητα είναι <b>σταματημένα</b>. - What a traffi jam! All the cars are stationary.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of σταματιέμαι, passive voice of σταματάω, σταματώ (“stop”).</div><b>σταματιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be stopped, halted</div><b>σταματώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σταματάω">σταματάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the mediaeval Byzantine Greek σταματῶ and see σταματάω.</div><b>σταννάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) stannane (SnH₄)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English stannane, with the suffix -ιο (-io, neuter-noun suffix).</div><b>στατική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statics</div><b>στατιστική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στατιστικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statistics</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://στατιστικός">στατιστικός</a></div><b>στατιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στατιστική, neuter στατιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>statistical</div><b>σταυροβελονιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cross-stitch (stitch or embroidery made with such stitches)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σταυρο- (stavro-) + -βελονιά</div><b>σταυροδρόμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σταυροδρόμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crossroads</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) crossroads (meeting point; critical point)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σταυρός + δρόμος</div><b>σταυροκοπιούνται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cross oneself (make the sign of the cross)</div><b>σταυροπόδι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with legs crossed, cross-legged</div><b>σταυροφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταυροφορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. any of the Crusades</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an organised team effort, a campaign, a crusade</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σταυροφορία</b> κατά των ναρκωτικών - a crusade against drugs</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σταυρός + φέρω.</div><b>σταυροφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σταυροφόρα, neuter σταυροφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crossbearing, cruciferous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Koine Greek σταυροφόρος. By surface analysis, σταυρός + -φόρος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταυροφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) crossbearer, crucifer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) crusader</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) crusader (one who tirelessly advocates for something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Koine Greek σταυροφόρος. By surface analysis, σταυρός + -φόρος.</div><b>σταυρωμένα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of σταυρωμένος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of σταυρωμένος</div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of σταυρωμένος</div><b>σταυρόλεξο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σταυρόλεξα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crossword, crossword puzzle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σταυρός + λέξη. Calque of English crossword.</div><b>σταυρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταυροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cross (geometrical figure)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επειδή ο παππούς μου είναι αγράμματος, υπογράφει με <b>σταυρό</b>. - Since my grandfather is illiterate, he signs with a cross.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cross, crucifix (cross on which one is crucified; usually refers to the one upon which Jesus Christ died)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Χριστός πέθανε πάνω στο <b>σταυρό</b>. - Christ died on the cross.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) cross (difficult situation that must be endured)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο καθένας σηκώνει μεγάλο <b>σταυρό</b>. - Everyone has their own cross to bear.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. cross, crucifix (representation of the crucifixion stake of Christ worn or displayed by Christians)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αρχιεπίσκοπος φοράει χρυσό <b>σταυρό</b>. - The archbishop is wearing a gold cross.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. sign of the cross (gesture of the hand moving over the front of one&#x27;s body in the shape of a cross)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάνω τον <b>σταυρό</b> μου - I make (the sign of) the cross</div>
  <div class='def'>6. control bar (wooden device used to control marionettes)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (colloquial) glabella (space between the eyebrows and the nose)</div>
  <div class='def'>8. Iron Cross (maneuver in which both arms are extended straight out from the sides of the body while suspended mid air and holding onto rings)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σταυρός, from Proto-Indo-European *steh₂- (“to stand”).</div><b>σταυρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to crucify (to execute (a person) by nailing to a cross)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (excessive, figuratively, transitive) to crucify (to punish or otherwise express extreme anger at)</div>
  <div class='ex'>«[…] Όταν μίλαγα εγώ για γυναίκες και εγκυμοσύνη, με <b>σταυρώνανε</b>. Κάντε μου τη χάρη τώρα» - &quot; […] When I talked about women and pregnancy, they crucified me. Give me a break now.&quot;</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to cross, to make the sign of the cross over</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to cross (to mark with an X)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to cross (to place across or athwart; to cause to intersect)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σταυρώνω</b> τα χέρια (μου) - to cross one&#x27;s arms</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σταυρώνω, from Koine Greek σταυρῶ, from Ancient Greek σταυρόω. By surface analysis, σταυρ(ός)) + -ώνω.</div><b>σταυρώνω τα χέρια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) To cross one&#x27;s arms.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) To do nothing, to wait.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, σταυρώνω + τα (ta, “the”, plural) + χέρια.</div><b>σταφίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταφίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>raisin, sultana, currant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σταφίδα, from Koine Greek σταφίς, from Ancient Greek ἀσταφίς.</div><b>σταφιδόψωμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σταφιδόψωμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>raisin bread, stafidopsomo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σταφίδ(α), “raisin”) + -ό- (“interfix”) + -ψωμο (-psomo, “bread”).</div><b>σταφυλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταφυλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grape</div>
  <div class='def'>2. uvula</div><b>σταφυλίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταφυλίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σταφυλή">σταφυλή</a></div><b>σταφυλινίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Staphylinidae, the staphylinids, the rove beetles.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> translingual Staphylinidae, suffixed with -ίδες.</div><b>σταφυλόκοκκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταφυλόκοκκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>staphylococcus (Staphylococcus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Staphylococcus.</div><b>σταφύλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σταφύλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grape (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σταφύλιον, from Ancient Greek σταφυλή + -ιον (-ion).</div><b>σταχανοβίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταχανοβίτες, feminine σταχανοβίτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Stakhanovite</div><b>σταχανοβίτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταχανοβίτισσες, masculine σταχανοβίτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Stakhanovite</div><b>σταχανοβισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Stakhanovism</div><b>σταχτής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σταχτιά, neuter σταχτί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grey (UK), gray (US) (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ashen, pale (color/colour)</div><b>σταχτερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σταχτερή, neuter σταχτερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grey (UK), gray (US) (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ashen, pale (color/colour)</div><b>σταχτοδοχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σταχτοδοχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ashtray</div><b>σταχτοτσικνιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταχτοτσικνιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grey heron (Ardea cinerea)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σταχτής + τσικνιάς.</div><b>σταχτόνερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) lye (alkaline solution)</div><b>σταχωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σταχωτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bookbinder</div><b>σταχώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bind a book, bind books</div><b>σταύρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σταυρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crucifixion</div><b>στγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://στρατηγός">στρατηγός</a></div><b>στείβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στύβω">στύβω</a> (linguistically informed spelling)</div>
  <div class='ex'>See notes at στύβω.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στείβω. See Etymology at στύβω.</div><b>στείρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στείρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goldblotch grouper (Epinephelus costae)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://στείρος">στείρος</a>:; feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://στείρος">στείρος</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural</div><b>στείρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στείρα, neuter στείρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of people and animals) infertile, sterile, barren (unable to procreate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έκαναν παιδιά, διότι η γυναίκα του ήταν <b>στείρα</b>. - They didn&#x27;t have any children as his wife was infertile.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sterile, germless (free from all living or viable microorganisms)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βάλε το σ’ ένα <b>στείρο</b> φιαλίδιο. - Put it in a sterile vial.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of land) barren, infertile, fruitless (not producing vegetation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην φυτέψεις εκεί, είναι <b>στείρα</b> γη. - Don&#x27;t plant over there, it&#x27;s barren land.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) unproductive, fruitless (not producing anything of value)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δυστυχώς, οι συζητήσεις ήταν <b>στείρες</b>. - Unfortunately, the discussions were fruitless.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στεῖρος.</div><b>στείρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στειρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neutering, spaying, sterilisation (UK), sterilization (US) (act of making unable to procreate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μετά την <b>στείρωση</b>, ο γάτος άλλαξε την συμπεριφορά του. - After his neutering, the cat changed his behaviour.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek στείρωσις, from Ancient Greek στεῖρος.</div><b>στεατίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στεατίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steatite, soapstone</div><b>στεατοπυγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στεατοπυγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steatopygia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French stéatopygie. From Ancient Greek στέαρ + the interfix -ο- (-o-) + πῡγ(ή), “buttocks”) + the suffix -ία. First attested in 1896.</div><b>στεατόρροια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στεατόρροιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steatorrhoea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English steatorrhoea. From Ancient Greek στέαρ + the interfix -ο- (-o-) + the suffix -ρροια (-rroia). First attested in 1873.</div><b>στεγάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to roof (to cover or furnish with a roof)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to shelter</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to house, to accommodate (to admit to residence; to provide housing for)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στεγάζω. By surface analysis, στέγ(η)) + -άζω.</div><b>στεγανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στεγανή, neuter στεγανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. watertight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hermetic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στεγανός, from στέγω.</div><b>στεγαστικά δάνεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative singular of στεγαστικό <a href="bword://δάνειο">δάνειο</a></div><b>στεγαστικό δάνειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στεγαστικά δάνεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>housing loan, home loan</div><b>στεγαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στεγαστική, neuter στεγαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>housing</div><b>στεγνοκαθαριστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στεγνοκαθαριστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dry cleaner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from στεγν(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + καθαριστήριο, a calque of English dry cleaning or German Trockenreinigung.</div><b>στεγνωτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στεγνωτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dryer (for clothes)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dryer (for hair)</div><b>στεγνός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στεγνή, neuter στεγνό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dry (free from or lacking moisture)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) dried out (old; worn out)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) dry, dull (lacking interest, boring)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στεγνός.</div><b>στεγνώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek στεγνῶ, στεγνώνω; by surface analysis, στεγνός + -ώνω.</div><b>στειμμένα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένα</div><b>στειμμένε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένε</div><b>στειμμένες</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένες</div><b>στειμμένη</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένη</div><b>στειμμένης</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένης</div><b>στειμμένο</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένο</div><b>στειμμένοι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένοι</div><b>στειμμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine στειμμένη, neuter στειμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://στυμμένος">στυμμένος</a> (linguistic spelling)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στείβομαι (στύβομαι), passive voice of στείβω, στύβω (“I squeeze”). Spelt with -ει- (See Etymology at στύβω).</div><b>στειμμένου</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένου</div><b>στειμμένους</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένους</div><b>στειμμένων</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>linguistically informed spelling of στυμμένων</div><b>στειρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) infertility, sterility, barrenness (inability to procreate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek στειρότης, equivalent to στείρος + -ότητα.</div><b>στειρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of animals) to neuter, to sterilise (UK), sterilize (US), to spay (make unable to reproduce)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έφεραν την σκύλα στον κτηνίατρο για να <b>στειρωθεί</b>. - They brought the dog to the vet so that she would be neutered.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek στειρόω, from Ancient Greek στεῖρος.</div><b>στενή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (slang) prison, jail, gaol, clink</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) prison sentence</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://στενός">στενός</a></div><b>στεναγμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στεναγμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sigh, groan (low sound uttered in pain or grief)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας γέννησες φτωχούς
με την καρδιά πικρή
γεμάτη <b>στεναγμούς</b>. - You gave birth to us poor,
With a bitter heart
Full of sighs.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) lament</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στεναγμός, from στενάζω.</div><b>στεναχωράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στεναχωρώ">στεναχωρώ</a></div><b>στεναχωρημένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in sad mood</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://στεναχωρημένος">στεναχωρημένος</a></div><b>στεναχωρημένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine στεναχωρημένη, neuter στεναχωρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στενοχωρημένος">στενοχωρημένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στεναχωριέμαι / στεναχωρούμαι, passive voice of στεναχωρώ (“I upset, make someone sad”).</div><b>στεναχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στενοχωρώ">στενοχωρώ</a></div><b>στενογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural στενογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (UK) shorthand typist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (US) stenographer</div><b>στενογραφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στενογραφική, neuter στενογραφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stenographic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, στενογραφία + -ικός.</div><b>στενοχωρημένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in sad mood</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://στενοχωρημένος">στενοχωρημένος</a></div><b>στενοχωρημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine στενοχωρημένη, neuter στενοχωρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>worried, upset and depressed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στενοχωριέμαι / στενοχωρούμαι, passive voice of στενοχωρώ (“I upset, make someone sad”).</div><b>στενοχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sadden, to grieve, to distress</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to upset (to make (a person) angry, distressed, or unhappy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek στενοχωρῶ. στενός + χώρος. The first combining form with both [o] and [a]: στενο-χωρώ and στενα-χωρώ.</div><b>στενοχώρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στενοχώριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. distress (emotional suffering)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) distress, straits, woes (misfortunes causing emotional suffering)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek στενοχωρία with synizesis and stress retraction, from Ancient Greek στενοχωρία. Compare English stenochoria.</div><b>στενό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://στενός">στενός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://στενός">στενός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στενά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. narrow street (typically in a village)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. narrows, strait</div><b>στενόμακρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στενόμακρη, neuter στενόμακρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oblong (longer than wide)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> στενός + μακρός.</div><b>στενός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στενή, neuter στενό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. narrow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tight</div>
  <div class='def'>3. close</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>στενός</b> φίλος - close friend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek στενός.</div><b>στενόχωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στενόχωρη, neuter στενόχωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. narrow, confined, tight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. distressing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek στενόχωρος.</div><b>στερέωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στερεώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) support, framework</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) keel</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (literary) firmament, the heavens</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) group (of people of common interest); coterie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στερέωμα.</div><b>στερακτίνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στερακτίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steradian (units of solid angles)</div><b>στερεά γωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στερεές γωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>solid angle</div><b>στερεές γωνίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of στερεά <a href="bword://γωνία">γωνία</a></div><b>στερεοϊσομερές</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stereoisomer</div><b>στερεό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στερεά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>solid (state of matter)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://στερεός">στερεός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://στερεός">στερεός</a></div><b>στερεός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στερεή or στερεά, neuter στερεό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>firm, solid (of foundations, bodies etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στερεός, from Proto-Indo-European *ster- (“stiff”). Cognate with Latin stultus, stolidus, sterilis, strēnuus, stīria.</div><b>στερεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to become barren</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to dry out</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to dry out (less frequent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek στερεύω, from Ancient Greek στειρεύω.</div><b>στερητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στερητική, neuter στερητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. privative (causing privation; depriving)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. privative (indicating the absence of something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στερητικός. By surface analysis, στερη-) + -τικός.</div><b>στεριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>land (part of Earth which is not covered by oceans or other bodies of water)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek στεριά, from Ancient Greek στερεά.</div><b>στεριώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to become solid, to solidify, to become firm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to settle (to fix one&#x27;s residence in a place)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to settle (to be established in a profession or in employment)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to make solid, to solidify, to firm up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: ενισχύω (enischýo), ισχυροποιώ (ischyropoió), σταθεροποιώ (statheropoió)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στερε(ῶ), contracted form of στερεόω) + -ώνω with synizesis.</div><b>στερλίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στερλίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sterling (UK currency)</div>
  <div class='ex'>λίρα <b>στερλίνα</b> - pound sterling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Italian sterlina, from English sterling</div><b>στερνά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>old age</div><b>στερνοπαίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στερνοπαίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lastborn, youngest (child)</div><b>στερούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lack</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to do without</div><b>στερώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to deprive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek στερῶ, contracted form of στερέω.</div><b>στεφάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στεφάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ring, hoop, rim, collar, bezel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crown</div>
  <div class='def'>3. corolla</div>
  <div class='def'>4. corona</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek στεφάνη (“encircling item e.g. for the head”).</div><b>στεφάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στεφάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wreath, garland (of flowers)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα <b>στεφάνι</b> για κηδεία - funeral wreath</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crown, wreath (worn on the head)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το αγκάθινο <b>στεφάνι</b> - the crown of thorns</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the mediaeval Byzantine Greek στεφάνιν, from Hellenistic Koine Greek στεφάνιον, diminutive of the Ancient Greek στέφανος.</div><b>στεφανώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to crown with a wreath, to wreathe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to crown (to form the topmost or finishing part of; to complete; to consummate; to perfect)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to marry, to marry off (to arrange for the marriage of)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to marry, to wed (to perform the marriage ceremony for; to unite in wedlock)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to marry (to enter into the conjugal or connubial state; to take a husband or a wife)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek στεφανῶ in the sense &#x27;to marry&#x27; and learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στεφανῶ in the sense &#x27;to crown/wreathe&#x27;. By surface analysis, στέφανος or στεφάνι + -ώνω.</div><b>στη μνήμη</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in memory of, in memoriam</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Contraction στη &amp; accusative singular of feminine noun μνήμη.</div><b>στη μπάντα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) out of the way (move aside)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Στη μπάντα</b>, πρέπει να περάσει το ασθενοφόρο! - Out of the way, the ambulance has to pass by!</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see στη (sti), <a href="bword://μπάντα">μπάντα</a>.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) out of the way, to the side (not in the way)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι έκαναν <b>στη μπάντα</b> για να περάσει ο δάσκαλος. - Everyone moved aside so the teacher could pass.</div><b>στηθάγχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural στηθάγχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) angina pectoris</div><b>στηθαίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στηθαία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. safety barrier</div>
  <div class='def'>2. parapet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek στηθαῖον.</div><b>στηθοσκόπηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) stethoscopy</div><b>στηθοσκόπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στηθοσκόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stethoscope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French stéthoscope, from Ancient Greek στῆθος + σκοπέω.</div><b>στηθόδεσμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στηθόδεσμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brassiere, bra</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Koine Greek στηθόδεσμος. By surface analysis, στήθος + δεσμός, literally “chest-restrainer”.</div><b>στηλιτεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to stigmatise (UK), stigmatize (US), to pillory, to denounce</div><b>στην άκρη της γλώσσας</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see στην (stin), <a href="bword://άκρη">άκρη</a>, <a href="bword://της">της</a> (tis), γλώσσας.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν γεύομαι <b>στην άκρη της γλώσσας</b> για κάποιο λόγο. - I can&#x27;t taste on the tip of my tongue for some reason.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) on the tip of one&#x27;s tongue (known but not quite remembered)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω τ’ όνομά της <b>στην άκρη της γλώσσας</b> αλλά δε μου έρχεται. - I have her name on the tip of my tongue but it won&#x27;t come to me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French sur le bout de la langue.</div><b>στην υγειά μας</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to our health! cheers!</div><b>στην υγειά σας</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(polite) to your health! cheers!</div><b>στην υγειά σου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to your health! cheers!</div><b>στην ώρα μου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>on time</div><b>στηρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive of <a href="bword://στηρίζω">στηρίζω</a>; to lean</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive of <a href="bword://στηρίζω">στηρίζω</a>; to count, rely</div><b>στηρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to uphold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to support</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to base, rest, build on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek στηρίζω.</div><b>στηριγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine στηριγμένη, neuter στηριγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. upheld</div>
  <div class='def'>2. supported (also figuratively)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στηρίζομαι, passive voice of στηρίζω.</div><b>στιβάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στιβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>layer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantic loan from French couche. As in accusative στῐβάδα of Ancient Greek στῐβάς (“bed of straw”). The stem στι- by dissimilation from στείβω.</div><b>στιβαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στιβαρή, neuter στιβαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>robust, firm, strong, sturdy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στιβαρός.</div><b>στιγμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στιγμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. moment, instant, jiffy</div>
  <div class='ex'>περιμένετε μια <b>στιγμή</b> - wait a moment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. point (unit)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. full stop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek στιγμή.</div><b>στιγματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to stigmatise (UK), stigmatize (US); brand, label</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to tarnish, disgrace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek στιγματίζω. By surface analysis, στίγμα + -ίζω.</div><b>στιγματισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine στιγματισμένη, neuter στιγματισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stigmatised (UK), stigmatized (US), labelled</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένας άνθρωπος <b>στιγματισμένος</b> ως κλέφτης δεν μπορεί να βρει εύκολα δουλειά. - A man labelled as a thief will find work easily.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στιγματίζομαι, passive voice of στιγματίζω</div><b>στιγματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) stigmatisation (UK), stigmatization (US)</div><b>στιγμιαίοι καφέδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://στιγμιαίος">στιγμιαίος</a> <a href="bword://καφές">καφές</a></div><b>στιγμιαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στιγμιαία, neuter στιγμιαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brief, instantaneous, instant, momentary</div><b>στιγμιαίος καφές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στιγμιαίοι καφέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>instant coffee</div><b>στιγμιότυπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στιγμιότυπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. snapshot, snap</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) brief description</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of screen) screenshot, (of memory) snapshot</div><b>στιγμοεκτυπωτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στιγμοεκτυπωτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dot matrix printer</div><b>στιγμούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στιγμούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moment, instant, trice (short period of time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> στιγμή + -ούλα</div><b>στιλέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στιλέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dagger</div><b>στιλιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stylistic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek word.</div><b>στιλογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στιλογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στυλογράφος">στυλογράφος</a></div><b>στιμμένα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένα</div><b>στιμμένε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένε</div><b>στιμμένες</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένες</div><b>στιμμένη</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένη</div><b>στιμμένης</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένης</div><b>στιμμένο</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένο</div><b>στιμμένοι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένοι</div><b>στιμμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine στιμμένη, neuter στιμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://στυμμένος">στυμμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στίβομαι (στύβομαι, passive voice of στίβω, στύβω (“I squeeze”). Spelt with -ι- (See Etymology at στύβω).</div><b>στιμμένου</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένου</div><b>στιμμένους</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένους</div><b>στιμμένων</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of στυμμένων</div><b>στιφάδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στιφάδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stifado (a savoury stew)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan stufado, from Latin extufare, from Ancient Greek τύφος. Compare Italian stufato.</div><b>στιχάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στιχάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alb (priest&#x27;s vestment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek στιχάριον, a diminutive of Ancient Greek στίχος.</div><b>στιχουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural στιχουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. versewright</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lyricist (a person who writes the lyrics of a song)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek στιχουργός. By surface analysis, στίχ(ος)) + -ουργός.</div><b>στλεγγίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στλεγγίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strigil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στλεγγίς, from Proto-Indo-European *streng-.</div><b>στο καλό</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. goodbye, cheerio</div>
  <div class='def'>2. farewell (dated)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally: στο (sto, “to the”) καλό</div><b>στο πηγάδι κατούρησα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) What did I do? Have I offended someone?</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι κάτι περιμένουν σ’ αυτόν του κόσμο. Εγώ τι διάολο <b>στο πηγάδι κατούρησα</b>; Περιμένω λοιπόν και γω την κληρονομιά από κάπιον που δεν υπάρχει. - Everyone&#x27;s waiting for something in this world. What the hell did I do? So I&#x27;m waiting too for an inheritance from someone who doesn&#x27;t exist.</div><b>στο πι και φι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>immediately, unhesitatingly, at the drop of a hat</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έκανε όλες τις διορθώσεις <b>στο πι και φι</b>. - She made all the corrections instantly.</div><b>στο χείλος του γκρεμού</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) on/towards the edge, the brink of a precipice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) at the verge of peril</div><b>στοίβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στοίβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stack, pile (usually organised, usually similar things)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from στοιβάζω + suffix -α (-a).</div><b>στοίχημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στοιχήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bet, wager, punt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek στοίχημα, from Ancient Greek στοίχημᾰ, from στοιχέω + -μα (-ma); see στοῖχος for more.</div><b>στοίχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στοίχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>row, line</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στοῖχος.</div><b>στοιβάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cram, pack</div><b>στοιβάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to heap, heap up, stack, pile, pile up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek στοιβάζω, itself from Ancient Greek στοιβή + -άζω. Stem στοι- by dissimilation from the related στείβω.</div><b>στοιχίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cost</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σπίτι αυτό του <b>στοίχισε</b> μια περιουσία. - The house has cost him a fortune.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cost, claim</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο σεισμός <b>στοίχισε</b> πολλές ζωές. - The earthquake claimed many lives.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to arrange in rows, lines, line up, dress, align</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο λοχίας <b>στοίχισε</b> τους άνδρες του. - The sergeant lined up his men.</div><b>στοιχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στοιχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unit, element, cell (a portion of a whole); element</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unit, element, cell (a portion of a whole); item of data, piece of information</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unit, element, cell (a portion of a whole); letter, piece of type</div>
  <div class='def'>4. unit, element, cell (a portion of a whole); cell, battery</div>
  <div class='def'>5. unit, element, cell (a portion of a whole); subset of a population</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek στοιχεῖον. Compare the inherited doublet στοιχειό.</div><b>στοιχειοθέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στοιχειοθέτες, feminine στοιχειοθέτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compositor, typographer</div><b>στοιχειοθέτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στοιχειοθέτρια, masculine στοιχειοθέτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compositor, typographer</div><b>στοιχειολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) stoichiology</div>
  <div class='ex'>Καὶ τοιαύτην μὲν ἐξέτασιν ἐν γένει ποιῶν τὴν φυσικὴν καλουμένην κυρίως συνεστήσατο· τὰ διάφορα δὲ στοιχεῖα ἐξ ὧν τὰ σώματα σύγκεινται θεωρῶν, εἰς τὴν καλουμένην χυμείαν, εἰ τε στοιχειακὴν, ἢ στοιχειολογίαν ἀρχὴν ἔδωκεν! - (please add an English translation of this quotation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the French stœchiologie.</div><b>στοιχειομετρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) stoichiometry</div><b>στοιχειωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine στοιχειωμένη, neuter στοιχειωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>haunted, eerie</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι ένα <b>στοιχειωμένο</b> σπίτι - It is a haunted house</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στοιχειώνομαι, passive voice of στοιχειώνω</div><b>στοιχειό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στοιχειά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ghost, poltergeist, sprite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from Ancient Greek στοιχεῖον. Doublet of στοιχείο.</div><b>στοιχειώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στοιχειώδης, neuter στοιχειώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. basic, elementary</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>στοιχειώδης</b> εκπαίδευση - elementary education</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fundamental (particle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek στοιχειώδης</div><b>στοιχειώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to haunt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to become obsessed with</div><b>στοιχηματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to bet, wager</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι νόμιμο να <b>στοιχηματίζω</b>; - Is it legal to bet?</div><b>στολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>costume / uniform (clothing distinctive to a profession, soldier, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>αποκριάτικη <b>στολή</b> - carnival costume</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly inherited from Ancient Greek στολή.</div><b>στολίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στολίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>decoration, ornament, adornment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From στολίζω + -ίδι. Compare Ancient Greek στολίδιον.</div><b>στολίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dress up, be decorated</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η πόλη <b>στολίζεται</b> για τα Χριστούγεννα! - The city is decorated for Christmas!</div><b>στολίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to adorn, decorate, ornament</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Hellenistic Koine Greek στολίζω, with ancient sense &quot;arm, equip&quot;.</div><b>στολισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adorning, adornment, decorating, decoration (the act of adorning)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek στολισμός with semantic adaptation to στολίζω. By surface analysis, στολίζω + -μός.</div><b>στομάχι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στομάχια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stomach</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek στομάχιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek στόμαχος.</div><b>στομίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στομίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bit (part of horse&#x27;s headgear)</div><b>στοματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στοματική, neuter στοματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oral (relating to the mouth)</div><b>στομαχόπονος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στομαχόπονοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stomachache</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From στόμαχος + πόνος.</div><b>στομωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στομωμένη, neuter στομωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dull, not sharp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive participle of στομώνω, from Ancient Greek στομόω, from στόμα.</div><b>στοπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) stop, block (device to block path)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) stop sign</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural, indeclinable) brake lights</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα στοπ</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English stop.</div><b>στοργή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) deep love and affection, as of a mother for her child.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek στοργή.</div><b>στοργικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στοργική, neuter στοργικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loving</div><b>στου κουφού την πόρτα, όσο θέλεις βρόντα</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>there are none so blind as those who will not see (understanding cannot be forced on someone who chooses to be ignorant)</div><b>στοχάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary, poetic) to think, contemplate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to think, calculate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στοχᾰ́ζομαι (“aim; guess”).</div><b>στοχαστικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stochastically</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://στοχαστικός">στοχαστικός</a></div><b>στοχαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στοχαστική, neuter στοχαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stochastic</div><b>στοχεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to target</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from στόχ(ος)) + -εύω, a calque of French viser.</div><b>στούντιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) studio</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English studio, from Italian studio (“study”).</div><b>στράτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στράτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. street</div>
  <div class='def'>2. baby walker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Latin strata. Compare Italian strada.</div><b>στράτευμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στρατεύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>military forces (as a whole, for a country or alliance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το Ελληνικό <b>στράτευμα</b> πήγε στην Τροία. - The Greek forces went to Troy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στράτευμα.</div><b>στράφι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(to go) to waste, down the drain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Turkish israf (plus στ- from a preceding definite article), from Arabic إِسْرَاف.</div><b>στρέμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στρέμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. The royal stremma or decare of ¹⁄₁₀ hectare (1,000 m²)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) The historical stremma or square plethron, an area of 10,000 square Greek feet</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, historical) The historical stremma, the area able to be plowed by a team of oxen in a day, the Greek equivalent of the English acre</div><b>στρέφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be turned, directed</div><b>στρέφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to turn, direct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably borrowed from Ancient Greek στρέφω.</div><b>στρίβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be twisted, turned</div><b>στρίβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to twist, turn, twirl</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek στρίβω, from the ancient στρέφω from the perfective stem στριψ- in the pattern of -ψ- &gt; -β- as in κλέπτω, κλεψ- &gt; κλέβω.</div><b>στρίγκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρίγκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strix</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) hag, harpy, witch (a malevolent woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: μέγαιρα f (mégaira)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek στρίγκλα, from Latin strigula, diminutive of striga, from the στριγγ- stem of Koine Greek στρίγξ, from Ancient Greek στρίξ.</div><b>στρίγκλισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στριγκλίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>screech, screeching; squeal (action and noise made)</div><b>στραβά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of στραβός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crookedly, askew, awry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wrong, wrongly, the wrong way</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν ένας τύπος μάγκας που μάλωνε με οποιονδήποτε τον κοιτούσε <b>στραβά</b>. - There was a tough guy who was picking fights with anyone who looked at him wrong.</div><b>στραβισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στραβισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strabismus, squint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στραβισμός, from στραβίζω, from στραβός.</div><b>στραβομουτσουνιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to grimace in displeasure</div><b>στραβώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to collapse, distort, twist (wheel, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal, transitive) to blind</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (informal, transitive) to turn out badly</div><b>στραγγίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be drained, be strained, be wrung</div><b>στραγγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to drain, wring, drip-dry, dry off (let something lose its dampness by hanging or twisting)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Στράγγιξε</b> καλά τα ρούχα, πριν τα απλώσεις. - Wring the clothes well before you lay them out.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to strain, filter (separate solid from liquid by passing through a strainer or colander)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Στραγγίζω</b> το γιαούρτι για να φτιάξω στραγγιστό γιαούρτι. - I strain the yoghurt in order to make Greek yoghurt.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to pan (wash in a pan when searching for gold)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη δεκαετία του 1840, πολλοί στην Αμερική <b>στράγγιζαν</b> για χρυσό. - In the 1840s, many in America were panning for gold.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, intransitive) to drink dry (completely drink a liquid)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο τέλος, <b>στράγγιξαν</b> εντελώς το βαρέλι η δυό τους. - In the end, the two of them drank the barrel dry.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) to wear out, drain, bleed dry / bleed white (remove all vitality or life by hard work etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μ’ έχει <b>στραγγίξει</b> η δουλειά σήμερα. - Work has worn me out today.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek στραγγίζω from Ancient Greek στραγγός.</div><b>στραμπουλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sprain, wrench, dislocate</div><b>στραπατσάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strapatsada (tomato and egg dish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan strapazzada, from strapazzar (“to treat roughly; to scramble (eggs)”) + -ada (forms verbal nouns). Related to Italian strapazzare.</div><b>στρατάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural στρατάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated) An army rank with the NATO grade OF-10 (obsolete in most modern armies); field marshal in the British army</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) An army rank with the NATO grade OF-10 (obsolete in most modern armies); general of the army in the US army</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στρατός + Ancient Greek άρχης.</div><b>στρατηγική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρατηγικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strategy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στρατηγική with semantic loan from French stratégie for non-military contexts.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://στρατηγικός">στρατηγικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>στρατηγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στρατηγική, neuter στρατηγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. strategic (of or relating to military operations that are more large-scale or long-range than local or tactical ones)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. strategic (of or pertaining to strategy)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (relational) general&#x27;s, of a general</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> In the &#x27;strategic&#x27; sense, learnedly formed from στρατηγ(ική)) + -ικός with semantic loan from English strategic for non-military contexts. In the relational sense &#x27;of a general&#x27;, learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στρατηγικός.</div><b>στρατηγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural στρατηγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>general, an army rank with the NATO grade OF-9</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek στρατηγός</div><b>στρατιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρατιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. army (especially a very large one)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>στρατιές</b> του Χίτλερ απειλούσαν την Ευρώπη. - Hitler&#x27;s armies threatened Europe.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) army, a large mass people</div>
  <div class='ex'>Mια <b>στρατιά</b> εργατών ... - An army of labourers ...</div><b>στρατιωτίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρατιώτες, masculine στρατιώτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female soldier (member of an army)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. private (army rank), the NATO military rank OR-1</div><b>στρατιωτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://στρατιωτικός">στρατιωτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>militarily</div><b>στρατιωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στρατιωτική, neuter στρατιωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>military (describing members of the armed forces)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στρατιωτικός, from στρατιώτης + -ικός, with semantic loan from French militaire.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στρατιωτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>serviceman (especially an officer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στρατιωτικός, from στρατιώτης + -ικός, with semantic loan from French militaire.</div><b>στρατιωτικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>militarily</div><b>στρατιώτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στρατιώτες, feminine στρατιωτίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. soldier (member of an army)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. private (army rank), the NATO military rank OR-1</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pawn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek στρατιώτης.</div><b>στρατοδικείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural αεροδικεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>army court martial</div><b>στρατοκράτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στρατοκράτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>militarist</div><b>στρατοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) militarism</div><b>στρατοπεδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to camp, encamp, bivouac</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στρατοπεδεύω.</div><b>στρατσιατέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρατσιατέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stracciatella (soup)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stracciatella (ice cream)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian stracciatella, from stracciare (“to rip, to tear up”), of ultimate Latin origin.</div><b>στρατόπεδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στρατόπεδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. permanent or temporary base or camp</div>
  <div class='def'>2. camp or site for refugees, gypsies etc</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) faction, camp, grouping (with similar views/policies)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στρατόπεδον.</div><b>στρατόπεδο εξόντωσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στρατόπεδα εξόντωσης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>death camp, extermination camp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably a calque of English extermination camp.</div><b>στρατόπεδο συγκέντρωσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στρατόπεδα συγκέντρωσης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concentration camp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably a calque of English concentration camp.</div><b>στρατός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στρατοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>army, military force</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek στρατός.</div><b>στρατόσφαιρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) stratosphere</div><b>στρεπτοκοκκικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στρεπτοκοκκική, neuter στρεπτοκοκκικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>streptococcal</div><b>στρεπτόκοκκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στρεπτόκοκκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>streptococcus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> στρεπτός + κόκκος, calque of French streptocoque, itself from the equivalent Ancient Greek words.</div><b>στρες</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) stress (emotional pressure)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>στρες</b> είναι αναπόσπαστο μέρος της ζωής. - Stress is an integral part of life.</div><b>στριμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine στριμμένη, neuter στριμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. twisted (rope, wire, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) grouchy, crotchety (mood, character, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive perfect participle of στρίβω.</div><b>στριφογυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive, transitive) to twirl, gyrate, whirl, spin</div><b>στριφτό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://στριφτός">στριφτός</a>:; masculine accusative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://στριφτός">στριφτός</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative singular</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στριφτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ellipsis of <a href="bword://στριφτό">στριφτό</a> <a href="bword://τσιγάρο">τσιγάρο</a>: rollup, rollie (cigarette rolled by hand)</div><b>στριφτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στριφτή, neuter στριφτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twisted, rolled up</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>στριφτό</b> τσιγάρο - rolled-up cigarette, rollup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek στρίφ(ω)) + -τός.</div><b>στρογγυλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στρογγυλή, neuter στρογγυλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. round</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rounded</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>στρογγυλά</b> φωνήεντα - rounded vowels</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στρογγύλος, with accent shift by analogy with oxytone words such as ομαλός, μακρουλός.</div><b>στρογγύλα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλε</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vocative masculine singular of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλες</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine plural of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλη</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλης</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive feminine singular of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of στρογγύλος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλοι</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative masculine plural of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλου</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive masculine/neuter singular of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλους</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative masculine plural of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρογγύλων</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive masculine/feminine/neuter plural of στρογγύλος</div><b>στρουθιόμορφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Passeriformes, the perching birds.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Passeriformes, as στρούθιο + -μορφα (-morfa, “-morpha”).</div><b>στρουθοκάμηλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρουθοκάμηλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ostrich, Struthio camelus.</div><b>στροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στροφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bend, turning, turn (change of direction)</div>
  <div class='ex'>στη <b>στροφή</b> του δρόμου - at the bend in the road</div>
  <div class='def'>2. turning, turn, revolving, twisting (rotation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η μπαλαρίνα έκανε δυο <b>στροφές</b> - the ballerina made two turns</div>
  <div class='def'>3. verse, stanza</div>
  <div class='def'>4. tack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek στροφή.</div><b>στροφαλοφόροι άξονες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://στροφαλοφόρος">στροφαλοφόρος</a> <a href="bword://άξονας">άξονας</a></div><b>στροφαλοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine στροφαλοφόρα, neuter στροφαλοφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crank, cranked</div><b>στροφαλοφόρος άξονας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στροφαλοφόροι άξονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crankshaft</div><b>στρούγκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρούγκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. goat or sheep fence, fold, pen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) goat or sheep fence, fold, pen; prison</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory, humorous) flock; flock (group religious followers)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (derogatory) flock; political party, ideological group, etc. or its followers</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare Albanian shtrungë, Aromanian strungã, Serbo-Croatian strȕga /стру̏га.</div><b>στρωατοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρωατοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stratification</div><b>στρωματογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρωματογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stratigraphy</div><b>στρωματοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στρωματοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stratification</div><b>στρόγγυλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στρογγυλός">στρογγυλός</a></div><b>στρόντιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) strontium</div><b>στρύχνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στρύχνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nightshade</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek στρύχνον found in Theophrastus and Dioscorides)</div><b>στρώμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mattress (for sleeping)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. layer, sheet (which separates or cushions)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. stratus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στρῶμα.</div><b>στρώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. first-person singular present passive indicative of <a href="bword://στρώνω">στρώνω</a>; passive senses of <a href="bword://στρώνω">στρώνω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (familiar, figuratively) first-person singular present passive indicative of <a href="bword://στρώνω">στρώνω</a>; I settle down, relaxed, for a long time, usually where I am not welcomed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι μου <b>στρώθηκες</b> εδώ; Να πας στο σπίτι σου. - literally: What?/Why did you [upon me] make yourself comfortable over here? Go home! (to your house)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) first-person singular present passive indicative of <a href="bword://στρώνω">στρώνω</a>; set about doing something, with great devotion</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πρέπει να <b>στρωθώ</b> να μάθω όλα τα χημικά στοιχεία γιατί αύριο έχουμε διαγώνισμα. - I must set about learning all the chemical elements, because tomorrow we have a test.</div><b>στρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of beds or tables) to make (put sheet on)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of other areas) to cover (with a sheet)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of carpets) to lay</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of clothes) to smooth out, removes creases from</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of roads) to pave</div>
  <div class='def'>6. and see: <a href="bword://στρώνομαι">στρώνομαι</a> (senses for the passive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek στρώνω, a metaplasm of Koine Greek στρωννύω -thematicization of Ancient Greek στρώννῡμι, a variant form of στόρνυμι- based on the past tense stem στρωσ- of ἔστρωσα -Modern έστρωσα- in the pattern of φθάνω - ἔφθᾰσα and integration to the -ώνω verb-system. Ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *sterh₃- (“to spread, extend”).</div><b>στυλοβάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στυλοβάτες, feminine στυλοβάτρια or στυλοβάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pillar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stylobate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) backer, financial backer</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) founder, mainstay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στυλοβάτης.</div><b>στυλογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στυλογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pen, ballpoint, biro</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pen, fountain pen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French stylographe (“pen”).</div><b>στυλό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pen, ballpoint, biro</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pen, fountain pen</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pen, gladius (solid, internal structure of squid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French stylo (“pen”), which is an abbreviation of stylographe (“pen”), from English stylograph.</div><b>στυλό διαρκείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ballpoint pen, ballpoint, biro</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;duration pen&quot;.</div><b>στυλός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στυλοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στυλό">στυλό</a></div><b>στυμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine στυμμένη, neuter στυμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>squeezed, wrung</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of στύβομαι, passive voice of στύβω (“I squeeze”).</div><b>στυφάδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στυφάδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στιφάδο">στιφάδο</a></div><b>στχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://στρατάρχης">στρατάρχης</a></div><b>στόκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. putty, stucco</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) stupid, silly</div><b>στόλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στόλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fleet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στόλος.</div><b>στόμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στόματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mouth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. person</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μάνα του είχε δέκα <b>στόματα</b> να θρέψει. - His mother had ten mouths to feed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στόμα, from Proto-Indo-European *stomn̥, *stomen- (“mouth, muzzle”).</div><b>στόμαχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στόμαχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://στομάχι">στομάχι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek στόμαχος.</div><b>στόμιο υδροληψίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στόμια υδροληψίας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydrant, fire hydrant</div><b>στόχαστρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural στόχαστρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sight, sights, gunsight, foresight (aiming device on weapon)</div><b>στόχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural στόχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. target</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aim, intent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στόχος.</div><b>στύβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to squeeze, wring (to force out liquid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στείβω. Its stems στει-, στoι- and στι- resulted into multiple spellings of the modern verb and some of its derivatives. Also, the stem στυ‑ from ancient verb στῡ́φω introduced through paretymology one more spelling. See notes.</div><b>στύση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural στύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>erection (of the penis)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek στύω + -ση (-si).</div><b>συγγένεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγγένειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. kinship, affinity (related by blood, marriage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. affinity, similarity (similar in properties or appearance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγγένειᾰ</div><b>συγγενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγγενής, neuter συγγενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. related</div>
  <div class='def'>2. congenital</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek συγγενής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συγγενείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relative, relation</div>
  <div class='ex'>μακρινός συγγενής (distant relative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek συγγενής.</div><b>συγγενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγγενική, neuter συγγενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related, interrelated, relational</div><b>συγγνώμη</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sorry (an apology)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. excuse me (UK), pardon me (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγγνώμη and semantic loan from French pardon.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγγνώμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forgiveness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγγνώμη and semantic loan from French pardon.</div><b>συγγράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be written</div><b>συγγράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to author, write</div><b>συγγραφέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συγγραφείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>writer, author</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγγραφεύς</div><b>συγγραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγγραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>writing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγγραφή.</div><b>συγγραφεύς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συγγραφεῖς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) Older form of <a href="bword://συγγραφέας">συγγραφέας</a></div><b>συγκάλυψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκαλύψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. camouflage, disguise (action &amp; result)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cover-up, coverup (action &amp; result)</div><b>συγκάτοικος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συγκάτοικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>housemate, flatmate, roommate</div><b>συγκέντρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκεντρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. centralisation (UK), centralization (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mental concentration</div>
  <div class='def'>3. congregation (large gathering of people)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. assembly (legislative body)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. concentration</div>
  <div class='def'>6. accumulation</div>
  <div class='def'>7. gathering</div>
  <div class='ex'>ασχολούμαι με τη συγκέντρωση αποδείξεων για την αθωότητα του κατηγορούμενου - I am working on gathering evidence for the innocence of the accused</div><b>συγκίνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκινήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emotion, excitement, thrill, stir (emotional affection)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπήρξε μεγάλη <b>συγκίνηση</b> στην κηδεία του. - There was great emotion at his funeral.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συγκίνησις, from συν- (sun-, “with”) + κινέω.</div><b>συγκαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to call together to assembly; summon (others) to meeting; invite (guests) to gathering; convoke, convene, congregate, assemble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek σῠγκᾰλῶ, contracted form of Ancient Greek σῠγκᾰλέω, συγ- (syg-, “with, together”) equivalent to συν- (syn-) + καλώ.</div><b>συγκατάθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. consent (voluntary agreement or permission)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. acquiescence (silent or passive assent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συγκατάθεσις with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>συγκατοίκηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκατοικήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cohabitation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. concubinage</div><b>συγκεκριμένα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συγκεκριμένος">συγκεκριμένος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. specifically</div>
  <div class='def'>2. concretely</div><b>συγκεκριμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγκεκριμένη, neuter συγκεκριμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. concrete (not abstract)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. specific (not vague)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συγκεκριμένος, perfect passive participle of Ancient Greek συγκρίνω. With semantic loan from French concret.</div><b>συγκεντρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (passive) to concentrate, focus, gather one&#x27;s attention or mental effort onto a particular object or activity</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Συγκεντρωνόμαστε</b> στις μελέτες μας. - We concentrate on our studies.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to gather, collect, bring together</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to centralise (UK), centralize (US),</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συν- (syn-) + κέντρο + -ώνω, formed as a calque of French concentrer. First attested in 1814.</div><b>συγκινημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine συγκινημένη, neuter συγκινημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moved, touched (affected emotionally) (often implies tears or crying)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φάνηκε πολύ <b>συγκινημένη</b> στην απονομή των βραβείων. - She looked very moved at the [ceremony of] prize awards.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of συγκινούμαι, passive voice of συγκινώ (“I move emotionally”).</div><b>συγκινητικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συγκινητικός">συγκινητικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συγκινητικός">συγκινητικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συγκινητικός">συγκινητικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>movingly, touchingly (in an emotionally affecting manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μια <b>συγκινητικά</b> ωραία ιστορία - a movingly lovely story</div><b>συγκινητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγκινητική, neuter συγκινητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moving, touching (emotionally affecting)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το τραγούδι είναι πολύ <b>συγκινητικό</b>. - That song is very moving.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συγκινητικός, from συν- (sun-, “with”) + κινέω + -τικός.</div><b>συγκινητικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) movingly, touchingly (in an emotionally affecting manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>μια <b>συγκινητικώς</b> ωραία ιστορία - a movingly lovely story</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested 1864.</div><b>συγκινούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be moved, be touched (be affected emotionally) (often implies tears or crying)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Συγκινήθηκα</b> όταν είδα το φέρετρο. - I was moved (to tears) when I saw the coffin.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to care, be interested</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ό,τι και να του πεις αυτουνού, δε <b>συγκινείται</b>! - No matter what you tell him, he doesn&#x27;t care!</div><b>συγκινώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to move, touch, affect, thrill, excite (affect emotionally)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με <b>συγκίνησε</b> πολύ αυτή η ταινία. - That film really touched me.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to appeal to, interest</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν με <b>συγκινεί</b> η μοντέρνα κλασσική. - Modern classical (music) doesn&#x27;t appeal to me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγκινῶ, contracted form of συγκινέω from συν- (sun-, “with”) + κινέω. Equiavalent to (συν) συγ- + κινώ.</div><b>συγκλίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to converge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συγκλίνω, from Ancient Greek συγκλίνω, with semantic loan from French converger. By surface analysis, συγ- (syg-) + κλίνω.</div><b>συγκλητικόν</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Katharevousa form of συγκλητικό, accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://συγκλητικός">συγκλητικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. Katharevousa form of συγκλητικό, nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://συγκλητικός">συγκλητικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of συγκλητικό, accusative singular of <a href="bword://συγκλητικός">συγκλητικός</a></div><b>συγκλητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγκλητική, neuter συγκλητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>senatorial</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συγκλητικές</b> αποφάσεις - senatorial decisions</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σῠγκλητῐκός, a calque from the Latin senātōrius. From σῠ́γκλητος + -κός, equivalent to συγ- (syg-, “with, together”) + κλητός from verbal root κλη- of συγκαλώ, συν- (syn-) + καλώ, from Ancient Greek σῠγκᾰλέω.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συγκλητικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>senator, member of senate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σῠγκλητῐκός, a calque from the Latin senātōrius. From σῠ́γκλητος + -κός, equivalent to συγ- (syg-, “with, together”) + κλητός from verbal root κλη- of συγκαλώ, συν- (syn-) + καλώ, from Ancient Greek σῠγκᾰλέω.</div><b>συγκοινωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκοινωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transport, transportation (means of conveyance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek συγκοινωνία, from Ancient Greek συγκοινωνέω, from συγ- (sug-) + κοινωνέω, ultimately from κοινός.</div><b>συγκοινωνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγκοινωνιακή, neuter συγκοινωνιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transport (attributive), transportational</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συγκοινωνιακές</b> συνδέσεις - transport links</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συγκοινωνί(α)) + -ακός.</div><b>συγκολλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to solder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγκολλῶ. By surface analysis, συγ- (syg-) + κολλώ.</div><b>συγκομιδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκομιδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. harvest (the process or the product)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) the accumulation of goods or riches</div><b>συγκοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. syncope, cardiac arrest, fainting, missed heartbeats</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έπαθε <b>συγκοπή</b>. <b>Συγκοπή</b> καρδίας, που δυστυχώς ήταν μοιραία. - He suffered a syncope. A cardiac arrest, which unfortunately was fatal.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. syncopation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. The loss or elision of a vowel between two consonants from the interior of a word. Similar to syncope, contraction.</div>
  <div class='ex'>e.g. περιπατώ &gt; περπατώ (perpató) syncope of -ι-</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek συγκοπή.</div><b>συγκρίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to compare (to assess the similarities and differences between two or more things)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to compare (to declare two things to be similar in some respect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγκρίνω. By surface analysis, συγ- (syg-) + κρίνω.</div><b>συγκρίσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγκρίσιμη, neuter συγκρίσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comparable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σύγκρισ(η)) + -ιμος (-imos).</div><b>συγκρατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>restrain, support, hold</div><b>συγκριτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://συγκριτικός">συγκριτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comparatively</div><b>συγκριτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγκριτική, neuter συγκριτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(comparative) comparative</div>
  <div class='ex'>συγκριτικός βαθμός του επιθέτου (the comparative degree of the adjective)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συγκριτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(comparative) comparative</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο θετικός, ο συγκριτικός και ο υπερθετικός (the positive, the comparative and superlative)</div><b>συγκροτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to form, compose, organise (UK), organize (USA)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συγκροτέω, συγκροτῶ. Morphologically, συγ- (“together”) + κροτώ (“make rattling strong sound”). The association of sound, from the ancient ironmaker (striking two things together). Also ancient is the figurative meaning of &quot;creation, formation&quot;.</div><b>συγκρουόμενα αυτοκινητάκια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://συγκρουόμενο">συγκρουόμενο</a> αυτοκινητάκι</div><b>συγκρουόμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συγκρουόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural) bumper car, dodgem</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγαμε στο λούνα παρκ, στα <b>συγκρουόμενα</b> και περάσαμε πολύ ωραία. - We went to the amusement park, at the dodgems, and we had a really good time.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nominalised neuter of the participle συγκρουόμενος, in elliptical use of the term συγκρουόμενο αυτοκινητάκι.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://συγκρουόμενος">συγκρουόμενος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://συγκρουόμενος">συγκρουόμενος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>συγκρουόμενο αυτοκινητάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συγκρουόμενα αυτοκινητάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>full utterance of <a href="bword://το">το</a> <a href="bword://συγκρουόμενο">συγκρουόμενο</a></div><b>συγκρουόμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine συγκρουόμενη, neuter συγκρουόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. colliding</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conflicting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of συγκρούομαι, a verb with no active forms.</div><b>συγκρούομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to collide, clash</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συγκρούομαι, passive of συγκρούω.</div><b>συγκρότημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συγκροτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. complex, unit, block</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rock group, rock band, ensemble</div>
  <div class='def'>3. set</div>
  <div class='def'>4. facility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συγκροτώ + -μα (-ma)</div><b>συγκυρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκυρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conjuncture, (set of) circumstances, situation (combination of events or circumstances)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κατά <b>συγκυρία</b> - by chance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγκυρία.</div><b>συγκόλληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκολλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>welding</div><b>συγκόπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be shortened</div><b>συγκόπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to shorten, contract, abridge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cut short a sound or syllable in or of a word with syncope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγκόπτω. By surface analysis, συγ- (συν-) (“together”) + κόπτω (“cut”).</div><b>συγχέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(imperfective, transitive, usually) to confuse (to fail to correctly distinguish between; to muddle up, to mix up)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγχέω, from συγ- (sug-, “syn-”) + χέω.</div><b>συγχαίρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to congratulate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literally) to rejoice together with (rare)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγχαίρω. Morphologically, from συγ- (συν-) + χαίρω.</div><b>συγχαρήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of συγχαρήκαν, third-person plural simple past of <a href="bword://συγχαίρω">συγχαίρω</a></div><b>συγχαρητήρια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://συγχαρητήριος">συγχαρητήριος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συγχαρητήριος">συγχαρητήριος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>congratulations!</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>congratulations</div><b>συγχαρητήριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγχαρητήρια, neuter συγχαρητήριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>congratulatory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συγχαίρω + -τήριος.</div><b>συγχορδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγχορδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chord (combination of notes)</div><b>συγχρονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to synchronise (UK), synchronize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to modernise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συγχρονίζω, from σύγχρονος, from συν- (sun-) + χρόνος.</div><b>συγχρονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συγχρονισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synchronisation (UK), synchronization (US), synchronism</div>
  <div class='ex'>τέλειος <b>συγχρονισμός</b> - perfect synchronization, perfect timing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συγχρονισμός &lt; Ancient Greek συγχρονίζω and semantic loan from French synchronisation and simultanéité.</div><b>συγχρόνως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>simultaneously, contemporaneously</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σύγχρονος + -ως.</div><b>συγχωνεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to merge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συγχωνεύω with semantic loan from French fusionner. By surface analysis, συγ- (syg-) + χωνεύω.</div><b>συγχωράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://συγχωρώ">συγχωρώ</a></div><b>συγχωρητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συγχωρητική, neuter συγχωρητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>forgiving (tending to forgive)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συγχωρώ + -τικός.</div><b>συγχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to forgive, pardon, excuse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to pardon, excuse</div>
  <div class='ex'>με <b>συγχωρείτε</b> - pardon (UK), excuse me (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συγχωρῶ, contracted form of συγχωρέω.</div><b>συγχύζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to get upset, to get worked up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to get confused, to get flustered</div><b>συγχύζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to upset</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to confuse, to fluster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek, from Ancient Greek σύγχυσις, with semantic loan from Italian turbare in the sense &#x27;to upset&#x27;.</div><b>συγχώνευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγχωνεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. merging, merger (the act whereby things are merged)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. merger</div>
  <div class='def'>3. merger</div>
  <div class='def'>4. melding</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συγχωνεύ(ω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>συγχώρεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. forgiveness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. absolution</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned variant of συχώρεση influenced by συγχώρηση.</div><b>συζήτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συζητήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>discussion, debate, conversation (verbal exchange concerning a particular topic)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συζήτησις, from συζητῶ + -σῐς, the former a contracted form of συζητέω, from σῠν- + ζητέω.</div><b>συζητάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to discuss, debate, talk over</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χαίρομαι πάντοτε να ακούω όσα έχετε να πείτε και να <b>συζητάω</b> μαζί σας διάφορα θέματα. - I always enjoy listening to what you have to say and discussing various issues with you.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συζυτ(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω. Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συζητῶ, contracted form of συζητέω, from συ- (su-) + ζητέω. Also, a semantic loan from French discuter.</div><b>συζητημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine συζητημένη, neuter συζητημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>discussed, talked about</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of συζητιέμαι, passive voice of συζητάω, συζητώ (“discuss”) and of συζητούμαι, passive voice of συζητώ.</div><b>συζητιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be discussed, be talked about</div><b>συζητούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be discussed, be talked about</div><b>συζητώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://συζητάω">συζητάω</a></div><b>συζυγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συζυγική, neuter συζυγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conjugal, married, marital, connubial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σύζυγ(ος)) + -ικός, a calque of French conjugal.</div><b>συκάμινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συκάμινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym of <a href="bword://μούρο">μούρο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare συκαμινιά, Ancient Greek σῡκᾰ́μῑνος.</div><b>συκή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σύκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fig leaf (when used as an item of clothing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) fig leaf (something used to hide an inconvenient truth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συκέα.</div><b>συκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fig tree</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) Homosexual or effeminate man.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συκέα.</div><b>συκοφάντης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine συκοφάντισσα or συκοφάντρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calumniator, slanderer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συκοφάντης. But how a φάντης (“who shows”) σῦκα came to mean &quot;informant&quot;, is debated.</div><b>συκοφαντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to slander, to calumniate, to defame</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συκοφαντῶ, contracted form of συκοφαντέω</div><b>συκώτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συκώτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. liver</div>
  <div class='def'>2. liver</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek συκώτιον, a diminutive of the neuter of Hellenistic Koine Greek συκωτός from the phrase ἧπαρ συκωτόν. From the ancient σῦκον. Similarly, derivation from an expression, for the Latin fīcātum, from iecur ficatum.</div><b>συλλέγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to collect, gather; to pick fruit from trees or plants</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to collect, gather; to gather, accumulate, reposit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to collect, gather; to gather information</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to make a collection of items</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συλλέγω</b> νομίσματα, γραμματόσημα - I collect coins, stamps</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συλλέγω. Sense make a collection, semantic loan from French collectionner.</div><b>συλλέκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συλλέκτες, feminine συλλέκτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. collector (person who collects things)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. collector (apparatus for collecting things)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ηλιακός συλλέκτης (solar collector)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek συλλέκτης, from συλλέγω. Morphologically, from συλ- (συν-) (“plus”) + λέγω (“ancient sense: choose”) Sense collector of items, semantic loan from French collectionneur. Sense apparatus for collection, semantic loan from French collecteur.</div><b>συλλέκτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συλλέκτριες, masculine συλλέκτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>collector (person who collects things)</div><b>συλλαβή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συλλαβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>syllable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek συλλαβή.</div><b>συλλαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to arrest (take into custody)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to conceive; to develop an idea</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to conceive; to understand</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to conceive; to become pregnant</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to receive, accept into the mind, understand</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to detect signals</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συλλαμβάνω.</div><b>συλληφθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of συλλαμβάνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://συλλαμβάνω">συλλαμβάνω</a>:; third-person singular dependent passive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of συλλαμβάνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://συλλαμβάνω">συλλαμβάνω</a>:; passive nonfinite form</div><b>συλλογή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συλλογές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. collection, compilation, assortment, picking</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάνω <b>συλλογή</b> νομισμάτων. - I make a collection of coins. (I collect coins)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meditation, mental absorption, preoccupation, musing, contemplation, care, concern</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έπεσε σε βαθιά <b>συλλογή</b>. - literally: She/He fell into deep thought.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συλλογή, from συλλέγω, from σύν (συλ-), “with”) + λέγω.
For sense &quot;mediation&quot; inherited from Koine Greek συλλογή from Ancient Greek συλλογή.</div><b>συλλογίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to contemplate, ponder, think about, ruminate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συλλογίζομαι.</div><b>συλλογιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://συλλογίζομαι">συλλογίζομαι</a></div><b>συλλογισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reasoning</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συλλογισμός. By surface analysis, συλλογίζομαι + -μός.</div><b>συλλυπητήρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>condolences (expression used to give sympathy to a person who is mourning)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δέξου τα <b>συλλυπητήριά</b> μου. - Accept my condolences.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://συλλυπητήριος">συλλυπητήριος</a></div><b>συλλυπητήριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συλλυπητήρια, neuter συλλυπητήριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>expressing grief, offering condolences</div>
  <div class='ex'>συλλυπητήριο μήνυμα (message of condolence)</div><b>συμβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to contribute</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to join, to meet (to converge and finally touch or intersect)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to contract (to make an agreement or contract; to covenant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμβάλλω. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + βάλλω.</div><b>συμβάν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμβάντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>event, incident, occurrence (instance of something happening)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> In the singular, a back-formation from learnedly borrowed Ancient Greek συμβάντα, plural aorist participle of συμβαίνω.</div><b>συμβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμβίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wife</div>
  <div class='def'>2. better half (slang term)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek συμβία, from Ancient Greek σύμβιος.</div><b>συμβαίνει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to happen, to occur, to take place</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Συνέβη</b> στην Αθήνα. - It happened in Athens.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek συμβαίνω, at the third person of present tense, συμβαίνει.</div><b>συμβαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>* see: <a href="bword://συμβαίνει">συμβαίνει</a></div><b>συμβιβασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμβιβασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. compromise (the settlement of differences by arbitration or by consent reached by mutual concessions)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. compromise (the settlement of differences by arbitration or by consent reached by mutual concessions); settlement</div>
  <div class='ex'>δικαστικός <b>συμβιβασμός</b> - court settlement</div>
  <div class='def'>3. compromise (a committal to something derogatory or objectionable; a prejudicial concession)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμβιβασμός with semantic loan from French compromis and English compromise. By surface analysis, συμβιβασ-) + -μός.</div><b>συμβιωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμβιωτική, neuter συμβιωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>symbiotic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French symbiotique. By surface analysis, συμβιωτ(ής)) + -ικός.</div><b>συμβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. junction, join</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contribution</div><b>συμβολίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to symbolize (to be symbolic of; to represent)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to symbolize (to use symbols; to represent ideas symbolically)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σύμβολ(ο)) + -ίζω, a calque of French symboliser.</div><b>συμβολαιογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συμβολαιογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. notary (EU)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. public notary (UK)</div><b>συμβολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμβολική, neuter συμβολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. symbolic (pertaining to a symbol)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. symbolic (implicitly representing or referring to another thing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμβολικός with semantic loan from French symbolique. By surface analysis, σύμβολο + -ικός.</div><b>συμβολισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμβολισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. symbolism (representation of a concept through symbols or underlying meanings of objects or qualities)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. notation (the act, process, method, or an instance of representing by a system or set of marks, signs, figures, or characters)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. notation (a system of characters, symbols, or abbreviated expressions used in an art or science or in mathematics or logic to express technical facts or quantities)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. symbolism (an art movement originating in late 19th-century France and Belgium, seeking to represent absolute truths symbolically through language and metaphorical images, mainly as a reaction against naturalism and realism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French symbolisme. By surface analysis, σύμβολ(ο)) + -ισμός.</div><b>συμβολομεταφραστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμβολομεταφραστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>assembler (computer program that turns assembly language source code into machine language)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neologism of the end of 20th century. Semantic loan from English assembler. Analysed as σύμβολ(ο) (“symbol”) + -ο- (“linking vowel”) + μεταφραστής (“translator”).</div><b>συμβουλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμβουλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>advice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμβουλή.</div><b>συμβουλευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμβουλευτική, neuter συμβουλευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. advisory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consultative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμβουλευτικός with semantic loan from French consultatif. By surface analysis, συμβουλεύ(ω)) + -τικός.</div><b>συμβουλεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to consult</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συμβουλεύομαι</b> δικηγόρο - I consult a lawyer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σῠμβουλεύομαι with semantic loan from French consulter.</div><b>συμβουλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to advise, counsel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμβουλεύω. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + βουλή + -εύω.</div><b>συμβούλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμβούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. board (committee that manages the business of an organization)</div>
  <div class='ex'>διοικητικό <b>συμβούλιο</b> - board of directors</div>
  <div class='def'>2. council (committee of elected members that leads or governs)</div>
  <div class='ex'>οικογενειακό <b>συμβούλιο</b> - family meeting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμβούλιον, a Hellenistic semantic loan from Latin consilium. For sense &quot;managing board&quot;, a free translation or semantic loan for the French conseil.</div><b>συμβόλαιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμβόλαια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contract, policy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Coordinate term: συμφωνητικό (symfonitikó)</div><b>συμμαθητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμμαθητές, feminine συμμαθήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. classmate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) schoolmate, fellow pupil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμμαθητής. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + μαθητής.</div><b>συμμαχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμμαχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alliance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμμαχία. By surface analysis, σύμμαχ(ος)) + -ία.</div><b>συμμετέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to participate, take part</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek συμμετέχω. Morphologically, (συν-) συμ- ((syn-) sym-, “together, syn-”) + μετέχω &lt; (μετα-) μετ- ((meta-) met-) + έχω</div><b>συμμετέχων</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine συμμετέχουσα, neuter συμμετέχον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>active present participle of <a href="bword://συμμετέχω">συμμετέχω</a>: participating</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμμετέχοντες, feminine συμμετέχουσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>participant</div><b>συμμετοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμμετοχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. participation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. involvement (state of being involved)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμμετοχή with semantic loan from French participation.</div><b>συμμετρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμμετρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>symmetry, harmony, proportion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συμμετρία</div><b>συμμετρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμμετρική, neuter συμμετρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>symmetrical, symmetric, regular</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French symétrique, from symétr(ie) &lt; Ancient Greek συμμετρ(ία) f) + -ique &lt; -ικός A different sense for the Hellenistic συμμετρικός.</div><b>συμμορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμμορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gang, band (group of criminals)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συμμορία</b> ληστών - gang of robbers/thieves</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gang, crew, bunch (group of friends or associates)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. symmoria (group of wealthy citizens in Classical Athens assessed together for the purposes of taxation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμμορία, with semantic loan from French bande in the sense &#x27;gang, band&#x27;.</div><b>συμμορφώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to conform, to comply (to adapt to something by more closely matching it, especially something normative)</div><b>συμμορφώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to conform, to make conform, to make comply</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to conform, to comply (to adapt to something by more closely matching it, especially something normative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμμορφῶ with -ώνω ending and semantic loan from French se conformer.</div><b>συμμόρφωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμμορφώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conformity, conformance, compliance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμμόρφωσις with semantic loan from French conformité and English conformity. By surface analysis, συμμορφώνω + -ση (-si).</div><b>συμπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμπάθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fellow feeling, sympathy</div><b>συμπάσχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to suffer together, feel the same pain as someone else</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sympathize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συμπάσχω.</div><b>συμπέθερος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμπέθεροι, feminine συμπεθέρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relation by marriage; (male) in-law</div>
  <div class='def'>2. co-father-in-law</div><b>συμπέρασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμπεράσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. deduction, conclusion (results of reasoning)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conclusion (final summary part of document)</div><b>συμπίεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. compression (the act of compressing, or the state of being compressed)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. compression</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συμπίεση</b> δεδομένων - data compression</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμπίεσις with -ση (-si) ending and semantic loan from French compression. By surface analysis, συμπιέζω + -ση (-si).</div><b>συμπίπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to coincide (to occur at the same time)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to coincide (to occupy exactly the same space)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to coincide, to concur (to correspond or agree)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμπίπτω. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + πίπτω.</div><b>συμπαίκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμπαίκτες, feminine συμπαίκτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. teammate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. partner (someone with whom one plays on the same side in a game, such as card games or doubles tennis.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμπαίκτης. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + παίκτης.</div><b>συμπαγής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμπαγής, neuter συμπαγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. solid, massive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. solid, tubeless (tyre, tire)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. united (people)</div><b>συμπαθάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to forgive</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Συμπάθα</b> με, αλλά δεν μπορώ να σου δανείσω χρήματα. - Forgive me, but I cannot lend you and money.</div><b>συμπαθητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμπαθητική, neuter συμπαθητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sympathetic, pleasant, agreeable</div><b>συμπαθιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be liked (reciprocal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου φαίνεται ότι αυτοί οι δυο <b>συμπαθιούνται</b>. - It seems to me that these two are attracted to each other.</div><b>συμπαθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to like</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συμπαθέω.</div><b>συμπαράσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>support (moral or material backing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned formation from συμπαρίσταμαι, modelled on παρίσταμαι → παράσταση.</div><b>συμπατριώτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμπατριώτες, feminine συμπατριώτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compatriot, fellow countryman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμπατριώτης. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + πατριώτης.</div><b>συμπεθέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμπεθέρες, masculine συμπέθερος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female relation by marriage; female in-law</div>
  <div class='def'>2. co-mother-in-law</div><b>συμπεθερεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to become related to somebody by marriage</div><b>συμπεθεριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://συμπεθερεύω">συμπεθερεύω</a></div><b>συμπερίληψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inclusion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Deverbal from συμπεριλαμβάνω.</div><b>συμπεριλαμβάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>include</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμπεριλαμβάνω. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + περιλαμβάνω &lt; περι- (peri-) + λαμβάνω.</div><b>συμπεριφέρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to behave</div><b>συμπεριφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμπεριφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. behaviour (UK), behavior (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>συμπεριφορά</b> είναι αυτή; - What kind of behaviour is that?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. attitude</div><b>συμπλέκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμπλέκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clutch</div><b>συμπλέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sail together (of ships)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to be in agreement</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι απόψεις μας <b>συμπλέουν</b>. - Our opinions are of one mind.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συμπλέω. Morphologically, from συν+π&gt;συμπ (“together”) + πλέω (“sail, float”).</div><b>συμπληρωματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμπληρωματική, neuter συμπληρωματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. complementary (acting as a complement; making up a whole with something else)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. supplementary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμπληρωματικός with semantic loan from French complémentaire and supplémentaire. By surface analysis, συμπληρωματ-) + -ικός.</div><b>συμπληρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fill, fill in, fill out</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συμπληρώστε</b> την αίτηση - fill in the form</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to complete</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συμπληρόω. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-, “syn-, with”) + πληρώνω.</div><b>συμπολίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συμπολίτες, feminine συμπολίτις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fellow citizen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμπολίτης. Morphologically, from συμ- (sym-) + πολίτης.</div><b>συμπολεμίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμπολεμίστριες, masculine συμπολεμιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. comrade (companion in war)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. comrade (fellow socialist)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συμ- (συν-) (sym- (syn-), “with”) + πολεμίστρια. First attested 1896.</div><b>συμπολεμιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμπολεμιστές, feminine συμπολεμίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brother-in-arms, comrade in arms, battle buddy, comrade (companion in war)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. comrade (fellow socialist)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συμ- (συν-) (sym- (syn-), “with”) + πολεμιστής. First attested 1887.</div><b>συμπονετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμπονετική, neuter συμπονετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compassionate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συμπονε-) + -τικός.</div><b>συμπρωταγωνιστές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of συμπρωταγωνιστής</div><b>συμπρωταγωνιστή</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive/accusative/vocative singular of συμπρωταγωνιστής</div><b>συμπρωταγωνιστών</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of συμπρωταγωνιστής</div><b>συμπτωματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμπτωματική, neuter συμπτωματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coincidental (occurring as or resulting from coincidence)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. symptomatic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμπτωματικός.</div><b>συμπυκνωμένο γάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>condensed milk</div><b>συμπυκνωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine συμπυκνωμένη, neuter συμπυκνωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concentrated, condensed, compressed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of συμπυκνώνομαι, passive voice of συμπυκνώνω.</div><b>συμπόνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compassion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συμπον(ώ)) + -ια (-ia).</div><b>συμπόσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμπόσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. symposium, convivium, drinking party</div>
  <div class='def'>2. symposium (a conference or other meeting for discussion of a topic, especially one in which the participants make presentations)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμπόσιον with semantic loan from English symposium (“conference or other meeting for discussion of a topic”).</div><b>συμπύκνωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμπυκνώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concentrate, condensate (material of increased concentration)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συμπυκνώνω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>συμφέρον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμφέροντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interest, rights (business connection with material advantage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter of participle συμφέρων. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek συμφέρον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://συμφέρων">συμφέρων</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised neuter of participle συμφέρων. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek συμφέρον.</div><b>συμφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in the third person) to benefit, to be of benefit (to), to be advantageous (to)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (impersonal) to be in the interests of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμφέρω. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + φέρω.</div><b>συμφέρων</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine συμφέρουσα, neuter συμφέρον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>which is to one&#x27;s interest, which is a good deal, advantageous</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συμφέρουσα</b> τιμή, <b>συμφέρουσα</b> προσφορά - advantageous price, advantageous offer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek συμφέρων. Present participle of συμφέρω. The neuter, also substantivised.</div><b>συμφεροντολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>self-interested</div><b>συμφιλίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμφιλιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reconciliation</div><b>συμφοιτητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμφοιτητές, feminine συμφοιτήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fellow student</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμφοιτητής with semantic loan from French condisciple. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + φοιτητής.</div><b>συμφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calamity</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: καταστροφή f (katastrofí)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συμφορά.</div><b>συμφυρμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συμφυρμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the result of mixing up of dissimilar elements</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) coexistance of people of different and disreputable background</div>
  <div class='def'>3. blend of two cognate elements (sounds, morphemes, phrases) which violates the rules of word formation. Associated with analogy and paretymology. e.g.</div>
  <div class='ex'>• διάβολος (/ˈðʝavolos/ “devil”) &amp; σατανάς (/sataˈnas/ “satan”) → διάτανος (/ˈðʝatanos/ “devil”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek συμφῡρμός from verb συμφύρω. For sense in linguistics, calque of French contamination.</div><b>συμφωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμφωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. agreement, concurrence, accordance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consent</div>
  <div class='def'>3. arrangement, pact</div>
  <div class='def'>4. symphony</div>
  <div class='def'>5. settlement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συμφωνία. Doublet of τσαμπούνα.</div><b>συμφωνημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine συμφωνημένη, neuter συμφωνημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>agreed upon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of συμφωνούμαι, passive voice of συμφωνώ (“agree”).</div><b>συμφωνητικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμφωνητικού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>agreement; memorandum of understanding</div>
  <div class='ex'>Coordinate term: συμβόλαιο (symvólaio)</div><b>συμφωνικά ποιήματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of συμφωνικό <a href="bword://ποίημα">ποίημα</a></div><b>συμφωνικό ποίημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμφωνικά ποιήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>symphonic poem</div><b>συμφωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συμφωνική, neuter συμφωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>symphonic</div><b>συμφωνούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be agreed and accepted</div><b>συμφωνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to agree, agree with</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to correspond, match</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to accord with, in harmony with, consistent with</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek συμφωνῶ, contracted form of συμφωνέω, from σύμφωνος + -έω.</div><b>συμφόρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμφορήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. congestion, overcrowding</div>
  <div class='def'>2. congestion</div><b>συμφύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, intransitive) to grow together or in the same time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συμφῠ́ομαι (“grow together”) of active συμφῠ́ω (“make to grow together”). Morphologically, from συμ- (συν-) (“together”) + φύομαι.</div><b>συμφώνως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accordingly, in accordance with</div><b>συμψηφίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to offset</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συμψηφίζω, from Ancient Greek συμψηφίζω. By surface analysis, συμ- (sym-) + ψηφίζω.</div><b>συν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) plus; positive quantity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) plus; useful addition</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) plus; plus sign</div>
  <div class='ex'>πέντε <b>συν</b> ένα ίσον έξι - five plus one equals six</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with accusative) plus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύν.</div><b>συνάδελφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συνάδελφοι, feminine συναδέλφισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colleague</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek συνάδελφος company”), from Ancient Greek συνάδελφος, with semantic loan from French confrère. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + αδελφός.</div><b>συνάθροιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναθροίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>assembly, gathering, meeting</div><b>συνάλλαγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συναλλάγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>currency exchange, bureau de change</div><b>συνάνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνάνθρωποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fellow human, fellow man</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συν- (syn-, “together, with”) + άνθρωπος.</div><b>συνάντηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναντήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. meeting, encounter (a coming together in one place, especially unplanned)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meeting (a gathering of persons for a purpose)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. encounter (a match between two opposing sides)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. meeting (a place or instance of junction or intersection; a confluence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συνάντησις, from συναντάω + -σις (-sis). By surface analysis, συναντάω + -ση (-si).</div><b>συνάπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to join, attach, append</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to contract, enter into, conclude (an agreement, marriage, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek συνάπτω. By surface analysis, συν- (“togehter”) + the ancient verb ἅπτω.</div><b>συνάρτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναρτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. function</div>
  <div class='ex'>τριγωνομετρική <b>συνάρτηση</b> - trigonometric function</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interdependence, interrelation</div><b>συνάφεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνάφειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relevance, pertinence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relation</div><b>συνάχι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>catarrh</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνάγχη.</div><b>συνέδριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνέδρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>congress, colloquium, conference (gathering of people for the purpose of discussion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνέδριον.</div><b>συνέλευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνελεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>assembly, congress, diet, synod (legislative bodies)</div><b>συνένζυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνένζυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coenzyme</div><b>συνέντευξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεντεύξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>interview</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συνέντευξις, from συν- (syn-) + Ancient Greek ἔντευξις.</div><b>συνέντευξη τύπου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεντεύξεις τύπου)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>press conference</div><b>συνέπεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνέπειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. consistency (reliability or uniformity; the quality of being consistent)</div>
  <div class='ex'>με <b>συνέπεια</b> - consistently</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consequence (an effect; something that follows a cause as a result)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κατά <b>συνέπεια</b> - as a consequence, consequently, accordingly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συνέπεια, from συν- (sun-) + ἔπ(ος)) + -εια (-eia), with semantic loan from French suite and conséquence.</div><b>συνέρχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with someone) to meet, assemble (also see sense 2); to meet (for common purpose), cohere</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with someone) to meet, assemble (also see sense 2); to have sex</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (deponent, intransitive) to recover (also see sense 1); to recover (from illness or fainting)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (deponent, intransitive) to recover (also see sense 1); to calm down, get a grip</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σύνελθε</b>! - Calm down!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνέρχομαι (“come together, assemble, meet”). Morphologically, from συν- (“together”) + έρχομαι (“come”).
For sense: recover: Semantic loan from French revenir à soi</div><b>συνέχεια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. continuously, nonstop (without pause)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σηκώθηκε και μιλούσε τρεις ώρες <b>συνέχεια</b>. - He got up and spoke nonstop for three hours.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. continuously, consecutively (without break)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα δωμάτιά τους είναι <b>συνέχεια</b> το ένα στο άλλο. - Their rooms are consecutively next to one another.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνέχεια, from συνεχής + -εια (-eia).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνέχειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. continuation, continuity, continuance (the quality of being continuous without interruption)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η αδιάσπαστη <b>συνέχεια</b> της ελληνικής γλώσσας - the unbroken continuity of the Greek language</div>
  <div class='def'>2. continuation, rest (that which extends, increases, supplements, or carries on)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποτέ δε μάθαμε τη <b>συνέχεια</b> της ιστορίας. - We never found out the rest of the story.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bits and pieces, little by little, bit by bit (information or parts presented gradually but still making up a cohesive whole)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έμαθα την ιστορία σε <b>συνέχειες</b>, διότι διέκοπταν συνεχώς. - I learnt the story in bits and pieces because they were constantly interrupting.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. hyphen</div>
  <div class='def'>5. symphysis (cartilaginous material that adjoins and facilitates the junction of bones)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ενδομητρική <b>συνέχεια</b> - pubic symphysis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνέχεια, from συνεχής + -εια (-eia).</div><b>συνήγορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συνήγοροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lawyer, advocate, counsel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) advocate (person who argues the case of another)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly) ombudsman</div><b>συνήθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνήθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>habit (habitual action)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συνήθεια.</div><b>συνήθης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνήθης, neuter σύνηθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usual</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek συνήθης.</div><b>συνήθως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usually, generally</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνηθῶς, and related to συνήθης; see there for more.</div><b>συνίζηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνιζήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synizesis (the fusion of two syllables into one without the formation of a recognized diphthong, especially to fit a certain poetic meter)</div><b>συναίνεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναινέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. consent (voluntary agreement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>με κοινή <b>συναίνεση</b> - by common consent, by mutual consent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consensus</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα αυστηρά οικονομικά μέτρα απαιτούν κοινωνική <b>συναίνεση</b>. - The stringent economic measures require a public consensus.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συναίνεσις. By surface analysis, συναινώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>συναίρεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναιρέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synaeresis (contraction of two vowels into a diphthong or a long vowel)</div>
  <div class='ex'>e.g. of [ao] to [o] as in τραγουδάω (tragoudáo) &gt; τραγουδώ (tragoudó, “I sing”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συναίρεσις, from Ancient Greek συναιρέω, συναιρῶ, from συν- (sun-, “with, together”) + ἀϝείρω, ἀείρω, αἴρω, from Proto-Hellenic *aweřřō, from Proto-Indo-European *h₂wer-, equivalent to συναιρώ from συν- (syn-) + αίρω. Or from συν- (sun-, “with, together”) + αἱρέω, from Proto-Hellenic *héřřō, from Proto-Indo-European *ser- (“to bind, tie together”).</div><b>συναίσθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συναισθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>emotion, feelings, sentiment</div><b>συναίσθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>awareness, sense (the state or quality of being aware of something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: επίγνωση f (epígnosi)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συναίσθησις, with -ση (-si) suffix.</div><b>συναγερμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συναγερμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alert (state of readiness in case of emergency)</div>
  <div class='ex'>σε κατάσταση <b>συναγερμού</b> - on a state of alert</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alarm (a summons to arms, as on the approach of an enemy)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rally (mass gathering or mobilization)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. alarm (notice of danger)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συναγερμός</b> πυρκαγιάς - fire alarm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συναγερμός, with semantic loan from French alarme.</div><b>συναγωγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. synagogue (Jewish place of worship)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conclusion (reached by a meeting)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. gathering (of people)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. congregation (religious gathering)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συναγωγή.</div><b>συναγωνισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>competition (the act of competing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εκτός <b>συναγωνισμού</b> - out of competition; unrivalled</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek συναγωνισμός with adaptation of meaning to συναγωνίζομαι. By surface analysis, συναγωνίζ(ομαι)) + -μός.</div><b>συναινετικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consensually</div><b>συναινετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συναινετική, neuter συναινετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consensual (with consensus)</div><b>συναισθηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συναισθηματική, neuter συναισθηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. emotional</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sentimental</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the συναισθηματ- stem of συναίσθημα + -ικός, with semantic loan from French sentimental.</div><b>συναισθησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) synaesthesia</div><b>συναλλαγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναλλαγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. transaction, mutual exchange of material</div>
  <div class='def'>2. transaction, the transfer of funds into, out of, or from an account.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. commercial relationship</div>
  <div class='def'>4. supply made for political advantage</div><b>συναλλαγματική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναλλαγματικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>banker&#x27;s draft, bill of exchange</div><b>συναναστρέφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to associate with, spend time with friends</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned, from Koine Greek συναναστρέφομαι, of συναναστρέφω (“turn back together”). Morphologically from συν- (“together”) + ανα- (“again”) + στρέφω (“turn”).</div><b>συνανθρωπότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) shared humanity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συν- (syn-, “together, with”) + ανθρωπότητα, or equally συνάνθρωπος + -ότητα.</div><b>συναντάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to meet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συναντάω.</div><b>συναντώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://συναντάω">συναντάω</a></div><b>συναπτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συναπτή, neuter συναπτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consecutive</div><b>συναρμολόγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναρμολογήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>assembly, assemblage</div><b>συναρμόδιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συναρμόδια, neuter συναρμόδιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>co-competent, jointly competent (having joint jurisdiction or authority over a particular issue or question)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συν- (syn-) + αρμόδιος.</div><b>συναρπάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to enrapture, fascinate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συναρπάζω. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + αρπάζω.</div><b>συναρπαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συναρπαστική, neuter συναρπαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>exciting, enthralling, fascinating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συναρπάζω + -τικός.</div><b>συνασπισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνασπισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coalition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the tightly interlocking of shields in a phalanx of soldiers.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συν- (syn-, “with”) + ασπίς.</div><b>συναυλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συναυλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concert, performance</div>
  <div class='ex'>for recital, see ρεσιτάλ n (resitál)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συναυλία, from συν- (syn-) + αυλός.</div><b>συναφής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine or feminine (neuter συναφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>united, connected, related to</div><b>συνδέομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be connected → and see <a href="bword://συνδέω">συνδέω</a></div><b>συνδέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to connect, link, bind</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Συνδέστε</b> το καλώδιο με το ρεύμα. - Connect the wire to the current.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to associate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας <b>συνδέουν</b> κοινές αναμνήσεις. - Common memories connect us.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to have an affair</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Συνδέονται</b> εδώ και τρία χρόνια, αλλά δεν παντρεύονται. - They (have been) are together for three years, but they do not (won&#x27;t) get married.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to interface</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῠνδέω. Morphologically, from συν- (“together”) + δέω in its ancient sense.</div><b>συνδετήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνδετήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. paper clip</div>
  <div class='def'>2. staple (a wire fastener used to secure stacks of paper by penetrating all the sheets and curling around)</div><b>συνδιαλέγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to converse, to have a dialogue together</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συνδιαλέγομαι. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + διαλέγομαι.</div><b>συνδιασκέπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be in conference along with others</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συνδιασκέπτομαι. Morphologically, from συν- (“co-, plus”) + δια- (“through”) + σκέπτομαι (“think”).</div><b>συνδικάτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνδικάτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trade union</div><b>συνδικαλίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to form or to join a trade union</div><b>συνδρομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνδρομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. subscription (to society, club, magazine, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. help, assistance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνδρομή. For the modern sense of &quot;help&quot;, semantic loan from French secours.</div><b>συνδρομήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνδρομήτριες, masculine συνδρομητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subscriber</div><b>συνδρομητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνδρομητές, feminine συνδρομήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subscriber</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συνδρομή + -της (-tis).</div><b>συνδυάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be combined</div><b>συνδυάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to combine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to match</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to link</div><b>συνδυασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνδυασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. combination; combination of numbers for vault, safe or other locked object</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι κλέφτες ήξεραν τον <b>συνδυασμό</b> του χρηματοκιβωτίου. - The thieves knew the combination of the safe.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. combination; all the names of candidates in the same ballot paper</div>
  <div class='ex'>Λόγω του σκανδάλου, το όνομα του υποψήφιου Χ αποσύρθηκε από τον <b>συνδυασμό</b>. - Because of the scandal, the candidate X&#x27;s name was withdrawn from the list of candidates (&#x27;combination&#x27;) of the party.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. match (fit together, or make suitable for fitting together)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το φόρεμά της είχε έναν τολμηρό χρωματικό <b>συνδυασμό</b> πορτοκαλί και κόκκινου. - Her dress had a daring chromatic combination of pink and red.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συνδυασμός, from σύνδυο &lt; σύν + δύο.</div><b>συνείδηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνειδήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. conscience</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consciousness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνείδησις.</div><b>συνεδέθην</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) older form of συνδέθηκα, first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://συνδέομαι">συνδέομαι</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://συνδέω">συνδέω</a></div><b>συνεδρίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεδριάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>session, sitting, meeting (of a committee, council, etc. to conduct business)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συνεδριάζω + -ση (-si), a calque of French séance and German Sitzung.</div><b>συνειδητά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συνειδητός">συνειδητός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consciously</div><b>συνειδητοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to realise, be aware of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συνειδητός + ποιώ.</div><b>συνειδητό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://συνειδητός">συνειδητός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://συνειδητός">συνειδητός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conscious</div><b>συνειδητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνειδητή, neuter συνειδητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. conscious (deliberate, intentional, done with awareness of what one is doing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conscious (known or felt personally, internally by a person)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. conscious (aware of, sensitive to; observing and noticing, or being strongly interested in or concerned about)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συνείδη(ση)) + -τός, a calque of French conscient.</div><b>συνεισφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to contribute, make a contribution</div><b>συνεισφορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεισφορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contribution</div><b>συνεκδ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of συνεκδοχικά: by extension</div><b>συνεκτιμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to take into account, to factor in</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συνεκτιμῶ. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + εκτιμώ.</div><b>συνελήφθη</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of συλλαμβάνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://συλλαμβάνω">συλλαμβάνω</a></div><b>συνελήφθησαν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person plural simple past of συλλαμβάνομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://συλλαμβάνω">συλλαμβάνω</a></div><b>συνεννοούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to communicate (with), get on with (exchange information pleasantly/courteously)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς να <b>συνεννοηθούν</b> αφού ο ένας μιλά ισπανικά κι ο άλλος κινέζικα; - How can they communicate with each other since one speaks Spanish and the other Chinese?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to come to an understanding / agreement, agree to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι αδύνατον να <b>συνεννοηθείς</b> μαζί του, αφού επιμένει στις απόψεις του. - It&#x27;s impossible to come to an agreement with him since he insists on his views.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to deliberate, consult (consider carefully)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα κόμματα <b>συνεννοούνται</b> για να βρουν άκρη στο ζήτημα. - The parties are deliberating to come to an agreement on the issue.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek συνεννοοῦμαι, from middle voice of Koine Greek συνεννοέω, from σύν + ἐννοέω.</div><b>συνεννόηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεννοήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. consultation, deliberation, exchange of views</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι χώρες της ΕΕ κάνουν <b>συνεννοήσεις</b> για το κυπριακό ζήτημα. - The countries of the EU are having deliberations on the Cyprus issue.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. collusion (secret agreement for an illegal purpose)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει <b>συνεννόηση</b> με τους εχθρούς μας. - She&#x27;s in collusion with our enemies.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. communication</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν υπάρχει <b>συνεννόηση</b> ανάμεσα σε παιδιά και γονείς σήμερα. - There&#x27;s no communication between children and parents these days.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. understanding, agreement</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχουμε <b>συνεννόηση</b> μεταξύ μας. - We have an understanding with each other.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συνεννοη- + -ση (-si). First attested 1833.</div><b>συνεντεύξεις τύπου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://συνέντευξη">συνέντευξη</a> τύπου</div><b>συνεπής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνεπής, neuter συνεπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consistent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested around 1871. From συνέπεια + -ής, calque of French conséquent.</div><b>συνεπώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) consistently</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consequently, as a consequence, accordingly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συνεπ(ής)) + -ώς.</div><b>συνερίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be insulted, irritated, concerned, to take to heart</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην τον <b>συνερίζεσαι</b> και μη θυμώνεις όταν σε μαλώνει. - Don&#x27;t mind him and do not get angry when he scolds you.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. compete against, rival being jealous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek συνερίζομαι, middle voice of συνερίζω, from ancient συν- (syn-) + ἐρίζω for which, see ἔρις f.</div><b>συνεργάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to collaborate, to cooperate, to synergise (UK), synergize (US)</div><b>συνεργάσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνεργάσιμη, neuter συνεργάσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cooperative (willing to cooperate, give joint effort to a common purpose)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cooperative (willing to do as you are requested)</div><b>συνεργάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνεργάτες, feminine συνεργάτρια or συνεργάτιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coworker, collaborator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accomplice</div><b>συνεργάτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεργάτιδες, masculine συνεργάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coworker, collaborator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accomplice</div><b>συνεργάτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεργάτριες, masculine συνεργάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coworker, collaborator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accomplice</div><b>συνεργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. synergy, collaboration</div>
  <div class='def'>2. complicity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνεργίᾱ. By surface analysis, συνεργός + -ία.</div><b>συνεργασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεργασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>working together, cooperation, collaboration, partnership</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συν (syn, “with”) + εργασία.</div><b>συνεργατική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεργατικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cooperative, collective, (employee controlled business)</div><b>συνεργατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνεργατική, neuter συνεργατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. co-operative, cooperative, collaborative</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relating to workers&#x27; or employees&#x27; control</div><b>συνεργείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνεργεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. garage (for car repairs)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. workshop</div>
  <div class='def'>3. crew, gang (group working on joint enterprise)</div><b>συνεργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συνεργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coworker</div>
  <div class='def'>2. working together</div>
  <div class='def'>3. accessory</div>
  <div class='def'>4. accomplice (associate in the commission of a crime)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνεργός. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + έργο + -ος (-os).</div><b>συνεταιρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνεταιρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cooperative</div>
  <div class='ex'>αγροτικός συνεταιρισμός (farmers&#x27; cooperative)</div><b>συνεταιριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνεταιριστική, neuter συνεταιριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relating to the cooperative movement or cooperatives</div><b>συνεφαπτομένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνεφαπτομένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cotangent</div><b>συνεχές ρεύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) direct current</div><b>συνεχής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνεχής, neuter συνεχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constant, continuous, sustained</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek συνεχής, from συνέχω, combined form of συν- (sun-, “together, with”) + ἔχω. Compare Latin contineō (“to hold, to keep together”), combined form of con- (“together, with”) + teneō (“to have, to hold”).</div><b>συνεχίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be continued</div><b>συνεχίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to continue, keep going, carry on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνεχίζω.</div><b>συνεχόμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνεχόμενη, neuter συνεχόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. continuous, contiguous, abutting, adjacent (without intervening space)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consecutive, back-to-back (following, in succession, without interruption)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly formed as a passive present participle of Ancient Greek συνέχω. A calque of French contigu.</div><b>συνεχώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>continuously</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνεχῶς. By surface analysis, συνεχής + -ώς.</div><b>συνηθίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to become accustomed to, get used to</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be in the habit of</div>
  <div class='def'>3. for the impersonal 3rd persons, see συνηθίζεται</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek συνηθίζω, from συνήθης.</div><b>συνηθισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine συνηθισμένη, neuter συνηθισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ordinary, habitual, routine, common</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accustomed, used to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of συνηθίζομαι, passive voice of συνηθίζω (“become accustomed”).</div><b>συνημίτονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνημίτονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cosine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συν- (syn-) + ημίτονο.</div><b>συνθ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in compounds) combining form</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Abbreviation of συνθετικό.</div><b>συνθέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνθέτες, feminine συνθέτιδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. composer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. compositor</div><b>συνθέτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνθέτιδες, masculine συνθέτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. composer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. compositor</div><b>συνθέτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to compose (to make something by merging parts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to synthesize (to combine two or more things to produce a new product)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to compose, to make up (to constitute the components of a whole)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to compose (to construct by mental labor; to think up; particularly, to produce or create a literary or musical work)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συντίθημῐ. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + θέτω.</div><b>συνθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. treaty, agreement, mutual acceptance</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>Συνθήκη</b> για τη Μη Διάδοση των Πυρηνικών Όπλων - The Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) conditions, circumstances (a set of something make up a situation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ζούσε κάτω από πολύ δύσκολες <b>συνθήκες</b>. - She used to live under very difficult conditions.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek συνθήκη.</div><b>συνθετητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνθετητές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synthesizer</div><b>συνθετικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συνθετικός">συνθετικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. synthetically</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in a composite way</div>
  <div class='def'>3. from the aspect of composition</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://συνθετικό">συνθετικό</a> &quot;components, combining forms&quot;</div><b>συνθετικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνθετικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in compounds) combining form</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://συνθετικός">συνθετικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://συνθετικός">συνθετικός</a></div><b>συνθετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνθετική, neuter συνθετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. synthetic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. composite, component</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek συνθετικός.</div><b>συνθλίβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be compressed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be pressed psychologically</div><b>συνθλίβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to compress, crush</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to shatter, press psychologically</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συνθλίβω. By surface analysis, συν- (“together”) + θλίβω (“ancient: squeeze; modern: depress, make sad”).</div><b>συνθλιφτήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of συνθλιφτήκαν, third-person plural simple past of <a href="bword://συνθλίβομαι">συνθλίβομαι</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://συνθλίβω">συνθλίβω</a></div><b>συνιστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to recommend, suggest</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η έκθεση είναι εξαίρετη και <b>συνιστώ</b> την έγκρισή της. - The report is excellent and I recommend its adoption.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to consist of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συνίστημι; see there for more.</div><b>συννεφιασμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine συννεφιασμένη, neuter συννεφιασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perfect participle of συννεφιάζω: cloudy, clouded, clouded over, overclouded</div><b>συννυφάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συννυφάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>co-sister-in-law (one&#x27;s husband&#x27;s brother&#x27;s wife)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek σύννυμφος, with addition of -άδα ending akin to κουνιάδα. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + νύφ(η)) + -άδα.</div><b>συνοδεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνοδείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. escort, entourage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accompaniment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. procession</div><b>συνοδευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνοδευτική, neuter συνοδευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accompanying</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συνοδεύ(ω)) + -τικός.</div><b>συνοδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to accompany, go with, escort, chaperone</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένα ποτήρι καλό κόκκινο κρασί <b>συνοδεύει</b> κάθε μας επιθυμία. - A glass of good red wine accompanies our every desire.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συνοδεύω with semantic loan from French accompagner.</div><b>συνοδός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συνοδοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>companion, chaperone</div><b>συνοικία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνοικίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>district, quarter, neighbourhood (of town)</div><b>συνολικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συνολικός">συνολικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in total, all told</div><b>συνολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνολική, neuter συνολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. total (entire; relating to the whole of something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. overall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σύνολ(ο)) + -ικός, a calque of French entier and en totalité.</div><b>συνομιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνομιλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dialogue, conversation, exchange of views</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συνομιλώ + -ία or συν- (syn-) + ομιλία.</div><b>συνομιλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to converse, to have a discussion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συνομιλῶ, contracted form of συνομιλέω, with semantic loan from French converser.</div><b>συνομοσπονδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνομοσπονδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confederation (of states)</div><b>συνομοταξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνομοταξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phylum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συν- (syn-, “co-”) + ομοταξία</div><b>συνοπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνοπτική, neuter συνοπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synoptic, concise, succinct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συνοπτικός, from σύνοπτ(ος)) (&lt; συν- (syn-) + ὀπτός) + -ικός Also see σύνοψη &amp; οπτικός.</div><b>συνορεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to border [with <a href="bword://με">με</a> (me, + accusative) ‘on’] (to touch at a border)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: γειτονεύω (geitonévo)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σύνορ(ο)) + -εύω.</div><b>συνοριοφύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνοριοφύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>border guard, border patrol</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σύνορο + φύλακας. From Byzantine Greek σῠ́νορον from Koine Greek σῠ́νορος, and Ancient Greek φῠ́λᾰξ, φῠ́λᾰκος.</div><b>συνουσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνουσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. copulation, coition, coupling, sexual intercourse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meeting, coming together, social intercourse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνουσίᾱ. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + ουσία.</div><b>συνοψίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to summarise (UK), summarize (US)</div><b>συντάκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συντάκτες, feminine συντάκτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. editor, copy editor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reporter</div>
  <div class='def'>3. compiler, draftsman</div><b>συντάκτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντάκτριες, masculine συντάκτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. editor, copy editor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reporter</div>
  <div class='def'>3. compiler, draftsman</div><b>συντάσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to draft (a document)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to compose (a statement)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to compile</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to design</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to indite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συντάσσω. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + τάσσω</div><b>συντάχθηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of συντάσσομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://συντάσσω">συντάσσω</a></div><b>συντέκνισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντέκνισσες, masculine σύντεκνος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>godmother</div><b>συντέμνουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cosecant</div><b>συντήρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντηρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. maintenance, conservation, preservation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. support, upkeep, subsistence, maintenance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συντήρησις. By surface analysis, συντηρώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>συντήρησις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://συντήρηση">συντήρηση</a></div><b>συνταγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνταγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prescription</div>
  <div class='def'>2. recipe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συνταγή, from Ancient Greek συντάσσω; some senses based on French recette.</div><b>συνταγματάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συνταγματάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the rank of colonel in the British and US armies with the NATO grade OF-5</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συνταγματάρχης. By surface analysis, σύνταγμα (συνταγματ-)) + -άρχης See άρχω &lt; ἄρχω).</div><b>συνταγματικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constitutionally</div><b>συνταγματικές βασιλείες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of συνταγματική <a href="bword://βασιλεία">βασιλεία</a></div><b>συνταγματικές μοναρχίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of συνταγματική <a href="bword://μοναρχία">μοναρχία</a></div><b>συνταγματική βασιλεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνταγματικές βασιλείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constitutional monarchy</div><b>συνταγματική μοναρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνταγματικές μοναρχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constitutional monarchy</div><b>συνταγματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνταγματική, neuter συνταγματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>constitutional</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>συνταγματική</b> μοναρχία - constitutional monarchy</div><b>συνταγματικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) constitutionality</div><b>συνταγογράφηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνταγογραφήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prescribing (the act of writing prescriptions)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the συνταγογραφη- stem of συνταγογραφώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>συνταγογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prescribing (the act of writing prescriptions)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συνταγ(ή)) + -ο- (-o-) + -γραφία.</div><b>συνταξιδιώτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συνταξιδιώτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fellow traveller</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συν- (syn-) + ταξιδιώτης.</div><b>συνταξιούχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural συνταξιούχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pensioner, retiree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σύνταξη + -ούχος. Attested since 1833 in Katharevousa as συνταξιοῦχος.</div><b>συνταρακτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://συνταρακτικός">συνταρακτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shockingly, shatteringly</div><b>συνταρακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνταρακτική, neuter συνταρακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shocking, shattering (inspiring great mental disturbance or shock)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the συνταρακ- stem of συνταράσσω + -τικός.</div><b>συντελεστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συντελεστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coefficient</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>συντελεστής</b> γραμμικής θερμικής διαστολής - the coefficient of linear thermal expansion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μεταφορά <b>συντελεστού</b> θεσπίσθηκε … - The transfer rate was established …</div><b>συντελεστής συσχέτισης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>correlation coefficient</div><b>συντελικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συντελική, neuter συντελικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relating to perfective aspect of tenses, with the sense: completed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>συντελικοί</b> χρόνοι στα νέα ελληνικά είναι: παρακείμενος, υπερσυντέλικος, τετελεσμένος μέλλοντας - The perfective tenses in Modern Greek are: present perfect, past perfect, future perfect.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συντελικός. Morphologically, συντελ(ής)) + -ικός</div><b>συντεταγμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντεταγμένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coordinate, abscissa</div><b>συντεχνίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συντεχνίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σύντεχνος">σύντεχνος</a></div><b>συντεχνίτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντεχνίτισσες, masculine σύντεχνος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fellow-craftswoman</div><b>συντηρήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντηρήτριες, masculine συντηρητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. maintenance technician, conservator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conserver</div><b>συντηρητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντηρητές, feminine συντηρήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. maintenance technician, conservator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conserver</div><b>συντηρητισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural συντηρητισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) conservatism</div><b>συντηρούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to subsist, survive, live on</div><b>συντηρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to conserve, maintain, preserve, support</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συν- (syn-) + τηρώ, literally “keep together”.</div><b>συντομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) brevity, briefness, shortness</div><b>συντομεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shorten, abbreviate, abridge, cut short, reduce (in extent or duration)</div><b>συντομογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντομογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation</div>
  <div class='ex'>«μτφρ.» είναι μια <b>συντομογραφία</b> για τη μετάφραση - “trans.” is an abbreviation for translation</div><b>συντομομορφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντομομορφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) abbreviation: initialism, acronym, etc</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σύντομη + μορφή</div><b>συντονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to coordinate, effect by concerted or joint effort</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to syntonize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σῠντονίᾱ intensity”) + -ίζω, with semantic loan from German abstimmen (“(reflexive) to synchronize”).
For the &quot;syntonize&quot; meaning, reborrowing and semantic loan from French syntoniser.</div><b>συντονισμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συντονισμένη, neuter συντονισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coordinated, concerted, by joint effort</div><b>συντρέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to help, support, aid, assist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to contribute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek συντρέχω, from συν- (syn-, “co-”) + τρέχω. The aorist stem δραμ- from the ancient ἔδραμον.</div><b>συντρίβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be crushed, shattered, devastated</div><b>συντρίβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to shatter, crush</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to beat, win completely (of a battle, conflict)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to cause deep sorrow, to cause someone to be devastated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek συντρίβω. Morphologically, from συν- (“together”) + τρίβω (“grate, grind”).</div><b>συντρίμμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συντρίμμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural) debris, wreckage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek *συντρίμμιον, diminutive of σύντριμμα.</div><b>συντριβάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συντριβάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://σιντριβάνι">σιντριβάνι</a></div><b>συντριβή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντριβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. destruction, demolition, catastrophe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) defeat, extreme grief</div><b>συντριπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συντριπτική, neuter συντριπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. crushing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. devastating</div>
  <div class='def'>3. overwhelming</div><b>συντριφτήκανε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>colloquial variation of συντριφτήκαν, third-person plural simple past of <a href="bword://συντρίβομαι">συντρίβομαι</a>, the passive of <a href="bword://συντρίβω">συντρίβω</a></div><b>συντροφιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντροφιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. group, company, party (of people)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated) company</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συντροφία, from Ancient Greek σύντροφος + -ία.</div><b>συντροφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συντροφική, neuter συντροφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of companionship, companionly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. comradely (of or relating to comradeship; between or among comrades)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek συντροφικός. By surface analysis, σύντροφ(ος)) + -ικός.</div><b>συντρόφισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντρόφισσες, masculine σύντροφος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. partner</div>
  <div class='def'>2. companion, mate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. comrade</div>
  <div class='def'>4. spouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σύντροφος + -ισσα (-issa).</div><b>συντόμευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντομεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shortcut</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shortcut, symbolic link</div><b>συντόμως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σύντομα">σύντομα</a></div><b>συνυπολογίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to factor in, to include, to take into account</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συν- (syn-) + υπολογίζω.</div><b>συνυπώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνυπώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coordinate term</div><b>συνωμοσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνωμοσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conspiracy</div><b>συνωμοτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to conspire, to collude</div><b>συνωνυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνωνυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. synonymity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Having the same name.</div><b>συνύπαρξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coexistence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συνύπαρξις. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + ύπαρξη.</div><b>συνώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonym</div><b>συνώνυμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συνώνυμη, neuter συνώνυμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>synonymous</div><b>συριζαίος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συριζαίοι, feminine συριζαία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) A supporter of Greek radical left-wing party Syriza.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) An elected member of Parliament (<a href="bword://Βουλή">Βουλή</a>) for radical left-wing party Syriza.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ΣΥΡΙΖΑ (SYRIZA, “Syriza”) + -αίος</div><b>συρμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συρμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mild epidemic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. furrow, groove in the ground</div>
  <div class='def'>3. household goods, furnishings</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύρω.</div><b>συρματόπλεγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συρματοπλέγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wire netting, wire mesh</div>
  <div class='def'>2. barbed wire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σύρμα + πλέγμα</div><b>συρματόσχοινο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συρματόσχοινα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cable (a rope made of wire)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σύρμα + σχοινί, calque of German Drahtseil</div><b>συρμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συρμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. train (engine and carriages as a whole)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mild epidemic</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fashion</div>
  <div class='ex'>ενδύματα του τελευταίου <b>συρμού</b> των Παρισίων - clothing of the latest Parisian fashion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συρμός.</div><b>συρράπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be stiched or sewn together</div><b>συρράπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (modern) to bind, staple pieces of paper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to put together, patch fragments of text to create a new one</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stich or sew together</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συρράπτω. By surface analysis, συρ- (συν-) (“together”) + ράπτω (“sew”) (ancient spelling ῥάπτω), a formal version of ράβω.</div><b>συρρέω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of people) to flock</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of water) to flow together</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek συρρέω. Morphologically, from συν+ρ&gt;συρρ (“together”) + ρέω (“flow”).</div><b>συρτάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συρτάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sirtaki</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συρτός + -άκι</div><b>συρτάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συρτάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drawer (for storage)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek συρτάριν, earlier συρτάριον, from a diminutive of Ancient Greek συρτός, from σύρω.</div><b>συρταριέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συρταριέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chest of drawers</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συρτάρι + -ιέρα</div><b>συσκέπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be in conference</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συσκέπτομαι (“contemplate together”). Morphologically, from συ- (συν-) (“with, co-”) + σκέπτομαι (“think”).</div><b>συσκευή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συσκευές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>device, appliance, apparatus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek συσκευή; by surface analysis, συ- (sy-) + -σκευή from Ancient Greek σύν + σκευή, as a calque of French appareil.</div><b>συσκευασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>package, packaging</div>
  <div class='ex'>αεροστεγής <b>συσκευασία</b> - vacuum packaging</div><b>συσπειρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) to coil</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to rally (to a common cause)</div><b>συσσωρευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συσσωρευτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accumulator, car battery, wet cell</div><b>συσσωρευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συσσωρευτική, neuter συσσωρευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cumulative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συσσωρεύ(ω)) + -τικός.</div><b>συσσωρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to accumulate</div><b>συστήνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to introduce, present</div>
  <div class='ex'>συσταίνω (systaíno)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to recommend, introduce recommendations</div>
  <div class='ex'>(&quot;suggest, recommend&quot;)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to set up, establish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συστήσω, the subjunctive of the infinitive of συνιστῶ. See συνιστώ for more.</div><b>συστατικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συστατικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ingredient, constituent</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://συστατικός">συστατικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://συστατικός">συστατικός</a></div><b>συστατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συστατική, neuter συστατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. constituent</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα συστατικά στοιχεία (consituent parts)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. recommendation, testimonial</div>
  <div class='ex'>η συστατική επιστολή (letter of recommendation, reference)</div><b>συστρατιώτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συστρατιώτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comrade (fellow soldier)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συ- (συν-) (sy- (syn-), “co-”) + στρατιώτης</div><b>συσχέτιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συσχετίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>correlation</div>
  <div class='ex'>συντελεστής συσχέτισης - correlation coefficient</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συσχετίζω + -ση (-si).</div><b>συσχετίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to correlate (to compare things and bring them into a relation having corresponding characteristics)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to associate (to connect evidentially, or in the mind or imagination)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συ- (sy-) + σχετίζω, with semantic loan from English correlate.</div><b>συσχετισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural συσχετισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. association, connection, link (the state of being associated)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. association (any relationship between two measured quantities that renders them statistically dependent (but not necessarily causal or a correlation))</div>
  <div class='def'>3. correlation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from συσχετίζω + -μός.</div><b>συχνά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>often, frequently</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συχνός, from Ancient Greek συχνός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://συχνός">συχνός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From συχνός, from Ancient Greek συχνός.</div><b>συχνάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to go or visit (somewhere) often; to frequent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek συχνάζω.</div><b>συχνός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine συχνή, neuter συχνό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frequent, repeated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συχνός.</div><b>συχνότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συχνότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (usually) frequency</div>
  <div class='def'>2. frequency</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The general sense is from Byzantine Greek συχνότης, equivalent to συχνός + -ότητα. The physics sense is a calque of English frequency.</div><b>συχωρνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://συχωρώ">συχωρώ</a></div><b>συχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://συγχωρώ">συγχωρώ</a></div><b>σφάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to make a mistake, err, blunder, lapse</div><b>σφάλμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφάλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>error, mistake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek σφᾰ́λμᾰ</div><b>σφάξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφαξίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slaughter (of animal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σφάζω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>σφάχτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σφάχτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slaughterer (profession or job)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. butcher (brutal or indiscriminate killer)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) sharp stabbing pain</div><b>σφήκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wasp, hornet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) a wicked, spiteful person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφήξ.</div><b>σφήνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wedge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφήν. Cognate with Mariupol Greek сфи́на.</div><b>σφίγγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to squeeze, tighten</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to tie (e.g. shoelaces)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to thicken (e.g. a sauce)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to shake (one&#x27;s hand)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) to behave in a constrained, cautious manner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφῐ́γγω.</div><b>σφίξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tightening (the process or result of becoming tight)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σφίγγω + -ιμο (-imo, “nominalizing suffix”)</div><b>σφαίρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφαίρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bullet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ball, sphere, orb</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) sphere, realm (of influence)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. shot put</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφαῖρα.</div><b>σφαίρα χιονιού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφαίρες χιονιού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snow globe</div><b>σφαίρες χιονιού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://σφαίρα">σφαίρα</a> χιονιού</div><b>σφαγέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σφαγείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slayer, butcher, slaughterer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφαγεύς.</div><b>σφαγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφαγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slaughter (of many animals)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. massacre (mass killing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφαγή.</div><b>σφαδάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to agonize, to writhe in pain, to flail</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be racked, to be harrowed (by pain, grief, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σφαδᾴζω.</div><b>σφαιρίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφαιρίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>globule, bead, pellet</div><b>σφαιρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σφαιρική, neuter σφαιρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spherical, global</div><b>σφαλιάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφαλιάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smack, slap (blow on the cheek given with the open hand)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν δεν σταματήσεις τις αταξίες, θα σου δώσω μια <b>σφαλιάρα</b>! - If you don&#x27;t stop misbehaving, I&#x27;ll give you a smack!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) blow (damaging occurrence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει φάει πολλές <b>σφαλιάρες</b> στη ζωή του. - He&#x27;s been dealt many blows in his life.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian sfaglio (“I discard, I jink”) + Greek ending -άρα.</div><b>σφαλιάρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφαλιαρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slapping, smacking (the act of being given a smack or slap)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το <b>σφαλιάρισμα</b> δεν θα το ξεχάσει ποτέ. - He&#x27;ll never forget that slap.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σφαλιαρίζω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>σφαλιαρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to smack, slap (give a blow on the face with the open hand)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταμάτα ή θα σε <b>σφαλιαρίσω</b>! - Stop or I&#x27;ll slap you!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σφαλιάρα + -ίζω.</div><b>σφενδάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφενδάμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maple, acer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σφένδαμνος.</div><b>σφεντάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφεντάμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σφενδάμι">σφενδάμι</a></div><b>σφεντόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφεντόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. catapult</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sling, slingshot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφενδόνη.</div><b>σφετερίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφετερίστριες, masculine σφετεριστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usurper (female)</div><b>σφετεριστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σφετεριστές, feminine σφετερίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usurper (male)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφετεριστής, equivalent to σφετερίζομαι + -ιστής.</div><b>σφηνόλιθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σφηνόλιθοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>keystone</div><b>σφιγκτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σφιγκτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sphincter (band of muscle around an orifice)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clamp</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek σφιγκτήρ.</div><b>σφικτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σφικτή, neuter σφικτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://σφιχτός">σφιχτός</a></div><b>σφιχτά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σφιχτός">σφιχτός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tightly</div><b>σφιχταγκαλιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be embraced, be hugged tightly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cuddle tightly</div><b>σφιχταγκαλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to embrace, clasp tightly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cuddle, hug tightly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σφιχτά (“tightly”) + αγκαλιάζω (“embrace”).</div><b>σφιχτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σφιχτή, neuter σφιχτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tight, firm, hidebound</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) thrifty, mean, tight-fisted</div><b>σφοδρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σφοδρή, neuter σφοδρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vehement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. violent, fierce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σφοδρός.</div><b>σφουγγάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφουγγάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sponge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σφουγγάρι(ν)), from earlier σφουγγάριον, from Koine Greek σπογγάριον, diminutive of Ancient Greek σπόγγος.</div><b>σφουγγαρόπανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. any rug, tatter or shred used for washing floors</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mop head</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σφουγγαρ- (sfoungar-) + -ό- + -πανο (-pano, “cloth, fabric”)</div><b>σφουγγοκωλάριος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σφουγγοκωλάριοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally, obsolete) groom of the stool (Byzantine officer tasked with wiping the emperor&#x27;s bottom)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) arse-kisser, ass-licker, brown-noser (someone who succumbs to authority, doing whatever authority figures ask and attempting to please them in every possible way)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φυσικά, έχει τους <b>σφουγγοκωλάριούς</b> του που ποτέ δεν του λένε όχι. - Of course, he has his brown-nosers who never say no to him.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σφουγγίζω + κώλος + -άριος.</div><b>σφράγισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφραγίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sealing; using a stamp</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sealing; securing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sealing; authenticating</div>
  <div class='def'>4. filling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφράγισμα.</div><b>σφραγίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφραγίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stamp (indentation or imprint made by stamping)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sigil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφραγίς.</div><b>σφραγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to seal (a document)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to close tightly</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stamp (a passport)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to fill/stop (tooth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek, derived from σφραγίς.</div><b>σφυγμογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφυγμογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sphygmography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French sphygmographie. By surface analysis, σφυγμός + -γραφία.</div><b>σφυγμομέτρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφυγμομετρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pulse measurement, heart rate measurement (initial meaning)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) opinion poll, survey</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σύμφωνα με πρόσφατη <b>σφυγμομέτρηση</b> της κοινής γνώμης, οι Έλληνες ενδιαφέρονται περισσότερο για την οικονομία και την ασφάλεια παρά για την ανεργία. - According to a recent public opinion poll, Greek people care more about economy and security than unemployment.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From σφυγμός + μέτρηση. Attested since 1891 in Katharevousa as σφυγμομέτρησις.</div><b>σφυγμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σφυγμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pulse (of an artery)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφῠγμός.</div><b>σφυρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σφυριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hammer, mallet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gavel (of auctioneer)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σφῦρα. Cognate with Mariupol Greek сфири́.</div><b>σφυρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be whistled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be hissed at</div><b>σφυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to whisper</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to whistle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to hiss at</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σφυρίζω, from Ancient Greek σῡρίζω, from σῦρῐγξ + -ίζω, with epenthesis of an -φ- (-f-), possibly under the influence of Hellenistic σφῠρίον.</div><b>σφυρηλατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to forge, to hammer</div><b>σφόνδυλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σφόνδυλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σπόνδυλος">σπόνδυλος</a></div><b>σφύζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to throb, pulsate, be vibrant (also figuratively)</div><b>σφύρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σφύρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hammer (throwing event)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. malleus (ear component)</div><b>σχάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σχάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. griddle, grill (used for roasting, barbecuing, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. grate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σχάρα, from ἐσχάρα.</div><b>σχέδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχέδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. design, plan, drawing, pattern (representation of something on paper, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. draft (early version)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. design, planning (action or taught subject)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. plan (for future activities)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σχέδιον, neuter form of σχέδιος; in the modern sense of &quot;design, draft&quot;, semantic loan from Italian schizzo (“rough sketch”) (though etymologically unrelated, despite similar phonetics and semantics).</div><b>σχέση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σχέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relationship (personal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relation, connection</div>
  <div class='def'>3. relationship (between two things)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. gear, ratio (a particular combination or choice of interlocking gears)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σχέσις, from the aorist stem σχε- (skhe-) of ἔχω + -σις (-sis). The modern sense of &quot;relation&quot; is a semantic loan from French relation.</div><b>σχήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>figure, shape</div>
  <div class='ex'>ελλειψοειδές <b>σχήμα</b> - elliptical shape</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σχῆμα.</div><b>σχίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be slit</div><b>σχίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σκίζω">σκίζω</a> to only for the literary sense: slit, tear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek σχίζω. See σκίζω.</div><b>σχίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σχίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trees and shrubs of the Pistacia genus, which includes:; mastic tree (Pistacia lentiscus)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trees and shrubs of the Pistacia genus, which includes:; pistachio tree (Pistacia vera)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trees and shrubs of the Pistacia genus, which includes:; turpentine tree (Pistacia terebinthus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σχῖνος.</div><b>σχίσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. schism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. split, rift, division (of a group or political party)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σχίσμα, from verb σχίζω, stem σχισ- + -μα (-ma).
For sense &quot;division of a group of political party&quot; a semantic loan from French schisme.</div><b>σχεδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σχεδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>raft</div><b>σχεδίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. designing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. drawing (the act of a picture, likeness, diagram or representation with lines)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σχεδιά(ζω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>σχεδιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to draw, design, plan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Semantic loan from French esquisser or dessiner, or English design. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek σχεδιάζω, from σχέδιον, neuter of σχέδιος.
: figurative sense &quot;plan&quot;: Semantic loan from English plan</div><b>σχεδιάστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σχεδιάστριες, masculine σχεδιαστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>designer, drawer, draughtswoman</div><b>σχεδιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σχεδιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. drawing up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. planning</div>
  <div class='def'>3. design</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σχεδιασμός, adapted to the modern meaning of σχεδιάζω, with semantic loan from English planning and drawing. By surface analysis, σχεδιάζω + -μός.</div><b>σχεδιαστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχεδιαστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. drawing board, drafting table</div>
  <div class='def'>2. drawing office</div><b>σχεδιαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σχεδιαστές, feminine σχεδιάστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>designer, drawer, draughtsman</div><b>σχεδόν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>almost</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σχεδόν.</div><b>σχετίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to relate, to associate (to bring into a relation, association, or connection)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to be related</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to have relations</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from σχετικός + -ίζω, with semantic loan from French mettre en relation (active) and être en relations (passive).</div><b>σχετικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σχετικός">σχετικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σχετικός">σχετικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σχετικός">σχετικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relatively (proportionally, in relation to some larger scale thing)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πλοίο μας ήταν <b>σχετικά</b> μικρό σε σύγκριση με τα αλλά. - Our boat was relatively small compared with the others.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with <a href="bword://με">με</a> (me) ) about, concerning, regarding (on the subject of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα ήθελα να σας μιλήσω <b>σχετικά</b> με τον λογαριασμό σας. - I&#x27;d like to speak to you regarding your account.</div><b>σχετικό υπερθετικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relative, superlative) relative superlative</div><b>σχετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σχετική, neuter σχετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. related</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relative</div>
  <div class='def'>3. proportional</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σχετικός; synchronically analyzable as an adjectival derivative of σχέση. Τhe modern sense of &quot;relative, proportional&quot; is a semantic loan from French relatif.</div><b>σχηματίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be formed</div><b>σχηματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to form, make, create, organize, shape</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πρωθυπουργός δεν μπόρεσε να <b>σχηματίσει</b> κυβέρνηση. - The prime minister was unable to form a government.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σχηματίζω. Partially possibly a semantic loan from French former.</div><b>σχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://συνταγματάρχης">συνταγματάρχης</a></div><b>σχιζοφρένεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) schizophrenia</div><b>σχοινάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχοινάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skipping rope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * diminutive of σχοινί</div><b>σχοινένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σχοινένια, neuter σχοινένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rope (consisting of or related with)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σχοινένια</b> σκάλα - rope ladder</div><b>σχοινί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχοινιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σχοινίν, from Ancient Greek σχοινίον, diminutive of σχοῖνος.</div><b>σχοινοβάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σχοινοβάτες, feminine σχοινοβάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tightrope walker, aerialist, highwire walker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σχοινί + -βάτης</div><b>σχοινοβάτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σχοινοβάτισσες, masculine σχοινοβάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tightrope walker, aerialist, highwire walker</div><b>σχοινοποιείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχοινοποιεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ropewalk, ropery (rope manufactory)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σχοινί + -ποιείο</div><b>σχοινοτενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σχοινοτενής, neuter σχοινοτενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thorough, detailed, extensive, lengthy, long</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σχοινοτενής. By surface analysis, σχοινί + τείνω + -ής.</div><b>σχοινόπρασο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχοινόπρασα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chives (herb), chive (plant)</div><b>σχολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σχολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>faculty, school (division of institutions of higher education)</div>
  <div class='ex'>νομική <b>σχολή</b> - faculty of law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σχολή.</div><b>σχολείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχολεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>school</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σχολεῖον, from σχολή.</div><b>σχολιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to comment on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek or Byzantine Greek σχολιάζω with semantic loan from French commenter. By surface analysis, σχόλι(ο)) + -άζω.</div><b>σχολιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σχολιασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. commentary (series of explanatory or evaluative comments about something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. annotation (critical or explanatory commentary or analysis; process of writing this)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the σχολιασ- stem of σχολιάζω + -μός.</div><b>σχολιαστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>commentator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σχολιαστής.</div><b>σχολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σχολική, neuter σχολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scholastic, for students</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σχολικά</b> βιβλία - scholastic books</div>
  <div class='def'>2. school</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σχολική</b> χορωδία - school choir</div><b>σχόλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σχόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leisure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. festival, holy day, holiday</div><b>σχόλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σχόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. comment, remark, statement (presentation of opinion or position)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν θέλω τα <b>σχόλια</b> σου όσον αφορά την οικογένειά μου. - I don&#x27;t want any comments from you concerning my family.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. criticism, critique (opinion writing in which another piece of work is criticised)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν της άρεσαν τα <b>σχόλια</b> που γράφτηκαν για την ταινία της. - She didn&#x27;t like the critiques that were written about her film.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. opinion piece (article in which the author expresses their personal opinion on a given subject)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έγραψε ένα <b>σχόλιο</b> στην εφημερίδα για τους ομοφυλόφιλους. - He wrote an opinion piece in the paper on homosexuals.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. annotation, scholia (critical or explanatory commentary or analysis added to a text)</div>
  <div class='ex'>φιλολογικά <b>σχόλια</b> - literary annotations</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σχόλιον, from the ancient σχολή.</div><b>σωβινισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σωβινισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σοβινισμός">σοβινισμός</a></div><b>σωβινιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σωβινιστική, neuter σωβινιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σοβινιστικός">σοβινιστικός</a></div><b>σωθικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in set expressions) entrails, guts, innards, insides</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σωθικά, from Ancient Greek ἔσωθεν.</div><b>σωλήνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σωλήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pipe, tube, duct</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pipe, tube, duct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σωλήν.</div><b>σωλήνωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σωληνώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pipelaying, pipelining</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) plumbing, pipage (a system of pipes)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) pipeline (physical or logical)</div><b>σωματίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σωματίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>particle, corpuscle</div>
  <div class='ex'>υποατομικά <b>σωματίδια</b> - subatomic particles</div><b>σωματείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σωματεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>union, association, club, league (a group or association of cooperating members)</div>
  <div class='ex'>εργατικό <b>σωματείο</b> - labour union</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σωματεῖον.</div><b>σωματική ποινή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σωματικές ποινές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corporal punishment (punishment involving hurt to the body)</div><b>σωματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σωματική, neuter σωματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>physical, bodily, corporal (relating to the body)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έφυγα από τη μέση για να προστατέψω τη <b>σωματική</b> ακεραιότητά μου. - I got out of the way in order to protect my physical integrity.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σωματικός. By surface analysis, σώμα + -τικός</div><b>σωματικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>physically, bodily, corporally (by/through the body)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον πέταξαν έξω <b>σωματικώς</b>. - They ejected him bodily.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σώμα + -ώς</div><b>σωματοδόμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) bodybuilding</div><b>σωματολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σωματολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>somatology (the study of the physical nature of human beings)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French somatologie or English somatology. By surface analysis, σώμα + -λογία.</div><b>σωματοφύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σωματοφύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bodyguard (protector)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. musketeer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek σωματοφύλαξ. By surface analysis, σώμα + φύλακας.</div><b>σωπαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>I remain silent, keep silent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σωπαίνω, from Ancient Greek σιωπῶ, contracted form of σιωπάω, with deletion of [i] between [s] and vowel plus the verb suffix -αίνω.</div><b>σωρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σωροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heap, pile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σωρός.</div><b>σως</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://σος">σος</a> (sos) (sauce)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French sauce.</div><b>σωσίβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σωσίβια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>life buoy, life jacket</div><b>σωστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σωστή, neuter σωστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. correct, right</div>
  <div class='def'>2. real, true</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) well-mannered, well-behaved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Hellenistic Koine Greek σωστός. The modern meaning, since the mediaeval period.
Verbal adjective of the ancient verb σῴζω, modern σώζω, hence literally &quot;safe and sound; secure; sure, certain&quot;.</div><b>σωτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σωτήρες, feminine σώτειρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rescuer, saviour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek σωτήρ.</div><b>σόδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σόδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. soda, soda water, club soda (carbonated water)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carbonated soft drink</div>
  <div class='def'>3. baking soda, sodium bicarbonate</div>
  <div class='def'>4. washing soda, sodium carbonate</div>
  <div class='def'>5. caustic soda, sodium hydroxide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From New Latin soda, from Arabic سُوَّد (suwwad, “saltwort”).</div><b>σόι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. family, clan, kin (group of people who one is descended from or shares blood with)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν φτάνει που ήρθε αυτός, έφερε και όλο το <b>σόι</b> του μαζί. - It&#x27;s not enough that he came, he brought his whole family with him.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, usually) kind of, type of</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>σόι</b> παιδί είναι αυτό, είναι πολύ άτακτο! - What kind of a child is that, he&#x27;s very naughty!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. good quality</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν μου φαίνεται <b>σόι</b> αυτό το ύφασμα – δε θα διαρκέσει πολύ! - That fabric doesn&#x27;t look good quality to me – it won&#x27;t last long!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish صوی (soy, “ancestry, descent”).</div><b>σόλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) solo (piece of music for one)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) solo (performance by one person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian solo (“solo”), from Latin solus (“alone”).</div><b>σόου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) show (a performed entertainment spectacle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English show.</div><b>σόρβον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) trees of the genus Sorbus</div><b>σόργο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) sorghum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian sorgo (“sorghum”), from Vulgar Latin *syricum (“Syrian”), from Ancient Greek Συρικόν.</div><b>σύγαμπρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σύγαμπροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>co-brother-in-law (one&#x27;s wife&#x27;s sister&#x27;s husband)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek σύγγαμβρος, from συγ- (sug-) + γαμβρός, with alteration of -μβρ- to -μπρ- like γαμβρός → γαμπρός.</div><b>σύγκληση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκλήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>convocation; the act of convoking; convening, assembling; gathering a congregation; summoning others, calling together to assembly, meeting, conference</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σύγκληση</b> του διοικητικού συμβουλίου - meeting of the board of directors</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French convocation from Latin convocātiō, con- (“with, together”) + vocātiō (“calling, summons”), hence literally συγ- (syg-, “with, together”) + κλήση; from Byzantine Greek σύγκλησις from roots of the verb συγκαλώ from Koine Greek σῠγκᾰλῶ, contracted form of Ancient Greek σῠγκᾰλέω, equivalent to συν- (syn-, “together”) + καλώ.</div><b>σύγκλητο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative singular of <a href="bword://σύγκλητος">σύγκλητος</a></div><b>σύγκλητον</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σύγκλητο">σύγκλητο</a>, accusative singular of <a href="bword://σύγκλητος">σύγκλητος</a></div><b>σύγκλητος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σύγκλητοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. senate; university or HE governing body</div>
  <div class='def'>2. senate; of national government</div>
  <div class='def'>3. senate; of Rome or Byzantine Roman Empire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σῠ́γκλητος, from the ancient adjective σῠ́γκλητος, from Ancient Greek σῠγκᾰλέω, συγ- (syg-, “with, together”) + κλητός, equivalent to συν- (syn-) + καλώ + -τος (-tos). In historical sense, a semantic loan from Latin senātus.</div><b>σύγκλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκλίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>convergence</div><b>σύγκριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκρίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comparison, contrast</div><b>σύγκρουση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγκρούσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. conflict, clash, difference of opinion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. collision, crash</div><b>σύγχρονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σύγχρονη, neuter σύγχρονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contemporary, contemporaneous</div>
  <div class='def'>2. synchronous</div>
  <div class='def'>3. simultaneous</div>
  <div class='def'>4. contemporary, modern</div><b>σύγχυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συγχύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confusion (state of mind or situation characterised by a lack of clarity or order)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek σύγχυσις, from the verb συγχέω, composed of συν- (sun-) + χέω.</div><b>σύδεντρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύδεντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wood, dense forest (place with many trees)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> συ- (συν-) (“together”) + δέντρο (“tree”). Substantivized Hellensitic adjective σύνδενδρος.</div><b>σύζυγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σύζυγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. yokefellow (close companion, co-worker, or mate)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spouse (husband or wife)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) couple (husband and wife)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σύζυγος, from συ(ν) (su(n), “together”) + ζυγός, from Proto-Indo-European *yewg- (“to join, tie together”). Cognate with English syzygy, itself a borrowing from the related συζυγία.</div><b>σύκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fig (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῦκον.</div><b>σύλληψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συλλήψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. arrest, capture (the act or instance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. conception (formation of a concept)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. conception (fertilisation of an egg)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύλληψις, from συλλαμβάνω.</div><b>σύλλογος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σύλλογοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>association, club</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύλλογος. By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + λέγω + -ος (-os)</div><b>σύμβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. agreement, contract (legally binding pact between two or more parties)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. agreement, contract (legally binding pact between two or more parties); convention</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Συμβάσεις</b> της Γενεύης - Geneva Conventions</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (plural normally) convention (a practice or procedure widely observed in a group, especially to facilitate social interaction; a custom)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύμβασις with -ση (-si) ending and semantic loan from French convention.</div><b>σύμβολο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύμβολα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>symbol, character, glyph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύμβολον with semantic loan from French symbole.</div><b>σύμβουλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σύμβουλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. adviser, advisor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. consultant</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>σύμβουλος</b> υπέβαλε την έκθεσή του στην Επιτροπή το Δεκέμβριο του 2005. - The consultant transmitted his report to the Commission in December 2005.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek σύμβουλος. See συμβουλή f</div><b>σύμμαχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σύμμαχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ally (a person, group, state, etc., which is associated or united by treaty with another for a common (especially military or political) purpose; a confederate)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ally (a person who co-operates with or helps another; an associate; a friend)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The noun is a learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύμμαχος. The adjective is derived from the noun.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σύμμαχη, neuter σύμμαχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>allied</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The noun is a learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύμμαχος. The adjective is derived from the noun.</div><b>σύμπαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύμπαντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>universe</div><b>σύμπτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συμπτώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. symptom</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) sign</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύμπτωμα.</div><b>σύμπτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συμπτώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coincidence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. concurrence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύμπτωσις, from σῠν- + πτῶσις).</div><b>σύμφωνα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accordingly, in accordance with, according to</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σύμφωνος">σύμφωνος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://σύμφωνο">σύμφωνο</a></div><b>σύμφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύμφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. consonant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contract, pact</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύμφωνον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://σύμφωνος">σύμφωνος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://σύμφωνος">σύμφωνος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>σύμφωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σύμφωνη, neuter σύμφωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. agreed, in agreement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coherent, congruent</div><b>σύναξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνάξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>assembly, congregation, gathering, meeting</div><b>σύναψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνάψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. joining, pooling (of resources)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. joining, waging (in war, combat)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. connection, coupling</div>
  <div class='def'>4. unity</div>
  <div class='def'>5. synapse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύναψις.</div><b>σύνδεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνδέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>link, connection, login, logon</div><b>σύνδεσμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σύνδεσμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. conjunction</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ligament</div>
  <div class='def'>3. HTML link</div>
  <div class='def'>4. union, relationship, contact</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek σῠ́νδεσμος</div><b>σύνδρομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of σύνδρομος</div><b>σύνδρομα αποστερήσεων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://σύνδρομο">σύνδρομο</a> αποστέρησης</div><b>σύνδρομο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύνδρομα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>syndrome (recognizable pattern of symptoms or behaviours)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σύνδρομο</b> ευερέθιστου εντέρου - irritable bowel syndrome</div><b>σύνδρομο αποστέρησης</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύνδρομα αποστερήσεων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>withdrawal symptom</div><b>σύνεργα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tools</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tackle</div><b>σύνθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. composition (the formation of the complex by combination of simpler)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. synthesis (especially by condensation)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. composition, musical work</div>
  <div class='def'>4. compounding (the formation of long word by combination of shorter ones)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σύνθεσις; the maintenance of /n/ before /θ/ (except colloquially) is due to learned influence.</div><b>σύνθετη λέξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compound word</div><b>σύνθετος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σύνθετη, neuter σύνθετο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>composite, compound, synthetic</div><b>σύνθημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συνθήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. watchword, password, codeword, code</div>
  <div class='def'>2. signal, cue, prompt (a sign or utterance made to indicate the start of a concerted action)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. watchword, motto (a short, suggestive expression of a guiding principle)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. slogan (political or commercial)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύνθημα with semantic loan from French signal and Italian segnale in the sense &#x27;signal&#x27;, from French mot d&#x27;ordre in the sense &#x27;watchword, motto&#x27; and from English slogan.</div><b>σύννεφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύννεφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cloud</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σύννεφο, from Koine Greek σύννεφος, from Ancient Greek συν- (sun-) + νέφος.</div><b>σύνοδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σύνοδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. meeting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. session</div>
  <div class='def'>3. synod, ecclesiastic council</div>
  <div class='def'>4. conjunction</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σύνοδος.</div><b>σύνολο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύνολα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. set</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sum</div><b>σύνορο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύνορα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. border, boundary, limit, dividing line</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) borders; the border, common border area; borderzone, borderland; frontier</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τουρκικό τεθωρακισμένο προσπαθεί με σχοινί να ρίξει τον φράχτη στα <b>σύνορα</b> - Turkish tank tries to knock over fence with rope at the border</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek σῠ́νορον from Koine Greek σῠ́νορος, equivalent to συν- (syn-, “with”) + όριο.</div><b>σύνορον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύνορα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) older form of <a href="bword://σύνορο">σύνορο</a></div><b>σύνοψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συνόψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. summary, precis, synopsis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breviary, missal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύνοψις σύνοψ(ις) + modern ending -ση (-si). By surface analysis, συν- (syn-) + όψη from the ancient σύν + ὄψῐς.</div><b>σύνταγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συντάγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) syntagma (a formation of the Macedonian phalanx)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. constitution</div>
  <div class='def'>3. regiment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σύνταγμα. By surface analysis, συντάσσω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>σύνταξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντάξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. syntax</div>
  <div class='def'>2. writing, drafting, composition (the act of creating a document)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. retirement (from work)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pension (money received after retirement)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύνταξις.</div><b>σύντεκνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σύντεκνοι, feminine συντέκνισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>best man, godfather</div><b>σύντεχνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σύντεχνοι, feminine συντεχνίτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fellow-craftsman</div><b>σύντμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συντμήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>short cut, abbreviation</div><b>σύντομα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soon, quickly (within a short time)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σύντομα</b> θα είστε ευτυχισμένοι! - Soon you will be happy!</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://σύντομος">σύντομος</a></div><b>σύντομος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σύντομη, neuter σύντομο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. short, curtailed (in extent)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. short, brief, quick (in duration)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύντομος.</div><b>σύντροφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural σύντροφοι, feminine συντρόφισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. partner</div>
  <div class='def'>2. companion, mate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. comrade</div>
  <div class='def'>4. spouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σύντροφος, from συν- (sun-) + τρέφω + -ος (-os), with semantic loan from French compagnon and Italian compagno.</div><b>σύριγγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σύριγγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. syringe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pan pipes, pan flute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σῦριγξ. The medical term is a reborrowing/semantic loan from French seringue (“syringe”)</div><b>σύριγξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://σύριγγα">σύριγγα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῦριγξ.</div><b>σύρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σύρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. electrical wire or cable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wire (thin flexible metal thread or threads)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. scourer, metal mesh scouring pad</div>
  <div class='def'>4. whisk, beater</div>
  <div class='def'>5. wire netting</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek σύρμα, from Koine Greek σύρμα, from Ancient Greek σύρμα. From σύρω.</div><b>σύρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to be dragged</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Σύρεται</b> στα δικαστήρια. - S/he is dragged to court.</div><b>σύρραξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συρράξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conflict, brawl, fray</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σύρραξις.</div><b>σύρριζα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to the roots, very closely</div><b>σύρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to drag, alternative form of <a href="bword://σέρνω">σέρνω</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. to draw a line</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῡ́ρω.</div><b>σύσκεψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συσκέψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>conference, meeting (a gathering of persons for collective consultation and decision-making)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek σύσκεψις with -ψη (-psi).</div><b>σύσπαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συσπάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>twitch, spasm, contraction, convulsion (involuntary muscular movement)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σύσπασις with -ση (-si) suffix and semantic loan from French contraction.</div><b>σύσταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural συστάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. composition, analysis, make up. formulation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>σύσταση</b> του νερού ελέγχεται στο εργαστήριο. - The analysis of the water tested in the laboratory.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. forming, setting up, formation</div>
  <div class='ex'>τη <b>σύσταση</b> της επιτροπής - the setting up of the committee</div>
  <div class='def'>3. recommendation, introduction</div>
  <div class='ex'>related terms: συστήνω (systíno, “recommend, introduce”)</div><b>σύστημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural συστήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>system</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σύστημα.</div><b>σώβρακο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σώβρακα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>underpants (male; US, UK), pants (male; UK)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σώ- + βρακί.</div><b>σώγαμπρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural σώγαμπροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. live-in son-in-law (a man who marries and lives with his wife&#x27;s family instead of setting up his own home or living with his family)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Στάθης είναι <b>σώγαμπρος</b>· ζει με τα πεθερικά του. - Stathis lives with his in-laws.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, derogatory, humorous) A husband seen as weak or who isn&#x27;t seen to serve as the head of his household.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> σώ- + γαμπρός.</div><b>σώζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to save, rescue</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to save (a file)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to redeem, save</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek σῴζω. For sense save file, a semantic loan from English save.</div><b>σώμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural σώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. body</div>
  <div class='def'>2. corps</div>
  <div class='def'>3. force, police force</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι καινούργιος στο σώμα; - Is he a new guy on the force?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. body (group of people)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. corpus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek σῶμα.</div><b>σώνει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular present active indicative of σώνω</div><b>σώος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σώα, neuter σώο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>safe</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>σώος</b> και αβλαβής - safe and sound</div><b>σώος και αβλαβής</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) safe and sound</div><b>σώσε</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, uncountable) mayhem, pandemonium, commotion, upheaval</div>
  <div class='ex'>Απόψε στους δρόμους γίνεται το <b>σώσε</b> από αυτοκίνητα. - There&#x27;s mayhem on the roads tonight with cars.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second-person singular imperative of <a href="bword://σώζω">σώζω</a></div><b>σώτειρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural σώτειρες, masculine σωτήρας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female rescuer, saviour (UK), savior (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σώτειρα.</div><b>σώφρων</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine σώφρων, neuter σώφρον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sane</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek σώφρων</div><b>τ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contraction of <a href="bword://το">το</a> (to, “the n sg”)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τ’</b> αγόρι και το κορίτσι - The boy and the girl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. contraction of <a href="bword://τα">τα</a> (ta, “the n pl”)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τ’</b> αγόρια και τα κορίτσια - The boys and the girls</div><b>τ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. abbreviation of <a href="bword://τεμάχιο">τεμάχιο</a> (piece)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. abbreviation of <a href="bword://τεύχος">τεύχος</a> (issue)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. abbreviation of τόμος (volume)</div><b>τ.μ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of τετραγωνικό <a href="bword://μέτρο">μέτρο</a>: square metre</div><b>τ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Τ΄">Τ΄</a></div><b>τάβλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάβλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>backgammon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τάβλι. Ultimately, from Koine Greek τάβλα, from Latin tabula.</div><b>τάγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. battalion (army unit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. religious order; a group living according to a set of rules</div>
  <div class='def'>3. order of chivalry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τάγμα. Doublet of τάμα.</div><b>τάδε</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) such-and-such, so-and-so</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε θέλω να μου έρχεται ο κύριος <b>τάδε</b> να με ενοχλεί πάλι. - I don&#x27;t want Mr So-and-so bothering me again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τᾰ́δε.</div><b>τάιγκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) taiga</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Russian тайга (tajga).</div><b>τάκα-τάκα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>straight away</div><b>τάλαντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάλαντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) talent (unit of weight and money used in ancient times in Greece, the Roman Empire, and the Middle East)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δύο <b>τάλαντα</b> χρυσό - two talents of gold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal) talent (marked natural ability or skill)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν προικισμένος με ένα σπάνιο <b>τάλαντο</b>. - He was endowed with a rare talent.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τάλαντον.</div><b>τάλαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάλαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τάλιρο">τάλιρο</a></div><b>τάληρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάλιρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τάλιρο">τάλιρο</a></div><b>τάλιρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάλιρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, obsolete) A coin or banknote with a value of five drachmas.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) A coin or banknote with a value of five of any other currency; fiver.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly, colloquial) The five euro banknote; fiver.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τρελάθηκες; Μην δίνεις το <b>τάλιρο</b> στον ζητιάνο! - Are you mad? Don&#x27;t give that fiver to the beggar!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian tallero.</div><b>τάλληρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάλληρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated form of <a href="bword://τάλιρο">τάλιρο</a></div><b>τάμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. oblation, votive offering, ex-voto</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τάγμα. Doublet of τάγμα.</div><b>τάντεμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tandem, bicycle for two</div><b>τάξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τάξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. class (set sharing attributes: people, things, classroom, grade level, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. order</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rank</div>
  <div class='def'>4. classroom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τάξις.</div><b>τάπερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Tupperware, plastic container for food</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English Tupperware (“plastic container used for food”). Named after American businessman Earl Tupper (1907–1983).</div><b>τάπητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τάπητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carpet (fabric floor covering)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sports ground, (turf)</div><b>τάρανδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τάρανδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reindeer (Rangifer tarandus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τάρανδος.</div><b>τάρταρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hell (a situation of great suffering in life)</div><b>τάση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. voltage, potential difference</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tension, stretching</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trend, inclination, tendency</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τά(σις), “stretching, tension”) + -ση (-si). The sense ‘voltage, potential difference’ is a semantic loan from French tension. See τείνω.</div><b>τάσπιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τάσπιν)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) synonym of <a href="bword://σκουπιδοτενεκές">σκουπιδοτενεκές</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English dustbin.</div><b>τάφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τάφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grave, tomb</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τάφος.</div><b>τάφρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τάφροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. moat, fosse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. large ditch, large trench</div>
  <div class='def'>3. marine trench</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>τάφρος</b> των Μαριανών - Mariana Trench</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τάφρος.</div><b>τάχα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. supposedly, ostensibly, allegedly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δικαιολογήθηκε πως <b>τάχα</b> ήταν άρρωστος. - His excuse was that he was supposedly sick.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. I wonder, perhaps</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αργήσαμε <b>τάχα</b>; Δεν υπάρχει κανείς εδώ. - I wonder if we&#x27;re late? There&#x27;s no one here.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τάχα (sense 2-3).</div><b>τάχα μου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supposedly, as if</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προσποιόταν ότι <b>τάχα μου</b> την πλήγωσα. - She was acting as if I&#x27;d hurt her.</div><b>τέιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τέια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) tea</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French thé. Doublet of τσάι.</div><b>τέκνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τέκνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) offspring, son or daughter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek τέκνον.</div><b>τέλεια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://τέλειος">τέλειος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τέλειος">τέλειος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. perfectly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πιανίστας έπαιξε το κομμάτι <b>τέλεια</b>. - The pianist played the piece perfectly.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) greatly, fantastically, superbly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περάσαμε <b>τέλεια</b> σ’ αυτές τις διακοπές. - We had a great time on this holiday.</div><b>τέλειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τέλεια, neuter τέλειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perfect, ideal, complete</div><b>τέλεξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) telex (teleprinter network)</div><b>τέλεφαξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) fax</div><b>τέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thin wire</div>
  <div class='def'>2. metallic cord</div>
  <div class='def'>3. string (metallic, as in guitars, bouzoukis, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω κέφια, θέλω γέλια, κι η φωνή μου ν’ ακουστεί
Απ’ του μπουζουκιού τα <b>τέλια</b> έχω απόψε κρεμαστεί. - I want fun, I want laughter and my voice to be heard
I have hung myself up by the bouzouki&#x27;s strings tonight.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish تل (tel).</div><b>τέλμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τέλματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. swamp, quagmire, bog</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) impasse, predicament</div><b>τέλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τέλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. end, finish</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα έρθω στο <b>τέλος</b> του μήνα. - I will come at the end of the month.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tax, dues paid to the government for specific public service, or product</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τέλος</b> ακίνητης περιουσίας (ΤΑΠ) - tax of real estate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τέλος. Sense &quot;tax, dues&quot;, learnedly from Ancient Greek τέλος.</div><b>τέλος καλό, όλα καλά</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all&#x27;s well that ends well</div><b>τέλος πάντων</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. anyway (to indicate change of subject)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχω ακούσει από την κόρη μου. <b>Τέλος πάντων</b>, εσύ έχεις ακούσει από τον Γιάννη; - I haven&#x27;t heard from my daughter. Anyway, have you heard from Giannis?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. anyway, after all (to indicate that a statement explains or supports a previous statement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχε αφήσει την γυναίκα του και <b>τέλος πάντων</b> γι’ αυτό δεν είχε που να πάει. - He had left his wife and after all, that&#x27;s why he didn&#x27;t have anywhere to go.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally means &#x27;end of all&#x27;.</div><b>τέμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τεμένη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a sacred enclosure</div>
  <div class='ex'>also see ναός m (naós, “temple”)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a mosque</div>
  <div class='ex'>μουσουλμανικό <b>τέμενος</b> - a muslim mosque</div><b>τέμνουσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>secant</div><b>τέμνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cut, <a href="bword://το">το</a> cut off</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to intersect, to meet</div><b>τένις</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tennis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> English tennis</div><b>τέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τέρατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>monster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τέρας.</div><b>τέρβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) terbium</div><b>τέρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τέρματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. conclusion, end, goal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. finishing line</div>
  <div class='def'>3. goal (the physical entity and what is scored)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τέρμᾰ, from Proto-Indo-European *térmn̥. Cognate with Latin terminus (“boundary, limit, end”).</div><b>τέρματα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τέρμᾰ</div><b>τέρπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to enjoy</div><b>τέρπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to regale, please, delight</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τέρπω</b> τους καλεσμένους με ιστορίες. - I regale the guests with stories.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τέρπω.</div><b>τέρψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τέρψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pleasure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τέρψις, from Proto-Hellenic *térptis, from Proto-Indo-European *térp-ti-s ~ *tr̥p-téy-s (“fulfillment, satisfaction, pleasure, delight”). Cognate with Sanskrit तृप्ति, Gothic 𐌸𐌰𐌿𐍂𐍆𐍄𐍃 and dialectal German Durft (“necessity”).</div><b>τέσλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tesla (SI Unit magnetic flux density or magnetic inductivity.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of the name of the Serbian electrical engineer Nikola Tesla (1856-1943).</div><b>τέσσερα</b>
  <div><i>num, neuter (masculine and feminine τέσσερις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>four, 4</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τέσσᾰρᾰ, neuter form of τέσσᾰρες.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek тэ́сера.</div><b>τέσσερεις</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine or feminine (neuter τέσσερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>four, 4</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τέσσᾰρες, from Proto-Hellenic *kʷétwores, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷetwóres.</div><b>τέσσερις</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine or feminine (neuter τέσσερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>four, 4</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τέσσᾰρες, from Proto-Hellenic *kʷétwores, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷetwóres.</div><b>τέτανος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tetanus, lockjaw</div><b>τέταρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τέταρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. quarter, a fourth, one of four equal parts.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ώρα είναι επτά και τέταρτο. - It is a quarter past seven.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. quarter of an hour.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. crotchet, quarter note.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized use of the adjective τέταρτος.</div><b>τέταρτον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fourthly</div><b>τέταρτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τέταρτη, neuter τέταρτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fourth (the ordinal form of <a href="bword://τέσσερα">τέσσερα</a>)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>Τέταρτη</b> Διεθνής υπέστη διάσπαση το 1940. - The Fourth International suffered a split in 1940</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τέταρτος.</div><b>τέτοιος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>such, like, like that</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Γιάννης έχει <b>τέτοιο</b> αυτοκίνητο. - John has such a car.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek τοῖος.</div><b>τέφρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τέφρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ash, cinder, cinders</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τέφρα.</div><b>τέχνασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τεχνάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trick, artifice, ploy, ruse, subterfuge, wile (deceptive device or stratagem)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τέχνασμα.</div><b>τέχνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τέχνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>art, craftsmanship, style</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τέχνη.</div><b>τέως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>previous, former, ex-</div><b>τήξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τήξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>melting</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>τήξη</b> των μετάλλων - the melting of metals</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τῆ(ξις)) + modern ending -ξη (-xi). From τήκω &gt; τηκ- + -σις (-sis) &gt; -ξις (-xis).</div><b>τί</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Polytonic spelling of <a href="bword://τι">τι</a>; (ti?, “what?”)</div><b>τίγρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τίγρεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tiger</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tigress, female tiger</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) brave man</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) wild woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τίγρις.</div><b>τίγρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τίγρεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>male tiger</div><b>τίγρις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://τίγρη">τίγρη</a></div><b>τίμημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τιμήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. monetary value</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) value in terms of time, effort, emotion, etc.</div><b>τίμια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://τίμιος">τίμιος</a>:; feminine nominative/accusative/vocative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://τίμιος">τίμιος</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>honestly</div><b>τίμιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τίμια, neuter τίμιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. honest, honourable, fair, decent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. holy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τίμιος, from τιμή, τιμῶ.</div><b>τίποτα</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in a negative clause or question) nothing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δε ζητάω <b>τίποτα</b>. - I ask for nothing.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) something, anything, any</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλεις <b>τίποτα</b>; Θέλεις κάτι; - Do you wantᵀ⁻ᶠᵒʳᵐ anything/something?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τίποτα, a commonly used variant form of τίποτε. Both forms from the Ancient Greek τίποτε, also as phrase τί ποτε. Sense &quot;something&quot; is Hellenistic.</div><b>τίποτε</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τίποτα">τίποτα</a></div><b>τίς</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Older polytonic spelling of <a href="bword://τις">τις</a> (tis) interrogative in old set phrases like</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τίς</b> πταίει; - who is at fault?</div><b>τίτλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τίτλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. title, heading (name of book, film, project, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. title, honorific (additions to someone&#x27;s name intended to add dignity)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) titles, credits (at beginning and end of film)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. security, bond</div><b>τα</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τά.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, weak) they (3rd person neuter plural, nominative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal, pronoun, weak) them (3rd person neuter plural, accusative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τά.</div><b>τα αγαθά κόποις κτώνται</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>no pain, no gain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from Ancient Greek τά ἀγαθά κόποις κτῶνται.</div><b>τα εκατό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of τοις <a href="bword://εκατό">εκατό</a></div><b>τα εν οίκω μη εν δήμω</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, literary) don&#x27;t air your dirty linen in public (private matters should remain so)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τὰ ἐν οἴκῳ μὴ ἐν δήμῳ.</div><b>τα κουμπιά της Αλέξαινας</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>therein lies the rub</div><b>τα λέμε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>see you later! bye!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τα, weak form of the pronoun αυτά &amp; λέμε of verb λέω.</div><b>τα προς το ζην</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>livelihood</div><b>τα σιγανά ποτάμια να φοβάσαι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>still waters run deep</div><b>τα φέρνω βόλτα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic) make ends meet, get by, make do</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύσκολα <b>τα φέρνει βόλτα</b>, γιατί είναι άνεργος τα δύο τελευταία χρόνια. - He barely makes ends meet, because he has been unemployed for the last two years.</div><b>ταΐζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to feed</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to host guests, accommodate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to provide someone with the necessities of life, support</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ταγίζω from Koine Greek ταγή from Ancient Greek τάσσω, from Proto-Indo-European *teh₂g-.</div><b>ταίρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ταίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. partner, mate (the matching one of a pair)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. partner, mate (one of an unmarried couple)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ταίριν, ταίριον, diminutive with loss of initial [e] of ancient ἑταῖρος.</div><b>ταβάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ταβάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ceiling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish تاوان (tavan).</div><b>ταβάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flat saucepan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish تاوه, from Classical Persian تاوه.</div><b>ταβέρνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταβέρνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>taverna, cafe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Latin taberna (“inn, tavern, shop”).</div><b>ταβερνιάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταβερνιάρες, feminine ταβερνιάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>restaurant owner, owner of a tavern.</div><b>ταβερνούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταβερνούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://ταβέρνα">ταβέρνα</a> f</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ταβέρν(α)) + -ούλα.</div><b>ταγάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ταγάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. shoulder bag, haversack, knapsack (often decorated)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nosebag (for animals)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. betel bag</div>
  <div class='def'>4. peasant&#x27;s woollen shoulder bag</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ταγάριον, from Koine Greek ταγή.</div><b>ταγέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) skirt suit, and skirt, η <a href="bword://φούστα">φούστα</a>)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French tailleur (“woman&#x27;s suit”).</div><b>ταγματάρχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ταγματάρχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the rank of major in the British and US armies with the NATO grade OF-3</div><b>ταινία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταινίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. film (UK), movie (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εκείνη η <b>ταινία</b> ακόμα δεν παίζεται στα σινεμά. - That film/movie is not yet showing in theaters.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ribbon, tape, band</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tape measure</div>
  <div class='def'>4. streak</div>
  <div class='def'>5. tapeworm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ταινία.</div><b>ταινιογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταινιογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>filmography</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ταινία + -γραφία</div><b>ταιριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive, transitive) to match, go with, correspond with</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ταιριάζω from ταίρ(ι), “pair”) + -ιάζω. Related to the ancient ἑταῖρος.</div><b>ταις</b>
  <div><i>article, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) monotonic form of ταῖς, feminine dative plural of ὁ (ho)</div><b>τακούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τακούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heel (of shoe)</div><b>τακτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tact</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French tact.</div><b>τακτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>regularly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τακτικός">τακτικός</a></div><b>τακτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τακτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tactics</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://τακτικός">τακτικός</a></div><b>τακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τακτική, neuter τακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. usual, regular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. frequent, regular</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tactical</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (ordinal) ordinal</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τακτικό</b> αριθμητικό - ordinal number</div><b>τακτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>taxis</div><b>τακτοποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be sorted out, be fixed</div><b>τακτοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to organise, arrange, settle, sort out, fix (to put into an orderly sequence or arrangement or to plan)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τακτοποιώ</b> τη ζωή μου - to sort out one&#x27;s life</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) to sort out, fix (to punish someone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη φοβάσαι, θα τον <b>τακτοποιήσω</b> εγώ. - Don&#x27;t worry, I&#x27;ll sort him out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τακτ(ός), “arranged, fixed, set”) + -ο- + -ποιώ. First part from the ancient τάξη/τάσσω and suffix from the ancient ποιέω / ποιῶ. First attested in 1855, a calque of French mettre en ordre.</div><b>ταλάντωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταλαντώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oscillation</div>
  <div class='ex'>απλή αρμονική <b>ταλάντωση</b> - simple harmonic motion</div><b>ταλέντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ταλέντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. talent (marked natural ability or skill)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει φυσικό <b>ταλέντο</b> στο βιολί. - She has a natural talent for the violin.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) talent (talented person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο κυνηγός <b>ταλέντων</b> παρακολουθούσε το ματς. - The talent scout was watching the match.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian talento, from Latin talentum (“Grecian weight, talent of money”), from Ancient Greek τάλαντον.</div><b>ταλαίπωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ταλαίπωρη, neuter ταλαίπωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poor, miserable, wretched (very sad, ill, or poor)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς ο <b>ταλαίπωρος</b> δεν μπορεί πια να περπατήσει. - Grandad, the poor thing, can no longer walk.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ταλαίπωρος.</div><b>ταλαιπωρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταλαιπωρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hardship, suffering, trial (hard times)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια ζωή όλο <b>ταλαιπωρίες</b> έχω! - All I&#x27;ve ever had in my life is hardship!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) hassle, trouble, inconvenience</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συγγνώμη για την <b>ταλαιπωρία</b>. - Sorry for the inconvenience.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τᾰλαιπωρῐ́ᾱ, from Ancient Greek ταλαίπωρος.</div><b>ταλαιπωρημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ταλαιπωρημένη, neuter ταλαιπωρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>miserable from hardships, wretched</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η υγεία του είναι πολύ <b>ταλαιπωρημένη</b>. - His health has suffered many hardships.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ταλαιπωρούμαι and ταλαιπωριέμαι, passive voices of ταλαιπωρώ (“trouble, pester”).</div><b>ταλαιπωριέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of ταλαιπωρούμαι</div><b>ταλαιπωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to trouble, bother, pester, beleaguer, worry, badger (to make suffer)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα σας δώσω το χαρτί τώρα για να μην σας <b>ταλαιπωρώ</b> αύριο. - I&#x27;ll give you the sheet now so I won&#x27;t trouble you tomorrow.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ταλαιπωρῶ, contracted form of τᾰλαιπωρέω, from Ancient Greek ταλαίπωρος.</div><b>ταλαντεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to oscillate (to swing back and forth, especially if with a regular rhythm)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sway, to vacillate (to move or swing from side to side, or backward and forward, or unsteadily; to rock)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to vacillate, to waver (to swing indecisively from one course of action or opinion to another)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: διστάζω (distázo)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ταλαντεύομαι.</div><b>ταλαντούχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ταλαντούχος or ταλαντούχα, neuter ταλαντούχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>talented, gifted</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το βραβείο Όσκαρ πήγε στον πιο <b>ταλαντούχο</b> ηθοποιό. - The Oscar went to the most talented actor.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ταλαντοῦχος, equivalent to τάλαντο + -ούχος.</div><b>ταμίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ταμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cashier, teller, bursar, treasurer</div><b>ταμείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ταμεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cash desk, checkout, cashier&#x27;s desk</div>
  <div class='def'>2. box office, ticket office</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ταμεῖον, from Ancient Greek ταμιεῖον, with semantic loan from French caisse and semantic loan from Latin thesaurus.</div><b>ταμειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ταμειακή, neuter ταμειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cash, fiscal</div><b>ταμπάκης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tanner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish طبق (tabak) From Arabic طَبَق.</div><b>ταμπάσκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Tabasco (sauce)</div><b>ταμπέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. signpost, board, sign</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) label, stereotype</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian tabella (“table, board”).</div><b>ταμπελάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a (small) label or tag (e.g. on a garment or wall)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ταμπέλα + -άκι.</div><b>ταμπλό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) dashboard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) backboard (board supporting basket)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) table</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French tableau (“board”).</div><b>ταμπλώ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ταμπλό">ταμπλό</a></div><b>ταμπουράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταμπουράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tambura or tamburas, stringed musical instrument similar to a lute</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a family of instruments similar to a lute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish طنبوره (tambura), from Classical Persian تنبور.</div><b>ταμπού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) taboo</div><b>ταμπόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) tampon (for menstruation or wound treatment)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) ink-pad (for rubber stamps)</div><b>τανάλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τανάλιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tongs, pliers, forceps, pincers (used for gripping/extraction)</div><b>τανκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τανκς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tank, armoured vehicle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of English tank</div><b>ταντάλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tantalum</div><b>ταξί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) taxi</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French taxi.</div><b>ταξίαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ταξίαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an army rank with the NATO grade OF-6; brigadier in the British army</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an army rank with the NATO grade OF-6; brigadier general in the US army</div>
  <div class='def'>3. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-6; air commodore in the RAF</div>
  <div class='def'>4. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-6; brigadier general in the USAF</div><b>ταξίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ταξίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trip, journey, voyage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τάξις.</div><b>ταξιανθία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταξιανθίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inflorescence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τάξη + άνθος + -ία.</div><b>ταξιαρχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταξιαρχίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brigade</div><b>ταξιδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to travel, sail</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to transport in the imagination</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ταξιδεύω, from ταξίδ(ι), “voyage”) + -εύω.</div><b>ταξιδιωτικές επιταγές</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of ταξιδιωτική <a href="bword://επιταγή">επιταγή</a></div><b>ταξιδιωτική επιταγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταξιδιωτικές επιταγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traveller&#x27;s cheque (UK), traveler&#x27;s check (US)</div><b>ταξιδιωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ταξιδιωτική, neuter ταξιδιωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traveller&#x27;s, relating to travel</div><b>ταξιδιώτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταξιδιώτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traveller, wayfarer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ταξίδι + -ώτης.</div><b>ταξιθέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταξιθέτες, feminine ταξιθέτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usher (male; in theatre, cinema etc)</div><b>ταξιθέτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταξιθέτριες, masculine ταξιθέτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usher, usherette (female; in cinema, theatre, etc)</div><b>ταξινόμηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>categorization, classification, sorting, taxonomy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the ταξινομη- stem of ταξινομώ + -ση (-si), a calque of French taxinomie.</div><b>ταξιτζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταξιτζήδες, feminine ταξιτζού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) taxi driver, cabdriver, cabbie (person who drives a taxicab)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>ταξιτζής</b> μας χρέωσε πενήντα ευρώ για τη διαδρομή από το αεροδρόμιο μέχρι το κέντρο της Αθήνας. - The taxi driver charged us fifty euros for the route from the airport to the centre of Athens.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ταξί + -τζής.</div><b>ταξιτζού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταξιτζούδες, masculine ταξιτζής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) taxi driver, cabdriver, cabbie (person who drives a taxicab)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το να δεις μια <b>ταξιτζού</b> είναι αρκετό σπάνιο στην Ελλάδα. - To see a female taxi driver is quite rare in Greece.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ταξί + -τζού.</div><b>ταξχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ταξίαρχος">ταξίαρχος</a></div><b>ταπέτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταπέτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>floor mat, rug</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian tappeto, from Latin tapēte, from Ancient Greek τάπης</div><b>ταπεινότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural ταπεινότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) humility</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable, usually) modesty</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncountable, usually) baseness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ταπεινότης, equivalent to ταπεινός + -ότητα.</div><b>ταπεινώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be humbled, be humiliated</div><b>ταπεινώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to humiliate, humble</div><b>ταπετσαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταπετσαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wallpaper, other wall covering</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fabric used to cover furniture, upholstery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian tappezzeria.</div><b>ταπητουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταπητουργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carpet manufacture, carpet industry</div><b>ταπητουργείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταπητουργεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carpet workshop or factory</div><b>ταπητουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταπητουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carpetmaker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ταπέτο + -ουργός.</div><b>ταπιόκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tapioca</div><b>ταράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to agitate, trouble someone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to disturb (of pain, stressful situation)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to shake (e.g. a liquid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ταράζω, from Ancient Greek τᾰράσσω / τᾰράττω, from past tense:‑αξα to present:‑άζω, in the pattern of στάζω, ἔσταξα.</div><b>ταράτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταράτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terrace, veranda, balcony</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish تراچه, from Italian terrazza.</div><b>ταραμάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταραμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tarama (cured fish roe - most commonly: cod or carp)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish طرامه (tarama), طاراما (tarama); see Turkish taramak (“to comb”), which is from the same root, for more.</div><b>ταραμοσαλάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταραμοσαλάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>taramosalata</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ταραμάς + σαλάτα.</div><b>ταραχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταραχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. agitation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. disturbance, unrest, turmoil</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) disorder, disturbances, unrest (interruption of civic peace or of public order)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ταραχή.</div><b>ταραχοποιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ταραχοποιός or ταραχοποιά, neuter ταραχοποιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>troublemaking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ταραχοποιός. By surface analysis, ταραχ(ή)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ταραχοποιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>troublemaker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ταραχοποιός. By surface analysis, ταραχ(ή)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιός.</div><b>ταραχώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ταραχώδης, neuter ταραχώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tumultuous, turbulent (period, life, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ταραχώδης. By surface analysis, ταραχή + -ώδης.</div><b>ταριχεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be embalmed</div><b>ταριχεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to embalm, mummify</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to stuff</div><b>ταρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) tarot (any of the card games)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) tarot card (any of the seventy-eight cards)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French tarot.</div><b>τας</b>
  <div><i>article, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://τις">τις</a> (tis) (the feminine, accusative, plural form of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o))</div>
  <div class='ex'>νίπτω <b>τας</b> χείρας μου - I wash my hands (of)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τάς.</div><b>τασάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τασάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ashtray</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τάσι + -άκι</div><b>τασμανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τασμανική, neuter τασμανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Tasmanian (relating to the Australian state of Tasmania or its people)</div><b>τατουάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tattoo (skin marking)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French tatouage.</div><b>ταυ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) tau, the 19th letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) A T-square.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ταῦ.</div><b>ταυρομάχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταυρομάχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>matador, bullfighter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ταύρος + μάχομαι</div><b>ταυτολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tautology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ταὐτολογία.</div><b>ταυτολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to repeat the same concept with different words</div><b>ταυτοχρονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ταυτοχρονισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. simultaneousness, simultaneity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. concurrency</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ταυτόχρονος + -ισμός.</div><b>ταυτότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ταυτότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. identity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) ID, identity card</div>
  <div class='def'>3. identity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The general sense is from Ancient Greek ταὐτότης, equivalent to ταὐτός + -ότητα. The mathematics sense is a calque of French identité.</div><b>ταυτόχρονα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
