  <div class='def'>the one who is a perfectionist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τέλειος + -ο- (-o-) + -μανής</div><b>τελειομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τελειομανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>perfectionism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τέλει(ος), “perfect”) + -ο- (-o-) + μανία, a free translation of the English perfectionism.</div><b>τελειοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to perfect (to make perfect; to improve or hone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek τελειοποιώ. By surface analysis, τέλει(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + -ποιώ.</div><b>τελειότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) perfection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek τελειότης, from the accusative singular &quot;τὴν τελειότητα&quot;, equivalent to τέλειος + -ότητα.</div><b>τελειώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with different sense) to make oneself morally perfect</div><b>τελειώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (passive) to finish, complete, end</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ταινία δεν έχει <b>τελειώσει</b> ακόμα. - The film has not ended yet.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (passive) to dismiss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τελειώνω, from Ancient Greek τελειῶ, contracted form of τελειόω + -ώνω.</div><b>τελεσίγραφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τελεσίγραφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ultimatum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly formed from the τελεσι- stem of Ancient Greek τέλος and -γραφον, neuter singular of -γραφος (-graphos). A loose calque of Latin ultimātum.</div><b>τελεσμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τελεσμένη, neuter τελεσμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>done, accomplished</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τελούμαι, passive voice of τελώ (“perform, fulfill”).</div><b>τελεστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τελεστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>operator</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τελεστής (translation of English operator).</div><b>τελετή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τελετές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ceremony, ceremonial, rite, celebration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τελετή”).
From τελέω.</div><b>τελετουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rite, ceremony</div>
  <div class='def'>2. procedure, protocol (way of carrying out a simple operation)</div><b>τελετουργικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τελετουργικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ritual</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://τελετουργικός">τελετουργικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://τελετουργικός">τελετουργικός</a></div><b>τελετουργικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τελετουργική, neuter τελετουργικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ritual, ceremonial</div><b>τελευταία</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://τελευταίος">τελευταίος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τελευταίος">τελευταίος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lately, recently (in recent times)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν τον έχω δει <b>τελευταία</b>. - I haven&#x27;t seen him lately.</div><b>τελευταίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τελευταία, neuter τελευταίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. last</div>
  <div class='def'>2. recent, latest</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lowest, least important, least effective</div>
  <div class='def'>4. latter</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (noun) last remaining, ultimate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τελευταῖος. By surface analysis, τελευτή + -αίος.</div><b>τελευταίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) lately, recently (in recent times)</div><b>τελεφερίκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cable car; hanging carriage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French téléphérique.</div><b>τελεόστεος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τελεόστεοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teleost (Teleostei)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from τελεόστεοι / Τελεόστεοι, itself a learned borrowing from translingual Teleostei, from Ancient Greek τέλειος + Ancient Greek ὀστέον.</div><b>τελικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. finally, at last, after all, all things considered, in the end</div>
  <div class='def'>2. eventually, at last</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι έγινε όμως τελικά; - What ended up happening?</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://τελικός">τελικός</a></div><b>τελικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τελική, neuter τελικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>final, last</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τελικό</b> δοκίμιο - final proof</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τελικός. By surface analysis, τέλος + -ικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τελικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. end, final</div>
  <div class='def'>2. final</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τελικός. By surface analysis, τέλος + -ικός.</div><b>τελλούριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tellurium</div><b>τελούριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τελλούριο">τελλούριο</a></div><b>τελωνειακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τελωνειακή, neuter τελωνειακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>customs</div>
  <div class='ex'>τελωνειακή αρχή (&quot;customs, customs authority&quot;)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τελωνειακοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>customs officer</div><b>τελώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to perform, do, accomplish a formal procedure; to perform (a marriage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to perform, do, accomplish a formal procedure; to hold (a memorial service etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to perform, do, accomplish a formal procedure; to commit (a crime)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to be in a state of</div><b>τελώνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τελώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>customs officer, tax collector</div><b>τεμάχιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τεμάχια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fragment, share, part, piece</div>
  <div class='def'>2. piece (extract from a larger musical work)</div><b>τεμαχίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cut into pieces</div><b>τεμενάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bow, curtsey</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish تمنا (temenna), from Arabic تَمَنَّى, the semantic shift from wish to bow is an Ottoman innovation.</div><b>τεμπέλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τεμπέλα, neuter τεμπέλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lazy, idle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish تنبل (tembel), from Classical Persian تنبل (tanbal).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τεμπέληδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>idler, sluggard, layabout, lazybones, loafer, slouch</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι <b>τεμπέληδες</b> δεν προκόβουν. - The idle do not prosper.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish تنبل (tembel), from Classical Persian تنبل (tanbal).</div><b>τεμπελιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεμπελιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(common) laziness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τεμπέλ(ης)) + -ιά</div><b>τεμπελιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to idle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τεμπέλ(ης) (“lazy”) + -ιάζω</div><b>τεμπεσίρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τεμπεσίρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cue chalk used in billiards</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) the act of buying on credit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a piece of chalk used for writing on a blackboard (dated)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish تباشیر, from Classical Persian تباشیر), ultimately from Sanskrit तवक्षीर.</div><b>τενέσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tennessine</div><b>τενίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τενίστες, feminine τενίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tennis player</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian tennista with -ς (-s) suffix.</div><b>τενίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (masculine τενίστας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female tennis player</div><b>τεντούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τεντούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tentura (a sweetish spirit flavoured with herbs and spices, including cinnamon and cloves)</div><b>τεπές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τεπέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hill, summit (dated)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crown of a hat; the hemispherical part of a cap which covers the head</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish تپه (tepe, “hill, crown of the head”). See Turkish tepe for further cognates.</div><b>τεράστιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τεράστια, neuter τεράστιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>huge, monstrous, enormous, immense, vast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τεράστιος, from τέρας.</div><b>τεράτσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τεράτσ̌ια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) fruit of the carob tree</div><b>τεραβάτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) terawatt</div><b>τερατογένεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teratogenesis</div><b>τερατολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teratology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τερᾰτολογῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>τερατσιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τερατσ̌ιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) carob tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κερατέα</div><b>τερηδόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τερηδόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caries, tooth decay</div><b>τερματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to end, terminate, conclude, wind up</div><b>τερματίσει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>active nonfinite form of <a href="bword://τερματίζω">τερματίζω</a></div><b>τερματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τερματισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>final, terminating (used as a gerund), termination</div>
  <div class='ex'>γραμμή <b>τερματισμού</b> - finishing / finish line</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τερματίζω + -ισμός.</div><b>τερματοφύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τερματοφύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goalkeeper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τέρμα + φύλακας</div><b>τερπένιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τερπένια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terpene, hydrocarbon derived from and structurally related to isoprene</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English terpene or French terpène or German Terpen, the latter coined by Kekulé in analogy to German Terpentine (“turpentine”), from Latin terebinthina (“turpentine”), from Ancient Greek τερέβινθος.</div><b>τες</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, weak) they (3rd person feminine plural, nominative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal, pronoun, weak) them (3rd person feminine plural, accusative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the medieval article τὲς (feminine accusative plural), from Ancient Greek τάς. It survives as weak pronoun form in Standard Modern Greek. Also as article form in idioms, equivalent to standard τις (tis).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>article, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative feminine plural of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o) (the)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the medieval article τὲς (feminine accusative plural), from Ancient Greek τάς. It survives as weak pronoun form in Standard Modern Greek. Also as article form in idioms, equivalent to standard τις (tis).</div><b>τεσσάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τεσσάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. four (describing a set or group with four elements)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. four-roomed apartment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. four</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τέσσερα + -άρι.</div><b>τεσσαρακοστή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine nominative/vocative/accusative singular of τεσσᾰρᾰκοστός</div><b>τεσσαρακοστοί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masculine nominative/vocative plural of τεσσᾰρᾰκοστός</div><b>τεσσαρακοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τεσσαρακοστή, neuter τεσσαρακοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fortieth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τεσσαρακοστός.</div><b>τεσσερισήμισι</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine or feminine (neuter τεσσεράμισι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. four and a half</div>
  <div class='def'>2. half past four</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τέσσερα + μισό.</div><b>τεστ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) test</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τεστ</b> Ρόρσαχ - Rorschach test</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English test.</div><b>τεστοστερόνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural τεστοστερόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) testosterone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English testosterone.</div><b>τετέλεσται</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a dramatic pronouncement of impending death or other end</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek τετέλεσται, the 3sg perfect passive form of Ancient Greek τελέω.</div><b>τεταγμένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεταγμένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ordinate (y-axis value)</div><b>τεταμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τεταμένη, neuter τεταμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tense (characterized by strain (on the nerves, emotions, etc.))</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tense (showing signs of stress or strain; not relaxed)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τεταμένος, passive perfect participle of τείνω, with semantic loan from French tendu.</div><b>τεταρτοετής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fourth year student</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τεταρτο- (tetarto-) + -ετής, from 1894</div><b>τετμημένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τετμημένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abscissa (x-axis value)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized passive perfect participle of τέμνω. Its use in modern mathematical geometry is as a semantic loan from Latin abscissa.</div><b>τετράγωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τετράγωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) quadrilateral, tetragon, square, rectangle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. square</div>
  <div class='def'>3. square (number raised to the power two)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το τέσσερα είναι το <b>τετράγωνο</b> του δύο. - Four is the square of two.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. quarter, block, city block (a group of buildings not intersected by public streets)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τετράγωνον, from τετράγωνος &lt; τετρα- (tetra-, “tetra-, four”) + γων(ία), “corner, angle”) + -ος (-os).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://τετράγωνος">τετράγωνος</a>:; masculine accusative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://τετράγωνος">τετράγωνος</a>:; neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>τετράγωνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τετράγωνη, neuter τετράγωνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>square</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τετράγωνος. By surface analysis, τετρά- + γων(ία)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>τετράδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>notebook</div><b>τετράκις</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fourfold, four times</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τετράκις</b> εκατομμύριο - quadrillion</div><b>τετράκις εκατομμύριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τετρακισεκατομμύριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. quadrillion, thousand million million (10¹⁵)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (British, obsolete) quadrillion, million million million million (10²⁴)</div><b>τετράνυχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τετράνυχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Any mite of the genus Tetranychus or related taxa.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Tetranychus. By surface analysis, τετρα- (tetra-, “four-”) + Ancient Greek ὄνῠχος.</div><b>τετράπατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τετράπατη, neuter τετράπατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>four-story (of a building)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τετρα- (tetra-, “tetra-, four”) + πάτος.</div><b>τετράπλευρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τετράπλευρη, neuter τετράπλευρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>four-sided, quadrilateral (having four sides)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek τετράπλευρος. By surface analysis, τετρά- + -πλευρος (-plevros).</div><b>τετράωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τετράωρη, neuter τετράωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) four hours, lasting four hours</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τετρα- (tetra-, “tetra-”) + ώρ(α), “hour”) + -ος (-os)</div><b>τετραήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>four-day (lasting four days)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τετρα- (tetra-) + ημέρα + -ος (-os)</div><b>τετραγωνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to make square</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to square (to draw a square with the same area as)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τετραγωνίζω</b> τον κύκλο - to square the circle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to square (to multiply by itself; to raise to the second power)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τετραγωνίζω. By surface analysis, τετράγων(ος)) + -ίζω.</div><b>τετραγωνίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τετραγωνίστριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quadratrix</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τετραγωνίζω + -ίστρια, a calque of New Latin quadratrix.</div><b>τετραγωνικά μέτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τετραγωνικό <a href="bword://μέτρο">μέτρο</a></div><b>τετραγωνικές ρίζες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τετραγωνική <a href="bword://ρίζα">ρίζα</a></div><b>τετραγωνική ρίζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τετραγωνικές ρίζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>square root</div><b>τετραγωνικό εκατοστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τετραγωνικά εκατοστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>square centimetre</div><b>τετραγωνικό μέτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τετραγωνικά μέτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>square metre</div>
  <div class='ex'>Abbreviation: τ.μ. (t.m.)</div><b>τετραγωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τετραγωνική, neuter τετραγωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>square</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τετράγωνος + -ικός.</div><b>τετρακισεκατομμύριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τετρακισεκατομμύριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τετράκις">τετράκις</a> <a href="bword://εκατομμύριο">εκατομμύριο</a></div><b>τετρακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(noun, numeral) four hundred</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of τετρακόσιοι</div><b>τετραπλάσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τετραπλάσια, neuter τετραπλάσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The proportional number fourfold, quadruple, four times the size</div><b>τετραπλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τετραπλή, neuter τετραπλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quadruple, fourfold</div><b>τετραχλωράνθρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) carbon tetrachloride, tetrachloromethane</div><b>τετραχλωρομεθάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tetrachloromethane, carbon tetrachloride</div><b>τετριμμένα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in mundane, boring way</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τετριμμένος">τετριμμένος</a></div><b>τετριμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τετριμμένη, neuter τετριμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, formal) mundane, commonplace, platitudinous, trite</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βαριέμαι όταν μιλάει· όλο <b>τετριμμένα</b> πράγματα λέει. - I am bored when he/she talks; he/she says boring things all the time.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek τετρῐμμένος. Formal perfect participle of τρίβομαι, passive voice of τρίβω. It retains the ancient reduplication τ-ε-τρ....</div><b>τεττιγονίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Tettigoniidae, the tettigoniids or katydids.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μόλις το έκανε αυτό, συνειδητοποίησε ότι ανήκει στο είδος των <b>τεττιγονίδων</b>. - As soon as he did this, he realized that it was a species of the Tettigoniidae.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From translingual Tettigoniidae, suffixed with -ίδες.</div><b>τεφροδόχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεφροδόχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hearth, ash pan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τέφρα + δοχείο.</div><b>τεφροδόχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεφροδόχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>funerary urn, cinerary urn, burial urn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τέφρα + δοχείο.</div><b>τεφτέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ledger, accounts book</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish دفتر (defter), from Arabic دَفْتَر (daftar), from Aramaic דהפתּיר (defter), from Ancient Greek διφθέρα.</div><b>τεχνήτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) technetium</div><b>τεχνίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τεχνίτες, feminine τεχνίτρια or τεχνίτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. technician</div>
  <div class='def'>2. artisan, craftsman</div><b>τεχνίτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεχνίτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τεχνίτρια">τεχνίτρια</a></div><b>τεχνίτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεχνίτριες, masculine τεχνίτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. technician</div>
  <div class='def'>2. artisan, craftswoman</div><b>τεχνητές γονιμοποίησες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τεχνητή <a href="bword://γονιμοποίηση">γονιμοποίηση</a></div><b>τεχνητές νοημοσύνες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τεχνητή <a href="bword://νοημοσύνη">νοημοσύνη</a></div><b>τεχνητή γονιμοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεχνητές γονιμοποίησες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>artificial insemination, AI</div><b>τεχνητή νοημοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεχνητές νοημοσύνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>artificial intelligence</div><b>τεχνητοί χλοοτάπητες</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://τεχνητός">τεχνητός</a> <a href="bword://χλοοτάπητας">χλοοτάπητας</a></div><b>τεχνητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τεχνητή, neuter τεχνητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>artificial, synthetic, man-made, false</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τεχνητός. By surface analysis, τέχνη + -τός.</div><b>τεχνητός αερισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) air conditioning</div><b>τεχνητός χλοοτάπητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τεχνητοί χλοοτάπητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>artificial turf</div><b>τεχνικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τεχνικός">τεχνικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>technically (in terms of technique; in a technical way)</div><b>τεχνική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεχνικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>technique</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://τεχνικός">τεχνικός</a></div><b>τεχνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τεχνική, neuter τεχνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>technical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τεχνικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τεχνικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>technician</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τεχνικός.</div><b>τεχνογνωσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>know-how, technical expertise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τέχν(η)) + -ο- (-o-) + γνώσ(η)) + -ία.</div><b>τεχνολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεχνολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>technology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French technologie, from Ancient Greek τέχνη + -λογία.</div><b>τεχνολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τεχνολογική, neuter τεχνολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>technological, technologic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τεχνολογία + -ικός.</div><b>τεχνολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τεχνολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. technologist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in a radiography department) radiographer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French technologue from technologie. The Hellenistic Koine τεχνολόγος has different sense:.</div><b>τεϊομαντεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τεϊομαντείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tea tasseography, tea tasseomancy, tea reading (the supposed prediction of the future with the leaves left in a cup of tea after drinking it)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τέιο + -μαντεία.</div><b>τεύχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τεύχη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>issue, edition (of a newspaper or magazine)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek τεῦχος, from Ancient Greek τεῦχος.</div><b>τζάγκουαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) jaguar</div><b>τζάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fireplace; hearth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) dynasty; house</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὀτζάκιν, from Ottoman Turkish اوجاق (ocak).</div><b>τζάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τζάμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. window glass, glazing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) a job finished to perfection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish جام (cam, “glass”).</div><b>τζάμπα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) free, free of charge, gratis, for nothing (without paying)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αμάξι του ήταν παλιό και μου το ’δωσε <b>τζάμπα</b>. - His car was old and he gave it to me for free.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) for next to nothing, for a song (cheaply, at a very cheap price)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το πουκάμισο το αγόρασα <b>τζάμπα</b> στις προσφορές. - I bought this shirt for next to nothing in the sales.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) in vain, for nothing (for no reason, wastefully, or pointlessly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ρολόι δεν δούλεψε ποτέ· <b>τζάμπα</b> έδωσα τόσα λεφτά. - The watch never worked; I paid so much money in vain.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish جابا (caba, “gratis, free of charge”).</div><b>τζάνερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τζάνερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cherry plum (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From analysis of the earlier form τζανερίκι (now dialectal) as τζάνερο + -ίκι; in fact from Ottoman Turkish جان اریكی.</div><b>τζάουλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) joule (SI Unit of work, energy and heat)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Transliteration of the name of the English physicist James Joule</div><b>τζίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τζίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. genie, djinn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) genius</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish جن (cin), from Arabic جِنّ (jinn, “genie”).</div><b>τζίντζερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ginger (spice)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English ginger, ultimately from Late Ancient Greek ζιγγίβερις, from Sauraseni Prakrit 𑀲𑀺𑀁𑀕𑀺𑀯𑁂𑀭. See ginger for more. Doublet of ζιγγίβερη, inherited from Ancient Greek.</div><b>τζίρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turnover (money taken in a calendar year)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian giro, from Latin gȳrus, from Ancient Greek γῦρος.</div><b>τζίτζικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τζίτζικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cicada</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>τζίτζικας</b> ζει στα δέντρα. - The cicada lives in the trees.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τζίτζιξ, τζίτζικας, from Ancient Greek τέττιξ, of imitative origin.</div><b>τζίτζιφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τζίτζιφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jujube fruit (Ziziphus jujuba)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ζίζυφον, ultimately unknown, perhaps Pre-Greek.</div><b>τζίφος</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strikeout (the act of yielding nothing)</div><b>τζίφρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τζίφρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) signature, especially when sloppy, hasty or illegible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan zifra.</div><b>τζαζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) jazz</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> English jazz</div><b>τζαμί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τζαμιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mosque</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish جامع (camiʼ). Displaced Byzantine Greek μασγίδιον.</div><b>τζαμαϊκανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τζαμαϊκανή, neuter τζαμαϊκανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Jamaican</div><b>τζαμπατζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deadhead, freeloader (anyone spectating or traveling without charge)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τσάμπα + -τζής.</div><b>τζαούσιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τζαούσιοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) tzaousios</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek τζαούσιος, from Ottoman Turkish چاوش, from Proto-Common Turkic *čabïš.</div><b>τζατζίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τζατζίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tzatziki</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish جاجیق.</div><b>τζερεμές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τζερεμέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unjust fine or penalty, payment for damage caused by someone else or by accident</div>
  <div class='ex'>θα ξαναπληρώσω <b>τζερεμέ</b> - I will again pay an unjust fine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) a lazy, useless person; a good-for-nothing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish جریمه, from Classical Persian جریمه, from Arabic جَرِيمَة and perhaps under the semantic influence of Arabic غَرَامَة. Compare the Turkish cereme descendant from the Ottoman usage of the term with the same meaning of &quot;unjust fine, damage to be paid due to someone other&#x27;s fault&quot; as in the sense borrowed into Greek.</div><b>τζερτζελές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τζερτζελέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) uproar, commotion, roughhouse, horseplay (in a positive light)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) noisy person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish زلزله (zelzele), from Arabic زَلْزَلَة (zalzala).</div><b>τζετ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) jet, jet plane</div><b>τζετ σετ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) jet set</div><b>τζετ σκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) jet ski</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unadapted borrowing from English jet ski.</div><b>τζην</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>denim, jean (cloth)</div><b>τζιγέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τζιγέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) viscera, entrails of an animal, consumed as food</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish جكر (ciger, “liver, lung”), from Classical Persian جِگَر (jigar, “liver”).</div><b>τζιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) gin (alcoholic drink)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπαίνοντας στο μπαρ, ο Κέιτερ της είχε παραγγείλει ένα <b>τζιν</b> τόνικ … - On entering the bar, Kate had ordered a gin and tonic …</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) denim (cloth)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) jeans</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English gin.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) jean, denim (made of that cloth)</div>
  <div class='ex'>παντελόνι <b>τζιν</b> - jeans</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English jean, jeans, from French Gênes.</div><b>τζιτζίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cicada</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Greek τζίτζικας.</div><b>τζουγκρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peak, rough rock</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τ’ ἀγρίμι στέκει ’ς τὸ <b>τζουγκρὶ</b> κ’ ἡ σκύλα ’ς τσ’ ἀλυσίδες</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Variant of τσικάρι, τσουγκάρι.</div><b>τζουκμπόξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) jukebox</div><b>τζουράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τζουράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tzouras, stringed musical instrument similar to a lute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish جوره (cura), from either Classical Persian جوره or جره (jarra).</div><b>τζους</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) to leave, go away</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Kaliarda, indeclinable) to someone who left or someone who tends not to stay in one place for a long time</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Kaliarda, indeclinable) to exiled</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) to without</div><b>τζουτζές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τζουτζέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated) dwarf, midget</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jester, court fool</div>
  <div class='def'>3. jokester, funnyman, clown</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Turkish cüce, from Ottoman Turkish جوجه, from Classical Persian جوجه.</div><b>τζούμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. A kind of copper bucket, dome-shaped without welds or glue.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rock with a pointed top</div>
  <div class='def'>3. large stone mortar for pounding coffee, salt, etc.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. small hill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Variant of γκιούμι. Compare Albanian çumë.</div><b>τζούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τζούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. puff (of cigarette, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nip, wee dram (of whisky, etc)</div><b>τζόκερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) joker</div><b>τζόκιν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τζόκινγκ">τζόκινγκ</a></div><b>τζόκινγκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) jog, jogging</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάθε πρωί κάνω είκοσι λεπτά <b>τζόκινγκ</b>. - Every morning I go for a twenty minute jog.</div><b>τη</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. feminine accusative singular of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. monotonic form of the ancient dative τῇ, feminine dative singular of ὁ (o)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Form of την (tin), from Ancient Greek τήν</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, weak) she (3rd person feminine singular, nominative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Form of την (tin), from Ancient Greek τήν</div><b>τηγάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τηγάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frying pan, frypan</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τηγάνι</b> αντικολλητικό - non-stick frying pan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τηγάνιν, from Koine Greek τηγάνιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek τήγανον or τάγηνον.</div><b>τηγανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to fry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ταγηνίζω, τηγανίζω, from τάγηνον + -ίζω.</div><b>τηγανίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηγανίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pancake</div><b>τηγανητά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fried foods</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τηγανητός">τηγανητός</a></div><b>τηγανητές πατάτες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τηγανητή <a href="bword://πατάτα">πατάτα</a></div><b>τηγανητή πατάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηγανητές πατάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chip, french fry, fry</div><b>τηγανητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τηγανητή, neuter τηγανητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fried</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Hellenistic Koine Greek τηγανητόν, neuter of *τηγανητός. By surface analysis, τηγάνι + -ητός. Also see the Hellenistic synonym τηγανιστός.</div><b>τηγανιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηγανιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. contents of a pan, panful</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Greek dish containing pork or chicken</div><b>τηλέγραφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τηλέγραφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>telegraph</div><b>τηλέτυπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τηλέτυπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. teletype (obsolete computer input/output peripheral)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. teleprinter (telegraphic keyboard/printer)</div><b>τηλέφωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τηλέφωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. telephone, phone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. telephone number</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English telephone or French téléphone, ultimately coined from Greek elements; by surface analysis, τηλε- (tile-) + φωνή + -ον (-on).</div><b>τηλεγράφημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τηλεγραφήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>telegram (telegraphed message)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τηλε- (tile-, “tele-”) + English grapheme, from Ancient Greek γράφημα).</div><b>τηλεθέαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεθεάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>viewership, ratings (number of people who watched a certain programme)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η εκπομπή συγκέντρωσε μεγάλη <b>τηλεθέαση</b>. - The show had great ratings.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τῆλε + θεάομαι.</div><b>τηλεθεάτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεθεάτριες, masculine τηλεθεατής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>viewer (someone who watches television)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια εξοργισμένη <b>τηλεθεάτρια</b> κάλεσε την εκπομπή να διαμαρτυρηθεί. - A furious viewer called the show to complain.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τηλεθεατής + -τρια (-tria).</div><b>τηλεθεαματικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεθεαματικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>viewership, ratings (number who watched a particular programme)</div><b>τηλεθεατής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τηλεθεατές, feminine τηλεθεάτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>viewer (someone who watches television)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η εκπομπή προσφέρει διαγωνισμούς για τους <b>τηλεθεατές</b>. - The show offers competitions for viewers.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French téléspectateur, from Ancient Greek τῆλε + θεατής.</div><b>τηλεκοντρόλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(invariable) remote control</div><b>τηλεκπαίδευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεκπαιδεύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) teletraining, teleteaching, distance learning</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τηλ(ε)- (til(e)-, “far, tele-”) + εκπαίδευση, calque of English teleteaching.</div><b>τηλεομοιοτυπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεομοιοτυπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fax</div><b>τηλεομοιότυπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεομοιότυπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fax</div><b>τηλεοπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τηλεοπτική, neuter τηλεοπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. TV, television</div>
  <div class='ex'>τηλεοπτικό πρόγραμμα (television programme)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. televisual</div><b>τηλεπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) telepathy</div><b>τηλεπαιχνίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τηλεπαιχνίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>game show</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τηλε- (tile-, “tele-”) + παιχνίδι</div><b>τηλεπικοινωνία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεπικοινωνίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(plural normally) telecommunication(s)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οργανοσμός <b>Τηλεπικοινωνιών</b> Ελλάδος - Greek Telecommunications Organization</div><b>τηλεπικοινωνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τηλεπικοινωνιακή, neuter τηλεπικοινωνιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>telecommunication</div><b>τηλεργασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεργασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) teleworking, telecommuting</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ελληνική κυβέρνηση θέσπισε νόμους, που καθορίζουν τα δικαιώματα και τις υποχρεώσεις εργοδοτών και εργαζομένων, όταν οι υπάλληλοι απασχολούνται με <b>τηλεργασία</b>. - The Greek government has enacted laws that define the rights and obligations of employers and employees when employees are employed by telecommuting.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τηλ(ε)- (til(e)-, “far, tele-”) + εργασία, calque of French télétravail or calque of English teleworking.</div><b>τηλεσκοπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τηλεσκοπική, neuter τηλεσκοπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. telescopic (seen by means of a telescope; only visible through a telescope)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. telescopic (able to be extended or retracted by the use of parts that slide over one another)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. telescopic (pertaining to, or carried out by means of, a telescope)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French télescopique or English telescopic. By surface analysis, τηλεσκόπιο + -ικός.</div><b>τηλεσκόπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τηλεσκόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>telescope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from New Latin telescopium, from Ancient Greek τηλεσκόπος + -ιον (-ion), from τηλε- + σκοπέω, σκοπῶ.</div><b>τηλεσυνεδρίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεσυνεδριάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>teleconference</div><b>τηλεφωνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τηλεφωνώ">τηλεφωνώ</a></div><b>τηλεφωνήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεφωνήτριες, masculine τηλεφωνητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>telephonist, telegraphist, operator</div><b>τηλεφωνητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τηλεφωνητές, feminine τηλεφωνήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. telephonist, telegraphist, operator</div>
  <div class='def'>2. voicemail, answering machine (telephone device that records and transmits answers or information)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άφησε μήνυμα στον <b>τηλεφωνητή</b> σου. - She left you a voicemail.</div><b>τηλεφωνιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(reciprocal) to phone each other</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τηλεφωνιόμαστε</b> κάθε μέρα. - We phone each other daily.</div><b>τηλεφωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τηλεφωνική, neuter τηλεφωνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>telephone (attributive), telephonic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French téléphonique or English telephonic. By surface analysis, τηλέφων(ο)) + -ικός.</div><b>τηλεφωνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to telephone, phone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to phone each other</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French téléphoner or English telephone; by surface analysis, τηλέφωνο + -ώ.</div><b>τηλεφώνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τηλεφωνήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>telephone call, phone call, call, telephone conversation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τηλεφωνώ + -μα (-ma)</div><b>τηλεχειριστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τηλεχειριστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>remote control</div><b>τηλεόραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τηλεοράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>television (the display device, the shows displayed and the industry in which shows are made)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τηλε- (tile-, “far, tele-”) + όραση, a calque of French télévision.</div><b>την</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine accusative singular of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τήν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, weak) her (3rd person feminine singular, accusative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τήν.</div><b>την επόμενη</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the next day</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είπε πως θα τηλεφωνούσε <b>την επόμενη</b>. - She said he&#x27;d call the next day.</div><b>τηρούνται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person plural present indicative of τηρούμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://τηρώ">τηρώ</a></div><b>τηρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to abide (by), to adhere (to), to comply (with), to keep (to), to observe, to respect, to stick (to) (to follow or obey the custom, practice, or rules; to act in accordance with)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to keep, to be true to (to remain faithful to a given promise or word)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τηρώ</b> τις υποσχέσεις μου. - I keep my promises.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to keep, to maintain (to preserve order or function)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αστυνομία <b>τηρεί</b> την τάξη. - The police maintains order.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τηρῶ, with semantic loan from French garder (“keep”).</div><b>της</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine genitive singular of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τῆς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indirect object, personal) her</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Της</b> είπα την αλήθεια. - I told her the truth.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (possessive) her</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το όνομά <b>της</b> είναι Ελένη. - Her name is Eleni.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τῆς.</div><b>της καλομάνας το παιδί το πρώτο να &#x27;ναι κόρη</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>consoling proverb for women whose firstborn was a daughter, in times when a son was highly desired</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “may the good mother&#x27;s firstborn be a daughter”.</div><b>της καλομοίρας το παιδί το πρώτο κόρη να &#x27;ναι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://της">της</a> καλομοίρας <a href="bword://το">το</a> <a href="bword://παιδί">παιδί</a> <a href="bword://το">το</a> πρώτο να ’<a href="bword://ναι">ναι</a> <a href="bword://κόρη">κόρη</a></div><b>της καλομοίρας το παιδί το πρώτο να &#x27;ναι κόρη</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://της">της</a> καλομάνας <a href="bword://το">το</a> <a href="bword://παιδί">παιδί</a> <a href="bword://το">το</a> πρώτο να ’<a href="bword://ναι">ναι</a> <a href="bword://κόρη">κόρη</a></div><b>της μουρλής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, uncountable) mayhem, pandemonium, commotion, upheaval (literally: of the lunatic)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Απόψε θα γίνει <b>της μουρλής</b> στην Αθήνα με τις διαδηλώσεις. - There&#x27;ll be mayhem tonight in Athens because of the protests.</div><b>της πουτάνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, uncountable, vulgar) mayhem, pandemonium, commotion, upheaval, all hell breaks loose (literally: of the whore)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μέθυσαν μερικοί στον γάμο και έγινε <b>της πουτάνας</b>. - A few got drunk at the wedding and all hell broke loose.</div><b>της πουτάνας το κάγκελο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine or neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://της">της</a> πουτάνας: mayhem, pandemonium, commotion, upheaval, all hell breaks loose; the shit hits the fan</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θύμωσε ο άντρας της και έγινε <b>της πουτάνας το κάγκελο</b> στο σπίτι. - Her husband got mad and all hell broke loose in the house.</div><b>της προκοπής</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) decent, nice, becoming, respectable (literally: of progress, of success)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βάλε σε παρακαλώ ένα ρούχο <b>της προκοπής</b>. - Please put on some decent clothes.</div><b>της τρελής</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, idiomatic, uncountable) mayhem, pandemonium, commotion, upheaval (literally: of the crazy one)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην τράπεζα γινόταν <b>της τρελής</b> επειδή χάλασαν οι υπολογιστές. - There was pandemonium at the bank because the computers broke.</div><b>τι</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) what?</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τι</b> θέλετε; - What do you (pl.) want?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for stress) how, what</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τι</b> ομορφιά! - How beautiful, what beauty!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) anything, something, a little</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τί, neuter of the interrogative pronoun τίς;, from Proto-Indo-European *kʷís.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>what</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τι</b>; Είπε τέτοιο πράγμα; - What? He said that?</div><b>τι δέον γενέσθαι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) what is to be done?</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η οικονομική κρίση μαίνεται· <b>τι δέον γενέσθαι;</b> - The economic crisis is at its peak; what is to be done?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned stereotype interrogative expression from Katharevousa τί δέον γενέσθαι;, literally, “what needs to become?”
Interrogative pronoun τί &amp; neuter present participle δέον (of δέω² “to need”) &amp; middle aorist infinitive Ancient Greek γενέσθαι of γίγνομαι (“to become”).</div><b>τι κάνει νιάου νιάου στα κεραμίδια</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(humorous) no shit, Sherlock, does a bear shit in the woods (response to anything where the answer is completely obvious)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος έφαγε πάλι όλα τα μπισκότα μου; <b>Τι κάνει νιάου νιάου στα κεραμίδια;</b> Ο αδερφός σου! - Who ate all my biscuits again? Who do you think? Your brother!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;What goes meow meow on the roof tiles?&quot;</div><b>τι κάνεις</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) How are you? (singular^†)</div><b>τι κάνετε</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) How are you? (plural^†)</div><b>τι ώρα είναι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>what time is it?</div><b>τιμάριθμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τιμάριθμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>price index, cost of living index</div><b>τιμάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) timar (Ottoman fiefdom)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τιμάριον, from Ottoman Turkish تیمار (timar), from Classical Persian تیمار.</div><b>τιμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to honour (UK), honor (US)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Tίμα</b> τον πατέρα σου και τη μητέρα σου. - Honour your father and mother.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to respect</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to commemorate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τιμώ + modern suffix -άω. From Ancient Greek τιμῶ, contracted form of τιμάω.</div><b>τιμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τιμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. quotation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. honour, virtue, reputation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. faithfulness, virginity, fidelity</div>
  <div class='def'>4. price, value (of something, asset, fare, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η προτεινόμενη λιανική <b>τιμή</b> είναι 19,50€. - The suggested retail price is €19.50.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. pride, credit</div>
  <div class='def'>6. value of a variable</div>
  <div class='def'>7. coefficient</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τιμή.</div><b>τιμαριθμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τιμαριθμική, neuter τιμαριθμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cost of living, indexing</div>
  <div class='ex'>ΑΤΑ, Αυτόματη <b>Τιμαριθμική</b> Αναπροσαρμογή - automatic cost of living adjustment</div><b>τιμοκατάλογος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τιμοκατάλογοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>price list, pricing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν τελικά ισχύσει αυτός ο <b>τιμοκατάλογος</b>, τότε οι τιμές θα έχουν μείνει περίπου στα ίδια επίπεδα με τις αντίστοιχες περσινές. - If this price list finally comes into effect, the prices will be at around the same levels as the previous year.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English price list. From τιμ(ή), “price”) + -ο- (interfix) + κατάλογος.</div><b>τιμολόγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τιμολόγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>invoice, bill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τιμή + -ο- (-o-) + λόγος, calque of German Preiskurant. First attested 1856.</div><b>τιμονιέρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τιμονιέρηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. helmsman</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) guide, leader</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan timonier.</div><b>τιμωρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τιμωρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>punishment</div><b>τιμωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to punish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τῑμωρῶ, contracted form of τῑμωρέω.</div><b>τιμόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τιμόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. steering wheel (of car), helm, tiller (of boat) (steering apparatus)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να κρατάς γερά το <b>τιμόνι</b> όταν οδηγάς. - Hold the wheel tight when you&#x27;re driving.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) helmsman (member of a ship&#x27;s crew who is responsible for steering)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τιμόνι, from Venetan timon.</div><b>τιμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τιμάω">τιμάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τιμῶ, contracted form of τιμάω.</div><b>τινάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to shake, twitch, throw, jerk, jolt, blow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to check out, flake out, get lost, die</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάλη θα ήταν, αν τα τίναζες. - Do me a favor and get lost.</div><b>τιρζεπατίδη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural τιρζεπατίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) tirzepatide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English tirzepatide.</div><b>τιρκουάζ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) of turquoise color/colour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French turquoise (“turquoise -gemstone-”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) turquoise (gem stone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>see also περουζές m (perouzés)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) turquoise (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='ex'>see also γαλαζοπράσινο (galazoprásino)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French turquoise (“turquoise -gemstone-”).</div><b>τιρμπουσόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) corkscrew</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French tire-bouchon.</div><b>τις</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminine accusative plural of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>— <b>Τις</b> συνάντησα χθες. — Ποιες; — <b>Τις</b> δύο αδερφές σου. - — I met them yesterday. — Whom? — Your [the] two sisters.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τὲς of the 11th century, through morphological levelling of the accusative and nominative plurals.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron, feminine plural (weak personal pronoun)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, weak) them (3rd person feminine plural, accusative; used before the verb)</div>
  <div class='ex'>— <b>Τις</b> συνάντησα χθες. — Ποιες; — <b>Τις</b> δύο αδερφές σου. - — I met them yesterday. — Whom? — Your two sisters.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic, formal) who? (what? / which person or people?)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τις</b> πταίει; - who is at fault?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. see the genitive singular τίνος</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τίνος</b> είσαι συ; - Whose [child] are you?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. see the genitive plural τίνων</div>
  <div class='ex'>of ποιος (poios)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τὲς of the 11th century, through morphological levelling of the accusative and nominative plurals.</div><b>τις προάλλες</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the other day</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the Katharevousa dative plural form ταις προάλλαις, itself a calque of French ces derniers jours.</div><b>τιτάνας</b>
  <div><i>name, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) a person of extraordinary intellectual power</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>τιτάνες</b> της τέχνης και του πνεύματος - the titans of art and &#x27;spirit&#x27; (intellect/culture)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See Τιτάνας.</div><b>τιτάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) titanium</div><b>τιτάνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τιτάνια, neuter τιτάνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. titanic, giant like, large and strong</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Titanic, like the Titans</div><b>τιτανομαχία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Titanomachy, War of the Titans</div><b>τιτιβίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of birds) to chirp, to chirrup, to tweet, to twitter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of children) to chirp away (to speak incessantly at a high pitch)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic, inherited from Koine Greek τιττυβίζω.</div><b>τμήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τμήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. part, section</div>
  <div class='ex'>ρίζες ονομάζουμε το <b>τμήμα</b> του φυτού που βρίσκεται μέσα στο έδαφος. - roots are the part of the plant that is in the soil.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. department, division, section, branch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τμῆμα.</div><b>το</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”):; neuter nominative/accusative singular</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”):; masculine accusative singular</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τό. Cognate with Bulgarian -то (-to), Macedonian -то (-to), German das.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal, pronoun, weak) it (3rd person neuter singular, nominative)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal, pronoun, weak) it (3rd person neuter singular, accusative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τό. Cognate with Bulgarian -то (-to), Macedonian -то (-to), German das.</div><b>το αίμα νερό δεν γίνεται</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blood is thicker than water</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;Blood doesn&#x27;t/can&#x27;t become water&quot;.</div><b>το βάζω στα πόδια</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(idiomatic) to run away, to run off, to take to one&#x27;s heels, to hightail it (to flee)</div><b>το γινάτι βγάζει μάτι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cutting off one&#x27;s nose to spite one&#x27;s face, hoist by one&#x27;s own petard (of cases where someone stubborn is hurt more than the person with whom they&#x27;re being stubborn)</div><b>το δέον γενέσθαι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) that which must be done</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ας σκεφτούν οι πολιτικοί <b>το δέον γενέσθαι</b>. - The politicians should contemplate on what must be done.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned stereotype expression from Katharevousa. τὸ δέον γενέσθαι, literally, “that which needs to become”.
Singular neuter article τό, neuter present participle δέον (of δέω² “need”) &amp; middle aorist infinitive Ancient Greek γενέσθαι of γίγνομαι (“to become”).</div><b>το καλό πράγμα αργεί να γίνει</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>good things come to those who wait (a patient seeker will be satisfied in due time)</div><b>το μήλο κάτω απ&#x27; τη μηλιά θα πέσει</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apple does not fall far from the tree, apple does not fall far from the stem</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “the apple will fall under the apple tree”.</div><b>το μουνί σέρνει καράβι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) one hair of a woman can draw more than a hundred pair of oxen (women have great influence on their male partners/husbands and can have anything done for them)</div><b>το πολύ το κυρ&#x27; ελέησον το βαριέται κι ο Θεός</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>too much of a good thing (something enjoyable becomes bothersome in large quantities or over an extended period of time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “even God is tired of too much &#x27;Lord, have mercy&#x27;”.</div><b>το πολύ το κυρ&#x27; ελέησον το βαριέται κι ο παπάς</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>too much of a good thing (something enjoyable becomes bothersome in large quantities or over an extended period of time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “even the priest is tired of too much &#x27;Lord, have mercy&#x27;”.</div><b>το σκάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to flee, escape</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Tο ’σκασαν</b> από τη φυλακή. - They fled from prison.</div><b>το όνομά μου είναι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>my name is …</div><b>το όνομα μου είναι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative typography of <a href="bword://το">το</a> όνομά <a href="bword://μου">μου</a> <a href="bword://είναι">είναι</a></div><b>τοίχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τοίχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wall (side or division in a building)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τοῖχος</div><b>τοι</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, weak) they (3rd person masculine plural, nominative)</div><b>τοις εκατό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>percent, per cent</div>
  <div class='ex'>δύο τοις εκατό (two percent)</div><b>τοις εκατόν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of τοις <a href="bword://εκατό">εκατό</a></div><b>τοις μετρητοίς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in cash</div>
  <div class='ex'>εξόφληση τοις μετρητοίς - payment in cash</div><b>τοιχογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τοιχογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wall-painting, mural, fresco, graffito</div><b>τοκετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τοκετοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confinement, lying-in, giving birth</div><b>τοκιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τοκιστές, feminine τοκίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>moneylender</div><b>τοκογλυφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usurious</div><b>τοκογλύφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τοκογλύφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loan shark, usurer, usuress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τοκογλύφος.</div><b>τολμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to dare</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to dare</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive) to be daring, take risks</div>
  <div class='def'>4. and see: <a href="bword://τολμώ">τολμώ</a> (for learned expressions)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τολμ(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω, from the Ancient Greek τολμῶ, contracted form of τολμάω, from τόλμη</div><b>τολμηρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τολμηρή, neuter τολμηρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bold, courageous, daring, intrepid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. risque, racy</div><b>τολμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τολμάω">τολμάω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τολμώ</b> να πω - I dare say</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek τολμῶ, contracted form of τολμάω, from τόλμη.</div><b>τομ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of τόμος (volume)</div><b>τομάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τομάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hide, pelt (flayed skin of an animal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>H καλύβα ήταν στρωμένη με <b>τομάρια</b>. - The hut&#x27;s floor was laid out with pelts.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, sarcastic) hide, skin (one&#x27;s own life or personal safety, especially when in peril)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Mόνο για το <b>τομάρι</b> του νοιάζεται. - He only cares for his own hide.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory) wretch, rascal, scoundrel, ragamuffin (unhappy, unfortunate, or miserable person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Aυτό το <b>τομάρι</b> λογαριάζεις! - You&#x27;re still thinking about that wretch!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τομάριον.</div><b>τομέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τομείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. area, district (of town)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sector (of circle)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. field, area (of interest, knowledge)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. incisor (tooth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τομεύς + accusative case ending -έα, from τέμνω, with semantic loan from French secteur.</div><b>τομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. intersection (in geometry and set theory)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incision, section</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τομή.</div><b>τοματάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τοματάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ντοματάκι">ντοματάκι</a></div><b>τοματίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τοματίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ντοματίνι">ντοματίνι</a></div><b>τοματιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τοματιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ντοματιά">ντοματιά</a></div><b>τοματοκεφτές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τοματοκεφτέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural, often) tomatokeftedes, a Cycladic Greek dish of fried Santorini tomatoes that originated in Santorini.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τομάτα + κεφτές.</div><b>τον</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masculine accusative singular of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τό. Compare Polish ten, Czech ten or Slovak ten.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, weak) him (3rd person masculine singular, accusative)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τό. Compare Polish ten, Czech ten or Slovak ten.</div><b>τον γάιδαρο, όσο και να τον στολίσεις, άλογο δεν γίνεται</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>you can&#x27;t make a silk purse of a sow&#x27;s ear, you can&#x27;t polish a turd (it is not possible to produce something refined, admirable, or valuable from something which is unrefined, unpleasant, or of little or no value)</div><b>τον κακό σου τον καιρό</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) curse you, damn you, to hell with you (to show displeasure or disregard)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τον κακό σου τον καιρό</b>, που θα μου πεις τι να κάνω! - To hell with you, telling me what to do!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “your bad weather”.</div><b>τον κώλο βάζεις μάγειρα, σκατά θα μαγειρέψει</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, vulgar) if you pay peanuts, you get monkeys (if one gives a task to someone incompetent, they will receive results of the same standard)</div><b>τονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to accent, to accentuate (to mark with a written stress mark)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to stress, to accentuate (to pronounce with emphasis)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to stress, to accentuate, to emphasize, to highlight (to bring out distinctly; to make more noticeable or prominent)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to set to music</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek τονίζω with semantic loan from French accentuer. By surface analysis, τόνος + -ίζω.</div><b>τονισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τονισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accentuation, stress (when speaking)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η σημασία του τονισμού και του επιτονισμού - the importance of stress and intonation</div><b>τοξίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τοξίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toxin</div><b>τοξευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τοξευτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>archer</div><b>τοξικομανής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τοξικομανής, neuter τοξικομανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drug addicted, drug dependent</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τοξικομανείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drug addict</div><b>τοξικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τοξική, neuter τοξικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toxic</div><b>τοξικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toxicity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τοξικ(ός)) + -ότητα.</div><b>τοξοβολία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural τοξοβολίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) archery</div><b>τοξότης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τοξότες, feminine τοξότρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. archer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sagittarius</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τοξότης.</div><b>τοξότρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τοξότριες, masculine τοξότης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>archeress</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From masculine τοξό(της)) + feminine ending -τρια (-tria). For the ancient &quot;archeress&quot; see τοξότις.</div><b>τοπίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>landscape, scenery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek τοπίον, from Koine Greek τόπιον with shift of accent to -ίον, with semantic loan from French paysage or Semantic loan from Italian paesaggio.</div><b>τοπικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τοπικός">τοπικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. locally</div>
  <div class='def'>2. topically, locally (at a localized part of the body)</div><b>τοπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τοπική, neuter τοπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. local (from or in a nearby location)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. topical, local (applied to a localized part of the body)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τοπική</b> αναισθησία - local anaesthesia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τοπῐκός with semantic loan from French topique (in the medical sense) and local.</div><b>τοποθέτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τοποθετήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. positioning, placement, placing (the action by which something is placed)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. installation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. placement (the act of matching a person with a job)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. position (an opinion, stand or stance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τοποθετώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>τοποθεσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τοποθεσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. place, location</div>
  <div class='def'>2. site, situation</div><b>τοποθετημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τοποθετημένη, neuter τοποθετημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>placed, positioned</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τοποθετούμαι, passive voice of τοποθετώ (“I position, place”).</div><b>τοποθετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to place, position</div><b>τοπωνυμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τοπωνυμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tautonym (absolute synonym) of <a href="bword://τοπωνύμιο">τοπωνύμιο</a> n</div>
  <div class='def'>2. also; as synonym of <a href="bword://τοπωνυμιολογία">τοπωνυμιολογία</a>: the study of toponyms</div>
  <div class='def'>3. also; collection of toponyms of one area</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Already attested in 1874 in analogy to επωνυμία f. Compound of τοπ- (τόπος), “place”) + -ωνυμία.
According to Charalambakis, also the sense &quot;toponymy&quot;, as a learned borrowing from English toponymy or French toponymie.</div><b>τοπωνυμικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τοπωνυμική, neuter τοπωνυμικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toponymic, relating to toponyms</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Already attested in 1891 from τοπωνυμ(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>τοπωνυμιολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τοπωνυμιολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toponymy, toponymics</div><b>τοπωνύμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τοπωνύμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toponym, placename</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Katharevousa τοπωνύμι(ον) created in analogy to Koine Greek ἐπωνύμιον + -ο (Standard Modern Greek neuter suffix). Compound of τοπ- (τόπος), “place”) + -ωνύμιο.</div><b>τορναδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τορναδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately (through Venetan tornidor), from Latin tornator
: note: τορνάρω + -αδόρος.</div><b>τορπίλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τορπίλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>torpedo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French torpille. First attested 1879.</div><b>τορπιλάκατος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τορπιλάκατοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fast attack craft (FAC), a small and quick yet still heavily-armed warship.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τορπίλη + άκατος.</div><b>τος</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(personal, pronoun, weak) he (3rd person masculine singular, nominative)</div><b>τοσκανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τοσκανική, neuter τοσκανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Tuscan (relating to Tuscany or its people)</div><b>τοσοδούλης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τοσοδούλα, neuter τοσοδούλι or τοσοδούλικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tiny, small, tiddly</div><b>τοστ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) toasted sandwich: toasted or grilled cheese sandwich (please note that this word does not represent a piece of toasted bread)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English toast.</div><b>τοστιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τοστιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. toaster oven (a type of small countertop electric oven with a rack, used to make toast)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sandwich toaster, toastie maker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τοστ (tost, “toast”) + -ιέρα</div><b>του</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masculine/neuter genitive singular of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τοῦ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indirect object, personal) him, it</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Του</b> είπα την αλήθεια. - I told him the truth.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (possessive) his, its</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το όνομά <b>του</b> είναι Μιχάλης. - His name is Michael.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τοῦ.</div><b>του δίνω</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clear off, go away, push off</div><b>του κώλου</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://του">του</a> (tou), κώλου.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γιατί μου έστειλες μια φωτογραφία <b>του κώλου</b> σου; - Why did you send me a picture of your arse?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, indeclinable) worthless, awful, crappy, shitty</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πέταξε αυτά τα βιβλία <b>του κώλου</b> αυτού του άθλιου συγγραφέα! - Throw those crappy books by that wretched author out!</div><b>του χρόνου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>next year (the year after the present one)</div><b>τουαλέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τουαλέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. toilet, lavatory, WC (the fixture and the room)</div>
  <div class='ex'>See synonyms at αποχωρητήριο n (apochoritírio)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dressing table</div>
  <div class='def'>3. toilet, washing yourself</div>
  <div class='def'>4. very formal woman&#x27;s dress, usually long</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French toilette.</div><b>τουβλάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τουβλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) bricklayer, brickie</div><b>τουκάν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) toucan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French toucan, borrowed from Old Tupi tukana, from Proto-Tupi-Guarani *tũkãn, from Proto-Tupian *jɨokãn.</div><b>τουλάχιστον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>at least</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek τοὐλάχιστον, crasis of τὸ and ἐλάχιστον, superlative of ἐλαχύς.</div><b>τουλούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τουλούμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goatskin bag (especially: for bagpipes or for storing cheese)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish طولوم (tulum).</div><b>τουμπελέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τουμπελέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) toubeleki (a metal drum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish دمبلك.</div><b>τουμπερλέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τουμπερλέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τουμπελέκι">τουμπελέκι</a></div><b>τουρίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τουρίστες, feminine τουρίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tourist (usually male)</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι αφίξεις των <b>τουριστών</b> - tourist arrivals</div><b>τουρίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τουρίστριες, masculine τουρίστας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tourist (female)</div><b>τουρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tourism</div><b>τουριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τουριστική, neuter τουριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tourist, touristic</div><b>τουρκικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turkish (language)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τουρκικό</div><b>τουρκικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τουρκική, neuter τουρκικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Turkish (of Turkish ethnicity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Turkic</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>τουρκικές</b> γλώσσες - the Turkic languages</div><b>τουρκμενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τουρκμενική, neuter τουρκμενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turkmen</div><b>τουρκοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) Turkish occupation/rule (for example, of the Balkans or Cyprus)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative letter-case form of <a href="bword://Τουρκοκρατία">Τουρκοκρατία</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τουρκο- (tourko-, “Turkish”) + -κρατία. First attested 1834.</div><b>τουρκουάζ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τυρκουάζ">τυρκουάζ</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τυρκουάζ">τυρκουάζ</a></div><b>τουρσί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τουρσιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pickle</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω μια συνταγή για το λεμόνι <b>τουρσί</b>. - I have a recipe for lemon pickle.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish ترشی, from Classical Persian ترشی.</div><b>τουρτουά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) foot, leg</div><b>τουρτουαντρέ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) a walk, a stroll, a ride</div><b>τους</b>
  <div><i>article</i></div>
  <div class='def'>masculine accusative plural of <a href="bword://ο">ο</a> (o, “the”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τούς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (personal) their (3rd person masculine plural, genitive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (personal) their (3rd person feminine plural, genitive)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (personal) their (3rd person neuter plural, genitive)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (personal) them (3rd person masculine plural, accusative)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (possessive) their (belonging to them)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τούς.</div><b>τουτέστι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τουτέστιν">τουτέστιν</a></div><b>τουτέστιν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) i.e., namely</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η εκτίμηση είναι 15.000, <b>τουτέστιν</b> 286 κάτοικοι ανά τετραγωνικό χιλιόμετρο. - The estimate is 15,000, ie 286 inhabitants per square kilometer.</div><b>τουτού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(childish, indeclinable) car</div><b>τουφέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τουφέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rifle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τουφέκι, form of τουφέκιον, from Ottoman Turkish تفك, from Classical Persian تفنگ (tufang).</div><b>τούβλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τούβλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brick (hardened rectangular block of mud, clay etc., used for building)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Προσπαθούσε να φτιάξει την ταράτσα και του έπεσε το <b>τούβλο</b> στο κεφάλι. - He was trying to fix the roof and a brick fell on his head.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) blockhead, dolt, dunce, ignoramus (stupid person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άντε να βρεις άκρη μ’ αυτό το <b>τούβλο</b>! - Good luck figuring anything out with that blockhead!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τοῦβλον, from Koine Greek τούβουλον, from Latin tubulus (“small tube”).</div><b>τούμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τούμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small hill</div>
  <div class='def'>2. somersault</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) somersault; a bad fall</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γλίστρησα και πήρα μία <b>τούμπα</b>. - I slipped and had a big fall.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. tuba</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Reborrowing from Latin tumba (“small mound”), from Ancient Greek τύμβος</div><b>τούμπανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://τύμπανο">τύμπανο</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) swollen thing, distended thing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γίνανε τα δάχτυλά μου <b>τούμπανο</b>. - My fingers became swollen.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Doublet of τύμπανο.</div><b>τούνελ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tunnel</div><b>τούρκικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turkish (language)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://τούρκικος">τούρκικος</a></div><b>τούρκικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τούρκικη, neuter τούρκικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal or colloquial form of <a href="bword://τουρκικός">τουρκικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Τούρκ(ος) (“Turk”) + -ικος (“suffix for adjectives”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τούρκικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Turkish coffee, also known as Greek coffee, served in small demitasse</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πιάσε έναν <b>τούρκικο</b> πολλά βαρύ και όχι! - Fetch a Turkish [coffee] very strong and without [sugar]!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivised adjective τούρκικος by ellipsis of the noun καφές.</div><b>τούρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τούρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cake with layers of filling and icing, similar to birthday or wedding cakes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τούρτα (“kind of baked bread”), from Latin turta, perhaps ultimately from Latin tŏrta (“twisted”).
The association with a &quot;round cake&quot; is an influence by the Italian torta.</div><b>τούτος</b>
  <div><i>det, masculine (feminine τούτη, neuter τούτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) this, this one, that</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τούτος</b> εδώ είναι ο αδελφός μου. - This one here is my brother.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τοῦτος, from Ancient Greek οὗτος.</div><b>τράβηγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραβήγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pulling (the act by which something is pulled)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pull, tug (an act by which something is pulled)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. pull (an injury resulting from excessive pulling on a muscle)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. withdrawal (an act of withdrawing or a state of being withdrawn)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. drawing (the process of drawing or pulling something)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (in plural) difficulties, problems (awkward situation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the τραβηγ- stem of τραβώ + -μα (-ma).</div><b>τράγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τράγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. billy goat, male goat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tragus</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, derogatory) sleaze, sleazeball, sleazebag (a lecherous man)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, derogatory) priest)</div><b>τράπεζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τράπεζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bank</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ceremonial table</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τράπεζα.</div><b>τράπουλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τράπουλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pack/deck of playing cards</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια <b>τράπουλα</b> των 52 φύλλων περιέχει τέσσερα χρώματα: σπαθιά, κούπες, καρό και μπαστούνια. - A 52-card deck contains four suits: clubs, hearts, diamonds and spades.</div><b>τράτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τράτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trawl, fishing net used for trawling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trawler, commercial fishing boat fishing by trawling</div>
  <div class='def'>3. traditional dance of Megara and of the Greek islands</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan trata, from Italian tratto, from Latin tractus, perfect passive participle of trahō (“I drag; extract”).</div><b>τρέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. madness, insanity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) fun, high jinks</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from τρελ(αίνω)) + -α (-a).</div><b>τρέλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρέλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τρέλα">τρέλα</a></div><b>τρέμω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to tremble, shake, shiver</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to fear, shake from fear</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρέμω.
Compare Mariupol Greek тре́му.</div><b>τρέμω σαν το ψάρι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to shake like a leaf (literally: Ι shake like a fish)</div><b>τρένο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρένα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>train (engine and carriages as a whole)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian treno.</div><b>τρέξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρεξίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. running (the action of moving forward quickly upon two feet by alternately making a short jump off either foot)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. running (the action of moving forward quickly upon two feet by alternately making a short jump off either foot); running</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural, often) comings and goings, running around (bustling activity in order to deal with a matter)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. running (that which runs or flows; the quantity of a liquid which flows in a certain time or during a certain operation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τρέξιμον. By surface analysis, τρεξ-) + -ιμο (-imo).</div><b>τρέπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to turn</div><b>τρέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to divert, convert</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τρέπομαι</b> σε φυγή - I flee, run away</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to turn, veer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τρέπω.</div><b>τρέφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to feed, nourish, maintain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to heal, be raised</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τρέφω, probably from Proto-Hellenic *tʰrépʰō.</div><b>τρέχα γύρευε</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>go figure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “run and look”, or more loosely, “go and find out.”</div><b>τρέχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to run, move quickly, sprint</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έτρεξα</b> για να προλάβω το τρένο. - I ran to catch the train.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to flee, run away</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to flow, flow from, run</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τρέχει</b> η μύτη μου. - My nose is running.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to run, execute (a computer or computer program)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα μπορούμε να <b>τρέχουμε</b> προγράμματα Windows. - We shall be able to run Windows programs.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τρέχω, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰregʰ- (“to run”).</div><b>τρέχων</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τρέχουσα, neuter τρέχον)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>current</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>τρέχων</b> μήνας - the current month</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Participle of τρέχω. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek τρέχων &amp; semantic loan from French courant. Also substantivised by ellipsis of a noun.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρέχοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of the current month, instant, inst.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα ιδωθούμε στις 10 <b>τρέχοντος</b>. - We&#x27;ll see each other on the 10th of this month.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Participle of τρέχω. Learnedly, from Ancient Greek τρέχων &amp; semantic loan from French courant. Also substantivised by ellipsis of a noun.</div><b>τρία</b>
  <div><i>num, neuter (masculine and feminine τρεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>three</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρία, neuter form of τρεῖς, from Proto-Hellenic *tréyes, from Proto-Indo-European *tréyes.
Compare Mariupol Greek три́я.</div><b>τρίαινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρίαινες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trident (three tined harpoon)</div><b>τρίβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be grated</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το τυρί <b>τρίβεται</b> με τον τρίφτη. - The cheese is grated with a grater.</div><b>τρίβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to grate, grind</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τρίβω</b> το τυρί - grate the cheese</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to rub, chafe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρίβω.</div><b>τρίβω τα μάτια μου</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://τρίβω">τρίβω</a>, <a href="bword://μάτι">μάτι</a>.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (idiomatic) I am very surprised</div><b>τρίγλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρίγλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gurnard (Triglidae)</div><b>τρίγωνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρίγωνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. triangle (geometric shape)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. triangle (musical instrument)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρίγωνον.</div><b>τρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to squeak, creak</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to crack, collapse</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to squeeze, grind, gnash</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρίζω, from the same imitative Proto-Indo-European root as Latin strido (“I screech, grate”).</div><b>τρίκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) knitting, knitted fabric</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) knitting (the activity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French tricot (“knitting”).</div><b>τρίκυκλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρίκυκλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. three-wheeled vehicle; three-wheeler</div>
  <div class='ex'>see: τρίκυκλος (tríkyklos, adjective)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. three-wheeled vehicle; motorcycle combination, motorcycle and sidecar</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncommon) three-wheeled vehicle; tricycle (three-wheeled bicycle)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. anything with three wheels or three cycles</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French tricycle from Ancient Greek τρι- (tri-) &amp; κύκλος. By surface analysis, τρί- + κύκλ(ος)) + suffix -ο for neuters. Also see the adjective τρίκυκλος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of τρίκυκλος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of τρίκυκλος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div><b>τρίλιζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρίλιζες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tic-tac-toe (US), noughts and crosses (UK)</div><b>τρίμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρίμερη, neuter τρίμερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>triduan, three-day (lasting three days)</div><b>τρίμηνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρίμηνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trimester</div><b>τρίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) trio, group of three, triad, troika</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) trio</div>
  <div class='def'>3. alternative form of <a href="bword://τρία">τρία</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. (capitalized, indeclinable) the Trinity</div><b>τρίπα αλά βενετσιάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρίπες αλά βενετσιάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) A style of tripe with red sauce.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian trippa alla veneziana.</div><b>τρίπλευρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρίπλευρη, neuter τρίπλευρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>three-sided</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek τρίπλευρος. By surface analysis, τρί- + -πλευρος (-plevros).</div><b>τρίποντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρίποντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>three-pointer (a basket made from beyond the three-point line)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τρι- (tri-) + πόντ(ος)) + -ο).</div><b>τρίτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρίτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>one third, 1/3</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://τρίτος">τρίτος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://τρίτος">τρίτος</a></div><b>τρίτον</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thirdly</div><b>τρίτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρίτη, neuter τρίτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο <b>τρίτος</b> δρόμος - the third way</div>
  <div class='def'>2. third-party</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρίτος.</div><b>τρίτωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρίτωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>newt (Pleurodelinae)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Τρίτωνας. Likely a semantic loan from translingual Triton, an now-synonymous genus of newts (as Triturus).</div><b>τρίφτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρίφτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grater a kitchen utensil used for grating or shredding cheese, onions</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a special type of abrasive cleaning sponge or device used in bathing</div>
  <div class='def'>3. one who grinds and polishes marbles and mosaics</div>
  <div class='def'>4. grinding machine for grinding marbles and mosaics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to τρίβω.
Compare Mariupol Greek трифты́р.</div><b>τρίχες</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rubbish!, nonsense!</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τρίχα (hairs)</div><b>τρίχωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fur, coat (hairs covering an animal&#x27;s skin)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. body hair (of humans)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τρίχωμα, whence also English trichoma and trichome. By surface analysis, τρίχα + -ωμα (-oma).</div><b>τρίωρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρίωρη, neuter τρίωρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) three hours, lasting three hours</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek τρίωρος. Morphologically, τρι- (tri-, “tri-, τρία three”) + ώρα + -ος (-os)</div><b>τραίνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τρένο">τρένο</a></div><b>τραβάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to pull, pull in, drag, tow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to pull out, withdraw</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to pluck (stringed instrument)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to suffer, endure, be troubled with smth.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to last for a long time (of something unpleasant)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively) to call for, require</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an earlier τραβώ + -άω, from Byzantine Greek τραβῶ &lt; τραβίζω &lt; ταυρίζω”), from Ancient Greek ταῦρος + -ίζω. Cognate with Mariupol Greek траву́.</div><b>τραβεστί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable) transvestite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French travesti.</div><b>τραβηγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τραβηγμένη, neuter τραβηγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pulled</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω τα μαλλιά μου <b>τραβηγμένα</b> πίσω με μια κοτσίδα. - I have my hair pulled back with a pigtail.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τραβιέμαι, passive voice of τραβάω, τραβώ (“pull, drag”).</div><b>τραβώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τραβάω">τραβάω</a></div><b>τραγάνισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραγανίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crunch, scrunch</div><b>τραγανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to crunch, scrunch</div><b>τραγανιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τραγανιστή, neuter τραγανιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crunchy</div><b>τραγελαφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τραγελαφική, neuter τραγελαφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grotesque</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τραγέλαφ(ος)) + -ικός.</div><b>τραγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τραγική, neuter τραγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tragic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τραγικός.</div><b>τραγικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tragicness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τραγικ(ός)) + -ότητα.</div><b>τραγουδάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sing</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>τραγουδάω</b> ωραία, είμαι φάλτσος. - I don&#x27;t sing well, I am out of tune.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of birds) to chirp, tweet</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με ενοχλεί παρά πολύ όταν <b>τραγουδάνε</b> πρωί πρωί αυτά τα πουλιά! - It bothers me greatly when those birds sing very early in the morning.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of poets) to sing of, praise, have as main theme</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τραγουδ-ώ + -άω. From Byzantine Greek τραγουδῶ, from Ancient Greek τρᾰγῳδῶ. See τραγῳδίᾱ, English tragedy.</div><b>τραγουδίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τραγουδίστριες, masculine τραγουδιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>singer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τραγουδιστής + -τρια (-tria).</div><b>τραγουδημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τραγουδημένη, neuter τραγουδημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(literary) sung, a less frequent variant of of <a href="bword://τραγουδισμένος">τραγουδισμένος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τραγουδιέμαι, passive voice of τραγουδάω, τραγουδώ (“I sing”).</div><b>τραγουδιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be sung</div><b>τραγουδισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τραγουδισμένη, neuter τραγουδισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sung</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unexpected formation of participle, as though from an -ίζω verb. Perfect participle of τραγουδιέμαι, passive voice of τραγουδάω, τραγουδώ (“I sing”). The expected participle is the more rare τραγουδημένος.</div><b>τραγουδιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τραγουδιστές, feminine τραγουδίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>singer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τραγουδιστής, equivalent to τραγούδι + -ιστής.</div><b>τραγουδιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τραγουδιστή, neuter τραγουδιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dulcet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. melodious (voice)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sung</div><b>τραγουδώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τραγουδάω">τραγουδάω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν <b>τραγουδώ</b>, διότι δεν έχω καλή φωνή. - I don&#x27;t sing, because I don&#x27;t have a good voice.</div><b>τραγούδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραγούδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. song (music with words)</div>
  <div class='ex'>χαρούμενο <b>τραγούδι</b> - joyful song</div>
  <div class='def'>2. song (the subject and art of singing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τραγούδιν, from the verb τραγουδώ.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek траго́дъ.</div><b>τραγωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τραγωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tragedy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek τραγῳδία</div><b>τραγωδός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τραγωδοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tragic poet, tragedian</div><b>τραγόπαπας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τραγοπαπάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, humorous) immoral priest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τράγος + παπάς</div><b>τραγόπαππας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τραγοπαππάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τραγόπαπας">τραγόπαπας</a></div><b>τρακάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to collide, bump</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to run into</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to cause someone to have stage fright</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian attraccare, with deletion of initial vowel.</div><b>τρακαδόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρακαδόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scrounger, cadger</div><b>τρακαρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of τρακάρομαι</div><b>τρακαρισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τρακαρισμένη, neuter τρακαρισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. collided, bumped, crashed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αυτοκίνητο είναι <b>τρακαρισμένο</b>. - The car is crashed.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. with stage fright</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ρεσιτάλ της θα ήταν καλύτερο αν δεν ήταν τόσο <b>τρακαρισμένη</b>. - Her recital would have been better, if she were not so nervous (from stage fright).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τρακάρομαι, passive voice of τρακάρω (both senses).</div><b>τρακτέρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tractor (farm vehicle)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French tracteur.</div><b>τρακόσα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τριακόσια">τριακόσια</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>nominative/accusative neuter plural of τρακόσοι (trakósoi), colloquial form of τριακόσιοι (triakósioi)</div><b>τρακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τριακόσια">τριακόσια</a></div><b>τραμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tram, tramcar, streetcar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English tram. Possibly a doublet of τέρμα.</div><b>τραμουντάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tramontana, north wind</div><b>τραμπάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τραμπάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seesaw, teeter-totter</div><b>τραμπούκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τραμπούκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (derogatory) thug</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bully</div><b>τρανς</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable, transitive) trans, transgender, transsexual (having a gender which is different from the sex one was assigned at birth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English trans.</div><b>τρανσέξουαλ</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transgender, transsexual</div><b>τρανσυλβανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρανσυλβανική, neuter τρανσυλβανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Transylvanian (relating to Transylvania or its people)</div><b>τρανός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρανή, neuter τρανό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. physically strong, mighty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. great, grand, highly famous, important</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek τρανός, from Ancient Greek τρανής. See τείρω.</div><b>τραπέζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραπέζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. table (item of furniture with a flat top surface raised above the ground, usually on one or more legs)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγε στην ΙΚΕΑ να αγοράσει ένα νέο <b>τραπέζι</b>. - He went to IKEA to buy a new table.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) dinner, supper, meal (food prepared and eaten)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με κάλεσαν σε <b>τραπέζι</b> απόψε. - They invited me for a meal tonight.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τραπέζιν, via Koine Greek from Ancient Greek τραπέζιον, diminutive of τράπεζα. This is conventionally taken to be from a (virtual?) *τετράπεζα, from τετράς and πέζα, from πούς. Sihler (1995) instead relates the first element to τρεῖς, noting that early tables had three legs, not four. Compare English trapeze, trapezium, trapezophoron. Doublet of τραπέζιον, a borrowing.</div><b>τραπέζιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραπέζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trapezoid, trapezium</div><b>τραπέζιον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://τραπέζι">τραπέζι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τραπέζιον. Doublet of τραπέζι.</div><b>τραπεζίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τραπεζίτες, feminine τραπεζίτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. banker, bank owner or shareholder</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (obsolete) bank manager</div>
  <div class='def'>3. molar, grinder (tooth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τραπεζῑ́της. By surface analysis, τράπεζα + -ίτης.</div><b>τραπεζαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τραπεζαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dining room</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dining table</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τραπεζαρία, from τραπεζάρης, from τραπέζιν).</div><b>τραπεζικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τραπεζική, neuter τραπεζικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) bank</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τραπεζική</b> επιταγή - bank cheque</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τράπεζ(α)) + -ικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τραπεζικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bank clerk, teller</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τράπεζ(α)) + -ικός.</div><b>τραπεζογραμμάτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραπεζογραμμάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>banknote (paper money)</div><b>τραπεζομάντηλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραπεζομάντηλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tablecloth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τραπεζομάντηλο, equivalent to τραπέζι + -ο- (-o-) + μαντήλι.</div><b>τραπεζομάντιλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραπεζομάντιλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τραπεζομάντηλο">τραπεζομάντηλο</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τραπέζι + -ο- (-o-) + μαντήλι.</div><b>τραπεζώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to entertain at dinner.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο γάμο τους, <b>τραπέζωσαν</b> πολύ κόσμο. - At their wedding, they entertained many people at dinner.</div><b>τραπουλόχαρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραπουλόχαρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>playing card</div><b>τραυματίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τραυματίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>injured person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τραυματίας.</div><b>τραυματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to wound, damage, injure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τραυματίζω.</div><b>τραυματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τραυματισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>injury</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek τραυματισμός. By surface analysis, τραυματισ-) + -μός.</div><b>τραχεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τραχείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trachea (thin-walled, cartilaginous tube connecting the larynx to the bronchi)</div><b>τραχύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τραχιά or τραχεία, neuter τραχύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abrasive, of coarse manner, gruff, grating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρᾱχύς.</div><b>τραύμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τραύματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wound, injury</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) trauma, blow, shock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from Ancient Greek τραῦμα. For sense in psychiatry, semantic loan from English trauma, from the Ancient Greek τραῦμα.</div><b>τρεις</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine or feminine (neuter τρία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>three, 3</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρεῖς, from Proto-Hellenic *tréyes, from Proto-Indo-European *tréyes.</div><b>τρεισήμισι</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine or feminine (neuter τριάμισι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. three and a half</div>
  <div class='def'>2. half past three</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τρεις (treis) + μισό.</div><b>τρελά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τρελός">τρελός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crazily, madly</div><b>τρελαίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to go mad, go insane, go crazy</div><b>τρελαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to madden, drive insane, drive crazy (cause someone to go insane)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι εμπειρίες του στον πόλεμο τον <b>τρέλαναν</b>. - His experiences of war drove him insane.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive) to madden, drive insane, create a stir (annoy, bother or stir someone greatly)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας έχει <b>τρελάνει</b> αυτό το σκυλί! - This dog has driven us mad!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Verb derived from the adjective τρελός.</div><b>τρελοκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρελοκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, familiar) madhouse, nuthouse, bedlam, lunatic asylum (mental hospital)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με αυτά που λέει, θα τον βάλουν στο <b>τρελοκομείο</b>. - With what he&#x27;s saying, they&#x27;ll put him in the madhouse.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, humorous) madhouse, nuthouse, bedlam (any place where there is no logic, order or sense)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σπίτι τους είναι σκέτο <b>τρελοκομείο</b> μ’ αυτό το ζωηρό παιδί που έχουν. - Their house is pure bedlam with that lively child of theirs.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively, humorous) nutter, lunatic, madman (person who behaves in an insane manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο δάσκαλος μας ήταν σκέτο <b>τρελοκομείο</b>. - Our teacher was a complete lunatic.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρελός + -κομείο. First attested 1840.</div><b>τρελός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρελή, neuter τρελό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crazy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τρελός, from Koine Greek τρηρός, from Ancient Greek τρήρων. The chess sense is a semantic loan from French fou.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρελοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. madman</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) bishop</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τρελός, from Koine Greek τρηρός, from Ancient Greek τρήρων. The chess sense is a semantic loan from French fou.</div><b>τρεχαντήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρεχαντήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trehantiri (small, double-ended boat, chiefly used for fishing)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) caique (a small sea-going vessel used for trading or fishing, originally made of wood and powered by sail)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek τροχαντήρ, from Ancient Greek τροχός.</div><b>τρεχούμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τρεχούμενη, neuter τρεχούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>current, running</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο τραπεζικός λογαριασμός δεν είναι απαραίτητο να είναι <b>τρεχούμενος</b> λογαριασμός. - The bank account does not need to be a current account.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive present participle of τρέχω (“I run”), a verb without passive voice forms + -ούμενος.</div><b>τριάκοντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://τριάντα">τριάντα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τριάκοντα.</div><b>τριάντα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thirty</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek τριάντα, from Ancient Greek τριάκοντα, from Proto-Indo-European *tridḱómt (“thirty”).</div><b>τριάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τριάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. number three</div>
  <div class='def'>2. three-roomed apartment</div>
  <div class='def'>3. three, trey</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρί(α)) + -άρι.</div><b>τριήμερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριήμερη, neuter τριήμερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>triduan, three-day (lasting three days)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρι- (tri-) + ημέρα + -ος (-os)</div><b>τριακοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριακοστή, neuter τριακοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thirtieth</div><b>τριακόσα</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τριακόσια">τριακόσια</a></div><b>τριακόσια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(noun, numeral) three hundred</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative plural neuter of τριακόσιοι</div><b>τριαντάφυλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τριαντάφυλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rose</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spoon sweet made from rose petals</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τριαντάφυλλον, from τριάντα + φύλλον.</div><b>τριανταφυλλή</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative masculine singular of <a href="bword://τριανταφυλλής">τριανταφυλλής</a></div><b>τριανταφυλλής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριανταφυλλιά, neuter τριανταφυλλί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rose (color/colour)</div><b>τριανταφυλλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) rose, pink (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://τριανταφυλλής">τριανταφυλλής</a></div><b>τριανταφυλλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τριανταφυλλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rosebush</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://τριανταφυλλής">τριανταφυλλής</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τριανταφυλλής">τριανταφυλλής</a></div><b>τριβή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τριβές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. friction (mechanical resistance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wear (resulting from friction)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. friction (conflict)</div><b>τριβολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tribology</div><b>τριβόμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριβόμενη, neuter τριβόμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fricative</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τριβόμενα</b> σύμφωνα - fricative consonants</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of τρίβω.</div><b>τριγλυκερίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τριγλυκερίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>triglyceride</div><b>τριγυρνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to wander, roam, walk around</div><b>τριγωνέλλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τριγωνέλλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fenugreek (spice)</div><b>τριγωνομετρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τριγωνομετρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trigonometry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τρίγωνον + -μετρία, from μέτρον + -ια (-ia, “-y”).</div><b>τριγωνομετρικές συναρτήσεις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τριγωνομετρική <a href="bword://συνάρτηση">συνάρτηση</a></div><b>τριγωνομετρική συνάρτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τριγωνομετρικές συναρτήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trigonometric function</div><b>τριγύρω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>around, all around</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τριγύρω. By surface analysis, τρι- (tri-) + γύρω.</div><b>τριζόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τριζόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cricket (insect)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σπίτι γέμισε τριζόνια
χτυπούν σαν άρρυθμα ρολόγια
λαχανιασμένα. … - The house was filled with crickets
ticking like rhythmless clocks
panting. …</div><b>τρικάταρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρικάταρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a three-masted ship or boat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρι- (tri-, “three”) + κατάρτι</div><b>τρικάταρτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>three-masted, triple-masted</div><b>τρικράνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρικράνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trident (three tined harpoon)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. three tined fork</div><b>τριλωπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>triplopia</div><b>τριμμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τριμμένη, neuter τριμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grated, shredded</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τριμμένο</b> τυρί - grated cheese</div>
  <div class='def'>2. worn out</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τριμμένο</b> ρούχο - worn out garment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τρίβομαι, passive voice of τρίβω (“I grate”).</div><b>τριπλάσιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριπλάσια, neuter τριπλάσιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The proportional number threefold, treble, thrice, three times the size</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρία + -πλάσιος</div><b>τριπλασιασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tripling</div><b>τριπλοκατοικία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τριπλοκατοικίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>triplex</div><b>τριπλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριπλή, neuter τριπλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>triple, threefold</div><b>τρισαλί</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thrice woe</div>
  <div class='ex'>αλί αλί και <b>τρισαλί</b>! - woe, woe and thrice woe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρισ- (tris-, “three times”) + αλί</div><b>τρισδιάστατος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρισδιάστατη, neuter τρισδιάστατο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>three-dimensional, existing in three dimensions</div><b>τρισεκατομμύριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρισεκατομμύρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trillion, million million (10¹²)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (British, obsolete) trillion, million million million (10¹⁸)</div><b>τρισύλλαβος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρισύλλαβη, neuter τρισύλλαβο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trisyllabic (having three syllables)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek τρισύλλαβος. By surface analysis, τρι- (tri-) + συλλαβ(ή)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>τριταγωνιστές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τριταγωνιστής</div><b>τριταγωνιστή</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive/accusative/vocative singular of τριταγωνιστής</div><b>τριταγωνιστών</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of τριταγωνιστής</div><b>τριτοπρόσωπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριτοπρόσωπη, neuter τριτοπρόσωπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(third person) third-person</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τριτοπρόσωπο</b> ρήμα - third-person verb</div><b>τριφύλλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τριφύλλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>clover, trefoil, shamrock (plant in genus Trifolium)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τ’ αλφαβητάρι πάνω στο <b>τριφύλλι</b>,
Σου μάθαινε το αύριο και το χθες. - The schoolbook on top of the clover,
Taught you about tomorrow and yesterday.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρίφυλλον, equivalent to τρία + φύλλο.</div><b>τριχίουρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τριχίουροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Trichiurus (genus of cutlassfish)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. whipworm (Trichuris trichiura)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from New Latin trichiurus, from Ancient Greek θρίξ + οὐρά.</div><b>τριχιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τριχιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>round sardinella (Sardinella aurita)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, τρίχα + -ιος (-ios, nominative-adjectival suffix).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριχια, neuter τριχιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(nonstandard) hairy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, τρίχα + -ιος (-ios, nominative-adjectival suffix).</div><b>τριχωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριχωτή, neuter τριχωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hairy</div><b>τριχόπτερα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Trichoptera.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Trichoptera.</div><b>τριχόπτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hair loss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τρίχ(α)) + -ό- + πτώση.</div><b>τριώροφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τριώροφη, neuter τριώροφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. three-storey</div>
  <div class='def'>2. triple-decker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τριώροφος. By surface analysis, τρι- (tri-) + -ώροφος.</div><b>τρολ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) troll (Nordic mythical, storybook character)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) troll (internet abuser)</div><b>τρομάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to frighten, scare, terrify, spook (cause to feel fear)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αδελφός μου με <b>τρόμαξε</b>· εμφανίστηκε ξαφνικά κάτω από το κρεββάτι μου. - My brother scared me; he popped out from under my bed suddenly.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be frightened, be scared, be terrified, be spooked</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τρόμαξα</b> όταν είδα ελάφι μπροστά μου στο δρόμο. - I was frightened when I saw a deer in front of me on the road.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (followed by να (na) ) have a job (have great difficulty in order to)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τρομάξαμε</b> να σε βρούμε! - We had a job finding you!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τρομάζω, from Ancient Greek τρομάσσω, from τρομέω.</div><b>τρομακτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρομακτική, neuter τρομακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. terrifying, fearsome, frightening, scary (causing fear or anxiety)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. terrifying, terrible (of a formidable nature; intense; extreme in degree or extent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τρομάζω + -τικός.</div><b>τρομερά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τρομερός">τρομερός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (emphatic, intensifier) awfully, dreadfully, terribly (extremely)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (emphatic, intensifier) tremendously (greatly; enormously)</div><b>τρομερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. terrific; astounding</div>
  <div class='def'>2. terrific; terrifying</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρομερός, from τρόμος + -ερός.</div><b>τρομοκράτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρομοκράτες, feminine τρομοκράτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terrorist (person, group, or organization that uses violent action, or the threat of violent action, to further political goals)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεκαεννιά <b>τρομοκράτες</b> διέπραξαν τις επιθέσεις της 11ης Σεπτεμβρίου 2001. - Nineteen terrorists committed the September 11th 2001 attacks.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρόμος + -κράτης, calque of French terroriste. First attested 1850.</div><b>τρομοκράτισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρομοκράτισσες, masculine τρομοκράτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terrorist</div><b>τρομοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) terrorism, terror (commission of an act of violence to create a political or emotional response)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>τρομοκρατία</b> είναι ο λόγος που καταστράφηκαν οι δίδυμοι πύργοι. - Terrorism is the reason that the twin towers were destroyed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρόμος + -κρατία, calque of French terrorisme. First attested 1840.</div><b>τρομοκρατημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τρομοκρατημένη, neuter τρομοκρατημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. terrorised (UK), terrorized (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. terrified, intimidated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τρομοκρατούμαι, passive voice of τρομοκρατώ (“terrorise”).</div><b>τρομοκρατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρομοκρατική, neuter τρομοκρατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terrorism, terrorist</div><b>τρομοκρατούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be terrified, passive of <a href="bword://τρομοκρατώ">τρομοκρατώ</a></div><b>τρομοκρατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to terrorise (UK), terrorize (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to intimidate, terrify</div><b>τρομπέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρομπέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trumpet</div><b>τρομπόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρομπόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. trombone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blunderbuss</div><b>τροπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) mod (user-made modification to software or a game)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κατέβασα ένα νέο <b>τροπ</b> για να αλλάξω τα γραφικά του παιχνιδιού. - I downloaded a new mod to change the game&#x27;s graphics.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τροπ &lt; clipping of τροποποίηση, modelled after English mod (short for modification).</div><b>τροπ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://τροποποίηση">τροποποίηση</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> abbreviation of τροποποίηση.</div><b>τροπάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τροπάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>troparion</div><b>τροπικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tropic</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. of or pertaining to manners</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tropical</div><b>τροπικός κυκλώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τροπικοί κυκλώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tropical cyclone</div><b>τροπισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τροπισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. taxis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tropism</div>
  <div class='def'>3. positioning of a ship for maintenance, careening</div><b>τροπολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τροπολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amendment (to text, lae, etc)</div><b>τροποποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τροποποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. change, modification</div>
  <div class='def'>2. amendment</div><b>τροποποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to modify, alter, change, amend</div><b>τροτέζα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, informal) A street prostitute.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French trotteuse. Compare with English streetwalker, Serbo-Croatian uličarka.</div><b>τροτσκισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Trotskyism (political doctrine/philosophy named after Leon Trosky)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French trotskisme.</div><b>τροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τροφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>food, nourishment</div>
  <div class='ex'>and less formal φαΐ n (faḯ)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τροφή.</div><b>τροφοδοσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) supply, provisioning</div><b>τροφοδοτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τροφοδοτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. power supply (source of mains/line power electricity)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. power supply (for instruments requiring a supply voltage different to that of the electricity supply)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://τροφοδοτικός">τροφοδοτικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://τροφοδοτικός">τροφοδοτικός</a></div><b>τροφοδοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τροφοδοτική, neuter τροφοδοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relating to internal or external supply of electrical power</div><b>τροφοδοτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to supply, provision</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to provide (necessities)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τροφο(δότης)) + -δοτώ.</div><b>τροχαίος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τροχαίοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trochee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τροχαῖος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τροχαία, neuter τροχαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wheeled, on wheels</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τροχαίο</b> υλικό - rolling stock</div>
  <div class='def'>2. road traffic, traffic (attributive)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τροχαίο</b> ατύχημα - (road) traffic accident</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (noun from verb) traffic officer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from τροχαία, from τροχ(ός)) + -αία. Compare Koine Greek τροχαῖος.</div><b>τροχαλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τροχαλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pulley</div><b>τροχιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τροχιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. orbit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. course</div>
  <div class='def'>3. trajectory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from the Hellenistic Koine Greek τροχιά, from the ancient τροχ(ός), “wheel”) + -ιά.
For sense orbit, semantic loan from French orbite.</div><b>τροχιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τροχιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sharpener, grinder (occupation)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τροχ- (troch-, “to whet, to sharpen”) + -ιστής</div><b>τροχονόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τροχονόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traffic police officer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τροχ(ός), “wheel”) as in τροχαία + -ο- (infix) + -νόμος in analogy to the word αστυνόμος.</div><b>τροχός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τροχοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wheel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. potter&#x27;s wheel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τροχός (in some senses, such as potter&#x27;s wheel; in the general sense, it may have been a later learned borrowing).</div><b>τροχόσπιτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τροχόσπιτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caravan, motor home, camper (furnished vehicle (towed or self-powered) used as a dwelling)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φέτος θα πάμε διακοπές με το <b>τροχόσπιτο</b> στην εξοχή. - This year, we&#x27;ll go on holidays to the countryside with the caravan.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τροχός + -ό- + σπίτι.</div><b>τρούλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρούλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dome</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τροῦλλος, a borrowing from Latin trulla. Cognate with Greek τούρλα.</div><b>τρυγόνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρυγόνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female turtle dove, Streptopelia turtur</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a Pontian dance</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, informal) graceful woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> With feminine -α (-a) suffix at neuter τρυγόνι. As in ancient accusative τρῡγόνᾰ of the feminine τρῡγών.</div><b>τρυγόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρυγόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turtle dove, Streptopelia turtur</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek τρυγόνιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek τρυγών.</div><b>τρυγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to harvest (especially grapes, but also honey, fruit, etc)</div><b>τρυπάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρυπάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. drill (tool for making holes)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trepanner (tool for trepanning)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρῡπάνιον, diminutive of τρῡ́πανον, from τρῡπάω.</div><b>τρυπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pierce, puncture, drill, prick (make a hole in)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the modern τρυπώ, τρυπ- + -άω, from Ancient Greek τρῡπῶ, contracted form of τρῡπάω.</div><b>τρυπημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τρυπημένη, neuter τρυπημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pierced, with holes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τρυπιέμαι, passive voice of τρυπάω, τρυπώ (“make hole”).</div><b>τρυποκάρυδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρυποκάρυδοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>woodpecker</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γούντι ο <b>Τρυποκάρυδος</b> - Woody Woodpecker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τρυπ(ώ), “to make a hole in”) + καρυδ(ια) (karyd(ia), “walnut tree”)</div><b>τρυποφοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) trypophobia</div><b>τρυπτοφάνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural τρυπτοφάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) tryptophan (amino acid)</div><b>τρυπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τρυπάω">τρυπάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τρυπῶ, contracted form of τρυπάω (ancient). Also see the variant τρυπάω (modern).</div><b>τρωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τρωική, neuter τρωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Trojan (of, or relating to, the famed city of Troy or its inhabitants)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τρωικός</b> Πόλεμος - Trojan War</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek Τρωϊκός.</div><b>τρωκτικά</b>
  <div><i>name, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Rodentia</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://τρωκτικό">τρωκτικό</a></div><b>τρωκτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τρωκτικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rodent (mammal of the order Rodentia)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα ποντίκια κι οι σκίουροι είναι <b>τρωκτικά</b>. - Mice and squirrels are rodents.</div><b>τρόικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρόικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. troika (Russian sleigh)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. troika (group of three, usually referring to people in power)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from Russian тро́йка, from три (tri, “three”)</div><b>τρόλεϊ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) trolley bus, trolleybus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English trolley.</div><b>τρόλεϋ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τρόλεϊ">τρόλεϊ</a></div><b>τρόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) terror, extreme fear</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b><b>τρόμ</b>ος</b> δεν επιτρέπεται να καταπολεμάται με <b>τρόμο</b>. - Terror must not be fought with terror.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) trembling, tremor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρόμος, from Proto-Indo-European *trem- (“to tremble”).
Compare Mariupol Greek тре́мус.</div><b>τρόμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρόμπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pump</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian tromba.</div><b>τρόπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρόποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. method, manner, behaviour, conduct</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) manners</div>
  <div class='ex'>καλοί <b>τρόποι</b> - good manners</div>
  <div class='def'>3. knack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρόπος.</div><b>τρόπος άρθρωσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τρόποι άρθρωσης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>manner of articulation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English manner of articulation or French mode d&#x27;articulation.</div><b>τρόφιμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τρόφιμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inmate (of prison or other institution)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boarder, paying guest (of boarding house etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. boarder (in school)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. regular (ironic)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τρόφῐμος. By surface analysis, τρέφω + -ιμος (-imos).</div><b>τρύπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τρύπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hole</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek τρῦπα.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular present imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://τρυπάω">τρυπάω</a> and <a href="bword://τρυπώ">τρυπώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative form: τρύπαγε (trýpage)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular simple past perfective imperative of <a href="bword://τρυπάω">τρυπάω</a> and <a href="bword://τρυπώ">τρυπώ</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative form: τρύπησε (trýpise)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Verb form.</div><b>τρώγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τρώω">τρώω</a></div><b>τρώω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to eat; to chew and swallow food</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλω να <b>τρώω</b> υγιεινά, αλλά χθες <b>έφαγα</b> τρία χάμπουργκερ. - I want to eat healthily, but yesterday I ate three hamburgers.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to eat; to eat, breakfast, dine (have a meal)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to eat; to eat nonfasting foods</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to corrode, abrade, eat into, rust away</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to bite, sting (by insect,etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively) to take bribes</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (active) (<a href="bword://τις">τις</a> ~) to be beaten, beaten up, to take a beating, to be spanked, smashed, physically attacked, stomped, to lose a game or match, (idiomatic): have one&#x27;s ass kicked, take an ass kicking, to be finished, to take licks, take a licking</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο τύπος <b>τις έφαγε</b> πριν μαχαιρωθεί. - The guy got the shit kicked out of him before he was stabbed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τρώγω. Forms with stem -φαγ- derived from the stem of Ancient Greek ἔφαγον.</div><b>τσάγαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσάγαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>unripe almond in its outer envelope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چاغلا, from Classical Persian چغاله.</div><b>τσάι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσάγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tea (drink and leaves)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چای, either from Russian чай, or from Classical Persian چای from Sinitic, Chinese 茶. Doublet of τέιο.</div><b>τσάμπα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τζάμπα">τζάμπα</a></div><b>τσάμπουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσάμπουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grape stem</div><b>τσάντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσάντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bag, purse, handbag</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چانطه, from Classical Persian تنچه, تمچه, from تنجیدن.</div><b>τσάπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσάπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hoe, mattock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian zappa.</div><b>τσάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσάροι, feminine τσαρίνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tsar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Russian царь (carʹ, “tsar”).</div><b>τσέλιγκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσελιγκάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. owner of many sheep or goats</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chief shepherd</div><b>τσέλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσέλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cello</div><b>τσέμπαλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσέμπαλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harpsichord</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian cembalo.</div><b>τσέπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσέπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pocket (in clothing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish جیب (ceb, cep), from Arabic جَيْب (jayb).</div><b>τσέχικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσεχικά">τσεχικά</a></div><b>τσέχικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσέχικη, neuter τσέχικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσεχικός">τσεχικός</a></div><b>τσίγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσίγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. zinc</div>
  <div class='def'>2. zinc in the form of thin sheets (synecdoche)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. zinc-plated metal sheet</div>
  <div class='def'>4. zinc plate used in zincography/offset printing (dated)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. zinc oxide used in the preparation of house painter&#x27;s putty)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian zinco (“zinc”), from German Zink (“zinc”).</div><b>τσίκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσίκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσίχλα">τσίχλα</a></div><b>τσίκνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσίκνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the smell of roasting or burning meat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κνῖσα; see κνῑ́ση for more.</div><b>τσίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) chilli (UK), chili (US)</div><b>τσίμπημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιμπήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bite, sting, prick</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τσίμπημα</b> κουνουπιού - mosquito bite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσιμπάω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>τσίου-τσίου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(onomatopoeic) cheep (short, high-pitched sound made by a small bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) cheep (short, high-pitched sound made by a small bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>τσίπουρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσίπουρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tsipouro (an alcoholic spirit made from the distillation of grape pomace, sometimes flavoured with aniseed or other herbs)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Uncertain. Has been claimed to have been borrowed from Ottoman Turkish جبره (cibre, “grape pomace”), although according to different sources the borrowing occurred the other way round.
Alternatively, a relation to Ancient Greek σίκερα n, which is from Biblical Hebrew שֵׁכָר m, has also been suggested.
Unrelated to the superficially similar τσιπούρα f.</div><b>τσίρκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσίρκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>circus (travelling entertainment)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian circo (“circus”).</div><b>τσίρλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσίρλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) diarrhea (watery or very soft excrement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάλι άφησε το σκυλί την <b>τσίρλα</b> του χάμω. - The dog left its diarrhea on the floor again.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τσιρλώ, from Ancient Greek τῖλος, from Proto-Indo-European *teh₂w- (“to melt”).</div><b>τσίσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσίσια">τσίσια</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσίσια, borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چیش. With elision of &lt;-ι-&gt; as in the sequence [s - ç - vowel].</div><b>τσίσια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(childish, colloquial) wee, pee (urine)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μωρό κάνει <b>τσίσια</b> στο γιογιό. - The baby is peeing in the potty.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish چیش.</div><b>τσίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (rare, vernacular) a wooden flask, especially for wine</div>
  <div class='ex'>σήκωσε την τσίτσα και τράβηξε δυο ρουφηξιές - he lifted the flask and took two sips</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare, vernacular) a kind of basket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably from Slavic, from Proto-Slavic *cica, cognate with Bulgarian цица (cica, “breast”).</div><b>τσίτσιδος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσίτσιδη, neuter τσίτσιδο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous) stark naked, starkers, completely nude, naked as the day one was born</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σοκαριστήκαμε όταν είδαμε να περνάει ένας <b>τσίτσιδος</b> άντρας. - We were shocked to see a stark naked man go by.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσιτσί.</div><b>τσίχλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσίχλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thrush; especially Turdus philomelos: song thrush</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) thin, scrawny man</div>
  <div class='def'>3. chewing gum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κίχλη.</div><b>τσαγιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσαγιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. teapot</div>
  <div class='def'>2. kettle</div><b>τσαγκάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσαγκάρηδες, feminine τσαγκάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maker or repairer of shoes or sandals</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τσαγκάρης, from Mediaeval and Late Koine Greek τσαγκάριος &amp; τζαγκάριος, τζαγγάριος with simplification of ⟨γγ &gt; γκ⟩, from Koine Greek τζάγγη + -άριος. The Hellenistic term, chiefly in the plural, found as a transcription of Late Latin zancha / tzanga, from Persian ظانگا as in Parthian. Cognates include the Byzantine Greek τζαγγίον n and possibly the modern τσαγανό n. Compare Italian zingaro (“Gypsy”).</div><b>τσακάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσακάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jackal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چقال, from Classical Persian شغال, from Sanskrit शृगाल.</div><b>τσακίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (familiar, transitive) to break, to smash (to separate into pieces violently)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (familiar, transitive) to crush, to smash (to defeat overwhelmingly; to overcome completely)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (familiar, figuratively, transitive) to break, to crush (to cause to lose spirit or will; to oppress or grievously burden)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (familiar, figuratively) to start to age (to show marks of age)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (familiar) to bend over backwards (to make a great effort)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (of paper) to fold (to become folded)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (familiar, imperative, slang, vulgar) get out of here, go away, leave, beat it, get lost, disappear, fuck off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τσακίζω, either from onomatopoeic τσακ (tsak, “crack, snap”) or from τσακί from Turkish çakı, with -ίζω ending.</div><b>τσακίρ κέφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar, indeclinable) at the peak of <a href="bword://κέφι">κέφι</a>, high spirits, also implying slight drunkenness</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο καθηγητής ήταν πολύ σοβαρός, αλλά όταν ήρθε στο <b>τσακίρ κέφι</b>, πέταξε τη γραβάτα και σηκώθηκε να χορέψει. - The professor was looking very serious, but when he came to full kefi, he tossed aside his necktie, stood up and danced.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چاقر كیف, from Ottoman Turkish چاقر and Arabic كَيْف (kayf, “state; pleasure, enjoyment”).</div><b>τσακίρικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσακίρικη, neuter τσακίρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of eye colour) grey-green, blue-grey</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσακίρ(ης)) + -ικος (-ikos), from Ottoman Turkish چاقر.</div><b>τσακμάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσακμάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cigarette lighter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bright spark</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چاقماق.</div><b>τσακωμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσακωμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>argument, quarrel, row</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσακώ(νομαι)) + -μός.</div><b>τσακωνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Tsakonian (learned and less frequent than <a href="bword://τσακώνικος">τσακώνικος</a>)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Τσάκων(ας) (“demonym Tsakonian”) + -ικός (“suffix for adjectives”). Of uncertain etymon. See etymology of Τσάκωνας.</div><b>τσακώνικα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τσακώνικος">τσακώνικος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the Tsakonian language</div><b>τσακώνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Tsakonian</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τσακώνικος</b> χορός - Tsakonian dance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Τσάκων(ας) (“demonym Tsakonian”) + -ικος (“suffix for adjectives”). Of uncertain etymon. See etymology of Τσάκωνας.</div><b>τσακώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with different sense) to argue, to fight, to quarrel</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τσακώθηκαν</b> και από τότε δεν βλέπονται. - They had a fight and since then, they don&#x27;t see one another.</div><b>τσακώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, transitive) to catch, capture (seize by force)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αστυνομία <b>τσάκωσε</b> τον διαρρήκτη. - The police caught the intruder.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, transitive) to grab, swipe (seize by force physically)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τσάκωσα</b> το μπουκάλι από το τραπέζι. - I grabbed the bottle from the table.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τσακώνω, probably from τσακίον.</div><b>τσαλαπετεινός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσαλαπετεινοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hoopoe, Upupa epops</div><b>τσαμπί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσαμπιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bunch (of fruit)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τσαμπί</b> σταφύλι - bunch of grapes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan zambin (“animal&#x27;s leg”), from Italian zampa (“animal&#x27;s leg, paw”).
Compare Mariupol Greek цамби́.</div><b>τσαμπουκάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσαμπουκάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, slang) bullying behaviour, tough guy behaviour, toughness (typically linked to mangas-type people)</div>
  <div class='ex'>πουλάω <b>τσαμπουκά</b> σε κάποιον - to act the bully with someone, to play the tough guy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial, slang) bully, tough guy (person who adopts the above behaviour, usually male)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παίζει τον <b>τσαμπουκά</b> αλλά όλοι ξέρουν ότι κατά βάθος είναι δειλός. - He acts like a tough guy but we all know that deep down he&#x27;s a coward.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively, slang) boldness, guts, daring, nerve, balls (the courageousness to do something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θέλεις <b>τσαμπουκά</b> για να πετύχεις σ’ αυτή τη δουλειά. - You need guts to succeed in that job.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish سابقه, from Arabic سَابِقَة, feminine singular of سَابِق.</div><b>τσαμπουνάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) to prattle, prate, be a windbag</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literally, rare) to play bagpipe</div><b>τσαμπουνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσαμπουνώ">τσαμπουνώ</a></div><b>τσαμπουνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσαμπουνάω">τσαμπουνάω</a></div><b>τσαμπούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tsampouna (bagpipes of the Greek islands)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> An αντιδάνειο. -From medieval *τσαμπούνα for which see verb τσαμπουνίζω- borrowed from Italian zampogna (“bagpipes”), from Latin symphonia (“music, symphony”), from Ancient Greek συμφωνία, already denoting &quot;musical instrument&quot; in Koine. Doublet of συμφωνία. Also see the medieval form τσαμπούρνα.</div><b>τσανάκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bowl, earthenware pot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish چناق.</div><b>τσαντάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσαντάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a small bag)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσάντ(α), “bag”) + -άκι.</div><b>τσαντίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσατίζω">τσατίζω</a>: to vex, annoy, irritate, drive around the bend, peeve, rub the wrong way, nettle, get to, tick off, piss off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσατίζω, with voicing τ (t) ([t]) &gt; ντ (nt) ([d]).</div><b>τσαπί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσαπιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσάπα">τσάπα</a></div><b>τσαρλατάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσαρλατάνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>charlatan, imposter, con man, mountebank</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian ciarlatano (“charlatan”).</div><b>τσαρτσάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσαρτσάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mistle thrush, Turdus viscivorus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan zazzara, compare Italian zazzera.</div><b>τσατίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) to vex, annoy, irritate, drive around the bend, peeve, rub the wrong way, nettle, get to, tick off, piss off</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Turkish çatı (“to clash, strike”) + -ίζω.</div><b>τσατσάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσατσάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comb (tool for combing one&#x27;s hair)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan zazzara (“a type of long hairstyle for men”).</div><b>τσεκάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be checked</div><b>τσεκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) to check</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English check + -άρω.</div><b>τσεκούρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσεκούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>axe (UK), ax (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τσεκούριον, from Koine Greek σεκούριον, from Latin securis (“axe”). Compare Romanian secure and more distantly Serbo-Croatian сѐкира, Bulgarian секи́ра.</div><b>τσεμπέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>traditional headscarf (worn for warmth, cleanliness or fashion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چنبر, from Classical Persian چنبر.</div><b>τσεπώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to take money illegally, embezzle, pocket</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τσέπωσε</b> τις επιδοτήσεις κι εξαφανίστηκε. - He pocketed the subsidies and disappeared.</div><b>τσεχικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Czech language.</div><b>τσεχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσεχική, neuter τσεχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Czech (relating to the Czech Republic, its people or its language)</div><b>τσεχοσλοβάκικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσεχοσλοβάκικη, neuter τσεχοσλοβάκικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσεχοσλοβακικός">τσεχοσλοβακικός</a></div><b>τσεχοσλοβακικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσεχοσλοβακική, neuter τσεχοσλοβακικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Czechoslovakian (relating to the country of Czechoslovakia or its people)</div><b>τσιάντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσ̌ιάντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) bag</div><b>τσιάντες</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of τσ̌ιάντα</div><b>τσιγάρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιγάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cigarette</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Venetan cigàro (“cigarette”).</div><b>τσιγαρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to brown, to roast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to bother, to pester</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to strongly reprimand, to tell off strongly, a roasting to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τσιγαρίζω, from Venetan cigar or Italian zigare + -ίζω, of onomatopoeic origin.</div><b>τσιγαρίλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, uncountable) cigarette smell (unpleasant smell of smoke left by tobacco use)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Από τότε που απαγόρευσαν το κάπνισμα στους δημόσιους χώρους, έχει φύγει εντελώς η <b>τσιγαρίλα</b>. - Since they banned smoking in public places, the smell of cigarettes has gone completely.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τσιγάρο + -ίλα.</div><b>τσιγαριλίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιγαριλίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joint, marijuana cigarette</div><b>τσιγαρλίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιγαρλίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσιγαριλίκι">τσιγαριλίκι</a></div><b>τσιγγάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσιγγάνες, masculine τσιγγάνος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gypsy, gipsy</div><b>τσιγγάνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσιγγάνοι, feminine τσιγγάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Gypsy, Gipsy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Via Byzantine Greek Ἀτσίγγανος and Ἀθίγγανος from Ancient Greek ἀθίγγανος, ultimately a compound of privative ἀ- (a-) + θιγγάνω. The Middle Greek word had initially been the name of a Christian sect, the Melchizedekian sect, which was subsequently associated to Romani people.
Nişanyan suggests, due to the very recent first attestation of the term in Greek (1378 AD), Proto-Turkic *čï̄gań (“poor”) as its origin, which was already attested in the Bilge Qaghan inscriptions dated to 735, which, in turn, explains the replacement of the accent. This modern accent could also be influenced by the feminine form Ἀτσιγγάνα.
Petrounias, however, explains αθίγγανος as a paretymological variant of ἀτσίγγανος, influenced by the name of the Christian sect.
Also see Italian zingano, German Zigeuner, Turkish çingene.</div><b>τσιγκέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rod</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hook</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish چنگل.</div><b>τσιγκουνιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheapness, niggardliness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τσιγκούν(ης)) + -ιά</div><b>τσιγκούνης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσιγκούνα, neuter τσιγκούνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>miserly, stingy, penny-pinching, scrooge-like, tight-fisted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چنگانه, چنكانه, from Classical Persian چنگانه, likely ultimately from Ancient Greek ἀθίγγανος. Compare the etymological script τσιγγούνης after τσιγγάνος and αθίγγανος, described as a repatriated term by some dictionaries.</div><b>τσικάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσικάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mountaintop, peak</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare Macedonian чукар, Bulgarian чука́р, as well as τσιούκα.</div><b>τσικνιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσικνιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egret (bird)</div><b>τσικουδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσικουδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tsikoudia (a spirit sometimes flavoured with aniseed or other herbs)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Cretan dialect form of <a href="bword://τσίπουρο">τσίπουρο</a></div><b>τσιμέντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. concrete</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian cemento.</div><b>τσιμεντένιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσιμεντένια, neuter τσιμεντένιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>concrete (made of concrete)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσιμέντο, which is a transliteration of cement.</div><b>τσιμεντοκονία</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιμεντοκονίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cement mortar</div><b>τσιμινιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσιμινιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>funnel</div><b>τσιμπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to nip, pinch; literally: &quot;I nip/pinch&quot;</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sting, prick; literally: &quot;I sting/prick&quot;</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Multiple options:</div><b>τσιμπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσιμπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tongs, tweezers</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) hand, clamp (of law, tax authorities, etc.)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η τσιμπίδα του νόμου (the long arm of the law)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Albanian cimb.</div><b>τσιμπίδα του νόμου</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>long arm of the law</div><b>τσιμπιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be nipped, be pinched</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be stung</div><b>τσιμπουκότρυπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσιμπουκότρυπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, slang, vulgar) glory hole (hole in a wall through which fellatio or masturbation is carried out for the sake of anonymity)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσιμπούκι + τρύπα.</div><b>τσιμπουκώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) to blow, go down on, give head, suck off (to fellate the penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η πουτάνα <b>τσιμπουκώνει</b> πολλούς άνδρες κάθε νύχτα. - The whore sucks off a lot of men every night.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τσιμπούκι + -ώνω.</div><b>τσιμπούκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιμπούκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pipe (used for inhaling the smoke of burning tobacco)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς καπνίζει το <b>τσιμπούκι</b> του στη γωνιά. - Grandpa is smoking his pipe in the corner.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, vulgar) blowjob, head (act of fellatio)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η φιλενάδα του είναι ιδιαίτερα καλή στο <b>τσιμπούκι</b>. - His girlfriend is especially good at sucking dick.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish چبوق.</div><b>τσιμπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>variant of <a href="bword://τσιμπάω">τσιμπάω</a></div><b>τσιπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) chip, integrated circuit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable, irregular) crisps (UK), chips (US) (fried sliced potato)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English chip.</div><b>τσιπούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gilt-head bream (Sparus aurata); sea bream</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τσιπούρα, from an unattested *ἵππουρα, feminine of Ancient Greek ἵππουρος.</div><b>τσιράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. apprentice, underling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) creep, sycophant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چراغ, چراق, from Classical Persian چراغ.</div><b>τσιρλονέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιρλονέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a spring whose water has purgative properties</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τσίρλα + νερό</div><b>τσιτάτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιτάτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quotation, citation (a fragment of a human expression that is repeated by somebody else)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from German Zitat with -ο (-o) ending, from Latin citātus.</div><b>τσιτσί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(childish, colloquial, indeclinable) meat</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φάε λίγο <b>τσιτσί</b>, αγόρι μου. - Eat some meat, son.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably a creative expression from children&#x27;s language; cf. Italian ciccia, cicci, also Ancient Greek τιτθός.</div><b>τσιτσίδι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous) stark naked</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η γριά τρελάθηκε και βγήκε <b>τσιτσίδι</b> στον δρόμο έξω. - The old woman went mad and went out stark naked into the street.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τσιτσί + -ίδι</div><b>τσιφούτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσιφούτηδες, feminine τσιφούτα or τσιφούτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stingy, miserly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish چفوت.</div><b>τσιφτετέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιφτετέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tsifteteli (a sensuous belly dance of Turkish origin, that is found in many of the territories of the former Ottoman Empire, particularly Greece.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Turkish çiftetelli.</div><b>τσιχλαηδόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσιχλαηδόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Luscinia luscinia, the thrush nightingale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τσίχλα + αηδόνι</div><b>τσογλάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσογλάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) brat, scoundrel, scamp, ne&#x27;er-do-well, rapscallion, scumbag (a young male cheeky person or troublemaker)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το <b>τσογλάνι</b> πάλι μας έσπασε το παράθυρο με τη μπάλα του! - That little brat broke our window with this ball again!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish ایچ اوغلانی, composed of ایچ + اوغلان + ـی.</div><b>τσοκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) chuck (part of lathe, electric drill, milling machine etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) choke (carburetor component)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (indeclinable) choke (constriction in a shotgun barrel)</div><b>τσοπάνης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τσοπάνηδες or τσοπαναραίοι, feminine τσοπάνισσα or τσομπάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shepherd</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چوبان, from Classical Persian چوپان.</div><b>τσορίθο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσορίθα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) chorizo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Spanish chorizo.</div><b>τσουάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Tswana (the language of the Bantu people living in Botswana)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English Tswana.</div><b>τσουγγάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rock peak</div>
  <div class='def'>2. small or large protruding rock from sloping terrain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Variant of τσικάρι. Compare Aromanian ciungane.</div><b>τσουγγρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small or large protruding rock from sloping terrain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare τζουγκρί.</div><b>τσουγκάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hill</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ἐγερθέντες ἀπεσύρθημεν εἰς τοὺς πρόποδας λόφου τινὸς, ὅτε δὲ ἐπλησίαζεν ἡ ἡμέρα, ἀνήλθομεν εἰς τὸν λόφον, ἢ ὡς τὸν ὀνόμαζον οἱ λῃσταὶ <b>τσουγκάρι</b>, καὶ ἐτοποθετήθημεν ὑπὸ βράχον, ὅπου κατακλιθεὶς ἐκοιμήθην μέχρι τῆς ἐννάτης.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Variant of τσικάρι.</div><b>τσουγκράνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσουγκράνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rake, garden rake</div><b>τσουγκρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to tap (two red Easter eggs together)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to clink (two wine glasses together)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to strike (two vehicles colliding gently)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek συγκρούω. By surface analysis, σύν + κρούω.</div><b>τσουγκρανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rake, scarify the soil</div><b>τσουλήθρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσουλήθρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slide (item of play equipment that children can climb up and then slide down again)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσουλώ + -θρα (-thra).</div><b>τσουνάμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) tsunami, tidal wave</div><b>τσουράπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσουράπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(vernacular) thick woollen sock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چوراب, from Classical Persian جوراب.</div><b>τσουρέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσουρέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tsoureki (sweet, leavened bread, enriched with egg and butter, similar to brioche)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چورك.</div><b>τσουρουφλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to burn something at its edges or surface</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (passive, regional) to endure long-term hardship, suffering</div><b>τσουτσούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσουτσούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(childish, colloquial, vulgar) willy, pecker, dick (penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταμάτα να παίζεις με το <b>τσουτσούνι</b> σου! - Stop playing with your pecker!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Reduplication of τσούνι.</div><b>τσουχτερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τσουχτερή, neuter τσουχτερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bitter, biting (cold, wind, winter, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. stinging, biting, sharp (criticism, words, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. eye-watering (prices)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τσούζω + -τερός.</div><b>τσούζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in the third person) to sting, to smart (to cause or be the site of sharp pain)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τσούζουν</b> τα μάτια μου. - My eyes are stinging.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in the third person) to sting (to cause a sharp emotional pain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τσούζω, from τσούκζω, possibly from Ancient Greek σίζω.</div><b>τσούκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mountaintop, peak, summit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hill</div>
  <div class='def'>3. large vertical stone</div>
  <div class='def'>4. egg (swelling on the head)</div>
  <div class='ex'>το ’καναν <b>τσούκα</b> του κιφάλ’ - they gave him a bump on the head</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare Albanian çukë, Proto-Slavic *čuka, Romanian ciucă, Aromanian ciucã. Compare also τσούμα and τσικάρι, τσουκάρι, τσουγκάρι.</div><b>τσούκτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσούκτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσούχτρα">τσούχτρα</a></div><b>τσούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, offensive) slut, tart, hussy, whore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Broken variant of Italian fanciulla (“little girl”).</div><b>τσούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τσόλι">τσόλι</a></div><b>τσούμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cluster of trees on a pointed mountain peak</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mountaintop, peak, smaller than <a href="bword://τσουγγάνι">τσουγγάνι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare Aromanian ciumã (“summit, peak”), as well as τσούκα. The variant with /b/ may have arisen by influence of τούμπα.</div><b>τσούμπα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mountaintop, peak, smaller than <a href="bword://τσουγγάνι">τσουγγάνι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. rock with a pointed top</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Variant of τσούμα, compare Aromanian ciumbã.</div><b>τσούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσούνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) stalk, stem</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (childish, colloquial) penis</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) skittles</div><b>τσούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) goat</div><b>τσούχτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσούχτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. jellyfish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) sly evil woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Modern: Demotic verb τσούζω &quot;tingle, sting, nip&quot; → feminine noun τσούκτρα → τσούχτρα</div><b>τσόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tattered blanket</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rag rug</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) worthless man</div>
  <div class='def'>4. male whore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish چول.</div><b>τσόντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσόντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gusset (small piece of cloth inserted in a garment, for the purpose of strengthening some part or giving it a tapering enlargement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ράφτρα βάζει <b>τσόντα</b> στη φούστα. - The seamstress is putting a gusset on the skirt.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) supplement, addition (anything added onto something else)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (broadly, colloquial) A scene of pornography added into an unrelated film.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (broadly, colloquial) porno, porn film, blue movie, skin flick (a pornographic film)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του φίλου μου του αρέσει να βλέπουμε <b>τσόντες</b> μαζί πριν κάνουμε σεξ. - My boyfriend likes us to watch porn films together before we have sex.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan zonta (“addition”), a variant form of xonta.</div><b>τσόφλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τσόφλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. eggshell, shell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. peel, skin</div><b>τσόχα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τσόχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>type of wool; felt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τσόχα, from Ottoman Turkish چوخه</div><b>τυλίγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to wind up, wrap around, coil up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ρολόι <b>τυλίγεται</b> στον καρπό σας. - The watch wraps around your wrist.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to wind oneself up, become engulfed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Τυλίγεται</b> στις φλόγες. - It was engulfed in flames.</div><b>τυλίγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to roll, coil, wind</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to roll up, wind up</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τυλίγω, from Hellenistic Koine Greek τῠλί̆σσω with metaplasm of -σσ- (-ss-) &gt; -γ- (-g-) from the aorist stem τυλιξ- (tulix-), based on the model seen in verbs such as ανοίγω and its perfect stem άνοιξ-.</div><b>τυλιγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine τυλιγμένη, neuter τυλιγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wrapped</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of τυλίγομαι, passive voice of τυλίγω (“wrap”).</div><b>τυμπανοκρουσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τυμπανοκρουσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a drum roll on snare drum, tattoo</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) noisy presentation, fanfare</div><b>τυπικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of τυπικός</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>typically (in a typical manner)</div><b>τυπική απόκλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>standard deviation</div><b>τυπογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τυπογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. printer (the occupation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. typographer</div><b>τυπογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) typography, printing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Hellenised form of New Latin typographia</div><b>τυπογραφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τυπογραφεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>print shop, printer&#x27;s, printing works (a printing business building)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τυπογραφία + -είο.</div><b>τυπογραφεῖον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τυπογραφεῖα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://τυπογραφείο">τυπογραφείο</a></div><b>τυπογραφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυπογραφική, neuter τυπογραφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>typographical, typographic</div><b>τυποποιημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυποποιημένη, neuter τυποποιημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>standard, basic, original, standardised, unoriginal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> from τυποποιώ</div><b>τυπώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to print</div><b>τυρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τυριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheese</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τυρίν, from Ancient Greek τυρίον; diminutive of τυρός, from Proto-Hellenic *tūrós, from Proto-Indo-European *tuh₂-ró-s, from *tewh₂- (“to swell”). Cognate with Mariupol Greek тыри́.</div><b>τυραννάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to torment</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to tyrannise (UK), tyrannize (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τυρανν(ώ)) + -άω, inherited from the Ancient Greek τυραννῶ, contracted form of τυραννάω, from τύραννος</div><b>τυραννία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τυραννίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tyranny, despotism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. torment, torture</div><b>τυραννιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive of <a href="bword://τυραννώ">τυραννώ</a>; to be tortured</div>
  <div class='def'>2. passive of <a href="bword://τυραννώ">τυραννώ</a>; torture oneself</div><b>τυραννικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυραννική, neuter τυραννικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tyrannical (of, or relating to tyranny)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τυραννικός. By surface analysis, τύραννος + -ικός.</div><b>τυραννώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τυραννάω">τυραννάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the Ancient Greek τυραννῶ, contracted form of τυραννάω, from τύραννος.</div><b>τυρκουάζ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) old spelling of <a href="bword://τιρκουάζ">τιρκουάζ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Older transliteration of the French turquoise. See τιρκουάζ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) old spelling of <a href="bword://τιρκουάζ">τιρκουάζ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Older transliteration of the French turquoise. See τιρκουάζ.</div><b>τυροί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of τυρός</div><b>τυροκαυτερή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τυροκαυτερές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tirokafteri (spicy appetizer, consisting of cheese, olive oil, hot peppers, and various other ingredients)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τυρί + -ο- (-o-, “linking vowel”) + καυτερή.</div><b>τυροκομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheesemaking, cheese production</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τυρί + -κομία. First attested 1854.</div><b>τυροκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τυροκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheese factory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τυροκόμ(ος), “a maker of cheese”) + -είο. Compare to the Hellenistic Koine Greek τυροκομεῖον</div><b>τυροκομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυροκομική, neuter τυροκομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to cheese or cheesemaking</div><b>τυροκόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural τυροκόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheesemaker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τυρί + -κόμος. First attested 1889.</div><b>τυροσίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tyrosine</div><b>τυρρηνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυρρηνική, neuter τυρρηνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Etruscan (relating to Etruria or its people or language)</div><b>τυρόπηγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τυροπήγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>curd</div><b>τυρόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τυρόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheese pie (Greek pastry made with phyllo and containing cheese, usually feta)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> τυρί + πίτα.</div><b>τυρόφιλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cheese-lover, turophile</div><b>τυφέκιον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τυφέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://τουφέκι">τουφέκι</a></div><b>τυφλοπόντικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τυφλοπόντικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mole (mammal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τυφλός + ποντικός.</div><b>τυφλόμυγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) blind man&#x27;s buff</div><b>τυφλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυφλή, neuter τυφλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τῠφλός.</div><b>τυφλότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) blindness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τυφλότης.</div><b>τυφλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to blind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τυφλώνω, from Ancient Greek τυφλῶ. By surface analysis, τυφλός + -ώνω.</div><b>τυφοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυφοειδής, neuter τυφοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>typhoid (relating to typhus)</div><b>τυφοειδής πυρετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) typhoid fever</div><b>τυφώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τυφώνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hurricane, typhoon</div><b>τυχαία</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. randomly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. by chance</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://τυχαίος">τυχαίος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://τυχαίος">τυχαίος</a></div><b>τυχαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to happen, happen on, chance, occur by chance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek τυχαίνω, from Ancient Greek τυγχάνω, from Proto-Hellenic *tunkʰanō, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰewgʰ-.</div><b>τυχαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυχαία, neuter τυχαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>random, chance, casual, fortuitous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek τυχαῖος.</div><b>τυχερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine τυχερή, neuter τυχερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fortunate, lucky</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek τυχερός, from Ancient Greek τυχηρός.</div><b>τυχοδιώκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τυχοδιώκτες, feminine τυχοδιώκτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adventurer, chancer, fortune hunter, fortune seeker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from τύχ(η)) + -ο- (-o-) + διώκτης, a calque of English fortune hunter.</div><b>τυχοδιώκτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τυχοδιώκτριες, masculine τυχοδιώκτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adventurer, adventuress</div><b>τχης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://ταγματάρχης">ταγματάρχης</a></div><b>τω</b>
  <div><i>article, neuter singular</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa) monotonic form of τῷ, masculine/neuter dative singular of ὁ (o)</div><b>τωόντι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>indeed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From τῷ + ὄντι.</div><b>τόκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τόκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interest (charge made for credit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. interest rate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τόκος.</div><b>τόλμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) daring, boldness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) audacity, effrontery</div><b>τόλμημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τολμήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>act of daring, boldness, audacity or effrontery</div><b>τόννος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τόννοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://τόνος">τόνος</a></div><b>τόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τόνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accent (΄), tonos (the stress accent used in modern Greek)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. note, pitch, tone</div>
  <div class='def'>3. shade</div>
  <div class='def'>4. tonne, tonnage (unit of weight)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. tuna (fish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τόνος.</div><b>τόξο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τόξα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bow, longbow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τόξον.</div><b>τόπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ball (round rubber object used in games)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν έρθει ξανά αυτό το <b>τόπι</b> στην αυλή μου, θα το σκάσω! - If that ball comes into my yard again, I&#x27;ll burst it!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. roll (of cloth)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κατέβασε ένα σωρό <b>τόπια</b>. - He took down a load of rolls of cloth.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, dated) cannonball</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (broadly, colloquial, dated) cannonball; cannon</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βάλτε φωτιά στα <b>τόπια</b>, στα <b>τόπια</b>,
κάψτε τα Γιάννενα. - Set fire to the cannon, to the cannon,
burn Yannena.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish طوپ (top, “ball, cannon”).</div><b>τόπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τόποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. place, location, locality; country</div>
  <div class='def'>2. place, location, locality; native land</div>
  <div class='def'>3. place, location, locality; home town</div>
  <div class='def'>4. space, room (occupied by something)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. soil, land</div>
  <div class='def'>6. locus (set of points)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τόπος. The mathematical sense, a semantic loan from New Latin locus.</div><b>τόπος άρθρωσης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τόποι άρθρωσης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>place of articulation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English place of articulation or French lieu d&#x27;articulation.</div><b>τόρνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τόρνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lathe</div><b>τόσο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>so, such, so much</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν <b>τόσο</b> μακριά που δεν το είδα. - It was so far away that I didn&#x27;t see it.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τόσον, neuter form of τόσος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://τόσος">τόσος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://τόσος">τόσος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τόσον, neuter form of τόσος.</div><b>τόσος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>so, so much, such</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>τόσο</b> μεγάλο όσο ένας ελέφαντας - as big as an elephant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τόσος.</div><b>τότε</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. then</div>
  <div class='def'>2. therefore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek τότε.</div><b>τόφφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (countable and uncountable, plural τόφφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) tuff</div><b>τύμπανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural τύμπανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. timpani, drum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ear drum</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tympanum</div>
  <div class='def'>4. drum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τύμπανον. Doublet of τούμπανο.</div><b>τύπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τύποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sort, type, mould, stamp (of a person character)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. model, type (of car, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. shape, form</div>
  <div class='def'>4. the press, the newspapers collectively</div>
  <div class='def'>5. formality, convention</div>
  <div class='def'>6. formula</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (colloquial) a man, a guy, a chap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek τύπος.
The colloquial meaning of &quot;guy, chap&quot; is a semantic loan from French type. Sense &quot;the press&quot;, semantic loan from French presse.</div><b>τύραννος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural τύραννοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tyrant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) tyrant (an absolute ruler in ancient Greece)</div><b>τύρφη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τύρφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peat, turf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from both English turf and German Torf; further from Proto-West Germanic *turb, Proto-Germanic *turbz and Proto-Indo-European *derbʰ-.</div><b>τύφλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural τύφλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) blindness</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) blind (drunk)</div><b>τύφλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τυφλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blindness, sightlessness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) inability to understand</div><b>τύφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) typhus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ancient Greek τῦφος, fever, stupor, which in turn is derived from τύφω.</div><b>τύχη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τύχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fate, chance, luck</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek τύχη, from Proto-Hellenic *túkʰā, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰewgʰ-.</div><b>τύψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural τύψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pang of remorse</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) remorse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek τύψις, as in a &quot;blow to the conscience&quot;.</div><b>τώρα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>now</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek τώρα, from Ancient Greek τῇ ὥρᾳ, from τῇ + ὥρα.</div><b>τώρα στα γεράματα, μάθε γέρο γράμματα</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>you can&#x27;t teach an old dog new tricks, old habits die hard (it is impossible to change people&#x27;s habits, traits or mindset)</div><b>υ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Υ΄">Υ΄</a></div><b>υάκινθος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hyacinth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑάκινθος.</div><b>υαλοπίνακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υαλοπίνακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>windowpane</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ύαλος + πίνακας.</div><b>υαλοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υαλοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>china shop</div><b>υαλοπωλείω</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://υαλοπωλείο">υαλοπωλείο</a> in the dative case</div><b>υαλοπώλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υαλοπώλες, feminine υαλοπώλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glazier</div><b>υαλοπώλισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υαλοπώλισσες, masculine υαλοπώλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glazier</div><b>υαλόνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υαλονήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncommon) </div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα προϊόντα CFGF κατασκευάζονται κατά κανόνα από ατέρμον <b>υαλόνημα</b> τύπου E με διάμετρο μεγαλύτερη από 8 µm και με παράλληλο προσανατολισμό.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ύαλος + νήμα.</div><b>υβρίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υβρίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hybrid, cross, crossbreed</div><b>υβριστ.</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://υβριστικός">υβριστικός</a>: offensive, insulting</div><b>υβριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υβριστική, neuter υβριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>insulting, abusive, rude</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑβριστικός.</div><b>υγίεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υγίειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Ancient Greek, archaic) monotonic form of ancient ὑγίεια</div><b>υγεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υγείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>health</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εθνικό Σύστημα <b>Υγείας</b> - National Health Service</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὑγεία from Ancient Greek ὑγίεια, from ὑγιής, from Proto-Indo-European *h₂yu-gʷih₃- (“long life”).
Cognate with Mariupol Greek и́я.</div><b>υγειά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υγειές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://υγεία">υγεία</a> (“health”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δόξα τω Θεώ, έχω την <b>υγειά</b> μου. - Thank God, I am in good health.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From medieval Byzantine Greek γειά, from Koine Greek ὑγεία, from Ancient Greek ὑγίεια, from ὑγιής.</div><b>υγιής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υγιής, neuter υγιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>healthy, sane, sound, well</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑγιής.</div><b>υγιεινή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hygiene</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://υγιεινός">υγιεινός</a></div><b>υγιεινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υγιεινή, neuter υγιεινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>healthy, hygienic, healthful, sanitary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑγιεινός.</div><b>υγρασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) moisture, humidity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑγρασία.</div><b>υγροποιημένο φυσικό αέριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(singular only) liquid natural gas, liquefied natural gas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English liquefied natural gas.</div><b>υγρό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υγρά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fluid</div>
  <div class='ex'>γαστρικό <b>υγρό</b> - gastric juice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. liquid, fluid</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υγρών</b> καυσίμων’’ - liquid fuel, petrol</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivization of υγρός.</div><b>υγρό πιάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>washing-up liquid</div><b>υγρόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υγρόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hygrometer</div><b>υγρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υγρή, neuter υγρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wet (covered with or impregnated with liquid, usually water)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. liquid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ὑγρός.</div><b>υδάτινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδάτινη, neuter υδάτινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. water (attributive), aqueous (consisting of water)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. aquatic (of environment, resources, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑδάτινος.</div><b>υδατάνθρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδατάνθρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural) carbohydrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υδατ(ο)- (ydat(o)-) + άνθρακας, a calque of French hydrates de carbone.</div><b>υδατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδατική, neuter υδατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) water (attributive), aquatic (relating to water)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑδατικός.</div><b>υδατογράφημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υδατογραφήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>watermark</div><b>υδατογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υδατογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>watercolour (method and end product)</div><b>υδατοδιαλυτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδατοδιαλυτή, neuter υδατοδιαλυτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>water-soluble, hydrosoluble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υδατο- (ydato-) + διαλυτός, a calque of English water-soluble.</div><b>υδατοκαλλιέργεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aquaculture</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υδατο- (ydato-) + καλλιέργεια.</div><b>υδατοστεγής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδατοστεγής, neuter υδατοστεγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>watertight, waterproof</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υδατο- (ydato-) + Ancient Greek στέγ(ω)) + -ής as a calque of English waterproof, or a misspelling of Koine Greek ὑδασιστεγής.</div><b>υδατοσφαίριση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) water polo</div><b>υδατοφράκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδατοφράκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. weir, sluice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dam</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lock (on harbour or canal)</div><b>υδατοφράχτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδατοφράχτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://υδατοφράκτης">υδατοφράκτης</a></div><b>υδράργυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) mercury</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὑδράργυρος, equivalent to υδρ- (ydr-, “water”) + άργυρος.</div><b>υδρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υδρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jar, pitcher, urn (water vessel)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ύδωρ + -ία</div><b>υδραγωγείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υδραγωγεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aqueduct</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑδραγωγεῖον. By surface analysis, υδρ- (ydr-) + αγωγ(ός)) + -είο.</div><b>υδραντλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υδραντλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>water pump</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υδρ- (ydr-) + αντλία, a calque of English water pump.</div><b>υδρατμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδρατμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural, often) water vapour (UK), water vapor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υδρ- (ydr-) + ατμός, a calque of English water vapour.</div><b>υδραυλικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://υδραυλικός">υδραυλικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plumbing (the pipes and other fixtures of a water or sewage system in a building)</div><b>υδραυλική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://υδραυλικός">υδραυλικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hydraulics (the engineering science that deals with practical applications where liquid is in motion and transmits energy)</div><b>υδραυλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδραυλική, neuter υδραυλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydraulic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὑδραυλικός, equivalent to υδρ- (ydr-, “water”) + αυλός + -ικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδραυλικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plumber</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hydraulic engineer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὑδραυλικός, equivalent to υδρ- (ydr-, “water”) + αυλός + -ικός.</div><b>υδρογονάνθρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδρογονάνθρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydrocarbon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υδρογόν(ο), “hydrogen”) + άνθρακας, a calque of French hydrocarbure.</div><b>υδρογόνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural υδρογόνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) hydrogen (element and gas)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French hydrogène, a French-coined term adopted into Greek, equivalent to υδρο- (ydro-, “water, hydro-”) + γόνος. First attested 1871.</div><b>υδροδότηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>water supply (through a network)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the υδροδοτη- stem of υδροδοτώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>υδροηλεκτρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδροηλεκτρική, neuter υδροηλεκτρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydroelectric, hydroelectrical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French hydroélectrique. By surface analysis, υδρο- (ydro-) + ηλεκτρικός.</div><b>υδροκρίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>watershed, drainage divide (the topographical boundary of a drainage basin)</div><b>υδροκυάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υδροκυάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydrogen cyanide</div><b>υδρολίσθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υδρολισθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aquaplane, aquaplaning</div><b>υδροληψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υδροληψίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. water supply</div>
  <div class='def'>2. water outlet, hydrant</div><b>υδρολισθητήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδρολισθητήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) hydrofoil</div><b>υδρολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υδρολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydrologist</div><b>υδροναύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδροναύτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aquanaut</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υδρο- (ydro-, “water”) + ναύτης</div><b>υδροξείδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural υδροξείδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) hydroxide</div>
  <div class='ex'>υδροξείδιο του καλίου - potassium hydroxide</div><b>υδροξείδιο του ασβεστίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) calcium hydroxide, slaked lime</div><b>υδροξείδιο του καλίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) potassium hydroxide</div><b>υδροπέπων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υδροπέπονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) watermelon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υδρο- (ydro-, “water related”) + πέπων, a calque of French melon d&#x27;eau.</div><b>υδροπλάνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υδροπλάνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seaplane</div><b>υδροπλανοφόρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υδροπλανοφόρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seaplane tender</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υδροπλάνο + -φόρο.</div><b>υδροπτέρυγο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υδροπτέρυγα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydrofoil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υδρο- (ydro-, “water, hydro-”) + πτερύγιο, calque of English hydrofoil.</div><b>υδρορροή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gutter (on roof)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. downpipe, downspout</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Variant of υδρορρόη with stress shift.</div><b>υδροφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδροφόρος or υδροφόρα, neuter υδροφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aquiferous, water-bearing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑδροφόρος. By surface analysis, υδρο- (ydro-) + -φόρος.</div><b>υδροϊώδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural υδροϊώδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) hydrogen iodide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From By surface analysis, υδρο- (ydro-, “hydro-”) + ιώδιο.</div><b>υδρόβιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδρόβια, neuter υδρόβιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>aquatic (living in or near water)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ένα καλλιεργούμενο <b>υδρόβιο</b> φυτό. - A cultivated aquatic plant.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὑδρόβιος, equivalent to υδρό- + βίος.</div><b>υδρόγειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδρόγειος or υδρόγεια, neuter υδρόγειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>terraqueous</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υδρόγειος</b> σφαίρα - globe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υδρό- + -γειος (-geios), a calque of French terraqué.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υδρόγειοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ellipsis of <a href="bword://υδρόγειος">υδρόγειος</a> <a href="bword://σφαίρα">σφαίρα</a>; globe (planet Earth)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ellipsis of <a href="bword://υδρόγειος">υδρόγειος</a> <a href="bword://σφαίρα">σφαίρα</a>; globe (a spherical model of Earth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υδρό- + -γειος (-geios), a calque of French terraqué.</div><b>υδρόλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hydrolysis</div><b>υδρόμελι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) mead</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) a drink of water and honey</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υδρο- (ydro-) + μέλι.</div><b>υδρόφιλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υδρόφιλη, neuter υδρόφιλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hydrophilic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. water loving (of wetland plants)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French hydrophile, a French-coined term adopted into Greek, equivalent to υδρό- + -φιλος (-filos, “friend of, -phile”). First attested 1888.</div><b>υετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υετοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>precipitation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑετός.</div><b>υιοθέτηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υιοθετήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>adoption (choosing and making that to be one&#x27;s own which originally was not so; the act of accepting and putting into effect or practice: idea, measure, proposal, technique, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek υιοθέτησις. By surface analysis, υιοθέτη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>υιοθετούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be adopted</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: υἱοθετοῦμαι</div><b>υιοθετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to adopt (a child)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to adopt, endorse (an idea or policy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek υἱοθετέω / υἱοθετῶ. The figurative sense, semantic loan from French adopter. Morphologically, from υιός) + θέτω).</div><b>υιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://γιος">γιος</a> (gios)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek υἱός.</div><b>υλίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υλίστριες, masculine υλιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>materialist</div><b>υλικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υλικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. material, matter, stuff</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ingredient</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to ύλη, or from a derivative of Ancient Greek υλικό.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://υλικός">υλικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://υλικός">υλικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to ύλη, or from a derivative of Ancient Greek υλικό.</div><b>υλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υλική, neuter υλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>material, physical</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι καιρός να συνειδητοποιήσουμε όλοι ότι ο <b>υλικός</b> μας πλούτος δεν θα διατηρηθούν επ’ άπειρον από μόνα τους. - It&#x27;s high time we realised that our material wealth won&#x27;t last forever.</div><b>υλισμικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υλισμικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hardware</div><b>υλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) materialism</div><b>υλιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υλιστές, feminine υλίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>materialist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ύλη + -ιστής, calque of French matérialiste. First attested 1794.</div><b>υλιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υλιστική, neuter υλιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>materialistic</div><b>υλοζωία</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hylozoism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French hylozoïsme.</div><b>υλοζωισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hylozoism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the French hylozoïsme. With internal borrowing of original Greek combining forms: ὕλη (“matter”) for hylo- + ζωή (“life”) + Hellenistic -ισμός for -isme.</div><b>υλοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υλοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>implementation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα σχέδια είναι έτοιμα, αλλά θα χρειστούν χρήματα για την <b>υλοποίησή</b> τους. - The plans are ready, but I will need money to implement them.</div><b>υλοτομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) logging, lumbering</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑλοτομία.</div><b>υμένας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υμένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. membrane</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially) hymen</div><b>υμένιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υμένια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. film (thin layer)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pellicle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bloom (natural powdery coating on some fruits or leaves)</div><b>υμνώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to praise, to hymn, to laud</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑμνῶ, contracted form of ὑμνέω.</div><b>υπ&#x27; όψιν</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of weak pronouns or nouns) into account; under consideration</div>
  <div class='ex'>(syntax with verbs) έχω (écho, “have”), λαμβάνω (lamváno, “receive”)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with the verb missing) keep in mind; remember (bear in mind)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υπ’ όψιν</b>! Να έχετε πάντα μαζί σας το διαβατήρια. - Remember! Always carry your passport with you.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. for the attention of</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υπ’ όψιν</b> κυρίας Χ. - For the attention of Mrs. X.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. remember that</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (as noun) στα υπ’ όψιν</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Polytonic spelling: ὑπ’ ὄψιν, from the phrase: is omitted), ὑπό + ancient ὄψις f in accusative singular. Literally: (have) under view.</div><b>υπάκουος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπάκουη, neuter υπάκουο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obedient, dutiful</div><b>υπάλληλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υπάλληλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. employee</div>
  <div class='def'>2. clerk, assistant</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υπάλληλος</b> υποδοχής - receptionist</div><b>υπάρχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to exist, be, live</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σκέφτομαι, άρα <b>υπάρχω</b>. - I think, therefore I am.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπάρχω.</div><b>υπέρ</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with genitive) for, in support, in favor</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μια μάχη <b>υπέρ</b> της ελευθερίας. - A battle for freedom.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (with accusative) more than, above</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτοκτόνησαν <b>υπέρ</b> τους χιλίους. - More than a thousand committed suicide.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπέρ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ψηφίσαμε όλοι <b>υπέρ</b>. - We all voted for.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπέρ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) advantages, benefits, pros</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συζητήστε τα <b>υπέρ</b> της δημοκρατίας. - Discuss the advantages of democracy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπέρ.</div><b>υπέρβαρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπέρβαρη, neuter υπέρβαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>overweight</div>
  <div class='ex'>υπέρβαρες αποσκευές (excess baggage)</div><b>υπέρβαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπερβάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transcendence</div><b>υπέρηχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπέρηχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ultrasound</div><b>υπέρμετρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπέρμετρη, neuter υπέρμετρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>excessive, immoderate, inordinate, unmeasured</div><b>υπέροχα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative neuter plural of <a href="bword://υπέροχος">υπέροχος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative neuter plural of <a href="bword://υπέροχος">υπέροχος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://υπέροχος">υπέροχος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wonderfully, greatly, fantastically, superbly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περάσαμε <b>υπέροχα</b> σ’ αυτές τις διακοπές. - We had a wonderful time on this holiday.</div><b>υπέροχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπέροχη, neuter υπέροχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>excellent, exquisite, marvellous, superb, wonderful</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο γιος μου έχει <b>υπέροχη</b> φωνή. - My son has a marvellous voice.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπέροχος, from ὑπερέχω, from ῠ̔πέρ + ἔχω.</div><b>υπέρταση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hypertension, high blood pressure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπέρτασις. By surface analysis, υπέρ + τάση. Semantic loan from French hypertension.</div><b>υπέρτιμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπέρτιμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>something of extreme value, something very precious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὑπέρτιμος.</div><b>υπέρυθρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπέρυθρη, neuter υπέρυθρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. infrared</div>
  <div class='ex'>υπέρυθρη ακτινοβολία (infrared radiation)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rufous, reddish</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ruddy, rubicund</div><b>υπήκοος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υπήκοοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. citizen, national (of a country)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορείτε να ταξιδέψετε στην Αυστραλία εάν είστε Αυστραλός <b>υπήκοος</b>, μόνιμος κάτοικος ή <b>υπήκοος</b> Νέας Ζηλανδίας που διαμένει συνήθως στην Αυστραλία. - You can travel to Australia if you are an Australian national, permanent resident or New Zealand national who normally resides in Australia.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subject (of a monarch)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπήκοος, from ὑπό + ἀκοή.</div><b>υπήνεμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπήνεμη, neuter υπήνεμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leeward</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπήνεμος, from ὑπό + ἄνεμος.</div><b>υπίλαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υπίλαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (obsolete) the rank of lieutenant in a cavalry unit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. the rank of lieutenant in an armoured unit (NATO grade OF-1 in the British and US armies)</div><b>υπαίθριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. outdoor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alfresco</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὑπαίθριος.</div><b>υπαίτιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπαίτια, neuter υπαίτιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. guilty, responsible</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun) guilty person</div><b>υπαγορεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be dictated</div><b>υπαγορεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to dictate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑπαγορεύω. By surface analysis, (υπο-) υπ- (“under”) + αγορεύω (“make formal speech”).</div><b>υπαγόρευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dictation</div><b>υπαινίσσομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to insinuate, imply, hint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπαινίσσομαι.</div><b>υπακοή</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) obedience</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) ypakoë</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπακοή.</div><b>υπακούω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to obey, comply, be obedient</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εκείνα τα παιδιά δεν <b>υπακούνε</b> ποτέ τους γονείς τους. - Those children never obey their parents.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπακούω.</div><b>υπακτικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπακτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laxative</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of υπακτικός</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of υπακτικός</div><b>υπαξιωματικός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπαξιωματικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>non-commissioned officer, NCO</div><b>υπαρκτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπαρκτή, neuter υπαρκτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>existent, existing</div><b>υπαρξιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπαρξιακή, neuter υπαρξιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>existentialist</div><b>υπαρξισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) existentialism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ύπαρξη + -ισμός, calque of German Existentialismus.</div><b>υπαρξιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπαρξιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>existentialist</div><b>υπγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://υποστράτηγος">υποστράτηγος</a></div><b>υπενθυμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to remind</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπ- (yp-) + ενθυμίζω, influenced by Ancient Greek ὑπομιμνήσκω.</div><b>υπενθύμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπενθυμίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reminder</div><b>υπεράνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπεράνθρωπη, neuter υπεράνθρωπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. superhuman</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in Nietzschean philosophy) übermensch, superman</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (noun from verb) superhuman, superman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑπεράνθρωπος with semantic loan from French surhumain and German Übermensch. By surface analysis, υπερ- (yper-) + άνθρωπος.</div><b>υπερήπειρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπερήπειροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supercontinent</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Γη ήταν μία <b>υπερήπειρο</b>, η Παγγαία. - The Earth used to be a supercontinent, Pangaea.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπερ- (yper-, “hyper, super”) + ήπειρος</div><b>υπερήρωας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπερήρωες, feminine υπερηρωίδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>superhero</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English superhero, from υπερ- (yper-) + ήρωας.</div><b>υπεραγορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπεραγορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypermarket, supermarket</div><b>υπεραλίευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) overfishing</div><b>υπεραξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπεραξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>surplus value</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπερ- (yper-, “over-, surplus”) + αξία.</div><b>υπερασπίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to defend, stand up for</div><b>υπερασπίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to defend, stand up for</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to protect, defend</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὑπερασπίζω, from Ancient Greek ὑπέρ + ἀσπίς.</div><b>υπερασπιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπερασπιστές, feminine υπερασπίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) One who holds a shield over another, a hyperaspist.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. protector, defender</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπερασπιστής. By surface analysis, υπερ- (yper-, “over”) + ασπιστής or υπερασπίζω + -τής.</div><b>υπεραστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπεραστική, neuter υπεραστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. long-distance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. intercity</div><b>υπερβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to exaggerate, overstate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to surpass, exceed, better</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑπερβάλλω. Morphologically υπερ- (yper-) + βάλλω.</div><b>υπερβασίλειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural αυτοκρατορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. empire</div>
  <div class='ex'>το ρωμαϊκό <b>υπερβασίλειο</b> - the Roman empire</div>
  <div class='def'>2. domain (the highest taxonomic rank of organisms)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπερ- (yper-, “over-”) + βασίλειο</div><b>υπερβατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπερβατική, neuter υπερβατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transcendental, transcendent</div><b>υπερβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπερβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. exaggeration, overstatement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hyperbole (figure of speech)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hyperbola (geometric curve)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑπερβολή (from Koine Greek in the sense &#x27;hyperbola&#x27;).</div><b>υπερβολικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>excessively</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://υπερβολικός">υπερβολικός</a></div><b>υπερβολικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπερβολική, neuter υπερβολικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. excessive, exorbitant, exaggerated, rampant</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hyperbolic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὑπερβολικός.</div><b>υπεργεννητικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high birth rate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπερ- (yper-) + γεννητικότητα, a calque of English high birth rate.</div><b>υπεργλυκαιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hyperglycaemia</div><b>υπεργλυχαιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://υπεργλυκαιμία">υπεργλυκαιμία</a></div><b>υπεργολάβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υπεργολάβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subcontractor</div><b>υπερεγώ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) superego</div><b>υπερηφάνεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://περηφάνια">περηφάνια</a></div><b>υπερθερμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hyperthermia (high temperature)</div><b>υπερθετικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural yperthetikά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(superlative) superlative</div><b>υπερθετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπερθετική, neuter υπερθετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(superlative) superlative</div>
  <div class='ex'>υπερθετικός βαθμός του επιθέτου (the superlative degree of the adjective)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπερθετικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(superlative) superlative</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο θετικός, ο συγκριτικός και ο υπερθετικός (the positive, the comparative and superlative)</div><b>υπερικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπερικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypericum, St. John&#x27;s wort</div><b>υπερισχύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary, transitive, with genitive) to prevail over; win a battle, competition</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υπερίσχυσε</b> των αντιπάλων του. - S/he prevailed over his/her opponents.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, literary) to prevail, to dominate, to have primacy</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υπερισχύει</b> το σύνταγμα. - The constitution has primacy.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ὑπερισχύω.</div><b>υπεριώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπεριώδης, neuter υπεριώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ultraviolet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπερ- (yper-, “ultra”) + ιώδης, calque of French ultraviolet.</div><b>υπερκαινοφανής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπερκαινοφανείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supernova (star which explodes, increasing its brightness)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπερ- (yper-, “hyper, super”) + καινοφανής, calque of English supernova.</div><b>υπερκείμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπερκείμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypertext</div><b>υπερκείμενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπερκείμενη, neuter υπερκείμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypertext</div><b>υπερμαγγανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπερμαγγανική, neuter υπερμαγγανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>permanganate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπερ- (yper-) + μαγγάνιο + -ικός, calque of French permanganate where the prefix per- has been paretymologically attributed as υπερ- (yper-).</div><b>υπερνατριαιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural υπερνατριαιμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) hypernatremia</div><b>υπερνικώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to overcome, to surmount, to get over</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑπερνικῶ. By surface analysis, υπερ- (yper-) + νικώ.</div><b>υπεροπτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπεροπτική, neuter υπεροπτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>haughty, disdainful, supercilious</div><b>υπεροσμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hyperosmia</div><b>υπεροχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) advantage (over)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) superiority, supremacy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπεροχή, from ὑπερέχω, from ὑπερ (huper, “above, more”) + ἔχω.</div><b>υπερρεαλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) surrealism (the artistic movement and aesthetic philosophy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπερ- (yper-, “super-, ultra-, sur-”) + ρεαλισμός, calque of French surréalisme.</div><b>υπερσαχάριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>super-Saharan</div><b>υπερσιτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural υπερσιτισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) feeding up, fattening, overfeeding</div><b>υπερσυντέλικος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπερσυντέλικοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pluperfect, pluperfect tense, past perfect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑπερσυντέλικος. By surface analysis, υπερ- (yper-) + συντελικός.</div><b>υπερσυνταγογράφηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπερσυνταγογραφήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) overprescribing, overprescription</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπερ- (yper-) + συνταγογράφηση.</div><b>υπερτιμάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://υπερτιμώ">υπερτιμώ</a></div><b>υπερτιμημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine υπερτιμημένη, neuter υπερτιμημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. overvalued, overpriced</div>
  <div class='def'>2. overrated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of υπερτιμώμαι, passive voice of υπερτιμώ. Morphologically, υπερ- (yper-, “hyper-”) + τιμημένος.</div><b>υπερτιμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to overrate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to overprice, overvalue</div><b>υπερτουρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπερτουρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) overtourism</div><b>υπερτροφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπερτροφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hypertrophy (increase in the size of an organ due to swelling of the individual cells)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υπερτροφία</b> μυός - muscular hypertrophy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. overnourishment, overnutrition, hyperalimentation (excessive intake of nutrients)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) overdevelopment</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ατροφία του κοινωνικού κράτους αντιστοιχεί στην <b>υπερτροφία</b> του ποινικού κράτους. - The lack of development of the welfare state corresponds to the overdevelopment of the criminal state.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French hypertrophie, a French-coined term adopted into Greek, equivalent to υπερ- (yper-, “over, hyper-”) + τροφ(ή), “food, nourishment”) + -ία. First attested 1856.</div><b>υπερυψώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to raise something above a certain level, above normal</div><b>υπερφορτώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be overloaded</div><b>υπερφορτώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to overload</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to surcharge</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to overflow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ὑπερφορτώνω. By surface analysis, υπερ- (yper-, “over”) + φορτώνω.</div><b>υπερφυσικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπερφυσικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the supernatural (beings and events collectively)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://υπερφυσικός">υπερφυσικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://υπερφυσικός">υπερφυσικός</a></div><b>υπερφυσικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπερφυσική, neuter υπερφυσικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supernatural, unearthly, superhuman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ὑπερφυσικός.</div><b>υπερχείλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπερχειλίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. overflow, flood (material which has overflowed)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. overflow, flume (outlet for excess water to escape)</div><b>υπερχειλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to overflow, flood</div><b>υπερωικοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπερωικοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>velarization</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπερωικός + -ποίηση.</div><b>υπερωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπερωική, neuter υπερωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>velar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπερώα + -ικός. Calque of French vélaire.</div><b>υπερωκεάνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ocean liner</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπερωκεάνιος; by surface analysis, υπερ- (yper-) + ωκεάνιο. The modern sense of &quot;ocean liner&quot; is a semantic loan from French transocéanien.</div><b>υπερωρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπερωρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>overtime work, after-hours work</div><b>υπερόχως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) wonderfully, greatly, fantastically, superbly</div><b>υπερώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπερώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypernym</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English hypernym.</div><b>υπευθυνότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. responsibility</div>
  <div class='def'>2. accountability</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπεύθυν(ος)) + -ότητα.</div><b>υπεύθυνος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπεύθυνη, neuter υπεύθυνο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>responsible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπεύθῡνος.</div><b>υπηκοότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) citizenship, nationality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπήκοος + -ότητα. First attested 1831.</div><b>υπηνέμως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leeward</div><b>υπηρέτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπηρέτες, feminine υπηρέτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>domestic servant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑπηρέτης.</div><b>υπηρέτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπηρέτριες, masculine υπηρέτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>domestic servant</div><b>υπηρεσία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπηρεσίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. service</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>υπηρεσίες</b> του στην ανθρωπότητα - his services to humanity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. service (the army, police, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ευρωπαϊκή Αστυνομική <b>Υπηρεσία</b> - European Police Service (Europol)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. service (to the public)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υπηρεσίες</b> υγείας από το Εθνικό Σύστημα Υγείας (ΕΣΥ) - health services from the National Health Service (NHS)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (dated) service (as a housemaid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπηρεσία.</div><b>υπηρετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to serve (to provide a service)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑπηρετῶ, from ὑπηρέτης + -ω, with semantic loan from French servir. By surface analysis, υπηρέτης + -ω (-o).</div><b>υπλγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://υπίλαρχος">υπίλαρχος</a></div><b>υπλγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://υπολοχαγός">υπολοχαγός</a></div><b>υπναράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπναράδες, feminine υπναρού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sleepyhead (someone who sleeps a lot)</div><b>υπνοβάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (feminine υπνοβάτισσα or υπνοβάτις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sleepwalker, somnambulist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French somnambule. From ύπν(ος) (“sleeping”) + -ο- + -βάτης (“walker”).</div><b>υπνοδωμάτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπνοδωμάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bedroom</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of German Schlafzimmer. By surface analysis, ύπν(ος), “sleep”) + -ο- (-o-) + δωμάτιο.</div><b>υπνχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://υποναύαρχος">υποναύαρχος</a></div><b>υπνωτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπνωτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dormitory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπνώ(νω)) + -τήριο, a calque of French dortoir.</div><b>υποαλλεργικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποαλλεργική, neuter υποαλλεργικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypoallergenic (containing few allergens)</div><b>υποατομικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποατομική, neuter υποατομικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subatomic</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υποατομικό</b> σωματίδιο - subatomic particle</div><b>υποβάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to submit, to put (to present for approval, consideration, assessment, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to subject (to cause to undergo a particular experience, especially one that is unpleasant or unwanted)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to undergo</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to go to, to put oneself to (bother, trouble)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποβάλλω with semantic loan from French soumettre and suggérer.</div><b>υποβαστάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to support (place a physical support upon something)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to support, help a person to stand or to walk</div><b>υποβλέπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to distrust, look askance at</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι συνάδελφοί του τον <b>υποβλέπουν</b>. - His colleagues look at him askance.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to have one&#x27;s eye on (to seek or to pursue)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υποβλέπουν</b> τη θέση της. - They have their eye on her position.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῠ̔ποβλέπω.</div><b>υποβλήθηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>first-person singular simple past of υποβάλλομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://υποβάλλω">υποβάλλω</a></div><b>υποβλήθηκε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular simple past of υποβάλλομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://υποβάλλω">υποβάλλω</a></div><b>υποβληθεί</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. inflection of υποβάλλομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://υποβάλλω">υποβάλλω</a>:; third-person singular dependent passive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. inflection of υποβάλλομαι, the passive of <a href="bword://υποβάλλω">υποβάλλω</a>:; passive nonfinite form</div><b>υποβοηθούμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine υποβοηθούμενη, neuter υποβοηθούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who is being assisted, abetted</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υποβοηθούμενη</b> αναπνοή - assisted breathing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of υποβοηθούμαι, passive voice of υποβοηθώ (“assist”). Morphologically, υπο- (ypo-, “under-”) + βοηθούμενος, literally: assisted, sustained from underneath. Also see the ancient ὑποβοηθούμενος.</div><b>υποβολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποβολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. submission (the act of submitting or giving: documents, proposals, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. suggestion (implicit communication of ideas to the mind)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποβολή.</div><b>υποβρυχιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποβρυχιακή, neuter υποβρυχιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>submarine</div><b>υποβρύχιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποβρύχια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>submarine</div><b>υποβρύχιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποβρύχιη, neuter υποβρύχιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>underwater, submerged, undersea</div><b>υπογεννητικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>low birth rate, subreplacement birth rate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπο- (ypo-) + γεννητικότητα, a calque of English low birth rate.</div><b>υπογλυκαιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hypoglycemia</div><b>υπογλυχαιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://υπογλυκαιμία">υπογλυκαιμία</a></div><b>υπογράμμιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπογραμμίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. underline, underlining, underscore (line underneath text)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) emphasising, stressing</div><b>υπογράφομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be signed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ὑπογράφομαι</div><b>υπογράφω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to sign, put signature to</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to add byline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ῠ̔πογρᾰ́φω and semantic loan from French souscrire and signer. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-, “under”) + γράφω.</div><b>υπογραμμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to underline, underscore</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to stress, emphasise</div><b>υπογραμμιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπογραμμιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>highlighter, highlighter pen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπογραμμ- (ypogramm-, “to underline, to highlight”) + -ιστής, calque of English highlighter.</div><b>υπογραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπογραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. signature</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) agreement</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπογράφω. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-) + γραφή.</div><b>υποδέχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to soak up, take (something falling or flowing from above)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η δεξαμενή αυτή σχεδιάστηκε για να <b>υποδέχεται</b> τα λύματα. - This water collector was designed to receive the drainage.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to welcome, greet, receive (to affirm the arrival of someone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας <b>υποδέχτηκαν</b> πολύ θερμά. - We were welcomed very warmly.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to greet, welcome, receive (accept information either positively or negatively)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς <b>υποδέχτηκε</b> τα νέα ότι πέθανε ο πατέρας του; - How did he greet the news that his father died?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποδέχομαι, equivalent to υπο- (ypo-, “under”) + δέχομαι.</div><b>υποδήλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποδηλώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>connotation (a meaning of a word or phrase that is suggested or implied)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποδήλωσις. By surface analysis, υποδηλώνω + -ση (-si).</div><b>υποδαυλίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to foment, to stir up (to incite)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπο- (ypo-) + δαυλ(ός)) + -ίζω, a calque of French attiser.</div><b>υποδεκάμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποδεκάμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. decimetre (10 cm)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rule, ruler (straightedge for measuring and drawing, usually about 20cm long)</div><b>υποδεκανέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποδεκανείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lance corporal (the NATO military rank OR-3)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπο- (ypo-, “under”) + δεκανέας (First attested 1833)</div><b>υποδηματοποιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υποδηματοποιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a formal synonym of of <a href="bword://τσαγκάρης">τσαγκάρης</a>: shoemaker, cobbler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποδηματοποιός.</div><b>υποδιαστολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποδιαστολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) The hypodiastole, a mark used in scripta continua Classical and Byzantine Greek to note that a series of letters should be read as two words rather than all together, now identical to the comma.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. The Greek decimal point, identical to the comma.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (uncommon) synonym of <a href="bword://κόμμα">κόμμα</a> in its other uses.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποδιαστολή, from the combining form of ὑπό + διαστολή.</div><b>υποδιεύθυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποδιευθύνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. subdirectorate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. position of subdirector</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπο- (ypo-) + διεύθυνση.</div><b>υποδομή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποδομές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. infrastructure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. foundations, substructure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποδομή. Semantic loan from French infrastructure.</div><b>υποδουλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to enslave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek υποδουλώ. By surface analysis, υπόδουλ(ος)) + -ώνω.</div><b>υποδοχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποδοχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. welcome, reception (greeting of a guest or newcomer)</div>
  <div class='ex'>θερμή <b>υποδοχή</b> - warm welcome</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) reception, reception desk (room or desk where guests are received)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Περάστε στην <b>υποδοχή</b>, παρακαλώ. - Go on through to the reception, please.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. reception (the way something is received)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπήρξε ψυχρή <b>υποδοχή</b> για το νέο του βιβλίο. - His new book was coldly received.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. port, slot (opening into which a plug or other connecting part is designed to fit)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υποδοχή</b> για USB - USB port</div>
  <div class='def'>5. pass, reception (any form of attack)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υποδοχή</b> τής μπάλας ύστερα από σερβίς - passing the ball after serving</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποδοχή, from Ancient Greek ὑποδέχομαι.</div><b>υποδούλωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. enslavement</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subjugation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υποδουλώ(νω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>υποδύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of actors) to play a role</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to pretend to be</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῠ̔ποδύομαι (“get under; put on a character -as of actors&#x27; masks-”), active of ῠ̔ποδύω or ῠ̔ποδύνω. Morphologically, from υπο- (“under”) + δύομαι of active δύω.</div><b>υποείδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποείδη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subspecies</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπο- (ypo-, “sub”) + είδος</div><b>υποζύγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποζύγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beast of burden; draught animal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. beast of burden; pack animal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποζύγιον. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-) + ζυγ(ός), “yoke”) + -ιο (-io).</div><b>υποθάλαμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hypothalamus</div><b>υποθάλπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be secretly supported</div><b>υποθάλπω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to give refuge, support — (especially in secret)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to incite, foster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποθᾰ́λπω. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-, “under”) + θάλπω.</div><b>υποθέτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to suppose, guess</div><b>υποθήκευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypothecation (use of property as security for a loan)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested around 1848. From υποθηκεύω, from the Ancient Greek ὑποθήκη.</div><b>υποθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mortgage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. archive moral advice, legacy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποθήκη.</div><b>υποθερμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hypothermia (low temperature)</div><b>υποθετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποθετική, neuter υποθετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hypothetical</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (conditional) conditional</div><b>υποθηκευμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine υποθηκευμένη, neuter υποθηκευμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mortgaged</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of υποθηκεύομαι, passive voice of υποθηκεύω.</div><b>υποθηκεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to mortgage</div><b>υποκατάστημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποκαταστήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A branch, a subsidiary location.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επίσης, είναι σύνηθες, κυρίως σε <b>υποκαταστήματα</b> που έχουν περιορισμένη ζήτηση για Ενοικίαση με Οδηγό, οι οδηγοί να στέλνονται από την Έδρα της Επιχείρησης στα αρχεία της οποίας βρίσκονται και τα αποδεικτικά για τις προϋποθέσεις που πρέπει να πληρούνται. - It is also common, especially in branches that have limited demand for Rental with a Driver, for drivers to be sent from the Company&#x27;s Headquarters, in whose files are also the evidence of the conditions that must be met.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπο- (ypo-, “under, below”) + κατάστημα.</div><b>υποκείμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποκείμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (subjective) subject</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>υποκείμενο</b> του ρήματος βρίσκεται πάντα σε ονομαστική πτώση. - The subject of the verb is always in the nominative case.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. An individual person as a unit within a survey, a statistic, etc.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (diminutive) vile, disgusting or suspicious person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποκείμενον.</div><b>υποκειμενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποκειμενική, neuter υποκειμενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (subjective) subjective</div>
  <div class='def'>2. relating to the subject of a sentence</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υποκείμενο + -ικός.</div><b>υποκειμενικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) subjectivity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υποκειμενικός + -ότητα, calque of French subjectivité.</div><b>υποκλέπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be stolen cunningly, filched</div><b>υποκλέπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to filch, pilfer, purloin, abstract (steal cunningly)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to earwig, eavesdrop (especially telephone conversations)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποκλέπτω. By surface analysis, υπο- (“under”) + κλέπτω, ancient form of κλέβω.</div><b>υποκλοπή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποκλοπές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. interception (act of intercepting something, especially deceptively or surreptitiously)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. eavesdropping (the interception of electronic communication)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. intercept</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπο(κλέπτω)) + -κλοπή, modelled on Ancient Greek κλέπτω → κλοπή.</div><b>υποκορισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υποκορισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (the formation of diminutive terms)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hypocorism (creation of terms of endearment, like pet names, with an emotional or familiar nuance of diminutives)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποκορισμός.</div><b>υποκοριστικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://υποκοριστικός">υποκοριστικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://υποκοριστικός">υποκοριστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποκοριστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive (may be strictly diminutive or in an emaotional diminution, also with ironic, derogatory senses)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι Έλληνες χρησιμοποιούν πάρα πολλά <b>υποκοριστικά</b> όταν μιλούν. Όπως: &quot;το απογεματάκι θα πιούμε το καφεδάκι μας και θα φάμε και γλυκάκια&quot;. - The Greeks use plenty (very many) diminutives whey they speak. Like: &quot;in the little-evening we shall drink our little coffee and we&#x27;ll also eat little sweets.</div><b>υποκοριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποκοριστική, neuter υποκοριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (diminutive) diminutive</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, uncommon) hypocoristic</div>
  <div class='ex'>note: for hypocoristic see χαϊδευτικός (chaïdeftikós), also θωπευτικός (thopeftikós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑποκοριστικός; pet name”).</div><b>υποκρίτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποκρίτριες, masculine υποκριτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. actress, (female) actor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hypocrite (female)</div><b>υποκρισία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hypocrisy, insincerity</div><b>υποκριτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υποκριτές, feminine υποκρίτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. actor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hypocrite, dissembler</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ὑποκριτής.</div><b>υποκρύπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to conceal, dissemble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποκρύπτω. Morphologically, from υπο- (“uner”) + the ancient κρύπτω.</div><b>υποκύπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to succumb</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to die</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υπέκυψε</b> στα τραύματά του. - He succumbed to his injuries.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποκύπτω. Morphologically, from υπο- (“under”) + κύπτω (“bend”).</div><b>υπολήπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to esteem, respect</div><b>υπολείπομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to lack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπολείπομαι of ὑπολείπω. Morphologically, from υπο- (“under”) + λείπω (“I am absent”).</div><b>υπολογίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be calculated, estimated</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα έξοδα είχαν <b>υπολογιστεί</b>, αλλά η επιπλέον δαπάνη δεν <b>υπολογίστηκε</b>. - The expenses were calculated, but the extra cost has not been calculated.</div><b>υπολογίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to calculate, compute</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to reckon, estimate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to gauge, estimate</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to think highly of someone</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπολογίζομαι (“take under consideration”). By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-, “hypo-, under”) + λογίζομαι. Also, semantic loan from French calculer.</div><b>υπολογίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπολογίστριες, masculine υπολογιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>selfish person, self-seeker</div><b>υπολογισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine υπολογισμένη, neuter υπολογισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>calculated, estimated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of υπολογίζομαι, passive voice of υπολογίζω (“I calculate”).</div><b>υπολογισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπολογισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. calculation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. computation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. estimate, guess</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑπολογισμός with semantic loan from French calcul. By surface analysis, υπολογίζω + -μός.</div><b>υπολογιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπολογιστές, feminine υπολογίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. self-seeker, selfish person (person who calculates before acting)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. calculator, computer</div>
  <div class='ex'>ηλεκτρονικός <b>υπολογιστής</b> - electronic computer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French calculateur (“calculating person”), after the English electronic calculator using the Greek υπολογίζω.</div><b>υπολογιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπολογιστική, neuter υπολογιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>computational</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>υπολογιστική</b> μηχανή - the computational machine</div><b>υπολοχαγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υπολοχαγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the rank of lieutenant in the British and US armies with the NATO grade OF-1</div><b>υπομένω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to endure (to suffer patiently)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑπομένω. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-) + μένω.</div><b>υπομειδίαμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπομειδιάματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sly smile, inward smile</div><b>υπομειδιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to smile slyly or inwardly</div><b>υπομετάφραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural υπομεταφράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) undertranslation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-, “under-, hypo-”) + μετάφραση.</div><b>υπομονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) patience, forbearance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπομονή.</div><b>υπομονετικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://υπομονετικός">υπομονετικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patiently</div><b>υπομονετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπομονετική, neuter υπομονετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>patient, calm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπομονή.</div><b>υποναύαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποναύαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rear admiral in the Royal Navy and in the US navy, a rank with the NATO grade OF-7</div>
  <div class='def'>2. rear admiral, a rank in the Hellenic Coast Guard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπο- (ypo-, “sub-”) + ναύαρχος.</div><b>υπονομευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπονομευτική, neuter υπονομευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subversive, seditious,</div><b>υπονοώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to imply, insinuate, suggest</div><b>υποξείδιο του αζώτου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) nitrous oxide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, υποξείδιο + του (tou, “of”) + άζωτο.</div><b>υποπλοίαρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποπλοίαρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the rank of lieutenant in the Royal Navy and US navy with the NATO grade OF-2</div><b>υποπτέραρχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποπτέραρχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-7; air vice-marshal in the RAF</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-7; major general in the USAF</div><b>υποπτεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to suspect, be suspicious of</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὑποπτεύω + -ομαι (-omai, suffix for passive verbs)</div><b>υποπυραγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποπυραγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fire lieutenant</div>
  <div class='ex'>Older spelling: ὑποπυραγός</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπο- (ypo-) + πυραγός &lt; πυρ- (pyr-) + -αγός. See υπό, πυρ (pyr) and άγω.</div><b>υποπόδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποπόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. footstool</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hassock</div><b>υποσαχάριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποσαχάρια, neuter υποσαχάριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sub-Saharan</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υποσαχάρια</b> Αφρική - sub-Saharan Africa</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπο- (ypo-, “sub, under”) + σαχάριος</div><b>υποσιτισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural υποσιτισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) malnutrition, undernourishment</div><b>υποσκάπτομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be undermined</div><b>υποσκάπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to undermine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to dig under</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποσκάπτω. By surface analysis, υπο- (“under”) and the ancient σκάπτω.</div><b>υποσμηναγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποσμηναγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-1; flying officer in the RAF</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an air force rank with the NATO grade OF-1; first lieutenant in the USAF</div><b>υποσπαδίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υποσπαδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypospadias (birth defect of the male urethra that involves an abnormally placed urinary meatus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποσπαδίας, from ὑπό + σπάω.</div><b>υποστήριξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποστηρίξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. support (something which supports)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. support, backing (financial, moral or other help)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. support (answers to questions and resolution of problems regarding something sold)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υποστήριξη</b> πελατών - customer support</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek ὑποστήρι(ξις)) + -ξη (-xi). From the Hellenistic verb ὑποστηρίζω, stem ὑποστηρικ- + -σις (-sis) &gt; -ξις (-xis) &gt; modern -ξη (-xi). Also see υπο- (ypo-, “under”) and στήριξη.</div><b>υποστηρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to support, back, bolster</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to assert, claim</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to support, root for</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑποστηρίζω, with semantic loan from French soutenir, appuyer, as well as semantic loan from English support. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-) + στηρίζω.</div><b>υποστηρικτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υποστηρικτές, feminine υποστηρίκτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. supporter, backer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. advocate, proponent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υποστηρίζω + -τής.</div><b>υποστιγμή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποστιγμές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the lower point or comma from early Greek texts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπο- (ypo-, “below, underneath”) + στιγμή</div><b>υποστράτηγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποστράτηγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>major general, an army rank with the NATO grade OF-7</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ῠ̔ποστρᾰ́τηγος m. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-, “sub-”) + στρατηγός.</div><b>υποσυνείδητα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://υποσυνείδητος">υποσυνείδητος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subconsciously</div><b>υποσυνείδητο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://υποσυνείδητος">υποσυνείδητος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://υποσυνείδητος">υποσυνείδητος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subconscious</div><b>υποσυνείδητος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποσυνείδητη, neuter υποσυνείδητο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subconscious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπο- (ypo-) + συνείδη(ση)) + -τος (-tos), possibly a calque of English subconscious.</div><b>υποτάσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to subjugate</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to subordinate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑποτάσσω with semantic loan from French subordonner.</div><b>υποτίθεται</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular present of υποτίθεμαι, the passive of <a href="bword://υποθέτω">υποθέτω</a></div><b>υποτακτική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποτακτικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subjunctive mood</div><b>υποτελής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποτελής, neuter υποτελές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. subjugated, subordinate (controlled by authority)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. submissive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποτελής.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποτελείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vassal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποτελής.</div><b>υποτιμώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to depreciate, to bring down, to mark down (prices)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to underestimate, to underrate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποτιμῶ, contracted form of ὑποτιμάω, with semantic loan from French déprécier and dévaluer.</div><b>υποτροφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποτροφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scholarship (financial award to student)</div><b>υποτυπώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποτυπώδης, neuter υποτυπώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>basic, undeveloped, embryonic</div><b>υπουργείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπουργεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ministry, department</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υπουργείο</b> Εσωτερικών - Ministry of the Interior</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπουργός + -είο.</div><b>υπουργικό συμβούλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cabinet, ministerial committee</div>
  <div class='ex'>το άτυπο υπουργικό συμβούλιο για τις αποκρατικοποιήσεις - the informal cabinet for privatisation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υπουργικό + συμβούλιο</div><b>υπουργικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπουργική, neuter υπουργικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ministerial</div>
  <div class='ex'>υπουργικός μισθός (ministerial salary)</div><b>υπουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υπουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minister, secretary (politician who heads a ministry or department)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>υπουργός</b> υγείας παραιτήθηκε μετά το σκάνδαλο. - The health minister resigned after the scandal.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπουργός, from ὑπό + -ουργός).
The modern sense is a semantic loan from Italian ministro.</div><b>υποφέρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to suffer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to suffer, to endure (to experience as pain or discomfort)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to bear, to endure, to stand (to tolerate)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑποφέρω. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-) + φέρω.</div><b>υποχρέωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποχρεώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>obligation, duty, responsibility, liability</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κανείς δεν έχει την <b>υποχρέωση</b> να κάνει αυτό που δεν θέλει. - Nobody has an obligation to do what they don&#x27;t want to.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Noun formed from Ancient Greek adjective ὐπόχρεος</div><b>υποχρεωτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://υποχρεωτικός">υποχρεωτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compulsorily, mandatorily, obligatorily</div><b>υποχρεωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποχρεωτική, neuter υποχρεωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. obligatory, compulsory, mandatory</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην Ελλάδα, είναι <b>υποχρεωτικό</b> να πας στον στρατό. - In Greece, it is compulsory to join the army.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. obliging, helpful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὐπόχρεος.</div><b>υποχρεώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to oblige, force, obligate, compel</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο νόμος μας <b>υποχρεώνει</b> να τον φυλακίσουμε. - The law obliges us to imprison him.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to oblige, indebt, cause someone to owe a favour</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με βοήθησε τόσο πολύ! Έχω <b>υποχρεωθεί</b> σ’ αυτόν τον καλό άνθρωπο! - He helped me so much! I am indebted to this good fellow!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Verb formed from the adjective υπόχρεος, from υπο- (upo-) + χρέος), + -ώνω (suffix for verbs). For sense &quot;oblige&quot;, calque of French obliger.</div><b>υποχωρητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποχωρητική, neuter υποχωρητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (relational) retreat, withdrawal (attributive)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. yielding, compliant (docile, or inclined to give way to pressure; willing to do what someone wants)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. regressive</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υποχωρητική</b> αφομοίωση - regressive assimilation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑποχωρητικός with semantic adaptation to the modern meanings of υποχωρώ and semantic loan from French régressif in the phonetic sense. By surface analysis, υποχωρη-) + -τικός.</div><b>υποχωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to retreat, to withdraw, to fall back</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>υποχωρώ</b> από τις υποσχέσεις - to withdraw from commitments</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to give way (to collapse or break under physical stresses)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to give in, to give way, to relent (to yield to pressure, persuasion, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to back down, to back off (to become less aggressive or insistent)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑποχωρέω. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-) + χωρώ.</div><b>υποχόνδριος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποχόνδρια, neuter υποχόνδριο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hypochondriac, hypochondriacal</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υποχόνδριοι, feminine υποχόνδρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hypochondriac</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hypochondrium</div><b>υποχώρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποχωρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. retreat, fall back, withdrawal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subsidence</div>
  <div class='def'>3. decline</div><b>υποψήφιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υποψήφια, neuter υποψήφιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(prospective) prospective</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υποψήφιοι, feminine υποψήφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>candidate (in election or for position)</div><b>υποψία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποψίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suspicion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὑποψία.</div><b>υποψηφιότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποψηφιότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>candidacy, candidature</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> υποψήφιος + -ότητα. First attested 1854.</div><b>υποψιάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to suspect</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον <b>υποψιάζομαι</b> ότι λέει ψέματα. - I suspect him of lying.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υποψία + -άζομαι. The active form, υποψιάζω, was back-formed from this verb.</div><b>υποψιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cause someone to become suspicious</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η συμπεριφορά του με <b>υποψίασε</b> πριν κλέψει το πορτοφόλι. - His behaviour made me suspicious before he stole the wallet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neologism. Active form created from the deponent verb υποψιάζομαι.</div><b>υπσγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://υποσμηναγός">υποσμηναγός</a></div><b>υπτίως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ύπτια">ύπτια</a></div><b>υπτχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://υποπτέραρχος">υποπτέραρχος</a></div><b>υπχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://υποπλοίαρχος">υποπλοίαρχος</a></div><b>υπό κατάληψη</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) under occupation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πολλά σχολεία τελούσαν <b>υπό κατάληψη</b> με αίτημα να μειωθεί ο αριθμός μαθητών μέσα στις τάξεις.</div><b>υπόγεια ύδατα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://υπόγειο">υπόγειο</a> ύδατο</div><b>υπόγειο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπόγεια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>basement, cellar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the adjective υπόγειος, from υπό- + γη (gi, “ground, earth”).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://υπόγειος">υπόγειος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://υπόγειος">υπόγειος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the adjective υπόγειος, from υπό- + γη (gi, “ground, earth”).</div><b>υπόγειο ύδατο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>groundwater, ground water</div><b>υπόγειος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπόγεια, neuter υπόγειο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. underground, metro</div>
  <div class='def'>2. underground, subterranean, hypogeous, hypogean, hypogeal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὑπόγειος, from Ancient Greek ὑπόγαιος, from ὑπό + γαῖα/γῆ.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπόγειοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>underground railway, subway</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὑπόγειος, from Ancient Greek ὑπόγαιος, from ὑπό + γαῖα/γῆ.</div><b>υπόδειγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποδείγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. model, exemplar, paragon (something fit to be imitated)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. specimen (an individual instance that represents a class; an example)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. template, pattern (a generic model from which other objects are based or derived)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑπόδειγμα.</div><b>υπόδειξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>recommendation, suggestion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπόδειξις.</div><b>υπόδημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υποδήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) shoe, shoewear, boot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπόδημα.</div><b>υπόδουλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπόδουλη, neuter υπόδουλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enslaved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὑπόδουλος.</div><b>υπόθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποθέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. business, matter, affair</div>
  <div class='def'>2. case</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hypothesis, conjecture</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υπόθεση</b> Αβογκάντρο - Avogadro&#x27;s hypothesis</div>
  <div class='def'>4. plot</div>
  <div class='def'>5. assumption</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπόθεσις.</div><b>υπόθετο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπόθετα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suppository</div><b>υπόκλιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποκλίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. obeisance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bow, curtsey</div><b>υπόκοσμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>underworld (part of society engaged in crime or vice)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υπό + κόσμος, calque of English underworld.</div><b>υπόλευκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπόλευκη, neuter υπόλευκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>off-white, whitish; not quite white.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ὑπόλευκος. By surface analysis, υπο- (ypo-, “hypo-, sub-, under-”) + λευκός.</div><b>υπόλογος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπόλογη, neuter υπόλογο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accountable, answerable, liable</div><b>υπόλοιπο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://υπόλοιπος">υπόλοιπος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://υπόλοιπος">υπόλοιπος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπόλοιπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>residue, remainder, rest</div><b>υπόλοιπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπόλοιπη, neuter υπόλοιπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>remaining (that which remains)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑπόλοιπος. By surface analysis, υπολείπομαι + -ος (-os).</div><b>υπόμνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπόμνησεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reminder</div><b>υπόνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπόνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suspicion</div><b>υπόπνοια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural υπόπνοιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) hypopnea</div><b>υπόστεγο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπόστεγα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>an awning shelter, shed, gazebo, or other structure with a roof and at least two sides open (without walls)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ύπόστεγον, nominative neuter of the adjective ὑπόστεγος.</div><b>υπόστηκα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>demotic, rare form of υπέστην, first-person singular simple past of <a href="bword://υφίσταμαι">υφίσταμαι</a></div><b>υπόσχεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υποσχέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>promise, vow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῠ̔πόσχεσῐς.</div><b>υπόσχομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to promise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὑπόσχομαι, from Ancient Greek ὑπισχνοῦμαι, itself from an older ὑπίσχομαι, an alternative form of ὑπισχνέομαι.</div><b>υπότιτλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υπότιτλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. subtitle (a heading below or after a title)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. subtitle (textual versions of the dialogue in films (and similar media such as television or video games), usually displayed at the bottom of the screen)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υπο- (ypo-) + τίτλος, a calque of French sous-titre.</div><b>υπότροφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υπότροφη, neuter υπότροφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to a scholar or a scholarship</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υπότροφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scholar (holder of a scholarship)</div><b>υπόψη</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of υπ’ όψιν (expression)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Metaplasm of υπ’ όψιν + -η (-i), feminine demotic ending, as in όψη.</div><b>υπώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υπώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hyponym</div><b>υπώρεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foot (of a mountain)</div><b>υστερία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υστερίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hysteria</div>
  <div class='def'>2. an instance of hysterical behavior</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑστερία.</div><b>υστεροβουλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υστεροβουλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ulterior motive (alternative reason for doing something, especially when concealed)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η καλοσύνη του και η καλή διάθεση έκρυβαν <b>υστεροβουλία</b>. - His kindness and good nature hid an ulterior motive.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Koine Greek ὑστεροβουλία, equivalent to υστερο- (ystero-, “after, subsequent”) + βουλή.</div><b>υστεροσκόπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υστεροσκόπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hysteroscope</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, υστερο- (ystero-, “womb, hystero-”) + -σκόπιο.</div><b>υστερόβουλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υστερόβουλη, neuter υστερόβουλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>self-seeking, insincere, selfish</div><b>υστερόγραφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υστερόγραφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>postscript, PS (footnote or addendum in correspondence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υστερό- + γραφή, a calque of New Latin postscriptum. First attested 1840.</div><b>υστερώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to lag, fall short, lack</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο μαθητής <b>υστερεί</b> στα μαθηματικά. - The student is lagging behind in mathematics.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὑστερῶ, contracted form of ὑστερέω.</div><b>υττέρβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ytterbium</div><b>υφάντρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υφάντρες, masculine υφαντής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. weaver</div>
  <div class='def'>2. European penduline tit, Remiz pendulinus (specifically)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. penduline tit (more generally: one of that family)</div><b>υφάντρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υφάντριες, masculine υφαντής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>weaver</div><b>υφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. texture, weave, grain (composition giving characteristics to material)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η υφή ξύλου (the grain of wood)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. structure, arrangement</div>
  <div class='ex'>η υφή βιολογικού υλικού (the structure of biological material)</div><b>υφήλιος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) world</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑφήλιος, from ὑπό and ἥλιος.</div><b>υφίσταμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to suffer, undergo</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to exist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑφίσταμαι, middle/passive form of ὑφίστημι.</div><b>υφαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to weave</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὑφαίνω, from Proto-Indo-European *webʰ-.</div><b>υφαλοκρηπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υφαλοκρηπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>continental shelf</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ύφαλ(ος)) + -ο- (-o-) + Ancient Greek κρηπ(ίς)) + -ίδα, a calque of Italian zoccolo continentale.</div><b>υφαντής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural υφαντές, feminine υφάντρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>weaver</div><b>υφασμάτινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υφασμάτινη, neuter υφασμάτινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cloth, fabric, textile (attributive) (made of woven material)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From υφασμάτ-) + -ινος (-inos).</div><b>υφυπουργός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural υφυπουργοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deputy minister, undersecretary, state secretary (minister ranking below the minister in charge of a department or ministry)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υφ- (yf-) + υπουργός, a calque of French sous-ministre.</div><b>υψίπεδο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υψίπεδα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>highland, high plateau, high plain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὑψίπεδον, neuter of ὑψίπεδος, with semantic loan from French haut plateau and German Hochebene.</div><b>υψηλό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://υψηλός">υψηλός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://υψηλός">υψηλός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υψηλά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high (pressure area)</div><b>υψηλόβαθμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υψηλόβαθμη, neuter υψηλόβαθμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high-ranking</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from υψηλ(ός)) + -ό- + βαθμ(ός)) + -ος (-os).</div><b>υψηλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine υψηλή, neuter υψηλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ψηλός">ψηλός</a></div><b>υψικάμινος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υψικάμινοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blast furnace</div><b>υψωμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine υψωμένη, neuter υψωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lifted, elevated (for objects, abstract nouns)</div>
  <div class='ex'>υψωμένη σημαία - a lifted/hoisted flag</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of υψώνομαι, passive voice of υψώνω.</div><b>υψόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υψόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. altimeter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. altitude</div><b>υψώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to elevate, raise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὑψ(ῶ)), contracted form of ὑψόω + -ώνω, from ὕψος n.</div><b>υἱός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://γιος">γιος</a> (gios)</div><b>φ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://φύλλο">φύλλο</a>: page, sheet</div><b>φ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Φ΄">Φ΄</a></div><b>φάγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phage, bacteriophage</div><b>φάκελλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φάκελλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φάκελος">φάκελος</a></div><b>φάκελος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φάκελοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. envelope (stationery)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. file, dossier</div>
  <div class='def'>3. directory, folder (file storage)</div><b>φάλαγγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάλαγγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. phalanx (finger or toe bone)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. battle order, phalanx</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φάλαγξ.</div><b>φάλαινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάλαινες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. whale</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, figuratively) fat woman, landwhale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φάλλαινα, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰleh₃- (“to inflate, blow, swell”), from *bʰel- (“to bloom”). Cognates include Ancient Greek φύλλον, Gothic 𐌱𐌻𐍉𐌼𐌰, Old English blōstm, blæd (“leaf”) (English blossom, blade) and Sanskrit फुलम् (phulam).</div><b>φάλλαινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάλλαινες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φάλαινα">φάλαινα</a></div><b>φάντασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαντάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ghost, phantasm, phantom, spectre (UK), specter (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek φάντασμα.</div><b>φάντης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φάντηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>jack</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian fante.</div><b>φάντης μπαστούνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φάντηδες μπαστούνι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. jack of spades</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) unexpected visitor</div><b>φάουλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) foul</div><b>φάρδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φάρδη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>width, breadth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek. By surface analysis, φαρδ(ύς)) + -ος (-os), analogously to παχύς → πάχος.</div><b>φάρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάρμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>farm (a place where agricultural and similar activities take place, especially the growing of crops or the raising of livestock)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English farm.</div><b>φάρμακο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φάρμακα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>medicine, medication</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φάρμακον. Doublet of φαρμάκι.</div><b>φάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lighthouse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek φάρος, from the Ancient Greek Φάρος, the island at Alexandria&#x27;s port and its famous lighthouse. Uncertain.</div><b>φάρσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάρσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prank, practical joke</div>
  <div class='def'>2. farce</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hoax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian farsa (“farce”), from French farce (“farce”).</div><b>φάρυγγας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φάρυγγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pharynx</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φάρυγξ.</div><b>φάση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. phase, stage (a distinguishable part of a sequence or cycle occurring over time)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. phase (period of play)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. phase</div>
  <div class='def'>4. phase</div>
  <div class='def'>5. phase</div>
  <div class='def'>6. phase</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>φάσεις</b> της Σελήνης - the phases of the Moon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φᾰ́σῐς, with semantic loan from English phase for the non-astronomical senses.</div><b>φάσκελο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάσκελα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) moutza, mountza (a rude, insulting and contemptuous gesture made with the hands)</div><b>φάσκιωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φασκιώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. swaddling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. swaddling clothes (material used for swaddling)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φασκιώνω, from Koine Greek φασκιῶ, from Koine Greek φασκία, from Latin fascia (“strip, ribbon”).</div><b>φάσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spectrum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) spectrum</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ellipsis of <a href="bword://ακουστικό">ακουστικό</a> <a href="bword://φάσμα">φάσμα</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. ellipsis of ηλεκτρομαγνητικό <a href="bword://φάσμα">φάσμα</a></div>
  <div class='def'>5. ghost, spectre (UK), specter (US) (lingering unpleasant prospect)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>φάσμα</b> του κομμουνισμού - the spectre of communism</div><b>φάσσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάσσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wood pigeon, Columba palumbus</div><b>φάτνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φάτνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a nativity scene tableau or its crib; crèche</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (formal, literary) manger, crib</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φάτνη.</div><b>φάτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) face</div>
  <div class='ex'>μια <b>φάτσα</b> μια ράτσα - one face one race</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) guy, person (usually of cunning, adept or dexterous person)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. façade (as of a building)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan fazza, and later from Italian faccia in sense 3.</div><b>φέγγει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular present of <a href="bword://φέγγω">φέγγω</a>; he/she/it shines, sheds light on</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (impersonal) third-person singular present of <a href="bword://φέγγω">φέγγω</a>; there is light</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (impersonal) third-person singular present of <a href="bword://φέγγω">φέγγω</a>; it dawns</div><b>φέγγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to shine</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (impersonal) to dawn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited form of Ancient Greek φέγγω, from φέγγος.</div><b>φέρετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φέρετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coffin, casket (oblong closed box for burying the dead)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο νεκροθάφτης καλύπτει το <b>φέρετρο</b> με χώμα. - The gravedigger is covering the coffin with dirt.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek φέρετρον, from Ancient Greek φέρετρον. See φέρνω.</div><b>φέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ferry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English ferry.</div><b>φέρι μποτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) ferry, ferryboat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English ferryboat, from ferry + boat.</div><b>φέρι-μποτ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φέριμποτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φέρμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) fermium</div><b>φέρνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to behave, bear oneself, carry oneself</div><b>φέρνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to bear, convey (carry something from one place to another)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to carry, fetch, bring (carry something here from somewhere else)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to bring, cause (consequences, results)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φέρω with addition of a nasal infix, from Proto-Hellenic *pʰérō, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰéreti. Doublet of φέρω.</div><b>φέρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to behave</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να <b>φέρεσαι</b> με σεβασμό στους ηλικιωμένους. - You will behave with respect towards the elderly.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be considered, to be reported</div>
  <div class='ex'>Συνελήφθησαν 200 άτομα που <b>φέρονται</b> να σχετίζονται με το αποτυχημένο πραξικόπημα. - 200 persons considered to be linked to the failed coup d&#x27;état were arrested.</div><b>φέρρυ μπωτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φέρρυ-μπωτ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φέρσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φερσίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>manner, behaviour (UK), behavior (US), demeanour (UK), demeanor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φέρομαι + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>φέρυ μποτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φέρυ-μποτ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φέρυμποτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φέσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fez</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unpaid debt</div>
  <div class='def'>3. drunken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish فس (fes) from Ottoman Turkish فاس (fas) from Arabic فَاس.</div><b>φέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. slice</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορείτε να φάτε μία <b>φέτα</b> άσπρου ψωμιού. - You can eat a slice of white bread.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. feta (cheese)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian fetta (“slice”).</div><b>φέτος</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>this year</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φέτος</b> θα πάμε ταξίδι στην Αμερική. - This year we&#x27;ll travel to America.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ἐφέτος, from Ancient Greek ἐφ’ ἔτος, from ἐφ’ (eph’, “on, at”) + ἔτος.</div><b>φήμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φήμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fame</div>
  <div class='def'>2. reputation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. rumour (UK), rumor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φήμη.</div><b>φίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. snake, serpent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) snake in the grass, devious person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φίδιν, ὀφίδιν, from Koine Greek ὀφίδιον; diminutive of Ancient Greek ὄφις, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁ógʷʰis, *h₃égʷʰis (“snake”). Cognate with Mariupol Greek фидъ.</div><b>φίλαθλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φίλαθλη, neuter φίλαθλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loving sport</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fan (in general or of a particular team)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (as a noun) person with these attributes</div><b>φίλαυτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φίλαυτη, neuter φίλαυτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) selfish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek φίλαυτος. By surface analysis, φίλος + αυτός.</div><b>φίλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φίλες, masculine φίλος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. female friend</div>
  <div class='def'>2. girlfriend</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://φίλος">φίλος</a></div><b>φίλντισι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) ivory</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فیل دیشی.</div><b>φίλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φίλοι, feminine φίλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. friend</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boyfriend</div>
  <div class='def'>3. acquaintance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *bʰil-o-s
Ancient Greek φῐ́λος
Greek φίλος
Inherited from Ancient Greek φῐ́λος, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰil-o-s.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φίλη, neuter φίλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. friendly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dear</div>
  <div class='def'>3. beloved (obsolete, literary)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Proto-Indo-European *bʰil-o-s
Ancient Greek φῐ́λος
Greek φίλος
Inherited from Ancient Greek φῐ́λος, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰil-o-s.</div><b>φίλτρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φίλτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. filter</div>
  <div class='ex'>καφές <b>φίλτρου</b> - filter coffee</div>
  <div class='def'>2. philtre, philter, charm, love potion</div>
  <div class='def'>3. love, especially parental love for child</div>
  <div class='def'>4. philtrum, filtrum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian filtro (“filter”), from Latin filtrum (“filter”).</div><b>φίμωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιμώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. muzzling (putting a muzzle on an animal&#x27;s mouth)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gagging (by using physical means)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) silencing, muzzling (without using physical means)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. phimosis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek φίμω(σις)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>φίσκα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>intensifier: packed, full</div><b>φα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) fa</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) F (fourth note or subdominant in the C major scale)</div><b>φαΐ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>food</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φάε το <b>φαΐ</b> σου! - Eat your food!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φαγί(ν)), from Ancient Greek φαγεῖν, infinitive of ἔφαγον, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰh₂g-, zero-grade of *bʰeh₂g- (“to divide, distribute”).</div><b>φαίνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to seem, appear</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Γιώργος <b>φαίνεται</b> άρρωστος σήμερα. - George seems ill today.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to appear</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πλοίο <b>φαίνεται</b> στον ορίζοντα. - The ship appears on the horizon.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φαίνομαι, mediopassive form of the active φαίνω.</div><b>φαβέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>favela</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Brazilian Portuguese favela.</div><b>φαβορί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) favourite (UK), favorite (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French favori.</div><b>φαβορίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαβορίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sideburns (in plural, chiefly US), sideboards (in plural, UK), sidelevers (in plural, Australia)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French favorites.</div><b>φαγάδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαγάδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diner, eatery</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φαγάς + -άδικο</div><b>φαγάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>glutton, greedy person, greedyguts</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φαγ (phag).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαγού, neuter φαγάδικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) greedy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φαγ (phag).</div><b>φαγητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαγητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. food (substance consumed by living organisms to sustain life)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meal (e.g.,: breakfast, dinner)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Derived from forms of Ancient Greek ἔφαγον.</div><b>φαγκοτίστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαγκοτίστες, masculine φαγκοτίστας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bassoonist</div><b>φαγκοτίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαγκοτίστες, feminine φαγκοτίστα or φαγκοτίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bassoonist</div><b>φαγκότο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαγκότα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bassoon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian fagotto.</div><b>φαγουρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(third person) to itch</div>
  <div class='ex'>Mε <b>φαγουρίζει</b> η πλάτη μου. - My back is itching (me).</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φαγούρ(α), “itch”) + -ίζω.</div><b>φαγούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαγούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>itch, itching</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φαγούρα. By surface analysis, from φαγ- (fag-), secondary stem of τρώω, + -ούρα.</div><b>φαγώσιμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>groceries, foodstuffs, provisions</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φαγώσιμος">φαγώσιμος</a></div><b>φαγώσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαγώσιμη, neuter φαγώσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>edible, eatable</div><b>φαινομενικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαινομενική, neuter φαινομενικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ostensible, superficial</div><b>φαιντανύλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fentanyl</div><b>φαινυλαλανίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phenylalanine</div><b>φαινυλαμίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαινυλαμίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Alternative name for <a href="bword://ανιλίνη">ανιλίνη</a></div><b>φαινόμενο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαινόμενα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phenomenon</div><b>φαιός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαιά, neuter φαιό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of colours and hues) dull (having no particular luster or brightness)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, formal) grey, ashy (having a color somewhere between white and black)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εδώ είναι Αττική, <b>φαιό</b> νταμάρι. - This is Attica, a dull grey quarry.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φαιός.</div><b>φακή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φακές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lentil (leguminous seed and grocery product)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dish made from lentils</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φακῆ, contracted of φακέα, from φακός.</div><b>φακίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φακίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>freckle</div><b>φακίρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φακίρηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fakir</div><b>φακλάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φακλάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, derogatory, literally) old whore, old hooker (elderly prostitute)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, offensive) heifer, cow, biffer (overweight and ugly woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε απ’ τη μέση, μωρή <b>φακλάνα</b>! - Get out of the way, you fat cow!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, offensive) tart, hussy (vulgar woman)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο μπαρ πηγαίνουν μόνο οι <b>φακλάνες</b>. - Only tarts go to bars.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology unknown.</div><b>φακός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φακοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lens</div>
  <div class='def'>2. torch (UK), flashlight (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φακός.</div><b>φακός επαφής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φακοί επαφής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>contact lens</div><b>φαλάφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαλάφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) falafel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic فلافل.</div><b>φαλάφελ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φαλάφα">φαλάφα</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic فلافل.</div><b>φαλαινοκέφαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαλαινοκέφαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shoebill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Balaeniceps, as φάλαινα + κεφαλή.</div><b>φαλαινοκαρχαρίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαλαινοκαρχαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whale shark (Rhincodon typus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, φάλαινα + καρχαρίας.</div><b>φαλακροκόρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαλακροκόρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cormorant (Phalacrocorax carbo including subspecies)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φαλακρός + κόρακας</div><b>φαλακρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαλακρή, neuter φαλακρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bald, hairless</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) bald, denuded, barren</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τώρα έχει μείνει ένα <b>φαλακρό</b> κτήμα, αλλά παλιά ήταν δίπατο σπίτι, είχε ελιές γύρω γύρω, κι ο γέρος μου, που κάποτε δεν ήταν καθόλου γέρος, έπαιζε μετ’ αδέρφια του μακριά γαϊδούρα. - Now a barren farm remains, but in the olden days it was a duplex house; it had olives all around and my old man, who at one time wasn&#x27;t old at all, used to play a kids&#x27; game with his brothers.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φαλακρός.</div><b>φαλακρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) baldness</div><b>φαλαρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαλαρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>coot (Fulica)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φᾰλᾱρῐ́ς.</div><b>φαλιρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) to go bankrupt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φαλίρω, from Italian fallire.</div><b>φαλλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phallic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φαλλός + -ικός</div><b>φαλλός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαλλοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phallus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φαλλός.</div><b>φαμίλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>family</div><b>φανάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φανάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. traffic lights</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σταμάτησε στο <b>φανάρι</b>. - He stopped at the traffic lights.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lantern, lamp, torch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φανάριν, φανάριον; see φάος.</div><b>φανέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φανέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flannel (soft cloth material)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. undershirt, vest, singlet (sleeveless or short-sleeved buttonless garment for the upper body, often with a low-cut neck)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. shirt, jersey</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan fanela, from Italian flanella, from French flanelle.</div><b>φαναράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαναράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diminutive of <a href="bword://φανάρι">φανάρι</a> n:; small light (on a vehicle)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diminutive of <a href="bword://φανάρι">φανάρι</a> n:; small lantern</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φανάρ(ι)) + -άκι.</div><b>φανατικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φανατική, neuter φανατικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fanatical</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fanatic</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bigoted, intolerant of ideologies other than one&#x27;s own</div>
  <div class='def'>4. bigot</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French fanatique.</div><b>φανελάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φανελάκι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diminutive of <a href="bword://φανέλα">φανέλα</a> f; vest, singlet, undershirt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diminutive of <a href="bword://φανέλα">φανέλα</a> f; vest (for a particular purpose, e.g. sports)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φανέλα + -άκι.</div><b>φανερά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>overtly, in plain view</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φανερός">φανερός</a></div><b>φανεροζωικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φανεροζωική, neuter φανεροζωικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Phanerozoic</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φανεροζωικός</b> αιώνας - Phanerozoic eon</div><b>φανερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φανερή, neuter φανερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. apparent, manifest (capable of being seen, or easily seen; open to view; visible to the eye)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. apparent, clear, evident, manifest, obvious, overt (easily apprehensible; plain; not obscure or hidden)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φανερός.</div><b>φανερώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to reveal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to manifest, to show</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φανερώνω, from Koine Greek φανερῶ, contracted form of Ancient Greek φᾰνερόω. By surface analysis, φανερ(ός)) + -ώνω.</div><b>φανκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) funk</div><b>φανουρόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φανουρόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fanouropita (a Greek oil-based sweet cake, traditionally Lenten and served on Saint Phanourios’ feast day on August 27, not containing any butter or eggs so that it can be eaten on holy fasting days)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From (Άγιος) Φανούρ(ιος) Fanoúr(ios), “Saint Phanourios”) + -ό- + πίτα.</div><b>φαντάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to imagine</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φαντάζομαι</b> έναν κόσμο χωρίς πολέμους, χωρίς φτώχεια. - I imagine a world without wars, without poverty.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to form an opinion, assume, expect</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φαντάζομαι</b> ότι θα πάρω αύξηση στο μισθό μου. - I assume that I will get a raise to my salary.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to guess so</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα παραιτηθεί ο διευθυντής; Έτσι <b>φαντάζομαι</b>, με τέτοιο σκάνδαλο που ξέσπασε. - Will the director resign? I guess so, with such a scandal beaking out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Τhis passive voice, from the ancient middle voice φᾰντᾰ́ζομαι, of active Ancient Greek φᾰντᾰ́ζω.</div><b>φαντάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (informal) give the impression. +accusative or + <a href="bword://σαν">σαν</a> (san, “like”); to seem, to look like</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπροστά σ’ αυτό, όλα <b>φαντάζουν</b> ασήμαντα. - Compared to that, everything seems trivial.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) give the impression. +accusative or + <a href="bword://σαν">σαν</a> (san, “like”); to make a sensation as, stand out</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν μπήκε στην αίθουσα, <b>φάνταζε σαν</b> σταρ του κινηματογράφου με τη χρυσή τουαλέτα της. - When she entered the room, she looked like a movie star in her gold gown.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (informal, intransitive) to impress, to catch the eye</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μου <b>φάνταξε</b> το κόκκινο φουστάνι, και το αγόρασα. - The red dress caught my eye, and I bought it.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φᾰντᾰ́ζω. Passive voice, from the ancient middle voice φᾰντᾰ́ζομαι.</div><b>φαντάρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαντάροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(familiar) infantryman, (infantry) soldier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φάντης, from Italian fante, with -άρος ending influenced by φανταρία, borrowed from Venetan fantaria.</div><b>φαντασία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαντασίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. imagination, fantasy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fantasia (when referring to specific title, it is written with capital Φ)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χρωματική <b>Φαντασία</b> και Φούγκα του Μπαχ - Chromatic Fantasia and Fugue by Bach</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φᾰντᾰσῐ́ᾱ, via Italian fantasia from the Ancient Greek.</div><b>φαντασίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαντασιώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. imagination</div>
  <div class='def'>2. daydream</div>
  <div class='def'>3. make-believe</div>
  <div class='def'>4. whim-wham</div><b>φανταστικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fantastically</div><b>φανταστικοί αριθμοί</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://φανταστικός">φανταστικός</a> <a href="bword://αριθμός">αριθμός</a></div><b>φανταστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φανταστική, neuter φανταστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. imaginary</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fantastical, fantastic</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fictional, fictionary</div>
  <div class='def'>4. unreal</div>
  <div class='def'>5. imaginary, not real</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φανταστικός</div><b>φανταστικός αριθμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φανταστικοί αριθμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>imaginary number</div><b>φανταχτερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φανταχτερή, neuter φανταχτερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>garish, flamboyant, flashy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an unattested verbal adjective *φανταχτός + -ερός, the former from a stem *φαντακ- (*fantak-), from the aorist form φάνταξα of φαντάζω.</div><b>φαξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) fax</div><b>φαράγγι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαράγγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gorge, canyon</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φαράγγι</b> της Σαμαριάς - Samaria Gorge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φᾰ́ρᾰγξ.</div><b>φαράκλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαράκλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of φαλάκρα</div><b>φαράντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) farad (SI unit of electrical capacitance)</div><b>φαρίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>farina, fine white flour</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian farina (“flour”).</div><b>φαρδαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to widen (to make wide or wider (mainly clothes))</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to widen (to become wide or wider)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φαρδαίνω. By surface analysis, φαρδ(ύς)) + -αίνω.</div><b>φαρδύς</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαρδιά, neuter φαρδύ)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of clothing) wide, broad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φαρδύς, from Koine Greek εὐφραδής, from Ancient Greek εὖ + φράζω.</div><b>φαρμάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαρμάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. venom, poison</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bitter substance</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sorrows, chagrin (mainly in the plural)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φαρμάκιν, from a diminutive *φαρμάκιον of the Ancient Greek φάρμακον. Also see the Ancient φαρμάκιον with sense &quot;mild purgative&quot;. Doublet of φάρμακο.</div><b>φαρμακείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαρμακεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pharmacy, drugstore (US), chemist&#x27;s (UK)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. apothecary</div><b>φαρμακερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαρμακερή, neuter φαρμακερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>venomous, poisonous</div><b>φαρμακευτική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://φαρμακευτικός">φαρμακευτικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pharmaceutics, pharmacy (science of medicinal substances)</div><b>φαρμακευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαρμακευτική, neuter φαρμακευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pharmaceutical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φαρμακευτικός with semantic loan from French pharmaceutique.</div><b>φαρμακολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pharmacology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French pharmacologie from φάρμακο + -λογία from Ancient Greek φάρμᾰκον + λόγος + -ῐ́ᾱ.</div><b>φαρμακοποιία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαρμακοποιίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pharmacopoeia</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pharmacy (occupation of a pharmacist)</div><b>φαρμακοποιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural φαρμακοποιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pharmacist, chemist (UK), druggist (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. apothecary (archaic)</div><b>φαρμακώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to poison</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to offend, embitter, hurt deeply</div><b>φαροφύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαροφύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lighthouse keeper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φαρο (faro, “lighthouse”) + φύλακας</div><b>φαρσί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of languages and subjects) fluently, correctly, perfectly</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μιλάει τα γερμανικά, <b>φαρσί</b>. - He/She speaks German perfectly.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish فارسی, from Persian فارسی “the language Farsi, Persian”.
:&quot;Among the old Turks of the Ottoman Empire […] Persian [was] the language of poetry and romantic literature.&quot; (and therefore eloquently spoken)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Farsi (the language)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα <b>φαρσί</b> είναι η περσική γλώσσα. - Farsi is the Persian language.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish فارسی, from Persian فارسی “the language Farsi, Persian”.
:&quot;Among the old Turks of the Ottoman Empire […] Persian [was] the language of poetry and romantic literature.&quot; (and therefore eloquently spoken)</div><b>φαρόπλοιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαρόπλοια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lightship, lightvessel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φάρος + πλοίο.</div><b>φασίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>process of general cleaning in a house</div><b>φασίολος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(used only for taxonomic terms) phaseolus, bean</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φασίολος</b> ο κοινός - Phaseolus vulgaris, the common bean</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek φασίολος, with its sense from Translingual Phaseolus.</div><b>φασίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φασίστες, feminine φασίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fascist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian fascista.</div><b>φασίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φασίστριες, masculine φασίστας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fascist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian fascista.</div><b>φασαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φασαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. noise, racket (loud, unwanted noise disturbance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ακούω τι λες με την <b>φασαρία</b> έξω. - I can&#x27;t hear you with all the noise outside.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, colloquial) trouble, commotion, bother (difficulty, problem, condition, or action contributing to such a situation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έγιναν πάλι <b>φασαρίες</b> στο κέντρο της Αθήνας. - There was trouble again in the centre of Athens.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian fesseria (“stupidity, nonsense”).</div><b>φασιανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φασιανοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pheasant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φασιανός.</div><b>φασισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φασισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fascism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian fascismo.</div><b>φασιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φασιστές, feminine φασίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fascist</div><b>φασιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φασιστική, neuter φασιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fascist</div><b>φασκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural φασκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) swaddling</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural, uncountable, usually) swaddling clothes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek φασκία, from Latin fascia (“strip, ribbon”).</div><b>φασκιώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to swaddle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φασκιώνω, from Koine Greek φασκιῶ + -ώνω (suffix for verbs), from φασκία, from Latin fascia (“strip, ribbon”).</div><b>φασκομηλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φασκομηλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sage</div><b>φασκωλόμυς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wombat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek φασκωλός + μῦς</div><b>φασκόμηλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φασκόμηλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sage (herb)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sage infusion</div><b>φασματίδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Phasmatidae, the phasmatid stick insects.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From translingual Phasmatidae, suffixed with -ίδες.</div><b>φασολάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bean soup</div><b>φασολάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φασολάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>green bean, string bean, French bean (bean of the common bean plant Phaseolus vulgaris)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φασόλ(ι)) + -άκι.</div><b>φασολιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φασολιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bean plant</div><b>φασούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φασούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://φασόλι">φασόλι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, idiomatic) an unexpected problem</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φασούλιν, diminutive of Hellenistic Koine Greek φάσουλος, from φασίολος, from Latin phaseolus, diminutive of phaselus, itself from Ancient Greek φάσηλος. Also see φασόλι.</div><b>φασσοπερίστερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φασσοπερίστερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stock dove, Columba oenas</div><b>φαστ φουντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) fast food</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English fast food.</div><b>φαστ-φουντ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of φαστ φουντ (fast fount)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English fast food.</div><b>φαστφουντάδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φαστφουντάδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fast food outlet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φαστ φουντ (fast fount, “fast food”) + -άδικο</div><b>φαστφούντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of φαστ φουντ (fast fount)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English fast food.</div><b>φασόλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φασόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bean, of the genus Phaseolus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φασόλιν &lt; *φασιόλιον, from diminutive of Hellenistic Koine Greek φασίολος, a reborrowing from Latin phaseolus, diminutive of phaselus, from Ancient Greek φάσηλος a bean probably of the Vigna genus. Also see φασούλι.</div><b>φασόλι κακάο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cocoa bean, seed of cacao tree</div><b>φασώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal, slang) to make out, snog (embrace and kiss passionately)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι δύο τους <b>φασωθήκανε</b> στο πάρτι. - The two of them made out at the party.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φάση + -ώνομαι.</div><b>φατνιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φατνιακή, neuter φατνιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. relating to tooth sockets</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alveolar (formed with the tip of the tongue touching the inner ridge of the gums of the upper front teeth)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φατνίο + -ακός. Calque of French alvéolaire.</div><b>φατρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φατρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>faction, party</div><b>φαυλότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) villainy, corruption</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) wickedness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φαυλότης, equivalent to φαύλος + -ότητα.</div><b>φαφλατάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαφλατάδες, feminine φαφλατού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blabbermouth, loudmouth</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν έχεις μυστικό, μην το λες του Κώστα. Είναι <b>φαφλατάς</b>. - If you&#x27;ve got a secret, don&#x27;t tell it to Kostas. He&#x27;s a blabbermouth.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chatterbox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φαφλατίζω (onomatopoeic).</div><b>φαφλατού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φαφλατούδες, masculine φαφλατάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blabbermouth, loudmouth</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν έχεις μυστικό, μην το λες της Ελένης. Είναι <b>φαφλατού</b>. - If you&#x27;ve got a secret, don&#x27;t tell it to Eleni. She&#x27;s a blabbermouth.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chatterbox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φαφλατίζω (onomatopoeic).</div><b>φαύλοι κύκλοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://φαύλος">φαύλος</a> <a href="bword://κύκλος">κύκλος</a></div><b>φαύλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φαύλη, neuter φαύλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wicked, unscrupulous, lacking morals</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φαῦλος.</div><b>φαύλος κύκλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φαύλοι κύκλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vicious circle</div>
  <div class='ex'>—«Πώς βγαίνει κανείς από τον <b>φαύλο κύκλο</b>;»
—«Δύσκολα. Τέτοια προβλήματα είναι δυσεπίλυτα: επειδή είναι κυκλικά, δεν ξέρεις από πού να αρχίσεις. - “How do you get out of a vicious circle?”
“With difficulty. Such problems are difficult to solve: since they&#x27;re cyclical, you don&#x27;t know where to begin.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. petitio principii</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of Medieval Latin circulus vitiōsus.</div><b>φεγγάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φεγγάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, vernacular) the Moon</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (countable) moon (satellite, (lunar) month)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. moonlight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φεγγάριν, from φεγγάριον; diminutive of Ancient Greek φέγγος.</div><b>φεγγαρόψαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φεγγαρόψαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ocean sunfish, mola mola (Mola mola)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, φεγγάρι + ψάρι + -ο (-o, neuter suffix).</div><b>φειδωλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φειδωλή, neuter φειδωλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sparing, thrifty, parsimonious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φειδωλός.</div><b>φειδώ</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) thriftiness, frugality, parsimony</div>
  <div class='ex'>με <b>φειδώ</b> - sparingly, thriftily, frugally, parsimoniously</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φειδώ.</div><b>φελλός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φελλοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cork (bottle stopper)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cork (material)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. fishing float</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, derogatory) simpleton, airhead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φελλός.</div><b>φελούκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>felucca</div><b>φεμινίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φεμινίστριες, masculine φεμινιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminist</div><b>φεμινισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φεμινισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French féminisme, from Latin femina (“woman”).</div><b>φεμινιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φεμινιστές, feminine φεμινίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French féministe.</div><b>φεμινιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φεμινιστική, neuter φεμινιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>feminist, feministic</div><b>φενέκ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) fennec (Vulpes zerda)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French fennec, from Arabic فَنَك (fanak), from Classical Persian فنک (fanak), ultimately from Proto-Mongolic *hünegen (“fox”).</div><b>φερ&#x27; ειπείν</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for example</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek φέρ&#x27; εἰπεῖν, from φέρω + εἰπεῖν.</div><b>φεριμπότ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φερκόν</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) Larnacan dialectal form of θερκόν Cypriot form of θεριό: beast, monster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From θερκόν with [θ] &gt; [f], from Byzantine Greek θεριό, inherited from Ancient Greek θηρίον</div><b>φερυμπότ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φέρι">φέρι</a> <a href="bword://μποτ">μποτ</a></div><b>φεστιβάλ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) festival</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French festival.</div><b>φετίχ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) fetish (something which is believed to possess, contain, or cause spiritual or magical powers)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) fetish (sexual attraction to or arousal at something sexual or nonsexual such as an object or a part of the body)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα τακούνια είναι το <b>φετίχ</b> του. - High heels are his fetish.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, indeclinable) fetish, obsession (irrational or abnormal fixation or preoccupation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει <b>φετίχ</b> με τα βιβλία. - He has an obsession with books.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French fétiche (“fetish”).</div><b>φετινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φετινή, neuter φετινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>this year&#x27;s, of this year (occurring or concerning the present year)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>φετινό</b> φεστιβάλ θα είναι μεγαλύτερο από το περσινό. - This year&#x27;s festival will be bigger than last year&#x27;s.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ἐφετινός, from ἐφ’ (eph’, “on, at”) + ἔτος + -ηνός.</div><b>φευ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic) alas (sadly, unfortunately)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φεῦ.</div><b>φευγάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φευγάτη, neuter φευγάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gone, escaped</div>
  <div class='def'>2. absent (inattentive)</div><b>φευγαλέος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φευγαλέα, neuter φευγαλέο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>furtive, transient, elusive; fleeting</div>
  <div class='ex'>ένα <b>φευγαλέο</b> συναίσθημα - a fleeting feeling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φεύγω + -αλέος.</div><b>φεύγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to leave, depart, go</div>
  <div class='ex'>πότε <b>φεύγει</b>; - when does it leave?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to leave from, depart from</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πλοίο <b>φεύγει</b> από τον Πειραιά. - The boat leaves from Piraeus.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φεύγω in the Ancient Greek sense of &quot;to flee&quot;.</div><b>φημίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to distinguish oneself in, become famous for, win fame for</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek φημίζομαι (“I am uttered; promised”), with the active φημίζω.</div><b>φθάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φτάνω">φτάνω</a></div><b>φθείρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to wear away, to erode</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to corrode</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to erode (to destroy gradually by an ongoing process)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to wear out (to cause (something) to become damaged, useless, or ineffective through continued use, especially hard, heavy, or careless use)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φθείρω.</div><b>φθηνός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φθηνή, neuter φθηνό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φτηνός">φτηνός</a></div><b>φθινοπωριάτικα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φθινοπωριάτικος">φθινοπωριάτικος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form of an adjective.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. in the autumn, alternative form of φθινοπωρινά</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, humorous, sometimes) in the autumn, in the fall (usually with reference to something not appropriate or usual for the season)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν της έφτανε ένας μήνας καλοκαιρινών διακοπών· ήθελε να ξανακάνει διακοπές <b>φθινοπωριάτικα</b>. - One month of summer vacation was not enough for her; she wanted to leave again for vacation in autumn, too.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φθινόπωρ(ο) (“autumn”) + -ιάτικα.</div><b>φθινοπωριάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φθινοπωριάτικη, neuter φθινοπωριάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. during autumn, resembling autumn</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φθινοπωριάτικες</b> βροχερές μέρες, μέσα στον Ιούλιο! - Autumnal rainy days, in the middle of July!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. alternative form of <a href="bword://φθινοπωρινός">φθινοπωρινός</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Φθινοπωρινά φύλλα με <b>φθινοπωριάτικα</b> χρώματα. - Leaves of autumn with autumnal colours.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φθινόπωρ(ο), “autumn”) + -ιάτικος.</div><b>φθινοπωρινές ισημερίες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of φθινοπωρινή <a href="bword://ισημερία">ισημερία</a></div><b>φθινοπωρινή ισημερία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φθινοπωρινές ισημερίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>autumn equinox, autumnal equinox</div><b>φθινοπωρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φθινοπωρινή, neuter φθινοπωρινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>autumnal, autumn, autumn-, fall, fall- (occurring during, suitable for or typical of the season of autumn/fall)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φθινοπωρινή</b> ισημερία - autumnal equinox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φθινοπωρινός. By surface analysis, φθινόπωρ(ο), “autumn, fall”) + -ινός.</div><b>φθινόπωρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φθινόπωρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. autumn (UK &amp; US), fall (US) (third of the four seasons, between summer and winter)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) autumn (time period when someone or something is past its prime)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο παππούς βρίσκεται στο <b>φθινόπωρο</b> της ζωής του. - Grandad is in the autumn of his life.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φθινόπωρον, from φθίνω and ὀπώρ(α) f, “last part of summer; fruit”) + -ον n (-on). Doublet of φτινόπωρο. Also see χινόπωρο.</div><b>φθορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φθορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wear, wear and tear, abrasion, corrosion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. decay, decline, deterioration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek φθορά.</div><b>φθορίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φθορίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fluoride</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φθόριο + -ίδιο, calque of English fluoride.</div><b>φθοροβενζόλιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φθοροβενζόλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fluorobenzene</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English fluorobenzene, as φθόριο + βενζόλιο.</div><b>φθόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) envy, jealousy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek φθόνος. Doublet of φτόνος.</div><b>φθόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural φθόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) fluorine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French phthore (an obsolete early synonym of fluor) derived from Ancient Greek φθορά on account of the corrosive nature of hydrofluoric acid. Russian фтор (ftor) is also from the same root, in contrast to most European languages which use derivatives of fluor.</div><b>φι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) phi, the 21st letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φεῖ.</div><b>φιάλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bottle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. saucer</div>
  <div class='def'>3. gas cylinder, gas bottle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek φιάλη.</div><b>φιάσκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιάσκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fiasco, debacle, flop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. swindle scheme</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian fiasco (“debacle”).</div><b>φιγούρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιγούρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. figure (shape of a human body)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. face card</div>
  <div class='def'>3. figure (complex dance move)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. figurehead (carved figure on the prow of a sailing ship)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. impression (outward appearance)</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάνω <b>φιγούρα</b> - to make an impression, to cut a figure</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian figura, with the &#x27;face card&#x27; sense from Genoese.</div><b>φιδάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιδάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diminutive of <a href="bword://φίδι">φίδι</a> n</div>
  <div class='def'>2. snakes and ladders (children&#x27;s luck-based board game)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mosquito coil</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φίδι + -άκι.</div><b>φιλάδελφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φιλάδελφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mock-orange, philadelphus (Philadelphus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φιλάδελφος, with a semantic loan from translingual Philadelphus. Compare also Koine Greek φιλάδελφον.</div><b>φιλάνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλάνθρωπη, neuter φιλάνθρωπο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of a person) charitable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. showing love of fellow human beings</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (as a noun) philanthropist</div><b>φιλάργυρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλάργυρη, neuter φιλάργυρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. avaricious</div>
  <div class='def'>2. miserly</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (noun from verb) miser</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φιλάργυρος. By surface analysis, φιλ- (fil-) + άργυρος.</div><b>φιλάρεσκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλάρεσκη, neuter φιλάρεσκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. appearance-minded, image-conscious (eager to look good)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. appearance-minded, image-conscious (eager to look good); dressy (fond of dressing up in order to look good)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from φιλ- (fil-) + -άρεσκος), modelled on αυτάρεσκος. A calque of German gefallsüchtig.</div><b>φιλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to kiss</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for the variant <a href="bword://φιλώ">φιλώ</a>) to greet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φιλώ + -άω. Inherited from Ancient Greek φιλῶ, contracted form of φιλέω.</div><b>φιλέλληνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φιλέλληνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. philhellene, Hellenophile (a lover of Greece or Greek culture)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) philhellene (specifically, a supporter of Greek independence, especially during the Greek war of independence in 1821-29)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φιλέλλην, from φίλος + Έλλην, with semantic loan from French philhellènes, from the plural of the same etymon, in the sense &#x27;supporter of Greek independence&#x27;.</div><b>φιλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kiss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the mediaeval Byzantine Greek φιλί(ν)), from substantivised infinitive τό φιλεῖν of verb φιλέω.</div><b>φιλία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>friendship, companionship</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φιλία.</div><b>φιλανδικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φινλανδικά">φινλανδικά</a></div><b>φιλανδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλανδική, neuter φιλανδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φινλανδικός">φινλανδικός</a></div><b>φιλανθρωπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλανθρωπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philanthropy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek φιλανθρωπία.</div><b>φιλανθρωπικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλανθρωπική, neuter φιλανθρωπικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philanthropic, charitable (relating to charitable organisations or people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French philanthropique, from Ancient Greek. By surface analysis, φιλάνθρωπος + -ικός.</div><b>φιλαράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>friend, bro, buddy (an endearment form)</div><b>φιλελευθερισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φιλελευθερισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. liberalism</div>
  <div class='def'>2. liberalism (the quality of being liberal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from φιλελεύθερ(ος)) + -ισμός, a calque of English liberalism.</div><b>φιλελεύθερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλελεύθερη, neuter φιλελεύθερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. liberal, broadminded</div>
  <div class='def'>2. liberal</div><b>φιλελληνικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλελληνική, neuter φιλελληνικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. philhellenic, philhellene, Hellenophile (loving Greece or Greek culture)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (historical) philhellenic, philhellene (specifically, supportive of Greek national independence)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French philhellénique. By surface analysis, φιλέλλην(ας)) + -ικός.</div><b>φιλενάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλενάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. girlfriend (female friend of a woman or girl)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. girlfriend (female partner in a romantic relationship)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φίλαινα + -άδα.</div><b>φιλημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine φιλημένη, neuter φιλημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kissed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of φιλιέμαι, passive voice of φιλάω/φιλώ (“I kiss”).</div><b>φιλιέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be kissed, kiss each other</div><b>φιλικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in a friendly manner</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φιλικός">φιλικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://φιλικό">φιλικό</a></div><b>φιλικό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιλικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>friendly match or game</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://φιλικός">φιλικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://φιλικός">φιλικός</a></div><b>φιλικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλική, neuter φιλικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. friendly, amiable</div>
  <div class='def'>2. informal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φίλος + -ικός</div><b>φιλιππινέζικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Filipino</div><b>φιλμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) film(s), movie(s).</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) photographic film.</div><b>φιλοδοξία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλοδοξίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ambition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φιλοδοξία. By surface analysis, φιλόδοξος + -ία.</div><b>φιλοδοξώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to have the ambition, to aspire</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φιλοδοξῶ, contracted form of φιλοδοξέω.</div><b>φιλοδώρημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιλοδωρήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tip (money left for waiter, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φιλοδώρω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>φιλοευρωπαϊσμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural φιλοευρωπαϊσμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) Europeanism, pro-Europeanism (support of the European Union)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, φιλο- (filo-, “pro-, for”) + Ευρωπαίος + -ισμός.</div><b>φιλολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philology</div><b>φιλολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλολογική, neuter φιλολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philological</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French philologique. By surface analysis, φιλολογ(ία)) + -ικός.</div><b>φιλοξενία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλοξενίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hospitality</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) accommodation</div><b>φιλοξενούμενος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φιλοξενούμενοι, feminine φιλοξενουμένη or φιλοξενούμενη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guest or visitor staying for prolonged period of time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of φιλοξενούμαι, passive of φιλοξενώ, from Hellenistic Koine Greek φιλοξενέω, φιλοξενῶ. Also substantivized.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine φιλοξενούμενη, neuter φιλοξενούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>who is guest or visitor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Present participle of φιλοξενούμαι, passive of φιλοξενώ, from Hellenistic Koine Greek φιλοξενέω, φιλοξενῶ. Also substantivized.</div><b>φιλοξενώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to accommodate, to put up, to host</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to host, to play host to, to invite (to/at an event)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek φιλοξενῶ, contracted form of φιλοξενέω, with semantic loan from French hospitaliser (in its rare and dated meaning &#x27;to give hospitality&#x27;) and English host.</div><b>φιλοσοφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλοσοφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philosophy (science and belief system)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φιλοσοφία.</div><b>φιλοσοφικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φιλοσοφικός">φιλοσοφικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philosophically</div><b>φιλοσοφική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλοσοφικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>school of philosophy: normally written with capital Φιλοσοφική with ellipsis of Σχολή f</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://φιλοσοφικός">φιλοσοφικός</a></div><b>φιλοσοφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλοσοφική, neuter φιλοσοφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philosophical</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek φιλοσοφικός. By surface analysis, φιλοσοφ(ία) f) + -ικός.</div><b>φιλοσοφικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) philosophical nature</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φιλοσοφικός + -ότητα.</div><b>φιλοσοφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to philosophise (UK), philosophize (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φιλοσοφέω.</div><b>φιλοτελιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φιλοτελιστές, feminine φιλοτελίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philatelist, stamp collector</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French philatéliste. By surface analysis, φιλοτελ(ισμός)) + -ιστής.</div><b>φιλοφρόνηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιλοφρονήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>compliment, statement of praise</div><b>φιλοχρήματος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλοχρήματη, neuter φιλοχρήματο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>avaricious, money-loving</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φιλοχρήματος.</div><b>φιλόδικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλόδικη, neuter φιλόδικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>litigious, argumentative, combative</div><b>φιλόδοξα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://φιλόδοξος">φιλόδοξος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ambitiously</div><b>φιλόδοξος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλόδοξη, neuter φιλόδοξο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ambitious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φιλόδοξος.</div><b>φιλόλογος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural φιλόλογοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philologist</div><b>φιλόμουσος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλόμουση, neuter φιλόμουσο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. music-loving, art-loving, cultured</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) music lover</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φιλόμουσος.</div><b>φιλόξενος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλόξενη, neuter φιλόξενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hospitable</div><b>φιλόπονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φιλόπονη, neuter φιλόπονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diligent, industrious</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: επιμελής (epimelís), εργατικός (ergatikós), ευσυνείδητος (efsyneíditos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φιλόπονος, from φιλό- + πόνος.</div><b>φιλόσοφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural φιλόσοφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>philosopher (person devoted to studying philosophy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φιλόσοφος.</div><b>φιλότιμο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of φιλότιμος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sense of honour</div>
  <div class='def'>2. earnestness</div><b>φιλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φιλάω">φιλάω</a></div><b>φινλανδικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Finnish (language)</div><b>φινλανδικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φινλανδική, neuter φινλανδικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Finnish (related to the country, people or language of Finland)</div><b>φινοσκανδιναβικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φινοσκανδιναβική, neuter φινοσκανδιναβικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Fennoscandian</div><b>φιντάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιντάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. any young plant, sapling, scion used in grafting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a young, inexperienced person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish فدان (fidan, “young plant, sapling”), from Byzantine Greek φυτόν (a reborrowing).</div><b>φινόκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φινόκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Florence fennel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian finocchio, from Latin fēnuculum.</div><b>φιξάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to become fixed, settled, final</div><b>φιξάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fix (eg colour), finalise (decision)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fix (a photographic image)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French fixer + -άρω (“suffix for verbs”).</div><b>φισέκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φισέκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cartridge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) an intelligent, astute person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فشك, from Classical Persian فشنگ; see there for more. One theory derives the Persian word from Ancient Greek φῦσιγξ, making φισέκι a doublet of φυσίγγιο, but this is not certain at all.</div><b>φιστίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιστίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pistachio nut</div>
  <div class='def'>2. peanut</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فستق, from Classical Persian فستق (fustuq, fustaq), from Arabic فِسْتَق (fistaq), ultimately from Middle Persian pstk&#x27; (pistag). Cognate with English pistachio.</div><b>φιστικιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φιστικιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pistachio tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φιστίκι + -ιά.</div><b>φιστικοβούτυρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιστικοβούτυρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peanut butter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φιστίκι + βούτυρο</div><b>φιτίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φιτίλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fuse (for explosives)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فتیل (fitil), from Arabic فَتِيل.</div><b>φιτίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>misspelling of <a href="bword://φυτίνη">φυτίνη</a></div><b>φιόγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φιόγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bow, bow knot, shoelace knot</div><b>φλάουτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φλάουτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flute</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian flauto, from Occitan and Old Occitan flaut.</div><b>φλάουτο με ράμφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φλάουτα με ράμφος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>recorder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French flûte à bec.</div><b>φλέβα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλέβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vein</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vein, seam</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek or Koine Greek φλέβα, from Ancient Greek φλέψ.</div><b>φλέγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to burn, to set alight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φλέγω</div><b>φλίπερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) pinball machine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French flipper, from English flip.</div><b>φλαμίνγκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) flamingo</div><b>φλαουτίστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλαουτίστες, masculine φλαουτίστας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flautist, flutist</div><b>φλαουτίστας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φλαουτίστες, feminine φλαουτίστα or φλαουτίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flautist, flutist</div><b>φλαουτίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλαουτίστριες, masculine φλαουτίστας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φλαουτίστα">φλαουτίστα</a></div><b>φλεβικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φλεβική, neuter φλεβικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>venous</div><b>φλεβοτομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) phlebotomy</div><b>φλεβοτόμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φλεβοτόμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. phlebotomist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fleam, phleam</div>
  <div class='def'>3. anopheles mosquito</div><b>φλεγμονή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλεγμονές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>inflammation</div><b>φλερτ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, indeclinable) flirting, flirtation, wooing (the act of being flirted with)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν της αρέσει το <b>φλερτ</b>. - She doesn&#x27;t like flirting.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, indeclinable) flirting, flirting with (the act of experimenting or tentatively engaging with)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι αμερικανικές εταιρείες συχνά κάνουν <b>φλερτ</b> με ασιατικούς κολοσσούς. - American businesses often flirt with Asian moguls.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively, indeclinable) fancy piece, other half (one someone is casually going out with)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να η Δανάη και το νέο της <b>φλερτ</b>! - There&#x27;s Danae and her new fancy piece!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English flirt.</div><b>φλερτάρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φλερταρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flirting, wooing (the act of being flirted with)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είπα όχι στο <b>φλερτάρισμά</b> της. - I said no to her flirting.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φλερτάρω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>φλερτάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, intransitive, transitive) to flirt, seduce, woo (insinuate sexual attraction in a playful way)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νομίζω ότι αυτός με <b>φλερτάρει</b>. - I think he’s flirting with me.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, intransitive) to flirt with (experiment or tentatively engage with)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φλερτάρω</b> με την ιδέα να πάω πίσω στο κολέγιο. - I’m flirting with the idea of going back to college..</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φλερτ (flert, “flirt, flirting”) + -άρω.</div><b>φλερόβιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) flerovium</div><b>φλιπάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, intransitive) to flip, flip out (to go berserk or crazy)</div>
  <div class='ex'>1982, Haris Katsimihas, song «Μια βραδιά στο λούκι»
Βοήθεια, Χριστιανοί, κοντεύω να φλιπάρω
Voḯtheia, Christianoí, kontévo na flipáro - Help me, people, I&#x27;m about to flip out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English flip, + -άρω.</div><b>φλιτζάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φλιτζάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cup</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Ancient Greek πίναξbor.
Aramaic פִּינְכָא
Classical Syriac ܦܝܢܟܐbor.?
Classical Persian پِنْگَانbor.
Arabic فِنْجَانbor.
Ottoman Turkish فنجان (fincan)bor.
Greek φλιτζάνι
Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فنجان (fincan), borrowed from Arabic فِنْجَان, borrowed from Classical Persian پِنْگَان, possibly borrowed from Classical Syriac ܦܝܢܟܐ, from Aramaic פִּינְכָא, borrowed from Ancient Greek πίναξ.</div><b>φλοίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλοίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φλούδα">φλούδα</a></div><b>φλογέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλογέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. floghera, Greek flute (folk instrument)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. recorder</div>
  <div class='def'>3. flageolet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Albanian flojere.</div><b>φλογοβόλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φλογοβόλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flamethrower</div><b>φλογοκρύπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φλογοκρύπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flash suppressor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φλόγα + -ο- (-o-) + κρύπτω + -ης (-is).</div><b>φλοιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φλοιοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bark (of tree)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. peel (of fruit)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. husk (of grain)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. crust of the Earth</div>
  <div class='def'>5. cortex</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φλοιός.</div><b>φλοιώδης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φλοιώδης, neuter φλοιώδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barky (having bark)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. barky (with appearance of bark)</div><b>φλου</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hazy, fuzzy, blurry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vague, indeterminate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French flou.</div><b>φλουρί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φλουριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncommon) a gold coin of Byzantium</div>
  <div class='def'>2. any old gold coin</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a coin baked in a vasilopita at New Year</div><b>φλούδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. peel, skin (of fruit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bark (of tree)</div><b>φλυαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλυαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blather, chatter, jabber, prattle (empty or foolish talk)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φλυαρία.</div><b>φλυτζάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φλυτζάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φλιτζάνι">φλιτζάνι</a></div><b>φλόγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φλόγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flame, blaze</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) ardour</div>
  <div class='def'>3. straight flush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek φλόξ.</div><b>φλόκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φλόκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flock, shag (coarse tufts of wool or cotton used in bedding)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, in plural, vulgar) cum, spunk, jism, jizz (semen)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έρχονται τα <b>φλόκια</b>! - Here comes the spunk!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian flocco (“flock, tuft”). Sense 2 from Kaliarda.</div><b>φλόκκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (Cyprus) mop</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στριμωγμένος στο μπάνιο ο υδραυλικός μού φώναξε «έφυγες βουριστός για το <b>φλόκκο</b>!».</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Cyprus, archaic) fez tassel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Provençal flòc.
A variant of φλόκος, φλόκι.</div><b>φλόκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φλόκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flock, shag (coarse tufts of wool or cotton used in bedding)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. jib (triangular staysail set forward of the foremast)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian flocco (“flock, tuft”).</div><b>φλύαρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φλύαρη, neuter φλύαρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chatty, gabby, prattlesome, talkative</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of animals) chattering</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φλύαρος.</div><b>φλώρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φλώροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. greenfinch (any of the birds in the genus Chloris)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) nancyboy, fag, wimp (effeminate and/or weak-willed man)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι πήγε και παντρεύτηκε αυτόν τον <b>φλώρο</b>; - What did she go and marry that nancyboy for?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φλῶρος, from Ancient Greek χλωρίων, from χλωρός.</div><b>φοίνικας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φοίνικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. palm tree</div>
  <div class='def'>2. phoenix (bird which rose from the ashes)</div><b>φοβάμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (for the form <a href="bword://φοβάμαι">φοβάμαι</a>) to fear, dread, be afraid, be frightened</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for the form <a href="bword://φοβούμαι">φοβούμαι</a>) to be afraid, sorry</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φοβάμαι</b> πως δε θα μπορέσουμε … - I am afraid that we are unable to …</div><b>φοβέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φοβέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>threat</div><b>φοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φοβίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phobia</div><b>φοβίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to browbeat, challenge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to frighten, scare</div><b>φοβερά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φοβερός">φοβερός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. frightfully, fearfully</div>
  <div class='def'>2. extremely, a lot</div>
  <div class='def'>3. amazingly</div><b>φοβερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φοβερή, neuter φοβερό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. frightening, terrifying, horrifying</div>
  <div class='def'>2. awesome, formidable, dreadful, fearful</div><b>φοβητσιάρης</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φοβητσιάρα, neuter φοβητσιάρικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cowardly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (as a noun) coward</div><b>φοβούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φοβάμαι">φοβάμαι</a></div><b>φοινικιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φοινικιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>palm tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably from the φοινικ- stem of Ancient Greek φοῖνιξ + -ιά.</div><b>φοινικικά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Phoenician</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, neuter plural of φοινικικός.</div><b>φοινικοτρύγονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φοινικοτρύγονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laughing dove, Streptopelia senegalensis</div><b>φοινικόδασος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φοινικοδάση)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>palm forest</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φοίνικας + -ό- + δάσος.</div><b>φοιτήτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φοιτήτριες, masculine φοιτητής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undergraduate, university student</div><b>φοιτητής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φοιτητές, feminine φοιτήτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>undergraduate, university student</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φοιτητής.</div><b>φοιτητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φοιτητική, neuter φοιτητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) student (attributive)</div>
  <div class='ex'>η <b>φοιτητική</b> ζωή - student life</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from φοιτητ(ής)) + -ικός.</div><b>φολίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φολίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scale (keratin pieces covering the skin of certain animals)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flake</div>
  <div class='def'>3. lamella</div>
  <div class='def'>4. lamina</div>
  <div class='def'>5. squame</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φολῐ́ς.</div><b>φολιδωτά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Squamata, the squamates, many reptiles.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of translingual Squamata, as φολίδα + -ωτά.</div><b>φον ντε τεν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) foundation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French fond de teint.</div><b>φονεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to murder, kill</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φονεύω.</div><b>φονιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φονιάδες, feminine φόνισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>murderer, killer, slayer (one who kills or murders)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Γιάννης ο <b>φονιάς</b>,
Παιδί μιας Πατρινιάς
κι ενός Μεσολογγίτη. - Giannis the killer,
Child of a Patras woman
And a Missolonghi man.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φόνος.</div><b>φονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φονική, neuter φονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lethal, deadly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. murderous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φονικός.</div><b>φονταμενταλίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φονταμενταλίστριες, masculine φονταμενταλιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fundamentalist</div><b>φονταμενταλισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) fundamentalism</div><b>φονταμενταλιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φονταμενταλιστές, feminine φονταμενταλίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fundamentalist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From French fondamentaliste.</div><b>φονταμενταλιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φονταμενταλιστική, neuter φονταμενταλιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fundamentalist</div><b>φορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. time (instance or occurrence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>την πρώτη <b>φορά</b> - the first time</div>
  <div class='def'>2. times</div>
  <div class='ex'>πέντε <b>φορές</b> - five times</div>
  <div class='def'>3. speed, impetus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φορά.</div><b>φοράδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φοράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mare (female horse)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) clumsy, large woman</div><b>φοράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to wear</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to don, put on</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φορώ + -άω. Inherited from Ancient Greek φορῶ contracted form of φορέω, frequentative verb of φέρω. Corresponds with Proto-Hellenic *pʰoréyō, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰoréyeti.</div><b>φοράω κέρατα</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Used other than figuratively or idiomatically: see <a href="bword://φοράω">φοράω</a>, κέρατα.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κορούλα μου είναι ντυμένη ως διάβολος για τις απόκριες και <b>φοράει κέρατα</b>. - My young daughter is dressed as the devil for Halloween and is wearing horns.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, idiomatic, transitive) to put horns on, horn, cuckold, cuck (make a cuckold of someone by being unfaithful)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι ατυχία αυτή, είκοσι χρόνια παντρεμένοι και ξαφνικά του <b>φόρεσε κέρατα</b>! - What bad luck that was, twenty years married and she suddenly cuckolded him!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare similar horn-related terms in French (cocufier) and Italian (fare le corna).</div><b>φορέας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φορείς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vector, carrier (of disease, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carrier, vector, vehicle (of ideas, culture, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. body (organisation, company or other authoritative group)</div><b>φοραδίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φοραδίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>filly, young mare</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φοραδ (forad, “mare”) + -ίτσα</div><b>φορείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φορεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stretcher, litter (to carry the sick and injured)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φορεῖον.</div><b>φορεμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine φορεμένη, neuter φορεμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beaten</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of φοριέμαι, passive voice of φοράω, φορώ (“wear”).</div><b>φορεσιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dress (for special occasions)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φορεσία, from Koine Greek φόρεσις + -ία; see φόρησῐς for more.</div><b>φορητοί υπολογιστές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://φορητός">φορητός</a> <a href="bword://υπολογιστής">υπολογιστής</a></div><b>φορητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φορητή, neuter φορητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>portable, portative</div>
  <div class='ex'>φορητός υπολογιστής (laptop computer)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φορητοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a laptop computer</div><b>φορητός υπολογιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φορητοί υπολογιστές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laptop computer</div><b>φορμαλδεΰδη</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) formaldehyde, methanal</div><b>φοροαπαλλαγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φοροαπαλλαγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tax exemption, tax break (deduction in tax that is given in order to encourage a certain economic activity or a social objective)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>φοροαπαλλαγή</b> επειδή είμαι παντρεμένος. - I have a tax exemption for being married.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φόρος + απαλλαγή, calque of English tax exemption.</div><b>φοροαποφυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tax avoidance (legal exploitation of tax rules to minimise tax payments)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χρησιμοποιεί τις ελβετικές τράπεζες για <b>φοροαποφυγή</b>. - She uses Swiss banks for tax avoidance.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φόρος + αποφυγή, calque of English tax avoidance.</div><b>φοροδιαφεύγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to engage in tax evasion, to evade taxes, to dodge taxes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from φορο(διαφυγή)) + διαφεύγω.</div><b>φοροδιαφυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tax evasion, tax dodging (illegal avoidance of paying tax)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον έστειλαν φυλακή για <b>φοροδιαφυγή</b>. - They sent him to prison for tax evasion.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φόρος + διαφυγή, calque of English tax evasion.</div><b>φοροκλέπτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to commit tax evasion (literally: to steal taxes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> An uncommon composition: φορο(κλοπή), “tax evasion”) + κλέπτω, ancient form of κλέβω.</div><b>φορολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φορολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>taxation, tax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φόρος + -λογία.</div><b>φορολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φορολογική, neuter φορολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tax, taxation</div><b>φορολογικός παράδεισος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tax haven</div><b>φορολογούμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine φορολογούμενη, neuter φορολογούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. taxpaying</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) taxpayer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive present participle of φορολογώ.</div><b>φορολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to tax (to impose and collect a tax from (a person or company)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to tax (to impose and collect a tax on (something))</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek φορολογῶ, from Ancient Greek φορολογέω. By surface analysis, φόρ(ος)) + -λογώ</div><b>φορτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to load, charge</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φορτίζω</b> την μπαταρία του τηλεφώνου μου. - I charge my phone battery.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φορτί̆ζω.</div><b>φορτίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φορτία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>load, freight, goods, cargo</div><b>φορτηγίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φορτηγίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>barge, lighter</div><b>φορτηγό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φορτηγά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lorry, wagon, truck</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Neuter nominal form of φορτηγός.</div><b>φορτηγό τρένο</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φορτηγά τρένα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goods train (UK), freight train (US)</div><b>φορτώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to load (to put a load on or in)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to freight (to load with freight/cargo)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to load (to encumber with something negative, to place as an encumbrance)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to load (to provide in abundance)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to charge (to replenish energy to (a battery, or a device containing a battery) by use of an electrical device plugged into a power outlet)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to load (to read into memory)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (intransitive) to load (to receive a load)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φορτώνω, from Koine Greek φορτῶ with -ώνω suffix.</div><b>φορώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φοράω">φοράω</a></div><b>φουγάρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φουγάρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smokestack, chimney, funnel (of factory or ship)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) heavy smoker</div><b>φουκαράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φουκαράδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wretch, poor man, loser</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فقرا (fukara), with suffix -άς.</div><b>φουμάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Greek, Modern, Standard, colloquial, informal) to smoke (cigarette, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Alternative (vernacular): φουμέρνω (foumérno)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian fumare, from Latin fumare.</div><b>φουντουκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φουντουκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Hazel bush or tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φουντούκι + -ιά.</div><b>φουντούκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φουντούκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hazelnut</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Ancient Greek Ποντικόν
Ancient Greek κάρυον
Ancient Greek Ποντικόν κάρυονbor.
Middle Persian pndk&#x27; (pondik)bor.
Arabic فُنْدُق (funduq)bor.
Classical Persian فُنْدُق (funduq)bor.
Ottoman Turkish فندقbor.
Greek φουντούκι
Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish فندق, borrowed from Classical Persian فُنْدُق (funduq), borrowed from Arabic فُنْدُق (funduq), borrowed from Middle Persian pndk&#x27; (pondik), borrowed from Ancient Greek Ποντικόν κάρυον, from Ποντικόν + κάρυον.</div><b>φουριόζος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φουριόζα, neuter φουριόζο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>furious</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian furioso, from Latin furiōsus.</div><b>φουρκέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φουρκέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hairpin, hairgrip</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) hairpin bend</div><b>φουρνάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φουρνάρηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φούρναρης">φούρναρης</a></div><b>φουρνάρισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φουρνάρισσες, masculine φούρναρης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φούρναρ(ης), “baker”, masculine) + -ισσα (-issa, femminine suffix). See φούρνος, from Latin furnus.</div><b>φουρτούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φουρτούνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rough sea, storm, tempest, squall</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν τα χταπόδια χώνονται στα λαγούμια τους και τα σκεπάζουν με βότσαλα, προμηνύεται <b>φουρτούνα</b>. - When octopuses burrow down and cover themselves with pebbles it forecasts a storm.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) a chain of unexpected and unpleasant events</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φορτούνα, borrowed from Venetan fortuna, from Latin fortūna.</div><b>φουσκάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φουσκάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>blister, pus filled blister</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φουσκώνω + -άλα</div><b>φουσκώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to blow up, inflate, puff up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to exaggerate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to annoy</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to blow up, inflate, puff up</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (intransitive) to be out of breath</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (intransitive) to be bloated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φουσκώνω, from φούσκα + -ώνω.</div><b>φουστάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φουστάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dress</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) woman, women</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φουστάνιν, φυστάνιον, from Italian fustagno or Venetan fustagno (“fustian”).</div><b>φουστανέλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φουστανέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fustanella (skirt, part of Greek male national costume)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φουσταν (foustan, “dress”) + -έλα</div><b>φουφού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φουφούδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brazier</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From dialectal Turkish fufu (likely from an unidentifiable dialectal Ottoman Turkish), from the Greek form φουβού, from Greek φουγού, from dialectal Italian fogon(e) (“a square grill used in ships”) (probably Genovese) with assimilation of pronunciation of consonants and vowel change [o] &gt; [u] because of the presence of [f] + feminine ending -ού, from Latin focus (“fire; family altar”). Also see the Ottoman Turkish فوغه, the Venetan fogo (“fire”) and Italian fuoco.</div><b>φούγκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) fugue</div><b>φούμαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φούμαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baloney, drivel</div>
  <div class='ex'>πουλάω <b>φούμαρα</b> - tell silly lies</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from φουμάρω.</div><b>φούντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φούντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tuft, a bunch of yarns joined at only one end</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek φοῦνδα, from Latin funda (“slingshot”).</div><b>φούξια</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) fuchsia (color/colour)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) fuchsia (plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (indeclinable) fuchsia (color/colour)</div><b>φούρναρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φουρνάρηδες, feminine φουρνάρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Stress shift from Byzantine Greek φουρνάρης, derived from Late Latin furnārius.</div><b>φούρνοι μικροκυμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative plural of <a href="bword://φούρνος">φούρνος</a> μικροκυμάτων</div><b>φούρνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φούρνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oven</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bakery, baker&#x27;s shop</div>
  <div class='def'>3. furnace</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) oven, furnace (an extremely hot place)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φοῦρνος, from Latin furnus (“oven, bakery”).</div><b>φούρνος μικροκυμάτων</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φούρνοι μικροκυμάτων)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>microwave oven</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φούρνος + μικροκυμάτων</div><b>φούσκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φούσκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bubble</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blister</div>
  <div class='def'>3. bladder</div>
  <div class='def'>4. bubble (surge of speculation in a financial market)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek φοῦσκα, from Ancient Greek φύσκη, from Ancient Greek φῦσα. Related to Ancient Greek φυσάω.</div><b>φούστα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φούστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>skirt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φουστάνι, from the Italian fustagno (“fustian”).</div><b>φούτερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) sweatshirt, hoodie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from German Futter in its second sense (“lining”). This meaning is preserved in the doublet φόδρα, which entered the language via Venetan fodra, itself a borrowing tracing back to Proto-Germanic *fōdrą (“sheath”), from Proto-Indo-European *peh₂- (“to protect, guard”).</div><b>φούχτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φούχτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χούφτα">χούφτα</a></div><b>φράγκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) francium</div><b>φράγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φράγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barrier</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dam, barrage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek φράγμα, from the verb φράσσω.</div><b>φράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be fenced</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be blocked, obstructed, clogged</div><b>φράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to fence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive, transitive) to block, obstruct, clog</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έφραξε</b> ο νεροχύτης. - The sink clogged up.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φράζω from Ancient Greek φράσσω or φράττω from past tense ‑αξα to present ‑άζω, in the pattern of στάζω, ἔσταξα.
Unrelated to the ancient φράζω. Stems φρακ-, φραγ- unrelated to φρακάρω or φράξια (see Related terms).</div><b>φράκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φράκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φράχτης">φράχτης</a></div><b>φράουλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φράουλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>strawberry (fruit and plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φράγουλα, from Vulgar Latin *frāgula.</div><b>φράση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phrase, expression</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φράσις.</div><b>φράχτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φράχτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fence</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wall</div>
  <div class='def'>3. hedge</div>
  <div class='def'>4. partition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φράκτης, from Ancient Greek φράσσω.</div><b>φρέαρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρέατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. well (water source)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shaft (of lift or elevator)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. manhole</div>
  <div class='def'>4. deep, trench (on sea bed)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φρέαρ</b> της Καλυψώς - Calypso Deep</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φρέαρ.</div><b>φρέαρ κλιμακοστασίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stairwell</div><b>φρένιασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρενιάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>frenzy, rage</div><b>φρένο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρένα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brake</div>
  <div class='ex'>πατώ <b>φρένο</b> - apply the brakes</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) something which slows an action</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Italian freno, from Latin frēnum.</div><b>φρέσκα κρεμμυδάκια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://φρέσκο">φρέσκο</a> κρεμμυδάκι</div><b>φρέσκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρέσκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fresco (wall painting and the technique for its production)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian fresco. Doublet of φρέσκος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://φρέσκος">φρέσκος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://φρέσκος">φρέσκος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian fresco. Doublet of φρέσκος.</div><b>φρέσκο κρεμμυδάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρέσκα κρεμμυδάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spring onion, scallion</div><b>φρέσκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φρέσκια, neuter φρέσκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fresh (vegetables etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wet (paint)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. latest (news, ideas, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian fresco, from Frankish *frisk (“fresh, unsalted”).</div><b>φραγκολίνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φραγκολίνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>francolin, Francolinus (birds of that genus)</div><b>φραγκοστάφυλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φραγκοστάφυλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gooseberry (fruit)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. redcurrant (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Φράγκος + σταφύλι</div><b>φραγκοσταφυλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φραγκοσταφυλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gooseberry bush</div>
  <div class='def'>2. redcurrant bush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φραγκοστάφυλο + -ιά.</div><b>φραγκοσυκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φραγκοσυκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prickly pear (plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Φράγκος + συκιά</div><b>φραγκοφονιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φραγκοφονιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, humorous) penny pincher, cheapskate, skinflint (a miserly person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην περιμένεις να σου αγοράσει καλό χριστουγεννιάτικο δώρο αυτός ο <b>φραγκοφονιάς</b>! - Don&#x27;t expect that penny pincher to buy you a nice Christmas present!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φράγκο + φονιάς.</div><b>φραγκόκοτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φραγκόκοτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guinea fowl (game bird of the family Numididae)</div><b>φραγκόσυκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φραγκόσυκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>prickly pear, cactus pear, the edible fruit of Opuntia ficus-indica</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Φράγκος + σύκο in the sense of a &quot;foreign fig&quot;.</div><b>φραγμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φραγμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively) restraint, barrier in anything, mainly in feelings, expressions etc.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχει κανένα ηθικό <b>φραγμό</b> να ξελογιάσει τη γυναίκα σου. - He doesn&#x27;t have any moral restraint in seducing your wife.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek φραγμός, from the verb φράσσω</div><b>φρακτή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φρακτές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bulkhead, partition in ships</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek φρακτός (feminine form), from Ancient Greek φράσσω</div><b>φραμπουάζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) raspberry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French framboise.</div><b>φραντζόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φραντζόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oblong loaf (of bread)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish فرانجله, فرانجالا (francala), according to Nisanyan, this is from Italian frangella which lacks any written examples, perhaps a vulgarization of Italian frangere, ultimately from Latin frangere. Compare Ladino frandjola.</div><b>φραπέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) prepared by shaking to produce cold drink with foam</div>
  <div class='ex'>καφές φραπέ (frappe, iced coffee)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French frappé.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, indeclinable) iced coffee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from French frappé.</div><b>φραπές</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φραπέδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) iced coffee</div><b>φρασεολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φρασεολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phraseology</div><b>φρενάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to brake (to apply brakes, to bring something to a halt)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to stop something (also with passive forms): to be stopped from doing something</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Italian frenare, equivalent to φρένο + -άρω.</div><b>φρενίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. frenzy, madness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. delirium</div><b>φρενιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to rage, lose your temper</div><b>φρεσκάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>freshness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φρέσκ(ος)) + -άδα.</div><b>φρεσκάρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be refreshed</div><b>φρεσκάρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to refresh, freshen up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to do up, renovate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to brush up, do up</div><b>φρεσκοστυμμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φρεσκοστυμμένη, neuter φρεσκοστυμμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>freshly squeezed</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φρεσκοστυμμένος</b> χυμός πορτοκαλιού - freshly-squeezed orange juice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φρέσκος + στυμμένος</div><b>φρικτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φρικτή, neuter φρικτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>horrible</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φρικτός (“awful”).</div><b>φριτιλαρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φριτιλαρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fritillary (Fritillaria-genus plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Fritillaria.</div><b>φρονιμίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φρονιμίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wisdom tooth</div><b>φροντίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φροντίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>attention, care</div><b>φροντίδα υγείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) health care</div><b>φροντίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to take care of (a child, an obligation, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to care for, attend to</div><b>φροντίστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φροντίστριες, masculine φροντιστής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. assistant stage manager</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tutor</div>
  <div class='def'>4. flight attendant</div><b>φροντιστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φροντιστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. preparatory course, additional teaching or studying hours</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prep school (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cram school</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φροντιστήριον.</div><b>φροντιστής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φροντιστές, feminine φροντίστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. assistant stage manager</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tutor</div>
  <div class='def'>4. flight attendant</div>
  <div class='def'>5. groundsman</div>
  <div class='def'>6. quartermaster</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φροντιστής, equivalent to φροντ- (front-, “to look after, to take care of”) + -ιστής.</div><b>φρουκτόζη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) fructose, fruit sugar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English fructose.</div><b>φρουρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φρουρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. guard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. guarding, guard duty</div>
  <div class='def'>3. garrison</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φρουρᾱ.</div><b>φρουρός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φρουροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guard, guardian (a person who, or thing that, protects or watches over something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φρουρός.</div><b>φρουρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to guard (to protect or watch over)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φρουρῶ, contracted form of φρουρέω, from φρουρός.</div><b>φρουτοχυμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φρουτοχυμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fruit juice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φρούτο + χυμός</div><b>φρούριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρούρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fortress, stronghold (a fortified place; a place built to withstand attack)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: οχυρό n (ochyró), κάστρο n (kástro)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φρούριον.</div><b>φρούτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρούτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fruit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) freak</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin fructus.</div><b>φρυγανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to crisp, toast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Hellenistic Koine Greek φρυγανίζω, synchronically analyzable as φρύγανο + -ίζω.</div><b>φρυγανιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φρυγανιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toast (toasted bread)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φρυγανίζω + -ιά.</div><b>φρυγανιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φρυγανιέρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toaster (electrical device for toasting sliced bread)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φρυγαν(ιά)) + -ιέρα.</div><b>φρυκτωρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φρυκτωρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) phryctoria</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φρυκτωρία.</div><b>φρόνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρονήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spirit, belief, conscience</div>
  <div class='def'>2. morale</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural) beliefs, ideology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φρόνημα.</div><b>φρόνιμα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φρόνιμος">φρόνιμος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. judiciously, prudently</div>
  <div class='def'>2. with care, carefully</div><b>φρόνιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φρόνιμη, neuter φρόνιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sensible, wise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. judicious, prudent</div>
  <div class='def'>3. well behaved</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φρόνιμος.</div><b>φρύγανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρύγανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brush, twigs (generic name for small, twiggy, usually prickly, plant)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) such vegetation dominating an area of countryside, garrigue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φρύγανον.</div><b>φρύδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φρύδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eyebrow</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φρύδι, φρύδιν, from Koine Greek ὀφρύδιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek ὀφρύς, from Proto-Hellenic *opʰrū́s, from Proto-Indo-European *h₃bʰrúHs.</div><b>φρύνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toad</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φρῦνος, a variant of φρῡ́νη.</div><b>φτάνει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. third-person singular present of <a href="bword://φτάνω">φτάνω</a>: &quot;he arrives, she arrives, it arrives&quot;</div>
  <div class='ex'>πότε <b>φτάνει</b>; - when does s/he/it arrive?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. &quot;it suffices, it is enough&quot;</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (impersonal) &quot;it is enough&quot; (also used as interjection)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>enough!</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φτάνει</b>! Μη μου μιλάς άλλο γι’ αυτόν! - Enough! Do not talk to me anymore about him!</div><b>φτάνουν στο απροχώρητο</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of <a href="bword://φτάνω">φτάνω</a> στο <a href="bword://απροχώρητο">απροχώρητο</a></div><b>φτάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to arrive</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι ώρα <b>φτάνουμε</b>; - What time do we arrive?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to come, be imminent, draw near, near</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φτάνει</b> το καλοκαίρι. - Summer is coming.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to reach, stretch, come up to, extend</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κατέβασέ μου αυτό το κουτί, εσύ που <b>φτάνεις</b>. - Get me down that box, since you can reach it.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to be reduced to, come to, end up</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στο τέλος, <b>έφτασε</b> να ζητιανεύει στους δρόμους. - In the end, he was reduced to begging on the streets.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. to be enough, suffice</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>φτάσει</b> άραγε το φαγητό; - I wonder if there&#x27;ll be enough food?</div>
  <div class='def'>6. to equal, rival, touch, approach</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σ’ αυτό το σπορ, κανείς δεν τον <b>φτάνει</b>. - In this sport, no one can equal him.</div>
  <div class='def'>7. to amount to, come to</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα χρέη του <b>φτάνουν</b> τα 5.000 ευρώ. - His debts amount to 5,000 euros.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. also see the impersonal third person singular <a href="bword://φτάνει">φτάνει</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φθάνω.</div><b>φτάνω στο απροχώρητο</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to reach the end of one&#x27;s tether</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to reach the end of one&#x27;s rope</div><b>φτάρνισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φταρνίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sneeze, sneezing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek *πτάρνισμα / πτέρνισμα with dissimilation of [pt] to [ft].</div><b>φτέρνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φτέρνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>heel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φτέρνα, from Koine Greek πτέρνα, from Ancient Greek πτέρνη.</div><b>φτήνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cheapness, inexpensiveness (low price)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. cheapness, shoddiness (low quality)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φτην(ός)) or φτην(αίνω)) + -ια (-ia).</div><b>φταίξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fault, blame (responsibility for a mistake)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φταίξ- + -ιμο (-imo).</div><b>φταίω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be to blame, be at fault, be in the wrong</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος <b>φταίει</b> γι’ αυτό το ατύχημα; - Who is to blame for this accident?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in past tenses) to do wrong, do a misdeed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν <b>έφταιξα</b>, ζήτω συγγνώμη. - If I did wrong, I&#x27;m sorry.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πταίω. Doublet of πταίω.</div><b>φτερνίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sneeze</div><b>φτερουγίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to flap, fly (wings - also used figuratively of heart)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φτερουγίζω</b> - I flap (my) wings to fly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πτερυγίζω with [pt] &gt; [ft] and [o] &gt; [u].</div><b>φτερούγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φτερούγες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wing (of a bird)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek πτερούγα, from πτερούγ(ι)) + -α (-a, augmentative suffix), from Ancient Greek πτερύγιον, from πτέρυξ + -ιον (-ion, diminutive suffix).</div><b>φτερωτός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φτερωτή, neuter φτερωτό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. winged</div>
  <div class='def'>2. feathered</div>
  <div class='ex'>οι <b>φτερωτοί</b> μας φίλοι - our feathered friends</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πτερωτός with πτ- → φτ- alteration, per πτερόν → φτερό. By surface analysis, φτερ(ό)) + -ωτός.</div><b>φτερό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φτερά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. feather</div>
  <div class='def'>3. wing (UK), fender (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. shuttlecock</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φτερόν, φτερό, from Ancient Greek πτερόν.</div><b>φτηνός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φτηνή, neuter φτηνό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cheap, inexpensive, of low value</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shoddy, of poor quality</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek εὐτηνός, from Koine Greek εὐθηνός.</div><b>φτιάχνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to build oneself up, establish oneself</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to sort oneself out, tidy oneself up</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to become drunk</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to become high or euphoric (with drugs etc)</div><b>φτιάχνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to make, build, create, construct</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fix, mend, repair</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to improve, get better</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ultimately from forms of Byzantine Greek εὐθειάζω / εὐτειάζω, εὐθειάνω / εὐτειάνω, from Ancient Greek εὐθεῖα, feminine of εὐθύς, + -άζω. The exact morphological path is debated:</div><b>φτου</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ptui, ptooey (the sound of spitting)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φτου</b>, ατυχία. - Damn it, what bad luck.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>φτυάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φτυάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. garden spade, shovel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. peel (used by bakers)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πτυάριον.</div><b>φτυστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(for persons) spitting image</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φτυσ- from φτύνω + -τός.</div><b>φτωχικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φτωχικόή, neuter φτωχικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. humble, lowly, poor</div>
  <div class='def'>2. meagre, scant</div><b>φτωχοί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/vocative masculine plural of <a href="bword://φτωχός">φτωχός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the poor</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχει την μεγάλη διαφορά του προσδόκιμου ζωής μεταξύ των πλούσιων και των <b>φτωχών</b>. - There is a large difference in life expectancy between the rich and poor.</div><b>φτωχοκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φτωχοκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://πτωχοκομείο">πτωχοκομείο</a></div><b>φτωχομάνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φτωχομάνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>city/place of the poor</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θεσσαλονίκη μου μεγάλη <b>φτωχομάνα</b>
εσύ που βγάζεις τα καλύτερα παιδιά
Θεσσαλονίκη μου μεγάλη <b>φτωχομάνα</b>
όπου κι αν πάω σ’ έχω πάντα στην καρδιά - My Thessaloniki</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φτωχ(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + -μάνα. Literally, “poor-mother”.</div><b>φτωχός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φτωχή, neuter φτωχό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. poor (without sufficient money)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. poor, cheap (costing little, of bad quality)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (as a plural noun) the poor</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχει μεγάλη διαφορά στο προσδόκιμο ζωής μεταξύ πλούσιων και <b>φτωχών</b>. - There is a large difference in life expectancy between the rich and poor.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek πτωχός.</div><b>φτωχόσπιτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φτωχόσπιτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poorhouse</div><b>φτύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to spit, spit out, spit at</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη <b>φτύνεις</b> αυτούς που του δίνουν ψωμί. - Don&#x27;t spit on those that give you bread.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to tag (in the children&#x27;s game hide-and-go-seek)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek πτύω with addition of a nasal infix.</div><b>φτώχεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φτώχειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φτώχια">φτώχια</a></div><b>φτώχια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φτώχιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. destitution, poverty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) spiritual or cultural poverty</div><b>φυγάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φυγάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fugitive, runaway, escapee</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φυγάς.</div><b>φυγαδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to help to escape, to spirit out</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to smuggle out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φυγαδεύω, semantically adapted to φυγάς.</div><b>φυγομαχώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to avoid conflict, to shun battle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek φυγομαχῶ, contracted form of φυγομαχέω.</div><b>φυγοπονώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be workshy, to shirk</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek φυγοπονῶ. contracted form of φυγοπονέω.</div><b>φυγόκεντρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυγόκεντρη, neuter φυγόκεντρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centrifugal</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φυγόκεντρος</b> δύναμη - centrifugal force</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φυγόκεντροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>centrifuge</div><b>φυγόπονος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυγόπονη, neuter φυγόπονο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. workshy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) shirker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φυγόπονος.</div><b>φυλάγομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to look after yourself</div><b>φυλάγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φυλάω">φυλάω</a> and <a href="bword://φυλώ">φυλώ</a></div><b>φυλάκιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φυλακίσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>confinement in prison, imprisonment, incarceration</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from φυλακί(ζω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>φυλάσσομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to be guarded, protected</div><b>φυλάσσω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to guard, protect, watch over; synonym of <a href="bword://φυλάω">φυλάω</a>, <a href="bword://φυλώ">φυλώ</a>, or <a href="bword://φυλάγω">φυλάγω</a></div><b>φυλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to guard, protect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to guard, watch over</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to be on sentry duty</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to be &quot;it&quot; in hide and seek (the children&#x27;s game) in the expression</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα φυλάω (ta fyláo) but never *τα φυλώ</div><b>φυλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φυλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>race, tribe, nation, breed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φυλή.</div><b>φυλαγμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine φυλαγμένη, neuter φυλαγμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guarded, protected</div>
  <div class='ex'>καλά <b>φυλαγμένο</b> μυστικό - a well-guarded secret</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of φυλάγομαι, passive voice of φυλάω/φυλώ (“I guard”) &amp; φυλάγω, as well as perfect participle of φυλάσσομαι, passive voice of the formal φυλάσσω.</div><b>φυλακή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φυλακές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prison, jail, gaol</div>
  <div class='ex'>κελί <b>φυλακής</b> - prison cell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. imprisonment</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φυλακή.</div><b>φυλακίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to imprison, jail, gaol</div><b>φυλακισμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine φυλακισμένη, neuter φυλακισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>passive perfect participle of <a href="bword://φυλακίζω">φυλακίζω</a>: imprisoned</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φυλακισμένοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. prisoner (person incarcerated in a prison)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. prisoner (any person held against his or her will)</div><b>φυλακτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυλακτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φυλαχτό">φυλαχτό</a></div><b>φυλαχτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυλαχτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>amulet, talisman, charm (an item with magic power)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φυλαχτό</b> να τη φυλάει
και ας μη με αγαπάει. - An amulet to protect her,
Even if she doesn&#x27;t love me.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek adjective φυλακτός, from Ancient Greek φυλάσσω.</div><b>φυλετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυλετική, neuter φυλετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. racial, tribal, clannish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sexual</div><b>φυλετισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) racism</div><b>φυλλάδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pamphlet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. brochure</div>
  <div class='def'>3. leaflet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, a noun derived from φύλλο (leaf; whence, English leaflet), cognate with Latin folium (whence, English folio)</div><b>φυλλοβόλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυλλοβόλα, neuter φυλλοβόλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>deciduous</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα πλατάνια είναι φυλλοβόλα. - Plane trees are deciduous</div><b>φυλλοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυλλοειδής, neuter φυλλοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>leaflike, foliate</div><b>φυλλομετράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φυλλομετρώ">φυλλομετρώ</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φυλλομετρώ φυλλομετρ- + -άω.</div><b>φυλλομετρημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine φυλλομετρημένη, neuter φυλλομετρημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>browsed, with pages flipped through (of a book)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of φυλλομετρούμαι and φυλλομετριέμαι, passive forms of φυλλομετρώ, φυλλομετράω (“flip through the pages of a book”) respectively.</div><b>φυλλομετρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to riffle, leaf through, flip through (the pages of a book)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φύλλο + μετρώ/μετράω, a free translation of French feuilleter.</div><b>φυλλονόμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φυλλονόμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phyllonoma (Phyllonoma)</div><b>φυλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φυλάω">φυλάω</a></div><b>φυματίωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) tuberculosis, TB</div><b>φυσάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to blow (wind, nose, trumpet, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φυσάω</b> τη φλόγα του κεριού για να σβήσει. - I blow the flame of the candle to put it out.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to blow</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (impersonal) to blow, to be windy</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φυσάει</b> πολύ σήμερα. - It is very windy today</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φυσ(ώ)) + modern suffix -άω, inherited from Ancient Greek φυσῶ, contracted form of φυσάω.</div><b>φυσίγγιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυσίγγια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cartridge, round</div>
  <div class='ex'>άσφαιρο <b>φυσίγγιο</b> - blank round</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fuse</div><b>φυσαλίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φυσαλίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bubble</div>
  <div class='def'>2. blister</div><b>φυσαλλίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φυσαλλίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φυσαλίδα">φυσαλίδα</a></div><b>φυσαρμόνικα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φυσαρμόνικες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harmonica; mouth organ</div><b>φυσητήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυσητήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bellows</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sperm whale</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φυσητήρ.</div><b>φυσητήρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυσητήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bellows</div><b>φυσικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. naturally (by natural means)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το σαπούνι δεν παράγεται <b>φυσικά</b>. - Soap is not produced naturally.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. naturally (in a natural manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Απάντησε πολύ <b>φυσικά</b> σαν να μην έγινε τίποτα. - He replied very naturally, as if nothing had happened.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. naturally, of course, certainly</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Φυσικά</b> και μπορείς νά ’ρθεις. - Of course you can come.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φύση + -κά</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φυσικός">φυσικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>φυσική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) physics</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Nominalisation of φυσικός.</div><b>φυσική επιλογή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) natural selection</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English natural selection.</div><b>φυσικοχημεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) physical chemistry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φυσικός + χημεία.</div><b>φυσικό αέριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(singular only) natural gas</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English natural gas.</div><b>φυσικό πρόσωπο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυσικά πρόσωπα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>natural person</div><b>φυσικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυσική, neuter φυσικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. natural, normal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. physical (relating to physics)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φυσικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural φυσικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>physicist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φυσικός.</div><b>φυσιολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) physiology</div><b>φυσιολογικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://φυσιολογικός">φυσιολογικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. normally</div>
  <div class='def'>2. physiologically</div><b>φυσιολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυσιολογική, neuter φυσιολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. normal, natural</div>
  <div class='def'>2. physiological</div><b>φυσιολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural φυσιολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>physiologist</div><b>φυστίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυστίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φιστίκι">φιστίκι</a></div><b>φυστικιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυστικιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φιστικιά">φιστικιά</a></div><b>φυστικοβούτυρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυστικοβούτυρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φιστικοβούτυρο">φιστικοβούτυρο</a> (peanut butter)</div><b>φυσώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φυσάω">φυσάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φυσῶ, contracted form of φυσάω. Also see the modern φυσάω.</div><b>φυτίλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυτίλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φιτίλι">φιτίλι</a></div><b>φυτίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) a type of margarine or spread used as an alternative to butter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) a calcium and magnesium compound of phytic acid</div><b>φυτεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φυτείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plantation</div><b>φυτεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be planted</div><b>φυτεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to plant (place growing thing in soil)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν έξω στον κήπο της και <b>φύτευε</b> τριαντάφυλλα. - She was out planting roses in her garden.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, transitive) to plant, shove, put (especially in the context of injuring someone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Του <b>φύτεψε</b> μια σφαίρα στο κεφάλι. - He planted a bullet in his head.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, derogatory, humorous) to plant (bury someone&#x27;s coffin in the ground)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Την <b>φυτέψανε</b> χθες το απόγευμα. - They planted her yesterday afternoon.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φυτεύω.</div><b>φυτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυτική, neuter φυτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>of or belonging to plants</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φυτικός.</div><b>φυτοκομία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) horticulture</div><b>φυτοκομείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυτοκομεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nursery, smallholding</div><b>φυτολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) botany, plant biology, plant science, plant sciences</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φυτό + -λογία.</div><b>φυτοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φυτοφάγος or φυτοφάγα, neuter φυτοφάγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>herbivorous, plant eating</div><b>φυτοφάρμακο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pesticide</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Katharevousa φυτοφάρμακον, from φυτό + φάρμακο.</div><b>φυτρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to grow roots (of plants)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to emerge, become visible</div><b>φυτό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φυτά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. plant (organism)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a person in a vegetative state</div>
  <div class='def'>3. swot, nerd (someone obsessed with reading and study)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φυτόν.</div><b>φωλιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωλιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nest (a structure built by a bird as a place to incubate eggs and rear young)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nest (a place used by a monotreme, fish, amphibian or insect, for depositing eggs and hatching young)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. animal&#x27;s home: burrow (rabbit), den (lion, tiger, wolf, etc), drey (squirrel), earth (fox), eyrie (eagle), form (hare), hive (bee), holt (otter), lair (tiger), lodge (beaver), rookery (rook), sett (badger), vespiary (wasp), warren (rabbit)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) hideout</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) den</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φωλεά, an alternative form of φωλεός, with the ending replaced by -ιά.</div><b>φωλιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to nest, build a nest, sit in a nest</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to hibernate</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to take refuge from danger</div><b>φωνάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to shout, yell, bawl</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to name (only for persons)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to call, summon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek φωνάζω form the ancient Ancient Greek φωνέω, φωνῶ.
Compare Italiot Greek fonàzzo, Mariupol Greek фона́зу.</div><b>φωνή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωνές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. voice, sound</div>
  <div class='def'>2. voice, note</div>
  <div class='def'>3. voice</div>
  <div class='ex'>ενεργητική <b>φωνή</b> - active voice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φωνή, from Proto-Hellenic *pʰōnā́, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰoh₂-néh₂, from *bʰeh₂- (“to speak”).</div><b>φωνήεν</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φωνήεντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vowel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. letter representing a vowel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φωνῆεν the noun formed from φωνήεις, from φωνή + suffix -ήεις.</div><b>φωνητική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) phonetics</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://φωνητικός">φωνητικός</a></div><b>φωνητικό αλφάβητο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phonetic alphabet</div><b>φωνητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φωνητική, neuter φωνητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vocal (pertaining to the voice or speech; uttered or modulated by the voice)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φωνητικές</b> χορδές, <b>φωνητική</b> μουσική - vocal cords, vocal music</div>
  <div class='def'>2. phonetic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φωνητικός, from φωνητóς, from φωνέω + -τικός.</div><b>φωνογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φωνογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phonograph</div><b>φως</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φώτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>light</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φῶς, φᾰ́ος, from Proto-Hellenic *pʰáos, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰéh₂os, from *bʰeh₂- (“shine”).
Cognate with Mariupol Greek фос (fos).</div><b>φωσφορίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to phosphoresce (glow in the dark)</div><b>φωσφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural φωσφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) phosphorus, the chemical element (symbol P)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φωσφόρος.</div><b>φωτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to light, to light up, to illuminate (to shine light on)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to illuminate (to glow; to emit light)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (intransitive, third person) to dawn, to get light</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, transitive) to brighten, to light up</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively, transitive) to illuminate (to clarify or make something understandable)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively, transitive) to enlighten (to give knowledge or understanding to)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάνε ό,τι σε <b>φωτίσει</b> ο Θεός. - Do what you think is best.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φωτίζω. By surface analysis, φωτ- (fot-) + -ίζω.</div><b>φωτεινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φωτεινή, neuter φωτεινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bright, illuminated, light, luminous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φωτεινός.</div><b>φωτεινότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτεινότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. brightness</div>
  <div class='def'>2. luminosity, luminance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φωτεινός + -ότητα. First attested 1880.</div><b>φωτιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fire</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flame, sparkle</div>
  <div class='def'>3. light (for cigarette, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχω <b>φωτιά</b>. - I don&#x27;t have a light.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek φωτία, from Ancient Greek φῶς, variant of φᾰ́ος, from Proto-Hellenic *pʰáos, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰéh₂os.
Compare Mariupol Greek фоты́я, футы́я.</div><b>φωτιστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φωτιστική, neuter φωτιστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lighting, light</div>
  <div class='ex'>φωτιστικό πετρέλαιο (kerosene, paraffin)</div><b>φωτοαντίγραφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φωτοαντίγραφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>photocopy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English photocopy:φωτο- (foto-) + αντίγραφο.</div><b>φωτοβολίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτοβολίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. flare (firework that produces a brilliant light or intense heat without an explosion)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. flare (source of brightly burning light or intense heat used to attract attention in an emergency)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φωτο- (foto-, “light”) + βολίδα, calque of German Leuchtkugel. First attested 1887.</div><b>φωτοβολταϊκό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive masculine/neuter singular of <a href="bword://φωτοβολταϊκός">φωτοβολταϊκός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φωτοβολταϊκά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>photovoltaic cell</div><b>φωτοβολταϊκός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φωτοβολταϊκή, neuter φωτοβολταϊκό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>photovoltaic</div><b>φωτογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural φωτογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>photographer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φῶς (light) + γράφω.</div><b>φωτογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. photography</div>
  <div class='def'>2. photograph, photo, snap</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned translation of French photographie. Morphologically, from φωτο- (“photo”) + -γραφ- (“-graphy”) + -ία; see φως (fos, “light”), γραφή for more.</div><b>φωτογραφίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be photographed</div><b>φωτογραφίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to photograph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φωτογραφ(ία) (“photograph”) + -ίζω. Also + -ώ for the more formal variation φωτογραφώ. Calque of French photographier.</div><b>φωτογραφείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φωτογραφεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>photographic studio</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φωτογράφ(ος), “photographter”) + -είο.</div><b>φωτογραφική μηχανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτογραφικές μηχανές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>camera (usually for still photographs)</div><b>φωτογραφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φωτογραφική, neuter φωτογραφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>photographic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φωτογραφία + -ικός.</div><b>φωτογραφούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be photographed</div><b>φωτογραφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φωτογραφίζω">φωτογραφίζω</a></div><b>φωτοδιαπερατός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine φωτοδιαπερατή, neuter φωτοδιαπερατό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>transparent, translucent, clear</div><b>φωτομαρτυρία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτομαρτυρίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) A phototestimony, a photographic testimony.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φωτο- (foto-, “photo-”) + μαρτυρία.</div><b>φωτονεφέλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτονεφέλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) nebula</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> φωτο- (foto-, “photo-, light”) + νεφέλη</div><b>φωτορύπανση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural φωτορυπάνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) light pollution</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η έκθεση στην υπερβολική <b>φωτορύπανση</b> φαίνεται πως επηρεάζει τις πιθανότητες εμφάνισης της νόσου Αλτσχάιμερ για τα άτομα που είναι πάνω από 65 ετών, σύμφωνα με έρευνα στο περιοδικό «Frontiers in Neuroscience». - According to a research in the journal Frontiers in Neuroscience, exposure to excessive light pollution appears to affect the chances of developing Alzheimer&#x27;s disease for people over 65.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φωτο- (foto-, “light”) + ρύπανση, calque of English light pollution.</div><b>φωτοσκίαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτοσκιάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chiaroscuro</div><b>φωτοστέφανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φωτοστέφανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>halo</div><b>φωτοσύνθεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) photosynthesis</div><b>φωτοτυπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτοτυπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. photocopy, (facsimile)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. photocopying (method, process)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. whiteprint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> First attested around 1848. Calque of French phototypie: φωτο- (foto-) + -τυπία.</div><b>φωτοφοβία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) photophobia (excessive sensitivity to light)</div><b>φωτόλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φωτολύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. photolysis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. A method of depilation with use of laser.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φῶς + Ancient Greek λύσις (lusis)</div><b>φωτόνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φωτόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>photon</div><b>φωτόσπαθο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φωτόσπαθα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lightsaber (sword having a blade made of a powerful beam of light)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φωτο- (foto-, “light”) + σπαθί; calque of English lightsaber.</div><b>φόβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φόβοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fear</div>
  <div class='ex'>μην έχεις <b>φόβο</b> - don&#x27;t be afraid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. apprehension, awe</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φόβος</b> των θεών - awe of the gods</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φόβος, ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *bʰegʷ- (“to run, to flee”).</div><b>φόδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φόδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lining, inner coating</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Venetan fodra, itself a loan tracing back to Proto-Germanic *fōdrą (“sheath”), from Proto-Indo-European *peh₂- (“to protect, guard”). Doublet of φούτερ, borrowed directly from Germanic, which has come to mean “sweatshirt”.</div><b>φόλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φόλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>poisoned bait (to kill vermin)</div><b>φόνισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φόνισσες, masculine φονιάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>murderess</div><b>φόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φόνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>murder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek φόνος, which is cognate to Ancient Greek θείνω. See there for further cognates.</div><b>φόντο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φόντα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. background (a part of the picture that depicts scenery to the rear or behind the main subject)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) background (information relevant to the current situation about past events; history)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian fondo.</div><b>φόρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively, literally, uncountable) speed, pace, momentum, impetus (force, either internal or external, that impels)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το τραίνο αρχίζει να παίρνει <b>φόρα</b>. - The train begins to gather speed.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) public, open (people as a whole as opposed to a private group)</div>
  <div class='ex'>βγάζω τ’ άπλυτα στη φόρα (vgázo t’ áplyta sti fóra, “air one&#x27;s dirty laundry in public”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φορά, with raising of the stress, possibly in allusion to φέρω.</div><b>φόρεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φορέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dress (item of clothing)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φόρημα. Cognate with Pontic Greek φόρεμα.</div><b>φόρμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φόρμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. form (the shape or visible structure of a thing or person)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. form (established method of expression or practice)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ποιητική <b>φόρμα</b> - poetic form</div>
  <div class='def'>3. form (level of performance)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. shape (condition of personal health, especially muscular health)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. training kit (clothing worn during training)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. uniform</div>
  <div class='def'>7. romper suit, onesie (one-piece infant bodysuit)</div>
  <div class='def'>8. form (a thing that gives shape to other things as in a mold)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian forma, from Latin forma.</div><b>φόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tax (payment levied by the state)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tribute (payment in cash or kind levied on a state or individual)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Formed from the Ancient Greek verb φέρω, akin to the similar suffix -φόρος m.</div><b>φόρος προστιθέμενης αξίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φόροι προστιθέμενης αξίας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>value added tax</div><b>φόρτιση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. charging (an act or process of charging (as of a battery))</div>
  <div class='ex'>ασύρματη <b>φόρτιση</b> - wireless charging</div>
  <div class='def'>2. charge (the quantity of unbalanced positive or negative ions in or on an object; measured in coulombs)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) charge (intensity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>συναισθηματική <b>φόρτιση</b> - emotional charge</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from φορτί(ζω)) + -ση (-si).</div><b>φόρτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural φόρτοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>load (volume of work required to be performed or material to be processed (especially an excessive or bothersome volume))</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φόρτος</b> εργασίας - workload</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φόρτος.</div><b>φύκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φύκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seaweed, alga</div><b>φύκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φύκη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φύκι">φύκι</a></div><b>φύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural φύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>guard, gatekeeper, attendant, warden, custodian</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φῠ́λᾰξ, φῠ́λᾰκος.</div><b>φύλλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φύλλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. leaf (of plant, etc); blade (leaf of grass, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φύλλο</b> δάφνης - bay leaf</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sheet (paper)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. newspaper, issue, number</div>
  <div class='def'>4. plate, foil (metal, glass, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. filo, phyllo (pastry)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>φύλλο</b> κρούστας - filo pastry</div>
  <div class='def'>6. playing card</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek φύλλον (ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *bʰleh₃- through a root *bʰolh₃-yom), and semantic loan from French feuille in some senses, such as &quot;sheet&quot;. For &quot;playing card&quot;, semantic loan from Italian carte. Cognate with Mariupol Greek фи́лу.</div><b>φύλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φύλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. sex, gender</div>
  <div class='ex'>το ωραίο <b>φύλο</b> - the fair sex</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tribe</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα βαρβαρικά <b>φύλα</b> - the barbarian tribes</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek φῦλον, from φύω.</div><b>φύλον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://φύλο">φύλο</a>, spelt in polytonic: φῦλον</div><b>φύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, intransitive) to grow, sprout, germinate</div>
  <div class='ex'>For transitive, see φυτεύω (fytévo, “I plant”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φῠ́ομαι (“I am produced, grown”), the present middle indicative first-person singular of active φῠ́ω.</div><b>φύση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nature</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φύσις.</div><b>φώκια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural φώκιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seal (marine animal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek φώκη.</div><b>φώνημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural φωνήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phoneme</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A reborrowing from French phonème (“phoneme”), from Ancient Greek φώνημα.</div><b>φώσφορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://φωσφόρος">φωσφόρος</a> phosphorus</div><b>χ.ε.</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://χωρίς">χωρίς</a> εκδότη: n.p. (no publisher) of books where the name of the publisher is not mentioned</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Preposition χωρίς (“without”) + accusative singular of masculine noun εκδότης (“publisher”).</div><b>χ.η.</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://χωρίς">χωρίς</a> <a href="bword://ημερομηνία">ημερομηνία</a>: n.d. (no date) of books, articles, websites where the year-month-day of their publication is not mentioned</div><b>χ.χ.</b>
  <div><i>prep_phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://χωρίς">χωρίς</a> <a href="bword://χρονολογία">χρονολογία</a>: n.d. (no date) of books where the year of their publication is not mentioned</div><b>χ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Χ΄">Χ΄</a></div><b>χάβρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) (a meeting with people talking on top of each other, resulting in nobody being understood)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) synagogue</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish خاوره (havra), from Hebrew חֶבְרָה.</div><b>χάβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χάφτω">χάφτω</a></div><b>χάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χάδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, literally) gentle caress; soft touch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χάδι, from an earlier χάιδι with elimination of the semivowel αι (ai), from ηχάδιον, a diminutive of ήχος with metathesis of η from ηχά &gt; χάι.</div><b>χάκερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) hacker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English hacker.</div><b>χάλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χάλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bad, wretched condition</div>
  <div class='ex'>είμαι ένα <b>χάλι</b> - I&#x27;m a mess.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish حال (hal, “state, situation”).</div><b>χάλια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in wretched, terrible, miserable, awful condition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adverbialized plural of χάλι.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://χάλι">χάλι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>χάλκινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χάλκινη, neuter χάλκινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. copper (made of copper)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of medal) bronze (made of bronze)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χάλκινο</b> μετάλλιο - bronze medal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek χάλκινος. By surface analysis, χαλκ(ός)) + -ινος (-inos).</div><b>χάλυβας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χάλυβες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek χάλυψ through the accusative singular &quot;τὸν&amp; χάλυβα&quot;.</div><b>χάμπουργκερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) hamburger</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English hamburger.</div><b>χάμστερ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable, irregular) hamster (small Eurasian rodent)</div><b>χάμω</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(informal) down, on the ground, on the floor</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βάλ’ το <b>χάμω</b>. - Put⁽ˢⁱⁿᵍᵘˡᵃʳ⁾ it down.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χάμω, from Ancient Greek χαμαί.</div><b>χάνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χάνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. caravanserai, khan for sense: caravanserai</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory) a dirty hotel in poor condition</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish خان (han), from Classical Persian خان.</div><b>χάνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to vanish, disappear</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to perish</div><b>χάντρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χάντρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bead</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χάντρα, probably borrowed from Arabic, apparently خَرَزَة, thus potentially relating χᾰρᾰ́σσω.</div><b>χάνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to lose</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Έχασα</b> ξανά τα κλειδιά. - I lost the keys again.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to miss, not have</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα <b>χάσει</b> το τρένο. - He will miss the train.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χάνω, developed from ἔχασα, ἐχάσα, from the Koine Greek aorist *ἐχάωσα) of a root *χαώνω, from the verb χαόω, χαῶ, from Ancient Greek χάος. Cognate with Mariupol Greek хану (xanu).</div><b>χάνω το λογαριασμό</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) lose count</div><b>χάος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chaos</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φύγε κι ἄσε με μοναχό, ποὺ βλέπω νὰ πληθαίνη
ἀπάνω ἡ νύχτα, καὶ βαθιὰ νὰ γίνωνται τὰ χάη.
Fýge ki áse me monachó, pou vlépo na plithaíni - Go and leave me alone looking at the growing
night upon [me], and the deepening chaoses.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) disorder, mess</div>
  <div class='def'>3. chaos</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χάος. For sense disorder, mess, semantic loan from French chaos (in that sense) from Latin chaos, from Ancient Greek χάος.</div><b>χάπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χάπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pill, capsule</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish حب (hab, hap), from Arabic حَبّ.</div><b>χάπι εντ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) happy end, happy ending</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English happy ending with the word end.</div><b>χάπι-εντ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) spelling with usage of a hyphen of <a href="bword://χάπι">χάπι</a> εντ</div><b>χάρακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χάρακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rule, ruler (straightedge for measuring and drawing)</div><b>χάραμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dawn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sunrise, daybreak</div><b>χάρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. thanks to</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (reflexive) for one&#x27;s sake [with <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia)] (for the benefit or regard of someone or something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για <b>χάρη</b> σου θα κάνουμε οτιδήποτε. - For your sake, we&#x27;ll do anything.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. favour (UK), favor (US) (a deed in which help is voluntarily provided)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. pardon (releasing order)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. grace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek χάρις. Also see χάριν and χάρις f.</div><b>χάρηκα πολύ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>It was a pleasure meeting you (literally: I have been very pleased)</div><b>χάριν</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (with genitive) for, as (equivalent to the Latin gratia)</div>
  <div class='ex'>see related formal expressions, some, with the alternative, not formal: χάρη (chári)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated, with genitive) for, for the benefit/sake of, (implies something positive)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πολλοί έχουν πεθάνει <b>χάριν</b> της Ελλάδος. - Many have died for Greece.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χάριν, accusative singular form of χάρις used adverbially.
For sense &quot;as&quot;, semantic loan from New Latin gratia.
Also see χάρις &amp; χάρη f.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative singular of χάρις - standard: <a href="bword://χάρη">χάρη</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χάριν, accusative singular form of χάρις used adverbially.
For sense &quot;as&quot;, semantic loan from New Latin gratia.
Also see χάρις &amp; χάρη f.</div><b>χάρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>endowment, gift (of a personal quality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είχε το <b>χάρισμα</b> της μαντικής. - She has the gift of divination.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek χάρισμα. By surface analysis, (χαρίζω) χαρισ- charis-, “to grant”) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>χάρτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. map, chart</div>
  <div class='def'>2. charter, project charter, constitution</div>
  <div class='ex'>καταστατικός <b>χάρτης</b> - charter</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (dated) synonym of <a href="bword://χαρτί">χαρτί</a> n in the set phrase</div>
  <div class='ex'>εμπόριο χάρτου (empório chártou, “trading of paper”)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek χάρτης (“a papyrus scroll”), semantic loan from Italian carta or from English chart, from Latin charta, from Ancient Greek χάρτης. Doublet of κάρτα.</div><b>χάρτης ηλιοτροπίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>litmus paper</div><b>χάρτινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χάρτινη, neuter χάρτινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. paper (made of paper)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (familiar, figuratively) paper (insubstantial)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek χάρτινος. By surface analysis, χαρτ(ί)) + -ινος (-inos).</div><b>χάσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χασίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loss</div>
  <div class='def'>2. waste</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χάνω, stem χασ- + χάνω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>χάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(usually) hassium</div><b>χάσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chasm</div>
  <div class='def'>2. gap</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) chasm, rift</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek χάσμα, from χάσκω. For the sense &quot;gap&quot;, semantic loan from English chasm.</div><b>χάφτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χάφτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scorpionfish: Scorpaena scrofa</div><b>χάφτω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to gobble up, eat up, devour, eat like a horse, binge, overindulge, (idiomatic): wolf down, scarf down, pig out, stuff one&#x27;s face (with gluttony)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι Ουισκόνσιναϊτ <b>χάφτουνε</b> τυροπήγματα, λουκάνικα και μπίρες. - Wisconsinites gorge themselves on cheese curds, brats and beer.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to swallow, buy, fall for, go for, be tricked, hoodwinked, fooled, believe, buy, buy into, swallow (a lie without questioning)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ό,τι του πεις, το <b>χάφτει</b>· τόσο αφελής είναι! - He&#x27;ll fall for anything you say; he&#x27;s ridiculously naive!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χάπτω, from Ancient Greek κάπτω. Also see χάβω.</div><b>χάχανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χάχανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) giggle, snigger, cackle, laugh loudly, unstoppably, often unnecessarily</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic, inherited from Byzantine Greek χάχανον.</div><b>χάχας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χάχες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, familiar) one who laughs unnecessarily</div>
  <div class='def'>2. booby, simpleton</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι γελάτε, ρε <b>χάχες</b>; - What are you laughing at, you idiots?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic, from the laughing sound χα χα (cha cha).</div><b>χάψιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) gobbling up</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) swallowing (accepting a lie without questioning)</div><b>χέζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (active, colloquial, vulgar) to shit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μωρό <b>χέζει</b> στο γκιογκιό. - The baby shits in the potty.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, vulgar) to shit oneself</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μωρό <b>χέστηκε</b>. - The baby shat itself.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, vulgar) to be on the verge of shitting oneself, badly need the toilet</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη μπάντα, <b>χέζομαι</b>! - Out of the way, I&#x27;m about to shit myself!</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, figuratively, vulgar) to fear, joy (shit oneself from)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλοι περίμεναν ότι θα ήταν γενναίος, αλλά <b>χέστηκε</b> απ’ τον φόβο του κι έτρεξε να φύγει. - Everyone expected him to be brave, but he shat himself from fear and ran away.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (colloquial, figuratively, vulgar) to be rich in, have a lot of, be be rolling in (money)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν ενδιαφέρεται να κάνει οικονομίες, <b>χέζεται</b> στα λεφτά. - She doesn&#x27;t care to economise, she&#x27;s rolling in money.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (in the simple past only) to be indifferent, not give a damn, not give a shit</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χέστηκα</b> άμα θα αργήσεις εσύ ή όχι. - I don&#x27;t give a shit if you are late or not.</div><b>χέζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, intransitive, vulgar) to shit, shite, take a shit, crap (defecate)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Που είναι η τουαλέτα; Πρέπει να <b>χέσω</b>. - Where&#x27;s the toilet? I have to shit.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, transitive, vulgar) to shit, beshit (soil something or someone with faeces)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κατά λάθος, <b>έχεσε</b> το βρακί της. - She shit her knickers by accident.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, transitive) to scold, berate, bollock, give shit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το αφεντικό του τον <b>έχεσε</b>. - His boss gave him shit.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χέζω.</div><b>χέλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χέλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χέλι(ν)), from Koine Greek [Term?], from Ancient Greek ἐγχέλειον, diminutive of ἔγχελυς.</div><b>χέλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Cyprus) want, wish</div><b>χένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>henna dye produced from plant Lawsonia inermis</div>
  <div class='ex'>For the plant, see feminine κίνα (kína)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English henna, from Arabic حِنّاء. Doublet of κινά (neuter) via Turkish.</div><b>χέννα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χέννες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dated spelling of <a href="bword://χένα">χένα</a> without simplification of double consonant</div><b>χέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. arm (portion of the upper human appendage from the shoulder to the wrist)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Της έκοψαν το ένα <b>χέρι</b> λόγω γάγγραινας. - They cut off one of her arms due to gangrene.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hand (part of the fore limb below the forearm or wrist)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πλύνε τα <b>χέρια</b> σου πριν φας. - Wash your hands before you eat.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) handle (of a tool or implement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πιάσε το <b>χέρι</b> του μπαστουνιού καλά, παππού. - Grab the handle of the walking stick tight, grandpa.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) coat (of paint)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα περάσουμε τον τοίχο δύο <b>χέρια</b> χρώμα ακόμη. - We will give the wall two further coats of paint.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (figuratively) handball (offence of touching the ball with the hands)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο διαιτητής σφύριξε το <b>χέρι</b>. - The referee blew the whistle for handball.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χέριν, from Koine Greek χέριον, diminutive of Ancient Greek χείρ, from Proto-Hellenic *kʰéhər, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰésōr (“hand”). Doublet of χειρ (cheir).</div><b>χέρι-χέρι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hand in hand</div><b>χέσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χεσίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) shit, dump, crap (the act of shitting)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάω για <b>χέσιμο</b>. - I&#x27;m going for a shit.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, vulgar) scolding, bollocking (the act of being berated or scolded)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν πάει στη δουλειά σήμερα, θα φάει ένα <b>χέσιμο</b> από τον προϊστάμενό του. - When he goes to work today, he&#x27;ll get a bollocking from his manager.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χέζω + -ιμο (-imo)</div><b>χέστηκε η φοράδα στ&#x27; αλώνι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, vulgar) Nothing significant or of importance has happened; so what, who cares.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, “the mare shat herself on the threshing floor”.</div><b>χέστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χέστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (offensive, vulgar) one who has fecal incontinence, whether momentary (e.g. from extreme fright), temporary (from immature age), or permanent (due to illness or advanced age)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, derogatory, vulgar) chickenshit, scaredy cat, wuss, pussy (someone afraid of everything)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πώς να αντιμετωπίσει έναν κλέφτη αυτός ο <b>χέστης</b>; - How can such a wimp face a burglar?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χέζω + -ης (-is, “masculine noun-forming suffix”)</div><b>χέστρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χέστρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) shitter, can (toilet)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγαινε να καθαρίσεις αυτήν την <b>χέστρα</b>! - Go and clean that shitter!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χέζω + -τρα (-tra)</div><b>χήνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χήνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χήν, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰans-.</div><b>χήρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χήρες, masculine χήρος)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>widow, relict</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χήρα, possibly from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰeh₁ro- (“derelict”), from the root *ǵʰeh₁- (“to leave behind, abandon”). Cognate with Latin heres.</div><b>χήρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χήροι, feminine χήρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>widower</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χῆρος.</div><b>χήτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χαίτη">χαίτη</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Dionysios Solomos, Ode to the death of Lord Byron @greek-language.gr, stanza 95.
«Πού είν’» θα λένε σαστισμένοι
«το Λεοντάρι το Αγγλικό;
Είναι η χήτη του πεσμένη
και το μούγκρισμα βουβό».
«Poú eín’» tha léne sastisménoi
«to Leontári to Anglikó?
Eínai i chíti tou pesméni
kai to moúgkrisma vouvó». - &quot;Where is&quot; they say astonished / &quot;the English Lion? / Its mane is fallen / and its roar is mute&quot;.</div><b>χίασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χιάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chiasma</div>
  <div class='def'>2. diagonal cross, saltire, Saint Andrew&#x27;s cross</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χῑ́ασμα.</div><b>χίλια</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>one thousand</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χίλια</b> χρόνια ιστορίας - a thousand years of history</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χῑ́λιοι, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰesliyoy, from *ǵʰes-.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of χίλιοι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χῑ́λιοι, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰesliyoy, from *ǵʰes-.</div><b>χίλια δυο</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) many, an indefinite number of</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (excessive, indeclinable) thousand, million, billion</div>
  <div class='ex'>χίλια δυο πράγματα - a million things</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (excessive, indeclinable) a thousand and one</div>
  <div class='ex'>χίλια δυο προβλήματα - a thousand and one problems</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (indeclinable) half a dozen (when used in an approximative sense)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (indeclinable) all sorts/kinds of</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (indeclinable) so many</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χίλια + δυο (dyo, “two”), two signifying plurality in general.</div><b>χίμαιρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χίμαιρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chimera, chimaera (an unrealistic dream)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chimera, chimaera (a monster)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. chimera, chimaera (an organism consisting of genetically different parts)</div><b>χίντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Hindi language</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μιλάτε <b>χίντι</b>; - Do you speak Hindi?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Unadapted learned borrowing from English Hindi, from Hindi हिंदी or Persian هندی.
As neuter plural with article τα (ta), in the pattern of substantivised adjectives for language names; compare αγγλικά n pl.
As feminine with article η (i), by ellipsis of the noun γλώσσα f.</div><b>χίπης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χίπηδες, feminine χίπισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hippy, hippie</div><b>χίπις</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χίπης">χίπης</a></div><b>χίπισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χίπισσες, masculine χίπης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hippy, hippie</div><b>χίππης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χίππηδες, feminine χίππισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χίπης">χίπης</a></div><b>χίππισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χίππισσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χίπισσα">χίπισσα</a></div><b>χαΐνης</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fugitive, mutineer, outlaw; partisan, rebel, insurgent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (slang) slacker</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Turkish hain (“deceitful, traitor”), from Arabic خائن.</div><b>χαίρε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second-person singular imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://χαίρω">χαίρω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χαῖρε.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) the last farewell (of an obituary speech) in expression</div>
  <div class='ex'>το ύστατο χαίρε (to ýstato chaíre)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Deverbal.</div><b>χαίρετε</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>second-person plural imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://χαίρω">χαίρω</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hello</div>
  <div class='def'>2. goodbye; farewell</div><b>χαίρομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be happy, to be glad</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χαίρω πολύ! <b>Χαίρομαι</b> που σας γνωρίζω από κοντά. Έχω ακούσει τόσα πολλά για σας. - Pleased to meet you! I am happy to meet you in person. I have heard so much about you.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to enjoy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>χάρηκα</b> πολύ το μπάνιο στη θάλασσα. Το νερό ήταν ζεστό και δεν είχε κύματα. - I enjoyed swimming in the sea very much. The water was warm and there were no waves.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (idiomatic, transitive) must be proud</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πόσων χρονών είναι η κορούλα σας; Πέντε! Να την <b>χαίρεστε</b>. - How old is your little daughter? Five! You must be so proud.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek χαίρομαι, from Ancient Greek χαίρω, from Proto-Hellenic *kʰəřřō, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰer-.</div><b>χαίρω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(no past) to be happy, be glad</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χαίρε</b> Μαρία - Hail Mary</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χαίρω.</div><b>χαίρω πολύ</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pleased to meet you (literally: I am very pleased)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, ironic, sarcastic) (of well known facts introduced as novel)</div>
  <div class='ex'>—Ξέρεις, έχει κάνει δύο πλαστικές εγχειρίσεις. —<b>Χαίρω πολύ</b>, κάτι μας είπες τώρα. Είναι πασίγνωστο. - —Have you heard? She has had two plastic surgeries. —Thanks for that, I didn&#x27;t know (!) It is well-known.</div><b>χαίτη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mane</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χαίτη.</div><b>χαβαρόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαβαρόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rook (Corvus frugilegus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, χάβαρο + -όνι. Possibly related to Czech havran and Slovak havran or another term from Proto-Slavic *gàvornъ (“corvid”) and altered by folk etymology.</div><b>χαβιάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαβιάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caviar (roe of the sturgeon or other large fish)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish خاویار.</div><b>χαγιάτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαγιάτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) covered balcony, open or closed with glass, an extension of the interior</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εκεί στεκόταν ένα, με χρωματιστά τζάμια, αρχοντικό, που έβλεπε σε δύο θάλασσες. […] Μπροστά του ήταν η παραλία, είχε όμορφες κούρμπες που κρατούσαν τα <b>χαγιάτια</b> του […]. - There stood a mansion with colored windows, overlooking two seas. […] In front of it there was the beach, it had beautiful curves that held its balconies […] .</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish حیاط (hayat, “courtyard”) -Turkish hayat (“in sense: courtyard”), from Arabic حِيَاط plural of حَائِط.
Not to be confused with the Persian حیات and Arabic حَيَاة.</div><b>χαζαμάρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of χαζαμάρα</div>
  <div class='ex'>alternative: χαζομάρας (chazomáras)</div><b>χαζαμάρες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χαζαμάρα</div>
  <div class='ex'>alternative: χαζομάρες (chazomáres)</div><b>χαζεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to become stupid</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to loiter, hang around, looking around</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάμε να <b>χαζέψουμε</b> τις βιτρίνες; - Shall we go take a look at shop windows?</div><b>χαζοκούτι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαζοκούτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, humorous) idiot box, boob tube (the television)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλη μέρα κάθεται και κοιτάει το <b>χαζοκούτι</b> αντί να μελετάει. - All day she sits and watches the idiot box instead of studying.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαζός χαζο- (chazo-, “stupid, idiotic”) + κουτί.</div><b>χαζομάρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαζομάρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) stupidity, idiocy (the quality of being stupid)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>χαζομάρα</b> αυτού του παιδιού είναι άλλο πράγμα. - The stupidity of this child is something else.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) nonsense, stupidity, idiocy, rubbish (stupid behaviour or talk)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μη λες <b>χαζομάρες</b> . - Stop saying silly things - literally: Don&#x27;t say silly things.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαζο- (chazo-, “stupid, idiotic”) + -μάρα or from χαζ(ός)) + -ομάρα &amp; -αμάρα for the variant χαζαμάρα.</div><b>χαζός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαζή, neuter χαζό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. unintelligent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. silly, daft, simple</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish حظ (haz), from Arabic حَظّ.</div><b>χαιρέκακα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χαιρέκακος">χαιρέκακος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>malevolently, maliciously</div><b>χαιρέκακος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαιρέκακη, neuter χαιρέκακο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maliciously gleeful, spiteful, rejoicing on someone&#x27;s misfortune</div><b>χαιρέτισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαιρετίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. greeting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) regards, greetings (an expression of good wishes to be passed to another person)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (in plural, ironic) regards, greetings (an expression of good wishes to be passed to another person); I&#x27;m not holding my breath (said cynically to suggest that what has just been mentioned is unlikely to happen soon or at all)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek or Byzantine Greek χαιρέτισμα. By surface analysis, χαιρετισ-) + -μα (-ma).</div><b>χαιρεκακία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαιρεκακίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>schadenfreude, gleeful malice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek χαιρεκακία, from χαιρέκακ(ος)) + -ία. See the ancient χαίρω &amp; κακός.</div><b>χαιρετάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to say hello, say goodbye, greet, salute</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to wave, bow</div><b>χαιρετισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαιρετισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. greeting, salutation</div>
  <div class='def'>2. salute</div><b>χαιρετώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χαιρετάω">χαιρετάω</a></div><b>χαιτοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαιτοφόρος or χαιτοφόρα, neuter χαιτοφόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maned</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαίτη + -φόρος.</div><b>χαιτοφόρος λύκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαιτοφόροι λύκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maned wolf (Chrysocyon brachyurus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, χαιτοφόρος + λύκος.</div><b>χακί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) khaki (of color/colour)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English khaki, with the pronunciation of French khaki.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) khaki (that range of colours from yellow to green/brown)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, indeclinable) the army; military service</div>
  <div class='ex'>ντύθηκε στο <b>χακί</b> - he serves his military service</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English khaki, with the pronunciation of French khaki.</div><b>χαλάζι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαλάζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hail (a storm of icy rain; individual pieces)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) particles falling in the manner of hail</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα βόλια έπεφταν <b>χαλάζι</b>. - A hail of bullets.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαλάζιν, from Koine Greek χαλάζιον, from Ancient Greek χάλαζα.</div><b>χαλάζιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαλάζια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chalazion (cyst on the eyelid)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek χαλάζιον, from Ancient Greek χάλαζα.</div><b>χαλάρωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαλαρώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relaxation</div><b>χαλάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to break, ruin, destroy, wreck, spoil (damage, either permanently or that it requires repair)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο αδελφός μου <b>χάλασε</b> το κινητό μου. - My brother broke my mobile phone.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to ruin, spoil (ruin the character of by overindulgence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δίνοντάς του ό,τι ήθελε, τον <b>χάλασε</b> η μάνα του. - By giving him whatever he wanted, his mother spoilt him.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of money) to break (divide into smaller units)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μπορείς να μου <b>χαλάσεις</b> ένα πενηντάρικο; - Can you break a fifty for me?</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to waste (squander uselessly)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χάλασε</b> την ζωή της με αυτόν τον άνθρωπο. - She wasted her life with that man.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of people) to kill (put to death)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (of a virgin) to deflower (take the virginity of)</div>
  <div class='def'>7. (also, figuratively, intransitive) to break, break down, become ruined (become damaged, unusable or no longer viable)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ρολόι <b>χάλασε</b> μέσα στη θάλασσα. - The watch broke in the sea.</div>
  <div class='def'>8. (of food) to spoil (become bad, sour or rancid)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χαλάσανε</b> τα φρούτα, πέταξέ τα. - The fruits have spoiled, throw them out.</div>
  <div class='def'>9. (of body parts) to become ruined</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χάλασαν</b> τα μάτια του επειδή δεν διάβαζε με γυαλιά όταν τα χρειαζόταν. - His eyes were ruined because he didn&#x27;t read with glasses when he needed them.</div>
  <div class='def'>10. (of teeth) to decay, rot</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αν εξακουλουθήσεις να τρως γλυκά, θα <b>χαλάσουν</b> τα δόντια σου. - If you continue to eat sweets, your teeth will rot.</div>
  <div class='def'>11. (of weather) to become bad, get worse</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όλη την εβδομάδα είχε λιακάδα αλλά ξαφνικά <b>χάλασε</b> ο καιρός. - There was sunshine all week but suddenly the weather turned.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χαλῶ) + -άω, from Ancient Greek χαλάω / χαλῶ.</div><b>χαλί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαλιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rug</div>
  <div class='def'>2. carpet</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish خالی, from Classical Persian خالی.</div><b>χαλίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαλίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gravel</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pebble</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαλίκι(ν)), from χαλίκιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek χάλιξ.</div><b>χαλίφης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαλίφηδες or χαλίφες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>caliph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed.</div><b>χαλαζίας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαλαζίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quartz</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek χαλαζίας, from Ancient Greek χάλαζα.</div><b>χαλαρά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χαλαρός">χαλαρός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>loosely</div><b>χαλαρωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαλαρωτική, neuter χαλαρωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relaxing (helping to relax)</div><b>χαλαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαλαρή, neuter χαλαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loose, slack</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) lax, relaxed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χᾰλᾰρός.</div><b>χαλαρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαλαρότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>laxity (state of not being strict)</div><b>χαλαρώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to loosen, slacken (rope, etc)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to relax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χᾰλᾰρόω, from χᾰλᾰρός + -όω.</div><b>χαλασμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine χαλασμένη, neuter χαλασμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rancid, stale, spoilt</div>
  <div class='def'>2. out of order, broken-down, unserviceable, spoilt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of χαλιέμαι, passive voice -with different sense- of χαλάω, χαλώ (“destroy, spoil”).</div><b>χαλβάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαλβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. halva, confection usually made from crushed sesame seeds and honey</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of a person) sluggish, slothful, languid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish حلوا (halva, helva), from Classical Persian حلوا, from Arabic حَلْوَى.</div><b>χαλινάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαλινάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bridle (headgear for horse, including bit and reins)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. bit (for horse)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek χαλινάριον.</div><b>χαλινός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαλινοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. formal variant of <a href="bword://χαλινάρι">χαλινάρι</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. frenulum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χαλινός.</div><b>χαλκογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαλκογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chalcography</div><b>χαλκογόνο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαλκογόνα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chalcogen, a member of the oxygen group of elements</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From chalco- (χαλκο- (chalko-)) + -gen instead of expected -gon, neuter of -γόνος, as in French chalcogène, English chalcogen, etc. From Ancient Greek χαλκός m + γόνος m. Morphologically, substantivised neuter of adjective χαλκογόνος.</div><b>χαλκοκολλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to braze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαλκός + κολλώ</div><b>χαλκοκουρούνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαλκοκουρούνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Coracias garrulus, the roller, the European roller</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαλκο- (chalko-, “like copper”) + κουρούνα</div><b>χαλκομανία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural χαλκομανίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) decalcomania</div><b>χαλκός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαλκοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>copper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek χαλκός &amp; semantic loan from English bronze.</div><b>χαλκόχρωμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαλκόχρωμη, neuter χαλκόχρωμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>copper-colored</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, χαλκο- (chalko-, “copper”) + χρώμα + -ος (-os, adjectival suffix).</div><b>χαλυβουργία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαλυβουργίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steel industry, steelworks</div><b>χαλυβουργείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαλυβουργεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>steelworks, steel plant</div><b>χαλώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χαλάω">χαλάω</a></div><b>χαμάμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) Turkish bath</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish حمام (hamam), from Arabic حَمَّام.</div><b>χαμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine χαμένη, neuter χαμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lost, missing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lost, defeated</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) lost</div>
  <div class='ex'>είναι <b>χαμένος</b> στις σκέψεις - he is lost in thought(s).</div>
  <div class='def'>4. wasted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of χάνομαι, passive voice of χάνω.</div><b>χαμίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>street urchin, guttersnipe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French gamin. First attested in the 1862 Greek translation by Ioannis-Isidoridis Skylitsis of Les Miserables (by Victor Hugo). Apparently, a translation‑neologism, [g] &gt; [x] instead of [γ], trying to avoid the derogatory *γαμίνι, or intended to remind of χαμένο (“lost”).</div><b>χαμαιλέοντας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαμαιλέοντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chameleon</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek χαμαιλέων, from the accusative singular τὸν χαμαιλέοντα.</div><b>χαμαιτυπείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμαιτυπεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brothel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek χαμαιτυπεῖον.</div><b>χαμηλά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χαμηλός">χαμηλός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>low</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το ελικόπτερο πέταξε <b>χαμηλά</b> πάνω από το νησί. - The helicopter flew low over the island.</div><b>χαμηλά ανάγλυφα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χαμηλό <a href="bword://ανάγλυφο">ανάγλυφο</a></div><b>χαμηλές παλίρροιες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χαμηλή <a href="bword://παλίρροια">παλίρροια</a></div><b>χαμηλή παλίρροια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαμηλές παλίρροιες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neap tide</div><b>χαμηλοβλεπούσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαμηλοβλεπούσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous) shrinking violet (very shy woman, who figuratively won&#x27;t even look anyone in the eye)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νομίζει ότι το να παίζει την <b>χαμηλοβλέπουσα</b> έλκει τους άνδρες. - She thinks that acting the shrinking violet attracts men.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαμηλο- (chamilo-, “low”) + βλέπ(ω), “I see, look”) + -ούσα</div><b>χαμηλό ανάγλυφο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμηλά ανάγλυφα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>low relief, bas relief</div><b>χαμηλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαμηλή, neuter χαμηλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. low, lowered</div>
  <div class='def'>2. base</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek χαμηλός. Then Ancient Greek sense: &quot;trivial&quot;.</div><b>χαμηλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to lower (to cause to descend)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to lower (to fall; to sink)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to lower (to depress as to direction)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (transitive) to lower (to reduce the height of)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to lower (to reduce the degree, intensity, strength, etc., of)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to lower (to reduce (something) in value, amount, etc.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαμηλώνω. By surface analysis, χαμηλ(ός)) + -ώνω.</div><b>χαμογελάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, literally) smile</div>
  <div class='ex'>μου <b>χαμογέλασε</b> - (he/she/it) smiled at me. (gave me a smile)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαμογελ(ώ)) + -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαμογελῶ. Morphologically, χαμο- (chamo-), here in sense &quot;a little&quot; + γελώ.</div><b>χαμογελαστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαμογελαστή, neuter χαμογελαστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>smiling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαμογελασ-) + -τός.</div><b>χαμογελώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>variant of <a href="bword://χαμογελάω">χαμογελάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαμογελῶ and see more at χαμογελάω.</div><b>χαμομήλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμομήλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>camomile (plant, dried plant and infusion)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From unattested diminutive form *χαμομήλιον of the Medieval Byzantine Greek χαμόμηλ(ον)) + -ιον (-ion), from Koine Greek χαμαίμηλον, from χαμαί (&gt; χάμ(ω)) + -ο- (-o-) &gt; χαμο- (chamo-) + μῆλον.</div><b>χαμομίλια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χαμομίλι</div><b>χαμομιλιού</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive singular of χαμομίλι</div><b>χαμομιλιών</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive plural of χαμομίλι</div><b>χαμπάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμπάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) news (new information of interest)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι <b>χαμπάρια</b> μας φέρνεις; - What news do you bring us?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish خبر (haber), from Arabic خَبَر.</div><b>χαμπέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμπέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χαμπάρι">χαμπάρι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish خبر (haber), from Arabic خَبَر.</div><b>χαμσίνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμσίνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot, Greek, Modern, Standard) khamsin (a hot wind in Egypt)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic خَمْسِين.</div><b>χαμωτίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαμωτίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>little bustard, Tetrax tetrax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαμο- (chamo-, “low; living near the ground”) + ωτίδα.</div><b>χαμόγελο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμόγελα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. boat neckline, a neckline which resembles a smile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from χαμογελ(ώ)) + -ο (-o, suffix for neutral nouns).</div><b>χαμόμηλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμόμηλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a less common form of <a href="bword://χαμομήλι">χαμομήλι</a>: camomile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαμόμηλον, from Koine Greek χαμαίμηλον, from χαμαί (&gt; χάμ(ω)) + -ο- (-o-) &gt; χαμό- + μῆλον. Compare to χαμομήλι.</div><b>χαμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loss</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εκτός από τη μάνα σου κανείς δε σε θυμάται,
Σε τούτο το τρομακτικό ταξίδι του <b>χαμού</b>. - Apart from your mother, no one remembers you,
On this frightening journey of loss.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, numeral) pandemonium, chaos, mayhem, upheaval</div>
  <div class='ex'>Γινότανε <b>χαμός</b> στον δρόμο απόψε. - There was mayhem on the road tonight.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαμός, from χά(νω), “lose”) + -μός.</div><b>χαμόσπιτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαμόσπιτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hovel</div><b>χαντάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαντάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gutter or ditch (beside a roadway)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χανδάκιον, diminutive of Koine Greek χάνδαξ, from Arabic خَنْدَق, ultimately from Iranian.</div><b>χαοτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαοτική, neuter χαοτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chaotic</div><b>χαρά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. joy, pleasure</div>
  <div class='def'>2. also a feminine name Χαρά</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek χαρά.</div><b>χαράδρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαράδρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gorge, ravine</div><b>χαράζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be curveded and see <a href="bword://χαράζω">χαράζω</a></div><b>χαράζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (figuratively) to engrave, carve; to imprint in memory</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to engrave, carve; to make rules</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to engrave, carve; to scar</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (impersonal) to dawn → χαράζει</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χαράσσω from past tense:‑αξα to present:‑άζω, in the pattern of στάζω, ἔσταξα.</div><b>χαράμι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>in vain, for nothing</div>
  <div class='ex'>όλες οι θυσίες μου πήγαν <b>χαράμι</b>. - All my sacrifices were in vain.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish حرام (haram) from Arabic حَرَام.</div><b>χαράτσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαράτσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (historical) poll tax (under the Ottomans)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) any heavy tax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαράτσι, from Ottoman Turkish خراج, from Arabic خَرَاج.</div><b>χαρέμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρέμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>harem</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish حرم (harem) (modern Turkish harem).</div><b>χαρίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be given as gift</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) to pardon, remit, do favour</div><b>χαρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to give or offer as a gift</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (informal) to remit, pardon see <a href="bword://χαρίζομαι">χαρίζομαι</a></div><b>χαραγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαραγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>notch</div><b>χαρακτήρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαρακτήρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. character (the qualities which identify a person)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. character (a person&#x27;s behaviours which identify them)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. character, letter, symbol</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek χᾰρᾰκτήρ, from the accusative singular &quot;τὸν χαρακτῆρα&quot;. From χαράσσω.</div><b>χαρακτηρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to characterize (to be typical of)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to characterize (to depict someone or something a particular way)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to categorize</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek χαρακτηρίζω. By surface analysis, χαρακτήρ + -ίζω</div><b>χαρακτηρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαρακτηρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. characterization</div>
  <div class='def'>2. denomination, designation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek χαρακτηρισμός. By surface analysis, χαρακτηρισ-) + -μός.</div><b>χαρακτηριστικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>characteristically</div>
  <div class='ex'>Older form: χαρακτηριστικῶς (kharaktēristikôs)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χαρακτηριστικός">χαρακτηριστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://χαρακτηριστικό">χαρακτηριστικό</a></div><b>χαρακτηριστικές ομάδες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χαρακτηριστική <a href="bword://ομάδα">ομάδα</a></div><b>χαρακτηριστική ομάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρακτηριστικές ομάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>functional group</div><b>χαρακτηριστικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://χαρακτηριστικός">χαρακτηριστικός</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Older form: χαρακτηριστικόν (charaktiristikón)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://χαρακτηριστικός">χαρακτηριστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρακτηριστικά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>characteristic, feature</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το πρόσωπό της είχε ωραία <b>χαρακτηριστικά</b>. - Her face had beautiful characteristics.</div><b>χαρακτηριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαρακτηριστική, neuter χαρακτηριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>characteristic, typical</div><b>χαρακώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to rule (with lines)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to entrench</div>
  <div class='def'>3. alternative form of <a href="bword://χαράζω">χαράζω</a></div><b>χαραμίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to waste, squander</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χαραμίσαμε</b> εκατομμύρια ευρώ και αρκετά χρόνια γι’ αυτό το έργο. - We squandered millions of euros and several years on that project.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to throw away</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαράμ(ι)) + -ίζω.</div><b>χαραμοφάης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαραμοφάηδες, feminine χαραμοφάισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) freeloader, good-for-nothing, parasite</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαράμ(ι), “for nothing, in vain”) + -ο- (-o-) + φα(ΐ), “food”) + ending -ης (-is).</div><b>χαραμοφάισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαραμοφάισσες, masculine χαραμοφάης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>freeloader, good-for-nothing, parasite</div><b>χαραυγή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαραυγές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dawn, daybreak</div><b>χαριτωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαριτωμένη, neuter χαριτωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pretty, lovely, attractive (especially of girls, children, women, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek κεχαριτωμένος, with apheresis of the first syllable.</div><b>χαρμολύπη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρμολύπες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>the state of feeling happiness and sadness at the same time</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek χαρμολύπη. From χάρμ(α), “joy”) + -ο- (-o-) + λύπη.</div><b>χαροποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cheer up. gladden</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to exhilarate</div><b>χαρουπιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρουπιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carob tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαρούπ(ι)) + -ιά.</div><b>χαρούμενα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>joyfully</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χαρούμενος">χαρούμενος</a></div><b>χαρούμενος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine χαρούμενη, neuter χαρούμενο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>happy, cheerful, joyful, merry</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χαρούμενο</b> τραγούδι - joyful song</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαρούμενος, from χαιρούμενος with influence of noun χαρά, a passive present participle of χαίρω, with ending -ούμενος.</div><b>χαρούπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρούπια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>carob (fruit)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Arabic خَرُّوب</div><b>χαρτάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a small piece of paper child)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sticker, label</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cigarette paper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαρτ(ί), “paper”) + -άκι</div><b>χαρτί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paper</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαρτί / χαρτίν, from Koine Greek χαρτίον, diminutive of the Ancient Greek χάρτης.</div><b>χαρτί ηλιοτροπίου</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>litmus paper</div><b>χαρτί κουζίνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτιά κουζίνας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paper towel, kitchen towel</div><b>χαρτί τουαλέτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτιά τουαλέτας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toilet paper, toilet tissue, lavatory paper</div><b>χαρτί υγείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτιά υγείας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>toilet paper, toilet tissue, lavatory paper</div><b>χαρταετός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαρταετοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kite (toy)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πέταξαν τα όχι, τα γιατί
στον αέρα σαν <b>χαρταετοί</b>,
παίρνω, αφήνω δρόμους και γυρνώ
λες κι έχω ένα φιλί παντοτινό. - The no&#x27;s, the why&#x27;s threw away
in the air like kites,
I take, leave roads and return
as if I have an everlasting kiss.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαρτ- (chart-, “paper”) + αετός. First attested in 1861.</div><b>χαρτζιλίκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτζιλίκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pocket money, allowance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish خرجلق.</div><b>χαρτιά κουζίνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://χαρτί">χαρτί</a> κουζίνας</div><b>χαρτιά τουαλέτας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://χαρτί">χαρτί</a> τουαλέτας</div><b>χαρτιά υγείας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://χαρτί">χαρτί</a> υγείας</div><b>χαρτογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural χαρτογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cartographer, mapmaker</div><b>χαρτογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρτογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cartography, mapmaking</div><b>χαρτοκόπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαρτοκόπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paperknife, letter opener</div><b>χαρτομάντης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαρτομάντες, feminine χαρτομάντισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cartomancer (fortune teller who uses playing cards)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαρτο- (charto-, “card; paper”) + μάντης. First attested 1886 in Katharevousa χαρτομάντις.</div><b>χαρτομάντιλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτομάντιλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tissue paper, paper handkerchief, paper towel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαρτο- (charto-, “of paper”) + μαντίλ(ι), “handkerchief”) + suffix for neuter -ο (-o).</div><b>χαρτομάντισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρτομάντισσες, masculine χαρτομάντης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cartomancer (fortune teller who uses playing cards)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαρτομάντ(ης)) + -ισσα (-issa).</div><b>χαρτομαντεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρτομαντείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cartomancy (the supposed prediction of the future with playing cards)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French cartomancie, from carto- + μαντεία f. By surface analysis, χαρτο- (charto-, “card”) + -μαντεία, from χαρτί n and μαντεία f.</div><b>χαρτονόμισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτονομίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>banknote (paper money)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαρτο- (charto-, “paper”) + νόμισμα, a calque of French papier-monnaie or English paper money.</div><b>χαρτοπετσέτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρτοπετσέτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paper napkin (part of table setting)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαρτο- (charto-, “of paper”) + πετσέτα, a calque of English paper napkin.</div><b>χαρτοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stationery shop, stationer&#x27;s</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαρτο- (charto-, “paper”) + -πωλείο</div><b>χαρτοπωλεῖον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://χαρτοπωλείο">χαρτοπωλείο</a></div><b>χαρτοπώλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαρτοπώλες, feminine χαρτοπώλισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stationer (a seller of stationery)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαρτο- (charto-, “paper”) + -πώλης</div><b>χαρτοπώλισσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρτοπώλισσες, masculine χαρτοπώλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stationer (a seller of stationery)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From masculine χαρτοπώλ(ης)) + -ισσα (-issa). Morphologically, χαρτο- (charto-, “paper”) + -πώλισσα</div><b>χαρτορίχτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρτορίχτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cartomancer (fortune teller who uses playing cards)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαρτο- (charto-, “card”) + ρίχ(νω), “to throw, drop”) + -τρα (-tra)</div><b>χαρτοσακούλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρτοσακούλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>paper bag</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαρτο- (charto-) + σακούλα.</div><b>χαρτοφυλάκιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτοφυλάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. portfolio (the office and duties of a government minister)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. portfolio (investments, share holding)</div><b>χαρτοφύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαρτοφύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>briefcase, portfolio, document case</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek χαρτοφύλαξ, from χάρτης + φύλαξ and semantic loan from French portefeuille or Italian portafoglio.</div><b>χαρτούμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτούμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Egyptiot) hose</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Egyptian Arabic خرطوم, from Arabic خُرْطُوم.</div><b>χαρτωσιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαρτωσιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>trick, hand (in a game of cards)</div><b>χαρτόσημο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαρτόσημα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stamp</div>
  <div class='def'>2. revenue stamp, tax stamp</div><b>χαρχάλεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) rummaging, fumbling (the act of searching awkwardly and usually loudly in trying to find something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πες του να σταματήσει αυτό το ενοχλητικό <b>χαρχάλεμα</b> στα κουτιά! - Tell him to stop that annoying rummaging through the boxes!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαρχαλεύω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>χαρχαλεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, dialectal, intransitive) to rummage, fumble (search awkwardly and usually loudly in trying to find something)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χαρχαλεύει</b> στα κουτιά από το πρωί ψάχνοντας κάτι. - He&#x27;s been fumbling in the boxes since the morning looking for something.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Probably onomatopoeic. If not, reduplication of Ancient Greek χηλή.</div><b>χασάπης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χασάπηδες, feminine χασάπισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. butcher, meat seller, slaughterer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. murderer, tyrant, butcher, slaughterer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قصاب, from Arabic قَصَّاب.</div><b>χασάπικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χασάπικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. butcher&#x27;s shop, butcher&#x27;s</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Greek folk dance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ottoman Turkish قصاب, from Arabic قَصَّاب; + -ικο (-iko).</div><b>χασίς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) hashish</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ούζο, μορφίνη και <b>χασίς</b>, αμάν αμάν, ήπια για να ξεχάσω - Ouzo, morphine and hashish, aman, aman, I drank to forget</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, indeclinable) cannabis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Via Ottoman Turkish [Term?] from Arabic حَشِيش.</div><b>χασμουρητό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χασμουρητά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yawn (the action of yawning)</div><b>χασμουριέμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to yawn, gape</div><b>χασμωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χασμωδίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hiatus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. hiatus, break, interruption</div><b>χασομέρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χασομέρηδες, feminine χασομέρισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dawdler, loafer, loiterer, slacker (one who hangs around idly, wasting time)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χασομέρ(ι)) + -ης (-is).</div><b>χασομερώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to dally, to dilly-dally, to dawdle, to fanny about, to loiter, to waste time</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: χρονοτριβώ (chronotrivó)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to be late (not to arrive or do something on time)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (transitive) to make (somebody) late</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χασομέρ(ης)) + -ώ.</div><b>χαστουκίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to smack, slap (give a blow on the face with the open hand)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα σε <b>χαστουκίσω</b> αν μου ξαναβάλεις χέρι! - I&#x27;ll slap you if you grope me again!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαστούκ(ι), “slap”) + -ίζω.</div><b>χαστουκιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαστουκιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χαστούκι">χαστούκι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαστούκ(ι) n) + -ιά.</div><b>χαστούκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαστούκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smack, slap (blow on the cheek given with the open hand)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Τζένη του έδωσε <b>χαστούκι</b> όταν προσπάθησε να της βάλει χέρι. - Jenny gave him a slap when he tried to grope her.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) blow (damaging occurrence)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η ζωή του έδωσε πολλά <b>χαστούκια</b> αλλά το μεγαλύτερο ήταν ο θάνατος της γυναίκας του. - Life dealt him many blows, but the biggest was the death of his wife.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χαστούκι, of unknown origin. Due to the phonetics and phonotactics of the term, it is suspected to be an Arabic borrowing, though particularly strong candidates are lacking. Possible comparanda include Arabic خَدّ under this theory. It also appears plausible that the term is onomatopoeic.</div><b>χαστούκισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χαστουκίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>slapping, smacking (the act of being given a smack or slap)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτό το <b>χαστούκισμα</b> δεν θα το ξεχάσει ποτέ. - He&#x27;ll never forget that slap.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χαστουκίζω, stem χαστουκισ- + -μα (-ma)</div><b>χατ τρικ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) hat trick</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English hat trick.</div><b>χατ-τρικ</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of χατ τρικ (chat trik)</div><b>χαχανίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to giggle, snigger, laugh loudly without stopping, often without cause</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic, inherited from Byzantine Greek χαχανίζω from χάχαν(ον)) + -ίζω.</div><b>χαχαχα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hahaha</div><b>χαψιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χαψιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gobbet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. mouthful, gulp</div><b>χαϊδευτικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(endearing) endearingly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χαϊδευτικός">χαϊδευτικός</a></div><b>χαϊδευτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χαϊδευτική, neuter χαϊδευτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. endearing, of endearment (of a name, word, etc.: indicating affection)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. affectionate (characterized by or proceeding from affection)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χαϊδεύ(ω)) + -τικός.</div><b>χαϊδεύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be fondled</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to behave to cause to be fondled</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χαϊδεύεται</b> σα γατούλα.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to masturbate</div><b>χαϊδεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to caress (touch lovingly), pet, stroke, fondle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χαϊδεύω from χάιδι, stem χαϊδ- + -εύω.</div><b>χαϊντούκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χαϊντούκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(historical) a Balkan guerrilla under Ottoman rule</div><b>χγ.</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>abbreviation of <a href="bword://χειρόγραφο">χειρόγραφο</a> (manuscript)</div><b>χείλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χείλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lip (of the mouth)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lip, edge</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (plural normally) labia (of the vulva)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χεῖλος, from Proto-Indo-European *gʰel- (“to shout”). Compare to χείλι.</div><b>χείμαρρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χείμαρροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a torrent, an arroyo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek χείμαρρος, from the Ancient Greek adjective χειμάρρους, from χεῖμα + ῥέω.</div><b>χεζάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χεζάδες, feminine χεζού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, vulgar) chickenshit, scaredy cat, wuss, pussy (someone afraid of everything)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην τον βάλεις αυτόν να οδηγήσει, είναι σκέτος <b>χεζάς</b>! - Don&#x27;t let him drive, he&#x27;s a complete wuss!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χέζω + -άς</div><b>χεζού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χεζούδες, masculine χεζάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, vulgar) chickenshit, scaredy cat, wuss, pussy (someone afraid of everything)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μην την βάλεις αυτή να οδηγήσει, είναι σκέτη <b>χεζού</b>! - Don&#x27;t let her drive, she&#x27;s a complete wuss!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χέζω + -ού</div><b>χειλικός</b>
