  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χεριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) handful, hand, fistful grasp (that which can be held in a hand at once)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χέρι + -ιά.</div><b>χερούλι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χερούλια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. doorknob, door handle</div>
  <div class='def'>2. handle</div><b>χερσόνησος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χερσόνησοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>peninsula</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χερσόνησος. Doublet of Χερσώνα.</div><b>χερτζ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) hertz, cycle per second (units of frequency)</div><b>χερτς</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χερτζ">χερτζ</a> (chertz)</div><b>χεσμένος</b>
  <div><i>verb, masculine (feminine χεσμένη, neuter χεσμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, vulgar) beshitted, shit-stained, soiled (with faeces)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πέταξε το αυτό το <b>χεσμένο</b> βρακί! - Throw out that shit-stained pair of knickers!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ) not give a shit about, not give a damn about (someone or something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον έχω <b>χεσμένο</b>, αυτόν και την οικογένειά του! - I don&#x27;t give a shit about him and his family!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of χέζομαι, passive of χέζω.</div><b>χηλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hoof (of an animal)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. unguis</div><b>χημεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) chemistry</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, uncountable) chemistry (mutual attraction between two people)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Late Latin chēmia, from Ancient Greek χυμεία.</div><b>χημικές αντιδράσεις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χημική <a href="bword://αντίδραση">αντίδραση</a></div><b>χημική αντίδραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χημικές αντιδράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chemical reaction</div><b>χημικό στοιχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χημικά στοιχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chemical element</div><b>χημικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χημική, neuter χημικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chemical</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural χημικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chemist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chemistry teacher</div><b>χθες</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yesterday</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χθές, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰǵʰyés.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) the past</div>
  <div class='ex'>από το <b>χθες</b> στο σήμερα - from yesterday to today</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χθές, from Proto-Indo-European *dʰǵʰyés.</div><b>χι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) chi, the 22nd letter in the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χεῖ.</div><b>χιλιάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χιλιάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thousand</div>
  <div class='ex'>δύο <b>χιλιάδες</b> - two thousand</div><b>χιλιάνικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χιλιάνικη, neuter χιλιάνικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Chilean (relating to Chile or its people)</div><b>χιλιάρικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χιλιάρικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>one-thousand(-denominated) banknote (especially 1,000 drachmas)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χίλι(α)) + -άρικο.</div><b>χιλιετία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χιλιετίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>millennium</div><b>χιλιομετροδείκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>milestone (numbered marker placed along a road indicating distance in kilometers)</div><b>χιλιοστό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χιλιοστά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thousandth (1/1000)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χιλιοστός, from Ancient Greek χίλιοι.</div><b>χιλιοστόγραμμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χιλιοστόγραμμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>milligram, 1/1000 gram</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χιλιοστό- + part of γραμμάριο</div><b>χιλιοστόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>millimeter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χιλιοστό- + μέτρο</div><b>χιλιοστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χιλιοστή, neuter χιλιοστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thousandth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χίλιοι.</div><b>χιλιόγραμμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χιλιόγραμμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kilogram (1000 grams)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χιλιό- + part of γραμμάριο</div><b>χιλιόκυκλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) kilocycle</div><b>χιλιόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χιλιόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kilometre (UK), kilometer (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χιλιο- (chilio-) + μέτρο.</div><b>χιμπαντζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χιμπαντζήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χιμπατζής">χιμπατζής</a></div><b>χιμπατζής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χιμπατζήδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chimpanzee (Pan troglodytes)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, figuratively) minger, munter, moose (ugly person)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τι θέλει αυτή η όμορφη κοπέλα μ’ έναν κακάσχημο <b>χιμπατζή</b> σαν αυτόν; - What is a beautiful girl like her doing with a hideous munter like him?</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English chimpanzee.</div><b>χιονάνθρωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χιονάνθρωποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snowman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χιόνι + άνθρωπος, calque of English snowman.</div><b>χιονίζει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to snow, to be snowing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Impersonal verb, inherited from 3rd person Ancient Greek χιονίζει. By surface analysis, χιόνι + -ίζω.</div><b>χιονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncommon) to snow, to have snow fall (in literature)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. see: <a href="bword://χιονίζει">χιονίζει</a> (impersonal)</div><b>χιονιάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snowy weather</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χιονι(ά)) + -άς.</div><b>χιονοθύελλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χιονοθύελλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. blizzard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. snowstorm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χιόνι + -ο- (-o-) + θύελλα.</div><b>χιονονιφάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χιονονιφάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snowflake</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χιόνι + νιφάδα.</div><b>χιονοστιβάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χιονοστιβάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. avalanche</div>
  <div class='def'>2. snowdrift</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χιονο (chiono, “snow”) + στιβάδα</div><b>χιονοψάλτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χιονοψάλτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alpine accentor (Prunella collaris)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χιόνι + -ο- (-o-) + ψάλτης.</div><b>χιονόμπαλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χιονόμπαλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. snowball</div>
  <div class='def'>2. snow globe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χιόνι + μπάλα.</div><b>χιονόπτωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χιονοπτώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>snowfall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from χιόνι + -ό- + πτώση, a calque of German Schneefall.</div><b>χιουμοριστικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χιουμοριστικός">χιουμοριστικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>humorously</div><b>χιουμοριστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χιουμοριστική, neuter χιουμοριστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>humorous</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from χιουμοριστ(ής)) + -ικός, a calque of English humoristic.</div><b>χιούμορ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) humour (UK), humor (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English humour, from Old French humor, from Latin ūmor.</div><b>χιτζάμπ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) hijab</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from English hijab, from Arabic حِجَاب.</div><b>χιτών</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://χιτώνας">χιτώνας</a></div><b>χιτώνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chiton, tunic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fibrous connective tissue</div><b>χιόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χιόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. snow</div>
  <div class='def'>2. snow (TV noise)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χιόνι, from Koine Greek χιόνιον, diminutive of Ancient Greek χιών, from Proto-Hellenic *kʰiṓn, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰéyōm. Compare Mariupol Greek шон.</div><b>χλαμυδωτίδα της Σαχάρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χλαμυδωτίδες της Σαχάρας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>houbara bustard, Chlamydotis undulata</div><b>χλαμυδόγαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χλαμυδόγαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>houbara bustard, Chlamydotis undulata</div><b>χλαμύδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chlamys</div>
  <div class='def'>2. official dress of kings and generals</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χλαμύς.</div><b>χλευάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to jeer, mock</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to deride, scoff, mock, laugh at</div><b>χλευαστικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. derisively</div>
  <div class='def'>2. derisory</div>
  <div class='def'>3. mockingly</div><b>χλευαστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χλευαστική, neuter χλευαστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mocking, taunting</div>
  <div class='def'>2. derisory, derisive</div><b>χλιαρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χλιαρή, neuter χλιαρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. lukewarm (temperature)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lukewarm (not very enthusiastic)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χλιαρός.</div><b>χλιμιντράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χλιμιντρίζω">χλιμιντρίζω</a></div><b>χλιμιντρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to neigh (the sound made by a horse)</div><b>χλιμιντρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χλιμιντρίζω">χλιμιντρίζω</a></div><b>χλοερός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χλοερή, neuter χλοερός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>verdant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χλόη.</div><b>χλομιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χλωμιάζω">χλωμιάζω</a></div><b>χλομός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χλομή, neuter χλομό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pale, wan, sallow (of a person)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dim, weak (of a light source)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χλομός, from an unattested *φλομός with dissimilation *φ- (*ph-) &gt; χ- (kh-), from Ancient Greek φλόμος”), a plant known for its pale coloration and potentially poisonous seeds.</div><b>χλοοτάπητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χλοοτάπητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>turf (real or artificial: an area of turf or section laid to produce one)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χλόη + τάπητας.</div><b>χλωμάδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) pallor, paleness, pallidity, wanness</div><b>χλωμιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to pale, turn pale</div><b>χλωμός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χλωμή, neuter χλωμό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χλομός">χλομός</a></div><b>χλωρά κρεμμυδάκια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χλωρό κρεμμυδάκι</div><b>χλωρίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χλωρίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flora</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χλωρίς.</div><b>χλωρίδιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χλωρίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chloride</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χλώριο + -ίδιο, calque of English chloride.</div><b>χλωριούχα νάτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of χλωριούχο <a href="bword://νάτριο">νάτριο</a></div><b>χλωριούχο νάτριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural χλωριούχα νάτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable, usually) sodium chloride</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (dated, uncountable, usually) table salt, common salt</div><b>χλωριούχος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χλωριούχος or χλωριούχα, neuter χλωριούχο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chloride</div><b>χλωρό κρεμμυδάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χλωρά κρεμμυδάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spring onion, scallion</div><b>χλωρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χλωρή, neuter χλωρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. just sprouted, tender and green (especially plant, grass, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) fresh</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek χλωρός.</div><b>χλόη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) grass, lawn</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek χλόη.</div><b>χλώριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (usually uncountable, plural χλώρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) chlorine (element and gas)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French chlore, derived from Ancient Greek χλωρός.</div><b>χνάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χνάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (obsolete) pattern</div>
  <div class='def'>2. track, footprint, spoor (trace left on ground)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) sign, vestige</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tα <b>χνάρια</b> του χρόνου στο πρόσωπό της. - The signs of time on her face.</div><b>χνούδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χνούδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fuzz, down, fluff</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From an unattested Ancient Greek *χνούδιον, from χνόος.</div><b>χοίρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χοίροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pig</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χοῖρος.</div><b>χοανόζωα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Choanozoa.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Choanozoa.</div><b>χοινίκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hub (the central part of a wheel)</div><b>χοιρινό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χοιρινά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pork meat</div>
  <div class='ex'>Φάγαμε <b>χοιρινό</b> με πατάτες. - We ate pork with potatoes.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adjective form. Substantivised neuter of χοιρινός, from Byzantine Greek χοιρινός, from χοῖρ or χοῖρος, with the suffix -ινός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://χοιρινός">χοιρινός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://χοιρινός">χοιρινός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Adjective form. Substantivised neuter of χοιρινός, from Byzantine Greek χοιρινός, from χοῖρ or χοῖρος, with the suffix -ινός.</div><b>χοιρινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χοιρινή, neuter χοιρινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>related to pig or pork</div><b>χοιρομέρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χοιρομέρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ham (haunch pork, often smoked)</div><b>χοιροστάσιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χοιροστάσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pigsty</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) pigsty (an untidy place)</div><b>χολέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cholera</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek χολέρα</div><b>χολή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χολές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gall, bile</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) gall bladder</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) bitterness, wrath, anger, bile</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χολή, from Proto-Indo-European *ǵʰolh₃éh₂ (“gall, bile”), from the root *ǵʰelh₃- (“yellow-green”). Cognate with Persian زهره (zahre, “gall”), English gall.</div><b>χολίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χολίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>choline</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of French choline or calque of English choline. Attested since 1854.</div><b>χολερικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χολερική, neuter χολερικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>relating to cholera, choleraic</div><b>χολερυθρίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bilirubin</div><b>χοληστερίνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χοληστερίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cholesterol</div><b>χονδρέμπορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χονδρέμποροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wholesaler</div><b>χονδρεμπόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χονδρεμπόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>wholesale outlet</div><b>χονδρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χονδρική, neuter χονδρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cartilaginous, relating to cartilage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. as a whole, approximate</div><b>χονδροκύτταρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chondrocyte</div><b>χονδρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χονδρή, neuter χονδρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χοντρός">χοντρός</a></div><b>χοντρέμπορος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χοντρέμποροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χονδρέμπορος">χονδρέμπορος</a></div><b>χοντραίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to get fat, to fatten</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make fat, to fatten</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of the voice) to thicken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χοντρ(ός)) + -αίνω.</div><b>χοντρεμπόριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χονδρεμπόρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χονδρεμπόριο">χονδρεμπόριο</a></div><b>χοντρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χοντρική, neuter χοντρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. wholesale</div>
  <div class='def'>2. as a whole, approximate</div><b>χοντροκώλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χοντροκώλες, masculine χοντροκώλης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, offensive) fat-ass, fatass (female person with fat buttocks)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πες σ’ αυτήν την <b>χοντροκώλα</b> να κάτσει κάτω, δεν φαίνεται η οθόνη. - Tell that fatass to sit down, I can&#x27;t see the screen.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χοντρός + κώλος + -α (-a)</div><b>χοντροκώλης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χοντροκώληδες, feminine χοντροκώλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, offensive) fat-ass, fatass (male person with fat buttocks)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πες σ’ αυτόν τον <b>χοντροκώλη</b> να κάτσει κάτω, δεν φαίνεται η οθόνη. - Tell that fatass to sit down, I can&#x27;t see the screen.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χοντρός + κώλος + -ης (-is)</div><b>χοντρομαλάκας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χοντρομαλάκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, offensive) wanker, knobhead, asshole, jerk, prick (general term of abuse for a male)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κοίτα τι πήγε κι έκανε ο <b>χοντρομαλάκας</b>! - Look what that asshole went and did!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χοντρο- (chontro-, “fat, great, big”) + μαλάκας</div><b>χοντρομπαλάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χοντρομπαλάδες, feminine χοντρομπαλού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, offensive) A fat or obese man who is short and round and resembles a ball; fatso, fat-ass, fatty.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν ήμουν νεαρός ήμουν παχύσαρκος και με λέγανε «<b>χοντρομπαλά</b>». - When I was young I was obese and they used to call me “fatso”.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χοντρός + μπάλα + -άς</div><b>χοντρομπαλού</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χοντρομπαλούδες, masculine χοντρομπαλάς)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory, offensive) A fat or obese woman who is short and round and resembles a ball; fatso, fat-ass, fatty.</div>
  <div class='ex'>Όταν ήμουν νεαρή ήμουν παχύσαρκη και με λέγανε «<b>χοντρομπαλού</b>». - When I was young I was obese and they used to call me “fatso”.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χοντρός + μπάλα + -ού</div><b>χοντρό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://χοντρός">χοντρός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative neuter singular of <a href="bword://χοντρός">χοντρός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>3. accusative neuter singular of <a href="bword://χοντρός">χοντρός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>4. vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://χοντρός">χοντρός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, euphemistic, uncountable) number two (the act of defecation)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με συγχωρείτε, πρέπει να πάω για το <b>χοντρό</b> μου. - Forgive me, I must do a number two.</div><b>χοντρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χοντρή, neuter χοντρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fat, obese, stout, corpulent, heavy (carrying more fat than usual on one&#x27;s body)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χοντρό</b> παιδί - fat child</div>
  <div class='def'>2. thick, heavy (relatively great in extent from one surface to the opposite)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χοντρά</b> ρούχα - heavy clothes</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) heavy, serious (important, weighty)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χοντρό</b> λάθος - grave mistake</div>
  <div class='def'>4. low, heavy</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χοντρή</b> φωνή - low voice</div>
  <div class='def'>5. coarse (consisting of big pieces or granules)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χοντρό</b> αλεύρι - coarse flour</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively) coarse, unrefined, crass (lacking refinement, taste or delicacy)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χοντρό</b> αστείο - coarse joke</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χονδρός, with spelling -ντ- (-nt-) to reflect the preservation of the pronunciation as /-nd-/ rather than /-νð-/, as well as semantic shift &quot;coarse&quot; &gt; &quot;thick&quot; &gt; &quot;fat&quot;, as attested in later usages in Ancient Greek.</div><b>χορδή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χορδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vocal cord, vocal fold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. string</div>
  <div class='ex'>θεωρία <b>χορδών</b> - string theory</div>
  <div class='def'>3. any long, very thin structure</div>
  <div class='def'>4. bow string</div>
  <div class='def'>5. chord (of a circle)</div>
  <div class='def'>6. string (of violin, piano, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χορδή.</div><b>χορδωτά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>The Chordata, the chordates.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Chordata.</div><b>χορευτής</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χορευτές, feminine χορεύτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dancer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek χορευτής.</div><b>χορεύτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χορεύτριες, masculine χορευτής)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dancer</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (euphemistic) stripper</div><b>χορεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to dance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to bounce about</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek χορεύω.</div><b>χορηγός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χορηγοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sponsor, backer, benefactor (especially for an artistic event)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χορηγός.</div><b>χορηγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to grant (to give)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: παραχωρώ (parachoró)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek χορηγῶ, contracted form of χορηγέω; to furnish with supplies”).</div><b>χορογράφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural χορογράφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>choreographer</div><b>χορογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χορογραφίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>choreography (the steps of a dance, and their composition)</div><b>χορογραφικά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>choreographically</div><b>χορογραφικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χορογραφική, neuter χορογραφικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>choreographical</div><b>χορογραφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to choreograph</div><b>χοροεσπερίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χοροεσπερίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ball, dance, dance party</div><b>χοροστατώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to officiate a ceremony, lead a ceremony</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χοροστατέω.</div><b>χορτάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χορτάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grass</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek χορτάριον.</div><b>χορτάτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χορτάτη, neuter χορτάτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>full, replete, sated, satiated</div>
  <div class='ex'>θέλω και την πίτα ολόκληρη και τον σκύλο <b>χορτάτο</b> - to want to have one&#x27;s cake and eat it too</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χορτ(αίνω)) + -άτος.</div><b>χορταίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to glut, satiate</div>
  <div class='ex'>ο λόγος σου με <b>χόρτασε</b> και το ψωμί σου φά’ το - you can have it all</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to glut somebody, satiate somebody</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χορταίνω, from Ancient Greek χορτάζω and metaplasm to the verb suffix -αίνω.</div><b>χορταριάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to sprout, to green, to become grassy</div><b>χορτοφάγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χορτοφάγη, neuter χορτοφάγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. herbivorous, plant-eating</div>
  <div class='def'>2. vegetarian</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural χορτοφάγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vegetarian</div>
  <div class='def'>2. herbivore</div><b>χορτοφαγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) vegetarianism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χόρτο + -φαγία. First attested 1897.</div><b>χορτοφαγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χορτοφαγική, neuter χορτοφαγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vegetarian</div><b>χορός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χοροί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dance</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chorus in a performance of Ancient Greek drama</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χορός.</div><b>χουντικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χουντική, neuter χουντικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(relational) junta</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χούντα + -ικός.</div><b>χουρμάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χουρμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>date (fruit of the date palm)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ottoman Turkish خرما (hurma), from Classical Persian خُرْمَا.</div><b>χουρμαδιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χουρμαδιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>date palm tree (Phoenix dactylifera)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χουρμάς + -ιά.</div><b>χούλιγκαν</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) hooligan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From English hooligan.</div><b>χούμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χούμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>humus (soil component)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Latin humus (“soil, earth”).</div><b>χούντα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χούντες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>junta</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Spanish junta.</div><b>χούφτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χούφτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. palm of hand, fist</div>
  <div class='def'>2. handful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From φούχτα with metathesis, but uncertain beyond that. Probably from an unattested *φουκτίζω, which would be derived from Koine Greek πυκτεύω, i.e. &quot;the body part that one fights with&quot;.</div><b>χούφταλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χούφταλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory) geezer, codger, crock (old infirm man, typically cranky and bad natured)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήγε και παντρεύτηκε νεαρή κοπέλα το <b>χούφταλο</b>! - The old codger went and married a young woman!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χούφτα, following the ending of θρύψαλο as a model.</div><b>χρέος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρέη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>debt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρέος.</div><b>χρέωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρεώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>debit, fee, charge, price</div><b>χρήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. capital (physical or monetary assets)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) liquid assets</div>
  <div class='def'>3. money</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρῆμα.</div><b>χρήση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. use, application, usage</div>
  <div class='ex'>για <b>χρήση</b> από τους μαθητές - for use by students</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fiscal year, financial year</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρῆσις.</div><b>χρήσιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρήσιμη, neuter χρήσιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>useful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρήσιμος. By surface analysis, χρήση + -ιμος (-imos).</div><b>χρήστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural χρήστες, feminine χρήστρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. user, utiliser (UK), utilizer (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. legal user</div>
  <div class='def'>3. drug user</div><b>χρήστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρήστριες, masculine χρήστης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. user, utiliser (UK), utilizer (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. legal user</div>
  <div class='def'>3. drug user</div><b>χρίσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. one of the seven sacraments of the Orthodox Church, performed immediately after baptism by anointing with holy chrism; chrismation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>χρίσμα</b> τελείται αμέσως μετά τη βάπτιση. - Chrismation is performed immediately after baptism.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) endorsement, investiture, formal approval or nomination</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήρε το <b>χρίσμα</b> του υποψηφίου για την προεδρία. - He received the endorsement as a candidate for the presidency.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from χρῖσμα (Ancient Greek), originally meaning “ointment, unction”; later specialized in ecclesiastical usage.</div><b>χρίω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to anoint</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρῑ́ω.</div><b>χρειάζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. I need, I require</div>
  <div class='def'>2. χρειάζεται it is needed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χρειάζομαι from the noun χρεί(α)) + -άζομαι.</div><b>χρεοκοπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρεοκοπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bankruptcy (legally declared or recognized condition of insolvency)</div><b>χρεωκοπία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρεωκοπίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χρεοκοπία">χρεοκοπία</a></div><b>χρεωκοπημένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρεωκοπημένη, neuter χρεωκοπημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bankrupt, insolvent</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Passive participle of χρεωκοπώ.</div><b>χρεωστικές κάρτες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χρεωστική <a href="bword://κάρτα">κάρτα</a></div><b>χρεωστική κάρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρεωστικές κάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>debit card</div><b>χρεωστικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρεωστική, neuter χρεωστικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>debit</div><b>χρεωστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χρωστάω">χρωστάω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek χρεωστῶ, contracted form of χρεωστέω.</div><b>χρεώνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be charged, be billed</div><b>χρεώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to charge, debit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χρέ(ος), “debt”) + -ώνω.</div><b>χρηματίζομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to take bribes, be bribed</div><b>χρηματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to be, occupy a place as public official (usually not used in present tense)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to bribe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρηματίζω. By surface analysis, χρήμα + -ίζω.</div><b>χρηματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρηματική, neuter χρηματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>monetary, pecuniary (of, pertaining to, or consisting of money)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek χρηματικός. By surface analysis, χρηματ-) + -ικός.</div><b>χρηματισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bribing (action or outcome of bribe)</div><b>χρηματιστήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρηματιστήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stock market, stock exchange</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek χρηματιστήριον.</div><b>χρηματοδοτώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to fund, to finance (provide money for)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Byzantine Greek χρηματοδοτώ. By surface analysis, χρηματο- (chrimato-) + -δοτώ.</div><b>χρηματοδότηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>funding, financing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the χρηματοδοτη- stem of χρηματοδοτώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>χρηματοκιβώτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρηματοκιβώτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>safe, coffer, strongbox (box, usually made of metal, in which valuables can be locked for safekeeping)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω τοποθετήσει τα λεφτά και τα κοσμήματα στο <b>χρηματοκιβώτιο</b>. - I&#x27;ve placed the money and the jewelry in the safe.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from χρηματο- (chrimato-, “money”) + κιβώτιο, calque of German Geldschrank. First attested 1868.</div><b>χρηματοπιστωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρηματοπιστωτική, neuter χρηματοπιστωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>financial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from χρηματο- (chrimato-) + πιστωτικός.</div><b>χρησικτησία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρησικτησίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>usucapion, acquisitive prescription</div><b>χρησιμεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to be useful, to be of use</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Etymology tree
Ancient Greek χρή
Proto-Indo-European *-h₂
Proto-Indo-European *-éh₂
Proto-Indo-European *-yéti
Proto-Indo-European *-eh₂yéti
Ancient Greek -άω
Ancient Greek χράομαι
Ancient Greek -ιμος (-imos)
Ancient Greek χρήσιμος
Ancient Greek -εύς
Ancient Greek -εύω
Ancient Greek χρησιμεύωlbor.
Greek χρησιμεύω
Learned borrowing from Koine Greek χρησιμεύω. By surface analysis, χρήσιμ(ος)) + -εύω.</div><b>χρησιμοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρησιμοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>use, utilization</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from χρησιμοποιώ + -ση (-si).</div><b>χρησιμοποιημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine χρησιμοποιημένη, neuter χρησιμοποιημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>used</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of χρησιμοποιούμαι, passive voice of χρησιμοποιώ (“make use”).</div><b>χρησιμοποιούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be used</div><b>χρησιμοποιώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to use, employ, put to use, utilise</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to employ (someone)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χρήσιμ(ος), “useful”) + -ο- + -ποιώ from the ancient ποιέω/ποιῶ. Calque of French utiliser.</div><b>χρησιμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) usefulness, utility</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek χρησιμότης, equivalent to χρήσιμος + -ότητα.</div><b>χριστιανή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χριστιανές, masculine χριστιανός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Christian</div><b>χριστιανικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χριστιανική, neuter χριστιανικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Christian (based on or connected with Jesus Christ, his churches or teachings)</div><b>χριστιανισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) Christianity</div><b>χριστιανοδημοκρατία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (countable and uncountable, plural χριστιανοδημοκρατίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(countable, uncountable) Christian Democracy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χριστιανός + δημοκρατία.</div><b>χριστιανοσύνη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (uncountable) Christendom</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (uncountable) the state of being Christian</div><b>χριστιανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χριστιανοί, feminine χριστιανή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. Christian</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) guy (person)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek Χρῑστιᾱνός. By surface analysis, Χριστός + -ιανός.</div><b>χριστουγεννιάτικα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χριστουγεννιάτικος">χριστουγεννιάτικος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflectional form.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, derogatory, humorous, sometimes) on Christmas Day (usually with reference to something not appropriate or usual for the day)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με υποχρέωσε να δουλέψω <b>χριστουγεννιάτικα</b> ενώ κανονικά είχα ρεπό. - He made me work on Christmas Day of all times, although I normally had a day off.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Χριστούγενν(α) (“Christmas”) + -ιάτικα.</div><b>χριστουγεννιάτικες κάρτες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χριστουγεννιάτικη <a href="bword://κάρτα">κάρτα</a></div><b>χριστουγεννιάτικη κάρτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χριστουγεννιάτικες κάρτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Christmas card</div><b>χριστουγεννιάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χριστουγεννιάτικη, neuter χριστουγεννιάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Christmas (relating to the occasion)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χριστουγεννιάτικο</b> δέντρο - Christmas tree</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Χριστούγεννα + -ιάτικος.</div><b>χριστόψωμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χριστόψωμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bread especially prepared for Christmas with rich symbolic ornamentation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Χριστ(ός) (“Christ”) + -ό- (“infix”) + -ψωμο (“bread”).</div><b>χροιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χροιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hue, tone (the shade or quality of a colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. timbre, tone color (the quality of a sound independent of its pitch and volume)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) tone, hue, tinge (general character, atmosphere, mood, or vibe (especially as a connotation imparted to an utterance))</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: υποδήλωση f (ypodílosi)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χροιά with semantic loan from French ton in the senses not literally connected with colour.</div><b>χρονίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to take a long time, drag</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to endure, last a long time</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to delay, drag</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to age one year</div><b>χρονιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρονιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. school year, academic year; year&#x27;s crop or production</div>
  <div class='def'>2. year</div><b>χρονικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρονική, neuter χρονικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (relational) time (attributive), temporal (relating to time)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χρονικό</b> διάστημα - interval of time, time period</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (relational) temporal (relating to or denoting time or tense)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek χρονικός.</div><b>χρονοβόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρονοβόρα, neuter χρονοβόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lengthy, time-consuming, drawn-out</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from χρονο- (chrono-) + -βόρος, a calque of English time-consuming.</div><b>χρονοδιάγραμμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρονοδιαγράμματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>timetable, schedule</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from χρονο- (chrono-) + διάγραμμα, a calque of English timetable.</div><b>χρονοδιακόπτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χρονοδιακόπτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>time switch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from χρονο- (chrono-) + διακόπτης, a calque of English time switch.</div><b>χρονοκαθυστέρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>time delay</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χρονο- (chrono-) + καθυστέρηση.</div><b>χρονολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρονολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. system of dating, chronology of a certain event</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αφετηρία της ρωμαϊκής <b>χρονολογίας</b> είναι το έτος κτίσεως της Ρώμης, το 753 π.Κ.Ε. - The starting point of the Roman dating system is the foundation of Rome in 753 B.C.E.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. determining or recording the year in which an event happened</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποια είναι η <b>χρονολογία</b> της γεννήσεώς σας; - What is the year of your birth?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. determining or recording the year in which an event happened; determining or recording the precise date (year, month, day) of an event</div>
  <div class='ex'>A more precise term: ημερομηνία (imerominía, “precise day, month (and year)”)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. chronology, the science of dating events</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The word, from medieval times, 8th-9th century. Morphologically from χρονο- (chrono-, “chrono-”) + -λογία &quot;speaking of time&quot;. Contemporary senses through French chronologie.</div><b>χρονολογώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to chronologize, date</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτός ο ναός <b>χρονολογείται</b> γύρω στο 200 προ Χριστού. - This temple is dated to around 200 B.C.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to date, write the date</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χρονολόγησε</b> τις αιτήσεις και βάλ’ τες στο συρτάρι. - Write the date on the petitions and put them in the drawer.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Back-formation from the feminine noun χρονολογ(ία)) + -ώ.</div><b>χρονολόγηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρονολογήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. the act of dating, chronology</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η <b>χρονολόγηση</b> του πίνακα ήταν λάθος. Η σωστή χρονολογία δημιουργίας ήταν το 1750. - The dating of the painting was wrong. The correct year date of its creation was 1750.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. marking, recording the date</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the past stem χρονολογη- of χρονολογώ (“I date”) + -ση (-si) from ancient -σις.</div><b>χρονοτριβώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(intransitive) to delay, to drag one&#x27;s feet, to procrastinate, to stall (to deliberately take longer than necessary to do something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonyms: αργοπορώ (argoporó), χασομερώ (chasomeró)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χρονοτριβῶ.</div><b>χρονόμετρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρονόμετρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stopwatch</div>
  <div class='def'>2. synonym of <a href="bword://μετρονόμος">μετρονόμος</a> m</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French chronomètre, from Ancient Greek χρόνος + μέτρον. By surface analysis, χρονό- + μέτρο.</div><b>χρυσάνθεμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρυσάνθεμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chrysanthemum (the plant and its flower)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek χρυσάνθεμον, from χρυσο- (khruso-) + ἄνθεμον).</div><b>χρυσάφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρυσάφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) wealth, money</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) source of wealth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χρυσάφιν, from Koine Greek χρυσάφιον, from a diminutive of Ancient Greek χρυσός.</div><b>χρυσαλλίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρυσαλλίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chrysalis, pupa, nymph</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρυσαλλίς.</div><b>χρυσαυγίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χρυσαυγίτες, feminine χρυσαυγίτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) A supporter or member of the Greek neo-Nazi Golden Dawn party.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) An elected member of parliament (<a href="bword://Βουλή">Βουλή</a>) for the Golden Dawn party.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the party name Χρυσή Αυγή f.</div><b>χρυσαφής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρυσαφιά, neuter χρυσαφί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>golden (color/colour)</div><b>χρυσαφί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gold (color/colour)</div><b>χρυσοκόκκινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρυσοκόκκινη, neuter χρυσοκόκκινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>golden-red</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χρυσο- (chryso-, “golden”) + κόκκινος</div><b>χρυσοτσίχλονο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρυσοτσίχλονα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yellowhammer (Emberiza citrinella)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Compare Greek χρυσότσιχλα.</div><b>χρυσοχοΐα</b>
  <div><i>noun</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goldsmithing</div><b>χρυσοχόος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural χρυσοχόοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goldsmith</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χρυσός.</div><b>χρυσωρυχείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρυσωρυχεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gold mine</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek χρυσωρυχεῖον. By surface analysis, χρυσο- (chryso-, “gold”) + ορυχείο.</div><b>χρυσό αβγό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(figuratively, idiomatic, uncountable) golden goose (something that generates much profit or gives great advantages) (literally: golden egg)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τον προσέλαβαν με την ιδέα ότι θα τους κάνει το <b>χρυσό αβγό</b>. - They hired him with the idea that he will be a golden goose.</div><b>χρυσόμαλλο δέρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Golden Fleece</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρυσόμαλλον δέρας.</div><b>χρυσόμαλλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρυσόμαλλη, neuter χρυσόμαλλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>golden haired</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χρυσό- + μαλλιά</div><b>χρυσός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χρυσοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gold (metal element)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. money, currency, cash</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) wealth, riches</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρυσός (already Mycenaean Greek 𐀓𐀬𐀰 (ku-ru-so)), Semitic loan, compare with Biblical Hebrew חָרוּץ, Akkadian 𒆬𒄀.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρυσή, neuter χρυσό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. golden, gold</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) handsome, lovely</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) good-hearted</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) dear, lovable</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χρυσός, from Ancient Greek χρυσοῦς, from Ancient Greek χρύσεος.</div><b>χρυσόσκονη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural χρυσόσκονες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) gold dust</div><b>χρυσότσιχλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρυσότσιχλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Zoothera dauma, the White&#x27;s thrush</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χρυσό- + τσίχλα</div><b>χρυσόψαρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρυσόψαρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>goldfish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χρυσό- + ψάρ(ι)) + -ο (-o).</div><b>χρωμίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρωμίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chromis (fish of the genus Chromis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω μπλε-πράσινα <b>χρωμίδες</b>, τον μοναδικό επιζώντα από μια τοξική καταστροφή ενυδρείου. - I have blue-green chromises, the sole survivors of a toxic aquarium disaster.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek χρωμίς (with a semantic loan from translingual Chromis), from χρῶμᾰ.</div><b>χρωματίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to paint, to colour (UK), color (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to χρώμα.</div><b>χρωματιστός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρωματιστή, neuter χρωματιστό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. coloured (UK), colored (US) (having a colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. coloured, colourful (UK), colored, colorful (US) (having prominent colours)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from the χρωματισ- stem of χρωματίζω + -τός.</div><b>χρωματογραφία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) chromatography</div><b>χρωματόσωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρωματοσώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χρωμόσωμα">χρωμόσωμα</a></div><b>χρωμόσωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρωμοσώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chromosome</div><b>χρωστάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to owe, be in debt</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χρωστώ + -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek χρωστῶ). From Hellenistic Koine Greek χρεωστῶ, with avoidance of the hiatus [eo], contracted form of χρεωστέω.</div><b>χρωστώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χρωστάω">χρωστάω</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: χρωστῶ (khrōstô)</div><b>χρόνια</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>days, years, time, ages</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χρόνια</b> και ζαμάνια - for ages</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Second neuter irregular plural of the masculine χρόνος m.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://χρόνιος">χρόνιος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://χρόνιος">χρόνιος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See the etymology of the corresponding lemma form.</div><b>χρόνια αποφρακτική πνευμονοπάθεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χρόνιες αποφρακτικές πνευμονοπάθειες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, COPD</div><b>χρόνια και ζαμάνια</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>for ages, been awhile, for years and years</div><b>χρόνια πολλά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. many happy returns: happy birthday, happy name day</div>
  <div class='def'>2. happy New Year</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Merry Christmas</div>
  <div class='def'>4. happy Easter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Expression with χρόνια, a neuter plural for the masculine χρόνος &amp; πολλά, neuter plural of πολύς.</div><b>χρόνιες αποφρακτικές πνευμονοπάθειες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://χρόνια">χρόνια</a> αποφρακτική <a href="bword://πνευμονοπάθεια">πνευμονοπάθεια</a></div><b>χρόνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χρόνια, neuter χρόνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. chronic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. permanent</div><b>χρόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χρόνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (abstract) time (with no plural)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο <b>χρόνος</b> φεύγει και δεν ξαναγυρνά. - Time passes and does not come back.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. year</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Χρόνια</b> πολλά - Many happy returns</div>
  <div class='def'>3. tense</div>
  <div class='def'>4. volume (issues of a periodical over a period of one year)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρόνος.</div><b>χρώμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χρώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. colour (UK), color (US)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. paint</div>
  <div class='def'>3. complexion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χρῶμα.</div><b>χρώμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) chromium (chemical element)</div><b>χτένα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χτένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>comb</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χτένι (with change of gender), from Byzantine Greek χτένι, κτένι(ν)), from Koine Greek κτένιον, a diminutive of Ancient Greek κτείς, κτένος.</div><b>χτένι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χτένια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scallop (mollusc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κτένιον, diminutive of κτείς.</div><b>χτένισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χτενίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hairstyle, coiffure, hairdo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χτενίζω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>χτήμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χτήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative spelling of <a href="bword://κτήμα">κτήμα</a></div><b>χτήνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χτήνη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κτήνος">κτήνος</a></div><b>χτίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to build, construct</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Τζων είχε <b>χτίσει</b> ένα σπίτι του κοντά στη θάλασσα. - John had built his house near the sea.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to create, found</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χτίζω, from Ancient Greek κτίζω with dissimilation of κτ- (kt-) &gt; χτ- (cht-).</div><b>χτίσμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural κτήσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://κτίσμα">κτίσμα</a></div><b>χτίστης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χτίστες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bricklayer (especially), mason, builder</div><b>χταπόδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χταπόδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>octopus</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ὀκταπόδιον, from Ancient Greek ὀκταπόδιον, diminutive of ὀκτάπους.</div><b>χτενίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to comb, groom</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to rake</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to comb, search</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek κτενίζω. By surface analysis, χτένα + -ίζω. The third sense, to &quot;comb, search&quot;, is a semantic loan from English comb.</div><b>χτενοτρίτωνας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χτενοτρίτωνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>crested newt (Triturus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, χτένα + τρίτωνας.</div><b>χτες</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χθες">χθες</a> (chthes)</div><b>χτεσινοβραδινός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χτεσινοβραδινή, neuter χτεσινοβραδινό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>yesterday evening&#x27;s, last night&#x27;s</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χτεσιν(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + βραδινός.</div><b>χτικιάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, transitive) to make consumptive, give tuberculosis</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή η δουλειά θα με <b>χτικιάσει</b> στο τέλος! - This job will give me consumption in the end!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, transitive) to make suffer, torment, torture, plague</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τ’ αηδόνια σε <b>χτικιάσανε</b> στην Τροία,
Που στράγγιξες χαμένα μια γενιά. - The nightingales tormented you in Troy,
Where you bled a whole generation dry.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, intransitive) to become consumptive, catch tuberculosis</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δυστυχώς <b>χτίκιασε</b> και πέθανε νέος. - Sadly, he caught TB and died young.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (colloquial, figuratively, intransitive) to suffer, undergo hardship</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δύσκολη η δουλειά αυτό τον μήνα, <b>χτίκιασα</b>! - Work was hard this month, I really suffered!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑκτικιάζω, from Koine Greek ἑκτικός, from ἕξις.</div><b>χτικιό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, dated, uncountable) consumption (pulmonary tuberculosis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Παλιά, παρά πολύ άνθρωποι έβγαλαν το <b>χτικιό</b> αλλά τώρα είναι θεραπεύσιμο. - In the olden days, many people got consumption but nowadays it&#x27;s treatable.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively, uncountable) hardship, suffering, trial, tribulation (difficulty or trouble)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η δουλειά μου είναι μεγάλο <b>χτικιό</b>. - My job is very trying.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek (ἑ)κτικιό ((he)ktikió), from (ἑ)κτικιάζω ((he)ktikiázō, “to get consumption, to contract tuberculosis”), from Koine Greek ἑκτικός, from ἕξις.</div><b>χτυπάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to strike, knock, hit, beat</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to clack (noise)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to beat (heart etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (intransitive) to be hurt</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to ring (bell etc.); to toll, ring</div>
  <div class='ex'>Για ποιον <b>χτυπά</b> η καμπάνα - For whom the bell tolls</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (transitive) to ring (bell etc.); to ring (bell, doorbell, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χτυπώ + -άω. Inherited from Byzantine Greek χτυπῶ, from Ancient Greek κτυπῶ with [kt] &gt; [xt], contracted form of κτυπέω.</div><b>χτυπημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine χτυπημένη, neuter χτυπημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. beaten, struck</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wounded</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of χτυπιέμαι, passive voice of χτυπάω, χτυπώ (“beat, strike”).</div><b>χτυπητά αβγά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χτυπητό <a href="bword://αβγό">αβγό</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. scrambled eggs</div><b>χτυπητά αυγά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χτυπητό <a href="bword://αυγό">αυγό</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. scrambled eggs</div><b>χτυπητό αβγό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χτυπητά αβγά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. a dessert made by beating yolk with sugar</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (plural normally) scrambled eggs</div><b>χτυπητό αυγό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χτυπητά αυγά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of χτυπητό <a href="bword://αβγό">αβγό</a> (more common in the plural)</div><b>χτυπώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χτυπάω">χτυπάω</a></div><b>χτύπημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χτυπήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rap, blow, knock (the action or the result of the action)</div>
  <div class='ex'>άκουσα ένα <b>χτύπημα</b> στην πόρτα - I heard a knock on the door.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wounding from a blow</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι σοβαρό <b>χτύπημα</b>. Θα σας αφήσει σημάδι. - It is a serious wounding. It will leave a mark on you.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) blow, knock (to psychological state)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek κτύπημα with a regular change [kt] &gt; [xt] from verb κτυπέω/κτυπώ, past tense stem χτυπη- + -μα (-ma). Sense &quot;blow, hitting&quot; since mediaeval times.</div><b>χτύπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χτύποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rap (on a door)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. beating (as of a drum)</div><b>χυδαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χυδαία, neuter χυδαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vile, vulgar</div><b>χυλοπίτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χυλοπίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in plural) hilopites (traditional Greek-style pasta cut into strips or squares)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χυλ(ός)) + -ο- (-o-) + πίτα.</div><b>χυλόπιτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χυλόπιτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(in the collocation <a href="bword://τρώω">τρώω</a> (<a href="bword://τη">τη</a>) <a href="bword://χυλόπιτα">χυλόπιτα</a>) romantic/sexual rejection</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έφαγε <b>χυλόπιτα</b>. - He got rejected. He struck out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χυλόπιτα, a food. By surface analysis, χυλ(ός)) + -ό- + -πιτα (-pita).</div><b>χυλός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χυλοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gruel</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χυλός.</div><b>χυλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to become mushy, to soften</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (transitive) to make mushy, to soften</div><b>χυμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χυμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>juice</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χυμός, from χέω.</div><b>χυμός μήλου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χυμοί μήλου)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apple juice</div><b>χυτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χυτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>foundry, smelter</div><b>χωλαίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literally) to limp, to hobble (to walk lamely)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to limp along (to perform weakly)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χωλαίνω with semantic loan from Italian zoppicare or French clocher in the figurative sense.</div><b>χωματερή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χωματερές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>landfill, tip, rubbish dump</div><b>χωνάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χωνάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, diminutive) cone</div><b>χωνί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χωνιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. funnel (utensil used in pouring liquids)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. megaphone, type of loudspeaker (funnel shaped items)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χῶνος, χώνη, contracted of χοάνη.</div><b>χωνεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to digest (food, etc.)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in the negative) to like</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) to digest (understand and accept) the causes of an event</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χωνεύω, from Hellenistic Koine Greek χωνεύω, χοανεύω, verbal formation from Ancient Greek χόανος.</div><b>χωράφι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χωράφια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>field</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χωράφι</b> σε αγρανάπαυση - fallow field</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek χωράφιον, a diminutive of Ancient Greek χώρα.</div><b>χωράω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to hold, contain</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to fit (in)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to take, hold</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> See χώρος for more.</div><b>χωρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to separate, divide</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα ήθελα να <b>χωρίσω</b> την παρουσίασή μου σε τέσσερα μέρη. - I&#x27;d like to divide my presentation into four parts.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (intransitive) to divorce</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek χωρίζω, from adverb χωρίς, from which χῶρος, χώρα.</div><b>χωρίς</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(with accusative) without, -less, -free</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χωρίς</b> γλουτένη - gluten-free</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χωρίς.</div><b>χωρητικότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χωρητικότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seating capacity</div>
  <div class='def'>2. volume, capacity</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (electrical) capacitance or capacity</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χωρητικός + -ότητα. First attested 1812.</div><b>χωριάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χωριάτες, masculine χωριάτης or χωρικός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female villager</div><b>χωριάτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χωριάτες, feminine χωριάτα or χωρική or χωριάτισσα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. villager, countryman (male from a rural setting)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly, derogatory) country bumpkin, yokel (unsophisticated and boorish person from the countryside)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χωριό + -άτης.</div><b>χωριάτικες σαλάτες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of χωριάτικη <a href="bword://σαλάτα">σαλάτα</a></div><b>χωριάτικη σαλάτα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χωριάτικες σαλάτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Greek salad</div><b>χωριάτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χωριάτικη, neuter χωριάτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rural, country, rustic</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χωριάτικο</b> λουκάνικο - rustic sausage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. home cooked</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory) provincial</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χωριάτης + -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>χωρικά ύδατα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>territorial waters, territorial sea</div><b>χωρική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χωρικές, masculine χωρικός or χωριάτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>female villager</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://χωρικός">χωρικός</a></div><b>χωρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine χωρική, neuter χωρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. territorial, country</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χωρικά</b> ύδατα - territorial waters</div>
  <div class='def'>2. village</div>
  <div class='def'>3. spatial</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χωρικοί, feminine χωρική or χωριάτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. villager</div>
  <div class='def'>2. peasant</div><b>χωρισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine χωρισμένη, neuter χωρισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. divided, separated</div>
  <div class='def'>2. divorced</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of χωρίζομαι, passive voice of χωρίζω (“separate”).</div><b>χωρισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χωρισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. separation, parting (the act or condition of two or more people being separated from one another)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. breakup (the termination of a friendship or a romantic relationship)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. separation (the act or condition of a married couple living in separate homes while remaining legally married)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δικαστικός <b>χωρισμός</b> - legal separation</div>
  <div class='def'>4. separation (the act of disuniting two or more things)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>χωρισμός</b> Εκκλησίας και Κράτους - separation of church and state</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek χωρισμός. By surface analysis, χωρίζω + -μός.</div><b>χωριό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χωριά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. village</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (collective) villagers</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek χωριόν, from Ancient Greek χωρίον. Doublet of χωρίο.</div><b>χωροφυλακή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) gendarmerie</div><b>χωροφύλακας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χωροφύλακες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>gendarme (member of the gendarmerie)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek χωροφύλαξ.</div><b>χωροχρόνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) spacetime (the four dimensional continuum)</div><b>χωρόχρονος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χωροχρόνος">χωροχρόνος</a></div><b>χωρώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://χωράω">χωράω</a></div><b>χόβολη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hot ashes that still have burning pieces of coal; embers</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek χοβόλη, of unknown provenance.</div><b>χόμπι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) hobby</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English hobby.</div><b>χόνδρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χόνδροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. cartilage, gristle, cartilaginous skeletal material</div>
  <div class='def'>2. groats</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek χόνδρος.</div><b>χόρευ&#x27;</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>apocopic form of χόρεψε, second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://χορεύω">χορεύω</a></div><b>χόρσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) horse</div><b>χόρτο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χόρτα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. grass, lawn</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural) greens, leaf vegetables</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χόρτος, with gender change from masculine to neuter.</div><b>χύμα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. untidy, disorganized</div>
  <div class='def'>2. at bulk, not in a standardised package (for things sold in retail (by volume or weight) in portions at client&#x27;s discretion, usually from a bigger package)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From a derivative of Ancient Greek χέω, along with χυμός.</div><b>χύνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to spill, pour, shed (cause to flow in a stream, deliberately or accidentally)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ποιος <b>έχυσε</b> το γάλα στο πάτωμα; - Who spilt the milk on the floor?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of non-edibles) to pour, cast, make (shape by pouring into a mould or make in such a way)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η δουλειά της είναι να <b>χύνει</b> κεριά. - Her job is to cast candles.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, intransitive, vulgar) to cum, come, jizz, spunk (ejaculate semen)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Μάρκος <b>έχυσε</b> στο πρόσωπο του Λάμπρου. - Markos ejaculated on Lambros&#x27; face.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek, from Koine Greek χύνω, from a late formation of Ancient Greek χέω.</div><b>χύσι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χύσια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(vulgar) cum (ejaculate, semen)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χύνω.</div><b>χύσιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χυσίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spilling, spillage</div>
  <div class='def'>2. casting</div>
  <div class='def'>3. ejaculation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χύνω + -ιμο (-imo).</div><b>χύτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χύτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stewpot, casserole, cauldron</div><b>χύτρα ταχύτητας</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χύτρες ταχύτητας)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pressure cooker</div><b>χώμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>earth, soil, dirt, mould</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χῶμα.</div><b>χώνεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. digestion</div>
  <div class='def'>2. casting (of metals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χωνεύω + -μα (-ma).</div><b>χώνεψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>digestion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From χώνευση, apparently a blend of χώνεμα”) + πέψη.</div><b>χώρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural χώρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. main town or village</div>
  <div class='def'>2. country, (especially) homeland, country of birth</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η Σκωτία είναι <b>χώρα</b> που αποτελεί τμήμα του ΗΒ. - Scotland is a country that is part of the UK.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. second-person singular imperfective imperative of <a href="bword://χωρώ">χωρώ</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. second-person singular perfective imperative of <a href="bword://χωρώ">χωρώ</a></div><b>χώρια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. separately</div>
  <div class='def'>2. besides, apart from</div><b>χώρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural χωρίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. separation, partition</div>
  <div class='def'>2. partition, cubicle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> χωρίζω + -μα (-ma)</div><b>χώρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural χώροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. area, space, room</div>
  <div class='ex'>ακάλυπτος <b>χώρος</b> - inner courtyard</div>
  <div class='def'>2. field</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek χῶρος.</div><b>ψ΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ψ΄">Ψ΄</a></div><b>ψάθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψάθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. broadleaf cattail, common cattail (Typha latifolia)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wicker</div>
  <div class='def'>3. straw mat</div>
  <div class='ex'>στην <b>ψάθα</b> - destitute</div>
  <div class='def'>4. straw beach mat</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψάθα.</div><b>ψάλλω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. alternative form of <a href="bword://ψέλνω">ψέλνω</a>”)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, transitive) to praise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ψάλλω. Doublet of ψέλνω.</div><b>ψάλτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψάλτες or ψαλτάδες, feminine ψάλτρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cantor, chanter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ψάλτης, from Ancient Greek ψάλλω.</div><b>ψάξιμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψαξίματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>search, searching (an attempt to find something)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the ψαξ- stem of ψάχνω + -ιμο (-imo).</div><b>ψάρεμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψαρέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fishing</div><b>ψάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) naive</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (derogatory) rookie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψάρι, from the Koine sense &quot;fish&quot; of the Ancient Greek ὀψάριον, diminutive of ὄψον. Cognate with Mariupol Greek псар (psar).</div><b>ψάχνομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to be searched for</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to be searched</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, idiomatic) to wonder and research</div><b>ψάχνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to look for, hunt for, search, rummage</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το μυστήριο που αναζητώ
αυτό που ψάχνουν κι όλοι οι άλλοι
είναι εδώ και είν’ αληθινό
είναι μια αγάπη πιο μεγάλη
To mystírio pou anazitó
aftó pou psáchnoun ki óloi oi álloi
eínai edó kai eín’ alithinó - The mystery I&#x27;m searching for,
for which all others are looking
It&#x27;s here and it&#x27;s real
It&#x27;s a greater love</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψάχνω, from Ancient Greek ψαύω”), based on past tense stem&#x27;s change ψαυσ- &gt; ψαξ- and further metaplasm similar to the pattern διωξ-, έδιωξα - διώχνω - compare ancient διώκω. ψαύω is likely related to ψάω; see there for more.</div><b>ψέγω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(rare) to criticize, fault</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ψέγω.</div><b>ψέλνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive, transitive) to sing</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to tell off, go on about</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Related to ψάλλω.</div><b>ψέμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψέματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lie</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψέμα, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ψεῦμα with simplification [vm] &gt; [m]. The expected double [mm] (ψέμμα) has not been written in mediaeval times because the simplification happened after the period when double consonants were pronounced -as in ῥεῦμα &gt; ρέμα. From Ancient Greek ψεῦσμα, a derivative of Ancient Greek ψεύδω with suffix -μα (-ma).
Cognate with Mariupol Greek псе́ма.</div><b>ψήγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψήγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (plural normally) metal shaving</div>
  <div class='ex'>σκόνες και <b>ψήγματα</b> νικελίου - nickel powder and shavings</div>
  <div class='def'>2. very small piece of metal in alluvial deposits, smaller than nuggets</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ψήγματα</b> χρυσού - small nuggets of gold</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) a very small amount, a trace</div>
  <div class='ex'>ούτε <b>ψήγμα</b> αλήθειας - not a trace of truth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψῆγμα.</div><b>ψήκτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψήκτρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (dated, formal) brush, comb</div>
  <div class='def'>2. special thin brush for cleaning laboratory glassware</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ψήκτρα</b> καθαρισμού δοκιμαστικών σωλήνων - test tube brush</div>
  <div class='def'>3. special thin brush for cleaning gun barrels</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψήκτρα.</div><b>ψήνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to cook, make; to boil, broil, steam</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to cook, make; to roast, stew</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to cook, make; to make, do, prepare</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψήνω, from Ancient Greek ἕψω based on the past tense stem of ἥψησα in the pattern of άφησα, stem αφησ- - αφήνω. Compare to ψένω. Also see Mariupol Greek пси́ну.</div><b>ψήφισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψηφίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>resolution (a motion voted for and accepted by a meeting)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Ancient Greek ψήφῐσμᾰ.</div><b>ψήφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψήφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vote at an election or meeting.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The feminine, learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ψῆφος f.
The colloquial masculine, inherited, perhaps from Byzantine Greek ψῆφος, in analogy to other -ος masculine nouns.</div><b>ψίθυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψίθυροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(onomatopoeic) whisper (speaking quietly)</div><b>ψαλίδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψαλίδια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. scissors</div>
  <div class='def'>2. shears</div>
  <div class='def'>3. cut</div>
  <div class='ex'>Νέο «<b>ψαλίδι</b>» στα εισοδήματα βλέπει το 38% των μισθωτών. - New &#x27;cut&#x27; to incomes hits 38% of employees.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψαλίδι, from Koine Greek ψαλίδιον, a diminutive of Ancient Greek ψαλίς.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek псалы́дъ.</div><b>ψαλιδιάρης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψαλιδιάρηδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>red kite (Milvus milvus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψαλίδι + -άρης, literally “cutter, scissorer”.</div><b>ψαλμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ψαλμένη, neuter ψαλμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>chanted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ψάλλομαι or ψέλνομαι, passive voices of ψάλλω and ψέλνω (“chant”) respectively.</div><b>ψαλμωδία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psalmody</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ψαλμῳδίᾱ, from ψαλμῳδ(ός) m, “psalmist”) + -ία, from ψαλμός + ᾠδή. Compare to the inherited colloquial synonym ψαλμουδιά f.</div><b>ψαλμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψαλμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psalm</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ψαλμός, from ψάλλω.</div><b>ψαλτήριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψαλτήρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) psalter</div><b>ψαμμίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψαμμίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sandstone</div><b>ψαράδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψιλικατζίδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fish shop, seafood store</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψαράδικος, synchronically analyzed as ψαρα- (psara-) + -δικο (-diko).</div><b>ψαράκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψαράκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. diminutive of <a href="bword://ψάρι">ψάρι</a> n: small fish, fishy</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πάμε στην ψαροταβέρνα για κανένα <b>ψαράκι</b>; - How about going to the fish-taverna for some fish?</div>
  <div class='def'>2. for the football term (soccer), see <a href="bword://κεφαλιά">κεφαλιά</a> <a href="bword://ψαράκι">ψαράκι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψάρ(ι)) + -άκι.</div><b>ψαράς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψαράδες, feminine ψαρού)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fisherman, trawlerman</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fishmonger</div>
  <div class='def'>3. angler</div><b>ψαρίλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψαρίλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) fishy smell, smell of fish</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άνοιξε το παράθυρο να φύγει η <b>ψαρίλα</b>! - Open the window so the fishy smell goes out!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψάρι + -ίλα.</div><b>ψαραγορά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψαραγορές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fishmarket</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψαρ- (psar-, “of fish”) + αγορά</div><b>ψαρεύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to fish, angle, dive for fish, hook fish</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, figuratively) to fish (investigate someone&#x27;s opinion in a deceiving way)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial, figuratively) to find</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψαρεύω, from ψάρ(ι) n, “fish”) + -εύω. Unrelated to ψαρός and to ψαρονέφρι n.</div><b>ψαριά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of ψαρής</div>
  <div class='ex'>alternative form: ψαρά (psará) of ψαρός (psarós)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψαριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>catch</div><b>ψαρονέφρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψαρονέφρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>tenderloin (cut of meat especially pork)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek *ψυάριον or *ψοιάριον, diminutive of ψόᾱ, ψύᾱ f + νεφρ(ός), “kidney”) + -ι (-i). Unrelated to ψάρι n.</div><b>ψαροντούφεκο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψαροντούφεκα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>speargun</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψαρο- (psaro-, “fish”, suffix) + ντουφέκ(ι), “rifle”) + -ο (-o, neuter ending).</div><b>ψαροταβέρνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψαροταβέρνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>seafood restaurant</div><b>ψαρόβαρκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψαρόβαρκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>small fishing boat</div>
  <div class='ex'>see also: αλιευτικό n (alieftikó) σκάφος n (skáfos, “vessel”) (formal, for bigger boats)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψαρό- + βάρκα</div><b>ψαρόνι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψαρόνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (usually) starling (a bird of the family Sturnidae)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. European starling, common starling, Sturnus vulgaris</div><b>ψαρώνει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>third-person singular present active indicative of ψαρώνω</div><b>ψεγάδι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) defect, fault</div>
  <div class='ex'>(more formal)</div><b>ψεκασμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψεκασμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. spray (plant treatment)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. spraying, dusting (process of treatment)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. spraying, dusting (other treatment processes)</div><b>ψες</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://εψές">εψές</a>: <a href="bword://χτες">χτες</a> (chtes, “yesterday”)</div>
  <div class='ex'>also ψε (pse)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψές, from Ancient Greek ὀψέ formed as ὀψές &lt; ψες influenced paretymologically by χθές &gt; χθες (chthes).</div><b>ψευδά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψευδός">ψευδός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lispingly</div><b>ψευδάργυρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψευδάργυροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>zinc</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned diachronic borrowing from Koine Greek ψευδάργυρος, based on the identification of the latter as zinc (see Strabo XIII, 1.56, p. 610). Morphologically, ψευδ- (psevd-, “false”) + άργυρος.</div><b>ψευδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψευδής, neuter ψευδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>false; sham; insincere; wearing masks</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψευδής.</div><b>ψευδίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to lisp</div><b>ψευδαίσθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψευδαισθήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. illusion, hallucination</div>
  <div class='def'>2. delusion</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψευδ- (psevd-, “pseudo-”) + αίσθηση, on the model of παραίσθηση. First attested as Katharevousa ψευδαίσθησις in 1888.</div><b>ψευδοδυσφαγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pseudodysphagia</div><b>ψευδοεπιστήμη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψευδοεπιστήμες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pseudoscience</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψευδο- (psevdo-, “pseudo-, false”) + επιστήμη</div><b>ψευδοκράτος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψευδοκράτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pseudostate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψευδο- (psevdo-) + κράτος</div><b>ψευδοκύηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψευδοκυήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>phantom pregnancy, pseudocyesis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψευδο- (psevdo-) + κύηση</div><b>ψευδολινάρι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>false flax</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψευδο- (psevdo-, “pseudo-”) + λινάρι</div><b>ψευδολόγος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψευδολόγος, neuter ψευδολόγο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(of a person) mendacious, systematically lying</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψευδολόγος, ψευδο- (psevdo-) + -λόγος. Also substantivized.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ψευδολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>mendacious, liar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψευδολόγος, ψευδο- (psevdo-) + -λόγος. Also substantivized.</div><b>ψευδομάρτυρας</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>false witness, perjurer</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψευδομάρτυς from the accusative singular ψευδομάρτυρα. Morphologically, ψευδο- (psevdo-, “pseudo-, lying”) + μάρτυρας.</div><b>ψευδοπάτωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψευδοπατώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>subfloor</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψευδο- (psevdo-, “pseudo-”) + πάτωμα, a calque of French faux plancher.</div><b>ψευδοροφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>dropped ceiling, false ceiling</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψευδ- (psevd-, “pseudo-”) + οροφή, a calque of French faux plafond.</div><b>ψευδωνυμοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (usually uncountable, plural ψευδωνυμοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) pseudonymization</div><b>ψευδός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψευδή, neuter ψευδό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lisper</div><b>ψευδότιτλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψευδότιτλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>half title</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψευδό- + τίτλος.</div><b>ψευδώνυμα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of ψευδώνυμος</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with a pseudonym</div>
  <div class='ex'>formal: ψευδωνύμως (psevdonýmos)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ψευδώνυμο">ψευδώνυμο</a></div><b>ψευδώνυμο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψευδώνυμα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pseudonym (fictitious name)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French pseudonyme, from Ancient Greek ψευδώνυμος. Morphologically, ψευδ- (psevd-, “pseudo-”) + -ώνυμο.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of ψευδώνυμος</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of ψευδώνυμος</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French pseudonyme, from Ancient Greek ψευδώνυμος. Morphologically, ψευδ- (psevd-, “pseudo-”) + -ώνυμο.</div><b>ψευδώνυμων</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>genitive masculine/feminine/neuter singular of ψευδώνυμος</div><b>ψευδώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>falsely</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ψευδῶς. By surface analysis, ψευδ(ής)) + -ώς</div><b>ψευδώς αρνητικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ψευδώς">ψευδώς</a> <a href="bword://αρνητικός">αρνητικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψευδώς">ψευδώς</a> <a href="bword://αρνητικός">αρνητικός</a></div><b>ψευδώς αρνητικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψευδώς αρνητική, neuter ψευδώς αρνητικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>false negative</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;falsely negative&quot;.</div><b>ψευδώς θετικό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ψευδώς">ψευδώς</a> <a href="bword://θετικός">θετικός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψευδώς">ψευδώς</a> <a href="bword://θετικός">θετικός</a></div><b>ψευδώς θετικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψευδώς θετική, neuter ψευδώς θετικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>false positive</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Literally, &quot;falsely positive&quot;.</div><b>ψευτιά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψευτιές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>lie, deceit</div><b>ψεύδομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) to lie, tell lies</div>
  <div class='ex'>common: λέω ψέματα (léo psémata)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψεύδομαι, from ψεύδω.</div><b>ψεύδος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψεύδη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) lie, something untrue, a more formal alternative of <a href="bword://ψέμα">ψέμα</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Older spelling: ψεῦδος (pseûdos)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψεῦδος.</div><b>ψεύτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψεύτες, feminine ψεύτρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>liar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ψεύστης, dropping the [s] to simplify the consonant cluster.</div><b>ψεύτικα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>falsely</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψεύτικος">ψεύτικος</a></div><b>ψεύτικος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψεύτικη or ψεύτικια, neuter ψεύτικο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. fake, false, spurious, bogus</div>
  <div class='def'>2. fake, counterfeit</div>
  <div class='def'>3. unreal, simulated</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψεύτ(ης) m, “liar”) + -ικος (-ikos).</div><b>ψεύτρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψεύτρες, masculine ψεύτης)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>liar</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψεύ(της) m, “liar”) + -τρα (-tra, feminine ending).</div><b>ψηλά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψηλός">ψηλός</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>more formal: υψηλά (ypsilá)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>high</div><b>ψηλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψηλή, neuter ψηλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. tall</div>
  <div class='def'>2. high</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψηλός, inherited as an aphetic form of Ancient Greek ὑψηλός. Doublet of υψηλός.</div><b>ψηλώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to make taller</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to raise</div><b>ψημένες Αλάσκες</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>plural of ψημένη <a href="bword://Αλάσκα">Αλάσκα</a></div><b>ψημένη Αλάσκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψημένες Αλάσκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baked Alaska</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English baked Alaska.</div><b>ψημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ψημένη, neuter ψημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>baked, roasted, stewed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ψήνομαι, passive voice of ψήνω.</div><b>ψησταριά</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψησταριές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. barbecue, grill, roaster</div>
  <div class='def'>2. chophouse, grill house</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψήστ(ης) m, “griller, person who grills”) + -αριά.</div><b>ψητοπωλείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψητοπωλεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>grill room</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψητό + -πωλείο</div><b>ψητός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψητή, neuter ψητό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>roast, baked</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἑψητός with apheresis of the initial ε (e), from ἕψω + -τός. By surface analysis, ψήνω + -τός.</div><b>ψηφίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to vote</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ψηφίζω.</div><b>ψηφίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψηφία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. digit, numeral</div>
  <div class='def'>2. character</div>
  <div class='def'>3. small piece to form a mosaic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From neuter Hellenistic Ancient Greek ψηφίον (“small pebble, gravel”) diminutive of feminine Ancient Greek ψῆφος.</div><b>ψηφιακά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψηφιακός">ψηφιακός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>digitally</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο ραδιοφωνικός σταθμός μεταδίδει <b>ψηφιακά</b>. - The radio station transmits digitally.</div><b>ψηφιακή υπογραφή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψηφιακές υπογραφές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>digital signature</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ψηφιακός + υπογραφή. Calque of English digital signature.</div><b>ψηφιακό βιβλίο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψηφιακά βιβλία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>e-book</div><b>ψηφιακός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψηφιακή, neuter ψηφιακό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>digital</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ψηφιακό</b> ρολόι - digital watch</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Calque of English digital. From ψηφί(ο) n, “digit”) + -ακός. Also see Koine Greek ψηφίον.</div><b>ψηφιοποίηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψηφιοποιήσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(neologism) digitisation (UK), digitization (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψηφίο + -ποίηση, calque of English digitization.</div><b>ψηφισμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ψηφισμένη, neuter ψηφισμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voted</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ψηφίζομαι, passive voice of ψηφίζω.</div><b>ψηφοδέλτιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψηφοδέλτια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. voting form, ballot paper on which a vote is cast</div>
  <div class='def'>2. list of candidates standing in an election</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψήφ(ος), “vote, ballot”) + -ο- + δελτίο, a calque of French bulletin de vote. First attested 1838 in Katharevousa as ψηφοδέλτιον.</div><b>ψηφοδόχοι</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ψηφοδόχος">ψηφοδόχος</a></div><b>ψηφοδόχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψηφοδόχοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ballot box</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψήφ(ος), “vote”) + -ο- (-o-, “infix”) + -δόχος.</div><b>ψηφοφορία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψηφοφορίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vote, voting procedure</div><b>ψηφοφόρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ψηφοφόροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>voter (one who votes)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek ψηφοφόρος, an adjective.</div><b>ψι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) psi (Greek letter)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ψεῖ.</div><b>ψι ψι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>puss, puss (when calling a cat)</div>
  <div class='ex'>sometimes written with ενωτικό (enotikó, “hyphen”): ψι-ψι</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic.</div><b>ψιθυρίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to whisper (voice)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to rustle (leaves)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. to lap (waves)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. to babble (stream)</div><b>ψιλά</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. loose change, coins</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pittance, small amount of money</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>neuter nominative/accusative/vocative plural of <a href="bword://ψιλός">ψιλός</a></div><b>ψιλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψιλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. smooth breathing, spiritus lenis (Ancient Greek diacritical mark)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. manual hair clippers</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ψιλός">ψιλός</a></div><b>ψιλικατζίδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψιλικατζίδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>corner shop (UK), convenience store (US)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψιλικατζής + -δικο (-diko).</div><b>ψιλοβρέχει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(impersonal, intransitive) to drizzle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψιλο- (psilo-) + βρέχει.</div><b>ψιλοκομμένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ψιλοκομμένη, neuter ψιλοκομμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>minced, chopped, shredded</div><b>ψιλοκόβομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. passive of <a href="bword://ψιλοκόβω">ψιλοκόβω</a>:; to be cut thinly</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (reflexive) passive of <a href="bword://ψιλοκόβω">ψιλοκόβω</a>:; to be wounded, cut (usually with a knife), but not seriously</div><b>ψιλοκόβω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to cut thinly</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψιλο- + κόβω.</div><b>ψιλό</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ψιλός">ψιλός</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ψιλός">ψιλός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, euphemistic) number one (the act of urination)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Με συγχωρείτε, πρέπει να πάω για το <b>ψιλό</b> μου. - Forgive me, I must do a number one.</div><b>ψιλόβροχο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψιλόβροχα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>drizzle, light rain</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψιλό- + βροχ(ή) f, “rain”) + -ο (-o, neuter ending)</div><b>ψιλός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψιλή, neuter ψιλό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>thin, fine, delicate</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ψιλός.</div><b>ψιτ</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, onomatopoeic) pst; psst (exclamation to get attention; not polite)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ψιτ</b>, κοίτα εδώ! - Psst, look over here!</div><b>ψιχάλα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψιχάλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. raindrop</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in plural, often) drizzle (light rain)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek *ψιχάλα, from Ancient Greek ψεκάδα, accusative of ψεκάς, variant of ψακάς.</div><b>ψιχαλίζει</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(impersonal, intransitive) to drizzle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψιχαλίζει. By surface analysis, ψιχάλ(α)) + -ίζει.</div><b>ψιψίνα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψιψίνες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>kitten, pussycat (affectionate term)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Onomatopoeic from ψι ψ(ι) (psi ps(i)) + -ίνα.</div><b>ψιψίνων</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sometimes, as genitive plural of <a href="bword://ψιψίνα">ψιψίνα</a></div><b>ψοφάω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) to die (of an animal)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Τα πρόβατα <b>ψόφησαν</b> από το κρύο. - The sheep died from the cold.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, intransitive) to die (of a person, see below)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχει <b>ψοφήσει</b> ακόμα ο παλιόγερος; - Has that old bastard died yet?</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, intransitive) to be worn out, to die (of the cold, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω <b>ψοφήσει</b> από τη δουλειά. - Work has killed me.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, intransitive) to have a strong desire for, to have a thirst for (of the cold, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η αδερφή μου <b>ψοφάει</b> για κουτσομπολιό. - My sister is crazy about gossip.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (transitive) to kill (an animal)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ψόφησε</b> το σκυλί της από την πείνα. - She killed her dog by starving it.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. (figuratively, transitive) to wear out, exhaust</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μας έχει <b>ψοφήσει</b> στις πρόβες, μέχρι να μάθουμε ολόκληρο το κομμάτι. - He&#x27;s exhausting us with practice, until we learn the entire piece.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψοφ(ώ)) + -άω. From Ancient Greek ψοφῶ, contracted form of ψοφέω to die (of an animal)”), from ψόφος. From the noise made by someone literally dropping dead.</div><b>ψοφίμι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψοφίμια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. carcass, carrion, dead animal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. runt, weakling</div><b>ψοφώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ψοφάω">ψοφάω</a></div><b>ψυγείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψυγεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. refrigerator, fridge</div>
  <div class='def'>2. refrigerated room, refrigerated lorry etc</div>
  <div class='def'>3. radiator</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) extremely cold (e.g. temperature of a room)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Koine Greek ψυγεῖον, from ψύχω, stem ψυγ- + -εῖον &gt; modern -είο.</div><b>ψυκτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυκτική, neuter ψυκτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cooling, refrigerating, freezing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ψῡκτικός.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψυκτικοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>refrigeration technician</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ψῡκτικός.</div><b>ψυχή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (also, figuratively) soul, spirit (essence of a person (or place or thing figuratively) usually thought to consist of one&#x27;s thoughts and personality)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Θεός να αναπαύσει την <b>ψυχή</b> της. - God rest her soul.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. person</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στον δρόμο δεν υπήρχε <b>ψυχή</b> τέτοια ώρα. - There wasn&#x27;t a person to be seen on the road at that hour.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) courage, bravery, valour (quality of a confident character not to be afraid or intimidated easily)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. butterfly</div>
  <div class='def'>5. sound post (of a string instrument, e.g. the violin)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ψῡχή.</div><b>ψυχίατρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ψυχίατροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychiatrist</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French psychiatre, from Ancient Greek ψυχ- (psukh-), ψυχή f + ἰατρός. By surface analysis, ψυχ- (psych-, “psych-”) + ιατρός.</div><b>ψυχαγωγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχαγωγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>entertainment, recreation</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ψυχαγωγία, from Ancient Greek ψῡχᾰγωγῐ́ᾱ, from ψῡχᾰγωγός.</div><b>ψυχαγωγημένος</b>
  <div><i>participle, masculine (feminine ψυχαγωγημένη, neuter ψυχαγωγημένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>entertained, who has been entertained</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Perfect participle of ψυχαγωγούμαι, passive voice of ψυχαγωγώ (“entertain”).</div><b>ψυχαγωγικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχαγωγική, neuter ψυχαγωγικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>entertaining, recreational</div>
  <div class='ex'>Near-synonym: διασκεδαστικός (diaskedastikós)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ψῡχᾰγωγῐκός. By surface analysis, ψυχαγωγ(ώ)) + -ικός.</div><b>ψυχαγωγώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to entertain, amuse</div><b>ψυχανάλυση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχαναλύσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychoanalysis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψυχ- (psych-, “psych-, soul”) + ανάλυση, learned borrowing from German Psychoanalyse or from French psychanalyse.</div><b>ψυχαναλύσεως</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) older form of ψυχανάλυσης, genitive singular of <a href="bword://ψυχανάλυση">ψυχανάλυση</a></div><b>ψυχαναλύω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to psychoanalyse (UK), psychoanalyze (US)</div><b>ψυχανθές</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψυχανθή)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>papilionaceous plant, legume (most frequently in the plural)</div><b>ψυχανώμαλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχανώμαλη, neuter ψυχανώμαλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) mental, not right in the head (mentally disordered)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, noun from verb) headcase, mental case, nutcase, nutjob, nutter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψυχ- (psych-) + ανώμαλος.</div><b>ψυχασθένεια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychasthenia</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French psychasthénie. By surface analysis, ψυχ- (psych-) + ασθένεια.</div><b>ψυχασθενής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχασθενής, neuter ψυχασθενές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. mentally ill</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (noun from verb) mentally ill person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ψυχ(ασθένεια)) + ασθενής, a calque of French psychasthénique (“psychasthenic”).</div><b>ψυχιατρείο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψυχιατρεία)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychiatric hospital, mental hospital</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ψυχίατρ(ος)) + -είο.</div><b>ψυχιατρική</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχιατρικές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychiatry</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized feminine of ψυχιατρικός &lt; ψυχ- (psych-, “psych-”) + ιατρικός. Learned borrowing from French psychiatrie, from psychiatre. See Ancient Greek ψυχή + ἰατρεία, from ἰάομαι.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ψυχιατρικός">ψυχιατρικός</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Substantivized feminine of ψυχιατρικός &lt; ψυχ- (psych-, “psych-”) + ιατρικός. Learned borrowing from French psychiatrie, from psychiatre. See Ancient Greek ψυχή + ἰατρεία, from ἰάομαι.</div><b>ψυχιατρικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχιατρική, neuter ψυχιατρικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychiatric</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψυχ- (psych-, “psyh-”) + ιατρικός. A learned borrowing from French psychiatrique, from psychiatrie + -ique. See Ancient Greek ψυχή f and ἰατρικός.</div><b>ψυχογιός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψυχογιοί, feminine ψυχοκόρη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. stepson</div>
  <div class='def'>2. errand boy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψυχο- (psycho-, “psycho- (soul)”) + γιος (gios, “son”)</div><b>ψυχοθεραπεία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχοθεραπείες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychotherapy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French psychothérapie or German Psychotherapie first attested in 1893. Morphologically, ψυχο- (psycho-, “psyxho- (soul)”) + θεραπεία. F</div><b>ψυχοκόρη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχοκόρες, masculine ψυχογιός)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>stepdaughter, foster daughter</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψυχο- (psycho-, “ψυχή soul”) + κόρη.</div><b>ψυχολογία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχολογίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychology</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French psychologie. Μorphologically ψυχο- (psycho-, “ψυχή soul”) + -λογία. First attested 1805.</div><b>ψυχολογικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψυχολογικός">ψυχολογικός</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychologically</div>
  <div class='ex'>older, more formal: ψυχολογικώς (psychologikós)</div><b>ψυχολογικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχολογική, neuter ψυχολογικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychological</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French psychologique, from psycholog(ie) + -ique, i.e. ψυχολογ(ία) f, “psychology”) + -ικός.</div><b>ψυχολογικώς</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) older and more formal of <a href="bword://ψυχολογικά">ψυχολογικά</a>: psychologically</div>
  <div class='ex'>older spelling of ending with -ῶς (-ôs)</div><b>ψυχολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ψυχολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychologist (expert in the field of psychology)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French psychologue. Μorphologically, ψυχο- (psycho-, “ψυχή soul”) + -λόγος. First attested 1851.</div><b>ψυχοπλακώνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to depress, to bring down (to make depressed or sad; to make someone feel bad emotionally)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψυχο- (psycho-) + πλακώνω.</div><b>ψυχοσωματικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχοσωματική, neuter ψυχοσωματικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychosomatic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from English psychosomatic. Morphologically, from ψυχο- (psycho-, “ψυχή soul”) + σωματικός.</div><b>ψυχοφθόρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχοφθόρη, neuter ψυχοφθόρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>soul-destroying, soul-crushing, psychologically draining</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ψυχοφθόρος, from ψυχο- (psukho-) + -φθόρος, from φθείρω.</div><b>ψυχραίνω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to chill or cool (something down)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) to alienate</div><b>ψυχραιμία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχραιμίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sangfroid, composure</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θαυμάζω την <b>ψυχραιμία</b> σου! - I admire your sang-froid!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Α learned borrowing from French sang-froid. Morphologically, ψυχρ- (psychr-, “psychro-, ψυχρός cold”) + αίμ(α), “blood”) + -ία.</div><b>ψυχρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχρή, neuter ψυχρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cold (literally, mainly of weather &amp; figuratively)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ψυχρός.</div><b>ψυχτικά</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτικά, nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψυκτικός">ψυκτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτικέ</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτικέ vocative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ψυχτικός">ψυχτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτικές</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτικές, nominative/accusative/vocative feminine plural of <a href="bword://ψυχτικός">ψυχτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτική</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτική nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ψυχτικός">ψυχτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτικής</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτικής genitive feminine singular of <a href="bword://ψυχτικός">ψυχτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτικοί</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτικοί, nominative/vocative masculine plural of <a href="bword://ψυχτικός">ψυχτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτικού</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτικού, genitive masculine/neuter singular of <a href="bword://ψυχτικός">ψυχτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτικούς</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτικούς, accusative masculine plural of <a href="bword://ψυχτικός">ψυχτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχτική, neuter ψυχτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of <a href="bword://ψυκτικός">ψυκτικός</a></div><b>ψυχτικών</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>less formal pronunciation of ψυκτικών, genitive masculine/feminine/neuter plural of <a href="bword://ψυχτικός">ψυχτικός</a></div><b>ψυχωμένος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχωμένη, neuter ψυχωμένο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>spirited, plucky, spunky (courageous)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from the passive perfect participle of Koine Greek ψυχῶ.</div><b>ψυχωτικός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψυχωτική, neuter ψυχωτικό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychotic</div><b>ψυχώτρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχώτριες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>psychotria (Psychotria)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from translingual Psychotria.</div><b>ψωλή</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψωλές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vulgar) cock, dick, prick, pecker (penis)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι γνωστό ότι η <b>ψωλή</b> του είναι μικρή. - It is well known that his dick is small.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ψωλή.</div><b>ψωλίτσα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψωλίτσες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>diminutive of <a href="bword://ψωλή">ψωλή</a> f</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψωλ(ή)) + -ίτσα</div><b>ψωλοβρόντι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψωλοβρόντια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) masturbation</div><b>ψωμάδικο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψωμάδικα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bakery, baker&#x27;s shop (rare)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψωμ(ί) (“bread”) + -άδικο (“suffix”)</div><b>ψωμάκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψωμάκια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. small piece of bread</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (especially) roll, bread roll</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, in plural) plumpy parts of body (of women)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψωμ(ί) (“bread”) + -άκι (“diminutive suffix”).</div><b>ψωμάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψωμάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) baker, informal synonym of <a href="bword://αρτοποιός">αρτοποιός</a>, <a href="bword://φούρναρης">φούρναρης</a></div><b>ψωμί</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψωμιά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bread</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) the financial means to sustain a tolerable standard of living</div>
  <div class='ex'>and see expressions for earn my living</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) in expressions for duration of life: years</div>
  <div class='ex'>see expressions</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ψωμίν, from ψωμίον, from Ancient Greek ψωμός, from the verb ψώω, an alternative form of ψάω.</div><b>ψωμί σικάλεως</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>rye bread</div><b>ψωμιέρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>breadbox</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψωμ(ί), “bread”) + -ιέρα</div><b>ψωνίζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (transitive) to shop, go shopping, buy</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, derogatory, slang) to select a sexual partner, or boyfriend, girlfriend</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (for the passive voice) ψωνίζομαι, prostitute one&#x27;s self</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψωνίζω, from Koine Greek ὀψωνίζομαι with aphesis, from ὀψωνέω + -ομαι (-omai).</div><b>ψωράλογο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψωράλογα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nag (old, weak, or useless horse)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (derogatory, idiomatic) term of abuse</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψώρα + άλογο.</div><b>ψωροκώσταινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(metonymically) Greece, an affectionate name used by Greeks of their homeland - it has intimations of poverty and affection.</div>
  <div class='ex'>&quot;Καλωσορίσατε στην Ψωροκώσταινα&quot; μου έγραψε ένας φίλος με e-mail όταν φτάσαμε στην Αθήνα ...
&quot;Kalosorísate stin Psorokóstaina&quot; mou égrapse énas fílos me e-mail ótan ftásame stin Athína ...</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ψωρο- + Κώσταινα, see Κωνσταντίνος.
Reputedly, the nickname of a very poor woman with surname Χατζηκώστα or Χατζηκώσταινα who contributed her meagre possession at a fund during the 1820s.
: Patrick Leigh Fermor likens the name to the English &quot;Mangy Betty&quot; (see quotation below.)</div><b>ψόφιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψόφια, neuter ψόφιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of animals) dead</div>
  <div class='ex'>κάνω τον <b>ψόφιο</b> κοριό - to play dead, to play possum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) dead tired</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) dead (lacking vitality)</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively) dying [with <a href="bword://για">για</a> (gia, + accusative) ‘for something’] (yearning intensely)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψοφ(ώ)) + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>ψόφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψόφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial, usually) freezing cold, chill</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κάνει <b>ψόφο</b> έξω! - It&#x27;s freezing cold outside!</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial, usually) death (especially for animals)</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (obsolete) noise</div>
  <div class='ex'>μυϊκός <b>ψόφος</b> - muscular noise</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψόφος, from Ancient Greek ψοφῶ. The senses, since mediaeval times. Also see ψοφάω.</div><b>ψύλλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ψύλλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>flea (parasitic insect)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ψύλλος, masculine form of feminine ψύλλα.
Cognate with Mariupol Greek пси́лу.</div><b>ψύξεως</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) older form of ψύξης, genitive singular of <a href="bword://ψύξη">ψύξη</a></div><b>ψύξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψύξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. refrigeration</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pain caused by low temperature (informal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ψῦ(ξις)) + modern ending -ξη (-xi). From ψύχω &gt; ψυχ- + -σις (-sis) &gt; -ξις (-xis).</div><b>ψύχος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) cold, coldness (a condition of low temperature; relative lack of heat)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ψῦχος.</div><b>ψύχραιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ψύχραιμη, neuter ψύχραιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cool-headed, cool, composed</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ψυχραιμία + -ος (-os).</div><b>ψύχω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(transitive) to chill, freeze</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ψύχω.</div><b>ψύχωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ψυχώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. psychosis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) informal extreme passion for something</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) hysteria</div>
  <div class='ex'>μαζική <b>ψύχωση</b> - mass hysteria</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from French psychose from Ancient Greek ψυχή f, ψυχ- of ψυχο- (psukho-) + -ose after névrose. Different sense for the Hellenistic ψύχωσις f.</div><b>ψώνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ψώνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (colloquial) foolish or naive person; dupe</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (colloquial) weirdo, wacko, person with weird behaviour, a little crazy, or megalomanic</div>
  <div class='ex'>είναι μεγάλο <b>ψώνιο</b> - (s)he is a big wacko</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (colloquial) persistent pastime or other activity</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έχω ψώνιο με τα νομίσματα κι έχω μεγάλη συλλογή. - I&#x27;ve a passion for coins and have a large collection.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (in plural, rare) see ψώνια npers groceries, purchases, shopping</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ψώνι, ψώνιν, from Hellenistic Koine Greek ὀψώνιον. The &quot;foolish, naive&quot; sense is attested since the Medieval period, perhaps with sense development along the lines of &quot;provisions&quot; &gt; &quot;one&#x27;s owned habits and pastimes&quot; &gt; &quot;one overly invested in one&#x27;s hobbies&quot; &gt; &quot;weirdo, fool&quot;.</div><b>ψώρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scabies, mange</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ψώρα.</div><b>ω</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oh!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὦ.</div><b>ω΄</b>
  <div><i>num</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://Ω΄">Ω΄</a></div><b>ωάριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ωάρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. egg cell, ovum</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ovule</div><b>ωδικό πτηνό</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ωδικά πτηνά)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>songbird</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> By surface analysis, ωδικός + πτηνό.</div><b>ωθούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to be pushed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Polytonic spelling: ὠθοῦμαι (ōthoûmai)</div><b>ωθώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to urge, push</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὠθῶ, contracted form of ὠθέω.</div><b>ωκεάνιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ωκεάνια, neuter ωκεάνιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Oceanic</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek Ὠκεάνειος). By surface analysis, ωκεανός + -ιος (-ios).</div><b>ωκεανός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ωκεανοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ocean</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο Ατλαντικός <b>Ωκεανός</b> είναι ο δεύτερος μεγαλύτερος <b>ωκεανός</b> της γης. - The Atlantic Ocean is the Earth&#x27;s second largest ocean.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. wealth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὠκεανός.</div><b>ωλένη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωλένες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ulna, elbow bone (bone of the forearm)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὠλένη.</div><b>ωμ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (indeclinable) ohm (derived SI unit of electrical resistance)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (Hinduism, indeclinable) om</div><b>ωμά</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>brutally, abruptly, bluntly</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ωμός">ωμός</a></div><b>ωμέγα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) omega, the 24th letter of the Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὦ μέγα. See μέγα.</div><b>ωμός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ωμή, neuter ωμό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. raw, uncooked</div>
  <div class='ex'>λεπτές στρώσεις <b>ωμού</b> μοσχαρίσιου κρέατος - thin layer of raw beef</div>
  <div class='def'>2. crude</div>
  <div class='def'>3. brutal</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὠμός.</div><b>ωογένεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωογενέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oogenesis</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> ωο- (oo-, “oo- (egg)”) + γένεση. Learned borrowing from English oogenesis.</div><b>ωοειδής</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ωοειδής, neuter ωοειδές)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>egg-shaped, oviform, ovoid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ᾠοειδής, from ᾠόν n and εἶδος n. By surface analysis, ωο- (oo-, “oo- (egg)”) + -ειδής).</div><b>ωοθήκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωοθήκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ovary</div><b>ωοτόκος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ωοτόκος, neuter ωοτόκο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oviparous, egg laying</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ᾠοτόκος, from ᾠόν n + τόκος m.</div><b>ωράριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ωράρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>work hours, working timetable</div><b>ωρίμανση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωριμάνσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ripening, maturing and see senses at verb <a href="bword://ωριμάζω">ωριμάζω</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Katharevousa ὡρίμανσις, already attested in 1881 and proscribed by Koumanoudis with ironic comments, (also at other dictionaries) because of its formation from nonexistent verb *ὡριμαίνω, instead of the expected form ὡρίμασις (attested since 1796) -modern ωρίμαση-.
Created with Koine Greek ὡριμάζω, modern ωριμά(ζω)) + feminine ending -ση (-si) with presence of ⟨-ν (n)-⟩ &gt; -ν-ση as a hypercorrection to sound &#x27;more ancient&#x27; according to the ancient pattern γηράσκω &gt; γήρανσις as though from *γηραίνω, itself according to ὑγιαίνω, past stem ὑγιαν- &gt; ὑγίανσις, also ὑγίασις.
notes: The ending -νση is expected from verbs in -αίνω or -ύνω, with the nu ⟨-ν-⟩ present in their stems. Still, the form ωρίμανση is most popular.</div><b>ωρίμαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωριμάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>etymologically expected form of <a href="bword://ωρίμανση">ωρίμανση</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> * see: ωρίμανση</div><b>ωρίμως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated, formal) maturely, ripely</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήρε <b>ωρίμως</b> την απόφαση να παραμείνει στη χώρα. - He maturely took the decision to stay in the country.</div><b>ωραία</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. nominative/accusative/vocative feminine singular of <a href="bword://ωραίος">ωραίος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Older spelling: ὡραία (hōraía)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ωραίος">ωραίος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Older spelling: ὡραῖα (hōraîa)</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nicely, beautifully</div>
  <div class='ex'>μαγειρεύει πολύ <b>ωραία</b> - he/she/it cooks very beautifully</div><b>ωραίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ωραία, neuter ωραίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. good, fine, nice</div>
  <div class='def'>2. beautiful, pretty, lovely, handsome</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Hellenistic Koine Greek ὡραῖος seasonable, timely”), shifting from &quot;timely&quot; &gt; &quot;good, beautiful&quot;.</div><b>ωριαίος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ωριαία, neuter ωριαίο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hourly</div><b>ωριλά</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) otolaryngologist, ear, nose and throat doctor</div><b>ωριλάς</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ωριλάδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, vernacular) otolaryngologist, ear, nose and throat doctor, vernacular form of <a href="bword://ωριλά">ωριλά</a> (indeclinable)</div><b>ωριμάζω</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (intransitive) ripen, literally: I become ripe</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ωριμάζουν</b> οι καρποί - the fruits (sense: products of plants) ripen</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) mature, literally:; I become mature</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ωρίμασα</b> μετά από τόση πείρα. - I have matured after so much/(long) experience.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively, intransitive, transitive) mature, literally:; I make someone mature</div>
  <div class='ex'>Οι εμπειρίες της ζωής με <b>ωρίμασαν</b>. - Life&#x27;s experience(s) matured me. (made me more mature).</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (figuratively, intransitive) I come of age</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of a procedure) I become completed</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η σκέψη του δεν έχει <b>ωριμάσει</b> ακόμη. - His thought/(mental activity) has not ripened yet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Koine Greek ὡριμάζω. For the figurative sense, semantic loan from French mûrir.</div><b>ωριμότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωριμότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maturity, ripeness (quality of being mature)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επέδειξε μεγάλη <b>ωριμότητα</b> στην απόφασή του. - He showed great maturity in his decision.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὡριμότης, equivalent to ώριμος + -ότητα.</div><b>ωροδείκτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ωροδείκτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hour hand</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly from ώρ(α)) + -ο- (-o-) + δείκτης.</div><b>ωρύομαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (formal) to howl (with anger, pain)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to howl (also of animals)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὠρῡ́ομαι. Cognate with Latin rāvis (“hoarseness”).</div><b>ως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as</div>
  <div class='ex'>Να δέχεσαι τα πράγματα ως έχουν. - To accept things as they are.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὡς.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to, as far as</div>
  <div class='ex'>πάω ως την άκρη του κόσμου. - I go to the end of the world.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. until, before, by</div>
  <div class='ex'>Θα είμαι στο σπίτι ως τις έξι. - I&#x27;ll be home before six.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὡς.</div><b>ως τα μπούνια</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>form of <a href="bword://μέχρι">μέχρι</a> <a href="bword://τα">τα</a> <a href="bword://μπούνια">μπούνια</a></div><b>ωσάν</b>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal, ironic, with accusative) formal from of <a href="bword://σαν">σαν</a> (san)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Old spelling: ὡσάν (hōsán)</div><b>ωσαννά</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Judaism) hosanna (cry of praise or adoration to God)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Koine Greek ὡσαννά, from Aramaic אושענא, from Biblical Hebrew הוֹשַׁע נָא.</div><b>ωσμώσεως</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formal, dated variant of of ώσμωσης, genitive singular of <a href="bword://ώσμωση">ώσμωση</a></div><b>ωστόσο</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nevertheless, nonetheless, however, but, yet, still (in spite of what preceded)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Βρέχει έξω, <b>ωστόσο</b> θα βγούμε. - It&#x27;s raining outside, nonetheless we&#x27;ll go out.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the expression &quot;ως τόσο&quot;, ως (os, “as”) + τόσο.</div><b>ωσότου</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) until (up to the time of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μάνα έκατσε στο προσκέφαλο του παιδιού της, <b>ωσότου</b> το πήρε ο ύπνος. - The mother sat at her child&#x27;s bedside until he was asleep.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ἕως ὅτου, consisting of ἕως + ὅτου, genitive singular of ὅστις.</div><b>ωτίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωτίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>bustard</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὠτίς, through the accusative singular ὠτίδα.</div><b>ωτίτης</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ωτίτες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>little finger (UK), pinkie(US)</div><b>ωτίτιδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωτίτιδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>otitis, ear inflammation</div>
  <div class='ex'>Older form: ὠτῖτις</div><b>ωταλγία</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωταλγίες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>otalgia, earache</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Hellenistic Ancient Greek ὠταλγία, from stem ὠτo-, ὠτ- of irregular Ancient Greek noun οὖς n + -αλγία, from ἄλγος.</div><b>ωτασπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωτασπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ωτοασπίδα">ωτοασπίδα</a>: earplug</div><b>ωτοασπίδα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωτοασπίδες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>earplug</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From stem ωτο- (oto-), from ὠτo-, ὠτ- of irregular Ancient Greek noun οὖς n + ασπίδα f, from Ancient Greek ἀσπίς.</div><b>ωτορινολαρυγγολόγος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural ωτορινολαρυγγολόγοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>otorhinolaryngologist</div><b>ωφέλιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ωφέλιμη, neuter ωφέλιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beneficial, useful</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὠφέλιμος.</div><b>ωφελιμισμός</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ωφελιμισμοί)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>utilitarianism</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From ωφέλιμος + -ισμός, a calque of English utilitarianism.</div><b>ωφελούμαι</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to profit, benefit</div><b>ωφελώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>to benefit, be good for, profit</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η μεσογειακή διατροφή <b>ωφελεί</b> την υγεία. - A Mediterranean diet benefits health.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὠφελῶ, contracted form of ὠφελέω, from ὄφελος n via combining forms like -ωφελής) with the omicron extended to omega in composition. Also see ὀφείλω</div><b>ωχράς</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>formal variant of ωχρής, genitive feminine singular of <a href="bword://ωχρός">ωχρός</a></div>
  <div class='ex'>Older spelling: ὠχρᾶς (ōkhrâs)</div><b>ωχριώ</b>
  <div><i>verb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. to blanch, turn pale</div>
  <div class='def'>2. to turn yellow</div><b>ωχρός</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ωχρή or ωχρά, neuter ωχρό)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ochre (color/colour)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. pale, haggard, pallid, wan</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὠχρός &amp; semantic loan from French pâle.</div><b>ωχρότητα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωχρότητες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>pallor, paleness</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learnedly, from Ancient Greek ὠχρότης, from the accusative singular τὴν ὠχρότητα. Equivalent to ωχρ(ός), “pallid, pale”) + -ότητα.</div><b>ό,τι</b>
  <div><i>pron</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (in place of a noun) whatever; that which</div>
  <div class='ex'>Είναι κακομαθημένο παιδί· <b>ό,τι</b> θέλει, του το δίνουν. - He&#x27;s a spoiled child; they give him anything he wants.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (in place of an adjective + noun) whatever; any, whoever/whatever</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ό,τι</b>^(όποιος) καθηγητής και αν με διδάξει, εγώ δε θα καταλάβω τίποτα. - Whoever professor teaches me, I&#x27;ll understand nothing. (literally, double negation: I will not understand nothing.)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὅ τι, also written ὅ⸒τι, neuter singular of ὅστις.</div><b>ό,τι και να</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>no matter what, whatever, any (anything, regardless of who, how much, what, etc)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ό,τί και να</b> του πεις, αυτός δεν ταράζεται. - No matter what you tell him, he isn&#x27;t shaken.</div><b>ό,τι κι αν</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of ό,<a href="bword://τι">τι</a> <a href="bword://και">και</a> να</div><b>ό,τι λάμπει δεν είναι χρυσός</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all that glitters is not gold, all that glisters is not gold</div><b>ό.π.</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>op. cit.</div><b>όαση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οάσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. oasis</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) quiet or sheltered place</div><b>όγδοος</b>
  <div><i>num, masculine (feminine όγδοη, neuter όγδοο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>eighth</div><b>όγκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όγκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. bulk, volume, mass, heap, weight</div>
  <div class='def'>2. tumor</div>
  <div class='ex'>Εκείνη είχε οράματα λόγω <b>όγκου</b> στον εγκέφαλο. - She had visions due to the brain tumor.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ὄγκος.</div><b>όζον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) ozone</div><b>όζος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όζοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. node</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nodule</div>
  <div class='def'>3. gnarl, knag</div>
  <div class='def'>4. gall</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὄζος.</div><b>όι-όι</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>ow, ouch, argh (on feeling pain, surprise, etc)</div><b>όκι</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Kaliarda) ear</div><b>όλα</b>
  <div><i>pron, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>everything</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Όλα</b> είναι εντάξει! - Everything is OK!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὅλος, from Proto-Indo-European *solh₂wós from *solh₂- (“whole”). The adjective όλος, also as pronoun when referring or replacing a noun.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://όλος">όλος</a></div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Όλα</b> τα παιδιά αγαπούν τα παιχνίδια. - All children love toys.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὅλος, from Proto-Indo-European *solh₂wós from *solh₂- (“whole”). The adjective όλος, also as pronoun when referring or replacing a noun.</div><b>όλα μαζί</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>altogether, all together</div><b>όλα του γάμου δύσκολα κι η νύφη γκαστρωμένη</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial, humorous, idiomatic) to add insult to injury, to make matters worse, to add fuel to the fire (said of occasions when things that are already in a difficult situation get worse or have an additional complication)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν φτάνει που μου είχε σκάσει το λάστιχο, πετάχτηκε και ένα χαλίκι και μου ’σπάσε το παρμπρίζ. <b>Όλα του γάμου δύσκολα</b>! - It&#x27;s not enough that my tyre had burst; to make matters worse, a pebble flew up and broke my windshield!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;Everything about the wedding is difficult and the bride is knocked up (pregnant)&quot;.</div><b>όλασπρος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine όλασπρη, neuter όλασπρο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ολόασπρος">ολόασπρος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> όλ- + άσπρος.</div><b>όλισβος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όλισβoι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(dated) dildo</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> A learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὄλισβος.</div><b>όλμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) holmium</div><b>όλο</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. always, constantly, nonstop</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αυτή <b>όλο</b> παραπονιέται. - She complains constantly.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. nothing but, full of, filled with (only)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στην οικογένειά του είναι <b>όλο</b> γιατροί. - In his family, there&#x27;s nothing but doctors.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (with <a href="bword://και">και</a>) ever, x and x-er (increasingly)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όλο</b> και ψηλότερα - higher and higher, ever higher</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅλον, from ὅλος. Used adverbially since Ancient times.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://όλος">όλος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://όλος">όλος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inflected forms.</div><b>όλοι ανεξαιρέτως</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all without exception</div><b>όλοι οι δρόμοι οδηγούν στη Ρώμη</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>all roads lead to Rome</div><b>όλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine όλη, neuter όλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. whole, entire</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όλη</b> η αλήθεια - the whole truth</div>
  <div class='def'>2. all</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όλα</b> τα παιδιά - all the children</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅλος, from Proto-Indo-European *solh₂wós from *solh₂- (“whole”).</div><b>όμηρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural όμηροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hostage</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅμηρος.</div><b>όμικρον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) omicron, the 15th letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὂ μικρόν, in contrast to ὦ μέγα. It took its name from the round shape of the Phoenician letter 𐤏 ayin, not from its name / ἄλφα from aleph, βήτα / βῆτα from beth, etc.)</div><b>όμιλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όμιλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>club, association (often relating to a sport)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Α.Π.Ο. Λεβαδειακός (Levadiakos F.C. - Levadiakos Football Club)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From the Ancient Greek ὅμιλος.</div><b>όμοιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine όμοια, neuter όμοιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. similar, alike, like</div>
  <div class='def'>2. geminate</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όμοια</b> σύμφωνα - geminate consonant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅμοιος.</div><b>όμορφα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://όμορφος">όμορφος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nicely</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(colloquial) behave!</div><b>όμορφος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine όμορφη, neuter όμορφο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>beautiful, pretty, handsome (especially of objects, pictures etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Byzantine Greek ὄμορφος &lt; ἔμορφος, from the Ancient Greek εὔμορφος, from εὖ + μορφή.</div><b>όμπλαστ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) oblast</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Russian о́бласть.</div><b>όμποε</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) oboe</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian oboe, from French hautbois.</div><b>όμως</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. however, but, although, though, still, yet</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (for emphasis) but still, but yet, but also, but then, but...though</div>
  <div class='ex'>Επιτέλους βρήκα δουλειά, αλλά <b>όμως</b> είναι πολύ κουραστική. - I finally found a job, but it&#x27;s still so tiring.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὅμως.</div><b>όναρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://όνειρο">όνειρο</a></div><b>όνειρο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όνειρα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. dream (imaginary events seen while sleeping)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. dream (aspiration)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄνειρος.</div><b>όνομα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ονόματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. name</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>όνομά</b> μου είναι Σαμ. - My name is Sam.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) name, reputation</div>
  <div class='ex'>το καλό <b>όνομα</b> της εταιρείας - the company&#x27;s good name</div>
  <div class='def'>3. noun (sensu lato), a word class including substantives (nouns, sensu stricto) and adjectives</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄνομα, from Proto-Indo-European *h₁nómn̥.</div><b>όνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine or feminine (plural όνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(archaic, formal) donkey, ass (Equus asinus or Equus africanus)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄνος.</div><b>όντως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) indeed, really, truly, in fact</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Όντως</b> ήταν εδώ, αλλά τώρα έχει φύγει. - He really was here but he&#x27;s left.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄντος, masculine and neuter singular genitive of ὤν, present participle of εἰμί.</div><b>όξινος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine όξινη, neuter όξινο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. acidic</div>
  <div class='def'>2. acidic, vinegary, acid</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὄξινος.</div><b>όξου</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(obsolete) Genitive singular of: όξος see ὄξος</div><b>όξυνση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) sharpening</div><b>όπα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hey!, olé! (indicating good cheer, often used during dancing, expressing fun, enthusiasm and excitement)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. Indicating admiration and surprise.</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Όπα</b>! για κοίτα ποιος ήρθε! - Hey! Look who&#x27;s arrived!</div>
  <div class='def'>3. Indicating endearment and care, accompanying singing to infants.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. whoa! (a command to stop)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. oops, expression for acknowledgment of a minor mistake.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Extension of οπ! (op!), οnomatopoeic from Turkish hop. (compare with Portuguese opa, English hop).</div><b>όπερ έδει δείξαι</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>quod erat demonstrandum, QED</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅπερ ἔδει δεῖξαι.</div><b>όπερα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural όπερες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. opera (theatrical work combining drama, music and song)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο «Μαγικός Αυλός» είναι μια από τις πιο γνωστές <b>όπερες</b> του Μότσαρτ. - The Magic Flute is one of Mozart&#x27;s best known operas.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) opera house, opera (building designed for the performance of such works)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο πατέρας μου πάει μια φορά κάθε μήνα στην <b>όπερα</b>. - My father goes to the opera once every month.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Italian opera.</div><b>όπιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>opium (addictive narcotic drug obtained from the dried juice of unripe pods of the opium poppy, Papaver somniferum)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄπιον.</div><b>όπισθεν</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic, formal, with genitive) behind, at the back</div>
  <div class='ex'>Κατοικεί στην Οδό Ιπποκράτους, <b>όπισθεν</b> του κολυμβητηρίου. - He lives on Ippokratous Street, behind the public swimming pool.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. astern (backward)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Όπισθεν</b> ολοταχώς! - Full astern!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄπισθεν.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) reverse (gear in car or motorbike that reverses the vehicle)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Έβαλε <b>όπισθεν</b> αντί για πρώτη και τράκαρε. - He put his car in reverse instead of first gear and crashed.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄπισθεν.</div><b>όπλο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όπλα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. gun, rifle, etc</div>
  <div class='def'>2. personal weapon, item of small arms</div>
  <div class='def'>3. a top-level branch of the Greek army</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅπλον.</div><b>όποιος</b>
  <div><i>det, masculine (feminine όποια, neuter όποιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whoever, whichever, whatever</div>
  <div class='ex'>χρωμάτισε με <b>όποιο</b> χρώμα θέλεις - painted in whichever colour you wish</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὅποιος, morphologically ό- + ποιος (poios).</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>pron, masculine (feminine όποια, neuter όποιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whoever, whichever, whatever, any</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όποιος</b> θέλει, μπορεί να διαβάσει το βιβλίο - whoever wants to may read the book</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὅποιος, morphologically ό- + ποιος (poios).</div><b>όποιος ανακατεύεται με τα πίτουρα, τον τρώνε οι κότες</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>if you lie down with dogs, you get up with fleas (those who get involved with undesirable people run the risk of being implicated themselves)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;Whoever gets mixed up with the bran gets eaten by the hens&quot;.</div><b>όποιος καεί με τον χυλό, φυσάει και το γιαούρτι</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>once bitten, twice shy (those who have been hurt in the past are doubly cautious)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;Whoever gets burnt with porridge, even blows on yoghurt&quot;.</div><b>όποτε</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>whenever</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν γίνεται να με βρίζεις <b>όποτε</b> έχεις νεύρα. - You should not insult me whenever you get angry.</div><b>όπου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. where, wherever</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η πόλη <b>όπου</b> ζει η μητέρα μου. - The town where my mother lives</div>
  <div class='def'>2. that</div>
  <div class='def'>3. when</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὅπου.</div><b>όπου λαλούν πολλοί κοκόροι αργεί να ξημερώσει</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>too many cooks spoil the broth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;Where many roosters crow, dawn is slow to come&quot;.</div><b>όπου να &#x27;ναι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>any minute now, any moment now (very soon)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Όπου να ’ναι</b> θα φτάσει το πλοίο τους. - Any moment now their ship will arrive.</div><b>όπως</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as, like, however</div>
  <div class='ex'>άρχισαν να μιλούν <b>όπως</b> πρώτα - they started to talk as before</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅπως.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>prep</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as, like</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όπως</b> τον παλιό καλό καιρό - like the good old days</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅπως.</div><b>όπως και να έχει</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. anyhow, in any manner, in any way</div>
  <div class='def'>2. in any case, anyhow</div><b>όπως στρώσεις, θα κοιμηθείς</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as you make your bed, so you must lie in it</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;As you make the bed you&#x27;ll sleep&quot;.</div><b>όραμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οράματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vision, goal</div>
  <div class='def'>2. supernatural vision</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅραμα.</div><b>όραση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural οράσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vision, sight, eyesight</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅρασις.</div><b>όργανο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όργανα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. organ, pipe organ, church organ</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) any musical instrument</div>
  <div class='ex'>μουσικό <b>όργανο</b> - musical instrument</div>
  <div class='def'>3. body organ</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η καρδιά είναι ένα <b>όργανο</b> του ανθρώπινου σώματος. - The heart is an organ of the human body.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. equipment, appliance</div>
  <div class='def'>5. officer</div>
  <div class='ex'>τα <b>όργανα</b> της τάξεως - the servants of the law</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄργανον.</div><b>όργιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όργια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. ancient religious ceremony</div>
  <div class='def'>2. sexual orgy</div><b>όρεξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ορέξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. appetite (desire for food or drink)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Απόψε έχω <b>όρεξη</b> για πίτσα. - Tonight, I feel like pizza.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) appetite, craving, longing, desire</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν έχω <b>όρεξη</b> σήμερα να πάω σχολείο. - I don&#x27;t feel like school today.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄρεξις.</div><b>όρθρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όρθροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orthros, last of the canonical hours (held shortly before dawn)</div><b>όριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter</i></div>
  <div class='def'>limit, boundary; border, dividing line</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὅριον diminutive of Ancient Greek ὅρος, from Proto-Hellenic *wórwos (whence Mycenaean Greek 𐀺𐀺 (wo-wo)), from Proto-Indo-European *werw-. Compare Latin urvō (“to trace, mark out bounds”).</div><b>όρισμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορίσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>argument (independent variable, function argument)</div><b>όρκα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural όρκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>orca, killer whale</div><b>όρκη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural όρκες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://όρκα">όρκα</a></div><b>όρκος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όρκοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oath (solemn pledge)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅρκος.</div><b>όρμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όρμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>cove, small bay</div><b>όρνεο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όρνεα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://όρνιο">όρνιο</a></div><b>όρνιθα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural όρνιθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hen, female chicken</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄρνις.</div><b>όρνιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όρνια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. vulture, especially griffon vulture</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (broadly) bird of prey</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (figuratively) stupid person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄρνεον.</div><b>όρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. term (word, phrase; limitation, restriction)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. definition, stipulation</div>
  <div class='def'>3. clause</div>
  <div class='def'>4. article</div>
  <div class='def'>5. mount, mountain</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>όρος</b> Έβερεστ είναι το ψηλότερο βουνό της οροσειράς των Ιμαλαΐων. - Mount Everest is the highest mountain in the Himalayan massif.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὅρος.</div><b>όροφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όροφοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>floor, storey, tier</div>
  <div class='ex'>Σήμερα στέκομαι στο πιο ψηλό μπαλκόνι της γειτονιάς· είναι ο δέκατος <b>όροφος</b>, εκπληκτική θέα. - Today I&#x27;m standing on the highest balcony in the neighbourhood; it&#x27;s on the tenth floor, amazing view.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὄροφος.</div><b>όρυγμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ορύγματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (literary) trench</div>
  <div class='def'>2. trench (a dug-out space in the ground to hide from the enemy)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὄρυγμα.</div><b>όρυζα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) rice (grain and plant)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄρυζα.</div><b>όρυξ</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>oryx (mammal)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ὄρυξ.</div><b>όρχις</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όρχεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>testicle</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄρχις. Compare the inherited αρχίδι.</div><b>όσμιο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) osmium</div><b>όσον</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>archaic form of <a href="bword://όσος">όσος</a></div><b>όσος</b>
  <div><i>pron, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>as many as, as much as</div>
  <div class='ex'>τ<b>όσο</b> μεγάλο <b>όσο</b> ένας ελέφαντας - as big as an elephant</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅσος.</div><b>όσπριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όσπρια)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>legume (seed of leguminous plants: pea, bean, etc)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄσπριον.</div><b>όστρακο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όστρακα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. seashell, shell</div>
  <div class='def'>2. ostracon (a fragment of a vase or other earthenware vessel, especially one used to cast a vote during the Ancient Greek process of ostracism)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄστρακον.</div><b>όστρια</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) the ostro (a southerly Mediterranean wind)</div><b>όσφρηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) smell</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄσφρησις.</div><b>όσχεο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural όσχεα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>scrotum</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄσχεον, ὄσχη.</div><b>όταν</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>when, while, during, whenever, if</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Όταν</b> είστε στην Αθήνα. - When you are in Athens.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅταν.</div><b>όταν έχεις τέτοιους φίλους, τι τους θέλεις τους εχθρούς</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>with friends like these who needs enemies</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;When you have such friends, what do you want enemies for&quot;.</div><b>όταν λείπει η γάτα χορεύουν τα ποντίκια</b>
  <div><i>proverb</i></div>
  <div class='def'>when the cat&#x27;s away, the mice will play</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> The literal meaning is &quot;When the cat is away, the mice dance&quot;.</div><b>ότι</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. that</div>
  <div class='ex'>Αποφάσισε <b>ότι</b> έπρεπε να φύγει. - He decided that it had to go.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (rare) because, for</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χαίρομαι <b>ότι</b> δικαιώθηκαν οι προβλέψεις μου. - I am glad because my predictions were vindicated.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὅτι.</div><b>όφελος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οφέλη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. profit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. benefit</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὄφελος.</div><b>όφις</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>Katharevousa form of <a href="bword://φίδι">φίδι</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄφις.</div><b>όχημα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural οχήματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>vehicle (a conveyance)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄχημα.</div><b>όχθη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural όχθες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. riverbank, bank</div>
  <div class='def'>2. lakeside, lake shore</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄχθη.</div><b>όχι</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. no, not (negation)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όχι</b> καλά - not good</div>
  <div class='def'>2. not, in elliptical clauses</div>
  <div class='ex'>Χρειάζομαι χρήματα, <b>όχι</b> υποσχέσεις. - I need money, not promises.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὄχι, ὤχι, from Ancient Greek οὐχί.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>no (negative reply)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Μιλάτε αγγλικά; <b>Όχι</b>. - Do you speak English? No.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὄχι, ὤχι, from Ancient Greek οὐχί.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) no</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το «<b>όχι</b>» των Ελλήνων … - The &quot;no&quot; of the Greeks …</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὄχι, ὤχι, from Ancient Greek οὐχί.</div><b>όχι Γιάννης, Γιαννάκης</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>A saying referring to terms said in a few different ways: &quot;You say potáto, I say potāto&quot;.</div><b>όχι άλλο</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>no more, enough</div><b>όχι ακόμα</b>
  <div><i>phrase</i></div>
  <div class='def'>not yet</div><b>όχλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural όχλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(derogatory) mob, populace</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄχλος.</div><b>όψη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural όψεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>appearance, look, aspect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὄψις.</div><b>όψιμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine όψιμη, neuter όψιμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. late; late in a season</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όψιμα</b> φρούτα - late fruits</div>
  <div class='def'>2. late; late in a period of time</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>όψιμη</b> αρχαιότητα - late antiquity</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (of a function or physiological condition) delayed, who appears later than expected</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (of a plant) which ripens slowly (later than others)</div>
  <div class='def'>5. (of a human action) too late (also used sarcastically)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το <b>όψιμο</b> ενδιαφέρον της κυβέρνησης δε θα λύσει κανένα πρόβλημα. - The government&#x27;s delayed interest will not solve any problem. (will solve no problem)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὄψιμος from ὀψέ.</div><b>ύαινα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ύαινες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hyena</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) malicious person, especially woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Ancient Greek ὕαινα, from ὗς / σῦς + -αινα (-aina).</div><b>ύαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ύαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. glass (the hard transparent material)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. glazing</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Borrowed from Ancient Greek ὕαλος. Doublet of γυαλί.</div><b>ύβρις</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ύβρεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (archaic, formal) insult, abuse (form of speech against someone deliberately intended to be rude)</div>
  <div class='ex'>ξεστομίζω <b>ύβρεις</b> εναντίον κάποιου - to utter insults to someone</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (archaic, formal) insult, affront (open or intentional offense)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η εκλογή του Τραμπ αποτελεί <b>ύβριν</b> για τις ΗΠΑ. - The election of Trump is an insult to the United States.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. (archaic, formal) hubris (excessive pride, presumption or arrogance, especially toward the Ancient Greek gods)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕβρις.</div><b>ύδατα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter plural</i></div>
  <div class='def'>waters</div>
  <div class='ex'>χωρικά <b>ύδατα</b> - territorial waters</div><b>ύδραρθρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (usually uncountable, plural ύδραρθροι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable, usually) hydrarthrosis</div><b>ύδρευση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>water supply</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὕδρευσις with -ση (-si) ending.</div><b>ύδωρ</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ύδατα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(formal) water</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕδωρ.</div><b>ύελος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ύελοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ύαλος">ύαλος</a></div><b>ύλη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ύλες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. matter</div>
  <div class='def'>2. matter, stuff, material, substance</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕλη.</div><b>ύμνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ύμνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hymn, anthem, song</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὕμνος.</div><b>ύπαιθρος</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) countryside (rural land, land without buildings)</div><b>ύπαρξη</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υπάρξεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. existence (the state of being, existing, or occurring)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. a creature, a human being; mainly a beautiful girl or woman</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕπαρξις. Synchronically, from υπάρχω.</div><b>ύπνος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ύπνοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>sleep</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὕπνος, from Proto-Indo-European *supnós (“sleep”).
Cognates include Mariupol Greek и́пно, English sweven, Sanskrit स्वप्न, Spanish sueño, Italian sonno and Latvian sapnis.</div><b>ύποπτος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ύποπτη, neuter ύποπτο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. suspicious, suspect</div>
  <div class='def'>2. incriminating</div>
  <div class='def'>3. sleazy</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕποπτος.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ύποπτοι, feminine ύποπτη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>suspect</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕποπτος.</div><b>ύπουλος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ύπουλη, neuter ύπουλο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. underhanded, sneaky, treacherous (dishonest, deceitful, sly)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. insidious (producing harm in a stealthy, often gradual, manner)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ύπουλη</b> αρρώστια - insidious disease</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὕπουλος, from Ancient Greek ὕπουλος.</div><b>ύπτια</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supinely, on your back</div><b>ύπτιος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ύπτια, neuter ύπτιο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>supine, on your back</div><b>ύσσωπος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ύσσωποι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>hyssop (herb)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕσσωπος.</div><b>ύστερα</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>after, afterwards; then; later</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ύστερος">ύστερος</a></div><b>ύστερο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ύστερα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>afterbirth</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕστερον.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. accusative masculine singular of <a href="bword://ύστερος">ύστερος</a></div>
  <div class='def'>2. nominative/accusative/vocative neuter singular of <a href="bword://ύστερος">ύστερος</a></div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕστερον.</div><b>ύστερος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ύστερη, neuter ύστερο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. later, posterior, next, subsequent</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (literary) last, final, ultimate</div>
  <div class='ex'>Tο <b>ύστερο</b> φιλί. - The last kiss</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ῠ̔́στερος, comparative form of an unattested adjective with superlative ὕστᾰτος from Proto-Indo-European *údteros (“higher, outer”) from *úd (“upwards, away”) which would make it cognate with English out. Doublet of στερνός via Byzantine Greek ὑστερνός from syncope of ὑστερινός.</div><b>ύτριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>alternative form of <a href="bword://ύττριο">ύττριο</a></div><b>ύττριο</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (uncountable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(uncountable) yttrium</div><b>ύφαλος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ύφαλοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>reef (rock near surface of the water but beneath it)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕφαλος.</div><b>ύφασμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υφάσματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>fabric, textile, cloth, material</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὕφασμα.</div><b>ύφεση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural υφέσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. depression (area of lowered air pressure)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Υπάρχει βαρομετρική <b>ύφεση</b> πάνω από την Κρήτη. - There&#x27;s a barometric depression over Crete.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (of notes) flat (lowered by one semitone)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το Κοντσέρτο για Φαγκότο σε σι <b>ύφεση</b> ματζόρε του Μότσαρτ. - The Concerto for Bassoon in B-flat major by Mozart.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. flat (♭, the symbol itself)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ο βιολιστής έπαιξε λάθος νότα, διότι ο αντιγραφέας ξέχασε να βάλει <b>ύφεση</b> μπροστά στη νότα. - The violinist played the wrong note since the copyist forgot to put a flat in front of the note.</div>
  <div class='def'>4. (usually) remission, abatement, subsidence (relaxing of tension, improvement)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Η κατάσταση του ασθενούς παρουσιάζει <b>ύφεση</b>. - There is some improvement in the condition of the patient.</div>
  <div class='def'>5. détente (relaxing of tension between countries)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Στη δεκαετία του 80, υπήρξε <b>ύφεση</b> μεταξύ των ΗΠΑ και της ΕΣΣΔ. - In the 1980s, there was a détente between the US and the USSR.</div>
  <div class='def'>6. recession, downturn, slump, depression (period of reduced economic activity)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Το 2008 ξεκίνησε η παγκόσμια οικονομική <b>ύφεση</b>. - In 2008, the global economic downturn began.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Koine Greek ὕφεσις, from Ancient Greek ὑφίημι.</div><b>ύφος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ύφη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. style (manner of doing things)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. style (manner of doing things); register</div>
  <div class='def'>3. expression (one&#x27;s mood as reflected by facial features)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὕφος, from ὑφή + -ος (-os, “action noun suffix”). For the &quot;linguistic register&quot; sense, semantic loan from English register; for the &quot;expression&quot; sense, semantic loan from French style.</div><b>ύψιλον</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (indeclinable)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(indeclinable) upsilon, the 20th letter of the modern Greek alphabet.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὖ ψῑλόν.</div><b>ύψος</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural ύψη)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. height (of a person)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. height (distance from bottom to top)</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὕψος.</div><b>ύψωμα</b>
  <div><i>noun, neuter (plural υψώματα)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. rise, elevation, high ground</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Υψώματα</b> του Γκολάν - Golan Heights</div>
  <div class='def'>2. communion wafer, host</div><b>ώθηση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. pushing, urging</div>
  <div class='def'>2. impetus, push, boost (anything that impels; a stimulating factor)</div>
  <div class='ex'>δίνω <b>ώθηση</b> σε - to give/provide an impetus/push/boost to</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Learned borrowing from Koine Greek ὤθησις. By surface analysis, ώθη-) + -ση (-si).</div><b>ώμος</b>
  <div><i>noun, masculine (plural ώμοι)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>shoulder</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὦμος.</div><b>ώπα</b>
  <div><i>intj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>a variant spelling of <a href="bword://όπα">όπα</a></div><b>ώρα</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ώρες)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. hour (¹⁄₂₄ of a day)</div>
  <div class='def'>2. time (A quantity of availability in time, time of day as indicated by a clock, some specific time, right time, time for something)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Δεν είχα αρκετή <b>ώρα</b>. - I did not have enough time.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὥρα, from Proto-Indo-European *yeh₁- (“year, season”). Cognate with Tsakonian ούρα.</div><b>ώριμα</b>
  <div><i>adj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>nominative/accusative/vocative neuter plural of <a href="bword://ώριμος">ώριμος</a></div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>maturely, ripely</div>
  <div class='ex'>Πήρε <b>ώριμα</b> την απόφαση να παραμείνει στη χώρα. - He maturely took the decision to stay in the country.</div><b>ώριμος</b>
  <div><i>adj, masculine (feminine ώριμη, neuter ώριμο)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. (of fruits) ripe, mature, mellow (ready for reaping or gathering, or having attained perfection)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ώριμα</b> φρούτα - ripe fruit</div>
  <div class='def'>2. (figuratively) mature, ripe (grown up in terms of physical appearance, behaviour or thinking)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ώριμος</b> άνθρωπος - mature person</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> Inherited from Ancient Greek ὥριμος, from Ancient Greek ὥρα. Some senses a semantic loan from French mûr.</div><b>ώσμωση</b>
  <div><i>noun, feminine (plural ωσμώσεις)</i></div>
  <div class='def'>osmosis</div><b>ώσπερ</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>(Katharevousa, archaic, formal, ironic) exactly like</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ομιλούσε με στόμφο <b>ώσπερ</b> ρήτωρ αγορεύων - He was speaking with a pomposity just like a proclaiming orator.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὥσπερ.</div><b>ώσπου</b>
  <div><i>adv</i></div>
  <div class='def'>by the time, when</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>ώσπου</b> να πεις αμήν - before you could say amen</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὥσπου, consisting of ὥς πού.</div>
  <br/><br/>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>until (up to the time of)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήμουν άνεργος, <b>ώσπου</b> με προσέλαβαν στο αεροδρόμιο. - I was unemployed, until they hired me at the airport.</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Byzantine Greek ὥσπου, consisting of ὥς πού.</div><b>ώστε</b>
  <div><i>conj</i></div>
  <div class='def'>1. so that, in order that, in order for (with the intended result that)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Άφησε εκεί την εφημερίδα, <b>ώστε</b> να την δει ο πατέρας σου. - Leave the newspaper there so that your father can see it.</div>
  <div class='def'>2. so, therefore (with the result that)</div>
  <div class='ex'>Ήταν μεθυσμένος, <b>ώστε</b> δεν είδε τον γκρεμό κι έπεσε. - He was drunk so he didn&#x27;t see the cliff and he fell.</div>
  <div class='def'>3. so (consequently, therefore)</div>
  <div class='ex'><b>Ώστε</b> θα ’ρθεις στο γάμο τους! - So you&#x27;ll be coming to their wedding!</div>
  <div class='etym'><b>Etymology:</b> From Ancient Greek ὥστε, consisting of ὥς + τε (te, “and”).</div><b>ώχρα</b>
